eep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:DBN-01","text":"Δεῖ ψευδὲς εἶναι ὅτι ἡ πόλις ἢ ὁ λόγος δύνανται νὰ θεμελιωθῶσιν ἐν ἑαυτοῖς, ἄνευ παιδείας, μνήμης, αἰτίας, καὶ ἔξωθεν κρίσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:DBN-02","CL_JP8E_04:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:DBN-02","text":"Δεῖ ψευδὲς εἶναι ὅτι ἡ μνήμη ἀποθηκεύει αὐτομάτως τὸ γεγονός, ἐπεὶ ἡ διατήρησις ἐξαρτᾶται ἀπὸ ἐπιζῶντας φορεῖς καὶ ἐξουσίαν ἐρμηνείας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:DBN-03","text":"Δεῖ ψευδὲς εἶναι ὅτι ἡ ἐλευθερία ὑπάρχει πρὸ πάσης συγκρούσεως ἢ ὅτι ἡ δύναμις ἀποκαθίσταται ἀμέσως ὡς δικαιοσύνη.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:GNX-05","CL_JP8E_03:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:DBN-04","text":"Δεῖ ψευδὲς εἶναι ὅτι τὸ γενητὸν καὶ αἰσθητὸν ὂν δύναται νὰ εἶναι ἀναίτιον καὶ ἀγέννητον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:GA-04","CL_JP8E_04:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSA-01","text":"Ἀπούσης ἀπολύτου ἐγγυήσεως, οὐδεμία μνήμη, πολιτεία, ἢ κοσμολογία δύναται νὰ κλείσῃ ὁριστικῶς τὸν κύκλον τῆς ἀληθείας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:GA-11","CL_JP8E_04:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSA-02","text":"Ἀπούσης ἀδιασπάστου παγκοσμίου μνήμης, τὸ παρελθὸν προσέρχεται μόνον ὡς θραῦσμα, μαρτυρία, μῦθος, καὶ ἀναγκαία ἐπανερμηνεία.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:BSA-01","CL_JP8E_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSA-03","text":"Ἀδύνατος μένει καθαρὸς χωρισμὸς ἐλευθερίας καὶ ἡγεμονίας, μύθου καὶ ἀληθείας, ἢ πολιτικῆς πράξεως καὶ τῶν ὅρων τῆς κοσμικῆς αἰτιότητος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:MME-07","CL_JP8E_04:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:XD-01","text":"Ἡ πολιτεία διασώζει ἑαυτὴν μόνον ἐὰν ἡ λειτουργικὴ τάξις γένηται συγχρόνως παιδευτικὴ, ἀρχειακὴ, καὶ πολεμικῶς δοκιμαζομένη μηχανή.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-04","CL_JP8E_01:BSD-07","CL_JP8E_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:XD-02","text":"Ἡ καταστροφὴ δὲν καταλύει μόνον τὰ πολιτικὰ σώματα, ἀλλὰ μεταφέρει τὴν ἐξουσίαν πρὸς τοὺς φορεῖς οἳ κατέχουσι τὴν ἐπιλεκτικὴν συνέχειαν τῆς μνήμης.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-05","CL_JP8E_03:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:XD-03","text":"Ἡ ἀξίωσις ἀληθοῦς λόγου μεταβάλλει τὸν μῦθον εἰς διαμεσολαβημένον ἀρχείον, ὃ χρειάζεται αἰτιολογίαν καὶ κοινὴν κρίσιν, οὐχὶ ἁπλῆν πίστιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-08","CL_JP8E_02:BSD-05","CL_JP8E_04:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:XD-04","text":"Ἡ ἀντίστασις πρὸς καθολικὴν δύναμιν μεταφέρει τὴν ἀρετὴν ἀπὸ ἐσωτερικὴν εὐταξίαν εἰς ἐλευθερωτικὴν πράξιν, ἀλλὰ διατηρεῖ ἀνοικτὴν τὴν ἡγεμονικὴν ἀμφισημίαν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-09","CL_JP8E_03:BSD-03","CL_JP8E_03:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:XD-05","text":"Ἡ φυσικὴ αἰτιότης καὶ ἡ πολιτικὴ μνήμη συγκλίνουσιν, διότι ἀμφότεραι ἀρνοῦνται ὅτι τὸ παρόν ἐξηγεῖ ἑαυτὸ ἐξ ἑαυτοῦ.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-08","CL_JP8E_04:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_04:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-01","text":"Ἡ ἱστορία γίνεται πεδίον πολιτικῆς ἐξουσίας, ἐπειδὴ ἡ κατοχὴ τῶν μέσων διασώσεως καθορίζει τί δύναται νὰ ἐμφανισθῇ ὡς ἀρχαιότης, ἀρετή, ἢ ἀλήθεια.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-05","CL_JP8E_02:BSD-01","CL_JP8E_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-02","text":"Ἡ πολιτικὴ φιλοσοφία γίνεται ἀρχαιολογία διακοπῶν καὶ αἰτιῶν, οὐχὶ ἀπλῶς θεωρία παρούσης εὐταξίας.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-08","CL_JP8E_02:BSD-04","CL_JP8E_04:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-03","text":"Ὁ πολιτικὸς ὑποκείμενος παύει νὰ εἶναι αὐτοτελὴς κύριος καὶ γίνεται φύλαξ, μάρτυς, ἐρμηνευτής, καὶ κόμβος ἐν δικτύῳ παιδείας καὶ μνήμης.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-07","CL_JP8E_03:HSI-02","CL_JP8E_04:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-04","text":"Ὁ χρόνος γίνεται μετακαταστροφικὸς καὶ σπειροειδής: οὐδὲν παρελθὸν ἐπιστρέφει αὐτό, ἀλλὰ μόνον ὡς νέα σύνθεσις ἡ ὁποία μεταβάλλει τὸ παρόν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-05","CL_JP8E_03:BSD-06","CL_JP8E_03:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-05","text":"Ἡ ἐπιστημικὴ ταπείνωσις γίνεται ὄρος τῆς πολιτικῆς ἀξιώσεως, διότι ὁ λόγος δεῖ μὲν νὰ ζητῇ αἰτίαν, οὐ δύναται δὲ νὰ κτᾶται πλήρη κατοχὴν αὐτῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-08","CL_JP8E_04:GA-10","CL_JP8E_04:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:RLE-01","text":"Διαίρεσις λειτουργιῶν → παιδεία φύλακος → κοινὸς βίος → πολιτικὴ τάξις → πολεμικὴ δοκιμασία → ἐπανέλεγχος τῆς παιδείας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:RLE-01","CL_JP8E_01:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:RLE-02","text":"Καταστροφή → ἀπώλεια γραφῆς → λήθη → ἀρχειακὴ ἀσυμμετρία → ξένη μαρτυρία → πολιτικὴ ἀνάκτησις → νέα ἐπισφάλεια.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:RLE-02","CL_JP8E_03:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:RLE-03","text":"Μῦθος → ἀμφιβολία → ἐρμηνευτικὴ μεταφορά → κοσμολογικὴ αἰτιολογία → πολιτικὴ ταυτότης → νέα δοκιμασία τοῦ μύθου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:FGS-B05","CL_JP8E_04:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:RLE-04","text":"Κόσμος → ἄνθρωπος → παιδεία → πόλις → ἱστορικὴ μνήμη → ἐπιστροφή πρὸς τὴν ἀνάγκην αἰτίας καὶ παραδείγματος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:RLE-04","CL_JP8E_04:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:RLE-05","text":"Ἑλκτικὸς πόλος τοῦ συστήματος ἐστὶν ὁ πρέπων λόγος ὃς δύναται νὰ συνδέῃ ἀρετήν, μνήμην, αἰτίαν, καὶ πράξιν, χωρὶς νὰ παγιοῦται εἰς τελικὴν βεβαιότητα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:RLE-07","CL_JP8E_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:FGS-B01","text":"Φυσικὴ διαφορά + παιδευτικὴ διαμόρφωσις → λειτουργικὴ κατανομή → πολιτικὴ ἑνότης διὰ διακεκριμένων ἔργων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:FGS-B02","CL_JP8E_03:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:FGS-B02","text":"Κοινὸς βίος + ἀφαίρεσις ἰδιωτικῶν δεσμῶν → κατασκευή πολιτικῆς συγγενείας → κρυφὴ διοίκησις τῆς συνεχείας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:FGS-B04","CL_JP8E_01:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:FGS-B03","text":"Πόλεμος + καθολικὴ ἀπειλή → δοκιμασία πολιτικῆς τάξεως → μεταβολὴ ἀρετῆς εἰς ἐλευθερωτικὴν πράξιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:FGS-B06","CL_JP8E_03:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:FGS-B04","text":"Καταστροφή + ἀπώλεια φορέων → λήθη → διασωθεῖσα ἀρχειακὴ ἀσυμμετρία → ἀνάγκη νέας ἐρμηνευτικῆς συνθέσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:FGS-B01","CL_JP8E_03:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:FGS-B05","text":"Μνήμη + μαρτυρία + κοινὸς ἔλεγχος → ἐλεγχόμενος μῦθος → ἀναθεωρήσιμος ἀληθὴς λόγος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:FGS-B03","CL_JP8E_04:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:FGS-B06","text":"Ἀγέννητον παράδειγμα → γενητὸς κόσμος → ἄνθρωπος → παιδευμένη πόλις → πολιτικὴ ἱστορία ὡς ἐξαρτώμενον ἀποτέλεσμα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:FGS-B05","CL_JP8E_04:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:AFM-B01","text":"Λειτουργικὴ διαίρεσις → παιδευτικὴ κατασκευή χαρακτήρος → κοινὸς βίος → πολιτικὴ ἑνότης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:AFM-B02","CL_JP8E_01:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:AFM-B02","text":"Πολιτικὴ ἑνότης → πόλεμος καὶ ἐξωτερικὴ ἀπειλή → πρακτικὴ ἀπόδειξις ἀρετῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:AFM-B05","CL_JP8E_03:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:AFM-B03","text":"Πρακτικὴ ἀπόδειξις → καταστροφή καὶ φθορά → λήθη τῶν γεγονότων καὶ τῶν πολιτικῶν σωμάτων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:BAS-07","CL_JP8E_03:AFM-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:AFM-B04","text":"Λήθη → μαρτυρικὴ καὶ ἀρχειακὴ ἀνάκτησις → ἀνασύστασις παλαιᾶς πολιτικῆς δυνατότητος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:AFM-B05","CL_JP8E_03:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:AFM-B05","text":"Ἀνασύστασις παρελθόντος → μῦθος ὡς ἐλεγχόμενον ὄργανον → κοσμολογικὴ καὶ αἰτιολογικὴ θεμελίωσις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:MME-04","CL_JP8E_04:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:AFM-B06","text":"Κοσμολογικὴ θεμελίωσις → ἀναθεωρήσιμος πολιτικὴ ταυτότης → νέος κύκλος μνήμης, κρίσεως, καὶ πράξεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:AFM-B05","CL_JP8E_04:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MME-01","text":"Ἡ πόλις ἀναταξινομεῖται ἀπὸ παροῦσα κοινότης εἰς πολυεπίπεδον μηχανὴν ἔργων, γενέσεως, μνήμης, καὶ δοκιμασίας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:MME-01","CL_JP8E_04:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MME-02","text":"Ἡ ἄγνοια ἀναταξινομεῖται ἀπὸ ἔνδειξιν ἀπουσίας εἰς σύμπτωμα διακεκομμένης ἀρχειακῆς καὶ θεσμικῆς συνεχείας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:MME-02","CL_JP8E_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MME-03","text":"Ἡ γεωγραφία ἀναφαίνεται ὡς αἰτία ἀνισότητος μνήμης, πολιτισμικῆς ἐπιβιώσεως, καὶ ἐρμηνευτικῆς κυριαρχίας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:MME-05","CL_JP8E_02:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MME-04","text":"Ἡ διάκρισις μύθου καὶ ἀληθείας παύει νὰ εἶναι σταθερὸν δίπολον καὶ γίνεται πρόβλημα ὀρθῆς μεταφορᾶς, αἰτιολογίας, καὶ ἐλέγχου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:MME-02","CL_JP8E_04:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MME-05","text":"Ἡ πολιτικὴ ἐλευθερία ἀναταξινομεῖται ὡς σχέσις κινδύνου, συλλογικῆς δυνάμεως, καὶ μνημονικῆς διασώσεως, οὐχὶ ὡς φυσικὴ ἰδιότης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:BSD-03","CL_JP8E_03:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B01","text":"Ἐπιφανειακῶς, τὸ σύστημα πραγματεύεται φύσεις, φύλακας, πόλεις, πολέμους, γραφάς, ἱερεῖς, καταστροφάς, μύθους, καὶ κόσμον γενητόν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:ALT-B01","CL_JP8E_03:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B02","text":"Δομικῶς, ἐνεργοῦσι κατανεμημένοι μηχανισμοὶ λειτουργικοῦ χωρισμοῦ, παιδείας, ἀρχειακῆς συνεχείας, φυσικῆς φθορᾶς, καὶ αἰτιολογικῆς ἱεραρχίας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:ALT-B02","CL_JP8E_04:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B03","text":"Μεταστρατηγικῶς, ἡ πάλη μεταφέρεται εἰς τὴν διοίκησιν τῶν ὅρων ὑφ᾽ οὓς παράγονται ἑνότης, ἐλευθερία, μνήμη, καὶ ἑρμηνευτικὸν κύρος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:ALT-B03","CL_JP8E_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B04","text":"Ὀντολογικῶς καὶ χρονικῶς, ἡ πραγματικότης εἶναι γενητὴ, αἰτιατὴ, κυκλικῶς φθειρομένη, καὶ μόνον μερικῶς ἀνακτήσιμος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:ALT-B04","CL_JP8E_04:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B05","text":"Μεθοδολογικῶς καὶ ἐπιτελεστικῶς, ὁ λόγος πράττει ὅπερ λέγει: ἀνασυνθέτει θραύσματα, ἱεραρχεῖ αἰτίας, μεταφέρει μύθους, καὶ ἀναβάλλει τὴν τελικὴν βεβαιότητα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:ALT-B05","CL_JP8E_04:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:FL-01","text":"Ἡ φύσις προβάλλεται ὡς θεμέλιον τάξεως, ἀλλὰ συγχρόνως μεταχειρίζεται ὡς διοικητὴ ὕλη παιδείας, ἀναπαραγωγῆς, καὶ γεωγραφικῆς διασώσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:FL-01","CL_JP8E_01:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:FL-02","text":"Ἡ ἀλήθεια ζητεῖ κοινὴν κρίσιν, ἀλλ᾽ ἡ κρίσις ἐξαρτᾶται ἀπὸ ἐπιλεκτικῶς διασωθέντας φορεῖς, θείαν εὔνοιαν, καὶ ἀνεπαρκῶς ἐγγυημένας μαρτυρίας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:FL-01","CL_JP8E_04:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:FL-03","text":"Ἡ ἐλευθερωτικὴ ἀποστολὴ τῆς πόλεως ἀπαιτεῖ ὑπεροχὴν ἰσχύος, ἥτις δὲ ἀπειλεῖ νὰ ἀναπαραγάγῃ τὸ ἡγεμονικὸν πρότυπον ὃ ἀντιμάχεται.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:FL-01","CL_JP8E_03:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:FL-04","text":"Ἡ ἀναγκαιότης τῆς αἰτίας ἰσχυροποιεῖ τὸν λόγον, ἀλλ᾽ ἡ ἀπροσδιοριστία τῆς αἰτίας ἐμποδίζει τὴν τελικὴν αὐτοθεμελίωσίν του.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:FL-03","CL_JP8E_04:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MP-01","text":"Ἡ μνήμη μεταλλάσσεται ὅταν παύῃ νὰ σημαίνῃ διατήρησιν παρελθόντος καὶ γίνεται δύναμις ἀναταξινομήσεως τοῦ παρόντος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:MP-01","CL_JP8E_04:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MP-02","text":"Ὁ πόλεμος μεταλλάσσεται ὅταν ἀπὸ ἔργον τάξεως γίνεται τεκμήριον ὅλης τῆς πολιτείας καὶ ὄργανον καθολικῆς ἐλευθερώσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:RMD-05","CL_JP8E_03:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MP-03","text":"Ὁ μῦθος μεταλλάσσεται ὅταν ἀπὸ ἀμφίβολον ἀφήγημα γίνεται ἐρμηνευτικὸν πρότυπον τοῦ πραγματικοῦ.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:RMD-03","CL_JP8E_04:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MP-04","text":"Ἡ πόλις μεταλλάσσεται ὅταν ἀπὸ τοπικὸν σῶμα γίνεται ἀποτέλεσμα κοσμικῆς γενέσεως καὶ φορεὺς παγκοσμίου πολιτικῆς ἀποστολῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:MP-03","CL_JP8E_04:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:HSI-01","text":"Ἡ πραγματικότης νοεῖται ὡς ἱεραρχικὴ καὶ κυκλικὴ μηχανή, ἐν ᾗ αἰτία, γένεσις, φθορά, μνήμη, καὶ πολιτικὴ μορφὴ ἀλληλοπαράγονται.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:HSI-01","CL_JP8E_04:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:HSI-02","text":"Ὁ ἄνθρωπος νοεῖται ὡς ἐνδιάμεσος κόμβος μεταξὺ κοσμικῆς αἰτίας, πολιτικῆς παιδείας, πολεμικῆς πράξεως, καὶ θραυσματικῆς μνήμης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:HSI-02","CL_JP8E_04:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:HSI-03","text":"Ὁ χρόνος νοεῖται ὡς σπείρα ἐπαναλήψεως, ἐν ᾗ ἡ καταστροφὴ οὐκ ἀφανίζει τὴν δυνατότητα ἀλλὰ μεταβάλλει τοὺς ὅρους τῆς ἐπανεμφανίσεως αὐτῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:HSI-03","CL_JP8E_03:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:HSI-04","text":"Ἡ δυνατὴ πλοήγησις δὲν συνίσταται εἰς ἀπόδρασιν ἐκ τῆς μηχανῆς, ἀλλὰ εἰς φύλαξιν μνήμης, ἔλεγχον γεφυρῶν, καὶ διάκρισιν ἐλευθερώσεως ἀπὸ κυριαρχίαν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:HSI-04","CL_JP8E_04:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MRT-B01","text":"Τὰ ἀξιώματα ἀρκοῦσιν πρὸς ἀνασύστασιν τῆς κύριας μηχανῆς: λειτουργικὴ τάξις, δοκιμασία, καταστροφή, μνήμη, κοσμολογικὴ θεμελίωσις, καὶ ἀναθεωρήσιμος λόγος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:MRT-B01","CL_JP8E_04:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MRT-B02","text":"Ἡ ἀρχιτεκτονικὴ εἶναι συνεκτικωτάτη ἐν τῇ συνδέσει διακοπῆς μνήμης, κατανεμημένης διασώσεως, πολιτικῆς τάξεως, καὶ πρακτικῆς δοκιμασίας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:MRT-B03","CL_JP8E_02:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MRT-B03","text":"Ὑποκαθορισμένα μένουσιν ἡ νομιμοποίησις τῆς ἱεραρχίας, τὸ μέτρον τῆς ἀξιοπιστίας τῆς μαρτυρίας, καὶ ἡ ταυτότης τοῦ κοσμικοῦ αἰτίου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:MRT-B04","CL_JP8E_04:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MRT-B04","text":"Ἡ πλήρης ἀνακατασκευή δὲν δύναται νὰ ἀφαιρέσῃ τὴν παραγωγικὴν ἀμφισημίαν τῆς ἰσχύος, τοῦ μύθου, καὶ τῆς ἀβεβαίου ἀληθείας, διότι αὗται κινοῦσιν αὐτὸ τὸ σύστημα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:MRT-B05","CL_JP8E_02:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261014T233233032340Z_a6b42997","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Jp8E L1 01","source_sha256":"bacacab30f70d8a5a8d3e010aff87fdf9cfef02294edad18d2c31a16cb71bb3c","sha256":"0134b08139c6c1239d4edb47fb619bf6a395b10b84bf91b54d34e10ddfbb3fc3","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-14T23:32:33.035658+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:AS-01","text":"The problem is how political order, historical memory, and true discourse may be constituted in a world of decay, forgetfulness, becoming, and conflict.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:AS-01","CL_JP8E_02:BAS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:AS-02","text":"The polity is no longer conceived as a self-sufficient present community, but as a mechanism for the distribution of functions, education, memory, generation, and resistance.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:GA-04","CL_JP8E_04:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:AS-03","text":"Truth passes from a static property into a revisable process of reconstruction, testimony, explanation, and common testing.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:GA-12","CL_JP8E_04:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:AS-04","text":"Forgetfulness does not signify a void of events, but an interruption of the bearers able to preserve, interpret, and legitimate the past.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:GA-01","CL_JP8E_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:AS-05","text":"The movement of the system is spiral: order produces testing, testing loss, loss memory, and memory a new political and cosmological synthesis.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:AS-08","CL_JP8E_02:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:AS-06","text":"External threat and natural catastrophe function as two crises of the same political mechanism: the former tests action, the latter the continuity of discourse.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:XD-01","CL_JP8E_03:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:AS-07","text":"Cosmology is not added as an external ornament, but makes political form a product of cause, paradigm, education, and memory.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:XD-04","CL_JP8E_04:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:AS-08","text":"The provisional closure of the system is not certainty, but fitting discourse, able to return to memory, judgment, action, and explanation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:GA-08","CL_JP8E_04:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Political unity is produced through functional differentiation and not through undifferentiated uniformity.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:GA-01","CL_JP8E_03:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-02","text":"[Mutating] Nature ceases to be merely a criterion of allocation and becomes material for education, geographical preservation, and political planning.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:GA-08","CL_JP8E_02:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Political virtue is demonstrated only in action, danger, war, and external testing.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:GA-10","CL_JP8E_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Memory is distributed political work of bearers, places, writings, institutions, and common judgment.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:GA-05","CL_JP8E_03:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Catastrophe and forgetfulness are not external deviations, but mechanisms regulating what can be known and what is compelled to be recovered.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:GA-08","CL_JP8E_03:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Myth is not simply opposed to truth, but can function as a controlled bearer of lost historical and cosmological knowledge.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:GA-09","CL_JP8E_04:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Political identity is the product of the chain: cosmos, becoming, education, memory, law, and resistance.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:GA-12","CL_JP8E_04:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] True discourse requires cause, paradigm, memory, the fitting, and common examination, but never attains absolute self-guarantee.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:GA-01","CL_JP8E_04:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Freedom is produced as resistance to a power of enslavement, but liberating force is never wholly separated from hegemonic possibility.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:GA-12","CL_JP8E_03:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:RMD-01","text":"The division of functions retains its identity, but changes from social allocation into an architecture of memory, knowledge, and power.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:RMD-01","CL_JP8E_03:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:RMD-02","text":"The guardian is displaced from martial protector into a node of education, community, generation, archive, and political judgment.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:RMD-03","CL_JP8E_01:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:RMD-03","text":"Memory mutates from the replenishment of what is absent into an active archival power that distributes political legitimation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:RMD-04","text":"Catastrophe reverses its sign: from an incidental loss it becomes a producer of historical time and of the necessity of interpretation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:RLE-05","CL_JP8E_03:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:RMD-05","text":"Myth is absorbed into a higher structure, in which metaphor, archival preservation, and cosmological explanation cooperate.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:BSD-05","CL_JP8E_04:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:CGD-01","text":"Details of marriage, food, festival, persons, and particular narratives remain local; the administration of bonds, transmissions, and experiences is scaled into a universal political logic.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:CGD-02","CL_JP8E_02:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:CGD-02","text":"Individual testimony and local recollection bridge the parts only when they become links in a distributed chain of memory.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:CGD-06","CL_JP8E_03:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:CGD-03","text":"Martial testing ceases to be the function of a single race and becomes a catalyst of transition from internal good order to a universal political claim.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:RMD-05","CL_JP8E_03:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:CGD-04","text":"Cosmogonic explanation is scaled up when it ceases to be discourse about the cosmos and becomes a condition of political legitimation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:CGD-06","CL_JP8E_04:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:GNX-01","text":"A polity that identifies unity with indiscriminate mixture and uncontrolled interchange of functions is rejected.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:GNX-01","CL_JP8E_03:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:GNX-02","text":"Political or cosmological discourse that remains self-sufficient, untested, without cause, and independent of the bearers of transmission is rejected.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:GNX-02","CL_JP8E_04:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:GNX-03","text":"Linear history is rejected, since decay and spatially unequal preservation interrupt the accumulation of knowledge.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:GNX-05","CL_JP8E_02:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:GNX-04","text":"The idea that the present, perception, or contemporary recognition can automatically measure political value is rejected.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:GNX-02","CL_JP8E_04:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that the city or discourse can be grounded in themselves, without education, memory, cause, and external judgment.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:DBN-02","CL_JP8E_04:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that memory automatically stores the event, since preservation depends on surviving bearers and the power of interpretation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that freedom exists prior to every conflict or that power is immediately restored as justice.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:GNX-05","CL_JP8E_03:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that a generated and perceptible being can be without cause and ungenerated.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:GA-04","CL_JP8E_04:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSA-01","text":"In the absence of absolute guarantee, no memory, polity, or cosmology can definitively close the circle of truth.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:GA-11","CL_JP8E_04:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSA-02","text":"In the absence of an unbroken universal memory, the past appears only as fragment, testimony, myth, and necessary reinterpretation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:BSA-01","CL_JP8E_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSA-03","text":"A pure separation of freedom and hegemony, myth and truth, or political action and the conditions of cosmic causality remains impossible.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:MME-07","CL_JP8E_04:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:XD-01","text":"The polity preserves itself only if functional order becomes simultaneously an educational, archival, and martially tested mechanism.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-04","CL_JP8E_01:BSD-07","CL_JP8E_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:XD-02","text":"Catastrophe not only destroys political bodies, but transfers power to the bearers who possess the selective continuity of memory.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-05","CL_JP8E_03:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:XD-03","text":"The claim of true discourse transforms myth into a mediated archive, which requires explanation and common judgment, not simple belief.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-08","CL_JP8E_02:BSD-05","CL_JP8E_04:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:XD-04","text":"Resistance to universal power transfers virtue from internal good order into liberating action, but keeps hegemonic ambiguity open.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-09","CL_JP8E_03:BSD-03","CL_JP8E_03:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:XD-05","text":"Natural causality and political memory converge, because both deny that the present explains itself from itself.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-08","CL_JP8E_04:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_04:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-01","text":"History becomes a field of political power, because possession of the means of preservation determines what can appear as antiquity, virtue, or truth.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-05","CL_JP8E_02:BSD-01","CL_JP8E_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-02","text":"Political philosophy becomes an archaeology of interruptions and causes, not simply a theory of present good order.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-08","CL_JP8E_02:BSD-04","CL_JP8E_04:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-03","text":"The political subject ceases to be a self-sufficient master and becomes guardian, witness, interpreter, and node in a network of education and memory.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-07","CL_JP8E_03:HSI-02","CL_JP8E_04:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-04","text":"Time becomes post-catastrophic and spiral: no past returns by itself, but only as a new synthesis that changes the present.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-05","CL_JP8E_03:BSD-06","CL_JP8E_03:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-05","text":"Epistemic humility becomes a condition of political claim, because discourse must seek cause, but cannot attain complete possession of it.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-08","CL_JP8E_04:GA-10","CL_JP8E_04:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:RLE-01","text":"Division of functions → education of the guardian → common life → political order → martial testing → re-examination of education.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:RLE-01","CL_JP8E_01:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:RLE-02","text":"Catastrophe → loss of writing → forgetfulness → archival asymmetry → foreign testimony → political recovery → new insecurity.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:RLE-02","CL_JP8E_03:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:RLE-03","text":"Myth → doubt → interpretive transfer → cosmological explanation → political identity → new testing of myth.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:FGS-B05","CL_JP8E_04:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:RLE-04","text":"Cosmos → human being → education → city → historical memory → return to the necessity of cause and paradigm.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:RLE-04","CL_JP8E_04:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:RLE-05","text":"The attractor of the system is fitting discourse, able to connect virtue, memory, cause, and action without becoming fixed in final certainty.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:RLE-07","CL_JP8E_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:FGS-B01","text":"Natural difference + educational formation → functional allocation → political unity through distinct functions.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:FGS-B02","CL_JP8E_03:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:FGS-B02","text":"Common life + removal of private bonds → construction of political kinship → hidden administration of continuity.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:FGS-B04","CL_JP8E_01:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:FGS-B03","text":"War + universal threat → testing of political order → transformation of virtue into liberating action.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:FGS-B06","CL_JP8E_03:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:FGS-B04","text":"Catastrophe + loss of bearers → forgetfulness → preserved archival asymmetry → necessity of a new interpretive synthesis.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:FGS-B01","CL_JP8E_03:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:FGS-B05","text":"Memory + testimony + common examination → controlled myth → revisable true discourse.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:FGS-B03","CL_JP8E_04:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:FGS-B06","text":"Ungenerated paradigm → generated cosmos → human being → educated city → political history as a dependent result.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:FGS-B05","CL_JP8E_04:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:AFM-B01","text":"Functional division → educational construction of character → common life → political unity.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:AFM-B02","CL_JP8E_01:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:AFM-B02","text":"Political unity → war and external threat → practical demonstration of virtue.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:AFM-B05","CL_JP8E_03:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:AFM-B03","text":"Practical demonstration → catastrophe and decay → forgetfulness of events and political bodies.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:BAS-07","CL_JP8E_03:AFM-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:AFM-B04","text":"Forgetfulness → testimonial and archival recovery → reconstruction of an ancient political possibility.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:AFM-B05","CL_JP8E_03:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:AFM-B05","text":"Reconstruction of the past → myth as a controlled instrument → cosmological and explanatory grounding.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:MME-04","CL_JP8E_04:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:AFM-B06","text":"Cosmological grounding → revisable political identity → new cycle of memory, judgment, and action.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:AFM-B05","CL_JP8E_04:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MME-01","text":"The city is reclassified from a present community into a multi-level mechanism of functions, generation, memory, and testing.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:MME-01","CL_JP8E_04:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MME-02","text":"Ignorance is reclassified from an indication of absence into a symptom of interrupted archival and institutional continuity.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:MME-02","CL_JP8E_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MME-03","text":"Geography emerges as a cause of inequality of memory, cultural survival, and interpretive dominance.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:MME-05","CL_JP8E_02:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MME-04","text":"The distinction between myth and truth ceases to be a fixed binary and becomes a problem of correct transfer, explanation, and testing.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:MME-02","CL_JP8E_04:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MME-05","text":"Political freedom is reclassified as a relation of danger, collective power, and mnemonic preservation, not as a natural property.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:BSD-03","CL_JP8E_03:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B01","text":"On the surface, the system treats natures, guardians, cities, wars, writings, priests, catastrophes, myths, and a generated cosmos.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:ALT-B01","CL_JP8E_03:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B02","text":"Structurally, distributed mechanisms of functional separation, education, archival continuity, natural decay, and explanatory hierarchy operate.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:ALT-B02","CL_JP8E_04:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategically, the struggle is transferred to the administration of the conditions under which unity, freedom, memory, and interpretive authority are produced.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:ALT-B03","CL_JP8E_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B04","text":"Ontologically and temporally, reality is generated, caused, cyclically decaying, and recoverable only in part.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:ALT-B04","CL_JP8E_04:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B05","text":"Methodologically and performatively, discourse does what it says: it reconstructs fragments, hierarchizes causes, transfers myths, and defers final certainty.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:ALT-B05","CL_JP8E_04:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:FL-01","text":"Nature is presented as the foundation of order, but is simultaneously employed as administrable material for education, reproduction, and geographical preservation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:FL-01","CL_JP8E_01:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:FL-02","text":"Truth seeks common judgment, but judgment depends on selectively preserved bearers, divine favor, and insufficiently guaranteed testimonies.","refs":["CL_JP8E_02:FL-01","CL_JP8E_04:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:FL-03","text":"The city's liberating mission requires superiority of force, which threatens to reproduce the hegemonic model that it opposes.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:FL-01","CL_JP8E_03:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:FL-04","text":"The necessity of cause strengthens discourse, but the indeterminacy of cause prevents its final self-grounding.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:FL-03","CL_JP8E_04:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MP-01","text":"Memory mutates when it ceases to signify preservation of the past and becomes a power of reclassifying the present.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:MP-01","CL_JP8E_04:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MP-02","text":"War mutates when from a function of order it becomes evidence of the whole polity and an instrument of universal liberation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:RMD-05","CL_JP8E_03:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MP-03","text":"Myth mutates when from a doubtful narrative it becomes an interpretive paradigm of the real.","refs":["CL_JP8E_04:RMD-03","CL_JP8E_04:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MP-04","text":"The city mutates when from a local body it becomes a result of cosmic generation and a bearer of a universal political mission.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:MP-03","CL_JP8E_04:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:HSI-01","text":"Reality is conceived as a hierarchical and cyclical mechanism in which cause, generation, decay, memory, and political form mutually produce one another.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:HSI-01","CL_JP8E_04:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:HSI-02","text":"The human being is conceived as an intermediate node between cosmic cause, political education, martial action, and fragmentary memory.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:HSI-02","CL_JP8E_04:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:HSI-03","text":"Time is conceived as a spiral of recurrence, in which catastrophe does not eliminate possibility but changes the conditions of its reappearance.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:HSI-03","CL_JP8E_03:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:HSI-04","text":"Possible navigation consists not in escape from the mechanism, but in safeguarding memory, testing bridges, and distinguishing liberation from domination.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:HSI-04","CL_JP8E_04:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms suffice for reconstruction of the principal mechanism: functional order, testing, catastrophe, memory, cosmological grounding, and revisable discourse.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:MRT-B01","CL_JP8E_04:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is most coherent in its connection of interruption of memory, distributed preservation, political order, and practical testing.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:MRT-B03","CL_JP8E_02:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MRT-B03","text":"The legitimation of hierarchy, the measure of the reliability of testimony, and the identity of the cosmic cause remain underdetermined.","refs":["CL_JP8E_01:MRT-B04","CL_JP8E_04:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_01:MRT-B04","text":"Complete reconstruction cannot remove the productive ambiguity of power, myth, and uncertain truth, because these move the system itself.","refs":["CL_JP8E_03:MRT-B05","CL_JP8E_02:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_JP8E_L1_01@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_JP8E_L1_01:v1","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Jp8E L1 01"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Timaeus","title":"Jp8E L1 01","context":"Chapters 01–12 · Section 17 · Section 18 · Section 19 · Section 20 · Section 21 · Section 22 · Section 23 · Section 24 · Section 25 · Section 26 · Section 27 · Section 28","original_title":"Jp8E L1 01","original_context":"Chapters 01–12 · Section 17 · Section 18 · Section 19 · Section 20 · Section 21 · Section 22 · Section 23 · Section 24 · Section 25 · Section 26 · Section 27 · Section 28"},"original_title":"Jp8E L1 01","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01","layer":"MC","title":"Jp8E L2 01","order":1,"parents":["MC_JP8E_L1_01","MC_JP8E_L1_02","MC_JP8E_L1_03","MC_JP8E_L1_04"],"revision":"MC_JP8E_L2_01@R001","sha256":"b80d754c9ebcf54425bde80e1b4bddc33a30bd34dcecece79899c25449f8bdb2","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B01","text":"Τὸ πρόβλημα τοῦ συστήματος ἐστὶ πῶς τὸ γενητὸν, πολυμερὲς, θνητὸν καὶ πολιτικῶς εὐάλωτον δύναται νὰ μένῃ κόσμος, ἄνευ καταργήσεως τῆς διαφορᾶς, τῆς ἀνάγκης καὶ τῆς φθορᾶς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:AS-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_04:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B02","text":"Ἡ ἑνότης ἀναπλαισιοῦται ὡς παραγόμενον σχέσεων, μεσιτειῶν, δεσμῶν καὶ διανομῶν, οὐχὶ ὡς πρωταρχικὴ ταυτότης ἢ γυμνὴ ἐπιβολὴ μορφῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:AS-B03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:AS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B03","text":"Τὸ ἀΐδιον παράδειγμα ἐνεργεῖ μόνον ἐὰν γένηται ἐσωτερικὸν μέτρον διὰ ψυχῆς, σώματος, αἰσθήσεως, ἀναλογίας καὶ διανεμημένης αἰτιότητος.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:AS-B02","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B04","text":"Ἡ τάξις δὲν καταλύει τὴν ἀνάγκην, τὴν ἀνομοιότητα ἢ τὴν μεταβολήν, ἀλλὰ ἐπιτελεῖται ὡς διαρκὴς ῥύθμισις καὶ ἀνασύνθεσις αὐτῶν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:AS-B05","MC_JP8E_L1_04:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B05","text":"Ἡ πολιτεία, ἡ παιδεία καὶ ἡ μνήμη προεκτείνουσι τὴν κοσμικὴν μηχανὴν εἰς ἱστορικὸν πεδίον, ἐν ᾧ ἡ αἰτία καὶ ἡ τάξις ἀνακτῶνται διὰ φορέων καὶ κρίσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:AS-07","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B06","text":"Ὁ ἀληθὴς λόγος μεταβάλλεται εἰς διαβαθμισμένην ἀνακατασκευήν, ἐπειδὴ τὰ γιγνόμενα, τὰ φαινόμενα καὶ τὰ σωζόμενα τεκμήρια οὐ παρέχουσιν ἴσην βεβαιότητα.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:AS-03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B07","text":"Ἡ ὅλη κίνησις εἶναι σπειροειδής: παράδειγμα καὶ μέτρον γίγνονται σώμα καὶ πολιτεία, ἡ δὲ διατάραξις καὶ ἡ λήθη ἐπαναφέρουσι τὸν λόγον εἰς νέαν ἀνάταξιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:AS-05","MC_JP8E_L1_03:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B08","text":"Ἡ σύγκλεισις παραμένει πρόσκαιρος καὶ πρέπουσα, διότι ἡ χώρα, ἡ φθορά καὶ ἡ ἀπουσία ἀπολύτου ἐγγυήσεως ἀποκλείουσι τὴν τελικὴν αὐτοθεμελίωσιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Ἡ ἑνότης παράγεται διὰ λειτουργικῶς διακεκριμένων καὶ ἐσωτερικῶς συσχετισμένων μερῶν, οὐ διὰ ὁμοιογενείας.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Πᾶσα τάξις ἀπαιτεῖ αἰτίαν, ἀναλογίαν, μεσότητα καὶ δεκτικὸν πεδίον, ἐν ᾧ ἡ διαφορὰ δύναται νὰ δεθῇ χωρὶς νὰ ἀφανισθῇ.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Τὸ παράδειγμα μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ ὑπερκείμενον μέτρον εἰς κατανεμημένην αἰτιότητα, ἥτις ἐνεργεῖ διὰ μορφῆς, κινήσεως, σώματος, ψυχῆς καὶ θεσμοῦ.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Ἡ τάξις εἶναι ῥυθμιστικὴ ἐπιτέλεσις, ἣ διασῴζεται μόνον ἐπειδὴ ἐνσωματοῖ τὴν ἀντίστασιν, τὴν ἀνάγκην καὶ τὴν παραγωγικὴν διαφοράν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Τὸ μέτρον καὶ ἡ ἀναλογία εἰσὶν αἱ μηχαναὶ αἳ μετατρέπουσι τὴν ἀνομοιότητα εἰς κίνησιν, γνῶσιν, παιδείαν καὶ συντονισμὸν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Σῶμα, αἴσθησις, ψυχὴ, μνήμη καὶ μαρτυρία εἰσὶν ἐνεργοὶ μεσίται, δι᾽ ὧν ἡ κοσμικὴ αἰτιότης γίνεται κρίσις, ταυτότης καὶ πολιτικὴ πράξις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Ὁ χρόνος μεταβαίνει ἀπὸ κινητὴν εἰκόνα τοῦ αἰῶνος εἰς μετακαταστροφικὴν σπείραν, ἐν ᾗ ἡ ἐπάνοδος παράγει νέαν μορφὴν καὶ οὐχὶ ταὐτὴν ἀποκατάστασιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Ἡ ἀλήθεια εἶναι διαβαθμισμένη καὶ ἀναθεωρήσιμος ἀνασύνθεσις, ἧς τὸ κύρος κρίνεται κατὰ τὴν σταθερότητα τοῦ ἀντικειμένου καὶ τὴν ἀξιοπιστίαν τῶν μεσιτειῶν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:AS-03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Ἡ καταστροφή, ἡ ἀνομοιότης καὶ ἡ ἀνάγκη δὲν εἰσὶ παρεκβάσεις τοῦ συστήματος, ἀλλὰ οἱ ὅροι δι᾽ ὧν ἡ τάξις ἀναγκάζεται νὰ ἀναπαράγῃ ἑαυτήν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Τὸ ὑποκείμενον οὐκέτι νοεῖται ὡς αὐτοτελὲς κέντρον, ἀλλ᾽ ὡς κόμβος σωμάτων, ῥοῶν, παιδείας, μνήμης, κρίσεως καὶ πολιτικῆς εὐθύνης.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSD-02","MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Ἡ ἐλευθερία δὲν εἶναι ἔξοδος ἐκ τῶν σχέσεων, ἀλλὰ ἐπανόρθωσις, πλοήγησις καὶ ἀντίστασις ἐντὸς τῆς μηχανῆς τῆς ἀνάγκης καὶ τῆς δυνάμεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSD-05","MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] Ἡ ὁμοιότης, ἡ γνώσις καὶ ἡ ἀλήθεια διατηροῦν τὴν ἰσχὺν αὐτῶν μόνον ἐὰν μὴ καταλύωσι τὴν ἀπόστασιν παραδείγματος, εἰκόνος, φαινομένου καὶ λόγου.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:BSA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-01","text":"Ἡ διαίρεσις τῶν ἔργων διατηρεῖ τὴν γενετικὴν λειτουργίαν της, ἀλλὰ μετακινείται ἀπὸ πολιτικὴν κατανομήν εἰς ἀναλογικὴν μεσότητα καὶ τέλος εἰς γεωμετρικὴν γραμματικὴν δυνάμεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:RMD-01","MC_JP8E_L1_02:CGD-02","MC_JP8E_L1_04:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-02","text":"Ἡ μεσότης μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ δεσμὸν ὑλικῶν στοιχείων εἰς ψυχικὴν ἀρχὴν καὶ ἐν συνεχείᾳ εἰς ὅρον κυβερνήσεως, κινήσεως καὶ γνώσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:RMD-02","MC_JP8E_L1_02:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-03","text":"Ἡ τάξις ἀντιστρέφει τὴν σημασίαν αὐτῆς: ἀπὸ προϋποτεθειμένη σταθερότης γίνεται ἀδιάλειπτος ἐπιτέλεσις ἀνασυνθέσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:RMD-04","MC_JP8E_L1_04:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-04","text":"Ἡ ψυχὴ μετακινείται ἀπὸ κοσμικὴν μεσότητα καὶ κυριαρχίαν εἰς ἐνσώματον κόμβον, ἐν ᾧ κίνησις, πάθος, κρίσις καὶ ἀνάταξις συμπλέκονται.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_03:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-05","text":"Ἡ μνήμη παύει νὰ σημαίνῃ ἀναπλήρωσιν τοῦ ἀπόντος καὶ γίνεται ἀρχειακὴ δύναμις ἡ ὁποία ἀναταξινομεῖ τὸ παρόν καὶ διανέμει νομιμοποίησιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:RMD-03","MC_JP8E_L1_01:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-06","text":"Ὁ μῦθος καὶ ἡ εἰκὼν ἀπορροφῶνται εἰς μίαν διαβαθμισμένην μηχανὴν, ἣ διασῴζει ἀπόστασιν ἀπὸ τὸ παράδειγμα καὶ ὅμως μεταφέρει ἀνακατασκευάσιμον γνώσιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:RMD-05","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-07","text":"Ἡ ὑποδοχὴ μεταβάλλεται ἀπὸ ὑλικὸν ὑπόβαθρον εἰς ἀποφατικὸν ὅρον φανερώσεως, ὅστις δέχεται τὰ εἴδη χωρὶς νὰ γίνεται ἓν εἶδος παρὰ τοῖς ἄλλοις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:RMD-05","MC_JP8E_L1_03:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-01","text":"Αἱ ἀριθμητικαί, στοιχειακαί, ἀστρονομικαί καὶ γεωμετρικαὶ διατάξεις μένουσι τοπικαὶ μηχαναί· ἡ κλιμακούμενη ἀρχὴ εἶναι ἡ μεσιτεία, ἣ συνδέει τὰ μέρη πρὸς τὸ ὅλον.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:CGD-01","MC_JP8E_L1_04:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-02","text":"Ἡ λειτουργικὴ διαίρεσις, ἡ μορφολογικὴ αἰτιότης καὶ ἡ ἀρχειακὴ συνέχεια κλιμακοῦνται εἰς κοινὴν λογικὴν διανεμημένης συγκροτήσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B02","MC_JP8E_L1_04:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-03","text":"Ἡ παρατήρησις τῶν κύκλων, ἡ χωρικὴ ἀσυμμετρία τῆς μνήμης καὶ ἡ μέτρησις τῆς κινήσεως γεφυροῦσι κοσμολογίαν, ἱστορίαν καὶ πολιτικὴν κρίσιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:RLE-02","MC_JP8E_L1_01:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-04","text":"Ἡ καταστροφή καὶ ἡ ἀντίστασις παύουσιν εἶναι ἐπιμέρους συμβεβηκότα καὶ γίγνονται καταλύται τῆς μεταβάσεως ἀπὸ τάξιν εἰς ἐπανόρθωσιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:RMD-04","MC_JP8E_L1_03:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-05","text":"Ἡ αὐτάρκεια πολιτείας, σώματος ἢ λόγου ἀποβάλλεται ὡς τοπικὴ ψευδαίσθησις καὶ ἀνακωδικοποιεῖται εἰς παράγωγον σχέσεων, μεταδόσεων καὶ ἐλέγχων.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GNX-02","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GNX-01","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἑνότης ἡ ὁποία ταυτίζεται πρὸς μῖξιν, ὁμοιογένειαν ἢ ἀπόλυτον ὁμοιότητα, διότι ἡ διαφορὰ εἶναι ὅρος τῆς συνοχῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GNX-01","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GNX-02","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GNX-02","text":"Ἀποκλείεται αὐτοθεμελιωμένη πόλις, αὐτόνομος κόσμος καὶ αὐτομορφοποιὸς ὕλη, διότι πᾶν γιγνόμενον ἐξαρτᾶται ἀπὸ αἰτίαν, μεσότητα, δεκτικότητα καὶ μετάδοσιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:DBN-01","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GNX-01","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GNX-03","text":"Ἀποκλείονται γραμμικὸς χρόνος, ἀπόλυτος τόπος καὶ καθολικὸς προσανατολισμός, διότι ὁ χρόνος καὶ ὁ τόπος παράγονται ἐκ κυκλικῶν, σχεσιακῶν καὶ ἱστορικῶν μεταβολῶν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GNX-03","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GNX-04","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GNX-04","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ὅτι παρόν, αἴσθησις, φαινόμενον, πάθος ἢ ὄνομα παρέχουσιν αὐτομάτως τὴν οὐσίαν ἢ τὴν αἰτίαν αὐτῶν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GNX-04","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GNX-04","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GNX-05","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ ἴση καὶ πλήρης ἀπόδειξις περὶ τοῦ ἀϊδίου, τοῦ γενητοῦ, τοῦ φαινομένου καὶ τῆς ὑπερτάτης αἰτίας.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GNX-05","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GNX-05","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:DBN-01","text":"Δεῖ ψεῦδος εἶναι ὅτι τὸ μέρος, τὸ σῶμα ἢ τὸ πράγμα δύναται νὰ ἀποτελέσῃ τὸ ἔσχατον ὑποκείμενον τάξεως, ἐπειδὴ ἕκαστον εἶναι ἀποτέλεσμα γενετικῶν σχέσεων.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:DBN-01","MC_JP8E_L1_04:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:DBN-02","text":"Δεῖ ψεῦδος εἶναι ὅτι ἡ διαφορὰ σημαίνει καθαρὰν διάλυσιν ἢ ὅτι ἡ ἑνότης ἀπαιτεῖ ἐξάλειψιν τῆς ἑτερότητος.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:DBN-02","MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:DBN-03","text":"Δεῖ ψεῦδος εἶναι ὅτι γεγονός, φαινόμενον, ὄνομα καὶ ἀλήθεια εἰσὶν αὐτοπαρόντα, διότι ἡ μνήμη καὶ ἡ φανέρωσις ἐξαρτῶνται ἀπὸ ἐπιλεκτικὰς μεσιτείας.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:DBN-02","MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:DBN-04","text":"Δεῖ ψεῦδος εἶναι ὅτι ἡ ἐλευθερία ἢ ἡ ψυχικὴ αὐτονομία προϋπάρχει τῆς συγκρούσεως, τοῦ σώματος, τοῦ χρόνου καὶ τῆς κοσμικῆς διανομῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:DBN-03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:DBN-05","text":"Δεῖ ψεῦδος εἶναι ὅτι ἡ εἰκών δύναται νὰ ταυτισθῇ τελείως πρὸς τὸ παράδειγμα, διότι ἡ ἀπόστασις εἶναι ὁ ὅρος αὐτῆς τῆς ὁμοιώσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:DBN-03","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSA-01","text":"Ἡ βαθύτατη ἀπουσία εἶναι ἡ ἀδύνατος ἀμεσότης μεταξὺ παραδείγματος, κόσμου, ἀληθείας καὶ μνήμης· πᾶσα σχέσις διέρχεται διὰ εἰκόνος, σώματος, λόγου ἢ μαρτυρίας.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_02:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSA-02","text":"Ἡ χώρα ὑποδεικνύει θεμέλιον ἀναγκαῖον ἀλλ᾽ ἀνέκφραστον, ὃ δέχεται τὴν φανέρωσιν καὶ ὅμως οὐ δύναται νὰ καταστῇ πλήρως παρὸν ἀντικείμενον γνώσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSA-03","text":"Ἀπουσιάζει ἀδιάσπαστον ἀρχεῖον καὶ καθολικὸς χάρτης τοῦ ὅλου, ὥστε ἡ ἀλήθεια νὰ ἐνεργῇ μόνον διὰ θραυσμάτων, σχέσεων καὶ διαρκῶν ἀνακατασκευῶν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSA-04","text":"Ἀπαγορεύεται καθαρὸν ἐκτός, διότι οὔτε ἡ ἐλευθερία οὔτε ἡ ἐρμηνεία δύνανται νὰ ἀποσπασθῶσι τελείως ἀπὸ δύναμιν, αἰτιότητα καὶ σχέσιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSA-05","text":"Ἀπουσιάζει τελικὴ σύμπτωσις μεταξὺ κλειστῆς κοσμικῆς τελειότητος καὶ ἱστορικῆς πολιτικῆς συνέχειας, διότι ἡ δευτέρα ἐξαρτᾶται ἀπὸ ἀπειλήν, φθορὰν καὶ διακοπὴν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_01:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-01","text":"Ἡ παραδειγματικὴ τάξις γίνεται πολιτικῶς καὶ ἱστορικῶς ἐνεργός μόνον ὅταν μεταδίδηται διὰ σωμάτων, παιδείας, θεσμῶν καὶ μνήμης.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-02","text":"Ἡ δυναμικὴ διατήρησις τοῦ κόσμου καὶ ἡ μετακαταστροφικὴ μνήμη συγκλίνουσιν: ἡ συνέχεια ὑπάρχει μόνον διὰ τῆς ἀναπαραγωγῆς μορφῶν μετὰ διακοπῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-03","text":"Ἡ χώρα καὶ ἡ ἀπουσία κενοῦ συγκροτοῦσι παραγωγικὴν ἔντασιν: ἡ δεκτικότης δὲν εἶναι καθαρὸν κενόν, ἀλλὰ πεδίον ἐν ᾧ πᾶσα εἴσοδος καὶ μορφοποίησις εἶναι αἰτιακῶς δεσμευμένη.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-12","MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_04:AS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-04","text":"Ὁ ἀληθὴς λόγος ἀπαιτεῖ διπλοῦν ἔλεγχον: μέτρησιν τῶν περιοδικῶν κινήσεων καὶ κοινὴν κρίσιν περὶ τῶν διασωζομένων μαρτυριῶν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L1_03:XD-05","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-05","text":"Ἡ ἐλευθερωτικὴ πράξις δὲν ἀναιρεῖ τὴν ἰσχὺν, ἀλλὰ μετατρέπει τὴν ἰσχὺν εἰς ἀσταθὲς ὄργανον ἀντιστάσεως, ἀνατάξεως καὶ κινδύνου νέας ἡγεμονίας.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-11","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-06","text":"Ὁ μῦθος καὶ ἡ εἰκὼν δύνανται νὰ λειτουργῶσιν ὡς ἐλεγχόμενα ἀρχεῖα, μόνον ἐὰν ἡ ὁμοιότης αὐτῶν διατηρῇ τὴν ἀπόστασιν ἀπὸ τὴν πλήρη ταύτισιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-12","MC_JP8E_L1_01:XD-03","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSD-01","text":"Ἡ πραγματικότης γίνεται πολυεπίπεδος κανονιστικὴ τοπολογία, ἐν ᾗ κοσμολογία, φυσικὴ, πολιτική καὶ ἱστορία εἰσὶ διαφορετικαὶ κλίμακες τῆς αὐτῆς μεσιτευμένης τάξεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L1_02:BSD-01","MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSD-02","text":"Ὁ χρόνος γίνεται διπλῆ μηχανή: κυκλικὴ ἐπανάληψις ἡ ὁποία μετρεῖ τὴν τάξιν, καὶ ἱστορικὴ διακοπὴ ἡ ὁποία ἀναγκάζει νέαν σύνθεσιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_02:HSI-03","MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSD-03","text":"Ἡ κυριότης μετατοπίζεται ἀπὸ κατοχὴν αὐτοτελοῦς δυνάμεως εἰς διοίκησιν τῶν μεταβάσεων, τῶν μέσων μνήμης καὶ τῶν ὅρων ἐρμηνευτικοῦ κύρους.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-10","MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-01","MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSD-04","text":"Ἡ ἐπιστήμη γίνεται ἱεραρχία προσήκοντων λόγων, ἐν ᾗ ἡ ἀκρίβεια οὐκ ἀποφασίζεται καθολικῶς, ἀλλὰ κατὰ τὴν ὀντολογικὴν σταθερότητα καὶ προσβασιμότητα τοῦ ἀντικειμένου.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-12","MC_JP8E_L1_03:MME-05","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSD-05","text":"Ὁ ἐλεύθερος πολιτικὸς καὶ γνωστικὸς δρῶν γίνεται φύλαξ μεσιτειῶν, ὅστις δὲν ἐξέρχεται τοῦ κόσμου, ἀλλὰ ρυθμίζει τὰς σχέσεις ἐντὸς αὐτοῦ.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-10","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-11","MC_JP8E_L1_01:HSI-04","MC_JP8E_L1_03:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RLE-01","text":"Παράδειγμα → εἰκών → σωματικὴ καὶ ψυχικὴ κίνησις → μέτρον → ἀναγνώρισις τοῦ παραδείγματος· ἡ ἐπιστροφή ἐπικυροῖ τὴν ὁμοιότητα χωρὶς νὰ καταργῇ τὴν ἀπόστασιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:RLE-04","MC_JP8E_L1_03:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RLE-02","text":"Μορφὴ → ἀνομοιότης → δύναμις → ἀντίστασις → ἀνάταξις → νέα μορφή· ἡ διαφορὰ ἐπανέρχεται ὡς ὅρος τῆς τάξεως ἣν ἐδόκει νὰ ἀπειλεῖ.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_04:RLE-01","MC_JP8E_L1_03:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RLE-03","text":"Καταστροφή → ἀπώλεια φορέων → ἀρχειακὴ ἀσυμμετρία → ἀνασύστασις πολιτικῆς δυνατότητος → νέα δοκιμασία τῆς ταυτότητος καὶ τοῦ λόγου.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:RLE-02","MC_JP8E_L1_01:AFM-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RLE-04","text":"Ὄψις → περιοδικότης → ἀριθμός → χρόνος → φιλοσοφικὴ μίμησις → διόρθωσις ψυχικῶν περιφορῶν· ἡ αἴσθησις γίνεται ἅμα εἴσοδος πλάνης καὶ ὁδὸς ἀνατάξεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:RLE-02","MC_JP8E_L1_03:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RLE-05","text":"Ὁ κεντρικὸς ἑλκτικὸς πόλος εἶναι ὁ πρέπων λόγος τῆς ὁμοιότητος μετὰ ἀποστάσεως, ὃς συνδέει αἰτίαν, τάξιν, μνήμην καὶ πράξιν ἄνευ ψευδοῦς τελειώσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:RLE-05","MC_JP8E_L1_02:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:FGS-B01","text":"Ἀγαθότης καὶ ἀΐδιον παράδειγμα + ἀπόρριψις αὐτογενοῦς ὕλης → ἀνάγκη μεσιτευμένης δημιουργίας.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:FGS-B02","text":"Ἀναλογία καὶ μεσότης + δεκτικὸν πεδίον → μορφοποιημένη διαφορὰ καὶ συνεκτικὴ σωματικὴ σύνθεσις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:FGS-B03","text":"Σωματικὴ σύνθεσις + ἀνάγκη + ἀπουσία κενοῦ → πίεσις, ἀντίστασις, μετάδοσις καὶ δυναμικὴ ἀνασύνθεσις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:FGS-B04","text":"Ψυχικὴ κίνησις + αἴσθησις + μέτρον → διάκρισις, γνῶσις, πλάνη καὶ δυνατότης ἐπανόρθωσις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:FGS-B05","text":"Παιδεία + πολιτικὴ διανομή + ἀρχειακὴ μνήμη → ἱστορικὴ ταυτότης, κοινὴ κρίσις καὶ πρακτικὴ ἀντίστασις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-10","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:FGS-B06","text":"Καταστροφή + λήθη + ὅριον ἀποδείξεως → εἰκὼς καὶ ἀποφατικὸς λόγος → νέα ἀναζήτησις τῆς τάξεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AFM-B01","text":"Ἀγαθότης καὶ παράδειγμα → κατανεμημένη δημιουργικὴ τάξις → ἀπόρριψις αὐτονομημένης γενέσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:FGS-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_03:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AFM-B02","text":"Ἑτερογένεια → ἀναλογία καὶ μορφολογικὴ διάκρισις → ἀνομοιότης, δύναμις καὶ σχέσις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:FGS-B02","MC_JP8E_L1_04:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AFM-B03","text":"Σωματικὴ σύνθεσις → μετάδοσις κινήσεων καὶ παθημάτων → αἴσθησις, πλάνη καὶ δυνατότης κοσμικῆς μαθήσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:FGS-B02","MC_JP8E_L1_04:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AFM-B04","text":"Σχετικὸν φαινόμενον → παρατήρησις περιοδικότητος → ἀριθμὸς καὶ χρόνος → παιδευτικὴ διόρθωσις ψυχῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:FGS-B03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AFM-B05","text":"Λήθη καὶ διακοπὴ → μαρτυρία καὶ ἀρχείον → ἀνασύστασις παρελθούσης πολιτικῆς δυνατότητος.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:AFM-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_01:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AFM-B06","text":"Τριμερὴς ὀντολογία καὶ ὅριον γνώσεως → εἰκὼς καὶ ἀποφατικὸς λόγος → ἐπανέναρξις τῆς ἀναζητήσεως θεμελίου.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:FGS-B06","MC_JP8E_L1_01:AFM-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-01","text":"Ἡ πόλις καὶ ὁ κόσμος ἀναταξινομοῦνται ὡς ὁλότητες παραγόμεναι διὰ διανεμημένων ἔργων, ψυχῶν, κινήσεων, μνημῶν καὶ ἐσωτερικῶν μέτρων.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:MME-01","MC_JP8E_L1_02:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-02","text":"Τὸ σῶμα καὶ ἡ ὕλη παύουσιν εἶναι παθητικὰ ὑπολείμματα καὶ γίγνονται δίκτυα ἐνεργῶν μεταδόσεων, μορφοποιήσεων καὶ αἰτιακῶν ἀλληλεξαρτήσεων.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:MME-02","MC_JP8E_L1_04:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-03","text":"Ὁ χρόνος γίνεται κοινὸν πεδίον κυκλικῆς μετρήσεως, φθορᾶς, λήθης, ἀνασυνθέσεως καὶ νέας πολιτικῆς ἐμφανίσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:MME-03","MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-04","text":"Ἡ γνώσις μεταβάλλεται ἀπὸ κατοχὴν πραγμάτων εἰς ἀνακατασκευὴν μηχανισμῶν, ἧς ἡ ἀκρίβεια κρίνεται κατὰ τὸν τρόπον καὶ τὴν σταθερότητα τοῦ ὄντος.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:MME-04","MC_JP8E_L1_03:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-05","text":"Τὰ δίπολα μύθου καὶ ἀληθείας, ἑνὸς καὶ πολλῶν, σταθερότητος καὶ μεταβολῆς παύουσιν εἶναι ἀπόλυτα καὶ γίγνονται σχέσεις ἐλέγχου, μεταφορᾶς καὶ δικτυώσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:MME-04","MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-06","text":"Ἡ στρατηγικὴ μεταφέρεται ἀπὸ τὴν κατάληψιν ἀντικειμένων εἰς τὴν διοίκησιν τῶν μεσιτειῶν δι᾽ ὧν παράγονται μνήμη, ἑνότης, ἐλευθερία καὶ ἐρμηνευτικὸν κύρος.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:ALT-B01","text":"Ἐπιφανειακῶς, φύσεις, φύλακες, σώματα, στοιχεῖα, πόλεις, πόλεμοι, ἄστρα, πάθη, μῦθοι καὶ τόποι συγκροτοῦσι τὰ ῥητὰ ἀντικείμενα τοῦ συστήματος.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_04:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:ALT-B02","text":"Δομικῶς, μεσότης, αἰτιότης, δεκτικότητα, μορφή, ῥοή, ἀνάγκη, ἀρχεῖον καὶ μετάδοσις ἀποτελοῦσι τὴν κοινὴν μηχανὴν γενέσεως καὶ συνεχείας.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B02","MC_JP8E_L1_03:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:ALT-B03","text":"Μεταστρατηγικῶς, ἡ πάλη ἀφορᾷ τὴν ῥύθμισιν τῶν ὅρων δι᾽ ὧν οἱ κινήσεις γίγνονται γνῶσις, οἱ μνῆμαι γίγνονται νομιμοποίησις καὶ ἡ δύναμις γίγνεται ἐλευθερία ἢ κυριαρχία.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:ALT-B04","text":"Ὀντολογικῶς καὶ χρονικῶς, ἡ πραγματικότης εἶναι γενητὴ καὶ αἰτιατή, ἀλλὰ οὐχὶ αὐτοφανής· διαμένει διὰ κυκλικῆς φθορᾶς, κινήσεως καὶ ἀνατάξεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_04:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:ALT-B05","text":"Μεθοδολογικῶς καὶ ἐπιτελεστικῶς, ὁ λόγος μιμεῖται τὸ ἀντικείμενον: διαιρεῖ, μετρεῖ, συνδέει, ἀνασυνθέτει καὶ καταλήγει εἰς ὅριον ἀποφατικῆς ἀναστολῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B05","MC_JP8E_L1_04:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:FL-01","text":"Ἡ κοσμικὴ κλειστότης καὶ αὐτάρκεια ἐντείνονται πρὸς τὴν πολιτικὴν συνέχεια, ἥτις ἐξαρτᾶται ἀπὸ ἐξωτερικὴν ἀπειλήν, πολεμικὴν δοκιμασίαν καὶ καταστροφικὴν διακοπήν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_01:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:FL-02","text":"Ἡ νοερὰ τάξις τίθεται ὡς ἀρχή, ἀλλὰ ἡ χώρα καὶ ἡ ἀνάγκη παραμένουσιν ἀναγκαῖοι καὶ μὴ πλήρως ὑποτεταγμένοι ὅροι τῆς γενέσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:FL-01","MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:FL-03","text":"Ἡ ἀλήθεια ζητεῖ κοινὴν κρίσιν, ἀλλὰ ἡ κρίσις ἐξαρτᾶται ἀπὸ θραυσματικῶς σωζομένας μαρτυρίας, ἐπιλεκτικοὺς φορεῖς καὶ ἀνίσως διανεμημένην μνήμην.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:FL-02","MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:FL-04","text":"Ἡ ἑνότης ἀπαιτεῖ πληθύν ἐσωτερικῶν σχέσεων, ἀλλὰ ἡ καθολικότης ἀδυνατεῖ νὰ συμφιλιώσῃ πλήρως τὴν πολλαπλότητα τῶν ἀντιθετικῶν φορῶν καὶ τόπων.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:FL-01","MC_JP8E_L1_04:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:FL-05","text":"Ἡ ἐλευθερωτικὴ ἀποστολὴ ἀπαιτεῖ ἰσχὺν ἱκανὴν πρὸς ἀντίστασιν, ἀλλ᾽ ἡ αὐτὴ ἰσχὺς διατηρεῖ τὴν δυνατότητα νέας ἡγεμονίας.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:FL-03","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MP-01","text":"Τὸ παράδειγμα μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ ἀΐδιον ἀρχέτυπον εἰς ἐνεργὸν κανόνα κοσμικῆς, γνωστικῆς καὶ πολιτικῆς ἀνασυνθέσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:CGD-04","MC_JP8E_L1_01:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MP-02","text":"Ἡ ψυχὴ μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ κυβερνητικὴ μεσότης τοῦ κόσμου εἰς ἐνσώματον τόπον πάθους, κρίσεως, μετρήσεως καὶ ἐπανόρθωσις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:MP-01","MC_JP8E_L1_03:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MP-03","text":"Ἡ χώρα μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ δεκτικὸν ὑπόβαθρον εἰς ἀποφατικὸν ὅριον, ὃ ἐπιτρέπει τὴν γένεσιν ἐπειδὴ ἀρνεῖται νὰ λάβῃ ἴδιον εἶδος.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:RMD-05","MC_JP8E_L1_03:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MP-04","text":"Ἡ μνήμη μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ διατήρησιν παρελθόντος εἰς δύναμιν ἐπιλογῆς, ἀναταξινομήσεως καὶ πολιτικῆς κυρώσεως τοῦ παρόντος.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:RMD-03","MC_JP8E_L1_01:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MP-05","text":"Ἡ τάξις μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ τελικὴ σταθερότης εἰς συνεχή ἐπιτέλεσιν, ἥτις χρειάζεται τὴν ἀνωμαλίαν ὡς ὕλην τῆς ἰδίας συνεχείας.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:RMD-04","MC_JP8E_L1_04:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:HSI-01","text":"Ἡ πραγματικότης εἶναι ἱεραρχικὴ καὶ πλήρης μηχανὴ μεταδόσεως, ἐν ᾗ μορφή, αἰτία, ἀνάγκη, χώρα, μνήμη καὶ ἀνασύνθεσις ἀλληλοπαράγονται.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:HSI-01","MC_JP8E_L1_03:HSI-01","MC_JP8E_L1_04:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:HSI-02","text":"Τὸ ὑποκείμενον εἶναι ἐνδοκοσμικὸς κόμβος καὶ οὐχὶ ἐξωτερικὸς κριτής, διότι σώμα, ῥοαί, πάθη, λόγος καὶ κρίσις συμπίπτουσιν ἐν αὐτῷ.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSD-02","MC_JP8E_L1_04:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:HSI-03","text":"Ὁ χρόνος εἶναι σπειροειδὴς κυκλοφορία, ἐν ᾗ ἡ ἐπάνοδος διασῴζει τὸ μέτρον, ἀλλὰ ἡ φθορὰ καὶ ἡ διακοπὴ παράγουσι νέαν φάσιν τοῦ αὐτοῦ συστήματος.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:HSI-03","MC_JP8E_L1_04:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:HSI-04","text":"Ἡ δυνατὴ πλοήγησις συνίσταται εἰς φύλαξιν μνήμης, διάκρισιν μεσιτειῶν, ῥύθμισιν σχέσεων καὶ ἀντίστασιν πρὸς τὴν μετατροπὴν τῆς ἐλευθερίας εἰς κυριαρχίαν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:HSI-04","MC_JP8E_L1_03:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MRT-B01","text":"Τὰ ἀξιώματα τῆς παραδειγματικῆς μεσιτείας, τῆς παραγωγικῆς διαφορᾶς, τῆς δεκτικότητος, τῆς δυναμικῆς τάξεως, τῆς μνήμης καὶ τοῦ διαβαθμισμένου λόγου ἀρκοῦσι πρὸς ἀνακατασκευήν τῆς κεντρικῆς μηχανῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:MRT-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_02:MRT-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_04:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MRT-B02","text":"Ἡ ἀρχιτεκτονικὴ εἶναι ἰσχυροτάτη ἐκεῖ ὅπου αἰτία, μορφή, σῶμα, ἀνάγκη, μνήμη καὶ ἀνασύνθεσις ἐνεργοῦσιν ὡς μία σπειροειδὴς ἀκολουθία.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:MRT-B02","MC_JP8E_L1_03:MRT-B02","MC_JP8E_L1_04:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MRT-B03","text":"Ὑποκαθορισμένα μένουσιν ὁ τρόπος τῆς νοερᾶς ἐνεργείας ἐπὶ τῆς ἀνάγκης, ἡ νομιμοποίησις τῆς ἱεραρχίας, ἡ σχέσις χώρας καὶ εἴδους, καὶ ἡ μετάβασις ἀπὸ μικροδομὴν εἰς φρόνησιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:MRT-B03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:MRT-B03","MC_JP8E_L1_04:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MRT-B04","text":"Ἡ συνοχὴ διατηρεῖται μόνον ἐὰν ἡ ἀνακατασκευή μὴ ἀφαιρῇ τὴν σπειροειδῆ διαδοχήν, τὴν εἰκαστικὴν συμπλήρωσιν καὶ τὴν ἀποφατικὴν ἀναστολήν, αἵτινες εἰσὶ λειτουργικοὶ καὶ οὐχὶ ἐλλειπτικοὶ ὅροι.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:MRT-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_03:MRT-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_04:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261014T233607256890Z_b3791775","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Jp8E L2 01","source_sha256":"b80d754c9ebcf54425bde80e1b4bddc33a30bd34dcecece79899c25449f8bdb2","sha256":"c5e00703ed0c1be464fdfe38f8a9171f7dbdc031232281fee3427cec9c53e3e2","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-14T23:36:07.260361+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B01","text":"The problem of the system is how the generated, multipartite, mortal, and politically vulnerable can remain a cosmos, without abolishing difference, necessity, and decay.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:AS-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_04:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B02","text":"Unity is reframed as a product of relations, mediations, bonds, and distributions, not as primordial identity or the bare imposition of form.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:AS-B03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:AS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B03","text":"The eternal paradigm operates only if it becomes an internal measure through soul, body, sensation, proportion, and distributed causality.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:AS-B02","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B04","text":"Order does not abolish necessity, dissimilarity, or change, but is accomplished as their continual regulation and recomposition.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:AS-B05","MC_JP8E_L1_04:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B05","text":"The polity, education, and memory extend the cosmic mechanism into a historical field in which cause and order are recovered through bearers and judgment.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:AS-07","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B06","text":"True account becomes graded reconstruction, because things coming to be, phenomena, and surviving evidence do not provide equal certainty.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:AS-03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B07","text":"The whole movement is spiral: paradigm and measure become body and polity, while disturbance and forgetfulness return the account to a new ordering.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:AS-05","MC_JP8E_L1_03:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B08","text":"Closure remains provisional and fitting, because chōra, decay, and the absence of an absolute guarantee preclude final self-foundation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Unity is produced through functionally distinct and internally related parts, not through homogeneity.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Every order requires cause, proportion, mediation, and a receptive field in which difference can be bound without being effaced.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] The paradigm transforms from a transcendent measure into distributed causality, which operates through form, motion, body, soul, and institution.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Order is a regulative accomplishment, preserved only because it incorporates resistance, necessity, and productive difference.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Measure and proportion are the mechanisms that transform dissimilarity into motion, knowledge, education, and coordination.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Body, sensation, soul, memory, and testimony are active mediators through which cosmic causality becomes judgment, identity, and political action.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Time passes from a moving image of eternity into a post-catastrophic spiral, in which return produces a new form rather than the same restoration.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Truth is graded and revisable reconstruction, whose validity is judged according to the stability of the object and the reliability of mediations.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:AS-03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Catastrophe, dissimilarity, and necessity are not deviations from the system, but the conditions through which order is compelled to reproduce itself.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] The subject is no longer conceived as a self-sufficient center, but as a node of bodies, flows, education, memory, judgment, and political responsibility.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSD-02","MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Freedom is not an exit from relations, but repair, navigation, and resistance within the mechanism of necessity and power.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSD-05","MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] Likeness, knowledge, and truth retain their force only if they do not abolish the distance between paradigm, image, phenomenon, and account.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:BSA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-01","text":"The division of works retains its generative function, but shifts from political distribution to proportional mediation and finally to a geometrical grammar of power.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:RMD-01","MC_JP8E_L1_02:CGD-02","MC_JP8E_L1_04:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-02","text":"Mediation transforms from a bond of material elements into a psychic principle and then into a condition of governance, motion, and knowledge.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:RMD-02","MC_JP8E_L1_02:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-03","text":"Order reverses its meaning: from presupposed stability it becomes an uninterrupted accomplishment of recomposition.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:RMD-04","MC_JP8E_L1_04:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-04","text":"Soul shifts from cosmic mediation and sovereignty into an embodied node in which motion, affection, judgment, and reordering are intertwined.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_03:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-05","text":"Memory ceases to mean the replenishment of what is absent and becomes an archival power that reclassifies the present and distributes legitimacy.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:RMD-03","MC_JP8E_L1_01:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-06","text":"Myth and image are absorbed into a single graded mechanism, which preserves distance from the paradigm and yet conveys reconstructible knowledge.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:RMD-05","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-07","text":"The receptacle transforms from a material substrate into an apophatic condition of manifestation, which receives the forms without becoming one form among the others.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:RMD-05","MC_JP8E_L1_03:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-01","text":"Numerical, elemental, astronomical, and geometrical arrangements remain local mechanisms; the scaling principle is mediation, which connects the parts to the whole.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:CGD-01","MC_JP8E_L1_04:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-02","text":"Functional division, morphological causality, and archival continuity scale into a common logic of distributed constitution.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B02","MC_JP8E_L1_04:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-03","text":"The observation of cycles, the spatial asymmetry of memory, and the measurement of motion bridge cosmology, history, and political judgment.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:RLE-02","MC_JP8E_L1_01:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-04","text":"Catastrophe and resistance cease to be particular occurrences and become catalysts of the transition from order to repair.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:RMD-04","MC_JP8E_L1_03:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-05","text":"The self-sufficiency of polity, body, or account is discarded as a local illusion and recoded as a derivative of relations, transmissions, and controls.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GNX-02","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GNX-01","text":"A unity identified with mixture, homogeneity, or absolute likeness is excluded, because difference is a condition of cohesion.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GNX-01","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GNX-02","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GNX-02","text":"A self-founded city, an autonomous cosmos, and self-forming matter are excluded, because everything that comes to be depends upon cause, mediation, receptivity, and transmission.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:DBN-01","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GNX-01","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GNX-03","text":"Linear time, absolute place, and universal orientation are excluded, because time and place are produced from cyclical, relational, and historical changes.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GNX-03","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GNX-04","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GNX-04","text":"It is excluded that presence, sensation, phenomenon, affection, or name automatically provide their essence or cause.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:GNX-04","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GNX-04","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:GNX-05","text":"Equal and complete demonstration concerning the eternal, the generated, the phenomenon, and the highest cause is excluded.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GNX-05","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GNX-05","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that the part, the body, or the thing can constitute the ultimate subject of order, since each is the result of generative relations.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:DBN-01","MC_JP8E_L1_04:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that difference signifies pure dissolution or that unity requires the elimination of otherness.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:DBN-02","MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that event, phenomenon, name, and truth are self-present, because memory and manifestation depend on selective mediations.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:DBN-02","MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that freedom or psychic autonomy pre-exists conflict, body, time, and cosmic distribution.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:DBN-03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that the image can be completely identified with the paradigm, because distance is the condition of likeness itself.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:DBN-03","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSA-01","text":"The deepest absence is the impossible immediacy between paradigm, cosmos, truth, and memory; every relation passes through image, body, account, or testimony.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_02:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSA-02","text":"Chōra indicates a necessary but inexpressible foundation, which receives manifestation and yet cannot become a fully present object of knowledge.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSA-03","text":"An unbroken archive and a universal map of the whole are absent, so that truth operates only through fragments, relations, and continual reconstructions.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSA-04","text":"A pure outside is prohibited, because neither freedom nor interpretation can be entirely detached from power, causality, and relation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSA-05","text":"A final coincidence between closed cosmic perfection and historical political continuity is absent, because the latter depends upon threat, decay, and interruption.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_01:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-01","text":"Paradigmatic order becomes politically and historically active only when it is transmitted through bodies, education, institutions, and memory.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-02","text":"The dynamic preservation of the cosmos and post-catastrophic memory converge: continuity exists only through the reproduction of forms after interruption.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-03","text":"Chōra and the absence of void constitute a productive tension: receptivity is not pure emptiness, but a field in which every entry and formation is causally bound.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-12","MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_04:AS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-04","text":"True account requires a double test: measurement of periodic motions and common judgment concerning preserved testimonies.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L1_03:XD-05","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-05","text":"Liberatory action does not annul power, but transforms power into an unstable instrument of resistance, reordering, and the risk of new hegemony.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-11","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-06","text":"Myth and image can function as controlled archives only if their likeness preserves distance from complete identification.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-12","MC_JP8E_L1_01:XD-03","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSD-01","text":"Reality becomes a multilayered normative topology, in which cosmology, physics, politics, and history are different scales of the same mediated order.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L1_02:BSD-01","MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSD-02","text":"Time becomes a double mechanism: cyclical repetition that measures order, and historical interruption that compels a new synthesis.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_02:HSI-03","MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSD-03","text":"Sovereignty shifts from possession of self-sufficient power to administration of transitions, media of memory, and the conditions of interpretive authority.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-10","MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-01","MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSD-04","text":"Science becomes a hierarchy of fitting accounts, in which precision is not decided universally, but according to the ontological stability and accessibility of the object.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-12","MC_JP8E_L1_03:MME-05","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSD-05","text":"The free political and cognitive agent becomes a guardian of mediations, who does not leave the cosmos but regulates relations within it.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-10","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-11","MC_JP8E_L1_01:HSI-04","MC_JP8E_L1_03:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RLE-01","text":"Paradigm → image → bodily and psychic motion → measure → recognition of the paradigm; the return confirms likeness without abolishing distance.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:RLE-04","MC_JP8E_L1_03:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RLE-02","text":"Form → dissimilarity → power → resistance → reordering → new form; difference returns as a condition of the order it seemed to threaten.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_04:RLE-01","MC_JP8E_L1_03:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RLE-03","text":"Catastrophe → loss of bearers → archival asymmetry → reconstitution of political possibility → new testing of identity and account.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:RLE-02","MC_JP8E_L1_01:AFM-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RLE-04","text":"Sight → periodicity → number → time → philosophical imitation → correction of psychic revolutions; sensation becomes at once an entry of error and a path of reordering.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:RLE-02","MC_JP8E_L1_03:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:RLE-05","text":"The central attractive pole is the fitting account of likeness with distance, which connects cause, order, memory, and action without false completion.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:RLE-05","MC_JP8E_L1_02:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:FGS-B01","text":"Goodness and eternal paradigm + rejection of self-generated matter → necessity of mediated creation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:FGS-B02","text":"Proportion and mediation + receptive field → formed difference and cohesive bodily composition.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:FGS-B03","text":"Bodily composition + necessity + absence of void → pressure, resistance, transmission, and dynamic recomposition.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:FGS-B04","text":"Psychic motion + sensation + measure → discrimination, knowledge, error, and possibility of repair.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:FGS-B05","text":"Education + political distribution + archival memory → historical identity, common judgment, and practical resistance.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-10","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:FGS-B06","text":"Catastrophe + forgetfulness + limit of demonstration → image and apophatic account → new search for order.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AFM-B01","text":"Goodness and paradigm → distributed creative order → rejection of autonomous genesis.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:FGS-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_03:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AFM-B02","text":"Heterogeneity → proportion and morphological distinction → dissimilarity, power, and relation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:FGS-B02","MC_JP8E_L1_04:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AFM-B03","text":"Bodily composition → transmission of motions and affections → sensation, error, and possibility of cosmic learning.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:FGS-B02","MC_JP8E_L1_04:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AFM-B04","text":"Relative phenomenon → observation of periodicity → number and time → educative correction of soul.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:FGS-B03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AFM-B05","text":"Forgetfulness and interruption → testimony and archive → reconstitution of past political possibility.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:AFM-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_01:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:AFM-B06","text":"Tripartite ontology and limit of knowledge → image and apophatic account → recommencement of the search for foundation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:FGS-B06","MC_JP8E_L1_01:AFM-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-01","text":"The city and the cosmos are reclassified as wholes produced through distributed works, souls, motions, memories, and internal measures.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:MME-01","MC_JP8E_L1_02:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-02","text":"Body and matter cease to be passive residues and become networks of active transmissions, formations, and causal interdependencies.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:MME-02","MC_JP8E_L1_04:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-03","text":"Time becomes a common field of cyclical measurement, decay, forgetfulness, recomposition, and new political appearance.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:MME-03","MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-04","text":"Knowledge transforms from possession of things into reconstruction of mechanisms, whose precision is judged according to the mode and stability of being.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:MME-04","MC_JP8E_L1_03:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-05","text":"The dualities of myth and truth, one and many, stability and change cease to be absolute and become relations of control, transmission, and networking.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:MME-04","MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-06","text":"Strategy shifts from the seizure of objects to the administration of mediations through which memory, unity, freedom, and interpretive authority are produced.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:ALT-B01","text":"On the surface, natures, guardians, bodies, elements, cities, wars, stars, affections, myths, and places constitute the explicit objects of the system.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_04:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:ALT-B02","text":"Structurally, mediation, causality, receptivity, form, flow, necessity, archive, and transmission constitute the common mechanism of generation and continuity.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B02","MC_JP8E_L1_03:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategically, the struggle concerns the regulation of the conditions through which motions become knowledge, memories become legitimacy, and power becomes freedom or domination.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:ALT-B04","text":"Ontologically and temporally, reality is generated and caused, but not self-manifest; it endures through cyclical decay, motion, and reordering.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_04:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:ALT-B05","text":"Methodologically and performatively, the account imitates its object: it divides, measures, connects, recomposes, and arrives at a limit of apophatic suspension.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:ALT-B05","MC_JP8E_L1_04:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:FL-01","text":"Cosmic closure and self-sufficiency are strained against political continuity, which depends upon external threat, martial trial, and catastrophic interruption.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_01:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:FL-02","text":"Intelligible order is posited as principle, but chōra and necessity remain necessary and not fully subordinated conditions of generation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:FL-01","MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:FL-03","text":"Truth seeks common judgment, but judgment depends on fragmentarily preserved testimonies, selective bearers, and unequally distributed memory.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:FL-02","MC_JP8E_L1_01:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:FL-04","text":"Unity requires a multitude of internal relations, but universality cannot fully reconcile the multiplicity of opposing motions and places.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:FL-01","MC_JP8E_L1_04:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:FL-05","text":"The liberatory mission requires power sufficient for resistance, but this same power preserves the possibility of new hegemony.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:FL-03","MC_JP8E_L1_01:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MP-01","text":"The paradigm transforms from an eternal archetype into an active rule of cosmic, cognitive, and political recomposition.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:CGD-04","MC_JP8E_L1_01:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MP-02","text":"Soul transforms from the governing mediation of the cosmos into an embodied site of affection, judgment, measurement, and repair.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:MP-01","MC_JP8E_L1_03:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MP-03","text":"Chōra transforms from a receptive substrate into an apophatic limit, which permits generation because it refuses to receive a form of its own.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:RMD-05","MC_JP8E_L1_03:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MP-04","text":"Memory transforms from preservation of the past into a power of selection, reclassification, and political ratification of the present.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:RMD-03","MC_JP8E_L1_01:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MP-05","text":"Order transforms from final stability into continuous accomplishment, which needs anomaly as the material of its own continuity.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:RMD-04","MC_JP8E_L1_04:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:HSI-01","text":"Reality is a hierarchical and complete mechanism of transmission, in which form, cause, necessity, chōra, memory, and recomposition mutually produce one another.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:HSI-01","MC_JP8E_L1_03:HSI-01","MC_JP8E_L1_04:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:HSI-02","text":"The subject is an intracosmic node and not an external judge, because body, flows, affections, account, and judgment coincide in it.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSD-02","MC_JP8E_L1_04:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:HSI-03","text":"Time is spiral circulation, in which return preserves measure, but decay and interruption produce a new phase of the same system.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_03:HSI-03","MC_JP8E_L1_04:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:HSI-04","text":"Possible navigation consists in guarding memory, distinguishing mediations, regulating relations, and resisting the conversion of freedom into domination.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:HSI-04","MC_JP8E_L1_03:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms of paradigmatic mediation, productive difference, receptivity, dynamic order, memory, and graded account suffice for reconstruction of the central mechanism.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:MRT-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_02:MRT-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_04:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest where cause, form, body, necessity, memory, and recomposition operate as a single spiral sequence.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:MRT-B02","MC_JP8E_L1_03:MRT-B02","MC_JP8E_L1_04:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MRT-B03","text":"Underdetermined remain the mode of intelligible activity upon necessity, the legitimation of hierarchy, the relation of chōra and form, and the transition from microstructure to practical wisdom.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:MRT-B03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:MRT-B03","MC_JP8E_L1_04:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_01:MRT-B04","text":"Cohesion is preserved only if reconstruction does not remove spiral succession, conjectural supplementation, and apophatic suspension, which are functional rather than deficient conditions.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_01:MRT-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_03:MRT-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_04:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_JP8E_L2_01@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_JP8E_L2_01:v1","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Jp8E L2 01"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Timaeus","title":"Jp8E L2 01","context":"Chapters 01–48 · Section 17 · Section 18 · Section 19 · Section 20 · Section 21 · Section 22 · Section 23 · Section 24 · Section 25 · Section 26 · Section 27 · Section 28 · Section 29 · Section 30 · Section 31 · Section 32 · Section 33 · Section 34 · Section 35 · Section 36 · Section 37 · Section 38 · Section 39 · Section 40 · Section 41 · Section 42 · Section 43 · Section 44 · Section 45 · Section 46 · Section 47 · Section 48 · Section 49 · Section 50 · Section 51 · Section 52 · Section 53 · Section 54 · Section 55 · Section 56 · Section 57 · Section 58 · Section 59 · Section 60 · Section 61 · Section 62 · Section 63 · Section 64","original_title":"Jp8E L2 01","original_context":"Chapters 01–48 · Section 17 · Section 18 · Section 19 · Section 20 · Section 21 · Section 22 · Section 23 · Section 24 · Section 25 · Section 26 · Section 27 · Section 28 · Section 29 · Section 30 · Section 31 · Section 32 · Section 33 · Section 34 · Section 35 · Section 36 · Section 37 · Section 38 · Section 39 · Section 40 · Section 41 · Section 42 · Section 43 · Section 44 · Section 45 · Section 46 · Section 47 · Section 48 · Section 49 · Section 50 · Section 51 · Section 52 · Section 53 · Section 54 · Section 55 · Section 56 · Section 57 · Section 58 · Section 59 · Section 60 · Section 61 · Section 62 · Section 63 · Section 64"},"original_title":"Jp8E L2 01","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02","layer":"MC","title":"Jp8E L1 02","order":2,"parents":["CL_JP8E_05","CL_JP8E_06","CL_JP8E_07","CL_JP8E_08"],"revision":"MC_JP8E_L1_02@R001","sha256":"43f073fe1afe233bfe97afd244a7a562509c42b7588f3fc31b2410b0d18cc577","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AS-B01","text":"Τὸ πρόβλημα τοῦ συστήματος συνίσταται εἰς τὸ πῶς τὸ γενητὸν καὶ πολυμερὲς δύναται νὰ γένηται ἓν κάλλιστον ὅλον χωρὶς νὰ ἀφανίζῃ τὴν διαφοράν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AS-B02","text":"Ἡ λύσις μεταφέρει τὴν αἰτίαν τῆς τάξεως ἀπὸ τὴν αὐτάρκειαν τῶν μερῶν εἰς τὴν ἀγαθότητα, τὸ ἀΐδιον παράδειγμα καὶ τὴν μεσολαβητικὴν δύναμιν τοῦ λόγου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:AFM-B03","CL_JP8E_05:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AS-B03","text":"Ἡ ἑνότης οὐ δίδοται ὡς ἁπλῆ ταυτότης, ἀλλὰ παράγεται διὰ δεσμοῦ, ἀναλογίας, μίξεως, ψυχῆς καὶ κυκλικῆς κινήσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:GA-07","CL_JP8E_07:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AS-B04","text":"Ἡ κλειστότης τοῦ κόσμου μετασχηματίζει τὴν τελειότητα εἰς ἐσωτερικὴν αὐτάρκειαν, ἐν ᾗ οὐδὲν ἔξωθεν δύναται νὰ συμπληρῶσῃ ἢ νὰ διαφθείρῃ τὸ ὅλον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:SP-04","CL_JP8E_06:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AS-B05","text":"Ἡ ψυχὴ γίνεται ἡ ἐσωτερικὴ ἀρχὴ δι’ ἧς ἡ γεωμετρικὴ καὶ ἀριθμητικὴ σύνθεσις μεταπίπτει εἰς αὐτοκίνησιν, γνῶσιν καὶ κυριαρχίαν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:GA-08","CL_JP8E_07:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AS-B06","text":"Ὁ χρόνος οὐκ ἔστιν αὐθύπαρκτος ῥοή, ἀλλὰ ἡ κινητὴ καὶ μετρητὴ εἰκὼν τῆς ἀϊδίου τάξεως, ἥτις φανερώνει συγχρόνως ὁμοιότητα καὶ ἀπόστασιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:GA-10","CL_JP8E_08:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AS-B07","text":"Ἡ τελικὴ κίνησις ἀπὸ τὸ παράδειγμα πρὸς τὴν εἰκόνα ἐπιστρέφει εἰς ὅριον γνώσεως, ὅπου ὁ λόγος ἀποδέχεται τὸν εἰκότα χαρακτῆρα καὶ τὴν ἀνθρώπινον περατότητα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:GA-10","CL_JP8E_08:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Ἡ ἀγαθότης καὶ τὸ ἀΐδιον παράδειγμα εἰσὶν αἱ ἀρχαὶ αἳ καθιστῶσι τὴν κοσμικὴν τάξιν δυνατὴν καὶ δεσμευτικήν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:GA-01","CL_JP8E_05:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-02","text":"[Mutating] Ἡ ἑνότης μεταβάλλεται ἀπὸ μοναδικότητα καὶ συναρμογὴν εἰς ἐσωτερικὴν αὐτοσχέσιν ἡ ὁποία περιέχει καὶ διοικεῖ τὴν διαφοράν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:RMD-04","CL_JP8E_06:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Ἡ ἀναλογία καὶ ἡ μεσότης μετατρέπουσι τὰ ἀνόμοια εἰς σχέσιν, καὶ τὴν σχέσιν εἰς συνεκτικὴν τάξιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:SP-02","CL_JP8E_07:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Ἡ ψυχὴ εἶναι ἡ παραγωγὸς μεσότης δι’ ἧς ἡ μίξις γίνεται κίνησις, γνῶσις, αὐτοκυβέρνησις καὶ κοσμικὴ ζωή.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:GA-05","CL_JP8E_06:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Ἡ τελειότης ὁρίζεται ὡς κλειστὴ αὐτάρκεια τοῦ ὅλου, ἣ ἀποκλείει πᾶσαν ἐξωτερικὴν χρείαν χωρὶς νὰ καταργῇ τὰ ἐσωτερικὰ μέρη.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:GA-04","CL_JP8E_06:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Ἡ διαφορά παύει νὰ σημαίνῃ ἀταξίαν καὶ γίνεται ὁ ἀναγκαῖος ὅρος τῆς ῥυθμισμένης ἑνότητος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:GA-11","CL_JP8E_07:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-07","text":"[Terminal] Ὁ χρόνος παράγεται ἐκ τῆς ψυχικῆς καὶ κοσμικῆς κινήσεως ὡς μετρητὴ εἰκὼν τοῦ αἰῶνος καὶ οὐχὶ ὡς περιέχον τῆς κινήσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:GA-10","CL_JP8E_08:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Ἡ εἰκὼν ὁμοιοῖ τὸ γενητὸν πρὸς τὸ ἀΐδιον μόνον διὰ τῆς διατηρήσεως τῆς ὀντολογικῆς ἀποστάσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:GA-12","CL_JP8E_08:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Τὸ μέτρον δὲν εἶναι ἐξωτερικὸς κανών, ἀλλὰ ἐσωτερικὴ σχέσις κύκλων, κινήσεων καὶ λόγων δι’ ἧς τὸ κοσμικὸν γίγνεται γνωστόν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:GA-05","CL_JP8E_08:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Ἡ γνώσις τοῦ γενητοῦ ὑποτάσσεται εἰς τὸ εἶδος τοῦ ὄντος αὐτῆς καὶ λήγει εἰς λόγον εἰκότα, ὄψιν, λογισμόν καὶ ὅριον παραδόσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:RMD-08","CL_JP8E_08:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] Ἡ ὁμοιότης πρὸς τὸ νοητὸν ζῷον συνάγει τὸ σῶμα, τὴν ψυχήν, τὴν κίνησιν, τὸν χρόνον καὶ τὴν γνώσιν εἰς μίαν κοσμικὴν μηχανήν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:GA-12","CL_JP8E_08:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:RMD-01","text":"Ἡ ἀγαθότης διατηρεῖ τὴν ταυτότητά της ὡς ἀρχὴ τάξεως, ἀλλὰ μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ ἠθικὴ βούλησις εἰς κοσμογονικὴν ἀναγκαιότητα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:RMD-01","CL_JP8E_05:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:RMD-02","text":"Ἡ μεσότης μετακινείται ἀπὸ δεσμὸν ὑλικῶν στοιχείων εἰς μαθηματικὸν λόγον, ψυχικὴν λειτουργίαν καὶ ἀρχὴν κυβερνήσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:RMD-02","CL_JP8E_07:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:RMD-03","text":"Ἡ κλειστότης ἀπὸ ἀποκλεισμὸς ἐξωτερικῆς φθορᾶς γίνεται καθολικὸς ὅρος ὀντολογικῆς τελειότητος καὶ αὐτογνωσίας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:GA-06","CL_JP8E_06:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:RMD-04","text":"Ὁ χρόνος μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ παράγωγον κυκλικῆς κινήσεως εἰς μέτρον περιόδων, ἐν συνεχείᾳ δὲ εἰς σημεῖον τῆς ἀποστάσεως εἰκόνος καὶ παραδείγματος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:RMD-07","CL_JP8E_08:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:RMD-05","text":"Ἡ διαίρεσις ἀπὸ ἀριθμητικὴ κατανομή μετατρέπεται εἰς παραγωγὴ κινήσεως, γνώσεως καὶ χρονικῆς πολλαπλότητος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:RMD-04","CL_JP8E_07:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:RMD-06","text":"Ἡ ἀπόδειξις διατηρεῖται ἐν τοῖς μετρητοῖς φαινομένοις, ἀλλὰ μετατίθεται εἰς παράδοσιν ὅταν τὸ ὑπεράνθρωπον ὑπερβῇ τὴν ἀνθρώπινον ἐποπτείαν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:RMD-06","CL_JP8E_08:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:CGD-01","text":"Αἱ λεπτομερεῖς διατάξεις στοιχείων, μεσοτήτων, ἀριθμῶν καὶ πλανητικῶν φορῶν μένουσι τοπικαὶ μηχαναί, ἐνῷ ἡ μεσολάβησις καθίσταται καθολικὴ γενετικὴ ἀρχή.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:CGD-01","CL_JP8E_07:CGD-01","CL_JP8E_08:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:CGD-02","text":"Ἡ ἀναλογία κλιμακοῦται ἀπὸ τεχνικὴν σύστασιν σωμάτων εἰς γενικὸν νόμον, δι’ οὗ πᾶσα διαφορὰ γίνεται νοητὴ, μετρητὴ καὶ ἐσωτερικῶς συνεκτική.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:CGD-01","CL_JP8E_07:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:CGD-03","text":"Ἡ ψυχὴ γεφυροῖ τὰ ἐπίπεδα, διότι ἀνασυνδέει τὴν ὀντολογικὴν μίξιν πρὸς τὸ σῶμα, τὴν κίνησιν, τὴν γνῶσιν καὶ τὸν χρόνον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:CGD-03","CL_JP8E_06:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:CGD-04","text":"Ἡ ὁμοιότης παραδείγματος καὶ εἰκόνος γίνεται καταλύτης ὅστις συνδέει τὴν κοσμολογίαν πρὸς τὴν ἐπιστημολογίαν καὶ τὴν χρονικότητα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:CGD-04","CL_JP8E_07:CGD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:CGD-05","text":"Ἡ στατικὴ σύλληψις τῆς τελειότητος καθίσταται ἀνεπαρκής, διότι τὸ τέλειον ὅλον ἐμφανίζεται ὡς συνεχῶς αὐτορυθμιζομένη καὶ χρονικῶς εἰκονικὴ τάξις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:CGD-04","CL_JP8E_07:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GNX-01","text":"Ἀποκλείεται κόσμος ὃς εἶναι αὐτόνομος συναθροισμὸς σωμάτων, διότι τὸ σῶμα ἀπαιτεῖ παράδειγμα, λόγον, ψυχὴν καὶ ἐσωτερικὴν τάξιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:GNX-01","CL_JP8E_07:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GNX-02","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἑνότης ἡ ὁποία καταργεῖ τὴν διαφοράν ἢ συνδέει τὰ μέρη μόνον κατὰ παράθεσιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:GNX-04","CL_JP8E_07:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GNX-03","text":"Ἀποκλείεται τελειότης ἐξαρτωμένη ἀπὸ ἔξωθεν περιβάλλον, ἀνταλλαγήν, αἴσθησιν ἢ προσθήκην.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:GNX-02","CL_JP8E_06:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GNX-04","text":"Ἀποκλείεται χρόνος αὐθύπαρκτος, γραμμικός καὶ ἀνεξάρτητος τῆς ψυχικῆς καὶ κοσμικῆς κινήσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:GNX-04","CL_JP8E_08:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GNX-05","text":"Ἀποκλείεται λόγος ὁ ὁποῖος ἀξιοῖ ἴσην βεβαιότητα περὶ τοῦ ἀϊδίου, τοῦ γενητοῦ καὶ τοῦ θείου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:GNX-05","CL_JP8E_08:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:DBN-01","text":"Δεῖ νὰ εἶναι ψεῦδος ὅτι τὸ μέρος, τὸ σῶμα ἢ ἡ ὕλη δύναται καθ’ ἑαυτὸ νὰ ἀποτελέσῃ τὸ ἔσχατον ὑποκείμενον τάξεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:DBN-02","CL_JP8E_06:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:DBN-02","text":"Δεῖ νὰ εἶναι ψεῦδος ὅτι ἡ ἑτερότης ἢ ἡ διαφορὰ δύναται νὰ γένηται κόσμος ἄνευ μεσότητος, κοινοῦ μέτρου καὶ ψυχικῆς τάξεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_07:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:DBN-03","text":"Δεῖ νὰ εἶναι ψεῦδος ὅτι ἡ ὁμοίωσις δύναται νὰ τελεσθῇ διὰ πλήρους ταυτίσεως, διότι ἡ εἰκών ζῇ μόνον ἐν τῇ ἀποστάσει ἀπὸ τὸ παράδειγμα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:BSA-04","CL_JP8E_07:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:BSA-01","text":"Ἀδύνατος μένει κόσμος ὅπου τὸ ἓν εἶναι ἄνευ πολλῶν ἢ τὰ πολλὰ εἶναι ἄνευ ἐσωτερικῆς ἀναφορᾶς πρὸς τὸ ἓν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:BSA-02","CL_JP8E_05:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:BSA-02","text":"Ἀπούσα μένει ἡ δυνατότης τελείου κόσμου ὃς ἀναπαύεται ἐν πλήρει συμπτώσει πρὸς τὸ παράδειγμα, διότι ἡ ἀνομοιότης ἀνανεοῖ τὴν κίνησιν τῆς συμπληρώσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:BSA-05","CL_JP8E_08:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:BSA-03","text":"Ἀπούσα μένει γνῶσις ἡ ὁποία κατέχει τὸ θεῖον ὡς πλήρως παρόν ἀντικείμενον, καὶ ἐκ ταύτης τῆς ἀπουσίας παράγονται ὅριον, εἰκὼς λόγος καὶ παράδοσις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:BSA-04","CL_JP8E_05:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:XD-01","text":"Ἡ ἀγαθότης τοῦ παραδείγματος καὶ ἡ ἀναλογικὴ μεσότης συντίθενται εἰς νόμον, καθ’ ὃν ἡ τάξις οὐχὶ ἐπιβάλλεται μόνον ἐπὶ τῆς ὕλης, ἀλλὰ παράγει τὸν τρόπον τῆς συνοχῆς αὐτῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-03","CL_JP8E_05:GA-05","CL_JP8E_06:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:XD-02","text":"Ἡ κλειστότης καὶ ἡ ψυχικὴ κυριαρχία καθιστῶσι τὸν κόσμον αὐτοσυντηρούμενον μόνον ἐπειδὴ ἡ ἐσωτερικὴ σχέσις διακρίνεται ἀπὸ πᾶσαν ἐξωτερικὴν ἐξάρτησιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-05","CL_JP8E_06:XD-01","CL_JP8E_06:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:XD-03","text":"Ἡ ψυχὴ ἐμφανίζεται ὡς ἡ κοινὴ μορφὴ ἐν ᾗ ὁ γεωμετρικὸς δεσμὸς, ὁ ἀριθμητικὸς λόγος καὶ ἡ κυκλικὴ κίνησις γίγνονται μία δύναμις διακρίσεως καὶ γνώσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-09","CL_JP8E_06:XD-06","CL_JP8E_07:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:XD-04","text":"Ὁ χρόνος γίνεται ἡ ἐπιτελεστικὴ ἀπόδειξις ὅτι ἡ εἰκὼν δύναται νὰ μιμῆται τὸ ἀΐδιον δίχα ταυτίσεως, διότι αἱ περίοδοι ἐπαναφέρουσι τὸ ταὐτὸν ἐν γενητῇ κινήσει.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-09","CL_JP8E_07:XD-05","CL_JP8E_08:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:XD-05","text":"Ἡ ἀδυναμία πλήρους ἐποπτείας οὐκ ἀναιρεῖ τὴν κοσμικὴν τάξιν, ἀλλὰ διανέμει τὸ κύρος τῆς γνώσεως μεταξὺ λογισμοῦ, εἰκότος λόγου καὶ παραδόσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-10","CL_JP8E_05:XD-06","CL_JP8E_08:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:BSD-01","text":"Ἡ πραγματικότης συγκροτεῖται ὡς ἱεραρχία μετασχηματισμῶν, ἐν ᾗ ὕλη, σῶμα, ψυχή, κίνησις, χρόνος καὶ γνῶσις εἰσὶ διαδοχικαὶ ἐνεργοποιήσεις τοῦ αὐτοῦ λόγου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:BSD-01","CL_JP8E_07:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:BSD-02","text":"Ἡ τελειότης οὐκ ἔστιν ἀκινησία, ἀλλὰ κυκλικὴ αὐτοσυντήρησις, καθ’ ἣν ἡ διαφορά διαρκῶς ῥυθμίζεται καὶ ἐπανεντάσσεται εἰς τὴν ἑνότητα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:BSD-05","CL_JP8E_06:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:BSD-03","text":"Ἡ κοσμολογία καὶ ἡ ἐπιστημολογία ἀποτελοῦσιν ἓν σύστημα, διότι ὁ τρόπος τῆς κινήσεως καὶ τὸ μέτρον τῆς εἰκόνος ὁρίζουσι τὸ εἶδος τῆς δυνατῆς γνώσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:BSD-03","CL_JP8E_05:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:BSD-04","text":"Ἡ ἀτέλεια τοῦ γενητοῦ οὐκ ἔστιν ἁπλὴ στέρησις, ἀλλὰ παραγωγικὴ ἔλλειψις ἡ ὁποία διατηρεῖ τὴν κίνησιν πρὸς ὁμοιότητα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:XD-03","CL_JP8E_08:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:BSD-05","text":"Ἡ ἐξωτερικὴ ἐλευθερία τῶν μερῶν ἀντικαθίσταται ὑπὸ ἐσωτερικῆς λειτουργικῆς διαφορᾶς, ὥστε τὸ μέρος νὰ ὑπάρχῃ μόνον καθ’ ὅσον μετέχει τῆς ζωῆς τοῦ ὅλου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:BSD-02","CL_JP8E_06:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:RLE-01","text":"Ἀγαθότης → ὁμοίωσις → τάξις → κάλλος → ἐπιβεβαίωσις τῆς ἀγαθότητος· οὗτος ὁ κύκλος μετατρέπει τὴν δημιουργίαν εἰς αὐτοβεβαιούμενον λόγον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:RLE-01","CL_JP8E_05:AFM-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:RLE-02","text":"Ἀνομοιότης → μεσότης → μίξις → λόγος → διαίρεσις → κύκλος → ῥυθμισμένη ἑνότης· ἡ διαφορὰ ἐπανέρχεται ὡς ὅρος τῆς τάξεως ἣν ἐφαίνετο νὰ ἀπειλῇ.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:RLE-01","CL_JP8E_07:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:RLE-03","text":"Σῶμα → ψυχὴ ἐντός → ψυχὴ περιέχουσα → αὐτοκίνησις → γνῶσις → ἀναδρομικὴ ἀνασύστασις τοῦ σώματος ὡς ζῴου κοσμικοῦ.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:RLE-04","CL_JP8E_07:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:RLE-04","text":"Παράδειγμα → εἰκών → κίνησις → χρόνος → μέτρον → σημεῖον → ἐπανεπιβεβαίωσις τοῦ παραδείγματος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:RLE-01","CL_JP8E_08:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:RLE-05","text":"Ἀτρακτὴρ τοῦ συστήματος μένει ἡ ὁμοιότης μετὰ ἀποστάσεως, διότι αὕτη μόνη συνάπτει τὸ ἀΐδιον πρὸς τὸ γενητὸν δίχα καταλύσεως ἑκατέρου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:RLE-06","CL_JP8E_08:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:FGS-B01","text":"Ἀγαθότης καὶ ἀΐδιον παράδειγμα → βούλησις ὁμοιώσεως → ἀπόρριψις ἀτάκτου αὐτονομίας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:FGS-B01","CL_JP8E_05:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:FGS-B02","text":"Ἑτερογένεια στοιχείων → ἀναλογία καὶ μεσότης → συνεκτικὸν σῶμα → κλειστὴ ὁλότης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:FGS-B01","CL_JP8E_06:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:FGS-B03","text":"Κλειστὴ ὁλότης → ψυχὴ περιέχουσα → κυκλικὴ αὐτοκίνησις → κόσμος ἔμψυχος καὶ νοήμων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:FGS-B05","CL_JP8E_05:AFM-B10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:FGS-B04","text":"Ψυχικὴ κίνησις → διάκρισις ταὐτοῦ καὶ θατέρου → γνῶσις καὶ χρονικὴ μέτρησις → κινητὴ εἰκὼν τοῦ αἰῶνος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:FGS-B04","CL_JP8E_07:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:FGS-B05","text":"Εἰκονικὴ ὁμοιότης → ὄψις καὶ λογισμός → ὅριον πλήρους ἀποδείξεως → εἰκὼς λόγος καὶ παράδοσις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:FGS-B06","CL_JP8E_05:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AFM-B01","text":"Ἄτακτος πολλότης → ἀγαθὸν παράδειγμα → ἀναλογικὴ μεσότης → τάξις ἡ διατηροῦσα διαφοράν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:AFM-B09","CL_JP8E_07:AFM-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AFM-B02","text":"Ἀναλογικὴ τάξις → κλειστὴ σφαιρικὴ ὁλότης → αὐτάρκεια → ψυχικὴ κυριαρχία.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:AFM-B01","CL_JP8E_06:AFM-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AFM-B03","text":"Ψυχικὴ κυριαρχία → κυκλικὴ κίνησις → διάκρισις γνώσεως → χρόνος ὡς εἰκὼν αἰῶνος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:AFM-B01","CL_JP8E_07:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AFM-B04","text":"Χρονικὸν μέτρον → ὁρατὰ σημεῖα → λογιστικὴ ἀνάγνωσις → ὅριον ἀνθρωπίνης γνώσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:AFM-B08","CL_JP8E_08:AFM-B11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AFM-B05","text":"Ὅριον γνώσεως → εἰκὼς λόγος καὶ παράδοσις → διατήρησις τῆς ἀποστάσεως παραδείγματος καὶ κόσμου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:FGS-B06","CL_JP8E_05:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:MME-01","text":"Ὁ κόσμος ἀναταξινομεῖται ἀπὸ ἄθροισμα πραγμάτων εἰς ζῷον αὐτοπεριεχόμενον, ἔμψυχον, νοήμον καὶ ἐσωτερικῶς μετρημένον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:MME-01","CL_JP8E_06:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:MME-02","text":"Ἡ σχέσις μέρους καὶ ὅλου ἀναστρέφεται, διότι τὰ μέρη οὐ παρέχουσιν τὸ μέτρον τοῦ παντός, ἀλλὰ λαμβάνουσι τὸ εἶναι αὐτῶν ἐκ τῆς μετοχῆς εἰς αὐτό.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:MME-02","CL_JP8E_05:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:MME-03","text":"Ὁ χρόνος ἀναταξινομεῖται ἀπὸ δοχεῖον συμβάντων εἰς δίκτυον κυκλικῶν καὶ συγχρονισμένων σχέσεων, δι’ ὧν ἡ αἰωνιότης γίνεται αἰσθητῶς ὁρατή.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:MME-04","CL_JP8E_08:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:MME-04","text":"Ἡ γνῶσις ἀνασυντάσσεται ὡς ἐνεργὸς διάκρισις, μετρητικὴ ἀνάγνωσις καὶ ἐνσυνείδητος παραίτησις ἀπὸ ψευδῆ καθολικὴν ἀκρίβειαν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:MME-05","CL_JP8E_08:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:ALT-B01","text":"Ἐπιφανειακῶς, τὸ σύστημα πραγματεύεται στοιχεῖα, σφαῖραν, ψυχήν, οὐρανίους κύκλους, θεῖα γένη καὶ χρονικὰ μεγέθη.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:ALT-B01","CL_JP8E_08:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:ALT-B02","text":"Δομικῶς, τὸ ὅλον παράγεται διὰ τῆς ἐπαναλήψεως μεσολαβήσεως μεταξὺ παραδείγματος, εἰκόνος, λόγου, κινήσεως καὶ ψυχῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:ALT-B02","CL_JP8E_07:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:ALT-B03","text":"Μεταστρατηγικῶς, ἡ ἐξήγησις ὀφείλει νὰ ἀρχίζῃ ἀπὸ τὸ ὅλον καὶ τὰς παραγωγικὰς σχέσεις, οὐχὶ ἀπὸ τὰ μεμονωμένα φαινόμενα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:ALT-B03","CL_JP8E_07:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:ALT-B04","text":"Ὀντολογικῶς καὶ χρονικῶς, τὸ ἀΐδιον προηγείται ὡς μέτρον, ἐνῷ τὸ γενητὸν διαρκῶς ἐνεργοποιεῖ τὴν ἀπόστασίν του διὰ κινήσεως καὶ χρόνου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:ALT-B04","CL_JP8E_07:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:ALT-B05","text":"Μεθοδολογικῶς καὶ ἐπιτελεστικῶς, ὁ λόγος τοῦ συστήματος μιμεῖται τὸ ἀντικείμενον, διαιρῶν, μετρῶν, ἐπανασυνδέων καὶ ὁριοθετῶν τὰ πεδία τῆς γνώσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:ALT-B05","CL_JP8E_08:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:FL-01","text":"Ἡ ἑνότης ἀπαιτεῖ πληθύν σχέσεων ἐντὸς τοῦ ὅλου, ἐνῷ ἡ τελειότης ἀπαιτεῖ ἀπουσίαν σχέσεως πρὸς τὸ ἔξω· ἡ διάκρισις ἐσωτερικοῦ καὶ ἐξωτερικοῦ λύει τὸ πρόβλημα μόνον μερικῶς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:FL-01","CL_JP8E_06:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:FL-02","text":"Ἡ ψυχὴ εἶναι συγχρόνως προϊόν προγενεστέρας μίξεως καὶ ἀρχὴ ἡ ὁποία παράγει κίνησιν, σῶμα, χρόνον καὶ γνῶσιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:FL-02","CL_JP8E_06:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:FL-03","text":"Ἡ ἀναλογία ἑνοποιεῖ εἰκόνα καὶ παράδειγμα, ἀλλὰ ἡ ἴδια σχέσις ἀπαιτεῖ νὰ μὴν ἀναιρεθῇ ἡ ἀδιάβατος ὀντολογικὴ διαφορά.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:FL-03","CL_JP8E_08:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:MP-01","text":"Ἡ μεσότης μεταλλάσσεται ὅταν ἀπὸ δεσμὸς μεταξὺ στοιχείων γίγνηται ψυχικὴ ἀρχὴ ἡ ὁποία ἐσωτερικῶς περιέχει καὶ ἀρχικῶς διοικεῖ.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:MP-01","CL_JP8E_07:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:MP-02","text":"Ὁ χρόνος μεταλλάσσεται ὅταν ἀπὸ ἀποτέλεσμα κινήσεως γίγνηται μέτρον, σημεῖον καὶ ὅρος τῆς ἀποστάσεως μεταξὺ τοῦ γενητοῦ καὶ τοῦ ἀϊδίου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:MP-04","CL_JP8E_08:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:MP-03","text":"Ἡ μετάβασις ἀπὸ ἀπόδειξιν εἰς παράδοσιν οὐκ ἔστιν ἁπλὴ ἔλλειψις, ἀλλὰ ἀσταθὴς μεταφορά τοῦ ἐρμηνευτικοῦ κύρους εἰς θεσμισμένην μνήμην.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:FL-01","CL_JP8E_08:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:HSI-01","text":"Ἡ πραγματικότης εἶναι ζῶσα, σφαιρικὴ καὶ αὐτοπεριεχομένη μηχανή, ἣ μετατρέπει τὴν ἀνομοιότητα εἰς ἐσωτερικῶς διατεταγμένην κίνησιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:HSI-01","CL_JP8E_08:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:HSI-02","text":"Τὸ ὑποκείμενον νοεῖται ὡς ψυχικὸς κόμβος ὃς διακρίνει, συνδέει, κινεί καὶ γιγνώσκει, οὐχὶ ὡς αὐτάρκης μονάς ἔξωθεν τοῦ παντός.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:HSI-02","CL_JP8E_06:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:HSI-03","text":"Ὁ χρόνος εἶναι κυκλικὴ ἐπανόρθωσις τῆς διαφορᾶς πρὸς τὸ ταὐτόν, ἥτις οὐδέποτε καταργεῖ τὴν ἀπόστασιν ἀπὸ τὸ παράδειγμα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:HSI-03","CL_JP8E_07:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:HSI-04","text":"Ἡ δυνατὴ κίνησις οὐκ ἔστιν ἔξοδος ἐκ τοῦ κόσμου, ἀλλὰ ἀκριβέστερη ἀνάγνωσις τῶν λόγων, τῶν κύκλων καὶ τοῦ ὁρίου τῆς γνῶσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:HSI-04","CL_JP8E_07:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:MRT-B01","text":"Αἱ ἀρχαὶ τῆς ἀγαθότητος, τοῦ παραδείγματος, τῆς μεσότητος, τῆς ψυχικῆς κυριαρχίας, τῆς κυκλικότητος καὶ τῆς εἰκονικῆς χρονικότητος ἀρκοῦσι πρὸς ἀνακατασκευὴν τοῦ συστήματος ἐν τῷ παρόντι ἐπιπέδῳ.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:MRT-B01","CL_JP8E_07:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:MRT-B02","text":"Ἡ ἰσχυροτέρα συνοχὴ κεῖται ἐν τῇ ἀκολουθίᾳ παραδείγματος, ἀναλογικῆς συνθέσεως, κλειστῆς ὁλότητος, ψυχῆς καὶ χρόνου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:MRT-B02","CL_JP8E_06:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:MRT-B03","text":"Ὑποκαθορισμένα μένουσιν ὁ τρόπος τῆς ἐνδογενοῦς συντηρήσεως, ἡ ἀκριβὴς μετάβασις ἀπὸ ἀναλογίαν εἰς ψυχικήν κυριαρχίαν, καὶ ἡ συμφιλίωσις βιαίας συναρμογῆς πρὸς ἁρμονικὴν τάξιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:MRT-B04","CL_JP8E_07:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:MRT-B04","text":"Ἡ ἀνακατασκευή διασῴζει τὴν γενετικὴν κίνησιν μόνον ἐὰν διατηρῇ τὰς ἀναδρομὰς ἀπὸ εἰκόνα εἰς μέτρον, ἀπὸ μέτρον εἰς σημεῖον, καὶ ἀπὸ σημεῖον εἰς ὅριον γνώσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:MRT-B05","CL_JP8E_05:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261014T233302643434Z_7e2ed99e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Jp8E L1 02","source_sha256":"43f073fe1afe233bfe97afd244a7a562509c42b7588f3fc31b2410b0d18cc577","sha256":"5d8e1a89fea14c21a1529d3e6612a2712b9d7d8408b97b37c62e4f4e85ef1dba","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-14T23:33:02.646418+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AS-B01","text":"The problem of the system consists in how what is generated and manifold can become one most beautiful whole without effacing difference.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AS-B02","text":"The solution transfers the cause of order from the self-sufficiency of the parts to goodness, the eternal paradigm, and the mediating power of logos.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:AFM-B03","CL_JP8E_05:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AS-B03","text":"Unity is not given as simple identity, but is produced through bond, proportion, mixture, soul, and circular motion.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:GA-07","CL_JP8E_07:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AS-B04","text":"The closure of the cosmos transforms perfection into internal self-sufficiency, in which nothing from outside can complete or corrupt the whole.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:SP-04","CL_JP8E_06:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AS-B05","text":"Soul becomes the internal principle through which geometrical and numerical composition passes over into self-motion, knowledge, and rule.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:GA-08","CL_JP8E_07:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AS-B06","text":"Time is not self-subsistent flux, but the moving and measurable image of eternal order, which manifests simultaneously likeness and distance.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:GA-10","CL_JP8E_08:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AS-B07","text":"The final movement from paradigm to image returns to a limit of knowledge, where logos accepts its likely character and human finitude.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:GA-10","CL_JP8E_08:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Goodness and the eternal paradigm are the principles that render cosmic order possible and binding.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:GA-01","CL_JP8E_05:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-02","text":"[Mutating] Unity changes from singularity and fitting-together into an internal self-relation that contains and governs difference.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:RMD-04","CL_JP8E_06:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Proportion and mediation transform dissimilars into relation, and relation into cohesive order.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:SP-02","CL_JP8E_07:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Soul is the productive mediation through which mixture becomes motion, knowledge, self-government, and cosmic life.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:GA-05","CL_JP8E_06:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Perfection is defined as the closed self-sufficiency of the whole, which excludes every external need without abolishing the internal parts.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:GA-04","CL_JP8E_06:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Difference ceases to signify disorder and becomes the necessary condition of regulated unity.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:GA-11","CL_JP8E_07:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-07","text":"[Terminal] Time is produced from psychic and cosmic motion as a measurable image of eternity, and not as that which contains motion.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:GA-10","CL_JP8E_08:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] The image assimilates what is generated to the eternal only through preserving ontological distance.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:GA-12","CL_JP8E_08:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Measure is not an external rule, but an internal relation of circles, motions, and logoi through which the cosmic becomes knowable.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:GA-05","CL_JP8E_08:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Knowledge of what is generated is subordinated to the kind of being it is and terminates in likely logos, vision, calculation, and a limit of transmission.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:RMD-08","CL_JP8E_08:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] Likeness to the intelligible living being brings together body, soul, motion, time, and knowledge into one cosmic machine.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:GA-12","CL_JP8E_08:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:RMD-01","text":"Goodness retains its identity as a principle of order, but changes from ethical will into cosmogenic necessity.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:RMD-01","CL_JP8E_05:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:RMD-02","text":"Mediation shifts from a bond of material elements into mathematical ratio, psychic function, and a principle of governance.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:RMD-02","CL_JP8E_07:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:RMD-03","text":"Closure changes from exclusion of external corruption into a universal condition of ontological perfection and self-knowledge.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:GA-06","CL_JP8E_06:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:RMD-04","text":"Time changes from a derivative of circular motion into a measure of periods, and thereafter into a sign of the distance between image and paradigm.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:RMD-07","CL_JP8E_08:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:RMD-05","text":"Division is transformed from numerical distribution into the production of motion, knowledge, and temporal multiplicity.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:RMD-04","CL_JP8E_07:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:RMD-06","text":"Demonstration is retained in measurable phenomena, but is displaced into transmission when the superhuman exceeds human inspection.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:RMD-06","CL_JP8E_08:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:CGD-01","text":"Detailed arrangements of elements, mediations, numbers, and planetary revolutions remain local mechanisms, whereas mediation becomes a universal generative principle.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:CGD-01","CL_JP8E_07:CGD-01","CL_JP8E_08:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:CGD-02","text":"Proportion is scaled up from a technical constitution of bodies into a general law through which every difference becomes intelligible, measurable, and internally cohesive.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:CGD-01","CL_JP8E_07:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:CGD-03","text":"Soul bridges the levels, because it reconnects ontological mixture to body, motion, knowledge, and time.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:CGD-03","CL_JP8E_06:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:CGD-04","text":"The likeness of paradigm and image becomes a catalyst connecting cosmology to epistemology and temporality.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:CGD-04","CL_JP8E_07:CGD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:CGD-05","text":"The static conception of perfection becomes inadequate, because the perfect whole appears as a continuously self-regulating and temporally imagistic order.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:CGD-04","CL_JP8E_07:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GNX-01","text":"A cosmos that is an autonomous aggregate of bodies is excluded, because body requires paradigm, logos, soul, and internal order.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:GNX-01","CL_JP8E_07:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GNX-02","text":"A unity that abolishes difference or connects the parts merely by juxtaposition is excluded.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:GNX-04","CL_JP8E_07:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GNX-03","text":"A perfection dependent on an external environment, exchange, sensation, or addition is excluded.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:GNX-02","CL_JP8E_06:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GNX-04","text":"A self-subsistent, linear time independent of psychic and cosmic motion is excluded.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:GNX-04","CL_JP8E_08:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:GNX-05","text":"A logos claiming equal certainty concerning the eternal, the generated, and the divine is excluded.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:GNX-05","CL_JP8E_08:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that the part, body, or matter can by itself constitute the ultimate subject of order.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:DBN-02","CL_JP8E_06:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that otherness or difference can become a cosmos without mediation, a common measure, and psychic order.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_07:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that assimilation can be accomplished through complete identification, since the image lives only in its distance from the paradigm.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:BSA-04","CL_JP8E_07:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:BSA-01","text":"Impossible remains a cosmos in which the one is without the many or the many are without an internal reference to the one.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:BSA-02","CL_JP8E_05:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:BSA-02","text":"Absent remains the possibility of a perfect cosmos resting in complete coincidence with the paradigm, because dissimilarity renews the movement of completion.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:BSA-05","CL_JP8E_08:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:BSA-03","text":"Absent remains knowledge that possesses the divine as a fully present object, and from this absence are produced limit, likely logos, and transmission.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:BSA-04","CL_JP8E_05:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:XD-01","text":"The goodness of the paradigm and analogical mediation are composed into a law according to which order is not merely imposed upon matter, but produces the mode of its cohesion.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-03","CL_JP8E_05:GA-05","CL_JP8E_06:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:XD-02","text":"Closure and psychic rule render the cosmos self-sustaining only because internal relation is distinguished from every external dependence.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-05","CL_JP8E_06:XD-01","CL_JP8E_06:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:XD-03","text":"Soul appears as the common form in which geometrical bond, numerical ratio, and circular motion become one power of distinction and knowledge.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-09","CL_JP8E_06:XD-06","CL_JP8E_07:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:XD-04","text":"Time becomes the performative demonstration that the image can imitate the eternal without identification, because the periods restore the same in generated motion.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-09","CL_JP8E_07:XD-05","CL_JP8E_08:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:XD-05","text":"The impossibility of complete inspection does not abolish cosmic order, but distributes the authority of knowledge among calculation, likely logos, and transmission.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_02:GA-10","CL_JP8E_05:XD-06","CL_JP8E_08:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:BSD-01","text":"Reality is constituted as a hierarchy of transformations, in which matter, body, soul, motion, time, and knowledge are successive actualizations of the same logos.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:BSD-01","CL_JP8E_07:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:BSD-02","text":"Perfection is not immobility, but circular self-sustenance, according to which difference is continually regulated and reintegrated into unity.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:BSD-05","CL_JP8E_06:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:BSD-03","text":"Cosmology and epistemology constitute one system, because the mode of motion and the measure of the image determine the kind of possible knowledge.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:BSD-03","CL_JP8E_05:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:BSD-04","text":"The imperfection of the generated is not simple privation, but a productive lack that maintains motion toward likeness.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:XD-03","CL_JP8E_08:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:BSD-05","text":"The external freedom of the parts is replaced by internal functional difference, so that the part exists only insofar as it participates in the life of the whole.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:BSD-02","CL_JP8E_06:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:RLE-01","text":"Goodness → assimilation → order → beauty → confirmation of goodness; this cycle transforms creation into a self-confirming logos.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:RLE-01","CL_JP8E_05:AFM-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:RLE-02","text":"Dissimilarity → mediation → mixture → logos → division → circle → regulated unity; difference returns as a condition of the order it seemed to threaten.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:RLE-01","CL_JP8E_07:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:RLE-03","text":"Body → soul within → encompassing soul → self-motion → knowledge → recursive reconstitution of the body as a cosmic living being.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:RLE-04","CL_JP8E_07:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:RLE-04","text":"Paradigm → image → motion → time → measure → sign → reconfirmation of the paradigm.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:RLE-01","CL_JP8E_08:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:RLE-05","text":"The attractor of the system remains likeness with distance, because this alone joins the eternal to the generated without dissolving either.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:RLE-06","CL_JP8E_08:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:FGS-B01","text":"Goodness and eternal paradigm → will of assimilation → rejection of disordered autonomy.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:FGS-B01","CL_JP8E_05:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:FGS-B02","text":"Heterogeneity of elements → proportion and mediation → cohesive body → closed wholeness.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:FGS-B01","CL_JP8E_06:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:FGS-B03","text":"Closed wholeness → encompassing soul → circular self-motion → ensouled and intelligent cosmos.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:FGS-B05","CL_JP8E_05:AFM-B10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:FGS-B04","text":"Psychic motion → distinction of same and other → knowledge and temporal measurement → moving image of eternity.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:FGS-B04","CL_JP8E_07:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:FGS-B05","text":"Imagistic likeness → vision and calculation → limit of full demonstration → likely logos and transmission.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:FGS-B06","CL_JP8E_05:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AFM-B01","text":"Disordered multiplicity → good paradigm → analogical mediation → order preserving difference.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:AFM-B09","CL_JP8E_07:AFM-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AFM-B02","text":"Analogical order → closed spherical wholeness → self-sufficiency → psychic rule.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:AFM-B01","CL_JP8E_06:AFM-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AFM-B03","text":"Psychic rule → circular motion → distinction of knowledge → time as image of eternity.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:AFM-B01","CL_JP8E_07:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AFM-B04","text":"Temporal measure → visible signs → calculative reading → limit of human knowledge.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:AFM-B08","CL_JP8E_08:AFM-B11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:AFM-B05","text":"Limit of knowledge → likely logos and transmission → preservation of the distance between paradigm and cosmos.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:FGS-B06","CL_JP8E_05:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:MME-01","text":"The cosmos is reclassified from an aggregate of things into a self-containing, ensouled, intelligent, and internally measured living being.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:MME-01","CL_JP8E_06:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:MME-02","text":"The relation of part and whole is reversed, because the parts do not provide the measure of the all, but receive their being from participation in it.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:MME-02","CL_JP8E_05:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:MME-03","text":"Time is reclassified from a receptacle of events into a network of circular and synchronized relations through which eternity becomes sensibly visible.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:MME-04","CL_JP8E_08:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:MME-04","text":"Knowledge is reconstituted as active distinction, measured reading, and conscious renunciation of false universal precision.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:MME-05","CL_JP8E_08:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system treats elements, sphere, soul, heavenly circles, divine kinds, and temporal magnitudes.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:ALT-B01","CL_JP8E_08:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:ALT-B02","text":"Structurally, the whole is produced through the repetition of mediation among paradigm, image, logos, motion, and soul.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:ALT-B02","CL_JP8E_07:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategically, explanation must begin from the whole and productive relations, not from isolated phenomena.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:ALT-B03","CL_JP8E_07:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:ALT-B04","text":"Ontologically and temporally, the eternal precedes as measure, while the generated continually actualizes its distance through motion and time.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:ALT-B04","CL_JP8E_07:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:ALT-B05","text":"Methodologically and performatively, the logos of the system imitates its object, dividing, measuring, reconnecting, and delimiting the fields of knowledge.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:ALT-B05","CL_JP8E_08:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:FL-01","text":"Unity requires a multitude of relations within the whole, whereas perfection requires the absence of relation to what is outside; the distinction between internal and external resolves the problem only partially.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:FL-01","CL_JP8E_06:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:FL-02","text":"Soul is at once the product of a prior mixture and the principle that produces motion, body, time, and knowledge.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:FL-02","CL_JP8E_06:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:FL-03","text":"Proportion unifies image and paradigm, but the same relation requires that the impassable ontological difference not be abolished.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:FL-03","CL_JP8E_08:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:MP-01","text":"Mediation changes when, from a bond between elements, it becomes a psychic principle that internally contains and originatively governs.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:MP-01","CL_JP8E_07:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:MP-02","text":"Time changes when, from an effect of motion, it becomes measure, sign, and condition of the distance between the generated and the eternal.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:MP-04","CL_JP8E_08:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:MP-03","text":"The transition from demonstration to transmission is not simple lack, but an unstable transfer of interpretive authority into institutionalized memory.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:FL-01","CL_JP8E_08:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:HSI-01","text":"Reality is a living, spherical, and self-containing machine that transforms dissimilarity into internally ordered motion.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:HSI-01","CL_JP8E_08:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:HSI-02","text":"The subject is conceived as a psychic node that distinguishes, connects, moves, and knows, not as a self-sufficient unit outside the all.","refs":["CL_JP8E_07:HSI-02","CL_JP8E_06:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:HSI-03","text":"Time is a circular restoration of difference toward the same, which never abolishes the distance from the paradigm.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:HSI-03","CL_JP8E_07:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:HSI-04","text":"Possible movement is not an exit from the cosmos, but a more precise reading of logoi, circles, and the limit of knowledge.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:HSI-04","CL_JP8E_07:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:MRT-B01","text":"The principles of goodness, paradigm, mediation, psychic rule, circularity, and imagistic temporality suffice for reconstructing the system at the present level.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:MRT-B01","CL_JP8E_07:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:MRT-B02","text":"The strongest cohesion lies in the sequence of paradigm, analogical composition, closed wholeness, soul, and time.","refs":["CL_JP8E_05:MRT-B02","CL_JP8E_06:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:MRT-B03","text":"Underdetermined remain the mode of endogenous maintenance, the precise transition from proportion to psychic rule, and the reconciliation of violent fitting-together with harmonious order.","refs":["CL_JP8E_06:MRT-B04","CL_JP8E_07:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_02:MRT-B04","text":"Reconstruction preserves the generative movement only if it retains the recursions from image to measure, from measure to sign, and from sign to the limit of knowledge.","refs":["CL_JP8E_08:MRT-B05","CL_JP8E_05:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_JP8E_L1_02@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_JP8E_L1_02:v1","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Jp8E L1 02"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Timaeus","title":"Jp8E L1 02","context":"Chapters 13–24 · Section 29 · Section 30 · Section 31 · Section 32 · Section 33 · Section 34 · Section 35 · Section 36 · Section 37 · Section 38 · Section 39 · Section 40","original_title":"Jp8E L1 02","original_context":"Chapters 13–24 · Section 29 · Section 30 · Section 31 · Section 32 · Section 33 · Section 34 · Section 35 · Section 36 · Section 37 · Section 38 · Section 39 · Section 40"},"original_title":"Jp8E L1 02","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02","layer":"MC","title":"Jp8E L2 02","order":2,"parents":["MC_JP8E_L1_05","MC_JP8E_L1_06","MC_JP8E_L1_07"],"revision":"MC_JP8E_L2_02@R001","sha256":"0b409c5e4a142f16fc0191272bf9c6e6916262faf8d87cb076ccc23b2dbd8088","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B01","text":"Τὸ πρόβλημα τοῦ συστήματος ἐστὶν ὅπως τὸ ζῷον, τὸ σῶμα καὶ ἡ τάξις νοηθῶσιν ὡς γιγνόμενα ἐκ σχέσεων καὶ μεταβολῶν, οὐχὶ ἐκ ἀκινήτων οὐσιῶν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B02","text":"Τὸ σῶμα γίνεται ἡ καθολικὴ μηχανὴ ἐν ᾗ κίνησις, ῥοή, πάθος, ὄργανον, σημεῖον καὶ μορφὴ ἀλλήλοις μεταγράφονται.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B03","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B03","text":"Ἡ ζωὴ καὶ ἡ φθορὰ προέρχονται ἐκ τοῦ αὐτοῦ δικτύου, ἀλλὰ διαφέρουσι κατὰ μέτρον, διέξοδον, ἀναλογίαν καὶ φοράν ἀναδράσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B04","text":"Ἡ ἱεραρχία δὲν ἀναιρεῖ τὰ μέρη, ἀλλὰ διανέμει τὰς διαφορὰς εἰς λειτουργικὴν σύνθεσιν διὰ μεσολαβήσεων.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B02","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B05","text":"Ἡ γνώσις καὶ ἡ θεραπεία συμπίπτουσιν ὡς ἀνασύνταξις κινήσεων, διότι ἡ ἀληθὴς κρίσις ὀφείλει νὰ μεταβάλλῃ τὸν γιγνώσκοντα.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B05","MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B06","text":"Ἡ ἀτέλεια καὶ ἡ θνητότης δὲν εἰσὶ μόνον στερήσεις, ἀλλὰ ὅροι ἀναπληρώσεως, ἀντιστάθμισης, παιδείας καὶ νέας διατάξεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B06","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B07","text":"Ὁ χρόνος γίνεται σπειροειδὴς αἰτιότης, καθ᾽ ἣν τὸ πρότερον ἐγγράφεται εἰς τὸ παρόν καὶ τὸ μέλλον προδιατάσσει τὴν νῦν μορφήν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-11","MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B08","text":"Ἡ κατάληξις εἶναι ἀνοικτὴ τάξις, ἣ διαμένει μόνον ἐὰν διορθοῖ τὰς ἰδίας ῥοὰς χωρὶς νὰ ἀξιοῖ αὐτάρκειαν ἢ ἔξοδον ἐκ τοῦ ὅλου.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B08","MC_JP8E_L1_07:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Τὸ ὂν παράγεται ἐκ σχέσεων, κινήσεων καὶ μεταβολῶν, οὐκ ἐκ αὐτοτελοῦς ποιότητος ἢ ἀμεταβλήτου πυρῆνος.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Τὸ σῶμα εἶναι κατανεμημένος κόμβος μεταφράσεως, ἐν ᾧ ὕλη, πάθος, ὄργανον, ῥοή καὶ μορφὴ γίγνονται λειτουργίαι ἀλλήλων.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_06:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Ἡ κίνησις μεταβάλλεται ἀπὸ φυσικὸν πάθος εἰς καθολικὸν κριτήριον ὑγιείας, γνώσεως, ἀρετῆς, μορφῆς καὶ κοσμικῆς συγγενείας.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Ἡ ἑνότης εἶναι ἱεραρχημένη μεσολάβησις διαφορών, καὶ οὐχὶ ὁμοιογένεια ἢ ἐκκαθάρισις τῶν κατωτέρων.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Ὁ λόγος, ἡ ῥοή, ἡ μεσότης καὶ ἡ διέξοδος συνιστῶσι τὸ κοινὸν μέτρον ὅπερ διακρίνει τὴν διατήρησιν ἀπὸ τὴν ἀναστροφήν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_06:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Ἡ νόσος, ἡ ἄνοια καὶ ἡ κακία εἰσὶν ὁμόλογοι τρόποι καθ᾽ οὓς ἡ αὐτὴ γενετικὴ μηχανὴ ἀναστρέφεται εἰς αὐτοφθορὰν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Ἡ γνώσις εἶναι θεραπευτικὴ μόνον ὅταν ἐπανεισέρχηται εἰς τὸ σῶμα καὶ τὴν ψυχὴν ὡς ῥύθμισις τῶν περιφορῶν αὐτῶν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Ὁ χρόνος οὐκ ἔστιν ἐξωτερικὸν μέτρον, ἀλλὰ αἰτία ἐπιστροφῆς, ἐξασθενήσεως, γενεαλογικῆς συσσωρεύσεως καὶ προνοητικῆς προδιατάξεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B07","MC_JP8E_L1_07:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Ἡ τελεολογία δὲν καταλύει τὴν ἀνάγκην, ἀλλὰ διοικεῖ τὴν ἀντίστασιν, τὴν ἐπιθυμίαν καὶ τὴν ὑλικὴν ἀνεπάρκειαν ὡς ὄργανα γενέσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Ἡ ἐλευθερία καὶ ἡ αὐτονομία νοοῦνται ὡς συνεργὸς αὐτορρύθμισις ἐντὸς τοῦ ὅλου, οὐχὶ ὡς ἀνεξαρτησία ἀπὸ σῶμα, χρόνον καὶ κόσμον.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_06:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-01","text":"Ἡ σχέσις διατηρεῖται ὡς γενετικὴ ἀρχή, ἀλλὰ μετακινείται ἀπὸ αἰσθητὴν παραγωγὴν φαινομένου εἰς φυσιολογίαν, παθολογίαν, παιδείαν καὶ κοσμικὴν τάξιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:CGD-02","MC_JP8E_L1_07:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-02","text":"Τὸ σῶμα μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ τόπον μεταδόσεως εἰς δίκτυον ῥοῶν, καὶ ἐκεῖθεν εἰς ὁρατὴν ἐγγραφὴν ψυχικῆς καὶ ἠθικῆς ἱστορίας.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-03","text":"Ἡ κίνησις μεταπίπτει ἀπὸ μηχανικὴν αἰτίαν εἰς μέτρον διαγνώσεως, θεραπείας, ἀγωγῆς καὶ ἐξομοιώσεως πρὸς τὸν κόσμον.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-07","MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-04","text":"Ἡ συμμετρία παύει νὰ σημαίνῃ στατικὴν ἰσότητα καὶ γίνεται δυναμικὸς κανὼν ἀνταποδόσεως, θεραπείας καὶ κοσμικῆς διαβαθμίσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L1_07:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-05","text":"Ἡ ἀνάγκη μεταβάλλεται ἀπὸ ὑλικὴν πίεσιν εἰς ὑπηρεσίαν τάξεως, ἀλλὰ ἐπανέρχεται ὡς φθορὰ ὅταν ἡ ῥύθμισις ἀποτύχῃ.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_06:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-06","text":"Ἡ νόσος μετακινείται ἀπὸ τοπικὴν ἀνωμαλίαν εἰς ἀναδραστικὸν κύκλον, καθ᾽ ὃν τὸ ἀποτέλεσμα ἐπανέρχεται καὶ ἐνισχύει τὴν αἰτίαν αὐτοῦ.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L1_06:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-07","text":"Ἡ ἀλήθεια μεταβάλλεται ἀπὸ διάκρισιν σημείων εἰς ἐνεργὸν μετασχηματισμὸν τοῦ γιγνώσκοντος καὶ τῶν ὅρων τῆς πράξεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-08","text":"Ἡ ἐπιθυμία παύει νὰ εἶναι μερικὸν πάθος καὶ γίνεται ὁ τρόπος καθ᾽ ὃν ἡ τάξις παράγει γένεσιν διὰ ἐντάσεως καὶ ἀντιστάσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-11","MC_JP8E_L1_07:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:CGD-01","text":"Αἱ τοπικαὶ θεωρίαι χυμῶν, στοιχείων, ὀργάνων, φαρμάκων καὶ εἰδικῶν παθῶν μένουσιν ὑπηρετικὰ παραδείγματα, ἐὰν μὴ ἀναχθῶσιν εἰς τύπους διανεμημένης αἰτιότητος.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:CGD-01","MC_JP8E_L1_07:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:CGD-02","text":"Ἡ ῥοή, ἡ ἀφομοίωσις, ἡ κίνησις καὶ ἡ μεσολάβησις κλιμακοῦνται εἰς καθολικὰ σχήματα, ἐπειδὴ συνδέουσιν αἴσθησιν, βίον, νόσον, ἦθος καὶ κόσμον.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:CGD-02","MC_JP8E_L1_07:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:CGD-03","text":"Γέφυρα τῶν μονάδων γίνεται ἡ ἀρχὴ ὅτι ὑγίεια, νόσος, φρόνησις καὶ κακία εἰσὶ διαφοραὶ κατευθύνσεως τῶν αὐτῶν κινήσεων.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:CGD-03","MC_JP8E_L1_07:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:CGD-04","text":"Καταλύτης μεταβάσεως γίνεται ἡ διάγνωσις ὅταν παύσῃ νὰ ὀνομάζῃ μόνον τὸ σύμπτωμα καὶ ἀνασυνθέτῃ ὁδοὺς, τόπους, δεσμοὺς καὶ ἀναστροφάς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSD-04","MC_JP8E_L1_06:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:CGD-05","text":"Ἡ ἠθικὴ κρίσις ἀναβαθμίζεται μόνον ὅταν ἀναγνωσθῇ ὡς γενεαλογία σωμάτων, κρασέων, παιδεύσεων καὶ περιβαλλόντων.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:CGD-06","text":"Ἡ τελεολογία παύει νὰ εἶναι τοπικὴ προσθήκη, ὅταν ἐξηγῇ κοινῶς τὴν ὀργάνωσιν τῆς ἀνάγκης, τὴν θεραπείαν καὶ τὴν ἀνοικτὴν διατήρησιν τοῦ ὅλου.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:CGD-03","MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-01","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ αὐτοτελὴς οὐσία, διότι τὸ φαινόμενον, ἡ ταυτότης καὶ ἡ λειτουργία παράγονται διὰ σχέσεως καὶ μεταβολῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GNX-01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-02","text":"Ἀποκλείεται τὸ σῶμα ὡς ἀδρανὲς περίβλημα ἢ ἄθροισμα ἀνεξαρτήτων μερῶν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GNX-01","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-03","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ γραμμικὴ καὶ μονόδρομος αἰτιότης, διότι τὰ ἀποτελέσματα δύνανται νὰ ἐπανέρχωνται ὡς αἴτια νέας τάξεως ἢ φθορᾶς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GNX-02","MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-04","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ ὑγίεια ὡς ἀκινησία καὶ ἡ νόσος ὡς καθαρῶς ἐξωτερικὸν συμβεβηκός.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GNX-03","MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-05","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ ψυχὴ ὡς ἀμέτοχος κυβερνήτης καὶ ἡ γνώσις ὡς πληροφορία ἄνευ μεταβολῆς τοῦ βίου.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-01","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-06","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ τελείωσις ὡς φυγὴ ἀπὸ σῶμα, ἐπιθυμίαν καὶ κατώτερα μέρη, διότι ἡ πληρότης συνίσταται ἐν τῇ ρυθμίσει τῆς πολλαπλότητος.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:DBN-01","text":"Ψευδὴς δεῖ εἶναι ἡ ὑπόθεσις ὅτι λειτουργία, γνώσις ἢ ἦθος δύνανται νὰ νοηθῶσι χωρὶς ὑλικὴν, ἀνατομικὴν καὶ χωρικὴν διάταξιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:DBN-01","MC_JP8E_L1_07:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:DBN-02","text":"Ψευδὴς δεῖ εἶναι ἡ ὑπόθεσις ὅτι ψυχή, σῶμα, παιδεία, πολιτεία καὶ κόσμος ἔχουσιν ἀνεξαρτήτους αἰτίας.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:DBN-01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:DBN-03","text":"Ψευδὴς δεῖ εἶναι ἡ ὑπόθεσις ὅτι ἡ φθορὰ εἶναι ἑτερότης πρὸς τὴν γένεσιν, ἐπειδὴ ἡ διάλυσις ἐκ τῆς ἀναστροφῆς τῆς γενέσεως παράγεται.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:DBN-03","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:DBN-04","text":"Ψευδὴς δεῖ εἶναι ἡ ὑπόθεσις ὅτι ἡ θεραπεία ἢ ἡ ἀλήθεια ἐπιβάλλεται ἔξωθεν δίχα χρόνου, μέτρου καὶ ἐσωτερικῆς μεταβολῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:DBN-02","MC_JP8E_L1_05:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:DBN-05","text":"Ψευδὴς δεῖ εἶναι ἡ ὑπόθεσις ὅτι ἡ ἱεραρχία σώζεται διὰ τῆς καταστροφῆς τῶν κατωτέρων, διότι ἡ ὁλότης ἀπαιτεῖ τὴν λειτουργικὴν αὐτῶν συμπλοκήν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_07:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSA-01","text":"Ἀδύνατος καθίσταται κόσμος ἐν ᾧ ἡ ἀλήθεια ἢ ἡ κυριότης δίδοται ὡς καθαρὰ παρουσία ἄνευ σώματος, σημείων καὶ μεταγραφῶν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSA-02","text":"Ἀδύνατος καθίσταται ἀκίνητον κέντρον ἢ ἀσυσχέτιστον ὑποκείμενον, ὃ κυβερνᾷ ἄνευ τοῦ μεταβάλλεσθαι ὑπὸ τοῦ δικτύου.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSA-03","text":"Ἀδύνατος καθίσταται βίος ἄνευ κυκλοφορίας, ἀνταποδόσεως, περιορισμοῦ καὶ διαμεσολαβήσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSA-04","text":"Ἀδύνατος καθίσταται θεραπεία ἡ σώζουσα ἓν μέρος χωρὶς νὰ ἀνατάσσῃ τὴν σχέσιν αὐτοῦ πρὸς τὸ ὅλον.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_07:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSA-05","text":"Ἀδύνατος καθίσταται χρόνος καθαρῶς γραμμικός, διότι ἡ ἕξις, ἡ μορφὴ καὶ ἡ φθορὰ ἐπανέρχονται ὡς αἴτια τοῦ ὕστερον.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-01","text":"Ἡ σχεσιακὴ γένεσις, ἡ σωματικὴ κυκλοφορία καὶ ἡ ψυχοηθικὴ μεταβολὴ εἰσὶ τρεῖς κλίμακες μιᾶς μηχανῆς, ἐν ᾗ ἡ σχέσις παράγει μορφὴν διὰ διαφορᾶς καὶ μεταδόσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-02","text":"Ἡ ἱεραρχία λειτουργεῖ ὡς τέχνη διοχετεύσεως καὶ ὄχι ὡς γυμνὴ κυριότης, διότι ἡ ἀκεραιότης τοῦ ὅλου ἀπαιτεῖ ἄνισα μέρη ἀλλήλοις ἀποδιδόντα.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-10","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_06:RMD-06","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-03","text":"Ἡ παθολογία καὶ ἡ ἠθικὴ παρεκτροπὴ γίγνονται κοινὸν πρόβλημα ἀναστροφῆς ρυθμῶν, ὥστε ἡ διάγνωσις καὶ ἡ παιδεία νὰ ζητῶσι τὴν αὐτὴν γενεαλογίαν κινήσεων.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_07:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-04","text":"Ἡ ἑρμηνεία, ἡ διάγνωσις καὶ ἡ θεραπεία εἰσὶν ὁμόλογοι πράξεις, διότι ἑκάστη ἀνασυνθέτει ἀπὸ σημείων καὶ παθημάτων τὴν ἀφανῆ τάξιν τῶν σχέσεων.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B05","MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-05","text":"Τὸ παρόν σῶμα γίνεται τόπος διπλῆς ἀναδρομῆς, ἐπειδὴ προγενέστεραι κινήσεις ἐγγράφονται εἰς αὐτό, ἐνῶ μέλλουσαι χρεῖαι κατευθύνουσι τὴν παροῦσαν κατασκευήν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-11","MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-06","text":"Ἡ ἐξωτερικὴ ἐπέμβασις δικαιοῦται μόνον ἐὰν ἐπαναφέρει τὴν ἐσωτερικὴν αὐτορρύθμισιν καὶ οὐχὶ ἐὰν ἀντικαθιστᾷ τὸν θεραπευόμενον.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-10","MC_JP8E_L1_07:XD-02","MC_JP8E_L1_07:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-01","text":"Ἡ πραγματικότης ἀνασυντίθεται ὡς πολυεπίπεδον δίκτυον μετασχηματισμῶν, ἐν ᾧ ὀντολογία, φυσιολογία, ἠθικὴ καὶ κοσμολογία εἰσὶ φάσεις τῆς αὐτῆς σχεσιακῆς παραγωγῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSD-01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-02","text":"Ἡ ὑγίεια, ἡ φρόνησις καὶ ἡ ἀρετὴ δὲν δηλοῦσιν οὐσίας, ἀλλὰ ἐπιτυχεῖς ἀναλογίας κινήσεων αἳ διατηροῦσι τὴν ἑνότης ἄνευ κατακλείσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-03","text":"Ἡ πολιτικὴ καὶ ἡ παιδεία γίγνονται διαχείρισις τῶν ὅρων ὑφ᾽ οὓς δύναται νὰ γεννηθῇ ἡ αὐτορρύθμισις, καὶ οὐχὶ ἐξωτερικὴ ἐπιβολὴ βουλήσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-10","MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSD-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-04","text":"Ὁ χρόνος γίνεται αἰτιακὴ μνήμη τοῦ σώματος, διότι τὸ γῆρας, ἡ ἕξις καὶ ἡ μορφὴ εἰσὶν ἐπιστροφαὶ παλαιοτέρων λόγων κινήσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-07","MC_JP8E_L1_07:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-05","text":"Ἡ ἐλευθερία γίνεται τέχνη πλοηγήσεως καὶ ἀναδιατάξεως ῥοῶν, δεσμῶν καὶ ἐπιθυμιῶν, οὐχὶ ἀπαλλαγὴ ἀπὸ τοὺς ὅρους τῆς ἐνσώματης τάξεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-10","MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSD-05","MC_JP8E_L1_07:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RLE-01","text":"Σχέσις → διαφορά → ὄργανον → σημεῖον → ἑρμηνεία → νέα σύνθεσις· ἡ ἐξήγησις ἐπανενεργεῖ ἐπὶ τοῦ ἐξηγουμένου.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:RLE-01","MC_JP8E_L1_05:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RLE-02","text":"Φθορὰ → ἀναπλήρωσις → ἀφομοίωσις → ῥυθμισμένη ῥοή → νέα φθορά· ἡ συντήρησις περιέχει τὸ αἴτιον τῆς ἰδίας ἐξασθενήσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:RLE-01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RLE-03","text":"Ἀναστροφὴ ῥοῆς → νόσος ἢ ἄνοια → διάγνωσις γενεαλογίας → ἀνασύνταξις κινήσεων → δυνατὴ αὐτορρύθμισις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:RLE-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RLE-04","text":"Ἐπιθυμία → ἀντίστασις → γένεσις ἢ νόσος → σωματικὴ μορφή → νέος ὁρίζων ἐπιθυμίας καὶ μεταβολῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:RLE-05","MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RLE-05","text":"Ἐλκυστὴς τοῦ συστήματος εἶναι ἡ ρυθμισμένη ἀνταπόδοσις, ἣ ἐπανέρχεται ὡς συμμετρία, ὑγίεια, φρόνησις, θεραπεία καὶ κοσμικὴ ἐξομοίωσις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:RLE-05","MC_JP8E_L1_07:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RLE-06","text":"Ἡ κατάκλεισις εἶναι ὁ ἀρνητικὸς ἑλκυστής, καθ᾽ ὃν ἡ ῥοὴ ἀποβάλλει τὴν διέξοδον, τὸ ἀποτέλεσμα ἐπανεισέρχεται εἰς τὴν αἰτίαν, καὶ ἡ διατήρησις γίνεται διάλυσις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-08","MC_JP8E_L1_06:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:FGS-B01","text":"Ἀνάγκη ἢ δύναμις προσβάλλει ὕλην, καὶ ἡ ὕλη ἀντιδρᾷ διὰ κινήσεως, κράσεως, ῥοῆς καὶ διαφορᾶς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:FGS-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:FGS-B02","text":"Ἡ ἀντίδρασις διανέμεται εἰς ὄργανα, πλέγματα, δεσμοὺς καὶ ὁδούς, ὥστε τὸ πάθος νὰ γίνηται αἴσθησις, μορφή, σήμα ἢ περιορισμός.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:FGS-B02","MC_JP8E_L1_06:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:FGS-B03","text":"Αἱ διαφοροποιημέναι λειτουργίαι ὑπάγονται εἰς ἱεραρχίαν καὶ συμμετρίαν, αἳ μετατρέπουσι τὴν πολλαπλότητα εἰς διατηρήσιμον ἑνότητα.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:FGS-B03","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:FGS-B04","text":"Ἡ μεσολάβησις γεννᾷ σημεῖα, καὶ τὰ σημεῖα ἀπαιτοῦσι κρίσιν, διάγνωσιν καὶ παιδείαν πρὸς ἀνασύστασιν τῆς λογικῆς τάξεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:FGS-B05","MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:FGS-B05","text":"Ἡ κρίσις ἐπανεισέρχεται εἰς τὸν κριτήν, καὶ ἡ ὀρθὴ γνώσις γίνεται θεραπευτικὴ μεταβολὴ ψυχῆς, σώματος καὶ τρόπου κατοικήσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:FGS-B06","text":"Ἐὰν ἡ ῥύθμισις γένηται κατάκλεισις, τότε ἡ ἀναπλήρωσις μεταστρέφεται εἰς φθοράν, καὶ ἡ μηχανὴ λύει τοὺς δεσμοὺς οὓς πρότερον διετήρει.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:FGS-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AFM-B01","text":"Σχεσιακὴ γένεσις → σωματικὴ κίνησις καὶ πάθος → φαινόμενον ὡς λειτουργικὴ διαφορά.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:AFM-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AFM-B02","text":"Ροὴ καὶ ἀφομοίωσις → πρόσκαιρος σωματικὴ ταυτότης → ὑγίεια ὡς ῥυθμισμένη ἀνταπόδοσις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:AFM-B02","MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AFM-B03","text":"Ἀπόφραξις ἢ ἀσυμμετρία → ἀναστροφὴ γενέσεως → νόσος, ἄνοια καὶ ἐνδεχομένη συστημικὴ λύσις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:AFM-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AFM-B04","text":"Σημεῖον καὶ σύμπτωμα → γενεαλογικὴ ἀνάγνωσις ὁδῶν → διάγνωσις τοῦ βάθους τῆς διαταραχῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:MME-04","MC_JP8E_L1_06:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AFM-B05","text":"Διάγνωσις ἀλληλεξαρτήσεως → ἡπία ἀγωγὴ καὶ ἐπανόρθωσις ῥυθμῶν → αὐτορρύθμισις καὶ θεραπευτικὴ γνώσις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:FGS-B03","MC_JP8E_L1_07:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AFM-B06","text":"Ἐπανόρθωσις τοῦ γνωρίζοντος → κοσμικὴ θεωρία → ἐξομοίωσις πρὸς τὴν διαβαθμισμένην τάξιν τοῦ ὅλου.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:AFM-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AFM-B07","text":"Ἐξομοίωσις → συνεχεῖς διορθώσεις καὶ νέαι μεταβολαί → ἀνοικτὴ συνοχὴ ἢ, διὰ κατάκλεισιν, νέα σπειροειδὴς φθορά.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B08","MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MME-01","text":"Καταλύεται ἡ διάκρισις φύσεως καὶ ἠθικῆς, διότι ἡ σωματικὴ ῥύθμισις φέρει ἤδη κρίσιν περὶ μέτρου, τάξεως καὶ φθορᾶς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:MME-02","MC_JP8E_L1_07:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MME-02","text":"Καταλύεται ἡ διάκρισις ὑγιείας καὶ νόσου ὡς διαφορᾶς οὐσίας, καὶ ἀντικαθίσταται ὑπὸ διαφορᾶς φοράς, τόπου, μέτρου καὶ ἀναδράσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSD-05","MC_JP8E_L1_06:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MME-03","text":"Τὸ ὑποκείμενον ἀναταξινομεῖται ἀπὸ αὐτοτελῆ κύριον εἰς κόμβον διελεύσεως, ἐγγραφῆς, θεραπείας καὶ μεταβολῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:HSI-02","MC_JP8E_L1_07:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MME-04","text":"Ἡ κοσμολογία παύει νὰ εἶναι ἐξωτερικὴ θεωρία καὶ γίνεται πρακτικὸν μέτρον διαγνώσεως, θεραπείας, παιδείας καὶ πολιτικῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:MME-05","MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MME-05","text":"Ἡ μέθοδος ἀναγκάζεται νὰ ἀκολουθῇ μεταδόσεις, ὁδούς, καθυστερήσεις, ἀναστροφὰς καὶ βαθμίδας, οὐχὶ νὰ ἀρκῆται εἰς ὀνόματα καὶ ἐπιφάνειας.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:MME-05","MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MME-06","text":"Ἡ μορφή, ὁ τόπος, ἡ ἀναπνοὴ καὶ τὸ εἶδος γίγνονται ἀναγνώσιμα ὡς ἀποτυπώματα ψυχικῆς, ἠθικῆς καὶ χρονικῆς ἱστορίας.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:MME-03","MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B01","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ ἐπιφανειακοῦ ἐπιπέδου, τροφαί, πνεύματα, χυμοί, πάθη, φάρμακα, μορφαὶ καὶ εἴδη φαίνονται ὡς διακεκριμένα συμβεβηκότα.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:ALT-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_07:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B02","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ δομικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, τὰ φαινόμενα συγκροτοῦσι δίκτυον ῥοῶν, δεσμῶν, ὀργάνων, μεταδόσεων, διαβαθμίσεων καὶ ἀναδράσεων.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:ALT-B02","MC_JP8E_L1_07:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B03","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ μεταστρατηγικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, ἡ παρέμβασις μετακινείται ἀπὸ μομφὴν, βίαν καὶ ἀντικατάστασιν εἰς παραγωγὴν ὅρων αὐτορρυθμίσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:ALT-B03","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B04","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ ὀντολογικοῦ καὶ χρονικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, τὸ ὂν εἶναι πρόσκαιρος σύστασις, ἣ ὁ χρόνος διατηρεῖ, μεταβάλλει καὶ ἐνδέχεται νὰ λύῃ.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:ALT-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_06:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B05","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ μεθοδολογικοῦ καὶ ἐπιτελεστικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, ὁ λόγος μιμεῖται τὸ ἀντικείμενον, συνάγων διεσπαρμένα μέρη εἰς συνεκτικὴν ἀλλὰ ἀνοικτὴν μηχανήν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:ALT-B05","MC_JP8E_L1_07:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:FL-01","text":"Ἡ τελεολογικὴ διοίκησις ἀπαιτεῖ τὰ φυσικὰ αἴτια ὡς ἐνεργά, ἀλλὰ δὲν ἀποδίδει πλήρως πῶς ἡ ὑπαγωγὴ αὐτῶν διασῴζει τὴν αὐτονομίαν τῆς μηχανικῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:FL-01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:FL-02","text":"Ἡ ἀπόρριψις τῆς καθαρᾶς ἑκουσιότητος ἀποδυναμοῖ τὴν προσωπικὴν ἐνοχήν, ἀλλὰ τὸ σύστημα ἐξακολουθεῖ νὰ ζητῇ παιδευτικὴν κατεύθυνσιν καὶ κρίσιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:FL-01","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:FL-03","text":"Ἡ ἱεραρχία διασῴζει τὴν ἑνότητα διὰ τῆς ἀσυμμετρίας, ἀλλὰ ἡ ἀμφίδρομος μεταβολὴ τῶν μορφῶν ἀπειλεῖ τὴν σταθερὰν ἀξιακὴν διάταξιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:FL-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:FL-04","text":"Ὁ αὐτὸς μηχανισμὸς ῥοῆς δύναται νὰ συντηρῇ ἢ νὰ διαλύῃ, καὶ τὸ ἀκριβὲς ὅριον τῆς μεταπτώσεως μένει ὑποκαθορισμένον.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_06:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MP-01","text":"Ἡ ποιότης μεταλλάσσεται εἰς δύναμιν καὶ λειτουργίαν, ὅταν τὸ ἐρώτημα περὶ τοῦ τί ἐστιν ὑποχωρῇ πρὸ τοῦ τί ποιεῖ ἐν σχέσει.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:MP-01","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MP-02","text":"Ἡ ὑγίεια μεταλλάσσεται εἰς πρόσκαιρον λόγον ῥοῶν, καὶ ἡ νόσος εἰς αὐτοτροφοδοτούμενον κύκλον τῆς ἰδίας μηχανῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-04","MC_JP8E_L1_06:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MP-03","text":"Ἡ ἠθικὴ μεταλλάσσεται εἰς φυσιολογίαν κινήσεων καὶ μορφῶν, χωρὶς ὅμως νὰ παύῃ νὰ διατηρῇ ἀξιακὴν φοράν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MP-04","text":"Ἡ μεσολάβησις μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ τεχνικὴν ἀνάγκην μεταδόσεως εἰς καθολικὸν σχήμα συνδέον θείαν τάξιν, ψυχήν, σῶμα, σημεῖον καὶ ἀλήθειαν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:MP-02","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:HSI-01","text":"Ἡ πραγματικότης εἶναι μηχανὴ διαβαθμισμένων ῥοῶν καὶ μεταγραφῶν, ἐν ᾗ πᾶσα δύναμις λαμβάνει μορφὴν διὰ ἀντιστάσεως, μέτρου, ὀργάνου καὶ δεσμοῦ.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:HSI-01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:HSI-02","text":"Τὸ ὑποκείμενον εἶναι μεταβατικὸς κόμβος, ἅμα θεραπευόμενος καὶ θεραπευτής, οὔτε παθητικὸς καθρέπτης οὔτε αὐτοκράτωρ τῶν κινήσεων αὐτοῦ.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:HSI-02","MC_JP8E_L1_07:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:HSI-03","text":"Ὁ χρόνος εἶναι ρυθμικὴ καὶ γενεαλογικὴ σπείρα, ἐν ᾗ ἡ διατήρησις φέρει ἤδη τὴν δυνατότητα φθορᾶς, ἀλλὰ καὶ τὴν δυνατότητα ἐπανορθώσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:HSI-03","MC_JP8E_L1_07:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:HSI-04","text":"Ἡ δυνατὴ διαφυγὴ εἶναι οὐκ ἔξοδος ἐκ σώματος ἢ κόσμου, ἀλλὰ μεταβολὴ ἐχθρᾶς κρατήσεως εἰς φιλικὴν σύνταξιν τῶν δυνάμεων.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:HSI-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B01","text":"Αἱ ἀρχαὶ τῆς σχεσιακῆς γενέσεως, τῆς σωματικῆς μεταγραφῆς, τῆς ρυθμισμένης ἀνταποδόσεως, τῆς ἀναστροφῆς καὶ τῆς θεραπευτικῆς γνώσεως ἀρκοῦσι πρὸς ἀνακατασκευὴν τῆς κεντρικῆς μηχανῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:MRT-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_07:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B02","text":"Ἡ ἀνακατασκευή διασῴζει τὴν ἀληθῆ κίνησιν μόνον ἐὰν διατηρῇ τὴν ἀναδρομὴν, καθ᾽ ἣν οἱ αὐτοὶ μηχανισμοὶ δύνανται νὰ γίγνωνται ζωτικοὶ ἢ νοσοποιοί.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:MRT-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_06:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B03","text":"Ὑποκαθορισμένα μένουσι τὸ ἀκριβὲς μέτρον τῆς ὀρθῆς κινήσεως, ὁ τρόπος μεταβάσεως ἀπὸ φυσικὴν κίνησιν εἰς ποιοτικὴν ἐμπειρίαν, καὶ τὸ ὅριον τῆς ἐξωτερικῆς ἐπεμβάσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:MRT-B03","MC_JP8E_L1_07:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B04","text":"Ἡ ἀρχιτεκτονικὴ εἶναι συνεκτικωτάτη ὅπου ἡ ἀβεβαιότης, ἡ ἀτέλεια, ἡ ἀναλογία καὶ ἡ ἐνσώματη μεσολάβησις νοοῦνται ὡς παραγωγικοὶ ὅροι.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:MRT-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B05","text":"Ἀνθεκτικώτατον εἰς ἀξιωματικὴν συμπίεσιν μένει τὸ ὅτι ἡ τάξις ζῇ ἐκ τῶν ἐντάσεων ἀνάγκης, αὐτονομίας, παιδείας, ἱεραρχίας καὶ θνητῆς φθορᾶς, οὐχὶ παρὰ τὰς ἐντάσεις ταύτας.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:MRT-B05","MC_JP8E_L1_07:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261014T233637861436Z_0d68e062","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Jp8E L2 02","source_sha256":"0b409c5e4a142f16fc0191272bf9c6e6916262faf8d87cb076ccc23b2dbd8088","sha256":"b35b714bd43faa41109596a64b79466daf8ec7516d766804de134541c866c5b6","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-14T23:36:37.862394+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B01","text":"The problem of the system is how the living being, the body, and order are to be understood as arising from relations and transformations, not from immobile essences.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B02","text":"The body becomes the universal mechanism in which motion, flow, affection, instrument, sign, and form are mutually transcribed.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B03","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B03","text":"Life and decay proceed from the same network, but differ according to measure, outlet, proportion, and direction of feedback.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B04","text":"Hierarchy does not abolish the parts, but distributes differences into a functional composition through mediations.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B02","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B05","text":"Knowledge and therapy coincide as a reordering of motions, because true judgment ought to transform the knower.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B05","MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B06","text":"Imperfection and mortality are not merely privations, but conditions of replenishment, compensation, education, and new arrangement.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B06","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B07","text":"Time becomes spiral causality, whereby what is prior is inscribed in the present and the future prearranges the present form.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-11","MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B08","text":"The conclusion is an open order, which persists only if it corrects its own flows without claiming self-sufficiency or exit from the whole.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B08","MC_JP8E_L1_07:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Being is produced from relations, motions, and transformations, not from self-complete quality or an immutable core.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] The body is a distributed node of translation, in which matter, affection, instrument, flow, and form become functions of one another.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_06:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Motion is transformed from a natural affection into a universal criterion of health, knowledge, virtue, form, and cosmic kinship.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Unity is hierarchized mediation of differences, and not homogeneity or the purging of lower things.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Logos, flow, mediation, and outlet constitute the common measure that distinguishes preservation from reversal.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_06:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Disease, folly, and vice are homologous modes whereby the same generative mechanism reverses into self-destruction.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Knowledge is therapeutic only when it re-enters the body and soul as regulation of their circuits.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Time is not an external measure, but a cause of return, weakening, genealogical accumulation, and provident prearrangement.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B07","MC_JP8E_L1_07:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Teleology does not abolish necessity, but governs resistance, desire, and material insufficiency as instruments of generation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Freedom and autonomy are understood as cooperative self-regulation within the whole, not as independence from body, time, and world.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_06:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-01","text":"Relation is maintained as a generative principle, but shifts from the sensible production of phenomenon to physiology, pathology, education, and cosmic order.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:CGD-02","MC_JP8E_L1_07:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-02","text":"The body mutates from a site of transmission into a network of flows, and thence into a visible inscription of psychic and ethical history.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-03","text":"Motion passes from mechanical cause into a measure of diagnosis, therapy, education, and assimilation to the cosmos.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-07","MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-04","text":"Symmetry ceases to signify static equality and becomes a dynamic rule of reciprocity, therapy, and cosmic gradation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L1_07:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-05","text":"Necessity changes from material pressure into the service of order, but returns as decay when regulation fails.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_06:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-06","text":"Disease shifts from local anomaly into a feedback cycle in which the effect returns and strengthens its cause.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L1_06:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-07","text":"Truth changes from discernment of signs into an active transformation of the knower and of the conditions of action.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-08","text":"Desire ceases to be a partial affection and becomes the mode whereby order produces generation through tension and resistance.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-11","MC_JP8E_L1_07:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:CGD-01","text":"Local theories of humors, elements, organs, medicines, and particular affections remain serviceable examples, unless they are elevated into types of distributed causality.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:CGD-01","MC_JP8E_L1_07:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:CGD-02","text":"Flow, assimilation, motion, and mediation are scaled into universal schemas because they connect sensation, life, disease, character, and cosmos.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:CGD-02","MC_JP8E_L1_07:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:CGD-03","text":"The bridge between units becomes the principle that health, disease, practical wisdom, and vice are differences in the direction of the same motions.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:CGD-03","MC_JP8E_L1_07:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:CGD-04","text":"Diagnosis becomes a catalyst of transition when it ceases merely to name the symptom and reconstructs paths, places, bonds, and reversals.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSD-04","MC_JP8E_L1_06:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:CGD-05","text":"Ethical judgment is upgraded only when it is read as a genealogy of bodies, mixtures, educations, and environments.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:CGD-06","text":"Teleology ceases to be a local addition when it jointly explains the organization of necessity, therapy, and the open preservation of the whole.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:CGD-03","MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-01","text":"Self-sufficient substance is excluded, because phenomenon, identity, and function are produced through relation and transformation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GNX-01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-02","text":"The body as an inert covering or an aggregate of independent parts is excluded.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GNX-01","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-03","text":"Linear and one-way causality is excluded, because effects can return as causes of new order or decay.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GNX-02","MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-04","text":"Health as immobility and disease as a purely external accident are excluded.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GNX-03","MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-05","text":"The soul as an uninvolved governor and knowledge as information without transformation of life are excluded.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-01","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-06","text":"Perfection as escape from body, desire, and lower parts is excluded, because completeness consists in regulating multiplicity.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:DBN-01","text":"The hypothesis that function, knowledge, or character can be understood without material, anatomical, and spatial arrangement must be false.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:DBN-01","MC_JP8E_L1_07:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:DBN-02","text":"The hypothesis that soul, body, education, polity, and cosmos have independent causes must be false.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:DBN-01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:DBN-03","text":"The hypothesis that decay is otherness with respect to generation must be false, because dissolution is produced from the reversal of generation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:DBN-03","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:DBN-04","text":"The hypothesis that therapy or truth is imposed from without apart from time, measure, and internal transformation must be false.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:DBN-02","MC_JP8E_L1_05:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:DBN-05","text":"The hypothesis that hierarchy is preserved through the destruction of lower things must be false, because wholeness requires their functional interweaving.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_07:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSA-01","text":"A world in which truth or sovereignty is given as pure presence without body, signs, and transcriptions becomes impossible.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSA-02","text":"An unmoved center or unrelated subject that governs without being changed by the network becomes impossible.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSA-03","text":"Life without circulation, reciprocity, limitation, and mediation becomes impossible.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSA-04","text":"A therapy that saves one part without restoring its relation to the whole becomes impossible.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_07:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSA-05","text":"Purely linear time becomes impossible, because disposition, form, and decay return as causes of what comes later.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-01","text":"Relational generation, bodily circulation, and psychoethical transformation are three scales of one mechanism, in which relation produces form through difference and transmission.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-02","text":"Hierarchy functions as an art of channeling and not as naked sovereignty, because the integrity of the whole requires unequal parts rendering to one another.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-10","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_06:RMD-06","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-03","text":"Pathology and ethical deviation become a common problem of reversal of rhythms, so that diagnosis and education seek the same genealogy of motions.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_07:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-04","text":"Interpretation, diagnosis, and therapy are homologous acts, because each reconstructs from signs and affections the invisible order of relations.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B05","MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-05","text":"The present body becomes a site of double recurrence, because earlier motions are inscribed in it, while future needs direct its present construction.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-11","MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-06","text":"External intervention is justified only if it restores internal self-regulation and not if it replaces the one being treated.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-10","MC_JP8E_L1_07:XD-02","MC_JP8E_L1_07:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-01","text":"Reality is reconstructed as a multilevel network of transformations, in which ontology, physiology, ethics, and cosmology are phases of the same relational production.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSD-01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-02","text":"Health, practical wisdom, and virtue do not designate essences, but successful proportions of motions that preserve unity without closure.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-03","text":"Politics and education become the management of the conditions under which self-regulation can arise, and not the external imposition of will.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-10","MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSD-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-04","text":"Time becomes the causal memory of the body, because old age, disposition, and form are returns of older logoi of motion.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-07","MC_JP8E_L1_07:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-05","text":"Freedom becomes an art of navigating and rearranging flows, bonds, and desires, not release from the conditions of embodied order.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-10","MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSD-05","MC_JP8E_L1_07:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RLE-01","text":"Relation → difference → instrument → sign → interpretation → new composition; explanation acts back upon what is explained.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:RLE-01","MC_JP8E_L1_05:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RLE-02","text":"Decay → replenishment → assimilation → regulated flow → new decay; preservation contains the cause of its own weakening.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:RLE-01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RLE-03","text":"Reversal of flow → disease or folly → diagnosis of genealogy → reordering of motions → possible self-regulation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:RLE-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RLE-04","text":"Desire → resistance → generation or disease → bodily form → new horizon of desire and transformation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:RLE-05","MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RLE-05","text":"The attractor of the system is regulated reciprocity, which returns as symmetry, health, practical wisdom, therapy, and assimilation to the cosmos.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:RLE-05","MC_JP8E_L1_07:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:RLE-06","text":"Closure is the negative attractor, whereby flow loses its outlet, the effect re-enters the cause, and preservation becomes dissolution.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-08","MC_JP8E_L1_06:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:FGS-B01","text":"Necessity or power impinges upon matter, and matter responds through motion, mixture, flow, and difference.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:FGS-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:FGS-B02","text":"The response is distributed into organs, networks, bonds, and paths, so that affection becomes sensation, form, signal, or limitation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:FGS-B02","MC_JP8E_L1_06:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:FGS-B03","text":"Differentiated functions are subordinated to hierarchy and symmetry, which transform multiplicity into preservable unity.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:FGS-B03","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:FGS-B04","text":"Mediation generates signs, and signs require judgment, diagnosis, and education for the restoration of rational order.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:FGS-B05","MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:FGS-B05","text":"Judgment re-enters the judge, and correct knowledge becomes therapeutic transformation of soul, body, and mode of dwelling.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:FGS-B06","text":"If regulation becomes closure, then replenishment turns into decay, and the mechanism dissolves the bonds that it formerly maintained.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:FGS-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AFM-B01","text":"Relational generation → bodily motion and affection → phenomenon as functional difference.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:AFM-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AFM-B02","text":"Flow and assimilation → temporary bodily identity → health as regulated reciprocity.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:AFM-B02","MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AFM-B03","text":"Obstruction or asymmetry → reversal of generation → disease, folly, and possible systemic dissolution.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:AFM-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AFM-B04","text":"Sign and symptom → genealogical reading of paths → diagnosis of the depth of disturbance.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:MME-04","MC_JP8E_L1_06:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AFM-B05","text":"Diagnosis of interdependence → gentle treatment and restoration of rhythms → self-regulation and therapeutic knowledge.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:FGS-B03","MC_JP8E_L1_07:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AFM-B06","text":"Restoration of the knower → cosmic theory → assimilation to the graded order of the whole.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:AFM-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:AFM-B07","text":"Assimilation → continuous corrections and new transformations → open coherence or, through closure, new spiral decay.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B08","MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MME-01","text":"The distinction between nature and ethics is dissolved, because bodily regulation already bears judgment concerning measure, order, and decay.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:MME-02","MC_JP8E_L1_07:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MME-02","text":"The distinction between health and disease as a difference of essence is dissolved and replaced by a difference of direction, place, measure, and feedback.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSD-05","MC_JP8E_L1_06:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MME-03","text":"The subject is reclassified from a self-sufficient master into a node of passage, inscription, therapy, and transformation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:HSI-02","MC_JP8E_L1_07:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MME-04","text":"Cosmology ceases to be an external theory and becomes a practical measure of diagnosis, therapy, education, and politics.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:MME-05","MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MME-05","text":"Method is compelled to follow transmissions, paths, delays, reversals, and degrees, rather than resting content with names and surfaces.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:MME-05","MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MME-06","text":"Form, place, breath, and species become readable as imprints of psychic, ethical, and temporal history.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:MME-03","MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, foods, spirits, humors, affections, medicines, forms, and species appear as distinct accidents.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:ALT-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_07:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, phenomena constitute a network of flows, bonds, organs, transmissions, gradations, and feedbacks.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:ALT-B02","MC_JP8E_L1_07:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, intervention shifts from blame, violence, and replacement to the production of conditions for self-regulation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:ALT-B03","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, being is a temporary composition, which time preserves, transforms, and may dissolve.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:ALT-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_06:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, logos imitates the object, gathering dispersed parts into a coherent yet open mechanism.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:ALT-B05","MC_JP8E_L1_07:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:FL-01","text":"Teleological governance requires natural causes as active, but does not fully account for how their subordination preserves the autonomy of mechanics.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:FL-01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:FL-02","text":"The rejection of pure voluntariness weakens personal culpability, but the system continues to demand educational direction and judgment.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:FL-01","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:FL-03","text":"Hierarchy preserves unity through asymmetry, but the bidirectional transformation of forms threatens a stable axiological arrangement.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:FL-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:FL-04","text":"The same mechanism of flow can preserve or dissolve, and the exact threshold of transition remains underdetermined.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_06:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MP-01","text":"Quality mutates into power and function when the question of what something is yields to what it does in relation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:MP-01","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MP-02","text":"Health mutates into a temporary logos of flows, and disease into a self-feeding cycle of the same mechanism.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-04","MC_JP8E_L1_06:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MP-03","text":"Ethics mutates into a physiology of motions and forms, yet does not cease to retain an axiological direction.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MP-04","text":"Mediation mutates from a technical necessity of transmission into a universal schema linking divine order, soul, body, sign, and truth.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:MP-02","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:HSI-01","text":"Reality is a mechanism of graded flows and transcriptions, in which every power receives form through resistance, measure, instrument, and bond.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:HSI-01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a transitional node, at once patient and healer, neither a passive mirror nor the autocrat of its motions.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:HSI-02","MC_JP8E_L1_07:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:HSI-03","text":"Time is a rhythmic and genealogical spiral, in which preservation already bears the possibility of decay, but also the possibility of restoration.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:HSI-03","MC_JP8E_L1_07:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:HSI-04","text":"Possible escape is not exit from body or cosmos, but transformation of hostile domination into a friendly ordering of powers.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:HSI-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B01","text":"The principles of relational generation, bodily transcription, regulated reciprocity, reversal, and therapeutic knowledge suffice for reconstruction of the central mechanism.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:MRT-B01","MC_JP8E_L1_07:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement only if it retains the recurrence whereby the same mechanisms can become vital or disease-producing.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:MRT-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_06:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B03","text":"The exact measure of correct motion, the manner of transition from natural motion to qualitative experience, and the limit of external intervention remain underdetermined.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:MRT-B03","MC_JP8E_L1_07:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is most coherent where uncertainty, imperfection, proportion, and embodied mediation are understood as productive conditions.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:MRT-B04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B05","text":"Most resistant to axiomatic compression remains the claim that order lives from the tensions of necessity, autonomy, education, hierarchy, and mortal decay, not despite these tensions.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:MRT-B05","MC_JP8E_L1_07:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_JP8E_L2_02@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_JP8E_L2_02:v1","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Jp8E L2 02"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Timaeus","title":"Jp8E L2 02","context":"Chapters 49–76 · Section 65 · Section 66 · Section 67 · Section 68 · Section 69 · Section 70 · Section 71 · Section 72 · Section 73 · Section 74 · Section 75 · Section 76 · Section 77 · Section 78 · Section 79 · Section 80 · Section 81 · Section 82 · Section 83 · Section 84 · Section 85 · Section 86 · Section 87 · Section 88 · Section 89 · Section 90 · Section 91 · Section 92","original_title":"Jp8E L2 02","original_context":"Chapters 49–76 · Section 65 · Section 66 · Section 67 · Section 68 · Section 69 · Section 70 · Section 71 · Section 72 · Section 73 · Section 74 · Section 75 · Section 76 · Section 77 · Section 78 · Section 79 · Section 80 · Section 81 · Section 82 · Section 83 · Section 84 · Section 85 · Section 86 · Section 87 · Section 88 · Section 89 · Section 90 · Section 91 · Section 92"},"original_title":"Jp8E L2 02","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03","layer":"MC","title":"Jp8E L1 03","order":3,"parents":["CL_JP8E_09","CL_JP8E_10","CL_JP8E_11","CL_JP8E_12"],"revision":"MC_JP8E_L1_03@R001","sha256":"a0e97ee6f4392f885d12b5aa3f3a2863e66c89b544dc96544cb6659cc5296521","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AS-B01","text":"Τὸ πρόβλημα τοῦ συστήματος ἐστὶν ὅπως ἡ τάξις ἐνδέχηται τὴν θνητὴν γένεσιν, τὴν σωματικὴν διατάραξιν, τὴν ἀνάγκην καὶ τὴν ἀσταθῆ φανέρωσιν χωρὶς ἀφανισμὸν τῆς ἀρχῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AS-B02","text":"Ἡ δημιουργία παύει εἶναι ἁπλῆ ἐπιβολὴ μορφῆς, καὶ γίνεται κατανεμημένη μετάδοσις αἰτιότητος διὰ σωμάτων, αἰσθήσεων, ψυχῶν καὶ δεκτικῶν πεδίων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:AS-B03","CL_JP8E_10:AFM-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AS-B03","text":"Τὸ σῶμα μετασχηματίζεται ἀπὸ κατώτερον περίβλημα εἰς μεσίτην, ὃς μεταδίδωσι κινήσεις, παράγει πάθη, καὶ ἐπιβάλλει τὴν ἀνάγκην κρίσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:AS-B04","CL_JP8E_10:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AS-B04","text":"Ἡ αἴσθησις ἀναβαίνει ἀπὸ σωματικὴν προσβολὴν εἰς ὁδὸν κοσμικῆς μετρήσεως, ἀλλὰ ταὐτὸν ἐπιμένει πηγὴ πλάνης διὰ τὴν σχετικὴν φύσιν τῆς μεταδόσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:SP-01","CL_JP8E_10:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AS-B05","text":"Ἡ τάξις δὲν δηλοῖ ἀπουσίαν ἀνάγκης, ἀλλὰ πειθὼν καὶ ἀνάταξιν ἐπὶ πεδίου ἀντιστεκομένων δυνάμεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:GA-06","CL_JP8E_11:SP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AS-B06","text":"Ἡ ἀναζήτησις τῆς τάξεως ἀναγκάζει τὸν λόγον νὰ εἰσάγῃ τὴν χώραν ὡς ἄμορφον ὅρον δυνατότητος, ὃς δέχεται μορφὰς χωρὶς αὐτὸς εἶναι μία μορφή.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:AS-03","CL_JP8E_12:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AS-B07","text":"Ὁ λόγος μεταβαίνει ἀπὸ ἀριθμητὴν καὶ τελεολογικὴν σύλληψιν εἰς εἰκότα καὶ ἀποφατικὸν τρόπον, ἐπειδὴ τὸ γιγνόμενον καὶ ἡ χώρα ἀντιστέκονται εἰς πλήρη καθήλωσιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:GA-10","CL_JP8E_12:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AS-B08","text":"Ἡ κίνησις τοῦ ὅλου εἶναι σπειροειδής: τάξις μεταδίδεται, διαταράσσεται, μετρεῖται, διορθοῦται, καὶ τέλος ἐπιστρέφει εἰς ἀποφατικὴν ἀναγνώρισιν τοῦ θεμελίου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:AS-B08","CL_JP8E_12:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Πᾶσα γένεσις ἀπαιτεῖ αἰτιακὴν διανομὴν καὶ δεκτικὸν πεδίον· οὔτε αὐτογένεσις οὔτε ἄμεσος παραγωγὴ ἐξαντλοῦσι τὴν πραγματικότητα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:GA-02","CL_JP8E_12:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Τὸ σῶμα καὶ ἡ αἴσθησις εἰσὶν ἐνεργοὶ μεσίται μεταξὺ κοσμικῶν κινήσεων καὶ ψυχικῆς τάξεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:GA-05","CL_JP8E_10:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Ἡ θνητότης εἶναι ἅμα ὅρος κοσμικῆς πληρότητος καὶ τόπος φθορᾶς, ὥστε τὸ ἀναγκαῖον μὴ ταυτίζεσθαι πρὸς τὸ ἀκίνδυνον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:GA-03","CL_JP8E_09:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Ἡ τάξις εἶναι ῥύθμισις καὶ πειθὼ ἐπὶ ἀντιστεκομένης ἀνάγκης, οὐχὶ ἄκαμπτος κατάστασις προϋπάρχουσα τῆς κινήσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:GA-06","CL_JP8E_11:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Ἡ ἀλήθεια τῶν φαινομένων ἀπαιτεῖ διάκρισιν αἰτίων, μεσιτειῶν, προτύπων καὶ δεκτικῶν, οὐχὶ ἁπλῆν προσκόλλησιν εἰς τὴν αἴσθησιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:GA-08","CL_JP8E_12:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Ἡ αἴσθησις εἶναι διττὴ δύναμις: μέτρον κοσμικῆς παιδείας καὶ ἀγωγὸς διαταράξεως καὶ πλάνης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:GA-01","CL_JP8E_10:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Ἡ πραγματικότης συγκροτεῖται τριμερῶς ἐκ ἀμεταβλήτου προτύπου, δεκτικῆς χώρας καὶ φθαρτοῦ γιγνομένου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:GA-06","CL_JP8E_12:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] Ἡ ἀνάταξις τῶν ψυχικῶν κινήσεων κατὰ κοσμικὸν μέτρον ἐλκύει τὴν φυσιολογίαν, τὴν ἠθικὴν, τὴν παιδείαν καὶ τὴν κοσμολογίαν πρὸς μίαν λειτουργικὴν σύγκλεισιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:GA-10","CL_JP8E_11:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Ὁ περὶ γενέσεως λόγος οὐ δύναται νὰ γίνῃ ἀπόλυτος ἀπόδειξις, ἀλλὰ δεῖ νὰ ἀκολουθῇ τὴν μεταβολὴν διὰ εἰκότος καὶ ἀποφατικοῦ λόγου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:GA-10","CL_JP8E_12:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Ἡ ἀπουσία ἰδίου εἴδους τῆς χώρας καθίσταται ὁ κρυφὸς ὅρος, δι᾽ οὗ πολλαὶ μορφαὶ δύνανται νὰ φαίνωνται χωρὶς τὸ θεμέλιον νὰ ταυτίζηται πρὸς αὐτάς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:GA-10","CL_JP8E_12:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:RMD-01","text":"Ἡ ἱεραρχία ἐκ τῆς δημιουργικῆς διανομῆς μεταναστεύει εἰς τὴν σχέσιν κεφαλῆς καὶ σώματος, πρώτων καὶ δευτέρων αἰτίων, καὶ τέλος προτύπου, χώρας καὶ γιγνομένου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:GA-02","CL_JP8E_10:RMD-01","CL_JP8E_12:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:RMD-02","text":"Ἡ αἴσθησις διατηρεῖ τὴν λειτουργίαν τῆς μεταδόσεως, ἀλλὰ μεταβάλλεται ἀπὸ σωματικὴν διάσεισιν εἰς ὄργανον ἀριθμοῦ, χρόνου καὶ φιλοσοφικῆς διορθώσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:RMD-06","CL_JP8E_11:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:RMD-03","text":"Ἡ ἀνάγκη μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ σωματικὴν καὶ μηχανικὴν συναίτιον εἰς ἀντιστεκόμενον πεδίον, ὃ ὁ νοῦς οὐ καταλύει ἀλλὰ πείθει.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:MP-03","CL_JP8E_11:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:RMD-04","text":"Ἡ τάξις ἀντιστρέφει τὴν σημασίαν της: ἀπὸ προϋποτεθειμένη ἀρμονία γίνεται ἐπίτευγμα ῥυθμίσεως ἐντός ἀσταθοῦς καὶ παθητικοῦ πεδίου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:MP-01","CL_JP8E_12:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:RMD-05","text":"Ἡ ὑποδοχὴ ἀπορροφᾷ τὴν ἁπλῆν ἔννοιαν ὕλης καὶ τόπου, γιγνομένη ὀντολογικὸς ὅρος φανερώσεως καὶ δυναμικὸν πεδίον ἀντιδράσεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:RMD-05","CL_JP8E_12:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:RMD-06","text":"Ὁ λόγος διατηρεῖ τὴν ἀξίωσιν διακρίσεως, ἀλλὰ ἀποβάλλει τὴν ἀξίωσιν ἀκινητοποιήσεως τοῦ γιγνομένου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:RMD-07","CL_JP8E_11:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:CGD-01","text":"Αἱ συγκεκριμέναι στοιχειακαὶ, ἀνατομικαὶ καὶ ἀστρονομικαὶ περιγραφαὶ μένουσι τοπικαὶ τεχνικαί, ἐνῶ ἡ μεσιτεία κινήσεως διὰ σώματος καὶ αἰσθήσεως κλιμακοῦται εἰς καθολικὴν ἀρχήν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:CGD-02","CL_JP8E_10:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:CGD-02","text":"Ἡ ἠθικὴ τοῦ λόγου καὶ τοῦ ἀλόγου παύει εἶναι μόνον κρίσις χαρακτῆρος καὶ γίνεται μηχανισμὸς ἀνατάξεως ἐνὸς κοσμικῶς ἐνσωματωμένου ὑποκειμένου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:GA-08","CL_JP8E_11:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:CGD-03","text":"Ἡ διάκρισις πρώτων καὶ δευτέρων αἰτίων γεφυροῖ τὴν φυσιολογίαν πρὸς τὴν κοσμολογίαν, ἀλλὰ ἡ τριμερὴς διάταξις ὑπερβαίνει τὴν διπλῆν αἰτιολογίαν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:CGD-06","CL_JP8E_11:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:CGD-04","text":"Ἡ θεραπεία διὰ μουσικῆς καὶ ἡ παρατήρησις τῶν ἄστρων μένουσιν τοπικαὶ πρακτικαί, ἀλλὰ συναθροίζονται ὡς τρόποι ρυθμιστικῆς μιμήσεως τῆς κοσμικῆς περιοδικότητος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:CGD-02","CL_JP8E_11:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:CGD-05","text":"Ἡ χώρα καταργεῖ τὴν τοπικὴν ὑπόθεσιν τῆς ὕλης ὡς αὐτοτελοῦς στοιχείου καὶ γίνεται ὁ κοινὸς ὅρος, ὃς συνδέει γένεσιν, φαινόμενον καὶ γνωστικὸν ὅριον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:CGD-03","CL_JP8E_12:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GNX-01","text":"Ἀποκλείεται αὐτόνομος γένεσις ἢ ὕλη αὐτομορφοποιός, διότι τὸ γιγνόμενον ἀπαιτεῖ αἰτίαν, δεκτικότητα καὶ σχέσιν πρὸς πρότυπον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:GNX-01","CL_JP8E_12:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GNX-02","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ ἐκδοχὴ τοῦ σώματος καὶ τῆς αἰσθήσεως ὡς παθητικῶν δοχείων ἄνευ μετασχηματιστικῆς ἐνεργείας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:GNX-03","CL_JP8E_10:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GNX-03","text":"Ἀποκλείεται κόσμος ἐν ᾧ ἡ τάξις ὑφίσταται ἄνευ ἀνάγκης, ἀντιστάσεως, φθορᾶς ἢ δεκτικοῦ πεδίου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_09:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GNX-04","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ ταύτισις φαινομένου, ὀνόματος καὶ σταθερᾶς οὐσίας, διότι τὰ φαινόμενα εἰσὶ μεταβαλλόμενα μιμήματα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:GNX-04","CL_JP8E_12:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GNX-05","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ ἀπόλυτος βεβαιότης περὶ τοῦ γιγνομένου, ἐπειδὴ ἡ ἀστάθεια τοῦ ἀντικειμένου μεταβάλλει τὸν πρέποντα τρόπον λόγου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:DBN-04","CL_JP8E_11:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:DBN-01","text":"Δεῖ ψεῦδος εἶναι ὅτι ἡ αἰτιακὴ ἀνάγκη καὶ ὁ νοῦς ἀλλήλοις πολέμιοι εἰσίν, διότι τὸ σύστημα χρῆται τῇ ἀνάγκῃ ὡς ὑποτεταγμένῳ ἀλλ᾽ ἐνεργῷ συναἰτίῳ.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:DBN-03","CL_JP8E_10:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:DBN-02","text":"Δεῖ ψεῦδος εἶναι ὅτι ἡ ψυχὴ ἢ ὁ ἄνθρωπος δύναται νὰ εἶναι αὐτάρκης, ἐπεὶ ἡ ψυχὴ διαμορφοῦται ἐν σώματι, χρόνῳ καὶ κοσμικῇ διανομῇ.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:BSA-04","CL_JP8E_10:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:DBN-03","text":"Δεῖ ψεῦδος εἶναι ὅτι τὸ φαινόμενον δύναται νὰ ἔχῃ αὐτοτελῆν οὐσίαν, ἐπεὶ ἡ φανέρωσις ἐξαρτᾶται ἀπὸ χώραν καὶ πρότυπον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_12:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:DBN-04","text":"Δεῖ ψεῦδος εἶναι ὅτι ἡ χώρα δύναται θετικῶς ὁρισθῆναι ὡς μία οὐσία παρὰ ταῖς ἄλλαις, ἐπεὶ τὸ ἴδιον εἶδος ἀναιρεῖ τὴν καθολικὴν δεκτικότητα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:DBN-02","CL_JP8E_12:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSA-01","text":"Ἡ βαθύτατη ἀπουσία εἶναι ἡ ἀδύνατος ἀμεσότης μεταξὺ νοῦ, κόσμου καὶ ὄντος· πᾶσα πρόσβασις διέρχεται διὰ μεσιτειῶν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:BSA-01","CL_JP8E_09:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSA-02","text":"Ἡ χώρα εἶναι τὸ ἀναγκαῖον ἀλλὰ ἀνέκφραστον θεμέλιον, ὃ οὔτε ἐμφανίζεται καθ᾽ αὑτὸ οὔτε δύναται ἀποβληθῆναι ἄνευ καταρρεύσεως τῆς γενέσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:BSA-05","CL_JP8E_12:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSA-03","text":"Ἀδύνατος μένει ἡ τελεία συμπτωσις ἀληθείας, ὀνόματος καὶ φαινομένου, διότι ἡ γένεσις διατηρεῖ ἀπόστασιν μεταξὺ εἴδους καὶ εἰκόνος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:BSA-03","CL_JP8E_11:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:XD-01","text":"Ἡ διανεμημένη δημιουργία καὶ ἡ τριμερὴς ὀντολογία συνεπάγονται ὅτι ἡ αἰτιότης δὲν ῥεῖ ἐκ κορυφῆς μόνον, ἀλλὰ πραγματοῦται διὰ τῆς ἀντιδράσεως προτύπου, σώματος, χώρας καὶ γιγνομένου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:AS-B03","CL_JP8E_12:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:XD-02","text":"Ἡ σωματικὴ μετάδοσις καὶ ἡ σχετικὴ φύσις τοῦ φαινομένου καθιστῶσι τὴν πλάνην ὄχι γυμνὴν ἔλλειψιν γνώσεως, ἀλλὰ παραγόμενον ἀποτέλεσμα μεσιτείας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:AS-B05","CL_JP8E_10:XD-03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:XD-03","text":"Ἡ ἐπανόρθωσις τῆς ψυχῆς καὶ ἡ πειθὼ τοῦ νοῦ πρὸς τὴν ἀνάγκην ἀποτελοῦσιν ἕνα σχῆμα: ἡ τάξις ἐπανέρχεται δι᾽ ἀνατάξεως καὶ οὐ δι᾽ ἐξαλείψεως τῆς ἀντιστάσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:XD-05","CL_JP8E_11:XD-02","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:XD-04","text":"Ἡ ἀμορφία τῆς χώρας καὶ ἡ ἀκρίβεια τῆς ἐκτυπώσεως ἀποδεικνύουσιν ὅτι ἡ στέρησις ἰδίου χαρακτῆρος δύναται νὰ εἶναι θετικὸς ὅρος δεκτικότητος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:RMD-02","CL_JP8E_12:XD-01","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:XD-05","text":"Ἡ μαθηματικὴ περιοδικότης καὶ ὁ εἰκὼς λόγος διακρίνουσι δύο ἐπιστημονικὰ μέτρα: ἀκρίβειαν περὶ σταθερῶν κινήσεων καὶ προσήκουσαν πιθανότητα περὶ γενέσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:GA-02","CL_JP8E_11:XD-05","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSD-01","text":"Ἡ πραγματικότης γίνεται πολυεπίπεδον κύκλωμα, ἐν ᾧ κάθε μετάδοσις τάξεως δύναται συγχρόνως νὰ παράγῃ νέαν ἀστάθειαν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:BSD-01","CL_JP8E_10:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSD-02","text":"Ὁ ὑποκείμενος γίνεται κόμβος ῥοῶν καὶ οὐχὶ αὐτάρκης κέντρον, διότι ἐν αὐτῷ συμπίπτουσι κοσμικὸς νόμος, σωματικὸν πάθος, αἴσθησις καὶ κρίσις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:BSD-02","CL_JP8E_10:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSD-03","text":"Ὁ χρόνος γίνεται ἀναδραστικὴ ἀκολουθία, ἐν ᾗ ἡ διαταραχὴ παράγει κρίσιν, ἡ κρίσις μορφοποιεῖ τὸν φορέα, καὶ ἡ νέα μορφὴ ἐπανεκκινεί τὸν κύκλον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:BSD-03","CL_JP8E_11:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSD-04","text":"Ἡ ἐπιστήμη γίνεται διάγνωσις συνθηκῶν δυνατότητος καὶ ὁρίων λόγου, οὐχὶ κατοχὴ ἀκινήτων ὀνομάτων ἢ αὐτοτελῶν φαινομένων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:BSD-04","CL_JP8E_12:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSD-05","text":"Ἡ σωτηρία ἢ ἐλευθερία ὁρίζεται ὡς ἐπανίδρυσις ἡγεμονίας ἐντὸς ὅρων ἀνάγκης καὶ ἐνσωματώσεως, οὐχὶ ὡς ἀπόδρασις ἐκ τοῦ συστήματος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:BSD-05","CL_JP8E_10:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:RLE-01","text":"Δημιουργικὴ αἰτία → σωματικὴ μεσιτεία → αἴσθησις → ψυχικὴ κρίσις → ἀνάταξις ἢ διατάραξις → νέα ἐνσώματος ἔκθεσις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:RLE-02","text":"Ὄψις → περιοδικότης → ἀριθμὸς → χρόνος → φιλοσοφικὴ μίμησις → διόρθωσις ψυχικῶν περιφορῶν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:RLE-03","text":"Νοῦς → πειθὼ → ἀνάγκη → μεικτὴ γένεσις → ἀνάγκη χώρας → ἀποφατικὴ ἀβεβαιότης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:RLE-04","text":"Ἀστάθεια φαινομένων → ἀναζήτησις ὑποκειμένου → ἀφαίρεσις μορφῆς → χώρα → νέα ἐξήγησις τῆς ἀστάθειας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:RLE-05","text":"Ἡ ἱεραρχικὴ ἀνάταξις εἶναι ὁ πρακτικὸς ἑλκτικὸς πόλος, ἡ δὲ χώρα ὁ ὀντολογικὸς ἑλκτικὸς πόλος τοῦ ὅλου μηχανήματος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:RLE-05","CL_JP8E_12:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:FGS-B01","text":"Δημιουργικὴ τάξις → κατανεμημένη αἰτιότης → θνητὴ καὶ σωματικὴ σύνθεσις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:FGS-B02","text":"Σωματικὴ σύνθεσις → μετάδοσις κινήσεων → αἴσθησις → πάθος καὶ σχετικὸν φαινόμενον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:FGS-B03","CL_JP8E_10:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:FGS-B03","text":"Σχετικὸν φαινόμενον → παρατήρησις περιοδικότητος → ἀριθμὸς καὶ χρόνος → παιδευτικὴ μίμησις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:FGS-B01","CL_JP8E_11:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:FGS-B04","text":"Παιδευτικὴ τάξις → ἐρώτησις περὶ γένεσεως → νοῦς καὶ ἀνάγκη → ἀναγνώρισις ἀντιστάσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:FGS-B05","text":"Ἀντίστασις καὶ μεταβολή → ἀνεπάρκεια δυαδισμοῦ → χώρα ἄνευ ἰδίου εἴδους → τριμερὴς ὀντολογία.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:FGS-B04","CL_JP8E_12:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:FGS-B06","text":"Τριμερὴς ὀντολογία → ἀποφατικὴ γνώσις τοῦ θεμελίου → εἰκὼς λόγος → διαρκὴς ἀνακύκλησις τῆς διακρίσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:FGS-B07","CL_JP8E_11:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AFM-B01","text":"Δημιουργικὴ διανομή → θνητὴ πληρότης → σωματικὴ μεσιτεία.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AFM-B02","text":"Σωματικὴ μεσιτεία → αἴσθησις ὡς μετάδοσις → πλάνη ἢ κοσμικὴ μάθησις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AFM-B03","text":"Κοσμικὴ μάθησις → μέτρησις περιοδικότητος → χρόνος → διόρθωσις τῆς ψυχῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AFM-B04","text":"Διόρθωσις → ἀναζήτησις τῆς αἰτίας → νοῦς καὶ ἀνάγκη → προκοσμικὴ ἀντίστασις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AFM-B05","text":"Προκοσμικὴ ἀντίστασις → χώρα → τριμερὴς ὀντολογία → διάκρισις νοῦ καὶ δόξης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:AFM-B03","CL_JP8E_12:AFM-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AFM-B06","text":"Ἀποφατικὴ σύγκλεισις → ἀναγνώρισις ἀβεβαιότητος → ἐπανέναρξις τῆς ἀναζητήσεως τοῦ θεμελίου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:AFM-B06","CL_JP8E_11:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MME-01","text":"Ἡ θνητότης ἀναταξινομεῖται ὡς ἀναγκαία πληρότης καὶ ταὐτόχρονος ἐστία κινδύνου καὶ φθορᾶς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MME-02","text":"Τὸ σῶμα ἀναταξινομεῖται ὡς ἐνεργὸν δίκτυον μεταδόσεων, καὶ οὐκέτι ὡς ὑλικὸν ὑπόλειμμα ψυχῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:MME-01","CL_JP8E_09:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MME-03","text":"Ἡ αἴσθησις ἀναταξινομεῖται ὡς ἀμφίσημος μηχανὴ μετρήσεως, μεσιτείας, πλάνης καὶ παιδείας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:MME-01","CL_JP8E_10:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MME-04","text":"Ἡ ὕλη καὶ ὁ τόπος ἀναταξινομοῦνται ἀπὸ ἐμπειρικὰ περιεχόμενα εἰς ὀντολογικοὺς ὅρους δεκτικότητος καὶ γενέσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:MME-02","CL_JP8E_12:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MME-05","text":"Ἡ ἐπιστήμη ἀναπλαισιοῦται ὡς πλῆθος ἀντιστοίχων λόγων, ὧν ἡ ἀκρίβεια κρίνεται κατὰ τὴν σταθερότητα τοῦ ἀντικειμένου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:BSD-03","CL_JP8E_11:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:ALT-B01","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ ἐπιφανειακοῦ ἐπιπέδου, θεοὶ, σώματα, ὄργανα, φῶς, ἄστρα, στοιχεῖα καὶ φαινόμενα παρέχουσι τὰ ῥητὰ ἀντικείμενα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:ALT-B01","CL_JP8E_11:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:ALT-B02","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ δομικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, ἱεραρχία, ῥοή, μεσιτεία, ἀνάγκη, μίμησις καὶ δεκτικότητα συγκροτοῦσι τὸν μηχανισμὸν τῆς γενέσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:ALT-B02","CL_JP8E_11:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:ALT-B03","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ μεταστρατηγικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, ἡ ὀρθὴ μέθοδος ζητεῖ διάκρισιν μεσιτειῶν καὶ κυριαρχίαν τῶν ἐσωτερικῶν κινήσεων μᾶλλον ἢ φυγὴν ἐκ τοῦ φαινομένου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:ALT-B05","CL_JP8E_12:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:ALT-B04","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ ὀντολογικοῦ καὶ χρονικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, τὸ εἶδος καὶ ἡ χώρα διαμένουσιν, τὰ δὲ γιγνόμενα κυκλοφοροῦσι διὰ φθορᾶς, μεταβολῆς καὶ ἐπανατάξεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:ALT-B04","CL_JP8E_09:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:ALT-B05","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ μεθοδολογικοῦ καὶ ἐπιτελεστικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, ὁ λόγος ἀρχίζει ἀπὸ μηχανισμοὺς καὶ φθάνει εἰς ἀποφατικὴν ἀφαίρεσιν, ἐκτελῶν αὐτὸς τὴν κίνησιν τῆς θεωρίας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:ALT-B06","CL_JP8E_12:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:FL-01","text":"Ἡ θεία καὶ νοερὰ τάξις τίθεται ὡς ἀρχή, ἀλλὰ ὁ ἀκριβὴς τρόπος καθ᾽ ὃν ἐνεργεῖ ἐπὶ σωματικῶν κινήσεων καὶ ἀνάγκης μένει ὑποκαθορισμένος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:FL-02","CL_JP8E_10:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:FL-02","text":"Ἡ ψυχικὴ εὐθύνη ἀπαιτεῖται, ἀλλὰ τὰ σωματικὰ πάθη καὶ αἱ κοσμικαὶ διανομὰς περιορίζουσι τὴν αὐτονομίαν τῆς κρίσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:FL-03","CL_JP8E_09:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:FL-03","text":"Ἡ χώρα δεῖ εἶναι ἀπαθὴς πρὸς ἴδιον εἶδος καὶ ὅμως σειομένη πρὸς ἀνίσους δυνάμεις· ἡ διάκρισις ταύτη σώζει τὴν θεωρίαν ἀλλ᾽ οὐκ ἐξαλείφει τὴν ἀμφισημίαν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:FL-02","CL_JP8E_12:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:FL-04","text":"Ὁ λόγος παραιτεῖται τῆς ἀπολύτου βεβαιότητος, ἀλλὰ διατηρεῖ ἰσχυρὰν ἀξίωσιν περὶ τῶν ἀρχῶν, καὶ οὕτως ἡ εἰκὼς ἐξήγησις μένει ἐντάσει πρὸς τὴν ὀντολογικὴν βεβαίωσιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:FL-04","CL_JP8E_11:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MP-01","text":"Ἡ αἴσθησις μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ παθητικὴν ἐντύπωσιν εἰς δραστικὸν κύκλωμα, ὃ μεταβάλλει ὅσα μεταδίδει.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:MP-01","CL_JP8E_10:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MP-02","text":"Ἡ ἀταξία μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ στέρησιν εἰς θετικῶς παραγόμενον πλῆθος ἀντικρουομένων κινήσεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:MP-02","CL_JP8E_09:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MP-03","text":"Ἡ χώρα μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ δεκτικὸν ὑπόβαθρον εἰς ἀναγκαῖον καὶ ἀποφατικὸν πεδίον, ὅπου τὸ πάσχειν δὲν ἐπιφέρει ἴδιον εἶδος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:RMD-01","CL_JP8E_12:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MP-04","text":"Ὁ λόγος μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ ἀπόδειξιν εἰς ἐπιεικῆ καὶ ἀποφατικὴν παρακολούθησιν γιγνομένου κόσμου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:MP-03","CL_JP8E_12:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:HSI-01","text":"Ἡ πραγματικότης εἶναι μηχανὴ μεταδόσεως καὶ ἐκτυπώσεως, ἐν ᾗ ἡ μορφὴ διανέμεται, ἡ ἀνάγκη ἀνθίσταται, καὶ ἡ χώρα δέχεται χωρὶς νὰ ταυτίζηται.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:HSI-01","CL_JP8E_12:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:HSI-02","text":"Ὁ ὑποκείμενος εἶναι κόμβος καὶ μικρογραφία τοῦ κοσμικοῦ μηχανήματος, ἐν ᾧ ἀθάνατοι ρυθμοὶ, σωματικαὶ ῥοαί, πάθη καὶ λόγος συγκρούονται.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:HSI-02","CL_JP8E_11:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:HSI-03","text":"Ὁ χρόνος εἶναι σπειροειδὴς μηχανὴ, ἐν ᾗ ἡ ἐπάνοδος οὐ καταλύει τὴν ἔκθεσιν εἰς νέαν διατάραξιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:HSI-03","CL_JP8E_09:AFM-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:HSI-04","text":"Ἡ δυνατὴ διαφυγὴ συνίσταται εἰς διάκρισιν, ῥύθμισιν καὶ μίμησιν τῆς τάξεως, οὐχὶ εἰς ἀκύρωσιν σώματος, φαινομένου ἢ ἀνάγκης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:HSI-04","CL_JP8E_11:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MRT-B01","text":"Τὰ ἀξιώματα ἀρκοῦσι πρὸς ἀνακατασκευήν τῆς μείζονος κινήσεως ἀπὸ δημιουργικὴν διανομήν διὰ σωματικῆς μεσιτείας πρὸς τριμερῆ καὶ ἀποφατικὴν ὀντολογίαν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:MRT-B01","CL_JP8E_12:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MRT-B02","text":"Ἡ ἀρχιτεκτονικὴ εἶναι ἰσχυροτάτη ὅταν συνάπτῃ αἰτίαν, σῶμα, αἴσθησιν, ἀνάγκην, χώραν καὶ κυκλικὴν ψυχικὴν ἀνάταξιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:MRT-B03","CL_JP8E_12:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MRT-B03","text":"Ὑποκαθορίζονται ὁ τρόπος τῆς νοερᾶς ἐνεργείας ἐπὶ τῆς ἀνάγκης, ἡ σχέσις χώρας πρὸς εἶδος, καὶ ἡ ἀκριβὴς ἔκτασις τῆς ψυχικῆς εὐθύνης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:MRT-B04","CL_JP8E_12:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MRT-B04","text":"Ἡ συνοχὴ τοῦ μετασυστήματος ἐξαρτᾶται ἀπὸ τὴν διατήρησιν τῆς σπειροειδοῦς ἐπιστροφῆς καὶ τῆς ἀποφατικῆς ἀσάφειας, ἃ οὐ δύνανται ἀβλαβῶς νὰ μειωθῶσιν εἰς ἀκίνητα ἀξιώματα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:MRT-B05","CL_JP8E_12:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261014T233330928816Z_e77ca90b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Jp8E L1 03","source_sha256":"a0e97ee6f4392f885d12b5aa3f3a2863e66c89b544dc96544cb6659cc5296521","sha256":"1221161cce623534d9f54a4ffc4304a262ae9ff6f69377421d19082bd474609e","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-14T23:33:30.929789+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AS-B01","text":"The problem of the system is how order may admit mortal generation, bodily disturbance, necessity, and unstable manifestation without the annihilation of the principle.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AS-B02","text":"Creation ceases to be a simple imposition of form and becomes a distributed transmission of causality through bodies, senses, souls, and receptive fields.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:AS-B03","CL_JP8E_10:AFM-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AS-B03","text":"The body is transformed from an inferior covering into an intermediary, which transmits motions, produces affections, and imposes the necessity of judgment.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:AS-B04","CL_JP8E_10:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AS-B04","text":"Sensation rises from bodily impact into a path of cosmic measurement, but at the same time remains a source of error owing to the relative nature of transmission.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:SP-01","CL_JP8E_10:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AS-B05","text":"Order does not signify the absence of necessity, but persuasion and reordering upon a field of resistant forces.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:GA-06","CL_JP8E_11:SP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AS-B06","text":"The inquiry into order compels discourse to introduce χώρα as a formless condition of possibility, which receives forms without itself being one form.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:AS-03","CL_JP8E_12:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AS-B07","text":"Discourse moves from an arithmetical and teleological apprehension to a likely and apophatic mode, because that which comes to be and χώρα resist complete fixation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:GA-10","CL_JP8E_12:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AS-B08","text":"The movement of the whole is spiral: order is transmitted, disturbed, measured, corrected, and finally returns to an apophatic recognition of the foundation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:AS-B08","CL_JP8E_12:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Every generation requires causal distribution and a receptive field; neither self-generation nor immediate production exhaust reality.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:GA-02","CL_JP8E_12:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Body and sensation are active intermediaries between cosmic motions and psychic order.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:GA-05","CL_JP8E_10:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Mortality is at once a condition of cosmic completeness and a site of destruction, so that the necessary is not identical with the harmless.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:GA-03","CL_JP8E_09:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Order is regulation and persuasion over resistant necessity, not a rigid state pre-existing motion.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:GA-06","CL_JP8E_11:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] The truth of phenomena requires the distinction of causes, mediations, paradigms, and receptacles, not simple attachment to sensation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:GA-08","CL_JP8E_12:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Sensation is a twofold power: a measure of cosmic education and a conduit of disturbance and error.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:GA-01","CL_JP8E_10:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Reality is constituted tripartitely from an immutable paradigm, receptive χώρα, and perishable becoming.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:GA-06","CL_JP8E_12:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] The reordering of psychic motions according to a cosmic measure draws physiology, ethics, education, and cosmology toward one functional convergence.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:GA-10","CL_JP8E_11:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Discourse concerning generation cannot become absolute demonstration, but must follow change through likely and apophatic discourse.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:GA-10","CL_JP8E_12:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] The absence of a proper form in χώρα becomes the hidden condition through which many forms can appear without the foundation being identified with them.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:GA-10","CL_JP8E_12:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:RMD-01","text":"The hierarchy from creative distribution migrates into the relation of head and body, first and secondary causes, and finally paradigm, χώρα, and becoming.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:GA-02","CL_JP8E_10:RMD-01","CL_JP8E_12:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:RMD-02","text":"Sensation retains the function of transmission, but changes from bodily concussion into an instrument of number, time, and philosophical correction.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:RMD-06","CL_JP8E_11:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:RMD-03","text":"Necessity mutates from a bodily and mechanical co-cause into a resistant field, which intellect does not dissolve but persuades.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:MP-03","CL_JP8E_11:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:RMD-04","text":"Order reverses its meaning: from presupposed harmony it becomes an achievement of regulation within an unstable and passive field.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:MP-01","CL_JP8E_12:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:RMD-05","text":"The receptacle absorbs the simple notion of matter and place, becoming an ontological condition of manifestation and a dynamic field of reactions.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:RMD-05","CL_JP8E_12:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:RMD-06","text":"Discourse retains the claim of distinction, but abandons the claim of immobilizing becoming.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:RMD-07","CL_JP8E_11:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:CGD-01","text":"Specific elemental, anatomical, and astronomical descriptions remain local techniques, while the mediation of motion through body and sensation is scaled up into a universal principle.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:CGD-02","CL_JP8E_10:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:CGD-02","text":"The ethics of reason and the irrational ceases to be merely a judgment of character and becomes a mechanism for reordering a cosmically embodied subject.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:GA-08","CL_JP8E_11:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:CGD-03","text":"The distinction of first and secondary causes bridges physiology to cosmology, but the tripartite arrangement exceeds dual causality.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:CGD-06","CL_JP8E_11:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:CGD-04","text":"Therapy through music and observation of the stars remain local practices, but are gathered together as modes of regulative imitation of cosmic periodicity.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:CGD-02","CL_JP8E_11:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:CGD-05","text":"χώρα abolishes the local hypothesis of matter as a self-subsistent element and becomes the common condition that connects generation, phenomenon, and cognitive limit.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:CGD-03","CL_JP8E_12:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GNX-01","text":"Autonomous generation or self-forming matter is excluded, because what comes to be requires cause, receptivity, and relation to a paradigm.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:GNX-01","CL_JP8E_12:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GNX-02","text":"The conception of body and sensation as passive containers without transformative activity is excluded.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:GNX-03","CL_JP8E_10:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GNX-03","text":"A cosmos in which order subsists without necessity, resistance, destruction, or a receptive field is excluded.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_09:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GNX-04","text":"The identification of phenomenon, name, and stable essence is excluded, because phenomena are changing imitations.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:GNX-04","CL_JP8E_12:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:GNX-05","text":"Absolute certainty concerning becoming is excluded, because the instability of the object alters the fitting mode of discourse.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:DBN-04","CL_JP8E_11:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that causal necessity and intellect are hostile to one another, because the system employs necessity as a subordinate yet active co-cause.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:DBN-03","CL_JP8E_10:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that soul or human being can be self-sufficient, since the soul is formed in body, time, and cosmic distribution.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:BSA-04","CL_JP8E_10:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that phenomenon can possess self-subsistent essence, since manifestation depends upon χώρα and paradigm.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_12:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that χώρα can be positively defined as one substance alongside the others, since a proper form negates universal receptivity.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:DBN-02","CL_JP8E_12:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSA-01","text":"The deepest absence is impossible immediacy between intellect, cosmos, and being; every access passes through mediations.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:BSA-01","CL_JP8E_09:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSA-02","text":"χώρα is the necessary yet inexpressible foundation, which neither appears by itself nor can be removed without the collapse of generation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:BSA-05","CL_JP8E_12:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSA-03","text":"The perfect coincidence of truth, name, and phenomenon remains impossible, because generation preserves a distance between form and image.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:BSA-03","CL_JP8E_11:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:XD-01","text":"Distributed creation and tripartite ontology entail that causality does not flow from the summit alone, but is actualized through the interaction of paradigm, body, χώρα, and becoming.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:AS-B03","CL_JP8E_12:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:XD-02","text":"Bodily transmission and the relative nature of phenomenon make error not a bare lack of knowledge, but a produced result of mediation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:AS-B05","CL_JP8E_10:XD-03","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:XD-03","text":"The restoration of the soul and the persuasion of necessity by intellect constitute one schema: order returns through reordering and not through the elimination of resistance.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:XD-05","CL_JP8E_11:XD-02","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:XD-04","text":"The formlessness of χώρα and the precision of imprinting prove that the privation of a proper character can be a positive condition of receptivity.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:RMD-02","CL_JP8E_12:XD-01","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:XD-05","text":"Mathematical periodicity and likely discourse distinguish two scientific measures: precision concerning stable motions and appropriate probability concerning generation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:GA-02","CL_JP8E_11:XD-05","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_03:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSD-01","text":"Reality becomes a multilevel circuit in which every transmission of order can simultaneously produce new instability.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:BSD-01","CL_JP8E_10:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSD-02","text":"The subject becomes a node of flows and not a self-sufficient center, because cosmic law, bodily affection, sensation, and judgment converge in it.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:BSD-02","CL_JP8E_10:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSD-03","text":"Time becomes a recursive sequence in which disturbance produces judgment, judgment gives form to the bearer, and the new form restarts the cycle.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:BSD-03","CL_JP8E_11:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSD-04","text":"Science becomes a diagnosis of conditions of possibility and limits of discourse, not possession of immutable names or self-subsistent phenomena.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:BSD-04","CL_JP8E_12:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:BSD-05","text":"Salvation or freedom is defined as the re-establishment of governance within conditions of necessity and embodiment, not as escape from the system.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:BSD-05","CL_JP8E_10:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:RLE-01","text":"Creative cause → bodily mediation → sensation → psychic judgment → reordering or disturbance → new embodied exposure.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:RLE-02","text":"Sight → periodicity → number → time → philosophical imitation → correction of psychic revolutions.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:RLE-03","text":"Intellect → persuasion → necessity → mixed generation → necessity of χώρα → apophatic uncertainty.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:RLE-04","text":"Instability of phenomena → search for substrate → abstraction of form → χώρα → new explanation of instability.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:RLE-05","text":"Hierarchical reordering is the practical attractor, while χώρα is the ontological attractor of the whole apparatus.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:RLE-05","CL_JP8E_12:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:FGS-B01","text":"Creative order → distributed causality → mortal and bodily composition.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:FGS-B02","text":"Bodily composition → transmission of motions → sensation → affection and relative phenomenon.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:FGS-B03","CL_JP8E_10:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:FGS-B03","text":"Relative phenomenon → observation of periodicity → number and time → educational imitation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:FGS-B01","CL_JP8E_11:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:FGS-B04","text":"Educational order → question concerning generation → intellect and necessity → recognition of resistance.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:FGS-B05","text":"Resistance and change → inadequacy of dualism → χώρα without a proper form → tripartite ontology.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:FGS-B04","CL_JP8E_12:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:FGS-B06","text":"Tripartite ontology → apophatic knowledge of the foundation → likely discourse → continuous recurrence of distinction.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:FGS-B07","CL_JP8E_11:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AFM-B01","text":"Creative distribution → mortal completeness → bodily mediation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AFM-B02","text":"Bodily mediation → sensation as transmission → error or cosmic learning.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AFM-B03","text":"Cosmic learning → measurement of periodicity → time → correction of the soul.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AFM-B04","text":"Correction → search for the cause → intellect and necessity → pre-cosmic resistance.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AFM-B05","text":"Pre-cosmic resistance → χώρα → tripartite ontology → distinction of intellect and opinion.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:AFM-B03","CL_JP8E_12:AFM-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:AFM-B06","text":"Apophatic convergence → recognition of uncertainty → recommencement of the search for the foundation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:AFM-B06","CL_JP8E_11:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MME-01","text":"Mortality is reclassified as necessary completeness and a simultaneous focus of danger and destruction.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MME-02","text":"The body is reclassified as an active network of transmissions, and no longer as the material residue of soul.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:MME-01","CL_JP8E_09:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MME-03","text":"Sensation is reclassified as an ambiguous mechanism of measurement, mediation, error, and education.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:MME-01","CL_JP8E_10:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MME-04","text":"Matter and place are reclassified from empirical contents into ontological conditions of receptivity and generation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:MME-02","CL_JP8E_12:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MME-05","text":"Science is reframed as a plurality of corresponding discourses, whose precision is judged according to the stability of the object.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:BSD-03","CL_JP8E_11:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, gods, bodies, organs, light, stars, elements, and phenomena provide the explicit objects.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:ALT-B01","CL_JP8E_11:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, hierarchy, flow, mediation, necessity, imitation, and receptivity constitute the mechanism of generation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:ALT-B02","CL_JP8E_11:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:ALT-B03","text":"At the meta-strategic level, the correct method seeks the distinction of mediations and mastery of internal motions rather than flight from phenomenon.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:ALT-B05","CL_JP8E_12:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, form and χώρα remain, while things that come to be circulate through destruction, change, and reordering.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:ALT-B04","CL_JP8E_09:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, discourse begins from mechanisms and reaches apophatic abstraction, itself enacting the movement of theory.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:ALT-B06","CL_JP8E_12:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:FL-01","text":"Divine and intelligible order is posited as principle, but the precise manner in which it acts upon bodily motions and necessity remains underdetermined.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:FL-02","CL_JP8E_10:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:FL-02","text":"Psychic responsibility is required, but bodily affections and cosmic distributions restrict the autonomy of judgment.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:FL-03","CL_JP8E_09:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:FL-03","text":"χώρα must be unaffected with respect to a proper form and yet shaken by unequal forces; this distinction saves the theory but does not eliminate the ambiguity.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:FL-02","CL_JP8E_12:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:FL-04","text":"Discourse renounces absolute certainty, but retains a strong claim concerning principles, and thus likely explanation remains in tension with ontological affirmation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:FL-04","CL_JP8E_11:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MP-01","text":"Sensation mutates from passive impression into an active circuit that changes what it transmits.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:MP-01","CL_JP8E_10:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MP-02","text":"Disorder mutates from privation into a positively produced plurality of conflicting motions.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:MP-02","CL_JP8E_09:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MP-03","text":"χώρα mutates from a receptive substrate into a necessary and apophatic field, where being affected does not bring about a proper form.","refs":["CL_JP8E_12:RMD-01","CL_JP8E_12:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MP-04","text":"Discourse mutates from demonstration into equitable and apophatic tracking of a world in becoming.","refs":["CL_JP8E_11:MP-03","CL_JP8E_12:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:HSI-01","text":"Reality is a machine of transmission and imprinting, in which form is distributed, necessity resists, and χώρα receives without being identified.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:HSI-01","CL_JP8E_12:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a node and miniature of the cosmic apparatus, in which immortal rhythms, bodily flows, affections, and discourse conflict.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:HSI-02","CL_JP8E_11:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:HSI-03","text":"Time is a spiral machine, in which return does not abolish exposure to new disturbance.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:HSI-03","CL_JP8E_09:AFM-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:HSI-04","text":"Possible escape consists in distinction, regulation, and imitation of order, not in the annulment of body, phenomenon, or necessity.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:HSI-04","CL_JP8E_11:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms suffice for reconstruction of the major movement from creative distribution through bodily mediation toward tripartite and apophatic ontology.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:MRT-B01","CL_JP8E_12:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest when it joins cause, body, sensation, necessity, χώρα, and circular psychic reordering.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:MRT-B03","CL_JP8E_12:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MRT-B03","text":"The manner of intelligible activity upon necessity, the relation of χώρα to form, and the precise extent of psychic responsibility are underdetermined.","refs":["CL_JP8E_10:MRT-B04","CL_JP8E_12:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_03:MRT-B04","text":"The coherence of the metasystem depends upon preserving spiral return and apophatic indeterminacy, which cannot harmlessly be reduced to immutable axioms.","refs":["CL_JP8E_09:MRT-B05","CL_JP8E_12:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_JP8E_L1_03@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_JP8E_L1_03:v1","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Jp8E L1 03"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Timaeus","title":"Jp8E L1 03","context":"Chapters 25–36 · Section 41 · Section 42 · Section 43 · Section 44 · Section 45 · Section 46 · Section 47 · Section 48 · Section 49 · Section 50 · Section 51 · Section 52","original_title":"Jp8E L1 03","original_context":"Chapters 25–36 · Section 41 · Section 42 · Section 43 · Section 44 · Section 45 · Section 46 · Section 47 · Section 48 · Section 49 · Section 50 · Section 51 · Section 52"},"original_title":"Jp8E L1 03","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04","layer":"MC","title":"Jp8E L1 04","order":4,"parents":["CL_JP8E_13","CL_JP8E_14","CL_JP8E_15","CL_JP8E_16"],"revision":"MC_JP8E_L1_04@R001","sha256":"2331d2e65dde6f516d2e5854589c7a8f90c34fa1db6a53791816f45d0cc5682e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AS-B01","text":"Τὸ σύστημα ζητεῖ νὰ ἐξηγήσῃ πῶς ἡ πολλότης γίνεται κόσμος, χωρὶς ἡ γένεσις νὰ καταλήγῃ εἰς ἄμορφον ῥοὴν ἢ ἀκίνητον τάξιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AS-B02","text":"Ἡ γεωμετρία μεταβάλλεται ἀπὸ ἀρχὴ στατικῆς μορφοποιήσεως εἰς μηχανὴ σχέσεων, ἡ οποία παράγει κίνησιν, ἀντίστασιν, μεῖξιν, πάθος καὶ αἴσθησιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:GA-01","CL_JP8E_16:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AS-B03","text":"Ἡ τάξις δὲν προηγείται τῆς μεταβολῆς, ἀλλὰ διασῴζεται διὰ πεπερασμένων διαφοροποιήσεων, πιέσεων, διαλύσεων καὶ νέων συστάσεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:GA-01","CL_JP8E_14:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AS-B04","text":"Ἡ ἀπουσία κενοῦ καθιστᾷ τὸν κόσμον καθολικῶς συμπεπλεγμένον, ὥστε πᾶσα τοπικὴ εἴσοδος, μετάθεσις ἢ βία νὰ ἀναδιατάσσῃ πλέγμα σχέσεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:GA-07","CL_JP8E_15:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AS-B05","text":"Ἡ ἐξήγησις ἀναβαίνει ἀπὸ τὰ σώματα εἰς τὰ πάθη καὶ τὴν αἴσθησιν, καὶ ἐπιστρέφει εἰς τὴν διόρθωσιν τῶν ὀνομάτων διὰ τῶν μηχανισμῶν οἳ αὐτὰ παράγουσιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:GA-09","CL_JP8E_16:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AS-B06","text":"Ἡ τελικὴ σύνθεσις εἶναι ἐνιαῖος ἀλλὰ μὴ ὁμοιογενὴς κόσμος, ὅπου ἡ ἀκρίβεια τῶν σχέσεων συνυπάρχει μὲ ὅρια ἀποδείξεως καὶ μερικῶς ἀνοικτὰς μεταβάσεις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:GA-12","CL_JP8E_15:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Τὰ σώματα δὲν εἰσὶν αὐτοτελεῖς οὐσίαι, ἀλλὰ πρόσκαιραι συστάσεις μορφῆς, ἀριθμοῦ, πυκνότητος, κινήσεως καὶ σχέσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:GA-01","CL_JP8E_14:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-02","text":"[Mutating] Ἡ γεωμετρικὴ μορφὴ παύει νὰ εἶναι μόνον ὑπόβαθρον εἴδους καὶ γίνεται αἰτία κινήσεως, δυνάμεως, παθήματος καὶ φυσικῆς φορᾶς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:GA-03","CL_JP8E_16:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Ἡ διαφορά, ἡ ἀνισότης καὶ ἡ ἀνομοιότης εἰσὶν οἱ παραγωγικοὶ ὅροι κινήσεως, ἀντιμαχίας καὶ νέας συστάσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:GA-03","CL_JP8E_14:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Ἡ ἀπουσία κενοῦ ὑποχρεοῖ τὴν μεταβολὴν νὰ εἶναι ἀμοιβαία, πιεστική, τοπικῶς μεταδοτική καὶ καθολικῶς κυκλοτερής.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:GA-07","CL_JP8E_15:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Ἡ ταυτότης διατηρεῖται οὐ διὰ ἀκινησίας, ἀλλὰ διὰ ῥυθμιζομένης ἀνασυνθέσεως, συγγενείας, εἰσόδου καὶ ἀντιστάσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:GA-11","CL_JP8E_15:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Ἡ αἰτιότης εἶναι κατανεμημένη κατὰ κλίμακας: μορφὴ παράγει κίνησιν, κίνησις μετάδοσιν, μετάδοσις πάθος, καὶ πάθος δύναται νὰ γίνῃ αἴσθησις καὶ ὀνομασία.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:GA-03","CL_JP8E_16:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Ὁ τόπος δὲν εἶναι κενὸν περιέχον, ἀλλὰ δίκτυον συγγενῶν φορῶν, μεταδόσεων, ἀντιστάσεων καὶ ἀναδιανομῶν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:GA-08","CL_JP8E_16:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] Ἡ τάξις τοῦ κόσμου εἶναι δυναμικὴ διατήρησις, ἥτις διασῴζεται μόνον ἐπειδὴ ἀναπαράγει τὰς ἰδίας προϋποθέσεις τῆς διαφορᾶς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:XD-08","CL_JP8E_14:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Ἡ ὀνοματοθεσία εἶναι δευτερογενὴς κρίσις, ἣ ὀφείλει νὰ ἀκολουθῇ τὴν φυσικὴν δομὴν καὶ οὐχὶ νὰ ὑποκαθιστᾷ τὴν αἰτίαν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:GA-06","CL_JP8E_16:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Ἡ καθολικὴ ἑνότης δὲν συνεπάγεται ἕνα ἀπόλυτον σύστημα τόπων, φορῶν ἢ ποιοτήτων, ἀλλὰ συνίσταται ἐκ πολλῶν σχετικῶν συστημάτων ἐντὸς σφαιρικῆς τάξεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:GA-10","CL_JP8E_16:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Ἡ ἐξήγησις ἀναγκαίως παραμένει διαβαθμισμένη, διότι ἡ γενετικὴ μηχανὴ δύναται νὰ ἀνακατασκευασθῇ ἀκριβέστερον ἢ αἱ ὑπέρταται ἀρχαὶ καὶ αἱ λεπτομέρειαι τῆς φρονήσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:GA-12","CL_JP8E_16:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:RMD-01","text":"Ἡ κίνησις διατηρεῖ τὴν γενετικὴν λειτουργίαν της, ἀλλὰ μεταπίπτει ἀπὸ κοσμογονικὸν χωρισμὸν εἰς ἀντιμαχίαν, εἴσοδον, μετάδοσιν παθήματος καὶ φυσικὴν φοράν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:RMD-02","CL_JP8E_16:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:RMD-02","text":"Ἡ γεωμετρία μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ ὀντολογικὸν κριτήριον σωμάτων εἰς δυναμικὴν γραμματικὴν ἀνισότητος καὶ τέλος εἰς ἔμμεσον ὑπόδειγμα τῆς αἰσθήσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:RMD-07","CL_JP8E_14:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:RMD-03","text":"Ἡ ταυτότης ἀπορροφᾶται ὑπὸ τῆς σχέσεως: ἀπὸ προσωρινὴ ἑνότης ἀνασυνθέσιμων στοιχείων γίνεται κόμβος πιέσεων, τόπων, χρόνων καὶ μεταδόσεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:RMD-05","CL_JP8E_15:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:RMD-04","text":"Ἡ ἀπουσία κενοῦ παραμένει σταθερὸς ἀποκλεισμός, ἀλλὰ ἡ λειτουργία της διευρύνεται ἀπὸ κοσμικὴν κυκλοφορίαν εἰς μικροφυσικὴν διαπερατότητα καὶ αἰσθητικὴν μετάδοσιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:CGD-04","CL_JP8E_15:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:RMD-05","text":"Ἡ συγγένεια μεταστρέφεται ἀπὸ ὅρον περιορισμένης μεταβολῆς εἰς ἀρχὴ τοπολογικῆς φορᾶς, ἀφομοιώσεως καὶ κατανομῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:GA-05","CL_JP8E_14:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:RMD-06","text":"Τὸ κοινὸν ὄνομα ἐκτελεῖ ἀρχικῶς ἐμπειρικὴν λειτουργίαν, ἀλλὰ τελικῶς ὑποτάσσεται εἰς τὸν ἔλεγχον τοῦ δικτύου μορφῆς, κινήσεως καὶ αἰτίας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:RMD-06","CL_JP8E_16:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:CGD-01","text":"Αἱ ἀκριβείς κατασκευαὶ τριγώνων, στερεῶν, βάσεων καὶ ἀριθμητικῶν ἀναλογιῶν μένουσιν κατώτερα τεχνικὰ ὄργανα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:CGD-02","CL_JP8E_16:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:CGD-02","text":"Ἡ μορφολογικὴ αἰτιότης κλιμακοῦται, διότι συνδέει τὴν μικροδομὴν τῶν σωμάτων μὲ τὴν κοσμικὴν κίνησιν, τὴν αἴσθησιν καὶ τὴν γλωσσικὴν κρίσιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:CGD-03","CL_JP8E_16:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:CGD-03","text":"Ἡ ἀπουσία κενοῦ, ἡ διαπερατότης καὶ ἡ ἀναπλήρωσις γεφυροῦσι τὴν κοσμικὴν θεωρίαν τῆς κυκλοφορίας πρὸς τὴν θεωρίαν τῆς τήξεως, τῆς μεταδόσεως καὶ τοῦ παθήματος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:CGD-04","CL_JP8E_15:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:CGD-04","text":"Ἡ ἀκύρωσις ἀπολύτου ἄνω καὶ κάτω γίνεται καταλύτης γενικωτέρας σχετικοποιήσεως τῶν ποιοτήτων, τῶν τόπων καὶ τῶν ὀνομάτων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:CGD-04","CL_JP8E_16:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:CGD-05","text":"Ἡ ἐξωτερικὴ θεία διακόσμησις ἀνακωδικοποιεῖται ὡς ἐσωτερικὴ συνοχή τῶν σχέσεων, ἀλλὰ δὲν ἐξαλείφεται τελείως ὡς ὅρος τῆς ὑπέρτατης θεμελιώσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:CGD-05","CL_JP8E_13:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GNX-01","text":"Ἀπορρίπτεται ἡ ἄμορφος, ἀκίνητος καὶ αὐτάρκης ὕλη, διότι πᾶσα ὕλη ὑπάρχει ἤδη ἐν μορφώσει, διαφορᾷ καὶ σχέσει.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:GNX-02","CL_JP8E_15:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GNX-02","text":"Ἀπορρίπτεται ἡ μεταβολὴ ὡς γένεσις ἐκ μηδενὸς ἢ ἄτακτος ῥοή, διότι μεταβολὴ σημαίνει λύσιν, εἴσοδον, ἀντιμαχίαν καὶ ἀνασύνθεσιν σχέσεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:GNX-02","CL_JP8E_15:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GNX-03","text":"Ἀπορρίπτεται ἡ ἀπόλυτος ὁμοιότης, ἐπειδὴ ἄνευ ἀνομοιότητος δὲν ὑπάρχει δύναμις, μετάδοσις, φυσικὴ φορά ἢ αἴσθησις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:GNX-03","CL_JP8E_16:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GNX-04","text":"Ἀπορρίπτεται ὁ τόπος ὡς κενὸν καὶ καθολικὸν πλαίσιον προσανατολισμοῦ, διότι ὁ τόπος ὑφίσταται μόνον διὰ συγγενείας, πιέσεως καὶ φορᾶς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:GNX-04","CL_JP8E_16:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GNX-05","text":"Ἀπορρίπτεται ἡ ταύτισις τοῦ ὀνόματος, τῆς ποιότητος, τοῦ παθήματος καὶ τῆς ἡδονῆς ὡς ἑνὸς ἀδιαιρέτου δεδομένου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:GNX-03","CL_JP8E_16:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GNX-06","text":"Ἀπορρίπτεται ἡ ταύτισις μαθηματικῆς δυνατότητος, φυσικῆς ὑπάρξεως καὶ πλήρους ἀποδείξεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:GNX-06","CL_JP8E_15:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:DBN-01","text":"Δεῖ νὰ εἶναι ψεῦδος ὅτι τὰ πράγματα προϋπάρχουσι τῶν διαδικασιῶν, διότι τὸ σῶμα, ἡ ποιότης καὶ ἡ ταυτότης εἰσὶν ἀποτελέσματα γενετικῶν σχέσεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_15:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:DBN-02","text":"Δεῖ νὰ εἶναι ψεῦδος ὅτι μία μόνη ἀρχὴ δύναται νὰ ἐξηγήσῃ τὴν φύσιν, διότι αἰτιότης διανέμεται εἰς μορφήν, ἀριθμόν, κίνησιν, τόπον, πίεσιν καὶ μετάδοσιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:DBN-02","CL_JP8E_15:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:DBN-03","text":"Δεῖ νὰ εἶναι ψεῦδος ὅτι ἡ ἐξωτερικὴ ἐπαφὴ ἀρκεῖ πρὸς μεταβολήν, διότι τὸ εἰσελθεῖν ἢ ἀντιστῆναι κρίνεται ὑπὸ τῆς ἐσωτερικῆς διατάξεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:DBN-03","CL_JP8E_15:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:DBN-04","text":"Δεῖ νὰ ἀποκλείεται ἡ ὑπόθεσις ὅτι ἡ ἐμπειρία παρέχει αὐτομάτως τὴν αἰτίαν αὐτῆς, διότι ἡ ἀναγνώρισις προϋποθέτει μεσολάβησιν σωμάτων καὶ φρονίμου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_16:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:DBN-05","text":"Δεῖ νὰ εἶναι ψεῦδος ὅτι ἡ φύσις δύναται νὰ ἐκτεθῇ ταυτοχρόνως καὶ ἐξ ἴσου ἐπὶ πάσης κλίμακος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:DBN-04","CL_JP8E_15:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSA-01","text":"Ἡ βαθύτερα ἀπούσα δυνατότης εἶναι κόσμος ἐν ᾧ ἡ ἑνότης θὰ ἐσήμαινεν ὁμοιογένειαν, ἡ δὲ διαφορὰ καθαρὰν διάλυσιν τῆς τάξεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:BSA-05","CL_JP8E_16:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSA-02","text":"Ἀπουσιάζει καθολικὸς χάρτης ὁ ὁποῖος θὰ ὥριζε ἀπολύτως τόπον, ποιότητα, πάθος καὶ σημασίαν ἄνευ τῶν σχέσεων αἳ τὰ παράγουσιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:BSA-05","CL_JP8E_16:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSA-03","text":"Ἀπουσιάζει πλήρης καὶ ταυτόχρονος γνῶσις τοῦ ὅλου, διότι τὸ ὅλον ἐνεργεῖ ὡς διαδοχὴ μετασχηματισμῶν καὶ ὄχι ὡς παρόν ἀκίνητον ἀντικείμενον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:BSA-03","CL_JP8E_15:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSA-04","text":"Ἀπαγορεύεται ἡ ἔξοδος εἰς ἀπόλυτον ἐκτός, διότι πᾶσα διαφυγὴ εἶναι μετάβασις εἰς ἑτέραν σύστασιν, δύναμιν καὶ σχέσιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:HSI-04","CL_JP8E_16:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:XD-01","text":"Ἡ πεπερασμένη γεωμετρικὴ μορφοποίησις καὶ ἡ κυκλοτερὴς ἀναπαραγωγὴ διαφορῶν συντίθενται εἰς κόσμον, ὅπου ἡ μορφὴ οὐ περιορίζει τὴν κίνησιν ἀλλὰ ὁρίζει τοὺς τρόπους τῆς παραγωγῆς της.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-08","CL_JP8E_13:GA-09","CL_JP8E_14:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:XD-02","text":"Ἡ ἀπουσία κενοῦ καὶ ἡ διαπερατότης τῶν διακένων συγκροτοῦσιν ἀντὶ ἀντιφάσεως μίαν μηχανὴ, καθ᾽ ἣν ἡ πληρότης καθιστᾷ τὴν εἴσοδον αἰτιακῶς δεσμευμένην.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-05","CL_JP8E_15:FL-01","CL_JP8E_15:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:XD-03","text":"Ἡ μετάβασις ἀπὸ στοιχειακὴν κίνησιν εἰς αἴσθησιν ἀναγκάζει τὴν αἰτιότητα νὰ γίνῃ ἱεραρχικὴ μετάδοσις καὶ οὐχὶ ἁπλὴ τοπικὴ κρούσις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-07","CL_JP8E_15:XD-05","CL_JP8E_16:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:XD-04","text":"Ἡ ἀκύρωσις τοῦ ἀπολύτου τόπου καὶ ἡ ἀκύρωσις τῆς αὐθύπαρκτης ποιότητος ἀποτελοῦσι μίαν καὶ τὴν αὐτὴν σχεσιακὴν ἀρχήν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-10","CL_JP8E_16:XD-03","CL_JP8E_16:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:XD-05","text":"Ἡ ἀναγκαία ἀνακατασκευή τῶν σχέσεων καὶ ὁ εἰκὼς χαρακτὴρ τῶν ὑστάτων λεπτομερειῶν ὁρίζουσι γνώσιν ἀκριβῆ κατὰ μηχανισμούς, ἀλλὰ μὴ κυρίαν κατὰ πᾶσαν θεμελίωσιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-11","CL_JP8E_15:XD-08","CL_JP8E_13:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSD-01","text":"Ἡ πραγματικότης γίνεται πεπερασμένη γενετικὴ τοπολογία, ἐν ᾗ τὰ ὄντα εἰσὶν κόμβοι μεταβαλλομένων σχέσεων καὶ οὐκ ἀνεξάρτηται μονάδες.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:BSD-01","CL_JP8E_15:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSD-02","text":"Ὁ χρόνος γίνεται ἐσωτερικὸς τρόπος τῆς ὕλης, διότι δηλοῖ λύσιν, παλαίωσιν, κυκλοφορίαν, μετάθεσιν καὶ ἐπανόρθωσιν συστάσεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:BSD-03","CL_JP8E_15:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSD-03","text":"Ἡ ὀντολογία τοῦ σώματος ἐπεκτείνεται εἰς θεωρίαν τοῦ αἰσθανομένου, ὥστε τὸ ὑποκείμενον νὰ γίνεται ἐνδοκοσμικὸς κόμβος μεταδόσεως καὶ ἀναγνώσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:BSD-06","CL_JP8E_16:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSD-04","text":"Ἡ ἐπιστήμη μετασχηματίζεται ἀπὸ ταξινόμησιν πραγμάτων εἰς ἀνακατασκευήν μεταβάσεων, ἡ δὲ γλῶσσα γίνεται πεδίον ἐλέγχου τῶν ἐξηγητικῶν ἐπιπέδων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:BSD-05","CL_JP8E_16:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSD-05","text":"Ἡ ἑνότης τοῦ κόσμου δὲν εἶναι καθολικὴ ὁμοιομορφία, ἀλλὰ ἡ ἱκανότης δικτύου νὰ συνδέῃ ἀσύμμετρα γένη, τοπικὰς φοράς καὶ διακεκριμένα πάθη.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:BSD-02","CL_JP8E_16:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:RLE-01","text":"Μορφὴ → ἀνομοιότης → δύναμις → ἀντιμαχία → λύσις → νέα μορφή· ὁ βρόχος μετατρέπει τὴν γεωμετρίαν εἰς αὐτοαναπαραγόμενον φυσικὸν μηχανισμόν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:RLE-01","CL_JP8E_15:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:RLE-02","text":"Πληρότης → ἀναπλήρωσις → πίεσις → εἴσοδος → ἀναδιάταξις → νέα πληρότης· ἡ ἀπουσία κενοῦ ἐνεργεῖ ὡς βρόχος καθολικῆς ἀλληλεξαρτήσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:RLE-04","CL_JP8E_15:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:RLE-03","text":"Πάθος σώματος → μετάδοσις ἢ ἀπομόνωσις → αἴσθησις ἢ ἀναισθησία → ἔλεγχος αἰτίας → νέος προσδιορισμὸς παθήματος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:RLE-03","CL_JP8E_16:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:RLE-04","text":"Ἐμπειρικὸν ὄνομα → μηχανιστικὴ ἀναγωγή → σχεσιακὸς ὁρισμός → διόρθωσις ὀνόματος· ἡ γλῶσσα ἐπιστρέφει εἰς ἑαυτὴν ἤδη μετασχηματισμένη.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:RLE-01","CL_JP8E_16:AFM-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:RLE-05","text":"Ὁ κύριος ἐλκτικὸς πόλος εἶναι ἡ ἀρχὴ ὅτι ἡ τάξις σώζεται μόνον ὅταν ἐμπεριέχῃ, ὁρίζῃ καὶ ἀναπαράγῃ τὴν διαφοράν ἣν ἀποκλείει ὡς ἄτακτον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:RLE-07","CL_JP8E_14:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:FGS-B01","text":"Ἄμετρος πολλότης → πεπερασμένη μορφοποίησις → στοιχειακὴ συγγένεια καὶ ἀσυμμετρία.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:FGS-B01","CL_JP8E_13:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:FGS-B02","text":"Γεωμετρικὴ διαφορά → ἀνομοιότης → δύναμις, ἀντίστασις, πίεσις καὶ κυκλοφορία.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:FGS-B01","CL_JP8E_14:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:FGS-B03","text":"Πληρότης τοῦ κόσμου → διαπερατὰ διάκενα → εἴσοδος ἢ ἀντίστασις → λύσις, τήξις, στερεοποίησις ἢ νέα μεῖξις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:FGS-B02","CL_JP8E_15:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:FGS-B04","text":"Σωματικὴ ἀναδιάταξις → μετάδοσις πάθους → αἴσθησις καὶ βίωμα → ἀναθεώρησις τῆς ὀνοματοθεσίας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:FGS-B05","CL_JP8E_16:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:FGS-B05","text":"Σχεσιακὸς τόπος → πολλαπλαὶ φυσικαὶ φοραί → ἀναίρεσις ἀπολύτων κατευθύνσεων → ἑνότης διὰ διαφορικῶν δικτύων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:FGS-B05","CL_JP8E_16:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:FGS-B06","text":"Μηχανικὴ ἀνάγκη → εἰκαστικὴ συμπλήρωσις → διαβαθμισμένη γνώσις, ἣ διατηρεῖ τὴν γενετικὴν δομὴν ἀνοικτήν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:FGS-B06","CL_JP8E_15:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AFM-B01","text":"Ἄτακτος πολλότης → γεωμετρικὴ διάκρισις → πεπερασμένα γένη καὶ συγγένειαι.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:AFM-B01","CL_JP8E_13:AFM-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AFM-B02","text":"Πεπερασμένα γένη → ἀνισότης καὶ ἀνομοιότης → ἀντιμαχία, διάλυσις, διαφυγὴ καὶ ἀνασύνθεσις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:AFM-B02","CL_JP8E_14:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AFM-B03","text":"Ἀνασύνθεσις → διαπερατότης καὶ πίεσις → πάθη, αἰσθηταὶ ποιότητες καὶ λειτουργικὰ ὀνόματα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:AFM-B02","CL_JP8E_15:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AFM-B04","text":"Πάθος καὶ μετάδοσις → αἴσθησις ἢ ἀναισθησία → ἐνδοκοσμικὴ φρόνησις καὶ ἔλεγχος αἰτίας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:AFM-B01","CL_JP8E_16:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AFM-B05","text":"Σφαιρικὴ ἑνότης → πολλαπλοὶ συγγενεῖς τόποι → σχεσιακὴ ἀναθεώρησις βάρους, κουφότητος, ἄνω καὶ κάτω.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:AFM-B02","CL_JP8E_16:AFM-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AFM-B06","text":"Σχεσιακὴ φυσικὴ → ἀπόρριψις ἀπολύτων → ἀνοικτὸς καὶ διαβαθμισμένος λόγος περὶ ἑνὸς δυναμικῶς συνεχομένου κόσμου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:AFM-B06","CL_JP8E_13:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MME-01","text":"Ἡ ὕλη ἐπαναταξινομεῖται ἀπὸ ὑπόστρωμα εἰς πλέγμα μορφοποιημένων, διαπερατῶν καὶ αἰτιακῶς ἀλληλεξαρτωμένων συστάσεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:MME-01","CL_JP8E_15:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MME-02","text":"Ἡ διάκρισις μικροκλίμακος καὶ μακροκλίμακος παύει νὰ εἶναι ἀπόλυτος, διότι ἡ μικροδομὴ ἐνεργεῖ ὡς μηχανισμὸς κοσμικῆς κινήσεως καὶ βιώματος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:MME-02","CL_JP8E_16:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MME-03","text":"Ἡ ἀντίθεσις σταθερότητος καὶ μεταβολῆς καταρρέει, διότι ἡ σταθερὰ μορφὴ εἶναι πρόσκαιρος ῥύθμισις ἐντὸς κυκλοφορίας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:BSD-07","CL_JP8E_14:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MME-04","text":"Ὁ τόπος μετασχηματίζεται ἀπὸ ἀδρανὲς πλαίσιον εἰς παραγόμενον πεδίον φυσικῶν φορῶν, συγγενειῶν καὶ ἀντιστάσεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:MME-04","CL_JP8E_16:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MME-05","text":"Ἡ αἴσθησις καθίσταται ἰδία βαθμίς τῆς φυσικῆς, ἣ οὔτε ἀποχωρίζεται τοῦ σώματος οὔτε ταυτίζεται πρὸς τὴν ἡδονὴν καὶ τὴν λύπην.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:MME-04","CL_JP8E_16:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MME-06","text":"Ἡ ἀνάγνωσις τῶν ὀνομάτων γίνεται μέθοδος ἀνασυστάσεως τῶν μηχανισμῶν, οὐχὶ ἀποδοχὴ φαινομενικῶν κατηγοριῶν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:MME-07","CL_JP8E_16:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:ALT-B01","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ ἐπιφανειακοῦ ἐπιπέδου, θερμόν, ψυχρόν, σκληρόν, μαλακόν, βαρύ, κοῦφον, τήξις, ῥοὴ καὶ αἴσθησις ἐμφανίζονται ὡς προβλήματα φυσικῆς ἐξηγήσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:ALT-B01","CL_JP8E_14:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:ALT-B02","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ δομικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, μορφή, ἀναλογία, διάκενα, πίεσις, συγγένεια, ἀντίστασις καὶ μετάδοσις συγκροτοῦσι τὴν μηχανὴν τῶν φαινομένων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:ALT-B02","CL_JP8E_16:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:ALT-B03","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ μεταστρατηγικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, ὁ λόγος μετακινείται ἀπὸ τὴν περιγραφὴν ἀντικειμένων καὶ ὀνομάτων εἰς τὴν ἀνακατασκευήν μηχανισμῶν καὶ μεταβάσεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:ALT-B03","CL_JP8E_15:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:ALT-B04","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ ὀντολογικοῦ καὶ χρονικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, τὸ εἶναι εἶναι πρόσκαιρος ἰσορροπία ἐν κυκλοφορίᾳ λύσεως, ἀνασυνθέσεως καὶ ἐπανόδου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:ALT-B04","CL_JP8E_15:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:ALT-B05","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ μεθοδολογικοῦ καὶ ἐπιτελεστικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, ἡ ἀκολουθία τοῦ λόγου μιμεῖται τὴν γενετικὴν ἀκολουθίαν τῶν πραγμάτων καὶ ἀναγκάζεται εἰς ἐπανόδους.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:RLE-05","CL_JP8E_15:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:FL-01","text":"Ἡ γεωμετρικὴ ἀνάγκη ἐνεργεῖ ὡς ἐσωτερικὸς μηχανισμὸς, ἀλλὰ ἡ τελικὴ ἑνότης ἐξακολουθεῖ νὰ ἀπαιτῇ ὑπερβατικώτερον ὅρον διατάξεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:FL-01","CL_JP8E_14:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:FL-02","text":"Ἡ πληρότης τοῦ κόσμου καὶ ἡ διείσδυσις τῶν στοιχείων συνιστῶσι παραγωγικὴν ἔντασιν, διότι τὸ διάκενον δὲν δύναται νὰ σημαίνῃ κενὸν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:FL-01","CL_JP8E_15:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:FL-03","text":"Ἡ σφαιρικὴ καθολικότης καὶ ἡ πολλαπλότης ἀντιθετικῶν φυσικῶν φορῶν δὲν συμφιλιώνονται πλήρως, ἀλλὰ ἀναγκάζουσι τὸν τόπον νὰ νοηθῇ σχεσιακῶς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:FL-01","CL_JP8E_16:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:FL-04","text":"Ἡ ἀκρίβεια τῆς μορφολογικῆς ἐξηγήσεως καὶ ἡ ἀδυναμία πλήρους μεταβάσεως εἰς φρόνησιν ἀφήνουσι ἀσθενῆ τὸν δεσμὸν μικροδομῆς καὶ γνώσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:FL-02","CL_JP8E_16:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MP-01","text":"Ἡ γεωμετρία μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ περιγραφὴν εἰς ἐνεργὸν αἰτίαν, καὶ ἀπὸ αἰτίαν εἰς γενετικὴν λογικὴν ὅλου δικτύου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:MP-03","CL_JP8E_14:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MP-02","text":"Ἡ σχετικότης μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ σχέσιν σωματικῶν μορίων εἰς σχέσιν τόπου, φορᾶς, αἰσθήσεως, φρονήσεως καὶ ὀνόματος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:MP-01","CL_JP8E_16:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MP-03","text":"Ἡ τάξις μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ τελικὴ σταθερότητα εἰς συνεχή ἐπιτέλεσιν, ἣ χρειάζεται τὴν ἀνωμαλίαν ποὺ φαίνεται ἀρχικῶς νὰ ἀπειλῇ αὐτήν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:FL-01","CL_JP8E_14:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:HSI-01","text":"Ἡ πραγματικότης εἶναι πλήρης καὶ σφαιρικὴ μηχανὴ μορφοποιήσεως, πιέσεως, κυκλοφορίας, διαπερατότητος καὶ ἀνασυστάσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:HSI-01","CL_JP8E_16:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:HSI-02","text":"Τὸ σῶμα εἶναι πρόσκαιρος κόμβος, ὅπου ἡ ταυτότης, ἡ ποιότης, ἡ αἴσθησις καὶ ἡ δύναμις ἀποτελοῦσι διαφορετικὰς ὄψεις τῆς αὐτῆς διατάξεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:HSI-02","CL_JP8E_15:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:HSI-03","text":"Ὁ χρόνος εἶναι σπειροειδὴς κυκλοφορία, ἐν ᾗ ἡ ἐπάνοδος δὲν ἀποκαθιστᾷ τὸ αὐτό, ἀλλὰ παράγει νέα φάσιν συστάσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:HSI-03","CL_JP8E_13:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:HSI-04","text":"Τὸ ὑποκείμενον δὲν δύναται νὰ σταθῇ ἐκτὸς τοῦ μηχανισμοῦ, ἀλλὰ δύναται νὰ μετατοπίζῃ σχέσεις διὰ ἀναγνώσεως, διαφυγῆς, ἀφομοιώσεως καὶ νέας συνθέσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:HSI-04","CL_JP8E_16:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:HSI-05","text":"Ὁ κόσμος ὑπονοεῖται ὡς μηχανὴ ἧς ἡ ἑνότης δὲν ἐξαλείφει τὴν ἑτερότητα, ἀλλὰ τὴν μετατρέπει εἰς ὅρον τῆς συνεχείας της.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:HSI-01","CL_JP8E_13:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MRT-B01","text":"Τὰ ἀξιώματα τῆς μορφολογικῆς αἰτιότητος, τῆς παραγωγικῆς διαφορᾶς, τῆς ἀπουσίας κενοῦ, τῆς σχεσιακῆς ταυτότητος καὶ τῆς διαβαθμισμένης γνώσεως ἀρκοῦσι πρὸς ἀνακατασκευὴν τῆς κεντρικῆς μηχανῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:MRT-B01","CL_JP8E_15:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MRT-B02","text":"Ἡ ἀρχιτεκτονικὴ εἶναι συνεκτικωτάτη ἐκεῖ ὅπου μορφή, ἀνομοιότης, πίεσις, εἴσοδος, λύσις καὶ ἀνασύνθεσις ἐνεργοῦσιν ὡς μία γενετικὴ ἀκολουθία.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:MRT-B03","CL_JP8E_14:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MRT-B03","text":"Ὑποκαθορισμένα μένουσιν ἡ ἀρχὴ τῶν στοιχειωδῶν μορφῶν, ὁ μηχανισμὸς τῆς μεταβάσεως εἰς φρόνησιν, καὶ ὁ ἀποκλειστικὸς λόγος τῆς τελικῆς κοσμικῆς ἑνότητος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:MRT-B02","CL_JP8E_16:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MRT-B04","text":"Ἡ ἀνακατασκευή διασῴζει τὴν πραγματικὴν κίνησιν μόνον ἐὰν διατηρῇ τὴν σπειροειδῆ ἐπάνοδον ἀπὸ τὸ φαινόμενον εἰς τὸν μηχανισμόν καὶ ἀπὸ τὸν μηχανισμὸν εἰς τὴν ὀνοματοθεσίαν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:MRT-B04","CL_JP8E_13:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MRT-B05","text":"Ἀντιστέκεται εἰς πλήρη ἀξιωματικὴν μείωσιν ἡ ἀνάγκη τῆς διαδοχῆς, τῆς εἰκαστικῆς συμπληρώσεως καὶ τῆς ἀποφατικῆς ἀναστολῆς, αἵτινες οὐκ εἰσὶν ἐλλείψεις ἔξωθεν τοῦ συστήματος ἀλλὰ ὅροι τῆς λειτουργίας του.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:MRT-B04","CL_JP8E_15:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261014T233401638572Z_23dcd50e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Jp8E L1 04","source_sha256":"2331d2e65dde6f516d2e5854589c7a8f90c34fa1db6a53791816f45d0cc5682e","sha256":"486dbfbcc63c1b5333c8e933c9259464feca1399818b6579149bf6ad5d3f9e35","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-14T23:34:01.641369+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AS-B01","text":"The system seeks to explain how multiplicity becomes cosmos, without genesis ending in formless flux or immobile order.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AS-B02","text":"Geometry is transformed from a principle of static formation into a mechanism of relations that produces motion, resistance, mixture, affection, and sensation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:GA-01","CL_JP8E_16:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AS-B03","text":"Order does not precede change, but is preserved through finite differentiations, pressures, dissolutions, and new compositions.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:GA-01","CL_JP8E_14:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AS-B04","text":"The absence of void renders the cosmos universally interwoven, so that every local entry, displacement, or force rearranges a network of relations.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:GA-07","CL_JP8E_15:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AS-B05","text":"The explanation ascends from bodies to affections and sensation, and returns to the correction of names through the mechanisms that produce them.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:GA-09","CL_JP8E_16:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AS-B06","text":"The final synthesis is a unified but non-homogeneous cosmos, in which the precision of relations coexists with limits of demonstration and partially open transitions.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:GA-12","CL_JP8E_15:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Bodies are not self-subsistent substances, but temporary compositions of form, number, density, motion, and relation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:GA-01","CL_JP8E_14:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-02","text":"[Mutating] Geometrical form ceases to be merely the substrate of kind and becomes the cause of motion, power, affection, and natural direction.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:GA-03","CL_JP8E_16:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Difference, inequality, and dissimilarity are the productive conditions of motion, conflict, and new composition.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:GA-03","CL_JP8E_14:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] The absence of void compels change to be reciprocal, pressurizing, locally transmissive, and universally circular.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:GA-07","CL_JP8E_15:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Identity is maintained not through immobility, but through regulated recomposition, affinity, entry, and resistance.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:GA-11","CL_JP8E_15:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Causality is distributed across scales: form produces motion, motion transmission, transmission affection, and affection can become sensation and naming.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:GA-03","CL_JP8E_16:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Place is not an empty container, but a network of kindred directions, transmissions, resistances, and redistributions.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:GA-08","CL_JP8E_16:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] The order of the cosmos is dynamic preservation, which is sustained only because it reproduces the very presuppositions of difference.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:XD-08","CL_JP8E_14:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Naming is a secondary judgment, which must follow natural structure and not replace the cause.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:GA-06","CL_JP8E_16:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Universal unity does not entail one absolute system of places, directions, or qualities, but consists of many relative systems within a spherical order.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:GA-10","CL_JP8E_16:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Explanation necessarily remains graded, because the generative mechanism can be reconstructed more precisely than the highest principles and the details of intelligence.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:GA-12","CL_JP8E_16:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:RMD-01","text":"Motion retains its generative function, but shifts from cosmogonic separation to conflict, entry, transmission of affection, and natural direction.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:RMD-02","CL_JP8E_16:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:RMD-02","text":"Geometry mutates from an ontological criterion of bodies into a dynamic grammar of inequality and finally into an indirect model of sensation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:RMD-07","CL_JP8E_14:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:RMD-03","text":"Identity is absorbed by relation: from the temporary unity of recomposable elements it becomes a node of pressures, places, times, and transmissions.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:RMD-05","CL_JP8E_15:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:RMD-04","text":"The absence of void remains a fixed exclusion, but its function expands from cosmic circulation to microphysical penetrability and sensory transmission.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:CGD-04","CL_JP8E_15:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:RMD-05","text":"Affinity is transformed from a condition of limited change into a principle of topological direction, assimilation, and distribution.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:GA-05","CL_JP8E_14:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:RMD-06","text":"The common name initially performs an empirical function, but is finally subordinated to the scrutiny of the network of form, motion, and cause.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:RMD-06","CL_JP8E_16:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:CGD-01","text":"Precise constructions of triangles, solids, bases, and numerical proportions remain lower-level technical instruments.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:CGD-02","CL_JP8E_16:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:CGD-02","text":"Morphological causality is scaled up, because it connects the microstructure of bodies with cosmic motion, sensation, and linguistic judgment.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:CGD-03","CL_JP8E_16:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:CGD-03","text":"The absence of void, penetrability, and replenishment bridge the cosmic theory of circulation with the theory of melting, transmission, and affection.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:CGD-04","CL_JP8E_15:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:CGD-04","text":"The annulment of absolute above and below becomes a catalyst for a more general relativization of qualities, places, and names.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:CGD-04","CL_JP8E_16:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:CGD-05","text":"External divine ordering is recoded as internal coherence of relations, but is not wholly eliminated as a condition of supreme grounding.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:CGD-05","CL_JP8E_13:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GNX-01","text":"Formless, immobile, and self-sufficient matter is rejected, because all matter already exists in formation, difference, and relation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:GNX-02","CL_JP8E_15:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GNX-02","text":"Change as generation from nothing or disorderly flux is rejected, because change signifies dissolution, entry, conflict, and recomposition of relations.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:GNX-02","CL_JP8E_15:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GNX-03","text":"Absolute sameness is rejected, since without dissimilarity there is no power, transmission, natural direction, or sensation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:GNX-03","CL_JP8E_16:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GNX-04","text":"Place as an empty and universal framework of orientation is rejected, because place exists only through affinity, pressure, and direction.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:GNX-04","CL_JP8E_16:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GNX-05","text":"The identification of name, quality, affection, and pleasure as one indivisible datum is rejected.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:GNX-03","CL_JP8E_16:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:GNX-06","text":"The identification of mathematical possibility, physical existence, and complete demonstration is rejected.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:GNX-06","CL_JP8E_15:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that things preexist processes, because body, quality, and identity are results of generative relations.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_15:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that a single principle can explain nature, because causality is distributed among form, number, motion, place, pressure, and transmission.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:DBN-02","CL_JP8E_15:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that external contact suffices for change, because entering or resisting is determined by internal arrangement.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:DBN-03","CL_JP8E_15:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:DBN-04","text":"The hypothesis that experience automatically provides its own cause must be excluded, because recognition presupposes the mediation of bodies and intelligence.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_16:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that nature can be expounded simultaneously and equally at every scale.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:DBN-04","CL_JP8E_15:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSA-01","text":"The deepest absent possibility is a cosmos in which unity would mean homogeneity, and difference the pure dissolution of order.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:BSA-05","CL_JP8E_16:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSA-02","text":"Absent is a universal map that would define place, quality, affection, and meaning absolutely without the relations that produce them.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:BSA-05","CL_JP8E_16:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSA-03","text":"Complete and simultaneous knowledge of the whole is absent, because the whole operates as a succession of transformations and not as a present immobile object.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:BSA-03","CL_JP8E_15:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSA-04","text":"Exit into an absolute outside is prohibited, because every escape is a transition into another composition, power, and relation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:HSI-04","CL_JP8E_16:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:XD-01","text":"Finite geometrical formation and the circular reproduction of differences combine into a cosmos in which form does not limit motion but determines the modes of its production.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-08","CL_JP8E_13:GA-09","CL_JP8E_14:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:XD-02","text":"The absence of void and the penetrability of interstices constitute, rather than a contradiction, a mechanism whereby fullness makes entry causally constrained.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-05","CL_JP8E_15:FL-01","CL_JP8E_15:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:XD-03","text":"The transition from elemental motion to sensation compels causality to become hierarchical transmission and not mere local impact.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-07","CL_JP8E_15:XD-05","CL_JP8E_16:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:XD-04","text":"The annulment of absolute place and the annulment of self-subsistent quality constitute one and the same relational principle.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-10","CL_JP8E_16:XD-03","CL_JP8E_16:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:XD-05","text":"The necessary reconstruction of relations and the conjectural character of the ultimate details define knowledge that is precise with respect to mechanisms, but not sovereign with respect to every foundation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_04:GA-11","CL_JP8E_15:XD-08","CL_JP8E_13:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSD-01","text":"Reality becomes a finite generative topology, in which beings are nodes of changing relations and not independent units.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:BSD-01","CL_JP8E_15:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSD-02","text":"Time becomes an internal mode of matter, because it signifies dissolution, aging, circulation, displacement, and restoration of compositions.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:BSD-03","CL_JP8E_15:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSD-03","text":"The ontology of the body extends into a theory of the sentient being, so that the subject becomes an intracosmic node of transmission and recognition.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:BSD-06","CL_JP8E_16:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSD-04","text":"Science is transformed from a classification of things into a reconstruction of transitions, while language becomes a field for scrutinizing explanatory levels.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:BSD-05","CL_JP8E_16:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:BSD-05","text":"The unity of the cosmos is not universal uniformity, but the capacity of a network to connect asymmetrical kinds, local directions, and distinct affections.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:BSD-02","CL_JP8E_16:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:RLE-01","text":"Form → dissimilarity → power → conflict → dissolution → new form; the loop transforms geometry into a self-reproducing physical mechanism.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:RLE-01","CL_JP8E_15:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:RLE-02","text":"Fullness → replenishment → pressure → entry → rearrangement → new fullness; the absence of void acts as a loop of universal interdependence.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:RLE-04","CL_JP8E_15:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:RLE-03","text":"Affection of body → transmission or isolation → sensation or insensibility → examination of cause → new determination of affection.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:RLE-03","CL_JP8E_16:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:RLE-04","text":"Empirical name → mechanistic reduction → relational definition → correction of name; language returns to itself already transformed.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:RLE-01","CL_JP8E_16:AFM-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:RLE-05","text":"The principal attractor is the principle that order is preserved only when it contains, determines, and reproduces the difference that it excludes as disorderly.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:RLE-07","CL_JP8E_14:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:FGS-B01","text":"Unmeasured multiplicity → finite formation → elemental affinity and asymmetry.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:FGS-B01","CL_JP8E_13:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:FGS-B02","text":"Geometrical difference → dissimilarity → power, resistance, pressure, and circulation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:FGS-B01","CL_JP8E_14:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:FGS-B03","text":"Fullness of the cosmos → penetrable interstices → entry or resistance → dissolution, melting, solidification, or new mixture.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:FGS-B02","CL_JP8E_15:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:FGS-B04","text":"Bodily rearrangement → transmission of affection → sensation and experience → revision of naming.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:FGS-B05","CL_JP8E_16:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:FGS-B05","text":"Relational place → multiple natural directions → abolition of absolute directions → unity through differential networks.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:FGS-B05","CL_JP8E_16:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:FGS-B06","text":"Mechanical necessity → conjectural supplementation → graded knowledge, which keeps the generative structure open.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:FGS-B06","CL_JP8E_15:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AFM-B01","text":"Disorderly multiplicity → geometrical distinction → finite kinds and affinities.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:AFM-B01","CL_JP8E_13:AFM-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AFM-B02","text":"Finite kinds → inequality and dissimilarity → conflict, dissolution, escape, and recomposition.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:AFM-B02","CL_JP8E_14:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AFM-B03","text":"Recomposition → penetrability and pressure → affections, sensible qualities, and functional names.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:AFM-B02","CL_JP8E_15:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AFM-B04","text":"Affection and transmission → sensation or insensibility → intracosmic intelligence and examination of cause.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:AFM-B01","CL_JP8E_16:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AFM-B05","text":"Spherical unity → multiple kindred places → relational revision of weight, lightness, above, and below.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:AFM-B02","CL_JP8E_16:AFM-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:AFM-B06","text":"Relational physics → rejection of absolutes → open and graded discourse concerning one dynamically continuous cosmos.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:AFM-B06","CL_JP8E_13:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MME-01","text":"Matter is reclassified from substrate into a network of formed, penetrable, and causally interdependent compositions.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:MME-01","CL_JP8E_15:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MME-02","text":"The distinction between micro-scale and macro-scale ceases to be absolute, because microstructure operates as a mechanism of cosmic motion and experience.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:MME-02","CL_JP8E_16:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MME-03","text":"The opposition between stability and change collapses, because stable form is a temporary regulation within circulation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:BSD-07","CL_JP8E_14:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MME-04","text":"Place is transformed from an inert framework into a produced field of natural directions, affinities, and resistances.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:MME-04","CL_JP8E_16:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MME-05","text":"Sensation becomes a proper level of physics, which neither separates from the body nor identifies with pleasure and pain.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:MME-04","CL_JP8E_16:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MME-06","text":"The reading of names becomes a method of reconstructing mechanisms, not an acceptance of apparent categories.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:MME-07","CL_JP8E_16:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, hot, cold, hard, soft, heavy, light, melting, flux, and sensation appear as problems of physical explanation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:ALT-B01","CL_JP8E_14:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, form, proportion, interstices, pressure, affinity, resistance, and transmission constitute the mechanism of phenomena.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:ALT-B02","CL_JP8E_16:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, discourse shifts from the description of objects and names to the reconstruction of mechanisms and transitions.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:ALT-B03","CL_JP8E_15:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, being is a temporary equilibrium in a circulation of dissolution, recomposition, and return.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:ALT-B04","CL_JP8E_15:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the sequence of discourse imitates the generative sequence of things and is compelled into returns.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:RLE-05","CL_JP8E_15:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:FL-01","text":"Geometrical necessity operates as an internal mechanism, but final unity continues to require a more transcendent condition of arrangement.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:FL-01","CL_JP8E_14:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:FL-02","text":"The fullness of the cosmos and the penetration of elements constitute a productive tension, because the interstice cannot signify void.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:FL-01","CL_JP8E_15:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:FL-03","text":"Spherical universality and the multiplicity of opposing natural directions are not fully reconciled, but compel place to be conceived relationally.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:FL-01","CL_JP8E_16:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:FL-04","text":"The precision of morphological explanation and the inability fully to transition to intelligence leave weak the bond between microstructure and knowledge.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:FL-02","CL_JP8E_16:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MP-01","text":"Geometry mutates from description into active cause, and from cause into the generative logic of an entire network.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:MP-03","CL_JP8E_14:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MP-02","text":"Relativity mutates from a relation of bodily parts into a relation of place, direction, sensation, intelligence, and name.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:MP-01","CL_JP8E_16:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MP-03","text":"Order mutates from final stability into continuous enactment, which requires the irregularity that initially appears to threaten it.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:FL-01","CL_JP8E_14:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:HSI-01","text":"Reality is a full and spherical mechanism of formation, pressure, circulation, penetrability, and recomposition.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:HSI-01","CL_JP8E_16:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:HSI-02","text":"The body is a temporary node, where identity, quality, sensation, and power constitute different aspects of the same arrangement.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:HSI-02","CL_JP8E_15:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:HSI-03","text":"Time is spiral circulation, in which return does not restore the same thing, but produces a new phase of composition.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:HSI-03","CL_JP8E_13:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:HSI-04","text":"The subject cannot stand outside the mechanism, but can displace relations through recognition, escape, assimilation, and new composition.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:HSI-04","CL_JP8E_16:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:HSI-05","text":"The cosmos is implied as a mechanism whose unity does not eliminate otherness, but transforms it into a condition of its continuity.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:HSI-01","CL_JP8E_13:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms of morphological causality, productive difference, the absence of void, relational identity, and graded knowledge suffice for reconstruction of the central mechanism.","refs":["CL_JP8E_14:MRT-B01","CL_JP8E_15:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is most coherent where form, dissimilarity, pressure, entry, dissolution, and recomposition operate as one generative sequence.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:MRT-B03","CL_JP8E_14:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MRT-B03","text":"The origin of elementary forms, the mechanism of transition to intelligence, and the exclusive rationale of final cosmic unity remain underdetermined.","refs":["CL_JP8E_13:MRT-B02","CL_JP8E_16:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MRT-B04","text":"The reconstruction preserves real movement only if it retains the spiral return from phenomenon to mechanism and from mechanism to naming.","refs":["CL_JP8E_16:MRT-B04","CL_JP8E_13:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_04:MRT-B05","text":"Resisting complete axiomatic reduction is the necessity of succession, conjectural supplementation, and apophatic suspension, which are not deficiencies external to the system but conditions of its operation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_15:MRT-B04","CL_JP8E_15:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_JP8E_L1_04@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_JP8E_L1_04:v1","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Jp8E L1 04"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Timaeus","title":"Jp8E L1 04","context":"Chapters 37–48 · Section 53 · Section 54 · Section 55 · Section 56 · Section 57 · Section 58 · Section 59 · Section 60 · Section 61 · Section 62 · Section 63 · Section 64","original_title":"Jp8E L1 04","original_context":"Chapters 37–48 · Section 53 · Section 54 · Section 55 · Section 56 · Section 57 · Section 58 · Section 59 · Section 60 · Section 61 · Section 62 · Section 63 · Section 64"},"original_title":"Jp8E L1 04","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05","layer":"MC","title":"Jp8E L1 05","order":5,"parents":["CL_JP8E_17","CL_JP8E_18","CL_JP8E_19","CL_JP8E_20"],"revision":"MC_JP8E_L1_05@R001","sha256":"e9ccdad7e6079807ce7c7b9577c0e3e5975bf12f4d7d33bc12ff18eebbc71a9c","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B01","text":"Τὸ σύστημα ζητεῖ νὰ ἐξηγήσῃ πῶς τὰ αἰσθητὰ, τὰ ψυχικὰ καὶ τὰ σωματικὰ φαινόμενα γίγνεται ἐκ σχέσεων καὶ μεταβολῶν, οὐκ ἐκ αὐτοτελῶν ποιοτήτων ἢ οὐσιῶν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:GA-01","CL_JP8E_18:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B02","text":"Ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς σχεσιακῆς γενέσεως κλιμακοῦται εἰς ἀρχὴν ἱεραρχικῆς τάξεως, καθ᾽ ἣν αἱ διαφοραὶ οὐκ ἐξαλείφονται ἀλλὰ διανέμονται κατὰ λειτουργίαν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GA-05","CL_JP8E_20:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B03","text":"Τὸ σῶμα γίνεται ὁ γενικὸς τόπος ὅπου κίνησις, πάθος, σημεῖον, ὄργανον, ἠθικὴ ῥύθμισις καὶ νοερὸς σκοπὸς συνδέονται.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:GA-02","CL_JP8E_19:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B04","text":"Ἡ ἀνάγκη οὔτε ἀναιρεῖται οὔτε κυριαρχεῖ τελικῶς, ἀλλὰ γίνεται ὕλη, ὄργανον καὶ ὅριον προνοητικῆς συνθέσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GA-04","CL_JP8E_20:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B05","text":"Ἡ ἀλήθεια δὲν δίδοται δι᾽ ἀμέσου παρουσίας, ἀλλὰ διὰ μεταγραφῶν σώματος, παθῶν, σημείων, ἑρμηνείας καὶ κανόνων κρίσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:GA-11","CL_JP8E_19:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B06","text":"Ἡ ἀτέλεια μεταβάλλεται ἀπὸ στέρησιν εἰς παραγωγικὸν ὅρον, διότι ἡ ἀδυναμία, ἡ θνητότης καὶ ἡ ἀνεπάρκεια γεννῶσι μεσολαβήσεις, περιορισμοὺς καὶ νέας δυνάμεις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:GA-10","CL_JP8E_20:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B07","text":"Ὁ χρόνος παύει νὰ εἶναι ἐξωτερικὸν μέτρον, διότι ρυθμὸς, καθυστέρησις, ἐπιστροφή καὶ μέλλουσα χρῆσις συγκροτοῦσι τὴν αἰτίαν τῆς παρούσης μορφῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:GA-05","CL_JP8E_20:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B08","text":"Ἡ κατάληξις εἶναι ἀνοικτὴ συνεκτικὴ μηχανή: ἡ τάξις διατηρεῖται μόνον διὰ συνεχοῦς συνθέσεως, ἑρμηνείας, μετριασμοῦ καὶ ἐπανιδρύσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GA-12","CL_JP8E_19:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Τὸ φαινόμενον παράγεται ἐκ σχέσεως κινήσεων, σωμάτων, ὀργάνων καὶ μέτρων, οὐκ ἐκ αὐτοτελοῦς ποιότητος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Τὸ σῶμα εἶναι ἐνεργὸς μετασχηματιστὴς, μεταφραστὴς καὶ ῥυθμιστὴς τῶν δυνάμεων αἳ εἰσέρχονται εἰς αὐτό.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:GA-02","CL_JP8E_19:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Ἡ σχέσις μεταβάλλεται ἀπὸ αἰσθητικὴν ἀντίδρασιν εἰς κοσμικὴν, ψυχικὴν καὶ ἀνατομικὴν ἀρχιτεκτονικήν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:GA-10","CL_JP8E_18:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Ἡ ἑνότης οὐκ ἔστιν ὁμοιογένεια, ἀλλὰ ἱεραρχημένη σύνθεσις διαφορῶν τόπων, βαθμῶν καὶ λειτουργιῶν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GA-05","CL_JP8E_20:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Ἡ ἀνάγκη εἶναι ἀναγκαία συναιτία, ἀλλὰ στερεῖται τελικῆς κυριότητος ἐφ᾽ ὅσον ὑπάγεται εἰς ὀργάνωσιν καὶ σκοπόν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GA-04","CL_JP8E_20:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Ἡ μεσολάβησις εἶναι ὁ καθολικὸς νόμος τῆς κυβερνήσεως, τῆς γνῶσεως καὶ τῆς ζωτικῆς συνοχῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GA-06","CL_JP8E_19:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-07","text":"[Attractor] Ἡ τάξις παράγεται διὰ διοικήσεως τῆς συγκρούσεως καὶ οὐ διὰ τῆς ἐξαλείψεως τῶν ἀντιθέσεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GA-08","CL_JP8E_20:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Τὸ ὄργανον μεταβάλλεται ἀπὸ τοπικὸν μέρος εἰς κόμβον μεταδόσεως, σημάνσεως, πειθαρχίας καὶ ἱεραρχικῆς ἐπικοινωνίας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:GA-04","CL_JP8E_19:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Ἡ ἀλήθεια εἶναι κατανεμημένη διαδικασία, ἐν ᾗ πάθος, σημεῖον, ὄργανον καὶ ἑρμηνευτὴς οὐ δύνανται νὰ συγχωνευθῶσιν εἰς μίαν ἄμεσον κρίσιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:GA-06","CL_JP8E_19:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Ἡ θνητὴ ὕπαρξις διατηρεῖται διὰ ἀντισταθμίσεως, περιορισμοῦ καὶ ἐλεγχόμενης ἀνεπαρκείας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:GA-02","CL_JP8E_19:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Ὁ χρόνος γίνεται αἰτιακὴ διάστασις, καθ᾽ ἣν ὁ ρυθμὸς μορφοῖ τὸ παρόν καὶ ἡ μέλλουσα χρῆσις προκατασκευάζει τὴν μορφήν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:GA-05","CL_JP8E_20:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Ἡ τελεία αὐτάρκεια τοῦ σώματος, τῆς γνώσεως καὶ τῆς τάξεως ἀποκλείεται· ἀντὶ ταύτης ὑπάρχει διαρκὴς ἐπανίδρυσις μερικῶς ἐλεγχόμενων σχέσεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GA-12","CL_JP8E_20:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RMD-01","text":"Ἡ κίνησις ἐπαναλαμβάνεται ἐν αἰσθήσει, κράσει καὶ ἀνατομίᾳ, ἀλλὰ μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ τοπικὴν μετάδοσιν εἰς καθολικὴν παραγωγὴν μορφῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:BAS-05","CL_JP8E_19:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RMD-02","text":"Ἡ κράσις μεταναστεύει ἀπὸ φυσικὴν ἐξήγησιν εἰς σχήμα κοσμήσεως, καὶ ἐκεῖθεν εἰς ἱεραρχικὴν διοίκησιν ψυχῆς καὶ σώματος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GA-03","CL_JP8E_18:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RMD-03","text":"Ἡ ἀνάγκη διατηρεῖ τὴν αἰτιακὴν ἐνέργειαν, ἀλλὰ ἀναστρέφει τὴν ἀξίαν αὐτῆς, γιγνομένη πόρος καὶ ὑπηρεσία τῆς τάξεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:RMD-03","CL_JP8E_18:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RMD-04","text":"Ἡ ἀφροσύνη μετατρέπεται ἀπὸ στέρησιν κρίσεως εἰς ἀτελῆ φορέα σημείων, ἀλλὰ δὲν γίνεται αὐτόνομος πηγή ἀληθείας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:RMD-04","CL_JP8E_19:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RMD-05","text":"Ἡ ἀνεπάρκεια μεταβάλλεται ἀπὸ ἔλλειψιν εἰς γενετικὴν δύναμιν, διότι ἡ στέρησις ἑνὸς ἀγαθοῦ καθιστᾷ δυνατὴν ἑτέραν λειτουργίαν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:GA-02","CL_JP8E_20:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RMD-06","text":"Ἡ προστασία ἀποσπᾶται ἀπὸ τὸ ἐξωτερικὸν περίβλημα καὶ γίνεται ἐσωτερικὴ ἀρχὴ διαφορᾶς, περιορισμοῦ καὶ λειτουργικῆς διαβαθμίσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:RMD-01","CL_JP8E_20:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RMD-07","text":"Ἡ ἀβεβαιότης δὲν ἐξαλείφεται, ἀλλὰ ἀπορροφᾶται εἰς ἑρμηνευτικὸν κανόνα, ὅστις διατηρεῖ τὴν διάκρισιν μεταξὺ ὑλικοῦ σημείου καὶ ἀληθοῦς κρίσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:RMD-07","CL_JP8E_19:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RMD-08","text":"Ἡ ἀρχικὴ διάκρισις νοῦ καὶ σώματος καταργεῖται ὡς ἀπόλυτος χωρισμός καὶ διασῴζεται ὡς ἱεραρχημένη μετάφρασις μεταξὺ ἀοράτου καὶ ὑλικοῦ.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:RMD-08","CL_JP8E_19:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:CGD-01","text":"Αἱ τοπικαὶ διακρίσεις γεύσεων, χρωμάτων, ἥπατος, ὀστέων καὶ δερμάτων παραμένουσιν ὑπηρετικὰ παραδείγματα, ἐφ᾽ ὅσον δὲν ἐκτίθενται ὡς καθολικὰ σχήματα σχέσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:CGD-01","CL_JP8E_20:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:CGD-02","text":"Ἡ σχεσιακὴ γένεσις κλιμακοῦται εἰς καθολικὴν ἀρχήν μόνον ὅταν συνδέῃ αἴσθησιν, κοσμικὴν τάξιν, ψυχικὴν ἱεραρχίαν καὶ σωματικὴν κατασκευήν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:CGD-03","CL_JP8E_19:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:CGD-03","text":"Ἡ τελεολογία παύει νὰ εἶναι τοπικὴ προσθήκη, ὅταν ἐξηγῇ τὴν ὑπαγωγὴν φυσικῶν συναιτίων, σωματικῶν περιορισμῶν καὶ μελλοντικῶν χρειῶν εἰς κοινὸν τέλος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:XD-02","CL_JP8E_20:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:CGD-04","text":"Ἡ μαντικὴ λειτουργία γίνεται γέφυρα τοῦ συστήματος μόνον ὅταν ἀναγνωσθῇ ὡς ὑπόδειγμα διαμεσολαβημένης ἀληθείας καὶ οὐχὶ ὡς μεμονωμένη θεία ἀποκάλυψις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:CGD-03","CL_JP8E_19:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:CGD-05","text":"Ἡ ἀντισταθμιστικὴ κατασκευή γίνεται καταλύτης μεταβάσεως, διότι μεταφέρει τὸ σῶμα ἀπὸ τὴν μηχανικὴ περιγραφήν εἰς ἀξιακὴν ἱεράρχησιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:XD-01","CL_JP8E_20:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:CGD-06","text":"Ἡ πλήρης ποσολογικὴ ἀκρίβεια ὑποχωρεῖ ὡς τελικὸ κριτήριον, ἀλλὰ ἡ ἀναλογία παραμένει ἀναγκαία ὡς ὅριον καὶ μέσον τῆς ἀνακατασκευῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:RMD-06","CL_JP8E_19:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GNX-01","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ αὐτοτελὴς ποιότης, διότι πᾶν αἰσθητὸν καὶ πᾶσα λειτουργία ἐξαρτᾶται ἀπὸ κίνησιν, σχέσιν καὶ ὄργανον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:GNX-01","CL_JP8E_18:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GNX-02","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ μονόδρομος καὶ αὐτάρκης αἰτιότης, διότι ἡ γένεσις ἀπαιτεῖ σύμπραξιν, ἀντίδρασιν, μετάδοσιν καὶ διαβάθμισιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GNX-02","CL_JP8E_17:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GNX-03","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ ἄμεσος κοινωνία νοῦ, σώματος, ἀλόγου καὶ ἀληθείας, διότι ἡ τάξις λειτουργεῖ μόνον διὰ ἐνδιάμεσων μηχανῶν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:GNX-01","CL_JP8E_18:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GNX-04","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ ἁρμονία ὡς ἀπουσία συγκρούσεως, διότι ἡ συνοχὴ παράγεται διὰ χαλιναγωγήσεως καὶ ἀντισταθμίσεως τῶν ἀντιμαχομένων δυνάμεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GNX-05","CL_JP8E_20:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GNX-05","text":"Ἀποκλείεται τὸ ὁμοιογενὲς καὶ ἀδιαβάθμητον σῶμα, διότι ἡ διαφοροποίησις εἶναι ὁ ὅρος τῆς κινήσεως, τῆς αἰσθήσεως καὶ τῆς φρονήσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:GNX-02","CL_JP8E_20:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GNX-06","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ πλήρης αὐτάρκεια τοῦ θνητοῦ, διότι πᾶσα διατήρησις ἀπαιτεῖ φθοράν, περιορισμόν, ἀναβολὴν καὶ συνεχεῖς διορθώσεις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:BSA-03","CL_JP8E_20:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:DBN-01","text":"Εἰ ἡ ὕλη ἢ ἡ ἀνάγκη ἦν αὐτάρκης ἀρχή, οὔτε ἡ ἱεραρχία οὔτε ἡ τελικὴ σύνθεσις θὰ εἶχον ἀναγκαῖον ἔργον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_20:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:DBN-02","text":"Εἰ ἡ ψυχὴ ἦν ὁμογενὴς καὶ ὁ νοῦς ἄμεσος, ἡ σωματικὴ μεσολάβησις, τὸ σημεῖον καὶ ὁ ἑρμηνευτὴς θὰ ἦσαν περιττά.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_19:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:DBN-03","text":"Εἰ ἡ ἀνεπάρκεια ἦν μόνον κακόν, οὐδεμία ἀντιστάθμισις θὰ ἠδύνατο νὰ ἐξηγήσῃ τὴν γένεσιν φρονήσεως, κινήσεως καὶ αἰσθήσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_20:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:DBN-04","text":"Εἰ ἡ φυσιολογία ἐγγυᾶτο αὐτομάτως τὴν ἀλήθειαν, ἡ διάκρισις μεταξὺ σημείου, ὑποθέσεως, κρίσεως καὶ θείας κυρώσεως θὰ κατέρρεεν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:DBN-05","CL_JP8E_19:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSA-01","text":"Ἀδύνατος καθίσταται κόσμος ἐν ᾧ ἡ ἀλήθεια εἴη καθαρὰ παρουσία ἀνεξάρτητος σώματος, πάθους, σημείου καὶ ἑρμηνείας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:BSA-01","CL_JP8E_19:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSA-02","text":"Ἀδύνατος καθίσταται κόσμος ἐν ᾧ ἡ ἑνότης ἐγγίγνεται ἄνευ ἀσυμμετρίας, μεσολαβήσεως καὶ διοικήσεως τῶν κατωτέρων δυνάμεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:BSA-03","CL_JP8E_18:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSA-03","text":"Ἀδύνατος καθίσταται κόσμος ἐν ᾧ τὸ θνητὸν σῶμα ἅμα κτᾶται ἀπόλυτον ἰσχύν, ἀσφάλειαν, αἴσθησιν, φρόνησιν καὶ ἀφθαρσίαν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:BSA-01","CL_JP8E_20:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSA-04","text":"Τὸ σύστημα κυκλοῖ τὴν ἀπουσίαν ἀμέσου ἀρχῆς, διότι πᾶσα κυριότης ὀφείλει νὰ ἐνσαρκωθῇ εἰς ὄργανα, ὅρια καὶ διαδικασίας μεταγραφῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:BSA-04","CL_JP8E_19:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:XD-01","text":"Ἡ αἴσθησις, ἡ κοσμικὴ τάξις καὶ ἡ σωματικὴ μορφὴ εἰσὶν τρεῖς κλίμακες τοῦ αὐτοῦ μηχανισμοῦ, καθ᾽ ὃν ἡ σχέσις παράγει φαινόμενον διὰ διαφορᾶς καὶ μεσολαβήσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-06","CL_JP8E_17:GA-01","CL_JP8E_18:GA-03","CL_JP8E_20:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:XD-02","text":"Ἡ προνοητικὴ τάξις δὲν ἀντικαθιστᾷ τὴν φυσικὴν αἰτιότητα, ἀλλὰ μετατρέπει τὰ φυσικὰ πάθη εἰς ἐντεταγμένα μέσα διατηρήσεως καὶ κυβερνήσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-07","CL_JP8E_18:GA-04","CL_JP8E_20:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:XD-03","text":"Ἡ ἐρμηνεία καὶ ἡ ὀργανικὴ ῥύθμισις ἀποτελοῦσιν ὁμόλογα ἔργα, διότι ἀμφότεραι μετατρέπουσιν ἀδιαμόρφωτον πάθος εἰς λειτουργικῶς περιορισμένην τάξιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-09","CL_JP8E_19:XD-07","CL_JP8E_19:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:XD-04","text":"Ἡ ἀτέλεια γίνεται ὁ κοινὸς μηχανισμὸς τῆς γνώσεως καὶ τῆς ζωῆς, διότι ἡ ἀδυναμία κρίσεως γεννᾷ ἑρμηνείαν, ἐνῶ ἡ ὑλικὴ ἀδυναμία γεννᾷ ἀντιστάθμισιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-10","CL_JP8E_19:BSD-05","CL_JP8E_20:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:XD-05","text":"Ἡ ἱεραρχία εἶναι ὁ τρόπος καθ᾽ ὃν ἡ ἑνότης διατηρεῖ τὴν διαφοράν δίχως νὰ παραδίδεται εἰς αὐτονομίαν τῶν μερῶν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-07","CL_JP8E_18:XD-06","CL_JP8E_19:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:XD-06","text":"Ἡ μελλοντικὴ χρῆσις καὶ ὁ ἐρμηνευτικὸς χρόνος συγκροτοῦσιν κοινὴν ἀναδρομικὴν αἰτιότητα, καθ᾽ ἣν τὸ μὴ παρὸν μορφοῖ τὴν παροῦσαν διάταξιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-11","CL_JP8E_19:BSD-06","CL_JP8E_20:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:XD-07","text":"Ἡ ἀξίωσις προνοητικῆς τάξεως καὶ ἡ ἐπιμονὴ τῆς φθορᾶς συγκροτοῦσιν παραγωγικὴν ἔντασιν, διότι ἡ πρόνοια φαίνεται ὡς ῥύθμισις θνητότητος καὶ οὐχὶ ὡς ἄρνησις αὐτῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-10","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-12","CL_JP8E_19:FL-02","CL_JP8E_19:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSD-01","text":"Ἡ πραγματικότης ἀνασυντίθεται ὡς πολυεπίπεδον δίκτυον μεταφράσεων, ἐν ᾧ τὸ αἰσθητόν, τὸ ψυχικόν, τὸ ἠθικὸν καὶ τὸ σωματικὸν εἰσὶν ἀμοιβαῖαι φάσεις γενέσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:HSI-01","CL_JP8E_19:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSD-02","text":"Ἡ γνώσις ἀνασχηματίζεται ὡς τέχνη ἀνακατασκευῆς καὶ κρίσεως σχέσεων, οὐχὶ ὡς κατοχὴ αὐτοφανοῦς περιεχομένου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:BSD-02","CL_JP8E_19:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSD-03","text":"Ἡ πολιτικὴ καὶ ἠθικὴ τάξις ἐνσωματοῦται, διότι ἡ διακυβέρνησις τῶν παθῶν καὶ ἡ κατανομή τῶν ὀργάνων εἰσὶν ὁμόλογες ἐκφάνσεις μιᾶς ἱεραρχικῆς μηχανῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:BSD-05","CL_JP8E_19:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSD-04","text":"Ἡ θνητότης παύει νὰ σημαίνῃ ἁπλῶς ἔλλειψιν καὶ γίνεται ὁ τόπος ὅπου προκύπτουσιν ἀνάγκη ἐρμηνείας, ὀργανικὴ παιδεία καὶ προνοητικὴ ἀναστολή.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:XD-08","CL_JP8E_19:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSD-05","text":"Ἡ ἐλευθερία δὲν εἶναι ἀπαλλαγὴ ἀπὸ ὅρους, ἀλλὰ ὀρθὴ πλοήγησις διὰ μεσολαβήσεων, περιορισμῶν καὶ διαβαθμίσεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:HSI-04","CL_JP8E_20:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSD-06","text":"Ἡ τελεολογία γίνεται ἀναδρομικὴ λογικὴ, διότι ἡ μελλοντικὴ χρεία ἐνεργεῖ ἐν τῇ παρούσῃ κατασκευῇ χωρὶς νὰ ἐξαλείφῃ τὰ φυσικὰ αἴτια.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:BSD-02","CL_JP8E_20:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RLE-01","text":"Ποιότης → μηχανικὴ ἀνάλυσις → σχέσις → νέον φαινόμενον· ἡ ἐξήγησις ἐπαναχαράσσει τὸ ἐξηγούμενον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RLE-02","text":"Μίξις → διαφορά → σύνθεσις → ἱεράρχησις → νέα διαίρεσις· ἡ ἑνότης παράγει τὴν πολλαπλότητα ἣν πάλιν διοικεῖ.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RLE-03","text":"Θεία διάταξις → σωματικὴ μετάφρασις → πάθος καὶ σημεῖον → ἑρμηνεία → ἐπανενέργεια τῆς λογικῆς ἀρχῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:RLE-01","CL_JP8E_19:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RLE-04","text":"Προστασία → στερεότης → ἀκαμψία → μεσολάβησις → κίνησις → νέα ἀνάγκη προστασίας· ἡ λύσις μιᾶς ἀνεπαρκείας γεννᾷ τὴν ἑπομένην.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RLE-05","text":"Ἀβεβαιότης → εἰκὼς λόγος → διάκρισις → ἀναζήτησις κυρώσεως → διατήρησις τῆς ἀβεβαιότητος· ἡ ἔλλειψις βεβαιότητος γίνεται μέθοδος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:RLE-03","CL_JP8E_18:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RLE-06","text":"Ὁ ἑλκυστὴς τοῦ συστήματος εἶναι ἡ ἱεραρχικὴ μεσολάβησις, ἣ ἐπανέρχεται ὡς σχέσις, κράσις, ὄργανον, ἐρμηνεία, διοίκησις καὶ τελικὴ διάταξις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:RLE-06","CL_JP8E_19:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:FGS-B01","text":"Δύναμις ἢ ἀνάγκη προσβάλλει ὕλην, καὶ ἡ ὕλη ἀντιδρᾷ διὰ κινήσεως, κράσεως καὶ διαφορᾶς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:FGS-B01","CL_JP8E_18:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:FGS-B02","text":"Ἡ ἀντίδρασις διανέμεται εἰς ὄργανα καὶ μέρη, ὥστε τὸ πάθος νὰ γίνηται αἴσθησις, μορφή, σήμα ἢ περιορισμὸς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:FGS-B03","CL_JP8E_19:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:FGS-B03","text":"Ἡ διαφοροποιημένη λειτουργία ὑπάγεται εἰς ἱεραρχίαν, ἣ μετατρέπει τὴν πολλαπλότητα εἰς διατηρήσιμον ἑνότητα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:FGS-B04","CL_JP8E_20:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:FGS-B04","text":"Ἡ ἱεραρχία ἀπαιτεῖ μεσολαβητικὰ ὄργανα, ἐπειδὴ ἡ ἀρχὴ οὐ δύναται νὰ ἐνεργῇ ἀμέσως ἐπὶ τῶν κατωτέρων δυνάμεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GA-06","CL_JP8E_19:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:FGS-B05","text":"Ἡ μεσολάβησις παράγει σημεῖα καὶ ὅρια, καὶ τὰ σημεῖα ἀπαιτοῦσιν ἑρμηνείαν πρὸς ἀνασύστασιν λογικῆς τάξεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:FGS-B03","CL_JP8E_19:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:FGS-B06","text":"Ἡ προνοητικὴ σύνθεσις οὐκ ἀναιρεῖ τὴν θνητὴν ἀτέλειαν, ἀλλὰ μετατρέπει αὐτὴν εἰς ὅρον ῥυθμίσεως, διατηρήσεως καὶ μελλοντικῆς χρήσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:FGS-B04","CL_JP8E_20:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AFM-B01","text":"Αἰσθητὴ ποιότης → σωματικὴ κίνησις καὶ πάθος → σχεσιακὴ γένεσις φαινομένου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AFM-B02","text":"Σχεσιακὴ γένεσις → κράσις καὶ διαφορά → κοσμικὴ σύνθεσις καὶ ἱεραρχία.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:AFM-B01","CL_JP8E_18:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AFM-B03","text":"Ἱεραρχία → σωματικὴ μεσολάβησις → πάθος καὶ σημεῖον → ἑρμηνευτικὴ κρίσις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:AFM-B01","CL_JP8E_19:AFM-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AFM-B04","text":"Σωματικὴ ἀνεπάρκεια → ἀντιστάθμισις καὶ διαφοροποίησις → κίνησις, αἴσθησις καὶ φρόνησις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:AFM-B02","CL_JP8E_20:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AFM-B05","text":"Ἑρμηνεία καὶ ἀντιστάθμισις → προνοητικὴ ῥύθμισις → ἀνοικτὴ τάξις διὰ συνεχοῦς ἐπανιδρύσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:AFM-B05","CL_JP8E_20:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MME-01","text":"Τὸ πεδίον τῶν αἰσθήσεων, τῆς ψυχῆς καὶ τῆς ἀνατομίας ἀναχαράσσεται ἀπὸ κατάλογον οὐσιῶν εἰς χάρτην μεταδόσεων, σχέσεων καὶ μετασχηματισμῶν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:MME-01","CL_JP8E_19:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MME-02","text":"Ἡ διάκρισις φύσεως καὶ ἠθικῆς καταρρεῖ, διότι ἡ βιολογικὴ λειτουργία ἤδη φέρει κανόνα περιορισμοῦ, σκοποῦ καὶ διατηρήσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:BSD-02","CL_JP8E_19:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MME-03","text":"Ἡ διάκρισις ὕλης καὶ νοῦ ἀντικαθίσταται ὑπὸ διαβαθμισμένης μεταγραφῆς, ἐν ᾗ ἡ ὕλη σημαίνει καὶ ὁ νοῦς ἐνσαρκώνεται.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:MME-06","CL_JP8E_20:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MME-04","text":"Ὁ χρόνος ἀναταξινομεῖται ἀπὸ πλαίσιον γεγονότων εἰς ἐνεργὸν αἴτιον ρυθμοῦ, ἀναβολῆς, γενέσεως καὶ προνοητικῆς προδιατάξεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:MME-03","CL_JP8E_20:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MME-05","text":"Νέος τρόπος ἀναγνώσεως καθίσταται ἀναγκαῖος: ὁ ἐξεταστὴς ὀφείλει νὰ παρακολουθῇ μεσολαβήσεις, μεταβολὰς, ὅρια καὶ ἱεραρχικὰς μεταφράσεις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:MME-07","CL_JP8E_18:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:ALT-B01","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ ἐπιφανειακοῦ ἐπιπέδου, τὸ σύστημα πραγματεύεται αἰσθήσεις, χρώματα, ἧπαρ, τροφήν, μυελόν, ὀστᾶ, σάρκας, δέρμα καὶ κεφαλήν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:ALT-B01","CL_JP8E_19:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:ALT-B02","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ δομικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, τὰ φαινόμενα εἰσὶ δίκτυα κινήσεων, κράσεων, μεταδόσεων, ὀργάνων, διαφορῶν καὶ ἀντισταθμίσεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:ALT-B02","CL_JP8E_20:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:ALT-B03","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ μεταστρατηγικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, ἡ ὀρθὴ διοίκησις δὲν καταστρέφει τὰ κατώτερα μέρη, ἀλλὰ τὰ μεταβάλλει εἰς δεδεμένα καὶ λειτουργικὰ ὄργανα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:ALT-B03","CL_JP8E_18:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:ALT-B04","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ ὀντολογικοῦ καὶ χρονικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, τὸ ὂν εἶναι γεγονὸς διαρκῶς παραγομένης σχέσεως, καθ᾽ ἣν ἀνάγκη, σκοπός, φθορά καὶ μέλλον συμπλέκονται.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:ALT-B04","CL_JP8E_20:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:ALT-B05","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ μεθοδολογικοῦ καὶ ἐπιτελεστικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, ὁ λόγος μιμεῖται τὴν ἰδίαν μηχανὴν ἣν ἐξηγεῖ, συνάγων διεσπαρμένα μέρη εἰς ἱεραρχικὴν συνοχήν ἄνευ ἀπολύτου κλεισίματος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:ALT-B05","CL_JP8E_19:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:FL-01","text":"Ἡ τελεολογικὴ κυριότης ἀπαιτεῖ τὰ φυσικὰ αἴτια ὡς πραγματικῶς ἐνεργὰ, ἀλλὰ δὲν δύναται νὰ ἐξηγήσῃ πλήρως πῶς ἡ ὑποταγὴ αὐτῶν διασῴζει τὴν αὐτονομίαν τῆς μηχανικῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:FL-01","CL_JP8E_20:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:FL-02","text":"Ἡ ἀλήθεια ἀπαιτεῖ τὴν ἀδυναμίαν τοῦ πάσχοντος φορέως, ἀλλὰ ἡ ἀδυναμία αὕτη ταυτοχρόνως ἀπειλεῖ τὴν ἀξιοπιστίαν τοῦ σημείου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:FL-01","CL_JP8E_19:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:FL-03","text":"Ἡ φρόνησις τίθεται ὡς ὑψηλότερον τέλος, ἀλλὰ ἐξαρτᾶται ἀπὸ ὑλικὴν ἀδυναμίαν καὶ μειωμένην προστασίαν, ἃ δύνανται νὰ ἀπειλήσωσι τὴν ἴδιαν αὐτῆς διατήρησιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:FL-03","CL_JP8E_20:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:FL-04","text":"Ἡ ἑνότης διασῴζεται μόνον δι᾽ ἀνίσων μερῶν, ὥστε ἡ συνοχὴ τῆς ὅλης τάξεως νὰ προϋποτίθεται ἀσύμμετρον κυριότητα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:FL-02","CL_JP8E_18:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MP-01","text":"Ἡ ποιότης μεταλλάσσεται εἰς δύναμιν καὶ λειτουργίαν, ὅταν τὸ ἐρώτημα περὶ τοῦ τί ἐστιν ἀντικαθίσταται ὑπὸ τοῦ τί ποιεῖ ἐν σχέσει πρὸς ἕτερον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:MP-01","CL_JP8E_20:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MP-02","text":"Ἡ μεσολάβησις μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ τεχνικὴν ἀνάγκην μετάδοσης εἰς καθολικὸν σχήμα μεταγραφῆς μεταξὺ θείας τάξεως, ψυχῆς, σώματος καὶ ἀληθείας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:GA-04","CL_JP8E_19:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MP-03","text":"Ἡ ἔλλειψις μεταλλάσσεται εἰς θετικὴν ἀρχήν, ὅταν ἡ ἀνεπάρκεια ἑνὸς στοιχείου γίνηται προϋπόθεσις λειτουργίας ἑτέρου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:MP-01","CL_JP8E_20:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:HSI-01","text":"Ἡ πραγματικότης εἶναι μηχανὴ διαβαθμισμένων μεταδόσεων, ἐν ᾗ πᾶσα δύναμις λαμβάνει μορφὴν μόνον διὰ ἀντιστάσεως, μέτρου καὶ ὀργάνου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:HSI-01","CL_JP8E_20:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:HSI-02","text":"Τὸ ὑποκείμενον εἶναι ἱεραρχημένος κόμβος λόγου, πάθους, ἀλόγου καὶ σώματος, οὔτε αὐτοκράτωρ τῶν φαινομένων οὔτε παθητικὸς καθρέπτης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:HSI-02","CL_JP8E_19:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:HSI-03","text":"Ὁ χρόνος εἶναι ἐπιστρεπτικὴ καὶ προνοιακὴ ἀκολουθία, ἐν ᾗ ῥυθμοί, καθυστερήσεις, φθοραὶ καὶ μέλλουσαι χρεῖαι διαμορφοῦσι τὸ παρόν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:HSI-03","CL_JP8E_20:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:HSI-04","text":"Ἡ δυνατὴ διαφυγὴ εἶναι πλοήγησις καὶ ἀναδιάταξις τῶν μηχανισμῶν, οὐχὶ ἔξοδος ἐκ σώματος, ἀνάγκης ἢ μεσολαβήσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:HSI-04","CL_JP8E_20:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MRT-B01","text":"Αἱ ἀρχαὶ τῆς σχεσιακῆς γενέσεως, τῆς ἱεραρχικῆς συνθέσεως, τῆς σωματικῆς μεσολαβήσεως, τῆς προνοητικῆς ἀνάγκης καὶ τῆς παραγωγικῆς ἀτελείας ἀρκοῦσι πρὸς ἀνασύστασιν τῆς κύριας κινήσεως τοῦ συστήματος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:MRT-B01","CL_JP8E_18:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MRT-B02","text":"Ἡ ἀνασύστασις διασῴζει τὴν κίνησιν ἀπὸ τὴν αἴσθησιν πρὸς τὴν σχέσιν, ἀπὸ τὴν σχέσιν πρὸς τὴν ἱεραρχίαν, καὶ ἀπὸ τὴν ἱεραρχίαν πρὸς τὴν ἐνσώματον ἐρμηνείαν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:MRT-B02","CL_JP8E_19:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MRT-B03","text":"Ὑποκαθορισμένα μένει ὁ ἀκριβὴς μηχανισμὸς τῶν μεταδόσεων, ἡ ἀναλογία φυσιολογίας καὶ συμβόλου, καὶ ὁ τρόπος καθ᾽ ὃν ἡ τελικὴ διάνοια ἐπιλέγει μεταξὺ συγκρουομένων ἀγαθῶν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:MRT-B03","CL_JP8E_20:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MRT-B04","text":"Τὸ σύστημα εἶναι συνεκτικὸν καθ᾽ ὅσον ἡ ἀβεβαιότης, ἡ ἀναλογία καὶ ἡ ἀτέλεια νοοῦνται ὡς συστατικοὶ ὅροι καὶ οὐχὶ ὡς ἁπλᾶ κενά.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:MRT-B04","CL_JP8E_17:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MRT-B05","text":"Ἰσχυρότατον μέρος τῆς ἀρχιτεκτονικῆς εἶναι ἡ ἱεραρχικὴ μεσολάβησις· ἀσθενέστατον δὲ ἡ πλήρης μετάβασις ἀπὸ ποσοτικὴν καὶ μηχανικὴν αἰτίαν εἰς ποιοτικὴν καὶ τελεολογικὴν μορφήν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:MRT-B04","CL_JP8E_17:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261014T233432972276Z_b510a998","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Jp8E L1 05","source_sha256":"e9ccdad7e6079807ce7c7b9577c0e3e5975bf12f4d7d33bc12ff18eebbc71a9c","sha256":"4a7d716d25184ff2910e107da51602c176067d19f3a30e6e64f97780dd60b7df","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-14T23:34:32.975425+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B01","text":"The system seeks to explain how sensible, psychic, and bodily phenomena come to be from relations and changes, not from self-subsisting qualities or substances.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:GA-01","CL_JP8E_18:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B02","text":"The principle of relational generation is escalated into a principle of hierarchical order, according to which differences are not eliminated but distributed according to function.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GA-05","CL_JP8E_20:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B03","text":"The body becomes the general locus where motion, affection, sign, instrument, ethical regulation, and intellectual purpose are connected.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:GA-02","CL_JP8E_19:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B04","text":"Necessity is neither abolished nor ultimately sovereign, but becomes matter, instrument, and limit of providential composition.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GA-04","CL_JP8E_20:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B05","text":"Truth is not given through immediate presence, but through transcriptions of body, affections, signs, interpretation, and rules of judgment.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:GA-11","CL_JP8E_19:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B06","text":"Imperfection is transformed from privation into a productive condition, because weakness, mortality, and insufficiency generate mediations, limitations, and new powers.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:GA-10","CL_JP8E_20:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B07","text":"Time ceases to be an external measure, because rhythm, delay, return, and future use constitute the cause of present form.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:GA-05","CL_JP8E_20:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AS-B08","text":"The conclusion is an open coherent mechanism: order is maintained only through continuous composition, interpretation, moderation, and refoundation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GA-12","CL_JP8E_19:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] The phenomenon is produced from the relation of motions, bodies, instruments, and measures, not from a self-subsisting quality.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] The body is an active transformer, translator, and regulator of the powers that enter into it.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:GA-02","CL_JP8E_19:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Relation changes from sensory reaction into cosmic, psychic, and anatomical architecture.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:GA-10","CL_JP8E_18:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Unity is not homogeneity, but a hierarchized composition of different places, degrees, and functions.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GA-05","CL_JP8E_20:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Necessity is a necessary co-cause, but lacks final sovereignty insofar as it is subordinated to organization and purpose.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GA-04","CL_JP8E_20:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Mediation is the universal law of governance, knowledge, and vital cohesion.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GA-06","CL_JP8E_19:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-07","text":"[Attractor] Order is produced through the administration of conflict and not through the elimination of oppositions.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GA-08","CL_JP8E_20:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] The instrument changes from a local part into a node of transmission, signification, discipline, and hierarchical communication.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:GA-04","CL_JP8E_19:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Truth is a distributed process, in which affection, sign, instrument, and interpreter cannot be fused into a single immediate judgment.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:GA-06","CL_JP8E_19:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Mortal existence is maintained through compensation, limitation, and controlled insufficiency.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:GA-02","CL_JP8E_19:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Time becomes a causal dimension, in which rhythm shapes the present and future use preconstructs form.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:GA-05","CL_JP8E_20:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Complete self-sufficiency of body, knowledge, and order is excluded; in its place there is a continual refoundation of partially controlled relations.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GA-12","CL_JP8E_20:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RMD-01","text":"Motion recurs in sensation, mixture, and anatomy, but changes from local transmission into universal production of form.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:BAS-05","CL_JP8E_19:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RMD-02","text":"Mixture migrates from physical explanation into a schema of adornment, and thence into hierarchical governance of soul and body.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GA-03","CL_JP8E_18:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RMD-03","text":"Necessity retains its causal efficacy, but reverses its value, becoming a resource and service of order.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:RMD-03","CL_JP8E_18:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RMD-04","text":"Unreason is transformed from a privation of judgment into an imperfect bearer of signs, but does not become an autonomous source of truth.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:RMD-04","CL_JP8E_19:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RMD-05","text":"Insufficiency changes from deficiency into a generative power, because the privation of one good makes another function possible.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:GA-02","CL_JP8E_20:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RMD-06","text":"Protection is detached from the external covering and becomes an internal principle of difference, limitation, and functional gradation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:RMD-01","CL_JP8E_20:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RMD-07","text":"Uncertainty is not eliminated, but absorbed into an interpretive rule that preserves the distinction between material sign and true judgment.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:RMD-07","CL_JP8E_19:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RMD-08","text":"The original distinction between intellect and body is abolished as an absolute separation and preserved as a hierarchized translation between the invisible and the material.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:RMD-08","CL_JP8E_19:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:CGD-01","text":"The local distinctions of tastes, colors, liver, bones, and skins remain subordinate examples, insofar as they are not set forth as universal schemas of relation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:CGD-01","CL_JP8E_20:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:CGD-02","text":"Relational generation is escalated into a universal principle only when it connects sensation, cosmic order, psychic hierarchy, and bodily construction.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:CGD-03","CL_JP8E_19:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:CGD-03","text":"Teleology ceases to be a local addition when it explains the subordination of natural co-causes, bodily limitations, and future needs to a common end.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:XD-02","CL_JP8E_20:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:CGD-04","text":"Divinatory function becomes a bridge of the system only when it is read as a model of mediated truth and not as an isolated divine revelation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:CGD-03","CL_JP8E_19:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:CGD-05","text":"Compensatory construction becomes a catalyst of transition, because it carries the body from mechanical description into evaluative hierarchization.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:XD-01","CL_JP8E_20:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:CGD-06","text":"Complete quantitative precision recedes as a final criterion, but proportion remains necessary as the limit and means of reconstruction.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:RMD-06","CL_JP8E_19:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GNX-01","text":"Self-subsisting quality is excluded, because every sensible thing and every function depends on motion, relation, and instrument.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:GNX-01","CL_JP8E_18:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GNX-02","text":"One-way and self-sufficient causality is excluded, because generation requires cooperation, reaction, transmission, and gradation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GNX-02","CL_JP8E_17:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GNX-03","text":"Immediate communion of intellect, body, the irrational, and truth is excluded, because order operates only through intermediate mechanisms.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:GNX-01","CL_JP8E_18:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GNX-04","text":"Harmony as the absence of conflict is excluded, because cohesion is produced through the restraint and compensation of contending powers.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GNX-05","CL_JP8E_20:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GNX-05","text":"The homogeneous and ungraded body is excluded, because differentiation is the condition of motion, sensation, and practical wisdom.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:GNX-02","CL_JP8E_20:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:GNX-06","text":"The complete self-sufficiency of the mortal is excluded, because every preservation requires decay, limitation, delay, and continual corrections.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:BSA-03","CL_JP8E_20:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:DBN-01","text":"If matter or necessity were a self-sufficient principle, neither hierarchy nor final composition would have a necessary task.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_20:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:DBN-02","text":"If the soul were homogeneous and the intellect immediate, bodily mediation, the sign, and the interpreter would be superfluous.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_19:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:DBN-03","text":"If insufficiency were merely evil, no compensation could explain the generation of practical wisdom, motion, and sensation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_20:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:DBN-04","text":"If physiology automatically guaranteed truth, the distinction between sign, hypothesis, judgment, and divine ratification would collapse.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:DBN-05","CL_JP8E_19:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSA-01","text":"A world in which truth would be pure presence independent of body, affection, sign, and interpretation is rendered impossible.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:BSA-01","CL_JP8E_19:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSA-02","text":"A world in which unity comes to be without asymmetry, mediation, and governance of inferior powers is rendered impossible.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:BSA-03","CL_JP8E_18:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSA-03","text":"A world in which the mortal body simultaneously possesses absolute strength, security, sensation, practical wisdom, and incorruptibility is rendered impossible.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:BSA-01","CL_JP8E_20:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSA-04","text":"The system circles around the absence of an immediate principle, because every sovereignty must be embodied in instruments, limits, and processes of transcription.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:BSA-04","CL_JP8E_19:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:XD-01","text":"Sensation, cosmic order, and bodily form are three scales of the same mechanism, according to which relation produces phenomenon through difference and mediation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-06","CL_JP8E_17:GA-01","CL_JP8E_18:GA-03","CL_JP8E_20:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:XD-02","text":"Providential order does not replace natural causality, but transforms natural affections into ordered means of preservation and governance.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-07","CL_JP8E_18:GA-04","CL_JP8E_20:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:XD-03","text":"Interpretation and organic regulation are homologous works, because both transform unformed affection into functionally delimited order.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-09","CL_JP8E_19:XD-07","CL_JP8E_19:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:XD-04","text":"Imperfection becomes the common mechanism of knowledge and life, because weakness of judgment generates interpretation, whereas material weakness generates compensation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-10","CL_JP8E_19:BSD-05","CL_JP8E_20:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:XD-05","text":"Hierarchy is the manner in which unity preserves difference without surrendering to the autonomy of the parts.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-07","CL_JP8E_18:XD-06","CL_JP8E_19:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:XD-06","text":"Future use and interpretive time constitute a common retroactive causality, according to which what is not present shapes the present arrangement.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-11","CL_JP8E_19:BSD-06","CL_JP8E_20:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:XD-07","text":"The claim of providential order and the persistence of decay constitute a productive tension, because providence appears as regulation of mortality and not as its negation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-10","MC_JP8E_L1_05:GA-12","CL_JP8E_19:FL-02","CL_JP8E_19:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSD-01","text":"Reality is recomposed as a multilayered network of translations, in which the sensible, the psychic, the ethical, and the bodily are reciprocal phases of generation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:HSI-01","CL_JP8E_19:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSD-02","text":"Knowledge is reshaped as an art of reconstructing and judging relations, not as possession of self-evident content.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:BSD-02","CL_JP8E_19:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSD-03","text":"Political and ethical order is embodied, because the governance of affections and the distribution of instruments are homologous manifestations of one hierarchical mechanism.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:BSD-05","CL_JP8E_19:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSD-04","text":"Mortality ceases simply to signify deficiency and becomes the place where the need for interpretation, organic education, and providential suspension arise.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:XD-08","CL_JP8E_19:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSD-05","text":"Freedom is not release from conditions, but right navigation through mediations, limitations, and gradations.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:HSI-04","CL_JP8E_20:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:BSD-06","text":"Teleology becomes retroactive logic, because future need operates in present construction without eliminating natural causes.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:BSD-02","CL_JP8E_20:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RLE-01","text":"Quality → mechanical analysis → relation → new phenomenon; explanation redraws what is explained.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RLE-02","text":"Mixture → difference → composition → hierarchization → new division; unity produces the multiplicity that it in turn governs.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RLE-03","text":"Divine arrangement → bodily translation → affection and sign → interpretation → reactivation of the rational principle.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:RLE-01","CL_JP8E_19:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RLE-04","text":"Protection → solidity → rigidity → mediation → motion → new need for protection; the solution of one insufficiency generates the next.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RLE-05","text":"Uncertainty → probable account → distinction → search for ratification → preservation of uncertainty; the lack of certainty becomes method.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:RLE-03","CL_JP8E_18:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:RLE-06","text":"The attractor of the system is hierarchical mediation, which returns as relation, mixture, instrument, interpretation, governance, and final arrangement.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:RLE-06","CL_JP8E_19:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:FGS-B01","text":"Power or necessity impinges upon matter, and matter reacts through motion, mixture, and difference.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:FGS-B01","CL_JP8E_18:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:FGS-B02","text":"The reaction is distributed into instruments and parts, so that affection becomes sensation, form, signal, or limitation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:FGS-B03","CL_JP8E_19:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:FGS-B03","text":"Differentiated function is subordinated to hierarchy, which transforms multiplicity into sustainable unity.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:FGS-B04","CL_JP8E_20:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:FGS-B04","text":"Hierarchy requires mediating instruments, because the principle cannot act immediately upon inferior powers.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:GA-06","CL_JP8E_19:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:FGS-B05","text":"Mediation produces signs and limits, and signs require interpretation for the reconstitution of rational order.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:FGS-B03","CL_JP8E_19:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:FGS-B06","text":"Providential composition does not abolish mortal imperfection, but transforms it into a condition of regulation, preservation, and future use.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:FGS-B04","CL_JP8E_20:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AFM-B01","text":"Sensible quality → bodily motion and affection → relational generation of phenomenon.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AFM-B02","text":"Relational generation → mixture and difference → cosmic composition and hierarchy.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:AFM-B01","CL_JP8E_18:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AFM-B03","text":"Hierarchy → bodily mediation → affection and sign → interpretive judgment.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:AFM-B01","CL_JP8E_19:AFM-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AFM-B04","text":"Bodily insufficiency → compensation and differentiation → motion, sensation, and practical wisdom.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:AFM-B02","CL_JP8E_20:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:AFM-B05","text":"Interpretation and compensation → providential regulation → open order through continual refoundation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:AFM-B05","CL_JP8E_20:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MME-01","text":"The field of the senses, the soul, and anatomy is redrawn from a catalogue of substances into a map of transmissions, relations, and transformations.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:MME-01","CL_JP8E_19:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MME-02","text":"The distinction between nature and ethics collapses, because biological function already bears a norm of limitation, purpose, and preservation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:BSD-02","CL_JP8E_19:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MME-03","text":"The distinction between matter and intellect is replaced by graded transcription, in which matter signifies and intellect is embodied.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:MME-06","CL_JP8E_20:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MME-04","text":"Time is reclassified from a framework of events into an active cause of rhythm, delay, generation, and providential prearrangement.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:MME-03","CL_JP8E_20:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MME-05","text":"A new mode of reading becomes necessary: the investigator must follow mediations, changes, limits, and hierarchical translations.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:MME-07","CL_JP8E_18:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system treats sensations, colors, liver, nourishment, marrow, bones, flesh, skin, and head.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:ALT-B01","CL_JP8E_19:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, phenomena are networks of motions, mixtures, transmissions, instruments, differences, and compensations.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:ALT-B02","CL_JP8E_20:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, correct governance does not destroy the inferior parts, but transforms them into bound and functional instruments.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:ALT-B03","CL_JP8E_18:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, being is an event of continuously produced relation, in which necessity, purpose, decay, and the future are intertwined.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:ALT-B04","CL_JP8E_20:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, discourse imitates the very mechanism that it explains, gathering dispersed parts into hierarchical cohesion without absolute closure.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:ALT-B05","CL_JP8E_19:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:FL-01","text":"Teleological sovereignty requires natural causes as genuinely active, but cannot fully explain how their subordination preserves the autonomy of mechanics.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:FL-01","CL_JP8E_20:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:FL-02","text":"Truth requires the weakness of the affected bearer, but this weakness simultaneously threatens the reliability of the sign.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:FL-01","CL_JP8E_19:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:FL-03","text":"Practical wisdom is posited as the highest end, but depends on material weakness and diminished protection, which can threaten its own preservation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:FL-03","CL_JP8E_20:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:FL-04","text":"Unity is preserved only through unequal parts, so that the cohesion of the whole order presupposes asymmetric sovereignty.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:FL-02","CL_JP8E_18:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MP-01","text":"Quality changes into power and function when the question of what it is is replaced by that of what it does in relation to another.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:MP-01","CL_JP8E_20:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MP-02","text":"Mediation changes from a technical necessity of transmission into a universal schema of transcription between divine order, soul, body, and truth.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:GA-04","CL_JP8E_19:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MP-03","text":"Deficiency changes into a positive principle when the insufficiency of one element becomes the precondition of another's function.","refs":["CL_JP8E_20:MP-01","CL_JP8E_20:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:HSI-01","text":"Reality is a mechanism of graded transmissions, in which every power receives form only through resistance, measure, and instrument.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:HSI-01","CL_JP8E_20:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a hierarchized node of reason, affection, the irrational, and body, neither sovereign of phenomena nor a passive mirror.","refs":["CL_JP8E_18:HSI-02","CL_JP8E_19:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:HSI-03","text":"Time is a recurrent and providential sequence, in which rhythms, delays, decays, and future needs shape the present.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:HSI-03","CL_JP8E_20:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:HSI-04","text":"Possible escape is navigation and rearrangement of mechanisms, not an exit from body, necessity, or mediation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:HSI-04","CL_JP8E_20:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MRT-B01","text":"The principles of relational generation, hierarchical composition, bodily mediation, providential necessity, and productive imperfection suffice to reconstruct the system's principal movement.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:MRT-B01","CL_JP8E_18:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement from sensation to relation, from relation to hierarchy, and from hierarchy to embodied interpretation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:MRT-B02","CL_JP8E_19:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MRT-B03","text":"The precise mechanism of transmissions, the proportion between physiology and symbol, and the manner in which final intellect chooses among conflicting goods remain underdetermined.","refs":["CL_JP8E_17:MRT-B03","CL_JP8E_20:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MRT-B04","text":"The system is coherent insofar as uncertainty, proportion, and imperfection are understood as constituent conditions and not as mere gaps.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:MRT-B04","CL_JP8E_17:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_05:MRT-B05","text":"The strongest part of the architecture is hierarchical mediation; the weakest, the complete transition from quantitative and mechanical cause to qualitative and teleological form.","refs":["CL_JP8E_19:MRT-B04","CL_JP8E_17:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_JP8E_L1_05@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_JP8E_L1_05:v1","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Jp8E L1 05"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Timaeus","title":"Jp8E L1 05","context":"Chapters 49–60 · Section 65 · Section 66 · Section 67 · Section 68 · Section 69 · Section 70 · Section 71 · Section 72 · Section 73 · Section 74 · Section 75 · Section 76","original_title":"Jp8E L1 05","original_context":"Chapters 49–60 · Section 65 · Section 66 · Section 67 · Section 68 · Section 69 · Section 70 · Section 71 · Section 72 · Section 73 · Section 74 · Section 75 · Section 76"},"original_title":"Jp8E L1 05","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06","layer":"MC","title":"Jp8E L1 06","order":6,"parents":["CL_JP8E_21","CL_JP8E_22","CL_JP8E_23"],"revision":"MC_JP8E_L1_06@R001","sha256":"0cb2ea85e6c993703955636ba7a028781a04bc62990869932d408f3fbb783541","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-01","text":"Τὸ σύστημα ζητεῖ νὰ ἐξηγήσῃ πῶς τὸ θνητὸν σῶμα διαμένει μόνον διὰ μεταβολῶν, ἀναπληρώσεων καὶ ῥυθμίσεων, ἐνώ ἡ αὐτὴ κίνησις περιέχει τὴν φθορὰν αὐτοῦ.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:AS-01","CL_JP8E_22:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-02","text":"Ἡ ἐξήγησις ἀντικαθιστᾷ τὴν ἀκίνητον οὐσίαν διὰ δικτύου ῥοῶν, δεσμῶν, πόρων, θερμοτήτων καὶ ἀντιρρόπων κινήσεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:AS-02","CL_JP8E_23:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-03","text":"Ἡ τροφή, ἡ ἀναπνοή καὶ ἡ κίνησις τῆς ὕλης γίνονται κοινὸς μηχανισμὸς, ὃς ἢ ἀφομοιοῖ τὸ ἔξωθεν εἰς σῶμα ἢ ἀναστρέφεται εἰς νοσηρὰν διάλυσιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:GA-06","CL_JP8E_23:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-04","text":"Ἡ ὑγίεια δὲν ἐστὶν ἀκινησία, ἀλλὰ πρόσκαιρος λόγος ῥοῆς, ὁμοιότητος, θερμότητος καὶ διεξόδων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:GA-06","CL_JP8E_23:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-05","text":"Ἡ νόσος δὲν προσπίπτει ἔξωθεν ἐπὶ τετελεσμένον σώμα, ἀλλὰ ἐστὶν ἡ ἐσωτερικὴ ἀναστροφὴ τῆς γενέσεως, τῆς ἀφομοιώσεως καὶ τῆς κυκλοφορίας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:AS-06","CL_JP8E_23:AFM-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-06","text":"Ἡ τοπικὴ βλάβη ἀποκτᾷ καθολικὴν ἰσχύν, ὅταν ἡ διεφθαρμένη ὕλη διὰ τῶν συνδέσμων καὶ τῶν ῥοῶν ἐπανέρχηται ὡς αἰτία νέας βλάβης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:AFM-B03","CL_JP8E_23:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-07","text":"Ὁ χρόνος ἐνεργεῖ ὡς μεταβολὴ τοῦ λόγου τῶν δεσμῶν, δι’ ἧς ἡ συντήρησις μεταπίπτει εἰς γῆρας, λύσιν καὶ θάνατον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:GA-08","CL_JP8E_22:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-08","text":"Ἡ τελικὴ κατάληξις εἶναι κυκλικὴ καὶ σπειροειδής: ἡ ζωή διαμένει διὰ τῆς ἀνταποδόσεως, ἡ δὲ φθορὰ ἐπικρατεῖ ὅταν ἡ ἀνταπόδοσις γένηται κατάκλεισις καὶ αὐτοπολλαπλασιαζομένη διάλυσις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:GA-10","CL_JP8E_23:AFM-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Τὸ ζῶν σῶμα οὐκ ἔστιν ἀκίνητος οὐσία, ἀλλὰ πρόσκαιρος συνοχὴ ῥοῶν, δεσμῶν, μεταβολῶν καὶ διανομῶν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:GA-04","CL_JP8E_23:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Ἡ ζωή καὶ ἡ ὑγίεια διατηροῦνται μόνον διὰ συνεχούς ἀναπληρώσεως καὶ ρυθμισμένης ἀναλογίας ἐπιρροῆς καὶ ἀπορροῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:GA-03","CL_JP8E_22:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Τὸ αὐτὸ δίκτυον τροφῆς, πνεύματος, θερμότητος καὶ ῥοῆς δύναται καὶ νὰ συγκροτῇ καὶ νὰ διαλύῃ τὸ σῶμα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:GA-07","CL_JP8E_23:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Ἡ παθολογία εἶναι ἀναστροφὴ τῶν παραγωγικῶν διαδικασιῶν, καθ’ ἣν ἡ ἀφομοίωσις γίνεται διάλυσις καὶ ἡ κυκλοφορία γίνεται μετάδοσις φθορᾶς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:GA-10","CL_JP8E_23:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Ἡ φυσικὴ τάξις ὁρίζεται ὡς λόγος συγγενείας, τόπου, μέτρου, ῥοῆς καὶ ἀκεραιότητος συνδέσμων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:GA-11","CL_JP8E_23:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Ἡ σωματικὴ ταυτότης παράγεται διὰ μετασχηματισμοῦ τοῦ ἀνομοίου εἰς ὁμοιότητα, οὐ διὰ διατηρήσεως ἀμεταβλήτου πυρήνος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:GA-07","CL_JP8E_22:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Ἡ αἰτιότης εἶναι κατανεμημένη καὶ ἀναδραστική, ἐπειδὴ τὸ ἀποτέλεσμα δύναται νὰ ἐπανέλθῃ διὰ τῶν ῥοῶν ὡς αἴτιον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:XD-06","CL_JP8E_23:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] Ὁ θάνατος εἶναι λύσις δεσμῶν καὶ παῦσις τῆς λειτουργικῆς ἀνταποδόσεως, εἴτε κατὰ φυσικὴν ἐξασθένησιν εἴτε διὰ βιαίας ἀναστροφῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:GA-12","CL_JP8E_21:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Ἡ διάκρισις φυσικῆς ῥυθμίσεως καὶ παρὰ φύσιν ἀναστροφῆς ἀποφασίζει τὴν ἑρμηνείαν τῆς ζωῆς, τῆς νόσου καὶ τῆς λύσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:GA-09","CL_JP8E_22:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RMD-01","text":"Ἡ ῥοὴ διατηρεῖ τὴν ταυτότητά της ὡς ὅρος ζωῆς, ἀλλὰ μεταλλάσσεται ἐκ κυκλοφορίας ἀναπληρώσεως εἰς ὁδὸν διασπορᾶς νοσηρᾶς ὕλης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:RMD-05","CL_JP8E_23:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RMD-02","text":"Ἡ τροφὴ μετακινείται ἀπὸ ἐξωτερικὴν ὕλην πρὸς ἀφομοιωμένην συνοχὴν καὶ τέλος πρὸς διαβρωτικὸν παράγοντα, ὅταν ἀποτύχῃ ὁ μετασχηματισμός.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:RMD-02","CL_JP8E_23:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RMD-03","text":"Ἡ ἀναπνοὴ μεταβάλλεται ἀπὸ ἀντίρροπον κύκλον διανομῆς καὶ ψύξεως εἰς τόπον ἀποφράξεως, σήψεως καὶ συστημικῆς φθορᾶς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:AS-06","CL_JP8E_23:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RMD-04","text":"Ἡ ὁμοιότης μεταναστεύει ἀπὸ ἀρχὴν αἰσθητικῆς συμφωνίας εἰς μηχανισμὸν θρεπτικῆς ἀφομοιώσεως καὶ κριτήριον σωματικῆς ταυτότητος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:RMD-03","CL_JP8E_22:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RMD-05","text":"Ἡ φθορὰ μετατρέπεται ἀπὸ ἀναγκαίαν ἐσωτερικὴν κατανάλωσιν εἰς ἀναδρομικὴν μηχανὴν, ἣ τρέφεται ἐκ τῶν ἰδίων αἰτίων τῆς συγκροτήσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:RMD-01","CL_JP8E_23:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RMD-06","text":"Ἡ τάξις παύει νὰ σημαίνῃ μόνον κοσμικὴν ἢ φυσιολογικὴν κανονικότητα καὶ γίνεται ἀνατομικὴ ἀκεραιότης, ῥύθμισις διεξόδων καὶ διαγνωστικὸν μέτρον βάθους.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:GA-11","CL_JP8E_23:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:CGD-01","text":"Αἱ ἀκριβείς ταξινομίαι ψυχικῶν δυνάμεων, χολῶν, φλεγμάτων, τριγώνων καὶ ἰδιαιτέρων λίθων μένουσιν τοπικαὶ ὡς ὑποδείγματα, οὐχὶ ὡς ἀρχαὶ τοῦ ὅλου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:CGD-01","CL_JP8E_23:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:CGD-02","text":"Ἡ ῥοή, ἡ συγγένεια, ἡ ἀφομοίωσις, ὁ δεσμὸς καὶ ἡ ἀναστροφὴ κλιμακοῦνται εἰς καθολικὰς κατηγορίας, διότι διαπερῶσι φυσιολογίαν, αἴσθησιν, γῆρας καὶ νόσον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:CGD-02","CL_JP8E_22:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:CGD-03","text":"Γέφυρα τῶν μονάδων γίνεται ἡ ἀρχὴ ὅτι ἡ ὑγίεια καὶ ἡ νόσος εἰσὶ φάσεις τοῦ αὐτοῦ συστήματος μετασχηματισμῶν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:CGD-03","CL_JP8E_23:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:CGD-04","text":"Καταλύτης μεταβάσεως εἶναι ἡ μεταφορά τῆς κοσμικῆς κινητικότητος εἰς τὸ σῶμα, δι’ ἧς ἡ ἀνατομία γίνεται τοπικὴ ἐκδοχὴ καθολικῆς αἰτιότητος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:CGD-04","CL_JP8E_22:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:CGD-05","text":"Ἡ ὀνοματολογία τῶν νοσημάτων ὑπερβαίνει τὴν περιγραφὴν, ὅταν ἀκολουθῇ ὁδόν, τόπον, ποιότητα καὶ ἕξιν τῆς ὕλης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:GA-10","CL_JP8E_23:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GNX-01","text":"Ἀποκλείεται τὸ σῶμα ὡς ἀδρανὲς περίβλημα ἢ ἄθροισμα αὐτοτελῶν μερῶν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:GNX-02","CL_JP8E_23:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GNX-02","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ ταυτότης ὡς ἀμετάβλητος πυρήν, διότι ἡ ὕπαρξις συγκροτεῖται διὰ συνεχῶν ἀναπληρώσεων καὶ μεταβολῶν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:GNX-04","CL_JP8E_22:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GNX-03","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ ὑγίεια ὡς ἀκινησία, διότι ἡ διάρκειά της εἶναι ῥυθμισμένη κίνησις καὶ οὐχὶ ἀπουσία μεταβολῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:DBN-04","CL_JP8E_22:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GNX-04","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ νόσος ὡς ἁπλοῦν τοπικὸν συμβεβηκός ἢ ὄνομα τελικοῦ συμπτώματος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:GNX-02","CL_JP8E_23:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GNX-05","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ γραμμικὴ αἰτιότης, καθ’ ἣν ἐξωτερικὴ αἰτία παρὰ ἑνὶ χρόνῳ παράγει ἀμετάβλητον ἀποτέλεσμα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:GNX-04","CL_JP8E_22:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GNX-06","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ πλήρης διάκρισις φυσικοῦ, αἰσθητικοῦ, βιολογικοῦ καὶ παθολογικοῦ πεδίου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:GNX-06","CL_JP8E_22:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:DBN-01","text":"Ψευδὴς δεῖ εἶναι ἡ ὑπόθεσις ὅτι λειτουργία δύναται νὰ νοηθῇ χωρὶς ὑλικὴν, γεωμετρικὴν καὶ ἀνατομικὴν διάταξιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_21:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:DBN-02","text":"Ψευδὴς δεῖ εἶναι ἡ ὑπόθεσις ὅτι ἡ ἐξωτερικὴ ὕλη προστίθεται εἰς τὸ σῶμα ἄνευ μετασχηματισμοῦ καὶ ὁμοιώσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:DBN-03","CL_JP8E_22:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:DBN-03","text":"Ψευδὴς δεῖ εἶναι ἡ ὑπόθεσις ὅτι ἡ φθορὰ εἶναι ἑτερότης πρὸς τὴν γένεσιν, διότι ἐκ τῆς ἀναστροφῆς τῆς γενέσεως παράγεται.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:DBN-05","CL_JP8E_22:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:DBN-04","text":"Ψευδὴς δεῖ εἶναι ἡ ὑπόθεσις ὅτι αἴτιον, ὁδὸς, τόπος καὶ ἕξις τῆς ὕλης δύνανται νὰ χωρισθῶσιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:DBN-02","CL_JP8E_23:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:DBN-05","text":"Ψευδὴς δεῖ εἶναι ἡ ὑπόθεσις ὅτι ἡ ψυχὴ καὶ τὸ σῶμα ἢ ταυτίζονται ἁπλῶς ἢ χωρίζονται ἀπολύτως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:DBN-06","CL_JP8E_22:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSA-01","text":"Ἀπούσης μένει ἡ δυνατότης ζωῆς ἄνευ κυκλοφορίας, διαμεσολαβήσεως καὶ ἀνταποδόσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSA-02","text":"Ἀπούσης μένει ἡ δυνατότης ἀκινήτου κέντρου, ὃ κινεῖ τὰ πάντα χωρὶς αὐτὸ μεταβάλλεσθαι.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSA-03","text":"Ἀδύνατος ἐστὶ κόσμος ἐν ᾧ ἡ ὕλη παραμένει ἁπλῆ, ἀδρανὴς καὶ ἀνεπηρέαστος ὑπὸ τόπου, μίξεως, θερμότητος καὶ κινήσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:BSA-01","CL_JP8E_23:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSA-04","text":"Ἀδύνατος ἐστὶν ἀσφαλὴς διάκρισις μεταξὺ τοῦ θεραπεύοντος καὶ τοῦ νοσοποιοῦ μηχανισμοῦ, διότι ἡ αὐτὴ ῥοὴ δύναται νὰ γένηται ἀνάτασις ἢ διάλυσις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:BSA-04","CL_JP8E_23:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSA-05","text":"Ἀδύνατος μένει ἡ πλήρης μετάβασις ἀπὸ κινήσεις καὶ συστάσεις εἰς τὴν ποιοτικὴν ἐμπειρίαν ἡδονῆς ἢ πόνου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:BSA-04","CL_JP8E_22:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:XD-01","text":"Ἡ φυσιολογία καὶ ἡ παθολογία ἀποτελοῦσιν μίαν θεωρίαν μετασχηματισμοῦ, διότι ἡ αὐτὴ ἀναπλήρωσις ὑπὸ ὀρθὸν λόγον συντηρεῖ, ὑπὸ ἀναστροφὴν δὲ διαφθείρει.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-04","CL_JP8E_22:XD-03","CL_JP8E_23:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:XD-02","text":"Ἡ θρέψις καὶ ἡ ἀναπνοὴ εἰσὶ συμμετρικαὶ πύλαι, δι’ ὧν τὸ ἐκτὸς εἴτε μεταβάλλεται εἰς ἐσωτερικὴν ὁμοιότητα εἴτε ἐγκαθιδρύει κατάκλεισιν καὶ διάλυσιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-06","CL_JP8E_21:XD-02","CL_JP8E_23:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:XD-03","text":"Ἡ νόσος κλιμακοῦται μόνον ἐπειδὴ ἡ ἀνατομικὴ συνδεσιμότης μετατρέπει τὴν τοπικὴν ἀλλοίωσιν εἰς κυκλοφοροῦσαν καὶ αὐτοενισχυομένην αἰτίαν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-07","CL_JP8E_23:GA-06","CL_JP8E_23:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:XD-04","text":"Ὁ χρόνος δὲν προστίθεται ἐξωτερικῶς εἰς τὸ σῶμα, ἀλλὰ ἐκδηλοῖ τὴν βαθμιαίαν μεταβολὴν τοῦ λόγου μεταξὺ ἀφομοιώσεως καὶ λύσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-08","CL_JP8E_22:XD-04","CL_JP8E_22:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:XD-05","text":"Ἡ φυσικὴ τάξις δὲν εἶναι θετικῶς παροῦσα οὐσία, ἀλλὰ ὁρατὸς γίνεται διὰ τῆς ἀπουσίας διεξόδου, τοῦ σπασμοῦ τῶν δεσμῶν καὶ τῆς ἐπιστροφῆς τῆς νοσηρᾶς ὕλης.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-09","CL_JP8E_23:BSA-03","CL_JP8E_23:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:XD-06","text":"Ἡ ἐνότητα τοῦ σώματος ἀπαιτεῖ νὰ νοηθῇ ἅμα ὡς ἐσωτερικὴ μηχανὴ καὶ ὡς κοσμικὴ ἀναλογία, ἀλλὰ ἡ μετάβασις ἀπὸ τὴν κίνησιν εἰς τὴν αἴσθησιν μένει ἀνεπίλυτος.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-07","CL_JP8E_22:BSD-01","CL_JP8E_22:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSD-01","text":"Ἡ ὀντολογία τοῦ σώματος γίνεται θεωρία σχέσεων: οὐδὲν μέρος ἔχει τὴν ἰδίαν λειτουργίαν χωρὶς τὸ δίκτυον ὅπερ τὸ τρέφει, θερμαίνει, ψύχει καὶ διανέμει.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:BSD-02","CL_JP8E_23:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSD-02","text":"Ἡ αἰτιότης γίνεται γενεαλογία μεταβολῶν, ἐν ᾗ τὸ πάθημα οὐχὶ τέλος ἀλλὰ κόμβος νέας αἰτιακῆς ἀκολουθίας ἐστίν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:BSD-02","CL_JP8E_23:AFM-B13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSD-03","text":"Ἡ θνητότης ἀνασημαίνεται ὡς ὅριον διατηρήσεως λόγου καὶ συνδέσμου, οὐχὶ ὡς ἁπλὴ ἀπώλεια ὕλης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:BSD-03","CL_JP8E_22:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSD-04","text":"Ἡ διάγνωσις γίνεται ἀνάγνωσις ὁδῶν καὶ μετασχηματισμῶν, διότι τὸ ἐμφανὲς σύμπτωμα δὲν ἀρκεί πρὸς τὴν γνῶσιν τοῦ μηχανισμοῦ.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:MME-06","CL_JP8E_23:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSD-05","text":"Ἡ διαφορά μεταξὺ ζωῆς καὶ νόσου δὲν ἐστι διαφορά οὐσίας, ἀλλὰ διαφορά κατευθύνσεως, μέτρου, τόπου καὶ ἀναδράσεως τῶν αὐτῶν δυνάμεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:BSD-04","CL_JP8E_23:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RLE-01","text":"Φθορὰ → ἀνάγκη ἀναπληρώσεως → τροφή καὶ πνεῦμα → ἀφομοίωσις καὶ διανομή → ἐσωτερικὴ θερμότης → νέα φθορὰ.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:RLE-01","CL_JP8E_22:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RLE-02","text":"Ὁμοιότης → συμφωνία → σωματικὴ συνοχή → ἐξασθένησις δεσμῶν → ἀνομοιότης → λύσις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:RLE-02","CL_JP8E_22:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RLE-03","text":"Ρυθμισμένη ῥοή → ἀποφόρτισις → συντήρησις· κατάκλεισις → ἀλλοίωσις → ἐπανείσοδος νοσηρᾶς ὕλης → πολλαπλασιασμὸς βλάβης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:RLE-01","CL_JP8E_23:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RLE-04","text":"Ἡ ἀναστροφή τῆς γενέσεως εἶναι μεταβατικὸν σημεῖον, ὅπου ὁ μηχανισμὸς ὁ τρέφων τὴν ταυτότητα γίνεται φορεὺς διαλύσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:RLE-04","CL_JP8E_22:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RLE-05","text":"Ἐλκτικὸς πόλος τοῦ συστήματος ἐστὶν οὐκ ἀκίνητον κέντρον, ἀλλὰ ἡ κυκλικὴ ἀνταπόδοσις ἢ, ἐν τῇ παθολογίᾳ, ἡ σπειροειδὴς αὐτοδιαφθορά.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:RLE-06","CL_JP8E_23:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RLE-06","text":"Ἡ ἐπανάληψις δὲν δηλοῖ ταὐτότητα, ἀλλὰ μεταβολὴν φάσεως, καθ’ ἣν ἡ συντήρησις γίνεται γῆρας καὶ ἡ ῥοὴ γίνεται νόσος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:RLE-08","CL_JP8E_23:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:FGS-B01","text":"Ἐσωτερικὴ κατανάλωσις → κένωσις καὶ φθορά → ἀνάγκη ἐξωτερικῆς ὕλης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:FGS-B01","CL_JP8E_22:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:FGS-B02","text":"Ἐξωτερικὴ ὕλη → διαμεσολάβησις διὰ κοιλοτήτων, πλεγμάτων, φλεβῶν καὶ πνεύματος → ἀφομοίωσις ἢ ἀποτυχία μετασχηματισμοῦ.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:FGS-B02","CL_JP8E_23:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:FGS-B03","text":"Ὀρθὸς λόγος ῥοῆς, θερμότητος, πυκνότητος καὶ διεξόδων → προσωρινὴ σωματικὴ ταυτότης καὶ ὑγίεια.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:AFM-B04","CL_JP8E_22:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:FGS-B04","text":"Ἀπόφραξις, ἀνομοιότης ἢ ἀναστροφὴ διαδοχῆς → διεφθαρμένη ὕλη → μετάδοσις διὰ ἱστῶν καὶ αἵματος → συστημικὴ φθορά.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:FGS-B02","CL_JP8E_23:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:FGS-B05","text":"Ἡ ἐπανείσοδος τοῦ ἀποτελέσματος εἰς τὰς αἰτίας αὐτοῦ → αὐτοενίσχυσις νόσου → λύσις δεσμῶν ἢ παῦσις τῆς συστάσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:FGS-B06","CL_JP8E_22:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AFM-B01","text":"Φθορὰ τοῦ θνητοῦ → ἀνάγκη ἀναπληρώσεως → ἀνατομικὴ διαμεσολάβησις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AFM-B02","text":"Ἀνατομικὴ διαμεσολάβησις → ῥοὴ, ἀφομοίωσις καὶ ὁμοιότης → δυναμικὴ σωματικὴ ταυτότης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:FGS-B04","CL_JP8E_22:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AFM-B03","text":"Δυναμικὴ ταυτότης → χρονικὴ ἐξασθένησις δεσμῶν → γῆρας, φυσικὴ λύσις ἢ βίαιος θάνατος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:FGS-B05","CL_JP8E_22:AFM-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AFM-B04","text":"Ἀναστροφὴ ῥοῆς ἢ μίξεως → νόσος → μετάδοσις καὶ βάθυνσις τῆς βλάβης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:FGS-B06","CL_JP8E_23:AFM-B11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AFM-B05","text":"Βάθυνσις καὶ ἀναδρομὴ τῆς φθορᾶς → συστημικὴ ἀναστροφή → ἀναχαρτογράφησις τοῦ σώματος ὡς δικτύου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:AFM-B12","CL_JP8E_21:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MME-01","text":"Καταλύεται ἡ διαίρεσις ἐσωτερικοῦ καὶ ἐξωτερικοῦ, διότι τὸ ζῷον ὑπάρχει μόνον ἐν τῇ ἀνταλλαγῇ πρὸς τὸ περιβάλλον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:MME-05","CL_JP8E_21:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MME-02","text":"Καταλύεται ἡ διαίρεσις κοσμολογίας καὶ φυσιολογίας, διότι ἡ σωματικὴ κίνησις γίνεται μικρογραφία καθολικῆς τάξεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:XD-01","CL_JP8E_22:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MME-03","text":"Ἡ ὑγίεια ἀναταξινομεῖται ὡς διαρκὴς ῥύθμισις καὶ ἡ νόσος ὡς μετρήσιμος ἀπόκλισις ὁδοῦ, τόπου, ποσότητος καὶ βάθους.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:MME-04","CL_JP8E_23:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MME-04","text":"Ἡ διάγνωσις ἀναγκάζεται νὰ ζητῇ τὴν γενετικὴν ἀκολουθίαν τοῦ πάθους καὶ οὐ τὸ ὄνομα μόνον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:MME-06","CL_JP8E_23:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MME-05","text":"Ἡ ἐπιφάνεια τοῦ συμπτώματος ὑποτάσσεται εἰς τὴν ἱεραρχίαν τοῦ βάθους, ὅπου ἡ ἀνατομικὴ συνδεσιμότης καθορίζει τὴν βαρύτητα τῆς νόσου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:GA-08","CL_JP8E_23:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:ALT-B01","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ ἐπιφανειακοῦ ἐπιπέδου, τροφή, πνεῦμα, αἷμα, χυμοί, πυρετός, πόνος καὶ σπασμὸς δηλοῦσι τὰ ἐμφανῆ πάθη τοῦ σώματος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:ALT-B01","CL_JP8E_23:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:ALT-B02","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ δομικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, πλέγματα, φλέβες, ἱστοί, δεσμοί, διεξόδοι, μίξεις καὶ θερμότητες συγκροτοῦσι τὴν λειτουργικὴν τάξιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:ALT-B02","CL_JP8E_23:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:ALT-B03","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ μεταστρατηγικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, ἡ ἔρευνα μετακινείται ἀπὸ τὸ σύμπτωμα πρὸς τὴν γενεαλογίαν, τὴν ὁδὸν καὶ τὴν ἀναστροφὴν τῆς διαδικασίας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:ALT-B03","CL_JP8E_23:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:ALT-B04","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ ὀντολογικοῦ καὶ χρονικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, τὸ ὂν ἐστὶ προσωρινὴ σύστασις, ἣ ὁ χρόνος μεταβάλλει διὰ ἐπαναλήψεως, ἐξασθενήσεως καὶ λύσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:ALT-B04","CL_JP8E_23:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:ALT-B05","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ μεθοδολογικοῦ καὶ ἐπιτελεστικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, ὁ λόγος μιμεῖται τὸ ἀντικείμενον διὰ τῆς ἐπαναλήψεως, τῆς συνδέσεως, τοῦ μετασχηματισμοῦ καὶ τῆς ἀναστροφῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:ALT-B05","CL_JP8E_23:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:FL-01","text":"Ἡ θεία τελεολογία καὶ ἡ ἐσωτερικὴ μηχανικὴ συμπίπτουσι λειτουργικῶς, ἀλλὰ οὐκ ἀνάγονται εἰς μίαν ἀδιαίρετον ἀρχήν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:FL-01","CL_JP8E_21:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:FL-02","text":"Ἡ φυσικὴ τάξις ἀπαιτεῖ λόγον καὶ ἀναλογίαν, ἀλλὰ ὁ αὐτὸς χρόνος ὅστις τὴν διατηρεῖ ἐνεργεῖ καὶ τὴν ἐξασθένησιν αὐτῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:FL-04","CL_JP8E_22:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:FL-03","text":"Ἡ ἀναγωγὴ τῶν πολλῶν νοσημάτων εἰς ὀλίγους μηχανισμοὺς ἀπειλεῖ τὴν ἑτερογένειαν, ἣν ἡ διαγνωστικὴ ταξινόμησις ὀφείλει συγχρόνως νὰ διασώζῃ.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:FL-01","CL_JP8E_23:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:FL-04","text":"Ἡ ἔννοια τῆς ῥοῆς δὲν ἀντιτίθεται ἁπλῶς πρὸς τὴν πήξιν, διότι ἡ πήξις δύναται καὶ νὰ ῥυθμίζῃ καὶ νὰ κατακλείῃ.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:FL-03","CL_JP8E_23:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MP-01","text":"Ἡ «ζωή» μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ ἱεραρχίαν ψυχικῶν δυνάμεων εἰς διαρκῆ παραγωγὴν καὶ ῥύθμισιν ῥευμάτων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MP-02","text":"Ἡ «ἀναπνοή» μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ δίοδο ἀέρος εἰς καθολικὸν μηχανισμὸν διανομῆς, ψύξεως, ἀποφορτίσεως καὶ ἐνδεχομένης παθολογικῆς ἀποφράξεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:MP-02","CL_JP8E_23:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MP-03","text":"Ἡ «νόσος» μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ ἀνωμαλίαν ποιότητος εἰς αὐτοτροφοδοτούμενον κύκλον, ἐν ᾧ τὸ ἀποτέλεσμα ἐνισχύει τὴν προτέραν αἰτίαν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:MP-03","CL_JP8E_23:AFM-B13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:HSI-01","text":"Ἡ πραγματικότης νοεῖται ὡς ὑδραυλικὸν, ἀναπνευστικὸν καὶ μετασχηματιστικὸν δίκτυον, ἐν ᾧ ἡ ὕπαρξις εἶναι σχέσις ῥοῶν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:HSI-01","CL_JP8E_22:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:HSI-02","text":"Τὸ ὑποκείμενον εἶναι κόμβος καὶ τόπος διελεύσεως, οὐχὶ αὐτοτελὴς κύριος τῶν κινήσεων αὐτοῦ.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:HSI-02","CL_JP8E_23:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:HSI-03","text":"Ὁ χρόνος εἶναι κύκλος καὶ λύσις ἐν κινήσει, ἐν ᾧ ἡ διατήρησις περιέχει ἤδη τὴν δυνατότητα τῆς ἀναστροφῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:HSI-03","CL_JP8E_23:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:HSI-04","text":"Ἡ μόνη δυνατὴ πράξις ἐντὸς τοῦ συστήματος εἶναι ἡ διατήρησις ἢ ἀποκατάστασις ῥοῆς, μέτρου, διεξόδου καὶ συνδέσμου, οὐχὶ ἡ ἔξοδος ἐκ τοῦ δικτύου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:HSI-04","CL_JP8E_23:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MRT-B01","text":"Τὰ παρόντα ἀξιώματα ἀρκοῦσι πρὸς ἀνακατασκευὴν τῆς κινήσεως ἀπὸ φθορὰν καὶ ἀναπλήρωσιν πρὸς ὑγίειαν, γῆρας, νόσον καὶ λύσιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:MRT-B01","CL_JP8E_22:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MRT-B02","text":"Ἡ ἀρχιτεκτονικὴ εἶναι συνεκτικωτάτη ὅπου ἡ αἰτιότης νοεῖται ὡς ῥοή, γενετικὴ διαδοχή, ἀναλογία, ἀνατροφοδότησις καὶ ἀναστροφή.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:MRT-B03","CL_JP8E_23:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MRT-B03","text":"Ὑποκαθορισμέναι μένουσιν ἡ ἀκριβὴς σχέσις πυρός, πνεύματος καὶ ἀέρος, ἡ μετάβασις ἀπὸ φυσικὴν κίνησιν εἰς ποιοτικὴν ἐμπειρίαν, καὶ ἡ πλήρης ἱεράρχησις τῶν μίξεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:MRT-B04","CL_JP8E_22:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MRT-B04","text":"Ἡ ἀνακατασκευή διασῴζει τὴν πραγματικὴν κίνησιν μόνον ἐὰν διατηρῇ τὴν ἀναδρομὴν, καθ’ ἣν ἡ ῥύθμισις δύναται νὰ γένηται αἰτία διαφθορᾶς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:MRT-B05","CL_JP8E_23:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MRT-B05","text":"Ἀνθεκτικὸν εἰς ἀξιωματικὴν συμπίεσιν μένει ὅτι οἱ αὐτοὶ μηχανισμοὶ οὔτε καθαρῶς ζωτικοὶ οὔτε καθαρῶς νοσοποιοὶ εἰσίν, ἀλλὰ μεταβάλλουσι λειτουργίαν κατὰ λόγον, τόπον καὶ χρόνον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261014T233503500878Z_78f82312","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Jp8E L1 06","source_sha256":"0cb2ea85e6c993703955636ba7a028781a04bc62990869932d408f3fbb783541","sha256":"29a9b779cfa22340e0b1fcdc53ae67586ca9f256e883da98609b9e5886bb6604","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-14T23:35:03.502921+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-01","text":"The system seeks to explain how the mortal body persists only through changes, replenishments, and regulations, while that same movement contains its decay.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:AS-01","CL_JP8E_22:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-02","text":"The explanation replaces immobile substance with a network of flows, bonds, passages, heats, and countervailing movements.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:AS-02","CL_JP8E_23:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-03","text":"Nourishment, respiration, and the movement of matter become a common mechanism, which either assimilates what is external into body or reverses into diseased dissolution.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:GA-06","CL_JP8E_23:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-04","text":"Health is not immobility, but a temporary proportion of flow, likeness, heat, and outlets.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:GA-06","CL_JP8E_23:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-05","text":"Disease does not befall a completed body from outside, but is the internal reversal of generation, assimilation, and circulation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:AS-06","CL_JP8E_23:AFM-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-06","text":"Local damage acquires universal force when corrupted matter, through connections and flows, returns as the cause of new damage.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:AFM-B03","CL_JP8E_23:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-07","text":"Time operates as a change in the proportion of bonds, through which maintenance passes into old age, dissolution, and death.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:GA-08","CL_JP8E_22:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AS-08","text":"The final outcome is circular and spiral: life persists through reciprocity, while decay prevails when reciprocity becomes enclosure and self-multiplying dissolution.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:GA-10","CL_JP8E_23:AFM-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] The living body is not an immobile substance, but a temporary cohesion of flows, bonds, changes, and distributions.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:GA-04","CL_JP8E_23:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Life and health are maintained only through continuous replenishment and a regulated proportion of influx and absorption.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:GA-03","CL_JP8E_22:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] The same network of nourishment, pneuma, heat, and flow can both constitute and dissolve the body.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:GA-07","CL_JP8E_23:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Pathology is a reversal of productive processes, in which assimilation becomes dissolution and circulation becomes the transmission of decay.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:GA-10","CL_JP8E_23:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Natural order is defined as a proportion of kinship, place, measure, flow, and the integrity of connections.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:GA-11","CL_JP8E_23:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Bodily identity is produced through the transformation of the dissimilar into likeness, not through the preservation of an unchanging core.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:GA-07","CL_JP8E_22:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Causality is distributed and recursive, because the effect can return through the flows as a cause.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:XD-06","CL_JP8E_23:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] Death is the dissolution of bonds and cessation of functional reciprocity, whether by natural weakening or violent reversal.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:GA-12","CL_JP8E_21:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] The distinction between natural regulation and reversal contrary to nature determines the interpretation of life, disease, and dissolution.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:GA-09","CL_JP8E_22:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RMD-01","text":"Flow retains its identity as a condition of life, but mutates from a circuit of replenishment into a path for the dissemination of diseased matter.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:RMD-05","CL_JP8E_23:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RMD-02","text":"Nourishment moves from external matter to assimilated cohesion and finally to a corrosive agent when transformation fails.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:RMD-02","CL_JP8E_23:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RMD-03","text":"Respiration changes from a countervailing cycle of distribution and cooling into a site of obstruction, putrefaction, and systemic decay.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:AS-06","CL_JP8E_23:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RMD-04","text":"Likeness migrates from a principle of perceptual concord to a mechanism of nutritive assimilation and a criterion of bodily identity.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:RMD-03","CL_JP8E_22:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RMD-05","text":"Decay is transformed from necessary internal consumption into a recursive engine nourished by the very causes of constitution.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:RMD-01","CL_JP8E_23:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RMD-06","text":"Order ceases to signify only cosmic or physiological regularity and becomes anatomical integrity, regulation of outlets, and a diagnostic measure of depth.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:GA-11","CL_JP8E_23:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:CGD-01","text":"Precise classifications of psychic powers, biles, phlegms, triangles, and particular stones remain local as examples, not as principles of the whole.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:CGD-01","CL_JP8E_23:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:CGD-02","text":"Flow, kinship, assimilation, bond, and reversal are scaled up into universal categories because they traverse physiology, sensation, old age, and disease.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:CGD-02","CL_JP8E_22:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:CGD-03","text":"The bridge between the units is the principle that health and disease are phases of the same system of transformations.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:CGD-03","CL_JP8E_23:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:CGD-04","text":"The catalyst of transition is the transfer of cosmic mobility into the body, through which anatomy becomes a local version of universal causality.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:CGD-04","CL_JP8E_22:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:CGD-05","text":"The nomenclature of diseases surpasses description when it follows the path, place, quality, and condition of matter.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:GA-10","CL_JP8E_23:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GNX-01","text":"The body as an inert covering or an aggregate of self-sufficient parts is excluded.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:GNX-02","CL_JP8E_23:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GNX-02","text":"Identity as an unchanging core is excluded, because existence is constituted through continuous replenishments and changes.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:GNX-04","CL_JP8E_22:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GNX-03","text":"Health as immobility is excluded, because its duration is regulated movement and not the absence of change.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:DBN-04","CL_JP8E_22:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GNX-04","text":"Disease as a simple local accident or the name of a final symptom is excluded.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:GNX-02","CL_JP8E_23:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GNX-05","text":"Linear causality is excluded, according to which an external cause at one time produces an unchanging effect.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:GNX-04","CL_JP8E_22:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:GNX-06","text":"The complete distinction of the physical, perceptual, biological, and pathological domains is excluded.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:GNX-06","CL_JP8E_22:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:DBN-01","text":"The hypothesis that function can be conceived without material, geometrical, and anatomical arrangement must be false.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_21:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:DBN-02","text":"The hypothesis that external matter is added to the body without transformation and assimilation must be false.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:DBN-03","CL_JP8E_22:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:DBN-03","text":"The hypothesis that decay is other than generation must be false, because it is produced from the reversal of generation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:DBN-05","CL_JP8E_22:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:DBN-04","text":"The hypothesis that cause, path, place, and the condition of matter can be separated must be false.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:DBN-02","CL_JP8E_23:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:DBN-05","text":"The hypothesis that soul and body are either simply identical or absolutely separated must be false.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:DBN-06","CL_JP8E_22:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSA-01","text":"The possibility of life without circulation, mediation, and reciprocity remains absent.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSA-02","text":"The possibility of an immobile center which moves everything without itself changing remains absent.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSA-03","text":"A world in which matter remains simple, inert, and unaffected by place, mixture, heat, and movement is impossible.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:BSA-01","CL_JP8E_23:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSA-04","text":"A secure distinction between the healing and disease-producing mechanism is impossible, because the same flow can become restoration or dissolution.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:BSA-04","CL_JP8E_23:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSA-05","text":"The complete transition from movements and constitutions to the qualitative experience of pleasure or pain remains impossible.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:BSA-04","CL_JP8E_22:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:XD-01","text":"Physiology and pathology constitute one theory of transformation, because the same replenishment under a correct proportion preserves, but under reversal corrupts.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-04","CL_JP8E_22:XD-03","CL_JP8E_23:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:XD-02","text":"Nutrition and respiration are symmetrical gates through which the external either changes into internal likeness or establishes enclosure and dissolution.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-06","CL_JP8E_21:XD-02","CL_JP8E_23:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:XD-03","text":"Disease escalates only because anatomical connectivity transforms local alteration into a circulating and self-reinforcing cause.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-07","CL_JP8E_23:GA-06","CL_JP8E_23:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:XD-04","text":"Time is not externally added to the body, but manifests the gradual change in the proportion between assimilation and dissolution.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-08","CL_JP8E_22:XD-04","CL_JP8E_22:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:XD-05","text":"Natural order is not a positively present substance, but becomes visible through the absence of an outlet, the rupture of bonds, and the return of diseased matter.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-09","CL_JP8E_23:BSA-03","CL_JP8E_23:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:XD-06","text":"The unity of the body requires being conceived at once as an internal mechanism and as a cosmic analogy, but the transition from movement to sensation remains unresolved.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_06:GA-07","CL_JP8E_22:BSD-01","CL_JP8E_22:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSD-01","text":"The ontology of the body becomes a theory of relations: no part has its own function without the network that nourishes, heats, cools, and distributes it.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:BSD-02","CL_JP8E_23:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSD-02","text":"Causality becomes a genealogy of changes, in which the affection is not an end but a node of a new causal sequence.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:BSD-02","CL_JP8E_23:AFM-B13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSD-03","text":"Mortality is resignified as a limit to the preservation of proportion and bond, not as a simple loss of matter.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:BSD-03","CL_JP8E_22:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSD-04","text":"Diagnosis becomes a reading of paths and transformations, because the manifest symptom is insufficient for knowledge of the mechanism.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:MME-06","CL_JP8E_23:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:BSD-05","text":"The difference between life and disease is not a difference of substance, but a difference of direction, measure, place, and feedback of the same powers.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:BSD-04","CL_JP8E_23:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RLE-01","text":"Decay → need for replenishment → nourishment and pneuma → assimilation and distribution → internal heat → new decay.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:RLE-01","CL_JP8E_22:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RLE-02","text":"Likeness → concord → bodily cohesion → weakening of bonds → dissimilarity → dissolution.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:RLE-02","CL_JP8E_22:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RLE-03","text":"Regulated flow → discharge → maintenance; enclosure → alteration → re-entry of diseased matter → multiplication of damage.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:RLE-01","CL_JP8E_23:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RLE-04","text":"The reversal of generation is a transitional point at which the mechanism that nourishes identity becomes a bearer of dissolution.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:RLE-04","CL_JP8E_22:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RLE-05","text":"The system's attractive pole is not an immobile center, but circular reciprocity or, in pathology, spiral self-corruption.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:RLE-06","CL_JP8E_23:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:RLE-06","text":"Repetition does not signify identity, but a change of phase, in which maintenance becomes old age and flow becomes disease.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:RLE-08","CL_JP8E_23:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:FGS-B01","text":"Internal consumption → emptiness and decay → need for external matter.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:FGS-B01","CL_JP8E_22:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:FGS-B02","text":"External matter → mediation through cavities, networks, veins, and pneuma → assimilation or failure of transformation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:FGS-B02","CL_JP8E_23:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:FGS-B03","text":"Correct proportion of flow, heat, density, and outlets → temporary bodily identity and health.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:AFM-B04","CL_JP8E_22:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:FGS-B04","text":"Obstruction, dissimilarity, or reversal of succession → corrupted matter → transmission through tissues and blood → systemic decay.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:FGS-B02","CL_JP8E_23:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:FGS-B05","text":"The re-entry of the effect into its causes → self-reinforcement of disease → dissolution of bonds or cessation of constitution.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:FGS-B06","CL_JP8E_22:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AFM-B01","text":"Decay of the mortal → need for replenishment → anatomical mediation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AFM-B02","text":"Anatomical mediation → flow, assimilation, and likeness → dynamic bodily identity.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:FGS-B04","CL_JP8E_22:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AFM-B03","text":"Dynamic identity → temporal weakening of bonds → old age, natural dissolution, or violent death.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:FGS-B05","CL_JP8E_22:AFM-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AFM-B04","text":"Reversal of flow or mixture → disease → transmission and deepening of damage.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:FGS-B06","CL_JP8E_23:AFM-B11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:AFM-B05","text":"Deepening and recursion of decay → systemic reversal → remapping of the body as a network.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:AFM-B12","CL_JP8E_21:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MME-01","text":"The division between internal and external is dissolved, because the living creature exists only in exchange with its environment.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:MME-05","CL_JP8E_21:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MME-02","text":"The division between cosmology and physiology is dissolved, because bodily movement becomes a microcosm of universal order.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:XD-01","CL_JP8E_22:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MME-03","text":"Health is reclassified as continuous regulation and disease as a measurable deviation of path, place, quantity, and depth.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:MME-04","CL_JP8E_23:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MME-04","text":"Diagnosis is compelled to seek the generative sequence of the affection and not the name alone.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:MME-06","CL_JP8E_23:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MME-05","text":"The surface of the symptom is subordinated to the hierarchy of depth, in which anatomical connectivity determines the gravity of disease.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:GA-08","CL_JP8E_23:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, nourishment, pneuma, blood, humors, fever, pain, and spasm indicate the manifest affections of the body.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:ALT-B01","CL_JP8E_23:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, networks, veins, tissues, bonds, outlets, mixtures, and heats constitute functional order.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:ALT-B02","CL_JP8E_23:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, inquiry moves from the symptom toward the genealogy, path, and reversal of the process.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:ALT-B03","CL_JP8E_23:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, being is a temporary constitution, which time transforms through repetition, weakening, and dissolution.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:ALT-B04","CL_JP8E_23:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, discourse imitates the object through repetition, connection, transformation, and reversal.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:ALT-B05","CL_JP8E_23:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:FL-01","text":"Divine teleology and internal mechanics coincide functionally, but are not reducible to one indivisible principle.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:FL-01","CL_JP8E_21:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:FL-02","text":"Natural order requires proportion and analogy, but the same time that preserves it also effects its weakening.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:FL-04","CL_JP8E_22:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:FL-03","text":"The reduction of many diseases to a few mechanisms threatens the heterogeneity that diagnostic classification must simultaneously preserve.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:FL-01","CL_JP8E_23:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:FL-04","text":"The concept of flow is not simply opposed to coagulation, because coagulation can both regulate and enclose.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:FL-03","CL_JP8E_23:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MP-01","text":"“Life” mutates from a hierarchy of psychic powers into the continuous production and regulation of currents.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MP-02","text":"“Respiration” mutates from a passage of air into a universal mechanism of distribution, cooling, discharge, and possible pathological obstruction.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:MP-02","CL_JP8E_23:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MP-03","text":"“Disease” mutates from an abnormality of quality into a self-feeding cycle in which the effect reinforces the prior cause.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:MP-03","CL_JP8E_23:AFM-B13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:HSI-01","text":"Reality is conceived as a hydraulic, respiratory, and transformative network in which existence is a relation of flows.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:HSI-01","CL_JP8E_22:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a node and place of passage, not the self-sufficient master of its movements.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:HSI-02","CL_JP8E_23:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:HSI-03","text":"Time is cycle and dissolution in movement, in which preservation already contains the possibility of reversal.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:HSI-03","CL_JP8E_23:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:HSI-04","text":"The only possible action within the system is the preservation or restoration of flow, measure, outlet, and bond, not exit from the network.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:HSI-04","CL_JP8E_23:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MRT-B01","text":"The present axioms suffice to reconstruct the movement from decay and replenishment toward health, old age, disease, and dissolution.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:MRT-B01","CL_JP8E_22:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is most coherent where causality is conceived as flow, generative succession, analogy, feedback, and reversal.","refs":["CL_JP8E_22:MRT-B03","CL_JP8E_23:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MRT-B03","text":"The precise relation of fire, pneuma, and air, the transition from physical movement to qualitative experience, and the complete hierarchy of mixtures remain underdetermined.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:MRT-B04","CL_JP8E_22:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MRT-B04","text":"The reconstruction preserves the real movement only if it retains the recursion through which regulation can become a cause of corruption.","refs":["CL_JP8E_21:MRT-B05","CL_JP8E_23:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_06:MRT-B05","text":"What remains resistant to axiomatic compression is that the same mechanisms are neither purely vital nor purely disease-producing, but change function according to proportion, place, and time.","refs":["CL_JP8E_23:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_JP8E_L1_06@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_JP8E_L1_06:v1","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Jp8E L1 06"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Timaeus","title":"Jp8E L1 06","context":"Chapters 61–69 · Section 77 · Section 78 · Section 79 · Section 80 · Section 81 · Section 82 · Section 83 · Section 84 · Section 85","original_title":"Jp8E L1 06","original_context":"Chapters 61–69 · Section 77 · Section 78 · Section 79 · Section 80 · Section 81 · Section 82 · Section 83 · Section 84 · Section 85"},"original_title":"Jp8E L1 06","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07","layer":"MC","title":"Jp8E L1 07","order":7,"parents":["CL_JP8E_24","CL_JP8E_25","CL_JP8E_26"],"revision":"MC_JP8E_L1_07@R001","sha256":"e177a1d4e678ba3043cacf34fef49dfcd2d5a2015c32cf777d9d74c35a2404f0","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B01","text":"Τὸ πρόβλημα γίνεται ἡ σύλληψις τοῦ ζῴου ὡς πεδίου ἐν ᾧ ἠθικὴ παρεκτροπή, νόσος, μάθησις, μορφὴ καὶ πολιτεία συμπαράγονται.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:BAS-01","CL_JP8E_26:ARG-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B02","text":"Ἡ αἰτιότης μετακινείται ἀπὸ ἀτομικὴν βούλησιν εἰς διανεμημένον κύκλον σώματος, ψυχῆς, ἀγωγῆς, ἐπιθυμίας καὶ κοσμικῆς τάξεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:BAS-04","CL_JP8E_26:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B03","text":"Ἡ θεραπεία γίνεται ῥύθμισις καὶ μετασχηματισμὸς κινήσεων, οὐχὶ διόρθωσις ἑνὸς μεμονωμένου συμπτώματος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:BAS-07","CL_JP8E_25:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B04","text":"Ἡ γνώσις ἰσχύει μόνον ὅταν ἐπανεισέρχηται εἰς τὸν γνωρίζοντα καὶ ἀνατάσσῃ τὸν ῥυθμὸν τῆς ψυχῆς καὶ τοῦ σώματος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:AS-06","CL_JP8E_25:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B05","text":"Ἡ μορφὴ παύει νὰ εἶναι ἀδρανὴς ἐπιφάνεια καὶ γίνεται ὁρατὴ ἐγγραφή νοῦ, ἀνοίας, ἐπιθυμίας καὶ προτέρου βίου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:ARG-02","CL_JP8E_26:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B06","text":"Ἡ ἱεραρχία τῶν ὄντων διατηρεῖ τὴν ἀξιακὴν φοράν, ἀλλὰ μεταβάλλεται εἰς κύκλον μεταπτώσεων καὶ ἀνατάσεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:GA-08","CL_JP8E_26:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B07","text":"Ἡ κοσμικὴ τάξις λειτουργεῖ ἅμα ὡς πρότυπον θεραπείας καὶ ὡς ὅρος ὃς περιλαμβάνει τὰ κατώτερα δίχως νὰ ἀναιρῇ τὴν διαβάθμισιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:GA-10","CL_JP8E_26:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B08","text":"Ἡ κίνησις τοῦ συστήματος εἶναι σπειροειδής: ἀπὸ διαταραχὴν εἰς ἐξήγησιν, ἀπὸ ἐξήγησιν εἰς ἀγωγήν, ἀπὸ ἀγωγὴν εἰς κοσμικὴν ἐξομοίωσιν, καὶ ἐκεῖθεν πάλιν εἰς μεταμόρφωσιν τοῦ ἐνσώματου βίου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:AS-07","CL_JP8E_26:ARG-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Τὸ ζῷον εἶναι συναμφότερον ψυχῆς, σώματος, ἔθους καὶ περιβάλλοντος, καὶ οὐδὲν τούτων ἐνεργεῖ αὐτάρκως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:GA-01","CL_JP8E_26:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-02","text":"[Mutating] Ἡ κίνησις μεταβάλλεται ἀπὸ φυσιολογικὸν συμβεβηκὸς εἰς καθολικὸν κριτήριον ὑγείας, γνώσεως, ἀρετῆς, μορφῆς καὶ κοσμικῆς συγγενείας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:RLE-05","CL_JP8E_25:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Ἡ ἀσυμμετρία καὶ ἡ ἀταξία τῶν κινήσεων παράγουσι νόσον, ἄνοιαν, κακίαν καὶ μορφολογικὴν καθίζησιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:GA-03","CL_JP8E_26:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Ἡ θεραπεία, ἡ παιδεία καὶ ἡ φιλοσοφία συγκροτοῦσιν ἓν μηχανισμὸν ἐπανενεργοποιήσεως καὶ ἀνατάξεως τῆς ἐσωτερικῆς τάξεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:GA-07","CL_JP8E_25:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Ἡ αὐτονομία δὲν σημαίνει ἀνεξαρτησίαν, ἀλλὰ ἱκανότητα τοῦ μέρους νὰ συνεργῇ ἐντὸς προϋπαρχούσης φυσικῆς καὶ κοσμικῆς τάξεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:GA-01","CL_JP8E_25:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Ἡ γνώσις εἶναι θεραπευτικὴ μόνον ἐὰν μεταβάλῃ τὰς περιφορὰς τοῦ γνωρίζοντος καὶ ἀνοίγῃ δυνατὸτητα ἀναβάσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:GA-07","CL_JP8E_26:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Ἡ ἠθικὴ ποιότης δὲν μένει ἐσωτερικὴ κρίσις, ἀλλὰ ἐγγράφεται εἰς σῶμα, στάσιν, ἀναπνοήν, οἴκησιν καὶ εἶδος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:GA-01","CL_JP8E_26:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] Ἡ συμμετρία εἶναι δυναμικὴ ἀνταπόδοσις τῶν μερῶν κατὰ τὸ πρότυπον τοῦ παντός, οὐχὶ στατικὴ ἰσότης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:GA-09","CL_JP8E_25:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Ἡ τελείωσις δὲν συνίσταται εἰς φυγὴν ἀπὸ τὸ σῶμα ἢ εἰς ἐξάλειψιν τῶν κατωτέρων, ἀλλὰ εἰς ἐξομοίωσιν καὶ συμπλήρωσιν τοῦ ὅλου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:GA-08","CL_JP8E_26:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Ὁ κόσμος νοεῖται ὡς ἓν ἔμψυχον καὶ ὁρατὸν σύστημα, ἐν ᾧ αἱ διαβαθμίσεις τῶν μορφῶν ἀναφέρονται εἰς κοινὴν τάξιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:GA-09","CL_JP8E_26:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Ἡ ἐπιθυμία εἶναι ἀντιστασιακὴ δύναμις ἡ ὁποία, ἀντὶ νὰ μένῃ ἔξω τῆς τάξεως, ἀποτελεῖ τὸν ἐνσώματον τρόπον τῆς γενέσεως καὶ τῆς τελεολογίας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:GA-05","CL_JP8E_26:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-01","text":"Ἡ ἀλληλεξάρτησις ψυχῆς καὶ σώματος διατηρεῖται, ἀλλὰ μετακινείται ἀπὸ μετάδοσιν παθήματος εἰς παραγωγὴν μορφῆς καὶ εἴδους.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:RMD-01","CL_JP8E_26:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-02","text":"Ἡ κίνησις ἐπαναλαμβάνεται κατὰ κλίμακας σώματος, ψυχῆς, ἀγωγῆς καὶ κόσμου, ἀλλὰ ἑκάστη κλίμαξ μετασχηματίζει τὸ νόημα τῆς προηγουμένης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:RMD-01","CL_JP8E_24:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-03","text":"Ἡ συμμετρία μεταπίπτει ἀπὸ κριτήριον κάλλους εἰς τελεστὴν θεραπείας καὶ τέλος εἰς κανόνα κοσμικῆς ἐξομοιώσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:RMD-05","CL_JP8E_25:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-04","text":"Ἡ ἠθικὴ κατηγορία ἀπορροφᾶται εἰς γενεαλογίαν κρασέων, τροφῶν, παιδείας καὶ προτέρων ψυχικῶν μεταβολῶν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:RMD-03","CL_JP8E_26:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-05","text":"Ἡ θεραπεία μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ ἐπανόρθωσιν πάσχοντος σώματος εἰς ἀναδιοργάνωσιν τοῦ τρόπου καθ᾽ ὃν τὸ ὂν κινείται, γιγνώσκει καὶ κατοικεῖ.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:MP-02","CL_JP8E_25:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-06","text":"Ἡ ἱεραρχία τῶν εἰδῶν διατηρεῖ τὴν ἀξιακὴν φοράν, ἀλλὰ χάνει τὴν ἀκινησίαν της διὰ τῆς ἀμφιδρόμου μεταβολῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:RMD-02","CL_JP8E_26:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-07","text":"Ἡ ἐπιθυμία ἀπὸ μερικὸν γενετήσιον πάθος γίνεται γενικὸν πρότυπον φύσεως ἡ ὁποία ἐνεργεῖ διὰ ἐντάσεως καὶ ἀντιστάσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:RMD-03","CL_JP8E_26:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-08","text":"Ἡ κοσμολογία παύει νὰ εἶναι τελικὴ θεωρία καὶ γίνεται ἀναδρομικὸς κανὼν κρίσεως πάσης θεραπείας, παιδείας καὶ μορφολογικῆς μεταβολῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:CGD-04","CL_JP8E_25:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:CGD-01","text":"Αἱ θεωρίαι χυμῶν, στοιχείων, μήτρας, φαρμάκων καὶ ἐπιμέρους ψυχικῶν μερῶν μένουσι τοπικαὶ μηχαναί, ὅταν δὲ ἀναβαίνωσιν διασώζουσι μόνον τὸν τύπον τῆς διανεμημένης αἰτιότητος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:CGD-01","CL_JP8E_26:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:CGD-02","text":"Ἡ διαβάθμισις μέσων θεραπείας κλιμακοῦται εἰς καθολικὴν ἀρχὴν ὅτι ἡ ὀρθὴ παρέμβασις αὐξάνει τὴν ἐσωτερικὴν ἐνεργοποίησιν καὶ περιορίζει τὴν βίαν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:GA-02","CL_JP8E_25:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:CGD-03","text":"Ἡ τριμερὴς ψυχὴ καὶ αἱ εἰδικαὶ ζωικαὶ διαιρέσεις ὑποχωροῦσιν ὑπὸ τὴν καθολικωτέραν ἀρχὴν διαφοροποιημένων ῥυθμῶν καὶ μεταβάσεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:CGD-02","CL_JP8E_26:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:CGD-04","text":"Ἡ γέφυρα τῶν μονάδων εἶναι ἡ κίνησις, διότι αὕτη συνδέει φυσιολογίαν, ψυχικὴν ἀγωγήν, ἠθικὴν μεταβολὴν καὶ ζωικὴν μορφολογίαν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:CGD-03","CL_JP8E_26:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:CGD-05","text":"Ἡ κοσμικὴ μίμησις ἀναβαθμίζεται εἰς ὑπερκείμενον μέτρον, ὃ ἀνακωδικοποιεῖ τὴν ὑγείαν, τὴν παιδείαν καὶ τὴν τελείωσιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:CGD-04","CL_JP8E_25:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:CGD-06","text":"Ἡ ἀκριβὴς μεσότης τῆς κινήσεως καὶ ὁ μηχανισμὸς μεταπλάσεως ψυχῆς εἰς εἶδος μένουσιν ὑποκαθορισμένα ὡς ὅρια τῆς παρούσης ἀρχιτεκτονικῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:CGD-06","CL_JP8E_26:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-01","text":"Ἀπορρίπτεται ἡ ψυχὴ ὡς αὐτοτελὴς καὶ ἀμέτοχος κυβερνήτης τοῦ σώματος, τῶν παθῶν καὶ τῶν πράξεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:GNX-01","CL_JP8E_26:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-02","text":"Ἀπορρίπτεται ἡ κακία ὡς καθαρῶς ἑκούσιος καὶ ἀδιαμεσολάβητος ἀπόφασις ἀποκεκομμένη ἀπὸ κράσιν, τροφήν καὶ ἀγωγήν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:GNX-02","CL_JP8E_24:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-03","text":"Ἀπορρίπτεται ἡ θεραπεία ὡς ἐξωτερικὴ κυριαρχία ἢ βιαία ἀντικατάστασις τῆς ἐνεργείας τοῦ θεραπευομένου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:GNX-01","CL_JP8E_25:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-04","text":"Ἀπορρίπτεται ἡ γνώσις ἡ ὁποία μένει πληροφορία καὶ δὲν μετασχηματίζει τὸν τρόπον τοῦ γιγνώσκειν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:GNX-04","CL_JP8E_25:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-05","text":"Ἀπορρίπτεται ἡ φύσις ὡς ἀξιολογικῶς οὐδετέρα ὕλη ἢ ἄτακτος πολλαπλότης ἀνεξαρτήτων εἰδῶν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:GNX-01","CL_JP8E_26:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-06","text":"Ἀπορρίπτεται ἡ τελείωσις ὡς μονομερὴς ὑπεροχὴ ἢ ἐκκαθάρισις τῶν κατωτέρων μορφῶν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:GNX-04","CL_JP8E_26:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-07","text":"Ἀπορρίπτεται ἡ ἀκινησία ὡς σωτηρία, διότι ἡ ὑγεία προϋποτίθεται τεταγμένην, μετρίαν καὶ συνεχή κίνησιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:GNX-05","CL_JP8E_24:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:DBN-01","text":"Ψευδὴς δεῖ εἶναι πᾶσα ὑπόθεσις ὅτι ψυχή, σῶμα, πολιτεία, παιδεία καὶ κόσμος ἔχουσιν ἀνεξαρτήτους αἰτίας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_25:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:DBN-02","text":"Ψευδὴς δεῖ εἶναι πᾶσα ὑπόθεσις ὅτι ἡ θεραπεία δύναται νὰ ἐπιβάλληται ἔξωθεν ἄνευ χρόνου, μέτρου καὶ ἐσωτερικῆς μεταβολῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_25:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:DBN-03","text":"Ψευδὴς δεῖ εἶναι πᾶσα ὑπόθεσις ὅτι ἡ γνώσις μόνη ἀρκεῖ, ἐὰν μὴ συμπράττῃ πρὸς ἔξιν, κίνησιν καὶ περιβάλλον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:DBN-03","CL_JP8E_25:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:DBN-04","text":"Ψευδὴς δεῖ εἶναι πᾶσα ὑπόθεσις ὅτι ἡ μορφὴ, ἡ ἀναπνοὴ καὶ ὁ τόπος εἶναι ἐξωτερικὰ δεδομένα ἄνευ ψυχικῆς καὶ ὀντολογικῆς συνεπείας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:GNX-03","CL_JP8E_26:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:DBN-05","text":"Ψευδὴς δεῖ εἶναι πᾶσα ὑπόθεσις ὅτι τὸ κατώτατον εἶδος διαθέτει ἀνεξάρτητον νόημα ἔξω τῆς κοσμικῆς ὁλότητος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:BSA-03","CL_JP8E_26:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSA-01","text":"Ἀδύνατος καθίσταται ἡ ὕπαρξις ἀσυσχετίστου ὑποκειμένου, ὃ ἂν ἦν αὐτοκυβέρνητον καὶ ἀπρόσβλητον ὑπὸ σώματος, χρόνου καὶ κόσμου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:BSA-01","CL_JP8E_25:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSA-02","text":"Ἀδύνατος καθίσταται κόσμος ἐν ᾧ ἡ ἠθικὴ ἀξία δὲν ἐνσαρκώνεται εἰς μορφὰς, κινήσεις καὶ τόπους.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:BSA-01","CL_JP8E_26:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSA-03","text":"Ἀδύνατος καθίσταται θεραπεία ἡ ὁποία σώζει ἓν μέρος χωρὶς νὰ μεταβάλλῃ τὴν σχέσιν αὐτοῦ πρὸς τὸ ὅλον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:BSA-04","CL_JP8E_24:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSA-04","text":"Ἀδύνατος καθίσταται χρόνος καθαρῶς γραμμικὸς ἢ ἄμοιρος ἐπιστροφῆς, διότι ὁ πρότερος βίος ἐπανέρχεται ὡς ἕξις, μορφὴ καὶ ὅρος μεταβολῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:BSD-02","CL_JP8E_26:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:XD-01","text":"Ἡ θεραπεία γίνεται πολιτικὴ καὶ κοσμολογικὴ τέχνη, ἐπειδὴ ἡ διόρθωσις τῶν ἐσωτερικῶν κινήσεων ἀπαιτεῖ μεταβολὴν τῶν ὅρων οἳ παράγουσι σώματα, ἔθη καὶ μορφάς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:BSD-05","CL_JP8E_25:BSD-05","CL_JP8E_26:BSD-01","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:XD-02","text":"Ἡ αὐτοκίνησις γίνεται ὄρος ἠθικῆς ἀνατάσεως, ἀλλὰ οὐκ ἀναιρεῖ τὴν ἀνάγκην ἐξωτερικῆς ἀγωγῆς· ἡ ἐπέμβασις κρίνεται ἐκ τοῦ εἰ ἐπαναφέρει ἢ ὑποκαθιστᾷ τὴν ἐσωτερικὴν ἐνέργειαν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:XD-05","CL_JP8E_26:GA-11","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:XD-03","text":"Ἡ μορφολογία γίνεται θεραπευτικὸν καὶ γνωστικὸν πεδίον, διότι τὸ σῶμα δεικνύει τὰς ψυχικὰς περιφορὰς αἳ δύνανται νὰ μεταβληθῶσιν διὰ παιδείας καὶ θεωρίας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:BSD-06","CL_JP8E_25:BSD-02","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:XD-04","text":"Ἡ πτῶσις δὲν εἶναι ἁπλῶς ἀπώλεια, διότι συγχρόνως ἐκφράζει ἀξιακὴν καθίζησιν καὶ πληροῖ τὴν κοσμικὴν ὁλότητα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:XD-01","CL_JP8E_26:BSD-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:XD-05","text":"Ἡ ἐπιθυμία ἀποδεικνύει ὅτι ἡ τάξις οὐκ ἐνεργεῖ διὰ ἀπλῆς ὑποταγῆς, ἀλλὰ διὰ δυνάμεων αἳ ἀντιστέκονται καὶ ὅμως φέρουσι τὴν γένεσιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:XD-02","CL_JP8E_26:BSD-02","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSD-01","text":"Τὸ ὑποκείμενον γίνεται μεταβατικὸς κόμβος, ἐν ᾧ προγενέστεραι κράσεις, παιδεύσεις, ἐπιθυμίαι καὶ κοσμικαὶ σχέσεις ἐγγράφονται εἰς παροῦσαν πράξιν καὶ μορφήν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:BSD-01","CL_JP8E_26:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSD-02","text":"Ἡ ὀντολογία γίνεται ὀντολογία ῥυθμῶν καὶ μεταμορφώσεων, ὅπου τὸ εἶναι τοῦ ὄντος κρίνεται ἐκ τῆς ἱκανότητος πρὸς διατήρησιν, διόρθωσιν ἢ ἀπώλειαν τάξεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:BSD-01","CL_JP8E_26:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSD-03","text":"Ὁ χρόνος γίνεται γενεαλογικὸς καὶ σπειροειδής, διότι τὸ πρὸτερον οὐ παρέρχεται ἀλλὰ ἐπανεμφανίζεται ὡς σωματικὴ καὶ ἠθικὴ δυνατότης τοῦ ὕστερον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:BSD-02","CL_JP8E_26:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSD-04","text":"Ἡ πολιτικὴ καὶ ἡ παιδεία παύουσι νὰ εἶναι ἐξωτερικαὶ διοικήσεις καὶ γίνονται διαχείρισις τῶν συνθηκῶν ὑφ᾽ ὧν δύναται νὰ γεννηθῇ αὐτορύθμισις.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:BSD-05","CL_JP8E_25:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSD-05","text":"Ἡ τελείωσις τοῦ ὅλου δὲν καταργεῖ τὴν ἱεραρχίαν, ἀλλὰ μετατρέπει τὴν ἱεραρχίαν εἰς λειτουργικὴν συνάρτησιν διαβαθμίσεων ἐντὸς ἑνὸς κόσμου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:GA-10","CL_JP8E_26:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RLE-01","text":"Κρᾶσις καὶ ἀταξία → πάθος καὶ ἄνοια → φαινομένη κακία → ἀναγωγὴ εἰς παιδευτικὰ καὶ πολιτικὰ αἴτια → ἀνασύνταξις τοῦ πεδίου τῶν κινήσεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:RLE-01","CL_JP8E_24:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RLE-02","text":"Ἐσωτερικὴ κίνησις → αὐτονομία → ἁρμονία → ἐνίσχυσις τῆς ἱκανότητος πρὸς ἑαυτορρύθμισιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RLE-03","text":"Ψυχικὴ ποιότης → σωματικὴ μορφή → ζωικὴ βαθμίς → νέος ὁρίζων ψυχικῆς μεταβολῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RLE-04","text":"Θεωρία τοῦ κόσμου → ἐπανόρθωσις ἐσωτερικῶν περιφορῶν → ἐξομοίωσις → ἀναδρομικὴ ἐπικύρωσις τῆς θεραπείας ὡς συνεργίας πρὸς τάξιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:RLE-04","CL_JP8E_25:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RLE-05","text":"Ἐπιθυμία → γένεσις ἢ νόσος → σωματικὴ ἔνδειξις ἀνεκπληρώτου λειτουργίας → ἐπανεγγραφή τῆς τελεολογίας διὰ τῆς ὕλης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:RLE-02","CL_JP8E_26:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RLE-06","text":"Ὁ ἐλκυστὴς τοῦ συστήματος εἶναι ἡ ἐξομοίωσις ἡ ὁποία ἀξιολογεῖ τὴν κίνησιν, τὴν γνώσιν, τὴν θεραπείαν καὶ τὴν μορφολογικὴν ἀνάβασιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:RLE-05","CL_JP8E_26:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:FGS-B01","text":"Κρᾶσις, πάθος, τροφή καὶ ἀγωγή → διαμόρφωσις κινήσεων ψυχῆς καὶ σώματος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:FGS-B02","text":"Ἀσύμμετρος κίνησις → νόσος, ἄνοια καὶ διαταραχὴ κρίσεως → πράξις φαινομένη ἑκούσιος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:FGS-B02","CL_JP8E_24:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:FGS-B03","text":"Διάγνωσις τῆς ἀλληλεξαρτήσεως → ἀπόρριψις μονομεροῦς κυριαρχίας → ἡπία ἀγωγὴ καὶ ἐνεργοποίησις τοῦ θεραπευομένου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:FGS-B05","CL_JP8E_25:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:FGS-B04","text":"Ἐπανόρθωσις ῥυθμῶν → συμμετρία ψυχῆς καὶ σώματος → γνώσις ὡς ἐσωτερικὴ μεταβολή.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:XD-02","CL_JP8E_25:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:FGS-B05","text":"Κτῆσις ἢ ἀποβολὴ νοῦ → μεταβολὴ μορφῆς, κινήσεως, ἀναπνοῆς καὶ οἰκήσεως → δυναμικὴ ἱεραρχία εἰδῶν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:FGS-B05","CL_JP8E_26:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:FGS-B06","text":"Ἱεραρχικὴ πολλαπλότης → κοσμικὴ πληρότης → ἐξομοίωσις πρὸς τὴν τάξιν τοῦ ὁρατοῦ ζῴου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:FGS-B06","CL_JP8E_25:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AFM-B01","text":"Σωματικὴ καὶ ψυχικὴ ἀταξία → ἠθικὴ παρεκτροπή καὶ διανεμημένη αἰτιότης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:AFM-B01","CL_JP8E_24:AFM-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AFM-B02","text":"Διανεμημένη αἰτιότης → θεραπευτικὴ ἀντιστροφή → ἀνασύνταξις σώματος, ἔθους, παιδείας καὶ πολιτείας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:XD-06","CL_JP8E_24:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AFM-B03","text":"Ἱεράρχησις κινήσεων → προτίμησις ἡπίας αὐτορυθμίσεως → ἐπανόρθωσις τῶν ψυχικῶν περιφορῶν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:FGS-B02","CL_JP8E_25:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AFM-B04","text":"Ἐπανόρθωσις τοῦ γνωρίζοντος → κοσμικὴ θεωρία → ἐξομοίωσις καὶ ἄριστος βίος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:AFM-B04","CL_JP8E_25:AFM-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AFM-B05","text":"Ψυχικὴ πτῶσις ἢ ἀνάτασις → μορφολογικὴ καὶ ζωικὴ μεταβολή → ἱεραρχικὴ πολλαπλότης ἐντός κοσμικῆς πληρότητος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:AFM-B01","CL_JP8E_26:AFM-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MME-01","text":"Ἡ ἠθικὴ κρίσις ἀντικαθίσταται ὑπὸ γενεαλογικῆς χαρτογραφήσεως τῶν σωματικῶν, παιδευτικῶν καὶ κοσμικῶν ὅρων τῆς πράξεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:MME-01","CL_JP8E_26:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MME-02","text":"Ἡ διάκρισις φύσεως καὶ τέχνης ἀνασυντάσσεται ὡς διάκρισις συνεργοῦσης τέχνης καὶ βιαίας τέχνης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:MME-05","CL_JP8E_25:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MME-03","text":"Ἡ μορφή, τὸ φύλον, τὸ εἶδος καὶ ἡ οἴκησις γίνονται ἀναγνώσιμα ὡς ἀποτυπώματα ψυχικῆς ἱστορίας καὶ ὀντολογικῆς βαθμίδος.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:MME-01","CL_JP8E_26:ALT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MME-04","text":"Ἡ ὑγεία ἀναταξινομεῖται ὡς ἐνεργὸς τάξις σχέσεων, ἡ δὲ τελείωσις ὡς πληρότης τοῦ συνόλου καὶ οὐχὶ ὡς ὑπεροχὴ ἑνὸς μέρους.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:MME-04","CL_JP8E_26:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MME-05","text":"Ἡ κοσμολογία καθίσταται πρακτικὸν ὄργανον ἀνασχηματισμοῦ, διότι ἡ θεωρία τοῦ ὅλου ρυθμίζει τὸν τρόπον θεραπείας τοῦ μέρους.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:MME-05","CL_JP8E_25:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:ALT-B01","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ ἐπιφανειακοῦ ἐπιπέδου, νοσήματα, πάθη, τροφαί, φάρμακα, φύλα, εἴδη, ἀναπνοαὶ καὶ τόποι φαίνονται ὡς διακεκριμένα φαινόμενα.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:ALT-B01","CL_JP8E_26:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:ALT-B02","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ δομικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, τὰ φαινόμενα ταῦτα συγκροτοῦν δίκτυον ῥυθμῶν, διαβαθμίσεων, μεταδόσεων καὶ ἀμοιβαίων διαμορφώσεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:ALT-B02","CL_JP8E_26:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:ALT-B03","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ μεταστρατηγικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, ἡ ἐπέμβασις μετακινείται ἀπὸ μομφὴν καὶ κατάκτησιν εἰς ὅρους γενέσεως αὐτορυθμίσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:ALT-B03","CL_JP8E_25:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:ALT-B04","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ ὀντολογικοῦ καὶ χρονικοῦ ἐπιπέδου, τὸ ζῷον εἶναι γίγνεσθαι σχέσεων, ὅπου ὁ πρότερος βίος ἐπανέρχεται ὡς ὕστερον σῶμα καὶ δυνατότης.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:ALT-B04","CL_JP8E_26:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:ALT-B05","text":"Ἐπὶ τοῦ μεθοδολογικοῦ καὶ performative ἐπιπέδου, ἡ σύνθεσις ἐπιτελεῖ τὸ λεγόμενον, συνδέουσα κατακερματισμένα σώματα, ψυχὰς, μορφὰς καὶ κόσμον εἰς μίαν ἀρχιτεκτονικὴν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:ALT-B05","CL_JP8E_26:ALT-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:FL-01","text":"Ἡ ἀπόρριψις τῆς καθαρᾶς ἑκουσιότητος ἀποδυναμοῖ τὴν προσωπικὴν ἐνοχήν, ἀλλὰ δὲν καταργεῖ τὴν ἀνάγκην ὑπευθύνου παιδευτικῆς κατευθύνσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:FL-01","CL_JP8E_24:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:FL-02","text":"Ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς αὐτοκινήσεως συγκρούεται πρὸς τὴν ἀνάγκην ἰατρικῆς καὶ παιδαγωγικῆς ἐπεμβάσεως, ἥτις ὀφείλει νὰ ἐνεργῇ χωρίς νὰ ἀκυροῖ τὸν θεραπευόμενον.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:FL-01","CL_JP8E_25:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:FL-03","text":"Ἡ ἱεραρχία τῶν μορφῶν διατηρεῖ ἀξιακὴν σταθερότητα, ἐνῶ ἡ ἀμφίδρομος μετάβασις εἰδῶν καταλύει τὴν σταθερὰν ταυτότητα τῶν βαθμίδων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:FL-01","CL_JP8E_26:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:FL-04","text":"Ἡ τελείωσις ἀπαιτεῖ καὶ τὴν ἀνάβασιν πρὸς νοῦν καὶ τὴν διατήρησιν τῶν κατωτέρων ὡς ἀναγκαίων μερῶν τοῦ κόσμου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:FL-04","CL_JP8E_26:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MP-01","text":"Ἡ κακία μεταλλάσσεται εἰς νόσον, ἡ νόσος εἰς ἀταξίαν κινήσεων, καὶ ἡ ἀταξία εἰς γενικὴν λογικὴν κοσμικῆς καὶ μορφολογικῆς μεταβολῆς.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:MP-01","CL_JP8E_26:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MP-02","text":"Ἡ συμμετρία μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ μορφολογικὸν κριτήριον εἰς θεραπευτικὸν τελεστὴν καὶ τέλος εἰς κοσμικὸν μέτρον ἐξομοιώσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:MP-02","CL_JP8E_25:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MP-03","text":"Ἡ φύσις μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ σωματικὸν ὑπόβαθρον εἰς ἠθικο-κοσμολογικὸν μηχανισμόν, ἐν ᾧ ἡ μορφὴ καὶ ὁ χῶρος ἤδη φέρουσι κρίσιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:MP-03","CL_JP8E_26:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:HSI-01","text":"Ἡ πραγματικότης εἶναι ζῶν μεταμορφωτικὸν δίκτυον ἀλληλοκρατουμένων κινήσεων, ἐν ᾧ τὸ μέρος οὐδέποτε ὑφίσταται ἔξω τοῦ ὅλου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:HSI-01","CL_JP8E_26:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:HSI-02","text":"Τὸ ὑποκείμενον εἶναι θεραπευόμενος καὶ θεραπευτής, μεταβατικὸς φορεὺς νοῦ ἢ ἀνοίας, ὃς παράγεται ἐν τῇ ἀνταποδόσει ψυχικῶν, σωματικῶν καὶ παιδευτικῶν κινήσεων.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:HSI-02","CL_JP8E_26:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:HSI-03","text":"Ὁ χρόνος εἶναι ῥυθμικὴ καὶ γενεαλογικὴ σπείρα, ἡ ὁποία συσσωρεύει κράσεις καὶ βίους, ἀλλὰ παρέχει δυνατότητα ἐπανoρθώσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:HSI-03","CL_JP8E_25:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:HSI-04","text":"Ἡ δυνατὴ ἔξοδος δὲν εἶναι ἀπόδρασις ἐκ τοῦ σώματος ἢ ἐκ τῆς ὁλότητος, ἀλλὰ μεταβολὴ τῆς ἐχθρᾶς κρατήσεως εἰς φιλικὴν σύνταξιν καὶ θεωρητικὴν ἀνάτασιν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:HSI-04","CL_JP8E_26:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MRT-B01","text":"Αἱ ἀρχαὶ τῆς διανεμημένης αἰτιότητος, τῆς δυναμικῆς συμμετρίας, τῆς θεραπευτικῆς γνώσεως καὶ τῆς μορφολογικῆς μεταβολῆς ἀρκοῦσι πρὸς ἀνακατασκευὴν τῆς κεντρικῆς κινήσεως.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:MRT-B01","CL_JP8E_26:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MRT-B02","text":"Ἡ ἀνακατασκευὴ διασώζει τὸν σπειροειδῆ μηχανισμόν: διαταραχὴ → ἀναγωγὴ εἰς αἴτια → ῥύθμισις → ἐξομοίωσις → μεταβολὴ μορφῆς καὶ βίου.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:MRT-B02","CL_JP8E_25:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MRT-B03","text":"Ὑποκαθορισμένα μένουσι τὸ ἀκριβὲς μέτρον τῆς μετρίας κινήσεως, τὸ ὅριον τῆς ἐξωτερικῆς ἐπεμβάσεως καὶ ὁ μηχανισμὸς τῆς μεταπλάσεως ψυχικῆς διαθέσεως εἰς συγκεκριμένην μορφήν.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:MRT-B03","CL_JP8E_26:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MRT-B04","text":"Ἡ ἀρχιτεκτονικὴ εἶναι ἰσχυροτάτη ἐν τῇ συνδέσει σώματος, ψυχῆς, κινήσεως, γνώσεως, μορφῆς καὶ κόσμου, ἀλλὰ ἐξαρτᾶται ἀπὸ παραγωγικὰς ἐντάσεις μεταξὺ ἀνάγκης, παιδείας, αὐτονομίας καὶ ἱεραρχίας.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:MRT-B04","CL_JP8E_26:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261014T233533732998Z_4cf06ae9","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Jp8E L1 07","source_sha256":"e177a1d4e678ba3043cacf34fef49dfcd2d5a2015c32cf777d9d74c35a2404f0","sha256":"3cf626e26bebc724452cb3b9fa456d03686fad53dc7283d0b0024b8a6b58749a","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-14T23:35:33.736257+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B01","text":"The problem becomes the conception of the living being as a field in which ethical deviation, disease, learning, form, and polity are jointly produced.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:BAS-01","CL_JP8E_26:ARG-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B02","text":"Causality shifts from individual will to a distributed cycle of body, soul, upbringing, desire, and cosmic order.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:BAS-04","CL_JP8E_26:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B03","text":"Therapy becomes the regulation and transformation of motions, not the correction of a single isolated symptom.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:BAS-07","CL_JP8E_25:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B04","text":"Knowledge is valid only when it re-enters the knower and restores the rhythm of soul and body.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:AS-06","CL_JP8E_25:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B05","text":"Form ceases to be an inert surface and becomes a visible inscription of intellect, folly, desire, and prior life.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:ARG-02","CL_JP8E_26:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B06","text":"The hierarchy of beings retains its axiological direction, but is transformed into a cycle of descents and ascents.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:GA-08","CL_JP8E_26:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B07","text":"Cosmic order functions at once as a model of therapy and as a condition that includes the lower things without abolishing gradation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:GA-10","CL_JP8E_26:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AS-B08","text":"The movement of the system is spiral: from disturbance to explanation, from explanation to education, from education to cosmic assimilation, and thence again to transformation of embodied life.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:AS-07","CL_JP8E_26:ARG-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] The living being is conjointly of soul, body, habit, and environment, and none of these acts self-sufficiently.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:GA-01","CL_JP8E_26:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-02","text":"[Mutating] Motion changes from a physiological concomitant into a universal criterion of health, knowledge, virtue, form, and cosmic kinship.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:RLE-05","CL_JP8E_25:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Asymmetry and disorder of motions produce disease, folly, vice, and morphological decline.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:GA-03","CL_JP8E_26:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Therapy, education, and philosophy constitute one mechanism of reactivation and restoration of internal order.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:GA-07","CL_JP8E_25:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Autonomy does not mean independence, but the capacity of the part to cooperate within a pre-existing natural and cosmic order.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:GA-01","CL_JP8E_25:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Knowledge is therapeutic only if it changes the circuits of the knower and opens the possibility of ascent.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:GA-07","CL_JP8E_26:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Ethical quality does not remain an internal judgment, but is inscribed in body, posture, breathing, dwelling, and species.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:GA-01","CL_JP8E_26:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] Symmetry is a dynamic reciprocity of the parts according to the model of the whole, not static equality.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:GA-09","CL_JP8E_25:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Perfection consists not in flight from the body or in the elimination of lower things, but in assimilation to and completion of the whole.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:GA-08","CL_JP8E_26:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] The cosmos is conceived as one ensouled and visible system, in which the gradations of forms refer to a common order.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:GA-09","CL_JP8E_26:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Desire is a resistant force which, rather than remaining outside order, constitutes the embodied mode of generation and teleology.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:GA-05","CL_JP8E_26:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-01","text":"The interdependence of soul and body is retained, but shifts from transmission of affection to production of form and species.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:RMD-01","CL_JP8E_26:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-02","text":"Motion recurs across the scales of body, soul, education, and cosmos, but each scale transforms the meaning of the preceding one.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:RMD-01","CL_JP8E_24:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-03","text":"Symmetry passes from a criterion of beauty to an operator of therapy and finally to a rule of cosmic assimilation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:RMD-05","CL_JP8E_25:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-04","text":"The ethical category is absorbed into a genealogy of mixtures, foods, education, and prior psychic changes.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:RMD-03","CL_JP8E_26:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-05","text":"Therapy changes from the repair of a suffering body to the reorganization of the way in which the being moves, knows, and dwells.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:MP-02","CL_JP8E_25:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-06","text":"The hierarchy of species retains its axiological direction, but loses its immobility through reciprocal transformation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:RMD-02","CL_JP8E_26:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-07","text":"Desire changes from a particular generative passion into a general model of nature that acts through tension and resistance.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:RMD-03","CL_JP8E_26:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RMD-08","text":"Cosmology ceases to be a final theory and becomes a retrospective rule for judging every therapy, education, and morphological change.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:CGD-04","CL_JP8E_25:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:CGD-01","text":"The theories of humors, elements, womb, drugs, and particular parts of the soul remain local mechanisms; when they ascend, they preserve only the type of distributed causality.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:CGD-01","CL_JP8E_26:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:CGD-02","text":"The gradation of therapeutic means is elevated into the universal principle that correct intervention increases internal activation and limits force.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:GA-02","CL_JP8E_25:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:CGD-03","text":"The tripartite soul and specific animal divisions recede beneath the more universal principle of differentiated rhythms and transitions.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:CGD-02","CL_JP8E_26:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:CGD-04","text":"The bridge between the units is motion, because it connects physiology, psychic education, ethical change, and animal morphology.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:CGD-03","CL_JP8E_26:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:CGD-05","text":"Cosmic imitation is elevated into a superordinate measure that recodes health, education, and perfection.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:CGD-04","CL_JP8E_25:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:CGD-06","text":"The exact mean of motion and the mechanism of the transformation of soul into species remain underdetermined as limits of the present architecture.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:CGD-06","CL_JP8E_26:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-01","text":"The soul is rejected as a self-subsistent and uninvolved governor of the body, affections, and actions.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:GNX-01","CL_JP8E_26:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-02","text":"Vice is rejected as a purely voluntary and unmediated decision severed from mixture, food, and education.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:GNX-02","CL_JP8E_24:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-03","text":"Therapy is rejected as external domination or violent substitution for the activity of the person being treated.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:GNX-01","CL_JP8E_25:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-04","text":"Knowledge that remains information and does not transform the manner of knowing is rejected.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:GNX-04","CL_JP8E_25:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-05","text":"Nature is rejected as axiologically neutral matter or a disorderly multiplicity of independent species.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:GNX-01","CL_JP8E_26:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-06","text":"Perfection is rejected as one-sided superiority or the purgation of lower forms.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:GNX-04","CL_JP8E_26:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:GNX-07","text":"Immobility is rejected as salvation, because health presupposes ordered, moderate, and continuous motion.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:GNX-05","CL_JP8E_24:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:DBN-01","text":"Every hypothesis that soul, body, polity, education, and cosmos have independent causes must be false.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_25:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:DBN-02","text":"Every hypothesis that therapy can be imposed externally without time, measure, and internal change must be false.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:DBN-01","CL_JP8E_25:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:DBN-03","text":"Every hypothesis that knowledge alone suffices, unless it cooperates with habit, motion, and environment, must be false.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:DBN-03","CL_JP8E_25:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:DBN-04","text":"Every hypothesis that form, breathing, and place are external givens without psychic and ontological consequence must be false.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:GNX-03","CL_JP8E_26:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:DBN-05","text":"Every hypothesis that the lowest species possesses independent meaning outside cosmic totality must be false.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:BSA-03","CL_JP8E_26:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSA-01","text":"The existence of an unrelated subject, which would be self-governing and unaffected by body, time, and cosmos, becomes impossible.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:BSA-01","CL_JP8E_25:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSA-02","text":"A cosmos in which ethical value is not embodied in forms, motions, and places becomes impossible.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:BSA-01","CL_JP8E_26:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSA-03","text":"A therapy that saves one part without changing its relation to the whole becomes impossible.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:BSA-04","CL_JP8E_24:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSA-04","text":"Time that is purely linear or devoid of return becomes impossible, because prior life returns as habit, form, and a condition of change.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:BSD-02","CL_JP8E_26:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:XD-01","text":"Therapy becomes a political and cosmological art, because the correction of internal motions requires changing the conditions that produce bodies, habits, and forms.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:BSD-05","CL_JP8E_25:BSD-05","CL_JP8E_26:BSD-01","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:XD-02","text":"Self-motion becomes a condition of ethical restoration, but does not abolish the need for external education; intervention is judged by whether it restores or substitutes for internal activity.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:XD-05","CL_JP8E_26:GA-11","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:XD-03","text":"Morphology becomes a therapeutic and cognitive field, because the body shows psychic circuits that can be changed through education and contemplation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:BSD-06","CL_JP8E_25:BSD-02","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:XD-04","text":"Descent is not simply loss, because it simultaneously expresses axiological decline and completes cosmic totality.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:XD-01","CL_JP8E_26:BSD-04","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:XD-05","text":"Desire proves that order does not act through simple subordination, but through forces that resist and yet carry generation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:XD-02","CL_JP8E_26:BSD-02","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L1_07:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSD-01","text":"The subject becomes a transitional node in which prior mixtures, educations, desires, and cosmic relations are inscribed in present action and form.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:BSD-01","CL_JP8E_26:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSD-02","text":"Ontology becomes an ontology of rhythms and transformations, where the being of a being is judged by its capacity to preserve, correct, or lose order.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:BSD-01","CL_JP8E_26:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSD-03","text":"Time becomes genealogical and spiral, because what is prior does not pass away but reappears as the bodily and ethical possibility of what is later.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:BSD-02","CL_JP8E_26:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSD-04","text":"Politics and education cease to be external administrations and become management of the conditions under which self-regulation can arise.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:BSD-05","CL_JP8E_25:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:BSD-05","text":"The perfection of the whole does not abolish hierarchy, but transforms hierarchy into a functional interdependence of gradations within one cosmos.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:GA-10","CL_JP8E_26:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RLE-01","text":"Mixture and disorder → affection and folly → apparent vice → reduction to educational and political causes → reorganization of the field of motions.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:RLE-01","CL_JP8E_24:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RLE-02","text":"Internal motion → autonomy → harmony → strengthening of the capacity for self-regulation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RLE-03","text":"Psychic quality → bodily form → animal grade → new horizon of psychic change.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RLE-04","text":"Theory of the cosmos → restoration of internal circuits → assimilation → retrospective validation of therapy as cooperation with order.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:RLE-04","CL_JP8E_25:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RLE-05","text":"Desire → generation or disease → bodily indication of unfulfilled function → reinscription of teleology through matter.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:RLE-02","CL_JP8E_26:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:RLE-06","text":"The attractor of the system is assimilation, which evaluates motion, knowledge, therapy, and morphological ascent.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:RLE-05","CL_JP8E_26:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:FGS-B01","text":"Mixture, affection, food, and education → formation of the motions of soul and body.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:FGS-B02","text":"Asymmetrical motion → disease, folly, and disturbance of judgment → action appearing voluntary.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:FGS-B02","CL_JP8E_24:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:FGS-B03","text":"Diagnosis of interdependence → rejection of one-sided domination → gentle education and activation of the person being treated.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:FGS-B05","CL_JP8E_25:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:FGS-B04","text":"Restoration of rhythms → symmetry of soul and body → knowledge as internal change.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:XD-02","CL_JP8E_25:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:FGS-B05","text":"Acquisition or loss of intellect → change of form, motion, breathing, and dwelling → dynamic hierarchy of species.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:FGS-B05","CL_JP8E_26:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:FGS-B06","text":"Hierarchical multiplicity → cosmic completeness → assimilation to the order of the visible living being.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:FGS-B06","CL_JP8E_25:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AFM-B01","text":"Bodily and psychic disorder → ethical deviation and distributed causality.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:AFM-B01","CL_JP8E_24:AFM-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AFM-B02","text":"Distributed causality → therapeutic reversal → reorganization of body, habit, education, and polity.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:XD-06","CL_JP8E_24:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AFM-B03","text":"Hierarchization of motions → preference for gentle self-regulation → restoration of psychic circuits.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:FGS-B02","CL_JP8E_25:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AFM-B04","text":"Restoration of the knower → cosmic contemplation → assimilation and the best life.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:AFM-B04","CL_JP8E_25:AFM-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:AFM-B05","text":"Psychic descent or ascent → morphological and animal change → hierarchical multiplicity within cosmic completeness.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:AFM-B01","CL_JP8E_26:AFM-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MME-01","text":"Ethical judgment is replaced by genealogical mapping of the bodily, educational, and cosmic conditions of action.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:MME-01","CL_JP8E_26:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MME-02","text":"The distinction between nature and art is reorganized as the distinction between cooperative art and violent art.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:MME-05","CL_JP8E_25:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MME-03","text":"Form, sex, species, and dwelling become legible as imprints of psychic history and ontological grade.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:MME-01","CL_JP8E_26:ALT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MME-04","text":"Health is reclassified as an active order of relations, and perfection as completeness of the whole rather than superiority of one part.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:MME-04","CL_JP8E_26:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MME-05","text":"Cosmology becomes a practical instrument of reshaping, because the theory of the whole regulates the mode of treating the part.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:MME-05","CL_JP8E_25:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, diseases, affections, foods, drugs, sexes, species, breathings, and places appear as distinct phenomena.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:ALT-B01","CL_JP8E_26:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, these phenomena constitute a network of rhythms, gradations, transmissions, and mutual formations.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:ALT-B02","CL_JP8E_26:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, intervention shifts from blame and conquest to the conditions for the emergence of self-regulation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:ALT-B03","CL_JP8E_25:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, the living being is a becoming of relations, where prior life returns as later body and possibility.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:ALT-B04","CL_JP8E_26:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the synthesis performs what it says, connecting fragmented bodies, souls, forms, and cosmos into one architecture.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:ALT-B05","CL_JP8E_26:ALT-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:FL-01","text":"The rejection of pure voluntariness weakens personal guilt, but does not abolish the need for responsible educational direction.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:FL-01","CL_JP8E_24:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:FL-02","text":"The principle of self-motion conflicts with the need for medical and pedagogical intervention, which must act without nullifying the person being treated.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:FL-01","CL_JP8E_25:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:FL-03","text":"The hierarchy of forms retains axiological stability, while reciprocal transition of species dissolves the stable identity of the grades.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:FL-01","CL_JP8E_26:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:FL-04","text":"Perfection requires both ascent toward intellect and preservation of the lower things as necessary parts of the cosmos.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:FL-04","CL_JP8E_26:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MP-01","text":"Vice changes into disease, disease into disorder of motions, and disorder into a general logic of cosmic and morphological change.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:MP-01","CL_JP8E_26:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MP-02","text":"Symmetry changes from a morphological criterion into a therapeutic operator and finally into a cosmic measure of assimilation.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:MP-02","CL_JP8E_25:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MP-03","text":"Nature changes from a bodily substrate into an ethical-cosmological mechanism, in which form and space already bear judgment.","refs":["CL_JP8E_26:MP-03","CL_JP8E_26:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:HSI-01","text":"Reality is a living transformative network of mutually sustaining motions, in which the part never exists outside the whole.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:HSI-01","CL_JP8E_26:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:HSI-02","text":"The subject is both patient and healer, a transitional bearer of intellect or folly, produced in the reciprocity of psychic, bodily, and educational motions.","refs":["CL_JP8E_25:HSI-02","CL_JP8E_26:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:HSI-03","text":"Time is a rhythmic and genealogical spiral that accumulates mixtures and lives, yet affords the possibility of restoration.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:HSI-03","CL_JP8E_25:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:HSI-04","text":"The possible exit is not escape from the body or from totality, but transformation of hostile holding into friendly arrangement and theoretical restoration.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:HSI-04","CL_JP8E_26:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MRT-B01","text":"The principles of distributed causality, dynamic symmetry, therapeutic knowledge, and morphological change suffice for reconstructing the central movement.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:MRT-B01","CL_JP8E_26:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction preserves the spiral mechanism: disturbance → reduction to causes → regulation → assimilation → change of form and life.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:MRT-B02","CL_JP8E_25:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MRT-B03","text":"The exact measure of moderate motion, the limit of external intervention, and the mechanism by which psychic disposition is transformed into a determinate form remain underdetermined.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:MRT-B03","CL_JP8E_26:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_JP8E_L1_07:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest in its connection of body, soul, motion, knowledge, form, and cosmos, but depends on productive tensions among necessity, education, autonomy, and hierarchy.","refs":["CL_JP8E_24:MRT-B04","CL_JP8E_26:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_JP8E_L1_07@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_JP8E_L1_07:v1","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Jp8E L1 07"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Timaeus","title":"Jp8E L1 07","context":"Chapters 70–76 · Section 86 · Section 87 · Section 88 · Section 89 · Section 90 · Section 91 · Section 92","original_title":"Jp8E L1 07","original_context":"Chapters 70–76 · Section 86 · Section 87 · Section 88 · Section 89 · Section 90 · Section 91 · Section 92"},"original_title":"Jp8E L1 07","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"WB_JP8E","layer":"WB","title":"Timaeus","order":1,"parents":["MC_JP8E_L2_01","MC_JP8E_L2_02"],"revision":"WB_JP8E@R002","sha256":"aa8bcb0bc09f9be74630b74344a0dc1726230dd3cf99db473b842254b3a7f4fb","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:BAS-01","text":"Τὸ σύστημα ζητεῖ νὰ κατανοήσῃ πῶς κόσμος, πόλις, ζῷον καὶ σῶμα συγκροτοῦνται ἐν διαφορᾷ, θνητότητι καὶ μεταβολῇ, χωρὶς ἀναγωγὴν εἰς ἀκίνητον οὐσίαν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BAS-02","text":"Ἡ ἑνότης ἀναπλάττεται ὡς ἱεραρχημένη ἀλλὰ ἀμφίδρομος μεσιτεία σχέσεων, οὐχὶ ὡς ὁμοιογενὴς ταυτότης ἢ γυμνὴ κυριότης.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BAS-03","text":"Τὸ παράδειγμα καὶ ὁ σκοπὸς καθίστανται ἐνεργοί, μόνον ἐπειδὴ μεταγράφονται εἰς σώματα, κινήσεις, ὄργανα, θεσμοὺς καὶ μνήμας.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BAS-04","text":"Ἡ ἀνάγκη καὶ ἡ ἀντίστασις οὔτε ἀναιροῦνται οὔτε μένουσιν ἔξω τῆς τάξεως, ἀλλὰ παράγουσι ζωήν ἢ φθορὰν κατὰ τὸ μέτρον τῆς ῥοῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BAS-05","text":"Ὁ λόγος γίνεται διαβαθμισμένη ἀνακατασκευή, ἥτις ἀληθεύει πρακτικῶς ὅταν ἐπανέρχηται ὡς παιδεία, διάγνωσις, θεραπεία καὶ πολιτικὴ κρίσις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B06","MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BAS-06","text":"Ὁ χρόνος συνάπτει κοσμικὴν περιοδικότητα, σωματικὴν γενεαλογίαν καὶ ἱστορικὴν διακοπήν εἰς σπείραν, ἐν ᾗ ἡ ἐπάνοδος μεταμορφοῖ τὸ ἐπανερχόμενον.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B07","MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BAS-07","text":"Ἡ κίνησις τοῦ ὅλου εἶναι ἀναδρομική: μορφὴ γεννᾷ ῥοήν, ἡ ῥοὴ διαταράσσεται, ἡ διαταραχὴ γίνεται σημεῖον, καὶ ἡ κρίσις ἐπιχειρεῖ νέαν μορφήν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BAS-08","text":"Ἡ σύγκλεισις μένει πρόσκαιρος καὶ ἀνοικτή, διότι πᾶσα διατήρησις φέρει ἐν ἑαυτῇ δεκτικότητα, φθοράν, ἀναθεώρησιν καὶ νέαν ἀνάγκην ῥυθμίσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B08","MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Τὸ ὂν καὶ ἡ ἑνότης παράγονται ἐκ σχέσεων, κινήσεων καὶ λειτουργικῶς διακεκριμένων μερῶν, οὐκ ἐξ αὐτοτελοῦς πυρῆνος.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Πᾶσα διατηρήσιμος τάξις ἀπαιτεῖ μεσότητα, ἀναλογίαν καὶ ἱεραρχημένην συμπλοκὴν διαφορών.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Τὸ παράδειγμα μεταλλάσσεται εἰς τελεολογικὴν καὶ κατανεμημένην αἰτιότητα, ἥτις διοικεῖ τὴν ἀνάγκην διὰ τῶν ἰδίων αὐτῆς ἀντιστάσεων.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Ἡ μεσολάβησις εἶναι καθολικὴ ἀρχὴ μεταγραφῆς, δι᾽ ἧς μορφή, αἴσθησις, ψυχή, μνήμη, σημεῖον καὶ ἀλήθεια συνδέονται.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Τὸ σῶμα μεταβαίνει ἀπὸ μεσίτην κινήσεων εἰς κατανεμημένον ἀρχεῖον, ὄργανον καὶ τόπον ἐγγραφῆς φυσικῆς καὶ ἠθικῆς ἱστορίας.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] Τάξις, ὑγίεια καὶ φρόνησις εἰσὶν ὀνόματα ῥυθμισμένης ἀνταποδόσεως, ἐν ᾗ λόγος, ῥοή, μέτρον καὶ διέξοδος ἀμοιβαίως διατηροῦνται.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Γένεσις καὶ φθορά, ἀρετὴ καὶ κακία, ὑγίεια καὶ νόσος παράγονται ὑπὸ τῆς αὐτῆς μηχανῆς, διαφέροντα κατὰ φοράν, μέτρον καὶ ἀνάδρασιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Ὁ χρόνος μεταλλάσσεται εἰς πολυμερῆ σπείραν περιοδικότητος, φθορᾶς, γενεαλογικῆς συσσωρεύσεως, διακοπῆς καὶ προδιατάξεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Ἡ ἀλήθεια εἶναι διαβαθμισμένη καὶ θεραπευτικὴ ἀνασύνθεσις, ἧς τὸ κύρος ἐξαρτᾶται ἀπὸ τὰς μεσιτείας καὶ τὴν δύναμιν μεταβολῆς τοῦ γιγνώσκοντος.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Ἡ δεκτικότης καὶ ἡ ἀπόστασις δὲν εἰσὶ στερήσεις, ἀλλὰ οἱ ὅροι δι᾽ ὧν μορφὴ, ὁμοίωσις καὶ γνώσις καθίστανται δυναταί.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-12","MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Τὸ ὑποκείμενον εἶναι μεταβατικὸς κόμβος σωμάτων, ῥοῶν, παθημάτων, μνημῶν καὶ κρίσεων, ὃς μεταβάλλεται ὑπὸ τῶν σχέσεων ἃς ρυθμίζει.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-10","MC_JP8E_L2_02:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Ἡ ἐλευθερία εἶναι συνεργὸς αὐτορρύθμισις, πλοήγησις καὶ ἐπανόρθωσις ἐντὸς σώματος, χρόνου, κόσμου καὶ δυνάμεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-11","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:RMD-01","text":"Ἡ σχέσις διατηρεῖ τὴν γενετικὴν ταυτότητα αὐτῆς, ἀλλὰ ἐπεκτείνεται ἀπὸ κοσμικὴν συγκρότησιν εἰς φυσιολογίαν, παθολογίαν, παιδείαν καὶ πολιτικήν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RMD-02","text":"Ἡ μεσότης μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ τοπικὸν δεσμὸν εἰς καθολικὴν μηχανὴ μεταδόσεως, κυβερνήσεως, σημειώσεως καὶ ἀληθείας.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RMD-03","text":"Ἡ ψυχὴ καὶ τὸ σῶμα παύουσιν νὰ ἀντιτίθενται ὡς κυβερνήτης καὶ ὄργανον, καὶ συμπλέκονται εἰς κόμβον πάθους, κρίσεως καὶ ἐγγεγραμμένης ἕξεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RMD-04","text":"Ἡ κίνησις μεταναστεύει ἀπὸ φυσικὴν αἰτίαν εἰς κοινὸν κριτήριον γνώσεως, θεραπείας, ἀρετῆς καὶ κοσμικῆς ὁμοιώσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RMD-05","text":"Τὸ μέτρον καὶ ἡ συμμετρία διατηροῦσι τὴν κανονιστικὴν λειτουργίαν, ἀλλὰ μεταβάλλονται ἀπὸ στατικὴν ἰσότητα εἰς δυναμικὴν ἀνταπόδοσιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RMD-06","text":"Ἡ μνήμη μετατοπίζεται ἀπὸ ἀρχειακὴν διατήρησιν εἰς σωματικὴν αἰτιότητα, καθ᾽ ἣν τὸ παρελθὸν ἐπανέρχεται ὡς μορφή, ἕξις καὶ φθορά.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RMD-07","text":"Ἡ καταστροφὴ καὶ ἡ νόσος ἀπορροφῶνται εἰς κοινὸν σχήμα ἀναστροφῆς, ὅπου ἡ διατήρησις μεταπίπτει εἰς αὐτοτροφοδοτουμένην λύσιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RMD-08","text":"Ἡ ἱεραρχία ἀντιστρέφει τὴν λειτουργίαν αὐτῆς: ἀπὸ σταθερὰν ἀνωτερότητα γίνεται τέχνη διοχετεύσεως καὶ ἀμοιβαίας ἀποδόσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RMD-09","text":"Ἡ χώρα ὑποχωρεῖ ὡς ἴδιον ὄνομα, ἀλλ᾽ ἡ λειτουργία αὐτῆς διασπείρεται εἰς ὁδούς, πλέγματα καὶ δεκτικὰ σώματα ἃ μορφοῦνται διὰ ἀντιστάσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-07","MC_JP8E_L2_02:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:CGD-01","text":"Αἱ ἀριθμητικαί, γεωμετρικαί, ἀστρονομικαί, χυμικαί, ἀνατομικαὶ καὶ φαρμακολογικαὶ διατάξεις μένουσι τοπικαὶ μηχαναὶ παραδειγματισμοῦ.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:CGD-02","text":"Εἰς τὸ παρὸν ἐπίπεδον κλιμακοῦνται μόνον αἱ ἀρχαὶ τῆς διανεμημένης αἰτιότητος, τῆς ῥοῆς, τῆς μεσιτείας καὶ τῆς λειτουργικῆς διαφορᾶς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:CGD-03","text":"Τὸ σῶμα γεφυροῖ κοσμολογίαν, φυσιολογίαν, ἠθικὴν καὶ πολιτικήν, ἐπειδὴ μεταγράφει τὰς ἀφανεῖς σχέσεις εἰς κινήσεις, μορφὰς καὶ σημεῖα.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:CGD-04","text":"Χρόνος καὶ μνήμη γεφυροῦσι τὰς μονάδας, ἐπειδὴ μετατρέπουσι τὴν περιοδικότητα, τὴν ἕξιν καὶ τὴν καταστροφὴν εἰς παροῦσαν αἰτιότητα.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:CGD-05","text":"Ἡ διαταραχὴ κλιμακοῦται ἀπὸ τοπικὸν συμβεβηκὸς εἰς καταλύτην, ὅταν τὸ σύμπτωμα ἀναγκάζῃ γενεαλογικὴν ἀνασύνθεσιν τοῦ ὅλου.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:CGD-06","text":"Ἡ αὐτοτελὴς οὐσία καὶ ἡ γυμνὴ ἱεραρχία καθίστανται ἀπαρχαιωμένα σχήματα καὶ ἀνακωδικοποιοῦνται ὡς παράγωγα σχέσεων καὶ λειτουργικῶν ἀνταποδόσεων.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:GNX-01","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἑνότης διὰ ὁμοιογενείας, μίξεως ἢ ἐκκαθαρίσεως τῆς ἑτερότητος.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GNX-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GNX-02","text":"Ἀποκλείεται αὐτοτελὴς οὐσία καὶ ἀδρανὲς σῶμα, ἐπειδὴ ταυτότης καὶ λειτουργία παράγονται σχεσιακῶς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GNX-03","text":"Ἀποκλείονται γραμμικὸς χρόνος καὶ μονόδρομος αἰτιότης, ἐπειδὴ τὰ ἀποτελέσματα ἐπανέρχονται ὡς αἴτια.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GNX-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GNX-04","text":"Ἀποκλείονται ἀσώματος ψυχή, ἀδιαμεσολάβητος ἀλήθεια καὶ γνώσις ἥτις οὐ μεταβάλλει τὸν βίον.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GNX-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GNX-05","text":"Ἀποκλείονται ὑγίεια ὡς ἀκινησία καὶ τελείωσις ὡς φυγὴ ἀπὸ σῶμα, ἐπιθυμίαν καὶ πολλαπλότητα.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:DBN-01","text":"Ψευδὲς δεῖ εἶναι ὅτι μέρος, σῶμα ἢ ποιότης δύναται νὰ ἀποτελέσῃ ἔσχατον καὶ ἀσυσχέτιστον ὑποκείμενον.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:DBN-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:DBN-02","text":"Ψευδὲς δεῖ εἶναι ὅτι ψυχή, σῶμα, πόλις, μνήμη καὶ κόσμος ἔχουσιν ἀνεξαρτήτους αἰτιακὰς ἱστορίας.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:DBN-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:DBN-03","text":"Ψευδὲς δεῖ εἶναι ὅτι φθορὰ καὶ καταστροφὴ προσπίπτουσιν ἔξωθεν, ἀντὶ νὰ παράγωνται ἐκ τῆς ἀναστροφῆς τῆς γενέσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L2_02:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:DBN-04","text":"Ψευδὲς δεῖ εἶναι ὅτι ἐλευθερία, θεραπεία ἢ ἀλήθεια δύνανται νὰ ἐπιβληθῶσιν ἔξωθεν ἄνευ χρόνου καὶ ἐσωτερικῆς μεταβολῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:DBN-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:DBN-05","text":"Ψευδὲς δεῖ εἶναι ὅτι ἡ ὁμοίωσις ἀπαιτεῖ ταύτισιν ἢ ὅτι ἡ ἱεραρχία σώζεται διὰ καταστροφῆς τῶν κατωτέρων.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:DBN-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:BSA-01","text":"Ἡ βαθύτατη ἀπουσία εἶναι ἡ ἀμεσότης: παράδειγμα, ἀλήθεια, κυριότης καὶ μνήμη οὐδέποτε δίδονται ἄνευ σώματος, εἰκόνος, σημείου ἢ μαρτυρίας.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSA-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BSA-02","text":"Ἀπουσιάζει ἀδιάσπαστον ἀρχεῖον καὶ ἀκριβὲς καθολικὸν μέτρον, ὥστε πᾶσα ἀνακατασκευή νὰ μένῃ ἐπιλεκτικὴ καὶ ἀναθεωρήσιμος.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSA-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BSA-03","text":"Καθαρὸν ἐκτὸς καὶ ἀκίνητον κέντρον εἰσὶν ὀντολογικῶς ἀδύνατα, ἐπειδὴ πᾶς κυβερνῶν μεταβάλλεται ὑπὸ τοῦ κυβερνωμένου δικτύου.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSA-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BSA-04","text":"Ἀπουσιάζει τελικὴ τάξις ἀπαλλαγμένη φθορᾶς, διότι ἡ συνέχεια περιέχει τὴν ἕξιν, τὴν διακοπὴν καὶ τὴν δυνατότητα νέας λύσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSA-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:XD-01","text":"Ἡ παραδειγματικὴ αἰτιότης δύναται νὰ γένηται πραγματικὴ μόνον ἐὰν μεταγράφηται εἰς σχεσιακὴν σωματικὴν γένεσιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-01","WB_JP8E:GA-03","WB_JP8E:GA-04","WB_JP8E:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:XD-02","text":"Ἡ ἱστορικὴ καταστροφὴ καὶ ἡ σωματικὴ παθολογία εἰσὶν ὁμόλογοι ἀναστροφαί, καθ᾽ ἃς ἡ μηχανὴ διατηρήσεως ἐνισχύει τὴν ἰδίαν λύσιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-03","WB_JP8E:GA-06","WB_JP8E:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:XD-03","text":"Ἀρχεῖον καὶ σῶμα εἰσὶ δύο μορφαὶ αἰτιακῆς μνήμης, δι᾽ ὧν τὸ ἀπόν παρελθὸν διατάσσει τὴν παροῦσαν μορφήν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-05","WB_JP8E:GA-05","WB_JP8E:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:XD-04","text":"Ἡ ἑρμηνεία, ἡ διάγνωσις καὶ ἡ θεραπεία συγκροτοῦσι μίαν πράξιν, ἐπειδὴ ἀνασυνθέτουσι τὴν ἀφανῆ τάξιν καὶ ἐπανενεργοῦσιν ἐπ᾽ αὐτῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-04","WB_JP8E:GA-04","WB_JP8E:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:XD-05","text":"Παιδεία, πολιτική καὶ θεραπεία δικαιοῦνται ὡς κατασκευαὶ ὅρων αὐτορρυθμίσεως, οὐχὶ ὡς ἐξωτερικαὶ ἀντικαταστάσεις τοῦ δρώντος.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-03","WB_JP8E:GA-09","WB_JP8E:GA-11","WB_JP8E:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:XD-06","text":"Ἡ ἱεραρχία διατηρεῖ συστημικὸν κύρος μόνον ὅταν λειτουργῇ ὡς ἀμφίδρομος διοχέτευσις καὶ οὐχὶ ὡς μονόδρομος ἐπιβολή.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-02","WB_JP8E:GA-02","WB_JP8E:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:XD-07","text":"Ἡ ἐλευθερωτικὴ ἐπέμβασις ἀποτυγχάνει ὅταν ἡ ἰσχὺς ἀντικαθιστᾷ τὴν ἐσωτερικὴν ῥύθμισιν, ἀλλ᾽ ἄνευ ἰσχύος ἀδυνατεῖ νὰ λύσῃ τὴν κατάκλεισιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-06","WB_JP8E:GA-07","WB_JP8E:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:XD-08","text":"Ἡ δεκτικότης δὲν εἶναι κενὸν ὑπόβαθρον, ἀλλὰ κατανεμημένον πεδίον ὁδῶν καὶ ἀντιστάσεων, ὃ συναποφασίζει τὴν μορφήν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:FGS-B02","WB_JP8E:GA-04","WB_JP8E:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:BSD-01","text":"Ἡ πραγματικότης καθίσταται πολυεπίπεδος τοπολογία μετασχηματισμῶν, ἐν ᾗ κοσμολογία, φυσιολογία, πολιτικὴ καὶ ἠθικὴ εἰσὶ κλίμακες μιᾶς σχεσιακῆς παραγωγῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSD-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-01","WB_JP8E:GA-01","WB_JP8E:GA-04","WB_JP8E:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BSD-02","text":"Ὁ χρόνος γίνεται τριπλῆ αἰτιότης: κύκλος μετρήσεως, γενεαλογικὴ ἐγγραφή, καὶ διακοπὴ ἡ ὁποία ἀναγκάζει νέαν σύνθεσιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSD-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-04","WB_JP8E:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BSD-03","text":"Ἡ κυριότης μετατίθεται ἀπὸ κατοχὴν δυνάμεως εἰς διοίκησιν ῥοῶν, μεταβάσεων, μνημῶν καὶ ὅρων αὐτορρυθμίσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSD-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-05","WB_JP8E:GA-04","WB_JP8E:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BSD-04","text":"Ἡ αἰτιότης γίνεται κύκλωμα, ἐπειδὴ τὸ ἀποτέλεσμα δύναται νὰ ἐπανέλθῃ ὡς αἴτιον διατηρήσεως ἢ φθορᾶς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:FL-04","WB_JP8E:GA-06","WB_JP8E:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BSD-05","text":"Ἡ ἐπιστήμη γίνεται ἱεραρχία προσηκόντων καὶ ἐπεμβατικῶν λόγων, ὧν ἡ ἀκρίβεια κρίνεται κατὰ τὸ ἀντικείμενον καὶ τὴν θεραπευτικὴν ἀπόδοσιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSD-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-04","WB_JP8E:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BSD-06","text":"Ὁ δρῶν καθίσταται φύλαξ μεσιτειῶν, ἅμα θεραπευόμενος καὶ θεραπευτής, ὃς ρυθμίζει δίκτυα ὑφ᾽ ὧν αὐτὸς συγκροτεῖται.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSD-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:HSI-02","WB_JP8E:GA-11","WB_JP8E:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BSD-07","text":"Ἡ πολιτικὴ γίνεται οἰκολογία μεσιτειῶν, ἐπειδὴ ἡ πάλη μεταφέρεται εἰς τοὺς ὅρους παραγωγῆς μνήμης, ὑγιείας, κρίσεως καὶ αὐτορρυθμίσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-06","MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B03","WB_JP8E:GA-04","WB_JP8E:GA-06","WB_JP8E:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:RLE-01","text":"Παράδειγμα → μεσιτεία → σωματικὴ μορφὴ → μέτρον → ἀναγνώρισις παραδείγματος· ἡ ἐπιστροφή διατηρεῖ τὴν ὁμοιότητα διὰ τῆς ἀποστάσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:RLE-01","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RLE-02","text":"Διαφορά → ἀντίστασις → δύναμις → μορφὴ → νέα διαφορά· ἡ ἀπειλὴ τῆς τάξεως γίνεται ὕλη τῆς ἀναπαραγωγῆς αὐτῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:RLE-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RLE-03","text":"Φθορά → ἀναπλήρωσις → ῥυθμισμένη ῥοή → νέα φθορά· ἡ συντήρησις περιέχει τὴν δυνατότητα τῆς ἰδίας ἐξασθενήσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:RLE-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RLE-04","text":"Κατάκλεισις → ἀναστροφὴ ῥοῆς → νόσος ἢ καταστροφή → γενεαλογικὴ διάγνωσις → δυνατὴ ἐπανόρθωσις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-06","MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RLE-05","text":"Σημεῖον → ἑρμηνεία → μεταβολὴ τοῦ ἑρμηνεύοντος → νέα σύνθεσις· ἡ ἐξήγησις ἐπανενεργεῖ ἐπὶ τοῦ πράγματος.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:RLE-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RLE-06","text":"Ὁ θετικὸς ἑλκυστὴς εἶναι ἡ ῥυθμισμένη ἀνταπόδοσις, ἥτις ἐπανέρχεται ὡς τάξις, ὑγίεια, φρόνησις, μνήμη καὶ ἐλευθερία.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:RLE-05","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RLE-07","text":"Ὁ ἀρνητικὸς ἑλκυστὴς εἶναι ἡ κατάκλεισις, ἐν ᾗ ἡ διατήρησις χάνει τὴν διέξοδον καὶ μεταστρέφεται εἰς αὐτοενισχυομένην διάλυσιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:RLE-06","MC_JP8E_L2_02:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:FGS-B01","text":"Διαφορά + δεκτικὸν πεδίον + αἰτιακὴ σχέσις → γενητὸν καὶ πολυμερὲς ὄν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:FGS-B02","text":"Παράδειγμα + τελεολογικὴ διοίκησις τῆς ἀνάγκης → κατανεμημένη μορφοποιὸς αἰτιότης.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:FGS-B03","text":"Κατανεμημένη αἰτιότης + σωματικαὶ ὁδοί → κίνησις, πάθος, σημεῖον καὶ ἐγγεγραμμένη μορφή.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:FGS-B04","text":"Μέτρον + διέξοδος + ἀμφίδρομος ἀνταπόδοσις → πρόσκαιρος τάξις, ὑγίεια καὶ φρόνησις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:FGS-B05","text":"Ἀσυμμετρία + κατάκλεισις + θετικὴ ἀνάδρασις → νόσος, λήθη, καταστροφὴ καὶ συστημικὴ λύσις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:FGS-B06","text":"Θραύσματα + σημεῖα + διαβαθμισμένη κρίσις → γενεαλογικὴ διάγνωσις τῆς ἀφανοῦς τάξεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:FGS-B07","text":"Διάγνωσις + παιδεία + ἐσωτερικὴ μεταβολή → ἐπανόρθωσις ἡ ὁποία παραμένει ἀνοικτὴ εἰς νέαν φθοράν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:AFM-B01","text":"Ἀκίνητον παράδειγμα → ἀδυναμία ἀμέσου ἐπιβολῆς → ἀνάγκη μεσιτείας.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B03","MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:AFM-B02","text":"Μεσιτεία → δεκτικὴ διαφορά → μορφοποιημένη σχεσιακὴ γένεσις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-07","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:AFM-B03","text":"Σχεσιακὴ γένεσις → σωματικὴ κίνησις → πάθος, ὄργανον, σημεῖον καὶ μνήμη.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:AFM-B04","text":"Μέτρον καὶ ἀνταπόδοσις → πρόσκαιρος συνοχὴ → ζωή, ὑγίεια καὶ πολιτικὴ τάξις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:AFM-B05","text":"Κατάκλεισις καὶ ἀσυμμετρία → ἀναστροφὴ τῆς γενέσεως → νόσος, κακία καὶ καταστροφή.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L2_02:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:AFM-B06","text":"Σύμπτωμα, θραῦσμα καὶ μαρτυρία → γενεαλογικὴ ἀνάγνωσις → διάγνωσις τῶν ἀφανῶν σχέσεων.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSA-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:AFM-B07","text":"Διάγνωσις → θεραπευτικὴ παιδεία → αὐτορρύθμισις → ἀνοικτὴ συνοχὴ ἢ νέα σπειροειδὴς φθορά.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L2_02:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:MME-01","text":"Κόσμος, πόλις καὶ σῶμα ἀναταξινομοῦνται ὡς διαφορετικαὶ κλίμακες διανεμημένης καὶ μεσιτευμένης παραγωγῆς.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MME-02","text":"Ἡ ὕλη παύει νὰ εἶναι παθητικὸν ὑπόλειμμα καὶ γίνεται ἐνεργὸς ἀντίστασις, ὁδὸς μεταδόσεως καὶ ἀρχεῖον μορφῶν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MME-03","text":"Ὑγίεια, τάξις καὶ ταυτότης γίνονται χρονικὰ καθεστῶτα ῥυθμίσεως, οὐχὶ σταθεραὶ οὐσίαι.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MME-04","text":"Ἡ γνώσις μεταβαίνει ἀπὸ κατοχὴν πραγμάτων εἰς ἀνακατασκευήν μηχανισμῶν καὶ ἐπανόρθωσιν σχέσεων.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MME-05","text":"Τὰ δίπολα φύσεως καὶ ἠθικῆς, ὑγιείας καὶ νόσου, σταθερότητος καὶ μεταβολῆς μετατρέπονται εἰς διαφορὰς φοράς, μέτρου καὶ ἀναδράσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MME-06","text":"Ἡ στρατηγικὴ μεταφέρεται ἀπὸ κατάληψιν ἀντικειμένων εἰς κατασκευήν τῶν ὅρων δι᾽ ὧν σχέσεις δύνανται νὰ αὐτορρυθμίζωνται.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-06","MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:ALT-B01","text":"Ἐπιφανειακῶς, πόλεις, πόλεμοι, ἄστρα, στοιχεῖα, τροφαί, χυμοί, πάθη, φάρμακα καὶ μορφαὶ φαίνονται ὡς διακεκριμένα ἀντικείμενα.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:ALT-B01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:ALT-B02","text":"Δομικῶς, τὰ ἀντικείμενα συνδέονται διὰ μεσότητος, ῥοῆς, δεκτικότητος, ὀργάνων, ἀρχείων καὶ ἀναδράσεων.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:ALT-B02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:ALT-B03","text":"Μεταστρατηγικῶς, ἡ πάλη ἀφορᾷ τίς ρυθμίζει τὰς μεσιτείας δι᾽ ὧν δύναμις, μνήμη καὶ πάθος γίνονται κυριαρχία ἢ αὐτορρύθμισις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:ALT-B03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:ALT-B04","text":"Ὀντολογικῶς καὶ χρονικῶς, τὸ ὂν εἶναι πρόσκαιρος σύστασις σχέσεων, ἣ διατηρεῖται καὶ λύεται διὰ σπειροειδοῦς αἰτιότητος.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:ALT-B04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:ALT-B05","text":"Μεθοδολογικῶς καὶ ἐπιτελεστικῶς, ὁ λόγος μιμεῖται τὴν μηχανὴν ἣν ἐξετάζει, διαιρῶν, συνδέων, ἀναστρέφων καὶ ἐπανασυνθέτων θραύσματα.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:ALT-B05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:FL-01","text":"Ἡ νοερὰ καὶ τελεολογικὴ διοίκησις προϋποθέτει ἐνεργὰ φυσικὰ αἴτια, ἀλλὰ οὐ διευκρινίζει πλήρως πῶς ἡ ὑπαγωγὴ διασῴζει τὴν αὐτονομίαν αὐτῶν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:FL-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:FL-02","text":"Ἡ ἱεραρχία χρειάζεται ἀσυμμετρίαν πρὸς διατήρησιν ἑνότητος, ἀλλ᾽ ἡ ἀμφίδρομος μεταβολὴ τῶν μερῶν ἀποσταθεροποιεῖ τὴν μόνιμον ἀξιακὴν τάξιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:FL-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:FL-03","text":"Ἡ σχεσιακὴ γενεαλογία μειοῖ τὴν καθαρὰν προσωπικὴν αἰτιότητα, ἀλλὰ τὸ σύστημα διατηρεῖ κρίσιν, παιδείαν καὶ πολιτικὴν εὐθύνην.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:FL-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:FL-04","text":"Ἡ ἀλήθεια ἀπαιτεῖ κοινὴν κρίσιν, ἀλλὰ τὰ θραύσματα, τὰ συμπτώματα καὶ τὰ ἀνίσως διανεμημένα ἀρχεῖα οὐ παρέχουσιν ἀκριβὲς καθολικὸν μέτρον.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:FL-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:FL-05","text":"Ὁ αὐτὸς μηχανισμὸς δύναται νὰ παράγῃ τάξιν ἢ διάλυσιν, ἀλλὰ τὸ ἀκριβὲς κατώφλιον τῆς μεταπτώσεως μένει ἀπροσδιόριστον.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MP-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:FL-06","text":"Ἡ ἐπέμβασις πρέπει συγχρόνως νὰ ἔχῃ ἰσχὺν λύσεως τῆς κατακλείσεως καὶ νὰ μὴν ἀντικαθιστᾷ τὴν αὐτορρύθμισιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MP-01","text":"Τὸ παράδειγμα μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ ἀΐδιον ἀρχέτυπον εἰς κανόνα ὀργανώσεως ὕλης, κινήσεως καὶ ἀντιστάσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MP-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MP-02","text":"Ἡ μνήμη μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ διατήρησιν τοῦ παρελθόντος εἰς σωματικὴν καὶ πολιτικὴν αἰτίαν τοῦ παρόντος.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MP-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MP-03","text":"Ἡ ἀλήθεια μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ ὀρθὴν παράστασιν εἰς μετασχηματιστικὴν πράξιν ἐπὶ τοῦ γιγνώσκοντος καὶ τοῦ γιγνωσκομένου.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MP-04","text":"Ἡ χώρα μεταλλάσσεται ἀπὸ ἄμορφον δεκτικὸν ὅρον εἰς κατανεμημένον δίκτυον σωματικῶν ὁδῶν καὶ μεταγραφῶν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MP-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MP-05","text":"Ἡ ἠθικὴ μεταλλάσσεται εἰς φυσιολογίαν κινήσεων, ἀλλὰ διατηρεῖ ἀμείωτον κανονιστικὴν φοράν πρὸς ὑγίειαν, φρόνησιν καὶ τάξιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:CGD-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:HSI-01","text":"Ἡ πραγματικότης εἶναι διαβαθμισμένη μηχανὴ ῥοῶν, μεταδόσεων καὶ μεταγραφῶν, ἐν ᾗ μορφὴ καὶ δύναμις ἀλληλοπαράγονται διὰ ἀντιστάσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:HSI-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:HSI-02","text":"Τὸ ὑποκείμενον εἶναι ἐνδοκοσμικὸς καὶ μεταβατικὸς κόμβος, οὔτε ἀμέτοχος κριτὴς οὔτε αὐτοκράτωρ τῶν κινήσεων αὐτοῦ.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:HSI-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:HSI-03","text":"Ὁ χρόνος εἶναι ρυθμικὴ καὶ γενεαλογικὴ σπείρα, ἐν ᾗ πᾶσα διατήρησις φέρει δυνατότητα φθορᾶς καὶ ἐπανορθώσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:HSI-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:HSI-04","text":"Ἡ μόνη δυνατὴ διαφυγὴ εἶναι ἐσωτερικὴ μεταβολὴ τῶν δεσμῶν: φύλαξις μνήμης, διάνοιξις ῥοῶν καὶ μετατροπὴ ἐχθρᾶς κρατήσεως εἰς φιλικὴν σύνταξιν.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:HSI-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:MRT-B01","text":"Τὰ ἀξιώματα τῆς σχεσιακῆς γενέσεως, τῆς μεσιτείας, τῆς σωματικῆς μεταγραφῆς, τῆς ρυθμισμένης ἀνταποδόσεως καὶ τῆς θεραπευτικῆς γνώσεως ἀρκοῦσι πρὸς ἀνακατασκευήν τοῦ ὅλου.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MRT-B01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MRT-B02","text":"Ἡ ἀρχιτεκτονικὴ εἶναι ἰσχυροτάτη ὅπου μορφή, σῶμα, ἀνάγκη, ἀναλογία, μνήμη καὶ ἀνασύνθεσις λειτουργοῦσιν ὡς μία σπειροειδὴς αἰτιότης.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MRT-B02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MRT-B03","text":"Ὑποκαθορισμένα μένουσιν ἡ σχέσις νοερᾶς διοικήσεως καὶ μηχανικῆς αὐτονομίας, τὸ ἀκριβὲς μέτρον ὀρθῆς κινήσεως καὶ τὸ ὅριον θεμιτῆς ἐπεμβάσεως.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MRT-B03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MRT-B04","text":"Ἡ συνοχὴ τοῦ συστήματος διατηρεῖται μόνον ἐὰν ἡ ἀνακατασκευή φυλάσσῃ τὴν ἀναδρομήν, καθ᾽ ἣν οἱ αὐτοὶ μηχανισμοὶ γίγνονται ζωτικοὶ ἢ νοσοποιοί.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MRT-B04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MRT-B05","text":"Εἰς ἀξιωματικὴν συμπίεσιν ἀνθίσταται ἡ παραγωγικὴ ἔντασις μεταξὺ ἀνάγκης, ἱεραρχίας, αὐτονομίας, παιδείας καὶ θνητῆς φθορᾶς· ἄνευ αὐτῆς ἡ μηχανὴ μένει σχῆμα ἀλλ᾽ οὐ κίνησις.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B05","MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261014T233711181572Z_058c41d2","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Timaeus","source_sha256":"aa8bcb0bc09f9be74630b74344a0dc1726230dd3cf99db473b842254b3a7f4fb","sha256":"81854a03bd5505855e50becce6796c61586ee658127555e9d1c45530d5aeead4","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-14T23:37:11.183623+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:BAS-01","text":"The system seeks to understand how cosmos, city, animal, and body are constituted in difference, mortality, and change, without reduction to an unmoving substance.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BAS-02","text":"Unity is recast as a hierarchical yet reciprocal mediation of relations, not as homogeneous identity or naked sovereignty.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BAS-03","text":"The paradigm and the end become active only because they are transcribed into bodies, movements, organs, institutions, and memories.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BAS-04","text":"Necessity and resistance are neither annulled nor remain outside order, but produce life or decay according to the measure of flow.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BAS-05","text":"Discourse becomes a graduated reconstruction that is practically true when it returns as education, diagnosis, therapy, and political judgment.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B06","MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BAS-06","text":"Time joins cosmic periodicity, bodily genealogy, and historical interruption into a spiral, in which return transforms what returns.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B07","MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BAS-07","text":"The movement of the whole is recursive: form generates flow, flow is disturbed, disturbance becomes a sign, and judgment undertakes a new form.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BAS-08","text":"Closure remains temporary and open, because every preservation bears within itself receptivity, decay, revision, and a new need for regulation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B08","MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Being and unity are produced from relations, movements, and functionally differentiated parts, not from a self-sufficient core.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Every sustainable order requires a mean, proportion, and a hierarchical interweaving of differences.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] The paradigm mutates into teleological and distributed causality, which governs necessity through its own resistances.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Mediation is a universal principle of transcription through which form, sensation, soul, memory, sign, and truth are connected.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] The body shifts from mediator of movements into a distributed archive, instrument, and site of inscription of natural and ethical history.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] Order, health, and practical wisdom are names for regulated reciprocity, in which discourse, flow, measure, and outlet are mutually sustained.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Generation and decay, virtue and vice, health and disease are produced by the same mechanism, differing in direction, measure, and feedback.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Time mutates into a multipart spiral of periodicity, decay, genealogical accumulation, interruption, and predisposition.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Truth is a graduated and therapeutic recomposition whose authority depends on mediations and on the power to transform the knower.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Receptivity and distance are not privations, but the conditions through which form, assimilation, and knowledge become possible.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-12","MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] The subject is a transitional node of bodies, flows, affections, memories, and judgments, altered by the relations it regulates.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-10","MC_JP8E_L2_02:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Freedom is cooperative self-regulation, navigation, and restoration within body, time, cosmos, and power.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-11","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:RMD-01","text":"Relation preserves its genetic identity, but extends from cosmic constitution into physiology, pathology, education, and politics.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RMD-02","text":"The mean mutates from a local bond into a universal mechanism of transmission, governance, signification, and truth.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RMD-03","text":"Soul and body cease to be opposed as governor and instrument, and are interwoven into a node of affection, judgment, and inscribed disposition.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RMD-04","text":"Motion migrates from a natural cause into a common criterion of knowledge, therapy, virtue, and cosmic assimilation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RMD-05","text":"Measure and symmetry retain their normative function, but change from static equality into dynamic reciprocity.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RMD-06","text":"Memory shifts from archival preservation into bodily causality, according to which the past returns as form, disposition, and decay.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RMD-07","text":"Destruction and disease are absorbed into a common schema of reversal, where preservation turns into self-feeding dissolution.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RMD-08","text":"Hierarchy reverses its function: from fixed superiority it becomes an art of channeling and mutual return.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RMD-09","text":"χώρα recedes as a proper name, but its function is dispersed into pathways, networks, and receptive bodies formed through resistance.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-07","MC_JP8E_L2_02:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:CGD-01","text":"Numerical, geometrical, astronomical, humoral, anatomical, and pharmacological arrangements remain local engines of exemplification.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:CGD-02","text":"At the present level, only the principles of distributed causality, flow, mediation, and functional difference are scaled up.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:CGD-03","text":"The body bridges cosmology, physiology, ethics, and politics because it transcribes invisible relations into movements, forms, and signs.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:CGD-04","text":"Time and memory bridge the units because they transform periodicity, disposition, and destruction into present causality.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:CGD-05","text":"Disturbance is scaled up from a local accident into a catalyst when the symptom compels a genealogical recomposition of the whole.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:CGD-06","text":"Self-sufficient substance and naked hierarchy become obsolete schemas and are recoded as products of relations and functional reciprocities.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:GNX-01","text":"Unity through homogeneity, mixture, or the purification of otherness is excluded.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GNX-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GNX-02","text":"Self-sufficient substance and an inert body are excluded, because identity and function are produced relationally.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GNX-03","text":"Linear time and one-way causality are excluded, because effects return as causes.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GNX-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GNX-04","text":"A bodiless soul, unmediated truth, and knowledge that does not transform life are excluded.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GNX-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:GNX-05","text":"Health as immobility and perfection as flight from body, desire, and multiplicity are excluded.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that a part, body, or quality can constitute an ultimate and unrelated subject.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:DBN-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that soul, body, city, memory, and cosmos have independent causal histories.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:DBN-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that decay and destruction befall from outside, rather than being produced from the reversal of generation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L2_02:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that freedom, therapy, or truth can be imposed from outside without time and internal change.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:DBN-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that assimilation requires identity or that hierarchy is preserved through the destruction of the lower.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:DBN-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:BSA-01","text":"The deepest absence is immediacy: paradigm, truth, sovereignty, and memory are never given without body, image, sign, or testimony.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSA-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BSA-02","text":"An unbroken archive and an exact universal measure are absent, such that every reconstruction remains selective and revisable.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSA-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BSA-03","text":"A pure outside and an unmoving center are ontologically impossible, because every governor is altered by the governed network.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSA-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BSA-04","text":"A final order free of decay is absent, because continuity contains disposition, interruption, and the possibility of a new dissolution.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSA-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:XD-01","text":"Paradigmatic causality can become real only if it is transcribed into relational bodily generation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-01","WB_JP8E:GA-03","WB_JP8E:GA-04","WB_JP8E:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:XD-02","text":"Historical destruction and bodily pathology are homologous reversals, in which the mechanism of preservation reinforces its own dissolution.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-03","WB_JP8E:GA-06","WB_JP8E:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:XD-03","text":"Archive and body are two forms of causal memory through which the absent past orders present form.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-05","WB_JP8E:GA-05","WB_JP8E:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:XD-04","text":"Interpretation, diagnosis, and therapy constitute one practice, because they recompose the invisible order and act back upon it.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-04","WB_JP8E:GA-04","WB_JP8E:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:XD-05","text":"Education, politics, and therapy are justified as constructions of conditions of self-regulation, not as external substitutions for the agent.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-03","WB_JP8E:GA-09","WB_JP8E:GA-11","WB_JP8E:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:XD-06","text":"Hierarchy retains systemic validity only when it functions as reciprocal channeling and not as one-way imposition.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-02","WB_JP8E:GA-02","WB_JP8E:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:XD-07","text":"Liberating intervention fails when force replaces internal regulation, but without force it is unable to undo enclosure.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-06","WB_JP8E:GA-07","WB_JP8E:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:XD-08","text":"Receptivity is not an empty substrate, but a distributed field of pathways and resistances that co-determines form.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:FGS-B02","WB_JP8E:GA-04","WB_JP8E:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:BSD-01","text":"Reality becomes a multilevel topology of transformations, in which cosmology, physiology, politics, and ethics are scales of one relational production.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSD-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-01","WB_JP8E:GA-01","WB_JP8E:GA-04","WB_JP8E:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BSD-02","text":"Time becomes threefold causality: a cycle of measurement, genealogical inscription, and interruption that compels a new synthesis.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSD-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-04","WB_JP8E:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BSD-03","text":"Sovereignty shifts from possession of power to governance of flows, transitions, memories, and conditions of self-regulation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSD-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-05","WB_JP8E:GA-04","WB_JP8E:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BSD-04","text":"Causality becomes a circuit, because an effect can return as a cause of preservation or decay.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GNX-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:FL-04","WB_JP8E:GA-06","WB_JP8E:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BSD-05","text":"Knowledge becomes a hierarchy of appropriate and interventional discourses, whose precision is judged according to the object and therapeutic effect.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSD-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-04","WB_JP8E:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BSD-06","text":"The agent becomes guardian of mediations, at once patient and healer, regulating networks by which it is itself constituted.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSD-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:HSI-02","WB_JP8E:GA-11","WB_JP8E:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:BSD-07","text":"Politics becomes an ecology of mediations, because struggle shifts to the conditions of producing memory, health, judgment, and self-regulation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-06","MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B03","WB_JP8E:GA-04","WB_JP8E:GA-06","WB_JP8E:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:RLE-01","text":"Paradigm → mediation → bodily form → measure → recognition of paradigm; the return preserves similarity through distance.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:RLE-01","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RLE-02","text":"Difference → resistance → power → form → new difference; the threat to order becomes material for its reproduction.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:RLE-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RLE-03","text":"Decay → replenishment → regulated flow → new decay; maintenance contains the possibility of its own weakening.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:RLE-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RLE-04","text":"Enclosure → reversal of flow → disease or destruction → genealogical diagnosis → possible restoration.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-06","MC_JP8E_L2_01:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RLE-05","text":"Sign → interpretation → transformation of the interpreter → new synthesis; explanation acts back upon the thing.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:RLE-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RLE-06","text":"The positive attractor is regulated reciprocity, which returns as order, health, practical wisdom, memory, and freedom.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:RLE-05","MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:RLE-07","text":"The negative attractor is enclosure, in which preservation loses its outlet and turns into self-reinforcing dissolution.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:RLE-06","MC_JP8E_L2_02:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:FGS-B01","text":"Difference + receptive field + causal relation → generated and multipart being.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:FGS-B02","text":"Paradigm + teleological governance of necessity → distributed formative causality.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:FGS-B03","text":"Distributed causality + bodily pathways → motion, affection, sign, and inscribed form.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:FGS-B04","text":"Measure + outlet + reciprocal return → temporary order, health, and practical wisdom.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:FGS-B05","text":"Asymmetry + enclosure + positive feedback → disease, forgetfulness, destruction, and systemic dissolution.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-09","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:FGS-B06","text":"Fragments + signs + graduated judgment → genealogical diagnosis of the invisible order.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:FGS-B07","text":"Diagnosis + education + internal change → restoration that remains open to new decay.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:AFM-B01","text":"Unmoving paradigm → impossibility of immediate imposition → necessity of mediation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B03","MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:AFM-B02","text":"Mediation → receptive difference → form-giving relational generation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:RMD-07","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:AFM-B03","text":"Relational generation → bodily motion → affection, instrument, sign, and memory.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:AS-B02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:AFM-B04","text":"Measure and reciprocity → temporary cohesion → life, health, and political order.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:AFM-B05","text":"Enclosure and asymmetry → reversal of generation → disease, vice, and destruction.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-06","MC_JP8E_L2_02:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:AFM-B06","text":"Symptom, fragment, and testimony → genealogical reading → diagnosis of invisible relations.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:BSA-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:AFM-B07","text":"Diagnosis → therapeutic education → self-regulation → open cohesion or new spiral decay.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-07","MC_JP8E_L2_02:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:MME-01","text":"Cosmos, city, and body are reclassified as different scales of distributed and mediated production.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MME-02","text":"Matter ceases to be a passive remainder and becomes active resistance, a pathway of transmission, and an archive of forms.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MME-03","text":"Health, order, and identity become temporal regimes of regulation, not stable substances.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MME-04","text":"Knowledge shifts from possession of things to reconstruction of mechanisms and restoration of relations.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MME-05","text":"The binaries of nature and ethics, health and disease, stability and change are transformed into differences of direction, measure, and feedback.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MME-06","text":"Strategy shifts from seizure of objects to construction of the conditions through which relations can self-regulate.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MME-06","MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:ALT-B01","text":"On the surface, cities, wars, stars, elements, foods, humors, affections, drugs, and forms appear as distinct objects.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:ALT-B01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:ALT-B02","text":"Structurally, objects are connected through mediation, flow, receptivity, instruments, archives, and feedback.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:ALT-B02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategically, struggle concerns who regulates the mediations through which power, memory, and affection become domination or self-regulation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:ALT-B03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:ALT-B04","text":"Ontologically and temporally, being is a temporary composition of relations, preserved and dissolved through spiral causality.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:ALT-B04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:ALT-B05","text":"Methodologically and performatively, discourse imitates the mechanism it examines, dividing, connecting, reversing, and recomposing fragments.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:ALT-B05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:FL-01","text":"Intellectual and teleological governance presupposes active natural causes, but does not fully clarify how subordination preserves their autonomy.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:FL-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:FL-02","text":"Hierarchy requires asymmetry to preserve unity, but the reciprocal transformation of the parts destabilizes permanent axiological order.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:FL-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:FL-03","text":"Relational genealogy diminishes pure personal causality, but the system retains judgment, education, and political responsibility.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:FL-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:FL-04","text":"Truth requires common judgment, but fragments, symptoms, and unequally distributed archives do not provide an exact universal measure.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:FL-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:FL-05","text":"The same mechanism can produce order or dissolution, but the precise threshold of transition remains indeterminate.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MP-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:FL-06","text":"Intervention must at once possess the power to undo enclosure and not replace self-regulation.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:XD-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MP-01","text":"The paradigm mutates from an eternal archetype into a principle for organizing matter, motion, and resistance.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MP-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MP-02","text":"Memory mutates from preservation of the past into a bodily and political cause of the present.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MP-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MP-03","text":"Truth mutates from correct representation into a transformative practice upon knower and known.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:GA-08","MC_JP8E_L2_02:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MP-04","text":"χώρα mutates from a formless receptive condition into a distributed network of bodily pathways and transcriptions.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MP-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MP-05","text":"Ethics mutates into a physiology of movements, yet retains an undiminished normative direction toward health, practical wisdom, and order.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:CGD-05","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:HSI-01","text":"Reality is a graduated mechanism of flows, transmissions, and transcriptions, in which form and power mutually produce one another through resistance.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:HSI-01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:HSI-02","text":"The subject is an intracosmic and transitional node, neither a detached judge nor an autocrat of its movements.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:HSI-02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:HSI-03","text":"Time is a rhythmic and genealogical spiral, in which every preservation bears the possibility of decay and restoration.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:HSI-03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:HSI-04","text":"The only possible escape is an internal transformation of bonds: preservation of memory, opening of flows, and conversion of hostile holding into friendly arrangement.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:HSI-04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"WB_JP8E:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms of relational generation, mediation, bodily transcription, regulated reciprocity, and therapeutic knowledge suffice for reconstruction of the whole.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MRT-B01","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest where form, body, necessity, proportion, memory, and recomposition function as a single spiral causality.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MRT-B02","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MRT-B03","text":"The relation of intellectual governance and mechanical autonomy, the exact measure of correct movement, and the limit of legitimate intervention remain underdetermined.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MRT-B03","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MRT-B04","text":"The system's coherence is maintained only if reconstruction preserves the recursion by which the same mechanisms become life-sustaining or disease-producing.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_01:MRT-B04","MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_JP8E:MRT-B05","text":"Axiomatic compression is resisted by the productive tension between necessity, hierarchy, autonomy, education, and mortal decay; without it, the mechanism remains a schema but not movement.","refs":["MC_JP8E_L2_02:MRT-B05","MC_JP8E_L2_01:AS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"WB_JP8E@R002","source_variant_id":"canonical:WB_JP8E:v1","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Timaeus"}],"identity":{"layer":"WB","kicker":"Whole book","title":"Timaeus","context":"","original_title":"Timaeus"},"original_title":"Timaeus","title_language_code":"en"}],"seal":"SEAL_JP8E_D4E7C02C74F3D467010B","version_work_id":"WORK_STORE_PERSEUS_GREEK_TLG0059_TLG031_PERSEUS_GRC2","version_label":"Added 14 Oct 2026 · Run 15 Oct 2026 · gpt-5.6-terra / gpt-5.6-sol","version_revision":"628978274356321e6d46f1ef570c50cc8b14060962efef6952d2022a0d0074a8","original_title":"Timaeus","title_language_code":"en","title_translation":{"schema":"care-title-heading-translation/1","language_code":"en","rendition_type":"machine","originals_preserved":true}},{"id":"WORK_STORE_CANON_PG7205","slug":"beta-b01befca9f426e10","title":"Thus Spoke Zarathustra","author":"Friedrich Nietzsche","edition":"Project Gutenberg transcription; print edition review required","year":"1885","original_year":1885,"publication_year":1885,"description":"","cover_url":"./assets/covers/beta-b01befca9f426e10-4818676456175297.png","source_url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/7205","source_notices":{"provider":"Project Gutenberg originals","source_url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/7205","licence":"https://www.gutenberg.org/policy/license.html","statement":"Project Gutenberg declares public domain in the USA; edition and local rights review required"},"language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","sources":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100","title":"1.","order":1,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_1100","text":"## Erster Theil\n\n## Zarathustra’s Vorrede.\n\n## 1.\n\nAls Zarathustra dreissig Jahr alt war, verliess er seine Heimat und den See seiner Heimat und ging in das Gebirge. Hier genoss er seines Geistes und seiner Einsamkeit und wurde dessen zehn Jahr nicht müde. Endlich aber verwandelte sich sein Herz,—und eines Morgens stand er mit der Morgenröthe auf, trat vor die Sonne hin und sprach zu ihr also:\n\n„Du grosses Gestirn! Was wäre dein Glück, wenn du nicht Die hättest, welchen du leuchtest!\n\nZehn Jahre kamst du hier herauf zu meiner Höhle: du würdest deines Lichtes und dieses Weges satt geworden sein, ohne mich, meinen Adler und meine Schlange.\n\nAber wir warteten deiner an jedem Morgen, nahmen dir deinen Überfluss ab und segneten dich dafür.\n\nSiehe! Ich bin meiner Weisheit überdrüssig, wie die Biene, die des Honigs zu viel gesammelt hat, ich bedarf der Hände, die sich ausstrecken.\n\nIch möchte verschenken und austheilen, bis die Weisen unter den Menschen wieder einmal ihrer Thorheit und die Armen einmal ihres Reichthums froh geworden sind.\n\nDazu muss ich in die Tiefe steigen: wie du des Abends thust, wenn du hinter das Meer gehst und noch der Unterwelt Licht bringst, du überreiches Gestirn!\n\nIch muss, gleich dir, untergehen , wie die Menschen es nennen, zu denen ich hinab will.\n\nSo segne mich denn, du ruhiges Auge, das ohne Neid auch ein allzugrosses Glück sehen kann!\n\nSegne den Becher, welcher überfliessen will, dass das Wasser golden aus ihm fliesse und überallhin den Abglanz deiner Wonne trage!\n\nSiehe! Dieser Becher will wieder leer werden, und Zarathustra will wieder Mensch werden.“\n\n—Also begann Zarathustra’s Untergang.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0001","text":"## Erster Theil","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0002","text":"## Zarathustra’s Vorrede.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0003","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0004","text":"Als Zarathustra dreissig Jahr alt war, verliess er seine Heimat und den See seiner Heimat und ging in das Gebirge. Hier genoss er seines Geistes und seiner Einsamkeit und wurde dessen zehn Jahr nicht müde. Endlich aber verwandelte sich sein Herz,—und eines Morgens stand er mit der Morgenröthe auf, trat vor die Sonne hin und sprach zu ihr also:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0005","text":"„Du grosses Gestirn! Was wäre dein Glück, wenn du nicht Die hättest, welchen du leuchtest!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0006","text":"Zehn Jahre kamst du hier herauf zu meiner Höhle: du würdest deines Lichtes und dieses Weges satt geworden sein, ohne mich, meinen Adler und meine Schlange.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0007","text":"Aber wir warteten deiner an jedem Morgen, nahmen dir deinen Überfluss ab und segneten dich dafür.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0008","text":"Siehe! Ich bin meiner Weisheit überdrüssig, wie die Biene, die des Honigs zu viel gesammelt hat, ich bedarf der Hände, die sich ausstrecken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0009","text":"Ich möchte verschenken und austheilen, bis die Weisen unter den Menschen wieder einmal ihrer Thorheit und die Armen einmal ihres Reichthums froh geworden sind.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0010","text":"Dazu muss ich in die Tiefe steigen: wie du des Abends thust, wenn du hinter das Meer gehst und noch der Unterwelt Licht bringst, du überreiches Gestirn!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0011","text":"Ich muss, gleich dir, untergehen , wie die Menschen es nennen, zu denen ich hinab will.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0012","text":"So segne mich denn, du ruhiges Auge, das ohne Neid auch ein allzugrosses Glück sehen kann!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0013","text":"Segne den Becher, welcher überfliessen will, dass das Wasser golden aus ihm fliesse und überallhin den Abglanz deiner Wonne trage!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0014","text":"Siehe! Dieser Becher will wieder leer werden, und Zarathustra will wieder Mensch werden.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0015","text":"—Also begann Zarathustra’s Untergang.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"10bec55e9fdab0536629b0483a4bfb03e36c9d2ea0cb98e98de3b01d95ff0252","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181017172960Z_92be2f8c","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1.","source_sha256":"10bec55e9fdab0536629b0483a4bfb03e36c9d2ea0cb98e98de3b01d95ff0252","sha256":"9823c771c6c36f2eaa26cec80a1d4a5d0c16d8288e469687a121a715d74b2425","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:10:17.172973+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0001","text":"## Part One","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1100:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0002","text":"## Zarathustra’s Prologue.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1100:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0003","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1100:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0004","text":"When Zarathustra was thirty years old, he left his home and the lake of his home and went into the mountains. There he enjoyed his spirit and his solitude, and for ten years he did not grow weary of them. But at last his heart changed,—and one morning he rose with the dawn, stepped before the sun, and spoke to it thus:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1100:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0005","text":"“You great star! What would your happiness be, if you did not have those for whom you shine!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1100:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0006","text":"For ten years you have come up here to my cave: you would have grown weary of your light and of this path, had it not been for me, my eagle, and my serpent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1100:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0007","text":"But we awaited you every morning, took your superabundance from you, and blessed you for it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1100:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0008","text":"Behold! I am weary of my wisdom, like the bee that has gathered too much honey; I need hands that stretch themselves out.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1100:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0009","text":"I would like to give away and distribute, until the wise among men have once again become glad in their folly, and the poor once again in their wealth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1100:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0010","text":"For that I must descend into the depths: as you do in the evening, when you go behind the sea and still bring light to the underworld, you over-rich star!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1100:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0011","text":"Like you, I must go under, as men call it, to whom I wish to descend.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1100:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0012","text":"So bless me then, you tranquil eye, which can behold even an all-too-great happiness without envy!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1100:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0013","text":"Bless the cup that wishes to overflow, so that the water may flow golden from it and everywhere bear the reflection of your delight!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1100:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0014","text":"Behold! This cup wishes to become empty again, and Zarathustra wishes to become man again.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1100:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0015","text":"—Thus began Zarathustra’s going-under.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1100:P0015"]}],"source_digest":"8274afb0facd10b4863949cde0e03a1132f5835aa8a7223a8a64acfde55c7b9a","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"Zarathustra’s Prologue.","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part One"],"reading_title":"Zarathustra’s Prologue · 1","reading_title_context":"Part One","reading_heading_titles":["Part One","Zarathustra’s Prologue.","1."],"original_title":"1."}],"source_title_context":"Zarathustra’s Vorrede.","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Erster Theil"],"reading_title":"Zarathustra’s Vorrede · 1","reading_title_context":"Erster Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Erster Theil","Zarathustra’s Vorrede.","1."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"1.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200","title":"2.","order":2,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_2200","text":"## 2.\n\nZarathustra stieg allein das Gebirge abwärts und Niemand begegnete ihm. Als er aber in die Wälder kam, stand auf einmal ein Greis vor ihm, der seine heilige Hütte verlassen hatte, um Wurzeln im Walde zu suchen. Und also sprach der Greis zu Zarathustra:\n\nNicht fremd ist mir dieser Wanderer: vor manchem Jahre gieng er hier vorbei. Zarathustra hiess er; aber er hat sich verwandelt. Damals trugst du deine Asche zu Berge: willst du heute dein Feuer in die Thäler tragen? Fürchtest du nicht des Brandstifters Strafen?\n\nJa, ich erkenne Zarathustra. Rein ist sein Auge, und an seinem Munde birgt sich kein Ekel. Geht er nicht daher wie ein Tänzer?\n\nVerwandelt ist Zarathustra, zum Kind ward Zarathustra, ein Erwachter ist Zarathustra: was willst du nun bei den Schlafenden?\n\nWie im Meere lebtest du in der Einsamkeit, und das Meer trug dich. Wehe, du willst an’s Land steigen? Wehe, du willst deinen Leib wieder selber schleppen?\n\nZarathustra antwortete: „Ich liebe die Menschen.“\n\nWarum, sagte der Heilige, gieng ich doch in den Wald und die Einöde? War es nicht, weil ich die Menschen allzu sehr liebte?\n\nJetzt liebe ich Gott: die Menschen liebe ich nicht. Der Mensch ist mir eine zu unvollkommene Sache. Liebe zum Menschen würde mich umbringen.\n\nZarathustra antwortete: „Was sprach ich von Liebe! Ich bringe den Menschen ein Geschenk.“\n\nGieb ihnen Nichts, sagte der Heilige. Nimm ihnen lieber Etwas ab und trage es mit ihnen—das wird ihnen am wohlsten thun: wenn er dir nur wohlthut!\n\nUnd willst du ihnen geben, so gieb nicht mehr, als ein Almosen, und lass sie noch darum betteln!\n\n„Nein, antwortete Zarathustra, ich gebe kein Almosen. Dazu bin ich nicht arm genug.“\n\nDer Heilige lachte über Zarathustra und sprach also: So sieh zu, dass sie deine Schätze annehmen! Sie sind misstrauisch gegen die Einsiedler und glauben nicht, dass wir kommen, um zu schenken.\n\nUnsre Schritte klingen ihnen zu einsam durch die Gassen. Und wie wenn sie Nachts in ihren Betten einen Mann gehen hören, lange bevor die Sonne aufsteht, so fragen sie sich wohl: wohin will der Dieb?\n\nGehe nicht zu den Menschen und bleibe im Walde! Gehe lieber noch zu den Thieren! Warum willst du nicht sein, wie ich,—ein Bär unter Bären, ein Vogel unter Vögeln?\n\n„Und was macht der Heilige im Walde?“ fragte Zarathustra.\n\nDer Heilige antwortete: Ich mache Lieder und singe sie, und wenn ich Lieder mache, lache, weine und brumme ich: also lobe ich Gott.\n\nMit Singen, Weinen, Lachen und Brummen lobe ich den Gott, der mein Gott ist. Doch was bringst du uns zum Geschenke?\n\nAls Zarathustra diese Worte gehört hatte, grüsste er den Heiligen und sprach: „Was hätte ich euch zu geben! Aber lasst mich schnell davon, dass ich euch Nichts nehme!“—Und so trennten sie sich von einander, der Greis und der Mann, lachend, gleichwie zwei Knaben lachen.\n\nAls Zarathustra aber allein war, sprach er also zu seinem Herzen: „Sollte es denn möglich sein! Dieser alte Heilige hat in seinem Walde noch Nichts davon gehört, dass Gott todt ist!“—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0002","text":"Zarathustra stieg allein das Gebirge abwärts und Niemand begegnete ihm. Als er aber in die Wälder kam, stand auf einmal ein Greis vor ihm, der seine heilige Hütte verlassen hatte, um Wurzeln im Walde zu suchen. Und also sprach der Greis zu Zarathustra:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0003","text":"Nicht fremd ist mir dieser Wanderer: vor manchem Jahre gieng er hier vorbei. Zarathustra hiess er; aber er hat sich verwandelt. Damals trugst du deine Asche zu Berge: willst du heute dein Feuer in die Thäler tragen? Fürchtest du nicht des Brandstifters Strafen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0004","text":"Ja, ich erkenne Zarathustra. Rein ist sein Auge, und an seinem Munde birgt sich kein Ekel. Geht er nicht daher wie ein Tänzer?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0005","text":"Verwandelt ist Zarathustra, zum Kind ward Zarathustra, ein Erwachter ist Zarathustra: was willst du nun bei den Schlafenden?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0006","text":"Wie im Meere lebtest du in der Einsamkeit, und das Meer trug dich. Wehe, du willst an’s Land steigen? Wehe, du willst deinen Leib wieder selber schleppen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0007","text":"Zarathustra antwortete: „Ich liebe die Menschen.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0008","text":"Warum, sagte der Heilige, gieng ich doch in den Wald und die Einöde? War es nicht, weil ich die Menschen allzu sehr liebte?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0009","text":"Jetzt liebe ich Gott: die Menschen liebe ich nicht. Der Mensch ist mir eine zu unvollkommene Sache. Liebe zum Menschen würde mich umbringen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0010","text":"Zarathustra antwortete: „Was sprach ich von Liebe! Ich bringe den Menschen ein Geschenk.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0011","text":"Gieb ihnen Nichts, sagte der Heilige. Nimm ihnen lieber Etwas ab und trage es mit ihnen—das wird ihnen am wohlsten thun: wenn er dir nur wohlthut!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0012","text":"Und willst du ihnen geben, so gieb nicht mehr, als ein Almosen, und lass sie noch darum betteln!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0013","text":"„Nein, antwortete Zarathustra, ich gebe kein Almosen. Dazu bin ich nicht arm genug.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0014","text":"Der Heilige lachte über Zarathustra und sprach also: So sieh zu, dass sie deine Schätze annehmen! Sie sind misstrauisch gegen die Einsiedler und glauben nicht, dass wir kommen, um zu schenken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0015","text":"Unsre Schritte klingen ihnen zu einsam durch die Gassen. Und wie wenn sie Nachts in ihren Betten einen Mann gehen hören, lange bevor die Sonne aufsteht, so fragen sie sich wohl: wohin will der Dieb?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0016","text":"Gehe nicht zu den Menschen und bleibe im Walde! Gehe lieber noch zu den Thieren! Warum willst du nicht sein, wie ich,—ein Bär unter Bären, ein Vogel unter Vögeln?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0017","text":"„Und was macht der Heilige im Walde?“ fragte Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0018","text":"Der Heilige antwortete: Ich mache Lieder und singe sie, und wenn ich Lieder mache, lache, weine und brumme ich: also lobe ich Gott.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0019","text":"Mit Singen, Weinen, Lachen und Brummen lobe ich den Gott, der mein Gott ist. Doch was bringst du uns zum Geschenke?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0020","text":"Als Zarathustra diese Worte gehört hatte, grüsste er den Heiligen und sprach: „Was hätte ich euch zu geben! Aber lasst mich schnell davon, dass ich euch Nichts nehme!“—Und so trennten sie sich von einander, der Greis und der Mann, lachend, gleichwie zwei Knaben lachen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0021","text":"Als Zarathustra aber allein war, sprach er also zu seinem Herzen: „Sollte es denn möglich sein! Dieser alte Heilige hat in seinem Walde noch Nichts davon gehört, dass Gott todt ist!“—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"29ec41108cb40bff45380e86761253341dedc5a3cdc09d83c12f0fd1dbabdc66","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181028098963Z_d3698a37","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2.","source_sha256":"29ec41108cb40bff45380e86761253341dedc5a3cdc09d83c12f0fd1dbabdc66","sha256":"c2f941c6f6a2ef5d8b58262e029f5ee153f9b7b17074d279e70276a2f748ea6b","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:10:28.098991+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2200:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0002","text":"Zarathustra descended the mountains alone, and no one encountered him. But when he came into the forests, suddenly an old man stood before him, who had left his holy hut in order to seek roots in the forest. And thus spoke the old man to Zarathustra:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2200:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0003","text":"This wanderer is not unknown to me: many years ago he passed by here. Zarathustra was his name; but he has changed. Then you carried your ashes to the mountains: do you wish today to carry your fire into the valleys? Do you not fear the punishments of the incendiary?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2200:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0004","text":"Yes, I recognize Zarathustra. Pure is his eye, and no disgust hides itself about his mouth. Does he not come along like a dancer?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2200:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0005","text":"Zarathustra is changed; Zarathustra has become a child; Zarathustra is one awakened: what do you now want among the sleepers?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2200:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0006","text":"Like one in the sea, you lived in solitude, and the sea bore you. Woe, you wish to go ashore? Woe, you wish once more to drag your body yourself?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2200:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0007","text":"Zarathustra answered: “I love mankind.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2200:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0008","text":"Why, said the Saint, did I go into the forest and the wilderness? Was it not because I loved mankind all too much?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2200:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0009","text":"Now I love God: I do not love mankind. Man is to me too imperfect a thing. Love for mankind would kill me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2200:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0010","text":"Zarathustra answered: “What did I say of love! I bring mankind a gift.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2200:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0011","text":"Give them nothing, said the Saint. Rather take something from them and carry it with them—that will do them most good: if only it does you good!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2200:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0012","text":"And if you wish to give them something, then give no more than an alms, and make them beg even for that!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2200:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0013","text":"“No,” answered Zarathustra, “I give no alms. For that I am not poor enough.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2200:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0014","text":"The Saint laughed at Zarathustra and spoke thus: Then see to it that they accept your treasures! They are mistrustful of hermits and do not believe that we come in order to give.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2200:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0015","text":"Our steps sound too solitary to them through the streets. And just as when at night in their beds they hear a man walking, long before the sun rises, they ask themselves: where is the thief going?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2200:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0016","text":"Do not go to men, and remain in the forest! Better yet, go to the animals! Why do you not wish to be like me,—a bear among bears, a bird among birds?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2200:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0017","text":"“And what does the Saint do in the forest?” asked Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2200:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0018","text":"The Saint answered: I make songs and sing them, and when I make songs, I laugh, weep, and growl: thus I praise God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2200:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0019","text":"With singing, weeping, laughing, and growling I praise the God who is my God. But what do you bring us as a gift?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2200:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0020","text":"When Zarathustra had heard these words, he greeted the Saint and said: “What would I have to give you! But let me quickly be gone, lest I take anything from you!”—And thus they parted from one another, the old man and the man, laughing, just as two boys laugh.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2200:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2200:P0021","text":"But when Zarathustra was alone, he spoke thus to his heart: “Could it indeed be possible! This old Saint has heard nothing in his forest that God is dead!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2200:P0021"]}],"source_digest":"6d524f5284b5e9ce31b89bd787ced2e6e9abd8f73bece1139f7a03fc0ffdd8f4","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"Zarathustra’s Prologue.","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part One"],"reading_title":"Zarathustra’s Prologue · 2","reading_title_context":"Part One","reading_heading_titles":["Part One","Zarathustra’s Prologue.","2."],"original_title":"2."}],"source_title_context":"Zarathustra’s Vorrede.","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Erster Theil"],"reading_title":"Zarathustra’s Vorrede · 2","reading_title_context":"Erster Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Erster Theil","Zarathustra’s Vorrede.","2."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"2.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300","title":"3.","order":3,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_3300","text":"## 3.\n\nAls Zarathustra in die nächste Stadt kam, die an den Wäldern liegt, fand er daselbst viel Volk versammelt auf dem Markte: denn es war verheissen worden, das man einen Seiltänzer sehen solle. Und Zarathustra sprach also zum Volke:\n\nIch lehre euch den Übermenschen. Der Mensch ist Etwas, das überwunden werden soll. Was habt ihr gethan, ihn zu überwinden?\n\n„Alle Wesen bisher schufen etwas über sich hinaus: und ihr wollt die Ebbe dieser großen Flut sein und lieber noch zum Thiere zurückgehen, als den Menschen zu überwinden?“\n\nWas ist der Affe für den Menschen? Ein Gelächter oder eine schmerzliche Scham. Und ebendas soll der Mensch für den Übermenschen sein: ein Gelächter oder eine schmerzliche Scham.\n\nIhr habt den Weg vom Wurme zum Menschen gemacht, und Vieles ist in euch noch Wurm. Einst wart ihr Affen, und auch jetzt ist der Mensch mehr Affe, als irgend ein Affe.\n\nWer aber der Weiseste von euch ist, der ist auch nur ein Zwiespalt und Zwitter von Pflanze und von Gespenst. Aber heisse ich euch zu Gespenstern oder Pflanzen werden?\n\nSeht, ich lehre euch den Übermenschen!\n\nDer Übermensch ist der Sinn der Erde. Euer Wille sage: der Übermensch sei der Sinn der Erde!\n\nIch beschwöre euch, meine Brüder, bleibt der Erde treu und glaubt Denen nicht, welche euch von überirdischen Hoffnungen reden! Giftmischer sind es, ob sie es wissen oder nicht.\n\nVerächter des Lebens sind es, Absterbende und selber Vergiftete, deren die Erde müde ist: so mögen sie dahinfahren!\n\nEinst war der Frevel an Gott der grösste Frevel, aber Gott starb, und damit auch diese Frevelhaften. An der Erde zu freveln ist jetzt das Furchtbarste und die Eingeweide des Unerforschlichen höher zu achten, als der Sinn der Erde!\n\nEinst blickte die Seele verächtlich auf den Leib: und damals war diese Verachtung das Höchste:—sie wollte ihn mager, grässlich, verhungert. So dachte sie ihm und der Erde zu entschlüpfen.\n\nOh diese Seele war selbst noch mager, grässlich und verhungert: und Grausamkeit war die Wollust dieser Seele!\n\nAber auch ihr noch, meine Brüder, sprecht mir: was kündet euer Leib von eurer Seele? Ist eure Seele nicht Armuth und Schmutz und ein erbärmliches Behagen?\n\nWahrlich, ein schmutziger Strom ist der Mensch. Man muss schon ein Meer sein, um einen schmutzigen Strom aufnehmen zu können, ohne unrein zu werden.\n\nSeht, ich lehre euch den Übermenschen: der ist diess Meer, in ihm kann eure grosse Verachtung untergehn.\n\nWas ist das Grösste, das ihr erleben könnt? Das ist die Stunde der grossen Verachtung. Die Stunde, in der euch auch euer Glück zum Ekel wird und ebenso eure Vernunft und eure Tugend.\n\nDie Stunde, wo ihr sagt: „Was liegt an meinem Glücke! Es ist Armuth und Schmutz, und ein erbärmliches Behagen. Aber mein Glück sollte das Dasein selber rechtfertigen!“\n\nDie Stunde, wo ihr sagt: „Was liegt an meiner Vernunft! Begehrt sie nach Wissen wie der Löwe nach seiner Nahrung? Sie ist Armuth und Schmutz und ein erbärmliches Behagen!“\n\nDie Stunde, wo ihr sagt: „Was liegt an meiner Tugend! Noch hat sie mich nicht rasen gemacht. Wie müde bin ich meines Guten und meines Bösen! Alles das ist Armuth und Schmutz und ein erbärmliches Behagen!“\n\nDie Stunde, wo ihr sagt: „Was liegt an meiner Gerechtigkeit! Ich sehe nicht, dass ich Gluth und Kohle wäre. Aber der Gerechte ist Gluth und Kohle!“\n\nDie Stunde, wo ihr sagt: „Was liegt an meinem Mitleiden! Ist nicht Mitleid das Kreuz, an das Der genagelt wird, der die Menschen liebt? Aber mein Mitleiden ist keine Kreuzigung.“\n\nSpracht ihr schon so? Schriet ihr schon so? Ach, dass ich euch schon so schreien gehört hatte!\n\nNicht eure Sünde—eure Genügsamkeit schreit gen Himmel, euer Geiz selbst in eurer Sünde schreit gen Himmel!\n\nWo ist doch der Blitz, der euch mit seiner Zunge lecke? Wo ist der Wahnsinn, mit dem ihr geimpft werden müsstet?\n\nSeht, ich lehre euch den Übermenschen: der ist dieser Blitz, der ist dieser Wahnsinn!—\n\nAls Zarathustra so gesprochen hatte, schrie Einer aus dem Volke: „Wir hörten nun genug von dem Seiltänzer; nun lasst uns ihn auch sehen!“ Und alles Volk lachte über Zarathustra. Der Seiltänzer aber, welcher glaubte, dass das Wort ihm gälte, machte sich an sein Werk.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0001","text":"## 3.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0002","text":"Als Zarathustra in die nächste Stadt kam, die an den Wäldern liegt, fand er daselbst viel Volk versammelt auf dem Markte: denn es war verheissen worden, das man einen Seiltänzer sehen solle. Und Zarathustra sprach also zum Volke:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0003","text":"Ich lehre euch den Übermenschen. Der Mensch ist Etwas, das überwunden werden soll. Was habt ihr gethan, ihn zu überwinden?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0004","text":"„Alle Wesen bisher schufen etwas über sich hinaus: und ihr wollt die Ebbe dieser großen Flut sein und lieber noch zum Thiere zurückgehen, als den Menschen zu überwinden?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0005","text":"Was ist der Affe für den Menschen? Ein Gelächter oder eine schmerzliche Scham. Und ebendas soll der Mensch für den Übermenschen sein: ein Gelächter oder eine schmerzliche Scham.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0006","text":"Ihr habt den Weg vom Wurme zum Menschen gemacht, und Vieles ist in euch noch Wurm. Einst wart ihr Affen, und auch jetzt ist der Mensch mehr Affe, als irgend ein Affe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0007","text":"Wer aber der Weiseste von euch ist, der ist auch nur ein Zwiespalt und Zwitter von Pflanze und von Gespenst. Aber heisse ich euch zu Gespenstern oder Pflanzen werden?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0008","text":"Seht, ich lehre euch den Übermenschen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0009","text":"Der Übermensch ist der Sinn der Erde. Euer Wille sage: der Übermensch sei der Sinn der Erde!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0010","text":"Ich beschwöre euch, meine Brüder, bleibt der Erde treu und glaubt Denen nicht, welche euch von überirdischen Hoffnungen reden! Giftmischer sind es, ob sie es wissen oder nicht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0011","text":"Verächter des Lebens sind es, Absterbende und selber Vergiftete, deren die Erde müde ist: so mögen sie dahinfahren!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0012","text":"Einst war der Frevel an Gott der grösste Frevel, aber Gott starb, und damit auch diese Frevelhaften. An der Erde zu freveln ist jetzt das Furchtbarste und die Eingeweide des Unerforschlichen höher zu achten, als der Sinn der Erde!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0013","text":"Einst blickte die Seele verächtlich auf den Leib: und damals war diese Verachtung das Höchste:—sie wollte ihn mager, grässlich, verhungert. So dachte sie ihm und der Erde zu entschlüpfen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0014","text":"Oh diese Seele war selbst noch mager, grässlich und verhungert: und Grausamkeit war die Wollust dieser Seele!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0015","text":"Aber auch ihr noch, meine Brüder, sprecht mir: was kündet euer Leib von eurer Seele? Ist eure Seele nicht Armuth und Schmutz und ein erbärmliches Behagen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0016","text":"Wahrlich, ein schmutziger Strom ist der Mensch. Man muss schon ein Meer sein, um einen schmutzigen Strom aufnehmen zu können, ohne unrein zu werden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0017","text":"Seht, ich lehre euch den Übermenschen: der ist diess Meer, in ihm kann eure grosse Verachtung untergehn.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0018","text":"Was ist das Grösste, das ihr erleben könnt? Das ist die Stunde der grossen Verachtung. Die Stunde, in der euch auch euer Glück zum Ekel wird und ebenso eure Vernunft und eure Tugend.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0019","text":"Die Stunde, wo ihr sagt: „Was liegt an meinem Glücke! Es ist Armuth und Schmutz, und ein erbärmliches Behagen. Aber mein Glück sollte das Dasein selber rechtfertigen!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0020","text":"Die Stunde, wo ihr sagt: „Was liegt an meiner Vernunft! Begehrt sie nach Wissen wie der Löwe nach seiner Nahrung? Sie ist Armuth und Schmutz und ein erbärmliches Behagen!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0021","text":"Die Stunde, wo ihr sagt: „Was liegt an meiner Tugend! Noch hat sie mich nicht rasen gemacht. Wie müde bin ich meines Guten und meines Bösen! Alles das ist Armuth und Schmutz und ein erbärmliches Behagen!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0022","text":"Die Stunde, wo ihr sagt: „Was liegt an meiner Gerechtigkeit! Ich sehe nicht, dass ich Gluth und Kohle wäre. Aber der Gerechte ist Gluth und Kohle!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0023","text":"Die Stunde, wo ihr sagt: „Was liegt an meinem Mitleiden! Ist nicht Mitleid das Kreuz, an das Der genagelt wird, der die Menschen liebt? Aber mein Mitleiden ist keine Kreuzigung.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0024","text":"Spracht ihr schon so? Schriet ihr schon so? Ach, dass ich euch schon so schreien gehört hatte!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0025","text":"Nicht eure Sünde—eure Genügsamkeit schreit gen Himmel, euer Geiz selbst in eurer Sünde schreit gen Himmel!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0026","text":"Wo ist doch der Blitz, der euch mit seiner Zunge lecke? Wo ist der Wahnsinn, mit dem ihr geimpft werden müsstet?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0027","text":"Seht, ich lehre euch den Übermenschen: der ist dieser Blitz, der ist dieser Wahnsinn!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0028","text":"Als Zarathustra so gesprochen hatte, schrie Einer aus dem Volke: „Wir hörten nun genug von dem Seiltänzer; nun lasst uns ihn auch sehen!“ Und alles Volk lachte über Zarathustra. Der Seiltänzer aber, welcher glaubte, dass das Wort ihm gälte, machte sich an sein Werk.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"f73387e7d47d0e846a222e7453fd09b20e5087ab86bea465433506a15b16665f","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181041645975Z_1e23ff19","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"3.","source_sha256":"f73387e7d47d0e846a222e7453fd09b20e5087ab86bea465433506a15b16665f","sha256":"fd50b3f824c37854ba66c347a9bea6c1018f32ecd3f272bc8687aab8695021b3","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:10:41.645999+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0001","text":"## 3.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0002","text":"When Zarathustra came to the next town, which lies by the forests, he found there a great multitude assembled in the marketplace: for it had been promised that they would see a rope-dancer. And Zarathustra spoke thus to the people:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0003","text":"I teach you the Overman. Man is something that shall be overcome. What have you done to overcome him?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0004","text":"“All beings hitherto created something beyond themselves: and you want to be the ebb of this great flood and would rather return to the beast than overcome man?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0005","text":"What is the ape to man? A laughing-stock or a painful shame. And just that shall man be to the Overman: a laughing-stock or a painful shame.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0006","text":"You have made the way from the worm to man, and much in you is still worm. Once you were apes, and even now man is more ape than any ape.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0007","text":"But whoever among you is wisest is also only a discord and hybrid of plant and phantom. But do I bid you become phantoms or plants?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0008","text":"Behold, I teach you the Overman!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0009","text":"The Overman is the meaning of the earth. Let your will say: the Overman shall be the meaning of the earth!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0010","text":"I beseech you, my brothers, remain faithful to the earth and do not believe those who speak to you of supra-terrestrial hopes! They are poisoners, whether they know it or not.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0011","text":"They are despisers of life, moribund and themselves poisoned, of whom the earth is weary: so let them pass away!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0012","text":"Once the sacrilege against God was the greatest sacrilege, but God died, and with him these sacrilegious ones. To commit sacrilege against the earth is now the most terrible thing, and to value the entrails of the unfathomable more highly than the meaning of the earth!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0013","text":"Once the soul looked contemptuously upon the body: and at that time this contempt was the highest thing:—it wanted the body lean, ghastly, starved. Thus it thought to escape from it and from the earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0014","text":"Oh, this soul itself was still lean, ghastly, and starved: and cruelty was the voluptuousness of this soul!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0015","text":"But you too still, my brothers, tell me: what does your body proclaim of your soul? Is not your soul poverty and filth and a miserable contentment?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0016","text":"Truly, man is a filthy stream. One must already be a sea in order to be able to receive a filthy stream without becoming unclean.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0017","text":"Behold, I teach you the Overman: he is this sea; in him your great contempt can go under.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0018","text":"What is the greatest thing that you can experience? It is the hour of great contempt. The hour in which even your happiness becomes loathsome to you, and likewise your reason and your virtue.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0019","text":"The hour when you say: “What matters my happiness! It is poverty and filth, and a miserable contentment. But my happiness ought to justify existence itself!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0020","text":"The hour when you say: “What matters my reason! Does it desire knowledge as the lion desires its food? It is poverty and filth and a miserable contentment!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0021","text":"The hour when you say: “What matters my virtue! It has not yet made me rage. How weary I am of my good and my evil! All that is poverty and filth and a miserable contentment!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0022","text":"The hour when you say: “What matters my justice! I do not see that I am blaze and coal. But the just man is blaze and coal!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0023","text":"The hour when you say: “What matters my compassion! Is not compassion the cross upon which he who loves mankind is nailed? But my compassion is no crucifixion.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0024","text":"Have you ever spoken thus? Have you ever cried out thus? Ah, that I had already heard you cry out thus!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0025","text":"Not your sin—your contentment cries out to heaven, your avarice even in your sin cries out to heaven!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0026","text":"Where then is the lightning that would lick you with its tongue? Where is the madness with which you would have to be inoculated?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0027","text":"Behold, I teach you the overman: he is this lightning, he is this madness!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3300:P0028","text":"When Zarathustra had thus spoken, one of the people cried out: “We have now heard enough of the tightrope-walker; now let us see him too!” And all the people laughed at Zarathustra. But the tightrope-walker, who believed that the word was meant for him, set about his work.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3300:P0028"]}],"source_digest":"feaa2dd6ad972c31e3aeaf7d72092d3a30c59ed5961fadd7b881c0fa084f3d20","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"Zarathustra’s Prologue.","source_section_label":"3.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part One"],"reading_title":"Zarathustra’s Prologue · 3","reading_title_context":"Part One","reading_heading_titles":["Part One","Zarathustra’s Prologue.","3."],"original_title":"3."}],"source_title_context":"Zarathustra’s Vorrede.","source_section_label":"3.","source_title_hierarchy":["Erster Theil"],"reading_title":"Zarathustra’s Vorrede · 3","reading_title_context":"Erster Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Erster Theil","Zarathustra’s Vorrede.","3."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"3.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400","title":"4.","order":4,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_4400","text":"## 4.\n\nZarathustra aber sahe das Volk an und wunderte sich. Dann sprach er also:\n\nDer Mensch ist ein Seil, geknüpft zwischen Thier und Übermensch,—ein Seil über einem Abgrunde.\n\nEin gefährliches Hinüber, ein gefährliches Auf-dem-Wege, ein gefährliches Zurückblicken, ein gefährliches Schaudern und Stehenbleiben.\n\nWas gross ist am Menschen, das ist, dass er eine Brücke und kein Zweck ist: was geliebt werden kann am Menschen, das ist, dass er ein Übergang und ein Untergang ist.\n\nIch liebe Die, welche nicht zu leben wissen, es sei denn als Untergehende, denn es sind die Hinübergehenden.\n\nIch liebe die grossen Verachtenden, weil sie die grossen Verehrenden sind und Pfeile der Sehnsucht nach dem andern Ufer.\n\nIch liebe Die, welche nicht erst hinter den Sternen einen Grund suchen, unterzugehen und Opfer zu sein: sondern die sich der Erde opfern, dass die Erde einst der Übermenschen werde.\n\nIch liebe Den, welcher lebt, damit er erkenne, und welcher erkennen will, damit einst der Übermensch lebe. Und so will er seinen Untergang.\n\nIch liebe Den, welcher arbeitet und erfindet, dass er dem Übermenschen das Haus baue und zu ihm Erde, Thier und Pflanze vorbereite: denn so will er seinen Untergang.\n\nIch liebe Den, welcher seine Tugend liebt: denn Tugend ist Wille zum Untergang und ein Pfeil der Sehnsucht.\n\nIch liebe Den, welcher nicht einen Tropfen Geist für sich zurückbehält, sondern ganz der Geist seiner Tugend sein will: so schreitet er als Geist über die Brücke.\n\nIch liebe Den, welcher aus seiner Tugend seinen Hang und sein Verhängniss macht: so will er um seiner Tugend willen noch leben und nicht mehr leben.\n\nIch liebe Den, welcher nicht zu viele Tugenden haben will. Eine Tugend ist mehr Tugend, als zwei, weil sie mehr Knoten ist, an den sich das Verhängniss hängt.\n\nIch liebe Den, dessen Seele sich verschwendet, der nicht Dank haben will und nicht zurückgiebt: denn er schenkt immer und will sich nicht bewahren.\n\nIch liebe Den, welcher sich schämt, wenn der Würfel zu seinem Glücke fällt und der dann fragt: bin ich denn ein falscher Spieler?—denn er will zu Grunde gehen.\n\nIch liebe Den, welcher goldne Worte seinen Thaten voraus wirft und immer noch mehr hält, als er verspricht: denn er will seinen Untergang.\n\nIch liebe Den, welcher die Zukünftigen rechtfertigt und die Vergangenen erlöst: denn er will an den Gegenwärtigen zu Grunde gehen.\n\nIch liebe Den, welcher seinen Gott züchtigt, weil er seinen Gott liebt: denn er muss am Zorne seines Gottes zu Grunde gehen.\n\nIch liebe Den, dessen Seele tief ist auch in der Verwundung, und der an einem kleinen Erlebnisse zu Grunde gehen kann: so geht er gerne über die Brücke.\n\nIch liebe Den, dessen Seele übervoll ist, so dass er sich selber vergisst, und alle Dinge in ihm sind: so werden alle Dinge sein Untergang.\n\nIch liebe Den, der freien Geistes und freien Herzes ist: so ist sein Kopf nur das Eingeweide seines Herzens, sein Herz aber treibt ihn zum Untergang.\n\nIch liebe alle Die, welche schwere Tropfen sind, einzeln fallend aus der dunklen Wolke, die über den Menschen hängt: sie verkündigen, dass der Blitz kommt, und gehn als Verkündiger zu Grunde.\n\nSeht, ich bin ein Verkündiger des Blitzes und ein schwerer Tropfen aus der Wolke: dieser Blitz aber heisst Übermensch.—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0001","text":"## 4.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0002","text":"Zarathustra aber sahe das Volk an und wunderte sich. Dann sprach er also:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0003","text":"Der Mensch ist ein Seil, geknüpft zwischen Thier und Übermensch,—ein Seil über einem Abgrunde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0004","text":"Ein gefährliches Hinüber, ein gefährliches Auf-dem-Wege, ein gefährliches Zurückblicken, ein gefährliches Schaudern und Stehenbleiben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0005","text":"Was gross ist am Menschen, das ist, dass er eine Brücke und kein Zweck ist: was geliebt werden kann am Menschen, das ist, dass er ein Übergang und ein Untergang ist.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0006","text":"Ich liebe Die, welche nicht zu leben wissen, es sei denn als Untergehende, denn es sind die Hinübergehenden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0007","text":"Ich liebe die grossen Verachtenden, weil sie die grossen Verehrenden sind und Pfeile der Sehnsucht nach dem andern Ufer.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0008","text":"Ich liebe Die, welche nicht erst hinter den Sternen einen Grund suchen, unterzugehen und Opfer zu sein: sondern die sich der Erde opfern, dass die Erde einst der Übermenschen werde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0009","text":"Ich liebe Den, welcher lebt, damit er erkenne, und welcher erkennen will, damit einst der Übermensch lebe. Und so will er seinen Untergang.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0010","text":"Ich liebe Den, welcher arbeitet und erfindet, dass er dem Übermenschen das Haus baue und zu ihm Erde, Thier und Pflanze vorbereite: denn so will er seinen Untergang.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0011","text":"Ich liebe Den, welcher seine Tugend liebt: denn Tugend ist Wille zum Untergang und ein Pfeil der Sehnsucht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0012","text":"Ich liebe Den, welcher nicht einen Tropfen Geist für sich zurückbehält, sondern ganz der Geist seiner Tugend sein will: so schreitet er als Geist über die Brücke.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0013","text":"Ich liebe Den, welcher aus seiner Tugend seinen Hang und sein Verhängniss macht: so will er um seiner Tugend willen noch leben und nicht mehr leben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0014","text":"Ich liebe Den, welcher nicht zu viele Tugenden haben will. Eine Tugend ist mehr Tugend, als zwei, weil sie mehr Knoten ist, an den sich das Verhängniss hängt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0015","text":"Ich liebe Den, dessen Seele sich verschwendet, der nicht Dank haben will und nicht zurückgiebt: denn er schenkt immer und will sich nicht bewahren.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0016","text":"Ich liebe Den, welcher sich schämt, wenn der Würfel zu seinem Glücke fällt und der dann fragt: bin ich denn ein falscher Spieler?—denn er will zu Grunde gehen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0017","text":"Ich liebe Den, welcher goldne Worte seinen Thaten voraus wirft und immer noch mehr hält, als er verspricht: denn er will seinen Untergang.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0018","text":"Ich liebe Den, welcher die Zukünftigen rechtfertigt und die Vergangenen erlöst: denn er will an den Gegenwärtigen zu Grunde gehen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0019","text":"Ich liebe Den, welcher seinen Gott züchtigt, weil er seinen Gott liebt: denn er muss am Zorne seines Gottes zu Grunde gehen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0020","text":"Ich liebe Den, dessen Seele tief ist auch in der Verwundung, und der an einem kleinen Erlebnisse zu Grunde gehen kann: so geht er gerne über die Brücke.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0021","text":"Ich liebe Den, dessen Seele übervoll ist, so dass er sich selber vergisst, und alle Dinge in ihm sind: so werden alle Dinge sein Untergang.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0022","text":"Ich liebe Den, der freien Geistes und freien Herzes ist: so ist sein Kopf nur das Eingeweide seines Herzens, sein Herz aber treibt ihn zum Untergang.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0023","text":"Ich liebe alle Die, welche schwere Tropfen sind, einzeln fallend aus der dunklen Wolke, die über den Menschen hängt: sie verkündigen, dass der Blitz kommt, und gehn als Verkündiger zu Grunde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0024","text":"Seht, ich bin ein Verkündiger des Blitzes und ein schwerer Tropfen aus der Wolke: dieser Blitz aber heisst Übermensch.—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"8506800faa74ce64bb2fbb7692b7d296a69de0d9d1f2ff4b505bf8d6b65831d6","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181050927225Z_e321e205","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"4.","source_sha256":"8506800faa74ce64bb2fbb7692b7d296a69de0d9d1f2ff4b505bf8d6b65831d6","sha256":"38cf265841df0e8893b135b856fa6842ae3e33d1eec3fd041128017ab5d2457f","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:10:50.927262+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0001","text":"## 4.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0002","text":"But Zarathustra looked at the people and wondered. Then he spoke thus:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0003","text":"Man is a rope, tied between beast and Overman,—a rope over an abyss.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0004","text":"A dangerous crossing-over, a dangerous being-on-the-way, a dangerous looking-back, a dangerous shuddering and standing-still.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0005","text":"What is great in man is that he is a bridge and not an end: what can be loved in man is that he is a transition and a going-under.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0006","text":"I love those who do not know how to live except as those who go under, for they are those who cross over.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0007","text":"I love the great despisers, because they are the great reverers and arrows of longing for the other shore.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0008","text":"I love those who do not first seek beyond the stars a reason to go under and to be a sacrifice: but who sacrifice themselves to the earth, that the earth may one day become the earth of the Overman.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0009","text":"I love him who lives in order that he may know, and who wants to know in order that the Overman may one day live. And thus he wills his going-under.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0010","text":"I love him who labours and invents, that he may build the house for the Overman and prepare earth, beast, and plant for him: for thus he wills his going-under.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0011","text":"I love him who loves his virtue: for virtue is will to going-under and an arrow of longing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0012","text":"I love him who keeps back not a drop of spirit for himself, but wants to be wholly the spirit of his virtue: thus he strides as spirit over the bridge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0013","text":"I love him who makes of his virtue his inclination and his destiny: thus, for the sake of his virtue, he wills still to live and no longer to live.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0014","text":"I love him who does not want to have too many virtues. One virtue is more virtue than two, because it is more of a knot upon which destiny hangs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0015","text":"I love him whose soul squanders itself, who wants no thanks and gives nothing back: for he always gives and does not want to preserve himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0016","text":"I love him who is ashamed when the die falls in his favour and who then asks: am I then a false player?—for he wants to perish.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0017","text":"I love him who casts golden words before his deeds and always fulfils still more than he promises: for he wills his going-under.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0018","text":"I love him who justifies those to come and redeems those who have passed: for he wants to perish through those who are present.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0019","text":"I love him who chastises his god because he loves his god: for he must perish through the wrath of his god.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0020","text":"I love him whose soul is deep even in wounding, and who can perish from a small experience: thus he gladly goes over the bridge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0021","text":"I love him whose soul is overfull, so that he forgets himself, and all things are in him: thus all things become his going-under.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0022","text":"I love him who is of free spirit and free heart: thus his head is only the entrail of his heart, but his heart drives him to going-under.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0023","text":"I love all those who are heavy drops, falling one by one from the dark cloud that hangs over man: they proclaim that lightning is coming, and go under as proclaimers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4400:P0024","text":"Behold, I am a proclaimer of lightning and a heavy drop from the cloud: but this lightning is called Overman.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4400:P0024"]}],"source_digest":"226a5a3eac913af31eaee854ed632958aae2468a1d9f474bc0b95e8a4b3be998","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"Zarathustra’s Prologue.","source_section_label":"4.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part One"],"reading_title":"Zarathustra’s Prologue · 4","reading_title_context":"Part One","reading_heading_titles":["Part One","Zarathustra’s Prologue.","4."],"original_title":"4."}],"source_title_context":"Zarathustra’s Vorrede.","source_section_label":"4.","source_title_hierarchy":["Erster Theil"],"reading_title":"Zarathustra’s Vorrede · 4","reading_title_context":"Erster Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Erster Theil","Zarathustra’s Vorrede.","4."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"4.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500","title":"5.","order":5,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_5500","text":"## 5.\n\nAls Zarathustra diese Worte gesprochen hatte, sahe er wieder das Volk an und schwieg. „Da stehen sie“, sprach er zu seinem Herzen, „da lachen sie: sie verstehen mich nicht, ich bin nicht der Mund für diese Ohren.\n\nMuss man ihnen erst die Ohren zerschlagen, dass sie lernen, mit den Augen hören. Muss man rasseln gleich Pauken und Busspredigern? Oder glauben sie nur dem Stammelnden?\n\nSie haben etwas, worauf sie stolz sind. Wie nennen sie es doch, was sie stolz macht? Bildung nennen sie’s, es zeichnet sie aus vor den Ziegenhirten.\n\nDrum hören sie ungern von sich das Wort „Verachtung“. So will ich denn zu ihrem Stolze reden.\n\nSo will ich ihnen vom Verächtlichsten sprechen: das aber ist der letzte Mensch .“\n\nUnd also sprach Zarathustra zum Volke:\n\nEs ist an der Zeit, dass der Mensch sich sein Ziel stecke. Es ist an der Zeit, dass der Mensch den Keim seiner höchsten Hoffnung pflanze.\n\nNoch ist sein Boden dazu reich genug. Aber dieser Boden wird einst arm und zahm sein, und kein hoher Baum wird mehr aus ihm wachsen können.\n\nWehe! Es kommt die Zeit, wo der Mensch nicht mehr den Pfeil seiner Sehnsucht über den Menschen hinaus wirft, und die Sehne seines Bogens verlernt hat, zu schwirren!\n\nIch sage euch: man muss noch Chaos in sich haben, um einen tanzenden Stern gebären zu können. Ich sage euch: ihr habt noch Chaos in euch.\n\nWehe! Es kommt die Zeit, wo der Mensch keinen Stern mehr gebären wird. Wehe! Es kommt die Weit des verächtlichsten Menschen, der sich selber nicht mehr verachten kann.\n\nSeht! Ich zeige euch den letzten Menschen .\n\n„Was ist Liebe? Was ist Schöpfung? Was ist Sehnsucht? Was ist Stern“—so fragt der letzte Mensch und blinzelt.\n\nDie Erde ist dann klein geworden, und auf ihr hüpft der letzte Mensch, der Alles klein macht. Sein Geschlecht ist unaustilgbar, wie der Erdfloh; der letzte Mensch lebt am längsten.\n\n„Wir haben das Glück erfunden“—sagen die letzten Menschen und blinzeln.\n\nSie haben die Gegenden verlassen, wo es hart war zu leben: denn man braucht Wärme. Man liebt noch den Nachbar und reibt sich an ihm: denn man braucht Wärme.\n\nKrankwerden und Misstrauen-haben gilt ihnen sündhaft: man geht achtsam einher. Ein Thor, der noch über Steine oder Menschen stolpert!\n\nEin wenig Gift ab und zu: das macht angenehme Träume. Und viel Gift zuletzt, zu einem angenehmen Sterben.\n\nMan arbeitet noch, denn Arbeit ist eine Unterhaltung. Aber man sorgt, dass die Unterhaltung nicht angreife.\n\nMan wird nicht mehr arm und reich: Beides ist zu beschwerlich. Wer will noch regieren? Wer noch gehorchen? Beides ist zu beschwerlich.\n\nKein Hirt und Eine Heerde! Jeder will das Gleiche, Jeder ist gleich: wer anders fühlt, geht freiwillig in’s Irrenhaus.\n\n„Ehemals war alle Welt irre“—sagen die Feinsten und blinzeln.\n\nMan ist klug und weiss Alles, was geschehn ist: so hat man kein Ende zu spotten. Man zankt sich noch, aber man versöhnt sich bald—sonst verdirbt es den Magen.\n\nMan hat sein Lüstchen für den Tag und sein Lüstchen für die Nacht: aber man ehrt die Gesundheit.\n\n„Wir haben das Glück erfunden“—sagen die letzten Menschen und blinzeln—\n\nUnd hier endete die erste Rede Zarathustra’s, welche man auch „die Vorrede“ heisst: denn an dieser Stelle unterbrach ihn das Geschrei und die Lust der Menge. „Gieb uns diesen letzten Menschen, oh Zarathustra,—so riefen sie—mache uns zu diesen letzten Menschen! So schenken wir dir den Übermenschen!“ Und alles Volk jubelte und schnalzte mit der Zunge. Zarathustra aber wurde traurig und sagte zu seinem Herzen:\n\nSie verstehen mich nicht: ich bin nicht der Mund für diese Ohren.\n\nZu lange wohl lebte ich im Gebirge, zu viel horchte ich auf Bäche und Bäume: nun rede ich ihnen gleich den Ziegenhirten.\n\nUnbewegt ist meine Seele und hell wie das Gebirge am Vormittag. Aber sie meinen, ich sei kalt und ein Spötter in furchtbaren Spässen.\n\nUnd nun blicken sie mich an und lachen: und indem sie lachen, hassen sie mich noch. Es ist Eis in ihrem Lachen.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0001","text":"## 5.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0002","text":"Als Zarathustra diese Worte gesprochen hatte, sahe er wieder das Volk an und schwieg. „Da stehen sie“, sprach er zu seinem Herzen, „da lachen sie: sie verstehen mich nicht, ich bin nicht der Mund für diese Ohren.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0003","text":"Muss man ihnen erst die Ohren zerschlagen, dass sie lernen, mit den Augen hören. Muss man rasseln gleich Pauken und Busspredigern? Oder glauben sie nur dem Stammelnden?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0004","text":"Sie haben etwas, worauf sie stolz sind. Wie nennen sie es doch, was sie stolz macht? Bildung nennen sie’s, es zeichnet sie aus vor den Ziegenhirten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0005","text":"Drum hören sie ungern von sich das Wort „Verachtung“. So will ich denn zu ihrem Stolze reden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0006","text":"So will ich ihnen vom Verächtlichsten sprechen: das aber ist der letzte Mensch .“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0007","text":"Und also sprach Zarathustra zum Volke:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0008","text":"Es ist an der Zeit, dass der Mensch sich sein Ziel stecke. Es ist an der Zeit, dass der Mensch den Keim seiner höchsten Hoffnung pflanze.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0009","text":"Noch ist sein Boden dazu reich genug. Aber dieser Boden wird einst arm und zahm sein, und kein hoher Baum wird mehr aus ihm wachsen können.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0010","text":"Wehe! Es kommt die Zeit, wo der Mensch nicht mehr den Pfeil seiner Sehnsucht über den Menschen hinaus wirft, und die Sehne seines Bogens verlernt hat, zu schwirren!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0011","text":"Ich sage euch: man muss noch Chaos in sich haben, um einen tanzenden Stern gebären zu können. Ich sage euch: ihr habt noch Chaos in euch.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0012","text":"Wehe! Es kommt die Zeit, wo der Mensch keinen Stern mehr gebären wird. Wehe! Es kommt die Weit des verächtlichsten Menschen, der sich selber nicht mehr verachten kann.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0013","text":"Seht! Ich zeige euch den letzten Menschen .","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0014","text":"„Was ist Liebe? Was ist Schöpfung? Was ist Sehnsucht? Was ist Stern“—so fragt der letzte Mensch und blinzelt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0015","text":"Die Erde ist dann klein geworden, und auf ihr hüpft der letzte Mensch, der Alles klein macht. Sein Geschlecht ist unaustilgbar, wie der Erdfloh; der letzte Mensch lebt am längsten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0016","text":"„Wir haben das Glück erfunden“—sagen die letzten Menschen und blinzeln.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0017","text":"Sie haben die Gegenden verlassen, wo es hart war zu leben: denn man braucht Wärme. Man liebt noch den Nachbar und reibt sich an ihm: denn man braucht Wärme.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0018","text":"Krankwerden und Misstrauen-haben gilt ihnen sündhaft: man geht achtsam einher. Ein Thor, der noch über Steine oder Menschen stolpert!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0019","text":"Ein wenig Gift ab und zu: das macht angenehme Träume. Und viel Gift zuletzt, zu einem angenehmen Sterben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0020","text":"Man arbeitet noch, denn Arbeit ist eine Unterhaltung. Aber man sorgt, dass die Unterhaltung nicht angreife.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0021","text":"Man wird nicht mehr arm und reich: Beides ist zu beschwerlich. Wer will noch regieren? Wer noch gehorchen? Beides ist zu beschwerlich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0022","text":"Kein Hirt und Eine Heerde! Jeder will das Gleiche, Jeder ist gleich: wer anders fühlt, geht freiwillig in’s Irrenhaus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0023","text":"„Ehemals war alle Welt irre“—sagen die Feinsten und blinzeln.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0024","text":"Man ist klug und weiss Alles, was geschehn ist: so hat man kein Ende zu spotten. Man zankt sich noch, aber man versöhnt sich bald—sonst verdirbt es den Magen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0025","text":"Man hat sein Lüstchen für den Tag und sein Lüstchen für die Nacht: aber man ehrt die Gesundheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0026","text":"„Wir haben das Glück erfunden“—sagen die letzten Menschen und blinzeln—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0027","text":"Und hier endete die erste Rede Zarathustra’s, welche man auch „die Vorrede“ heisst: denn an dieser Stelle unterbrach ihn das Geschrei und die Lust der Menge. „Gieb uns diesen letzten Menschen, oh Zarathustra,—so riefen sie—mache uns zu diesen letzten Menschen! So schenken wir dir den Übermenschen!“ Und alles Volk jubelte und schnalzte mit der Zunge. Zarathustra aber wurde traurig und sagte zu seinem Herzen:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0028","text":"Sie verstehen mich nicht: ich bin nicht der Mund für diese Ohren.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0029","text":"Zu lange wohl lebte ich im Gebirge, zu viel horchte ich auf Bäche und Bäume: nun rede ich ihnen gleich den Ziegenhirten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0030","text":"Unbewegt ist meine Seele und hell wie das Gebirge am Vormittag. Aber sie meinen, ich sei kalt und ein Spötter in furchtbaren Spässen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0031","text":"Und nun blicken sie mich an und lachen: und indem sie lachen, hassen sie mich noch. Es ist Eis in ihrem Lachen.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"db2ddbca0969c5c1ad1a922d0cfcddc3a8dfff32a4e7391e45a2c8a83b52eac8","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181105457202Z_f6b3ed14","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"5.","source_sha256":"db2ddbca0969c5c1ad1a922d0cfcddc3a8dfff32a4e7391e45a2c8a83b52eac8","sha256":"76ffdeabb6f174ad134d39c75fb781b3bfab14f498305eacedb235512faceac8","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:11:05.457226+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0001","text":"## 5.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0002","text":"When Zarathustra had spoken these words, he looked again upon the people and was silent. “There they stand,” he said to his heart, “there they laugh: they do not understand me; I am not the mouth for these ears.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0003","text":"Must one first smash their ears so that they learn to hear with their eyes. Must one rattle like kettledrums and preachers of penance? Or do they believe only the stammerer?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0004","text":"They have something of which they are proud. What do they call it, then, that makes them proud? They call it education; it distinguishes them from the goatherds.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0005","text":"Therefore they dislike hearing the word “contempt” spoken of themselves. So I shall speak to their pride.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0006","text":"Thus I shall speak to them of what is most contemptible: but that is the last man.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0007","text":"And thus Zarathustra spoke to the people:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0008","text":"It is time that man should set himself his goal. It is time that man should plant the seed of his highest hope.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0009","text":"His soil is still rich enough for this. But this soil will one day be poor and tame, and no lofty tree will any longer be able to grow from it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0010","text":"Alas! The time is coming when man will no longer cast the arrow of his longing beyond man, and when the string of his bow will have forgotten how to whir!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0011","text":"I tell you: one must still have chaos within oneself in order to be able to give birth to a dancing star. I tell you: you still have chaos within you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0012","text":"Alas! The time is coming when man will no longer give birth to a star. Alas! The time of the most contemptible man is coming, who can no longer despise himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0013","text":"Behold! I show you the last man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0014","text":"“What is love? What is creation? What is longing? What is a star?”—thus asks the last man and blinks.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0015","text":"The earth has then become small, and upon it hops the last man, who makes everything small. His kind is ineradicable, like the earth-flea; the last man lives longest.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0016","text":"“We have invented happiness”—say the last men and blink.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0017","text":"They have left the regions where life was hard: for one needs warmth. One still loves one’s neighbour and rubs oneself against him: for one needs warmth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0018","text":"Falling ill and having mistrust count as sinful to them: one proceeds cautiously. A fool, who still stumbles over stones or men!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0019","text":"A little poison now and then: that makes pleasant dreams. And much poison at last, for a pleasant death.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0020","text":"One still works, for work is an amusement. But one takes care that the amusement does not become exhausting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0021","text":"One no longer becomes poor and rich: both are too burdensome. Who still wants to rule? Who still wants to obey? Both are too burdensome.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0022","text":"No shepherd and one herd! Everyone wants the same thing, everyone is the same: whoever feels differently goes voluntarily into the madhouse.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0023","text":"“Formerly all the world was mad”—say the most refined, and blink.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0024","text":"One is clever and knows everything that has happened: thus one has no end of mocking. One still quarrels, but one is soon reconciled—otherwise it spoils the stomach.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0025","text":"One has one's little pleasure for the day and one's little pleasure for the night: but one honours health.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0026","text":"“We have invented happiness”—say the last men, and blink—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0027","text":"And here ended Zarathustra’s first discourse, which is also called “the prologue”: for at this point he was interrupted by the shouting and delight of the crowd. “Give us this last man, O Zarathustra,”—thus they cried—“make us into these last men! Then we shall give you the overman!” And all the people rejoiced and clucked with their tongues. But Zarathustra became sad and said to his heart:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0028","text":"They do not understand me: I am not the mouth for these ears.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0029","text":"Too long, perhaps, have I lived in the mountains; too much have I listened to brooks and trees: now I speak to them as to goatherds.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0030","text":"Unmoved is my soul, and bright as the mountain in the forenoon. But they think that I am cold and a mocker in terrible jests.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5500:P0031","text":"And now they look at me and laugh: and while they laugh, they hate me still. There is ice in their laughter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5500:P0031"]}],"source_digest":"a9479767ed2a216438576eb4c2c7b29dd051ef8b870b13900315c07515b5e227","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"Zarathustra’s Prologue.","source_section_label":"5.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part One"],"reading_title":"Zarathustra’s Prologue · 5","reading_title_context":"Part One","reading_heading_titles":["Part One","Zarathustra’s Prologue.","5."],"original_title":"5."}],"source_title_context":"Zarathustra’s Vorrede.","source_section_label":"5.","source_title_hierarchy":["Erster Theil"],"reading_title":"Zarathustra’s Vorrede · 5","reading_title_context":"Erster Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Erster Theil","Zarathustra’s Vorrede.","5."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"5.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6600","title":"6.","order":6,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_6600","text":"## 6.\n\nDa aber geschah Etwas, das jeden Mund stumm und jedes Auge starr machte. Inzwischen nämlich hatte der Seiltänzer sein Werk begonnen: er war aus einer kleiner Thür hinausgetreten und gieng über das Seil, welches zwischen zwei Thürmen gespannt war, also, dass es über dem Markte und dem Volke hieng. Als er eben in der Mitte seines Weges war, öffnete sich die kleine Thür noch einmal, und ein bunter Gesell, einem Possenreisser gleich, sprang heraus und gieng mit schnellen Schritten dem Ersten nach. „Vorwärts, Lahmfuss, rief seine fürchterliche Stimme, vorwärts Faulthier, Schleichhändler, Bleichgesicht! Dass ich dich nicht mit meiner Ferse kitzle! Was treibst du hier zwischen Thürmen? In den Thurm gehörst du, einsperren sollte man dich, einem Bessern, als du bist, sperrst du die freie Bahn!“—Und mit jedem Worte kam er ihm näher und näher: als er aber nur noch einen Schritt hinter ihm war, da geschah das Erschreckliche, das jeden Mund stumm und jedes Auge starr machte:—er stiess ein Geschrei aus wie ein Teufel und sprang über Den hinweg, der ihm im Wege war. Dieser aber, als er so seinen Nebenbuhler siegen sah, verlor dabei den Kopf und das Seil; er warf seine Stange weg und schoss schneller als diese, wie ein Wirbel von Armen und Beinen, in die Tiefe. Der Markt und das Volk glich dem Meere, wenn der Sturm hineinfährt: Alles floh aus einander und übereinander, und am meisten dort, wo der Körper niederschlagen musste.\n\nZarathustra aber blieb stehen, und gerade neben ihn fiel der Körper hin, übel zugerichtet und zerbrochen, aber noch nicht todt. Nach einer Weile kam dem Zerschmetterten das Bewusstsein zurück, und er sah Zarathustra neben sich knieen. „Was machst du da? sagte er endlich, ich wusste es lange, dass mir der Teufel ein Bein stellen werde. Nun schleppt er mich zur Hölle: willst du’s ihm wehren?“\n\n„Bei meiner Ehre, Freund, antwortete Zarathustra, das giebt es Alles nicht, wovon du sprichst: es giebt keinen Teufel und keine Hölle. Deine Seele wird noch schneller todt sein als dein Leib: fürchte nun Nichts mehr!“\n\nDer Mann blickte misstrauisch auf. „Wenn du die Wahrheit sprichst, sagte er dann, so verliere ich Nichts, wenn ich das Leben verliere. Ich bin nicht viel mehr als ein Thier, das man tanzen gelehrt hat, durch Schläge und schmale Bissen.“\n\n„Nicht doch, sprach Zarathustra; du hast aus der Gefahr deinen Beruf gemacht, daran ist Nichts zu verachten. Nun gehst du an deinem Beruf zu Grunde: dafür will ich dich mit meinen Händen begraben.“\n\nAls Zarathustra diess gesagt hatte, antwortete der Sterbende nicht mehr; aber er bewegte die Hand, wie als ob er die Hand Zarathustra’s zum Danke suche.—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_6600:P0001","text":"## 6.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6600:P0002","text":"Da aber geschah Etwas, das jeden Mund stumm und jedes Auge starr machte. Inzwischen nämlich hatte der Seiltänzer sein Werk begonnen: er war aus einer kleiner Thür hinausgetreten und gieng über das Seil, welches zwischen zwei Thürmen gespannt war, also, dass es über dem Markte und dem Volke hieng. Als er eben in der Mitte seines Weges war, öffnete sich die kleine Thür noch einmal, und ein bunter Gesell, einem Possenreisser gleich, sprang heraus und gieng mit schnellen Schritten dem Ersten nach. „Vorwärts, Lahmfuss, rief seine fürchterliche Stimme, vorwärts Faulthier, Schleichhändler, Bleichgesicht! Dass ich dich nicht mit meiner Ferse kitzle! Was treibst du hier zwischen Thürmen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6600:P0003","text":"In den Thurm gehörst du, einsperren sollte man dich, einem Bessern, als du bist, sperrst du die freie Bahn!“—Und mit jedem Worte kam er ihm näher und näher: als er aber nur noch einen Schritt hinter ihm war, da geschah das Erschreckliche, das jeden Mund stumm und jedes Auge starr machte:—er stiess ein Geschrei aus wie ein Teufel und sprang über Den hinweg, der ihm im Wege war. Dieser aber, als er so seinen Nebenbuhler siegen sah, verlor dabei den Kopf und das Seil; er warf seine Stange weg und schoss schneller als diese, wie ein Wirbel von Armen und Beinen, in die Tiefe. Der Markt und das Volk glich dem Meere, wenn der Sturm hineinfährt: Alles floh aus einander und übereinander, und am meisten dort, wo der Körper niederschlagen musste.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6600:P0004","text":"Zarathustra aber blieb stehen, und gerade neben ihn fiel der Körper hin, übel zugerichtet und zerbrochen, aber noch nicht todt. Nach einer Weile kam dem Zerschmetterten das Bewusstsein zurück, und er sah Zarathustra neben sich knieen. „Was machst du da? sagte er endlich, ich wusste es lange, dass mir der Teufel ein Bein stellen werde. Nun schleppt er mich zur Hölle: willst du’s ihm wehren?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6600:P0005","text":"„Bei meiner Ehre, Freund, antwortete Zarathustra, das giebt es Alles nicht, wovon du sprichst: es giebt keinen Teufel und keine Hölle. Deine Seele wird noch schneller todt sein als dein Leib: fürchte nun Nichts mehr!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6600:P0006","text":"Der Mann blickte misstrauisch auf. „Wenn du die Wahrheit sprichst, sagte er dann, so verliere ich Nichts, wenn ich das Leben verliere. Ich bin nicht viel mehr als ein Thier, das man tanzen gelehrt hat, durch Schläge und schmale Bissen.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6600:P0007","text":"„Nicht doch, sprach Zarathustra; du hast aus der Gefahr deinen Beruf gemacht, daran ist Nichts zu verachten. Nun gehst du an deinem Beruf zu Grunde: dafür will ich dich mit meinen Händen begraben.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6600:P0008","text":"Als Zarathustra diess gesagt hatte, antwortete der Sterbende nicht mehr; aber er bewegte die Hand, wie als ob er die Hand Zarathustra’s zum Danke suche.—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"0a4ba70b1ffb07a0dac25d2dd8993c8c711221aba6dfe360ed2b2df5cff13598","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181115270566Z_e557f9ce","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"6.","source_sha256":"0a4ba70b1ffb07a0dac25d2dd8993c8c711221aba6dfe360ed2b2df5cff13598","sha256":"dc16e4b324d7536537622f0cb01363789cf6852de393d24615421c5a77e74704","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:11:15.270603+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_6600:P0001","text":"## 6.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6600:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6600:P0002","text":"But then something happened that made every mouth mute and every eye stare. Meanwhile the rope-dancer had begun his work: he had stepped out of a small door and was walking across the rope, which was stretched between two towers, so that it hung over the market-place and the people. Just as he was in the middle of his way, the small door opened once more, and a motley fellow, like a buffoon, sprang out and followed the first with swift steps. “Forward, lame-foot!” his terrible voice cried, “forward, sluggard, sneak-trader, pale-face! Lest I tickle you with my heel! What are you doing here between towers?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6600:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6600:P0003","text":"“You belong in the tower; one ought to lock you up; you block the free way for one better than you are!”—And with every word he came nearer and nearer to him: but when he was only one step behind him, then the terrible thing happened that made every mouth mute and every eye stare:—he uttered a cry like a devil and leapt over him who was in his way. But this man, when he thus saw his rival triumph, lost both his head and the rope; he threw away his pole and shot down more swiftly than it, a whirl of arms and legs, into the depths. The market-place and the people resembled the sea when the storm drives into it: everything fled apart and over one another, and most of all there where the body had to strike.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6600:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6600:P0004","text":"Zarathustra, however, remained standing, and the body fell down right beside him, badly mangled and broken, but not yet dead. After a while consciousness returned to the shattered man, and he saw Zarathustra kneeling beside him. “What are you doing there?” he said at last, “I knew long ago that the devil would trip me up. Now he is dragging me to hell: will you prevent him?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6600:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6600:P0005","text":"“Upon my honour, friend,” answered Zarathustra, “there is nothing at all of what you speak of: there is no devil and no hell. Your soul will be dead even sooner than your body: fear Nothing now!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6600:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6600:P0006","text":"The man looked up mistrustfully. “If you speak the truth,” he then said, “I lose nothing if I lose my life. I am not much more than an animal that has been taught to dance by blows and scanty morsels.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6600:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6600:P0007","text":"“Not so,” spoke Zarathustra; “you have made danger your calling, there is nothing contemptible in that. Now you perish through your calling: for that I will bury you with my own hands.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6600:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6600:P0008","text":"When Zarathustra had said this, the dying man answered no more; but he moved his hand, as though he were seeking Zarathustra’s hand in thanks.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6600:P0008"]}],"source_digest":"16bef423c9cfdd16bea0a346ddf7f750c554369cecb3c6dcbc3d4e86d452cf00","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"Zarathustra’s Prologue.","source_section_label":"6.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part One"],"reading_title":"Zarathustra’s Prologue · 6","reading_title_context":"Part One","reading_heading_titles":["Part One","Zarathustra’s Prologue.","6."],"original_title":"6."}],"source_title_context":"Zarathustra’s Vorrede.","source_section_label":"6.","source_title_hierarchy":["Erster Theil"],"reading_title":"Zarathustra’s Vorrede · 6","reading_title_context":"Erster Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Erster Theil","Zarathustra’s Vorrede.","6."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"6.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7700","title":"7.","order":7,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_7700","text":"## 7.\n\nInzwischen kam der Abend, und der Markt barg sich in Dunkelheit: da verlief sich das Volk, denn selbst Neugierde und Schrecken werden müde. Zarathustra aber sass neben dem Todten auf der Erde und war in Gedanken versunken: so vergass er die Zeit. Endlich aber wurde es Nacht, und ein kalter Wind blies über den Einsamen. Da erhob sich Zarathustra und sagte zu seinem Herzen:\n\nWahrlich, einen schönen Fischfang that heute Zarathustra! Keinen Menschen fieng er, wohl aber einen Leichnam.\n\nUnheimlich ist das menschliche Dasein und immer noch ohne Sinn: ein Possenreisser kann ihm zum Verhängniss werden.\n\nIch will die Menschen den Sinn ihres Seins lehren: welcher ist der Übermensch, der Blitz aus der dunklen Wolke Mensch.\n\nAber noch bin ich ihnen ferne, und mein Sinn redet nicht zu ihren Sinnen. Eine Mitte bin ich noch den Menschen zwischen einem Narren und einem Leichnam.\n\nDunkel ist die Nacht, dunkel sind die Wege Zarathustra’s. Komm, du kalter und steifer Gefährte! Ich trage dich dorthin, wo ich dich mit meinen Händen begrabe.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_7700:P0001","text":"## 7.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7700:P0002","text":"Inzwischen kam der Abend, und der Markt barg sich in Dunkelheit: da verlief sich das Volk, denn selbst Neugierde und Schrecken werden müde. Zarathustra aber sass neben dem Todten auf der Erde und war in Gedanken versunken: so vergass er die Zeit. Endlich aber wurde es Nacht, und ein kalter Wind blies über den Einsamen. Da erhob sich Zarathustra und sagte zu seinem Herzen:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7700:P0003","text":"Wahrlich, einen schönen Fischfang that heute Zarathustra! Keinen Menschen fieng er, wohl aber einen Leichnam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7700:P0004","text":"Unheimlich ist das menschliche Dasein und immer noch ohne Sinn: ein Possenreisser kann ihm zum Verhängniss werden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7700:P0005","text":"Ich will die Menschen den Sinn ihres Seins lehren: welcher ist der Übermensch, der Blitz aus der dunklen Wolke Mensch.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7700:P0006","text":"Aber noch bin ich ihnen ferne, und mein Sinn redet nicht zu ihren Sinnen. Eine Mitte bin ich noch den Menschen zwischen einem Narren und einem Leichnam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7700:P0007","text":"Dunkel ist die Nacht, dunkel sind die Wege Zarathustra’s. Komm, du kalter und steifer Gefährte! Ich trage dich dorthin, wo ich dich mit meinen Händen begrabe.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"b67a371b41a46d72c011b35e3f0154d6080b2b7ca08750ef1859e23228e69540","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181119401546Z_815849cb","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"7.","source_sha256":"b67a371b41a46d72c011b35e3f0154d6080b2b7ca08750ef1859e23228e69540","sha256":"7034e60a450b8fd7500f2e7cf40906376926fe720c72fbad9456fe548dcb83fa","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:11:19.401582+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_7700:P0001","text":"## 7.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7700:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7700:P0002","text":"Meanwhile evening came, and the marketplace hid itself in darkness: then the people dispersed, for even curiosity and terror grow weary. But Zarathustra sat beside the dead man on the ground and was sunk in thought: thus he forgot the time. But at last it became night, and a cold wind blew over the solitary one. Then Zarathustra rose and said to his heart:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7700:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7700:P0003","text":"Verily, Zarathustra made a fine catch today! He caught no man, but rather a corpse.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7700:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7700:P0004","text":"Uncanny is human existence and still without meaning: a buffoon can become its doom.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7700:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7700:P0005","text":"I will teach human beings the meaning of their being: which is the overman, the lightning from the dark cloud man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7700:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7700:P0006","text":"But I am still far from them, and my meaning does not speak to their senses. I am still a middle thing for human beings between a fool and a corpse.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7700:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7700:P0007","text":"Dark is the night, dark are Zarathustra’s ways. Come, thou cold and stiff companion! I shall carry thee thither, where I shall bury thee with my hands.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7700:P0007"]}],"source_digest":"4949d3afbdc9eed72154597b52486e9ffa8922a31cc5b95a8f08a43e60fa8251","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"Zarathustra’s Prologue.","source_section_label":"7.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part One"],"reading_title":"Zarathustra’s Prologue · 7","reading_title_context":"Part One","reading_heading_titles":["Part One","Zarathustra’s Prologue.","7."],"original_title":"7."}],"source_title_context":"Zarathustra’s Vorrede.","source_section_label":"7.","source_title_hierarchy":["Erster Theil"],"reading_title":"Zarathustra’s Vorrede · 7","reading_title_context":"Erster Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Erster Theil","Zarathustra’s Vorrede.","7."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"7.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800","title":"8.","order":8,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_8800","text":"## 8.\n\nAls Zarathustra diess zu seinem Herzen gesagt hatte, lud er den Leichnam auf seinem Rücken und machte sich auf den Weg. Und noch nicht war er hundert Schritte gegangen, da schlich ein Mensch an ihn heran und flüsterte ihm in’s Ohr—und siehe! Der, welcher redete, war der Possenreisser vom Thurme. „Geh weg von dieser Stadt, oh Zarathustra, sprach er; es hassen dich hier zu Viele. Es hassen dich die Guten und Gerechten und sie nennen dich ihren Feind und Verächter; es hassen dich die Gläubigen des rechten Glaubens, und sie nennen dich die Gefahr der Menge. Dein Glück war es, dass man über dich lachte: und wahrlich, du redetest gleich einem Possenreisser. Dein Glück war es, dass du dich dem todten Hunde geselltest; als du dich so erniedrigtest, hast du dich selber für heute errettet. Geh aber fort aus dieser Stadt—oder morgen springe ich über dich hinweg, ein Lebendiger über einen Todten.“ Und als er diess gesagt hatte, verschwand der Mensch; Zarathustra aber gieng weiter durch die dunklen Gassen.\n\nAm Thore der Stadt begegneten ihm die Todtengräber: sie leuchteten ihm mit der Fackel in’s Gesicht, erkannten Zarathustra und spotteten sehr über ihn. „Zarathustra trägt den todten Hund davon: brav, dass Zarathustra zum Todtengräber wurde! Denn unsere Hände sind zu reinlich für diesen Braten. Will Zarathustra wohl dem Teufel seinen Bissen stehlen? Nun wohlan! Und gut Glück zur Mahlzeit! Wenn nur nicht der Teufel ein besserer Dieb ist, als Zarathustra! —er stiehlt die Beide, er frisst sie Beide!“ Und sie lachten mit einander und steckten die Köpfe zusammen.\n\nZarathustra sagte dazu kein Wort und gieng seines Weges. Als er zwei Stunden gegangen war, an Wäldern und Sümpfen vorbei, da hatte er zu viel das hungrige Geheul der Wölfe gehört, und ihm selber kam der Hunger. So blieb er an einem einsamen Hause stehn, in dem ein Licht brannte.\n\nDer Hunger überfällt mich, sagte Zarathustra, wie ein Räuber. In Wäldern und Sümpfen überfällt mich mein Hunger und in tiefer Nacht.\n\nWunderliche Launen hat mein Hunger. Oft kommt er mir erst nach der Mahlzeit, und heute kam er den ganzen Tag nicht: wo weilte er doch?\n\nUnd damit schlug Zarathustra an das Thor des Hauses. Ein alter Mann erschien; er trug das Licht und fragte: „Wer kommt zu mir und zu meinem schlimmen Schlafe?“\n\n„Ein Lebendiger und ein Todter, sagte Zarathustra. Gebt mir zu essen und zu trinken, ich vergass es am Tage. Der, welcher den Hungrigen speiset, erquickt seine eigene Seele: so spricht die Weisheit.“\n\nDer Alte gieng fort, kam aber gleich zurück und bot Zarathustra Brod und Wein. „Eine böse Gegend ist’s für Hungernde, sagte er; darum wohne ich hier. Thier und Mensch kommen zu mir, dem Einsiedler. Aber heisse auch deinen Gefährten essen und trinken, er ist müder als du.“ Zarathustra antwortete: „Todt ist mein Gefährte, ich werde ihn schwerlich dazu überreden.“ „Das geht mich Nichts an, sagte der Alte mürrisch; wer an meinem Hause anklopft, muss auch nehmen, was ich ihm biete. Esst und gehabt euch wohl!“—\n\nDarauf gieng Zarathustra wieder zwei Stunden und vertraute dem Wege und dem Lichte der Sterne: denn er war ein gewohnter Nachtgänger und liebte es, allem Schlafenden in’s Gesicht zu sehn. Als aber der Morgen graute, fand sich Zarathustra in einem tiefen Walde, und kein Weg zeigte sich ihm mehr. Da legte er den Todten in einen hohlen Baum sich zu Häupten—denn er wollte ihn vor den Wölfen schützen—und sich selber auf den Boden und das Moos. Und alsbald schlief er ein, müden Leibes, aber mit einer unbewegten Seele.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800:P0001","text":"## 8.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800:P0002","text":"Als Zarathustra diess zu seinem Herzen gesagt hatte, lud er den Leichnam auf seinem Rücken und machte sich auf den Weg. Und noch nicht war er hundert Schritte gegangen, da schlich ein Mensch an ihn heran und flüsterte ihm in’s Ohr—und siehe! Der, welcher redete, war der Possenreisser vom Thurme. „Geh weg von dieser Stadt, oh Zarathustra, sprach er; es hassen dich hier zu Viele. Es hassen dich die Guten und Gerechten und sie nennen dich ihren Feind und Verächter; es hassen dich die Gläubigen des rechten Glaubens, und sie nennen dich die Gefahr der Menge. Dein Glück war es, dass man über dich lachte: und wahrlich, du redetest gleich einem Possenreisser. Dein Glück war es, dass du dich dem todten Hunde geselltest; als du dich so erniedrigtest, hast du dich selber für heute errettet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800:P0003","text":"Geh aber fort aus dieser Stadt—oder morgen springe ich über dich hinweg, ein Lebendiger über einen Todten.“ Und als er diess gesagt hatte, verschwand der Mensch; Zarathustra aber gieng weiter durch die dunklen Gassen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800:P0004","text":"Am Thore der Stadt begegneten ihm die Todtengräber: sie leuchteten ihm mit der Fackel in’s Gesicht, erkannten Zarathustra und spotteten sehr über ihn. „Zarathustra trägt den todten Hund davon: brav, dass Zarathustra zum Todtengräber wurde! Denn unsere Hände sind zu reinlich für diesen Braten. Will Zarathustra wohl dem Teufel seinen Bissen stehlen? Nun wohlan! Und gut Glück zur Mahlzeit! Wenn nur nicht der Teufel ein besserer Dieb ist, als Zarathustra! —er stiehlt die Beide, er frisst sie Beide!“ Und sie lachten mit einander und steckten die Köpfe zusammen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800:P0005","text":"Zarathustra sagte dazu kein Wort und gieng seines Weges. Als er zwei Stunden gegangen war, an Wäldern und Sümpfen vorbei, da hatte er zu viel das hungrige Geheul der Wölfe gehört, und ihm selber kam der Hunger. So blieb er an einem einsamen Hause stehn, in dem ein Licht brannte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800:P0006","text":"Der Hunger überfällt mich, sagte Zarathustra, wie ein Räuber. In Wäldern und Sümpfen überfällt mich mein Hunger und in tiefer Nacht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800:P0007","text":"Wunderliche Launen hat mein Hunger. Oft kommt er mir erst nach der Mahlzeit, und heute kam er den ganzen Tag nicht: wo weilte er doch?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800:P0008","text":"Und damit schlug Zarathustra an das Thor des Hauses. Ein alter Mann erschien; er trug das Licht und fragte: „Wer kommt zu mir und zu meinem schlimmen Schlafe?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800:P0009","text":"„Ein Lebendiger und ein Todter, sagte Zarathustra. Gebt mir zu essen und zu trinken, ich vergass es am Tage. Der, welcher den Hungrigen speiset, erquickt seine eigene Seele: so spricht die Weisheit.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800:P0010","text":"Der Alte gieng fort, kam aber gleich zurück und bot Zarathustra Brod und Wein. „Eine böse Gegend ist’s für Hungernde, sagte er; darum wohne ich hier. Thier und Mensch kommen zu mir, dem Einsiedler. Aber heisse auch deinen Gefährten essen und trinken, er ist müder als du.“ Zarathustra antwortete: „Todt ist mein Gefährte, ich werde ihn schwerlich dazu überreden.“ „Das geht mich Nichts an, sagte der Alte mürrisch; wer an meinem Hause anklopft, muss auch nehmen, was ich ihm biete. Esst und gehabt euch wohl!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800:P0011","text":"Darauf gieng Zarathustra wieder zwei Stunden und vertraute dem Wege und dem Lichte der Sterne: denn er war ein gewohnter Nachtgänger und liebte es, allem Schlafenden in’s Gesicht zu sehn. Als aber der Morgen graute, fand sich Zarathustra in einem tiefen Walde, und kein Weg zeigte sich ihm mehr. Da legte er den Todten in einen hohlen Baum sich zu Häupten—denn er wollte ihn vor den Wölfen schützen—und sich selber auf den Boden und das Moos. Und alsbald schlief er ein, müden Leibes, aber mit einer unbewegten Seele.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"93d9633fb5e5dff140562805479ebdb701fadb4c0f515f310bef5fe602e27250","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181131552434Z_0405174b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"8.","source_sha256":"93d9633fb5e5dff140562805479ebdb701fadb4c0f515f310bef5fe602e27250","sha256":"10074bb65d5c1cc54343acc7d11dbb5c28c602b6d8b797f3e58d1b4bbc6fc779","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:11:31.552444+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800:P0001","text":"## 8.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8800:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800:P0002","text":"When Zarathustra had said this to his heart, he lifted the corpse upon his back and set out on his way. And he had not yet gone a hundred steps when a man stole up to him and whispered in his ear—and behold! He who spoke was the buffoon from the tower. “Go away from this city, O Zarathustra,” he said; “too many here hate thee. The good and righteous hate thee, and call thee their enemy and despiser; the believers in the true faith hate thee, and call thee the danger of the multitude. It was thy good fortune that men laughed at thee: and verily, thou didst speak like a buffoon. It was thy good fortune that thou didst keep company with the dead dog; when thou didst so abase thyself, thou didst save thyself for today.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8800:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800:P0003","text":"But go away from this city—or tomorrow I shall leap over thee, a living man over a dead one.” And when he had said this, the man disappeared; but Zarathustra went on through the dark lanes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8800:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800:P0004","text":"At the gate of the city the grave-diggers met him: they shone the torch in his face, recognised Zarathustra, and greatly mocked him. “Zarathustra is carrying off the dead dog: well done, that Zarathustra has become a grave-digger! For our hands are too cleanly for this roast. Does Zarathustra mean to steal the devil’s morsel from him? Well then! And good luck with the meal! If only the devil is not a better thief than Zarathustra!—he will steal them both, he will devour them both!” And they laughed together and put their heads together.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8800:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800:P0005","text":"Zarathustra said not a word to this and went his way. When he had walked for two hours, past woods and swamps, he had heard too much the hungry howling of the wolves, and he himself grew hungry. Thus he stopped at a solitary house in which a light was burning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8800:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800:P0006","text":"“Hunger assails me,” said Zarathustra, “like a robber. In woods and swamps my hunger assails me, and in the deep night.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8800:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800:P0007","text":"“My hunger has strange whims. Often it comes to me only after the meal, and today it did not come all day: where, then, was it lingering?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8800:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800:P0008","text":"And with that Zarathustra knocked at the door of the house. An old man appeared; he bore the light and asked: “Who comes to me and to my evil sleep?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8800:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800:P0009","text":"“A living man and a dead one,” said Zarathustra. “Give me food and drink, I forgot it during the day. He who feeds the hungry refreshes his own soul: thus speaks wisdom.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8800:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800:P0010","text":"The old man went away, but immediately returned and offered Zarathustra bread and wine. “It is an evil region for the hungry,” he said; “therefore I dwell here. Beast and man come to me, the hermit. But bid thy companion too to eat and drink, he is wearier than thou.” Zarathustra answered: “My companion is dead; I shall scarcely persuade him to do so.” “That is no concern of mine,” said the old man grumpily; “he who knocks at my house must also take what I offer him. Eat and fare well!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8800:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8800:P0011","text":"Thereupon Zarathustra went on again for two hours and entrusted himself to the path and to the light of the stars: for he was accustomed to walking by night and loved to look in the face of all that slept. But when morning dawned, Zarathustra found himself in a deep forest, and no path showed itself to him any longer. Then he laid the dead man in a hollow tree at his head—for he wished to protect him from the wolves—and himself upon the ground and moss. And straightway he fell asleep, weary in body, but with an unmoving soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8800:P0011"]}],"source_digest":"77136962513ad92c1ebc3a631229e8aae5d1ff50990a29c618203e8a7203f568","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"Zarathustra’s Prologue.","source_section_label":"8.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part One"],"reading_title":"Zarathustra’s Prologue · 8","reading_title_context":"Part One","reading_heading_titles":["Part One","Zarathustra’s Prologue.","8."],"original_title":"8."}],"source_title_context":"Zarathustra’s Vorrede.","source_section_label":"8.","source_title_hierarchy":["Erster Theil"],"reading_title":"Zarathustra’s Vorrede · 8","reading_title_context":"Erster Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Erster Theil","Zarathustra’s Vorrede.","8."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"8.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2","title":"8.","order":9,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_8C_2","text":"## 8.\n\nLange schlief Zarathustra, und nicht nur die Morgenröthe gieng über sein Antlitz, sondern auch der Vormittag. Endlich aber that sein Auge sich auf: verwundert sah Zarathustra in den Wald und die Stille, verwundert sah er in sich hinein. Dann erhob er sich schnell, wie ein Seefahrer, der mit Einem Male Land sieht, und jauchzte: denn er sah eine neue Wahrheit. Und also redete er dann zu seinem Herzen:\n\nEin Licht gieng mir auf: Gefährten brauche ich und lebendige,—nicht todte Gefährten und Leichname, die ich mit mir trage, wohin ich will.\n\nSondern lebendige Gefährten brauche ich, die mir folgen, weil sie sich selber folgen wollen—und dorthin, wo ich will.\n\nEin Licht gieng mir auf: nicht zum Volke rede Zarathustra, sondern zu Gefährten! Nicht soll Zarathustra einer Heerde Hirt und Hund werden!\n\nViele wegzulocken von der Heerde—dazu kam ich. Zürnen soll mir Volk und Heerde: Räuber will Zarathustra den Hirten heissen.\n\nHirten sage ich, aber sie nennen sich die Guten und Gerechten. Hirten sage ich: aber sie nennen sich die Gläubigen des rechten Glaubens.\n\nSiehe die Guten und Gerechten! Wen hassen sie am meisten? Den, der zerbricht ihre Tafeln der Werthe, den Brecher, den Verbrecher:—das aber ist der Schaffende.\n\nSiehe die Gläubigen aller Glauben! Wen hassen sie am meisten? Den, der zerbricht ihre Tafeln der Werthe, den Brecher, den Verbrecher:—das aber ist der Schaffende.\n\nGefährten sucht der Schaffende und nicht Leichname, und auch nicht Heerden und Gläubige. Die Mitschaffenden sucht der Schaffende, Die, welche neue Werthe auf neue Tafeln schreiben.\n\nGefährten sucht der Schaffende, und Miterntende: denn Alles steht bei ihm reif zur Ernte. Aber ihm fehlen die hundert Sicheln: so rauft er Ähren aus und ist ärgerlich.\n\nGefährten sucht der Schaffende, und solche, die ihre Sicheln zu wetzen wissen. Vernichter wird man sie heissen und Verächter des Guten und Bösen. Aber die Erntenden sind es und die Feiernden.\n\nMitschaffende sucht Zarathustra, Miterntende und Mitfeiernde sucht Zarathustra: was hat er mit Heerden und Hirten und Leichnamen zu schaffen!\n\nUnd du, mein erster Gefährte, gehab dich wohl! Gut begrub ich dich in deinem hohlen Baume, gut barg ich dich vor den Wölfen.\n\nAber ich scheide von dir, die Zeit ist um. Zwischen Morgenröthe und Morgenröthe kam mir eine neue Wahrheit.\n\nNicht Hirt soll ich sein, nicht Todtengräber. Nicht reden einmal will ich wieder mit dem Volke; zum letzten Male sprach ich zu einem Todten.\n\nDen Schaffenden, den Erntenden, den Feiernden will ich mich zugesellen: den Regenbogen will ich ihnen zeigen und alle die Treppen des Übermenschen.\n\nDen Einsiedlern werde ich mein Lied singen und den Zweisiedlern; und wer noch Ohren hat für Unerhörtes, dem will ich sein Herz schwer machen mit meinem Glücke.\n\nZu meinem Ziele will ich, ich gehe meinen Gang; über die Zögernden und Saumseligen werde ich hinwegspringen. Also sei mein Gang ihr Untergang!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0001","text":"## 8.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0002","text":"Lange schlief Zarathustra, und nicht nur die Morgenröthe gieng über sein Antlitz, sondern auch der Vormittag. Endlich aber that sein Auge sich auf: verwundert sah Zarathustra in den Wald und die Stille, verwundert sah er in sich hinein. Dann erhob er sich schnell, wie ein Seefahrer, der mit Einem Male Land sieht, und jauchzte: denn er sah eine neue Wahrheit. Und also redete er dann zu seinem Herzen:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0003","text":"Ein Licht gieng mir auf: Gefährten brauche ich und lebendige,—nicht todte Gefährten und Leichname, die ich mit mir trage, wohin ich will.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0004","text":"Sondern lebendige Gefährten brauche ich, die mir folgen, weil sie sich selber folgen wollen—und dorthin, wo ich will.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0005","text":"Ein Licht gieng mir auf: nicht zum Volke rede Zarathustra, sondern zu Gefährten! Nicht soll Zarathustra einer Heerde Hirt und Hund werden!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0006","text":"Viele wegzulocken von der Heerde—dazu kam ich. Zürnen soll mir Volk und Heerde: Räuber will Zarathustra den Hirten heissen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0007","text":"Hirten sage ich, aber sie nennen sich die Guten und Gerechten. Hirten sage ich: aber sie nennen sich die Gläubigen des rechten Glaubens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0008","text":"Siehe die Guten und Gerechten! Wen hassen sie am meisten? Den, der zerbricht ihre Tafeln der Werthe, den Brecher, den Verbrecher:—das aber ist der Schaffende.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0009","text":"Siehe die Gläubigen aller Glauben! Wen hassen sie am meisten? Den, der zerbricht ihre Tafeln der Werthe, den Brecher, den Verbrecher:—das aber ist der Schaffende.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0010","text":"Gefährten sucht der Schaffende und nicht Leichname, und auch nicht Heerden und Gläubige. Die Mitschaffenden sucht der Schaffende, Die, welche neue Werthe auf neue Tafeln schreiben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0011","text":"Gefährten sucht der Schaffende, und Miterntende: denn Alles steht bei ihm reif zur Ernte. Aber ihm fehlen die hundert Sicheln: so rauft er Ähren aus und ist ärgerlich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0012","text":"Gefährten sucht der Schaffende, und solche, die ihre Sicheln zu wetzen wissen. Vernichter wird man sie heissen und Verächter des Guten und Bösen. Aber die Erntenden sind es und die Feiernden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0013","text":"Mitschaffende sucht Zarathustra, Miterntende und Mitfeiernde sucht Zarathustra: was hat er mit Heerden und Hirten und Leichnamen zu schaffen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0014","text":"Und du, mein erster Gefährte, gehab dich wohl! Gut begrub ich dich in deinem hohlen Baume, gut barg ich dich vor den Wölfen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0015","text":"Aber ich scheide von dir, die Zeit ist um. Zwischen Morgenröthe und Morgenröthe kam mir eine neue Wahrheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0016","text":"Nicht Hirt soll ich sein, nicht Todtengräber. Nicht reden einmal will ich wieder mit dem Volke; zum letzten Male sprach ich zu einem Todten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0017","text":"Den Schaffenden, den Erntenden, den Feiernden will ich mich zugesellen: den Regenbogen will ich ihnen zeigen und alle die Treppen des Übermenschen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0018","text":"Den Einsiedlern werde ich mein Lied singen und den Zweisiedlern; und wer noch Ohren hat für Unerhörtes, dem will ich sein Herz schwer machen mit meinem Glücke.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0019","text":"Zu meinem Ziele will ich, ich gehe meinen Gang; über die Zögernden und Saumseligen werde ich hinwegspringen. Also sei mein Gang ihr Untergang!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"1e38bc844a2b7cbcb067d416dcd3bf86a824dd31a30b8a07eed6d5109d7cb52f","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181140615029Z_a36423ff","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"8.","source_sha256":"1e38bc844a2b7cbcb067d416dcd3bf86a824dd31a30b8a07eed6d5109d7cb52f","sha256":"ef76e4097932e940d278d1ae9823de70adf1bd11fa2ad59323cfb4b825b55233","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:11:40.615039+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0001","text":"## 8.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0002","text":"For a long time Zarathustra slept, and not only the dawn passed over his face, but also the forenoon. But at last his eye opened: Zarathustra looked in wonder at the forest and the stillness, in wonder he looked within himself. Then he rose quickly, like a seafarer who suddenly sees land, and shouted for joy: for he saw a new truth. And thus he then spoke to his heart:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0003","text":"A light dawned on me: I need companions, and living ones,—not dead companions and corpses, which I carry with me wherever I will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0004","text":"Rather, I need living companions who follow me because they themselves wish to follow themselves—and to go where I will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0005","text":"A light dawned on me: Zarathustra speaks not to the people, but to companions! Zarathustra shall not become shepherd and dog to a herd!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0006","text":"To lure many away from the herd—that is why I came. People and herd shall be angry with me: Zarathustra wishes to call the shepherds robbers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0007","text":"I call them shepherds, but they call themselves the good and the just. I call them shepherds: but they call themselves the believers in the right faith.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0008","text":"Behold the good and the just! Whom do they hate most? Him who breaks their tablets of values, the breaker, the lawbreaker:—but he is the creator.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0009","text":"Behold the believers of all faiths! Whom do they hate most? Him who breaks their tablets of values, the breaker, the lawbreaker:—but he is the creator.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0010","text":"The creator seeks companions and not corpses, nor herds and believers either. The creator seeks fellow-creators, those who write new values upon new tablets.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0011","text":"The creator seeks companions, and fellow-harvesters: for with him everything stands ripe for harvest. But he lacks the hundred sickles: thus he tears out ears of grain and is angry.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0012","text":"The creator seeks companions, and such as know how to whet their sickles. They will be called destroyers and despisers of good and evil. But they are the harvesters and the celebrants.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0013","text":"Zarathustra seeks fellow-creators, Zarathustra seeks fellow-harvesters and fellow-celebrants: what has he to do with herds and shepherds and corpses!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0014","text":"And you, my first companion, fare well! I buried you well in your hollow tree, I hid you well from the wolves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0015","text":"But I part from you, the time is over. Between dawn and dawn a new truth came to me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0016","text":"I shall not be a shepherd, nor a gravedigger. I shall not even speak with the people again; for the last time I spoke to a dead man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0017","text":"I will join the creators, the harvesters, the celebrants: I will show them the rainbow and all the steps of the Overman.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0018","text":"I will sing my song to the solitaries and to the two-solitaries; and whoever still has ears for the unheard-of, I will make his heart heavy with my happiness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0019","text":"I will go toward my goal, I go my way; over the hesitating and the laggard I will leap. Thus may my going be their downfall!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_2:P0019"]}],"source_digest":"c2918ec3bb7e2f213b3c36ffa18e23172aef04ad7b244f6e9efe81ba2b17e5b0","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"Zarathustra’s Prologue.","source_section_label":"8.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part One"],"reading_title":"Zarathustra’s Prologue · 8","reading_title_context":"Part One","reading_heading_titles":["Part One","Zarathustra’s Prologue.","8."],"original_title":"8."}],"source_title_context":"Zarathustra’s Vorrede.","source_section_label":"8.","source_title_hierarchy":["Erster Theil"],"reading_title":"Zarathustra’s Vorrede · 8","reading_title_context":"Erster Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Erster Theil","Zarathustra’s Vorrede.","8."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"8.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH","title":"10.","order":10,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_10CH","text":"## 10.\n\nDiess hatte Zarathustra zu seinem Herzen gesprochen, als die Sonne im Mittag stand: da blickte er fragend in die Höhe—denn er hörte über sich den scharfen Ruf eines Vogels. Und siehe! Ein Adler zog in weiten Kreisen durch die Luft, und an ihm hieng eine Schlange, nicht einer Beute gleich, sondern einer Freundin: denn sie hielt sich um seinen Hals geringelt.\n\n„Es sind meine Thiere!“ sagte Zarathustra und freute sich von Herzen.\n\n„Das stolzeste Thier unter der Sonne und das klügste Thier unter der Sonne—sie sind ausgezogen auf Kundschaft.\n\nErkunden wollen sie, ob Zarathustra noch lebe. Wahrlich, lebe ich noch?\n\nGefährlicher fand ich’s unter Menschen als unter Thieren, gefährlicher Wege geht Zarathustra. Mögen mich meine Thiere führen!“\n\nAls Zarathustra diess gesagt hatte, gedachte er der Worte des Heiligen im Walde, seufzte und sprach also zu seinem Herzen:\n\nMöchte ich klüger sein! Möchte ich klug von Grund aus sein, gleich meiner Schlange!\n\nAber Unmögliches bitte ich da: so bitte ich denn meinen Stolz, dass er immer mit meiner Klugheit gehe!\n\nUnd wenn mich einst meine Klugheit verlässt:—ach, sie liebt es, davonzufliegen!—möge mein Stolz dann noch mit meiner Thorheit fliegen!\n\n—Also begann Zarathustra’s Untergang.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH:P0001","text":"## 10.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH:P0002","text":"Diess hatte Zarathustra zu seinem Herzen gesprochen, als die Sonne im Mittag stand: da blickte er fragend in die Höhe—denn er hörte über sich den scharfen Ruf eines Vogels. Und siehe! Ein Adler zog in weiten Kreisen durch die Luft, und an ihm hieng eine Schlange, nicht einer Beute gleich, sondern einer Freundin: denn sie hielt sich um seinen Hals geringelt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH:P0003","text":"„Es sind meine Thiere!“ sagte Zarathustra und freute sich von Herzen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH:P0004","text":"„Das stolzeste Thier unter der Sonne und das klügste Thier unter der Sonne—sie sind ausgezogen auf Kundschaft.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH:P0005","text":"Erkunden wollen sie, ob Zarathustra noch lebe. Wahrlich, lebe ich noch?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH:P0006","text":"Gefährlicher fand ich’s unter Menschen als unter Thieren, gefährlicher Wege geht Zarathustra. Mögen mich meine Thiere führen!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH:P0007","text":"Als Zarathustra diess gesagt hatte, gedachte er der Worte des Heiligen im Walde, seufzte und sprach also zu seinem Herzen:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH:P0008","text":"Möchte ich klüger sein! Möchte ich klug von Grund aus sein, gleich meiner Schlange!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH:P0009","text":"Aber Unmögliches bitte ich da: so bitte ich denn meinen Stolz, dass er immer mit meiner Klugheit gehe!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH:P0010","text":"Und wenn mich einst meine Klugheit verlässt:—ach, sie liebt es, davonzufliegen!—möge mein Stolz dann noch mit meiner Thorheit fliegen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH:P0011","text":"—Also begann Zarathustra’s Untergang.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"06eee12d841705a116d4049f087962a95d3a5960f45286686af48a47a66cf7eb","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181145584951Z_87d6965a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"10.","source_sha256":"06eee12d841705a116d4049f087962a95d3a5960f45286686af48a47a66cf7eb","sha256":"fec4e6b8b6855a2bd1be6945bf6bf12f5d30a241a08e9d589f92b94aaeb8cb41","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:11:45.584992+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH:P0001","text":"## 10.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10CH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH:P0002","text":"Thus had Zarathustra spoken to his heart when the sun stood at noon: then he looked questioningly upward—for he heard above him the piercing cry of a bird. And behold! An eagle was soaring through the air in wide circles, and from it hung a serpent, not like prey, but like a friend: for it held itself coiled around its neck.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10CH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH:P0003","text":"“They are my animals!” said Zarathustra, and rejoiced from his heart.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10CH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH:P0004","text":"“The proudest animal beneath the sun and the cleverest animal beneath the sun—they have set out to scout.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10CH:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH:P0005","text":"They wish to discover whether Zarathustra still lives. Verily, do I still live?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10CH:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH:P0006","text":"I found it more dangerous among men than among animals; Zarathustra travels more dangerous paths. May my animals guide me!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10CH:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH:P0007","text":"When Zarathustra had said this, he remembered the words of the saint in the forest, sighed, and spoke thus to his heart:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10CH:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH:P0008","text":"Would that I were cleverer! Would that I were clever from the ground up, like my serpent!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10CH:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH:P0009","text":"But there I ask for the impossible: so I ask my pride to go ever with my cleverness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10CH:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH:P0010","text":"And if one day my cleverness leaves me:—ah, it loves to fly away!—may my pride then still fly with my folly!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10CH:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10CH:P0011","text":"—Thus began Zarathustra’s downfall.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10CH:P0011"]}],"source_digest":"632c46e5581f9396b9267008430152d26549105cb000035582db1bf3fda80e25","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"Zarathustra’s Prologue.","source_section_label":"10.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part One"],"reading_title":"Zarathustra’s Prologue · 10","reading_title_context":"Part One","reading_heading_titles":["Part One","Zarathustra’s Prologue.","10."],"original_title":"10."}],"source_title_context":"Zarathustra’s Vorrede.","source_section_label":"10.","source_title_hierarchy":["Erster Theil"],"reading_title":"Zarathustra’s Vorrede · 10","reading_title_context":"Erster Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Erster Theil","Zarathustra’s Vorrede.","10."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"10.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV","title":"On the Three Metamorphoses","order":11,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDDV","text":"Drei Verwandlungen nenne ich euch des Geistes: wie der Geist zum Kamele wird, und zum Löwen das Kameel, und zum Kinde zuletzt der Löwe.\n\nVieles Schwere giebt es dem Geiste, dem starken, tragsamen Geiste, dem Ehrfurcht innewohnt: nach dem Schweren und Schwersten verlangt seine Stärke.\n\nWas ist schwer? so fragt der tragsame Geist, so kniet er nieder, dem Kameele gleich, und will gut beladen sein.\n\nWas ist das Schwerste, ihr Helden? so fragt der tragsame Geist, dass ich es auf mich nehme und meiner Stärke froh werde.\n\nIst es nicht das: sich erniedrigen, um seinem Hochmuth wehe zu thun? Seine Thorheit leuchten lassen, um seiner Weisheit zu spotten?\n\nOder ist es das: von unserer Sache scheiden, wenn sie ihren Sieg feiert? Auf hohe Berge steigen, um den Versucher zu versuchen?\n\nOder ist es das: sich von Eicheln und Gras der Erkenntniss nähren und um der Wahrheit willen an der Seele Hunger leiden?\n\nOder ist es das: krank sein und die Tröster heimschicken und mit Tauben Freundschaft schliessen, die niemals hören, was du willst?\n\nOder ist es das: in schmutziges Wasser steigen, wenn es das Wasser der Wahrheit ist, und kalte Frösche und heisse Kröten nicht von sich weisen?\n\nOder ist es das: Die lieben, die uns verachten, und dem Gespenste die Hand reichen, wenn es uns fürchten machen will?\n\nAlles diess Schwerste nimmt der tragsame Geist auf sich: dem Kameele gleich, das beladen in die Wüste eilt, also eilt er in seine Wüste.\n\nAber in der einsamsten Wüste geschieht die zweite Verwandlung: zum Löwen wird hier der Geist, Freiheit will er sich erbeuten und Herr sein in seiner eignen Wüste.\n\nSeinen letzten Herrn sucht er sich hier: feind will er ihm werden und seinem letzten Gotte, um Sieg will er mit dem grossen Drachen ringen.\n\nWelches ist der grosse Drache, den der Geist nicht mehr Herr und Gott heissen mag? „Du-sollst“ heisst der grosse Drache. Aber der Geist des Löwen sagt „Ich will“.\n\n„Du-sollst“ liegt ihm am Wege, goldfunkelnd, ein Schuppenthier, und auf jeder Schuppe glänzt golden „Du-sollst!“\n\nTausendjährige Werthe glänzen an diesen Schuppen, und also spricht der mächtigste aller Drachen „aller Werth der Dinge—der glänzt an mir.“\n\n„Aller Werth ward schon geschaffen, und aller geschaffene Werth—das bin ich. Wahrlich, es soll kein „Ich will“ mehr geben!“ Also spricht der Drache.\n\nMeine Brüder, wozu bedarf es des Löwen im Geiste? Was genügt nicht das lastbare Thier, das entsagt und ehrfürchtig ist?\n\nNeue Werthe schaffen—das vermag auch der Löwe noch nicht: aber Freiheit sich schaffen zu neuem Schaffen—das vermag die Macht des Löwen.\n\nFreiheit sich schaffen und ein heiliges Nein auch vor der Pflicht: dazu, meine Brüder bedarf es des Löwen.\n\nRecht sich nehmen zu neuen Werthen—das ist das furchtbarste Nehmen für einen tragsamen und ehrfürchtigen Geist. Wahrlich, ein Rauben ist es ihm und eines raubenden Thieres Sache.\n\nAls sein Heiligstes liebte er einst das „Du-sollst“: nun muss er Wahn und Willkür auch noch im Heiligsten finden, dass er sich Freiheit raube von seiner Liebe: des Löwen bedarf es zu diesem Raube.\n\nAber sagt, meine Brüder, was vermag noch das Kind, das auch der Löwe nicht vermochte? Was muss der raubende Löwe auch noch zum Kinde werden?\n\nUnschuld ist das Kind und Vergessen, ein Neubeginnen, ein Spiel, ein aus sich rollendes Rad, eine erste Bewegung, ein heiliges Ja-sagen.\n\nJa, zum Spiele des Schaffens, meine Brüder, bedarf es eines heiligen Ja-sagens: seinen Willen will nun der Geist, seine Welt gewinnt sich der Weltverlorene.\n\nDrei Verwandlungen nannte ich euch des Geistes: wie der Geist zum Kameele ward, und zum Löwen das Kameel, und der Löwe zuletzt zum Kinde. —\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra. Und damals weilte er in der Stadt, welche genannt wird: die bunte Kuh.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0001","text":"Drei Verwandlungen nenne ich euch des Geistes: wie der Geist zum Kamele wird, und zum Löwen das Kameel, und zum Kinde zuletzt der Löwe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0002","text":"Vieles Schwere giebt es dem Geiste, dem starken, tragsamen Geiste, dem Ehrfurcht innewohnt: nach dem Schweren und Schwersten verlangt seine Stärke.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0003","text":"Was ist schwer? so fragt der tragsame Geist, so kniet er nieder, dem Kameele gleich, und will gut beladen sein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0004","text":"Was ist das Schwerste, ihr Helden? so fragt der tragsame Geist, dass ich es auf mich nehme und meiner Stärke froh werde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0005","text":"Ist es nicht das: sich erniedrigen, um seinem Hochmuth wehe zu thun? Seine Thorheit leuchten lassen, um seiner Weisheit zu spotten?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0006","text":"Oder ist es das: von unserer Sache scheiden, wenn sie ihren Sieg feiert? Auf hohe Berge steigen, um den Versucher zu versuchen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0007","text":"Oder ist es das: sich von Eicheln und Gras der Erkenntniss nähren und um der Wahrheit willen an der Seele Hunger leiden?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0008","text":"Oder ist es das: krank sein und die Tröster heimschicken und mit Tauben Freundschaft schliessen, die niemals hören, was du willst?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0009","text":"Oder ist es das: in schmutziges Wasser steigen, wenn es das Wasser der Wahrheit ist, und kalte Frösche und heisse Kröten nicht von sich weisen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0010","text":"Oder ist es das: Die lieben, die uns verachten, und dem Gespenste die Hand reichen, wenn es uns fürchten machen will?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0011","text":"Alles diess Schwerste nimmt der tragsame Geist auf sich: dem Kameele gleich, das beladen in die Wüste eilt, also eilt er in seine Wüste.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0012","text":"Aber in der einsamsten Wüste geschieht die zweite Verwandlung: zum Löwen wird hier der Geist, Freiheit will er sich erbeuten und Herr sein in seiner eignen Wüste.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0013","text":"Seinen letzten Herrn sucht er sich hier: feind will er ihm werden und seinem letzten Gotte, um Sieg will er mit dem grossen Drachen ringen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0014","text":"Welches ist der grosse Drache, den der Geist nicht mehr Herr und Gott heissen mag? „Du-sollst“ heisst der grosse Drache. Aber der Geist des Löwen sagt „Ich will“.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0015","text":"„Du-sollst“ liegt ihm am Wege, goldfunkelnd, ein Schuppenthier, und auf jeder Schuppe glänzt golden „Du-sollst!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0016","text":"Tausendjährige Werthe glänzen an diesen Schuppen, und also spricht der mächtigste aller Drachen „aller Werth der Dinge—der glänzt an mir.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0017","text":"„Aller Werth ward schon geschaffen, und aller geschaffene Werth—das bin ich. Wahrlich, es soll kein „Ich will“ mehr geben!“ Also spricht der Drache.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0018","text":"Meine Brüder, wozu bedarf es des Löwen im Geiste? Was genügt nicht das lastbare Thier, das entsagt und ehrfürchtig ist?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0019","text":"Neue Werthe schaffen—das vermag auch der Löwe noch nicht: aber Freiheit sich schaffen zu neuem Schaffen—das vermag die Macht des Löwen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0020","text":"Freiheit sich schaffen und ein heiliges Nein auch vor der Pflicht: dazu, meine Brüder bedarf es des Löwen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0021","text":"Recht sich nehmen zu neuen Werthen—das ist das furchtbarste Nehmen für einen tragsamen und ehrfürchtigen Geist. Wahrlich, ein Rauben ist es ihm und eines raubenden Thieres Sache.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0022","text":"Als sein Heiligstes liebte er einst das „Du-sollst“: nun muss er Wahn und Willkür auch noch im Heiligsten finden, dass er sich Freiheit raube von seiner Liebe: des Löwen bedarf es zu diesem Raube.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0023","text":"Aber sagt, meine Brüder, was vermag noch das Kind, das auch der Löwe nicht vermochte? Was muss der raubende Löwe auch noch zum Kinde werden?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0024","text":"Unschuld ist das Kind und Vergessen, ein Neubeginnen, ein Spiel, ein aus sich rollendes Rad, eine erste Bewegung, ein heiliges Ja-sagen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0025","text":"Ja, zum Spiele des Schaffens, meine Brüder, bedarf es eines heiligen Ja-sagens: seinen Willen will nun der Geist, seine Welt gewinnt sich der Weltverlorene.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0026","text":"Drei Verwandlungen nannte ich euch des Geistes: wie der Geist zum Kameele ward, und zum Löwen das Kameel, und der Löwe zuletzt zum Kinde. —","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0027","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra. Und damals weilte er in der Stadt, welche genannt wird: die bunte Kuh.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"1e843790146257c10392c9b70d3b326dc8c72bb8ba5dbd692a117a9103065305","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181158030721Z_a8ee99a0","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Three Metamorphoses","source_sha256":"1e843790146257c10392c9b70d3b326dc8c72bb8ba5dbd692a117a9103065305","sha256":"be8871b8c0683618a9247145a1b1c65b66ce09b70faaea69c409214e288e632b","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:11:58.030731+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0001","text":"Three transformations of the spirit I name to you: how the spirit becomes a camel, and the camel a lion, and finally the lion a child.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0002","text":"There are many heavy things for the spirit, the strong, load-bearing spirit in which reverence dwells: its strength longs for the heavy and heaviest.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0003","text":"What is heavy? thus asks the load-bearing spirit; thus it kneels down, like the camel, and wants to be well laden.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0004","text":"What is the heaviest thing, you heroes? thus asks the load-bearing spirit, that I may take it upon myself and rejoice in my strength.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0005","text":"Is it not this: to humble oneself in order to hurt one's pride? To let one's folly shine, in order to mock one's wisdom?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0006","text":"Or is it this: to depart from our cause when it celebrates its victory? To climb high mountains in order to tempt the tempter?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0007","text":"Or is it this: to nourish oneself on acorns and grass of knowledge and, for the sake of truth, to suffer hunger in one's soul?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0008","text":"Or is it this: to be ill and send the comforters home and make friends with deaf people, who never hear what you want?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0009","text":"Or is it this: to step into filthy water when it is the water of truth, and not to reject cold frogs and hot toads?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0010","text":"Or is it this: to love those who despise us, and to extend one's hand to the spectre when it seeks to make us afraid?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0011","text":"The load-bearing spirit takes all this heaviest burden upon itself: like the camel that, laden, hastens into the desert, so it hastens into its desert.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0012","text":"But in the loneliest desert the second transformation occurs: here the spirit becomes a lion; it wants to seize freedom for itself and be lord in its own desert.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0013","text":"Here it seeks its last lord: it wants to become his enemy and the enemy of its last god; for victory it wants to contend with the great dragon.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0014","text":"Which is the great dragon whom the spirit no longer wishes to call lord and god? “Thou-shalt” is the name of the great dragon. But the spirit of the lion says “I will.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0015","text":"“Thou-shalt” lies in its path, sparkling with gold, a scaly beast; and upon every scale “Thou-shalt!” gleams golden.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0016","text":"Values of a thousand years gleam upon these scales, and thus speaks the mightiest of all dragons: “all value of things—that gleams upon me.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0017","text":"“All value has already been created, and all created value—that is I. Verily, there shall be no more ‘I will’!” Thus speaks the dragon.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0018","text":"My brothers, what need is there of the lion in the spirit? Why does not the beast of burden suffice, which renounces and is reverent?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0019","text":"Creating new values—the lion is not yet capable of that either: but creating freedom for itself for new creating—that the power of the lion is capable of.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0020","text":"To create freedom for itself and also a sacred No before duty: for that, my brothers, there is need of the lion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0021","text":"To take for itself the right to new values—that is the most terrible taking for a load-bearing and reverent spirit. Verily, it is robbery to it and the business of a beast of prey.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0022","text":"Once it loved “Thou-shalt” as its holiest thing: now it must find delusion and arbitrariness even in the holiest, so that it may rob itself of freedom from its love: the lion is needed for this robbery.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0023","text":"But tell me, my brothers, what is the child capable of that even the lion was not capable of? Why must the predatory lion become a child too?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0024","text":"The child is innocence and forgetting, a new beginning, a game, a wheel rolling out of itself, a first movement, a sacred saying-Yes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0025","text":"Yes, my brothers, for the game of creation there is need of a sacred saying-Yes: the spirit now wills its own will, the world-lost one wins its own world.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0026","text":"Three transformations of the spirit I named to you: how the spirit became a camel, and the camel a lion, and the lion at last a child. —","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0027","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra. And at that time he dwelt in the city which is called: the motley cow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDDV:P0027"]}],"source_digest":"8eec97439631893d36ed177c48f7fb290a28697bd7ac95fa0e2cfe3fddd7cd80","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von den drei Verwandlungen"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von den drei Verwandlungen","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH","title":"On the Teachers of Virtue","order":12,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDLH","text":"Man rühmte Zarathustra einen Weisen, der gut vom Schlafe und von der Tugend zu reden wisse: sehr werde er geehrt und gelohnt dafür, und alle Jünglinge sässen vor seinem Lehrstuhle. Zu ihm gieng Zarathustra, und mit allen Jünglingen sass er vor seinem Lehrstuhle. Und also sprach der Weise:\n\nEhre und Scham vor dem Schlafe! Das ist das Erste! Und Allen aus dem Wege gehn, die schlecht schlafen und Nachts wachen!\n\nSchamhaft ist noch der Dieb vor dem Schlafe: stets stiehlt er sich leise durch die Nacht. Schamlos aber ist der Wächter der Nacht, schamlos trägt er sein Horn.\n\nKeine geringe Kunst ist schlafen: es thut schon Noth, den ganzen Tag darauf hin zu wachen.\n\nZehn Mal musst du des Tages dich selber überwinden: das macht eine gute Müdigkeit und ist Mohn der Seele.\n\nZehn Mal musst du dich wieder dir selber versöhnen; denn Überwindung ist Bitterniss, und schlecht schläft der Unversöhnte.\n\nZehn Wahrheiten musst du des Tages finden: sonst suchst du noch des Nachts nach Wahrheit, und deine Seele blieb hungrig.\n\nZehn Mal musst du lachen am Tage und heiter sein: sonst stört dich der Magen in der Nacht, dieser Vater der Trübsal.\n\nWenige wissen das: aber man muss alle Tugenden haben, um gut zu schlafen. Werde ich falsch Zeugniss reden? Werde ich ehebrechen?\n\nWerde ich mich gelüsten lassen meines Nächsten Magd? Das Alles vertrüge sich schlecht mit gutem Schlafe.\n\nUnd selbst wenn man alle Tugenden hat, muss man sich noch auf Eins verstehn: selber die Tugenden zur rechten Zeit schlafen schicken.\n\nDass sie sich nicht mit einander zanken, die artigen Weiblein! Und über dich, du Unglückseliger!\n\nFriede mit Gott und dem Nachbar: so will es der gute Schlaf. Und Friede auch noch mit des Nachbars Teufel! Sonst geht er bei dir des Nachts um.\n\nEhre der Obrigkeit und Gehorsam, und auch der krummen Obrigkeit! So will es der gute Schlaf. Was kann ich dafür, dass die Macht gerne auf krummen Beinen Wandelt?\n\nDer soll mir immer der beste Hirt heissen, der sein Schaf auf die grünste Aue führt: so verträgt es sich mit dem gutem Schlafe.\n\nViel Ehren will ich nicht, noch grosse Schätze: das entzündet die Milz. Aber schlecht schläft es sich ohne einen guten Namen und einen kleinen Schatz.\n\nEine kleine Gesellschaft ist mir willkommener als eine böse: doch muss sie gehn und kommen zur rechten Zeit. So verträgt es sich mit gutem Schlafe.\n\nSehr gefallen mir auch die Geistig-Armen: sie fördern den Schlaf. Selig sind die, sonderlich, wenn man ihnen immer Recht giebt.\n\nAlso läuft der Tag dem Tugendsamen. Kommt nun die Nacht, so hüte ich mich wohl, den Schlaf zu rufen! Nicht will er gerufen sein, der Schlaf, der der Herr der Tugenden ist!\n\nSondern ich denke, was ich des Tages gethan und gedacht. Wiederkäuend frage ich mich, geduldsam gleich einer Kuh: welches waren doch deine zehn Überwindungen?\n\nUnd welches waren die zehn Versöhnungen und die zehn Wahrheiten und die zehn Gelächter, mit denen sich mein Herz gütlich that?\n\nSolcherlei erwägend und gewiegt von vierzig Gedanken, überfällt mich auf einmal der Schlaf, der Ungerufne, der Herr der Tugenden.\n\nDer Schlaf klopft mir auf meine Auge: da wird es schwer. Der Schlaf berührt mir den Mund: da bleibt er offen.\n\nWahrlich, auf weichen Sohlen kommt er mir, der liebste der Diebe, und stiehlt mir meine Gedanken: dumm stehe ich da wie dieser Lehrstuhl.\n\nAber nicht lange mehr stehe ich dann: da liege ich schon.—\n\nAls Zarathustra den Weisen also sprechen hörte, lachte er bei sich im Herzen: denn ihm war dabei ein Licht aufgegangen. Und also sprach er zu seinem Herzen:\n\nEin Narr ist mir dieser Weise da mit seinen vierzig Gedanken: aber ich glaube, dass er sich wohl auf das Schlafen versteht.\n\nGlücklich schon, wer in der Nähe dieses Weisen wohnt! Solch ein Schlaf steckt an, noch durch eine dicke Wand hindurch steckt er an.\n\nEin Zauber wohnt selbst in seinem Lehrstuhle. Und nicht vergebens sassen die Jünglinge vor dem Prediger der Tugend.\n\nSeine Weisheit heisst: wachen, um gut zu schlafen. Und wahrlich, hätte das Leben keinen Sinn und müsste ich Unsinn wählen, so wäre auch mir diess der wählenswürdigste Unsinn.\n\nJetzo verstehe ich klar, was einst man vor Allem suchte, wenn man Lehrer der Tugend suchte. Guten Schlaf suchte man sich und mohnblumige Tugenden dazu!\n\nAllen diesen gelobten Weisen der Lehrstühle war Weisheit der Schlaf ohne Träume: sie kannten keinen bessern Sinn des Lebens.\n\nAuch noch heute wohl giebt es Einige, wie diesen Prediger der Tugend, und nicht immer so Ehrliche: aber ihre Zeit ist um. Und nicht mehr lange stehen sie noch: da liegen sie schon.\n\nSelig sind diese Schläfrigen: denn sie sollen bald einnicken.—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0001","text":"Man rühmte Zarathustra einen Weisen, der gut vom Schlafe und von der Tugend zu reden wisse: sehr werde er geehrt und gelohnt dafür, und alle Jünglinge sässen vor seinem Lehrstuhle. Zu ihm gieng Zarathustra, und mit allen Jünglingen sass er vor seinem Lehrstuhle. Und also sprach der Weise:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0002","text":"Ehre und Scham vor dem Schlafe! Das ist das Erste! Und Allen aus dem Wege gehn, die schlecht schlafen und Nachts wachen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0003","text":"Schamhaft ist noch der Dieb vor dem Schlafe: stets stiehlt er sich leise durch die Nacht. Schamlos aber ist der Wächter der Nacht, schamlos trägt er sein Horn.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0004","text":"Keine geringe Kunst ist schlafen: es thut schon Noth, den ganzen Tag darauf hin zu wachen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0005","text":"Zehn Mal musst du des Tages dich selber überwinden: das macht eine gute Müdigkeit und ist Mohn der Seele.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0006","text":"Zehn Mal musst du dich wieder dir selber versöhnen; denn Überwindung ist Bitterniss, und schlecht schläft der Unversöhnte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0007","text":"Zehn Wahrheiten musst du des Tages finden: sonst suchst du noch des Nachts nach Wahrheit, und deine Seele blieb hungrig.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0008","text":"Zehn Mal musst du lachen am Tage und heiter sein: sonst stört dich der Magen in der Nacht, dieser Vater der Trübsal.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0009","text":"Wenige wissen das: aber man muss alle Tugenden haben, um gut zu schlafen. Werde ich falsch Zeugniss reden? Werde ich ehebrechen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0010","text":"Werde ich mich gelüsten lassen meines Nächsten Magd? Das Alles vertrüge sich schlecht mit gutem Schlafe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0011","text":"Und selbst wenn man alle Tugenden hat, muss man sich noch auf Eins verstehn: selber die Tugenden zur rechten Zeit schlafen schicken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0012","text":"Dass sie sich nicht mit einander zanken, die artigen Weiblein! Und über dich, du Unglückseliger!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0013","text":"Friede mit Gott und dem Nachbar: so will es der gute Schlaf. Und Friede auch noch mit des Nachbars Teufel! Sonst geht er bei dir des Nachts um.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0014","text":"Ehre der Obrigkeit und Gehorsam, und auch der krummen Obrigkeit! So will es der gute Schlaf. Was kann ich dafür, dass die Macht gerne auf krummen Beinen Wandelt?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0015","text":"Der soll mir immer der beste Hirt heissen, der sein Schaf auf die grünste Aue führt: so verträgt es sich mit dem gutem Schlafe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0016","text":"Viel Ehren will ich nicht, noch grosse Schätze: das entzündet die Milz. Aber schlecht schläft es sich ohne einen guten Namen und einen kleinen Schatz.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0017","text":"Eine kleine Gesellschaft ist mir willkommener als eine böse: doch muss sie gehn und kommen zur rechten Zeit. So verträgt es sich mit gutem Schlafe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0018","text":"Sehr gefallen mir auch die Geistig-Armen: sie fördern den Schlaf. Selig sind die, sonderlich, wenn man ihnen immer Recht giebt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0019","text":"Also läuft der Tag dem Tugendsamen. Kommt nun die Nacht, so hüte ich mich wohl, den Schlaf zu rufen! Nicht will er gerufen sein, der Schlaf, der der Herr der Tugenden ist!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0020","text":"Sondern ich denke, was ich des Tages gethan und gedacht. Wiederkäuend frage ich mich, geduldsam gleich einer Kuh: welches waren doch deine zehn Überwindungen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0021","text":"Und welches waren die zehn Versöhnungen und die zehn Wahrheiten und die zehn Gelächter, mit denen sich mein Herz gütlich that?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0022","text":"Solcherlei erwägend und gewiegt von vierzig Gedanken, überfällt mich auf einmal der Schlaf, der Ungerufne, der Herr der Tugenden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0023","text":"Der Schlaf klopft mir auf meine Auge: da wird es schwer. Der Schlaf berührt mir den Mund: da bleibt er offen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0024","text":"Wahrlich, auf weichen Sohlen kommt er mir, der liebste der Diebe, und stiehlt mir meine Gedanken: dumm stehe ich da wie dieser Lehrstuhl.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0025","text":"Aber nicht lange mehr stehe ich dann: da liege ich schon.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0026","text":"Als Zarathustra den Weisen also sprechen hörte, lachte er bei sich im Herzen: denn ihm war dabei ein Licht aufgegangen. Und also sprach er zu seinem Herzen:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0027","text":"Ein Narr ist mir dieser Weise da mit seinen vierzig Gedanken: aber ich glaube, dass er sich wohl auf das Schlafen versteht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0028","text":"Glücklich schon, wer in der Nähe dieses Weisen wohnt! Solch ein Schlaf steckt an, noch durch eine dicke Wand hindurch steckt er an.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0029","text":"Ein Zauber wohnt selbst in seinem Lehrstuhle. Und nicht vergebens sassen die Jünglinge vor dem Prediger der Tugend.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0030","text":"Seine Weisheit heisst: wachen, um gut zu schlafen. Und wahrlich, hätte das Leben keinen Sinn und müsste ich Unsinn wählen, so wäre auch mir diess der wählenswürdigste Unsinn.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0031","text":"Jetzo verstehe ich klar, was einst man vor Allem suchte, wenn man Lehrer der Tugend suchte. Guten Schlaf suchte man sich und mohnblumige Tugenden dazu!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0032","text":"Allen diesen gelobten Weisen der Lehrstühle war Weisheit der Schlaf ohne Träume: sie kannten keinen bessern Sinn des Lebens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0033","text":"Auch noch heute wohl giebt es Einige, wie diesen Prediger der Tugend, und nicht immer so Ehrliche: aber ihre Zeit ist um. Und nicht mehr lange stehen sie noch: da liegen sie schon.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0034","text":"Selig sind diese Schläfrigen: denn sie sollen bald einnicken.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0035","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"a9895d15a3b2d69e7b73aea369a662ead4cb0bc1087a98d99dce605e20db7a98","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181213423320Z_fbecc2f9","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Teachers of Virtue","source_sha256":"a9895d15a3b2d69e7b73aea369a662ead4cb0bc1087a98d99dce605e20db7a98","sha256":"864175830dea882c4462d20c067b2134c853146168f5dc2805c16209663ecead","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:12:13.423341+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0001","text":"They praised to Zarathustra a wise man who knew well how to speak of sleep and of virtue: for this he was greatly honoured and rewarded, and all the young men sat before his lectern. Zarathustra went to him, and with all the young men he sat before his lectern. And thus spoke the wise man:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0002","text":"Honour and shame before sleep! That is the first thing! And avoid all those who sleep badly and wake at night!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0003","text":"Even the thief is still ashamed before sleep: he always steals softly through the night. But shameless is the night-watchman; shamelessly he bears his horn.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0004","text":"Sleeping is no slight art: it is indeed necessary to have kept awake the whole day for its sake.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0005","text":"Ten times a day you must overcome yourself: that makes for a good weariness and is poppy for the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0006","text":"Ten times you must reconcile yourself again with yourself; for overcoming is bitterness, and the unreconciled sleeps badly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0007","text":"Ten truths you must find by day: otherwise you will still be seeking truth by night, and your soul will remain hungry.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0008","text":"Ten times you must laugh in the day and be cheerful: otherwise your stomach, this father of melancholy, will disturb you at night.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0009","text":"Few know this: but one must have all the virtues in order to sleep well. Shall I bear false witness? Shall I commit adultery?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0010","text":"Shall I let myself lust after my neighbour's maid? All that would accord badly with good sleep.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0011","text":"And even if one has all the virtues, one must still understand one thing: to send the virtues themselves to sleep at the right time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0012","text":"So that the pretty little women do not quarrel with one another! And over you, you unfortunate one!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0013","text":"Peace with God and neighbour: that is what good sleep requires. And peace even with the neighbour's devil! Otherwise he prowls about your house at night.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0014","text":"Honour to authority and obedience, even to crooked authority! That is what good sleep requires. What can I do about it, if power likes to walk on crooked legs?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0015","text":"Let him always be called the best shepherd by me who leads his sheep to the greenest pasture: thus it accords with good sleep.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0016","text":"I do not want many honours, nor great treasures: that inflames the spleen. But one sleeps badly without a good name and a small treasure.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0017","text":"A small company is more welcome to me than a bad one: yet it must go and come at the right time. Thus it accords with good sleep.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0018","text":"I also like the poor in spirit very much: they further sleep. Blessed are they, especially if one always gives them the right.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0019","text":"Thus the day passes for the virtuous man. When night comes, I take good care not to call sleep! Sleep, who is the lord of the virtues, does not wish to be called!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0020","text":"Rather, I think of what I have done and thought during the day. Ruminating, I ask myself, patiently like a cow: what then were your ten overcomings?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0021","text":"And what were the ten reconciliations and the ten truths and the ten laughs with which my heart regaled itself?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0022","text":"Pondering such things and rocked by forty thoughts, sleep suddenly comes over me—the uncalled one, the lord of the virtues.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0023","text":"Sleep knocks upon my eye: then it grows heavy. Sleep touches my mouth: then it remains open.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0024","text":"Truly, he comes to me on soft soles, the dearest of thieves, and steals my thoughts from me: stupidly I stand there like this lectern.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0025","text":"But I shall not stand much longer then: there I already lie.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0026","text":"When Zarathustra heard the sage speaking thus, he laughed within his heart: for a light had dawned on him thereby. And thus he spoke to his heart:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0027","text":"This sage here with his forty thoughts is a fool to me: but I believe that he understands sleeping well.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0028","text":"Happy already is he who lives near this sage! Such sleep is contagious; even through a thick wall it is contagious.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0029","text":"There dwells a magic even in his lectern. And it was not in vain that the young men sat before the preacher of virtue.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0030","text":"His wisdom is called: to keep awake in order to sleep well. And verily, if life had no meaning and I had to choose nonsense, this too would be for me the most choiceworthy nonsense.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0031","text":"Now I clearly understand what was once sought before all else when teachers of virtue were sought. Good sleep was sought for oneself, and poppy-flowered virtues besides!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0032","text":"For all these praised sages of the lecterns, wisdom was sleep without dreams: they knew no better meaning of life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0033","text":"Even today there are perhaps still some like this preacher of virtue, and not always so honest: but their time is over. And they too will not stand much longer: there they already lie.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0034","text":"Blessed are these sleepy ones: for they shall soon doze off.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0035","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDLH:P0035"]}],"source_digest":"881dbde1e14fc89d9b1d05a8a513ab235f0f5504d6a42d6edbe65f6c736598e6","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von den Lehrstühlen der Tugend"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von den Lehrstühlen der Tugend","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0","title":"On the Afterworldly","order":13,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDH0","text":"Einst warf auch Zarathustra seinen Wahn jenseits des Menschen, gleich allen Hinterweltlern. Eines leidenden und zerquälten Gottes Werk schien mir da die Welt.\n\nTraum schien mir da die Welt und Dichtung eines Gottes; farbiger Rauch vor den Augen eines göttlich Unzufriednen.\n\nGut und böse und Lust und Leid und Ich und Du—farbiger Rauch dünkte mich’s vor schöpferischen Augen. Wegsehn wollte der Schöpfer von sich,—da schuf er die Welt.\n\nTrunkne Lust ist’s dem Leidenden, wegzusehn von seinem Leiden und sich zu verlieren. Trunkne Lust Und Selbst-sich-Verlieren dünkte mich einst die Welt.\n\nDiese Welt, die ewig unvollkommene, eines ewigen Widerspruches Abbild und unvollkommnes Abbild—eine trunkne Lust ihrem unvollkommnen Schöpfer:—also dünkte mich einst die Welt.\n\nAlso warf auch ich einst meinen Wahn jenseits des Menschen, gleich allen Hinterweltlern. Jenseits des Menschen in Wahrheit?\n\nAch, ihr Brüder, dieser Gott, den ich schuf, war Menschen-Werk und -Wahnsinn, gleich allen Göttern!\n\nMensch war er, und nur ein armes Stück Mensch und Ich: aus der eigenen Asche und Gluth kam es mir, dieses Gespenst, und wahrlich! Nicht kam es mir von Jenseits!\n\nWas geschah, meine Brüder? Ich überwand mich, den Leidenden, ich trug meine eigne Asche zu Berge, eine hellere Flamme erfand ich mir. Und siehe! Da wich das Gespenst von mir!\n\nLeiden wäre es mir jetzt und Qual dem Genesenen, solche Gespenster zu glauben: Leiden wäre es mir jetzt und Erniedrigung. Also rede ich zu den Hinterweltlern.\n\nLeiden war’s und Unvermögen—das schuf alle Hinterwelten; und jener kurze Wahnsinn des Glücks, den nur der Leidendste erfährt.\n\nMüdigkeit, die mit Einem Sprunge zum Letzten will, mit einem Todessprunge, eine arme unwissende Müdigkeit, die nicht einmal mehr wollen will: die schuf alle Götter und Hinterwelten.\n\nGlaubt es mir, meine Brüder! Der Leib war’s, der am Leibe verzweifelte,—der tastete mit den Fingern des bethörten Geistes an die letzten Wände.\n\nGlaubt es mir, meine Brüder! Der Leib war’s, der an der Erde verzweifelte,—der hörte den Bauch des Seins zu sich reden.\n\nUnd da wollte er mit dem Kopfe durch die letzten Wände, und nicht nur mit dem Kopfe,—hinüber zu „jener Welt“.\n\nAber „jene Welt“ ist gut verborgen vor dem Menschen, jene entmenschte unmenschliche Welt, die ein himmlisches Nichts ist; und der Bauch des Seins redet gar nicht zum Menschen, es sei denn als Mensch.\n\nWahrlich, schwer zu beweisen ist alles Sein und schwer zum Reden zu bringen. Sagt mir, ihr Brüder, ist nicht das Wunderlichste aller Dinge noch am besten bewiesen?\n\nJa, diess Ich und des Ich’s Widerspruch und Wirrsal redet noch am redlichsten von seinem Sein, dieses schaffende, wollende, werthende Ich, welches das Maass und der Werth der Dinge ist.\n\nUnd diess redlichste Sein, das Ich—das redet vom Leibe, und es will noch den Leib, selbst wenn es dichtet und schwärmt und mit zerbrochnen Flügeln flattert.\n\nImmer redlicher lernt es reden, das Ich: und je mehr es lernt, um so mehr findet es Worte und Ehren für Leib und Erde.\n\nEinen neuen Stolz lehrte mich mein Ich, den lehre ich die Menschen:—nicht mehr den Kopf in den Sand der himmlischen Dinge zu stecken, sondern frei ihn zu tragen, einen Erden-Kopf, der der Erde Sinn schafft!\n\nEinen neuen Willen lehre ich die Menschen: diesen Weg wollen, den blindlings der Mensch gegangen, und gut ihn heissen und nicht mehr von ihm bei Seite schleichen, gleich den Kranken und Absterbenden!\n\nKranke und Absterbende waren es, die verachteten Leib und Erde und erfanden das Himmlische und die erlösenden Blutstropfen: aber auch noch diese süssen und düstern Gifte nahmen sie von Leib und Erde!\n\nIhrem Elende wollten sie entlaufen, und die Sterne waren ihnen zu weit. Da seufzten sie: „Oh dass es doch himmlische Wege gäbe, sich in ein andres Sein und Glück zu schleichen!“—da erfanden sie sich ihre Schliche und blutigen Tränklein!\n\nIhrem Leibe und dieser Erde nun entrückt wähnten sie sich, diese Undankbaren. Doch wem dankten sie ihrer Entrückung Krampf und Wonne? Ihrem Leibe und dieser Erde.\n\nMilde ist Zarathustra den Kranken. Wahrlich, er zürnt nicht ihren Arten des Trostes und Undanks. Mögen sie Genesende werden und Überwindende und einen höheren Leib sich schaffen!\n\nNicht auch zürnt Zarathustra dem Genesenden, wenn er zärtlich nach seinem Wahne blickt und Mitternachts um das Grab seines Gottes schleicht: aber Krankheit und kranker Leib bleiben mir auch seine Thränen noch.\n\nVieles krankhafte Volk gab es immer unter Denen, welche dichten und gottsüchtig sind; wüthend hassen sie den Erkennenden und jene jüngste der Tugenden, welche heisst: Redlichkeit.\n\nRückwärts blicken sie immer nach dunklen Zeiten: da freilich war Wahn und Glaube ein ander Ding; Raserei der Vernunft war Gottähnlichkeit, und Zweifel Sünde.\n\nAllzugut kenne ich diese Gottähnlichen: sie wollen, dass an sie geglaubt werde, und Zweifel Sünde sei. Allzugut weiss ich auch, woran sie selber am besten glauben.\n\nWahrlich nicht an Hinterwelten und erlösende Blutstropfen: sondern an den Leib glauben auch sie am besten, und ihr eigener Leib ist ihnen ihr Ding an sich.\n\nAber ein krankhaftes Ding ist er ihnen: und gerne möchten sie aus der Haut fahren. Darum horchen sie nach den Predigern des Todes und predigen selber Hinterwelten.\n\nHört mir lieber, meine Brüder, auf die Stimme des gesunden Leibes: eine redlichere und reinere Simme ist diess.\n\nRedlicher redet und reiner der gesunde Leib, der vollkommne und rechtwinklige: und er redet vom Sinn der Erde.\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0001","text":"Einst warf auch Zarathustra seinen Wahn jenseits des Menschen, gleich allen Hinterweltlern. Eines leidenden und zerquälten Gottes Werk schien mir da die Welt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0002","text":"Traum schien mir da die Welt und Dichtung eines Gottes; farbiger Rauch vor den Augen eines göttlich Unzufriednen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0003","text":"Gut und böse und Lust und Leid und Ich und Du—farbiger Rauch dünkte mich’s vor schöpferischen Augen. Wegsehn wollte der Schöpfer von sich,—da schuf er die Welt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0004","text":"Trunkne Lust ist’s dem Leidenden, wegzusehn von seinem Leiden und sich zu verlieren. Trunkne Lust Und Selbst-sich-Verlieren dünkte mich einst die Welt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0005","text":"Diese Welt, die ewig unvollkommene, eines ewigen Widerspruches Abbild und unvollkommnes Abbild—eine trunkne Lust ihrem unvollkommnen Schöpfer:—also dünkte mich einst die Welt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0006","text":"Also warf auch ich einst meinen Wahn jenseits des Menschen, gleich allen Hinterweltlern. Jenseits des Menschen in Wahrheit?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0007","text":"Ach, ihr Brüder, dieser Gott, den ich schuf, war Menschen-Werk und -Wahnsinn, gleich allen Göttern!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0008","text":"Mensch war er, und nur ein armes Stück Mensch und Ich: aus der eigenen Asche und Gluth kam es mir, dieses Gespenst, und wahrlich! Nicht kam es mir von Jenseits!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0009","text":"Was geschah, meine Brüder? Ich überwand mich, den Leidenden, ich trug meine eigne Asche zu Berge, eine hellere Flamme erfand ich mir. Und siehe! Da wich das Gespenst von mir!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0010","text":"Leiden wäre es mir jetzt und Qual dem Genesenen, solche Gespenster zu glauben: Leiden wäre es mir jetzt und Erniedrigung. Also rede ich zu den Hinterweltlern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0011","text":"Leiden war’s und Unvermögen—das schuf alle Hinterwelten; und jener kurze Wahnsinn des Glücks, den nur der Leidendste erfährt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0012","text":"Müdigkeit, die mit Einem Sprunge zum Letzten will, mit einem Todessprunge, eine arme unwissende Müdigkeit, die nicht einmal mehr wollen will: die schuf alle Götter und Hinterwelten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0013","text":"Glaubt es mir, meine Brüder! Der Leib war’s, der am Leibe verzweifelte,—der tastete mit den Fingern des bethörten Geistes an die letzten Wände.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0014","text":"Glaubt es mir, meine Brüder! Der Leib war’s, der an der Erde verzweifelte,—der hörte den Bauch des Seins zu sich reden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0015","text":"Und da wollte er mit dem Kopfe durch die letzten Wände, und nicht nur mit dem Kopfe,—hinüber zu „jener Welt“.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0016","text":"Aber „jene Welt“ ist gut verborgen vor dem Menschen, jene entmenschte unmenschliche Welt, die ein himmlisches Nichts ist; und der Bauch des Seins redet gar nicht zum Menschen, es sei denn als Mensch.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0017","text":"Wahrlich, schwer zu beweisen ist alles Sein und schwer zum Reden zu bringen. Sagt mir, ihr Brüder, ist nicht das Wunderlichste aller Dinge noch am besten bewiesen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0018","text":"Ja, diess Ich und des Ich’s Widerspruch und Wirrsal redet noch am redlichsten von seinem Sein, dieses schaffende, wollende, werthende Ich, welches das Maass und der Werth der Dinge ist.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0019","text":"Und diess redlichste Sein, das Ich—das redet vom Leibe, und es will noch den Leib, selbst wenn es dichtet und schwärmt und mit zerbrochnen Flügeln flattert.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0020","text":"Immer redlicher lernt es reden, das Ich: und je mehr es lernt, um so mehr findet es Worte und Ehren für Leib und Erde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0021","text":"Einen neuen Stolz lehrte mich mein Ich, den lehre ich die Menschen:—nicht mehr den Kopf in den Sand der himmlischen Dinge zu stecken, sondern frei ihn zu tragen, einen Erden-Kopf, der der Erde Sinn schafft!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0022","text":"Einen neuen Willen lehre ich die Menschen: diesen Weg wollen, den blindlings der Mensch gegangen, und gut ihn heissen und nicht mehr von ihm bei Seite schleichen, gleich den Kranken und Absterbenden!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0023","text":"Kranke und Absterbende waren es, die verachteten Leib und Erde und erfanden das Himmlische und die erlösenden Blutstropfen: aber auch noch diese süssen und düstern Gifte nahmen sie von Leib und Erde!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0024","text":"Ihrem Elende wollten sie entlaufen, und die Sterne waren ihnen zu weit. Da seufzten sie: „Oh dass es doch himmlische Wege gäbe, sich in ein andres Sein und Glück zu schleichen!“—da erfanden sie sich ihre Schliche und blutigen Tränklein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0025","text":"Ihrem Leibe und dieser Erde nun entrückt wähnten sie sich, diese Undankbaren. Doch wem dankten sie ihrer Entrückung Krampf und Wonne? Ihrem Leibe und dieser Erde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0026","text":"Milde ist Zarathustra den Kranken. Wahrlich, er zürnt nicht ihren Arten des Trostes und Undanks. Mögen sie Genesende werden und Überwindende und einen höheren Leib sich schaffen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0027","text":"Nicht auch zürnt Zarathustra dem Genesenden, wenn er zärtlich nach seinem Wahne blickt und Mitternachts um das Grab seines Gottes schleicht: aber Krankheit und kranker Leib bleiben mir auch seine Thränen noch.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0028","text":"Vieles krankhafte Volk gab es immer unter Denen, welche dichten und gottsüchtig sind; wüthend hassen sie den Erkennenden und jene jüngste der Tugenden, welche heisst: Redlichkeit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0029","text":"Rückwärts blicken sie immer nach dunklen Zeiten: da freilich war Wahn und Glaube ein ander Ding; Raserei der Vernunft war Gottähnlichkeit, und Zweifel Sünde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0030","text":"Allzugut kenne ich diese Gottähnlichen: sie wollen, dass an sie geglaubt werde, und Zweifel Sünde sei. Allzugut weiss ich auch, woran sie selber am besten glauben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0031","text":"Wahrlich nicht an Hinterwelten und erlösende Blutstropfen: sondern an den Leib glauben auch sie am besten, und ihr eigener Leib ist ihnen ihr Ding an sich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0032","text":"Aber ein krankhaftes Ding ist er ihnen: und gerne möchten sie aus der Haut fahren. Darum horchen sie nach den Predigern des Todes und predigen selber Hinterwelten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0033","text":"Hört mir lieber, meine Brüder, auf die Stimme des gesunden Leibes: eine redlichere und reinere Simme ist diess.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0034","text":"Redlicher redet und reiner der gesunde Leib, der vollkommne und rechtwinklige: und er redet vom Sinn der Erde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0035","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"cff7983361c323403619bac26c6401bca5f5a31d4b2853db944057dea80e6873","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181234264865Z_4455a0c0","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Afterworldly","source_sha256":"cff7983361c323403619bac26c6401bca5f5a31d4b2853db944057dea80e6873","sha256":"19e3fe8fe1cbff0013f0e401e1cdcdde10c059ac27d5e8620db78895e0c8f589","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:12:34.264882+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0001","text":"Once Zarathustra too cast his delusion beyond man, like all afterworldly ones. The world then seemed to me the work of a suffering and tormented God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0002","text":"The world then seemed to me a dream and the poetry of a God; coloured smoke before the eyes of a divinely dissatisfied one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0003","text":"Good and evil and pleasure and pain and I and thou—coloured smoke, it seemed to me, before creative eyes. The creator wished to look away from himself,—so he created the world.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0004","text":"To the sufferer it is drunken joy to look away from his suffering and to lose himself. Drunken joy and self-loss, the world once seemed to me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0005","text":"This world, eternally imperfect, the image of an eternal contradiction and an imperfect image—a drunken joy to its imperfect creator:—thus the world once seemed to me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0006","text":"Thus I too once cast my delusion beyond man, like all afterworldly ones. Beyond man in truth?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0007","text":"Ah, my brothers, this God whom I created was human work and human madness, like all Gods!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0008","text":"He was man, and only a poor piece of man and I: from my own ashes and glow there came to me this spectre, and truly! It did not come to me from Beyond!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0009","text":"What happened, my brothers? I overcame myself, the sufferer; I carried my own ashes up the mountain, I invented for myself a brighter flame. And behold! Then the spectre withdrew from me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0010","text":"It would now be suffering to me and torment to one recovered, to believe in such spectres: it would now be suffering to me and humiliation. Thus I speak to the afterworldly ones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0011","text":"It was suffering and incapacity—that created all afterworlds; and that brief madness of happiness which only the greatest sufferer experiences.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0012","text":"Weariness, which with one leap wants to reach the ultimate, with a death-leap, a poor ignorant weariness, which no longer even wants to will: that created all Gods and afterworlds.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0013","text":"Believe me, my brothers! It was the body that despaired of the body,—that groped at the ultimate walls with the fingers of the bewildered spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0014","text":"Believe me, my brothers! It was the body that despaired of the earth,—that heard the belly of Being speak to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0015","text":"And then it wanted to go with its head through the ultimate walls, and not only with its head,—across into “that world.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0016","text":"But “that world” is well concealed from man, that dehumanized inhuman world which is a heavenly nothingness; and the belly of Being does not speak to man at all, except as man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0017","text":"Truly, all Being is hard to prove and hard to bring to speech. Tell me, my brothers, is not the most wondrous of all things still the best proved?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0018","text":"Yes, this I and the contradiction and confusion of the I still speak most honestly of their Being, this creating, willing, valuing I, which is the measure and value of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0019","text":"And this most honest Being, the I—that speaks of the body, and it still wants the body, even when it poetizes and raves and flutters with broken wings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0020","text":"Ever more honestly it learns to speak, the I: and the more it learns, the more words and honours it finds for body and earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0021","text":"My I taught me a new pride, which I teach to men:—no longer to stick one’s head into the sand of heavenly things, but to carry it freely, an earthly head that creates a meaning for the earth!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0022","text":"I teach men a new will: to will this path which man has gone blindly, and to call it good and no longer to slink aside from it, like the sick and the dying!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0023","text":"It was the sick and the dying who despised body and earth and invented the heavenly and the redeeming drops of blood: but even these sweet and sombre poisons they took from body and earth!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0024","text":"They wanted to flee their misery, and the stars were too far away for them. Then they sighed: “Oh, if only there were heavenly ways of stealing into another being and happiness!”—then they invented for themselves their subterfuges and little bloody potions!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0025","text":"They imagined themselves now transported away from their bodies and from this earth, these ungrateful ones. But to whom did they owe the convulsion and bliss of their rapture? To their bodies and to this earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0026","text":"Zarathustra is gentle toward the sick. Verily, he is not angry at their kinds of consolation and ingratitude. May they become convalescents and overcomers and create for themselves a higher body!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0027","text":"Nor is Zarathustra angry at the convalescent when he tenderly looks upon his delusion and steals around the grave of his God at midnight: but even his tears still remain to me sickness and a sick body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0028","text":"There has always been much sickly people among those who compose poetry and are god-possessed; furiously they hate the knower and that youngest of virtues, which is called: probity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0029","text":"They always look backward toward dark ages: then indeed delusion and faith were another thing; frenzy of reason was godlikeness, and doubt sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0030","text":"All too well I know these godlike ones: they want to be believed in, and doubt to be sin. All too well I also know what they themselves believe in most strongly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0031","text":"Verily, not in afterworlds and redeeming drops of blood: rather, they too believe most strongly in the body, and their own body is their thing in itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0032","text":"But it is a sickly thing to them: and gladly they would like to get out of their skin. Therefore they listen out for the preachers of death and themselves preach afterworlds.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0033","text":"Rather listen to me, my brothers, to the voice of the healthy body: it is a more honest and purer voice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0034","text":"More honestly and more purely speaks the healthy body, the perfect and square-angled one: and it speaks of the meaning of the earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0035","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDH0:P0035"]}],"source_digest":"e3d41d246be2b838c718b7e45d70f48a31025965b2372d32850385a1142111dd","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von den Hinterweltlern"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von den Hinterweltlern","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC","title":"On the Despisers of the Body","order":14,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDVC","text":"Den Verächtern des Leibes will ich mein Wort sagen. Nicht umlernen und umlehren sollen sie mir, sondern nur ihrem eignen Leibe Lebewohl sagen—und also stumm werden.\n\n„Leib bin ich und Seele“ —so redet das Kind. Und warum sollte man nicht wie die Kinder reden?\n\nAber der Erwachte, der Wissende sagt: Leib bin ich ganz und gar, und Nichts ausserdem; und Seele ist nur ein Wort für ein Etwas am Leibe.\n\nDer Leib ist eine grosse Vernunft, eine Vielheit mit Einem Sinne, ein Krieg und ein Frieden, eine Heerde und ein Hirt.\n\nWerkzeug deines Leibes ist auch deine kleine Vernunft, mein Bruder, die du „Geist“ nennst, ein kleines Werk- und Spielzeug deiner grossen Vernunft.\n\n„Ich“ sagst du und bist stolz auf diess Wort. Aber das Grössere ist, woran du nicht glauben willst,—dein Leib und seine grosse Vernunft: die sagt nicht Ich, aber thut Ich.\n\nWas der Sinn fühlt, was der Geist erkennt, das hat niemals in sich sein Ende. Aber Sinn und Geist möchten dich überreden, sie seien aller Dinge Ende: so eitel sind sie.\n\nWerk- und Spielzeuge sind Sinn und Geist: hinter ihnen liegt noch das Selbst. Das Selbst sucht auch mit den Augen der Sinne, es horcht auch mit den Ohren des Geistes.\n\nImmer horcht das Selbst und sucht: es vergleicht, bezwingt, erobert, zerstört. Es herrscht und ist auch des Ich’s Beherrscher.\n\nHinter deinen Gedanken und Gefühlen, mein Bruder, steht ein mächtiger Gebieter, ein unbekannter Weiser—der heisst Selbst. In deinem Leibe wohnt er, dein Leib ist er.\n\nEs ist mehr Vernunft in deinem Leibe, als in deiner besten Weisheit. Und wer weiss denn, wozu dein Leib gerade deine beste Weisheit nöthig hat?\n\nDein Selbst lacht über dein Ich und seine stolzen Sprünge. „Was sind mir diese Sprünge und Flüge des Gedankens? sagt es sich. Ein Umweg zu meinem Zwecke. Ich bin das Gängelband des Ich’s und der Einbläser seiner Begriffe.“\n\nDas Selbst sagt zum Ich: „hier fühle Schmerz!“ Und da leidet es und denkt nach, wie es nicht mehr leide—und dazu eben soll es denken.\n\nDas Selbst sagt zum Ich: „hier fühle Lust!“ Da freut es sich und denkt nach, wie es noch oft sich freue—und dazu eben soll es denken.\n\nDen Verächtern des Leibes will ich ein Wort sagen. Dass sie verachten, das macht ihr Achten. Was ist es, das Achten und Verachten und Werth und Willen schuf?\n\nDas schaffende Selbst schuf sich Achten und Verachten, es schuf sich Lust und Weh. Der schaffende Leib schuf sich den Geist als eine Hand seines Willens.\n\nNoch in eurer Thorheit und Verachtung, ihr Verächter des Leibes, dient ihr eurem Selbst. Ich sage euch: euer Selbst selber will sterben und kehrt sich vom Leben ab.\n\nNicht mehr vermag es das, was es am liebsten wilI:—über sich hinaus zu schaffen. Das will es am liebsten, das ist seine ganze Inbrunst.\n\nAber zu spät ward es ihm jetzt dafür:—so will euer Selbst untergehn, ihr Verächter des Leibes.\n\nUntergehn will euer Selbst, und darum wurdet ihr zu Verächtern des Leibes! Denn nicht mehr vermögt ihr über euch hinaus zu schaffen.\n\nUnd darum zürnt ihr nun dem Leben und der Erde. Ein ungewusster Neid ist im scheelen Blick eurer Verachtung.\n\nIch gehe nicht euren Weg, ihr Verächter des Leibes! Ihr seid mir keine Brücken zum Übermenschen!—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0001","text":"Den Verächtern des Leibes will ich mein Wort sagen. Nicht umlernen und umlehren sollen sie mir, sondern nur ihrem eignen Leibe Lebewohl sagen—und also stumm werden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0002","text":"„Leib bin ich und Seele“ —so redet das Kind. Und warum sollte man nicht wie die Kinder reden?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0003","text":"Aber der Erwachte, der Wissende sagt: Leib bin ich ganz und gar, und Nichts ausserdem; und Seele ist nur ein Wort für ein Etwas am Leibe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0004","text":"Der Leib ist eine grosse Vernunft, eine Vielheit mit Einem Sinne, ein Krieg und ein Frieden, eine Heerde und ein Hirt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0005","text":"Werkzeug deines Leibes ist auch deine kleine Vernunft, mein Bruder, die du „Geist“ nennst, ein kleines Werk- und Spielzeug deiner grossen Vernunft.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0006","text":"„Ich“ sagst du und bist stolz auf diess Wort. Aber das Grössere ist, woran du nicht glauben willst,—dein Leib und seine grosse Vernunft: die sagt nicht Ich, aber thut Ich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0007","text":"Was der Sinn fühlt, was der Geist erkennt, das hat niemals in sich sein Ende. Aber Sinn und Geist möchten dich überreden, sie seien aller Dinge Ende: so eitel sind sie.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0008","text":"Werk- und Spielzeuge sind Sinn und Geist: hinter ihnen liegt noch das Selbst. Das Selbst sucht auch mit den Augen der Sinne, es horcht auch mit den Ohren des Geistes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0009","text":"Immer horcht das Selbst und sucht: es vergleicht, bezwingt, erobert, zerstört. Es herrscht und ist auch des Ich’s Beherrscher.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0010","text":"Hinter deinen Gedanken und Gefühlen, mein Bruder, steht ein mächtiger Gebieter, ein unbekannter Weiser—der heisst Selbst. In deinem Leibe wohnt er, dein Leib ist er.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0011","text":"Es ist mehr Vernunft in deinem Leibe, als in deiner besten Weisheit. Und wer weiss denn, wozu dein Leib gerade deine beste Weisheit nöthig hat?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0012","text":"Dein Selbst lacht über dein Ich und seine stolzen Sprünge. „Was sind mir diese Sprünge und Flüge des Gedankens? sagt es sich. Ein Umweg zu meinem Zwecke. Ich bin das Gängelband des Ich’s und der Einbläser seiner Begriffe.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0013","text":"Das Selbst sagt zum Ich: „hier fühle Schmerz!“ Und da leidet es und denkt nach, wie es nicht mehr leide—und dazu eben soll es denken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0014","text":"Das Selbst sagt zum Ich: „hier fühle Lust!“ Da freut es sich und denkt nach, wie es noch oft sich freue—und dazu eben soll es denken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0015","text":"Den Verächtern des Leibes will ich ein Wort sagen. Dass sie verachten, das macht ihr Achten. Was ist es, das Achten und Verachten und Werth und Willen schuf?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0016","text":"Das schaffende Selbst schuf sich Achten und Verachten, es schuf sich Lust und Weh. Der schaffende Leib schuf sich den Geist als eine Hand seines Willens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0017","text":"Noch in eurer Thorheit und Verachtung, ihr Verächter des Leibes, dient ihr eurem Selbst. Ich sage euch: euer Selbst selber will sterben und kehrt sich vom Leben ab.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0018","text":"Nicht mehr vermag es das, was es am liebsten wilI:—über sich hinaus zu schaffen. Das will es am liebsten, das ist seine ganze Inbrunst.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0019","text":"Aber zu spät ward es ihm jetzt dafür:—so will euer Selbst untergehn, ihr Verächter des Leibes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0020","text":"Untergehn will euer Selbst, und darum wurdet ihr zu Verächtern des Leibes! Denn nicht mehr vermögt ihr über euch hinaus zu schaffen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0021","text":"Und darum zürnt ihr nun dem Leben und der Erde. Ein ungewusster Neid ist im scheelen Blick eurer Verachtung.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0022","text":"Ich gehe nicht euren Weg, ihr Verächter des Leibes! Ihr seid mir keine Brücken zum Übermenschen!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0023","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"951bc2d257d14c3d93f045181441a3bd3f65f03774b1cedcdbd0c456623ae177","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181243654020Z_bcb1c85c","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Despisers of the Body","source_sha256":"951bc2d257d14c3d93f045181441a3bd3f65f03774b1cedcdbd0c456623ae177","sha256":"a13a6b1fe5edc1d89cf9450e1b5d173df7cd38514cb5e630595b1ab7cedbc94f","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:12:43.654089+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0001","text":"To the despisers of the body I will speak my word. They are not to relearn and reteach for my sake, but only bid farewell to their own body—and thus become silent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0002","text":"“Body am I and soul”—thus speaks the child. And why should one not speak like children?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0003","text":"But the awakened, the knowing one says: I am body entirely, and nothing besides; and soul is merely a word for something in the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0004","text":"The body is a great reason, a multiplicity with one sense, a war and a peace, a herd and a shepherd.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0005","text":"Your little reason too, my brother, which you call “spirit,” is an instrument of your body, a little work- and plaything of your great reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0006","text":"“I” you say and are proud of this word. But greater is that in which you do not wish to believe—your body and its great reason: it does not say I, but does I.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0007","text":"What sense feels, what spirit recognizes, never has its end in itself. But sense and spirit would persuade you that they are the end of all things: so vain are they.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0008","text":"Sense and spirit are work- and playthings: behind them still lies the Self. The Self also seeks with the eyes of the senses; it also listens with the ears of the spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0009","text":"Ever does the Self listen and seek: it compares, subdues, conquers, destroys. It rules and is also the ruler of the I.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0010","text":"Behind your thoughts and feelings, my brother, stands a mighty commander, an unknown wise one—he is called Self. He dwells in your body; he is your body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0011","text":"There is more reason in your body than in your best wisdom. And who knows what your body needs precisely your best wisdom for?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0012","text":"Your Self laughs at your I and its proud leaps. “What are these leaps and flights of thought to me? it says to itself. A detour to my purpose. I am the leading-string of the I and the prompter of its concepts.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0013","text":"The Self says to the I: “Here feel pain!” And there it suffers and reflects on how it might no longer suffer—and precisely for that it is meant to think.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0014","text":"The Self says to the I: “Here feel pleasure!” There it rejoices and reflects on how it may rejoice often still—and precisely for that it is meant to think.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0015","text":"To the despisers of the body I will speak a word. That they despise, that makes their esteeming. What is it that created esteeming and despising and value and willing?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0016","text":"The creating Self created for itself esteeming and despising; it created for itself pleasure and woe. The creating body created for itself the spirit as a hand of its will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0017","text":"Even in your folly and contempt, you despisers of the body, you serve your Self. I tell you: your Self itself wishes to die and turns away from life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0018","text":"No longer is it capable of that which it wills most dearly:—to create beyond itself. That it wills most dearly; that is its whole fervour.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0019","text":"But it has now become too late for that:—thus your Self wishes to perish, you despisers of the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0020","text":"Your Self wishes to perish, and therefore you became despisers of the body! For no longer are you capable of creating beyond yourselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0021","text":"And therefore you are now angry with life and the earth. An unwitting envy is in the squinting glance of your contempt.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0022","text":"I do not go your way, you despisers of the body! You are no bridges for me to the overman!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0023","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDVC:P0023"]}],"source_digest":"919c7963048c7d8bba40f1c4aab742580e3485c4c46924826da028e0b2094eb2","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von den Verächtern des Leibes"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von den Verächtern des Leibes","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU","title":"On the Joys and Passions","order":15,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDFU","text":"Mein Bruder, wenn du eine Tugend hast, und es deine Tugend ist, so hast du sie mit Niemandem gemeinsam.\n\nFreilich, du willst sie bei Namen nennen und liebkosen; du willst sie am Ohre zupfen und Kurzweil mit ihr treiben.\n\nUnd siehe! Nun hast du ihren Namen mit dem Volke gemeinsam und bist Volk und Heerde geworden mit deiner Tugend!\n\nBesser thätest du, zu sagen: „unaussprechbar ist und namenlos, was meiner Seele Qual und Süsse macht und auch noch der Hunger meiner Eingeweide ist.“\n\nDeine Tugend sei zu hoch für die Vertraulichkeit der Namen: und musst du von ihr reden, so schäme dich nicht, von ihr zu stammeln.\n\nSo sprich und stammle: „Das ist mein Gutes, das liebe ich, so gefällt es mir ganz, so allein will ich das Gute.\n\nNicht will ich es als eines Gottes Gesetz, nicht will ich es als eine Menschen-Satzung und -Nothdurft: kein Wegweiser sei es mir für Über-Erden und Paradiese.\n\nEine irdische Tugend ist es, die ich liebe: wenig Klugheit ist darin und am wenigsten die Vernunft Aller.\n\nAber dieser Vogel baute bei mir sich das Nest: darum liebe und herze ich ihn,—nun sitze er bei mir auf seinen goldnen Eiern.“\n\nSo sollst du stammeln und deine Tugend loben.\n\nEinst hattest du Leidenschaften und nanntest sie böse. Aber jetzt hast du nur noch deine Tugenden: die wuchsen aus deinen Leidenschaften.\n\nDu legtest dein höchstes Ziel diesen Leidenschaften an’s Herz: da wurden sie deine Tugenden und Freudenschaften.\n\nUnd ob du aus dem Geschlechte der Jähzornigen wärest oder aus dem der Wollüstigen oder der Glaubens-Wüthigen oder der Rachsüchtigen:\n\nAm Ende wurden alle deine Leidenschaften zu Tugenden und alle deine Teufel zu Engeln.\n\nEinst hattest du wilde Hunde in deinem Keller: aber am Ende verwandelten sie sich zu Vögeln und lieblichen Sängerinnen.\n\nAus deinen Giften brautest du dir deinen Balsam; deine Kuh Trübsal melktest du, —nun trinkst du die süsse Milch ihres Euters.\n\nUnd nichts Böses wächst mehr fürderhin aus dir, es sei denn das Böse, das aus dem Kampfe deiner Tugenden wächst.\n\nMein Bruder, wenn du Glück hast, so hast du Eine Tugend und nicht mehr: so gehst du leichter über die Brücke.\n\nAuszeichnend ist es, viele Tugenden zu haben, aber ein schweres Loos; und Mancher gieng in die Wüste und tödtete sich, weil er müde war, Schlacht und Schlachtfeld von Tugenden zu sein.\n\nMein Bruder, ist Krieg und Schlacht böse? Aber nothwendig ist diess Böse, nothwendig ist der Neid und das Misstrauen und die Verleumdung unter deinen Tugenden.\n\nSiehe, wie jede deiner Tugenden begehrlich ist nach dem Höchsten: sie will deinen ganzen Geist, dass er ihr Herold sei, sie will deine ganze Kraft in Zorn, Hass und Liebe.\n\nEifersüchtig ist jede Tugend auf die andre, und ein furchtbares Ding ist Eifersucht. Auch Tugenden können an der Eifersucht zu Grunde gehn.\n\nWen die Flamme der Eifersucht umringt, der wendet zuletzt, gleich dem Scorpione, gegen sich selber den vergifteten Stachel.\n\nAch, mein Bruder, sahst du noch nie eine Tugend sich selber verleumden und erstechen?\n\nDer Mensch ist Etwas, das überwunden werden muss: und darum sollst du deine Tugenden lieben,—denn du wirst an ihnen zu Grunde gehn.—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0001","text":"Mein Bruder, wenn du eine Tugend hast, und es deine Tugend ist, so hast du sie mit Niemandem gemeinsam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0002","text":"Freilich, du willst sie bei Namen nennen und liebkosen; du willst sie am Ohre zupfen und Kurzweil mit ihr treiben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0003","text":"Und siehe! Nun hast du ihren Namen mit dem Volke gemeinsam und bist Volk und Heerde geworden mit deiner Tugend!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0004","text":"Besser thätest du, zu sagen: „unaussprechbar ist und namenlos, was meiner Seele Qual und Süsse macht und auch noch der Hunger meiner Eingeweide ist.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0005","text":"Deine Tugend sei zu hoch für die Vertraulichkeit der Namen: und musst du von ihr reden, so schäme dich nicht, von ihr zu stammeln.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0006","text":"So sprich und stammle: „Das ist mein Gutes, das liebe ich, so gefällt es mir ganz, so allein will ich das Gute.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0007","text":"Nicht will ich es als eines Gottes Gesetz, nicht will ich es als eine Menschen-Satzung und -Nothdurft: kein Wegweiser sei es mir für Über-Erden und Paradiese.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0008","text":"Eine irdische Tugend ist es, die ich liebe: wenig Klugheit ist darin und am wenigsten die Vernunft Aller.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0009","text":"Aber dieser Vogel baute bei mir sich das Nest: darum liebe und herze ich ihn,—nun sitze er bei mir auf seinen goldnen Eiern.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0010","text":"So sollst du stammeln und deine Tugend loben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0011","text":"Einst hattest du Leidenschaften und nanntest sie böse. Aber jetzt hast du nur noch deine Tugenden: die wuchsen aus deinen Leidenschaften.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0012","text":"Du legtest dein höchstes Ziel diesen Leidenschaften an’s Herz: da wurden sie deine Tugenden und Freudenschaften.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0013","text":"Und ob du aus dem Geschlechte der Jähzornigen wärest oder aus dem der Wollüstigen oder der Glaubens-Wüthigen oder der Rachsüchtigen:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0014","text":"Am Ende wurden alle deine Leidenschaften zu Tugenden und alle deine Teufel zu Engeln.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0015","text":"Einst hattest du wilde Hunde in deinem Keller: aber am Ende verwandelten sie sich zu Vögeln und lieblichen Sängerinnen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0016","text":"Aus deinen Giften brautest du dir deinen Balsam; deine Kuh Trübsal melktest du, —nun trinkst du die süsse Milch ihres Euters.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0017","text":"Und nichts Böses wächst mehr fürderhin aus dir, es sei denn das Böse, das aus dem Kampfe deiner Tugenden wächst.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0018","text":"Mein Bruder, wenn du Glück hast, so hast du Eine Tugend und nicht mehr: so gehst du leichter über die Brücke.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0019","text":"Auszeichnend ist es, viele Tugenden zu haben, aber ein schweres Loos; und Mancher gieng in die Wüste und tödtete sich, weil er müde war, Schlacht und Schlachtfeld von Tugenden zu sein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0020","text":"Mein Bruder, ist Krieg und Schlacht böse? Aber nothwendig ist diess Böse, nothwendig ist der Neid und das Misstrauen und die Verleumdung unter deinen Tugenden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0021","text":"Siehe, wie jede deiner Tugenden begehrlich ist nach dem Höchsten: sie will deinen ganzen Geist, dass er ihr Herold sei, sie will deine ganze Kraft in Zorn, Hass und Liebe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0022","text":"Eifersüchtig ist jede Tugend auf die andre, und ein furchtbares Ding ist Eifersucht. Auch Tugenden können an der Eifersucht zu Grunde gehn.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0023","text":"Wen die Flamme der Eifersucht umringt, der wendet zuletzt, gleich dem Scorpione, gegen sich selber den vergifteten Stachel.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0024","text":"Ach, mein Bruder, sahst du noch nie eine Tugend sich selber verleumden und erstechen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0025","text":"Der Mensch ist Etwas, das überwunden werden muss: und darum sollst du deine Tugenden lieben,—denn du wirst an ihnen zu Grunde gehn.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0026","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"271a878bddf2cf11dd6f577111487d36be4949d637b45b5c585db72825a1d957","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181255051618Z_77cf0976","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Joys and Passions","source_sha256":"271a878bddf2cf11dd6f577111487d36be4949d637b45b5c585db72825a1d957","sha256":"7ac0d4613e8f1da9b1b018960ca4921035f6123e653a97aa891ead8584c73223","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:12:55.051656+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0001","text":"My brother, if you have a virtue, and it is your virtue, then you share it with no one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0002","text":"Certainly, you wish to call it by name and caress it; you wish to pull its ear and make merry with it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0003","text":"And behold! Now you have its name in common with the people, and with your virtue you have become people and herd!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0004","text":"You would do better to say: “Unutterable and nameless is that which is the torment and sweetness of my soul, and also the hunger of my entrails.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0005","text":"Let your virtue be too lofty for the familiarity of names: and if you must speak of it, do not be ashamed to stammer about it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0006","text":"Thus speak and stammer: “This is my good, this I love, thus does it wholly please me, thus alone do I will the good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0007","text":"I do not will it as the law of a god, I do not will it as a human ordinance and necessity: let it be no signpost for me toward over-earths and paradises.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0008","text":"It is an earthly virtue that I love: there is little cleverness in it, and least of all the reason of all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0009","text":"But this bird built its nest with me: therefore I love and cherish it,—now let it sit with me upon its golden eggs.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0010","text":"Thus shall you stammer and praise your virtue.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0011","text":"Once you had passions and called them evil. But now you have only your virtues: they grew out of your passions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0012","text":"You laid your highest goal upon the heart of these passions: then they became your virtues and joy-passions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0013","text":"And whether you were of the race of the hot-tempered or of the lustful or of the faith-frenzied or of the vengeful:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0014","text":"In the end all your passions became virtues and all your devils angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0015","text":"Once you had wild dogs in your cellar: but in the end they transformed themselves into birds and lovely songstresses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0016","text":"From your poisons you brewed yourself your balm; you milked your cow Melancholy, —now you drink the sweet milk of her udder.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0017","text":"And henceforth nothing evil grows out of you any more, except the evil that grows out of the conflict of your virtues.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0018","text":"My brother, if you are fortunate, then you have one virtue and no more: thus you cross the bridge more lightly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0019","text":"It is distinguishing to have many virtues, but a hard lot; and many a one went into the desert and killed himself because he was weary of being battle and battlefield of virtues.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0020","text":"My brother, are war and battle evil? But this evil is necessary, necessary are envy and mistrust and slander among your virtues.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0021","text":"Behold how each of your virtues is desirous of the highest: it wants your whole spirit, that it may be its herald; it wants all your strength in anger, hatred, and love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0022","text":"Each virtue is jealous of the other, and jealousy is a terrible thing. Virtues, too, may perish through jealousy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0023","text":"He whom the flame of jealousy encircles, in the end, like the scorpion, turns the poisoned sting against himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0024","text":"Ah, my brother, have you never yet seen a virtue slander and stab itself?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0025","text":"Man is something that must be overcome: and therefore you shall love your virtues,—for you will perish through them.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0026","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFU:P0026"]}],"source_digest":"5f91a410dfbfb1ab23f08fb5d285209c9e1beeca72c590bb15e0653a8dc7b868","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von den Freuden- und Leidenschaften"}],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"Von den Freuden- und Leidenschaften","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0","title":"On the Pale Criminal","order":16,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VBV0","text":"Ihr wollt nicht tödten, ihr Richter und Opferer, bevor das Thier nicht genickt hat? Seht, der bleiche Verbrecher hat genickt: aus seinem Auge redet die grosse Verachtung.\n\n„Mein Ich ist Etwas, das überwunden werden soll: mein Ich ist mir die grosse Verachtung des Menschen“ : so redet es aus diesem Auge.\n\nDass er sich selber richtete, war sein höchster Augenblick: lasst den Erhabenen nicht wieder zurück in sein Niederes!\n\nEs giebt keine Erlösung für Den, der so an sich selber leidet, es sei denn der schnelle Tod.\n\nEuer Tödten, ihr Richter, soll ein Mitleid sein und keine Rache. Und indem ihr tödtet, seht zu, dass ihr selber das Leben rechtfertiget!\n\nEs ist nicht genug, dass ihr euch mit Dem versöhnt, den ihr tödtet. Eure Traurigkeit sei Liebe zum Übermenschen: so rechtfertigt ihr euer Noch-Leben!\n\n„Feind“ sollt ihr sagen, aber nicht „Bösewicht“; „Kranker“ sollt ihr sagen, aber nicht „Schuft“; „Thor“ sollt ihr sagen, aber nicht „Sünder“.\n\nUnd du, rother Richter, wenn du laut sagen wolltest, was du Alles schon in Gedanken gethan hast: so würde Jedermann schreien: „Weg mit diesem Unflath und Giftwurm!“\n\nAber ein Anderes ist der Gedanke, ein Anderes die That, ein Anderes das Bild der That. Das Rad des Grundes rollt nicht zwischen ihnen.\n\nEin Bild machte diesen bleichen Menschen bleich. Gleichwüchsig war er seiner That, als er sie that: aber ihr Bild ertrug er nicht, als sie gethan war.\n\nImmer sah er sich nun als Einer That Thäter. Wahnsinn heisse ich diess: die Ausnahme verkehrte sich ihm zum Wesen.\n\nDer Strich bannt die Henne; der Streich, den er führte, bannte seine arme Vernunft—den Wahnsinn nach der That heisse ich diess.\n\nHört, ihr Richter! Einen anderen Wahnsinn giebt es noch: und der ist vor der That. Ach, ihr krocht mir nicht tief genug in diese Seele!\n\nSo spricht der rothe Richter: „was mordete doch dieser Verbrecher? Er wollte rauben.“ Aber ich sage euch: seine Seele wollte Blut, nicht Raub: er dürstete nach dem Glück des Messers!\n\nSeine arme Vernunft aber begriff diesen Wahnsinn nicht und überredete ihn. „Was liegt an Blut! sprach sie; willst du nicht zum Mindesten einen Raub dabei machen? Eine Rache nehmen?“\n\nUnd er horchte auf seine arme Vernunft: wie Blei lag ihre Rede auf ihm,—da raubte er, als er mordete. Er wollte sich nicht seines Wahnsinns schämen.\n\nUnd nun wieder liegt das Blei seiner Schuld auf ihm, und wieder ist seine arme Vernunft so steif, so gelähmt, so schwer.\n\nWenn er nur den Kopf schütteln könnte, so würde seine Last herabrollen: aber wer schüttelt diesen Kopf?\n\nWas ist dieser Mensch? Ein Haufen von Krankheiten, welche durch den Geist in die Welt hinausgreifen: da wollen sie ihre Beute machen.\n\nWas ist dieser Mensch? Ein Knäuel wilder Schlangen, welche selten bei einander Ruhe haben,—da gehn sie für sich fort und suchen Beute in der Welt.\n\nSeht diesen armen Leib! Was er litt und begehrte, das deutete sich diese arme Seele,—sie deutete es als mörderische Lust und Gier nach dem Glück des Messers.\n\nWer jetzt krank wird, den überfällt das Böse, das jetzt böse ist: wehe will er thun, mit dem, was ihm wehe thut. Aber es gab andre Zeiten und ein andres Böses und Gutes.\n\nEinst war der Zweifel böse und der Wille zum Selbst. Damals wurde der Kranke zum Ketzer und zur Hexe: als Ketzer und Hexe litt er und wollte leiden machen.\n\nAber diess will nicht in eure Ohren: euren Guten schade es, sagt ihr mir. Aber was liegt mir an euren Guten!\n\nVieles an euren Guten macht mir Ekel, und wahrlich nicht ihr Böses. Wollte ich doch, sie hätten einen Wahnsinn, an dem sie zu Grunde giengen, gleich diesem bleichen Verbrecher!\n\nWahrlich, ich wollte, ihr Wahnsinn hiesse Wahrheit oder Treue oder Gerechtigkeit: aber sie haben ihre Tugend, um lange zu leben und in einem erbärmlichen Behagen.\n\nIch bin ein Geländer am Strome: fasse mich, wer mich fassen kann! Eure Krücke aber bin ich nicht.—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0001","text":"Ihr wollt nicht tödten, ihr Richter und Opferer, bevor das Thier nicht genickt hat? Seht, der bleiche Verbrecher hat genickt: aus seinem Auge redet die grosse Verachtung.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0002","text":"„Mein Ich ist Etwas, das überwunden werden soll: mein Ich ist mir die grosse Verachtung des Menschen“ : so redet es aus diesem Auge.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0003","text":"Dass er sich selber richtete, war sein höchster Augenblick: lasst den Erhabenen nicht wieder zurück in sein Niederes!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0004","text":"Es giebt keine Erlösung für Den, der so an sich selber leidet, es sei denn der schnelle Tod.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0005","text":"Euer Tödten, ihr Richter, soll ein Mitleid sein und keine Rache. Und indem ihr tödtet, seht zu, dass ihr selber das Leben rechtfertiget!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0006","text":"Es ist nicht genug, dass ihr euch mit Dem versöhnt, den ihr tödtet. Eure Traurigkeit sei Liebe zum Übermenschen: so rechtfertigt ihr euer Noch-Leben!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0007","text":"„Feind“ sollt ihr sagen, aber nicht „Bösewicht“; „Kranker“ sollt ihr sagen, aber nicht „Schuft“; „Thor“ sollt ihr sagen, aber nicht „Sünder“.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0008","text":"Und du, rother Richter, wenn du laut sagen wolltest, was du Alles schon in Gedanken gethan hast: so würde Jedermann schreien: „Weg mit diesem Unflath und Giftwurm!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0009","text":"Aber ein Anderes ist der Gedanke, ein Anderes die That, ein Anderes das Bild der That. Das Rad des Grundes rollt nicht zwischen ihnen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0010","text":"Ein Bild machte diesen bleichen Menschen bleich. Gleichwüchsig war er seiner That, als er sie that: aber ihr Bild ertrug er nicht, als sie gethan war.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0011","text":"Immer sah er sich nun als Einer That Thäter. Wahnsinn heisse ich diess: die Ausnahme verkehrte sich ihm zum Wesen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0012","text":"Der Strich bannt die Henne; der Streich, den er führte, bannte seine arme Vernunft—den Wahnsinn nach der That heisse ich diess.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0013","text":"Hört, ihr Richter! Einen anderen Wahnsinn giebt es noch: und der ist vor der That. Ach, ihr krocht mir nicht tief genug in diese Seele!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0014","text":"So spricht der rothe Richter: „was mordete doch dieser Verbrecher? Er wollte rauben.“ Aber ich sage euch: seine Seele wollte Blut, nicht Raub: er dürstete nach dem Glück des Messers!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0015","text":"Seine arme Vernunft aber begriff diesen Wahnsinn nicht und überredete ihn. „Was liegt an Blut! sprach sie; willst du nicht zum Mindesten einen Raub dabei machen? Eine Rache nehmen?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0016","text":"Und er horchte auf seine arme Vernunft: wie Blei lag ihre Rede auf ihm,—da raubte er, als er mordete. Er wollte sich nicht seines Wahnsinns schämen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0017","text":"Und nun wieder liegt das Blei seiner Schuld auf ihm, und wieder ist seine arme Vernunft so steif, so gelähmt, so schwer.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0018","text":"Wenn er nur den Kopf schütteln könnte, so würde seine Last herabrollen: aber wer schüttelt diesen Kopf?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0019","text":"Was ist dieser Mensch? Ein Haufen von Krankheiten, welche durch den Geist in die Welt hinausgreifen: da wollen sie ihre Beute machen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0020","text":"Was ist dieser Mensch? Ein Knäuel wilder Schlangen, welche selten bei einander Ruhe haben,—da gehn sie für sich fort und suchen Beute in der Welt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0021","text":"Seht diesen armen Leib! Was er litt und begehrte, das deutete sich diese arme Seele,—sie deutete es als mörderische Lust und Gier nach dem Glück des Messers.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0022","text":"Wer jetzt krank wird, den überfällt das Böse, das jetzt böse ist: wehe will er thun, mit dem, was ihm wehe thut. Aber es gab andre Zeiten und ein andres Böses und Gutes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0023","text":"Einst war der Zweifel böse und der Wille zum Selbst. Damals wurde der Kranke zum Ketzer und zur Hexe: als Ketzer und Hexe litt er und wollte leiden machen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0024","text":"Aber diess will nicht in eure Ohren: euren Guten schade es, sagt ihr mir. Aber was liegt mir an euren Guten!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0025","text":"Vieles an euren Guten macht mir Ekel, und wahrlich nicht ihr Böses. Wollte ich doch, sie hätten einen Wahnsinn, an dem sie zu Grunde giengen, gleich diesem bleichen Verbrecher!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0026","text":"Wahrlich, ich wollte, ihr Wahnsinn hiesse Wahrheit oder Treue oder Gerechtigkeit: aber sie haben ihre Tugend, um lange zu leben und in einem erbärmlichen Behagen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0027","text":"Ich bin ein Geländer am Strome: fasse mich, wer mich fassen kann! Eure Krücke aber bin ich nicht.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0028","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"72b7140e7337674c5aaa4c72bda17ebce2badeeb2d58d61db569e9e41e58affd","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181311075456Z_6bbd02af","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Pale Criminal","source_sha256":"72b7140e7337674c5aaa4c72bda17ebce2badeeb2d58d61db569e9e41e58affd","sha256":"61db1ae2fef6d490ad354bae68cd31728ca82810825b7c7fe093cb3df2364008","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:13:11.075506+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0001","text":"You do not wish to kill, you judges and sacrificers, before the animal has nodded? Behold, the pale criminal has nodded: from his eye speaks the great contempt.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0002","text":"“My I is something that ought to be overcome: my I is for me the great contempt for man”: thus it speaks from this eye.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0003","text":"That he judged himself was his highest moment: do not let the sublime one fall back again into his lower state!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0004","text":"There is no redemption for one who suffers thus from himself, save swift death.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0005","text":"Your killing, you judges, shall be a compassion and not a revenge. And in killing, see to it that you yourselves justify life!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0006","text":"It is not enough that you reconcile yourselves with him whom you kill. Let your sadness be love for the Overman: thus you justify your still-living!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0007","text":"You shall say “enemy,” but not “evildoer”; you shall say “sick man,” but not “scoundrel”; you shall say “fool,” but not “sinner.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0008","text":"And you, red judge, if you wished to say aloud all that you have already done in thought: then everyone would cry: “Away with this filth and poison-worm!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0009","text":"But the thought is one thing, the deed another, the image of the deed another. The wheel of the ground does not roll between them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0010","text":"An image made this pale man pale. He was equal to his deed when he did it: but he could not bear its image when it was done.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0011","text":"From then on he always saw himself as the doer of one deed. I call this madness: the exception was transformed for him into the essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0012","text":"The line binds the hen; the blow that he dealt bound his poor reason—I call this the madness after the deed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0013","text":"Listen, you judges! There is yet another madness: and that is before the deed. Ah, you did not creep deeply enough into this soul for me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0014","text":"Thus speaks the red judge: “What, then, did this criminal murder for? He wished to rob.” But I tell you: his soul wanted blood, not robbery: he thirsted for the happiness of the knife!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0015","text":"But his poor reason did not comprehend this madness and persuaded him otherwise. “What does blood matter! it said; will you not at least make a robbery in the process? Take a revenge?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0016","text":"And he listened to his poor reason: like lead its speech lay upon him,—then he robbed when he murdered. He did not wish to be ashamed of his madness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0017","text":"And now again the lead of his guilt lies upon him, and again his poor reason is so stiff, so paralysed, so heavy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0018","text":"If only he could shake his head, then his burden would roll down: but who shakes this head?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0019","text":"What is this human being? A heap of diseases which reach out into the world through the spirit: there they wish to seize their prey.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0020","text":"What is this human being? A tangle of wild serpents, which rarely have rest together,—there they go off separately and seek prey in the world.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0021","text":"Behold this poor body! What it suffered and desired, this poor soul interpreted,—it interpreted it as murderous lust and greed for the happiness of the knife.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0022","text":"Whoever now becomes sick is assailed by the evil that is now evil: he wishes to inflict woe with that which inflicts woe upon him. But there were other times and another evil and good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0023","text":"Once doubt was evil, and the will to self. At that time the sick man became heretic and witch: as heretic and witch he suffered and wished to make others suffer.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0024","text":"But this will not enter your ears: it harms your good ones, you tell me. But what are your good ones to me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0025","text":"Much in your good people disgusts me, and truly not your evil. Would that they had a madness by which they perished, like this pale criminal!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0026","text":"Truly, I wish their madness were called truth or fidelity or justice: but they have their virtue in order to live long and in a miserable comfort.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0027","text":"I am a railing by the stream: let him who can grasp me, grasp me! But I am not your crutch.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0028","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBV0:P0028"]}],"source_digest":"e3faa32778f9d740fc6b646c1aeca196c23e20ec282a3a6d786cef357544aacd","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Vom bleichen Verbrecher"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Vom bleichen Verbrecher","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS","title":"On Reading and Writing","order":17,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VLUS","text":"Von allem Geschriebenen liebe ich nur Das, was Einer mit seinem Blute schreibt. Schreibe mit Blut: und du wirst erfahren, dass Blut Geist ist.\n\nEs ist nicht leicht möglich, fremdes Blut zu verstehen: ich hasse die lesenden Müssiggänger.\n\nWer den Leser kennt, der thut Nichts mehr für den Leser. Noch ein Jahrhundert Leser—und der Geist selber wird stinken.\n\nDass Jedermann lesen lernen darf, verdirbt auf die Dauer nicht allein das Schreiben, sondern auch das Denken.\n\nEinst war der Geist Gott, dann wurde er zum Menschen und jetzt wird er gar noch Pöbel.\n\nWer in Blut und Sprüchen schreibt, der will nicht gelesen, sondern auswendig gelernt werden.\n\nIm Gebirge ist der nächste Weg von Gipfel zu Gipfel: aber dazu musst du lange Beine haben. Sprüche sollen Gipfel sein: und Die, zu denen gesprochen wird, Grosse und Hochwüchsige.\n\nDie Luft dünn und rein, die Gefahr nahe und der Geist voll einer fröhlichen Bosheit: so passt es gut zu einander.\n\nIch will Kobolde um mich haben, denn ich bin muthig. Muth, der die Gespenster verscheucht, schafft sich selber Kobolde,—der Muth will lachen.\n\nIch empfinde nicht mehr mit euch: diese Wolke, die ich unter mir sehe, diese Schwärze und Schwere, über die ich lache,—gerade das ist eure Gewitterwolke.\n\nIhr seht nach Oben, wenn ihr nach Erhebung verlangt. Und ich sehe hinab, weil ich erhoben bin.\n\nWer von euch kann zugleich lachen und erhoben sein?\n\nWer auf den höchsten Bergen steigt, der lacht über alle Trauer-Spiele und Trauer-Ernste.\n\nMuthig, unbekümmert, spöttisch, gewaltthätig—so will uns die Weisheit: sie ist ein Weib und liebt immer nur einen Kriegsmann.\n\nIhr sagt mir: „das Leben ist schwer zu tragen.“ Aber wozu hättet ihr Vormittags euren Stolz und Abends eure Ergebung?\n\nDas Leben ist schwer zu tragen: aber so thut mir doch nicht so zärtlich! Wir sind allesammt hübsche lastbare Esel und Eselinnen.\n\nWas haben wir gemein mit der Rosenknospe, welche zittert, weil ihr ein Tropfen Thau auf dem Leibe liegt?\n\nEs ist wahr: wir lieben das Leben, nicht, weil wir an’s Leben, sondern weil wir an’s Lieben gewöhnt sind.\n\nEs ist immer etwas Wahnsinn in der Liebe. Es ist aber immer auch etwas Vernunft im Wahnsinn.\n\nUnd auch mir, der ich dem Leben gut bin, scheinen Schmetterlinge und Seifenblasen und was ihrer Art unter Menschen ist, am meisten vom Glücke zu wissen.\n\nDiese leichten thörichten zierlichen beweglichen Seelchen flattern zu sehen—das verführt Zarathustra zu Thränen und Liedern.\n\nIch würde nur an einen Gott glauben, der zu tanzen verstünde.\n\nUnd als ich meinen Teufel sah, da fand ich ihn ernst, gründlich, tief, feierlich: es war der Geist der Schwere,—durch ihn fallen alle Dinge.\n\nNicht durch Zorn, sondern durch Lachen tödtet man. Auf, lasst uns den Geist der Schwere tödten!\n\nIch habe gehen gelernt: seitdem lasse ich mich laufen. Ich habe fliegen gelernt: seitdem will ich nicht erst gestossen sein, um von der Stelle zu kommen.\n\nJetzt bin ich leicht, jetzt fliege ich, jetzt sehe ich mich unter mir, jetzt tanzt ein Gott durch mich.\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0001","text":"Von allem Geschriebenen liebe ich nur Das, was Einer mit seinem Blute schreibt. Schreibe mit Blut: und du wirst erfahren, dass Blut Geist ist.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0002","text":"Es ist nicht leicht möglich, fremdes Blut zu verstehen: ich hasse die lesenden Müssiggänger.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0003","text":"Wer den Leser kennt, der thut Nichts mehr für den Leser. Noch ein Jahrhundert Leser—und der Geist selber wird stinken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0004","text":"Dass Jedermann lesen lernen darf, verdirbt auf die Dauer nicht allein das Schreiben, sondern auch das Denken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0005","text":"Einst war der Geist Gott, dann wurde er zum Menschen und jetzt wird er gar noch Pöbel.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0006","text":"Wer in Blut und Sprüchen schreibt, der will nicht gelesen, sondern auswendig gelernt werden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0007","text":"Im Gebirge ist der nächste Weg von Gipfel zu Gipfel: aber dazu musst du lange Beine haben. Sprüche sollen Gipfel sein: und Die, zu denen gesprochen wird, Grosse und Hochwüchsige.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0008","text":"Die Luft dünn und rein, die Gefahr nahe und der Geist voll einer fröhlichen Bosheit: so passt es gut zu einander.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0009","text":"Ich will Kobolde um mich haben, denn ich bin muthig. Muth, der die Gespenster verscheucht, schafft sich selber Kobolde,—der Muth will lachen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0010","text":"Ich empfinde nicht mehr mit euch: diese Wolke, die ich unter mir sehe, diese Schwärze und Schwere, über die ich lache,—gerade das ist eure Gewitterwolke.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0011","text":"Ihr seht nach Oben, wenn ihr nach Erhebung verlangt. Und ich sehe hinab, weil ich erhoben bin.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0012","text":"Wer von euch kann zugleich lachen und erhoben sein?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0013","text":"Wer auf den höchsten Bergen steigt, der lacht über alle Trauer-Spiele und Trauer-Ernste.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0014","text":"Muthig, unbekümmert, spöttisch, gewaltthätig—so will uns die Weisheit: sie ist ein Weib und liebt immer nur einen Kriegsmann.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0015","text":"Ihr sagt mir: „das Leben ist schwer zu tragen.“ Aber wozu hättet ihr Vormittags euren Stolz und Abends eure Ergebung?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0016","text":"Das Leben ist schwer zu tragen: aber so thut mir doch nicht so zärtlich! Wir sind allesammt hübsche lastbare Esel und Eselinnen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0017","text":"Was haben wir gemein mit der Rosenknospe, welche zittert, weil ihr ein Tropfen Thau auf dem Leibe liegt?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0018","text":"Es ist wahr: wir lieben das Leben, nicht, weil wir an’s Leben, sondern weil wir an’s Lieben gewöhnt sind.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0019","text":"Es ist immer etwas Wahnsinn in der Liebe. Es ist aber immer auch etwas Vernunft im Wahnsinn.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0020","text":"Und auch mir, der ich dem Leben gut bin, scheinen Schmetterlinge und Seifenblasen und was ihrer Art unter Menschen ist, am meisten vom Glücke zu wissen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0021","text":"Diese leichten thörichten zierlichen beweglichen Seelchen flattern zu sehen—das verführt Zarathustra zu Thränen und Liedern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0022","text":"Ich würde nur an einen Gott glauben, der zu tanzen verstünde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0023","text":"Und als ich meinen Teufel sah, da fand ich ihn ernst, gründlich, tief, feierlich: es war der Geist der Schwere,—durch ihn fallen alle Dinge.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0024","text":"Nicht durch Zorn, sondern durch Lachen tödtet man. Auf, lasst uns den Geist der Schwere tödten!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0025","text":"Ich habe gehen gelernt: seitdem lasse ich mich laufen. Ich habe fliegen gelernt: seitdem will ich nicht erst gestossen sein, um von der Stelle zu kommen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0026","text":"Jetzt bin ich leicht, jetzt fliege ich, jetzt sehe ich mich unter mir, jetzt tanzt ein Gott durch mich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0027","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"d71f352e196f9b2705b61040083fe685b54abe2c8f6c674d5bac01484b9f51cd","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181321587910Z_05418807","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On Reading and Writing","source_sha256":"d71f352e196f9b2705b61040083fe685b54abe2c8f6c674d5bac01484b9f51cd","sha256":"845f7b5a918966e2562bca8c8847d7256a82c39b9d676a6b96c7f51d4b0a6133","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:13:21.587947+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0001","text":"Of all that is written I love only that which someone writes with his blood. Write with blood: and you will discover that blood is spirit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0002","text":"It is not easily possible to understand foreign blood: I hate the idle readers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0003","text":"Whoever knows the reader no longer does anything for the reader. One more century of readers—and spirit itself will stink.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0004","text":"That everyone may learn to read corrupts, in the long run, not only writing but also thinking.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0005","text":"Once spirit was God, then it became man, and now it is even becoming rabble.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0006","text":"Whoever writes in blood and aphorisms does not want to be read, but to be learned by heart.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0007","text":"In the mountains the nearest way is from summit to summit: but for that you must have long legs. Aphorisms should be summits: and those to whom they are spoken, great and tall of stature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0008","text":"The air thin and pure, danger near, and the spirit full of a cheerful malice: thus they suit one another well.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0009","text":"I want goblins about me, for I am courageous. Courage that drives away ghosts creates goblins for itself,—courage wants to laugh.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0010","text":"I no longer feel with you: this cloud that I see beneath me, this blackness and heaviness over which I laugh,—precisely this is your thundercloud.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0011","text":"You look upward when you desire elevation. And I look downward, because I am elevated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0012","text":"Which one among you can laugh and be elevated at the same time?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0013","text":"Whoever climbs upon the highest mountains laughs at all tragic plays and tragic seriousness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0014","text":"Courageous, unconcerned, mocking, violent—thus wisdom wants us: she is a woman and always loves only a warrior.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0015","text":"You say to me: “life is hard to bear.” But what would you have your pride in the morning and your resignation in the evening for?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0016","text":"Life is hard to bear: but do not therefore behave so tenderly toward me! We are all handsome, burden-bearing asses and she-asses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0017","text":"What have we in common with the rosebud that trembles because a drop of dew lies upon its body?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0018","text":"It is true: we love life, not because we are accustomed to life, but because we are accustomed to loving.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0019","text":"There is always some madness in love. But there is also always some reason in madness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0020","text":"And even to me, who am well disposed toward life, butterflies and soap bubbles and whatever is of their kind among human beings seem to know most about happiness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0021","text":"To see these light, foolish, dainty, lively little souls fluttering about—that tempts Zarathustra to tears and songs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0022","text":"I would believe only in a God who knew how to dance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0023","text":"And when I saw my devil, I found him serious, thorough, deep, solemn: he was the spirit of gravity,—through him all things fall.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0024","text":"One kills not through anger, but through laughter. Up, let us kill the spirit of gravity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0025","text":"I have learned to walk: since then I let myself run. I have learned to fly: since then I do not first want to be pushed, in order to get moving.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0026","text":"Now I am light, now I fly, now I see myself beneath myself, now a god dances through me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0027","text":"Thus Spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLUS:P0027"]}],"source_digest":"8f49ec47ebb586b82bcd1d38130174edd3c796e6ae5d9dd80db7f5239fdf4566","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Vom Lesen und Schreiben"}],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"Vom Lesen und Schreiben","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB","title":"On the Tree on the Mountainside","order":18,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VBAB","text":"Zarathustra’s Auge hatte gesehn, dass ein Jüngling ihm auswich. Und als er eines Abends allein durch die Berge gieng, welche die Stadt umschliessen, die genannt wird „die bunte Kuh“: siehe, da fand er im Gehen diesen Jüngling, wie er an einen Baum gelehnt sass und müden Blickes in das Thal schaute. Zarathustra fasste den Baum an, bei welchem der Jüngling sass, und sprach also:\n\nWenn ich diesen Baum da mit meinen Händen schütteln wollte, ich würde es nicht vermögen.\n\nAber der Wind, den wir nicht sehen, der quält und biegt ihn, wohin er will. Wir werden am schlimmsten von unsichtbaren Händen gebogen und gequält.\n\nDa erhob sich der Jüngling bestürzt und sagte: „ich höre Zarathustra und eben dachte ich an ihn.“ Zarathustra entgegnete:\n\n„Was erschrickst du desshalb?—Aber es ist mit dem Menschen wie mit dem Baume.\n\nJe mehr er hinauf in die Höhe und Helle will, um so stärker streben seine Wurzeln erdwärts, abwärts, in’s Dunkle, Tiefe,—in’s Böse.“\n\n„Ja in’s Böse! rief der Jüngling. Wie ist es möglich, dass du meine Seele entdecktest?“\n\nZarathustra lächelte und sprach: „Manche Seele wird man nie entdecken, es sei denn, dass man sie zuerst erfindet.“ „Ja in’s Böse! rief der Jüngling nochmals.\n\nDu sagtest die Wahrheit, Zarathustra. Ich traue mir selber nicht mehr, seitdem ich in die Höhe will, und Niemand traut mir mehr,—wie geschieht diess doch?\n\nIch verwandele mich zu schnell: mein Heute widerlegt mein Gestern. Ich überspringe oft die Stufen, wenn ich steige,—das verzeiht mir keine Stufe.\n\nBin ich oben, so finde ich mich immer allein. Niemand redet mit mir, der Frost der Einsamkeit macht mich zittern. Was will ich doch in der Höhe?\n\nMeine Verachtung und meine Sehnsucht wachsen mit einander; je höher ich steige, um so mehr verachte ich Den, der steigt. Was will er doch in der Höhe?\n\nWie schäme ich mich meines Steigens und Stolperns! Wie spotte ich meines heftigen Schnaubens! Wie hasse ich den Fliegenden! Wie müde bin ich in der Höhe!“\n\nHier schwieg der Jüngling. Und Zarathustra betrachtete den Baum, an dem sie standen, und sprach also:\n\nDieser Baum steht einsam hier am Gebirge; er wuchs hoch hinweg über Mensch und Thier.\n\nUnd wenn er reden wollte, er würde Niemanden haben, der ihn verstünde: so hoch wuchs er.\n\nNun wartet er und wartet,—worauf wartet er doch? Er wohnt dem Sitze der Wolken zu nahe: er wartet wohl auf den ersten Blitz?\n\nAls Zarathustra diess gesagt hatte, rief der Jüngling mit heftigen Gebärden: „Ja, Zarathustra, du sprichst die Wahrheit. Nach meinem Untergange verlangte ich, als ich in die Höhe wollte, und du bist der Blitz, auf den ich wartete! Siehe, was bin ich noch, seitdem du uns erschienen bist? Der Neid auf dich ist’s, der mich zerstört hat!“—So sprach der Jüngling und weinte bitterlich. Zarathustra aber legte seinen Arm um ihn und führte ihn mit sich fort.\n\nUnd als sie eine Weile mit einander gegangen waren, hob Zarathustra also an zu sprechen:\n\nEs zerreisst mir das Herz. Besser als deine Worte es sagen, sagt mir dein Auge alle deine Gefahr.\n\nNoch bist du nicht frei, du suchst noch nach Freiheit. Übernächtig machte dich dein Suchen und überwach.\n\nIn die freie Höhe willst du, nach Sternen dürstet deine Seele. Aber auch deine schlimmen Triebe dürsten nach Freiheit.\n\nDeine wilden Hunde wollen in die Freiheit; sie bellen vor Lust in ihrem Keller, wenn dein Geist alle Gefängnisse zu lösen trachtet.\n\nNoch bist du mir ein Gefangner, der sich Freiheit ersinnt: ach, klug wird solchen Gefangnen die Seele, aber auch arglistig und schlecht.\n\nReinigen muss sich noch der Befreite des Geistes. Viel Gefängniss und Moder ist noch in ihm zurück: rein muss noch sein Auge werden.\n\nJa, ich kenne deine Gefahr. Aber bei meiner Liebe und Hoffnung beschwöre ich dich: wirf deine Liebe und Hoffnung nicht weg!\n\nEdel fühlst du dich noch, und edel fühlen dich auch die Andern noch, die dir gram sind und böse Blicke senden. Wisse, dass Allen ein Edler im Wege steht.\n\nAuch den Guten steht ein Edler im Wege: und selbst wenn sie ihn einen Guten nennen, so wollen sie ihn damit bei Seite bringen.\n\nNeues will der Edle schaffen und eine neue Tugend. Altes will der Gute, und dass Altes erhalten bleibe.\n\nAber nicht das ist die Gefahr des Edlen, dass er ein Guter werde, sondern ein Frecher, ein Höhnender, ein Vernichter.\n\nAch, ich kannte Edle, die verloren ihre höchste Hoffnung. Und nun verleumdeten sie alle hohen Hoffnungen.\n\nNun lebten sie frech in kurzen Lüsten, und über den Tag hin warfen sie kaum noch Ziele.\n\n„Geist ist auch Wollust“—so sagten sie. Da zerbrachen ihrem Geiste die Flügel: nun kriecht er herum und beschmutzt im Nagen.\n\nEinst dachten sie Helden zu werden: Lüstlinge sind es jetzt. Ein Gram und ein Grauen ist ihnen der Held.\n\nAber bei meiner Liebe und Hoffnung beschwöre ich dich: wirf den Helden in deiner Seele nicht weg! Halte heilig deine höchste Hoffnung!—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0001","text":"Zarathustra’s Auge hatte gesehn, dass ein Jüngling ihm auswich. Und als er eines Abends allein durch die Berge gieng, welche die Stadt umschliessen, die genannt wird „die bunte Kuh“: siehe, da fand er im Gehen diesen Jüngling, wie er an einen Baum gelehnt sass und müden Blickes in das Thal schaute. Zarathustra fasste den Baum an, bei welchem der Jüngling sass, und sprach also:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0002","text":"Wenn ich diesen Baum da mit meinen Händen schütteln wollte, ich würde es nicht vermögen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0003","text":"Aber der Wind, den wir nicht sehen, der quält und biegt ihn, wohin er will. Wir werden am schlimmsten von unsichtbaren Händen gebogen und gequält.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0004","text":"Da erhob sich der Jüngling bestürzt und sagte: „ich höre Zarathustra und eben dachte ich an ihn.“ Zarathustra entgegnete:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0005","text":"„Was erschrickst du desshalb?—Aber es ist mit dem Menschen wie mit dem Baume.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0006","text":"Je mehr er hinauf in die Höhe und Helle will, um so stärker streben seine Wurzeln erdwärts, abwärts, in’s Dunkle, Tiefe,—in’s Böse.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0007","text":"„Ja in’s Böse! rief der Jüngling. Wie ist es möglich, dass du meine Seele entdecktest?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0008","text":"Zarathustra lächelte und sprach: „Manche Seele wird man nie entdecken, es sei denn, dass man sie zuerst erfindet.“ „Ja in’s Böse! rief der Jüngling nochmals.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0009","text":"Du sagtest die Wahrheit, Zarathustra. Ich traue mir selber nicht mehr, seitdem ich in die Höhe will, und Niemand traut mir mehr,—wie geschieht diess doch?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0010","text":"Ich verwandele mich zu schnell: mein Heute widerlegt mein Gestern. Ich überspringe oft die Stufen, wenn ich steige,—das verzeiht mir keine Stufe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0011","text":"Bin ich oben, so finde ich mich immer allein. Niemand redet mit mir, der Frost der Einsamkeit macht mich zittern. Was will ich doch in der Höhe?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0012","text":"Meine Verachtung und meine Sehnsucht wachsen mit einander; je höher ich steige, um so mehr verachte ich Den, der steigt. Was will er doch in der Höhe?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0013","text":"Wie schäme ich mich meines Steigens und Stolperns! Wie spotte ich meines heftigen Schnaubens! Wie hasse ich den Fliegenden! Wie müde bin ich in der Höhe!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0014","text":"Hier schwieg der Jüngling. Und Zarathustra betrachtete den Baum, an dem sie standen, und sprach also:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0015","text":"Dieser Baum steht einsam hier am Gebirge; er wuchs hoch hinweg über Mensch und Thier.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0016","text":"Und wenn er reden wollte, er würde Niemanden haben, der ihn verstünde: so hoch wuchs er.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0017","text":"Nun wartet er und wartet,—worauf wartet er doch? Er wohnt dem Sitze der Wolken zu nahe: er wartet wohl auf den ersten Blitz?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0018","text":"Als Zarathustra diess gesagt hatte, rief der Jüngling mit heftigen Gebärden: „Ja, Zarathustra, du sprichst die Wahrheit. Nach meinem Untergange verlangte ich, als ich in die Höhe wollte, und du bist der Blitz, auf den ich wartete! Siehe, was bin ich noch, seitdem du uns erschienen bist? Der Neid auf dich ist’s, der mich zerstört hat!“—So sprach der Jüngling und weinte bitterlich. Zarathustra aber legte seinen Arm um ihn und führte ihn mit sich fort.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0019","text":"Und als sie eine Weile mit einander gegangen waren, hob Zarathustra also an zu sprechen:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0020","text":"Es zerreisst mir das Herz. Besser als deine Worte es sagen, sagt mir dein Auge alle deine Gefahr.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0021","text":"Noch bist du nicht frei, du suchst noch nach Freiheit. Übernächtig machte dich dein Suchen und überwach.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0022","text":"In die freie Höhe willst du, nach Sternen dürstet deine Seele. Aber auch deine schlimmen Triebe dürsten nach Freiheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0023","text":"Deine wilden Hunde wollen in die Freiheit; sie bellen vor Lust in ihrem Keller, wenn dein Geist alle Gefängnisse zu lösen trachtet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0024","text":"Noch bist du mir ein Gefangner, der sich Freiheit ersinnt: ach, klug wird solchen Gefangnen die Seele, aber auch arglistig und schlecht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0025","text":"Reinigen muss sich noch der Befreite des Geistes. Viel Gefängniss und Moder ist noch in ihm zurück: rein muss noch sein Auge werden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0026","text":"Ja, ich kenne deine Gefahr. Aber bei meiner Liebe und Hoffnung beschwöre ich dich: wirf deine Liebe und Hoffnung nicht weg!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0027","text":"Edel fühlst du dich noch, und edel fühlen dich auch die Andern noch, die dir gram sind und böse Blicke senden. Wisse, dass Allen ein Edler im Wege steht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0028","text":"Auch den Guten steht ein Edler im Wege: und selbst wenn sie ihn einen Guten nennen, so wollen sie ihn damit bei Seite bringen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0029","text":"Neues will der Edle schaffen und eine neue Tugend. Altes will der Gute, und dass Altes erhalten bleibe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0030","text":"Aber nicht das ist die Gefahr des Edlen, dass er ein Guter werde, sondern ein Frecher, ein Höhnender, ein Vernichter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0031","text":"Ach, ich kannte Edle, die verloren ihre höchste Hoffnung. Und nun verleumdeten sie alle hohen Hoffnungen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0032","text":"Nun lebten sie frech in kurzen Lüsten, und über den Tag hin warfen sie kaum noch Ziele.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0033","text":"„Geist ist auch Wollust“—so sagten sie. Da zerbrachen ihrem Geiste die Flügel: nun kriecht er herum und beschmutzt im Nagen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0034","text":"Einst dachten sie Helden zu werden: Lüstlinge sind es jetzt. Ein Gram und ein Grauen ist ihnen der Held.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0035","text":"Aber bei meiner Liebe und Hoffnung beschwöre ich dich: wirf den Helden in deiner Seele nicht weg! Halte heilig deine höchste Hoffnung!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0036","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"aa89b2881812ed6682e34027a95a6432e82d56f9afef82c2e346d06f26d252c0","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181337423380Z_f1c3508f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Tree on the Mountainside","source_sha256":"aa89b2881812ed6682e34027a95a6432e82d56f9afef82c2e346d06f26d252c0","sha256":"19bdff06bd6b902bd70f714a915d4cfef7d570b0ff2aadceff8e04d69073c23b","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:13:37.423405+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0001","text":"Zarathustra’s eye had seen that a youth avoided him. And as one evening he went alone through the mountains which enclose the town called “the motley cow”: behold, as he went he found this youth, sitting leaned against a tree and looking wearily into the valley. Zarathustra grasped the tree beside which the youth sat, and spoke thus:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0002","text":"If I wished to shake this tree here with my hands, I should not be able to do so.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0003","text":"But the wind, which we do not see, torments and bends it wherever it will. We are bent and tormented worst by invisible hands.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0004","text":"Then the youth rose up startled and said: “I hear Zarathustra, and just now I was thinking of him.” Zarathustra replied:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0005","text":"“Why are you startled on that account?—But with man it is as with the tree.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0006","text":"The more he wishes upward into height and brightness, the more strongly do his roots strive earthward, downward, into the dark, the deep,—into evil.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0007","text":"“Yes, into evil! cried the youth. How is it possible that you discovered my soul?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0008","text":"Zarathustra smiled and said: “Some souls one will never discover, unless one first invents them.” “Yes, into evil! cried the youth once more.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0009","text":"You spoke the truth, Zarathustra. I no longer trust myself, since I wish to rise upward, and no one trusts me any longer,—how does this come about?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0010","text":"I transform myself too quickly: my today refutes my yesterday. I often skip the steps when I climb,—no step forgives me that.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0011","text":"When I am above, I always find myself alone. No one speaks with me; the frost of solitude makes me tremble. What do I want up in the heights?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0012","text":"My contempt and my longing grow together; the higher I climb, the more I despise him who climbs. What does he want up in the heights?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0013","text":"How ashamed I am of my climbing and stumbling! How I mock my violent panting! How I hate the flyer! How weary I am in the heights!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0014","text":"Here the youth fell silent. And Zarathustra regarded the tree beside which they stood, and spoke thus:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0015","text":"This tree stands solitary here upon the mountain; it grew high above man and beast.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0016","text":"And if it wished to speak, it would have no one who understood it: so high has it grown.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0017","text":"Now it waits and waits,—what then does it wait for? It dwells too near the seat of the clouds: perhaps it waits for the first lightning?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0018","text":"When Zarathustra had said this, the youth cried out with vehement gestures: “Yes, Zarathustra, you speak the truth. I longed for my downfall when I wished to rise upward, and you are the lightning for which I waited! Behold, what am I still, since you have appeared among us? It is envy of you that has destroyed me!”—Thus spoke the youth and wept bitterly. But Zarathustra put his arm around him and led him away with him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0019","text":"And when they had walked together for a while, Zarathustra began thus to speak:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0020","text":"It tears my heart apart. Better than your words say it, your eye tells me all your danger.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0021","text":"You are not yet free; you still seek freedom. Your seeking has made you sleepless and over-wakeful.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0022","text":"You wish to go into the free heights; your soul thirsts after stars. But your evil drives too thirst after freedom.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0023","text":"Your wild dogs want to be free; they bark with pleasure in their cellar when your spirit strives to unfasten all prisons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0024","text":"You are still to me a prisoner who invents freedom for himself: alas, the soul of such prisoners becomes clever, but also cunning and bad.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0025","text":"The liberated one of the spirit must still cleanse himself. Much prison and mould still remains in him: his eye must still become pure.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0026","text":"Yes, I know your danger. But by my love and hope I beseech you: do not cast away your love and hope!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0027","text":"You still feel noble, and the others too, who bear you a grudge and send evil glances, still feel you to be noble. Know that a noble one stands in everyone's way.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0028","text":"A noble one also stands in the way of the good: and even if they call him a good one, they want thereby to put him aside.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0029","text":"The noble one wants to create what is new and a new virtue. The good one wants what is old, and that what is old should be preserved.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0030","text":"But the noble one's danger is not that he should become a good one, but an insolent one, a mocker, a destroyer.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0031","text":"Ah, I knew noble ones who lost their highest hope. And now they slandered all high hopes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0032","text":"Now they lived insolently amid brief pleasures, and scarcely cast any goals beyond the day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0033","text":"“Spirit too is sensual pleasure”—thus they said. Then the wings of their spirit broke: now it crawls about and soils itself in gnawing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0034","text":"Once they thought to become heroes: now they are pleasure-seekers. The hero is to them a grief and a horror.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0035","text":"But by my love and hope I beseech you: do not cast away the hero in your soul! Hold your highest hope sacred!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0036","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBAB:P0036"]}],"source_digest":"1a6031dde4934176b6a18f13e19b3b681191a81d60369ecffafbc3b103f20dce","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Vom Baum am Berge"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Vom Baum am Berge","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD","title":"On the Preachers of Death","order":19,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDPD","text":"Es giebt Prediger des Todes: und die Erde ist voll von Solchen, denen Abkehr gepredigt werden muss vom Leben.\n\nVoll ist die Erde von Überflüssigen, verdorben ist das Leben durch die Viel-zu-Vielen. Möge man sie mit dem „ewigen Leben“ aus diesem Leben weglocken!\n\n„Gelbe“ : so nennt man die Prediger des Todes, oder „Schwarze“ . Aber ich will sie euch noch in andern Farben zeigen.\n\nDa sind die Fürchterlichen, welche in sich das Raubthier herumtragen und keine Wahl haben, es sei denn Lüste oder Selbstzerfleischung. Und auch ihre Lüste sind noch Selbstzerfleischung.\n\nSie sind noch nicht einmal Menschen geworden, diese Fürchterlichen: mögen sie Abkehr predigen vom Leben und selber dahinfahren!\n\nDa sind die Schwindsüchtigen der Seele: kaum sind sie geboren, so fangen sie schon an zu sterben und sehnen sich nach Lehren der Müdigkeit und Entsagung.\n\nSie wollen gerne todt sein, und wir sollten ihren Willen gut heissen! Hüten wir uns, diese Todten zu erwecken und diese lebendigen Särge zu versehren!\n\nIhnen begegnet ein Kranker oder ein Greis oder ein Leichnam; und gleich sagen sie „das Leben ist widerlegt!“\n\nAber nur sie sind widerlegt und ihr Auge, welches nur das Eine Gesicht sieht am Dasein.\n\nEingehüllt in dicke Schwermuth und begierig auf die kleinen Zufälle, welche den Tod bringen: so warten sie und beissen die Zähne auf einander.\n\nOder aber: sie greifen nach Zuckerwerk und spotten ihrer Kinderei dabei: sie hängen an ihrem Strohhalm Leben und spotten, dass sie noch an einem Strohhalm hängen.\n\nIhre Weisheit lautet: „ein Thor, der leben bleibt, aber so sehr sind wir Thoren! Und das eben ist das Thörichtste am Leben!“—\n\n„Das Leben ist nur Leiden“ —so sagen Andre und lügen nicht: so sorgt doch, dass ihr aufhört! So sorgt doch, dass das Leben aufhört, welches nur Leiden ist!\n\nUnd also laute die Lehre eurer Tugend „du sollst dich selber tödten! Du sollst dich selber davonstehlen!“—\n\n„Wollust ist Sünde,—so sagen die Einen, welche den Tod predigen—lasst uns bei Seite gehn und keine Kinder zeugen!“\n\n„Gebären ist mühsam,—sagen dich Andern—wozu noch gebären? Man gebiert nur Unglückliche!“ Und auch sie sind Prediger des Todes.\n\n„Mitleid thut noth—so sagen die Dritten. Nehmt hin, was ich habe! Nehmt hin, was ich bin! Um so weniger bindet mich das Leben!“\n\nWären sie Mitleidige von Grund aus, so würden sie ihren Nächsten das Leben verleiden. Böse sein—das wäre ihre rechte Güte.\n\nAber sie wollen loskommen vom Leben: was schiert es sie, dass sie Andre mit ihren Ketten und Geschenken noch fester binden!—\n\nUnd auch ihr, denen das Leben wilde Arbeit und Unruhe ist: seid ihr nicht sehr müde des Lebens? Seid ihr nicht sehr reif für die Predigt des Todes?\n\nIhr Alle, denen die wilde Arbeit lieb ist und das Schnelle, Neue, Fremde,—ihr ertragt euch schlecht, euer Fleiss ist Flucht und Wille, sich selber zu vergessen.\n\nWenn ihr mehr an das Leben glaubtet, würdet ihr weniger euch dem Augenblicke hinwerfen. Aber ihr habt zum Warten nicht Inhalt genug in euch—und selbst zur Faulheit nicht!\n\nÜberall ertönt die Stimme Derer, welche den Tod predigen: und die Erde ist voll von Solchen, welchen der Tod gepredigt werden muss.\n\nOder „das ewige Leben“ : das gilt mir gleich,—wofern sie nur schnell dahinfahren!\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0001","text":"Es giebt Prediger des Todes: und die Erde ist voll von Solchen, denen Abkehr gepredigt werden muss vom Leben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0002","text":"Voll ist die Erde von Überflüssigen, verdorben ist das Leben durch die Viel-zu-Vielen. Möge man sie mit dem „ewigen Leben“ aus diesem Leben weglocken!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0003","text":"„Gelbe“ : so nennt man die Prediger des Todes, oder „Schwarze“ . Aber ich will sie euch noch in andern Farben zeigen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0004","text":"Da sind die Fürchterlichen, welche in sich das Raubthier herumtragen und keine Wahl haben, es sei denn Lüste oder Selbstzerfleischung. Und auch ihre Lüste sind noch Selbstzerfleischung.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0005","text":"Sie sind noch nicht einmal Menschen geworden, diese Fürchterlichen: mögen sie Abkehr predigen vom Leben und selber dahinfahren!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0006","text":"Da sind die Schwindsüchtigen der Seele: kaum sind sie geboren, so fangen sie schon an zu sterben und sehnen sich nach Lehren der Müdigkeit und Entsagung.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0007","text":"Sie wollen gerne todt sein, und wir sollten ihren Willen gut heissen! Hüten wir uns, diese Todten zu erwecken und diese lebendigen Särge zu versehren!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0008","text":"Ihnen begegnet ein Kranker oder ein Greis oder ein Leichnam; und gleich sagen sie „das Leben ist widerlegt!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0009","text":"Aber nur sie sind widerlegt und ihr Auge, welches nur das Eine Gesicht sieht am Dasein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0010","text":"Eingehüllt in dicke Schwermuth und begierig auf die kleinen Zufälle, welche den Tod bringen: so warten sie und beissen die Zähne auf einander.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0011","text":"Oder aber: sie greifen nach Zuckerwerk und spotten ihrer Kinderei dabei: sie hängen an ihrem Strohhalm Leben und spotten, dass sie noch an einem Strohhalm hängen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0012","text":"Ihre Weisheit lautet: „ein Thor, der leben bleibt, aber so sehr sind wir Thoren! Und das eben ist das Thörichtste am Leben!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0013","text":"„Das Leben ist nur Leiden“ —so sagen Andre und lügen nicht: so sorgt doch, dass ihr aufhört! So sorgt doch, dass das Leben aufhört, welches nur Leiden ist!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0014","text":"Und also laute die Lehre eurer Tugend „du sollst dich selber tödten! Du sollst dich selber davonstehlen!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0015","text":"„Wollust ist Sünde,—so sagen die Einen, welche den Tod predigen—lasst uns bei Seite gehn und keine Kinder zeugen!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0016","text":"„Gebären ist mühsam,—sagen dich Andern—wozu noch gebären? Man gebiert nur Unglückliche!“ Und auch sie sind Prediger des Todes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0017","text":"„Mitleid thut noth—so sagen die Dritten. Nehmt hin, was ich habe! Nehmt hin, was ich bin! Um so weniger bindet mich das Leben!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0018","text":"Wären sie Mitleidige von Grund aus, so würden sie ihren Nächsten das Leben verleiden. Böse sein—das wäre ihre rechte Güte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0019","text":"Aber sie wollen loskommen vom Leben: was schiert es sie, dass sie Andre mit ihren Ketten und Geschenken noch fester binden!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0020","text":"Und auch ihr, denen das Leben wilde Arbeit und Unruhe ist: seid ihr nicht sehr müde des Lebens? Seid ihr nicht sehr reif für die Predigt des Todes?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0021","text":"Ihr Alle, denen die wilde Arbeit lieb ist und das Schnelle, Neue, Fremde,—ihr ertragt euch schlecht, euer Fleiss ist Flucht und Wille, sich selber zu vergessen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0022","text":"Wenn ihr mehr an das Leben glaubtet, würdet ihr weniger euch dem Augenblicke hinwerfen. Aber ihr habt zum Warten nicht Inhalt genug in euch—und selbst zur Faulheit nicht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0023","text":"Überall ertönt die Stimme Derer, welche den Tod predigen: und die Erde ist voll von Solchen, welchen der Tod gepredigt werden muss.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0024","text":"Oder „das ewige Leben“ : das gilt mir gleich,—wofern sie nur schnell dahinfahren!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0025","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"3aadf7db08cdebf3da790f3262fd92fb523a47172c45809ea0fd746f06bb10a8","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181352339707Z_713c7bcd","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Preachers of Death","source_sha256":"3aadf7db08cdebf3da790f3262fd92fb523a47172c45809ea0fd746f06bb10a8","sha256":"1e34761e208ab7629a4dfa98ac5f4debdfef430a6907f5cefdf4015b8722786d","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:13:52.339720+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0001","text":"There are preachers of death: and the earth is full of those to whom turning away from life must be preached.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0002","text":"The earth is full of the superfluous, life is corrupted by the far-too-many. May one lure them out of this life with “eternal life”!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0003","text":"“Yellow ones”: that is what one calls the preachers of death, or “Black ones”. But I will show them to you in still other colours.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0004","text":"There are the terrible ones, who carry the beast of prey about within themselves and have no choice save lusts or self-laceration. And even their lusts are still self-laceration.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0005","text":"They have not even become human beings yet, these terrible ones: may they preach turning away from life and themselves pass away!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0006","text":"There are the consumptives of the soul: scarcely are they born than they already begin to die and yearn for doctrines of weariness and renunciation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0007","text":"They would gladly be dead, and we should approve their will! Let us beware of awakening these dead and of injuring these living coffins!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0008","text":"They encounter a sick man or an old man or a corpse; and at once they say, “life is refuted!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0009","text":"But only they are refuted, and their eye, which sees only the one aspect of existence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0010","text":"Wrapped in thick melancholy and avid for the little accidents that bring death: thus they wait and clench their teeth together.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0011","text":"Or else: they reach for sweetmeats and mock their childishness as they do so: they cling to their straw of life and mock the fact that they still cling to a straw.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0012","text":"Their wisdom runs thus: “a fool is he who remains alive, but we are such great fools! And that is precisely what is most foolish about life!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0013","text":"“Life is only suffering”—thus say others, and they do not lie: then see to it that you cease! Then see to it that life ceases, which is only suffering!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0014","text":"And thus may the teaching of your virtue sound: “thou shalt kill thyself! Thou shalt steal thyself away from this!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0015","text":"“Sensual pleasure is sin,—thus say some who preach death—let us go aside and beget no children!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0016","text":"“Giving birth is laborious,—say the others—why give birth still more? One begets only the unfortunate!” And they too are preachers of death.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0017","text":"“Compassion is needed—thus say the third. Take what I have! Take what I am! So much the less does life bind me!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0018","text":"If they were compassionate from the ground up, they would make life distasteful to their neighbours. Being evil—that would be their proper goodness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0019","text":"But they want to get free from life: what do they care that they bind others still more firmly with their chains and gifts!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0020","text":"And you too, to whom life is wild labour and unrest: are you not very weary of life? Are you not very ripe for the preaching of death?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0021","text":"All of you to whom wild labour is dear, and the swift, new, strange,—you endure yourselves badly; your diligence is flight and a will to forget yourselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0022","text":"If you believed more in life, you would throw yourselves less upon the moment. But you do not have enough substance within yourselves for waiting—and not even for idleness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0023","text":"Everywhere resounds the voice of those who preach death: and the earth is full of those to whom death must be preached.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0024","text":"Or “eternal life”: it is all the same to me,—provided only that they quickly pass away!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0025","text":"Thus Spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDPD:P0025"]}],"source_digest":"d108b9aa1c05e54e7e308f5c92bf60fb0fd4929102600302f08c7562ca050f92","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von den Predigern des Todes"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von den Predigern des Todes","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK","title":"On War and Warriors","order":20,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VKUK","text":"Von unsern besten Feinden wollen wir nicht geschont sein, und auch von Denen nicht, welche wir von Grund aus lieben. So lasst mich denn euch die Wahrheit sagen!\n\nMeine Brüder im Kriege! Ich liebe euch von Grund aus, ich bin und war Euresgleichen. Und ich bin auch euer bester Feind. So lasst mich denn euch die Wahrheit sagen!\n\nIch weiss um den Hass und Neid eures Herzens. Ihr seid nicht gross genug, um Hass und Neid nicht zu kennen. So seid denn gross genug, euch ihrer nicht zu schämen!\n\nUnd wenn ihr nicht Heilige der Erkenntniss sein könnt, so seid mir wenigstens deren Kriegsmänner. Das sind die Gefährten und Vorläufer solcher Heiligkeit.\n\nIch sehe viel Soldaten: möchte ich viel Kriegsmänner sehn! „Ein-form“ nennt man’s, was sie tragen: möge es nicht Ein-form sein, was sie damit verstecken!\n\nIhr sollt mir Solche sein, deren Auge immer nach einem Feinde sucht—nach eurem Feinde. Und bei Einigen von euch giebt es einen Hass auf den ersten Blick.\n\nEuren Feind sollt ihr suchen, euren Krieg sollt ihr führen und für eure Gedanken! Und wenn euer Gedanke unterliegt, so soll eure Redlichkeit darüber noch Triumph rufen!\n\nIhr sollt den Frieden lieben als Mittel zu neuen Kriegen. Und den kurzen Frieden mehr, als den langen.\n\nEuch rathe ich nicht zur Arbeit, sondern zum Kampfe. Euch rathe ich nicht zum Frieden, sondern zum Siege. Eure Arbeit sei ein Kampf, euer Friede sei ein Sieg!\n\nMan kann nur schweigen und stillsitzen, wenn man Pfeil und Bogen hat: sonst schwätzt und zankt man. Euer Friede sei ein Sieg!\n\nIhr sagt, die gute Sache sei es, die sogar den Krieg heilige? Ich sage euch: der gute Krieg ist es, der jede Sache heiligt.\n\nDer Krieg und der Muth haben mehr grosse Dinge gethan, als die Nächstenliebe. Nicht euer Mitleiden, sondern eure Tapferkeit rettete bisher die Verunglückten.\n\nWas ist gut? fragt ihr. Tapfer sein ist gut. Lasst die kleinen Mädchen reden: „gut sein ist, was hübsch zugleich und rührend ist.“\n\nMan nennt euch herzlos: aber euer Herz ist ächt, und ich liebe die Scham eurer Herzlichkeit. Ihr schämt euch eurer Fluth, und Andre schämen sich ihrer Ebbe.\n\nIhr seid hässlich? Nun wohlan, meine Brüder! So nehmt das Erhabne um euch, den Mantel des Hässlichen!\n\nUnd wenn eure Seele gross wird, so wird sie übermüthig, und in eurer Erhabenheit ist Bosheit. Ich kenne euch.\n\nIn der Bosheit begegnet sich der Übermüthige mit dem Schwächlinge. Aber sie missverstehen einander. Ich kenne euch.\n\nIhr dürft nur Feinde haben, die zu hassen sind, aber nicht Feinde zum Verachten. Ihr müsst stolz auf euern Feind sein: dann sind die Erfolge eures Feindes auch eure Erfolge.\n\nAuflehnung—das ist die Vornehmheit am Sclaven. Eure Vornehmheit sei Gehorsam! Euer Befehlen selber sei ein Gehorchen!\n\nEinem guten Kriegsmanne klingt „du sollst“ angenehmer, als „ich will“. Und Alles, was euch lieb ist, sollt ihr euch erst noch befehlen lassen.\n\nEure Liebe zum Leben sei Liebe zu eurer höchsten Hoffnung: und eure höchste Hoffnung sei der höchste Gedanke des Lebens!\n\nEuren höchsten Gedanken aber sollt ihr euch von mir befehlen lassen—und er lautet: der Mensch ist Etwas, das überwunden werden soll.\n\nSo lebt euer Leben des Gehorsams und des Krieges! Was liegt am Lang-Leben! Welcher Krieger will geschont sein!\n\nIch schone euch nicht, ich liebe euch von Grund aus, meine Brüder im Kriege!—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0001","text":"Von unsern besten Feinden wollen wir nicht geschont sein, und auch von Denen nicht, welche wir von Grund aus lieben. So lasst mich denn euch die Wahrheit sagen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0002","text":"Meine Brüder im Kriege! Ich liebe euch von Grund aus, ich bin und war Euresgleichen. Und ich bin auch euer bester Feind. So lasst mich denn euch die Wahrheit sagen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0003","text":"Ich weiss um den Hass und Neid eures Herzens. Ihr seid nicht gross genug, um Hass und Neid nicht zu kennen. So seid denn gross genug, euch ihrer nicht zu schämen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0004","text":"Und wenn ihr nicht Heilige der Erkenntniss sein könnt, so seid mir wenigstens deren Kriegsmänner. Das sind die Gefährten und Vorläufer solcher Heiligkeit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0005","text":"Ich sehe viel Soldaten: möchte ich viel Kriegsmänner sehn! „Ein-form“ nennt man’s, was sie tragen: möge es nicht Ein-form sein, was sie damit verstecken!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0006","text":"Ihr sollt mir Solche sein, deren Auge immer nach einem Feinde sucht—nach eurem Feinde. Und bei Einigen von euch giebt es einen Hass auf den ersten Blick.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0007","text":"Euren Feind sollt ihr suchen, euren Krieg sollt ihr führen und für eure Gedanken! Und wenn euer Gedanke unterliegt, so soll eure Redlichkeit darüber noch Triumph rufen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0008","text":"Ihr sollt den Frieden lieben als Mittel zu neuen Kriegen. Und den kurzen Frieden mehr, als den langen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0009","text":"Euch rathe ich nicht zur Arbeit, sondern zum Kampfe. Euch rathe ich nicht zum Frieden, sondern zum Siege. Eure Arbeit sei ein Kampf, euer Friede sei ein Sieg!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0010","text":"Man kann nur schweigen und stillsitzen, wenn man Pfeil und Bogen hat: sonst schwätzt und zankt man. Euer Friede sei ein Sieg!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0011","text":"Ihr sagt, die gute Sache sei es, die sogar den Krieg heilige? Ich sage euch: der gute Krieg ist es, der jede Sache heiligt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0012","text":"Der Krieg und der Muth haben mehr grosse Dinge gethan, als die Nächstenliebe. Nicht euer Mitleiden, sondern eure Tapferkeit rettete bisher die Verunglückten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0013","text":"Was ist gut? fragt ihr. Tapfer sein ist gut. Lasst die kleinen Mädchen reden: „gut sein ist, was hübsch zugleich und rührend ist.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0014","text":"Man nennt euch herzlos: aber euer Herz ist ächt, und ich liebe die Scham eurer Herzlichkeit. Ihr schämt euch eurer Fluth, und Andre schämen sich ihrer Ebbe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0015","text":"Ihr seid hässlich? Nun wohlan, meine Brüder! So nehmt das Erhabne um euch, den Mantel des Hässlichen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0016","text":"Und wenn eure Seele gross wird, so wird sie übermüthig, und in eurer Erhabenheit ist Bosheit. Ich kenne euch.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0017","text":"In der Bosheit begegnet sich der Übermüthige mit dem Schwächlinge. Aber sie missverstehen einander. Ich kenne euch.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0018","text":"Ihr dürft nur Feinde haben, die zu hassen sind, aber nicht Feinde zum Verachten. Ihr müsst stolz auf euern Feind sein: dann sind die Erfolge eures Feindes auch eure Erfolge.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0019","text":"Auflehnung—das ist die Vornehmheit am Sclaven. Eure Vornehmheit sei Gehorsam! Euer Befehlen selber sei ein Gehorchen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0020","text":"Einem guten Kriegsmanne klingt „du sollst“ angenehmer, als „ich will“. Und Alles, was euch lieb ist, sollt ihr euch erst noch befehlen lassen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0021","text":"Eure Liebe zum Leben sei Liebe zu eurer höchsten Hoffnung: und eure höchste Hoffnung sei der höchste Gedanke des Lebens!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0022","text":"Euren höchsten Gedanken aber sollt ihr euch von mir befehlen lassen—und er lautet: der Mensch ist Etwas, das überwunden werden soll.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0023","text":"So lebt euer Leben des Gehorsams und des Krieges! Was liegt am Lang-Leben! Welcher Krieger will geschont sein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0024","text":"Ich schone euch nicht, ich liebe euch von Grund aus, meine Brüder im Kriege!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0025","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"7c19e61434a1fafd0abae034d15d1d87d363952695d849996eeaf5cbe47966a3","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181403666711Z_f324990b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On War and Warriors","source_sha256":"7c19e61434a1fafd0abae034d15d1d87d363952695d849996eeaf5cbe47966a3","sha256":"8334a9dee38cae8abde0a3fa41b1cfdc442fee6e77c8b4589808439e1dcb99d9","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:14:03.666746+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0001","text":"We do not wish to be spared by our best enemies, nor by those whom we love from the very ground up. So let me then tell you the truth!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0002","text":"My brothers in war! I love you from the very ground up, I am and was your like. And I am also your best enemy. So let me then tell you the truth!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0003","text":"I know the hatred and envy of your heart. You are not great enough not to know hatred and envy. Then be great enough not to be ashamed of them!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0004","text":"And if you cannot be saints of knowledge, then at least be its men of war for me. They are the companions and forerunners of such sainthood.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0005","text":"I see many soldiers: would that I saw many men of war! What they wear is called “uniform”: may that which they conceal with it not be uniform!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0006","text":"You shall be such to me as always seek with their eye for an enemy—for your enemy. And among some of you there is a hatred at first sight.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0007","text":"You shall seek your enemy, you shall wage your war—and for your thoughts! And if your thought is defeated, then let your honesty still cry triumph over it!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0008","text":"You shall love peace as a means to new wars. And the short peace more than the long.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0009","text":"I counsel you not to work, but to struggle. I counsel you not to peace, but to victory. Let your work be a struggle, your peace a victory!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0010","text":"One can be silent and sit still only when one has arrow and bow: otherwise one chatters and quarrels. Let your peace be a victory!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0011","text":"You say that it is the good cause which even sanctifies war? I say to you: it is good war that sanctifies every cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0012","text":"War and courage have done more great things than love of one’s neighbour. Not your pity, but your bravery has hitherto saved those who came to grief.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0013","text":"What is good? you ask. To be brave is good. Let the little girls speak: “good is that which is at once pretty and touching.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0014","text":"You are called heartless: but your heart is genuine, and I love the shame of your heartiness. You are ashamed of your flood, and others are ashamed of their ebb.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0015","text":"You are ugly? Well then, my brothers! So take the sublime about yourselves, the cloak of the ugly!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0016","text":"And when your soul becomes great, it becomes overweening, and in your sublimity there is malice. I know you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0017","text":"In malice the overweening man meets the weakling. But they misunderstand one another. I know you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0018","text":"You may have only enemies who are to be hated, but not enemies to be despised. You must be proud of your enemy: then your enemy’s successes are also your successes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0019","text":"Rebellion—that is the nobility in the slave. Let your nobility be obedience! Let your commanding itself be an obeying!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0020","text":"To a good man of war, “thou shalt” sounds more pleasant than “I will.” And all that is dear to you, you should first let yourselves be commanded to do.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0021","text":"Let your love of life be love of your highest hope: and let your highest hope be the highest thought of life!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0022","text":"But you shall let me command to you your highest thought—and it reads: man is something that shall be overcome.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0023","text":"Thus live your life of obedience and war! What matters long life! What warrior wishes to be spared!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0024","text":"I do not spare you, I love you from the very ground up, my brothers in war!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0025","text":"Thus Spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUK:P0025"]}],"source_digest":"9c89a7b4c2b8306c2b91cc5592292517a791e5d23ac7911631a5ea24cc2a9903","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Vom Krieg und Kriegsvolke"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Vom Krieg und Kriegsvolke","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT","title":"On the New Idol","order":21,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VNGT","text":"Irgendwo giebt es noch Völker und Heerden, doch nicht bei uns, meine Brüder: da giebt es Staaten.\n\nStaat? Was ist das? Wohlan! Jetzt thut mir die Ohren auf, denn jetzt sage ich euch mein Wort vom Tode der Völker.\n\nStaat heisst das kälteste aller kalten Ungeheuer. Kalt lügt es auch; und diese Lüge kriecht aus seinem Munde: „Ich, der Staat, bin das Volk.“\n\nLüge ist’s! Schaffende waren es, die schufen die Völker und hängten einen Glauben und eine Liebe über sie hin: also dienten sie dem Leben.\n\nVernichter sind es, die stellen Fallen auf für Viele und heissen sie Staat: sie hängen ein Schwert und hundert Begierden über sie hin.\n\nWo es noch Volk giebt, da versteht es den Staat nicht und hasst ihn als bösen Blick und Sünde an Sitten und Rechten.\n\nDieses Zeichen gebe ich euch: jedes Volk spricht seine Zunge des Guten und Bösen: die versteht der Nachbar nicht. Seine Sprache erfand es sich in Sitten und Rechten.\n\nAber der Staat lügt in allen Zungen des Guten und Bösen; und was er auch redet, er lügt—und was er auch hat, gestohlen hat er’s.\n\nFalsch ist Alles an ihm; mit gestohlenen Zähnen beisst er, der Bissige. Falsch sind selbst seine Eingeweide.\n\nSprachverwirrung des Guten und Bösen: dieses Zeichen gebe ich euch als Zeichen des Staates. Wahrlich, den Willen zum Tode deutet dieses Zeichen! Wahrlich, es winkt den Predigern des Todes!\n\nViel zu Viele werden geboren: für die Überflüssigen ward der Staat erfunden!\n\nSeht mir doch, wie er sie an sich lockt, die Viel-zu-Vielen! Wie er sie schlingt und kaut und wiederkäut!\n\n„Auf der Erde ist nichts Grösseres als ich: der ordnende Finger bin ich Gottes“ —also brüllt das Unthier. Und nicht nur Langgeohrte und Kurzgeäugte sinken auf die Kniee!\n\nAch, auch in euch, ihr grossen Seelen, raunt er seine düsteren Lügen! Ach, er erräth die reichen Herzen, die gerne sich verschwenden!\n\nJa, auch euch erräth er, ihr Besieger des alten Gottes! Müde wurdet ihr im Kampfe, und nun dient eure Müdigkeit noch dem neuen Götzen!\n\nHelden und Ehrenhafte möchte er um sich aufstellen, der neue Götze! Gerne sonnt er sich im Sonnenschein guter Gewissen,—das kalte Unthier!\n\nAlles will er euch geben, wenn ihr ihn anbetet, der neue Götze: also kauft er sich den Glanz eurer Tugend und den Blick eurer stolzen Augen.\n\nKödern will er mit euch die Viel-zu-Vielen! Ja, ein Höllenkunststück ward da erfunden, ein Pferd des Todes, klirrend im Putz göttlicher Ehren!\n\nJa, ein Sterben für Viele ward da erfunden, das sich selber als Leben preist: wahrlich, ein Herzensdienst allen Predigern des Todes!\n\nStaat nenne ich’s, wo Alle Gifttrinker sind, Gute und Schlimme: Staat, wo Alle sich selber verlieren, Gute und Schlimme: Staat, wo der langsame Selbstmord Aller—„das Leben“ heisst.\n\nSeht mir doch diese Überflüssigen! Sie stehlen sich die Werke der Erfinder und die Schätze der Weisen: Bildung nennen sie ihren Diebstahl—und Alles wird ihnen zu Krankheit und Ungemach!\n\nSeht mir doch diese Überflüssigen! Krank sind sie immer, sie erbrechen ihre Galle und nennen es Zeitung. Sie verschlingen einander und können sich nicht einmal verdauen.\n\nSeht mir doch diese Überflüssigen! Reichthümer erwerben sie und werden ärmer damit. Macht wollen sie und zuerst das Brecheisen der Macht, viel Geld,—diese Unvermögenden!\n\nSeht sie klettern, diese geschwinden Affen! Sie klettern über einander hinweg und zerren sich also in den Schlamm und die Tiefe.\n\nHin zum Throne wollen sie Alle: ihr Wahnsinn ist es,—als ob das Glück auf dem Throne sässe! Oft sitzt der Schlamm auf dem Thron—und oft auch der Thron auf dem Schlamme.\n\nWahnsinnige sind sie mir Alle und kletternde Affen und Überheisse. Übel riecht mir ihr Götze, das kalte Unthier: übel riechen sie mir alle zusammen, diese Götzendiener.\n\nMeine Brüder, wollt ihr denn ersticken im Dunste ihrer Mäuler und Begierden! Lieber zerbrecht doch die Fenster und springt in’s Freie!\n\nGeht doch dem schlechten Geruche aus dem Wege! Geht fort von der Götzendienerei der Überflüssigen!\n\nGeht doch dem schlechten Geruche aus dem Wege! Geht fort von dem Dampfe dieser Menschenopfer!\n\nFrei steht grossen Seelen auch jetzt noch die Erde. Leer sind noch viele Sitze für Einsame und Zweisame, um die der Geruch stiller Meere weht.\n\nFrei steht noch grossen Seelen ein freies Leben. Wahrlich, wer wenig besitzt, wird um so weniger besessen: gelobt sei die kleine Armuth!\n\nDort, wo der Staat aufhört, da beginnt erst der Mensch, der nicht überflüssig ist: da beginnt das Lied des Nothwendigen, die einmalige und unersetzliche Weise.\n\nDort, wo der Staat aufhört ,—so seht mir doch hin, meine Brüder! Seht ihr ihn nicht, den Regenbogen und die Brücken des Übermenschen?—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0001","text":"Irgendwo giebt es noch Völker und Heerden, doch nicht bei uns, meine Brüder: da giebt es Staaten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0002","text":"Staat? Was ist das? Wohlan! Jetzt thut mir die Ohren auf, denn jetzt sage ich euch mein Wort vom Tode der Völker.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0003","text":"Staat heisst das kälteste aller kalten Ungeheuer. Kalt lügt es auch; und diese Lüge kriecht aus seinem Munde: „Ich, der Staat, bin das Volk.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0004","text":"Lüge ist’s! Schaffende waren es, die schufen die Völker und hängten einen Glauben und eine Liebe über sie hin: also dienten sie dem Leben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0005","text":"Vernichter sind es, die stellen Fallen auf für Viele und heissen sie Staat: sie hängen ein Schwert und hundert Begierden über sie hin.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0006","text":"Wo es noch Volk giebt, da versteht es den Staat nicht und hasst ihn als bösen Blick und Sünde an Sitten und Rechten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0007","text":"Dieses Zeichen gebe ich euch: jedes Volk spricht seine Zunge des Guten und Bösen: die versteht der Nachbar nicht. Seine Sprache erfand es sich in Sitten und Rechten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0008","text":"Aber der Staat lügt in allen Zungen des Guten und Bösen; und was er auch redet, er lügt—und was er auch hat, gestohlen hat er’s.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0009","text":"Falsch ist Alles an ihm; mit gestohlenen Zähnen beisst er, der Bissige. Falsch sind selbst seine Eingeweide.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0010","text":"Sprachverwirrung des Guten und Bösen: dieses Zeichen gebe ich euch als Zeichen des Staates. Wahrlich, den Willen zum Tode deutet dieses Zeichen! Wahrlich, es winkt den Predigern des Todes!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0011","text":"Viel zu Viele werden geboren: für die Überflüssigen ward der Staat erfunden!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0012","text":"Seht mir doch, wie er sie an sich lockt, die Viel-zu-Vielen! Wie er sie schlingt und kaut und wiederkäut!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0013","text":"„Auf der Erde ist nichts Grösseres als ich: der ordnende Finger bin ich Gottes“ —also brüllt das Unthier. Und nicht nur Langgeohrte und Kurzgeäugte sinken auf die Kniee!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0014","text":"Ach, auch in euch, ihr grossen Seelen, raunt er seine düsteren Lügen! Ach, er erräth die reichen Herzen, die gerne sich verschwenden!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0015","text":"Ja, auch euch erräth er, ihr Besieger des alten Gottes! Müde wurdet ihr im Kampfe, und nun dient eure Müdigkeit noch dem neuen Götzen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0016","text":"Helden und Ehrenhafte möchte er um sich aufstellen, der neue Götze! Gerne sonnt er sich im Sonnenschein guter Gewissen,—das kalte Unthier!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0017","text":"Alles will er euch geben, wenn ihr ihn anbetet, der neue Götze: also kauft er sich den Glanz eurer Tugend und den Blick eurer stolzen Augen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0018","text":"Ködern will er mit euch die Viel-zu-Vielen! Ja, ein Höllenkunststück ward da erfunden, ein Pferd des Todes, klirrend im Putz göttlicher Ehren!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0019","text":"Ja, ein Sterben für Viele ward da erfunden, das sich selber als Leben preist: wahrlich, ein Herzensdienst allen Predigern des Todes!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0020","text":"Staat nenne ich’s, wo Alle Gifttrinker sind, Gute und Schlimme: Staat, wo Alle sich selber verlieren, Gute und Schlimme: Staat, wo der langsame Selbstmord Aller—„das Leben“ heisst.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0021","text":"Seht mir doch diese Überflüssigen! Sie stehlen sich die Werke der Erfinder und die Schätze der Weisen: Bildung nennen sie ihren Diebstahl—und Alles wird ihnen zu Krankheit und Ungemach!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0022","text":"Seht mir doch diese Überflüssigen! Krank sind sie immer, sie erbrechen ihre Galle und nennen es Zeitung. Sie verschlingen einander und können sich nicht einmal verdauen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0023","text":"Seht mir doch diese Überflüssigen! Reichthümer erwerben sie und werden ärmer damit. Macht wollen sie und zuerst das Brecheisen der Macht, viel Geld,—diese Unvermögenden!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0024","text":"Seht sie klettern, diese geschwinden Affen! Sie klettern über einander hinweg und zerren sich also in den Schlamm und die Tiefe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0025","text":"Hin zum Throne wollen sie Alle: ihr Wahnsinn ist es,—als ob das Glück auf dem Throne sässe! Oft sitzt der Schlamm auf dem Thron—und oft auch der Thron auf dem Schlamme.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0026","text":"Wahnsinnige sind sie mir Alle und kletternde Affen und Überheisse. Übel riecht mir ihr Götze, das kalte Unthier: übel riechen sie mir alle zusammen, diese Götzendiener.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0027","text":"Meine Brüder, wollt ihr denn ersticken im Dunste ihrer Mäuler und Begierden! Lieber zerbrecht doch die Fenster und springt in’s Freie!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0028","text":"Geht doch dem schlechten Geruche aus dem Wege! Geht fort von der Götzendienerei der Überflüssigen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0029","text":"Geht doch dem schlechten Geruche aus dem Wege! Geht fort von dem Dampfe dieser Menschenopfer!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0030","text":"Frei steht grossen Seelen auch jetzt noch die Erde. Leer sind noch viele Sitze für Einsame und Zweisame, um die der Geruch stiller Meere weht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0031","text":"Frei steht noch grossen Seelen ein freies Leben. Wahrlich, wer wenig besitzt, wird um so weniger besessen: gelobt sei die kleine Armuth!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0032","text":"Dort, wo der Staat aufhört, da beginnt erst der Mensch, der nicht überflüssig ist: da beginnt das Lied des Nothwendigen, die einmalige und unersetzliche Weise.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0033","text":"Dort, wo der Staat aufhört ,—so seht mir doch hin, meine Brüder! Seht ihr ihn nicht, den Regenbogen und die Brücken des Übermenschen?—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0034","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"f226574a107d946e757f8415cfb7881ef9f30f028dfdf6fb1cc22c3e514c47e8","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181419598321Z_6e2f3222","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the New Idol","source_sha256":"f226574a107d946e757f8415cfb7881ef9f30f028dfdf6fb1cc22c3e514c47e8","sha256":"ab669996b452f6528b6fb13284101f5037da994d4a3bdd91469bccf17c4825f9","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:14:19.598365+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0001","text":"Somewhere there are still peoples and herds, but not among us, my brothers: here there are states.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0002","text":"State? What is that? Well then! Now open your ears to me, for now I shall speak to you my word concerning the death of peoples.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0003","text":"State is the name of the coldest of all cold monsters. Coldly it lies too; and this lie crawls out of its mouth: “I, the state, am the people.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0004","text":"It is a lie! Creators they were who created the peoples and suspended a faith and a love above them: thus they served life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0005","text":"Destroyers they are who set traps for the many and call them state: they suspend a sword and a hundred desires above them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0006","text":"Where there is still a people, there it does not understand the state and hates it as the evil eye and a sin against customs and rights.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0007","text":"This sign I give you: every people speaks its own tongue of good and evil: the neighbour does not understand it. It invented its language for itself in customs and rights.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0008","text":"But the state lies in all tongues of good and evil; and whatever it says, it lies—and whatever it has, it has stolen.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0009","text":"Everything about it is false; with stolen teeth it bites, the biter. Even its entrails are false.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0010","text":"Confusion of tongues concerning good and evil: this sign I give you as the sign of the state. Verily, this sign indicates the will to death! Verily, it beckons to the preachers of death!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0011","text":"Far too many are born: for the superfluous the state was invented!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0012","text":"Just look at how it lures them to itself, the far-too-many! How it devours and chews and ruminates them!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0013","text":"“On earth there is nothing greater than I: I am the ordering finger of God”—thus roars the beast. And not only the long-eared and short-sighted sink to their knees!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0014","text":"Alas, even into you, great souls, it whispers its gloomy lies! Alas, it guesses the rich hearts that gladly squander themselves!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0015","text":"Yes, it guesses even you, you conquerors of the old God! You grew weary in the struggle, and now your weariness still serves the new idol!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0016","text":"Heroes and honourable men the new idol would like to set up around itself! Gladly it basks in the sunshine of good consciences,—the cold monster!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0017","text":"It wants to give you everything, if you worship it, the new idol: thus it buys for itself the splendour of your virtue and the gaze of your proud eyes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0018","text":"It wants to use you as bait for the far-too-many! Yes, there was invented an infernal contrivance, a horse of death, clanking in the finery of divine honours!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0019","text":"Yes, there was invented a dying for many that praises itself as life: verily, a service of the heart to all preachers of death!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0020","text":"I call it state where all are poison-drinkers, good and evil: state, where all lose themselves, good and evil: state, where the slow suicide of all—is called “life.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0021","text":"Just look at these superfluous ones! They steal the works of inventors and the treasures of the wise: they call their theft education—and everything becomes sickness and affliction to them!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0022","text":"Just look at these superfluous ones! They are always sick, they vomit their bile and call it a newspaper. They devour one another and cannot even digest themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0023","text":"Just look at these superfluous ones! They acquire riches and become poorer thereby. They want power and, first of all, the crowbar of power, much money,—these impotent ones!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0024","text":"See them climbing, these nimble apes! They climb over one another and thus drag themselves into the mud and the depths.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0025","text":"All of them want to get to the throne: it is their madness,—as though happiness sat upon the throne! Often mud sits upon the throne—and often too the throne upon mud.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0026","text":"To me they are all madmen and climbing apes and overheated. Their idol, the cold monster, smells bad to me: they all smell bad to me together, these idolaters.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0027","text":"My brothers, do you then wish to suffocate in the fumes of their mouths and desires! Better break the windows and leap out into the open!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0028","text":"Get out of the way of the bad smell! Go away from the idolatry of the superfluous!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0029","text":"Get out of the way of the bad smell! Go away from the vapour of these human sacrifices!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0030","text":"Even now the earth stands open to great souls. Many seats are still empty for solitaries and for twosomes, about whom the scent of quiet seas blows.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0031","text":"A free life still stands open to great souls. Verily, he who possesses little is possessed all the less: praised be small poverty!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0032","text":"There, where the state ends, only there does the human being begin who is not superfluous: there begins the song of the necessary, the unique and irreplaceable melody.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0033","text":"There, where the state ends,—just look there, my brothers! Do you not see it, the rainbow and the bridges of the Overman?—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0034","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VNGT:P0034"]}],"source_digest":"cc85bbafe0ca65498abc88dab716555a3b692202dfad352a5e5baa4b0c5fc26e","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Vom neuen Götzen"}],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"Vom neuen Götzen","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD","title":"On the Flies of the Marketplace","order":22,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDFD","text":"Fliehe, mein Freund, in deine Einsamkeit! Ich sehe dich betäubt vom Lärme der grossen Männer und zerstochen von den Stacheln der kleinen.\n\nWürdig wissen Wald und Fels mit dir zu schweigen. Gleiche wieder dem Baume, den du liebst, dem breitästigen: still und aufhorchend hängt er über dem Meere.\n\nWo die Einsamkeit aufhört, da beginnt der Markt; und wo der Markt beginnt, da beginnt auch der Lärm der grossen Schauspieler und das Geschwirr der giftigen Fliegen.\n\nIn der Welt taugen die besten Dinge noch Nichts, ohne Einen, der sie erst aufführt: grosse Männer heisst das Volk diese Aufführer.\n\nWenig begreift das Volk das Grosse, das ist: das Schaffende. Aber Sinne hat es für alle Aufführer und Schauspieler grosser Sachen.\n\nUm die Erfinder von neuen Werthen dreht sich die Welt:—unsichtbar dreht sie sich. Doch um die Schauspieler dreht sich das Volk und der Ruhm: so ist es der Welt Lauf.\n\nGeist hat der Schauspieler, doch wenig Gewissen des Geistes. Er glaubt immer an Das, womit er am stärksten glauben macht,—glauben an sich macht!\n\nMorgen hat er einen neuen Glauben und übermorgen einen neueren. Rasche Sinne hat er, gleich dem Volke, und veränderliche Witterungen.\n\nUmwerfen—das heisst ihm: beweisen. Toll machen—das heisst ihm: überzeugen. Und Blut gilt ihm als aller Gründe bester.\n\nEine Wahrheit, die nur in feine Ohren schlüpft, nennt er Lüge und Nichts. Wahrlich, er glaubt nur an Götter, die grossen Lärm in der Welt machen!\n\nVoll von feierlichen Possenreissern ist der Markt—und das Volk rühmt sich seiner grossen Männer! das sind ihm die Herrn der Stunde.\n\nAber die Stunde drängt sie: so drängen sie dich. Und auch von dir wollen sie Ja oder Nein. Wehe, du willst zwischen Für und Wider deinen Stuhl setzen?\n\nDieser Unbedingten und Drängenden halber sei ohne Eifersucht, du Liebhaber der Wahrheit! Niemals noch hängte sich die Wahrheit an den Arm eines Unbedingten.\n\nDieser Plötzlichen halber gehe zurück in deine Sicherheit: nur auf dem Markt wird man mit Ja? oder Nein? überfallen.\n\nLangsam ist das Erleben allen tiefen Brunnen: lange müssen sie warten, bis sie wissen, was in ihre Tiefe fiel.\n\nAbseits vom Markte und Ruhme begiebt sich alles Grosse: abseits vom Markte und Ruhme wohnten von je die Erfinder neuer Werthe.\n\nFliehe, mein Freund, in deine Einsamkeit: ich sehe dich von giftigen Fliegen zerstochen. Fliehe dorthin, wo rauhe, starke Luft weht!\n\nFliehe in deine Einsamkeit! Du lebtest den Kleinen und Erbärmlichen zu nahe. Fliehe vor ihrer unsichtbaren Rache! Gegen dich sind sie Nichts als Rache.\n\nHebe nicht mehr den Arm gegen sie! Unzählbar sind sie, und es ist nicht dein Loos, Fliegenwedel zu sein.\n\nUnzählbar sind diese Kleinen und Erbärmlichen; und manchem stolzen Baue gereichten schon Regentropfen und Unkraut zum Untergange.\n\nDu bist kein Stein, aber schon wurdest du hohl von vielen Tropfen. Zerbrechen und zerbersten wirst du mir noch von vielen Tropfen.\n\nErmüdet sehe ich dich durch giftige Fliegen, blutig geritzt sehe ich dich an hundert Stellen; und dein Stolz will nicht einmal zürnen.\n\nBlut möchten sie von dir in aller Unschuld, Blut begehren ihre blutlosen Seelen —und sie stechen daher in aller Unschuld.\n\nAber, du Tiefer, du leidest zu tief auch an kleinen Wunden; und ehe du dich noch geheilt hast, kroch dir der gleiche Giftwurm über die Hand.\n\nZu stolz bist du mir dafür, diese Naschhaften zu tödten. Hüte dich aber, dass es nicht dein Verhängniss werde, all ihr giftiges Unrecht zu tragen!\n\nSie summen um dich auch mit ihrem Lobe: Zudringlichkeit ist ihr Loben. Sie wollen die Nähe deiner Haut und deines Blutes.\n\nSie schmeicheln dir wie einem Gotte oder Teufel; sie winseln vor dir wie vor einem Gotte oder Teufel. Was macht es! Schmeichler sind es und Winsler und nicht mehr.\n\nAuch geben sie sich dir oft als Liebenswürdige. Aber das war immer die Klugheit der Feigen. Ja, die Feigen sind klug!\n\nSie denken viel über dich mit ihrer engen Seele,—bedenklich bist du ihnen stets! Alles, was viel bedacht wird, wird bedenklich.\n\nSie bestrafen dich für alle deine Tugenden. Sie verzeihen dir von Grund aus nur —deine Fehlgriffe.\n\nWeil du milde bist und gerechten Sinnes, sagst du: „unschuldig sind sie an ihrem kleinen Dasein.“ Aber ihre enge Seele denkt: „Schuld ist alles grosse Dasein.“\n\nAuch wenn du ihnen milde bist, fühlen sie sich noch von dir verachtet; und sie geben dir deine Wohlthat zurück mit versteckten Wehthaten.\n\nDein wortloser Stolz geht immer wider ihren Geschmack; sie frohlocken, wenn du einmal bescheiden genug bist, eitel zu sein.\n\nDas, was wir an einem Menschen erkennen, das entzünden wir an ihm auch. Also hüte dich vor den Kleinen!\n\nVor dir fühlen sie sich klein, und ihre Niedrigkeit glimmt und glüht gegen dich in unsichtbarer Rache.\n\nMerktest du nicht, wie oft sie stumm wurden, wenn du zu ihnen tratest, und wie ihre Kraft von ihnen gieng wie der Rauch von einem erlöschenden Feuer?\n\nJa, mein Freund, das böse Gewissen bist du deinen Nächsten: denn sie sind deiner unwerth. Also hassen sie dich und möchten gerne an deinem Blute saugen.\n\nDeine Nächsten werden immer giftige Fliegen sein; Das, was gross an dir ist,—das selber muss sie giftiger machen und immer fliegenhafter.\n\nFliehe, mein Freund, in deine Einsamkeit und dorthin, wo eine rauhe, starke Luft weht. Nicht ist es dein Loos, Fliegenwedel zu sein.—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0001","text":"Fliehe, mein Freund, in deine Einsamkeit! Ich sehe dich betäubt vom Lärme der grossen Männer und zerstochen von den Stacheln der kleinen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0002","text":"Würdig wissen Wald und Fels mit dir zu schweigen. Gleiche wieder dem Baume, den du liebst, dem breitästigen: still und aufhorchend hängt er über dem Meere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0003","text":"Wo die Einsamkeit aufhört, da beginnt der Markt; und wo der Markt beginnt, da beginnt auch der Lärm der grossen Schauspieler und das Geschwirr der giftigen Fliegen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0004","text":"In der Welt taugen die besten Dinge noch Nichts, ohne Einen, der sie erst aufführt: grosse Männer heisst das Volk diese Aufführer.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0005","text":"Wenig begreift das Volk das Grosse, das ist: das Schaffende. Aber Sinne hat es für alle Aufführer und Schauspieler grosser Sachen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0006","text":"Um die Erfinder von neuen Werthen dreht sich die Welt:—unsichtbar dreht sie sich. Doch um die Schauspieler dreht sich das Volk und der Ruhm: so ist es der Welt Lauf.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0007","text":"Geist hat der Schauspieler, doch wenig Gewissen des Geistes. Er glaubt immer an Das, womit er am stärksten glauben macht,—glauben an sich macht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0008","text":"Morgen hat er einen neuen Glauben und übermorgen einen neueren. Rasche Sinne hat er, gleich dem Volke, und veränderliche Witterungen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0009","text":"Umwerfen—das heisst ihm: beweisen. Toll machen—das heisst ihm: überzeugen. Und Blut gilt ihm als aller Gründe bester.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0010","text":"Eine Wahrheit, die nur in feine Ohren schlüpft, nennt er Lüge und Nichts. Wahrlich, er glaubt nur an Götter, die grossen Lärm in der Welt machen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0011","text":"Voll von feierlichen Possenreissern ist der Markt—und das Volk rühmt sich seiner grossen Männer! das sind ihm die Herrn der Stunde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0012","text":"Aber die Stunde drängt sie: so drängen sie dich. Und auch von dir wollen sie Ja oder Nein. Wehe, du willst zwischen Für und Wider deinen Stuhl setzen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0013","text":"Dieser Unbedingten und Drängenden halber sei ohne Eifersucht, du Liebhaber der Wahrheit! Niemals noch hängte sich die Wahrheit an den Arm eines Unbedingten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0014","text":"Dieser Plötzlichen halber gehe zurück in deine Sicherheit: nur auf dem Markt wird man mit Ja? oder Nein? überfallen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0015","text":"Langsam ist das Erleben allen tiefen Brunnen: lange müssen sie warten, bis sie wissen, was in ihre Tiefe fiel.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0016","text":"Abseits vom Markte und Ruhme begiebt sich alles Grosse: abseits vom Markte und Ruhme wohnten von je die Erfinder neuer Werthe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0017","text":"Fliehe, mein Freund, in deine Einsamkeit: ich sehe dich von giftigen Fliegen zerstochen. Fliehe dorthin, wo rauhe, starke Luft weht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0018","text":"Fliehe in deine Einsamkeit! Du lebtest den Kleinen und Erbärmlichen zu nahe. Fliehe vor ihrer unsichtbaren Rache! Gegen dich sind sie Nichts als Rache.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0019","text":"Hebe nicht mehr den Arm gegen sie! Unzählbar sind sie, und es ist nicht dein Loos, Fliegenwedel zu sein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0020","text":"Unzählbar sind diese Kleinen und Erbärmlichen; und manchem stolzen Baue gereichten schon Regentropfen und Unkraut zum Untergange.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0021","text":"Du bist kein Stein, aber schon wurdest du hohl von vielen Tropfen. Zerbrechen und zerbersten wirst du mir noch von vielen Tropfen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0022","text":"Ermüdet sehe ich dich durch giftige Fliegen, blutig geritzt sehe ich dich an hundert Stellen; und dein Stolz will nicht einmal zürnen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0023","text":"Blut möchten sie von dir in aller Unschuld, Blut begehren ihre blutlosen Seelen —und sie stechen daher in aller Unschuld.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0024","text":"Aber, du Tiefer, du leidest zu tief auch an kleinen Wunden; und ehe du dich noch geheilt hast, kroch dir der gleiche Giftwurm über die Hand.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0025","text":"Zu stolz bist du mir dafür, diese Naschhaften zu tödten. Hüte dich aber, dass es nicht dein Verhängniss werde, all ihr giftiges Unrecht zu tragen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0026","text":"Sie summen um dich auch mit ihrem Lobe: Zudringlichkeit ist ihr Loben. Sie wollen die Nähe deiner Haut und deines Blutes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0027","text":"Sie schmeicheln dir wie einem Gotte oder Teufel; sie winseln vor dir wie vor einem Gotte oder Teufel. Was macht es! Schmeichler sind es und Winsler und nicht mehr.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0028","text":"Auch geben sie sich dir oft als Liebenswürdige. Aber das war immer die Klugheit der Feigen. Ja, die Feigen sind klug!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0029","text":"Sie denken viel über dich mit ihrer engen Seele,—bedenklich bist du ihnen stets! Alles, was viel bedacht wird, wird bedenklich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0030","text":"Sie bestrafen dich für alle deine Tugenden. Sie verzeihen dir von Grund aus nur —deine Fehlgriffe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0031","text":"Weil du milde bist und gerechten Sinnes, sagst du: „unschuldig sind sie an ihrem kleinen Dasein.“ Aber ihre enge Seele denkt: „Schuld ist alles grosse Dasein.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0032","text":"Auch wenn du ihnen milde bist, fühlen sie sich noch von dir verachtet; und sie geben dir deine Wohlthat zurück mit versteckten Wehthaten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0033","text":"Dein wortloser Stolz geht immer wider ihren Geschmack; sie frohlocken, wenn du einmal bescheiden genug bist, eitel zu sein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0034","text":"Das, was wir an einem Menschen erkennen, das entzünden wir an ihm auch. Also hüte dich vor den Kleinen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0035","text":"Vor dir fühlen sie sich klein, und ihre Niedrigkeit glimmt und glüht gegen dich in unsichtbarer Rache.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0036","text":"Merktest du nicht, wie oft sie stumm wurden, wenn du zu ihnen tratest, und wie ihre Kraft von ihnen gieng wie der Rauch von einem erlöschenden Feuer?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0037","text":"Ja, mein Freund, das böse Gewissen bist du deinen Nächsten: denn sie sind deiner unwerth. Also hassen sie dich und möchten gerne an deinem Blute saugen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0038","text":"Deine Nächsten werden immer giftige Fliegen sein; Das, was gross an dir ist,—das selber muss sie giftiger machen und immer fliegenhafter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0039","text":"Fliehe, mein Freund, in deine Einsamkeit und dorthin, wo eine rauhe, starke Luft weht. Nicht ist es dein Loos, Fliegenwedel zu sein.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0040","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"1db06f28725639cc2de68e0a2805cebe3270bfcba20c730ff26778b3bdfe77b4","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181438044890Z_1d0ff119","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Flies of the Marketplace","source_sha256":"1db06f28725639cc2de68e0a2805cebe3270bfcba20c730ff26778b3bdfe77b4","sha256":"abb7be4dc6f2dcdbc1e4586f7f4e49b41ebcbf9a171c6a703815e195cdfdcd28","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:14:38.044929+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0001","text":"Flee, my friend, into your solitude! I see you stunned by the noise of the great men and pricked by the stings of the little ones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0002","text":"Forest and rock know how to be worthily silent with you. Become again like the tree you love, the broad-branched one: silent and listening, it hangs over the sea.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0003","text":"Where solitude ends, there the market-place begins; and where the market-place begins, there too begins the noise of the great actors and the buzzing of poisonous flies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0004","text":"In the world, even the best things are still good for nothing without someone first to stage them: the people call these producers great men.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0005","text":"The people understand little of what is great, that is: of the creating. But they have a sense for all producers and actors of great things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0006","text":"Around the inventors of new values the world revolves:—invisibly it revolves. But around the actors revolve the people and fame: thus runs the course of the world.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0007","text":"The actor has spirit, but little conscience of the spirit. He always believes in that by which he most strongly makes others believe,—makes them believe in himself!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0008","text":"Tomorrow he has a new belief, and the day after tomorrow a newer one. He has quick senses, like the people, and changeable moods.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0009","text":"To overthrow—that means to him: to prove. To make mad—that means to him: to convince. And blood counts for him as the best of all reasons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0010","text":"A truth that slips only into fine ears he calls a lie and nothing. Verily, he believes only in gods that make a great noise in the world!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0011","text":"The market-place is full of solemn buffoons—and the people pride themselves on their great men! To them these are the lords of the hour.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0012","text":"But the hour presses them: thus they press you. And from you, too, they want Yes or No. Woe unto you, if you wish to place your chair between For and Against?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0013","text":"For the sake of these unconditional and importunate ones, be without jealousy, you lover of truth! Never yet has truth hung upon the arm of an unconditional one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0014","text":"For the sake of these sudden ones, go back into your security: only in the market-place are you assailed with Yes? or No?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0015","text":"Slow is the experience of all deep wells: long must they wait before they know what has fallen into their depth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0016","text":"Everything great takes place apart from the market-place and fame: apart from the market-place and fame have the inventors of new values dwelt from time immemorial.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0017","text":"Flee, my friend, into your solitude: I see you pricked by poisonous flies. Flee thither, where rough, strong air blows!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0018","text":"Flee into your solitude! You have lived too near the little and miserable ones. Flee from their invisible revenge! Against you they are nothing but revenge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0019","text":"Raise your arm no more against them! They are innumerable, and it is not your lot to be a fly-swatter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0020","text":"Innumerable are these little and miserable ones; and for many a proud structure, raindrops and weeds have already sufficed to bring about ruin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0021","text":"You are no stone, but already you have been hollowed out by many drops. You will yet break and burst asunder for me from many drops.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0022","text":"I see you wearied by poisonous flies; I see you scratched bloody in a hundred places; and your pride does not even wish to be angry.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0023","text":"In all innocence they would like blood from you; their bloodless souls crave blood—and thus they sting in all innocence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0024","text":"But you deep one, you suffer too deeply even from small wounds; and before you have yet healed yourself, the same poisonous worm crawled over your hand.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0025","text":"You are too proud for me to kill these sweet-tooths. But beware lest it become your doom to bear all their poisonous injustice!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0026","text":"They buzz about you even with their praise: importunity is their praising. They want the proximity of your skin and your blood.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0027","text":"They flatter you as a god or devil; they whine before you as before a god or devil. What does it matter! They are flatterers and whiners and nothing more.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0028","text":"They also often present themselves to you as amiable people. But that was always the cleverness of cowards. Yes, cowards are clever!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0029","text":"They think much about you with their narrow soul,—you are always a matter for thought to them! Everything that is much thought about becomes a matter for thought.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0030","text":"They punish you for all your virtues. They forgive you thoroughly only—your missteps.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0031","text":"Because you are mild and of just disposition, you say: “they are innocent in their little existence.” But their narrow soul thinks: “Every great existence is guilty.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0032","text":"Even when you are mild toward them, they still feel despised by you; and they return your good deed to you with hidden evil deeds.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0033","text":"Your wordless pride is always against their taste; they exult when you are once modest enough to be vain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0034","text":"What we discern in a human being, we also kindle in him. Thus beware of the little ones!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0035","text":"Before you they feel small, and their baseness smoulders and glows against you in invisible revenge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0036","text":"Did you not notice how often they became silent when you came to them, and how their strength departed from them like smoke from an extinguishing fire?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0037","text":"Yes, my friend, you are the bad conscience of your neighbours: for they are unworthy of you. Thus they hate you and would gladly suck at your blood.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0038","text":"Your neighbours will always be poisonous flies; that which is great in you,—that itself must make them more poisonous and ever more fly-like.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0039","text":"Flee, my friend, into your solitude and there where a rough, strong air blows. It is not your lot to be a fly-swatter.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0040","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDFD:P0040"]}],"source_digest":"e07843b6ba415fdbb8e138e1b1909227eef2c30329dea24e614b7d8373a6e751","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von den Fliegen des Marktes"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von den Fliegen des Marktes","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0","title":"On Chastity","order":23,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDK0","text":"Ich liebe den Wald. In den Städten ist schlecht zu leben: da giebt es zu Viele der Brünstigen.\n\nIst es nicht besser, in die Hände eines Mörders zu gerathen, als in die Träume eines brünstigen Weibes?\n\nUnd seht mir doch diese Männer an: ihr Auge sagt es—sie wissen nichts Besseres auf Erden, als bei einem Weibe zu liegen.\n\nSchlamm ist auf dem Grunde ihrer Seele; und wehe, wenn ihr Schlamm gar noch Geist hat!\n\nDass ihr doch wenigstens als Thiere vollkommen wäret! Aber zum Thiere gehört die Unschuld.\n\nRathe ich euch, eure Sinne zu tödten? Ich rathe euch zur Unschuld der Sinne.\n\nRathe ich euch zur Keuschheit? Die Keuschheit ist bei Einigen eine Tugend, aber bei Vielen beinahe ein Laster.\n\nDiese enthalten sich wohl: aber die Hündin Sinnlichkeit blickt mit Neid aus Allem, was sie thun.\n\nNoch in die Höhen ihrer Tugend und bis in den kalten Geist hinein folgt ihnen diess Gethier und sein Unfrieden.\n\nUnd wie artig weiss die Hündin Sinnlichkeit um ein Stück Geist zu betteln, wenn ihr ein Stuck Fleisch versagt wird!\n\nIhr liebt Trauerspiele und Alles, was das Herz zerbricht? Aber ich bin misstrauisch gegen eure Hündin.\n\nIhr habt mir zu grausame Augen und blickt lüstern nach Leidenden. Hat sich nicht nur eure Wollust verkleidet und heisst sich Mitleiden?\n\nUnd auch diess Gleichniss gebe ich euch: nicht Wenige, die ihren Teufel austreiben wollten, fuhren dabei selber in die Säue.\n\nWem die Keuschheit schwer fällt, dem ist sie zu widerrathen: dass sie nicht der Weg zur Hölle werde—das ist zu Schlamm und Brunst der Seele.\n\nRede ich von schmutzigen Dingen? Das ist mir nicht das Schlimmste.\n\nNicht, wenn die Wahrheit schmutzig ist, sondern wenn sie seicht ist, steigt der Erkennende ungern in ihr Wasser.\n\nWahrlich, es giebt Keusche von Grund aus: sie sind milder von Herzen, sie lachen lieber und reichlicher als ihr.\n\nSie lachen auch über die Keuschheit und fragen: „was ist Keuschheit!\n\nIst Keuschheit nicht Thorheit? Aber diese Thorheit kam zu uns und nicht wir zur ihr.\n\nWir boten diesem Gaste Herberge und Herz: nun wohnt er bei uns,—mag er bleiben, wie lange er will!“\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0001","text":"Ich liebe den Wald. In den Städten ist schlecht zu leben: da giebt es zu Viele der Brünstigen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0002","text":"Ist es nicht besser, in die Hände eines Mörders zu gerathen, als in die Träume eines brünstigen Weibes?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0003","text":"Und seht mir doch diese Männer an: ihr Auge sagt es—sie wissen nichts Besseres auf Erden, als bei einem Weibe zu liegen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0004","text":"Schlamm ist auf dem Grunde ihrer Seele; und wehe, wenn ihr Schlamm gar noch Geist hat!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0005","text":"Dass ihr doch wenigstens als Thiere vollkommen wäret! Aber zum Thiere gehört die Unschuld.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0006","text":"Rathe ich euch, eure Sinne zu tödten? Ich rathe euch zur Unschuld der Sinne.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0007","text":"Rathe ich euch zur Keuschheit? Die Keuschheit ist bei Einigen eine Tugend, aber bei Vielen beinahe ein Laster.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0008","text":"Diese enthalten sich wohl: aber die Hündin Sinnlichkeit blickt mit Neid aus Allem, was sie thun.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0009","text":"Noch in die Höhen ihrer Tugend und bis in den kalten Geist hinein folgt ihnen diess Gethier und sein Unfrieden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0010","text":"Und wie artig weiss die Hündin Sinnlichkeit um ein Stück Geist zu betteln, wenn ihr ein Stuck Fleisch versagt wird!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0011","text":"Ihr liebt Trauerspiele und Alles, was das Herz zerbricht? Aber ich bin misstrauisch gegen eure Hündin.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0012","text":"Ihr habt mir zu grausame Augen und blickt lüstern nach Leidenden. Hat sich nicht nur eure Wollust verkleidet und heisst sich Mitleiden?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0013","text":"Und auch diess Gleichniss gebe ich euch: nicht Wenige, die ihren Teufel austreiben wollten, fuhren dabei selber in die Säue.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0014","text":"Wem die Keuschheit schwer fällt, dem ist sie zu widerrathen: dass sie nicht der Weg zur Hölle werde—das ist zu Schlamm und Brunst der Seele.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0015","text":"Rede ich von schmutzigen Dingen? Das ist mir nicht das Schlimmste.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0016","text":"Nicht, wenn die Wahrheit schmutzig ist, sondern wenn sie seicht ist, steigt der Erkennende ungern in ihr Wasser.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0017","text":"Wahrlich, es giebt Keusche von Grund aus: sie sind milder von Herzen, sie lachen lieber und reichlicher als ihr.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0018","text":"Sie lachen auch über die Keuschheit und fragen: „was ist Keuschheit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0019","text":"Ist Keuschheit nicht Thorheit? Aber diese Thorheit kam zu uns und nicht wir zur ihr.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0020","text":"Wir boten diesem Gaste Herberge und Herz: nun wohnt er bei uns,—mag er bleiben, wie lange er will!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0021","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"a53795eb1eb067b68f1a50dd412dc24fbd18737dd978ebdf17c1e02ac1584590","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181446094696Z_ebaf807d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On Chastity","source_sha256":"a53795eb1eb067b68f1a50dd412dc24fbd18737dd978ebdf17c1e02ac1584590","sha256":"1173d9539d8635a60326d1874569716573b23a1fb3580df28b49f3dadc8cbb90","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:14:46.094736+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0001","text":"I love the forest. In the cities it is bad to live: there are too many of the lustful there.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0002","text":"Is it not better to fall into the hands of a murderer than into the dreams of a lustful woman?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0003","text":"And just look at these men for me: their eye says it—they know nothing better on earth than to lie with a woman.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0004","text":"Mud lies at the bottom of their soul; and woe, if their mud should even have spirit!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0005","text":"If only you were at least perfect as animals! But innocence belongs to the animal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0006","text":"Do I counsel you to kill your senses? I counsel you to the innocence of the senses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0007","text":"Do I counsel you to chastity? Chastity is for some a virtue, but for many almost a vice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0008","text":"These do indeed abstain: but the bitch Sensuality looks enviously out of everything they do.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0009","text":"Even into the heights of their virtue and into the cold spirit, this beast and its disquiet follow them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0010","text":"And how charmingly the bitch Sensuality knows how to beg for a piece of spirit when she is denied a piece of flesh!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0011","text":"You love tragedies and everything that breaks the heart? But I distrust your bitch.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0012","text":"You have eyes too cruel for me, and gaze lustfully after sufferers. Has not merely your voluptuousness disguised itself and called itself pity?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0013","text":"And I give you this parable as well: not a few who wished to cast out their devil thereby themselves entered into the swine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0014","text":"He to whom chastity is difficult should be dissuaded from it: lest it become the path to hell—that is, to mud and lust of the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0015","text":"Do I speak of filthy things? That is not the worst thing for me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0016","text":"It is not when truth is filthy, but when it is shallow, that the knower reluctantly descends into its water.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0017","text":"Truly, there are chaste ones from the ground up: they are gentler at heart; they laugh more willingly and more abundantly than you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0018","text":"They also laugh at chastity and ask: “what is chastity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0019","text":"Is chastity not folly? But this folly came to us, and not we to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0020","text":"We offered this guest lodging and heart: now it dwells with us,—let it remain as long as it wishes!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0021","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDK0:P0021"]}],"source_digest":"579da86147022e27b16e9d77da6aa3edf6ec96ade35c8111c596993907dfb713","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von der Keuschheit"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von der Keuschheit","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF","title":"On the Friend","order":24,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VOMF","text":"„Einer ist immer zu viel um mich“—also denkt der Einsiedler. „Immer Einmal Eins—das giebt auf die Dauer Zwei!“\n\nIch und Mich sind immer zu eifrig im Gespräche: wie wäre es auszuhalten, wenn es nicht einen Freund gäbe?\n\nImmer ist für den Einsiedler der Freund der Dritte: der Dritte ist der Kork, der verhindert, dass das Gespräch der Zweie in die Tiefe sinkt.\n\nAch, es giebt zu viele Tiefen für alle Einsiedler. Darum sehnen sie sich so nach einem Freunde und nach seiner Höhe.\n\nUnser Glaube an Andre verräth, worin wir gerne an uns selber glauben möchten. Unsre Sehnsucht nach einem Freunde ist unser Verräther.\n\nUnd oft will man mit der Liebe nur den Neid überspringen. Und oft greift man an und macht sich einen Feind, um zu verbergen, dass man angreifbar ist.\n\n„Sei wenigstens mein Feind!“—so spricht die wahre Ehrfurcht, die nicht um Freundschaft zu bitten wagt.\n\nWill man einen Freund haben, so muss man auch für ihn Krieg führen wollen: und um Krieg zu führen, muss man Feind sein können .\n\nMan soll in seinem Freunde noch den Feind ehren. Kannst du an deinen Freund dicht herantreten, ohne zu ihm überzutreten?\n\nIn seinem Freunde soll man seinen besten Feind haben. Du sollst ihm am nächsten mit dem Herzen sein, wenn du ihm widerstrebst.\n\nDu willst vor deinem Freunde kein Kleid tragen? Es soll deines Freundes Ehre sein, dass du dich ihm giebst, wie du bist? Aber er wünscht dich darum zum Teufel!\n\nWer aus sich kein Hehl macht, empört: so sehr habt ihr Grund, die Nacktheit zu fürchten! Ja, wenn ihr Götter wäret, da dürftet ihr euch eurer Kleider schämen!\n\nDu kannst dich für deinen Freund nicht schön genug putzen: denn du sollst ihm ein Pfeil und eine Sehnsucht nach dem Übermenschen sein.\n\nSahst du deinen Freund schon schlafen,—damit du erfahrest, wie er aussieht? Was ist doch sonst das Gesicht deines Freundes? Es ist dein eignes Gesicht, auf einem rauhen und unvollkommnen Spiegel.\n\nSahst du deinen Freund schon schlafen? Erschrakst du nicht, dass dein Freund so aussieht? Oh, mein Freund, der Mensch ist Etwas, das überwunden werden muss.\n\nIm Errathen und Stillschweigen soll der Freund Meister sein: nicht Alles musst du sehn wollen. Dein Traum soll dir verrathen, was dein Freund im Wachen thut.\n\nEin Errathen sei dein Mitleiden: dass du erst wissest, ob dein Freund Mitleiden wolle. Vielleicht liebt er an dir das ungebrochne Auge und den Blick der Ewigkeit.\n\nDas Mitleiden mit dem Freunde berge sich unter einer harten Schale, an ihm sollst du dir einen Zahn ausbeissen. So wird es seine Feinheit und Süsse haben.\n\nBist du reine Luft und Einsamkeit und Brod und Arznei deinem Freunde? Mancher kann seine eignen Ketten nicht lösen und doch ist er dem Freunde ein Erlöser.\n\nBist du ein Sclave? So kannst du nicht Freund sein. Bist du ein Tyrann? So kannst du nicht Freunde haben.\n\nAllzulange war im Weibe ein Sclave und ein Tyrann versteckt. Desshalb ist das Weib noch nicht der Freundschaft fähig: es kennt nur die Liebe.\n\nIn der Liebe des Weibes ist Ungerechtigkeit und Blindheit gegen Alles, was es nicht liebt. Und auch in der wissenden Liebe des Weibes ist immer noch Überfall und Blitz und Nacht neben dem Lichte.\n\nNoch ist das Weib nicht der Freundschaft fähig: Katzen sind immer noch die Weiber, und Vögel. Oder, besten Falles, Kühe.\n\nNoch ist das Weib nicht der Freundschaft fähig. Aber sagt mir, ihr Männer, wer von euch ist denn fähig der Freundschaft?\n\nOh über eure Armuth, ihr Männer, und euren Geiz der Seele! Wie viel ihr dem Freunde gebt, das will ich noch meinem Feinde geben, und will auch nicht ärmer damit geworden sein.\n\nEs giebt Kameradschaft: möge es Freundschaft geben!\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0001","text":"„Einer ist immer zu viel um mich“—also denkt der Einsiedler. „Immer Einmal Eins—das giebt auf die Dauer Zwei!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0002","text":"Ich und Mich sind immer zu eifrig im Gespräche: wie wäre es auszuhalten, wenn es nicht einen Freund gäbe?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0003","text":"Immer ist für den Einsiedler der Freund der Dritte: der Dritte ist der Kork, der verhindert, dass das Gespräch der Zweie in die Tiefe sinkt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0004","text":"Ach, es giebt zu viele Tiefen für alle Einsiedler. Darum sehnen sie sich so nach einem Freunde und nach seiner Höhe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0005","text":"Unser Glaube an Andre verräth, worin wir gerne an uns selber glauben möchten. Unsre Sehnsucht nach einem Freunde ist unser Verräther.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0006","text":"Und oft will man mit der Liebe nur den Neid überspringen. Und oft greift man an und macht sich einen Feind, um zu verbergen, dass man angreifbar ist.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0007","text":"„Sei wenigstens mein Feind!“—so spricht die wahre Ehrfurcht, die nicht um Freundschaft zu bitten wagt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0008","text":"Will man einen Freund haben, so muss man auch für ihn Krieg führen wollen: und um Krieg zu führen, muss man Feind sein können .","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0009","text":"Man soll in seinem Freunde noch den Feind ehren. Kannst du an deinen Freund dicht herantreten, ohne zu ihm überzutreten?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0010","text":"In seinem Freunde soll man seinen besten Feind haben. Du sollst ihm am nächsten mit dem Herzen sein, wenn du ihm widerstrebst.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0011","text":"Du willst vor deinem Freunde kein Kleid tragen? Es soll deines Freundes Ehre sein, dass du dich ihm giebst, wie du bist? Aber er wünscht dich darum zum Teufel!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0012","text":"Wer aus sich kein Hehl macht, empört: so sehr habt ihr Grund, die Nacktheit zu fürchten! Ja, wenn ihr Götter wäret, da dürftet ihr euch eurer Kleider schämen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0013","text":"Du kannst dich für deinen Freund nicht schön genug putzen: denn du sollst ihm ein Pfeil und eine Sehnsucht nach dem Übermenschen sein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0014","text":"Sahst du deinen Freund schon schlafen,—damit du erfahrest, wie er aussieht? Was ist doch sonst das Gesicht deines Freundes? Es ist dein eignes Gesicht, auf einem rauhen und unvollkommnen Spiegel.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0015","text":"Sahst du deinen Freund schon schlafen? Erschrakst du nicht, dass dein Freund so aussieht? Oh, mein Freund, der Mensch ist Etwas, das überwunden werden muss.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0016","text":"Im Errathen und Stillschweigen soll der Freund Meister sein: nicht Alles musst du sehn wollen. Dein Traum soll dir verrathen, was dein Freund im Wachen thut.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0017","text":"Ein Errathen sei dein Mitleiden: dass du erst wissest, ob dein Freund Mitleiden wolle. Vielleicht liebt er an dir das ungebrochne Auge und den Blick der Ewigkeit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0018","text":"Das Mitleiden mit dem Freunde berge sich unter einer harten Schale, an ihm sollst du dir einen Zahn ausbeissen. So wird es seine Feinheit und Süsse haben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0019","text":"Bist du reine Luft und Einsamkeit und Brod und Arznei deinem Freunde? Mancher kann seine eignen Ketten nicht lösen und doch ist er dem Freunde ein Erlöser.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0020","text":"Bist du ein Sclave? So kannst du nicht Freund sein. Bist du ein Tyrann? So kannst du nicht Freunde haben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0021","text":"Allzulange war im Weibe ein Sclave und ein Tyrann versteckt. Desshalb ist das Weib noch nicht der Freundschaft fähig: es kennt nur die Liebe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0022","text":"In der Liebe des Weibes ist Ungerechtigkeit und Blindheit gegen Alles, was es nicht liebt. Und auch in der wissenden Liebe des Weibes ist immer noch Überfall und Blitz und Nacht neben dem Lichte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0023","text":"Noch ist das Weib nicht der Freundschaft fähig: Katzen sind immer noch die Weiber, und Vögel. Oder, besten Falles, Kühe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0024","text":"Noch ist das Weib nicht der Freundschaft fähig. Aber sagt mir, ihr Männer, wer von euch ist denn fähig der Freundschaft?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0025","text":"Oh über eure Armuth, ihr Männer, und euren Geiz der Seele! Wie viel ihr dem Freunde gebt, das will ich noch meinem Feinde geben, und will auch nicht ärmer damit geworden sein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0026","text":"Es giebt Kameradschaft: möge es Freundschaft geben!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0027","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"e930d3828d3e7df6d8a6b14805d3a16e8eaf485dda734bd474edbea793c2bb8a","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181458934024Z_5d0fcfd7","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Friend","source_sha256":"e930d3828d3e7df6d8a6b14805d3a16e8eaf485dda734bd474edbea793c2bb8a","sha256":"49cfa86268c988595cce49cee9ea59702c09728fd4871d98c7132b56f8b3ee1d","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:14:58.934101+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0001","text":"“One is always too many for me”—thus thinks the hermit. “Always once one—that in the long run makes two!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0002","text":"I and Me are always too eager in conversation: how could it be endured, if there were not a friend?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0003","text":"For the hermit the friend is always the third: the third is the cork that prevents the conversation of the two from sinking into the depths.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0004","text":"Alas, there are too many depths for all hermits. Therefore they so long for a friend and for his height.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0005","text":"Our faith in others betrays wherein we would gladly believe in ourselves. Our longing for a friend is our betrayer.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0006","text":"And often one wishes, with love, only to leap over envy. And often one attacks and makes oneself an enemy, in order to conceal that one is vulnerable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0007","text":"“Be at least my enemy!”—thus speaks true reverence, which does not dare to ask for friendship.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0008","text":"If one wishes to have a friend, one must also be willing to wage war for him: and in order to wage war, one must be able to be an enemy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0009","text":"One should still honour the enemy in one’s friend. Can you draw close to your friend without crossing over to him?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0010","text":"In one’s friend one should have one’s best enemy. You should be closest to him in your heart when you resist him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0011","text":"You wish to wear no clothing before your friend? Is it to be your friend’s honour that you give yourself to him as you are? But for that he wishes you to the devil!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0012","text":"He who conceals nothing about himself provokes: so much reason have you to fear nakedness! Yes, if you were gods, then you might be ashamed of your clothes!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0013","text":"You cannot adorn yourself beautifully enough for your friend: for you should be to him an arrow and a longing for the overman.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0014","text":"Have you ever seen your friend asleep,—so that you might learn what he looks like? What else, indeed, is your friend’s face? It is your own face, upon a rough and imperfect mirror.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0015","text":"Have you ever seen your friend asleep? Were you not frightened that your friend looks thus? Oh, my friend, man is something that must be overcome.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0016","text":"In divining and keeping silent the friend should be a master: you must not wish to see everything. Your dream should betray to you what your friend does while awake.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0017","text":"Let your compassion be a divining: that you may first know whether your friend wishes for compassion. Perhaps he loves in you the unbroken eye and the glance of eternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0018","text":"Compassion for the friend should hide beneath a hard shell; upon it you should break a tooth. Thus it will have its fineness and sweetness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0019","text":"Are you pure air and solitude and bread and medicine to your friend? Many a one cannot loosen his own chains and yet is a redeemer to his friend.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0020","text":"Are you a slave? Then you cannot be a friend. Are you a tyrant? Then you cannot have friends.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0021","text":"For all too long a slave and a tyrant have been concealed in woman. Therefore woman is not yet capable of friendship: she knows only love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0022","text":"In woman’s love there is injustice and blindness towards everything that it does not love. And even in woman’s knowing love there is always still assault and lightning and night beside the light.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0023","text":"Woman is not yet capable of friendship: women are still always cats, and birds. Or, at best, cows.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0024","text":"Woman is not yet capable of friendship. But tell me, you men, which one among you, then, is capable of friendship?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0025","text":"Oh, your poverty, you men, and your avarice of soul! What you give to a friend, that I would still give to my enemy, and would not thereby have become poorer either.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0026","text":"There is comradeship: may there be friendship!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0027","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMF:P0027"]}],"source_digest":"72e00071364c305378eb7f0333546733bbecc41b4c923a69c474e897f76a3ebf","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Vom Freunde"}],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"Vom Freunde","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE","title":"On the Thousand and One Goals","order":25,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VTUE","text":"VieIe Länder sah Zarathustra und viele Völker: so entdeckte er vieler Völker Gutes und Böses. Keine grössere Macht fand Zarathustra auf Erden, als Gut und Böse.\n\nLeben könnte kein Volk, das nicht erst schätzte; will es sich aber erhalten, so darf es nicht schätzen, wie der Nachbar schätzt.\n\nVieles, das diesem Volke gut hiess, hiess einem andern Hohn und Schmach: also fand ich’s. Vieles fand ich hier böse genannt und dort mit purpurnen Ehren geputzt.\n\nNie verstand ein Nachbar den andern: stets verwunderte sich seine Seele ob des Nachbarn Wahn und Bosheit.\n\nEine Tafel der Güter hängt über jedem Volke. Siehe, es ist seiner Überwindungen Tafel; siehe, es ist die Stimme seines Willens zur Macht.\n\nLöblich ist, was ihm schwer gilt; was unerlässlich und schwer, heisst gut, und was aus der höchsten Noth noch befreit, das Seltene, Schwerste,—das preist es heilig.\n\nWas da macht, dass es herrscht und siegt und glänzt, seinem Nachbarn zu Grauen und Neide: das gilt ihm das Hohe, das Erste, das Messende, der Sinn aller Dinge.\n\nWahrlich, mein Bruder, erkanntest du erst eines Volkes Noth und Land und Himmel und Nachbar: so erräthst du wohl das Gesetz seiner Überwindungen und warum es auf dieser Leiter zu seiner Hoffnung steigt.\n\n„Immer sollst du der Erste sein und den Andern vorragen: Niemanden soll deine eifersüchtige Seele lieben, es sei denn den Freund“—diess machte einem Griechen die Seele zittern: dabei gieng er seinen Pfad der Grösse.\n\n„Wahrheit reden und gut mit Bogen und Pfeil verkehren“ —so dünkte es jenem Volke zugleich lieb und schwer, aus dem mein Name kommt—der Name, welcher mir zugleich lieb und schwer ist.\n\n„Vater und Mutter ehren und bis in die Wurzel der Seele hinein ihnen zu Willen sein“ : diese Tafel der Überwindung hängte ein andres Volk über sich auf und wurde mächtig und ewig damit.\n\n„Treue üben und um der Treue Willen Ehre und Blut auch an böse und fährliche Sachen setzen“ : also sich lehrend bezwang sich ein anderes Volk, und also sich bezwingend wurde es schwanger und schwer von grossen Hoffnungen.\n\nWahrlich, die Menschen gaben sich alles ihr Gutes und Böses. Wahrlich, sie nahmen es nicht, sie fanden es nicht, nicht fiel es ihnen als Stimme vom Himmel.\n\nWerthe legte erst der Mensch in die Dinge, sich zu erhalten,—er schuf erst den Dingen Sinn, einen Menschen-Sinn! Darum nennt er sich „Mensch“, das ist: der Schätzende.\n\nSchätzen ist Schaffen: hört es, ihr Schaffenden! Schätzen selber ist aller geschätzten Dinge Schatz und Kleinod.\n\nDurch das Schätzen erst giebt es Werth: und ohne das Schätzen wäre die Nuss des Daseins hohl. Hört es, ihr Schaffenden!\n\nWandel der Werthe,—das ist Wandel der Schaffenden. Immer vernichtet, wer ein Schöpfer sein muss.\n\nSchaffende waren erst Völker und spät erst Einzelne; wahrlich, der Einzelne selber ist noch die jüngste Schöpfung.\n\nVölker hängten sich einst eine Tafel des Guten über sich. Liebe, die herrschen will, und Liebe, die gehorchen will, erschufen sich zusammen solche Tafeln.\n\nÄlter ist an der Heerde die Lust, als die Lust am Ich: und so lange das gute Gewissen Heerde heisst, sagt nur das schlechte Gewissen: Ich.\n\nWahrlich, das schlaue Ich, das lieblose, das seinen Nutzen im Nutzen Vieler will: das ist nicht der Heerde Ursprung, sondern ihr Untergang.\n\nLiebende waren es stets und Schaffende, die schufen Gut und Böse. Feuer der Liebe glüht in aller Tugenden Namen und Feuer des Zorns.\n\nViele Länder sah Zarathustra und viele Völker: keine grössere Macht fand Zarathustra auf Erden, als die Werke der Liebenden: „gut“ und „böse“ ist ihr Name.\n\nWahrlich, ein Ungethüm ist die Macht dieses Lobens und Tadelns. Sagt, wer bezwingt es mir, ihr Brüder? Sagt, wer wirft diesem Thier die Fessel über die tausend Nacken?\n\nTausend Ziele gab es bisher, denn tausend Völker gab es. Nur die Fessel der tausend Nacken fehlt noch, es fehlt das Eine Ziel. Noch hat die Menschheit kein Ziel.\n\nAber sagt mir doch, meine Brüder: wenn der Menschheit das Ziel noch fehlt, fehlt da nicht auch—sie selber noch?—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0001","text":"VieIe Länder sah Zarathustra und viele Völker: so entdeckte er vieler Völker Gutes und Böses. Keine grössere Macht fand Zarathustra auf Erden, als Gut und Böse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0002","text":"Leben könnte kein Volk, das nicht erst schätzte; will es sich aber erhalten, so darf es nicht schätzen, wie der Nachbar schätzt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0003","text":"Vieles, das diesem Volke gut hiess, hiess einem andern Hohn und Schmach: also fand ich’s. Vieles fand ich hier böse genannt und dort mit purpurnen Ehren geputzt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0004","text":"Nie verstand ein Nachbar den andern: stets verwunderte sich seine Seele ob des Nachbarn Wahn und Bosheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0005","text":"Eine Tafel der Güter hängt über jedem Volke. Siehe, es ist seiner Überwindungen Tafel; siehe, es ist die Stimme seines Willens zur Macht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0006","text":"Löblich ist, was ihm schwer gilt; was unerlässlich und schwer, heisst gut, und was aus der höchsten Noth noch befreit, das Seltene, Schwerste,—das preist es heilig.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0007","text":"Was da macht, dass es herrscht und siegt und glänzt, seinem Nachbarn zu Grauen und Neide: das gilt ihm das Hohe, das Erste, das Messende, der Sinn aller Dinge.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0008","text":"Wahrlich, mein Bruder, erkanntest du erst eines Volkes Noth und Land und Himmel und Nachbar: so erräthst du wohl das Gesetz seiner Überwindungen und warum es auf dieser Leiter zu seiner Hoffnung steigt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0009","text":"„Immer sollst du der Erste sein und den Andern vorragen: Niemanden soll deine eifersüchtige Seele lieben, es sei denn den Freund“—diess machte einem Griechen die Seele zittern: dabei gieng er seinen Pfad der Grösse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0010","text":"„Wahrheit reden und gut mit Bogen und Pfeil verkehren“ —so dünkte es jenem Volke zugleich lieb und schwer, aus dem mein Name kommt—der Name, welcher mir zugleich lieb und schwer ist.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0011","text":"„Vater und Mutter ehren und bis in die Wurzel der Seele hinein ihnen zu Willen sein“ : diese Tafel der Überwindung hängte ein andres Volk über sich auf und wurde mächtig und ewig damit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0012","text":"„Treue üben und um der Treue Willen Ehre und Blut auch an böse und fährliche Sachen setzen“ : also sich lehrend bezwang sich ein anderes Volk, und also sich bezwingend wurde es schwanger und schwer von grossen Hoffnungen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0013","text":"Wahrlich, die Menschen gaben sich alles ihr Gutes und Böses. Wahrlich, sie nahmen es nicht, sie fanden es nicht, nicht fiel es ihnen als Stimme vom Himmel.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0014","text":"Werthe legte erst der Mensch in die Dinge, sich zu erhalten,—er schuf erst den Dingen Sinn, einen Menschen-Sinn! Darum nennt er sich „Mensch“, das ist: der Schätzende.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0015","text":"Schätzen ist Schaffen: hört es, ihr Schaffenden! Schätzen selber ist aller geschätzten Dinge Schatz und Kleinod.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0016","text":"Durch das Schätzen erst giebt es Werth: und ohne das Schätzen wäre die Nuss des Daseins hohl. Hört es, ihr Schaffenden!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0017","text":"Wandel der Werthe,—das ist Wandel der Schaffenden. Immer vernichtet, wer ein Schöpfer sein muss.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0018","text":"Schaffende waren erst Völker und spät erst Einzelne; wahrlich, der Einzelne selber ist noch die jüngste Schöpfung.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0019","text":"Völker hängten sich einst eine Tafel des Guten über sich. Liebe, die herrschen will, und Liebe, die gehorchen will, erschufen sich zusammen solche Tafeln.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0020","text":"Älter ist an der Heerde die Lust, als die Lust am Ich: und so lange das gute Gewissen Heerde heisst, sagt nur das schlechte Gewissen: Ich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0021","text":"Wahrlich, das schlaue Ich, das lieblose, das seinen Nutzen im Nutzen Vieler will: das ist nicht der Heerde Ursprung, sondern ihr Untergang.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0022","text":"Liebende waren es stets und Schaffende, die schufen Gut und Böse. Feuer der Liebe glüht in aller Tugenden Namen und Feuer des Zorns.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0023","text":"Viele Länder sah Zarathustra und viele Völker: keine grössere Macht fand Zarathustra auf Erden, als die Werke der Liebenden: „gut“ und „böse“ ist ihr Name.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0024","text":"Wahrlich, ein Ungethüm ist die Macht dieses Lobens und Tadelns. Sagt, wer bezwingt es mir, ihr Brüder? Sagt, wer wirft diesem Thier die Fessel über die tausend Nacken?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0025","text":"Tausend Ziele gab es bisher, denn tausend Völker gab es. Nur die Fessel der tausend Nacken fehlt noch, es fehlt das Eine Ziel. Noch hat die Menschheit kein Ziel.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0026","text":"Aber sagt mir doch, meine Brüder: wenn der Menschheit das Ziel noch fehlt, fehlt da nicht auch—sie selber noch?—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0027","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"d83b26e5e7306108b4f9d6f82aee434d0b4c6c5b1eb690c423156be11285a9e4","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181512663892Z_a33870d3","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Thousand and One Goals","source_sha256":"d83b26e5e7306108b4f9d6f82aee434d0b4c6c5b1eb690c423156be11285a9e4","sha256":"8bd56736e0f365784e6430a37c02b03ff29337fc14ad35bd406821420c953562","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:15:12.663935+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0001","text":"Zarathustra saw many lands and many peoples: thus he discovered the good and evil of many peoples. Zarathustra found no greater power on earth than good and evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0002","text":"No people could live that did not first evaluate; but if it would preserve itself, it must not evaluate as its neighbour evaluates.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0003","text":"Much that was called good by this people was called mockery and disgrace by another: thus I found it. Much I found called evil here and decked out there with purple honours.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0004","text":"Never did one neighbour understand another: its soul was ever astonished at the neighbour's delusion and malice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0005","text":"A tablet of goods hangs over every people. Behold, it is the tablet of its overcomings; behold, it is the voice of its will to power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0006","text":"Praiseworthy is what counts as difficult to it; what is indispensable and difficult is called good, and what still liberates from the highest distress—that which is rarest and most difficult—it praises as holy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0007","text":"That which makes it rule and conquer and shine, to the horror and envy of its neighbour: that counts for it as the lofty, the first, the measuring, the meaning of all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0008","text":"Verily, my brother, if you have first recognized a people's distress and land and sky and neighbour, then you may well guess the law of its overcomings and why it climbs upon this ladder towards its hope.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0009","text":"“You shall always be the first and excel the others: your jealous soul shall love no one, unless it be the friend”—this made a Greek soul tremble: thereby he went his path of greatness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0010","text":"“To speak truth and to handle bow and arrow well”—thus did it seem at once dear and difficult to that people from which my name comes—the name which is at once dear and difficult to me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0011","text":"“Honour father and mother and, down to the root of the soul, be obedient to them”: this tablet of overcoming another people hung over itself, and thereby became mighty and eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0012","text":"“Practise fidelity and, for the sake of fidelity, stake honour and blood even upon evil and dangerous things”: thus teaching itself, another people overcame itself, and thus overcoming itself, it became pregnant and heavy with great hopes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0013","text":"Verily, human beings gave themselves all their good and evil. Verily, they did not take it, they did not find it, it did not fall to them as a voice from heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0014","text":"Only human beings placed values in things, in order to preserve themselves,—only they created a meaning for things, a human meaning! Therefore they call themselves “human,” that is: the evaluator.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0015","text":"Evaluating is creating: hear this, you creators! Evaluating itself is the treasure and jewel of all evaluated things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0016","text":"Only through evaluating is there value: and without evaluating the nut of existence would be hollow. Hear this, you creators!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0017","text":"Change of values—that is change of creators. Whoever must be a creator always destroys.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0018","text":"Peoples were creators first, and individuals only late; verily, the individual himself is still the youngest creation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0019","text":"Peoples once hung a tablet of the good above themselves. Love that would rule, and love that would obey, created such tablets for themselves together.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0020","text":"The pleasure in the herd is older than the pleasure in the I: and as long as the good conscience is called herd, only the bad conscience says: I.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0021","text":"Verily, the cunning I, the unloving one, that wants its own advantage in the advantage of many: that is not the origin of the herd, but its downfall.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0022","text":"It was always lovers and creators who created good and evil. The fire of love glows in the names of all virtues, and the fire of wrath.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0023","text":"Zarathustra saw many lands and many peoples: Zarathustra found no greater power on earth than the works of lovers: “good” and “evil” are their name.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0024","text":"Verily, the power of this praising and blaming is a monster. Tell me, who will overcome it, you brothers? Tell me, who will cast the fetter upon this beast's thousand necks?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0025","text":"A thousand goals have hitherto existed, for a thousand peoples have existed. Only the fetter for the thousand necks is still lacking; the one goal is lacking. Humanity still has no goal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0026","text":"But tell me, my brothers: if humanity still lacks the goal, does it not also lack—itself?—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0027","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VTUE:P0027"]}],"source_digest":"0353e7d298417f20c87494ae7580460dec14c36760ab1129b1644d3ebccc1e92","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von tausend und Einem Ziele"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von tausend und Einem Ziele","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC","title":"On Love of One’s Neighbour","order":26,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDNC","text":"Ihr drängt euch um den Nächsten und habt schöne Worte dafür. Aber ich sage euch: eure Nächstenliebe ist eure schlechte Liebe zu euch selber.\n\nIhr flüchtet zum Nächsten vor euch selber und möchtet euch daraus eine Tugend machen: aber ich durchschaue euer „Selbstloses“.\n\nDas Du ist älter als das Ich; das Du ist heilig gesprochen, aber noch nicht das Ich: so drängt sich der Mensch hin zum Nächsten.\n\nRathe ich euch zur Nächstenliebe? Lieber noch rathe ich euch zur Nächsten-Flucht und zur Fernsten-Liebe!\n\nHöher als die Liebe zum Nächsten ist die Liebe zum Fernsten und Künftigen; höher noch als die Liebe zu Menschen ist die Liebe zu Sachen und Gespenstern.\n\nDiess Gespenst, das vor dir herläuft, mein Bruder, ist schöner als du; warum giebst du ihm nicht dein Fleisch und deine Knochen? Aber du fürchtest dich und läufst zu deinem Nächsten.\n\nIhr haltet es mit euch selber nicht aus und liebt euch nicht genug: nun wollt ihr den Nächsten zur Liebe verführen und euch mit seinem Irrthum vergolden.\n\nIch wollte, ihr hieltet es nicht aus mit allerlei Nächsten und deren Nachbarn; so müsstet ihr aus euch selber euren Freund und sein überwallendes Herz schaffen.\n\nIhr ladet euch einen Zeugen ein, wenn ihr von euch gut reden wollt; und wenn ihr ihn verführt habt, gut von euch zu denken, denkt ihr selber gut von euch.\n\nNicht nur Der lügt, welcher wider sein Wissen redet, sondern erst recht Der, welcher wider sein Nichtwissen redet. Und so redet ihr von euch im Verkehre und belügt mit euch den Nachbar.\n\nAlso spricht der Narr: „der Umgang mit Menschen verdirbt den Charakter, sonderlich wenn man keinen hat.“\n\nDer Eine geht zum Nächsten, weil er sich sucht, und der Andre, weil er sich verlieren möchte. Eure schlechte Liebe zu euch selber macht euch aus der Einsamkeit ein Gefängniss.\n\nDie Ferneren sind es, welche eure Liebe zum Nächsten bezahlen; und schon wenn ihr zu fünfen mit einander seid, muss immer ein sechster sterben.\n\nIch liebe auch eure Feste nicht: zu viel Schauspieler fand ich dabei, und auch die Zuschauer gebärdeten sich oft gleich Schauspielern.\n\nNicht den Nächsten lehre ich euch, sondern den Freund. Der Freund sei euch das Fest der Erde und ein Vorgefühl des Übermenschen.\n\nIch lehre euch den Freund und sein übervolles Herz. Aber man muss verstehn, ein Schwamm zu sein, wenn man von übervollen Herzen geliebt sein will.\n\nIch lehre euch den Freund, in dem die Welt fertig dasteht, eine Schale des Guten,—den schaffenden Freund, der immer eine fertige Welt zu verschenken hat.\n\nUnd wie ihm die Welt auseinander rollte, so rollt sie ihm wieder in Ringen zusammen, als das Werden des Guten durch das Böse, als das Werden der Zwecke aus dem Zufalle.\n\nDie Zukunft und das Fernste sei dir die Ursache deines Heute: in deinem Freunde sollst du den Übermenschen als deine Ursache lieben.\n\nMeine Brüder, zur Nächstenliebe rathe ich euch nicht: ich rathe euch zur Fernsten-Liebe.\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0001","text":"Ihr drängt euch um den Nächsten und habt schöne Worte dafür. Aber ich sage euch: eure Nächstenliebe ist eure schlechte Liebe zu euch selber.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0002","text":"Ihr flüchtet zum Nächsten vor euch selber und möchtet euch daraus eine Tugend machen: aber ich durchschaue euer „Selbstloses“.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0003","text":"Das Du ist älter als das Ich; das Du ist heilig gesprochen, aber noch nicht das Ich: so drängt sich der Mensch hin zum Nächsten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0004","text":"Rathe ich euch zur Nächstenliebe? Lieber noch rathe ich euch zur Nächsten-Flucht und zur Fernsten-Liebe!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0005","text":"Höher als die Liebe zum Nächsten ist die Liebe zum Fernsten und Künftigen; höher noch als die Liebe zu Menschen ist die Liebe zu Sachen und Gespenstern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0006","text":"Diess Gespenst, das vor dir herläuft, mein Bruder, ist schöner als du; warum giebst du ihm nicht dein Fleisch und deine Knochen? Aber du fürchtest dich und läufst zu deinem Nächsten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0007","text":"Ihr haltet es mit euch selber nicht aus und liebt euch nicht genug: nun wollt ihr den Nächsten zur Liebe verführen und euch mit seinem Irrthum vergolden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0008","text":"Ich wollte, ihr hieltet es nicht aus mit allerlei Nächsten und deren Nachbarn; so müsstet ihr aus euch selber euren Freund und sein überwallendes Herz schaffen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0009","text":"Ihr ladet euch einen Zeugen ein, wenn ihr von euch gut reden wollt; und wenn ihr ihn verführt habt, gut von euch zu denken, denkt ihr selber gut von euch.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0010","text":"Nicht nur Der lügt, welcher wider sein Wissen redet, sondern erst recht Der, welcher wider sein Nichtwissen redet. Und so redet ihr von euch im Verkehre und belügt mit euch den Nachbar.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0011","text":"Also spricht der Narr: „der Umgang mit Menschen verdirbt den Charakter, sonderlich wenn man keinen hat.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0012","text":"Der Eine geht zum Nächsten, weil er sich sucht, und der Andre, weil er sich verlieren möchte. Eure schlechte Liebe zu euch selber macht euch aus der Einsamkeit ein Gefängniss.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0013","text":"Die Ferneren sind es, welche eure Liebe zum Nächsten bezahlen; und schon wenn ihr zu fünfen mit einander seid, muss immer ein sechster sterben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0014","text":"Ich liebe auch eure Feste nicht: zu viel Schauspieler fand ich dabei, und auch die Zuschauer gebärdeten sich oft gleich Schauspielern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0015","text":"Nicht den Nächsten lehre ich euch, sondern den Freund. Der Freund sei euch das Fest der Erde und ein Vorgefühl des Übermenschen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0016","text":"Ich lehre euch den Freund und sein übervolles Herz. Aber man muss verstehn, ein Schwamm zu sein, wenn man von übervollen Herzen geliebt sein will.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0017","text":"Ich lehre euch den Freund, in dem die Welt fertig dasteht, eine Schale des Guten,—den schaffenden Freund, der immer eine fertige Welt zu verschenken hat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0018","text":"Und wie ihm die Welt auseinander rollte, so rollt sie ihm wieder in Ringen zusammen, als das Werden des Guten durch das Böse, als das Werden der Zwecke aus dem Zufalle.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0019","text":"Die Zukunft und das Fernste sei dir die Ursache deines Heute: in deinem Freunde sollst du den Übermenschen als deine Ursache lieben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0020","text":"Meine Brüder, zur Nächstenliebe rathe ich euch nicht: ich rathe euch zur Fernsten-Liebe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0021","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"b896c5274e7fc76516b6bff3a854153dadcd73ceb4dd81c792afeaeffc75e2ad","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181520931963Z_b02b4aa3","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On Love of One’s Neighbour","source_sha256":"b896c5274e7fc76516b6bff3a854153dadcd73ceb4dd81c792afeaeffc75e2ad","sha256":"249497d47f9fde40e05498b9c96380dc8c22e4bc63aa4ba2d17b9749fc0aeb55","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:15:20.931993+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0001","text":"You crowd around your neighbor and have fine words for it. But I say to you: your love of the neighbor is your bad love of yourselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0002","text":"You flee to your neighbor from yourselves and would like to make a virtue of it: but I see through your “selfless ones.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0003","text":"The thou is older than the I; the thou has been pronounced holy, but not yet the I: thus man crowds toward his neighbor.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0004","text":"Do I counsel you to love of the neighbor? Rather do I counsel you to flee the neighbor and to love the most distant!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0005","text":"Higher than love of the neighbor is love of the most distant and the future; higher still than love of human beings is love of things and phantoms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0006","text":"This phantom that runs ahead of you, my brother, is more beautiful than you; why do you not give it your flesh and your bones? But you are afraid and run to your neighbor.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0007","text":"You cannot endure yourselves and do not love yourselves enough: now you want to seduce your neighbor into love and gild yourselves with his error.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0008","text":"I wish you could not endure all kinds of neighbors and their neighbors; then you would have to create out of yourselves your friend and his overflowing heart.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0009","text":"You invite yourselves a witness when you want to speak well of yourselves; and when you have seduced him into thinking well of you, you yourselves think well of yourselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0010","text":"Not only does he lie who speaks against his knowledge, but still more he who speaks against his non-knowledge. And thus you speak of yourselves in intercourse and lie to your neighbor with yourselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0011","text":"Thus speaks the fool: “intercourse with human beings corrupts character, especially when one has none.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0012","text":"One goes to his neighbor because he seeks himself, and another because he would like to lose himself. Your bad love of yourselves turns solitude into a prison for you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0013","text":"It is those farther away who pay for your love of the neighbor; and as soon as five of you are together, a sixth must always die.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0014","text":"Nor do I love your festivals: I found too many actors at them, and the spectators too often behaved like actors.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0015","text":"I do not teach you the neighbor, but the friend. Let the friend be for you the festival of the earth and a foretaste of the overman.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0016","text":"I teach you the friend and his overfull heart. But one must understand how to be a sponge if one wishes to be loved by overfull hearts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0017","text":"I teach you the friend in whom the world stands complete, a vessel of the good,—the creating friend, who always has a completed world to give away.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0018","text":"And as the world rolled apart for him, so it rolls together again for him in rings, as the becoming of the good through evil, as the becoming of ends out of chance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0019","text":"Let the future and the most distant be the cause of your today: in your friend you shall love the overman as your cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0020","text":"My brothers, I do not counsel you to love of the neighbor: I counsel you to love of the most distant.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0021","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDNC:P0021"]}],"source_digest":"fb7697deb2e5810b109d1386c89de0bfde30ffe04a4f217e921f191cb29780e0","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von der Nächstenliebe"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von der Nächstenliebe","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS","title":"On the Way of the Creator","order":27,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VWDS","text":"Willst du, mein Bruder, in die Vereinsamung gehen? Willst du den Weg zu dir selber suchen? Zaudere noch ein Wenig und höre mich.\n\n„Wer sucht, der geht leicht selber verloren. Alle Vereinsamung ist Schuld“: also spricht die Heerde. Und du gehörtest lange zur Heerde.\n\nDie Stimme der Heerde wird auch in dir noch tönen. Und wenn du sagen wirst „ich habe nicht mehr Ein Gewissen mit euch“, so wird es eine Klage und ein Schmerz sein.\n\nSiehe, diesen Schmerz selber gebar noch das Eine Gewissen: und dieses Gewissens letzter Schimmer glüht noch auf deiner Trübsal.\n\nAber du willst den Weg deiner Trübsal gehen, welches ist der Weg zu dir selber? So zeige mir dein Recht und deine Kraft dazu!\n\nBist du eine neue Kraft und ein neues Recht? Eine erste Bewegung? Ein aus sich rollendes Rad? Kannst du auch Sterne zwingen, dass sie um dich sich drehen?\n\nAch, es giebt so viel Lüsternheit nach Höhe! Es giebt so viel Krämpfe der Ehrgeizigen! Zeige mir, dass du keiner der Lüsternen und Ehrgeizigen bist!\n\nAch, es giebt so viel grosse Gedanken, die thun nicht mehr als ein Blasebalg: sie blasen auf und machen leerer.\n\nFrei nennst du dich? Deinen herrschenden Gedanken will ich hören und nicht, dass du einem Joche entronnen bist.\n\nBist du ein Solcher, der einem Joche entrinnen durfte ? Es giebt Manchen, der seinen letzten Werth wegwarf, als er seine Dienstbarkeit wegwarf.\n\nFrei wovon? Was schiert das Zarathustra! Hell aber soll mir dein Auge künden: frei wozu ?\n\nKannst du dir selber dein Böses und dein Gutes geben und deinen Willen über dich aufhängen wie ein Gesetz? Kannst du dir selber Richter sein und Rächer deines Gesetzes?\n\nFurchtbar ist das Alleinsein mit dem Richter und Rächer des eignen Gesetzes. Also wird ein Stern hinausgeworfen in den öden Raum und in den eisigen Athem des Alleinseins.\n\nHeute noch leidest du an den Vielen, du Einer: heute noch hast du deinen Muth ganz und deine Hoffnungen.\n\nAber einst wird dich die Einsamkeit müde machen, einst wird dein Stolz sich krümmen und dein Muth knirschen. Schreien wirst du einst „ich bin allein!“\n\nEinst wirst du dein Hohes nicht mehr sehn und dein Niedriges allzunahe; dein Erhabnes selbst wird dich fürchten machen wie ein Gespenst. Schreien wirst du einst: „Alles ist falsch!“\n\nEs giebt Gefühle, die den Einsamen tödten wollen; gelingt es ihnen nicht, nun, so müssen sie selber sterben! Aber vermagst du das, Mörder zu sein?\n\nKennst du, mein Bruder, schon das Wort „Verachtung“? Und die Qual deiner Gerechtigkeit, Solchen gerecht zu sein, die dich verachten?\n\nDu zwingst Viele, über dich umzulernen; das rechnen sie dir hart an. Du kamst ihnen nahe und giengst doch vorüber: das verzeihen sie dir niemals.\n\nDu gehst über sie hinaus: aber je höher du steigst, um so kleiner sieht dich das Auge des Neides. Am meisten aber wird der Fliegende gehasst.\n\n„Wie wolltet ihr gegen mich gerecht sein!—musst du sprechen—ich erwähle mir eure Ungerechtigkeit als den mir zugemessnen Theil.“\n\nUngerechtigkeit und Schmutz werfen sie nach dem Einsamen: aber, mein Bruder, wenn du ein Stern sein willst, so musst du ihnen desshalb nicht weniger leuchten!\n\nUnd hüte dich vor den Guten und Gerechten! Sie kreuzigen gerne Die, welche sich ihre eigne Tugend erfinden,—sie hassen den Einsamen.\n\nHüte dich auch vor der heiligen Einfalt! Alles ist ihr unheilig, was nicht einfältig ist; sie spielt auch gerne mit dem Feuer—der Scheiterhaufen.\n\nUnd hüte dich auch vor den Anfällen deiner Liebe! Zu schnell streckt der Einsame Dem die Hand entgegen, der ihm begegnet.\n\nManchem Menschen darfst du nicht die Hand geben, sondern nur die Tatze: und ich will, dass deine Tatze auch Krallen habe.\n\nAber der schlimmste Feind, dem du begegnen kannst, wirst du immer dir selber sein; du selber lauerst dir auf in Höhlen und Wäldern.\n\nEinsamer, du gehst den Weg zu dir selber! Und an dir selber fuhrt dein Weg vorbei und an deinen sieben Teufeln!\n\nKetzer wirst du dir selber sein und Hexe und Wahrsager und Narr und Zweifler und Unheiliger und Bösewicht.\n\nVerbrennen musst du dich wollen in deiner eignen Flamme: wie wolltest du neu werden, wenn du nicht erst Asche geworden bist!\n\nEinsamer, du gehst den Weg des Schaffenden: einen Gott willst du dir schaffen aus deinen sieben Teufeln!\n\nEinsamer, du gehst den Weg des Liebenden: dich selbst liebst du und desshalb verachtest du dich, wie nur Liebende verachten.\n\nSchaffen will der Liebende, weil er verachtet! Was weiss Der von Liebe, der nicht gerade verachten musste, was er liebte!\n\nMit deiner Liebe gehe in deine Vereinsamung und mit deinem Schaffen, mein Bruder; und spät erst wird die Gerechtigkeit dir nachhinken.\n\nMit meinen Thränen gehe in deine Vereinsamung, mein Bruder. Ich liebe Den, der über sich selber hinaus schaffen will und so zu Grunde geht.—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0001","text":"Willst du, mein Bruder, in die Vereinsamung gehen? Willst du den Weg zu dir selber suchen? Zaudere noch ein Wenig und höre mich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0002","text":"„Wer sucht, der geht leicht selber verloren. Alle Vereinsamung ist Schuld“: also spricht die Heerde. Und du gehörtest lange zur Heerde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0003","text":"Die Stimme der Heerde wird auch in dir noch tönen. Und wenn du sagen wirst „ich habe nicht mehr Ein Gewissen mit euch“, so wird es eine Klage und ein Schmerz sein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0004","text":"Siehe, diesen Schmerz selber gebar noch das Eine Gewissen: und dieses Gewissens letzter Schimmer glüht noch auf deiner Trübsal.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0005","text":"Aber du willst den Weg deiner Trübsal gehen, welches ist der Weg zu dir selber? So zeige mir dein Recht und deine Kraft dazu!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0006","text":"Bist du eine neue Kraft und ein neues Recht? Eine erste Bewegung? Ein aus sich rollendes Rad? Kannst du auch Sterne zwingen, dass sie um dich sich drehen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0007","text":"Ach, es giebt so viel Lüsternheit nach Höhe! Es giebt so viel Krämpfe der Ehrgeizigen! Zeige mir, dass du keiner der Lüsternen und Ehrgeizigen bist!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0008","text":"Ach, es giebt so viel grosse Gedanken, die thun nicht mehr als ein Blasebalg: sie blasen auf und machen leerer.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0009","text":"Frei nennst du dich? Deinen herrschenden Gedanken will ich hören und nicht, dass du einem Joche entronnen bist.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0010","text":"Bist du ein Solcher, der einem Joche entrinnen durfte ? Es giebt Manchen, der seinen letzten Werth wegwarf, als er seine Dienstbarkeit wegwarf.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0011","text":"Frei wovon? Was schiert das Zarathustra! Hell aber soll mir dein Auge künden: frei wozu ?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0012","text":"Kannst du dir selber dein Böses und dein Gutes geben und deinen Willen über dich aufhängen wie ein Gesetz? Kannst du dir selber Richter sein und Rächer deines Gesetzes?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0013","text":"Furchtbar ist das Alleinsein mit dem Richter und Rächer des eignen Gesetzes. Also wird ein Stern hinausgeworfen in den öden Raum und in den eisigen Athem des Alleinseins.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0014","text":"Heute noch leidest du an den Vielen, du Einer: heute noch hast du deinen Muth ganz und deine Hoffnungen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0015","text":"Aber einst wird dich die Einsamkeit müde machen, einst wird dein Stolz sich krümmen und dein Muth knirschen. Schreien wirst du einst „ich bin allein!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0016","text":"Einst wirst du dein Hohes nicht mehr sehn und dein Niedriges allzunahe; dein Erhabnes selbst wird dich fürchten machen wie ein Gespenst. Schreien wirst du einst: „Alles ist falsch!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0017","text":"Es giebt Gefühle, die den Einsamen tödten wollen; gelingt es ihnen nicht, nun, so müssen sie selber sterben! Aber vermagst du das, Mörder zu sein?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0018","text":"Kennst du, mein Bruder, schon das Wort „Verachtung“? Und die Qual deiner Gerechtigkeit, Solchen gerecht zu sein, die dich verachten?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0019","text":"Du zwingst Viele, über dich umzulernen; das rechnen sie dir hart an. Du kamst ihnen nahe und giengst doch vorüber: das verzeihen sie dir niemals.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0020","text":"Du gehst über sie hinaus: aber je höher du steigst, um so kleiner sieht dich das Auge des Neides. Am meisten aber wird der Fliegende gehasst.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0021","text":"„Wie wolltet ihr gegen mich gerecht sein!—musst du sprechen—ich erwähle mir eure Ungerechtigkeit als den mir zugemessnen Theil.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0022","text":"Ungerechtigkeit und Schmutz werfen sie nach dem Einsamen: aber, mein Bruder, wenn du ein Stern sein willst, so musst du ihnen desshalb nicht weniger leuchten!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0023","text":"Und hüte dich vor den Guten und Gerechten! Sie kreuzigen gerne Die, welche sich ihre eigne Tugend erfinden,—sie hassen den Einsamen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0024","text":"Hüte dich auch vor der heiligen Einfalt! Alles ist ihr unheilig, was nicht einfältig ist; sie spielt auch gerne mit dem Feuer—der Scheiterhaufen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0025","text":"Und hüte dich auch vor den Anfällen deiner Liebe! Zu schnell streckt der Einsame Dem die Hand entgegen, der ihm begegnet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0026","text":"Manchem Menschen darfst du nicht die Hand geben, sondern nur die Tatze: und ich will, dass deine Tatze auch Krallen habe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0027","text":"Aber der schlimmste Feind, dem du begegnen kannst, wirst du immer dir selber sein; du selber lauerst dir auf in Höhlen und Wäldern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0028","text":"Einsamer, du gehst den Weg zu dir selber! Und an dir selber fuhrt dein Weg vorbei und an deinen sieben Teufeln!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0029","text":"Ketzer wirst du dir selber sein und Hexe und Wahrsager und Narr und Zweifler und Unheiliger und Bösewicht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0030","text":"Verbrennen musst du dich wollen in deiner eignen Flamme: wie wolltest du neu werden, wenn du nicht erst Asche geworden bist!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0031","text":"Einsamer, du gehst den Weg des Schaffenden: einen Gott willst du dir schaffen aus deinen sieben Teufeln!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0032","text":"Einsamer, du gehst den Weg des Liebenden: dich selbst liebst du und desshalb verachtest du dich, wie nur Liebende verachten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0033","text":"Schaffen will der Liebende, weil er verachtet! Was weiss Der von Liebe, der nicht gerade verachten musste, was er liebte!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0034","text":"Mit deiner Liebe gehe in deine Vereinsamung und mit deinem Schaffen, mein Bruder; und spät erst wird die Gerechtigkeit dir nachhinken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0035","text":"Mit meinen Thränen gehe in deine Vereinsamung, mein Bruder. Ich liebe Den, der über sich selber hinaus schaffen will und so zu Grunde geht.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0036","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"9b5da8a24ac119a93c15dcde4ba29134a75c8c7dd94dd52e886eec9454f92370","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181536487162Z_07200b5e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Way of the Creator","source_sha256":"9b5da8a24ac119a93c15dcde4ba29134a75c8c7dd94dd52e886eec9454f92370","sha256":"65a237a4986fac5bc3d0496a09e076a8c32cbbec9e72cc3339bc554f0ac3c4cc","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:15:36.487183+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0001","text":"Do you wish, my brother, to go into solitude? Do you wish to seek the way to yourself? Hesitate a little while longer and listen to me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0002","text":"“He who seeks easily loses himself. All solitude is guilt”: thus speaks the herd. And you long belonged to the herd.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0003","text":"The voice of the herd will still resound in you. And when you say, “I no longer have One conscience with you,” it will be a lamentation and a pain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0004","text":"Behold, this pain itself was still begotten by the One conscience: and this conscience’s last gleam still glows upon your affliction.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0005","text":"But you wish to go the way of your affliction, which is the way to yourself? Then show me your right and your strength for it!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0006","text":"Are you a new strength and a new right? A first movement? A wheel rolling out of itself? Can you also compel stars to revolve around you?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0007","text":"Ah, there is so much lusting after height! There are so many convulsions of the ambitious! Show me that you are not one of the lustful and ambitious!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0008","text":"Ah, there are so many great thoughts that do no more than a bellows: they puff up and make emptier.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0009","text":"You call yourself free? I wish to hear your ruling thought, and not that you have escaped a yoke.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0010","text":"Are you one who was permitted to escape a yoke? There is many a one who threw away his last worth when he threw away his servitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0011","text":"Free from what? What does that matter to Zarathustra! But your eye shall clearly declare to me: free for what?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0012","text":"Can you give yourself your own evil and your own good, and hang your will over yourself like a law? Can you be your own judge and avenger of your law?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0013","text":"Terrible is the aloneness with the judge and avenger of one’s own law. Thus is a star cast out into desolate space and into the icy breath of aloneness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0014","text":"Even today you suffer from the many, you one: even today you still have your courage whole and your hopes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0015","text":"But one day solitude will make you weary, one day your pride will bend and your courage gnash. One day you will cry, “I am alone!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0016","text":"One day you will no longer see your high things, and your low things all too near; your sublime itself will make you afraid like a spectre. One day you will cry: “Everything is false!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0017","text":"There are feelings that wish to kill the solitary one; if they do not succeed, well, then they themselves must die! But are you capable of being a murderer?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0018","text":"Do you already know, my brother, the word “contempt”? And the torment of your justice, to be just to those who despise you?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0019","text":"You compel many to relearn concerning you; they hold that harshly against you. You came near to them and yet passed them by: they will never forgive you that.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0020","text":"You go beyond them: but the higher you climb, the smaller the eye of envy sees you. Yet the flying one is hated most of all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0021","text":"“How could you be just toward me!—you must say—I choose your injustice as the portion allotted to me.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0022","text":"They cast injustice and filth after the solitary one: but, my brother, if you wish to be a star, you must nevertheless shine upon them no less!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0023","text":"And beware of the good and the just! They gladly crucify those who invent their own virtue,—they hate the solitary one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0024","text":"Beware also of holy simplicity! Everything that is not simple is unholy to it; it too likes to play with fire—the stake.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0025","text":"And beware also of the assaults of your love! Too quickly does the solitary one extend his hand to him who meets him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0026","text":"To many a person you must not give your hand, but only your paw: and I will that your paw also have claws.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0027","text":"But the worst enemy you can encounter will always be yourself; you yourself lie in wait for yourself in caves and forests.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0028","text":"Solitary one, you are going the way to yourself! And your way leads past yourself and past your seven devils!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0029","text":"You will be a heretic to yourself, and witch and soothsayer and fool and doubter and unholy one and evildoer.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0030","text":"You must be willing to burn yourself in your own flame: how would you become new if you had not first become ashes!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0031","text":"Solitary one, you are going the way of the creator: you wish to create for yourself a god out of your seven devils!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0032","text":"Solitary one, you are going the way of the lover: you love yourself and therefore despise yourself, as only lovers despise.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0033","text":"The lover wishes to create because he despises! What does he know of love who has not precisely had to despise what he loved!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0034","text":"Go with your love into your solitude and with your creating, my brother; and only late will justice limp after you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0035","text":"Go with my tears into your solitude, my brother. I love him who wishes to create beyond himself and thus perishes.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0036","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VWDS:P0036"]}],"source_digest":"865a4738ffdc45feb1af2cb02211dda2d2c1e83d4ad31ef9e9445054d481a882","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Vom Wege des Schaffenden"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Vom Wege des Schaffenden","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ","title":"On Little Old and Young Women","order":28,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VAUJ","text":"„Was schleichst du so scheu durch die Dämmerung, Zarathustra? Und was birgst du behutsam unter deinem Mantel?\n\nIst es ein Schatz, der dir geschenkt? Oder ein Kind, das dir geboren wurde? Oder gehst du jetzt selber auf den Wegen der Diebe, du Freund der Bösen?“—\n\nWahrlich, mein Bruder! sprach Zarathustra, es ist ein Schatz, der mir geschenkt wurde: eine kleine Wahrheit ist’s, die ich trage.\n\nAber sie ist ungebärdig wie ein junges Kind; und wenn ich ihr nicht den Mund halte, so schreit sie überlaut.\n\nAls ich heute allein meines Weges gieng, zur Stunde, wo die Sonne sinkt, begegnete mir ein altes Weiblein und redete also zu meiner Seele:\n\n„Vieles sprach Zarathustra auch zu uns Weibern, doch nie sprach er uns über das Weib.“\n\nUnd ich entgegnete ihr: „über das Weib soll man nur zu Männern reden.“\n\n„Rede auch zu mir vom Weibe, sprach sie; ich bin alt genug, um es gleich wieder zu vergessen.“\n\nUnd ich willfahrte dem alten Weiblein und sprach also zu ihm:\n\nAlles am Weibe ist ein Räthsel, und Alles am Weibe hat Eine Lösung: sie heisst Schwangerschaft.\n\nDer Mann ist für das Weib ein Mittel: der Zweck ist immer das Kind. Aber was ist das Weib für den Mann?\n\nZweierlei will der ächte Mann: Gefahr und Spiel. Desshalb will er das Weib, als das gefährlichste Spielzeug.\n\nDer Mann soll zum Kriege erzogen werden und das Weib zur Erholung des Kriegers: alles Andre ist Thorheit.\n\nAllzusüsse Früchte—die mag der Krieger nicht. Darum mag er das Weib; bitter ist auch noch das süsseste Weib.\n\nBesser als ein Mann versteht das Weib die Kinder, aber der Mann ist kindlicher als das Weib.\n\nIm ächten Manne ist ein Kind versteckt: das will spielen. Auf, ihr Frauen, so entdeckt mir doch das Kind im Manne!\n\nEin Spielzeug sei das Weib, rein und fein, dem Edelsteine gleich, bestrahlt von den Tugenden einer Welt, welche noch nicht da ist.\n\nDer Strahl eines Sternes glänze in eurer Liebe! Eure Hoffnung heisse: „möge ich den Übermenschen gebären!“\n\nIn eurer Liebe sei Tapferkeit! Mit eurer Liebe sollt ihr auf Den losgehn, der euch Furcht einflösst!\n\nIn eurer Liebe sei eure Ehre! Wenig versteht sich sonst das Weib auf Ehre. Aber diess sei eure Ehre, immer mehr zu lieben, als ihr geliebt werdet, und nie die Zweiten zu sein.\n\nDer Mann fürchte sich vor dem Weibe, wenn es liebt: da bringt es jedes Opfer, und jedes andre Ding gilt ihm ohne Werth.\n\nDer Mann fürchte sich vor dem Weibe, wenn es hasst: denn der Mann ist im Grunde der Seele nur böse, das Weib aber ist dort schlecht.\n\nWen hasst das Weib am meisten?—Also sprach das Eisen zum Magneten: „ich hasse dich am meisten, weil du anziehst, aber nicht stark genug bist, an dich zu ziehen.“\n\nDas Glück des Mannes heisst: ich will. Das Glück des Weibes heisst: er will.\n\n„Siehe, jetzt eben ward die Welt vollkommen!“—also denkt ein jedes Weib, wenn es aus ganzer Liebe gehorcht.\n\nUnd gehorchen muss das Weib und eine Tiefe finden zu seiner Oberfläche. Oberfläche ist des Weibes Gemüth, eine bewegliche stürmische Haut auf einem seichten Gewässer.\n\nDes Mannes Gemüth aber ist tief, sein Strom rauscht in unterirdischen Höhlen: das Weib ahnt seine Kraft, aber begreift sie nicht.—\n\nDa entgegnete mir das alte Weiblein: „Vieles Artige sagte Zarathustra und sonderlich für Die, welche jung genug dazu sind.\n\nSeltsam ist’s, Zarathustra kennt wenig die Weiber, und doch hat er über sie Recht! Geschieht diess desshalb, weil beim Weibe kein Ding unmöglich ist?\n\nUnd nun nimm zum Danke eine kleine Wahrheit! Bin ich doch alt genug für sie!\n\nWickle sie ein und halte ihr den Mund: sonst schreit sie überlaut, diese kleine Wahrheit.“\n\n„Gieb mir, Weib, deine kleine Wahrheit!“ sagte ich. Und also sprach das alte Weiblein:\n\n„Du gehst zu Frauen? Vergiss die Peitsche nicht!“—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0001","text":"„Was schleichst du so scheu durch die Dämmerung, Zarathustra? Und was birgst du behutsam unter deinem Mantel?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0002","text":"Ist es ein Schatz, der dir geschenkt? Oder ein Kind, das dir geboren wurde? Oder gehst du jetzt selber auf den Wegen der Diebe, du Freund der Bösen?“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0003","text":"Wahrlich, mein Bruder! sprach Zarathustra, es ist ein Schatz, der mir geschenkt wurde: eine kleine Wahrheit ist’s, die ich trage.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0004","text":"Aber sie ist ungebärdig wie ein junges Kind; und wenn ich ihr nicht den Mund halte, so schreit sie überlaut.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0005","text":"Als ich heute allein meines Weges gieng, zur Stunde, wo die Sonne sinkt, begegnete mir ein altes Weiblein und redete also zu meiner Seele:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0006","text":"„Vieles sprach Zarathustra auch zu uns Weibern, doch nie sprach er uns über das Weib.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0007","text":"Und ich entgegnete ihr: „über das Weib soll man nur zu Männern reden.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0008","text":"„Rede auch zu mir vom Weibe, sprach sie; ich bin alt genug, um es gleich wieder zu vergessen.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0009","text":"Und ich willfahrte dem alten Weiblein und sprach also zu ihm:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0010","text":"Alles am Weibe ist ein Räthsel, und Alles am Weibe hat Eine Lösung: sie heisst Schwangerschaft.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0011","text":"Der Mann ist für das Weib ein Mittel: der Zweck ist immer das Kind. Aber was ist das Weib für den Mann?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0012","text":"Zweierlei will der ächte Mann: Gefahr und Spiel. Desshalb will er das Weib, als das gefährlichste Spielzeug.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0013","text":"Der Mann soll zum Kriege erzogen werden und das Weib zur Erholung des Kriegers: alles Andre ist Thorheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0014","text":"Allzusüsse Früchte—die mag der Krieger nicht. Darum mag er das Weib; bitter ist auch noch das süsseste Weib.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0015","text":"Besser als ein Mann versteht das Weib die Kinder, aber der Mann ist kindlicher als das Weib.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0016","text":"Im ächten Manne ist ein Kind versteckt: das will spielen. Auf, ihr Frauen, so entdeckt mir doch das Kind im Manne!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0017","text":"Ein Spielzeug sei das Weib, rein und fein, dem Edelsteine gleich, bestrahlt von den Tugenden einer Welt, welche noch nicht da ist.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0018","text":"Der Strahl eines Sternes glänze in eurer Liebe! Eure Hoffnung heisse: „möge ich den Übermenschen gebären!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0019","text":"In eurer Liebe sei Tapferkeit! Mit eurer Liebe sollt ihr auf Den losgehn, der euch Furcht einflösst!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0020","text":"In eurer Liebe sei eure Ehre! Wenig versteht sich sonst das Weib auf Ehre. Aber diess sei eure Ehre, immer mehr zu lieben, als ihr geliebt werdet, und nie die Zweiten zu sein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0021","text":"Der Mann fürchte sich vor dem Weibe, wenn es liebt: da bringt es jedes Opfer, und jedes andre Ding gilt ihm ohne Werth.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0022","text":"Der Mann fürchte sich vor dem Weibe, wenn es hasst: denn der Mann ist im Grunde der Seele nur böse, das Weib aber ist dort schlecht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0023","text":"Wen hasst das Weib am meisten?—Also sprach das Eisen zum Magneten: „ich hasse dich am meisten, weil du anziehst, aber nicht stark genug bist, an dich zu ziehen.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0024","text":"Das Glück des Mannes heisst: ich will. Das Glück des Weibes heisst: er will.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0025","text":"„Siehe, jetzt eben ward die Welt vollkommen!“—also denkt ein jedes Weib, wenn es aus ganzer Liebe gehorcht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0026","text":"Und gehorchen muss das Weib und eine Tiefe finden zu seiner Oberfläche. Oberfläche ist des Weibes Gemüth, eine bewegliche stürmische Haut auf einem seichten Gewässer.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0027","text":"Des Mannes Gemüth aber ist tief, sein Strom rauscht in unterirdischen Höhlen: das Weib ahnt seine Kraft, aber begreift sie nicht.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0028","text":"Da entgegnete mir das alte Weiblein: „Vieles Artige sagte Zarathustra und sonderlich für Die, welche jung genug dazu sind.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0029","text":"Seltsam ist’s, Zarathustra kennt wenig die Weiber, und doch hat er über sie Recht! Geschieht diess desshalb, weil beim Weibe kein Ding unmöglich ist?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0030","text":"Und nun nimm zum Danke eine kleine Wahrheit! Bin ich doch alt genug für sie!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0031","text":"Wickle sie ein und halte ihr den Mund: sonst schreit sie überlaut, diese kleine Wahrheit.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0032","text":"„Gieb mir, Weib, deine kleine Wahrheit!“ sagte ich. Und also sprach das alte Weiblein:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0033","text":"„Du gehst zu Frauen? Vergiss die Peitsche nicht!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0034","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"d119b59b8dc4c29ddc037944a5dae8a1da01decd7e2897b015608329a54e6dbd","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181550040220Z_969f58a1","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On Little Old and Young Women","source_sha256":"d119b59b8dc4c29ddc037944a5dae8a1da01decd7e2897b015608329a54e6dbd","sha256":"828dab2bb8fda13ca36f8f8dad4a31d071465fc2b1bf8856588a7ee2c4ab16da","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:15:50.040235+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0001","text":"“Why do you creep so shyly through the twilight, Zarathustra? And what do you carefully hide beneath your cloak?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0002","text":"“Is it a treasure that was given to you? Or a child that was born to you? Or are you yourself now going upon the paths of thieves, you friend of the wicked?”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0003","text":"“Verily, my brother!” spoke Zarathustra, “it is a treasure that was given to me: it is a little truth that I carry.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0004","text":"“But it is unruly like a young child; and if I do not keep its mouth shut, it cries out all too loudly.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0005","text":"“As I was going my way alone today, at the hour when the sun declines, I encountered an old woman, and she spoke thus to my soul:”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0006","text":"“‘Zarathustra spoke much to us women too, but never did he speak to us about woman.’”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0007","text":"“And I answered her: ‘One should speak of woman only to men.’”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0008","text":"“‘Speak to me too about woman,’ she said; ‘I am old enough to forget it again at once.’”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0009","text":"“And I complied with the old woman and spoke thus to her:”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0010","text":"“Everything about woman is a riddle, and everything about woman has one solution: it is called pregnancy.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0011","text":"“For woman, man is a means: the end is always the child. But what is woman for man?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0012","text":"“The genuine man wants two things: danger and play. Therefore he wants woman, as the most dangerous plaything.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0013","text":"“Man shall be educated for war, and woman for the recreation of the warrior: all else is folly.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0014","text":"“Over-sweet fruits—the warrior does not like them. Therefore he likes woman; even the sweetest woman is still bitter.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0015","text":"“Better than a man, woman understands children, but man is more childlike than woman.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0016","text":"“In the genuine man a child is hidden: it wants to play. Up then, you women, discover for me the child in man!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0017","text":"“Let woman be a plaything, pure and fine, like a precious stone, irradiated by the virtues of a world that is not yet here.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0018","text":"“May the ray of a star shine in your love! Let your hope be called: ‘may I bear the Overman!’”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0019","text":"“Let there be courage in your love! With your love you shall set upon him who inspires fear in you!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0020","text":"“Let your honour be in your love! Otherwise woman understands little of honour. But let this be your honour: always to love more than you are loved, and never to be second.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0021","text":"“Let man fear woman when she loves: then she makes every sacrifice, and every other thing is without value to her.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0022","text":"“Let man fear woman when she hates: for man is at bottom only evil in his soul, but woman is there base.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0023","text":"“Whom does woman hate most?—Thus spoke the iron to the magnet: ‘I hate you most, because you attract, but are not strong enough to draw unto yourself.’”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0024","text":"“The happiness of man is called: I will. The happiness of woman is called: he wills.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0025","text":"“Behold, just now the world became perfect!”—thus thinks every woman when she obeys out of whole love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0026","text":"And woman must obey and find a depth for her surface. Surface is woman's disposition, a mobile, stormy skin upon shallow water.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0027","text":"But man's disposition is deep; his stream roars in subterranean caverns: woman senses his strength, but does not comprehend it.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0028","text":"Then the old little woman replied to me: “Zarathustra said many pretty things, and especially for those who are young enough for them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0029","text":"It is strange, Zarathustra knows little about women, and yet he is right about them! Does this happen because, with woman, no thing is impossible?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0030","text":"And now take, in thanks, a little truth! For I am old enough for it!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0031","text":"Wrap it up and hold its mouth shut: otherwise it cries out too loudly, this little truth.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0032","text":"“Give me, woman, your little truth!” I said. And thus the old little woman spoke:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0033","text":"“Are you going to women? Do not forget the whip!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0034","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VAUJ:P0034"]}],"source_digest":"e0b9cb75b372188a08e9fc40686bc9f57b72cb9a3925472102d830a45a294a91","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von alten und jungen Weiblein"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von alten und jungen Weiblein","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN","title":"On the Bite of the Adder","order":29,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VBDN","text":"Eines Tages war Zarathustra unter einem Feigenbaume eingeschlafen, da es heiss war, und hatte seine Arme über das Gesicht gelegt. Da kam eine Natter und biss ihn in den Hals, so dass Zarathustra vor Schmerz aufschrie. Als er den Arm vom Gesicht genommen hatte, sah er die Schlange an: da erkannte sie die Augen Zarathustra’s, wand sich ungeschickt und wollte davon. „Nicht doch, sprach Zarathustra; noch nahmst du meinen Dank nicht an! Du wecktest mich zur Zeit, mein Weg ist noch lang.“ „Dein Weg ist noch kurz, sagte die Natter traurig; mein Gift tödtet.“ Zarathustra lächelte. „Wann starb wohl je ein Drache am Gift einer Schlange?—sagte er. Aber nimm dein Gift zurück! Du bist nicht reich genug, es mir zu schenken.“ Da fiel ihm die Natter von Neuem um den Hals und leckte ihm seine Wunde.\n\nAls Zarathustra diess einmal seinen Jüngern erzählte, fragten sie: „Und was, oh Zarathustra, ist die Moral deiner Geschichte?“ Zarathustra antwortete darauf also:\n\nDen Vernichter der Moral heissen mich die Guten und Gerechten: meine Geschichte ist unmoralisch.—\n\nSo ihr aber einen Feind habt, so vergeltet ihm nicht Böses mit Gutem: denn das würde beschämen. Sondern beweist, dass er euch etwas Gutes angethan hat.\n\nUnd lieber zürnt noch, als dass ihr beschämt! Und wenn euch geflucht wird, so gefällt es mir nicht, dass ihr dann segnen wollt. Lieber ein Wenig mitfluchen!\n\nUnd geschah euch ein grosses Unrecht, so thut mir geschwind fünf kleine dazu! Grässlich ist Der anzusehn, den allein das Unrecht drückt.\n\nWusstet ihr diess schon? Getheiltes Unrecht ist halbes Recht. Und Der soll das Unrecht auf sich nehmen, der es tragen kann!\n\nEine kleine Rache ist menschlicher, als gar keine Rache. Und wenn die Strafe nicht auch ein Recht und eine Ehre ist für den Übertretenden, so mag ich auch euer Strafen nicht.\n\nVornehmer ist’s, sich Unrecht zu geben als Recht zu behalten, sonderlich wenn man Recht hat. Nur muss man reich genug dazu sein.\n\nIch mag eure kalte Gerechtigkeit nicht; und aus dem Auge eurer Richter blickt mir immer der Henker und sein kaltes Eisen.\n\nSagt, wo findet sich die Gerechtigkeit, welche Liebe mit sehenden Augen ist?\n\nSo erfindet mir doch die Liebe, welche nicht nur alle Strafe, sondern auch alle Schuld trägt!\n\nSo erfindet mir doch die Gerechtigkeit, die Jeden freispricht, ausgenommen den Richtenden!\n\nWollt ihr auch diess noch hören? An Dem, der von Grund aus gerecht sein will, wird auch noch die Lüge zur Menschen-Freundlichkeit.\n\nAber wie wollte ich gerecht sein von Grund aus! Wie kann ich Jedem das Seine geben! Diess sei mir genug: ich gebe Jedem das Meine.\n\nEndlich, meine Brüder, hütet euch Unrecht zu thun allen Einsiedlern! Wie könnte ein Einsiedler vergessen! Wie könnte er vergelten!\n\nWie ein tiefer Brunnen ist ein Einsiedler. Leicht ist es, einen Stein hineinzuwerfen; sank er aber bis zum Grunde, sagt, wer will ihn wieder hinausbringen?\n\nHütet euch, den Einsiedler zu beleidigen! Thatet ihr’s aber, nun, so tödtet ihn auch noch!\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0001","text":"Eines Tages war Zarathustra unter einem Feigenbaume eingeschlafen, da es heiss war, und hatte seine Arme über das Gesicht gelegt. Da kam eine Natter und biss ihn in den Hals, so dass Zarathustra vor Schmerz aufschrie. Als er den Arm vom Gesicht genommen hatte, sah er die Schlange an: da erkannte sie die Augen Zarathustra’s, wand sich ungeschickt und wollte davon. „Nicht doch, sprach Zarathustra; noch nahmst du meinen Dank nicht an! Du wecktest mich zur Zeit, mein Weg ist noch lang.“ „Dein Weg ist noch kurz, sagte die Natter traurig; mein Gift tödtet.“ Zarathustra lächelte. „Wann starb wohl je ein Drache am Gift einer Schlange?—sagte er. Aber nimm dein Gift zurück! Du bist nicht reich genug, es mir zu schenken.“ Da fiel ihm die Natter von Neuem um den Hals und leckte ihm seine Wunde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0002","text":"Als Zarathustra diess einmal seinen Jüngern erzählte, fragten sie: „Und was, oh Zarathustra, ist die Moral deiner Geschichte?“ Zarathustra antwortete darauf also:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0003","text":"Den Vernichter der Moral heissen mich die Guten und Gerechten: meine Geschichte ist unmoralisch.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0004","text":"So ihr aber einen Feind habt, so vergeltet ihm nicht Böses mit Gutem: denn das würde beschämen. Sondern beweist, dass er euch etwas Gutes angethan hat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0005","text":"Und lieber zürnt noch, als dass ihr beschämt! Und wenn euch geflucht wird, so gefällt es mir nicht, dass ihr dann segnen wollt. Lieber ein Wenig mitfluchen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0006","text":"Und geschah euch ein grosses Unrecht, so thut mir geschwind fünf kleine dazu! Grässlich ist Der anzusehn, den allein das Unrecht drückt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0007","text":"Wusstet ihr diess schon? Getheiltes Unrecht ist halbes Recht. Und Der soll das Unrecht auf sich nehmen, der es tragen kann!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0008","text":"Eine kleine Rache ist menschlicher, als gar keine Rache. Und wenn die Strafe nicht auch ein Recht und eine Ehre ist für den Übertretenden, so mag ich auch euer Strafen nicht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0009","text":"Vornehmer ist’s, sich Unrecht zu geben als Recht zu behalten, sonderlich wenn man Recht hat. Nur muss man reich genug dazu sein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0010","text":"Ich mag eure kalte Gerechtigkeit nicht; und aus dem Auge eurer Richter blickt mir immer der Henker und sein kaltes Eisen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0011","text":"Sagt, wo findet sich die Gerechtigkeit, welche Liebe mit sehenden Augen ist?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0012","text":"So erfindet mir doch die Liebe, welche nicht nur alle Strafe, sondern auch alle Schuld trägt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0013","text":"So erfindet mir doch die Gerechtigkeit, die Jeden freispricht, ausgenommen den Richtenden!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0014","text":"Wollt ihr auch diess noch hören? An Dem, der von Grund aus gerecht sein will, wird auch noch die Lüge zur Menschen-Freundlichkeit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0015","text":"Aber wie wollte ich gerecht sein von Grund aus! Wie kann ich Jedem das Seine geben! Diess sei mir genug: ich gebe Jedem das Meine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0016","text":"Endlich, meine Brüder, hütet euch Unrecht zu thun allen Einsiedlern! Wie könnte ein Einsiedler vergessen! Wie könnte er vergelten!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0017","text":"Wie ein tiefer Brunnen ist ein Einsiedler. Leicht ist es, einen Stein hineinzuwerfen; sank er aber bis zum Grunde, sagt, wer will ihn wieder hinausbringen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0018","text":"Hütet euch, den Einsiedler zu beleidigen! Thatet ihr’s aber, nun, so tödtet ihn auch noch!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0019","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"ad8d36c774537ee4da76ee105e7d88b72e66d6b8b50f19f5da8bf53d9ed90df1","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181559257597Z_f7dc0562","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Bite of the Adder","source_sha256":"ad8d36c774537ee4da76ee105e7d88b72e66d6b8b50f19f5da8bf53d9ed90df1","sha256":"128daa741b045195d8fa50ceaec65d668dd4d3fe698b2caa306dbd90c7e2796c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:15:59.257631+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0001","text":"One day Zarathustra had fallen asleep beneath a fig tree, for it was hot, and had laid his arms over his face. Then an adder came and bit him in the neck, so that Zarathustra cried out in pain. When he had taken his arm from his face, he looked at the snake: then it recognised Zarathustra’s eyes, twisted itself awkwardly, and wished to get away. “Not so,” said Zarathustra; “you have not yet received my thanks! You awakened me in time; my way is still long.” “Your way is still short,” said the adder sadly; “my poison kills.” Zarathustra smiled. “When did a dragon ever die from the poison of a snake?—” he said. “But take back your poison! You are not rich enough to give it to me.” Then the adder again wound itself round his neck and licked his wound.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0002","text":"When Zarathustra once told this to his disciples, they asked: “And what, O Zarathustra, is the moral of your story?” Zarathustra answered them thus:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0003","text":"The good and the just call me the destroyer of morality: my story is immoral.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0004","text":"But if you have an enemy, do not repay him evil with good: for that would shame him. Rather prove that he has done you something good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0005","text":"And rather still be angry than be ashamed! And if you are cursed, I do not like it if you then wish to bless. Rather curse a little too!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0006","text":"And if a great wrong has been done to you, quickly do five little ones to me as well! Terrible to behold is he whom injustice alone oppresses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0007","text":"Did you know this already? Shared injustice is half justice. And he who can bear it should take the injustice upon himself!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0008","text":"A small revenge is more human than no revenge at all. And if punishment is not also a right and an honour for the transgressor, then I do not like your punishing either.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0009","text":"It is nobler to give oneself the wrong than to retain the right, especially when one is in the right. Only one must be rich enough for it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0010","text":"I do not like your cold justice; and from the eye of your judges the executioner and his cold iron always look out at me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0011","text":"Tell me, where is the justice that is love with seeing eyes?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0012","text":"Then invent for me the love that bears not only every punishment but also every guilt!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0013","text":"Then invent for me the justice that acquits everyone except the one who judges!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0014","text":"Do you wish to hear this too? In the case of him who wishes to be just from the ground up, even the lie becomes philanthropy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0015","text":"But how could I be just from the ground up! How can I give each his own! Let this be enough for me: I give each my own.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0016","text":"Finally, my brothers, beware of doing wrong to any hermit! How could a hermit forget! How could he repay!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0017","text":"Like a deep well is a hermit. It is easy to throw a stone into it; but if it has sunk to the bottom, tell me, who will bring it out again?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0018","text":"Beware of offending the hermit! But if you have done it, well then, kill him too!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0019","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VBDN:P0019"]}],"source_digest":"10809f3c55bd7479c7c5d71ed35d9aeef64b35aa2609b209f7e818fb6b6fb5af","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Vom Biss der Natter"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Vom Biss der Natter","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE","title":"On Child and Marriage","order":30,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VKUE","text":"Ich habe eine Frage für dich allein, mein Bruder: wie ein Senkblei werfe ich diese Frage in deine Seele, dass ich wisse, wie tief sie sei.\n\nDu bist jung und wünschest dir Kind und Ehe. Aber ich frage dich: bist du ein Mensch, der ein Kind sich wünschen darf ?\n\nBist du der Siegreiche, der Selbstbezwinger, der Gebieter der Sinne, der Herr deiner Tugenden? Also frage ich dich.\n\nOder redet aus deinem Wunsche das Thier und die Nothdurft? Oder Vereinsamung? Oder Unfriede mit dir?\n\nIch will, dass dein Sieg und deine Freiheit sich nach einem Kinde sehne. Lebendige Denkmale sollst du bauen deinem Siege und deiner Befreiung.\n\nÜber dich sollst du hinausbauen. Aber erst musst du mir selber gebaut sein, rechtwinklig an Leib und Seele.\n\nNicht nur fort sollst du dich pflanzen, sondern hinauf! Dazu helfe dir der Garten der Ehe!\n\nEinen höheren Leib sollst du schaffen, eine erste Bewegung, ein aus sich rollendes Rad,—einen Schaffenden sollst du schaffen.\n\nEhe: so heisse ich den Willen zu Zweien, das Eine zu schaffen, das mehr ist, als die es schufen. Ehrfurcht vor einander nenne ich Ehe als vor den Wollenden eines solchen Willens.\n\nDiess sei der Sinn und die Wahrheit deiner Ehe. Aber Das, was die Viel-zu-Vielen Ehe nennen, diese Überflüssigen,—ach, wie nenne ich das?\n\nAch, diese Armuth der Seele zu Zweien! Ach, dieser Schmutz der Seele zu Zweien! Ach diess erbärmliche Behagen zu Zweien!\n\nEhe nennen sie diess Alles; und sie sagen, ihre Ehen seien im Himmel geschlossen.\n\nNun, ich mag ihn nicht, diesen Himmel der Überflüssigen! Nein, ich mag sie nicht, diese im himmlischen Netz verschlungenen Thiere!\n\nFerne bleibe mir auch der Gott, der heranhinkt, zu segnen, was er nicht zusammenfügte!\n\nLacht mir nicht über solche Ehen! Welches Kind hätte nicht Grund, über seine Eltern zu weinen?\n\nWürdig schien mir dieser Mann und reif für den Sinn der Erde: aber als ich sein Weib sah, schien mir die Erde ein Haus für Unsinnige.\n\nJa, ich wollte, dass die Erde in Krämpfen bebte, wenn sich ein Heiliger und eine Gans mit einander paaren.\n\nDieser gieng wie ein Held auf Wahrheiten aus und endlich erbeutete er sich eine kleine geputzte Lüge. Seine Ehe nennt er’s.\n\nJener war spröde im Verkehre und wählte wählerisch. Aber mit Einem Male verdarb er für alle Male seine Gesellschaft: seine Ehe nennt er’s.\n\nJener suchte eine Magd mit den Tugenden eines Engels. Aber mit Einem Male wurde er die Magd eines Weibes, und nun thäte es Noth, dass er darüber noch zum Engel werde.\n\nSorgsam fand ich jetzt alle Käufer, und Alle haben listige Augen. Aber seine Frau kauft auch der Listigste noch im Sack.\n\nViele kurze Thorheiten—das heisst bei euch Liebe. Und eure Ehe macht vielen kurzer Thorheiten ein Ende, als Eine lange Dummheit.\n\nEure Liebe zum Weibe und des Weibes Liebe zum Manne: ach, möchte sie doch Mitleiden sein mit leidenden und verhüllten Göttern! Aber zumeist errathen zwei Thiere einander.\n\nAber auch noch eure beste Liebe ist nur ein verzücktes Gleichniss und eine schmerzhafte Gluth. Eine Fackel ist sie, die euch zu höheren Wegen leuchten soll.\n\nÜber euch hinaus sollt ihr einst lieben! So lernt erst lieben! Und darum musstet ihr den bittern Kelch eurer Liebe trinken.\n\nBitterniss ist im Kelch auch der besten Liebe: so macht sie Sehnsucht zum Übermenschen, so macht sie Durst dir, dem Schaffenden!\n\nDurst dem Schaffenden, Pfeil und Sehnsucht zum Übermenschen: sprich, mein Bruder, ist diess dein Wille zur Ehe?\n\nHeilig heisst mir solch ein Wille und solche Ehe.—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0001","text":"Ich habe eine Frage für dich allein, mein Bruder: wie ein Senkblei werfe ich diese Frage in deine Seele, dass ich wisse, wie tief sie sei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0002","text":"Du bist jung und wünschest dir Kind und Ehe. Aber ich frage dich: bist du ein Mensch, der ein Kind sich wünschen darf ?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0003","text":"Bist du der Siegreiche, der Selbstbezwinger, der Gebieter der Sinne, der Herr deiner Tugenden? Also frage ich dich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0004","text":"Oder redet aus deinem Wunsche das Thier und die Nothdurft? Oder Vereinsamung? Oder Unfriede mit dir?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0005","text":"Ich will, dass dein Sieg und deine Freiheit sich nach einem Kinde sehne. Lebendige Denkmale sollst du bauen deinem Siege und deiner Befreiung.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0006","text":"Über dich sollst du hinausbauen. Aber erst musst du mir selber gebaut sein, rechtwinklig an Leib und Seele.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0007","text":"Nicht nur fort sollst du dich pflanzen, sondern hinauf! Dazu helfe dir der Garten der Ehe!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0008","text":"Einen höheren Leib sollst du schaffen, eine erste Bewegung, ein aus sich rollendes Rad,—einen Schaffenden sollst du schaffen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0009","text":"Ehe: so heisse ich den Willen zu Zweien, das Eine zu schaffen, das mehr ist, als die es schufen. Ehrfurcht vor einander nenne ich Ehe als vor den Wollenden eines solchen Willens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0010","text":"Diess sei der Sinn und die Wahrheit deiner Ehe. Aber Das, was die Viel-zu-Vielen Ehe nennen, diese Überflüssigen,—ach, wie nenne ich das?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0011","text":"Ach, diese Armuth der Seele zu Zweien! Ach, dieser Schmutz der Seele zu Zweien! Ach diess erbärmliche Behagen zu Zweien!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0012","text":"Ehe nennen sie diess Alles; und sie sagen, ihre Ehen seien im Himmel geschlossen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0013","text":"Nun, ich mag ihn nicht, diesen Himmel der Überflüssigen! Nein, ich mag sie nicht, diese im himmlischen Netz verschlungenen Thiere!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0014","text":"Ferne bleibe mir auch der Gott, der heranhinkt, zu segnen, was er nicht zusammenfügte!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0015","text":"Lacht mir nicht über solche Ehen! Welches Kind hätte nicht Grund, über seine Eltern zu weinen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0016","text":"Würdig schien mir dieser Mann und reif für den Sinn der Erde: aber als ich sein Weib sah, schien mir die Erde ein Haus für Unsinnige.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0017","text":"Ja, ich wollte, dass die Erde in Krämpfen bebte, wenn sich ein Heiliger und eine Gans mit einander paaren.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0018","text":"Dieser gieng wie ein Held auf Wahrheiten aus und endlich erbeutete er sich eine kleine geputzte Lüge. Seine Ehe nennt er’s.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0019","text":"Jener war spröde im Verkehre und wählte wählerisch. Aber mit Einem Male verdarb er für alle Male seine Gesellschaft: seine Ehe nennt er’s.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0020","text":"Jener suchte eine Magd mit den Tugenden eines Engels. Aber mit Einem Male wurde er die Magd eines Weibes, und nun thäte es Noth, dass er darüber noch zum Engel werde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0021","text":"Sorgsam fand ich jetzt alle Käufer, und Alle haben listige Augen. Aber seine Frau kauft auch der Listigste noch im Sack.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0022","text":"Viele kurze Thorheiten—das heisst bei euch Liebe. Und eure Ehe macht vielen kurzer Thorheiten ein Ende, als Eine lange Dummheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0023","text":"Eure Liebe zum Weibe und des Weibes Liebe zum Manne: ach, möchte sie doch Mitleiden sein mit leidenden und verhüllten Göttern! Aber zumeist errathen zwei Thiere einander.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0024","text":"Aber auch noch eure beste Liebe ist nur ein verzücktes Gleichniss und eine schmerzhafte Gluth. Eine Fackel ist sie, die euch zu höheren Wegen leuchten soll.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0025","text":"Über euch hinaus sollt ihr einst lieben! So lernt erst lieben! Und darum musstet ihr den bittern Kelch eurer Liebe trinken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0026","text":"Bitterniss ist im Kelch auch der besten Liebe: so macht sie Sehnsucht zum Übermenschen, so macht sie Durst dir, dem Schaffenden!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0027","text":"Durst dem Schaffenden, Pfeil und Sehnsucht zum Übermenschen: sprich, mein Bruder, ist diess dein Wille zur Ehe?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0028","text":"Heilig heisst mir solch ein Wille und solche Ehe.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0029","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"e7d20ba711e8e924df1898eceb3b8de575269f05e8293ab98af058b00e75cace","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181613094028Z_cf5d914a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On Child and Marriage","source_sha256":"e7d20ba711e8e924df1898eceb3b8de575269f05e8293ab98af058b00e75cace","sha256":"9d5d7bce7e767204b607d317f7cffae7ce0f0875d96357aacc420c761a5e1ea8","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:16:13.094066+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0001","text":"I have a question for you alone, my brother: like a plumb-line I cast this question into your soul, that I may know how deep it is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0002","text":"You are young and desire a child and marriage. But I ask you: are you a human being who may be permitted to desire a child?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0003","text":"Are you the victorious one, the self-conqueror, the commander of the senses, the master of your virtues? Thus do I ask you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0004","text":"Or is it the animal and necessity that speak out of your desire? Or loneliness? Or discord with yourself?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0005","text":"I want your victory and your freedom to long for a child. Living monuments you shall build to your victory and your liberation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0006","text":"Beyond yourself you shall build. But first you yourself must be built for me, square in body and soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0007","text":"You shall not merely propagate yourself onward, but upward! May the garden of marriage help you toward this!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0008","text":"You shall create a higher body, a first movement, a wheel rolling out of itself,—you shall create a creator.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0009","text":"Marriage: thus I call the will of two to create the one that is more than those who created it. I call marriage reverence for one another as for those who will such a will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0010","text":"Let this be the meaning and the truth of your marriage. But that which the all-too-many, these superfluous ones, call marriage,—ah, what shall I call it?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0011","text":"Ah, this poverty of soul in two! Ah, this filth of soul in two! Ah, this miserable comfort in two!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0012","text":"They call all this marriage; and they say that their marriages are concluded in heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0013","text":"Well, I do not like it, this heaven of the superfluous! No, I do not like them, these animals entangled in the heavenly net!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0014","text":"May the god who limps up to bless what he did not join together likewise remain far from me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0015","text":"Do not laugh at such marriages in my presence! What child would not have cause to weep over its parents?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0016","text":"This man seemed worthy to me and ripe for the meaning of the earth: but when I saw his wife, the earth seemed to me a house for the insane.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0017","text":"Yes, I would have the earth tremble in convulsions when a saint and a goose mate with one another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0018","text":"This one set out after truths like a hero, and at last he captured for himself a little adorned lie. He calls it his marriage.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0019","text":"That one was fastidious in intercourse and chose choosily. But all at once he spoiled his company once and for all: he calls it his marriage.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0020","text":"That one sought a maid with the virtues of an angel. But all at once he became the maid of a woman, and now it would be necessary for him to become an angel over it as well.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0021","text":"I have now found all buyers careful, and all have cunning eyes. But even the most cunning still buys his wife in a sack.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0022","text":"Many brief follies—that is what you call love. And your marriage puts an end to many brief follies, as one long stupidity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0023","text":"Your love of woman and woman's love of man: ah, if only it were compassion with suffering and veiled gods! But for the most part two animals guess one another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0024","text":"But even your best love is only an enraptured parable and a painful ardour. It is a torch that is meant to shine for you upon higher paths.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0025","text":"Beyond yourselves shall you one day love! Thus first learn to love! And therefore you had to drink the bitter cup of your love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0026","text":"Bitterness is in the cup even of the best love: thus it creates longing for the Overman, thus it creates thirst in you, the creator!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0027","text":"Thirst for the creator, arrow and longing for the Overman: say, my brother, is this your will to marriage?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0028","text":"Such a will and such a marriage are called holy by me.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0029","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VKUE:P0029"]}],"source_digest":"257a9b2ade4ff4495f70f36fedce7ffab5a1b6e40704c0b006488ebbf3656fc5","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von Kind und Ehe"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von Kind und Ehe","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0","title":"On Voluntary Death","order":31,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VFT0","text":"Viele sterben zu spät, und Einige sterben zu früh. Noch klingt fremd die Lehre: „stirb zur rechten Zeit!“\n\nStirb zur rechten Zeit: also lehrt es Zarathustra.\n\nFreilich, wer nie zur rechten Zeit lebt, wie sollte der je zur rechten Zeit sterben? Möchte er doch nie geboren sein!—Also rathe ich den Überflüssigen.\n\nAber auch die Überflüssigen thun noch wichtig mit ihrem Sterben, und auch die hohlste Nuss will noch geknackt sein.\n\nWichtig nehmen Alle das Sterben: aber noch ist der Tod kein Fest. Noch erlernten die Menschen nicht, wie man die schönsten Feste weiht.\n\nDen vollbringenden Tod zeige ich euch, der den Lebenden ein Stachel und ein Gelöbniss wird.\n\nSeinen Tod stirbt der Vollbringende, siegreich, umringt von Hoffenden und Gelobenden.\n\nAlso sollte man sterben lernen; und es sollte kein Fest geben, wo ein solcher Sterbender nicht der Lebenden Schwüre weihte!\n\nAlso zu sterben ist das Beste; das Zweite aber ist: im Kampfe zu sterben und eine grosse Seele zu verschwenden.\n\nAber dem Kämpfenden gleich verhasst wie dem Sieger ist euer grinsender Tod, der heranschleicht wie ein Dieb—und doch als Herr kommt.\n\nMeinen Tod lobe ich euch, den freien Tod, der mir kommt, weil ich will.\n\nUnd wann werde ich wollen?—Wer ein Ziel hat und einen Erben, der will den Tod zur rechten Zeit für Ziel und Erben.\n\nUnd aus Ehrfurcht vor Ziel und Erben wird er keine dürren Kränze mehr im Heiligthum des Lebens aufhängen.\n\nWahrlich, nicht will ich den Seildrehern gleichen: sie ziehen ihren Faden in die Länge und gehen dabei selber immer rückwärts.\n\nMancher wird auch für seine Wahrheiten und Siege zu alt; ein zahnloser Mund hat nicht mehr das Recht zu jeder Wahrheit.\n\nUnd Jeder, der Ruhm haben will, muss sich bei Zeiten von der Ehre verabschieden und die schwere Kunst üben, zur rechten Zeit zu—gehn.\n\nMan muss aufhören, sich essen zu lassen, wenn man am besten schmeckt: das wissen Die, welche lange geliebt werden wollen.\n\nSaure Äpfel giebt es freilich, deren Loos will, dass sie bis auf den letzten Tag des Herbstes warten: und zugleich werden sie reif, gelb und runzelig.\n\nAndern altert das Herz zuerst und Andern der Geist. Und Einige sind greis in der Jugend: aber spät jung erhält lang jung.\n\nManchem missräth das Leben: ein Giftwurm frisst sich ihm an’s Herz. So möge er zusehn, dass ihm das Sterben um so mehr gerathe.\n\nMancher wird nie süss, er fault im Sommer schon. Feigheit ist es, die ihn an seinem Aste festhält.\n\nViel zu Viele leben und viel zu lange hängen sie an ihren Ästen. Möchte ein Sturm kommen, der all diess Faule und Wurmfressne vom Baume schüttelt!\n\nMöchten Prediger kommen des schnellen Todes! Das wären mir die rechten Stürme und Schüttler an Lebensbäumen Aber ich höre nur den langsamen Tod predigen und Geduld mit allem „Irdischen“ .\n\nAch, ihr predigt Geduld mit dem Irdischen? Dieses Irdische ist es, das zu viel Geduld mit euch hat, ihr Lästermäuler!\n\nWahrlich, zu früh starb jener Hebräer, den die Prediger des langsamen Todes ehren: und Vielen ward es seitdem zum Verhängniss, dass er zu früh starb.\n\nNoch kannte er nur Thränen und die Schwermuth des Hebräers, sammt dem Hasse der Guten und Gerechten,—der Hebräer Jesus: da überfiel ihn die Sehnsucht zum Tode.\n\nWäre er doch in der Wüste geblieben und ferne von den Guten und Gerechten! Vielleicht hätte er leben gelernt und die Erde lieben gelernt—und das Lachen dazu!\n\nGlaubt es mir, meine Brüder! Er starb zu früh; er selber hätte seine Lehre widerrufen, wäre er bis zu meinem Alter gekommen! Edel genug war er zum Widerrufen!\n\nAber ungereift war er noch. Unreif liebt der Jüngling und unreif hasst er auch Mensch und Erde. Angebunden und schwer ist ihm noch Gemüth und Geistesflügel.\n\nAber im Manne ist mehr Kind als im Jünglinge, und weniger Schwermuth: besser versteht er sich auf Tod und Leben.\n\nFrei zum Tode und frei im Tode, ein heiliger Nein-sager, wenn es nicht Zeit mehr ist zum Ja: also versteht er sich auf Tod und Leben.\n\nDass euer Sterben keine Lästerung sei auf Mensch und Erde, meine Freunde: das erbitte ich mir von dem Honig eurer Seele.\n\nIn eurem Sterben soll noch euer Geist und eure Tugend glühn, gleich einem Abendroth um die Erde: oder aber das Sterben ist euch schlecht gerathen.\n\nAlso will ich selber sterben, dass ihr Freunde um meinetwillen die Erde mehr liebt; und zur Erde will ich wieder werden, dass ich in Der Ruhe habe, die mich gebar.\n\nWahrlich, ein Ziel hatte Zarathustra, er warf seinen Ball: nun seid ihr Freunde meines Zieles Erbe, euch werfe ich den goldenen Ball zu.\n\nLieber als Alles sehe ich euch, meine Freunde, den goldenen Ball werfen! Und so verziehe ich noch ein Wenig auf Erden: verzeiht es mir!\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0001","text":"Viele sterben zu spät, und Einige sterben zu früh. Noch klingt fremd die Lehre: „stirb zur rechten Zeit!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0002","text":"Stirb zur rechten Zeit: also lehrt es Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0003","text":"Freilich, wer nie zur rechten Zeit lebt, wie sollte der je zur rechten Zeit sterben? Möchte er doch nie geboren sein!—Also rathe ich den Überflüssigen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0004","text":"Aber auch die Überflüssigen thun noch wichtig mit ihrem Sterben, und auch die hohlste Nuss will noch geknackt sein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0005","text":"Wichtig nehmen Alle das Sterben: aber noch ist der Tod kein Fest. Noch erlernten die Menschen nicht, wie man die schönsten Feste weiht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0006","text":"Den vollbringenden Tod zeige ich euch, der den Lebenden ein Stachel und ein Gelöbniss wird.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0007","text":"Seinen Tod stirbt der Vollbringende, siegreich, umringt von Hoffenden und Gelobenden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0008","text":"Also sollte man sterben lernen; und es sollte kein Fest geben, wo ein solcher Sterbender nicht der Lebenden Schwüre weihte!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0009","text":"Also zu sterben ist das Beste; das Zweite aber ist: im Kampfe zu sterben und eine grosse Seele zu verschwenden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0010","text":"Aber dem Kämpfenden gleich verhasst wie dem Sieger ist euer grinsender Tod, der heranschleicht wie ein Dieb—und doch als Herr kommt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0011","text":"Meinen Tod lobe ich euch, den freien Tod, der mir kommt, weil ich will.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0012","text":"Und wann werde ich wollen?—Wer ein Ziel hat und einen Erben, der will den Tod zur rechten Zeit für Ziel und Erben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0013","text":"Und aus Ehrfurcht vor Ziel und Erben wird er keine dürren Kränze mehr im Heiligthum des Lebens aufhängen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0014","text":"Wahrlich, nicht will ich den Seildrehern gleichen: sie ziehen ihren Faden in die Länge und gehen dabei selber immer rückwärts.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0015","text":"Mancher wird auch für seine Wahrheiten und Siege zu alt; ein zahnloser Mund hat nicht mehr das Recht zu jeder Wahrheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0016","text":"Und Jeder, der Ruhm haben will, muss sich bei Zeiten von der Ehre verabschieden und die schwere Kunst üben, zur rechten Zeit zu—gehn.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0017","text":"Man muss aufhören, sich essen zu lassen, wenn man am besten schmeckt: das wissen Die, welche lange geliebt werden wollen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0018","text":"Saure Äpfel giebt es freilich, deren Loos will, dass sie bis auf den letzten Tag des Herbstes warten: und zugleich werden sie reif, gelb und runzelig.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0019","text":"Andern altert das Herz zuerst und Andern der Geist. Und Einige sind greis in der Jugend: aber spät jung erhält lang jung.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0020","text":"Manchem missräth das Leben: ein Giftwurm frisst sich ihm an’s Herz. So möge er zusehn, dass ihm das Sterben um so mehr gerathe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0021","text":"Mancher wird nie süss, er fault im Sommer schon. Feigheit ist es, die ihn an seinem Aste festhält.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0022","text":"Viel zu Viele leben und viel zu lange hängen sie an ihren Ästen. Möchte ein Sturm kommen, der all diess Faule und Wurmfressne vom Baume schüttelt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0023","text":"Möchten Prediger kommen des schnellen Todes! Das wären mir die rechten Stürme und Schüttler an Lebensbäumen Aber ich höre nur den langsamen Tod predigen und Geduld mit allem „Irdischen“ .","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0024","text":"Ach, ihr predigt Geduld mit dem Irdischen? Dieses Irdische ist es, das zu viel Geduld mit euch hat, ihr Lästermäuler!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0025","text":"Wahrlich, zu früh starb jener Hebräer, den die Prediger des langsamen Todes ehren: und Vielen ward es seitdem zum Verhängniss, dass er zu früh starb.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0026","text":"Noch kannte er nur Thränen und die Schwermuth des Hebräers, sammt dem Hasse der Guten und Gerechten,—der Hebräer Jesus: da überfiel ihn die Sehnsucht zum Tode.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0027","text":"Wäre er doch in der Wüste geblieben und ferne von den Guten und Gerechten! Vielleicht hätte er leben gelernt und die Erde lieben gelernt—und das Lachen dazu!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0028","text":"Glaubt es mir, meine Brüder! Er starb zu früh; er selber hätte seine Lehre widerrufen, wäre er bis zu meinem Alter gekommen! Edel genug war er zum Widerrufen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0029","text":"Aber ungereift war er noch. Unreif liebt der Jüngling und unreif hasst er auch Mensch und Erde. Angebunden und schwer ist ihm noch Gemüth und Geistesflügel.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0030","text":"Aber im Manne ist mehr Kind als im Jünglinge, und weniger Schwermuth: besser versteht er sich auf Tod und Leben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0031","text":"Frei zum Tode und frei im Tode, ein heiliger Nein-sager, wenn es nicht Zeit mehr ist zum Ja: also versteht er sich auf Tod und Leben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0032","text":"Dass euer Sterben keine Lästerung sei auf Mensch und Erde, meine Freunde: das erbitte ich mir von dem Honig eurer Seele.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0033","text":"In eurem Sterben soll noch euer Geist und eure Tugend glühn, gleich einem Abendroth um die Erde: oder aber das Sterben ist euch schlecht gerathen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0034","text":"Also will ich selber sterben, dass ihr Freunde um meinetwillen die Erde mehr liebt; und zur Erde will ich wieder werden, dass ich in Der Ruhe habe, die mich gebar.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0035","text":"Wahrlich, ein Ziel hatte Zarathustra, er warf seinen Ball: nun seid ihr Freunde meines Zieles Erbe, euch werfe ich den goldenen Ball zu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0036","text":"Lieber als Alles sehe ich euch, meine Freunde, den goldenen Ball werfen! Und so verziehe ich noch ein Wenig auf Erden: verzeiht es mir!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0037","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"4d54f02a7350631cd795ec11f95db6570cb90a6040532ebb3bf19378b34de7db","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181636321152Z_3be83b1e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On Voluntary Death","source_sha256":"4d54f02a7350631cd795ec11f95db6570cb90a6040532ebb3bf19378b34de7db","sha256":"b32e62d07007b72c7c898ea86110fc30f6efd376de284c55f80c383d9c9d8fa9","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:16:36.321168+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0001","text":"Many die too late, and some die too early. Still the teaching sounds strange: “die at the right time!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0002","text":"Die at the right time: thus Zarathustra teaches it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0003","text":"To be sure, he who never lives at the right time, how should he ever die at the right time? Would that he had never been born!—Thus I counsel the superfluous.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0004","text":"But even the superfluous still make much of their dying, and even the hollowest nut still wants to be cracked.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0005","text":"All take dying seriously: but death is not yet a festival. Human beings have not yet learned how to consecrate the most beautiful festivals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0006","text":"I show you the consummating death, which becomes a spur and a vow to the living.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0007","text":"The consummator dies his death, victorious, surrounded by those who hope and those who vow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0008","text":"Thus one should learn to die; and there should be no festival at which such a dying man did not consecrate the vows of the living!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0009","text":"Thus to die is best; but second is this: to die in battle and squander a great soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0010","text":"But equally hateful to the fighter and to the victor is your grinning death, which creeps up like a thief—and yet comes as lord.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0011","text":"I praise to you my death, the free death, which comes to me because I will it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0012","text":"And when shall I will?—He who has a goal and an heir wills death at the right time for the sake of goal and heir.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0013","text":"And out of reverence for goal and heir, he will no longer hang up dry wreaths in the sanctuary of life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0014","text":"Verily, I do not wish to resemble the rope-makers: they draw their thread out to length and in doing so themselves always go backward.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0015","text":"Many a one also grows too old for his truths and victories; a toothless mouth no longer has the right to every truth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0016","text":"And everyone who wants to have fame must in good time take leave of honour and practise the difficult art of—going at the right time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0017","text":"One must stop allowing oneself to be eaten when one tastes best: those know this who wish to be loved for a long time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0018","text":"There are, to be sure, sour apples whose lot it is to wait until the last day of autumn: and at the same time they become ripe, yellow, and wrinkled.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0019","text":"In some the heart grows old first, and in others the spirit. And some are aged in youth: but being young late keeps one young long.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0020","text":"For many a one life turns out badly: a poison-worm eats its way into his heart. So may he see to it that dying turns out all the better for him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0021","text":"Many a one never becomes sweet; he rots already in summer. It is cowardice that holds him fast to his branch.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0022","text":"Far too many live, and far too long do they hang upon their branches. Would that a storm came which shook all this rotten and worm-eaten stuff from the tree!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0023","text":"Would that preachers of swift death came! These would be for me the proper storms and shakers of life-trees. But I hear only the slow death preached, and patience with all that is “earthly.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0024","text":"Ah, you preach patience with the earthly? It is this earthly that has too much patience with you, you blaspheming mouths!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0025","text":"Truly, that Hebrew whom the preachers of slow death honour died too early: and since then it has become fatal to many that he died too early.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0026","text":"He knew as yet only tears and the melancholy of the Hebrew, together with the hatred of the good and the just,—Jesus the Hebrew: then the longing for death overcame him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0027","text":"If only he had remained in the wilderness and far from the good and the just! Perhaps he would have learned to live and learned to love the earth—and laughter as well!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0028","text":"Believe me, my brothers! He died too early; he himself would have recanted his teaching, had he reached my age! He was noble enough to recant!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0029","text":"But he was still unripe. The youth loves unripe, and unripe too he hates human beings and the earth. His soul and spirit's wing are still bound and heavy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0030","text":"But in the man there is more child than in the youth, and less melancholy: he understands death and life better.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0031","text":"Free for death and free in death, a holy sayer of No when it is no longer time for Yes: thus he understands death and life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0032","text":"That your dying may not be a blasphemy against human beings and earth, my friends: this I entreat of the honey of your soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0033","text":"In your dying your spirit and your virtue should still glow, like an evening glow about the earth: or else your dying has turned out badly for you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0034","text":"Thus I myself wish to die, so that you friends may love the earth more for my sake; and I wish to become earth again, that I may have rest in her who bore me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0035","text":"Truly, Zarathustra had a goal; he threw his ball: now you friends are heirs to my goal; to you I throw the golden ball.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0036","text":"More gladly than anything I see you, my friends, throwing the golden ball! And so I still linger a little while on earth: forgive me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0037","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VFT0:P0037"]}],"source_digest":"8b3ab0a1a64d27f46051700a0f51c8606ec2b14fcd304884e462740512d4afb1","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Vom freien Tode"}],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"Vom freien Tode","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2","title":"1.","order":32,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_1C_2","text":"## 1.\n\nAls Zarathustra von der Stadt Abschied genommen hatte, welcher sein Herz zugethan war und deren Name lautet: „die bunte Kuh“—folgten ihm Viele, die sich seine Jünger nannten und gaben ihm das Geleit. Also kamen sie an einen Kreuzweg: da sagte ihnen Zarathustra, dass er nunmehr allein gehen wolle; denn er war ein Freund des Alleingehens. Seine Jünger aber reichten ihm zum Abschiede einen Stab, an dessen goldnem Griffe sich eine Schlange um die Sonne ringelte. Zarathustra freute sich des Stabes und stützte sich darauf; dann sprach er also zu seinen Jüngern.\n\nSagt mir doch: wie kam Gold zum höchsten Werthe? Darum, dass es ungemein ist und unnützlich und leuchtend und mild im Glanze; es schenkt sich immer.\n\nNur als Abbild der höchsten Tugend kam Gold zum höchsten Werthe. Goldgleich leuchtet der Blick dem Schenkenden. Goldes-Glanz schliesst Friede zwischen Mond und Sonne.\n\nUngemein ist die höchste Tugend und unnützlich, leuchtend ist sie und mild im Glanze: eine schenkende Tugend ist die höchste Tugend.\n\nWahrlich, ich errathe euch wohl, meine Jünger: ihr trachtet, gleich mir, nach der schenkenden Tugend. Was hättet ihr mit Katzen und Wölfen gemeinsam?\n\nDas ist euer Durst, selber zu Opfern und Geschenken zu werden: und darum habt ihr den Durst, alle Reichthümer in euren Seele zu häufen.\n\nUnersättlich trachtet eure Seele nach Schätzen und Kleinodien, weil eure Tugend unersättlich ist im Verschenken-Wollen.\n\nIhr zwingt alle Dinge zu euch und in euch, dass sie aus eurem Borne zurückströmen sollen als die Gaben eurer Liebe.\n\nWahrlich, zum Räuber an allen Werthen muss solche schenkende Liebe werden; aber heil und heilig heisse ich diese Selbstsucht.\n\nEine andre Selbstsucht giebt es, eine allzuarme, eine hungernde, die immer stehlen will, jene Selbstsucht der Kranken, die kranke Selbstsucht.\n\nMit dem Auge des Diebes blickt sie auf alles Glänzende; mit der Gier des Hungers misst sie Den, der reich zu essen hat; und immer schleicht sie um den Tisch der Schenkenden.\n\nKrankheit redet aus solcher Begierde und unsichtbare Entartung; von siechem Leibe redet die diebische Gier dieser Selbstsucht.\n\nSagt mir, meine Brüder: was gilt uns als Schlechtes und Schlechtestes? Ist es nicht Entartung ?—Und auf Entartung rathen wir immer, wo die schenkende Seele fehlt.\n\nAufwärts geht unser Weg, von der Art hinüber zur Über-Art. Aber ein Grauen ist uns der entartende Sinn, welcher spricht: „Alles für mich.“\n\nAufwärts fliegt unser Sinn: so ist er ein Gleichniss unsres Leibes, einer Erhöhung Gleichniss. Solcher Erhöhungen Gleichnisse sind die Namen der Tugenden.\n\nAlso geht der Leib durch die Geschichte, ein Werdender und ein Kämpfender. Und der Geist—was ist er ihm? Seiner Kämpfe und Siege Herold, Genoss und Wiederhall.\n\nGleichnisse sind alle Namen von Gut und Böse: sie sprechen nicht aus, sie winken nur. Ein Thor, welcher von ihnen Wissen will!\n\nAchtet mir, meine Brüder, auf jede Stunde, wo euer Geist in Gleichnissen reden will: da ist der Ursprung eurer Tugend.\n\nErhöht ist da euer Leib und auferstanden; mit seiner Wonne entzückt er den Geist, dass er Schöpfer wird und Schätzer und Liebender und aller Dinge Wohlthäter.\n\nWenn euer Herz breit und voll wallt, dem Strome gleich, ein Segen und eine Gefahr den Anwohnenden: da ist der Ursprung eurer Tugend.\n\nWenn ihr erhaben seid über Lob und Tadel, und euer Wille allen Dingen befehlen will, als eines Liebenden Wille: da ist der Ursprung eurer Tugend.\n\nWenn ihr das Angenehme verachtet und das weiche Bett, und von den Weichlichen euch nicht weit genug betten könnt: da ist der Ursprung eurer Tugend.\n\nWenn ihr Eines Willens Wollende seid, und diese Wende aller Noth euch Nothwendigkeit heisst: da ist der Ursprung eurer Tugend.\n\nWahrlich, ein neues Gutes und Böses ist sie! Wahrlich, ein neues tiefes Rauschen und eines neuen Quelles Stimme!\n\nMacht ist sie, diese neue Tugend; ein herrschender Gedanke ist sie und um ihn eine kluge Seele: eine goldene Sonne und um sie die Schlange der Erkenntniss.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0002","text":"Als Zarathustra von der Stadt Abschied genommen hatte, welcher sein Herz zugethan war und deren Name lautet: „die bunte Kuh“—folgten ihm Viele, die sich seine Jünger nannten und gaben ihm das Geleit. Also kamen sie an einen Kreuzweg: da sagte ihnen Zarathustra, dass er nunmehr allein gehen wolle; denn er war ein Freund des Alleingehens. Seine Jünger aber reichten ihm zum Abschiede einen Stab, an dessen goldnem Griffe sich eine Schlange um die Sonne ringelte. Zarathustra freute sich des Stabes und stützte sich darauf; dann sprach er also zu seinen Jüngern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0003","text":"Sagt mir doch: wie kam Gold zum höchsten Werthe? Darum, dass es ungemein ist und unnützlich und leuchtend und mild im Glanze; es schenkt sich immer.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0004","text":"Nur als Abbild der höchsten Tugend kam Gold zum höchsten Werthe. Goldgleich leuchtet der Blick dem Schenkenden. Goldes-Glanz schliesst Friede zwischen Mond und Sonne.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0005","text":"Ungemein ist die höchste Tugend und unnützlich, leuchtend ist sie und mild im Glanze: eine schenkende Tugend ist die höchste Tugend.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0006","text":"Wahrlich, ich errathe euch wohl, meine Jünger: ihr trachtet, gleich mir, nach der schenkenden Tugend. Was hättet ihr mit Katzen und Wölfen gemeinsam?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0007","text":"Das ist euer Durst, selber zu Opfern und Geschenken zu werden: und darum habt ihr den Durst, alle Reichthümer in euren Seele zu häufen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0008","text":"Unersättlich trachtet eure Seele nach Schätzen und Kleinodien, weil eure Tugend unersättlich ist im Verschenken-Wollen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0009","text":"Ihr zwingt alle Dinge zu euch und in euch, dass sie aus eurem Borne zurückströmen sollen als die Gaben eurer Liebe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0010","text":"Wahrlich, zum Räuber an allen Werthen muss solche schenkende Liebe werden; aber heil und heilig heisse ich diese Selbstsucht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0011","text":"Eine andre Selbstsucht giebt es, eine allzuarme, eine hungernde, die immer stehlen will, jene Selbstsucht der Kranken, die kranke Selbstsucht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0012","text":"Mit dem Auge des Diebes blickt sie auf alles Glänzende; mit der Gier des Hungers misst sie Den, der reich zu essen hat; und immer schleicht sie um den Tisch der Schenkenden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0013","text":"Krankheit redet aus solcher Begierde und unsichtbare Entartung; von siechem Leibe redet die diebische Gier dieser Selbstsucht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0014","text":"Sagt mir, meine Brüder: was gilt uns als Schlechtes und Schlechtestes? Ist es nicht Entartung ?—Und auf Entartung rathen wir immer, wo die schenkende Seele fehlt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0015","text":"Aufwärts geht unser Weg, von der Art hinüber zur Über-Art. Aber ein Grauen ist uns der entartende Sinn, welcher spricht: „Alles für mich.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0016","text":"Aufwärts fliegt unser Sinn: so ist er ein Gleichniss unsres Leibes, einer Erhöhung Gleichniss. Solcher Erhöhungen Gleichnisse sind die Namen der Tugenden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0017","text":"Also geht der Leib durch die Geschichte, ein Werdender und ein Kämpfender. Und der Geist—was ist er ihm? Seiner Kämpfe und Siege Herold, Genoss und Wiederhall.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0018","text":"Gleichnisse sind alle Namen von Gut und Böse: sie sprechen nicht aus, sie winken nur. Ein Thor, welcher von ihnen Wissen will!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0019","text":"Achtet mir, meine Brüder, auf jede Stunde, wo euer Geist in Gleichnissen reden will: da ist der Ursprung eurer Tugend.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0020","text":"Erhöht ist da euer Leib und auferstanden; mit seiner Wonne entzückt er den Geist, dass er Schöpfer wird und Schätzer und Liebender und aller Dinge Wohlthäter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0021","text":"Wenn euer Herz breit und voll wallt, dem Strome gleich, ein Segen und eine Gefahr den Anwohnenden: da ist der Ursprung eurer Tugend.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0022","text":"Wenn ihr erhaben seid über Lob und Tadel, und euer Wille allen Dingen befehlen will, als eines Liebenden Wille: da ist der Ursprung eurer Tugend.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0023","text":"Wenn ihr das Angenehme verachtet und das weiche Bett, und von den Weichlichen euch nicht weit genug betten könnt: da ist der Ursprung eurer Tugend.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0024","text":"Wenn ihr Eines Willens Wollende seid, und diese Wende aller Noth euch Nothwendigkeit heisst: da ist der Ursprung eurer Tugend.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0025","text":"Wahrlich, ein neues Gutes und Böses ist sie! Wahrlich, ein neues tiefes Rauschen und eines neuen Quelles Stimme!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0026","text":"Macht ist sie, diese neue Tugend; ein herrschender Gedanke ist sie und um ihn eine kluge Seele: eine goldene Sonne und um sie die Schlange der Erkenntniss.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"29de9e4b24afb5f2bc01b723f318c8e79d6a3ab0a68856b456578ce94d89488b","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181650396106Z_c08a9157","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1.","source_sha256":"29de9e4b24afb5f2bc01b723f318c8e79d6a3ab0a68856b456578ce94d89488b","sha256":"647eca15869495dcbab3cda684f408dfdaf864eeb811dd2a37c2c009b40b54c7","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:16:50.396117+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0002","text":"When Zarathustra had taken leave of the city to which his heart was devoted and whose name is: “the pied cow”—many who called themselves his disciples followed him and escorted him. Thus they came to a crossroads: there Zarathustra told them that he now wished to go alone; for he was a friend of going alone. But his disciples gave him, in farewell, a staff, around the golden handle of which a serpent coiled about the sun. Zarathustra rejoiced in the staff and leaned upon it; then he spoke thus to his disciples.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0003","text":"Tell me, then: how did gold attain the highest value? Because it is uncommon and useless and radiant and gentle in its gleam; it always bestows itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0004","text":"Only as an image of the highest virtue did gold attain the highest value. Like gold shines the glance of the giver. Gold's gleam makes peace between moon and sun.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0005","text":"Uncommon is the highest virtue and useless; radiant is it and gentle in its gleam: a bestowing virtue is the highest virtue.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0006","text":"Truly, I guess you well, my disciples: like me, you strive after the bestowing virtue. What would you have in common with cats and wolves?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0007","text":"This is your thirst, yourselves to become sacrifices and gifts: and therefore you have the thirst to heap up all riches in your soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0008","text":"Insatiably your soul strives after treasures and jewels, because your virtue is insatiable in wanting to bestow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0009","text":"You compel all things to come to you and into you, so that they may flow back out of your spring as the gifts of your love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0010","text":"Truly, such bestowing love must become a robber of all values; but I call this selfishness wholesome and holy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0011","text":"There is another selfishness, an all-too-poor, a hungry one, which always wants to steal, that selfishness of the sick, sick selfishness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0012","text":"With the eye of a thief it looks upon everything shining; with the greed of hunger it measures him who has rich food to eat; and it always sneaks around the table of those who bestow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0013","text":"Disease speaks out of such desire and invisible degeneration; the thievish greed of this selfishness speaks of a sickly body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0014","text":"Tell me, my brothers: what counts for us as bad and worst? Is it not degeneration?—And we always infer degeneration where the bestowing soul is lacking.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0015","text":"Our way goes upward, from the species over to the over-species. But the degenerating mind that speaks: “Everything for me,” is an abomination to us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0016","text":"Our mind flies upward: thus it is an image of our body, an image of an elevation. The names of the virtues are images of such elevations.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0017","text":"Thus the body goes through history, a becoming and a combatant. And the spirit—what is it to it? Herald, companion, and echo of its struggles and victories.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0018","text":"All names of good and evil are images: they do not speak out, they only beckon. A fool is he who wants knowledge from them!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0019","text":"Pay heed for me, my brothers, to every hour when your spirit wishes to speak in images: there is the origin of your virtue.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0020","text":"There your body is elevated and risen again; with its delight it enraptures the spirit, so that it becomes creator and evaluator and lover and benefactor of all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0021","text":"When your heart swells broad and full, like a stream, a blessing and a danger to those dwelling nearby: there is the origin of your virtue.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0022","text":"When you are exalted above praise and blame, and your will wishes to command all things, as the will of one who loves: there is the origin of your virtue.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0023","text":"When you despise the pleasant and the soft bed, and cannot make your bed far enough from the soft ones: there is the origin of your virtue.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0024","text":"When you are those who will one will, and this turning point of all need is called necessity by you: there is the origin of your virtue.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0025","text":"Truly, it is a new good and evil! Truly, a new deep roaring and the voice of a new spring!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0026","text":"It is power, this new virtue; it is a ruling thought, and around it a wise soul: a golden sun, and around it the serpent of knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_2:P0026"]}],"source_digest":"05b5700796768a45e7dc0818bde252c420ebd34c998f857fc550c58b80da42ea","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Gift-Giving Virtue","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part One"],"reading_title":"On the Gift-Giving Virtue · 1","reading_title_context":"Part One","reading_heading_titles":["Part One","On the Gift-Giving Virtue","1."],"original_title":"1."}],"source_title_context":"Von der schenkenden Tugend","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Erster Theil"],"reading_title":"Von der schenkenden Tugend · 1","reading_title_context":"Erster Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Erster Theil","Von der schenkenden Tugend","1."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"1.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2","title":"2.","order":33,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_2C_2","text":"## 2.\n\nHier schwieg Zarathustra eine Weile und sah mit Liebe auf seine Jünger. Dann fuhr er also fort zu reden:—und seine Stimme hatte sich verwandelt.\n\nBleibt mir der Erde treu, meine Brüder, mit der Macht eurer Tugend! Eure schenkende Liebe und eure Erkenntniss diene dem Sinn der Erde! Also bitte und beschwöre ich euch.\n\nLasst sie nicht davon fliegen vom Irdischen und mit den Flügeln gegen ewige Wände schlagen! Ach, es gab immer so viel verflogene Tugend!\n\nFührt, gleich mir, die verflogene Tugend zur Erde zurück—ja, zurück zu Leib und Leben: dass sie der Erde ihren Sinn gebe, einen Menschen-Sinn!\n\nHundertfältig verflog und vergriff sich bisher so Geist wie Tugend. Ach, in unserm Leibe wohnt jetzt noch all dieser Wahn und Fehlgriff: Leib und Wille ist er da geworden.\n\nHundertfältig versuchte und verirrte sich bisher so Geist wie Tugend. Ja, ein Versuch war der Mensch. Ach, viel Unwissen und Irrthum ist an uns Leib geworden!\n\nNicht nur die Vernunft von Jahrtausenden—auch ihr Wahnsinn bricht an uns aus. Gefährlich ist es, Erbe zu sein.\n\nNoch kämpfen wir Schritt um Schritt mit dem Riesen Zufall, und über der ganzen Menschheit waltete bisher noch der Unsinn, der Ohne-Sinn.\n\nEuer Geist und eure Tugend diene dem Sinn der Erde, meine Brüder: und aller Dinge Werth werde neu von euch gesetzt! Darum sollt ihr Kämpfende sein! Darum sollt ihr Schaffende sein!\n\nWissend reinigt sich der Leib; mit Wissen versuchend erhöht er sich; dem Erkennenden heiligen sich alle Triebe; dem Erhöhten wird die Seele fröhlich.\n\nArzt, hilf dir selber: so hilfst du auch deinem Kranken noch. Das sei seine beste Hülfe, dass er Den mit Augen sehe, der sich selber heil macht.\n\nTausend Pfade giebt es, die nie noch gegangen sind; tausend Gesundheiten und verborgene Eilande des Lebens. Unerschöpft und unentdeckt ist immer noch Mensch und Menschen-Erde.\n\nWachet und horcht, ihr Einsamen! Von der Zukunft her kommen Winde mit heimlichem Flügelschlagen; und an feine Ohren ergeht gute Botschaft.\n\nIhr Einsamen von heute, ihr Ausscheidenden, ihr sollt einst ein Volk sein: aus euch, die ihr euch selber auswähltet, soll ein auserwähltes Volk erwachsen:—und aus ihm der Übermensch.\n\nWahrlich, eine Stätte der Genesung soll noch die Erde werden! Und schon liegt ein neuer Geruch um sie, ein Heil bringender,—und eine neue Hoffnung!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0002","text":"Hier schwieg Zarathustra eine Weile und sah mit Liebe auf seine Jünger. Dann fuhr er also fort zu reden:—und seine Stimme hatte sich verwandelt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0003","text":"Bleibt mir der Erde treu, meine Brüder, mit der Macht eurer Tugend! Eure schenkende Liebe und eure Erkenntniss diene dem Sinn der Erde! Also bitte und beschwöre ich euch.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0004","text":"Lasst sie nicht davon fliegen vom Irdischen und mit den Flügeln gegen ewige Wände schlagen! Ach, es gab immer so viel verflogene Tugend!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0005","text":"Führt, gleich mir, die verflogene Tugend zur Erde zurück—ja, zurück zu Leib und Leben: dass sie der Erde ihren Sinn gebe, einen Menschen-Sinn!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0006","text":"Hundertfältig verflog und vergriff sich bisher so Geist wie Tugend. Ach, in unserm Leibe wohnt jetzt noch all dieser Wahn und Fehlgriff: Leib und Wille ist er da geworden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0007","text":"Hundertfältig versuchte und verirrte sich bisher so Geist wie Tugend. Ja, ein Versuch war der Mensch. Ach, viel Unwissen und Irrthum ist an uns Leib geworden!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0008","text":"Nicht nur die Vernunft von Jahrtausenden—auch ihr Wahnsinn bricht an uns aus. Gefährlich ist es, Erbe zu sein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0009","text":"Noch kämpfen wir Schritt um Schritt mit dem Riesen Zufall, und über der ganzen Menschheit waltete bisher noch der Unsinn, der Ohne-Sinn.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0010","text":"Euer Geist und eure Tugend diene dem Sinn der Erde, meine Brüder: und aller Dinge Werth werde neu von euch gesetzt! Darum sollt ihr Kämpfende sein! Darum sollt ihr Schaffende sein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0011","text":"Wissend reinigt sich der Leib; mit Wissen versuchend erhöht er sich; dem Erkennenden heiligen sich alle Triebe; dem Erhöhten wird die Seele fröhlich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0012","text":"Arzt, hilf dir selber: so hilfst du auch deinem Kranken noch. Das sei seine beste Hülfe, dass er Den mit Augen sehe, der sich selber heil macht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0013","text":"Tausend Pfade giebt es, die nie noch gegangen sind; tausend Gesundheiten und verborgene Eilande des Lebens. Unerschöpft und unentdeckt ist immer noch Mensch und Menschen-Erde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0014","text":"Wachet und horcht, ihr Einsamen! Von der Zukunft her kommen Winde mit heimlichem Flügelschlagen; und an feine Ohren ergeht gute Botschaft.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0015","text":"Ihr Einsamen von heute, ihr Ausscheidenden, ihr sollt einst ein Volk sein: aus euch, die ihr euch selber auswähltet, soll ein auserwähltes Volk erwachsen:—und aus ihm der Übermensch.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0016","text":"Wahrlich, eine Stätte der Genesung soll noch die Erde werden! Und schon liegt ein neuer Geruch um sie, ein Heil bringender,—und eine neue Hoffnung!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"6dd7a497a5d3a6d391014dee4d50a45967d972ff087fbbaeabc81430bc071db2","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181658290868Z_9445c582","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2.","source_sha256":"6dd7a497a5d3a6d391014dee4d50a45967d972ff087fbbaeabc81430bc071db2","sha256":"ecc88380c47738e75aa9400d1b2480b24f869c2627e5c1da40592cd6e41df893","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:16:58.290878+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0002","text":"Here Zarathustra was silent for a while and looked lovingly upon his disciples. Then he went on speaking thus:—and his voice had changed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0003","text":"Remain faithful to the earth, my brothers, with the power of your virtue! Let your bestowing love and your knowledge serve the meaning of the earth! Thus I ask and implore you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0004","text":"Do not let them fly away from the earthly and beat their wings against eternal walls! Alas, there has always been so much virtue that has flown astray!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0005","text":"Like me, lead virtue that has flown astray back to the earth—yes, back to body and life: that it may give the earth its meaning, a human meaning!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0006","text":"A hundredfold, spirit as well as virtue has hitherto flown astray and gone wrong. Alas, in our body all this delusion and misstep still dwells now: there it has become body and will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0007","text":"A hundredfold, spirit as well as virtue has hitherto tried and gone astray. Yes, man was an experiment. Alas, much ignorance and error has become body in us!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0008","text":"Not only the reason of millennia—also their madness breaks out in us. It is dangerous to be an heir.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0009","text":"Still we struggle step by step with the giant Chance, and over all humanity there has hitherto ruled nonsense, the Without-Meaning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0010","text":"Let your spirit and your virtue serve the meaning of the earth, my brothers: and let the value of all things be newly set by you! Therefore you shall be fighters! Therefore you shall be creators!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0011","text":"Through knowing, the body purifies itself; in experimenting with knowledge it elevates itself; to the knower all drives become sacred; to the elevated one the soul becomes cheerful.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0012","text":"Physician, help yourself: thus you will also still help your patient. Let it be his best help, that he sees with his eyes the one who makes himself whole.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0013","text":"There are a thousand paths that have never yet been trodden; a thousand healths and hidden islands of life. Man and man's earth are still unexhausted and undiscovered.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0014","text":"Keep watch and listen, you solitary ones! From the future come winds with secret beating of wings; and to fine ears glad tidings are given.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0015","text":"You solitary ones of today, you who have set yourselves apart, you shall one day be a people: from you, who chose yourselves, there shall grow a chosen people:—and from it the Overman.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0016","text":"Verily, the earth shall yet become a place of recovery! And already a new fragrance lies about it, one that brings healing,—and a new hope!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_2:P0016"]}],"source_digest":"cdc5a7baa0169cd53a60d2f47fce540aee80e9a3b56c0a3652e08c8c4112756f","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Gift-Giving Virtue","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part One"],"reading_title":"On the Gift-Giving Virtue · 2","reading_title_context":"Part One","reading_heading_titles":["Part One","On the Gift-Giving Virtue","2."],"original_title":"2."}],"source_title_context":"Von der schenkenden Tugend","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Erster Theil"],"reading_title":"Von der schenkenden Tugend · 2","reading_title_context":"Erster Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Erster Theil","Von der schenkenden Tugend","2."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"2.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2","title":"3.","order":34,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_3C_2","text":"## 3.\n\nAls Zarathustra diese Worte gesagt hatte, schwieg er, wie Einer, der nicht sein letztes Wort gesagt hat; lange wog er den Stab zweifelnd in seiner Hand. Endlich sprach er also:—und seine Stimme hatte sich verwandelt.\n\nAllein gehe ich nun, meine Jünger! Auch ihr geht nun davon und allein! So will ich es.\n\nWahrlich, ich rathe euch: geht fort von mir und wehrt euch gegen Zarathustra! Und besser noch: schämt euch seiner! Vielleicht betrog er euch.\n\nDer Mensch der Erkenntniss muss nicht nur seine Feinde lieben, sondern auch seine Freunde hassen können.\n\nMan vergilt einem Lehrer schlecht, wenn man immer nur der Schüler bleibt. Und warum wollt ihr nicht an meinem Kranze rupfen?\n\nIhr verehrt mich; aber wie, wenn eure Verehrung eines Tages umfällt? Hütet euch, dass euch nicht eine Bildsäule erschlage!\n\nIhr sagt, ihr glaubt an Zarathustra? Aber was liegt an Zarathustra! Ihr seid meine Gläubigen: aber was liegt an allen Gläubigen!\n\nIhr hattet euch noch nicht gesucht: da fandet ihr mich. So thun alle Gläubigen; darum ist es so wenig mit allem Glauben.\n\nNun heisse ich euch, mich verlieren und euch finden; und erst, wenn ihr mich Alle verleugnet habt, will ich euch wiederkehren.\n\nWahrlich, mit andern Augen, meine Brüder, werde ich mir dann meine Verlorenen suchen; mit einer anderen Liebe werde ich euch dann lieben.\n\nUnd einst noch sollt ihr mir Freunde geworden sein und Kinder Einer Hoffnung: dann will ich zum dritten Male bei euch sein, dass ich den grossen Mittag mit euch feiere.\n\nUnd das ist der grosse Mittag, da der Mensch auf der Mitte seiner Bahn steht zwischen Thier und Übermensch und seinen Weg zum Abende als seine höchste Hoffnung feiert: denn es ist der Weg zu einem neuen Morgen.\n\nAlsda wird sich der Untergehende selber segnen, dass er ein Hinübergehender sei; und die Sonne seiner Erkenntniss wird ihm im Mittage stehn.\n\n„Todt sind alle Götter: nun wollen wir, dass der Übermensch lebe.“—diess sei einst am grossen Mittage unser letzter Wille!—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0001","text":"## 3.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0002","text":"Als Zarathustra diese Worte gesagt hatte, schwieg er, wie Einer, der nicht sein letztes Wort gesagt hat; lange wog er den Stab zweifelnd in seiner Hand. Endlich sprach er also:—und seine Stimme hatte sich verwandelt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0003","text":"Allein gehe ich nun, meine Jünger! Auch ihr geht nun davon und allein! So will ich es.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0004","text":"Wahrlich, ich rathe euch: geht fort von mir und wehrt euch gegen Zarathustra! Und besser noch: schämt euch seiner! Vielleicht betrog er euch.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0005","text":"Der Mensch der Erkenntniss muss nicht nur seine Feinde lieben, sondern auch seine Freunde hassen können.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0006","text":"Man vergilt einem Lehrer schlecht, wenn man immer nur der Schüler bleibt. Und warum wollt ihr nicht an meinem Kranze rupfen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0007","text":"Ihr verehrt mich; aber wie, wenn eure Verehrung eines Tages umfällt? Hütet euch, dass euch nicht eine Bildsäule erschlage!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0008","text":"Ihr sagt, ihr glaubt an Zarathustra? Aber was liegt an Zarathustra! Ihr seid meine Gläubigen: aber was liegt an allen Gläubigen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0009","text":"Ihr hattet euch noch nicht gesucht: da fandet ihr mich. So thun alle Gläubigen; darum ist es so wenig mit allem Glauben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0010","text":"Nun heisse ich euch, mich verlieren und euch finden; und erst, wenn ihr mich Alle verleugnet habt, will ich euch wiederkehren.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0011","text":"Wahrlich, mit andern Augen, meine Brüder, werde ich mir dann meine Verlorenen suchen; mit einer anderen Liebe werde ich euch dann lieben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0012","text":"Und einst noch sollt ihr mir Freunde geworden sein und Kinder Einer Hoffnung: dann will ich zum dritten Male bei euch sein, dass ich den grossen Mittag mit euch feiere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0013","text":"Und das ist der grosse Mittag, da der Mensch auf der Mitte seiner Bahn steht zwischen Thier und Übermensch und seinen Weg zum Abende als seine höchste Hoffnung feiert: denn es ist der Weg zu einem neuen Morgen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0014","text":"Alsda wird sich der Untergehende selber segnen, dass er ein Hinübergehender sei; und die Sonne seiner Erkenntniss wird ihm im Mittage stehn.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0015","text":"„Todt sind alle Götter: nun wollen wir, dass der Übermensch lebe.“—diess sei einst am grossen Mittage unser letzter Wille!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0016","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"ff0dcbc301d07e159b6d76b174c063519262fc5521f6e222ebdf4ba49f5088dc","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181705803333Z_d24eabae","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"3.","source_sha256":"ff0dcbc301d07e159b6d76b174c063519262fc5521f6e222ebdf4ba49f5088dc","sha256":"47100678e3ae34f4132d5d6f90d6789b3cd769a1e83443890bacb90e73d391d6","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:17:05.803357+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0001","text":"## 3.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0002","text":"When Zarathustra had spoken these words, he fell silent, like one who had not spoken his last word; for a long time he weighed the staff doubtingly in his hand. At last he spoke thus:—and his voice had changed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0003","text":"Now I go alone, my disciples! You too now go away and alone! Thus I will it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0004","text":"Verily, I counsel you: go away from me and resist Zarathustra! And better still: be ashamed of him! Perhaps he deceived you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0005","text":"The man of knowledge must be able not only to love his enemies, but also to hate his friends.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0006","text":"One repays a teacher badly if one always remains only the pupil. And why do you not wish to pluck at my wreath?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0007","text":"You venerate me; but what if your veneration should one day fall over? Take care lest a statue crush you!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0008","text":"You say that you believe in Zarathustra? But what does Zarathustra matter! You are my believers: but what do all believers matter!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0009","text":"You had not yet sought yourselves: then you found me. Thus do all believers; therefore all belief amounts to so little.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0010","text":"Now I bid you lose me and find yourselves; and only when you have all denied me will I return to you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0011","text":"Verily, with other eyes, my brothers, shall I then seek my lost ones; with another love shall I then love you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0012","text":"And one day you shall yet have become friends to me and children of One hope: then I shall be with you for the third time, that I may celebrate the great noon with you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0013","text":"And this is the great noon, when man stands at the midpoint of his course between beast and overman and celebrates his way toward evening as his highest hope: for it is the way to a new morning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0014","text":"Then the one who goes under shall bless himself that he is one who goes across; and the sun of his knowledge shall stand for him at noon.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0015","text":"“All gods are dead: now we want the overman to live.”—this shall one day be our last will at the great noon!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0016","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_2:P0016"]}],"source_digest":"0b4ca6be79c4d1a95a3361970b8c8c17097b8e7530dee12b40520cfed1a28462","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Gift-Giving Virtue","source_section_label":"3.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part One"],"reading_title":"On the Gift-Giving Virtue · 3","reading_title_context":"Part One","reading_heading_titles":["Part One","On the Gift-Giving Virtue","3."],"original_title":"3."}],"source_title_context":"Von der schenkenden Tugend","source_section_label":"3.","source_title_hierarchy":["Erster Theil"],"reading_title":"Von der schenkenden Tugend · 3","reading_title_context":"Erster Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Erster Theil","Von der schenkenden Tugend","3."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"3.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ZWET","title":"Part Two","order":35,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_ZWET","text":"## Zweiter Theil\n\n„—und erst, wenn ihr mich Alle verleugnet habt, will ich euch wiederkehren. Wahrlich, mit andern Augen , meine Brüder, werde ich mir dann meine Verlorenen suchen; mit einer andern Liebe werde ich euch dann lieben.“\n\nZarathustra, von der schenkenden Tugend","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_ZWET:P0001","text":"## Zweiter Theil","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ZWET:P0002","text":"„—und erst, wenn ihr mich Alle verleugnet habt, will ich euch wiederkehren. Wahrlich, mit andern Augen , meine Brüder, werde ich mir dann meine Verlorenen suchen; mit einer andern Liebe werde ich euch dann lieben.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ZWET:P0003","text":"Zarathustra, von der schenkenden Tugend","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"25293679ed40def00d54f27e508c420e04d49837fa58ea604b914fdd477ef24b","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181707648372Z_20b8c2bf","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Two","source_sha256":"25293679ed40def00d54f27e508c420e04d49837fa58ea604b914fdd477ef24b","sha256":"ed9a0e85fbb271beaf45585f78d3bba871b1dc0d62680b0eca5ba8b9bfa64358","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:17:07.648390+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_ZWET:P0001","text":"## Second Part","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ZWET:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ZWET:P0002","text":"“—and only when you have all disowned me shall I return to you. Verily, with other eyes, my brothers, shall I then seek my lost ones; with another love shall I then love you.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ZWET:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ZWET:P0003","text":"Zarathustra, on the bestowing virtue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ZWET:P0003"]}],"source_digest":"923a89ddcea05f65e5b9f3e9d82718ae9c458c92813c131168300a63a14ec493","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Zweiter Theil"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Zweiter Theil","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD","title":"The Child with the Mirror","order":36,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_DKMD","text":"## Das Kind mit dem Spiegel\n\nHierauf gieng Zarathustra wieder zurück in das Gebirge und in die Einsamkeit seiner Höhle und entzog sich den Menschen: wartend gleich einem Säemann, der seinen Samen ausgeworfen hat. Seine Seele aber wurde voll von Ungeduld und Begierde nach Denen, welche er liebte: denn er hatte ihnen noch Viel zu geben. Diess nämlich ist das Schwerste, aus Liebe die offne Hand schliessen und als Schenkender die Scham bewahren.\n\nAlso vergiengen dem Einsamen Monde und Jahre; seine Weisheit aber wuchs und machte ihm Schmerzen durch ihre Fülle.\n\nEines Morgens aber wachte er schon vor der Morgenröthe auf, besann sich lange auf seinem Lager und sprach endlich zu seinem Herzen:\n\nWas erschrak ich doch so in meinem Traume, dass ich aufwachte? Trat nicht ein Kind zu mir, das einen Spiegel trug?\n\n„Oh Zarathustra—sprach das Kind zu mir—schaue Dich an im Spiegel!“\n\nAber als ich in den Spiegel schaute, da schrie ich auf, und mein Herz war erschüttert: denn nicht mich sahe ich darin, sondern eines Teufels Fratze und Hohnlachen.\n\nWahrlich, allzugut verstehe ich des Traumes Zeichen und Mahnung: meine Lehre ist in Gefahr, Unkraut will Weizen heissen!\n\nMeine Feinde sind mächtig worden und haben meiner Lehre Bildniss entstellt, also, dass meine Liebsten sich der Gaben schämen müssen, die ich ihnen gab.\n\nVerloren giengen mir meine Freunde; die Stunde kam mir, meine Verlornen zu suchen!—\n\nMit diesen Worten sprang Zarathustra auf, aber nicht wie ein Geängstigter, der nach Luft sucht, sondern eher wie ein Seher und Sänger, welchen der Geist anfällt. Verwundert sahen sein Adler und seine Schlange auf ihn hin: denn gleich dem Morgenrothe lag ein kommendes Glück auf seinem Antlitze.\n\nWas geschah mir doch, meine Thiere?—sagte Zarathustra. Bin ich nicht verwandelt! Kam mir nicht die Seligkeit wie ein Sturmwind?\n\nThöricht ist mein Glück und Thörichtes wird es reden: zu jung noch ist es—so habt Geduld mit ihm!\n\nVerwundet bin ich von meinem Glücke: alle Leidenden sollen mir Arzte sein!\n\nZu meinen Freunden darf ich wieder hinab und auch zu meinen Feinden! Zarathustra darf wieder reden und schenken und Lieben das Liebste thun!\n\nMeine ungeduldige Liebe fliesst über in Strömen, abwärts, nach Aufgang und Niedergang. Aus schweigsamem Gebirge und Gewittern des Schmerzes rauscht meine Seele in die Thäler.\n\nZu lange sehnte ich mich und schaute in die Ferne. Zu lange gehörte ich der Einsamkeit: so verlernte ich das Schweigen.\n\nMund bin ich worden ganz und gar, und Brausen eines Bachs aus hohen Felsen: hinab will ich meine Rede stürzen in die Thäler.\n\nUnd mag mein Strom der Liebe in Unwegsames stürzen! Wie sollte ein Strom nicht endlich den Weg zum Meere finden!\n\nWohl ist ein See in mir, ein einsiedlerischer, selbstgenugsamer; aber mein Strom der Liebe reisst ihn mit sich hinab—zum Meere!\n\nNeue Wege gehe ich, eine neue Rede kommt mir; müde wurde ich, gleich allen Schaffenden, der alten Zungen. Nicht will mein Geist mehr auf abgelaufnen Sohlen wandeln.\n\nZu langsam läuft mir alles Reden:—in deinen Wagen springe ich, Sturm! Und auch dich will ich noch peitschen mit meiner Bosheit!\n\nWie ein Schrei und ein jauchzen will ich über weite Meere hinfahren, bis ich die glückseligen Inseln finde, wo meine Freunde weilen:—\n\nUnd meine Feinde unter ihnen! Wie liebe ich nun jeden, zu dem ich nur reden darf! Auch meine Feinde gehören zu meiner Seligkeit.\n\nUnd wenn ich auf mein wildestes Pferd steigen will, so hilft mir mein Speer immer am besten hinauf: der ist meines Fusses allzeit bereiter Diener:—\n\nDer Speer, den ich gegen meine Feinde schleudere! Wie danke ich es meinen Feinden, dass ich endlich ihn schleudern darf!\n\nZu gross war die Spannung meiner Wolke: zwischen Gelächtern der Blitze will ich Hagelschauer in die Tiefe werfen.\n\nGewaltig wird sich da meine Brust heben, gewaltig wird sie ihren Sturm über die Berge hinblasen: so kommt ihr Erleichterung.\n\nWahrlich, einem Sturme gleich kommt mein Glück und meine Freiheit! Aber meine Feinde sollen glauben, der Böse rase über ihren Häuptern.\n\nJa, auch ihr werdet erschreckt sein, meine Freunde, ob meiner wilden Weisheit; und vielleicht flieht ihr davon sammt meinen Feinden.\n\nAch, dass ich’s verstünde, euch mit Hirtenflöten zurück zu locken! Ach, dass meine Löwin Weisheit zärtlich brüllen lernte! Und Vieles lernten wir schon mit einander!\n\nMeine wilde Weisheit wurde trächtig auf einsamen Bergen; auf rauhen Steinen gebar sie ihr Junges, Jüngstes.\n\nNun läuft sie närrisch durch die harte Wüste und sucht und sucht nach sanftem Rasen—meine alte wilde Weisheit!\n\nAuf eurer Herzen sanften Rasen, meine Freunde!—auf eure Liebe möchte sie ihr Liebstes betten!\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0001","text":"## Das Kind mit dem Spiegel","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0002","text":"Hierauf gieng Zarathustra wieder zurück in das Gebirge und in die Einsamkeit seiner Höhle und entzog sich den Menschen: wartend gleich einem Säemann, der seinen Samen ausgeworfen hat. Seine Seele aber wurde voll von Ungeduld und Begierde nach Denen, welche er liebte: denn er hatte ihnen noch Viel zu geben. Diess nämlich ist das Schwerste, aus Liebe die offne Hand schliessen und als Schenkender die Scham bewahren.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0003","text":"Also vergiengen dem Einsamen Monde und Jahre; seine Weisheit aber wuchs und machte ihm Schmerzen durch ihre Fülle.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0004","text":"Eines Morgens aber wachte er schon vor der Morgenröthe auf, besann sich lange auf seinem Lager und sprach endlich zu seinem Herzen:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0005","text":"Was erschrak ich doch so in meinem Traume, dass ich aufwachte? Trat nicht ein Kind zu mir, das einen Spiegel trug?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0006","text":"„Oh Zarathustra—sprach das Kind zu mir—schaue Dich an im Spiegel!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0007","text":"Aber als ich in den Spiegel schaute, da schrie ich auf, und mein Herz war erschüttert: denn nicht mich sahe ich darin, sondern eines Teufels Fratze und Hohnlachen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0008","text":"Wahrlich, allzugut verstehe ich des Traumes Zeichen und Mahnung: meine Lehre ist in Gefahr, Unkraut will Weizen heissen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0009","text":"Meine Feinde sind mächtig worden und haben meiner Lehre Bildniss entstellt, also, dass meine Liebsten sich der Gaben schämen müssen, die ich ihnen gab.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0010","text":"Verloren giengen mir meine Freunde; die Stunde kam mir, meine Verlornen zu suchen!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0011","text":"Mit diesen Worten sprang Zarathustra auf, aber nicht wie ein Geängstigter, der nach Luft sucht, sondern eher wie ein Seher und Sänger, welchen der Geist anfällt. Verwundert sahen sein Adler und seine Schlange auf ihn hin: denn gleich dem Morgenrothe lag ein kommendes Glück auf seinem Antlitze.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0012","text":"Was geschah mir doch, meine Thiere?—sagte Zarathustra. Bin ich nicht verwandelt! Kam mir nicht die Seligkeit wie ein Sturmwind?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0013","text":"Thöricht ist mein Glück und Thörichtes wird es reden: zu jung noch ist es—so habt Geduld mit ihm!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0014","text":"Verwundet bin ich von meinem Glücke: alle Leidenden sollen mir Arzte sein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0015","text":"Zu meinen Freunden darf ich wieder hinab und auch zu meinen Feinden! Zarathustra darf wieder reden und schenken und Lieben das Liebste thun!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0016","text":"Meine ungeduldige Liebe fliesst über in Strömen, abwärts, nach Aufgang und Niedergang. Aus schweigsamem Gebirge und Gewittern des Schmerzes rauscht meine Seele in die Thäler.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0017","text":"Zu lange sehnte ich mich und schaute in die Ferne. Zu lange gehörte ich der Einsamkeit: so verlernte ich das Schweigen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0018","text":"Mund bin ich worden ganz und gar, und Brausen eines Bachs aus hohen Felsen: hinab will ich meine Rede stürzen in die Thäler.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0019","text":"Und mag mein Strom der Liebe in Unwegsames stürzen! Wie sollte ein Strom nicht endlich den Weg zum Meere finden!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0020","text":"Wohl ist ein See in mir, ein einsiedlerischer, selbstgenugsamer; aber mein Strom der Liebe reisst ihn mit sich hinab—zum Meere!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0021","text":"Neue Wege gehe ich, eine neue Rede kommt mir; müde wurde ich, gleich allen Schaffenden, der alten Zungen. Nicht will mein Geist mehr auf abgelaufnen Sohlen wandeln.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0022","text":"Zu langsam läuft mir alles Reden:—in deinen Wagen springe ich, Sturm! Und auch dich will ich noch peitschen mit meiner Bosheit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0023","text":"Wie ein Schrei und ein jauchzen will ich über weite Meere hinfahren, bis ich die glückseligen Inseln finde, wo meine Freunde weilen:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0024","text":"Und meine Feinde unter ihnen! Wie liebe ich nun jeden, zu dem ich nur reden darf! Auch meine Feinde gehören zu meiner Seligkeit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0025","text":"Und wenn ich auf mein wildestes Pferd steigen will, so hilft mir mein Speer immer am besten hinauf: der ist meines Fusses allzeit bereiter Diener:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0026","text":"Der Speer, den ich gegen meine Feinde schleudere! Wie danke ich es meinen Feinden, dass ich endlich ihn schleudern darf!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0027","text":"Zu gross war die Spannung meiner Wolke: zwischen Gelächtern der Blitze will ich Hagelschauer in die Tiefe werfen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0028","text":"Gewaltig wird sich da meine Brust heben, gewaltig wird sie ihren Sturm über die Berge hinblasen: so kommt ihr Erleichterung.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0029","text":"Wahrlich, einem Sturme gleich kommt mein Glück und meine Freiheit! Aber meine Feinde sollen glauben, der Böse rase über ihren Häuptern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0030","text":"Ja, auch ihr werdet erschreckt sein, meine Freunde, ob meiner wilden Weisheit; und vielleicht flieht ihr davon sammt meinen Feinden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0031","text":"Ach, dass ich’s verstünde, euch mit Hirtenflöten zurück zu locken! Ach, dass meine Löwin Weisheit zärtlich brüllen lernte! Und Vieles lernten wir schon mit einander!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0032","text":"Meine wilde Weisheit wurde trächtig auf einsamen Bergen; auf rauhen Steinen gebar sie ihr Junges, Jüngstes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0033","text":"Nun läuft sie närrisch durch die harte Wüste und sucht und sucht nach sanftem Rasen—meine alte wilde Weisheit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0034","text":"Auf eurer Herzen sanften Rasen, meine Freunde!—auf eure Liebe möchte sie ihr Liebstes betten!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0035","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"5613d115bc537cb2e3d73c51c5ab7212e07e68c08469d016c1331f8ebcaa8183","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181723508202Z_61c11944","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"The Child with the Mirror","source_sha256":"5613d115bc537cb2e3d73c51c5ab7212e07e68c08469d016c1331f8ebcaa8183","sha256":"099866778e6da28f274f22ae31d147aa3e336f25bb621178abd1b7abeb302fb7","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:17:23.508245+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0001","text":"## The Child with the Mirror","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0002","text":"After this Zarathustra went back again into the mountains and into the solitude of his cave, and withdrew from men: waiting like a sower who has cast forth his seed. But his soul grew full of impatience and longing for those whom he loved: for he still had much to give them. For this indeed is the hardest thing: out of love to close the open hand and, as one who gives, to preserve one's shame.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0003","text":"Thus months and years passed for the solitary one; but his wisdom grew and caused him pain through its abundance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0004","text":"But one morning he awoke even before the dawn, pondered long upon his couch, and at last spoke to his heart:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0005","text":"What was it that so frightened me in my dream that I awoke? Did not a child come to me, carrying a mirror?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0006","text":"“O Zarathustra—said the child to me—look at yourself in the mirror!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0007","text":"But when I looked in the mirror, I cried out, and my heart was shaken: for I saw not myself in it, but the grimace and mocking laughter of a devil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0008","text":"Verily, I understand only too well the dream's sign and warning: my teaching is in danger, weeds would call themselves wheat!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0009","text":"My enemies have become powerful and have distorted the image of my teaching, so that my dearest ones must be ashamed of the gifts that I gave them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0010","text":"My friends were lost to me; the hour came for me to seek my lost ones!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0011","text":"With these words Zarathustra sprang up, but not like one distressed who is seeking air, rather like a seer and singer whom the spirit assails. His eagle and his serpent looked upon him in wonder: for, like the dawn, a coming happiness lay upon his countenance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0012","text":"What has happened to me, my animals?—said Zarathustra. Am I not transformed? Did not bliss come to me like a storm-wind?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0013","text":"My happiness is foolish, and it will speak foolish things: it is still too young—so have patience with it!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0014","text":"I am wounded by my happiness: let all sufferers be my physicians!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0015","text":"I may go down again to my friends and also to my enemies! Zarathustra may speak and give again and do what is dearest to love!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0016","text":"My impatient love flows over in streams, downward, toward sunrise and sunset. From silent mountains and thunderstorms of pain my soul rushes into the valleys.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0017","text":"Too long did I long and gaze into the distance. Too long did I belong to solitude: thus I unlearned silence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0018","text":"I have become wholly mouth, and the roaring of a brook from high rocks: down into the valleys I will hurl my speech.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0019","text":"And though my stream of love should plunge into the trackless! How should a stream not finally find its way to the sea!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0020","text":"To be sure, there is a lake within me, a hermit's lake, self-sufficient; but my stream of love drags it down with it—to the sea!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0021","text":"I go new ways; a new speech comes to me; like all creators, I have grown weary of the old tongues. My spirit no longer wishes to walk upon worn-out soles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0022","text":"All speech runs too slowly for me:—into your chariot I leap, Storm! And you too I shall yet whip with my wickedness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0023","text":"Like a cry and a jubilation I will travel over wide seas, until I find the blessed isles where my friends dwell:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0024","text":"And my enemies among them! How I now love everyone to whom I may but speak! My enemies too belong to my bliss.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0025","text":"And when I want to mount my wildest horse, my spear always helps me best to get up: it is my foot’s ever-ready servant:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0026","text":"The spear that I hurl against my enemies! How I thank my enemies that at last I may hurl it!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0027","text":"Too great was the tension of my cloud: amid the laughter of lightning I will cast showers of hail into the depths.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0028","text":"Mightily then will my breast rise, mightily will it blow its storm over the mountains: thus comes its relief.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0029","text":"Truly, like a storm comes my happiness and my freedom! But my enemies shall believe that the Evil One rages above their heads.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0030","text":"Yes, you too will be frightened, my friends, by reason of my wild wisdom; and perhaps you will flee from it along with my enemies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0031","text":"Ah, if only I understood how to lure you back with shepherds’ flutes! Ah, if only my lioness wisdom learned to roar tenderly! And much have we already learned together!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0032","text":"My wild wisdom became pregnant upon lonely mountains; upon rough stones it gave birth to its young, its youngest.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0033","text":"Now it runs foolishly through the harsh wilderness and searches and searches for soft turf—my old wild wisdom!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0034","text":"Upon the soft turf of your hearts, my friends!—upon your love it would lay its dearest one!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0035","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DKMD:P0035"]}],"source_digest":"eec247d6224cccf1b28eae1ffc3d4ed4ce0f3b60366d5d0fdc6c5eefc6aa6280","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Das Kind mit dem Spiegel"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Das Kind mit dem Spiegel","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC","title":"Upon the Blessed Isles","order":37,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_ADGC","text":"Die Feigen fallen von den Bäumen, sie sind gut und süss; und indem sie fallen, reisst ihnen die rothe Haut. Ein Nordwind bin ich reifen Feigen.\n\nAlso, gleich Feigen, fallen euch diese Lehren zu, meine Freunde: nun trinkt ihren Saft und ihr süsses Fleisch! Herbst ist es umher und reiner Himmel und Nachmittag.\n\nSeht, welche Fülle ist um uns! Und aus dem Überflusse heraus ist es schön hinaus zu blicken auf ferne Meere.\n\nEinst sagte man Gott, wenn man auf ferne Meere blickte; nun aber lehrte ich euch sagen: Übermensch.\n\nGott ist eine Muthmaassung; aber ich will, dass euer Muthmaassen nicht weiter reiche, als euer schaffender Wille.\n\nKönntet ihr einen Gott schaffen ?—So schweigt mir doch von allen Göttern! Wohl aber könntet ihr den Übermenschen schaffen.\n\nNicht ihr vielleicht selber, meine Brüder! Aber zu Vätern und Vorfahren könntet ihr euch umschaffen des Übermenschen: und Diess sei euer bestes Schaffen!—\n\nGott ist eine Muthmaassung: aber ich will, dass euer Muthmaassen begrenzt sei in der Denkbarkeit.\n\nKönntet ihr einen Gott denken ?—Aber diess bedeute euch Wille zur Wahrheit, dass Alles verwandelt werde in Menschen—Denkbares, Menschen—Sichtbares, Menschen—Fühlbares! Eure eignen Sinne sollt ihr zu Ende denken!\n\nUnd was ihr Welt nanntet, das soll erst von euch geschaffen werden: eure Vernunft, euer Bild, euer Wille, eure Liebe soll es selber werden! Und wahrlich, zu eurer Seligkeit, ihr Erkennenden!\n\nUnd wie wolltet ihr das Leben ertragen ohne diese Hoffnung, ihr Erkennenden? Weder in’s Unbegreifliche dürftet ihr eingeboren sein, noch in’s Unvernünftige.\n\nAber dass ich euch ganz mein Herz offenbare, ihr Freunde: wenn es Götter gäbe, wie hielte ich’s aus, kein Gott zu sein! Also giebt es keine Götter.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0001","text":"Die Feigen fallen von den Bäumen, sie sind gut und süss; und indem sie fallen, reisst ihnen die rothe Haut. Ein Nordwind bin ich reifen Feigen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0002","text":"Also, gleich Feigen, fallen euch diese Lehren zu, meine Freunde: nun trinkt ihren Saft und ihr süsses Fleisch! Herbst ist es umher und reiner Himmel und Nachmittag.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0003","text":"Seht, welche Fülle ist um uns! Und aus dem Überflusse heraus ist es schön hinaus zu blicken auf ferne Meere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0004","text":"Einst sagte man Gott, wenn man auf ferne Meere blickte; nun aber lehrte ich euch sagen: Übermensch.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0005","text":"Gott ist eine Muthmaassung; aber ich will, dass euer Muthmaassen nicht weiter reiche, als euer schaffender Wille.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0006","text":"Könntet ihr einen Gott schaffen ?—So schweigt mir doch von allen Göttern! Wohl aber könntet ihr den Übermenschen schaffen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0007","text":"Nicht ihr vielleicht selber, meine Brüder! Aber zu Vätern und Vorfahren könntet ihr euch umschaffen des Übermenschen: und Diess sei euer bestes Schaffen!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0008","text":"Gott ist eine Muthmaassung: aber ich will, dass euer Muthmaassen begrenzt sei in der Denkbarkeit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0009","text":"Könntet ihr einen Gott denken ?—Aber diess bedeute euch Wille zur Wahrheit, dass Alles verwandelt werde in Menschen—Denkbares, Menschen—Sichtbares, Menschen—Fühlbares! Eure eignen Sinne sollt ihr zu Ende denken!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0010","text":"Und was ihr Welt nanntet, das soll erst von euch geschaffen werden: eure Vernunft, euer Bild, euer Wille, eure Liebe soll es selber werden! Und wahrlich, zu eurer Seligkeit, ihr Erkennenden!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0011","text":"Und wie wolltet ihr das Leben ertragen ohne diese Hoffnung, ihr Erkennenden? Weder in’s Unbegreifliche dürftet ihr eingeboren sein, noch in’s Unvernünftige.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0012","text":"Aber dass ich euch ganz mein Herz offenbare, ihr Freunde: wenn es Götter gäbe, wie hielte ich’s aus, kein Gott zu sein! Also giebt es keine Götter.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"a50d45b47e8cb6d689ed7f615527b014b7f100eef8eb996b1e3bb65d35c4046f","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181730957073Z_5b6c382a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Upon the Blessed Isles","source_sha256":"a50d45b47e8cb6d689ed7f615527b014b7f100eef8eb996b1e3bb65d35c4046f","sha256":"224ed2d34c449d5af146c50ec649d44c4e1a9b332abaf882b194082c9eee686f","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:17:30.957096+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0001","text":"The figs fall from the trees, they are good and sweet; and as they fall, their red skin bursts. A north wind am I to ripe figs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0002","text":"Thus, like figs, these teachings fall to you, my friends: now drink their juice and their sweet flesh! It is autumn all around, and pure sky and afternoon.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0003","text":"See what abundance is about us! And out of abundance it is beautiful to look out upon distant seas.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0004","text":"Once one said God when one looked upon distant seas; but now I have taught you to say: Overman.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0005","text":"God is a conjecture; but I would have your conjecturing extend no further than your creative will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0006","text":"Could you create a god?—Then be silent to me, I pray you, of all gods! But you could indeed create the Overman.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0007","text":"Perhaps not you yourselves, my brothers! But you could remake yourselves into fathers and forefathers of the Overman: and let this be your best creation!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0008","text":"God is a conjecture: but I would have your conjecturing be bounded by what is thinkable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0009","text":"Could you think a god?—But let this mean for you will to truth, that everything be transformed into what is humanly thinkable—humanly visible—humanly perceptible! You shall think your own senses through to the end!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0010","text":"And what you called world shall first be created by you: your reason, your image, your will, your love shall itself become it! And truly, for your blessedness, you who know!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0011","text":"And how would you endure life without this hope, you who know? You may be born neither into the incomprehensible nor into the irrational.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0012","text":"But that I may reveal my whole heart to you, friends: if there were gods, how could I bear not to be a god! Thus there are no gods.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADGC:P0012"]}],"source_digest":"cc4ae4ab80011ea07938ca6a59111f208388e67c32b363e755b85710f43c9e70","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Auf den glückseligen Inseln"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Auf den glückseligen Inseln","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_WZID","title":"Well did I draw the conclusion; now, however, it draws me.—","order":38,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_WZID","text":"Wohl zog ich den Schluss; nun aber zieht er mich.—\n\nGott ist eine Muthmaassung: aber wer tränke alle Qual dieser Muthmaassung, ohne zu sterben? Soll dem Schaffenden sein Glaube genommen sein und dem Adler sein Schweben in Adler-Fernen?\n\nGott ist ein Gedanke, der macht alles Gerade krumm und Alles, was steht, drehend. Wie? Die Zeit wäre hinweg, und alles Vergängliche nur Lüge?\n\nDiess zu denken ist Wirbel und Schwindel menschlichen Gebeinen und noch dem Magen ein Erbrechen: wahrlich, die drehende Krankheit heisse ich’s, Solches zu muthmaassen.\n\nBöse heisse ich’s und menschenfeindlich: all diess Lehren vom Einen und Vollen und Unbewegten und Satten und Unvergänglichen!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_WZID:P0001","text":"Wohl zog ich den Schluss; nun aber zieht er mich.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_WZID:P0002","text":"Gott ist eine Muthmaassung: aber wer tränke alle Qual dieser Muthmaassung, ohne zu sterben? Soll dem Schaffenden sein Glaube genommen sein und dem Adler sein Schweben in Adler-Fernen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_WZID:P0003","text":"Gott ist ein Gedanke, der macht alles Gerade krumm und Alles, was steht, drehend. Wie? Die Zeit wäre hinweg, und alles Vergängliche nur Lüge?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_WZID:P0004","text":"Diess zu denken ist Wirbel und Schwindel menschlichen Gebeinen und noch dem Magen ein Erbrechen: wahrlich, die drehende Krankheit heisse ich’s, Solches zu muthmaassen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_WZID:P0005","text":"Böse heisse ich’s und menschenfeindlich: all diess Lehren vom Einen und Vollen und Unbewegten und Satten und Unvergänglichen!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"e8e892886c51e7863c2bdb7bc1acfd8730981cb4c8d969fa60640b073c0d949a","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181734996026Z_209f9975","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Well did I draw the conclusion; now, however, it draws me.—","source_sha256":"e8e892886c51e7863c2bdb7bc1acfd8730981cb4c8d969fa60640b073c0d949a","sha256":"e66921738017915d62ac14554345348b87a8d9e33ea18908509403130de745e1","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:17:34.996047+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_WZID:P0001","text":"Indeed, I drew the conclusion; but now it draws me.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_WZID:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_WZID:P0002","text":"God is a conjecture: but who could drink all the torment of this conjecture without dying? Should the creator be deprived of his faith, and the eagle of its soaring into eagle-distances?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_WZID:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_WZID:P0003","text":"God is a thought that makes all that is straight crooked and all that stands turn. What? Time would be gone, and everything transitory merely a lie?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_WZID:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_WZID:P0004","text":"To think this is a whirl and dizziness to human bones, and an emetic even to the stomach: verily, I call it the turning sickness, to conjecture such things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_WZID:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_WZID:P0005","text":"I call it evil and inimical to man: all this teaching of the One and Full and Unmoved and Sated and Imperishable!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_WZID:P0005"]}],"source_digest":"7ca169b33fb4e03955af95bdfdeb12a789eb0f927eded928e0e7ee4f68213b0e","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Wohl zog ich den Schluss; nun aber zieht er mich.—"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Wohl zog ich den Schluss; nun aber zieht er mich.—","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND","title":"All that is imperishable—is but a symbol! And the poets lie too much.—","order":39,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_AUND","text":"Alles Unvergängliche—das ist nur ein Gleichniss! Und die Dichter lügen zuviel.—\n\nAber von Zeit und Werden sollen die besten Gleichnisse reden: ein Lob sollen sie sein und eine Rechtfertigung aller Vergänglichkeit!\n\nSchaffen—das ist die grosse Erlösung vom Leiden, und des Lebens Leichtwerden. Aber dass der Schaffende sei, dazu selber thut Leid noth und viel Verwandelung.\n\nJa, viel bitteres Sterben muss in eurem Leben sein, ihr Schaffenden! Also seid ihr Fürsprecher und Rechtfertiger aller Vergänglichkeit.\n\nDass der Schaffende selber das Kind sei, das neu geboren werde, dazu muss er auch die Gebärerin sein wollen und der Schmerz der Gebärerin.\n\nWahrlich, durch hundert Seelen gieng ich meinen Weg und durch hundert Wiegen und Geburtswehen. Manchen Abschied nahm ich schon, ich kenne die herzbrechenden letzten Stunden.\n\nAber so will’s mein schaffender Wille, mein Schicksal. Oder, dass ich’s euch redlicher sage: solches Schicksal gerade—will mein Wille.\n\nAlles Fühlende leidet an mir und ist in Gefängnissen: aber mein Wollen kommt mir stets als mein Befreier und Freudebringer.\n\nWollen befreit: das ist die wahre Lehre von Wille und Freiheit—so lehrt sie euch Zarathustra.\n\nNicht-mehr-wollen und Nicht-mehr-schätzen und Nicht-mehr-schaffen! ach, dass diese grosse Müdigkeit mir stets ferne bleibe!\n\nAuch im Erkennen fühle ich nur meines Willens Zeuge- und Werde-Lust; und wenn Unschuld in meiner Erkenntniss ist, so geschieht diess, weil Wille zur Zeugung in ihr ist.\n\nHinweg von Gott und Göttem lockte mich dieser Wille; was wäre denn zu schaffen, wenn Götter—da wären!\n\nAber zum Menschen treibt er mich stets von Neuem, mein inbrünstiger Schaffens-Wille; so treibt’s den Hammer hin zum Steine.\n\nAch, ihr Menschen, im Steine schläft mir ein Bild, das Bild meiner Bilder! Ach, dass es im härtesten, hässlichsten Steine schlafen muss!\n\nNun wüthet mein Hammer grausam gegen sein Gefängniss. Vom Steine stäuben Stücke: was schiert mich das?\n\nVollenden will ich’s: denn ein Schatten kam zu mir—aller Dinge Stillstes und Leichtestes kam einst zu mir!\n\nDes Übermenschen Schönheit kam zu mir als Schatten. Ach, meine Brüder! Was gehen mich noch—die Götter an!—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0001","text":"Alles Unvergängliche—das ist nur ein Gleichniss! Und die Dichter lügen zuviel.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0002","text":"Aber von Zeit und Werden sollen die besten Gleichnisse reden: ein Lob sollen sie sein und eine Rechtfertigung aller Vergänglichkeit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0003","text":"Schaffen—das ist die grosse Erlösung vom Leiden, und des Lebens Leichtwerden. Aber dass der Schaffende sei, dazu selber thut Leid noth und viel Verwandelung.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0004","text":"Ja, viel bitteres Sterben muss in eurem Leben sein, ihr Schaffenden! Also seid ihr Fürsprecher und Rechtfertiger aller Vergänglichkeit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0005","text":"Dass der Schaffende selber das Kind sei, das neu geboren werde, dazu muss er auch die Gebärerin sein wollen und der Schmerz der Gebärerin.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0006","text":"Wahrlich, durch hundert Seelen gieng ich meinen Weg und durch hundert Wiegen und Geburtswehen. Manchen Abschied nahm ich schon, ich kenne die herzbrechenden letzten Stunden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0007","text":"Aber so will’s mein schaffender Wille, mein Schicksal. Oder, dass ich’s euch redlicher sage: solches Schicksal gerade—will mein Wille.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0008","text":"Alles Fühlende leidet an mir und ist in Gefängnissen: aber mein Wollen kommt mir stets als mein Befreier und Freudebringer.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0009","text":"Wollen befreit: das ist die wahre Lehre von Wille und Freiheit—so lehrt sie euch Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0010","text":"Nicht-mehr-wollen und Nicht-mehr-schätzen und Nicht-mehr-schaffen! ach, dass diese grosse Müdigkeit mir stets ferne bleibe!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0011","text":"Auch im Erkennen fühle ich nur meines Willens Zeuge- und Werde-Lust; und wenn Unschuld in meiner Erkenntniss ist, so geschieht diess, weil Wille zur Zeugung in ihr ist.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0012","text":"Hinweg von Gott und Göttem lockte mich dieser Wille; was wäre denn zu schaffen, wenn Götter—da wären!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0013","text":"Aber zum Menschen treibt er mich stets von Neuem, mein inbrünstiger Schaffens-Wille; so treibt’s den Hammer hin zum Steine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0014","text":"Ach, ihr Menschen, im Steine schläft mir ein Bild, das Bild meiner Bilder! Ach, dass es im härtesten, hässlichsten Steine schlafen muss!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0015","text":"Nun wüthet mein Hammer grausam gegen sein Gefängniss. Vom Steine stäuben Stücke: was schiert mich das?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0016","text":"Vollenden will ich’s: denn ein Schatten kam zu mir—aller Dinge Stillstes und Leichtestes kam einst zu mir!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0017","text":"Des Übermenschen Schönheit kam zu mir als Schatten. Ach, meine Brüder! Was gehen mich noch—die Götter an!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0018","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"9f0429f3cbbea698e65374bc1129edd78fbb58aeb02090064d741561c18e3e21","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181742891692Z_192711f0","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"All that is imperishable—is but a symbol! And the poets lie too much.—","source_sha256":"9f0429f3cbbea698e65374bc1129edd78fbb58aeb02090064d741561c18e3e21","sha256":"380153143c27fd0db0816b59345465af34ec68cbe72b8d19d12d693e28118f71","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:17:42.891727+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0001","text":"All that is imperishable—that is but a simile! And the poets lie too much.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUND:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0002","text":"But let the best similes speak of time and becoming: let them be a praise and a justification of all transience!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUND:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0003","text":"Creating—that is the great redemption from suffering, and life's becoming light. But for the creator to be, suffering itself is needed, and much transformation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUND:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0004","text":"Yes, much bitter dying must be in your life, you creators! Thus are you advocates and justifiers of all transience.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUND:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0005","text":"For the creator himself to be the child that is newly born, he must also be willing to be the woman giving birth and the pain of the woman giving birth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUND:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0006","text":"Truly, through a hundred souls I went my way, and through a hundred cradles and birth-pangs. Many a farewell have I already taken; I know the heart-breaking final hours.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUND:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0007","text":"But thus wills it my creating will, my destiny. Or, to tell it to you more honestly: it is precisely such a destiny—my will wills.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUND:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0008","text":"All that feels suffers in me and is in prisons: but my willing always comes to me as my liberator and bringer of joy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUND:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0009","text":"Willing liberates: that is the true teaching concerning will and freedom—thus Zarathustra teaches you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUND:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0010","text":"No-longer-willing and no-longer-valuing and no-longer-creating! ah, may this great weariness always remain far from me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUND:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0011","text":"Even in knowing I feel only my will's begetting- and becoming-pleasure; and if there is innocence in my knowledge, this happens because a will to begetting is in it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUND:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0012","text":"Away from God and gods this will enticed me; what would there be to create, if gods—were there!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUND:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0013","text":"But ever anew it drives me toward man, my fervent will to create; thus the hammer is driven toward the stone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUND:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0014","text":"Ah, you human beings, in the stone there sleeps for me an image, the image of my images! Ah, that it must sleep in the hardest, ugliest stone!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUND:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0015","text":"Now my hammer rages cruelly against its prison. Pieces fly from the stone: what do I care?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUND:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0016","text":"I will complete it: for a shadow came to me—the stillest and lightest of all things once came to me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUND:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0017","text":"The beauty of the overman came to me as a shadow. Ah, my brothers! What are the gods still—to me!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUND:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUND:P0018","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUND:P0018"]}],"source_digest":"09dc64639085476228d6aa070818f76d8ee58ef60408c00574052b3d41819763","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Alles Unvergängliche—das ist nur ein Gleichniss! Und die Dichter lügen zuviel.—"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Alles Unvergängliche—das ist nur ein Gleichniss! Und die Dichter lügen zuviel.—","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0","title":"On the Compassionate","order":40,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDM0","text":"Meine Freunde, es kam eine Spottrede zu eurem Freunde: „seht nur Zarathustra! Wandelt er nicht unter uns wie unter Thieren?“\n\nAber so ist es besser geredet: „der Erkennende wandelt unter Menschen als unter Thieren.“\n\nDer Mensch selber aber heisst dem Erkennenden: das Thier, das rothe Backen hat.\n\nWie geschah ihm das? Ist es nicht, weil er sich zu oft hat schämen müssen?\n\nOh meine Freunde! So spricht der Erkennende: Scham, Scham, Scham—das ist die Geschichte des Menschen!\n\nUnd darum gebeut sich der Edle, nicht zu beschämen: Scham gebeut er sich vor allem Leidenden.\n\nWahrlich, ich mag sie nicht, die Barmherzigen, die selig sind in ihrem Mitleiden: zu sehr gebricht es ihnen an Scham.\n\nMuss ich mitleidig sein, so will ich’s doch nicht heissen; und wenn ich’s bin, dann gern aus der Ferne.\n\nGerne verhülle ich auch das Haupt und fliehe davon, bevor ich noch erkannt bin: und also heisse ich euch thun, meine Freunde!\n\nMöge mein Schicksal mir immer Leidlose, gleich euch, über den Weg führen, und Solche, mit denen mir Hoffnung und Mahl und Honig gemein sein darf !\n\nWahrlich, ich that wohl Das und jenes an Leidenden: aber Besseres schien ich mir stets zu thun, wenn ich lernte, mich besser freuen.\n\nSeit es Menschen giebt, hat der Mensch sich zu wenig gefreut: Das allein, meine Brüder, ist unsre Erbsünde!\n\nUnd lernen wir besser uns freuen, so verlernen wir am besten, Andern wehe zu thun und Wehes auszudenken.\n\nDarum wasche ich mir die Hand, die dem Leidenden half, darum wische ich mir auch noch die Seele ab.\n\nDenn dass ich den Leidenden leidend sah, dessen schämte ich mich um seiner Scham willen; und als ich ihm half, da vergieng ich mich hart an seinem Stolze.\n\nGrosse Verbindlichkeiten machen nicht dankbar, sondern rachsüchtig; und wenn die kleine Wohlthat nicht vergessen wird, so wird noch ein Nage-Wurm daraus.\n\n„Seid spröde im Annehmen! Zeichnet aus damit, dass ihr annehmt!“—also rathe ich Denen, die Nichts zu verschenken haben.\n\nIch aber bin ein Schenkender: gerne schenke ich, als Freund den Freunden. Fremde aber und Arme mögen sich die Frucht selber von meinem Baume pflücken: so beschämt es weniger.\n\nBettler aber sollte man ganz abschaffen! Wahrlich, man ärgert sich ihnen zu geben und, ärgert sich ihnen nicht zu geben.\n\nUnd insgleichen die Sünder und bösen Gewissen! Glaubt mir, meine Freunde: Gewissensbisse erziehn zum Beissen.\n\nDas Schlimmste aber sind die kleinen Gedanken. Wahrlich, besser noch bös gethan, als klein gedacht!\n\nZwar ihr sagt: „die Lust an kleinen Bosheiten erspart uns manche grosse böse That.“ Aber hier sollte man nicht sparen wollen.\n\nWie ein Geschwür ist die böse That: sie juckt und kratzt und bricht heraus,—sie redet ehrlich.\n\n„Siehe, ich bin Krankheit“—so redet die böse That; das ist ihre Ehrlichkeit.\n\nAber dem Pilze gleich ist der kleine Gedanke: er kriecht und duckt sich und will nirgendswo sein—bis der ganze Leib morsch und welk ist vor kleinen Pilzen.\n\nDem aber, der vom Teufel besessen ist, sage ich diess Wort in’s Ohr: „besser noch, du ziehest deinen Teufel gross! Auch für dich giebt es noch einen Weg der Grösse!“—\n\nAch, meine Brüder! Man weiss von Jedermann Etwas zu viel! Und Mancher wird uns durchsichtig, aber desshalb können wir noch lange nicht durch ihn hindurch.\n\nEs ist schwer, mit Menschen zu leben, weil Schweigen so schwer ist.\n\nUnd nicht gegen Den, der uns zuwider ist, sind wir am unbilligsten, sondern gegen Den, welcher uns gar Nichts angeht.\n\nHast du aber einen leidenden Freund, so sei seinem Leiden eine Ruhestätte, doch gleichsam ein hartes Bett, ein Feldbett: so wirst du ihm am besten nützen.\n\nUnd thut dir ein Freund Übles, so sprich: „ich vergebe dir, was du mir thatest; dass du es aber dir thatest,—wie könnte ich das vergeben!“\n\nAlso redet alle grosse Liebe: die überwindet auch noch Vergebung und Mitleiden.\n\nMan soll sein Herz festhalten; denn lässt man es gehn, wie bald geht Einem da der Kopf durch!\n\nAch, wo in der Welt geschahen grössere Thorheiten, als bei den Mitleidigen? Und was in der Welt stiftete mehr Leid, als die Thorheiten der Mitleidigen?\n\nWehe allen Liebenden, die nicht noch eine Höhe haben, welche über ihrem Mitleiden ist!\n\nAlso sprach der Teufel einst zu mir: „auch Gott hat seine Hölle: das ist seine Liebe zu den Menschen.“\n\nUnd jüngst hörte ich ihn diess Wort sagen: „Gott ist todt; an seinem Mitleiden mit den Menschen ist Gott gestorben.“—\n\nSo seid mir gewarnt vordem Mitleiden: daher kommt noch den Menschen eine schwere Wolke! Wahrlich, ich verstehe mich auf Wetterzeichen!\n\nMerket aber auch diess Wort: alle grosse Liebe ist noch über all ihrem Mitleiden: denn sie will das Geliebte noch—schaffen!\n\n„Mich selber bringe ich meiner Liebe dar, und meinen Nächsten gleich mir“—so geht die Rede allen Schaffenden.\n\nAlle Schaffenden aber sind hart.—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0001","text":"Meine Freunde, es kam eine Spottrede zu eurem Freunde: „seht nur Zarathustra! Wandelt er nicht unter uns wie unter Thieren?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0002","text":"Aber so ist es besser geredet: „der Erkennende wandelt unter Menschen als unter Thieren.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0003","text":"Der Mensch selber aber heisst dem Erkennenden: das Thier, das rothe Backen hat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0004","text":"Wie geschah ihm das? Ist es nicht, weil er sich zu oft hat schämen müssen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0005","text":"Oh meine Freunde! So spricht der Erkennende: Scham, Scham, Scham—das ist die Geschichte des Menschen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0006","text":"Und darum gebeut sich der Edle, nicht zu beschämen: Scham gebeut er sich vor allem Leidenden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0007","text":"Wahrlich, ich mag sie nicht, die Barmherzigen, die selig sind in ihrem Mitleiden: zu sehr gebricht es ihnen an Scham.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0008","text":"Muss ich mitleidig sein, so will ich’s doch nicht heissen; und wenn ich’s bin, dann gern aus der Ferne.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0009","text":"Gerne verhülle ich auch das Haupt und fliehe davon, bevor ich noch erkannt bin: und also heisse ich euch thun, meine Freunde!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0010","text":"Möge mein Schicksal mir immer Leidlose, gleich euch, über den Weg führen, und Solche, mit denen mir Hoffnung und Mahl und Honig gemein sein darf !","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0011","text":"Wahrlich, ich that wohl Das und jenes an Leidenden: aber Besseres schien ich mir stets zu thun, wenn ich lernte, mich besser freuen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0012","text":"Seit es Menschen giebt, hat der Mensch sich zu wenig gefreut: Das allein, meine Brüder, ist unsre Erbsünde!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0013","text":"Und lernen wir besser uns freuen, so verlernen wir am besten, Andern wehe zu thun und Wehes auszudenken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0014","text":"Darum wasche ich mir die Hand, die dem Leidenden half, darum wische ich mir auch noch die Seele ab.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0015","text":"Denn dass ich den Leidenden leidend sah, dessen schämte ich mich um seiner Scham willen; und als ich ihm half, da vergieng ich mich hart an seinem Stolze.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0016","text":"Grosse Verbindlichkeiten machen nicht dankbar, sondern rachsüchtig; und wenn die kleine Wohlthat nicht vergessen wird, so wird noch ein Nage-Wurm daraus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0017","text":"„Seid spröde im Annehmen! Zeichnet aus damit, dass ihr annehmt!“—also rathe ich Denen, die Nichts zu verschenken haben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0018","text":"Ich aber bin ein Schenkender: gerne schenke ich, als Freund den Freunden. Fremde aber und Arme mögen sich die Frucht selber von meinem Baume pflücken: so beschämt es weniger.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0019","text":"Bettler aber sollte man ganz abschaffen! Wahrlich, man ärgert sich ihnen zu geben und, ärgert sich ihnen nicht zu geben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0020","text":"Und insgleichen die Sünder und bösen Gewissen! Glaubt mir, meine Freunde: Gewissensbisse erziehn zum Beissen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0021","text":"Das Schlimmste aber sind die kleinen Gedanken. Wahrlich, besser noch bös gethan, als klein gedacht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0022","text":"Zwar ihr sagt: „die Lust an kleinen Bosheiten erspart uns manche grosse böse That.“ Aber hier sollte man nicht sparen wollen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0023","text":"Wie ein Geschwür ist die böse That: sie juckt und kratzt und bricht heraus,—sie redet ehrlich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0024","text":"„Siehe, ich bin Krankheit“—so redet die böse That; das ist ihre Ehrlichkeit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0025","text":"Aber dem Pilze gleich ist der kleine Gedanke: er kriecht und duckt sich und will nirgendswo sein—bis der ganze Leib morsch und welk ist vor kleinen Pilzen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0026","text":"Dem aber, der vom Teufel besessen ist, sage ich diess Wort in’s Ohr: „besser noch, du ziehest deinen Teufel gross! Auch für dich giebt es noch einen Weg der Grösse!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0027","text":"Ach, meine Brüder! Man weiss von Jedermann Etwas zu viel! Und Mancher wird uns durchsichtig, aber desshalb können wir noch lange nicht durch ihn hindurch.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0028","text":"Es ist schwer, mit Menschen zu leben, weil Schweigen so schwer ist.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0029","text":"Und nicht gegen Den, der uns zuwider ist, sind wir am unbilligsten, sondern gegen Den, welcher uns gar Nichts angeht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0030","text":"Hast du aber einen leidenden Freund, so sei seinem Leiden eine Ruhestätte, doch gleichsam ein hartes Bett, ein Feldbett: so wirst du ihm am besten nützen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0031","text":"Und thut dir ein Freund Übles, so sprich: „ich vergebe dir, was du mir thatest; dass du es aber dir thatest,—wie könnte ich das vergeben!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0032","text":"Also redet alle grosse Liebe: die überwindet auch noch Vergebung und Mitleiden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0033","text":"Man soll sein Herz festhalten; denn lässt man es gehn, wie bald geht Einem da der Kopf durch!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0034","text":"Ach, wo in der Welt geschahen grössere Thorheiten, als bei den Mitleidigen? Und was in der Welt stiftete mehr Leid, als die Thorheiten der Mitleidigen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0035","text":"Wehe allen Liebenden, die nicht noch eine Höhe haben, welche über ihrem Mitleiden ist!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0036","text":"Also sprach der Teufel einst zu mir: „auch Gott hat seine Hölle: das ist seine Liebe zu den Menschen.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0037","text":"Und jüngst hörte ich ihn diess Wort sagen: „Gott ist todt; an seinem Mitleiden mit den Menschen ist Gott gestorben.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0038","text":"So seid mir gewarnt vordem Mitleiden: daher kommt noch den Menschen eine schwere Wolke! Wahrlich, ich verstehe mich auf Wetterzeichen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0039","text":"Merket aber auch diess Wort: alle grosse Liebe ist noch über all ihrem Mitleiden: denn sie will das Geliebte noch—schaffen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0040","text":"„Mich selber bringe ich meiner Liebe dar, und meinen Nächsten gleich mir“—so geht die Rede allen Schaffenden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0041","text":"Alle Schaffenden aber sind hart.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0042","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"bcd72b1f29f298e43af6c812862ddc467e9232197ea8f71c1966ee33dcd08144","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181800724268Z_cc687977","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Compassionate","source_sha256":"bcd72b1f29f298e43af6c812862ddc467e9232197ea8f71c1966ee33dcd08144","sha256":"cfee0aa1761d8d5ce57a4a492df6e8566711a1961eb272882f9727cf818b52df","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:18:00.724310+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0001","text":"My friends, a mocking speech was made about your friend: “Just look at Zarathustra! Does he not walk among us as among animals?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0002","text":"But it is better said thus: “The knower walks among human beings as among animals.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0003","text":"But man himself is called by the knower: the animal that has red cheeks.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0004","text":"How did this happen to him? Is it not because he has had to be ashamed too often?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0005","text":"Oh, my friends! Thus speaks the knower: Shame, shame, shame—that is the history of man!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0006","text":"And therefore the noble one commands himself not to shame: he commands himself shame in the presence of all who suffer.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0007","text":"Verily, I do not like them, the compassionate, who are blissful in their pity: they lack shame too greatly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0008","text":"If I must be compassionate, I do not wish it to be called so; and if I am, then gladly from afar.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0009","text":"Gladly, too, do I cover my head and flee away before I have yet been recognized: and thus I bid you do, my friends!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0010","text":"May my fate always bring upon my path the un-afflicted, like you, and such as those with whom I may share hope and meal and honey!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0011","text":"Verily, I did this and that well for sufferers: but I always seemed to myself to be doing better when I learned to rejoice better.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0012","text":"Ever since there have been human beings, man has rejoiced too little: that alone, my brothers, is our original sin!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0013","text":"And if we learn to rejoice better, we shall best unlearn to hurt others and to devise hurt.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0014","text":"Therefore I wash my hand that helped the sufferer; therefore I wipe off my soul as well.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0015","text":"For when I saw the sufferer suffering, I was ashamed for the sake of his shame; and when I helped him, I grievously transgressed against his pride.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0016","text":"Great obligations do not make one grateful, but vengeful; and if the small benefaction is not forgotten, it becomes a gnawing worm besides.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0017","text":"“Be coy in accepting! Distinguish yourselves by accepting!”—thus I counsel those who have nothing to give away.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0018","text":"But I am one who gives: gladly do I give, as a friend to friends. Strangers, however, and the poor may pluck the fruit from my tree themselves: thus it shames less.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0019","text":"But beggars ought to be abolished altogether! Verily, one is annoyed to give to them and annoyed not to give to them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0020","text":"And likewise sinners and bad consciences! Believe me, my friends: pangs of conscience educate toward biting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0021","text":"But the worst are little thoughts. Verily, even doing evil is better than thinking small!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0022","text":"To be sure, you say: “The pleasure in little wickedness saves us many a great evil deed.” But here one ought not to wish to save.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0023","text":"Like an ulcer is the evil deed: it itches and scratches and breaks out,—it speaks honestly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0024","text":"“Behold, I am disease”—thus speaks the evil deed; that is its honesty.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0025","text":"But the small thought is like a fungus: it creeps and cowers and wants to be nowhere—until the whole body is rotten and withered from small fungi.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0026","text":"But to him who is possessed by the devil I speak this word into his ear: “better still, you should raise your devil to greatness! For you, too, there is still a path to greatness!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0027","text":"Ah, my brothers! One knows somewhat too much about everyone! And many a one becomes transparent to us, but for that reason we still cannot see through him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0028","text":"It is difficult to live with human beings, because silence is so difficult.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0029","text":"And it is not toward him who is repugnant to us that we are most unjust, but toward him who does not concern us at all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0030","text":"But if you have a suffering friend, be a resting-place for his suffering, yet as it were a hard bed, a camp bed: thus you will be of most use to him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0031","text":"And if a friend does you evil, then say: “I forgive you what you did to me; but that you did it to yourself,—how could I forgive that!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0032","text":"Thus speaks all great love: it overcomes even forgiveness and pity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0033","text":"One should hold fast to one’s heart; for if one lets it go, how soon one’s head goes astray!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0034","text":"Ah, where in the world have greater follies occurred than among the compassionate? And what in the world has caused more suffering than the follies of the compassionate?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0035","text":"Woe to all lovers who do not yet have a height that is above their pity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0036","text":"Thus the devil once spoke to me: “God, too, has his hell: that is his love for human beings.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0037","text":"And recently I heard him speak this word: “God is dead; God died of his pity for human beings.”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0038","text":"Thus be warned by me against pity: from it there yet comes to human beings a heavy cloud! Truly, I understand weather-signs!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0039","text":"But mark this word as well: all great love is still above all its pity: for it yet wants—to create the beloved!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0040","text":"“I offer myself to my love, and my neighbour likewise as myself”—thus goes the speech of all creators.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0041","text":"But all creators are hard.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0042","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDM0:P0042"]}],"source_digest":"370ff5704b2851c6bb570597a6a5d6b23697baa6bd897820377a5e1ada0f6d8f","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von den Mitleidigen"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von den Mitleidigen","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0","title":"On the Priests","order":41,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDP0","text":"Und einstmals gab Zarathustra seinen Jüngern ein Zeichen und sprach diese Worte zu ihnen:\n\n„Hier sind Priester: und wenn es auch meine Feinde sind, geht mir still an ihnen vorüber und mit schlafendem Schwerte!\n\nAuch unter ihnen sind Helden; Viele von ihnen litten zuviel—: so wollen sie Andre leiden machen.\n\nBöse Feinde sind sie: Nichts ist rachsüchtiger als ihre Demuth. Und leicht besudelt sich Der, welcher sie angreift.\n\nAber mein Blut ist mit dem ihren verwandt; und ich will mein Blut auch noch in dem ihren geehrt wissen.“—\n\nUnd als sie vorüber gegangen waren, fiel Zarathustra der Schmerz an; und nicht lange hatte er mit seinem Schmerze gerungen, da hub er also an zu reden:\n\nEs jammert mich dieser Priester. Sie gehen mir auch wider den Geschmack; aber das ist mir das Geringste, seit ich unter Menschen bin.\n\nAber ich leide und litt mit ihnen: Gefangene sind es mir und Abgezeichnete. Der, welchen sie Erlöser nennen, schlug sie in Banden:—\n\nIn Banden falscher Werthe und Wahn-Worte! Ach dass Einer sie noch von ihrem Erlöser erlöste!\n\nAuf einem Eilande glaubten sie einst zu landen, als das Meer sie herumriss; aber siehe, es war ein schlafendes Ungeheuer!\n\nFalsche Werthe und Wahn-Worte: das sind die schlimmsten Ungeheuer für Sterbliche,—lange schläft und wartet in ihnen das Verhängniss.\n\nAber endlich kommt es und wacht und frisst und schlingt, was auf ihm sich Hütten baute.\n\nOh seht mir doch diese Hütten an, die sich diese Priester bauten! Kirchen heissen sie ihre süssduftenden Höhlen.\n\nOh über diess verfälschte Licht, diese versumpfte Luft! Hier, wo die Seele zu ihrer Höhe hinauf—nicht fliegen darf!\n\nSondern also gebietet ihr Glaube: „auf den Knien die Treppe hinan, ihr Sünder!“\n\nWahrlich, lieber sehe ich noch den Schamlosen, als die verrenkten Augen ihrer Scham und Andacht!\n\nWer schuf sich solche Höhlen und Buss-Treppen? Waren es nicht Solche, die sich verbergen wollten und sich vor dem reinen Himmel schämten?\n\nUnd erst wenn der reine Himmel wieder durch zerbrochne Decken blickt, und hinab auf Gras und rothen Mohn an zerbrochnen Mauern,—will ich den Stätten dieses Gottes wieder mein Herz zuwenden.\n\nSie nannten Gott, was ihnen widersprach und wehe that: und wahrlich, es war viel Helden-Art in ihrer Anbetung!\n\nUnd nicht anders wussten sie ihren Gott zu lieben, als indem sie den Menschen an’s Kreuz schlugen!\n\nAls Leichname gedachten sie zu leben, schwarz schlugen sie ihren Leichnam aus; auch aus ihren Reden rieche ich noch die üble Würze von Todtenkammern.\n\nUnd wer ihnen nahe lebt, der lebt schwarzen Teichen nahe, aus denen heraus die Unke ihr Lied mit süssem Tiefsinne singt.\n\nBessere Lieder müssten sie mir singen, dass ich an ihren Erlöser glauben lerne: erlöster müssten mir seine jünger aussehen!\n\nNackt möchte ich sie sehn: denn allein die Schönheit sollte Busse predigen. Aber wen überredet wohl diese vermummte Trübsal!\n\nWahrlich, ihre Erlöser selber kamen nicht aus der Freiheit und der Freiheit siebentem Himmel! Wahrlich, sie selber wandelten niemals auf den Teppichen der Erkenntniss!\n\nAus Lücken bestand der Geist dieser Erlöser; aber in jede Lücke hatten sie ihren Wahn gestellt, ihren Lückenbüsser, den sie Gott nannten.\n\nIn ihrem Mitleiden war ihr Geist ertrunken, und wenn sie schwollen und überschwollen von Mitleiden, schwamm immer obenauf eine grosse Thorheit.\n\nEifrig trieben sie und mit Geschrei ihre Heerde über ihren Steg: wie als ob es zur Zukunft nur Einen Steg gäbe! Wahrlich, auch diese Hirten gehörten noch zu den Schafen!\n\nKleine Geister und umfängliche Seelen hatten diese Hirten: aber, meine Brüder, was für kleine Länder waren bisher auch die umfänglichsten Seelen!\n\nBlutzeichen schrieben sie auf den Weg, den sie giengen, und ihre Thorheit lehrte, dass man mit Blut die Wahrheit beweise.\n\nAber Blut ist der schlechteste Zeuge der Wahrheit; Blut vergiftet die reinste Lehre noch zu Wahn und Hass der Herzen.\n\nUnd wenn Einer durch’s Feuer geht für seine Lehre,—was beweist diess! Mehr ist’s wahrlich, dass aus eignem Brande die eigne Lehre kommt!\n\nSchwüles Herz und kalter Kopf: wo diess zusammentrifft, da entsteht der Brausewind, der „Erlöser“.\n\nGrössere gab es wahrlich und Höher-Geborene, als Die, welche das Volk Erlöser nennt, diese hinreissenden Brausewinde!\n\nUnd noch von Grösseren, als alle Erlöser waren, müsst ihr, meine Brüder, erlöst werden, wollt ihr zur Freiheit den Weg finden!\n\nNiemals noch gab es einen Übermenschen. Nackt sah ich Beide, den grössten und den kleinsten Menschen:—\n\nAllzuähnlich sind sie noch einander. Wahrlich, auch den Grössten fand ich—allzumenschlich!\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0001","text":"Und einstmals gab Zarathustra seinen Jüngern ein Zeichen und sprach diese Worte zu ihnen:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0002","text":"„Hier sind Priester: und wenn es auch meine Feinde sind, geht mir still an ihnen vorüber und mit schlafendem Schwerte!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0003","text":"Auch unter ihnen sind Helden; Viele von ihnen litten zuviel—: so wollen sie Andre leiden machen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0004","text":"Böse Feinde sind sie: Nichts ist rachsüchtiger als ihre Demuth. Und leicht besudelt sich Der, welcher sie angreift.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0005","text":"Aber mein Blut ist mit dem ihren verwandt; und ich will mein Blut auch noch in dem ihren geehrt wissen.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0006","text":"Und als sie vorüber gegangen waren, fiel Zarathustra der Schmerz an; und nicht lange hatte er mit seinem Schmerze gerungen, da hub er also an zu reden:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0007","text":"Es jammert mich dieser Priester. Sie gehen mir auch wider den Geschmack; aber das ist mir das Geringste, seit ich unter Menschen bin.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0008","text":"Aber ich leide und litt mit ihnen: Gefangene sind es mir und Abgezeichnete. Der, welchen sie Erlöser nennen, schlug sie in Banden:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0009","text":"In Banden falscher Werthe und Wahn-Worte! Ach dass Einer sie noch von ihrem Erlöser erlöste!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0010","text":"Auf einem Eilande glaubten sie einst zu landen, als das Meer sie herumriss; aber siehe, es war ein schlafendes Ungeheuer!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0011","text":"Falsche Werthe und Wahn-Worte: das sind die schlimmsten Ungeheuer für Sterbliche,—lange schläft und wartet in ihnen das Verhängniss.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0012","text":"Aber endlich kommt es und wacht und frisst und schlingt, was auf ihm sich Hütten baute.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0013","text":"Oh seht mir doch diese Hütten an, die sich diese Priester bauten! Kirchen heissen sie ihre süssduftenden Höhlen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0014","text":"Oh über diess verfälschte Licht, diese versumpfte Luft! Hier, wo die Seele zu ihrer Höhe hinauf—nicht fliegen darf!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0015","text":"Sondern also gebietet ihr Glaube: „auf den Knien die Treppe hinan, ihr Sünder!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0016","text":"Wahrlich, lieber sehe ich noch den Schamlosen, als die verrenkten Augen ihrer Scham und Andacht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0017","text":"Wer schuf sich solche Höhlen und Buss-Treppen? Waren es nicht Solche, die sich verbergen wollten und sich vor dem reinen Himmel schämten?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0018","text":"Und erst wenn der reine Himmel wieder durch zerbrochne Decken blickt, und hinab auf Gras und rothen Mohn an zerbrochnen Mauern,—will ich den Stätten dieses Gottes wieder mein Herz zuwenden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0019","text":"Sie nannten Gott, was ihnen widersprach und wehe that: und wahrlich, es war viel Helden-Art in ihrer Anbetung!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0020","text":"Und nicht anders wussten sie ihren Gott zu lieben, als indem sie den Menschen an’s Kreuz schlugen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0021","text":"Als Leichname gedachten sie zu leben, schwarz schlugen sie ihren Leichnam aus; auch aus ihren Reden rieche ich noch die üble Würze von Todtenkammern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0022","text":"Und wer ihnen nahe lebt, der lebt schwarzen Teichen nahe, aus denen heraus die Unke ihr Lied mit süssem Tiefsinne singt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0023","text":"Bessere Lieder müssten sie mir singen, dass ich an ihren Erlöser glauben lerne: erlöster müssten mir seine jünger aussehen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0024","text":"Nackt möchte ich sie sehn: denn allein die Schönheit sollte Busse predigen. Aber wen überredet wohl diese vermummte Trübsal!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0025","text":"Wahrlich, ihre Erlöser selber kamen nicht aus der Freiheit und der Freiheit siebentem Himmel! Wahrlich, sie selber wandelten niemals auf den Teppichen der Erkenntniss!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0026","text":"Aus Lücken bestand der Geist dieser Erlöser; aber in jede Lücke hatten sie ihren Wahn gestellt, ihren Lückenbüsser, den sie Gott nannten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0027","text":"In ihrem Mitleiden war ihr Geist ertrunken, und wenn sie schwollen und überschwollen von Mitleiden, schwamm immer obenauf eine grosse Thorheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0028","text":"Eifrig trieben sie und mit Geschrei ihre Heerde über ihren Steg: wie als ob es zur Zukunft nur Einen Steg gäbe! Wahrlich, auch diese Hirten gehörten noch zu den Schafen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0029","text":"Kleine Geister und umfängliche Seelen hatten diese Hirten: aber, meine Brüder, was für kleine Länder waren bisher auch die umfänglichsten Seelen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0030","text":"Blutzeichen schrieben sie auf den Weg, den sie giengen, und ihre Thorheit lehrte, dass man mit Blut die Wahrheit beweise.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0031","text":"Aber Blut ist der schlechteste Zeuge der Wahrheit; Blut vergiftet die reinste Lehre noch zu Wahn und Hass der Herzen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0032","text":"Und wenn Einer durch’s Feuer geht für seine Lehre,—was beweist diess! Mehr ist’s wahrlich, dass aus eignem Brande die eigne Lehre kommt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0033","text":"Schwüles Herz und kalter Kopf: wo diess zusammentrifft, da entsteht der Brausewind, der „Erlöser“.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0034","text":"Grössere gab es wahrlich und Höher-Geborene, als Die, welche das Volk Erlöser nennt, diese hinreissenden Brausewinde!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0035","text":"Und noch von Grösseren, als alle Erlöser waren, müsst ihr, meine Brüder, erlöst werden, wollt ihr zur Freiheit den Weg finden!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0036","text":"Niemals noch gab es einen Übermenschen. Nackt sah ich Beide, den grössten und den kleinsten Menschen:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0037","text":"Allzuähnlich sind sie noch einander. Wahrlich, auch den Grössten fand ich—allzumenschlich!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0038","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"12fb662aa2b936971fe7d0930543885de1988aac70bf9bdc7832664a1a110042","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181817185246Z_29da849d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Priests","source_sha256":"12fb662aa2b936971fe7d0930543885de1988aac70bf9bdc7832664a1a110042","sha256":"9889a23a0f0c1573e2eada4750fd5bc81bddc39e348f7e45696fbb4cf3331a00","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:18:17.185287+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0001","text":"And once Zarathustra gave his disciples a sign and spoke these words to them:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0002","text":"“Here are priests: and though they are my enemies, pass them by quietly and with sleeping sword!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0003","text":"Among them too are heroes; many of them suffered too much—: thus they wish to make others suffer.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0004","text":"They are evil enemies: nothing is more vengeful than their humility. And he who attacks them easily soils himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0005","text":"But my blood is akin to theirs; and I would have my blood honoured even in theirs.”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0006","text":"And when they had passed by, pain assailed Zarathustra; and he had not long wrestled with his pain before he began to speak thus:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0007","text":"These priests grieve me. They too are against my taste; but that is the least thing to me, since I have been among men.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0008","text":"But I suffer and have suffered with them: to me they are prisoners and marked men. He whom they call Redeemer struck them into bonds:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0009","text":"Into bonds of false values and delusive words! Ah, that someone might yet redeem them from their Redeemer!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0010","text":"Once they believed they had landed on an island, when the sea flung them about; but behold, it was a sleeping monster!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0011","text":"False values and delusive words: these are the worst monsters for mortals,—long does fate sleep and wait within them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0012","text":"But at last it comes and wakes and devours and gulps down whatever built huts upon it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0013","text":"Oh, just look at these huts which these priests built for themselves! Churches they call their sweet-smelling caves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0014","text":"Oh, this falsified light, this swampy air! Here, where the soul is not permitted to fly up—to its height!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0015","text":"But thus their faith commands it: “up the stairs on your knees, ye sinners!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0016","text":"Verily, I would rather see even the shameless man than the contorted eyes of their shame and devotion!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0017","text":"Who made for themselves such caves and stairs of penance? Were they not such as wished to hide themselves and were ashamed before the pure heaven?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0018","text":"And only when the pure heaven again looks through shattered roofs, and down upon grass and red poppies by shattered walls,—then will I turn my heart once more to the abodes of this God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0019","text":"They called God that which contradicted and pained them: and verily, there was much hero-kind in their worship!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0020","text":"And they knew no other way to love their God than by nailing man to the cross!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0021","text":"They thought to live as corpses; blackly they decked out their corpse; even from their speeches I still smell the foul spice of mortuaries.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0022","text":"And whoever lives near them lives near black pools, out of which the toad sings its song with sweet profundity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0023","text":"They would have to sing me better songs, for me to learn to believe in their Redeemer: more redeemed would his disciples have to appear to me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0024","text":"I should like to see them naked: for beauty alone ought to preach penance. But whom, indeed, does this masked melancholy persuade!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0025","text":"Verily, their redeemers themselves did not come out of freedom and freedom’s seventh heaven! Verily, they themselves never walked upon the carpets of knowledge!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0026","text":"The spirit of these redeemers consisted of gaps; but into every gap they had placed their delusion, their gap-filler, which they called God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0027","text":"Their spirit was drowned in their compassion, and when they swelled and overflowed with compassion, a great folly always floated to the surface.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0028","text":"Zealously, and with shouting, they drove their herd across their bridge: as though there were only One bridge to the future! Verily, these shepherds too still belonged among the sheep!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0029","text":"These shepherds had little spirits and capacious souls: but, my brothers, what small countries even the most capacious souls have hitherto been!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0030","text":"They wrote signs of blood upon the path they went, and their folly taught that one proves truth with blood.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0031","text":"But blood is the worst witness of truth; blood poisons even the purest teaching into delusion and hatred of hearts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0032","text":"And if someone goes through fire for his teaching,—what does that prove! Verily, it is more true that one’s own teaching comes from one’s own burning!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0033","text":"Sultry heart and cold head: where these come together, there arises the blustering wind, the “redeemer.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0034","text":"Verily, there have been greater ones and those more highly born than those whom the people call redeemers, these sweeping blustering winds!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0035","text":"And you must, my brothers, be redeemed from still greater ones than all redeemers were, if you wish to find the way to freedom!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0036","text":"Never yet has there been an overman. Naked I saw them both, the greatest human being and the smallest human being:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0037","text":"They are still all too similar to one another. Verily, I found even the greatest—all too human!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0038","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDP0:P0038"]}],"source_digest":"b5206bce06540a6fb87579fa92c0d15ccb2d99ca48043b7a3eadcf80689fbee8","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von den Priestern"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von den Priestern","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0","title":"On the Virtuous","order":42,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDT0","text":"Mit Donnern und himmlischen Feuerwerken muss man zu schlaffen und schlafenden Sinnen reden.\n\nAber der Schönheit Stimme redet leise: sie schleicht sich nur in die aufgewecktesten Seelen.\n\nLeise erbebte und lachte mir heut mein Schild; das ist der Schönheit heiliges Lachen und Beben.\n\nÜber euch, ihr Tugendhaften, lachte heut meine Schönheit. Und also kam ihre Stimme zu mir: „sie wollen noch—bezahlt sein!“\n\nIhr wollt noch bezahlt sein, ihr Tugendhaften! Wollt Lohn für Tugend und Himmel für Erden und Ewiges für euer Heute haben?\n\nUnd nun zürnt ihr mir, dass ich lehre, es giebt keinen Lohn- und Zahlmeister? Und wahrlich, ich lehre nicht einmal, dass Tugend ihr eigener Lohn ist.\n\nAch, das ist meine Trauer: in den Grund der Dinge hat man Lohn und Strafe hineingelogen—und nun auch noch in den Grund eurer Seelen, ihr Tugendhaften!\n\nAber dem Rüssel des Ebers gleich soll mein Wort den Grund eurer Seelen aufreissen; Pflugschar will ich euch heissen.\n\nAlle Heimlichkeiten eures Grundes sollen an’s Licht; und wenn ihr aufgewühlt und zerbrochen in der Sonne liegt, wird auch eure Lüge von eurer Wahrheit ausgeschieden sein.\n\nDenn diess ist eure Wahrheit: ihr seid zu reinlich für den Schmutz der Worte: Rache, Strafe, Lohn, Vergeltung.\n\nIhr liebt eure Tugend, wie die Mutter ihr Kind; aber wann hörte man, dass eine Mutter bezahlt sein wollte für ihre Liebe?\n\nEs ist euer liebstes Selbst, eure Tugend. Des Ringes Durst ist in euch: sich selber wieder zu erreichen, dazu ringt und dreht sich jeder Ring.\n\nUnd dem Sterne gleich, der erlischt, ist jedes Werk eurer Tugend: immer ist sein Licht noch unterwegs und wandert—und wann wird es nicht mehr unterwegs sein?\n\nAlso ist das Licht eurer Tugend noch unterwegs, auch wenn das Werk gethan ist. Mag es nun vergessen und todt sein: sein Strahl von Licht lebt noch und wandert.\n\nDass eure Tugend euer Selbst sei und nicht ein Fremdes, eine Haut, eine Bemäntelung: das ist die Wahrheit aus dem Grunde eurer Seele, ihr Tugendhaften! -\n\nAber wohl giebt es Solche, denen Tugend der Krampf unter einer Peitsche heisst: und ihr habt mir zuviel auf deren Geschrei gehört!\n\nUnd Andre giebt es, die heissen Tugend das Faulwerden ihrer Laster; und wenn ihr Hass und ihre Eifersucht einmal die Glieder strecken, wird ihre „Gerechtigkeit“ munter und reibt sich die verschlafenen Augen.\n\nUnd Andre giebt es, die werden abwärts gezogen: ihre Teufel ziehn sie. Aber je mehr sie sinken, um so glühender leuchtet ihr Auge und die Begierde nach ihrem Gotte.\n\nAch, auch deren Geschrei drang zu euren Ohren, ihr Tugendhaften: was ich nicht bin, das, das ist mir Gott und Tugend!\n\nUnd Andre giebt es, die kommen schwer und knarrend daher, gleich Wägen, die Steine abwärts fahren: die reden viel von Würde und Tugend,—ihren Hemmschuh heissen sie Tugend!\n\nUnd Andre giebt es, die sind gleich Alltags-Uhren, die aufgezogen wurden; sie machen ihr Tiktak und wollen, dass man Tiktak—Tugend heisse.\n\nWahrlich, an Diesen habe ich meine Lust: wo ich solche Uhren finde, werde ich sie mit meinem Spotte aufziehn; und sie sollen mir dabei noch schnurren!\n\nUnd Andre sind stolz über ihre Handvoll Gerechtigkeit und begehen um ihrerwillen Frevel an allen Dingen: also dass die Welt in ihrer Ungerechtigkeit ertränkt wird.\n\nAch, wie übel ihnen das Wort „Tugend“ aus dem Munde läuft! Und wenn sie sagen: „ich bin gerecht,“ so klingt es immer gleich wie: „ich bin gerächt!“\n\nMit ihrer Tugend wollen sie ihren Feinden die Augen auskratzen; und sie erheben sich nur, um Andre zu erniedrigen.\n\nUnd wiederum giebt es Solche, die sitzen in ihrem Sumpfe und reden also heraus aus dem Schilfrohr: „Tugend—das ist still im Sumpfe sitzen.\n\nWir beissen Niemanden und gehen Dem aus dem Wege, der beissen will; und in Allem haben wir die Meinung, die man uns giebt.“\n\nUnd wiederum giebt es Solche, die lieben Gebärden und denken: Tugend ist eine Art Gebärde.\n\nIhre Kniee beten immer an, und ihre Hände sind Lobpreisungen der Tugend, aber ihr Herz weiss Nichts davon.\n\nUnd wiederum giebt es Solche, die halten es für Tugend, zu sagen: „Tugend ist nothwendig“; aber sie glauben im Grunde nur daran, dass Polizei nothwendig ist.\n\nUnd Mancher, der das Hohe an den Menschen nicht sehen kann, nennt es Tugend, dass er ihr Niedriges allzunahe sieht: also heisst er seinen bösen Blick Tugend.\n\nUnd Einige wollen erbaut und aufgerichtet sein und heissen es Tugend; und Andre wollen umgeworfen sein—und heissen es auch Tugend.\n\nUnd derart glauben fast Alle daran, Antheil zu haben an der Tugend; und zum Mindesten will ein jeder Kenner sein über „gut“ und „böse“ .\n\nAber nicht dazu kam Zarathustra, allen diesen Lügnern und Narren zu sagen: „was wisst ihr von Tugend! Was könntet ihr von Tugend wissen!“—\n\nSondern, dass ihr, meine Freunde, der alten Worte müde würdet, welche ihr von den Narren und Lügnern gelernt habt:\n\nMüde würdet der Worte „Lohn,“ „Vergeltung,“ „Strafe,“ „Rache in der Gerechtigkeit“—\n\nMüde würdet zu sagen: „dass eine Handlung gut ist, das macht, sie ist selbstlos.“\n\nAch, meine Freunde! Dass euer Selbst in der Handlung sei, wie die Mutter im Kinde ist: das sei mir euer Wort von Tugend!\n\nWahrlich, ich nahm euch wohl hundert Worte und eurer Tugend liebste Spielwerke; und nun zürnt ihr mir, wie Kinder zürnen.\n\nSie spielten am Meere,—da kam die Welle und riss ihnen ihr Spielwerk in die Tiefe: nun weinen sie.\n\nAber die selbe Welle soll ihnen neue Spielwerke bringen und neue bunte Muscheln vor sie hin ausschütten!\n\nSo werden sie getröstet sein; und gleich ihnen sollt auch ihr, meine Freunde, eure Tröstungen haben—und neue bunte Muscheln!—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0001","text":"Mit Donnern und himmlischen Feuerwerken muss man zu schlaffen und schlafenden Sinnen reden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0002","text":"Aber der Schönheit Stimme redet leise: sie schleicht sich nur in die aufgewecktesten Seelen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0003","text":"Leise erbebte und lachte mir heut mein Schild; das ist der Schönheit heiliges Lachen und Beben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0004","text":"Über euch, ihr Tugendhaften, lachte heut meine Schönheit. Und also kam ihre Stimme zu mir: „sie wollen noch—bezahlt sein!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0005","text":"Ihr wollt noch bezahlt sein, ihr Tugendhaften! Wollt Lohn für Tugend und Himmel für Erden und Ewiges für euer Heute haben?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0006","text":"Und nun zürnt ihr mir, dass ich lehre, es giebt keinen Lohn- und Zahlmeister? Und wahrlich, ich lehre nicht einmal, dass Tugend ihr eigener Lohn ist.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0007","text":"Ach, das ist meine Trauer: in den Grund der Dinge hat man Lohn und Strafe hineingelogen—und nun auch noch in den Grund eurer Seelen, ihr Tugendhaften!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0008","text":"Aber dem Rüssel des Ebers gleich soll mein Wort den Grund eurer Seelen aufreissen; Pflugschar will ich euch heissen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0009","text":"Alle Heimlichkeiten eures Grundes sollen an’s Licht; und wenn ihr aufgewühlt und zerbrochen in der Sonne liegt, wird auch eure Lüge von eurer Wahrheit ausgeschieden sein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0010","text":"Denn diess ist eure Wahrheit: ihr seid zu reinlich für den Schmutz der Worte: Rache, Strafe, Lohn, Vergeltung.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0011","text":"Ihr liebt eure Tugend, wie die Mutter ihr Kind; aber wann hörte man, dass eine Mutter bezahlt sein wollte für ihre Liebe?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0012","text":"Es ist euer liebstes Selbst, eure Tugend. Des Ringes Durst ist in euch: sich selber wieder zu erreichen, dazu ringt und dreht sich jeder Ring.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0013","text":"Und dem Sterne gleich, der erlischt, ist jedes Werk eurer Tugend: immer ist sein Licht noch unterwegs und wandert—und wann wird es nicht mehr unterwegs sein?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0014","text":"Also ist das Licht eurer Tugend noch unterwegs, auch wenn das Werk gethan ist. Mag es nun vergessen und todt sein: sein Strahl von Licht lebt noch und wandert.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0015","text":"Dass eure Tugend euer Selbst sei und nicht ein Fremdes, eine Haut, eine Bemäntelung: das ist die Wahrheit aus dem Grunde eurer Seele, ihr Tugendhaften! -","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0016","text":"Aber wohl giebt es Solche, denen Tugend der Krampf unter einer Peitsche heisst: und ihr habt mir zuviel auf deren Geschrei gehört!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0017","text":"Und Andre giebt es, die heissen Tugend das Faulwerden ihrer Laster; und wenn ihr Hass und ihre Eifersucht einmal die Glieder strecken, wird ihre „Gerechtigkeit“ munter und reibt sich die verschlafenen Augen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0018","text":"Und Andre giebt es, die werden abwärts gezogen: ihre Teufel ziehn sie. Aber je mehr sie sinken, um so glühender leuchtet ihr Auge und die Begierde nach ihrem Gotte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0019","text":"Ach, auch deren Geschrei drang zu euren Ohren, ihr Tugendhaften: was ich nicht bin, das, das ist mir Gott und Tugend!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0020","text":"Und Andre giebt es, die kommen schwer und knarrend daher, gleich Wägen, die Steine abwärts fahren: die reden viel von Würde und Tugend,—ihren Hemmschuh heissen sie Tugend!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0021","text":"Und Andre giebt es, die sind gleich Alltags-Uhren, die aufgezogen wurden; sie machen ihr Tiktak und wollen, dass man Tiktak—Tugend heisse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0022","text":"Wahrlich, an Diesen habe ich meine Lust: wo ich solche Uhren finde, werde ich sie mit meinem Spotte aufziehn; und sie sollen mir dabei noch schnurren!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0023","text":"Und Andre sind stolz über ihre Handvoll Gerechtigkeit und begehen um ihrerwillen Frevel an allen Dingen: also dass die Welt in ihrer Ungerechtigkeit ertränkt wird.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0024","text":"Ach, wie übel ihnen das Wort „Tugend“ aus dem Munde läuft! Und wenn sie sagen: „ich bin gerecht,“ so klingt es immer gleich wie: „ich bin gerächt!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0025","text":"Mit ihrer Tugend wollen sie ihren Feinden die Augen auskratzen; und sie erheben sich nur, um Andre zu erniedrigen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0026","text":"Und wiederum giebt es Solche, die sitzen in ihrem Sumpfe und reden also heraus aus dem Schilfrohr: „Tugend—das ist still im Sumpfe sitzen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0027","text":"Wir beissen Niemanden und gehen Dem aus dem Wege, der beissen will; und in Allem haben wir die Meinung, die man uns giebt.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0028","text":"Und wiederum giebt es Solche, die lieben Gebärden und denken: Tugend ist eine Art Gebärde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0029","text":"Ihre Kniee beten immer an, und ihre Hände sind Lobpreisungen der Tugend, aber ihr Herz weiss Nichts davon.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0030","text":"Und wiederum giebt es Solche, die halten es für Tugend, zu sagen: „Tugend ist nothwendig“; aber sie glauben im Grunde nur daran, dass Polizei nothwendig ist.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0031","text":"Und Mancher, der das Hohe an den Menschen nicht sehen kann, nennt es Tugend, dass er ihr Niedriges allzunahe sieht: also heisst er seinen bösen Blick Tugend.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0032","text":"Und Einige wollen erbaut und aufgerichtet sein und heissen es Tugend; und Andre wollen umgeworfen sein—und heissen es auch Tugend.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0033","text":"Und derart glauben fast Alle daran, Antheil zu haben an der Tugend; und zum Mindesten will ein jeder Kenner sein über „gut“ und „böse“ .","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0034","text":"Aber nicht dazu kam Zarathustra, allen diesen Lügnern und Narren zu sagen: „was wisst ihr von Tugend! Was könntet ihr von Tugend wissen!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0035","text":"Sondern, dass ihr, meine Freunde, der alten Worte müde würdet, welche ihr von den Narren und Lügnern gelernt habt:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0036","text":"Müde würdet der Worte „Lohn,“ „Vergeltung,“ „Strafe,“ „Rache in der Gerechtigkeit“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0037","text":"Müde würdet zu sagen: „dass eine Handlung gut ist, das macht, sie ist selbstlos.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0038","text":"Ach, meine Freunde! Dass euer Selbst in der Handlung sei, wie die Mutter im Kinde ist: das sei mir euer Wort von Tugend!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0039","text":"Wahrlich, ich nahm euch wohl hundert Worte und eurer Tugend liebste Spielwerke; und nun zürnt ihr mir, wie Kinder zürnen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0040","text":"Sie spielten am Meere,—da kam die Welle und riss ihnen ihr Spielwerk in die Tiefe: nun weinen sie.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0041","text":"Aber die selbe Welle soll ihnen neue Spielwerke bringen und neue bunte Muscheln vor sie hin ausschütten!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0042","text":"So werden sie getröstet sein; und gleich ihnen sollt auch ihr, meine Freunde, eure Tröstungen haben—und neue bunte Muscheln!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0043","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"deeb4d35ddde31633794607dcba386b6f2e6a3d6b1e80ceda0b0847fc59fbb3e","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181834393542Z_f1943d62","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Virtuous","source_sha256":"deeb4d35ddde31633794607dcba386b6f2e6a3d6b1e80ceda0b0847fc59fbb3e","sha256":"04baf593af13a5ba5a83c8d6fc3d99bb443afa7123f750983755c8638ef2c9f4","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:18:34.393556+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0001","text":"One must speak to sluggish and sleeping senses with thunder and heavenly fireworks.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0002","text":"But the voice of beauty speaks softly: it steals only into the most awakened souls.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0003","text":"Softly my shield trembled and laughed to me today; that is beauty’s holy laughter and trembling.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0004","text":"Over you, you virtuous ones, my beauty laughed today. And thus its voice came to me: “they still want—to be paid!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0005","text":"You still want to be paid, you virtuous ones! You want reward for virtue and heaven for earth and eternity for your today?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0006","text":"And now you are angry with me because I teach that there is no paymaster and accountant? And verily, I do not even teach that virtue is its own reward.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0007","text":"Ah, this is my sorrow: reward and punishment have been lied into the ground of things—and now even into the ground of your souls, you virtuous ones!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0008","text":"But like the snout of the boar shall my word tear open the ground of your souls; I will call you ploughshares.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0009","text":"All the secrets of your ground shall come to light; and when you lie churned up and broken in the sun, then your lie too shall be separated from your truth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0010","text":"For this is your truth: you are too cleanly for the dirt of the words: revenge, punishment, reward, retribution.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0011","text":"You love your virtue as a mother loves her child; but when has one heard that a mother wished to be paid for her love?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0012","text":"Your virtue is your dearest self. The thirst of the ring is in you: to reach itself again, every ring struggles and turns for that.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0013","text":"And like the star that is extinguished is every work of your virtue: its light is always still on its way and travelling—and when will it no longer be on its way?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0014","text":"Thus the light of your virtue is still on its way, even when the work is done. Though it may now be forgotten and dead: its ray of light still lives and travels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0015","text":"That your virtue be your self and not something alien, a skin, a cloak: that is the truth from the ground of your soul, you virtuous ones! -","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0016","text":"But indeed there are some for whom virtue means the convulsion under a whip: and you have listened too much to their cries!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0017","text":"And there are others who call virtue the becoming-lazy of their vices; and when their hatred and jealousy once stretch their limbs, their “justice” grows lively and rubs its sleepy eyes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0018","text":"And there are others who are drawn downward: their devils draw them. But the more they sink, the more ardently their eye and their desire for their god shine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0019","text":"Ah, the cries of these too reached your ears, you virtuous ones: what I am not, that, that is God and virtue to me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0020","text":"And there are others who come along heavily and creakingly, like carts carrying stones downhill: they speak much of dignity and virtue,—they call their brake-shoe virtue!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0021","text":"And there are others who are like everyday clocks that have been wound; they make their ticktock and want one to call ticktock—virtue.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0022","text":"Verily, I delight in these: where I find such clocks, I shall wind them up with my mockery; and they shall purr for me in the process!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0023","text":"And others are proud of their handful of justice and for its sake commit outrages against all things: so that the world is drowned in their injustice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0024","text":"Ah, how badly the word “virtue” runs from their mouths! And when they say: “I am just,” it always sounds like: “I am avenged!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0025","text":"With their virtue they want to scratch out the eyes of their enemies; and they raise themselves up only in order to humiliate others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0026","text":"And again there are those who sit in their swamp and speak thus out of the reed: “Virtue—that is sitting quietly in the swamp.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0027","text":"We bite no one and keep out of the way of him who wants to bite; and in all things we hold the opinion that is given to us.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0028","text":"And again there are those who love gestures and think: virtue is a kind of gesture.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0029","text":"Their knees are always praying, and their hands are praises of virtue, but their heart knows nothing of it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0030","text":"And again there are those who consider it virtue to say: “Virtue is necessary”; but at bottom they believe only that the police are necessary.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0031","text":"And many a one who cannot see what is high in human beings calls it virtue that he sees their lowliness all too closely: thus he calls his evil gaze virtue.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0032","text":"And some want to be edified and uplifted and call it virtue; and others want to be cast down—and call that virtue too.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0033","text":"And thus almost all believe that they have a share in virtue; and at the very least everyone wants to be a connoisseur of “good” and “evil.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0034","text":"But Zarathustra did not come to tell all these liars and fools: “what do you know of virtue! What could you know of virtue!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0035","text":"But rather that you, my friends, might grow weary of the old words which you have learned from fools and liars:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0036","text":"Might grow weary of the words “reward,” “requital,” “punishment,” “revenge in justice”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0037","text":"Might grow weary of saying: “that an action is good is due to its being selfless.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0038","text":"Ah, my friends! Let your self be in the action as the mother is in the child: let that be for me your word concerning virtue!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0039","text":"Truly, I took from you well-nigh a hundred words and virtue’s dearest playthings; and now you are angry with me, as children are angry.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0040","text":"They were playing by the sea,—then the wave came and tore their plaything down into the depths: now they weep.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0041","text":"But the same wave shall bring them new playthings and scatter new coloured shells before them!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0042","text":"Thus they shall be comforted; and like them you too, my friends, shall have your consolations—and new coloured shells!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0043","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDT0:P0043"]}],"source_digest":"194273ff6ce3ce14ecc0346f66e7c6dd5a625ae4cbbeabe1eb50efb0c9c5405c","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von den Tugendhaften"}],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"Von den Tugendhaften","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG","title":"On the Rabble","order":43,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VOMG","text":"Das Leben ist ein Born der Lust; aber wo das Gesindel mit trinkt, da sind alle Brunnen vergiftet.\n\nAllem Reinlichen bin ich hold; aber ich mag die grinsenden Mäuler nicht sehn und den Durst der Unreinen.\n\nSie warfen ihr Auge hinab in den Brunnen: nun glänzt mir ihr widriges Lächeln herauf aus dem Brunnen.\n\nDas heilige Wasser haben sie vergiftet mit ihrer Lüsternheit; und als sie ihre schmutzigen Träume Lust nannten, vergifteten sie auch noch die Worte.\n\nUnwillig wird die Flamme, wenn sie ihre feuchten Herzen an’s Feuer legen; der Geist selber brodelt und raucht, wo das Gesindel an’s Feuer tritt.\n\nSüsslich und übermürbe wird in ihrer Hand die Frucht: windfällig und wipfeldürr macht ihr Blick den Fruchtbaum.\n\nUnd Mancher, der sich vom Leben abkehrte, kehrte sich nur vom Gesindel ab: er wollte nicht Brunnen und Flamme und Frucht mit dem Gesindel theilen.\n\nUnd Mancher, der in die Wüste gieng und mit Raubthieren Durst litt, wollte nur nicht mit schmutzigen Kameeltreibern um die Cisterne sitzen.\n\nUnd Mancher, der wie ein Vernichter daher kam und wie ein Hagelschlag allen Fruchtfeldern, wollte nur seinen Fuss dem Gesindel in den Rachen setzen und also seinen Schlund stopfen.\n\nUnd nicht das ist der Bissen, an dem ich am meisten würgte, zu wissen, dass das Leben selber Feindschaft nöthig hat und Sterben und Marterkreuze:—\n\nSondern ich fragte einst und erstickte fast an meiner Frage: wie? hat das Leben auch das Gesindel nöthig ?\n\nSind vergiftete Brunnen nöthig und stinkende Feuer und beschmutzte Träume und Maden im Lebensbrode?\n\nNicht mein Hass, sondern mein Ekel frass mir hungrig am Leben! Ach, des Geistes wurde ich oft müde, als ich auch das Gesindel geistreich fand!\n\nUnd den Herrschenden wandt’ich den Rücken, als ich sah, was sie jetzt Herrschen nennen: schachern und markten um Macht—mit dem Gesindel!\n\nUnter Völkern wohnte ich fremder Zunge, mit verschlossenen Ohren: dass mir ihres Schacherns Zunge fremd bliebe und ihr Markten um Macht.\n\nUnd die Nase mir haltend, gieng ich unmuthig durch alles Gestern und Heute: wahrlich, übel riecht alles Gestern und Heute nach dem schreibenden Gesindel!\n\nEinem Krüppel gleich, der taub und blind und stumm wurde: also lebte ich lange, dass ich nicht mit Macht- und Schreib- und Lust-Gesindel lebte.\n\nMühsam stieg mein Geist Treppen, und vorsichtig; Almosen der Lust waren sein Labsal; am Stabe schlich dem Blinden das Leben.\n\nWas geschah mir doch? Wie erlöste ich mich vom Ekel? Wer verjüngte mein Auge? Wie erflog ich die Höhe, wo kein Gesindel mehr am Brunnen sitzt?\n\nSchuf mein Ekel selber mir Flügel und quellenahnende Kräfte? Wahrlich, in’s Höchste musste ich fliegen, dass ich den Born der Lust wiederfände!\n\nOh, ich fand ihn, meine Brüder! Hier im Höchsten quillt mir der Born der Lust! Und es giebt ein Leben, an dem kein Gesindel mit trinkt!\n\nFast zu heftig strömst du mir, Quell der Lust! Und oft leerst du den Becher wieder, dadurch dass du ihn füllen willst!\n\nUnd noch muss ich lernen, bescheidener dir zu nahen: allzuheftig strömt dir noch mein Herz entgegen:—\n\nMein Herz, auf dem mein Sommer brennt, der kurze, heisse, schwermüthige, überselige: wie verlangt mein Sommer-Herz nach deiner Kühle!\n\nVorbei die zögernde Trübsal meines Frühlings! Vorüber die Bosheit meiner Schneeflocken im Juni! Sommer wurde ich ganz und Sommer-Mittag!\n\nEin Sommer im Höchsten mit kalten Quellen und seliger Stille: oh kommt, meine Freunde, dass die Stille noch seliger werde! Denn diess ist unsre Höhe und unsre Heimat: zu hoch und steil wohnen wir hier allen Unreinen und ihrem Durste. Werft nur eure reinen Augen in den Born meiner Lust, ihr Freunde! Wie sollte er darob trübe werden! Entgegenlachen soll er euch mit seiner Reinheit.\n\nAuf dem Baume Zukunft bauen wir unser Nest; Adler sollen uns Einsamen Speise bringen in ihren Schnäbeln!\n\nWahrlich, keine Speise, an der Unsaubere mitessen dürften! Feuer würden sie zu fressen wähnen und sich die Mäuler verbrennen!\n\nWahrlich, keine Heimstätten halten wir hier bereit für Unsaubere! Eishöhle würde ihren Leibern unser Glück heissen und ihren Geistern!\n\nUnd wie starke Winde wollen wir über ihnen leben, Nachbarn den Adlern, Nachbarn dem Schnee, Nachbarn der Sonne: also leben starke Winde.\n\nUnd einem Winde gleich will ich einst noch zwischen sie blasen und mit meinem Geiste ihrem Geiste den Athem nehmen: so will es meine Zukunft.\n\nWahrlich, ein starker Wind ist Zarathustra allen Niederungen; und solchen Rath räth er seinen Feinden und Allem, was spuckt und speit: hütet euch gegen den Wind zu speien!\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0001","text":"Das Leben ist ein Born der Lust; aber wo das Gesindel mit trinkt, da sind alle Brunnen vergiftet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0002","text":"Allem Reinlichen bin ich hold; aber ich mag die grinsenden Mäuler nicht sehn und den Durst der Unreinen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0003","text":"Sie warfen ihr Auge hinab in den Brunnen: nun glänzt mir ihr widriges Lächeln herauf aus dem Brunnen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0004","text":"Das heilige Wasser haben sie vergiftet mit ihrer Lüsternheit; und als sie ihre schmutzigen Träume Lust nannten, vergifteten sie auch noch die Worte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0005","text":"Unwillig wird die Flamme, wenn sie ihre feuchten Herzen an’s Feuer legen; der Geist selber brodelt und raucht, wo das Gesindel an’s Feuer tritt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0006","text":"Süsslich und übermürbe wird in ihrer Hand die Frucht: windfällig und wipfeldürr macht ihr Blick den Fruchtbaum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0007","text":"Und Mancher, der sich vom Leben abkehrte, kehrte sich nur vom Gesindel ab: er wollte nicht Brunnen und Flamme und Frucht mit dem Gesindel theilen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0008","text":"Und Mancher, der in die Wüste gieng und mit Raubthieren Durst litt, wollte nur nicht mit schmutzigen Kameeltreibern um die Cisterne sitzen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0009","text":"Und Mancher, der wie ein Vernichter daher kam und wie ein Hagelschlag allen Fruchtfeldern, wollte nur seinen Fuss dem Gesindel in den Rachen setzen und also seinen Schlund stopfen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0010","text":"Und nicht das ist der Bissen, an dem ich am meisten würgte, zu wissen, dass das Leben selber Feindschaft nöthig hat und Sterben und Marterkreuze:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0011","text":"Sondern ich fragte einst und erstickte fast an meiner Frage: wie? hat das Leben auch das Gesindel nöthig ?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0012","text":"Sind vergiftete Brunnen nöthig und stinkende Feuer und beschmutzte Träume und Maden im Lebensbrode?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0013","text":"Nicht mein Hass, sondern mein Ekel frass mir hungrig am Leben! Ach, des Geistes wurde ich oft müde, als ich auch das Gesindel geistreich fand!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0014","text":"Und den Herrschenden wandt’ich den Rücken, als ich sah, was sie jetzt Herrschen nennen: schachern und markten um Macht—mit dem Gesindel!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0015","text":"Unter Völkern wohnte ich fremder Zunge, mit verschlossenen Ohren: dass mir ihres Schacherns Zunge fremd bliebe und ihr Markten um Macht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0016","text":"Und die Nase mir haltend, gieng ich unmuthig durch alles Gestern und Heute: wahrlich, übel riecht alles Gestern und Heute nach dem schreibenden Gesindel!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0017","text":"Einem Krüppel gleich, der taub und blind und stumm wurde: also lebte ich lange, dass ich nicht mit Macht- und Schreib- und Lust-Gesindel lebte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0018","text":"Mühsam stieg mein Geist Treppen, und vorsichtig; Almosen der Lust waren sein Labsal; am Stabe schlich dem Blinden das Leben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0019","text":"Was geschah mir doch? Wie erlöste ich mich vom Ekel? Wer verjüngte mein Auge? Wie erflog ich die Höhe, wo kein Gesindel mehr am Brunnen sitzt?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0020","text":"Schuf mein Ekel selber mir Flügel und quellenahnende Kräfte? Wahrlich, in’s Höchste musste ich fliegen, dass ich den Born der Lust wiederfände!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0021","text":"Oh, ich fand ihn, meine Brüder! Hier im Höchsten quillt mir der Born der Lust! Und es giebt ein Leben, an dem kein Gesindel mit trinkt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0022","text":"Fast zu heftig strömst du mir, Quell der Lust! Und oft leerst du den Becher wieder, dadurch dass du ihn füllen willst!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0023","text":"Und noch muss ich lernen, bescheidener dir zu nahen: allzuheftig strömt dir noch mein Herz entgegen:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0024","text":"Mein Herz, auf dem mein Sommer brennt, der kurze, heisse, schwermüthige, überselige: wie verlangt mein Sommer-Herz nach deiner Kühle!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0025","text":"Vorbei die zögernde Trübsal meines Frühlings! Vorüber die Bosheit meiner Schneeflocken im Juni! Sommer wurde ich ganz und Sommer-Mittag!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0026","text":"Ein Sommer im Höchsten mit kalten Quellen und seliger Stille: oh kommt, meine Freunde, dass die Stille noch seliger werde! Denn diess ist unsre Höhe und unsre Heimat: zu hoch und steil wohnen wir hier allen Unreinen und ihrem Durste. Werft nur eure reinen Augen in den Born meiner Lust, ihr Freunde! Wie sollte er darob trübe werden! Entgegenlachen soll er euch mit seiner Reinheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0027","text":"Auf dem Baume Zukunft bauen wir unser Nest; Adler sollen uns Einsamen Speise bringen in ihren Schnäbeln!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0028","text":"Wahrlich, keine Speise, an der Unsaubere mitessen dürften! Feuer würden sie zu fressen wähnen und sich die Mäuler verbrennen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0029","text":"Wahrlich, keine Heimstätten halten wir hier bereit für Unsaubere! Eishöhle würde ihren Leibern unser Glück heissen und ihren Geistern!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0030","text":"Und wie starke Winde wollen wir über ihnen leben, Nachbarn den Adlern, Nachbarn dem Schnee, Nachbarn der Sonne: also leben starke Winde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0031","text":"Und einem Winde gleich will ich einst noch zwischen sie blasen und mit meinem Geiste ihrem Geiste den Athem nehmen: so will es meine Zukunft.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0032","text":"Wahrlich, ein starker Wind ist Zarathustra allen Niederungen; und solchen Rath räth er seinen Feinden und Allem, was spuckt und speit: hütet euch gegen den Wind zu speien!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0033","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"08fe50f0d227d65999ea0abc53ae3621bf46e2f5a7a681bf4de0b6824e9f21b8","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181850471678Z_6709e6e8","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Rabble","source_sha256":"08fe50f0d227d65999ea0abc53ae3621bf46e2f5a7a681bf4de0b6824e9f21b8","sha256":"c9539c90074beeaeda81a34ecb5f2922ada2f60a0fe5a37fc042f5be53149718","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:18:50.471687+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0001","text":"Life is a wellspring of delight; but wherever the rabble drinks along with it, all wells are poisoned.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0002","text":"I am fond of all that is clean; but I do not like to see the grinning mouths and the thirst of the unclean.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0003","text":"They cast their eye down into the well: now their repulsive smile shines up at me from the well.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0004","text":"They have poisoned the holy water with their lustfulness; and when they called their filthy dreams delight, they poisoned the words too.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0005","text":"The flame grows ill-tempered when they lay their damp hearts to the fire; the spirit itself seethes and smokes where the rabble approaches the fire.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0006","text":"In their hand the fruit becomes sickly sweet and overripe: their gaze makes the fruit tree liable to windfall and withered at the top.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0007","text":"And many a one who turned away from life turned away only from the rabble: he did not wish to share well and flame and fruit with the rabble.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0008","text":"And many a one who went into the desert and suffered thirst with beasts of prey merely did not wish to sit around the cistern with filthy camel drivers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0009","text":"And many a one who came along like a destroyer and like a hailstorm to all fields of fruit merely wished to set his foot in the rabble's jaws and thus stop its gullet.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0010","text":"And this is not the morsel at which I choked most: to know that life itself needs enmity and dying and torture-crosses:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0011","text":"Rather, I once asked and nearly choked on my question: what? does life also need the rabble?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0012","text":"Are poisoned wells necessary, and stinking fires and soiled dreams and maggots in life's bread?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0013","text":"It was not my hatred, but my disgust that gnawed hungrily at life! Ah, I often grew weary of the spirit when I found even the rabble spiritual!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0014","text":"And I turned my back on the rulers when I saw what they now call ruling: huckstering and bargaining over power—with the rabble!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0015","text":"I lived among peoples of foreign tongue, with closed ears: so that the tongue of their huckstering and their bargaining over power might remain foreign to me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0016","text":"And holding my nose, I went resentfully through all yesterday and today: verily, all yesterday and today smell foul of the writing rabble!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0017","text":"Like a cripple who became deaf and blind and dumb: thus I lived for a long time, so as not to live with the power-rabble and writing-rabble and pleasure-rabble.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0018","text":"Laboriously my spirit climbed stairs, and cautiously; alms of pleasure were its refreshment; life crept along for the blind man on a staff.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0019","text":"What then happened to me? How did I redeem myself from disgust? Who rejuvenated my eye? How did I fly up to the height where no rabble sits any longer at the well?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0020","text":"Did my disgust itself create wings for me and powers that sense springs? Verily, I had to fly into the highest, so that I might find again the wellspring of delight!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0021","text":"Oh, I found it, my brothers! Here in the highest the wellspring of delight wells up for me! And there is a life from which no rabble drinks!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0022","text":"You flow almost too violently for me, spring of delight! And often you empty the cup again by wishing to fill it!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0023","text":"And still I must learn to approach you more modestly: my heart still flows too vehemently towards you:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0024","text":"My heart, upon which my summer burns—the brief, hot, melancholy, over-blissful one: how my summer-heart longs for your coolness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0025","text":"Gone is the hesitant melancholy of my spring! Gone the malice of my snowflakes in June! I became wholly summer and summer-noontide!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0026","text":"A summer at the highest height, with cold springs and blissful stillness: oh come, my friends, that the stillness may become yet more blissful! For this is our height and our home: too high and steeply do we dwell here for all the unclean and their thirst. Cast but your pure eyes into the fountain of my delight, you friends! How should it thereby become turbid! It shall laugh back at you with its purity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0027","text":"Upon the tree Future we build our nest; eagles shall bring food to us solitary ones in their beaks!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0028","text":"Verily, no food at which the unclean might be allowed to eat! They would fancy they were eating fire and burn their mouths!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0029","text":"Verily, we keep no homesteads ready here for the unclean! Our happiness would be called an ice-cave by their bodies and by their spirits!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0030","text":"And like strong winds we wish to live above them, neighbours to the eagles, neighbours to the snow, neighbours to the sun: thus do strong winds live.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0031","text":"And like a wind I will one day yet blow among them and with my spirit take the breath from their spirit: thus my future wills it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0032","text":"Verily, Zarathustra is a strong wind to all lowlands; and such counsel does he counsel his enemies and all that spits and spews: beware of spitting against the wind!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0033","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VOMG:P0033"]}],"source_digest":"fd28b0ce7fa5a903b8afa5e1ab84a6300d22747dbca9133222552ba5c34e9f04","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Vom Gesindel"}],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"Vom Gesindel","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2","title":"On the Tarantulas","order":44,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VD_2","text":"Siehe, das ist der Tarantel Höhle! Willst du sie selber sehn? Hier hängt ihr Netz: rühre daran, dass es erzittert.\n\nDa kommt sie willig: willkommen, Tarantel! Schwarz sitzt auf deinem Rücken dein Dreieck und Wahrzeichen; und ich weiss auch, was in deiner Seele sitzt.\n\nRache sitzt in deiner Seele: wohin du beissest, da wächst schwarzer Schorf; mit Rache macht dein Gift die Seele drehend!\n\nAlso rede ich zu euch im Gleichniss, die ihr die Seelen drehend macht, ihr Prediger der Gleichheit ! Taranteln seid ihr mir und versteckte Rachsüchtige!\n\nAber ich will eure Verstecke schon an’s Licht bringen: darum lache ich euch in’s Antlitz mein Gelächter der Höhe.\n\nDarum reisse ich an eurem Netze, dass eure Wuth euch aus eurer Lügen-Höhle locke, und eure Rache hervorspringe hinter eurem Wort „Gerechtigkeit.“\n\nDenn dass der Mensch erlöst werde von der Rache: das ist mir die Brücke zur höchsten Hoffnung und ein Regenbogen nach langen Unwettern.\n\nAber anders wollen es freilich die Taranteln. „Das gerade heisse uns Gerechtigkeit, dass die Welt voll werde von den Unwettern unsrer Rache“ —also reden sie mit einander.\n\n„Rache wollen wir üben und Beschimpfung an Allen, die uns nicht gleich sind“—so geloben sich die Tarantel-Herzen.\n\n„Und „Wille zur Gleichheit“—das selber soll fürderhin der Name für Tugend werden; und gegen Alles, was Macht hat, wollen wir unser Geschrei erheben!“\n\nIhr Prediger der Gleichheit, der Tyrannen-Wahnsinn der Ohnmacht schreit also aus euch nach „Gleichheit“: eure heimlichsten Tyrannen-Gelüste vermummen sich also in Tugend-Worte!\n\nVergrämter Dünkel, verhaltener Neid, vielleicht eurer Väter Dünkel und Neid: aus euch bricht’s als Flamme heraus und Wahnsinn der Rache.\n\nWas der Vater schwieg, das kommt im Sohne zum Reden; und oft fand ich den Sohn als des Vaters entblösstes Geheimniss.\n\nDen Begeisterten gleichen sie: aber nicht das Herz ist es, was sie begeistert, —sondern die Rache. Und wenn sie fein und kalt werden, ist’s nicht der Geist, sondern der Neid, der sie fein und kalt macht.\n\nIhre Eifersucht führt sie auch auf der Denker Pfade; und diess ist das Merkmal ihrer Eifersucht—immer gehn sie zu weit: dass ihre Müdigkeit sich zuletzt noch auf Schnee schlafen legen muss.\n\nAus jeder ihrer Klagen tönt Rache, in jedem ihrer Lobsprüche ist ein Wehethun; und Richter-sein scheint ihnen Seligkeit.\n\nAlso aber rathe ich euch, meine Freunde: misstraut Allen, in welchen der Trieb, zu strafen, mächtig ist!\n\nDas ist Volk schlechter Art und Abkunft; aus ihren Gesichtern blickt der Henker und der Spürhund.\n\nMisstraut allen Denen, die viel von ihrer Gerechtigkeit reden! Wahrlich, ihren Seelen fehlt es nicht nur an Honig.\n\nUnd wenn sie sich selber „die Guten und Gerechten“ nennen, so vergesst nicht, dass ihnen zum Pharisäer Nichts fehlt als—Macht!\n\nMeine Freunde, ich will nicht vermischt und verwechselt werden.\n\nEs giebt Solche, die predigen meine Lehre vom Leben: und zugleich sind sie Prediger der Gleichheit und Taranteln.\n\nDass sie dem Leben zu Willen reden, ob sie gleich in ihrer Höhle sitzen, diese Gift-Spinnen, und abgekehrt vom Leben: das macht, sie wollen damit wehethun.\n\nSolchen wollen sie damit wehethun, die jetzt die Macht haben: denn bei diesen ist noch die Predigt vom Tode am besten zu Hause.\n\nWäre es anders, so würden die Taranteln anders lehren: und gerade sie waren ehemals die besten Welt-Verleumder und Ketzer-Brenner.\n\nMit diesen Predigern der Gleichheit will ich nicht vermischt und verwechselt sein. Denn so redet mir die Gerechtigkeit: „die Menschen sind nicht gleich.“\n\nUnd sie sollen es auch nicht werden! Was wäre denn meine Liebe zum Übermenschen, wenn ich anders spräche?\n\nAuf tausend Brücken und Stegen sollen sie sich drängen zur Zukunft, und immer mehr Krieg und Ungleichheit soll zwischen sie gesetzt sein: so lässt mich meine grosse Liebe reden!\n\nErfinder von Bildern und Gespenstern sollen sie werden in ihren Feindschaften, und mit ihren Bildern und Gespenstern sollen sie noch gegeneinander den höchsten Kampf kämpfen!\n\nGut und Böse, und Reich und Arm, und Hoch und Gering, und alle Namen der Werthe: Waffen sollen es sein und klirrende Merkmale davon, dass das Leben sich immer wieder selber überwinden muss!\n\nIn die Höhe will es sich bauen mit Pfeilern und Stufen, das Leben selber: in weite Fernen will es blicken und hinaus nach seligen Schönheiten,— darum braucht es Höhe!\n\nUnd weil es Höhe braucht, braucht es Stufen und Widerspruch der Stufen und Steigenden! Steigen will das Leben und steigend sich überwinden.\n\nUnd seht mir doch, meine Freunde! Hier, wo der Tarantel Höhle ist, heben sich eines alten Tempels Trümmer aufwärts,—seht mir doch mit erleuchteten Augen hin!\n\nWahrlich, wer hier einst seine Gedanken in Stein nach Oben thürmte, um das Geheimniss alles Lebens wusste er gleich dem Weisesten!\n\nDass Kampf und Ungleiches auch noch in der Schönheit sei und Krieg um Macht und Übermacht: das lehrt er uns hier im deutlichsten Gleichniss.\n\nWie sich göttlich hier Gewölbe und Bogen brechen, im Ringkampfe: wie mit Licht und Schatten sie wider einander streben, die göttlich-Strebenden—\n\nAlso sicher und schön lasst uns auch Feinde sein, meine Freunde! Göttlich wollen wir wider einander streben!—\n\nWehe! Da biss mich selber die Tarantel, meine alte Feindin! Göttlich sicher und schön biss sie mich in den Finger!\n\n„Strafe muss sein und Gerechtigkeit—so denkt sie: nicht umsonst soll er hier der Feindschaft zu Ehren Lieder singen!“\n\nJa, sie hat sich gerächt! Und wehe! nun wird sie mit Rache auch noch meine Seele drehend machen!\n\nDass ich mich aber nicht drehe, meine Freunde, bindet mich fest hier an diese Säule! Lieber noch Säulen-Heiliger will ich sein, als Wirbel der Rachsucht!\n\nWahrlich, kein Dreh- und Wirbelwind ist Zarathustra; und wenn er ein Tänzer ist, nimmermehr doch ein Tarantel-Tänzer!—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0001","text":"Siehe, das ist der Tarantel Höhle! Willst du sie selber sehn? Hier hängt ihr Netz: rühre daran, dass es erzittert.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0002","text":"Da kommt sie willig: willkommen, Tarantel! Schwarz sitzt auf deinem Rücken dein Dreieck und Wahrzeichen; und ich weiss auch, was in deiner Seele sitzt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0003","text":"Rache sitzt in deiner Seele: wohin du beissest, da wächst schwarzer Schorf; mit Rache macht dein Gift die Seele drehend!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0004","text":"Also rede ich zu euch im Gleichniss, die ihr die Seelen drehend macht, ihr Prediger der Gleichheit ! Taranteln seid ihr mir und versteckte Rachsüchtige!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0005","text":"Aber ich will eure Verstecke schon an’s Licht bringen: darum lache ich euch in’s Antlitz mein Gelächter der Höhe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0006","text":"Darum reisse ich an eurem Netze, dass eure Wuth euch aus eurer Lügen-Höhle locke, und eure Rache hervorspringe hinter eurem Wort „Gerechtigkeit.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0007","text":"Denn dass der Mensch erlöst werde von der Rache: das ist mir die Brücke zur höchsten Hoffnung und ein Regenbogen nach langen Unwettern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0008","text":"Aber anders wollen es freilich die Taranteln. „Das gerade heisse uns Gerechtigkeit, dass die Welt voll werde von den Unwettern unsrer Rache“ —also reden sie mit einander.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0009","text":"„Rache wollen wir üben und Beschimpfung an Allen, die uns nicht gleich sind“—so geloben sich die Tarantel-Herzen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0010","text":"„Und „Wille zur Gleichheit“—das selber soll fürderhin der Name für Tugend werden; und gegen Alles, was Macht hat, wollen wir unser Geschrei erheben!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0011","text":"Ihr Prediger der Gleichheit, der Tyrannen-Wahnsinn der Ohnmacht schreit also aus euch nach „Gleichheit“: eure heimlichsten Tyrannen-Gelüste vermummen sich also in Tugend-Worte!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0012","text":"Vergrämter Dünkel, verhaltener Neid, vielleicht eurer Väter Dünkel und Neid: aus euch bricht’s als Flamme heraus und Wahnsinn der Rache.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0013","text":"Was der Vater schwieg, das kommt im Sohne zum Reden; und oft fand ich den Sohn als des Vaters entblösstes Geheimniss.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0014","text":"Den Begeisterten gleichen sie: aber nicht das Herz ist es, was sie begeistert, —sondern die Rache. Und wenn sie fein und kalt werden, ist’s nicht der Geist, sondern der Neid, der sie fein und kalt macht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0015","text":"Ihre Eifersucht führt sie auch auf der Denker Pfade; und diess ist das Merkmal ihrer Eifersucht—immer gehn sie zu weit: dass ihre Müdigkeit sich zuletzt noch auf Schnee schlafen legen muss.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0016","text":"Aus jeder ihrer Klagen tönt Rache, in jedem ihrer Lobsprüche ist ein Wehethun; und Richter-sein scheint ihnen Seligkeit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0017","text":"Also aber rathe ich euch, meine Freunde: misstraut Allen, in welchen der Trieb, zu strafen, mächtig ist!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0018","text":"Das ist Volk schlechter Art und Abkunft; aus ihren Gesichtern blickt der Henker und der Spürhund.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0019","text":"Misstraut allen Denen, die viel von ihrer Gerechtigkeit reden! Wahrlich, ihren Seelen fehlt es nicht nur an Honig.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0020","text":"Und wenn sie sich selber „die Guten und Gerechten“ nennen, so vergesst nicht, dass ihnen zum Pharisäer Nichts fehlt als—Macht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0021","text":"Meine Freunde, ich will nicht vermischt und verwechselt werden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0022","text":"Es giebt Solche, die predigen meine Lehre vom Leben: und zugleich sind sie Prediger der Gleichheit und Taranteln.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0023","text":"Dass sie dem Leben zu Willen reden, ob sie gleich in ihrer Höhle sitzen, diese Gift-Spinnen, und abgekehrt vom Leben: das macht, sie wollen damit wehethun.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0024","text":"Solchen wollen sie damit wehethun, die jetzt die Macht haben: denn bei diesen ist noch die Predigt vom Tode am besten zu Hause.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0025","text":"Wäre es anders, so würden die Taranteln anders lehren: und gerade sie waren ehemals die besten Welt-Verleumder und Ketzer-Brenner.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0026","text":"Mit diesen Predigern der Gleichheit will ich nicht vermischt und verwechselt sein. Denn so redet mir die Gerechtigkeit: „die Menschen sind nicht gleich.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0027","text":"Und sie sollen es auch nicht werden! Was wäre denn meine Liebe zum Übermenschen, wenn ich anders spräche?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0028","text":"Auf tausend Brücken und Stegen sollen sie sich drängen zur Zukunft, und immer mehr Krieg und Ungleichheit soll zwischen sie gesetzt sein: so lässt mich meine grosse Liebe reden!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0029","text":"Erfinder von Bildern und Gespenstern sollen sie werden in ihren Feindschaften, und mit ihren Bildern und Gespenstern sollen sie noch gegeneinander den höchsten Kampf kämpfen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0030","text":"Gut und Böse, und Reich und Arm, und Hoch und Gering, und alle Namen der Werthe: Waffen sollen es sein und klirrende Merkmale davon, dass das Leben sich immer wieder selber überwinden muss!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0031","text":"In die Höhe will es sich bauen mit Pfeilern und Stufen, das Leben selber: in weite Fernen will es blicken und hinaus nach seligen Schönheiten,— darum braucht es Höhe!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0032","text":"Und weil es Höhe braucht, braucht es Stufen und Widerspruch der Stufen und Steigenden! Steigen will das Leben und steigend sich überwinden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0033","text":"Und seht mir doch, meine Freunde! Hier, wo der Tarantel Höhle ist, heben sich eines alten Tempels Trümmer aufwärts,—seht mir doch mit erleuchteten Augen hin!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0034","text":"Wahrlich, wer hier einst seine Gedanken in Stein nach Oben thürmte, um das Geheimniss alles Lebens wusste er gleich dem Weisesten!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0035","text":"Dass Kampf und Ungleiches auch noch in der Schönheit sei und Krieg um Macht und Übermacht: das lehrt er uns hier im deutlichsten Gleichniss.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0036","text":"Wie sich göttlich hier Gewölbe und Bogen brechen, im Ringkampfe: wie mit Licht und Schatten sie wider einander streben, die göttlich-Strebenden—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0037","text":"Also sicher und schön lasst uns auch Feinde sein, meine Freunde! Göttlich wollen wir wider einander streben!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0038","text":"Wehe! Da biss mich selber die Tarantel, meine alte Feindin! Göttlich sicher und schön biss sie mich in den Finger!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0039","text":"„Strafe muss sein und Gerechtigkeit—so denkt sie: nicht umsonst soll er hier der Feindschaft zu Ehren Lieder singen!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0040","text":"Ja, sie hat sich gerächt! Und wehe! nun wird sie mit Rache auch noch meine Seele drehend machen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0041","text":"Dass ich mich aber nicht drehe, meine Freunde, bindet mich fest hier an diese Säule! Lieber noch Säulen-Heiliger will ich sein, als Wirbel der Rachsucht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0042","text":"Wahrlich, kein Dreh- und Wirbelwind ist Zarathustra; und wenn er ein Tänzer ist, nimmermehr doch ein Tarantel-Tänzer!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0043","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"7c01b2640ace94c85e26490404d668017959a93f6cb6afc2488fa72625f54487","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181911519667Z_ef998a20","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Tarantulas","source_sha256":"7c01b2640ace94c85e26490404d668017959a93f6cb6afc2488fa72625f54487","sha256":"7aa220f301ea179af846bb2e53fa9723fc3a1dc050d58d8e6105a37e9e2aca1f","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:19:11.519685+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0001","text":"Behold, this is the tarantula’s cave! Would you see it yourself? Here hangs its web: touch it, so that it trembles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0002","text":"There it comes willingly: welcome, tarantula! Black upon your back sits your triangle and emblem; and I know, too, what sits in your soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0003","text":"Revenge sits in your soul: wherever you bite, there grows black scab; with revenge your poison makes the soul turn!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0004","text":"Thus do I speak to you in parable, you who make souls turn, you preachers of equality! You are tarantulas to me, and hidden vindictive ones!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0005","text":"But I shall bring your hiding-places to light: therefore I laugh in your faces my laughter of the heights.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0006","text":"Therefore I pull at your web, so that your fury may lure you out of your cave of lies, and your revenge leap forth from behind your word “justice.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0007","text":"For that man be redeemed from revenge: that is to me the bridge to the highest hope and a rainbow after long storms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0008","text":"But of course the tarantulas want it otherwise. “That precisely is what justice means to us: that the world become full of the storms of our revenge”—thus they speak among themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0009","text":"“We want to practise revenge and abuse upon all who are not our equals”—thus do the tarantula-hearts pledge themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0010","text":"“And ‘will to equality’—that itself shall henceforth become the name for virtue; and against everything that has power we shall raise our outcry!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0011","text":"You preachers of equality, the tyrant-madness of impotence thus cries out from you after “equality”: your most secret tyrannical lusts thus disguise themselves in words of virtue!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0012","text":"Embittered arrogance, repressed envy, perhaps your fathers’ arrogance and envy: from you it bursts forth as flame and madness of revenge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0013","text":"What the father kept silent comes to speech in the son; and often have I found the son to be the father’s unveiled secret.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0014","text":"They resemble the inspired: but it is not the heart that inspires them,—but revenge. And when they become subtle and cold, it is not the spirit, but envy, that makes them subtle and cold.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0015","text":"Their jealousy leads them also along the paths of thinkers; and this is the mark of their jealousy—they always go too far: so that their weariness must at last even lay itself down to sleep upon snow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0016","text":"Revenge resounds from each of their complaints, in each of their praises there is an infliction of pain; and being judge seems bliss to them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0017","text":"But thus I counsel you, my friends: distrust all in whom the drive to punish is powerful!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0018","text":"They are people of a base kind and lineage; from their faces the executioner and the bloodhound look forth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0019","text":"Distrust all those who speak much of their justice! Verily, it is not honey alone that their souls lack.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0020","text":"And when they call themselves “the good and the just,” do not forget that, to be Pharisees, they lack nothing but—power!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0021","text":"My friends, I do not wish to be mixed up and confused.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0022","text":"There are some who preach my doctrine of life: and at the same time they are preachers of equality and tarantulas.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0023","text":"That they speak in favour of life, although they sit in their cave, these poisonous spiders, and turned away from life: this is because they wish thereby to inflict pain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0024","text":"They wish thereby to inflict pain upon those who now have power: for with these the preaching of death is still most at home.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0025","text":"Were it otherwise, then the tarantulas would teach otherwise: and it was precisely they who were formerly the best slanderers of the world and burners of heretics.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0026","text":"With these preachers of equality I will not be mixed up and confused. For thus justice speaks to me: “men are not equal.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0027","text":"And they should not become so either! What would my love for the overman be, if I spoke otherwise?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0028","text":"Upon a thousand bridges and gangways they shall press towards the future, and ever more war and inequality shall be set between them: thus does my great love make me speak!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0029","text":"Inventors of images and phantoms shall they become in their enmities, and with their images and phantoms they shall yet fight the highest battle against one another!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0030","text":"Good and evil, and rich and poor, and high and low, and all names of values: they shall be weapons and clattering tokens that life must again and again overcome itself!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0031","text":"It wants to build itself upwards with pillars and steps, life itself: it wants to look out into wide distances and forth toward blessed beauties,—therefore it needs height!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0032","text":"And because it needs height, it needs steps and contradiction between steps and climbers! Life wants to climb and, climbing, to overcome itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0033","text":"And just look, my friends! Here, where the tarantula’s cave is, the ruins of an old temple rise upwards,—just look thither with enlightened eyes!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0034","text":"Truly, whoever once here piled his thoughts upward in stone, knew the secret of all life, like the wisest man!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0035","text":"That struggle and inequality are also still in beauty, and war for power and over-power: this he teaches us here in the clearest parable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0036","text":"How divinely vault and arch here break one another, in wrestling combat: how they strive against one another with light and shadow, the divinely striving—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0037","text":"Thus, too, let us be enemies, secure and beautiful, my friends! Divinely would we strive against one another!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0038","text":"Woe! The tarantula itself, my old enemy, has bitten me! Divinely secure and beautiful it bit me on the finger!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0039","text":"“There must be punishment and justice—thus she thinks: not for nothing shall he here sing songs in honour of enmity!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0040","text":"Yes, she has avenged herself! And woe! now with vengeance she will make my soul too turn round!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0041","text":"But lest I turn round, my friends, bind me fast here to this pillar! I would rather be a pillar-saint than a whirl of vengefulness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0042","text":"Truly, Zarathustra is no turning and whirling wind; and if he is a dancer, never a tarantula-dancer!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0043","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VD_2:P0043"]}],"source_digest":"c701d12604b49409913bde8fb9519375bd84ad6db707381165ad90bb64e83907","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von den Taranteln"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von den Taranteln","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH","title":"On the Famous Wise Men","order":45,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDBH","text":"Dem Volke habt ihr gedient und des Volkes Aberglauben, ihr berühmten Weisen alle!—und nicht der Wahrheit! Und gerade darum zollte man euch Ehrfurcht.\n\nUnd darum auch ertrug man euren Unglauben, weil er ein Witz und Umweg war zum Volke. So lässt der Herr seine Sclaven gewähren und ergötzt sich noch an ihrem Übermuthe.\n\nAber wer dem Volke verhasst ist wie ein Wolf den Hunden: das ist der freie Geist, der Fessel-Feind, der Nicht-Anbeter, der in Wäldern Hausende.\n\nIhn zu jagen aus seinem Schlupfe—das hiess immer dem Volke „Sinn für das Rechte“ : gegen ihn hetzt es noch immer seine scharfzahnigsten Hunde.\n\n„Denn die Wahrheit ist da: ist das Volk doch da! Wehe, wehe den Suchenden!“—also scholl es von jeher.\n\nEurem Volke wolltet ihr Recht schaffen in seiner Verehrung: das hiesset ihr „Wille zur Wahrheit,“ ihr berühmten Weisen!\n\nUnd euer Herz sprach immer zu sich: „vom Volke kam ich: von dort her kam mir auch Gottes Stimme.“\n\nHart-nackig und klug, dem Esel gleich, wart ihr immer als des Volkes Fürsprecher.\n\nUnd mancher Mächtige, der gut fahren wollte mit dem Volke, spannte vor seine Rosse noch—ein Eselein, einen berühmten Weisen.\n\nUnd nun wollte ich, ihr berühmten Weisen, ihr würfet endlich das Fell des Löwen ganz von euch!\n\nDas Fell des Raubthiers, das buntgefleckte, und die Zotten des Forschenden, Suchenden, Erobernden!\n\nAch, dass ich an eure „Wahrhaftigkeit“ glauben lerne, dazu müsstet ihr mir erst euren verehrenden Willen zerbrechen.\n\nWahrhaftig—so heisse ich Den, der in götterlose Wüsten geht und sein verehrendes Herz zerbrochen hat.\n\nIm gelben Sande und verbrannt von der Sonne schielt er wohl durstig nach den quellenreichen Eilanden, wo Lebendiges unter dunkeln Bäumen ruht.\n\nAber sein Durst überredet ihn nicht, diesen Behaglichen gleich zu werden: denn wo Oasen sind, da sind auch Götzenbilder.\n\nHungernd, gewaltthätig, einsam, gottlos: so will sich selber der Löwen-Wille.\n\nFrei von dem Glück der Knechte, erlöst von Göttern und Anbetungen, furchtlos und fürchterlich, gross und einsam: so ist der Wille des Wahrhaftigen.\n\nIn der Wüste wohnten von je die Wahrhaftigen, die freien Geister, als der Wüste Herren; aber in den Städten wohnen die gutgefütterten, berühmten Weisen,—die Zugthiere.\n\nImmer nämlich ziehen sie, als Esel—des Volkes Karren!\n\nNicht dass ich ihnen darob zürne: aber Dienende bleiben sie mir und Angeschirrte, auch wenn sie von goldnem Geschirre glänzen.\n\nUnd oft waren sie gute Diener und preiswürdige. Denn so spricht die Tugend: musst du Diener sein, so suche Den, welchem dein Dienst am besten nützt!\n\n„Der Geist und die Tugend deines Herrn sollen wachsen, dadurch dass du sein Diener bist: so wächsest du selber mit seinem Geiste und seiner Tugend!“\n\nUnd wahrlich, ihr berühmten Weisen, ihr Diener des Volkes! Ihr selber wuchset mit des Volkes Geist und Tugend—und das Volk durch euch! Zu euren Ehren sage ich das!\n\nAber Volk bleibt ihr mir auch noch in euren Tugenden, Volk mit blöden Augen,—Volk, das nicht weiss, was Geist ist!\n\nGeist ist das Leben, das selber in’s Leben schneidet: an der eignen Qual mehrt es sich das eigne Wissen,—wusstet ihr das schon?\n\nUnd des Geistes Glück ist diess: gesalbt zu sein und durch Thränen geweiht zum Opferthier,—wusstet ihr das schon?\n\nUnd die Blindheit des Blinden und sein Suchen und Tappen soll noch von der Macht der Sonne zeugen, in die er schaute,—wusstet ihr das schon?\n\nUnd mit Bergen soll der Erkennende bauen lernen! Wenig ist es, dass der Geist Berge versetzt,—wusstet ihr das schon?\n\nIhr kennt nur des Geistes Funken: aber ihr seht den Ambos nicht, der er ist, und nicht die Grausamkeit seines Hammers!\n\nWahrlich, ihr kennt des Geistes Stolz nicht! Aber noch weniger würdet ihr des Geistes Bescheidenheit ertragen, wenn sie einmal reden wollte!\n\nUnd niemals noch durftet ihr euren Geist in eine Grube von Schnee werfen: ihr seid nicht heiss genug dazu! So kennt ihr auch die Entzückungen seiner Kälte nicht.\n\nIn Allem aber thut ihr mir zu vertraulich mit dem Geiste; und aus der Weisheit machtet ihr oft ein Armen- und Krankenhaus für schlechte Dichter.\n\nIhr seid keine Adler: so erfuhrt ihr auch das Glück im Schrecken des Geistes nicht. Und wer kein Vogel ist, soll sich nicht über Abgründen lagern.\n\nIhr seid mir Laue: aber kalt strömt jede tiefe Erkenntniss. Eiskalt sind die innersten Brunnen des Geistes: ein Labsal heissen Händen und Handelnden.\n\nEhrbar steht ihr mir da und steif und mit geradem Rücken, ihr berühmten Weisen! —euch treibt kein starker Wind und Wille.\n\nSaht ihr nie ein Segel über das Meer gehn, geründet und gebläht und zitternd vor dem Ungestüm des Windes?\n\nDem Segel gleich, zitternd vor dem Ungestüm des Geistes, geht meine Weisheit über das Meer—meine wilde Weisheit!\n\nAber ihr Diener des Volkes, ihr berühmten Weisen,—wie könntet ihr mit mir gehn!—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0001","text":"Dem Volke habt ihr gedient und des Volkes Aberglauben, ihr berühmten Weisen alle!—und nicht der Wahrheit! Und gerade darum zollte man euch Ehrfurcht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0002","text":"Und darum auch ertrug man euren Unglauben, weil er ein Witz und Umweg war zum Volke. So lässt der Herr seine Sclaven gewähren und ergötzt sich noch an ihrem Übermuthe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0003","text":"Aber wer dem Volke verhasst ist wie ein Wolf den Hunden: das ist der freie Geist, der Fessel-Feind, der Nicht-Anbeter, der in Wäldern Hausende.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0004","text":"Ihn zu jagen aus seinem Schlupfe—das hiess immer dem Volke „Sinn für das Rechte“ : gegen ihn hetzt es noch immer seine scharfzahnigsten Hunde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0005","text":"„Denn die Wahrheit ist da: ist das Volk doch da! Wehe, wehe den Suchenden!“—also scholl es von jeher.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0006","text":"Eurem Volke wolltet ihr Recht schaffen in seiner Verehrung: das hiesset ihr „Wille zur Wahrheit,“ ihr berühmten Weisen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0007","text":"Und euer Herz sprach immer zu sich: „vom Volke kam ich: von dort her kam mir auch Gottes Stimme.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0008","text":"Hart-nackig und klug, dem Esel gleich, wart ihr immer als des Volkes Fürsprecher.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0009","text":"Und mancher Mächtige, der gut fahren wollte mit dem Volke, spannte vor seine Rosse noch—ein Eselein, einen berühmten Weisen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0010","text":"Und nun wollte ich, ihr berühmten Weisen, ihr würfet endlich das Fell des Löwen ganz von euch!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0011","text":"Das Fell des Raubthiers, das buntgefleckte, und die Zotten des Forschenden, Suchenden, Erobernden!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0012","text":"Ach, dass ich an eure „Wahrhaftigkeit“ glauben lerne, dazu müsstet ihr mir erst euren verehrenden Willen zerbrechen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0013","text":"Wahrhaftig—so heisse ich Den, der in götterlose Wüsten geht und sein verehrendes Herz zerbrochen hat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0014","text":"Im gelben Sande und verbrannt von der Sonne schielt er wohl durstig nach den quellenreichen Eilanden, wo Lebendiges unter dunkeln Bäumen ruht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0015","text":"Aber sein Durst überredet ihn nicht, diesen Behaglichen gleich zu werden: denn wo Oasen sind, da sind auch Götzenbilder.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0016","text":"Hungernd, gewaltthätig, einsam, gottlos: so will sich selber der Löwen-Wille.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0017","text":"Frei von dem Glück der Knechte, erlöst von Göttern und Anbetungen, furchtlos und fürchterlich, gross und einsam: so ist der Wille des Wahrhaftigen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0018","text":"In der Wüste wohnten von je die Wahrhaftigen, die freien Geister, als der Wüste Herren; aber in den Städten wohnen die gutgefütterten, berühmten Weisen,—die Zugthiere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0019","text":"Immer nämlich ziehen sie, als Esel—des Volkes Karren!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0020","text":"Nicht dass ich ihnen darob zürne: aber Dienende bleiben sie mir und Angeschirrte, auch wenn sie von goldnem Geschirre glänzen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0021","text":"Und oft waren sie gute Diener und preiswürdige. Denn so spricht die Tugend: musst du Diener sein, so suche Den, welchem dein Dienst am besten nützt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0022","text":"„Der Geist und die Tugend deines Herrn sollen wachsen, dadurch dass du sein Diener bist: so wächsest du selber mit seinem Geiste und seiner Tugend!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0023","text":"Und wahrlich, ihr berühmten Weisen, ihr Diener des Volkes! Ihr selber wuchset mit des Volkes Geist und Tugend—und das Volk durch euch! Zu euren Ehren sage ich das!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0024","text":"Aber Volk bleibt ihr mir auch noch in euren Tugenden, Volk mit blöden Augen,—Volk, das nicht weiss, was Geist ist!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0025","text":"Geist ist das Leben, das selber in’s Leben schneidet: an der eignen Qual mehrt es sich das eigne Wissen,—wusstet ihr das schon?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0026","text":"Und des Geistes Glück ist diess: gesalbt zu sein und durch Thränen geweiht zum Opferthier,—wusstet ihr das schon?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0027","text":"Und die Blindheit des Blinden und sein Suchen und Tappen soll noch von der Macht der Sonne zeugen, in die er schaute,—wusstet ihr das schon?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0028","text":"Und mit Bergen soll der Erkennende bauen lernen! Wenig ist es, dass der Geist Berge versetzt,—wusstet ihr das schon?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0029","text":"Ihr kennt nur des Geistes Funken: aber ihr seht den Ambos nicht, der er ist, und nicht die Grausamkeit seines Hammers!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0030","text":"Wahrlich, ihr kennt des Geistes Stolz nicht! Aber noch weniger würdet ihr des Geistes Bescheidenheit ertragen, wenn sie einmal reden wollte!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0031","text":"Und niemals noch durftet ihr euren Geist in eine Grube von Schnee werfen: ihr seid nicht heiss genug dazu! So kennt ihr auch die Entzückungen seiner Kälte nicht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0032","text":"In Allem aber thut ihr mir zu vertraulich mit dem Geiste; und aus der Weisheit machtet ihr oft ein Armen- und Krankenhaus für schlechte Dichter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0033","text":"Ihr seid keine Adler: so erfuhrt ihr auch das Glück im Schrecken des Geistes nicht. Und wer kein Vogel ist, soll sich nicht über Abgründen lagern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0034","text":"Ihr seid mir Laue: aber kalt strömt jede tiefe Erkenntniss. Eiskalt sind die innersten Brunnen des Geistes: ein Labsal heissen Händen und Handelnden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0035","text":"Ehrbar steht ihr mir da und steif und mit geradem Rücken, ihr berühmten Weisen! —euch treibt kein starker Wind und Wille.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0036","text":"Saht ihr nie ein Segel über das Meer gehn, geründet und gebläht und zitternd vor dem Ungestüm des Windes?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0037","text":"Dem Segel gleich, zitternd vor dem Ungestüm des Geistes, geht meine Weisheit über das Meer—meine wilde Weisheit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0038","text":"Aber ihr Diener des Volkes, ihr berühmten Weisen,—wie könntet ihr mit mir gehn!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0039","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"f8b634fee3717e170275218221b9033e67058d20ff20921819e5542a667cfc62","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181928970315Z_03a6c3db","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Famous Wise Men","source_sha256":"f8b634fee3717e170275218221b9033e67058d20ff20921819e5542a667cfc62","sha256":"512829a587151baabfc2ee6565fb37ea48d07e6b0cec1a73b4e3df483b2ce1d9","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:19:28.970358+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0001","text":"You have served the people and the people’s superstition, all you famous wise men!—and not truth! And precisely for that reason one paid you reverence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0002","text":"And for that reason, too, one tolerated your unbelief, because it was a jest and a detour to the people. Thus the master lets his slaves have their way and even delights in their high-spiritedness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0003","text":"But he who is hateful to the people as a wolf is to dogs: that is the free spirit, the enemy of fetters, the non-worshipper, who makes his home in forests.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0004","text":"To hunt him out of his hiding-place—that has always meant to the people “a sense of justice”: against him it still sets its sharpest-toothed dogs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0005","text":"“For truth is there: after all, the people are there! Woe, woe to the seekers!”—thus has it rung out from time immemorial.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0006","text":"You wished to establish justice for your people in its reverence: this you called “will to truth,” you famous wise men!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0007","text":"And your heart always said to itself: “I came from the people: from there, too, came God’s voice to me.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0008","text":"Stiff-necked and clever, like the ass, you were always, as the people’s advocates.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0009","text":"And many a powerful man who wished to fare well with the people harnessed before his horses yet—an ass, a famous wise man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0010","text":"And now I would have it, you famous wise men, that you finally cast off the lion’s skin entirely!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0011","text":"The skin of the beast of prey, the variegated-spotted one, and the shaggy locks of the inquirer, seeker, conqueror!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0012","text":"Ah, if I am to learn to believe in your “truthfulness,” you would first have to break for me your revering will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0013","text":"Truthful—thus I call him who goes into godless deserts and has broken his revering heart.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0014","text":"In the yellow sand and scorched by the sun, he may well squint thirstily toward the spring-rich islands, where living things rest beneath dark trees.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0015","text":"But his thirst does not persuade him to become like these comfortable ones: for where there are oases, there are also idols.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0016","text":"Hungry, violent, solitary, godless: thus the lion-will wills itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0017","text":"Free from the happiness of slaves, redeemed from gods and worship, fearless and terrible, great and solitary: thus is the will of the truthful one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0018","text":"From time immemorial the truthful ones, the free spirits, have dwelt in the desert as lords of the desert; but in the cities dwell the well-fed, famous wise men,—the draught animals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0019","text":"For they always pull, as asses—the people’s carts!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0020","text":"Not that I am angry with them for it: but they remain for me servants and harnessed ones, even when they shine with golden harness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0021","text":"And often they were good servants and worthy of praise. For thus speaks virtue: if you must be a servant, then seek the one whom your service benefits most!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0022","text":"“The spirit and virtue of your master shall grow through your being his servant: thus you yourself grow with his spirit and his virtue!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0023","text":"And truly, you famous wise men, you servants of the people! You yourselves grew with the people’s spirit and virtue—and the people through you! I say this in your honour!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0024","text":"But you remain for me people even in your virtues, people with dim eyes,—people who do not know what spirit is!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0025","text":"Spirit is the life that cuts into life itself: at its own anguish it increases its own knowledge,—did you know that already?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0026","text":"And this is the spirit’s happiness: to be anointed and through tears consecrated as a sacrificial animal,—did you know that already?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0027","text":"And the blindness of the blind man, and his seeking and groping, shall yet bear witness to the power of the sun into which he gazed,—did you know that already?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0028","text":"And the knower shall learn to build with mountains! It is little that the spirit moves mountains,—did you know that already?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0029","text":"You know only the spirit’s sparks: but you do not see the anvil that it is, nor the cruelty of its hammer!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0030","text":"Truly, you do not know the spirit’s pride! But still less would you endure the spirit’s modesty, if it should ever wish to speak!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0031","text":"And never yet have you dared to throw your spirit into a pit of snow: you are not hot enough for that! Thus you do not know the raptures of its coldness either.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0032","text":"But in all things you are too familiar with the spirit for my liking; and of wisdom you have often made a poorhouse and hospital for bad poets.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0033","text":"You are no eagles: thus you have not experienced the happiness in the terror of the spirit. And whoever is not a bird should not lodge above abysses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0034","text":"You are lukewarm to me: but every deep knowledge flows cold. Ice-cold are the innermost wells of the spirit: a refreshment for hot hands and men of action.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0035","text":"Respectably you stand before me, stiff and with straight backs, you celebrated sages! —no strong wind and will drives you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0036","text":"Have you never seen a sail go over the sea, rounded and swollen and trembling before the impetuosity of the wind?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0037","text":"Like the sail, trembling before the impetuosity of the spirit, my wisdom goes over the sea—my wild wisdom!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0038","text":"But you servants of the people, you celebrated sages,—how could you go with me!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0039","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDBH:P0039"]}],"source_digest":"56dea9b749de8021d1402c2db22c0673f7e3136c36c0aae1e2e91cfe5578e624","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von den berühmten Weisen"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von den berühmten Weisen","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN","title":"The Night Song","order":46,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_DASN","text":"Nacht ist es: nun reden lauter alle springenden Brunnen. Und auch meine Seele ist ein springender Brunnen.\n\nNacht ist es: nun erst erwachen alle Lieder der Liebenden. Und auch meine Seele ist das Lied eines Liebenden.\n\nEin Ungestilltes, Unstillbares ist in mir; das will laut werden. Eine Begierde nach Liebe ist in mir, die redet selber die Sprache der Liebe.\n\nLicht bin ich: ach, dass ich Nacht wäre! Aber diess ist meine Einsamkeit, dass ich von Licht umgürtet bin.\n\nAch, dass ich dunkel wäre und nächtig! Wie wollte ich an den Brüsten des Lichts saugen!\n\nUnd euch selber wollte ich noch segnen, ihr kleinen Funkelsterne und Leuchtwürmer droben!—und selig sein ob eurer Licht-Geschenke.\n\nAber ich lebe in meinem eignen Lichte, ich trinke die Flammen in mich zurück, die aus mir brechen.\n\nIch kenne das Glück des Nehmenden nicht; und oft träumte mir davon, dass Stehlen noch seliger sein müsse, als Nehmen.\n\nDas ist meine Armuth, dass meine Hand niemals ausruht vom Schenken; das ist mein Neid, dass ich wartende Augen sehe und die erhellten Nächte der Sehnsucht.\n\nOh Unseligkeit aller Schenkenden! Oh Verfinsterung meiner Sonne! Oh Begierde nach Begehren! Oh Heisshunger in der Sättigung!\n\nSie nehmen von mir: aber rühre ich noch an ihre Seele? Eine Kluft ist zwischen Geben und Nehmen; und die kleinste Kluft ist am letzten zu überbrücken.\n\nEin Hunger wächst aus meiner Schönheit: wehethun möchte ich Denen, welchen ich leuchte, berauben möchte ich meine Beschenkten:—also hungere ich nach Bosheit.\n\nDie Hand zurückziehend, wenn sich schon ihr die Hand entgegenstreckt; dem Wasserfälle gleich zögernd, der noch im Sturze zögert:—also hungere ich nach Bosheit.\n\nSolche Rache sinnt meine Fülle aus; solche Tücke quillt aus meiner Einsamkeit.\n\nMein Glück im Schenken erstarb im Schenken, meine Tugend wurde ihrer selber müde an ihrem Überflusse!\n\nWer immer schenkt, dessen Gefahr ist, dass er die Scham verliere; wer immer austheilt, dessen Hand und Herz hat Schwielen vor lauter Austheilen.\n\nMein Auge quillt nicht mehr über vor der Scham der Bittenden; meine Hand wurde zu hart für das Zittern gefüllter Hände.\n\nWohin kam die Thräne meinem Auge und der Flaum meinem Herzen? Oh Einsamkeit aller Schenkenden! Oh Schweigsamkeit aller Leuchtenden!\n\nViel Sonnen kreisen im öden Räume: zu Allem, was dunkel ist, reden sie mit ihrem Lichte,—mir schweigen sie.\n\nOh diess ist die Feindschaft des Lichts gegen Leuchtendes, erbarmungslos wandelt es seine Bahnen.\n\nUnbillig gegen Leuchtendes im tiefsten Herzen: kalt gegen Sonnen,—also wandelt jede Sonne.\n\nEinem Sturme gleich fliegen die Sonnen ihre Bahnen, das ist ihr Wandeln. Ihrem unerbittlichen Willen folgen sie, das ist ihre Kälte.\n\nOh, ihr erst seid es, ihr Dunklen, ihr Nächtigen, die ihr Wärme schafft aus Leuchtendem! Oh, ihr erst trinkt euch Milch und Labsal aus des Lichtes Eutern!\n\nAch, Eis ist um mich, meine Hand verbrennt sich an Eisigem! Ach, Durst ist in mir, der schmachtet nach eurem Durste!\n\nNacht ist es: ach dass ich Licht sein muss! Und Durst nach Nächtigem! Und Einsamkeit!\n\nNacht ist es: nun bricht wie ein Born aus mir mein Verlangen,—nach Rede verlangt mich.\n\nNacht ist es: nun reden lauter alle springenden Brunnen. Und auch meine Seele ist ein springender Brunnen.\n\nNacht ist es: nun erst erwachen alle Lieder der Liebenden. Und auch meine Seele ist das Lied eines Liebenden.—\n\nAlso sang Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0001","text":"Nacht ist es: nun reden lauter alle springenden Brunnen. Und auch meine Seele ist ein springender Brunnen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0002","text":"Nacht ist es: nun erst erwachen alle Lieder der Liebenden. Und auch meine Seele ist das Lied eines Liebenden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0003","text":"Ein Ungestilltes, Unstillbares ist in mir; das will laut werden. Eine Begierde nach Liebe ist in mir, die redet selber die Sprache der Liebe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0004","text":"Licht bin ich: ach, dass ich Nacht wäre! Aber diess ist meine Einsamkeit, dass ich von Licht umgürtet bin.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0005","text":"Ach, dass ich dunkel wäre und nächtig! Wie wollte ich an den Brüsten des Lichts saugen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0006","text":"Und euch selber wollte ich noch segnen, ihr kleinen Funkelsterne und Leuchtwürmer droben!—und selig sein ob eurer Licht-Geschenke.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0007","text":"Aber ich lebe in meinem eignen Lichte, ich trinke die Flammen in mich zurück, die aus mir brechen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0008","text":"Ich kenne das Glück des Nehmenden nicht; und oft träumte mir davon, dass Stehlen noch seliger sein müsse, als Nehmen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0009","text":"Das ist meine Armuth, dass meine Hand niemals ausruht vom Schenken; das ist mein Neid, dass ich wartende Augen sehe und die erhellten Nächte der Sehnsucht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0010","text":"Oh Unseligkeit aller Schenkenden! Oh Verfinsterung meiner Sonne! Oh Begierde nach Begehren! Oh Heisshunger in der Sättigung!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0011","text":"Sie nehmen von mir: aber rühre ich noch an ihre Seele? Eine Kluft ist zwischen Geben und Nehmen; und die kleinste Kluft ist am letzten zu überbrücken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0012","text":"Ein Hunger wächst aus meiner Schönheit: wehethun möchte ich Denen, welchen ich leuchte, berauben möchte ich meine Beschenkten:—also hungere ich nach Bosheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0013","text":"Die Hand zurückziehend, wenn sich schon ihr die Hand entgegenstreckt; dem Wasserfälle gleich zögernd, der noch im Sturze zögert:—also hungere ich nach Bosheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0014","text":"Solche Rache sinnt meine Fülle aus; solche Tücke quillt aus meiner Einsamkeit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0015","text":"Mein Glück im Schenken erstarb im Schenken, meine Tugend wurde ihrer selber müde an ihrem Überflusse!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0016","text":"Wer immer schenkt, dessen Gefahr ist, dass er die Scham verliere; wer immer austheilt, dessen Hand und Herz hat Schwielen vor lauter Austheilen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0017","text":"Mein Auge quillt nicht mehr über vor der Scham der Bittenden; meine Hand wurde zu hart für das Zittern gefüllter Hände.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0018","text":"Wohin kam die Thräne meinem Auge und der Flaum meinem Herzen? Oh Einsamkeit aller Schenkenden! Oh Schweigsamkeit aller Leuchtenden!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0019","text":"Viel Sonnen kreisen im öden Räume: zu Allem, was dunkel ist, reden sie mit ihrem Lichte,—mir schweigen sie.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0020","text":"Oh diess ist die Feindschaft des Lichts gegen Leuchtendes, erbarmungslos wandelt es seine Bahnen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0021","text":"Unbillig gegen Leuchtendes im tiefsten Herzen: kalt gegen Sonnen,—also wandelt jede Sonne.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0022","text":"Einem Sturme gleich fliegen die Sonnen ihre Bahnen, das ist ihr Wandeln. Ihrem unerbittlichen Willen folgen sie, das ist ihre Kälte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0023","text":"Oh, ihr erst seid es, ihr Dunklen, ihr Nächtigen, die ihr Wärme schafft aus Leuchtendem! Oh, ihr erst trinkt euch Milch und Labsal aus des Lichtes Eutern!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0024","text":"Ach, Eis ist um mich, meine Hand verbrennt sich an Eisigem! Ach, Durst ist in mir, der schmachtet nach eurem Durste!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0025","text":"Nacht ist es: ach dass ich Licht sein muss! Und Durst nach Nächtigem! Und Einsamkeit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0026","text":"Nacht ist es: nun bricht wie ein Born aus mir mein Verlangen,—nach Rede verlangt mich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0027","text":"Nacht ist es: nun reden lauter alle springenden Brunnen. Und auch meine Seele ist ein springender Brunnen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0028","text":"Nacht ist es: nun erst erwachen alle Lieder der Liebenden. Und auch meine Seele ist das Lied eines Liebenden.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0029","text":"Also sang Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"c88a09a18d0a0384204372f71f54543b492faea5d8c53be91de4b0ad444eef9e","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181940749150Z_1a8c7497","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"The Night Song","source_sha256":"c88a09a18d0a0384204372f71f54543b492faea5d8c53be91de4b0ad444eef9e","sha256":"9a2a474f15306fb327b61a8a4e773946a725e93679658fcf98466997437b1adf","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:19:40.749208+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0001","text":"It is night: now all leaping fountains speak louder. And my soul too is a leaping fountain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0002","text":"It is night: only now do all the songs of lovers awaken. And my soul too is the song of a lover.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0003","text":"There is within me something unappeased, unappeasable; it wants to become loud. There is within me a desire for love that itself speaks the language of love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0004","text":"I am light: ah, if only I were night! But this is my solitude, that I am girded about with light.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0005","text":"Ah, if only I were dark and nocturnal! How I would suck at the breasts of light!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0006","text":"And I would yet bless you yourselves, you little sparkling stars and glow-worms above!—and be blessed because of your gifts of light.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0007","text":"But I live in my own light, I drink back into myself the flames that break forth from me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0008","text":"I do not know the happiness of the receiver; and I often dreamt that stealing must be still more blissful than receiving.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0009","text":"This is my poverty, that my hand never rests from giving; this is my envy, that I see waiting eyes and the illumined nights of longing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0010","text":"Oh unhappiness of all givers! Oh darkening of my sun! Oh desire for desire! Oh ravenous hunger amid satiety!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0011","text":"They take from me: but do I still touch their soul? There is a gulf between giving and taking; and the smallest gulf is the last to be bridged.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0012","text":"A hunger grows out of my beauty: I would like to hurt those to whom I shine, I would like to rob my beneficiaries:—thus I hunger after wickedness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0013","text":"Drawing back the hand when a hand is already stretched out towards it; hesitating like the waterfall that still hesitates in its plunge:—thus I hunger after wickedness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0014","text":"Such revenge does my abundance devise; such malice wells forth from my solitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0015","text":"My happiness in giving died away in giving, my virtue grew weary of itself in its abundance!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0016","text":"Whoever always gives, runs the danger of losing shame; whoever always distributes, his hand and heart grow calloused from all that distributing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0017","text":"My eye no longer overflows with shame at the shame of those who beg; my hand has become too hard for the trembling of filled hands.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0018","text":"Where have the tear in my eye and the down upon my heart gone? Oh solitude of all givers! Oh silence of all that shines!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0019","text":"Many suns circle in desolate space: to all that is dark they speak with their light,—to me they are silent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0020","text":"Oh this is the enmity of light against that which shines: pitilessly it goes its courses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0021","text":"Unjust towards that which shines in its deepest heart: cold towards suns,—thus every sun goes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0022","text":"Like a storm the suns fly along their courses, that is their going. They follow their inexorable will, that is their coldness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0023","text":"Oh, only you are they, you dark ones, you nocturnal ones, who create warmth out of that which shines! Oh, only you drink milk and refreshment from the udders of light!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0024","text":"Ah, ice is around me, my hand burns itself on what is icy! Ah, there is thirst in me, languishing for your thirst!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0025","text":"It is night: ah, that I must be light! And thirst for what is nocturnal! And solitude!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0026","text":"It is night: now my longing breaks forth from me like a spring,—I long to speak.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0027","text":"It is night: now all gushing fountains speak more loudly. And my soul too is a gushing fountain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0028","text":"It is night: only now do all the songs of lovers awaken. And my soul too is the song of a lover.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASN:P0029","text":"Thus sang Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASN:P0029"]}],"source_digest":"c564b77e09f1e8b36828d77513fc628fc13c84ac235c626373074a7597ac5b59","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Das Nachtlied"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Das Nachtlied","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST","title":"The Dance Song","order":47,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_DAST","text":"Eines Abends gieng Zarathustra mit seinen Jüngern durch den Wald; und als er nach einem Brunnen suchte, siehe, da kam er auf eine grüne Wiese, die von Bäumen und Gebüsch still umstanden war: auf der tanzten Mädchen mit einander. Sobald die Mädchen Zarathustra erkannten, liessen sie vom Tanze ab; Zarathustra aber trat mit freundlicher Gebärde zu ihnen und sprach diese Worte:\n\n„Lasst vom Tanze nicht ab, ihr lieblichen Mädchen! Kein Spielverderber kam zu euch mit bösem Blick, kein Mädchen-Feind.\n\nGottes Fürsprecher bin ich vor dem Teufel: der aber ist der Geist der Schwere. Wie sollte ich, ihr Leichten, göttlichen Tänzen feind sein? Oder Mädchen-Füssen mit schönen Knöcheln?\n\nWohl bin ich ein Wald und eine Nacht dunkler Bäume: doch wer sich vor meinem Dunkel nicht scheut, der findet auch Rosenhänge unter meinen Cypressen.\n\nUnd auch den kleinen Gott findet er wohl, der den Mädchen der liebste ist: neben dem Brunnen liegt er, still, mit geschlossenen Augen.\n\nWahrlich, am hellen Tage schlief er mir ein, der Tagedieb! Haschte er wohl zu viel nach Schmetterlingen?\n\nZürnt mir nicht, ihr schönen Tanzenden, wenn ich den kleinen Gott ein Wenig züchtige! Schreien wird er wohl und weinen,—aber zum Lachen ist er noch im Weinen!\n\nUnd mit Thränen im Auge soll er euch um einen Tanz bitten; und ich selber will ein Lied zu seinem Tanze singen:\n\nEin Tanz- und Spottlied auf den Geist der Schwere, meinen allerhöchsten grossmächtigsten Teufel, von dem sie sagen, dass er „der Herr der Welt“ sei.“—\n\nUnd diess ist das Lied, welches Zarathustra sang, als Cupido und die Mädchen zusammen tanzten.\n\nIn dein Auge schaute ich jüngst, oh Leben! Und in’s Unergründliche schien ich mir da zu sinken.\n\nAber du zogst mich mit goldner Angel heraus; spöttisch lachtest du, als ich dich unergründlich nannte.\n\n„So geht die Rede aller Fische, sprachst du; was sie nicht ergründen, ist unergründlich.\n\nAber veränderlich bin ich nur und wild und in Allem ein Weib, und kein tugendhaftes:\n\nOb ich schon euch Männern „die Tiefe“ heisse oder „die Treue“, „die Ewige“, „die Geheimnissvolle.“—\n\nDoch ihr Männer beschenkt uns stets mit den eignen Tugenden—ach, ihr Tugendhaften!“\n\nAlso lachte sie, die Unglaubliche; aber ich glaube ihr niemals und ihrem Lachen, wenn sie bös von sich selber spricht.\n\nUnd als ich unter vier Augen mit meiner wilden Weisheit redete, sagte sie mir zornig: „Du willst, du begehrst, du liebst, darum allein lobst du das Leben!“\n\nFast hätte ich da bös geantwortet und der Zornigen die Wahrheit gesagt; und man kann nicht böser antworten, als wenn man seiner Weisheit „die Wahrheit sagt.“\n\nSo nämlich steht es zwischen uns Dreien. Von Grund aus liebe ich nur das Leben —und, wahrlich, am meisten dann, wenn ich es hasse!\n\nDass ich aber der Weisheit gut bin und oft zu gut: das macht, sie erinnert mich gar sehr an das Leben!\n\nSie hat ihr Auge, ihr Lachen und sogar ihr goldnes Angelrüthchen: was kann ich dafür, dass die Beiden sich so ähnlich sehen?\n\nUnd als mich einmal das Leben fragte: Wer ist denn das, die Weisheit?—da sagte ich eifrig: „Ach ja! die Weisheit!\n\nMan dürstet um sie und wird nicht satt, man blickt durch Schleier, man hascht durch Netze.\n\nIst sie schön? Was weiss ich! Aber die ältesten Karpfen werden noch mit ihr geködert.\n\nVeränderlich ist sie und trotzig; oft sah ich sie sich die Lippe beissen und den Kamm wider ihres Haares Strich führen.\n\nVielleicht ist sie böse und falsch, und in Allem ein Frauenzimmer; aber wenn sie von sich selber schlecht spricht, da gerade verführt sie am meisten.“\n\nAls ich diess zu dem Leben sagte, da lachte es boshaft und machte die Augen zu. „Von wem redest du doch? sagte sie, wohl von mir?\n\nUnd wenn du Recht hättest,—sagt man das mir so in’s Gesicht! Aber nun sprich doch auch von deiner Weisheit!“\n\nAch, und nun machtest du wieder dein Auge auf, oh geliebtes Leben! Und in’s Unergründliche schien ich mir wieder zu sinken.—\n\nAlso sang Zarathustra. Als aber der Tanz zu Ende und die Mädchen fortgegangen waren, wurde er traurig.\n\n„Die Sonne ist lange schon hinunter, sagte er endlich; die Wiese ist feucht, von den Wäldern her kommt Kühle.\n\nEin Unbekanntes ist um mich und blickt nachdenklich. Was! Du lebst noch, Zarathustra?\n\nWarum? Wofür? Wodurch? Wohin? Wo? Wie? Ist es nicht Thorheit, noch zu leben?—\n\nAch, meine Freunde, der Abend ist es, der so aus mir fragt. Vergebt mir meine Traurigkeit!\n\nAbend ward es: vergebt mir, dass es Abend ward!“\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0001","text":"Eines Abends gieng Zarathustra mit seinen Jüngern durch den Wald; und als er nach einem Brunnen suchte, siehe, da kam er auf eine grüne Wiese, die von Bäumen und Gebüsch still umstanden war: auf der tanzten Mädchen mit einander. Sobald die Mädchen Zarathustra erkannten, liessen sie vom Tanze ab; Zarathustra aber trat mit freundlicher Gebärde zu ihnen und sprach diese Worte:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0002","text":"„Lasst vom Tanze nicht ab, ihr lieblichen Mädchen! Kein Spielverderber kam zu euch mit bösem Blick, kein Mädchen-Feind.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0003","text":"Gottes Fürsprecher bin ich vor dem Teufel: der aber ist der Geist der Schwere. Wie sollte ich, ihr Leichten, göttlichen Tänzen feind sein? Oder Mädchen-Füssen mit schönen Knöcheln?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0004","text":"Wohl bin ich ein Wald und eine Nacht dunkler Bäume: doch wer sich vor meinem Dunkel nicht scheut, der findet auch Rosenhänge unter meinen Cypressen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0005","text":"Und auch den kleinen Gott findet er wohl, der den Mädchen der liebste ist: neben dem Brunnen liegt er, still, mit geschlossenen Augen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0006","text":"Wahrlich, am hellen Tage schlief er mir ein, der Tagedieb! Haschte er wohl zu viel nach Schmetterlingen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0007","text":"Zürnt mir nicht, ihr schönen Tanzenden, wenn ich den kleinen Gott ein Wenig züchtige! Schreien wird er wohl und weinen,—aber zum Lachen ist er noch im Weinen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0008","text":"Und mit Thränen im Auge soll er euch um einen Tanz bitten; und ich selber will ein Lied zu seinem Tanze singen:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0009","text":"Ein Tanz- und Spottlied auf den Geist der Schwere, meinen allerhöchsten grossmächtigsten Teufel, von dem sie sagen, dass er „der Herr der Welt“ sei.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0010","text":"Und diess ist das Lied, welches Zarathustra sang, als Cupido und die Mädchen zusammen tanzten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0011","text":"In dein Auge schaute ich jüngst, oh Leben! Und in’s Unergründliche schien ich mir da zu sinken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0012","text":"Aber du zogst mich mit goldner Angel heraus; spöttisch lachtest du, als ich dich unergründlich nannte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0013","text":"„So geht die Rede aller Fische, sprachst du; was sie nicht ergründen, ist unergründlich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0014","text":"Aber veränderlich bin ich nur und wild und in Allem ein Weib, und kein tugendhaftes:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0015","text":"Ob ich schon euch Männern „die Tiefe“ heisse oder „die Treue“, „die Ewige“, „die Geheimnissvolle.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0016","text":"Doch ihr Männer beschenkt uns stets mit den eignen Tugenden—ach, ihr Tugendhaften!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0017","text":"Also lachte sie, die Unglaubliche; aber ich glaube ihr niemals und ihrem Lachen, wenn sie bös von sich selber spricht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0018","text":"Und als ich unter vier Augen mit meiner wilden Weisheit redete, sagte sie mir zornig: „Du willst, du begehrst, du liebst, darum allein lobst du das Leben!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0019","text":"Fast hätte ich da bös geantwortet und der Zornigen die Wahrheit gesagt; und man kann nicht böser antworten, als wenn man seiner Weisheit „die Wahrheit sagt.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0020","text":"So nämlich steht es zwischen uns Dreien. Von Grund aus liebe ich nur das Leben —und, wahrlich, am meisten dann, wenn ich es hasse!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0021","text":"Dass ich aber der Weisheit gut bin und oft zu gut: das macht, sie erinnert mich gar sehr an das Leben!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0022","text":"Sie hat ihr Auge, ihr Lachen und sogar ihr goldnes Angelrüthchen: was kann ich dafür, dass die Beiden sich so ähnlich sehen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0023","text":"Und als mich einmal das Leben fragte: Wer ist denn das, die Weisheit?—da sagte ich eifrig: „Ach ja! die Weisheit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0024","text":"Man dürstet um sie und wird nicht satt, man blickt durch Schleier, man hascht durch Netze.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0025","text":"Ist sie schön? Was weiss ich! Aber die ältesten Karpfen werden noch mit ihr geködert.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0026","text":"Veränderlich ist sie und trotzig; oft sah ich sie sich die Lippe beissen und den Kamm wider ihres Haares Strich führen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0027","text":"Vielleicht ist sie böse und falsch, und in Allem ein Frauenzimmer; aber wenn sie von sich selber schlecht spricht, da gerade verführt sie am meisten.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0028","text":"Als ich diess zu dem Leben sagte, da lachte es boshaft und machte die Augen zu. „Von wem redest du doch? sagte sie, wohl von mir?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0029","text":"Und wenn du Recht hättest,—sagt man das mir so in’s Gesicht! Aber nun sprich doch auch von deiner Weisheit!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0030","text":"Ach, und nun machtest du wieder dein Auge auf, oh geliebtes Leben! Und in’s Unergründliche schien ich mir wieder zu sinken.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0031","text":"Also sang Zarathustra. Als aber der Tanz zu Ende und die Mädchen fortgegangen waren, wurde er traurig.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0032","text":"„Die Sonne ist lange schon hinunter, sagte er endlich; die Wiese ist feucht, von den Wäldern her kommt Kühle.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0033","text":"Ein Unbekanntes ist um mich und blickt nachdenklich. Was! Du lebst noch, Zarathustra?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0034","text":"Warum? Wofür? Wodurch? Wohin? Wo? Wie? Ist es nicht Thorheit, noch zu leben?—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0035","text":"Ach, meine Freunde, der Abend ist es, der so aus mir fragt. Vergebt mir meine Traurigkeit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0036","text":"Abend ward es: vergebt mir, dass es Abend ward!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0037","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"0b09e202e769e111aed529e24619d1a8311a319ee88487be926230911c3cce70","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181957363054Z_fcc2deea","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"The Dance Song","source_sha256":"0b09e202e769e111aed529e24619d1a8311a319ee88487be926230911c3cce70","sha256":"c7092750efd76eb140016d838e93512fda5845499d77a4860311badd1b69820e","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:19:57.363071+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0001","text":"One evening Zarathustra was walking through the forest with his disciples; and as he was looking for a spring, behold, he came upon a green meadow, quietly encircled by trees and bushes: upon it maidens were dancing together. As soon as the maidens recognized Zarathustra, they ceased their dancing; but Zarathustra stepped up to them with a friendly gesture and spoke these words:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0002","text":"“Do not cease your dancing, you lovely maidens! No spoil-sport has come to you with an evil glance, no enemy of maidens.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0003","text":"I am God’s advocate before the devil: but he is the spirit of gravity. How should I, you light ones, be hostile to divine dances? Or to maidenly feet with beautiful ankles?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0004","text":"To be sure, I am a forest and a night of dark trees: yet whoever does not shrink from my darkness will find rose-covered slopes too beneath my cypresses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0005","text":"And he will also find, surely, the little god who is dearest to the maidens: beside the spring he lies, still, with closed eyes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0006","text":"Verily, in broad daylight he fell asleep for me, that day-thief! Did he perhaps chase after too many butterflies?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0007","text":"Do not be angry with me, you beautiful dancers, if I chastise the little god a little! He will surely cry and weep,—but even in weeping he is still fit to make one laugh!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0008","text":"And with tears in his eyes he shall ask you for a dance; and I myself will sing a song for his dance:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0009","text":"A dance- and mocking-song upon the spirit of gravity, my supreme, most mighty devil, of whom they say that he is ‘the lord of the world.’”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0010","text":"And this is the song that Zarathustra sang while Cupid and the maidens danced together.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0011","text":"Into your eye I looked lately, O Life! And I seemed to myself to be sinking there into the unfathomable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0012","text":"But you drew me out with a golden fishing-rod; mockingly you laughed when I called you unfathomable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0013","text":"‘Thus speaks every fish,’ you said; ‘what they cannot fathom is unfathomable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0014","text":"But I am only changeable and wild and in all things a woman, and no virtuous one:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0015","text":"Though among you men I am called “the depth,” or “the fidelity,” “the eternal,” “the mysterious.”’—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0016","text":"But you men always endow us with your own virtues—ah, you virtuous ones!’","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0017","text":"Thus she laughed, the unbelievable one; but I never believe her and her laughter when she speaks ill of herself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0018","text":"And when I spoke tête-à-tête with my wild wisdom, she said to me angrily: ‘You will, you desire, you love, and for that reason alone you praise life!’","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0019","text":"I almost answered wickedly then and told the angry one the truth; and one cannot answer more wickedly than when one ‘tells the truth’ to one’s wisdom.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0020","text":"For this is how matters stand among the three of us. Fundamentally I love only life—and, verily, most of all when I hate it!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0021","text":"But that I am fond of wisdom, and often too fond: that is because she reminds me so very much of life!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0022","text":"She has its eye, its laughter, and even its little golden fishing-rod: what can I do if the two look so alike?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0023","text":"And when life once asked me: Who then is that, wisdom?—I answered eagerly: ‘Ah yes! wisdom!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0024","text":"One thirsts for her and does not become sated, one looks through veils, one snatches through nets.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0025","text":"Is she beautiful? What do I know! But the oldest carp are still baited with her.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0026","text":"Changeable is she and defiant; often I saw her bite her lip and run the comb against the grain of her hair.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0027","text":"Perhaps she is wicked and false, and in everything a woman; but when she speaks ill of herself, it is precisely then that she seduces most.“","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0028","text":"When I said this to life, it laughed maliciously and closed its eyes. “Of whom are you speaking, then? she said, surely of me?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0029","text":"And if you were right,—does one say that to my face! But now do speak, too, of your wisdom!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0030","text":"Ah, and now you opened your eye again, O beloved life! And again I seemed to myself to be sinking into the unfathomable.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0031","text":"Thus sang Zarathustra. But when the dance was ended and the girls had gone away, he became sad.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0032","text":"“The sun has long since gone down, he said at last; the meadow is damp, coolness comes from the woods.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0033","text":"Something unknown is about me and gazes pensively. What! You are still alive, Zarathustra?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0034","text":"Why? For what? By what means? Whither? Where? How? Is it not folly still to live?—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0035","text":"Ah, my friends, it is evening that thus questions out of me. Forgive me my sadness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0036","text":"Evening came: forgive me that evening came!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DAST:P0037","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DAST:P0037"]}],"source_digest":"8e9444f86baef0b2fb038b3bd3b2a38cfa241e872b4fa40167cf0f8115b2b5a5","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Das Tanzlied"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Das Tanzlied","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG","title":"The Grave Song","order":48,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_DASG","text":"„Dort ist die Gräberinsel, die schweigsame; dort sind auch die Gräber meiner Jugend. Dahin will ich einen immergrünen Kranz des Lebens tragen.“\n\nAlso im Herzen beschliessend fuhr ich über das Meer.—\n\nOh ihr, meiner Jugend Gesichte und Erscheinungen! Oh, ihr Blicke der Liebe alle, ihr göttlichen Augenblicke! Wie starbt ihr mir so schnell! Ich gedenke eurer heute wie meiner Todten.\n\nVon euch her, meinen liebsten Todten, kommt mir ein süsser Geruch, ein herz- und thränenlösender. Wahrlich, er erschüttert und löst das Herz dem einsam Schiffenden.\n\nImmer noch bin ich der Reichste und Bestzubeneidende—ich der Einsamste! Denn ich hatte euch doch, und ihr habt mich noch: sagt, wem fielen, wie mir, solche Rosenäpfel vom Baume?\n\nImmer noch bin ich eurer Liebe Erbe und Erdreich, blühend zu eurem Gedächtnisse von bunten wildwachsenen Tugenden, oh ihr Geliebtesten!\n\nAch, wir waren gemacht, einander nahe zu bleiben, ihr holden fremden Wunder; und nicht schüchternen Vögeln gleich kamt ihr zu mir und meiner Begierde—nein, als Trauende zu dem Trauenden!\n\nJa, zur Treue gemacht, gleich mir, und zu zärtlichen Ewigkeiten: muss ich nun euch nach eurer Untreue heissen, ihr göttlichen Blicke und Augenblicke: keinen andern Namen lernte ich noch.\n\nWahrlich, zu schnell starbt ihr mir, ihr Flüchtlinge. Doch floht ihr mich nicht, noch floh ich euch: unschuldig sind wir einander in unsrer Untreue.\n\nMich zu tödten, erwürgte man euch, ihr Singvögel meiner Hoffnungen! Ja, nach euch, ihr Liebsten, schoss immer die Bosheit Pfeile—mein Herz zu treffen!\n\nUnd sie traf! Wart ihr doch stets mein Herzlichstes, mein Besitz und mein Besessen-sein: darum musstet ihr jung sterben und allzu frühe!\n\nNach dem Verwundbarsten, das ich besass, schoss man den Pfeil: das waret ihr, denen die Haut einem Flaume gleich ist und mehr noch dem Lächeln, das an einem Blick erstirbt!\n\nAber diess Wort will ich zu meinen Feinden reden: was ist alles Menschen-Morden gegen Das, was ihr mir thatet!\n\nBöseres thatet ihr mir, als aller Menschen-Mord ist; Unwiederbringliches nahmt ihr mir:—also rede ich zu euch, meine Feinde!\n\nMordetet ihr doch meiner Jugend Gesichte und liebste Wunder! Meine Gespielen nahmt ihr mir, die seligen Geister! Ihrem Gedächtnisse lege ich diesen Kranz und diesen Fluch nieder.\n\nDiesen Fluch gegen euch, meine Feinde! Machtet ihr doch mein Ewiges kurz, wie ein Ton zerbricht in kalter Nacht! Kaum als Aufblinken göttlicher Augen kam es mir nur,—als Augenblick!\n\nAlso sprach zur guten Stunde einst meine Reinheit: „göttlich sollen mir alle Wesen sein.“\n\nDa überfielt ihr mich mit schmutzigen Gespenstern; ach, wohin floh nun jene gute Stunde!\n\n„Alle Tage sollen mir heilig sein“ —so redete einst die Weisheit meiner Jugend: wahrlich, einer fröhlichen Weisheit Rede!\n\nAber da stahlt ihr Feinde mir meine Nächte und verkauftet sie zu schlafloser Qual: ach, wohin floh nun jene fröhliche Weisheit?\n\nEinst begehrte ich nach glücklichen Vogelzeichen: da führtet ihr mir ein Eulen-Unthier über den Weg, ein widriges. Ach, wohin floh da meine zärtliche Begierde?\n\nAllem Ekel gelobte ich einst zu entsagen: da verwandeltet ihr meine Nahen und Nächsten in Eiterbeulen. Ach, wohin floh da mein edelstes Gelöbniss?\n\nAls Blinder gieng ich einst selige Wege: da warft ihr Unflath auf den Weg des Blinden: und nun ekelte ihn des alten Blinden-Fusssteigs.\n\nUnd als ich mein Schwerstes that und meiner Überwindungen Sieg feierte: da machtet ihr Die, welche mich liebten, schrein, ich thue ihnen am wehesten.\n\nWahrlich, das war immer euer Thun: ihr vergälltet mir meinen besten Honig und den Fleiss meiner besten Bienen.\n\nMeiner Mildthätigkeit sandtet ihr immer die frechsten Bettler zu; um mein Mitleiden drängtet ihr immer die unheilbar Schamlosen. So verwundetet ihr meine Tugend in ihrem Glauben.\n\nUnd legte ich noch mein Heiligstes zum Opfer hin: flugs stellte eure „Frömmigkeit“ ihre fetteren Gaben dazu: also dass im Dampfe eures Fettes noch mein Heiligstes erstickte.\n\nUnd einst wollte ich tanzen, wie nie ich noch tanzte: über alle Himmel weg wollte ich tanzen. Da überredetet ihr meinen liebsten Sänger.\n\nUnd nun stimmte er eine schaurige dumpfe Weise an; ach, er tutete mir, wie ein düsteres Horn, zu Ohren!\n\nMörderischer Sänger, Werkzeug der Bosheit, Unschuldigster! Schon stand ich bereit zum besten Tanze: da mordetest du mit deinen Tönen meine Verzückung!\n\nNur im Tanze weiss ich der höchsten Dinge Gleichniss zu reden:—und nun blieb mir mein höchstes Gleichniss ungeredet in einen Gliedern!\n\nUngeredet und unerlöst blieb mir die höchste Hoffnung! Und es starben mir alle Gesichte und Tröstungen meiner Jugend!\n\nWie ertrug ich’s nur? Wie verwand und überwand ich solche Wunden? Wie erstand meine Seele wieder aus diesen Gräbern?\n\nJa, ein Unverwundbares, Unbegrabbares ist an mir, ein Felsensprengendes: das heisst mein Wille . Schweigsam schreitet es und unverändert durch die Jahre.\n\nSeinen Gang will er gehn auf meinen Füssen, mein alter Wille; herzenshart ist ihm der Sinn und unverwundbar.\n\nUnverwundbar bin ich allein an meiner Ferse. Immer noch lebst du da und bist dir gleich, Geduldigster! Immer noch brachst du dich durch alle Gräber!\n\nIn dir lebt auch noch das Unerlöste meiner Jugend; und als Leben und Jugend sitzest du hoffend hier auf gelben Grab-Trümmern.\n\nJa, noch bist du mir aller Gräber Zertrümmerer: Heil dir, mein Wille! Und nur wo Gräber sind, giebt es Auferstehungen.—\n\nAlso sang Zarathustra.—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0001","text":"„Dort ist die Gräberinsel, die schweigsame; dort sind auch die Gräber meiner Jugend. Dahin will ich einen immergrünen Kranz des Lebens tragen.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0002","text":"Also im Herzen beschliessend fuhr ich über das Meer.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0003","text":"Oh ihr, meiner Jugend Gesichte und Erscheinungen! Oh, ihr Blicke der Liebe alle, ihr göttlichen Augenblicke! Wie starbt ihr mir so schnell! Ich gedenke eurer heute wie meiner Todten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0004","text":"Von euch her, meinen liebsten Todten, kommt mir ein süsser Geruch, ein herz- und thränenlösender. Wahrlich, er erschüttert und löst das Herz dem einsam Schiffenden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0005","text":"Immer noch bin ich der Reichste und Bestzubeneidende—ich der Einsamste! Denn ich hatte euch doch, und ihr habt mich noch: sagt, wem fielen, wie mir, solche Rosenäpfel vom Baume?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0006","text":"Immer noch bin ich eurer Liebe Erbe und Erdreich, blühend zu eurem Gedächtnisse von bunten wildwachsenen Tugenden, oh ihr Geliebtesten!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0007","text":"Ach, wir waren gemacht, einander nahe zu bleiben, ihr holden fremden Wunder; und nicht schüchternen Vögeln gleich kamt ihr zu mir und meiner Begierde—nein, als Trauende zu dem Trauenden!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0008","text":"Ja, zur Treue gemacht, gleich mir, und zu zärtlichen Ewigkeiten: muss ich nun euch nach eurer Untreue heissen, ihr göttlichen Blicke und Augenblicke: keinen andern Namen lernte ich noch.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0009","text":"Wahrlich, zu schnell starbt ihr mir, ihr Flüchtlinge. Doch floht ihr mich nicht, noch floh ich euch: unschuldig sind wir einander in unsrer Untreue.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0010","text":"Mich zu tödten, erwürgte man euch, ihr Singvögel meiner Hoffnungen! Ja, nach euch, ihr Liebsten, schoss immer die Bosheit Pfeile—mein Herz zu treffen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0011","text":"Und sie traf! Wart ihr doch stets mein Herzlichstes, mein Besitz und mein Besessen-sein: darum musstet ihr jung sterben und allzu frühe!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0012","text":"Nach dem Verwundbarsten, das ich besass, schoss man den Pfeil: das waret ihr, denen die Haut einem Flaume gleich ist und mehr noch dem Lächeln, das an einem Blick erstirbt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0013","text":"Aber diess Wort will ich zu meinen Feinden reden: was ist alles Menschen-Morden gegen Das, was ihr mir thatet!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0014","text":"Böseres thatet ihr mir, als aller Menschen-Mord ist; Unwiederbringliches nahmt ihr mir:—also rede ich zu euch, meine Feinde!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0015","text":"Mordetet ihr doch meiner Jugend Gesichte und liebste Wunder! Meine Gespielen nahmt ihr mir, die seligen Geister! Ihrem Gedächtnisse lege ich diesen Kranz und diesen Fluch nieder.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0016","text":"Diesen Fluch gegen euch, meine Feinde! Machtet ihr doch mein Ewiges kurz, wie ein Ton zerbricht in kalter Nacht! Kaum als Aufblinken göttlicher Augen kam es mir nur,—als Augenblick!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0017","text":"Also sprach zur guten Stunde einst meine Reinheit: „göttlich sollen mir alle Wesen sein.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0018","text":"Da überfielt ihr mich mit schmutzigen Gespenstern; ach, wohin floh nun jene gute Stunde!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0019","text":"„Alle Tage sollen mir heilig sein“ —so redete einst die Weisheit meiner Jugend: wahrlich, einer fröhlichen Weisheit Rede!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0020","text":"Aber da stahlt ihr Feinde mir meine Nächte und verkauftet sie zu schlafloser Qual: ach, wohin floh nun jene fröhliche Weisheit?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0021","text":"Einst begehrte ich nach glücklichen Vogelzeichen: da führtet ihr mir ein Eulen-Unthier über den Weg, ein widriges. Ach, wohin floh da meine zärtliche Begierde?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0022","text":"Allem Ekel gelobte ich einst zu entsagen: da verwandeltet ihr meine Nahen und Nächsten in Eiterbeulen. Ach, wohin floh da mein edelstes Gelöbniss?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0023","text":"Als Blinder gieng ich einst selige Wege: da warft ihr Unflath auf den Weg des Blinden: und nun ekelte ihn des alten Blinden-Fusssteigs.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0024","text":"Und als ich mein Schwerstes that und meiner Überwindungen Sieg feierte: da machtet ihr Die, welche mich liebten, schrein, ich thue ihnen am wehesten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0025","text":"Wahrlich, das war immer euer Thun: ihr vergälltet mir meinen besten Honig und den Fleiss meiner besten Bienen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0026","text":"Meiner Mildthätigkeit sandtet ihr immer die frechsten Bettler zu; um mein Mitleiden drängtet ihr immer die unheilbar Schamlosen. So verwundetet ihr meine Tugend in ihrem Glauben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0027","text":"Und legte ich noch mein Heiligstes zum Opfer hin: flugs stellte eure „Frömmigkeit“ ihre fetteren Gaben dazu: also dass im Dampfe eures Fettes noch mein Heiligstes erstickte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0028","text":"Und einst wollte ich tanzen, wie nie ich noch tanzte: über alle Himmel weg wollte ich tanzen. Da überredetet ihr meinen liebsten Sänger.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0029","text":"Und nun stimmte er eine schaurige dumpfe Weise an; ach, er tutete mir, wie ein düsteres Horn, zu Ohren!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0030","text":"Mörderischer Sänger, Werkzeug der Bosheit, Unschuldigster! Schon stand ich bereit zum besten Tanze: da mordetest du mit deinen Tönen meine Verzückung!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0031","text":"Nur im Tanze weiss ich der höchsten Dinge Gleichniss zu reden:—und nun blieb mir mein höchstes Gleichniss ungeredet in einen Gliedern!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0032","text":"Ungeredet und unerlöst blieb mir die höchste Hoffnung! Und es starben mir alle Gesichte und Tröstungen meiner Jugend!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0033","text":"Wie ertrug ich’s nur? Wie verwand und überwand ich solche Wunden? Wie erstand meine Seele wieder aus diesen Gräbern?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0034","text":"Ja, ein Unverwundbares, Unbegrabbares ist an mir, ein Felsensprengendes: das heisst mein Wille . Schweigsam schreitet es und unverändert durch die Jahre.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0035","text":"Seinen Gang will er gehn auf meinen Füssen, mein alter Wille; herzenshart ist ihm der Sinn und unverwundbar.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0036","text":"Unverwundbar bin ich allein an meiner Ferse. Immer noch lebst du da und bist dir gleich, Geduldigster! Immer noch brachst du dich durch alle Gräber!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0037","text":"In dir lebt auch noch das Unerlöste meiner Jugend; und als Leben und Jugend sitzest du hoffend hier auf gelben Grab-Trümmern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0038","text":"Ja, noch bist du mir aller Gräber Zertrümmerer: Heil dir, mein Wille! Und nur wo Gräber sind, giebt es Auferstehungen.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0039","text":"Also sang Zarathustra.—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"edbc31f70d6bdb6eef604678fc86986e55981da2cd940cfb126fcc5a5f1d85ff","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182015397265Z_c38bbc86","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"The Grave Song","source_sha256":"edbc31f70d6bdb6eef604678fc86986e55981da2cd940cfb126fcc5a5f1d85ff","sha256":"f2adaa10a80f3445ca2e9d618e6fa1494de2aa760af468b4ef494ffeb81ffd7b","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:20:15.397302+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0001","text":"“There is the island of graves, the silent one; there too are the graves of my youth. There I will carry an ever-green wreath of life.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0002","text":"Thus resolving in my heart, I sailed across the sea.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0003","text":"Oh you visions and apparitions of my youth! Oh, all you glances of love, you divine moments! How quickly you died to me! Today I remember you as I do my dead.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0004","text":"From you, my dearest dead, there comes to me a sweet scent, one that melts the heart and draws tears. Truly, it shakes and loosens the heart of the one who sails alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0005","text":"Still I am the richest and most to be envied—I, the most solitary! For I did have you, and you still have me: tell me, upon whom, as upon me, have such rose-apples fallen from the tree?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0006","text":"Still I am the heir and soil of your love, blooming in your memory with variegated wild-grown virtues, oh you best-beloved!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0007","text":"Ah, we were made to remain near to one another, you gracious strange wonders; and you came to me and to my desire not like timid birds—no, as mourners to the mourning one!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0008","text":"Yes, made for fidelity, like me, and for tender eternities: must I now call you after your infidelity, you divine glances and moments: I have not yet learned any other name.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0009","text":"Truly, you died to me too quickly, you fugitives. Yet you did not flee from me, nor did I flee from you: we are innocent toward one another in our infidelity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0010","text":"In order to kill me, they strangled you, you songbirds of my hopes! Yes, after you, you dearest ones, malice always shot arrows—to strike my heart!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0011","text":"And it struck! For you were always what was closest to my heart, my possession and my being possessed: therefore you had to die young and all too early!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0012","text":"At the most vulnerable thing I possessed, they shot the arrow: that was you, whose skin is like down, and even more like the smile that dies at a glance!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0013","text":"But I will speak this word to my enemies: what is all the murder of human beings compared with what you did to me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0014","text":"You did worse to me than all murder of human beings is; you took from me what cannot be restored:—thus I speak to you, my enemies!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0015","text":"For you murdered the visions and dearest wonders of my youth! You took from me my playmates, the blessed spirits! To their memory I lay down this wreath and this curse.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0016","text":"This curse against you, my enemies! For you made my Eternal brief, as a sound breaks in the cold night! It came to me scarcely as the flashing-up of divine eyes,—as a moment!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0017","text":"Thus once, in a good hour, my purity spoke: “all beings shall be divine to me.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0018","text":"Then you assailed me with filthy spectres; ah, whither did that good hour now flee!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0019","text":"“All days shall be holy to me”—thus once spoke the wisdom of my youth: truly, the speech of a joyful wisdom!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0020","text":"But then you enemies stole my nights from me and sold them into sleepless torment: ah, whither did that joyful wisdom now flee?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0021","text":"Once I desired happy bird-signs: then you led an owl-monster across my path, a loathsome one. Ah, whither then fled my tender desire?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0022","text":"Once I vowed to renounce all disgust: then you transformed those near and nearest to me into boils of pus. Ah, whither then fled my noblest vow?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0023","text":"Once, as a blind man, I walked blessed paths: then you threw filth upon the blind man's path: and now he was disgusted by the old blind man's footpath.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0024","text":"And when I did my hardest thing and celebrated the victory of my overcomings: then you made those who loved me cry out that I was hurting them most grievously.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0025","text":"Truly, this was always your doing: you embittered for me my best honey and the diligence of my best bees.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0026","text":"To my beneficence you always sent the most impudent beggars; around my compassion you always pressed the incurably shameless. Thus you wounded my virtue in its faith.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0027","text":"And when I laid down even my holiest as a sacrifice: straightway your “piety” added its fatter offerings thereto: so that in the steam of your fat even my holiest was suffocated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0028","text":"And once I wished to dance as I had never yet danced: beyond all heavens I wished to dance. Then you persuaded my dearest singer.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0029","text":"And now he struck up a dreadful, dull tune; ah, he blared into my ears like a gloomy horn!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0030","text":"Murderous singer, instrument of malice, most innocent one! I was already standing ready for the best dance: then with your tones you murdered my rapture!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0031","text":"Only in dance do I know how to speak the parable of the highest things:—and now my highest parable remained unspoken in my limbs!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0032","text":"The highest hope remained unspoken and unredeemed for me! And all the visions and consolations of my youth died for me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0033","text":"How did I but endure it? How did I recover from and overcome such wounds? How did my soul arise again from these graves?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0034","text":"Yes, there is in me something invulnerable, unburyable, rock-blasting: that is called my will. Silently it strides, unchanged, through the years.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0035","text":"It wishes to go its way upon my feet, my old will; its disposition is hard of heart and invulnerable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0036","text":"I am invulnerable only at my heel. You still live there and remain the same, most patient one! You still broke your way through all graves!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0037","text":"In you there still lives also the unredeemed of my youth; and as life and youth you sit here in hope upon yellow fragments of graves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0038","text":"Yes, you are still for me the shatterer of all graves: hail to you, my will! And only where there are graves are there resurrections.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASG:P0039","text":"Thus sang Zarathustra.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASG:P0039"]}],"source_digest":"fdcdbb15ed3dc6d855b0e04f61fbbafb233f11b163ef2881ea9d8a4312b66a12","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Das Grablied"}],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"Das Grablied","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB","title":"On Self-Overcoming","order":49,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDSB","text":"„Wille zur Wahrheit“ heisst ihr’s, ihr Weisesten, was euch treibt und brünstig macht?\n\nWille zur Denkbarkeit alles Seienden: also heisse ich euren Willen!\n\nAlles Seiende wollt ihr erst denkbar machen : denn ihr zweifelt mit gutem Misstrauen, ob es schon denkbar ist.\n\nAber es soll sich euch fügen und biegen! So will’s euer Wille. Glatt soll es werden und dem Geiste unterthan, als sein Spiegel und Widerbild.\n\nDas ist euer ganzer Wille, ihr Weisesten, als ein Wille zur Macht; und auch wenn ihr vom Guten und Bösen redet und von den Werthschätzungen. Schaffen wollt ihr noch die Welt, vor der ihr knien könnt: so ist es eure letzte Hoffnung und Trunkenheit.\n\nDie Unweisen freilich, das Volk,—die sind gleich dem Flusse, auf dem ein Nachen weiter schwimmt: und im Nachen sitzen feierlich und vermummt die Werthschätzungen.\n\nEuren Willen und eure Werthe setztet ihr auf den Fluss des Werdens; einen alten Willen zur Macht verräth mir, was vom Volke als gut und böse geglaubt wird.\n\nIhr wart es, ihr Weisesten, die solche Gäste in diesen Nachen setzten und ihnen Prunk und stolze Namen gaben,—ihr und euer herrschender Wille!\n\nWeiter trägt nun der Fluss euren Nachen: er muss ihn tragen. Wenig thut’s, ob die gebrochene Welle schäumt und zornig dem Kiele widerspricht!\n\nNicht der Fluss ist eure Gefahr und das Ende eures Guten und Bösen, ihr Weisesten: sondern jener Wille selber, der Wille zur Macht,—der unerschöpfte zeugende Lebens-Wille.\n\nAber damit ihr mein Wort versteht vom Guten und Bösen: dazu will ich euch noch mein Wort vom Leben sagen und von der Art alles Lebendigen.\n\nDem Lebendigen gieng ich nach, ich gieng die grössten und die kleinsten Wege, dass ich seine Art erkenne.\n\nMit hundertfachem Spiegel fieng ich noch seinen Blick auf, wenn ihm der Mund geschlossen war: dass sein Auge mir rede. Und sein Auge redete mir.\n\nAber, wo ich nur Lebendiges fand, da hörte ich auch die Rede vom Gehorsame. Alles Lebendige ist ein Gehorchendes.\n\nUnd diess ist das Zweite: Dem wird befohlen, der sich nicht selber gehorchen kann. So ist es des Lebendigen Art.\n\nDiess aber ist das Dritte, was ich hörte: dass Befehlen schwerer ist, als Gehorchen. Und nicht nur, dass der Befehlende die Last aller Gehorchenden trägt, und dass leicht ihn diese Last zerdrückt:—\n\nEin Versuch und Wagniss erschien mir in allem Befehlen; und stets, wenn es befiehlt, wagt das Lebendige sich selber dran.\n\nJa noch, wenn es sich selber befiehlt: auch da noch muss es sein Befehlen büssen. Seinem eignen Gesetze muss es Richter und Rächer und Opfer werden.\n\nWie geschieht diess doch! so fragte ich mich. Was überredet das Lebendige, dass es gehorcht und befiehlt und befehlend noch Gehorsam übt?\n\nHört mir nun mein Wort, ihr Weisesten! Prüft es ernstlich, ob ich dem Leben selber in’s Herz kroch und bis in die Wurzeln seines Herzens!\n\nWo ich Lebendiges fand, da fand ich Willen zur Macht; und noch im Willen des Dienenden fand ich den Willen, Herr zu sein.\n\nDass dem Stärkeren diene das Schwächere, dazu überredet es sein Wille, der über noch Schwächeres Herr sein will: dieser Lust allein mag es nicht entrathen.\n\nUnd wie das Kleinere sich dem Grösseren hingiebt, dass es Lust und Macht am Kleinsten habe: also giebt sich auch das Grösste noch hin und setzt um der Macht willen—das Leben dran.\n\nDas ist die Hingebung des Grössten, dass es Wagniss ist und Gefahr und um den Tod ein Würfelspielen.\n\nUnd wo Opferung und Dienste und Liebesblicke sind: auch da ist Wille, Herr zu sein. Auf Schleichwegen schleicht sich da der Schwächere in die Burg und bis in’s Herz dem Mächtigeren—und stiehlt da Macht.\n\nUnd diess Geheimniss redete das Leben selber zu mir. Siehe, sprach es, ich bin das, was sich immer selber überwinden muss.\n\n„Freilich, ihr heisst es Wille zur Zeugung oder Trieb zum Zwecke, zum Höheren, Ferneren, Vielfacheren: aber all diess ist Eins und Ein Geheimniss.\n\nLieber noch gehe ich unter, als dass ich diesem Einen absagte; und wahrlich, wo es Untergang giebt und Blätterfallen, siehe, da opfert sich Leben—um Macht!\n\nDass ich Kampf sein muss und Werden und Zweck und der Zwecke Widerspruch: ach, wer meinen Willen erräth, erräth wohl auch, auf welchen krummen Wegen er gehen muss!\n\nWas ich auch schaffe und wie ich’s auch liebe,—bald muss ich Gegner ihm sein und meiner Liebe: so will es mein Wille.\n\nUnd auch du, Erkennender, bist nur ein Pfad und Fusstapfen meines Willens: wahrlich, mein Wille zur Macht wandelt auch auf den Füssen deines Willens zur Wahrheit!\n\nDer traf freilich die Wahrheit nicht, der das Wort nach ihr schoss vom „Willen zum Dasein“: diesen Willen—giebt es nicht!\n\nDenn: was nicht ist, das kann nicht wollen; was aber im Dasein ist, wie könnte das noch zum Dasein wollen!\n\nNur, wo Leben ist, da ist auch Wille: aber nicht Wille zum Leben, sondern—so lehre ich’s dich—Wille zur Macht!\n\nVieles ist dem Lebenden höher geschätzt, als Leben selber; doch aus dem Schätzen selber heraus redet—der Wille zur Macht!“—\n\nAlso lehrte mich einst das Leben: und daraus löse ich euch, ihr Weisesten, noch das Räthsel eures Herzens.\n\nWahrlich, ich sage euch: Gutes und Böses, das unvergänglich wäre—das giebt es nicht! Aus sich selber muss es sich immer wieder überwinden.\n\nMit euren Werthen und Worten von Gut und Böse übt ihr Gewalt, ihr Werthschätzenden: und diess ist eure verborgene Liebe und eurer Seele Glänzen, Zittern und Überwallen.\n\nAber eine stärkere Gewalt wächst aus euren Werthen und eine neue Überwindung: an der zerbricht Ei und Eierschale.\n\nUnd wer ein Schöpfer sein muss im Guten und Bösen: wahrlich, der muss ein Vernichter erst sein und Werthe zerbrechen.\n\nAlso gehört das höchste Böse zur höchsten Güte: diese aber ist die schöpferische.—\n\nReden wir nur davon, ihr Weisesten, ob es gleich schlimm ist. Schweigen ist schlimmer; alle verschwiegenere Wahrheiten werden giftig.\n\nUnd mag doch Alles zerbrechen, was an unseren Wahrheiten zerbrechen—kann! Manches Haus giebt es noch zu bauen!\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0001","text":"„Wille zur Wahrheit“ heisst ihr’s, ihr Weisesten, was euch treibt und brünstig macht?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0002","text":"Wille zur Denkbarkeit alles Seienden: also heisse ich euren Willen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0003","text":"Alles Seiende wollt ihr erst denkbar machen : denn ihr zweifelt mit gutem Misstrauen, ob es schon denkbar ist.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0004","text":"Aber es soll sich euch fügen und biegen! So will’s euer Wille. Glatt soll es werden und dem Geiste unterthan, als sein Spiegel und Widerbild.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0005","text":"Das ist euer ganzer Wille, ihr Weisesten, als ein Wille zur Macht; und auch wenn ihr vom Guten und Bösen redet und von den Werthschätzungen. Schaffen wollt ihr noch die Welt, vor der ihr knien könnt: so ist es eure letzte Hoffnung und Trunkenheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0006","text":"Die Unweisen freilich, das Volk,—die sind gleich dem Flusse, auf dem ein Nachen weiter schwimmt: und im Nachen sitzen feierlich und vermummt die Werthschätzungen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0007","text":"Euren Willen und eure Werthe setztet ihr auf den Fluss des Werdens; einen alten Willen zur Macht verräth mir, was vom Volke als gut und böse geglaubt wird.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0008","text":"Ihr wart es, ihr Weisesten, die solche Gäste in diesen Nachen setzten und ihnen Prunk und stolze Namen gaben,—ihr und euer herrschender Wille!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0009","text":"Weiter trägt nun der Fluss euren Nachen: er muss ihn tragen. Wenig thut’s, ob die gebrochene Welle schäumt und zornig dem Kiele widerspricht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0010","text":"Nicht der Fluss ist eure Gefahr und das Ende eures Guten und Bösen, ihr Weisesten: sondern jener Wille selber, der Wille zur Macht,—der unerschöpfte zeugende Lebens-Wille.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0011","text":"Aber damit ihr mein Wort versteht vom Guten und Bösen: dazu will ich euch noch mein Wort vom Leben sagen und von der Art alles Lebendigen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0012","text":"Dem Lebendigen gieng ich nach, ich gieng die grössten und die kleinsten Wege, dass ich seine Art erkenne.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0013","text":"Mit hundertfachem Spiegel fieng ich noch seinen Blick auf, wenn ihm der Mund geschlossen war: dass sein Auge mir rede. Und sein Auge redete mir.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0014","text":"Aber, wo ich nur Lebendiges fand, da hörte ich auch die Rede vom Gehorsame. Alles Lebendige ist ein Gehorchendes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0015","text":"Und diess ist das Zweite: Dem wird befohlen, der sich nicht selber gehorchen kann. So ist es des Lebendigen Art.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0016","text":"Diess aber ist das Dritte, was ich hörte: dass Befehlen schwerer ist, als Gehorchen. Und nicht nur, dass der Befehlende die Last aller Gehorchenden trägt, und dass leicht ihn diese Last zerdrückt:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0017","text":"Ein Versuch und Wagniss erschien mir in allem Befehlen; und stets, wenn es befiehlt, wagt das Lebendige sich selber dran.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0018","text":"Ja noch, wenn es sich selber befiehlt: auch da noch muss es sein Befehlen büssen. Seinem eignen Gesetze muss es Richter und Rächer und Opfer werden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0019","text":"Wie geschieht diess doch! so fragte ich mich. Was überredet das Lebendige, dass es gehorcht und befiehlt und befehlend noch Gehorsam übt?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0020","text":"Hört mir nun mein Wort, ihr Weisesten! Prüft es ernstlich, ob ich dem Leben selber in’s Herz kroch und bis in die Wurzeln seines Herzens!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0021","text":"Wo ich Lebendiges fand, da fand ich Willen zur Macht; und noch im Willen des Dienenden fand ich den Willen, Herr zu sein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0022","text":"Dass dem Stärkeren diene das Schwächere, dazu überredet es sein Wille, der über noch Schwächeres Herr sein will: dieser Lust allein mag es nicht entrathen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0023","text":"Und wie das Kleinere sich dem Grösseren hingiebt, dass es Lust und Macht am Kleinsten habe: also giebt sich auch das Grösste noch hin und setzt um der Macht willen—das Leben dran.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0024","text":"Das ist die Hingebung des Grössten, dass es Wagniss ist und Gefahr und um den Tod ein Würfelspielen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0025","text":"Und wo Opferung und Dienste und Liebesblicke sind: auch da ist Wille, Herr zu sein. Auf Schleichwegen schleicht sich da der Schwächere in die Burg und bis in’s Herz dem Mächtigeren—und stiehlt da Macht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0026","text":"Und diess Geheimniss redete das Leben selber zu mir. Siehe, sprach es, ich bin das, was sich immer selber überwinden muss.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0027","text":"„Freilich, ihr heisst es Wille zur Zeugung oder Trieb zum Zwecke, zum Höheren, Ferneren, Vielfacheren: aber all diess ist Eins und Ein Geheimniss.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0028","text":"Lieber noch gehe ich unter, als dass ich diesem Einen absagte; und wahrlich, wo es Untergang giebt und Blätterfallen, siehe, da opfert sich Leben—um Macht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0029","text":"Dass ich Kampf sein muss und Werden und Zweck und der Zwecke Widerspruch: ach, wer meinen Willen erräth, erräth wohl auch, auf welchen krummen Wegen er gehen muss!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0030","text":"Was ich auch schaffe und wie ich’s auch liebe,—bald muss ich Gegner ihm sein und meiner Liebe: so will es mein Wille.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0031","text":"Und auch du, Erkennender, bist nur ein Pfad und Fusstapfen meines Willens: wahrlich, mein Wille zur Macht wandelt auch auf den Füssen deines Willens zur Wahrheit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0032","text":"Der traf freilich die Wahrheit nicht, der das Wort nach ihr schoss vom „Willen zum Dasein“: diesen Willen—giebt es nicht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0033","text":"Denn: was nicht ist, das kann nicht wollen; was aber im Dasein ist, wie könnte das noch zum Dasein wollen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0034","text":"Nur, wo Leben ist, da ist auch Wille: aber nicht Wille zum Leben, sondern—so lehre ich’s dich—Wille zur Macht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0035","text":"Vieles ist dem Lebenden höher geschätzt, als Leben selber; doch aus dem Schätzen selber heraus redet—der Wille zur Macht!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0036","text":"Also lehrte mich einst das Leben: und daraus löse ich euch, ihr Weisesten, noch das Räthsel eures Herzens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0037","text":"Wahrlich, ich sage euch: Gutes und Böses, das unvergänglich wäre—das giebt es nicht! Aus sich selber muss es sich immer wieder überwinden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0038","text":"Mit euren Werthen und Worten von Gut und Böse übt ihr Gewalt, ihr Werthschätzenden: und diess ist eure verborgene Liebe und eurer Seele Glänzen, Zittern und Überwallen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0039","text":"Aber eine stärkere Gewalt wächst aus euren Werthen und eine neue Überwindung: an der zerbricht Ei und Eierschale.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0040","text":"Und wer ein Schöpfer sein muss im Guten und Bösen: wahrlich, der muss ein Vernichter erst sein und Werthe zerbrechen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0041","text":"Also gehört das höchste Böse zur höchsten Güte: diese aber ist die schöpferische.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0042","text":"Reden wir nur davon, ihr Weisesten, ob es gleich schlimm ist. Schweigen ist schlimmer; alle verschwiegenere Wahrheiten werden giftig.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0043","text":"Und mag doch Alles zerbrechen, was an unseren Wahrheiten zerbrechen—kann! Manches Haus giebt es noch zu bauen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0044","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"63602b43d75f6c3aacdb24da7e4bb84dd7ba12b1289288515a0f4d2400688432","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182036223582Z_60fbe524","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On Self-Overcoming","source_sha256":"63602b43d75f6c3aacdb24da7e4bb84dd7ba12b1289288515a0f4d2400688432","sha256":"dedd6a4fd41e20260574a73c4a39f83755f3358a7296cfb5b3a861f2b4e73788","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:20:36.223594+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0001","text":"“Will to truth” you call it, you wisest ones, that which drives you and makes you ardent?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0002","text":"Will to the thinkability of all that is: thus do I call your will!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0003","text":"You first want to make all that is thinkable: for with good distrust you doubt whether it is already thinkable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0004","text":"But it is to conform and bend itself to you! Thus your will wills it. Smooth shall it become and subject to the spirit, as its mirror and reflection.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0005","text":"This is your entire will, you wisest ones, as a will to power; and even when you speak of good and evil and of valuations. You still want to create the world before which you can kneel: thus it is your final hope and intoxication.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0006","text":"The unwise, to be sure, the people,—they are like the river upon which a boat floats onward: and in the boat the valuations sit solemnly and masked.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0007","text":"You set your will and your values upon the river of becoming; an old will to power is betrayed to me by what the people believe to be good and evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0008","text":"It was you, you wisest ones, who set such guests in this boat and gave them splendour and proud names,—you and your ruling will!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0009","text":"Now the river carries your boat onward: it must carry it. Little does it matter whether the broken wave foams and angrily contradicts the keel!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0010","text":"Not the river is your danger and the end of your good and evil, you wisest ones: but that will itself, the will to power,—the unexhausted procreative will of life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0011","text":"But that you may understand my word concerning good and evil: for that purpose I will tell you yet my word concerning life and concerning the kind of all living things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0012","text":"I pursued the living, I went the greatest and the smallest paths, that I might recognise its kind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0013","text":"With a hundredfold mirror I still caught its glance when its mouth was closed: that its eye might speak to me. And its eye spoke to me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0014","text":"But wherever I found living things, there too I heard the speech of obedience. All living things are obedient things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0015","text":"And this is the second: he who cannot obey himself is commanded. Thus is it the nature of the living.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0016","text":"But this is the third thing I heard: that commanding is harder than obeying. And not only because he who commands bears the burden of all who obey, and this burden easily crushes him:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0017","text":"In all commanding, an experiment and a venture appeared to me; and whenever it commands, the living thing risks itself thereby.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0018","text":"Indeed, even when it commands itself: even then it must pay for its commanding. It must become judge and avenger and sacrifice of its own law.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0019","text":"How does this happen! thus I asked myself. What persuades the living thing to obey and command and, even in commanding, to practise obedience?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0020","text":"Now hear my word, you wisest ones! Examine it seriously, whether I crept into the heart of life itself and even into the roots of its heart!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0021","text":"Wherever I found living things, there I found will to power; and even in the will of the servant I found the will to be master.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0022","text":"That the weaker should serve the stronger, thereto its will persuades it, which wills to be master over that which is still weaker: it cannot dispense with this pleasure alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0023","text":"And just as the smaller gives itself up to the greater, that it may have pleasure and power over the smallest: so too does the greatest still give itself up and, for the sake of power, stakes—life itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0024","text":"This is the self-surrender of the greatest, that it is venture and danger and a dice-game for death.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0025","text":"And where there are sacrifice and services and loving glances: there too is the will to be master. By secret paths the weaker one creeps into the fortress and even into the heart of the more powerful one—and there steals power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0026","text":"And life itself spoke this secret to me. Behold, it said, I am that which must always overcome itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0027","text":"“To be sure, you call it will to procreation or drive toward a purpose, toward the higher, more distant, more manifold: but all this is one and one secret.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0028","text":"I would rather perish than renounce this One; and verily, where there is perishing and falling of leaves, behold, there life sacrifices itself—for power!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0029","text":"That I must be struggle and becoming and purpose and the contradiction of purposes: ah, whoever guesses my will surely also guesses upon what crooked paths it must go!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0030","text":"Whatever I create and however I love it,—soon I must be its adversary and the adversary of my love: thus my will wills it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0031","text":"And you too, knower, are only a path and footprint of my will: verily, my will to power also walks on the feet of your will to truth!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0032","text":"He surely did not strike the truth who shot at it the phrase of the “will to existence”: such a will—does not exist!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0033","text":"For: what is not, cannot will; but what is in existence, how could it still will existence!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0034","text":"Only where there is life is there also will: but not will to life, rather—thus I teach it to you—will to power!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0035","text":"Many things are valued more highly by the living than life itself; yet out of valuing itself speaks—the will to power!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0036","text":"Thus life once taught me: and from this I shall yet unravel for you, you wisest ones, the riddle of your heart.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0037","text":"Verily, I say to you: good and evil that would be imperishable—there is no such thing! Out of itself it must always overcome itself anew.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0038","text":"With your values and words of good and evil you exercise power, you evaluators: and this is your hidden love and your soul’s shining, trembling, and overflowing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0039","text":"But a stronger power grows out of your values, and a new overcoming: against it egg and eggshell are shattered.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0040","text":"And whoever must be a creator in good and evil: verily, he must first be a destroyer and shatter values.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0041","text":"Thus the highest evil belongs to the highest good: but this is the creative.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0042","text":"Let us only speak of it, you wisest ones, even if it be bad. Silence is worse; all truths kept silent become poisonous.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0043","text":"And let everything break that can break upon our truths! There are still many houses to be built!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0044","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSB:P0044"]}],"source_digest":"b4ff43f47519ae72b8d98c93dab8e1aa5b5d0d403f34ff9a27b27bb30421286b","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von der Selbst-Überwindung"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von der Selbst-Überwindung","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0","title":"On the Sublime Ones","order":50,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDE0","text":"Still ist der Grund meines Meeres: wer erriethe wohl, dass er scherzhafte Ungeheuer birgt!\n\nUnerschütterlich ist meine Tiefe: aber sie glänzt von schwimmenden Räthseln und Gelächtern.\n\nEinen Erhabenen sah ich heute, einen Feierlichen, einen Büsser des Geistes: oh wie lachte meine Seele ob seiner Hässlichkeit!\n\nMit erhobener Brust und Denen gleich, welche den Athem an sich ziehn: also stand er da, der Erhabene, und schweigsam:\n\nBehängt mit hässlichen Wahrheiten, seiner Jagdbeute, und reich an zerrissenen Kleidern; auch viele Dornen hiengen an ihm—aber noch sah ich keine Rose.\n\nNoch lernte er das Lachen nicht und die Schönheit. Finster kam dieser Jäger zurück aus dem Walde der Erkenntniss.\n\nVom Kampfe kehrte er heim mit wilden Thieren: aber aus seinem Ernste blickt auch noch ein wildes Thier—ein unüberwundenes!\n\nWie ein Tiger steht er immer noch da, der springen will; aber ich mag diese gespannten Seelen nicht, unhold ist mein Geschmack allen diesen Zurückgezognen.\n\nUnd ihr sagt mir, Freunde, dass nicht zu streiten sei über Geschmack und Schmecken? Aber alles Leben ist Streit um Geschmack und Schmecken!\n\nGeschmack: das ist Gewicht zugleich und Wagschale und Wägender; und wehe allem Lebendigen, das ohne Streit um Gewicht und Wagschale und Wägende leben wollte!\n\nWenn er seiner Erhabenheit müde würde, dieser Erhabene: dann erst würde seine Schönheit anheben,—und dann erst will ich ihn schmecken und schmackhaft finden.\n\nUnd erst, wenn er sich von sich selber abwendet, wird er über seinen eignen Schatten springen—und, wahrlich! hinein in seine Sonne.\n\nAllzulange sass er im Schatten, die Wangen bleichten dem Büsser des Geistes; fast verhungerte er an seinen Erwartungen.\n\nVerachtung ist noch in seinem Auge; und Ekel birgt sich an seinem Munde. Zwar ruht er jetzt, aber seine Ruhe hat sich noch nicht in die Sonne gelegt.\n\nDem Stiere gleich sollte er thun; und sein Glück sollte nach Erde riechen und nicht nach Verachtung der Erde.\n\nAls weissen Stier möchte ich ihn sehn, wie er schnaubend und brüllend der Pflugschar vorangeht: und sein Gebrüll sollte noch alles Irdische preisen!\n\nDunkel noch ist sein Antlitz; der Hand Schatten spielt auf ihm. Verschattet ist noch der Sinn seines Auges.\n\nSeine That selber ist noch der Schatten auf ihm: die Hand verdunkelt den Handelnden. Noch hat er seine That nicht überwunden.\n\nWohl liebe ich an ihm den Nacken des Stiers: aber nun will ich auch noch das Auge des Engels sehn.\n\nAuch seinen Helden-Willen muss er noch verlernen: ein Gehobener soll er mir sein und nicht nur ein Erhabener:—der Äther selber sollte ihn heben, den Willenlosen!\n\nEr bezwang Unthiere, er löste Räthsel: aber erlösen sollte er auch noch seine Unthiere und Räthsel, zu himmlischen Kindern sollte er sie noch verwandeln.\n\nNoch hat seine Erkenntniss nicht lächeln gelernt und ohne Eifersucht sein; noch ist seine strömende Leidenschaft nicht stille geworden in der Schönheit.\n\nWahrlich, nicht in der Sattheit soll sein Verlangen schweigen und untertauchen, sondern in der Schönheit! Die Anmuth gehört zur Grossmuth des Grossgesinnten.\n\nDen Arm über das Haupt gelegt: so sollte der Held ausruhn, so sollte er auch noch sein Ausruhen überwinden.\n\nAber gerade dem Helden ist das Schöne aller Dinge Schwerstes. Unerringbar ist das Schöne allem heftigen Willen.\n\nEin Wenig mehr, ein Wenig weniger: das gerade ist hier Viel, das ist hier das Meiste.\n\nMit lässigen Muskeln stehn und mit abgeschirrtem Willen: das ist das Schwerste euch Allen, ihr Erhabenen!\n\nWenn die Macht gnädig wird und herabkommt in’s Sichtbare: Schönheit heisse ich solches Herabkommen.\n\nUnd von Niemandem will ich so als von dir gerade Schönheit, du Gewaltiger: deine Güte sei deine letzte Selbst- Überwältigung.\n\nAlles Böse traue ich dir zu: darum will ich von dir das Gute.\n\nWahrlich, ich lachte oft der Schwächlinge, welche sich gut glauben, weil sie lahme Tatzen haben!\n\nDer Säule Tugend sollst du nachstreben: schöner wird sie immer und zarter, aber inwendig härter und tragsamer, je mehr sie aufsteigt.\n\nJa, du Erhabener, einst sollst du noch schön sein und deiner eignen Schönheit den Spiegel vorhalten.\n\nDann wird deine Seele vor göttlichen Begierden schaudern; und Anbetung wird noch in deiner Eitelkeit sein!\n\nDiess nämlich ist das Geheimniss der Seele: erst, wenn sie der Held verlassen hat, naht ihr, im Traume,—der Über-Held.\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0001","text":"Still ist der Grund meines Meeres: wer erriethe wohl, dass er scherzhafte Ungeheuer birgt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0002","text":"Unerschütterlich ist meine Tiefe: aber sie glänzt von schwimmenden Räthseln und Gelächtern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0003","text":"Einen Erhabenen sah ich heute, einen Feierlichen, einen Büsser des Geistes: oh wie lachte meine Seele ob seiner Hässlichkeit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0004","text":"Mit erhobener Brust und Denen gleich, welche den Athem an sich ziehn: also stand er da, der Erhabene, und schweigsam:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0005","text":"Behängt mit hässlichen Wahrheiten, seiner Jagdbeute, und reich an zerrissenen Kleidern; auch viele Dornen hiengen an ihm—aber noch sah ich keine Rose.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0006","text":"Noch lernte er das Lachen nicht und die Schönheit. Finster kam dieser Jäger zurück aus dem Walde der Erkenntniss.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0007","text":"Vom Kampfe kehrte er heim mit wilden Thieren: aber aus seinem Ernste blickt auch noch ein wildes Thier—ein unüberwundenes!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0008","text":"Wie ein Tiger steht er immer noch da, der springen will; aber ich mag diese gespannten Seelen nicht, unhold ist mein Geschmack allen diesen Zurückgezognen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0009","text":"Und ihr sagt mir, Freunde, dass nicht zu streiten sei über Geschmack und Schmecken? Aber alles Leben ist Streit um Geschmack und Schmecken!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0010","text":"Geschmack: das ist Gewicht zugleich und Wagschale und Wägender; und wehe allem Lebendigen, das ohne Streit um Gewicht und Wagschale und Wägende leben wollte!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0011","text":"Wenn er seiner Erhabenheit müde würde, dieser Erhabene: dann erst würde seine Schönheit anheben,—und dann erst will ich ihn schmecken und schmackhaft finden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0012","text":"Und erst, wenn er sich von sich selber abwendet, wird er über seinen eignen Schatten springen—und, wahrlich! hinein in seine Sonne.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0013","text":"Allzulange sass er im Schatten, die Wangen bleichten dem Büsser des Geistes; fast verhungerte er an seinen Erwartungen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0014","text":"Verachtung ist noch in seinem Auge; und Ekel birgt sich an seinem Munde. Zwar ruht er jetzt, aber seine Ruhe hat sich noch nicht in die Sonne gelegt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0015","text":"Dem Stiere gleich sollte er thun; und sein Glück sollte nach Erde riechen und nicht nach Verachtung der Erde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0016","text":"Als weissen Stier möchte ich ihn sehn, wie er schnaubend und brüllend der Pflugschar vorangeht: und sein Gebrüll sollte noch alles Irdische preisen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0017","text":"Dunkel noch ist sein Antlitz; der Hand Schatten spielt auf ihm. Verschattet ist noch der Sinn seines Auges.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0018","text":"Seine That selber ist noch der Schatten auf ihm: die Hand verdunkelt den Handelnden. Noch hat er seine That nicht überwunden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0019","text":"Wohl liebe ich an ihm den Nacken des Stiers: aber nun will ich auch noch das Auge des Engels sehn.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0020","text":"Auch seinen Helden-Willen muss er noch verlernen: ein Gehobener soll er mir sein und nicht nur ein Erhabener:—der Äther selber sollte ihn heben, den Willenlosen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0021","text":"Er bezwang Unthiere, er löste Räthsel: aber erlösen sollte er auch noch seine Unthiere und Räthsel, zu himmlischen Kindern sollte er sie noch verwandeln.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0022","text":"Noch hat seine Erkenntniss nicht lächeln gelernt und ohne Eifersucht sein; noch ist seine strömende Leidenschaft nicht stille geworden in der Schönheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0023","text":"Wahrlich, nicht in der Sattheit soll sein Verlangen schweigen und untertauchen, sondern in der Schönheit! Die Anmuth gehört zur Grossmuth des Grossgesinnten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0024","text":"Den Arm über das Haupt gelegt: so sollte der Held ausruhn, so sollte er auch noch sein Ausruhen überwinden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0025","text":"Aber gerade dem Helden ist das Schöne aller Dinge Schwerstes. Unerringbar ist das Schöne allem heftigen Willen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0026","text":"Ein Wenig mehr, ein Wenig weniger: das gerade ist hier Viel, das ist hier das Meiste.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0027","text":"Mit lässigen Muskeln stehn und mit abgeschirrtem Willen: das ist das Schwerste euch Allen, ihr Erhabenen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0028","text":"Wenn die Macht gnädig wird und herabkommt in’s Sichtbare: Schönheit heisse ich solches Herabkommen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0029","text":"Und von Niemandem will ich so als von dir gerade Schönheit, du Gewaltiger: deine Güte sei deine letzte Selbst- Überwältigung.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0030","text":"Alles Böse traue ich dir zu: darum will ich von dir das Gute.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0031","text":"Wahrlich, ich lachte oft der Schwächlinge, welche sich gut glauben, weil sie lahme Tatzen haben!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0032","text":"Der Säule Tugend sollst du nachstreben: schöner wird sie immer und zarter, aber inwendig härter und tragsamer, je mehr sie aufsteigt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0033","text":"Ja, du Erhabener, einst sollst du noch schön sein und deiner eignen Schönheit den Spiegel vorhalten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0034","text":"Dann wird deine Seele vor göttlichen Begierden schaudern; und Anbetung wird noch in deiner Eitelkeit sein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0035","text":"Diess nämlich ist das Geheimniss der Seele: erst, wenn sie der Held verlassen hat, naht ihr, im Traume,—der Über-Held.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0036","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"d6af07c061e1f08d9059ea86d0ddf83f09eb674ef5f5b157044868b90e850286","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182052220641Z_671bc159","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Sublime Ones","source_sha256":"d6af07c061e1f08d9059ea86d0ddf83f09eb674ef5f5b157044868b90e850286","sha256":"129bb0c48c0890369b65f3a1580a82aeedc82826537460f32e21e1edb2082272","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:20:52.220654+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0001","text":"Still is the ground of my sea: who could guess that it harbours playful monsters!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0002","text":"Unshakable is my depth: but it gleams with swimming riddles and laughter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0003","text":"Today I saw a sublime man, a solemn man, a penitent of the spirit: oh, how my soul laughed at his ugliness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0004","text":"With uplifted breast and like those who draw in their breath: thus stood he there, the sublime man, and silent:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0005","text":"Hung about with ugly truths, his quarry, and rich in torn garments; many thorns too hung upon him—but as yet I saw no rose.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0006","text":"He has not yet learned laughter and beauty. Gloomy, this hunter came back from the forest of knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0007","text":"He returned home from the struggle with wild beasts: but out of his seriousness there still looks a wild beast—a beast not overcome!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0008","text":"Like a tiger he still stands there, ready to spring; but I do not like these tense souls; my taste finds all these withdrawn ones repellent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0009","text":"And you tell me, friends, that there can be no disputing about taste and tasting? But all life is a dispute over taste and tasting!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0010","text":"Taste: that is at once weight and scales and that which weighs; and woe to every living thing that would live without strife over weight and scales and that which weighs!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0011","text":"If this sublime man grew weary of his sublimity: only then would his beauty begin,—and only then will I taste him and find him savoury.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0012","text":"And only when he turns away from himself will he leap over his own shadow—and, truly! into his sun.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0013","text":"Far too long he sat in the shadow; the cheeks of the penitent of the spirit grew pale; he almost starved upon his expectations.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0014","text":"Contempt is still in his eye; and disgust hides itself at his mouth. To be sure, he rests now, but his rest has not yet lain down in the sun.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0015","text":"He should act like the bull; and his happiness should smell of earth and not of contempt for the earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0016","text":"I should like to see him as a white bull, snorting and bellowing as he goes before the ploughshare: and his bellowing should still praise all that is earthly!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0017","text":"His countenance is still dark; the shadow of the hand plays upon it. The meaning of his eye is still overshadowed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0018","text":"His deed itself is still the shadow upon him: the hand darkens the doer. He has not yet overcome his deed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0019","text":"Indeed, I love in him the neck of the bull: but now I would also like to see the eye of the angel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0020","text":"He must still unlearn his heroic will: he should be one who is raised up for me, and not merely a sublime one:—the ether itself should raise him up, the will-less one!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0021","text":"He conquered monsters, he solved riddles: but he should also still redeem his monsters and riddles, he should still transform them into heavenly children.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0022","text":"His knowledge has not yet learned to smile and to be without jealousy; his streaming passion has not yet become still in beauty.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0023","text":"Truly, his desire should not fall silent and plunge under in satiety, but in beauty! Grace belongs to the magnanimity of the great-minded.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0024","text":"With his arm laid over his head: thus should the hero rest, thus should he also still overcome his resting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0025","text":"But precisely for the hero, the beautiful is the most difficult of all things. The beautiful is unattainable for every vehement will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0026","text":"A little more, a little less: that is precisely here much, that is here the most.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0027","text":"To stand with relaxed muscles and with unharnessed will: that is the most difficult thing for you all, you sublime ones!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0028","text":"When power becomes gracious and descends into the visible: beauty I call such a descent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0029","text":"And from no one do I desire beauty precisely as from you, you mighty one: let your goodness be your final self-overcoming.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0030","text":"I believe you capable of all evil: therefore I desire the good from you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0031","text":"Verily, I have often laughed at the weaklings who believe themselves good because they have lame paws!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0032","text":"You should strive after the virtue of the pillar: it becomes ever more beautiful and more delicate, but inwardly harder and more load-bearing, the higher it rises.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0033","text":"Yes, you sublime one, one day you too shall be beautiful and hold up the mirror to your own beauty.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0034","text":"Then your soul will shudder with divine desires; and there will still be worship in your vanity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0035","text":"For this is the soul's secret: only when the hero has abandoned it does the over-hero draw near to it, in a dream,—the over-hero.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0036","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDE0:P0036"]}],"source_digest":"131441196caf05a2dcab236e27fe29ec51f1228194e80dbf7c34c29081ded980","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von den Erhabenen"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von den Erhabenen","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB","title":"On the Land of Culture","order":51,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VLDB","text":"Zu weit hinein flog ich in die Zukunft: ein Grauen überfiel mich.\n\nUnd als ich um mich sah, siehe! da war die Zeit mein einziger Zeitgenosse.\n\nDa floh ich rückwärts, heimwärts—und immer eilender: so kam ich zu euch, ihr Gegenwärtigen, und in’s Land der Bildung.\n\nZum ersten Male brachte ich ein Auge mit für euch, und gute Begierde: wahrlich, mit Sehnsucht im Herzen kam ich.\n\nAber wie geschah mir? So angst mir auch war,—ich musste lachen! Nie sah mein Auge etwas so Buntgesprenkeltes!\n\nIch lachte und lachte, während der Fuss mir noch zitterte und das Herz dazu: „hier ist ja die Heimat aller Farbentöpfe!“—sagte ich.\n\nMit fünfzig Klexen bemalt an Gesicht und Gliedern: so sasset ihr da zu meinem Staunen, ihr Gegenwärtigen!\n\nUnd mit fünfzig Spiegeln um euch, die eurem Farbenspiele schmeichelten und nachredeten!\n\nWahrlich, ihr könntet gar keine bessere Maske tragen, ihr Gegenwärtigen, als euer eignes Gesicht ist! Wer könnte euch— erkennen !\n\nVollgeschrieben mit den Zeichen der Vergangenheit, und auch diese Zeichen überpinselt mit neuen Zeichen: also habt ihr euch gut versteckt vor allen Zeichendeutern!\n\nUnd wenn man auch Nierenprüfer ist: wer glaubt wohl noch, dass ihr Nieren habt! Aus Farben scheint ihr gebacken und aus geleimten Zetteln.\n\nAlle Zeiten und Völker blicken bunt aus euren Schleiern; alle Sitten und Glauben reden bunt aus euren Gebärden.\n\nWer von euch Schleier und Überwürfe und Farben und Gebärden abzöge: gerade genug würde er übrig behalten, um die Vögel damit zu erschrecken.\n\nWahrlich, ich selber bin der erschreckte Vogel, der euch einmal nackt sah und ohne Farbe; und ich flog davon, als das Gerippe mir Liebe zuwinkte.\n\nLieber wollte ich doch noch Tagelöhner sein in der Unterwelt und bei den Schatten des Ehemals!—feister und voller als ihr sind ja noch die Unterweltlichen!\n\nDiess, ja diess ist Bitterniss meinen Gedärmen, dass ich euch weder nackt, noch bekleidet aushalte, ihr Gegenwärtigen!\n\nAlles Unheimliche der Zukunft, und was je verflogenen Vögeln Schauder machte, ist wahrlich heimlicher noch und traulicher als eure „Wirklichkeit“.\n\nDenn so sprecht ihr: „Wirkliche sind wir ganz, und ohne Glauben und Aberglauben“ : also brüstet ihr euch—ach, auch noch ohne Brüste!\n\nJa, wie solltet ihr glauben können , ihr Buntgesprenkelten!—die ihr Gemälde seid von Allem, was je geglaubt wurde!\n\nWandelnde Widerlegungen seid ihr des Glaubens selber, und aller Gedanken Gliederbrechen. Unglaubwürdige : also heisse ich euch, ihr Wirklichen!\n\nAlle Zeiten schwätzen wider einander in euren Geistern; und aller Zeiten Träume und Geschwätz waren wirklicher noch als euer Wachsein ist!\n\nUnfruchtbare seid ihr: darum fehlt es euch an Glauben. Aber wer schaffen musste, der hatte auch immer seine Wahr-Träume und Stern-Zeichen—und glaubte an Glauben!—\n\nHalboffne Thore seid ihr, an denen Todtengräber warten. Und das ist eure Wirklichkeit: „Alles ist werth, dass es zu Grunde geht.“\n\nAch, wie ihr mir dasteht, ihr Unfruchtbaren, wie mager in den Rippen! Und Mancher von euch hatte wohl dessen selber ein Einsehen.\n\nUnd er sprach: „es hat wohl da ein Gott, als ich schlief, mir heimlich Etwas entwendet? Wahrlich, genug, sich ein Weibchen daraus zu bilden!\n\nWundersam ist die Armuth meiner Rippen!“ also sprach schon mancher Gegenwärtige.\n\nJa, zum Lachen seid ihr mir, ihr Gegenwärtigen! Und sonderlich, wenn ihr euch über euch selber wundert!\n\nUnd wehe mir, wenn ich nicht lachen könnte über eure Verwunderung, und alles Widrige aus euren Näpfen hinunter trinken müsste!\n\nSo aber will ich’s mit euch leichter nehmen, da ich Schweres zu tragen habe; und was thut’s mir, wenn sich Käfer und Flügelwürmer noch auf mein Bündel setzen!\n\nWahrlich, es soll mir darob nicht schwerer werden! Und nicht aus euch, ihr Gegenwärtigen, soll mir die grosse Müdigkeit kommen.—Ach, wohin soll ich nun noch steigen mit meiner Sehnsucht! Von allen Bergen schaue ich aus nach Vater- und Mutterländern.\n\nAber Heimat fand ich nirgends: unstät bin ich in allen Städten und ein Aufbruch an allen Thoren.\n\nFremd sind mir und ein Spott die Gegenwärtigen, zu denen mich jüngst das Herz trieb; und vertrieben bin ich aus Vater- und Mutterländern.\n\nSo liebe ich allein noch meiner Kinder Land , das unentdeckte, im fernsten Meere: nach ihm heisse ich meine Segel suchen und suchen.\n\nAn meinen Kindern will ich es gut machen, dass ich meiner Väter Kind bin: und an aller Zukunft— diese Gegenwart!\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0001","text":"Zu weit hinein flog ich in die Zukunft: ein Grauen überfiel mich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0002","text":"Und als ich um mich sah, siehe! da war die Zeit mein einziger Zeitgenosse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0003","text":"Da floh ich rückwärts, heimwärts—und immer eilender: so kam ich zu euch, ihr Gegenwärtigen, und in’s Land der Bildung.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0004","text":"Zum ersten Male brachte ich ein Auge mit für euch, und gute Begierde: wahrlich, mit Sehnsucht im Herzen kam ich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0005","text":"Aber wie geschah mir? So angst mir auch war,—ich musste lachen! Nie sah mein Auge etwas so Buntgesprenkeltes!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0006","text":"Ich lachte und lachte, während der Fuss mir noch zitterte und das Herz dazu: „hier ist ja die Heimat aller Farbentöpfe!“—sagte ich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0007","text":"Mit fünfzig Klexen bemalt an Gesicht und Gliedern: so sasset ihr da zu meinem Staunen, ihr Gegenwärtigen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0008","text":"Und mit fünfzig Spiegeln um euch, die eurem Farbenspiele schmeichelten und nachredeten!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0009","text":"Wahrlich, ihr könntet gar keine bessere Maske tragen, ihr Gegenwärtigen, als euer eignes Gesicht ist! Wer könnte euch— erkennen !","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0010","text":"Vollgeschrieben mit den Zeichen der Vergangenheit, und auch diese Zeichen überpinselt mit neuen Zeichen: also habt ihr euch gut versteckt vor allen Zeichendeutern!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0011","text":"Und wenn man auch Nierenprüfer ist: wer glaubt wohl noch, dass ihr Nieren habt! Aus Farben scheint ihr gebacken und aus geleimten Zetteln.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0012","text":"Alle Zeiten und Völker blicken bunt aus euren Schleiern; alle Sitten und Glauben reden bunt aus euren Gebärden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0013","text":"Wer von euch Schleier und Überwürfe und Farben und Gebärden abzöge: gerade genug würde er übrig behalten, um die Vögel damit zu erschrecken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0014","text":"Wahrlich, ich selber bin der erschreckte Vogel, der euch einmal nackt sah und ohne Farbe; und ich flog davon, als das Gerippe mir Liebe zuwinkte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0015","text":"Lieber wollte ich doch noch Tagelöhner sein in der Unterwelt und bei den Schatten des Ehemals!—feister und voller als ihr sind ja noch die Unterweltlichen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0016","text":"Diess, ja diess ist Bitterniss meinen Gedärmen, dass ich euch weder nackt, noch bekleidet aushalte, ihr Gegenwärtigen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0017","text":"Alles Unheimliche der Zukunft, und was je verflogenen Vögeln Schauder machte, ist wahrlich heimlicher noch und traulicher als eure „Wirklichkeit“.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0018","text":"Denn so sprecht ihr: „Wirkliche sind wir ganz, und ohne Glauben und Aberglauben“ : also brüstet ihr euch—ach, auch noch ohne Brüste!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0019","text":"Ja, wie solltet ihr glauben können , ihr Buntgesprenkelten!—die ihr Gemälde seid von Allem, was je geglaubt wurde!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0020","text":"Wandelnde Widerlegungen seid ihr des Glaubens selber, und aller Gedanken Gliederbrechen. Unglaubwürdige : also heisse ich euch, ihr Wirklichen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0021","text":"Alle Zeiten schwätzen wider einander in euren Geistern; und aller Zeiten Träume und Geschwätz waren wirklicher noch als euer Wachsein ist!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0022","text":"Unfruchtbare seid ihr: darum fehlt es euch an Glauben. Aber wer schaffen musste, der hatte auch immer seine Wahr-Träume und Stern-Zeichen—und glaubte an Glauben!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0023","text":"Halboffne Thore seid ihr, an denen Todtengräber warten. Und das ist eure Wirklichkeit: „Alles ist werth, dass es zu Grunde geht.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0024","text":"Ach, wie ihr mir dasteht, ihr Unfruchtbaren, wie mager in den Rippen! Und Mancher von euch hatte wohl dessen selber ein Einsehen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0025","text":"Und er sprach: „es hat wohl da ein Gott, als ich schlief, mir heimlich Etwas entwendet? Wahrlich, genug, sich ein Weibchen daraus zu bilden!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0026","text":"Wundersam ist die Armuth meiner Rippen!“ also sprach schon mancher Gegenwärtige.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0027","text":"Ja, zum Lachen seid ihr mir, ihr Gegenwärtigen! Und sonderlich, wenn ihr euch über euch selber wundert!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0028","text":"Und wehe mir, wenn ich nicht lachen könnte über eure Verwunderung, und alles Widrige aus euren Näpfen hinunter trinken müsste!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0029","text":"So aber will ich’s mit euch leichter nehmen, da ich Schweres zu tragen habe; und was thut’s mir, wenn sich Käfer und Flügelwürmer noch auf mein Bündel setzen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0030","text":"Wahrlich, es soll mir darob nicht schwerer werden! Und nicht aus euch, ihr Gegenwärtigen, soll mir die grosse Müdigkeit kommen.—Ach, wohin soll ich nun noch steigen mit meiner Sehnsucht! Von allen Bergen schaue ich aus nach Vater- und Mutterländern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0031","text":"Aber Heimat fand ich nirgends: unstät bin ich in allen Städten und ein Aufbruch an allen Thoren.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0032","text":"Fremd sind mir und ein Spott die Gegenwärtigen, zu denen mich jüngst das Herz trieb; und vertrieben bin ich aus Vater- und Mutterländern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0033","text":"So liebe ich allein noch meiner Kinder Land , das unentdeckte, im fernsten Meere: nach ihm heisse ich meine Segel suchen und suchen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0034","text":"An meinen Kindern will ich es gut machen, dass ich meiner Väter Kind bin: und an aller Zukunft— diese Gegenwart!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0035","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"2c6f10d8391eb82fe5cd45f243f80b4c691e787a9942e3bd39a6f29eb7bde25c","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182108281111Z_21f43db5","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Land of Culture","source_sha256":"2c6f10d8391eb82fe5cd45f243f80b4c691e787a9942e3bd39a6f29eb7bde25c","sha256":"4fc542cdfa34bf79f6a93ae0d1a2a9cc5364ffb0bb8af480e1e2a8b55f8cf402","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:21:08.281131+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0001","text":"Too far did I fly into the future: a horror overcame me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0002","text":"And when I looked about me, behold! time was my sole contemporary.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0003","text":"Then I fled backwards, homewards—and ever more hastily: thus I came to you, you present-day people, and into the land of culture.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0004","text":"For the first time I brought an eye with me for you, and good desire: verily, I came with longing in my heart.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0005","text":"But what happened to me? Anxious as I was,—I had to laugh! Never did my eye see anything so variegated!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0006","text":"I laughed and laughed, while my foot still trembled and my heart as well: “here indeed is the homeland of all paint-pots!”—I said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0007","text":"Painted with fifty daubs upon face and limbs: thus you sat there, to my astonishment, you present-day people!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0008","text":"And with fifty mirrors about you, flattering and echoing your play of colours!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0009","text":"Verily, you could wear no better mask, you present-day people, than your own face is! Who could—recognise you!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0010","text":"Written all over with the signs of the past, and these signs, too, painted over with new signs: thus have you well hidden yourselves from all interpreters of signs!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0011","text":"And even if one is a tester of kidneys: who would still believe that you have kidneys! You seem baked from colours and pasted slips of paper.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0012","text":"All times and peoples peer variegatedly from your veils; all customs and beliefs speak variegatedly from your gestures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0013","text":"Whoever stripped from you veils and coverings and colours and gestures: he would retain just enough to frighten birds with.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0014","text":"Verily, I myself am the frightened bird that once saw you naked and without colour; and I flew away when the skeleton waved love to me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0015","text":"I would rather still be a day labourer in the underworld and among the shades of the Formerly!—the underworldly are indeed still fatter and fuller than you!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0016","text":"This, yes this, is bitterness to my bowels: that I can endure you neither naked nor clothed, you present-day people!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0017","text":"All that is uncanny in the future, and whatever has ever made fled birds shudder, is verily still more homely and familiar than your “reality.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0018","text":"For thus you speak: “We are entirely real, and without faith and superstition”: thus you vaunt yourselves—alas, even without breasts!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0019","text":"Yes, how should you be able to believe, you variegated ones!—you who are paintings of all that has ever been believed!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0020","text":"You are walking refutations of faith itself, and limb-breakers of every thought. Unbelievable ones: thus I call you, you real ones!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0021","text":"All times prattle against one another in your spirits; and the dreams and prattle of all times were still more real than your waking is!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0022","text":"You are barren: therefore you lack faith. But whoever had to create always also had his true-dreams and star-signs—and believed in belief!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0023","text":"You are half-open gates at which gravediggers wait. And this is your reality: “Everything is worthy of perishing.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0024","text":"Ah, how you stand there before me, you barren ones, how lean in the ribs! And many a one among you surely had insight into this himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0025","text":"And he spoke: “Has perhaps some god, while I slept, secretly stolen Something from me? Verily, enough to form a little woman out of it!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0026","text":"“Wondrous is the poverty of my ribs!” thus spoke many a present-day man already.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0027","text":"Yes, you present-day men are a laughing matter to me! And especially when you marvel at yourselves!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0028","text":"And woe unto me, if I could not laugh at your wonderment, and had to drink down everything repugnant from your bowls!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0029","text":"But thus I will take you more lightly, since I have heavy things to bear; and what does it matter to me if beetles and winged worms still settle upon my bundle!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0030","text":"Verily, it shall not become heavier for me on that account! And the great weariness shall not come to me from you, you present-day men.—Ah, whither shall I now still climb with my longing! From all mountains I look out for fatherlands and motherlands.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0031","text":"But nowhere have I found a homeland: unsettled am I in all cities and a departure at all gates.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0032","text":"Strange to me and a mockery are the present-day men, to whom my heart recently drove me; and I am driven out of fatherlands and motherlands.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0033","text":"Thus I love only the land of my children still, the undiscovered one, in the farthest sea: for it I bid my sails seek and seek.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0034","text":"Upon my children I will make good that I am my fathers’ child: and upon all the future—this present!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0035","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VLDB:P0035"]}],"source_digest":"b4936b74decdb55c1bb1c85a5cb6502fde0987d3257d54ddfb34f705aa3622ea","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Vom Lande der Bildung"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Vom Lande der Bildung","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE","title":"On Immaculate Perception","order":52,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDUE","text":"Als gestern der Mond aufgieng, wähnte ich, dass er eine Sonne gebären wolle: so breit und trächtig lag er am Horizonte.\n\nAber ein Lügner war er mir mit seiner Schwangerschaft; und eher noch will ich an den Mann im Monde glauben als an das Weib.\n\nFreilich, wenig Mann ist er auch, dieser schüchterne Nachtschwärmer. Wahrlich, mit schlechtem Gewissen wandelt er über die Dächer.\n\nDenn er ist lüstern und eifersüchtig, der Mönch im Monde, lüstern nach der Erde und nach allen Freuden der Liebenden.\n\nNein, ich mag ihn nicht, diesen Kater auf den Dächern! Widerlich sind mir Alle, die um halbverschlossne Fenster schleichen!\n\nFromm und schweigsam wandelt er hin auf Sternen-Teppichen:—aber ich mag alle leisetretenden Mannsfüsse nicht, an denen auch nicht ein Sporen klirrt.\n\nJedes Redlichen Schritt redet; die Katze aber stiehlt sich über den Boden weg. Siehe, katzenhaft kommt der Mond daher und unredlich.—\n\nDieses Gleichniss gebe ich euch empfindsamen Heuchlern, euch, den „Rein-Erkennenden!“ Euch heisse ich —Lüsterne!\n\nAuch ihr liebt die Erde und das Irdische: ich errieth euch wohl!—aber Scham ist in eurer Liebe und schlechtes Gewissen,—dem Monde gleicht ihr!\n\nZur Verachtung des Irdischen hat man euren Geist überredet, aber nicht eure Eingeweide: die aber sind das Stärkste an euch!\n\nUnd nun schämt sich euer Geist, dass er euren Eingeweiden zu willen ist und geht vor seiner eignen Scham Schleich- und Lügenwege.\n\n„Das wäre mir das Höchste—also redet euer verlogner Geist zu sich—auf das Leben ohne Begierde zu schaun und nicht gleich dem Hunde mit hängender Zunge:\n\nGlücklich zu sein im Schauen, mit erstorbenem Willen, ohne Griff und Gier der Selbstsucht—kalt und aschgrau am ganzen Leibe, aber mit trunkenen Mondesaugen!“\n\n„Das wäre mir das Liebste,—also verführt sich selber der Verführte—die Erde zu lieben, wie der Mond sie liebt, und nur mit dem Auge allein ihre Schönheit zu betasten.\n\nUnd das heisse mir aller Dinge unbefleckte Erkenntniss, dass ich von den Dingen Nichts will: ausser dass ich vor ihnen da liegen darf wie ein Spiegel mit hundert Augen.“—\n\nOh, ihr empfindsamen Heuchler, ihr Lüsternen! Euch fehlt die Unschuld in der Begierde: und nun verleumdet ihr drum das Begehren!\n\nWahrlich, nicht als Schaffende, Zeugende, Werdelustige liebt ihr die Erde!\n\nWo ist Unschuld? Wo der Wille zur Zeugung ist. Und wer über sich hinaus schaffen will, der hat mir den reinsten Willen.\n\nWo ist Schönheit? Wo ich mit allem Willen wollen muss ; wo ich lieben und untergehn will, dass ein Bild nicht nur Bild bleibe.\n\nLieben und Untergehn: das reimt sich seit Ewigkeiten. Wille zur Liebe: das ist, willig auch sein zum Tode. Also rede ich zu euch Feiglingen!\n\nAber nun will euer entmanntes Schielen „Beschaulichkeit“ heissen! Und was mit feigen Augen sich tasten lässt, soll „schön“ getauft werden! oh, ihr Beschmutzer edler Namen!\n\nAber das soll euer Fluch sein, ihr Unbefleckten, ihr Rein-Erkennenden, dass ihr nie gebären werdet: und wenn ihr auch breit und trächtig am Horizonte liegt!\n\nWahrlich, ihr nehmt den Mund voll mit edlen Worten: und wir sollen glauben, dass euch das Herz übergehe, ihr Lügenbolde?\n\nAber in eine Worte sind geringe, verachtete, krumme Worte: gerne nehme ich auf, was bei eurer Mahlzeit unter den Tisch fällt.\n\nImmer noch kann ich mit ihnen—Heuchlern die Wahrheit sagen! ja, meine Gräten, Muscheln und Stachelblätter sollen—Heuchlern die Nasen kitzeln!\n\nSchlechte Luft ist immer um euch und eure Mahlzeiten: eure lüsternen Gedanken, eure Lügen und Heimlichkeiten sind ja in der Luft!\n\nWagt es doch erst, euch selber zu glauben—euch und euren Eingeweiden! Wer sich selber nicht glaubt, lügt immer.\n\nEines Gottes Larve hängtet ihr um vor euch selber, ihr „Reinen“ : in eines Gottes Larve verkroch sich euer greulicher Ringelwurm.\n\nWahrlich, ihr täuscht, ihr „Beschaulichen“ ! Auch Zarathustra war einst der Narr eurer göttlichen Häute; nicht errieth er das Schlangengeringel, mit denen sie gestopft waren.\n\nEines Gottes Seele wähnte ich einst spielen zu sehn in euren Spielen, ihr Rein-Erkennenden! Keine bessere Kunst wähnte ich einst als eure Künste!\n\nSchlangen-Unflath und schlimmen Geruch verhehlte mir die Ferne: und dass einer Eidechse List lüstern hier herumschlich.\n\nAber ich kam euch nah : da kam mir der Tag—und nun kommt er euch,—zu Ende gieng des Mondes Liebschaft!\n\nSeht doch hin! Ertappt und bleich steht er da—vor der Morgenröthe!\n\nDenn schon kommt sie, die Glühende,— ihre Liebe zur Erde kommt! Unschuld und Schöpfer-Begier ist alle Sonnen-Liebe!\n\nSeht doch hin, wie sie ungeduldig über das Meer kommt! Fühlt ihr den Durst und den heissen Athem ihrer Liebe nicht?\n\nAm Meere will sie saugen und seine Tiefe zu sich in die Höhe trinken: da hebt sich die Begierde des Meeres mit tausend Brüsten.\n\nGeküsst und gesaugt will es sein vom Durste der Sonne; Luft will es werden und Höhe und Fusspfad des Lichts und selber Licht!\n\nWahrlich, der Sonne gleich liebe ich das Leben und alle tiefen Meere.\n\nUnd diess heisst mir Erkenntniss: alles Tiefe soll hinauf—zu meiner Höhe!\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0001","text":"Als gestern der Mond aufgieng, wähnte ich, dass er eine Sonne gebären wolle: so breit und trächtig lag er am Horizonte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0002","text":"Aber ein Lügner war er mir mit seiner Schwangerschaft; und eher noch will ich an den Mann im Monde glauben als an das Weib.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0003","text":"Freilich, wenig Mann ist er auch, dieser schüchterne Nachtschwärmer. Wahrlich, mit schlechtem Gewissen wandelt er über die Dächer.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0004","text":"Denn er ist lüstern und eifersüchtig, der Mönch im Monde, lüstern nach der Erde und nach allen Freuden der Liebenden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0005","text":"Nein, ich mag ihn nicht, diesen Kater auf den Dächern! Widerlich sind mir Alle, die um halbverschlossne Fenster schleichen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0006","text":"Fromm und schweigsam wandelt er hin auf Sternen-Teppichen:—aber ich mag alle leisetretenden Mannsfüsse nicht, an denen auch nicht ein Sporen klirrt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0007","text":"Jedes Redlichen Schritt redet; die Katze aber stiehlt sich über den Boden weg. Siehe, katzenhaft kommt der Mond daher und unredlich.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0008","text":"Dieses Gleichniss gebe ich euch empfindsamen Heuchlern, euch, den „Rein-Erkennenden!“ Euch heisse ich —Lüsterne!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0009","text":"Auch ihr liebt die Erde und das Irdische: ich errieth euch wohl!—aber Scham ist in eurer Liebe und schlechtes Gewissen,—dem Monde gleicht ihr!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0010","text":"Zur Verachtung des Irdischen hat man euren Geist überredet, aber nicht eure Eingeweide: die aber sind das Stärkste an euch!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0011","text":"Und nun schämt sich euer Geist, dass er euren Eingeweiden zu willen ist und geht vor seiner eignen Scham Schleich- und Lügenwege.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0012","text":"„Das wäre mir das Höchste—also redet euer verlogner Geist zu sich—auf das Leben ohne Begierde zu schaun und nicht gleich dem Hunde mit hängender Zunge:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0013","text":"Glücklich zu sein im Schauen, mit erstorbenem Willen, ohne Griff und Gier der Selbstsucht—kalt und aschgrau am ganzen Leibe, aber mit trunkenen Mondesaugen!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0014","text":"„Das wäre mir das Liebste,—also verführt sich selber der Verführte—die Erde zu lieben, wie der Mond sie liebt, und nur mit dem Auge allein ihre Schönheit zu betasten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0015","text":"Und das heisse mir aller Dinge unbefleckte Erkenntniss, dass ich von den Dingen Nichts will: ausser dass ich vor ihnen da liegen darf wie ein Spiegel mit hundert Augen.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0016","text":"Oh, ihr empfindsamen Heuchler, ihr Lüsternen! Euch fehlt die Unschuld in der Begierde: und nun verleumdet ihr drum das Begehren!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0017","text":"Wahrlich, nicht als Schaffende, Zeugende, Werdelustige liebt ihr die Erde!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0018","text":"Wo ist Unschuld? Wo der Wille zur Zeugung ist. Und wer über sich hinaus schaffen will, der hat mir den reinsten Willen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0019","text":"Wo ist Schönheit? Wo ich mit allem Willen wollen muss ; wo ich lieben und untergehn will, dass ein Bild nicht nur Bild bleibe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0020","text":"Lieben und Untergehn: das reimt sich seit Ewigkeiten. Wille zur Liebe: das ist, willig auch sein zum Tode. Also rede ich zu euch Feiglingen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0021","text":"Aber nun will euer entmanntes Schielen „Beschaulichkeit“ heissen! Und was mit feigen Augen sich tasten lässt, soll „schön“ getauft werden! oh, ihr Beschmutzer edler Namen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0022","text":"Aber das soll euer Fluch sein, ihr Unbefleckten, ihr Rein-Erkennenden, dass ihr nie gebären werdet: und wenn ihr auch breit und trächtig am Horizonte liegt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0023","text":"Wahrlich, ihr nehmt den Mund voll mit edlen Worten: und wir sollen glauben, dass euch das Herz übergehe, ihr Lügenbolde?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0024","text":"Aber in eine Worte sind geringe, verachtete, krumme Worte: gerne nehme ich auf, was bei eurer Mahlzeit unter den Tisch fällt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0025","text":"Immer noch kann ich mit ihnen—Heuchlern die Wahrheit sagen! ja, meine Gräten, Muscheln und Stachelblätter sollen—Heuchlern die Nasen kitzeln!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0026","text":"Schlechte Luft ist immer um euch und eure Mahlzeiten: eure lüsternen Gedanken, eure Lügen und Heimlichkeiten sind ja in der Luft!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0027","text":"Wagt es doch erst, euch selber zu glauben—euch und euren Eingeweiden! Wer sich selber nicht glaubt, lügt immer.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0028","text":"Eines Gottes Larve hängtet ihr um vor euch selber, ihr „Reinen“ : in eines Gottes Larve verkroch sich euer greulicher Ringelwurm.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0029","text":"Wahrlich, ihr täuscht, ihr „Beschaulichen“ ! Auch Zarathustra war einst der Narr eurer göttlichen Häute; nicht errieth er das Schlangengeringel, mit denen sie gestopft waren.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0030","text":"Eines Gottes Seele wähnte ich einst spielen zu sehn in euren Spielen, ihr Rein-Erkennenden! Keine bessere Kunst wähnte ich einst als eure Künste!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0031","text":"Schlangen-Unflath und schlimmen Geruch verhehlte mir die Ferne: und dass einer Eidechse List lüstern hier herumschlich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0032","text":"Aber ich kam euch nah : da kam mir der Tag—und nun kommt er euch,—zu Ende gieng des Mondes Liebschaft!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0033","text":"Seht doch hin! Ertappt und bleich steht er da—vor der Morgenröthe!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0034","text":"Denn schon kommt sie, die Glühende,— ihre Liebe zur Erde kommt! Unschuld und Schöpfer-Begier ist alle Sonnen-Liebe!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0035","text":"Seht doch hin, wie sie ungeduldig über das Meer kommt! Fühlt ihr den Durst und den heissen Athem ihrer Liebe nicht?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0036","text":"Am Meere will sie saugen und seine Tiefe zu sich in die Höhe trinken: da hebt sich die Begierde des Meeres mit tausend Brüsten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0037","text":"Geküsst und gesaugt will es sein vom Durste der Sonne; Luft will es werden und Höhe und Fusspfad des Lichts und selber Licht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0038","text":"Wahrlich, der Sonne gleich liebe ich das Leben und alle tiefen Meere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0039","text":"Und diess heisst mir Erkenntniss: alles Tiefe soll hinauf—zu meiner Höhe!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0040","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"aff1d0a7db0ce835f5162412d414509604d2670a0625eaa5a79c7651655ab1c0","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182128750989Z_61c3a729","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On Immaculate Perception","source_sha256":"aff1d0a7db0ce835f5162412d414509604d2670a0625eaa5a79c7651655ab1c0","sha256":"049fd76271fdff840a8535491f3412c0cc23bc08b7d79ce490e2faede5a5019d","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:21:28.751019+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0001","text":"When yesterday the moon rose, I imagined that it meant to give birth to a sun: so broad and pregnant it lay upon the horizon.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0002","text":"But it was a liar to me with its pregnancy; and sooner would I believe in the man in the moon than in the woman.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0003","text":"Certainly, little of a man is he too, this bashful night-roamer. Verily, with a bad conscience he wanders over the roofs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0004","text":"For he is lustful and jealous, the monk in the moon, lustful after the earth and after all the joys of lovers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0005","text":"No, I do not like him, this tomcat upon the roofs! All those who skulk about half-closed windows are loathsome to me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0006","text":"Pious and silent he goes his way upon star-carpets:—but I do not like all softly treading men's feet, upon which not even a spur jingles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0007","text":"Every honest step speaks; but the cat steals away over the ground. Behold, catlike the moon comes along, and dishonestly.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0008","text":"This parable I give to you sensitive hypocrites, to you, the “Pure Knowers!” You I call—Lustful ones!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0009","text":"You too love the earth and the earthly: I have guessed you well!—but shame is in your love and a bad conscience,—you resemble the moon!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0010","text":"Your spirit has been persuaded to despise the earthly, but not your entrails: yet these are the strongest thing in you!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0011","text":"And now your spirit is ashamed that it is compliant with your entrails, and because of its own shame it goes by furtive and lying paths.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0012","text":"“That would be the highest thing for me—thus your mendacious spirit speaks to itself—to look upon life without desire, and not, like the dog, with hanging tongue:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0013","text":"To be happy in beholding, with will extinguished, without grasp and greed of selfishness—cold and ash-grey over the whole body, but with drunken moon-eyes!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0014","text":"“That would be dearest to me,—thus the seduced one seduces himself—to love the earth as the moon loves it, and with the eye alone to touch its beauty.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0015","text":"And let this be called for me the untainted knowledge of all things, that I want Nothing from things: except that I may lie before them as a mirror with a hundred eyes.”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0016","text":"Oh, you sensitive hypocrites, you lustful ones! You lack innocence in desire: and now you slander desiring because of it!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0017","text":"Verily, it is not as creators, begetters, desirers-of-becoming that you love the earth!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0018","text":"Where is innocence? Where there is the will to beget. And whoever wants to create beyond himself, he has, to me, the purest will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0019","text":"Where is beauty? Where I must will with all my will; where I want to love and to perish, so that an image may not remain merely an image.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0020","text":"To love and to perish: that has rhymed for eternities. Will to love: that is, to be willing also for death. Thus I speak to you cowards!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0021","text":"But now your emasculated squinting wants to be called “contemplation”! And what can be felt out with cowardly eyes is to be baptized “beautiful”! oh, you defilers of noble names!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0022","text":"But this shall be your curse, you Undefiled Ones, you Pure Knowers, that you shall never give birth: even if you lie broad and pregnant upon the horizon!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0023","text":"Verily, you fill your mouths with noble words: and are we to believe that your hearts overflow, you lying fellows?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0024","text":"But your words are petty, contemptible, crooked words: gladly I take up what falls beneath the table at your meal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0025","text":"Still I can tell the truth to them—hypocrites! yes, my fishbones, shells, and thorn-leaves shall—tickle the noses of hypocrites!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0026","text":"Bad air is always about you and your meals: for your lustful thoughts, your lies and secretiveness are indeed in the air!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0027","text":"Only dare first to believe in yourselves—in yourselves and your entrails! He who does not believe in himself always lies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0028","text":"You hung the mask of a god before yourselves, you “Pure Ones”: within the mask of a god your hideous tapeworm crept.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0029","text":"Verily, you deceive, you “Contemplatives”! Zarathustra too was once the fool of your divine skins; he did not guess the serpent-coils with which they were stuffed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0030","text":"Once I thought I saw a god’s soul at play in your plays, you Pure Knowers! Once I thought there was no better art than your arts!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0031","text":"Distance concealed from me serpent-filth and foul smell: and that here the cunning of a lizard crept about lustfully.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0032","text":"But I came near to you: then day came to me—and now it comes to you,—the moon’s love-affair has come to an end!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0033","text":"Just look! Caught and pale it stands there—before the dawn!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0034","text":"For already she comes, the glowing one,—her love for the earth comes! Innocence and creative desire is all sun-love!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0035","text":"Just look how impatiently she comes over the sea! Do you not feel the thirst and the hot breath of her love?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0036","text":"At the sea she would suck and drink its depth up to herself into the height: then the sea’s desire rises with a thousand breasts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0037","text":"It wants to be kissed and sucked by the sun’s thirst; it wants to become air and height and the light’s footpath and light itself!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0038","text":"Verily, like the sun I love life and all deep seas.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0039","text":"And this is what knowledge means to me: all that is deep shall rise up—to my height!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0040","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDUE:P0040"]}],"source_digest":"34131476f96f5496e682caa158215351ee6218dbf74716362aca646c29065bb0","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von der unbefleckten Erkenntniss"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von der unbefleckten Erkenntniss","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0","title":"On the Scholars","order":53,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDG0","text":"Als ich im Schlafe lag, da frass ein Schaf am Epheukranze meines Hauptes,—frass und sprach dazu: „Zarathustra ist kein Gelehrter mehr.“\n\nSprach’s und gieng stotzig davon und stolz. Ein Kind erzählte mir’s.\n\nGerne liege ich hier, wo die Kinder spielen, an der zerbrochnen Mauer, unter Disteln und rothen Mohnblumen.\n\nEin Gelehrter bin ich den Kindern noch und auch den Disteln und rothen Mohnblumen. Unschuldig sind sie, selbst noch in ihrer Bosheit.\n\nAber den Schafen bin ich’s nicht mehr: so will es mein Loos—gesegnet sei es!\n\nDenn diess ist die Wahrheit: ausgezogen bin ich aus dem Hause der Gelehrten: und die Thür habe ich noch hinter mir zugeworfen.\n\nZu lange sass meine Seele hungrig an ihrem Tische; nicht, gleich ihnen, bin ich auf das Erkennen abgerichtet wie auf das Nüsseknacken.\n\nFreiheit liebe ich und die Luft über frischer Erde; lieber noch will ich auf Ochsenhäuten schlafen, als auf ihren Würden und Achtbarkeiten.\n\nIch bin zu heiss und verbrannt von eigenen Gedanken: oft will es mir den Athem nehmen. Da muss ich in’s Freie und weg aus allen verstaubten Stuben.\n\nAber sie sitzen kühl in kühlem Schatten: sie wollen in Allem nur Zuschauer sein und hüten sich dort zu sitzen, wo die Sonne auf die Stufen brennt.\n\nGleich Solchen, die auf der Strasse stehn und die Leute angaffen, welche vorübergehn: also warten sie auch und gaffen Gedanken an, die Andre gedacht haben.\n\nGreift man sie mit Händen, so stäuben sie um sich gleich Mehlsäcken, und unfreiwillig. aber wer erriethe wohl, dass ihr Staub vom Korne stammt und von der gelben Wonne der Sommerfelder?\n\nGeben sie sich weise, so fröstelt mich ihrer kleinen Sprüche und Wahrheiten: ein Geruch ist oft an ihrer Weisheit, als ob sie aus dem Sumpfe stamme: und wahrlich, ich hörte auch schon den Frosch aus ihr quaken!\n\nGeschickt sind sie, sie haben kluge Finger: was will meine Einfalt bei ihrer Vielfalt! Alles Fädeln und Knüpfen und Weben verstehn ihre Finger: also wirken sie die Strümpfe des Geistes!\n\nGute Uhrwerke sind sie: nur sorge man, sie richtig aufzuziehn! Dann zeigen sie ohne Falsch die Stunde an und machen einen bescheidnen Lärm dabei.\n\nGleich Mühlwerken arbeiten sie und Stampfen: man werfe ihnen nur seine Fruchtkörner zu!—sie wissen schon, Korn klein zu mahlen und weissen Staub daraus zu machen.\n\nSie sehen einander gut auf die Finger und trauen sich nicht zum Besten. Erfinderisch in kleinen Schlauheiten warten sie auf Solche, deren Wissen auf lahmen Füssen geht,—gleich Spinnen warten sie.\n\nIch sah sie immer mit Vorsicht Gift bereiten; und immer zogen sie gläserne Handschuhe dabei an ihre Finger.\n\nAuch mit falschen Würfeln wissen sie zu spielen; und so eifrig fand ich sie spielen, dass sie dabei schwitzten.\n\nWir sind einander fremd, und ihre Tugenden gehn mir noch mehr wider den Geschmack, als ihre Falschheiten und falschen Würfel.\n\nUnd als ich bei ihnen wohnte, da wohnte ich über ihnen. Darüber wurden sie mir gram.\n\nSie wollen Nichts davon hören, dass Einer über ihren Köpfen wandelt; und so legten sie Holz und Erde und Unrath zwischen mich und ihre Köpfe.\n\nAlso dämpften sie den Schall meiner Schritte: und am schlechtesten wurde ich bisher von den Gelehrtesten gehört.\n\nAller Menschen Fehl und Schwäche legten sie zwischen sich und mich:—„Fehlboden“ heissen sie das in ihren Häusern.\n\nAber trotzdem wandele ich mit meinen Gedanken über ihren Köpfen; und selbst, wenn ich auf meinen eignen Fehlern wandeln wollte, würde ich noch über ihnen sein und ihren Köpfen.\n\nDenn die Menschen sind nicht gleich: so spricht die Gerechtigkeit. Und was ich will, dürften sie nicht wollen!\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0001","text":"Als ich im Schlafe lag, da frass ein Schaf am Epheukranze meines Hauptes,—frass und sprach dazu: „Zarathustra ist kein Gelehrter mehr.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0002","text":"Sprach’s und gieng stotzig davon und stolz. Ein Kind erzählte mir’s.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0003","text":"Gerne liege ich hier, wo die Kinder spielen, an der zerbrochnen Mauer, unter Disteln und rothen Mohnblumen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0004","text":"Ein Gelehrter bin ich den Kindern noch und auch den Disteln und rothen Mohnblumen. Unschuldig sind sie, selbst noch in ihrer Bosheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0005","text":"Aber den Schafen bin ich’s nicht mehr: so will es mein Loos—gesegnet sei es!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0006","text":"Denn diess ist die Wahrheit: ausgezogen bin ich aus dem Hause der Gelehrten: und die Thür habe ich noch hinter mir zugeworfen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0007","text":"Zu lange sass meine Seele hungrig an ihrem Tische; nicht, gleich ihnen, bin ich auf das Erkennen abgerichtet wie auf das Nüsseknacken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0008","text":"Freiheit liebe ich und die Luft über frischer Erde; lieber noch will ich auf Ochsenhäuten schlafen, als auf ihren Würden und Achtbarkeiten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0009","text":"Ich bin zu heiss und verbrannt von eigenen Gedanken: oft will es mir den Athem nehmen. Da muss ich in’s Freie und weg aus allen verstaubten Stuben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0010","text":"Aber sie sitzen kühl in kühlem Schatten: sie wollen in Allem nur Zuschauer sein und hüten sich dort zu sitzen, wo die Sonne auf die Stufen brennt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0011","text":"Gleich Solchen, die auf der Strasse stehn und die Leute angaffen, welche vorübergehn: also warten sie auch und gaffen Gedanken an, die Andre gedacht haben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0012","text":"Greift man sie mit Händen, so stäuben sie um sich gleich Mehlsäcken, und unfreiwillig. aber wer erriethe wohl, dass ihr Staub vom Korne stammt und von der gelben Wonne der Sommerfelder?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0013","text":"Geben sie sich weise, so fröstelt mich ihrer kleinen Sprüche und Wahrheiten: ein Geruch ist oft an ihrer Weisheit, als ob sie aus dem Sumpfe stamme: und wahrlich, ich hörte auch schon den Frosch aus ihr quaken!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0014","text":"Geschickt sind sie, sie haben kluge Finger: was will meine Einfalt bei ihrer Vielfalt! Alles Fädeln und Knüpfen und Weben verstehn ihre Finger: also wirken sie die Strümpfe des Geistes!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0015","text":"Gute Uhrwerke sind sie: nur sorge man, sie richtig aufzuziehn! Dann zeigen sie ohne Falsch die Stunde an und machen einen bescheidnen Lärm dabei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0016","text":"Gleich Mühlwerken arbeiten sie und Stampfen: man werfe ihnen nur seine Fruchtkörner zu!—sie wissen schon, Korn klein zu mahlen und weissen Staub daraus zu machen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0017","text":"Sie sehen einander gut auf die Finger und trauen sich nicht zum Besten. Erfinderisch in kleinen Schlauheiten warten sie auf Solche, deren Wissen auf lahmen Füssen geht,—gleich Spinnen warten sie.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0018","text":"Ich sah sie immer mit Vorsicht Gift bereiten; und immer zogen sie gläserne Handschuhe dabei an ihre Finger.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0019","text":"Auch mit falschen Würfeln wissen sie zu spielen; und so eifrig fand ich sie spielen, dass sie dabei schwitzten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0020","text":"Wir sind einander fremd, und ihre Tugenden gehn mir noch mehr wider den Geschmack, als ihre Falschheiten und falschen Würfel.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0021","text":"Und als ich bei ihnen wohnte, da wohnte ich über ihnen. Darüber wurden sie mir gram.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0022","text":"Sie wollen Nichts davon hören, dass Einer über ihren Köpfen wandelt; und so legten sie Holz und Erde und Unrath zwischen mich und ihre Köpfe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0023","text":"Also dämpften sie den Schall meiner Schritte: und am schlechtesten wurde ich bisher von den Gelehrtesten gehört.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0024","text":"Aller Menschen Fehl und Schwäche legten sie zwischen sich und mich:—„Fehlboden“ heissen sie das in ihren Häusern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0025","text":"Aber trotzdem wandele ich mit meinen Gedanken über ihren Köpfen; und selbst, wenn ich auf meinen eignen Fehlern wandeln wollte, würde ich noch über ihnen sein und ihren Köpfen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0026","text":"Denn die Menschen sind nicht gleich: so spricht die Gerechtigkeit. Und was ich will, dürften sie nicht wollen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0027","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"342d4edf2977c0e1c244248a29a905ef4f39e042830698d7a97f43efdd78a8c2","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182142374832Z_093cb3d0","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Scholars","source_sha256":"342d4edf2977c0e1c244248a29a905ef4f39e042830698d7a97f43efdd78a8c2","sha256":"62f77df5c9a184da0c01b764589745958e5e22bfaacf22e0b0423bc2fab12d76","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:21:42.374847+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0001","text":"As I lay asleep, a sheep was eating at the ivy-wreath upon my head,—eating and saying besides: “Zarathustra is no longer a scholar.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0002","text":"Thus it spoke and went off haughtily and proudly. A child told me of it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0003","text":"Gladly I lie here, where the children play, by the broken wall, beneath thistles and red poppies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0004","text":"I am still a scholar to the children, and also to the thistles and red poppies. Innocent are they, even still in their wickedness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0005","text":"But to the sheep I am so no longer: thus wills my lot—blessed be it!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0006","text":"For this is the truth: I have departed from the house of scholars: and I have even slammed the door behind me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0007","text":"Too long did my soul sit hungrily at their table; not, like them, am I trained for knowledge as for cracking nuts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0008","text":"I love freedom and the air above fresh earth; rather would I sleep on ox-hides than on their dignities and respectabilities.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0009","text":"I am too hot and scorched by my own thoughts: often it almost takes my breath away. Then I must go out into the open and away from all dusty rooms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0010","text":"But they sit coolly in cool shade: in all things they wish only to be spectators, and take care not to sit where the sun burns upon the steps.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0011","text":"Like those who stand in the street and stare at the people passing by: thus they too wait and stare at thoughts which others have thought.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0012","text":"If one seizes them with one's hands, they scatter dust about them like sacks of flour, and involuntarily. But who would guess that their dust comes from grain and from the yellow delight of summer fields?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0013","text":"When they pose as wise, I shiver at their little sayings and truths: there is often a smell about their wisdom, as though it came from the swamp: and verily, I have already heard the frog croak out of it!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0014","text":"They are skilful, they have clever fingers: what can my simplicity do amid their multiplicity! Their fingers understand all threading and knotting and weaving: thus they weave the stockings of the spirit!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0015","text":"They are good clockworks: only take care to wind them correctly! Then they show the hour without deception and make a modest noise in doing so.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0016","text":"Like mills they work and pound: one need only throw them one's grains of fruit!—they know well enough how to grind grain small and make white dust of it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0017","text":"They watch one another's fingers closely and do not trust one another to the best advantage. Inventive in petty cunning, they wait for those whose knowledge goes on lame feet,—like spiders they wait.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0018","text":"I always saw them prepare poison with caution; and they always drew glass gloves onto their fingers as they did so.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0019","text":"They also know how to play with loaded dice; and I found them playing so eagerly that they sweated over it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0020","text":"We are strangers to one another, and their virtues offend my taste still more than their falsities and loaded dice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0021","text":"And when I dwelt among them, I dwelt above them. For this they became angry with me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0022","text":"They will hear nothing of anyone walking above their heads; and so they laid wood and earth and rubbish between me and their heads.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0023","text":"Thus they muffled the sound of my steps: and hitherto I have been heard worst by the most scholarly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0024","text":"They laid every human fault and weakness between themselves and me:—they call it a “false floor” in their houses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0025","text":"But nevertheless I walk with my thoughts above their heads; and even if I wanted to walk upon my own errors, I would still be above them and their heads.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0026","text":"For human beings are not equal: thus speaks justice. And what I will, they must not be permitted to will!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0027","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDG0:P0027"]}],"source_digest":"b47d0a5660b84dcacf81cb410a86a66dbdc5c504cb8ce1960529c91977d1762f","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von den Gelehrten"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von den Gelehrten","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0","title":"On Poets","order":54,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDD0","text":"„Seit ich den Leib besser kenne,—sagte Zarathustra zu einem seiner Jünger—ist mir der Geist nur noch gleichsam Geist; und alles das „Unvergängliche“—das ist auch nur ein Gleichniss.“\n\n„So hörte ich dich schon einmal sagen, antwortete der Jünger; und damals fügtest du hinzu: „aber die Dichter lügen zuviel.“ Warum sagtest du doch, dass die Dichter zuviel lügen?“\n\n„Warum? sagte Zarathustra. Du fragst warum? Ich gehöre nicht zu Denen, welche man nach ihrem Warum fragen darf.\n\nIst denn mein Erleben von Gestern? Das ist lange her, dass ich die Gründe meiner Meinungen erlebte.\n\nMüsste ich nicht ein Fass sein von Gedächtniss, wenn ich auch meine Gründe bei mir haben wollte?\n\nSchon zuviel ist mir’s, meine Meinungen selber zu behalten; und mancher Vogel fliegt davon.\n\nUnd mitunter finde ich auch ein zugezogenes Thier in meinem Taubenschlage, das mir fremd ist, und das zittert, wenn ich meine Hand darauf lege.\n\nDoch was sagte dir einst Zarathustra? Dass die Dichter zuviel lügen?—Aber auch Zarathustra ist ein Dichter.\n\nGlaubst du nun, dass er hier die Wahrheit redete? Warum glaubst du das?“\n\nDer Jünger antwortete: „ich glaube an Zarathustra.“ Aber Zarathustra schüttelte den Kopf und lächelte.\n\nDer Glaube macht mich nicht selig, sagte er, zumal nicht der Glaube an mich.\n\nAber gesetzt, dass jemand allen Ernstes sagte, die Dichter lügen zuviel: so hat er Recht,— wir lügen zuviel.\n\nWir wissen auch zu wenig und sind schlechte Lerner: so müssen wir schon lügen.\n\nUnd wer von uns Dichtern hätte nicht seinen Wein verfälscht? Manch giftiger Mischmasch geschah in unsern Kellern, manches Unbeschreibliche ward da gethan.\n\nUnd weil wir wenig wissen, so gefallen uns von Herzen die geistig Armen, sonderlich wenn es junge Weibchen sind!\n\nUnd selbst nach den Dingen sind wir noch begehrlich, die sich die alten Weibchen Abends erzählen. Das heissen wir selber an uns das Ewig-Weibliche.\n\nUnd als ob es einen besondren geheimen Zugang zum Wissen gäbe, der sich Denen verschütte , welche Etwas lernen: so glauben wir an das Volk und seine „Weisheit“.\n\nDas aber glauben alle Dichter: dass wer im Grase oder an einsamen Gehängen liegend die Ohren spitze, Etwas von den Dingen erfahre, die zwischen Himmel und Erde sind.\n\nUnd kommen ihnen zärtliche Regungen, so meinen die Dichter immer, die Natur selber sei in sie verliebt:\n\nUnd sie schleiche zu ihrem Ohre, Heimliches hinein zu sagen und verliebte Schmeichelreden: dessen brüsten und blähen sie sich vor allen Sterblichen!\n\nAch, es giebt so viel Dinge zwischen Himmel und Erden, von denen sich nur die Dichter Etwas haben träumen lassen!\n\nUnd zumal über dem Himmel: denn alle Götter sind Dichter-Gleichniss, Dichter-Erschleichniss!\n\nWahrlich, immer zieht es uns hinan—nämlich zum Reich der Wolken: auf diese setzen wir unsre bunten Bälge und heissen sie dann Götter und Übermenschen:—\n\nSind sie doch gerade leicht genug für diese Stühle!—alle diese Götter und Übermenschen.\n\nAch, wie bin ich all des Unzulänglichen müde, das durchaus Ereigniss sein soll! Ach, wie bin ich der Dichter müde!\n\nAls Zarathustra so sprach, zürnte ihm sein Jünger, aber er schwieg. Und auch Zarathustra schwieg; und sein Auge hatte sich nach innen gekehrt, gleich als ob es in weite Fernen sähe. Endlich seufzte er und holte Athem.\n\nIch bin von Heute und Ehedem, sagte er dann; aber Etwas ist in mir, das ist von Morgen und übermorgen und Einstmals.\n\nIch wurde der Dichter müde, der alten und der neuen: Oberflächliche sind sie mir Alle und seichte Meere.\n\nSie dachten nicht genug in die Tiefe: darum sank ihr Gefühl nicht bis zu den Gründen.\n\nEtwas Wollust und etwas Langeweile: das ist noch ihr bestes Nachdenken gewesen.\n\nGespenster-Hauch und -Huschen gilt mir all ihr Harfen-Klingklang; was wussten sie bisher von der Inbrunst der Töne!—\n\nSie sind mir auch nicht reinlich genug: sie trüben Alle ihr Gewässer, dass es tief scheine.\n\nUnd gerne geben sie sich damit als Versöhner: aber Mittler und Mischer bleiben sie mir und Halb-und-Halbe und Unreinliche!—\n\nAch, ich warf wohl mein Netz in ihre Meere und wollte gute Fische fangen; aber immer zog ich eines alten Gottes Kopf herauf.\n\nSo gab dem Hungrigen das Meer einen Stein. Und sie selber mögen wohl aus dem Meere stammen.\n\nGewiss, man findet Perlen in ihnen: um so ähnlicher sind sie selber harten Schalthieren. Und statt der Seele fand ich oft bei ihnen gesalzenen Schleim.\n\nSie lernten vom Meere auch noch seine Eitelkeit: ist nicht das Meer der Pfau der Pfauen?\n\nNoch vor dem hässlichsten aller Büffel rollt es seinen Schweif hin, nimmer wird es seines Spitzenfächers von Silber und Seide müde.\n\nTrutzig blickt der Büffel dazu, dem Sande nahe in seiner Seele, näher noch dem Dickicht, am nächsten aber dem Sumpfe.\n\nWas ist ihm Schönheit und Meer und Pfauen-Zierath! Dieses Gleichniss sage ich den Dichtern.\n\nWahrlich, ihr Geist selber ist der Pfau der Pfauen und ein Meer von Eitelkeit!\n\nZuschauer will der Geist des Dichters: sollten’s auch Büffel sein!—\n\nAber dieses Geistes wurde ich müde: und ich sehe kommen, dass er seiner selber müde wird.\n\nVerwandelt sah ich schon die Dichter und gegen sich selber den Blick gerichtet.\n\nBüsser des Geistes sah ich kommen: die wuchsen aus ihnen.\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0001","text":"„Seit ich den Leib besser kenne,—sagte Zarathustra zu einem seiner Jünger—ist mir der Geist nur noch gleichsam Geist; und alles das „Unvergängliche“—das ist auch nur ein Gleichniss.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0002","text":"„So hörte ich dich schon einmal sagen, antwortete der Jünger; und damals fügtest du hinzu: „aber die Dichter lügen zuviel.“ Warum sagtest du doch, dass die Dichter zuviel lügen?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0003","text":"„Warum? sagte Zarathustra. Du fragst warum? Ich gehöre nicht zu Denen, welche man nach ihrem Warum fragen darf.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0004","text":"Ist denn mein Erleben von Gestern? Das ist lange her, dass ich die Gründe meiner Meinungen erlebte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0005","text":"Müsste ich nicht ein Fass sein von Gedächtniss, wenn ich auch meine Gründe bei mir haben wollte?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0006","text":"Schon zuviel ist mir’s, meine Meinungen selber zu behalten; und mancher Vogel fliegt davon.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0007","text":"Und mitunter finde ich auch ein zugezogenes Thier in meinem Taubenschlage, das mir fremd ist, und das zittert, wenn ich meine Hand darauf lege.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0008","text":"Doch was sagte dir einst Zarathustra? Dass die Dichter zuviel lügen?—Aber auch Zarathustra ist ein Dichter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0009","text":"Glaubst du nun, dass er hier die Wahrheit redete? Warum glaubst du das?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0010","text":"Der Jünger antwortete: „ich glaube an Zarathustra.“ Aber Zarathustra schüttelte den Kopf und lächelte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0011","text":"Der Glaube macht mich nicht selig, sagte er, zumal nicht der Glaube an mich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0012","text":"Aber gesetzt, dass jemand allen Ernstes sagte, die Dichter lügen zuviel: so hat er Recht,— wir lügen zuviel.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0013","text":"Wir wissen auch zu wenig und sind schlechte Lerner: so müssen wir schon lügen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0014","text":"Und wer von uns Dichtern hätte nicht seinen Wein verfälscht? Manch giftiger Mischmasch geschah in unsern Kellern, manches Unbeschreibliche ward da gethan.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0015","text":"Und weil wir wenig wissen, so gefallen uns von Herzen die geistig Armen, sonderlich wenn es junge Weibchen sind!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0016","text":"Und selbst nach den Dingen sind wir noch begehrlich, die sich die alten Weibchen Abends erzählen. Das heissen wir selber an uns das Ewig-Weibliche.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0017","text":"Und als ob es einen besondren geheimen Zugang zum Wissen gäbe, der sich Denen verschütte , welche Etwas lernen: so glauben wir an das Volk und seine „Weisheit“.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0018","text":"Das aber glauben alle Dichter: dass wer im Grase oder an einsamen Gehängen liegend die Ohren spitze, Etwas von den Dingen erfahre, die zwischen Himmel und Erde sind.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0019","text":"Und kommen ihnen zärtliche Regungen, so meinen die Dichter immer, die Natur selber sei in sie verliebt:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0020","text":"Und sie schleiche zu ihrem Ohre, Heimliches hinein zu sagen und verliebte Schmeichelreden: dessen brüsten und blähen sie sich vor allen Sterblichen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0021","text":"Ach, es giebt so viel Dinge zwischen Himmel und Erden, von denen sich nur die Dichter Etwas haben träumen lassen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0022","text":"Und zumal über dem Himmel: denn alle Götter sind Dichter-Gleichniss, Dichter-Erschleichniss!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0023","text":"Wahrlich, immer zieht es uns hinan—nämlich zum Reich der Wolken: auf diese setzen wir unsre bunten Bälge und heissen sie dann Götter und Übermenschen:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0024","text":"Sind sie doch gerade leicht genug für diese Stühle!—alle diese Götter und Übermenschen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0025","text":"Ach, wie bin ich all des Unzulänglichen müde, das durchaus Ereigniss sein soll! Ach, wie bin ich der Dichter müde!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0026","text":"Als Zarathustra so sprach, zürnte ihm sein Jünger, aber er schwieg. Und auch Zarathustra schwieg; und sein Auge hatte sich nach innen gekehrt, gleich als ob es in weite Fernen sähe. Endlich seufzte er und holte Athem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0027","text":"Ich bin von Heute und Ehedem, sagte er dann; aber Etwas ist in mir, das ist von Morgen und übermorgen und Einstmals.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0028","text":"Ich wurde der Dichter müde, der alten und der neuen: Oberflächliche sind sie mir Alle und seichte Meere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0029","text":"Sie dachten nicht genug in die Tiefe: darum sank ihr Gefühl nicht bis zu den Gründen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0030","text":"Etwas Wollust und etwas Langeweile: das ist noch ihr bestes Nachdenken gewesen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0031","text":"Gespenster-Hauch und -Huschen gilt mir all ihr Harfen-Klingklang; was wussten sie bisher von der Inbrunst der Töne!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0032","text":"Sie sind mir auch nicht reinlich genug: sie trüben Alle ihr Gewässer, dass es tief scheine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0033","text":"Und gerne geben sie sich damit als Versöhner: aber Mittler und Mischer bleiben sie mir und Halb-und-Halbe und Unreinliche!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0034","text":"Ach, ich warf wohl mein Netz in ihre Meere und wollte gute Fische fangen; aber immer zog ich eines alten Gottes Kopf herauf.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0035","text":"So gab dem Hungrigen das Meer einen Stein. Und sie selber mögen wohl aus dem Meere stammen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0036","text":"Gewiss, man findet Perlen in ihnen: um so ähnlicher sind sie selber harten Schalthieren. Und statt der Seele fand ich oft bei ihnen gesalzenen Schleim.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0037","text":"Sie lernten vom Meere auch noch seine Eitelkeit: ist nicht das Meer der Pfau der Pfauen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0038","text":"Noch vor dem hässlichsten aller Büffel rollt es seinen Schweif hin, nimmer wird es seines Spitzenfächers von Silber und Seide müde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0039","text":"Trutzig blickt der Büffel dazu, dem Sande nahe in seiner Seele, näher noch dem Dickicht, am nächsten aber dem Sumpfe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0040","text":"Was ist ihm Schönheit und Meer und Pfauen-Zierath! Dieses Gleichniss sage ich den Dichtern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0041","text":"Wahrlich, ihr Geist selber ist der Pfau der Pfauen und ein Meer von Eitelkeit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0042","text":"Zuschauer will der Geist des Dichters: sollten’s auch Büffel sein!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0043","text":"Aber dieses Geistes wurde ich müde: und ich sehe kommen, dass er seiner selber müde wird.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0044","text":"Verwandelt sah ich schon die Dichter und gegen sich selber den Blick gerichtet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0045","text":"Büsser des Geistes sah ich kommen: die wuchsen aus ihnen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0046","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"eca74208d37193a7c6e4e543f5598fc066fd2aa9c7333f51deb2703661182bca","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182205075193Z_8daa5ec1","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On Poets","source_sha256":"eca74208d37193a7c6e4e543f5598fc066fd2aa9c7333f51deb2703661182bca","sha256":"ae6d299c5a0645e275dec9891bf2505e49548e9b894e5c8174c6d153d82fb7d5","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:22:05.075218+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0001","text":"“Since I have come to know the body better,” said Zarathustra to one of his disciples, “the spirit has become for me only, as it were, spirit; and all that ‘Imperishable’—that too is only a parable.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0002","text":"“I once heard you say that before,” replied the disciple; “and at that time you added: ‘but poets lie too much.’ Why, then, did you say that poets lie too much?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0003","text":"“Why?” said Zarathustra. “You ask why? I do not belong to those whom one may ask concerning their why.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0004","text":"Is my experience from yesterday? It is long ago that I experienced the grounds of my opinions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0005","text":"Would I not have to be a barrel of memory if I also wanted to have my grounds with me?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0006","text":"It is already too much for me to retain my opinions themselves; and many a bird flies away.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0007","text":"And at times I find, too, a creature that has strayed in, in my dovecote, which is strange to me and trembles when I lay my hand upon it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0008","text":"But what did Zarathustra once say to you? That poets lie too much?—But Zarathustra too is a poet.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0009","text":"Do you now believe that he spoke the truth here? Why do you believe that?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0010","text":"The disciple answered: “I believe in Zarathustra.” But Zarathustra shook his head and smiled.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0011","text":"Faith does not make me blessed, he said, least of all faith in me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0012","text":"But supposing that someone said in all seriousness that poets lie too much: then he is right,—we lie too much.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0013","text":"We also know too little and are poor learners: thus we must lie indeed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0014","text":"And which of us poets has not adulterated his wine? Many a poisonous mishmash occurred in our cellars; much that is indescribable was done there.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0015","text":"And because we know little, those poor in spirit please us from the heart, especially when they are young women!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0016","text":"And we are still desirous even of the things that old women tell one another in the evening. This we ourselves call in us the Eternal-Feminine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0017","text":"And as though there were a special secret access to knowledge, which closes itself off to Those who learn Something: thus we believe in the people and in its ‘wisdom’.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0018","text":"But all poets believe this: that whoever, lying in the grass or upon lonely slopes, pricks up his ears, learns Something of the things that are between heaven and earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0019","text":"And if tender stirrings come upon them, the poets always think that nature herself is in love with them:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0020","text":"And that she creeps to their ear to whisper secret things into it and loving flatteries: of this they boast and swell themselves before all mortals!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0021","text":"Ah, there are so many things between heaven and earth of which only poets have allowed themselves to dream Something!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0022","text":"And especially above heaven: for all gods are poet-parables, poetical things filched!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0023","text":"Truly, it always draws us upwards—namely, to the realm of the clouds: upon these we set our coloured bladders and then call them gods and overmen:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0024","text":"For they are just light enough for these chairs!—all these gods and overmen.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0025","text":"Ah, how weary I am of all the inadequate that is supposed to be event entirely! Ah, how weary I am of the poets!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0026","text":"When Zarathustra spoke thus, his disciple was angry with him, but he was silent. And Zarathustra too was silent; and his eye had turned inward, as though it were gazing into distant distances. At last he sighed and drew breath.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0027","text":"I am of Today and Formerly, he then said; but there is Something in me that is of Tomorrow and the day after tomorrow and Once upon a time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0028","text":"I grew weary of the poets, the old and the new: they are all superficial to me and shallow seas.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0029","text":"They did not think deeply enough: therefore their feeling did not sink down to the grounds.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0030","text":"A little lust and a little boredom: that has still been their best reflection.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0031","text":"Ghostly breath and flitting counts to me as all their harp-jingling; what did they hitherto know of the ardour of tones!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0032","text":"Nor are they cleanly enough for me: they all muddy their water so that it may seem deep.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0033","text":"And they like to present themselves thereby as reconcilers: but they remain to me mediators and mixers and half-and-halfers and uncleanly ones!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0034","text":"Ah, I indeed cast my net into their seas and wished to catch good fish; but I always drew up the head of an old god.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0035","text":"Thus the sea gave the hungry man a stone. And they themselves may well stem from the sea.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0036","text":"Certainly, one finds pearls in them: all the more are they themselves like hard shellfish. And instead of a soul I often found salty slime in them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0037","text":"They learned from the sea still further its vanity: is not the sea the peacock of peacocks?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0038","text":"Even before the ugliest of all buffaloes it rolls out its tail, never weary of its pointed fan of silver and silk.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0039","text":"Defiantly the buffalo looks on, close to the sand in its soul, still closer to the thicket, but closest to the swamp.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0040","text":"What are beauty and sea and peacock-ornament to it! I speak this parable to the poets.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0041","text":"Verily, their spirit itself is the peacock of peacocks and a sea of vanity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0042","text":"The spirit of the poet wants spectators: even if they should be buffaloes!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0043","text":"But I grew weary of this spirit: and I see coming to pass that it is growing weary of itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0044","text":"I already saw the poets transformed and their gaze directed against themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0045","text":"I saw penitents of the spirit coming: they grew out of them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0046","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDD0:P0046"]}],"source_digest":"e0ef39d5f0f13402f42ac293f1aa25f36ca6a92fe4bf882de0a83a5e42133758","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von den Dichtern"}],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"Von den Dichtern","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0","title":"On Great Events","order":55,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VGE0","text":"Es giebt eine Insel im Meere—unweit den glückseligen Inseln Zarathustra’s—auf welcher beständig ein Feuerberg raucht; von der sagt das Volk, und sonderlich sagen es die alten Weibchen aus dem Volke, dass sie wie ein Felsblock vor das Thor der Unterwelt gestellt sei: durch den Feuerberg selber aber führe der schmale Weg abwärts, der zu diesem Thore der Unterwelt geleite.\n\nUm jene Zeit nun, als Zarathustra auf den glückseligen Inseln weilte, geschah es, dass ein Schiff an der Insel Anker warf, auf welcher der rauchende Berg steht; und seine Mannschaft gieng an’s Land, um Kaninchen zu schiessen. Gegen die Stunde des Mittags aber, da der Capitän und seine Leute wieder beisammen waren, sahen sie plötzlich durch die Luft einen Mann auf sich zukommen, und eine Stimme sagte deutlich: „es ist Zeit! Es ist die höchste Zeit!“ Wie die Gestalt ihnen aber am nächsten war—sie flog aber schnell gleich einem Schatten vorbei, in der Richtung, wo der Feuerberg lag—da erkannten sie mit grösster Bestürzung, dass es Zarathustra sei; denn sie hatten ihn Alle schon gesehn, ausgenommen der Capitän selber, und sie liebten ihn, wie das Volk liebt: also dass zu gleichen Theilen Liebe und Scheu beisammen sind.\n\n„Seht mir an! sagte der alte Steuermann, da fährt Zarathustra zur Hölle!“—\n\nUm die gleiche Zeit, als diese Schiffer an der Feuerinsel landeten, lief das Gerücht umher, dass Zarathustra verschwunden sei; und als man seine Freunde fragte, erzählten sie, er sei bei Nacht zu Schiff gegangen, ohne zu sagen, wohin er reisen wolle.\n\nAlso entstand eine Unruhe; nach drei Tagen aber kam zu dieser Unruhe die Geschichte der Schiffsleute hinzu—und nun sagte alles Volk, dass der Teufel Zarathustra geholt habe. Seine jünger lachten zwar ob dieses Geredes; und einer von ihnen sagte sogar: „eher glaube ich noch, dass Zarathustra sich den Teufel geholt hat.“ Aber im Grunde der Seele waren sie Alle voll Besorgniss und Sehnsucht: so war ihre Freude gross, als am fünften Tage Zarathustra unter ihnen erschien.\n\nUnd diess ist die Erzählung von Zarathustra’s Gespräch mit dem Feuerhunde.\n\nDie Erde, sagte er, hat eine Haut; und diese Haut hat Krankheiten. Eine dieser Krankheiten heisst zum Beispiel: „Mensch.“\n\nUnd eine andere dieser Krankheiten heisst „Feuerhund“: über den haben sich die Menschen Viel vorgelogen und vorlügen lassen.\n\nDiess Geheimniss zu ergründen gieng ich über das Meer: und ich habe die Wahrheit nackt gesehn, wahrlich! barfuss bis zum Halse.\n\nWas es mit dem Feuerhund auf sich hat, weiss ich nun; und insgleichen mit all den Auswurf- und Umsturz-Teufeln, vor denen sich nicht nur alte Weibchen fürchten.\n\nHeraus mit dir, Feuerhund, aus deiner Tiefe! rief ich, und bekenne, wie tief diese Tiefe ist! Woher ist das, was du da heraufschnaubst?\n\nDu trinkst reichlich am Meere: das verräth deine versalzte Beredsamkeit! Fürwahr, für einen Hund der Tiefe nimmst du deine Nahrung zu sehr von der Oberfläche!\n\nHöchstens für den Bauchredner der Erde halt’ ich dich: und immer, wenn ich Umsturz- und Auswurf-Teufel reden hörte, fand ich sie gleich dir: gesalzen, lügnerisch und flach.\n\nIhr versteht zu brüllen und mit Asche zu verdunkeln! Ihr seid die besten Grossmäuler und lerntet sattsam die Kunst, Schlamm heiss zu sieden.\n\nWo ihr seid, da muss stets Schlamm in der Nähe sein, und viel Schwammichtes, Höhlichtes, Eingezwängtes: das will in die Freiheit.\n\n„Freiheit“ brüllt ihr Alle am liebsten: aber ich verlernte den Glauben an „grosse Ereignisse,“ sobald viel Gebrüll und Rauch um sie herum ist.\n\nUnd glaube mir nur, Freund Höllenlärm! Die grössten Ereignisse—das sind nicht unsre lautesten, sondern unsre stillsten Stunden.\n\nNicht um die Erfinder von neuem Lärme: um die Erfinder von neuen Werthen dreht sich die Welt; unhörbar dreht sie sich.\n\nUnd gesteh es nur! Wenig war immer nur geschehn, wenn dein Lärm und Rauch sich verzog. Was liegt daran, dass eine Stadt zur Mumie wurde, und eine Bildsäule im Schlamme liegt!\n\nUnd diess Wort sage ich noch den Umstürzern von Bildsäulen. Das ist wohl die grösste Thorheit, Salz in’s Meer und Bildsäulen in den Schlamm zu werfen.\n\nIm Schlamme eurer Verachtung lag die Bildsäule: aber das ist gerade ihr Gesetz, dass ihr aus der Verachtung wieder Leben und lebende Schönheit wächst!\n\nMit göttlicheren Zügen steht sie nun auf und leidendverführerisch; und wahrlich! sie wird euch noch Dank sagen, dass ihr sie umstürztet, ihr Umstürzer!\n\nDiesen Rath aber rathe ich Königen und Kirchen und Allem, was alters- und tugendschwach ist—lasst euch nur umstürzen! Dass ihr wieder zum Leben kommt, und zu euch—die Tugend!—\n\nAlso redete ich vor dem Feuerhunde: da unterbrach er mich mürrisch und fragte: „Kirche? Was ist denn das?“\n\nKirche? antwortete ich, das ist eine Art von Staat, und zwar die verlogenste. Doch schweig still, du Heuchelhund! Du kennst deine Art wohl am besten schon!\n\nGleich dir selber ist der Staat ein Heuchelhund; gleich dir redet er gern mit Rauch und Gebrülle,—dass er glauben mache, gleich dir, er rede aus dem Bauch der Dinge.\n\nDenn er will durchaus das wichtigste Thier auf Erden sein, der Staat; und man glaubt’s ihm auch.—\n\nAls ich das gesagt hatte, gebärdete sich der Feuerhund wie unsinnig vor Neid. „Wie? schrie er, das wichtigste Thier auf Erden? Und man glaubt’s ihm auch?“ Und so viel Dampf und grässliche Stimmen kamen ihm aus dem Schlunde, dass ich meinte, er werde vor Arger und Neid ersticken.\n\nEndlich wurde er stiller, und sein Keuchen liess nach; sobald er aber stille war, sagte ich lachend:\n\n„Du ärgerst dich, Feuerhund: also habe ich über dich Recht!\n\nUnd dass ich auch noch Recht behalte, so höre von einem andern Feuerhunde: der spricht wirklich aus dem Herzen der Erde.\n\nGold haucht sein Athem und goldigen Regen: so will’s das Herz ihm. Was ist ihm Asche und Rauch und heisser Schleim noch!\n\nLachen flattert aus ihm wie ein buntes Gewölke; abgünstig ist er deinem Gurgeln und Speien und Grimmen der Ein- geweide!\n\nDas Gold aber und das Lachen—das nimmt er aus dem Herzen der Erde: denn dass du’s nur weisst,—das Herz der Erde ist von Gold.“\n\nAls diess der Feuerhund vernahm, hielt er’s nicht mehr aus, mir zuzuhören. Beschämt zog er seinen Schwanz ein, sagte auf eine kleinlaute Weise Wau! Wau! und kroch hinab in seine Höhle.—\n\nAlso erzählte Zarathustra. Seine Jünger aber hörten ihm kaum zu: so gross war ihre Begierde, ihm von den Schiffsleuten, den Kaninchen und dem fliegenden Manne zu erzählen.\n\n„Was soll ich davon denken! sagte Zarathustra. Bin ich denn ein Gespenst?\n\nAber es wird mein Schatten gewesen sein. Ihr hörtet wohl schon Einiges vom Wanderer und seinem Schatten?\n\nSicher aber ist das: ich muss ihn kürzer halten,—er verdirbt mir sonst noch den Ruf.“\n\nUnd nochmals schüttelte Zarathustra den Kopf und wunderte sich. „Was soll ich davon denken!“ sagte er nochmals.\n\n„Warum schrie denn das Gespenst: es ist Zeit! Es ist die höchste Zeit!\n\nWozu ist es denn—höchste Zeit?“—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0001","text":"Es giebt eine Insel im Meere—unweit den glückseligen Inseln Zarathustra’s—auf welcher beständig ein Feuerberg raucht; von der sagt das Volk, und sonderlich sagen es die alten Weibchen aus dem Volke, dass sie wie ein Felsblock vor das Thor der Unterwelt gestellt sei: durch den Feuerberg selber aber führe der schmale Weg abwärts, der zu diesem Thore der Unterwelt geleite.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0002","text":"Um jene Zeit nun, als Zarathustra auf den glückseligen Inseln weilte, geschah es, dass ein Schiff an der Insel Anker warf, auf welcher der rauchende Berg steht; und seine Mannschaft gieng an’s Land, um Kaninchen zu schiessen. Gegen die Stunde des Mittags aber, da der Capitän und seine Leute wieder beisammen waren, sahen sie plötzlich durch die Luft einen Mann auf sich zukommen, und eine Stimme sagte deutlich: „es ist Zeit! Es ist die höchste Zeit!“ Wie die Gestalt ihnen aber am nächsten war—sie flog aber schnell gleich einem Schatten vorbei, in der Richtung, wo der Feuerberg lag—da erkannten sie mit grösster Bestürzung, dass es Zarathustra sei; denn sie hatten ihn Alle schon gesehn, ausgenommen der Capitän selber, und sie liebten ihn, wie das Volk liebt: also dass zu gleichen Theilen Liebe und Scheu beisammen sind.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0003","text":"„Seht mir an! sagte der alte Steuermann, da fährt Zarathustra zur Hölle!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0004","text":"Um die gleiche Zeit, als diese Schiffer an der Feuerinsel landeten, lief das Gerücht umher, dass Zarathustra verschwunden sei; und als man seine Freunde fragte, erzählten sie, er sei bei Nacht zu Schiff gegangen, ohne zu sagen, wohin er reisen wolle.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0005","text":"Also entstand eine Unruhe; nach drei Tagen aber kam zu dieser Unruhe die Geschichte der Schiffsleute hinzu—und nun sagte alles Volk, dass der Teufel Zarathustra geholt habe. Seine jünger lachten zwar ob dieses Geredes; und einer von ihnen sagte sogar: „eher glaube ich noch, dass Zarathustra sich den Teufel geholt hat.“ Aber im Grunde der Seele waren sie Alle voll Besorgniss und Sehnsucht: so war ihre Freude gross, als am fünften Tage Zarathustra unter ihnen erschien.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0006","text":"Und diess ist die Erzählung von Zarathustra’s Gespräch mit dem Feuerhunde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0007","text":"Die Erde, sagte er, hat eine Haut; und diese Haut hat Krankheiten. Eine dieser Krankheiten heisst zum Beispiel: „Mensch.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0008","text":"Und eine andere dieser Krankheiten heisst „Feuerhund“: über den haben sich die Menschen Viel vorgelogen und vorlügen lassen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0009","text":"Diess Geheimniss zu ergründen gieng ich über das Meer: und ich habe die Wahrheit nackt gesehn, wahrlich! barfuss bis zum Halse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0010","text":"Was es mit dem Feuerhund auf sich hat, weiss ich nun; und insgleichen mit all den Auswurf- und Umsturz-Teufeln, vor denen sich nicht nur alte Weibchen fürchten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0011","text":"Heraus mit dir, Feuerhund, aus deiner Tiefe! rief ich, und bekenne, wie tief diese Tiefe ist! Woher ist das, was du da heraufschnaubst?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0012","text":"Du trinkst reichlich am Meere: das verräth deine versalzte Beredsamkeit! Fürwahr, für einen Hund der Tiefe nimmst du deine Nahrung zu sehr von der Oberfläche!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0013","text":"Höchstens für den Bauchredner der Erde halt’ ich dich: und immer, wenn ich Umsturz- und Auswurf-Teufel reden hörte, fand ich sie gleich dir: gesalzen, lügnerisch und flach.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0014","text":"Ihr versteht zu brüllen und mit Asche zu verdunkeln! Ihr seid die besten Grossmäuler und lerntet sattsam die Kunst, Schlamm heiss zu sieden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0015","text":"Wo ihr seid, da muss stets Schlamm in der Nähe sein, und viel Schwammichtes, Höhlichtes, Eingezwängtes: das will in die Freiheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0016","text":"„Freiheit“ brüllt ihr Alle am liebsten: aber ich verlernte den Glauben an „grosse Ereignisse,“ sobald viel Gebrüll und Rauch um sie herum ist.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0017","text":"Und glaube mir nur, Freund Höllenlärm! Die grössten Ereignisse—das sind nicht unsre lautesten, sondern unsre stillsten Stunden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0018","text":"Nicht um die Erfinder von neuem Lärme: um die Erfinder von neuen Werthen dreht sich die Welt; unhörbar dreht sie sich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0019","text":"Und gesteh es nur! Wenig war immer nur geschehn, wenn dein Lärm und Rauch sich verzog. Was liegt daran, dass eine Stadt zur Mumie wurde, und eine Bildsäule im Schlamme liegt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0020","text":"Und diess Wort sage ich noch den Umstürzern von Bildsäulen. Das ist wohl die grösste Thorheit, Salz in’s Meer und Bildsäulen in den Schlamm zu werfen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0021","text":"Im Schlamme eurer Verachtung lag die Bildsäule: aber das ist gerade ihr Gesetz, dass ihr aus der Verachtung wieder Leben und lebende Schönheit wächst!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0022","text":"Mit göttlicheren Zügen steht sie nun auf und leidendverführerisch; und wahrlich! sie wird euch noch Dank sagen, dass ihr sie umstürztet, ihr Umstürzer!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0023","text":"Diesen Rath aber rathe ich Königen und Kirchen und Allem, was alters- und tugendschwach ist—lasst euch nur umstürzen! Dass ihr wieder zum Leben kommt, und zu euch—die Tugend!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0024","text":"Also redete ich vor dem Feuerhunde: da unterbrach er mich mürrisch und fragte: „Kirche? Was ist denn das?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0025","text":"Kirche? antwortete ich, das ist eine Art von Staat, und zwar die verlogenste. Doch schweig still, du Heuchelhund! Du kennst deine Art wohl am besten schon!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0026","text":"Gleich dir selber ist der Staat ein Heuchelhund; gleich dir redet er gern mit Rauch und Gebrülle,—dass er glauben mache, gleich dir, er rede aus dem Bauch der Dinge.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0027","text":"Denn er will durchaus das wichtigste Thier auf Erden sein, der Staat; und man glaubt’s ihm auch.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0028","text":"Als ich das gesagt hatte, gebärdete sich der Feuerhund wie unsinnig vor Neid. „Wie? schrie er, das wichtigste Thier auf Erden? Und man glaubt’s ihm auch?“ Und so viel Dampf und grässliche Stimmen kamen ihm aus dem Schlunde, dass ich meinte, er werde vor Arger und Neid ersticken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0029","text":"Endlich wurde er stiller, und sein Keuchen liess nach; sobald er aber stille war, sagte ich lachend:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0030","text":"„Du ärgerst dich, Feuerhund: also habe ich über dich Recht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0031","text":"Und dass ich auch noch Recht behalte, so höre von einem andern Feuerhunde: der spricht wirklich aus dem Herzen der Erde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0032","text":"Gold haucht sein Athem und goldigen Regen: so will’s das Herz ihm. Was ist ihm Asche und Rauch und heisser Schleim noch!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0033","text":"Lachen flattert aus ihm wie ein buntes Gewölke; abgünstig ist er deinem Gurgeln und Speien und Grimmen der Ein- geweide!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0034","text":"Das Gold aber und das Lachen—das nimmt er aus dem Herzen der Erde: denn dass du’s nur weisst,—das Herz der Erde ist von Gold.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0035","text":"Als diess der Feuerhund vernahm, hielt er’s nicht mehr aus, mir zuzuhören. Beschämt zog er seinen Schwanz ein, sagte auf eine kleinlaute Weise Wau! Wau! und kroch hinab in seine Höhle.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0036","text":"Also erzählte Zarathustra. Seine Jünger aber hörten ihm kaum zu: so gross war ihre Begierde, ihm von den Schiffsleuten, den Kaninchen und dem fliegenden Manne zu erzählen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0037","text":"„Was soll ich davon denken! sagte Zarathustra. Bin ich denn ein Gespenst?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0038","text":"Aber es wird mein Schatten gewesen sein. Ihr hörtet wohl schon Einiges vom Wanderer und seinem Schatten?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0039","text":"Sicher aber ist das: ich muss ihn kürzer halten,—er verdirbt mir sonst noch den Ruf.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0040","text":"Und nochmals schüttelte Zarathustra den Kopf und wunderte sich. „Was soll ich davon denken!“ sagte er nochmals.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0041","text":"„Warum schrie denn das Gespenst: es ist Zeit! Es ist die höchste Zeit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0042","text":"Wozu ist es denn—höchste Zeit?“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0043","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"7375e83138ec7846189e3c2cd3ae4fe0e588fdb636377a073dc8dfcdaf5c94d1","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182227071626Z_df1402b2","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On Great Events","source_sha256":"7375e83138ec7846189e3c2cd3ae4fe0e588fdb636377a073dc8dfcdaf5c94d1","sha256":"2ee6f702cc7e210906dd919d28a3a4b189902a863673ca6576b050677aa0883f","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:22:27.071653+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0001","text":"There is an island in the sea—near the Blessed Isles of Zarathustra—upon which a volcano continually smokes; of it the people say, and especially the old women among the people say, that it has been set like a block of rock before the gate of the underworld: but through the volcano itself runs the narrow path downward that leads to this gate of the underworld.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0002","text":"Now about the time when Zarathustra was dwelling on the Blessed Isles, it happened that a ship cast anchor at the island upon which the smoking mountain stands; and its crew went ashore to shoot rabbits. But toward the hour of noon, when the captain and his men were together again, they suddenly saw a man coming toward them through the air, and a voice said distinctly: “it is time! It is the highest time!” But when the figure was nearest to them—it flew swiftly past like a shadow, in the direction where the volcano lay—they recognized with the greatest dismay that it was Zarathustra; for they had all seen him already, except the captain himself, and they loved him as the people love: so that love and awe are mingled in equal measure.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0003","text":"“Just look! said the old helmsman, there goes Zarathustra to hell!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0004","text":"At the same time as these sailors landed on the fire-island, the rumour was spreading that Zarathustra had disappeared; and when his friends were asked, they related that he had gone aboard ship by night, without saying where he wished to travel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0005","text":"Thus an uneasiness arose; three days later, however, the story of the seamen was added to this uneasiness—and now all the people said that the devil had fetched Zarathustra. His disciples indeed laughed at this talk; and one of them even said: “I would sooner believe that Zarathustra has fetched himself the devil.” But in the depths of their souls they were all full of anxiety and longing: thus their joy was great when, on the fifth day, Zarathustra appeared among them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0006","text":"And this is the account of Zarathustra’s conversation with the fire-dog.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0007","text":"The earth, he said, has a skin; and this skin has diseases. One of these diseases is called, for example: “man.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0008","text":"And another of these diseases is called “fire-dog”: concerning it men have lied much to themselves and allowed themselves to be lied to.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0009","text":"To fathom this secret I went across the sea: and I saw truth naked, verily! bare-footed up to the neck.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0010","text":"What the fire-dog is about, I now know; and likewise all those devils of spewing-out and overthrow, of whom not only old women are afraid.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0011","text":"Out with you, fire-dog, from your depths! I cried, and confess how deep this depth is! Whence comes that which you snort up there?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0012","text":"You drink abundantly at the sea: your salt-laden eloquence betrays it! Verily, for a dog of the depths you take your nourishment too much from the surface!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0013","text":"At most I take you for the earth’s ventriloquist: and whenever I heard devils of overthrow and spewing-out speak, I found them like you: salty, mendacious, and shallow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0014","text":"You understand how to roar and to darken with ashes! You are the best loudmouths and have learned amply the art of boiling mud hot.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0015","text":"Where you are, mud must always be nearby, and much spongy, cavernous, constricted matter: that wants to get free.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0016","text":"“Freedom” is what you all most like to roar: but I have unlearned belief in “great events,” whenever there is much roaring and smoke around them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0017","text":"And only believe me, friend infernal din! The greatest events—these are not our loudest, but our stillest hours.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0018","text":"Not around the inventors of new noise: around the inventors of new values does the world revolve; inaudibly it revolves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0019","text":"And admit it! Little has ever happened when your noise and smoke dispersed. What does it matter that a city became a mummy, and a statue lies in the mud!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0020","text":"And I say this word also to the overthrowers of statues. It is surely the greatest folly to throw salt into the sea and statues into the mud.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0021","text":"In the mud of your contempt lay the statue: but this is precisely its law, that from contempt life and living beauty grow again for it!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0022","text":"With more divine features it now rises up, and seductively suffering; and verily! it will yet thank you for having overthrown it, you overthrowers!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0023","text":"But this counsel I counsel to kings and churches and to all that is weak through age and virtue—just let yourselves be overthrown! So that you may come again to life, and to yourselves—the virtue!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0024","text":"Thus I spoke before the fire-dog: then he interrupted me morosely and asked: “Church? What then is that?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0025","text":"“Church?” I replied, “that is a kind of state, and indeed the most mendacious kind. But keep quiet, you hypocritical dog! You surely know your own kind best already!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0026","text":"Like yourself, the state is a hypocritical dog; like yourself, it likes to speak with smoke and bellowing,—so as to make one believe that, like yourself, it speaks from the belly of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0027","text":"For it absolutely wants to be the most important animal on earth, the state; and people believe it too.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0028","text":"When I had said this, the fire-dog behaved as though mad with envy. “What?” he cried, “the most important animal on earth? And people believe it too?” And so much steam and dreadful voices came from his throat that I thought he would suffocate from vexation and envy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0029","text":"At last he grew quieter, and his panting subsided; but as soon as he was quiet, I said laughing:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0030","text":"“You are vexed, fire-dog: so I am right about you!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0031","text":"“And that I may remain right as well, hear of another fire-dog: he really speaks from the heart of the earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0032","text":"Gold his breath exhales, and golden rain: thus his heart wills it. What are ashes and smoke and hot slime to him still!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0033","text":"Laughter flutters forth from him like a variegated cloud; he is hostile to your gurgling and spitting and raging of the entrails!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0034","text":"But the gold and the laughter—he takes them from the heart of the earth: for, that you may know it,—the heart of the earth is of gold.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0035","text":"When the fire-dog heard this, he could no longer bear to listen to me. Ashamed, he tucked in his tail, said in a small voice, “Bow! Bow!” and crawled down into his cave.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0036","text":"Thus Zarathustra related. But his disciples scarcely listened to him: so great was their desire to tell him about the sailors, the rabbits, and the flying man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0037","text":"“What am I to think of this!” said Zarathustra. “Am I then a ghost?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0038","text":"But it will have been my shadow. You have surely already heard something of the Wanderer and his Shadow?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0039","text":"“But one thing is certain: I must keep him on a shorter leash,—otherwise he will yet ruin my reputation for me.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0040","text":"And once more Zarathustra shook his head and marvelled. “What am I to think of this!” he said once more.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0041","text":"“Why then did the ghost cry: it is time! It is the highest time!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0042","text":"For what is it—highest time?”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0043","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VGE0:P0043"]}],"source_digest":"fd3290abd2146a9f1040f4f2213daf9ebfef461fe1c34682de56a1a25a07adb9","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von grossen Ereignissen"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von grossen Ereignissen","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW","title":"The Soothsayer","order":56,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_DERW","text":"„- und ich sahe eine grosse Traurigkeit über die Menschen kommen. Die Besten wurden ihrer Werke müde.\n\nEine Lehre ergieng, ein Glauben lief neben ihr: „Alles ist leer, Alles ist gleich, Alles war!“\n\nUnd von allen Hügeln klang es wieder: „Alles ist leer, Alles ist gleich, Alles war!“\n\nWohl haben wir geerntet: aber warum wurden alle Früchte uns faul und braun? Was fiel vom bösen Monde bei der letzten Nacht hernieder?\n\nUmsonst war alle Arbeit, Gift ist unser Wein geworden, böser Blick sengte unsre Felder und Herzen gelb.\n\nTrocken wurden wir Alle; und fällt Feuer auf uns, so stäuben wir der Asche gleich:—ja das Feuer selber machten wir müde.\n\nAlle Brunnen versiegten uns, auch das Meer wich zurück. Aller Grund will reissen, aber die Tiefe will nicht schlingen!\n\n„Ach, wo ist noch ein Meer, in dem man ertrinken könnte“: so klingt unsre Klage - hinweg über flache Sümpfe.\n\nWahrlich, zum Sterben wurden wir schon zu müde; nun wachen wir noch und leben fort—in Grabkammern!“—\n\nAlso hörte Zarathustra einen Wahrsager reden; und seine Weissagung gieng ihm zu Herzen und verwandelte ihn. Traurig gieng er umher und müde; und er wurde Denen gleich, von welchen der Wahrsager geredet hatte.\n\nWahrlich, so sagte er zu seinen Jüngern, es ist um ein Kleines, so kommt diese lange Dämmerung. Ach, wie soll ich mein Licht hinüber retten!\n\nDass es mir nicht ersticke in dieser Traurigkeit! Ferneren Welten soll es ja Licht sein und noch fernsten Nächten!\n\nDergestalt im Herzen bekümmert gieng Zarathustra umher; und drei Tage lang nahm er nicht Trank und Speise zu sich, hatte keine Ruhe und verlor die Rede. Endlich geschah es, dass er in einen tiefen Schlaf verfiel. Seine jünger aber sassen um ihn in langen Nachtwachen und warteten mit Sorge, ob er wach werde und wieder rede und genesen sei von seiner Trübsal.\n\nDiess aber ist die Rede, welche Zarathustra sprach, als er aufwachte; seine Stimme aber kam zu seinen Jüngern wie aus weiter Ferne.\n\nHört mir doch den Traum, den ich träumte, ihr Freunde, und helft mir seinen Sinn rathen!\n\nEin Räthsel ist er mir noch, dieser Traum; sein Sinn ist verborgen in ihm und eingefangen und fliegt noch nicht über ihn hin mit freien Flügeln.\n\nAllem Leben hatte ich abgesagt, so träumte mir. Zum Nacht- und Grabwächter war ich worden, dort auf der einsamen Berg-Burg des Todes.\n\nDroben hütete ich seine Särge: voll standen die dumpfen Gewölbe von solchen Siegeszeichen. Aus gläsernen Särgen blickte mich überwundenes Leben an.\n\nDen Geruch verstaubter Ewigkeiten athmete ich: schwül und verstaubt lag meine Seele. Und wer hätte dort auch seine Seele lüften können!\n\nHelle der Mitternacht war immer um mich, Einsamkeit kauerte neben ihr; und, zudritt, röchelnde Todesstille, die schlimmste meiner Freundinnen.\n\nSchlüssel führte ich, die rostigsten aller Schlüssel; und ich verstand es, damit das knarrendste aller Thore zu öffnen.\n\nEinem bitterbösen Gekrächze gleich lief der Ton durch die langen Gänge, wenn sich des Thores Flügel hoben: unhold schrie dieser Vogel, ungern wollte er geweckt sein.\n\nAber furchtbarer noch und herzzuschnürender war es, wenn es wieder schwieg und rings stille ward, und ich allein sass in diesem tückischen Schweigen.\n\nSo gieng mir und schlich die Zeit, wenn Zeit es noch gab: was weiss ich davon! Aber endlich geschah das, was mich weckte.\n\nDreimal schlugen Schläge an’s Thor, gleich Donnern, es hallten und heulten die Gewölbe dreimal wieder: da gieng ich zum Thore.\n\nAlpa! rief ich, wer trägt seine Asche zu Berge? Alpa! Alpa! Wer trägt seine Asche zu Berge?\n\nUnd ich drückte den Schlüssel und hob am Thore und mühte mich. Aber noch keinen Fingerbreit stand es offen:\n\nDa riss ein brausender Wind seine Flügel auseinander: pfeifend, schrillend und schneidend warf er mir einen schwarzen Sarg zu:\n\nUnd im Brausen und Pfeifen und Schrillen zerbarst der Sarg und spie tausendfältiges Gelächter aus.\n\nUnd aus tausend Fratzen von Kindern, Engeln, Eulen, Narren und kindergrossen Schmetterlingen lachte und höhnte und brauste es wider mich.\n\nGrässlich erschrak ich darob: es warf mich nieder. Und ich schrie vor Grausen, wie nie ich schrie.\n\nAber der eigne Schrei weckte mich auf:—und ich kam zu mir.—\n\nAlso erzählte Zarathustra seinen Traum und schwieg dann: denn er wusste noch nicht die Deutung seines Traumes. Aber der jünger, den er am meisten lieb hatte, erhob sich schnell, fasste die Hand Zarathustra’s und sprach:\n\n„Dein Leben selber deutet uns diesen Traum, oh Zarathustra!\n\nBist du nicht selber der Wind mit schrillem Pfeifen, der den Burgen des Todes die Thore aufreisst?\n\nBist du nicht selber der Sarg voll bunter Bosheiten und Engelsfratzen des Lebens?\n\nWahrlich, gleich tausendfältigem Kindsgelächter kommt Zarathustra in alle Todtenkammern, lachend über diese Nacht- und Grabwächter, und wer sonst mit düstern Schlüsseln rasselt.\n\nSchrecken und umwerfen wirst du sie mit deinem Gelächter; Ohnmacht und Wachwerden wird deine Macht über sie beweisen.\n\nUnd auch, wenn die lange Dämmerung kommt und die Todesmüdigkeit, wirst du an unserm Himmel, nicht untergehn, du Fürsprecher des Lebens!\n\nNeue Sterne liessest du uns sehen und neue Nachtherrlichkeiten; wahrlich, das Lachen selber spanntest du wie ein buntes Gezelt über uns.\n\nNun wird immer Kindes-Lachen aus Särgen quellen; nun wird immer siegreich ein starker Wind kommen aller Todesmüdigkeit: dessen bist du uns selber Bürge und Wahrsager!\n\nWahrlich, sie selber träumtest du , deine Feinde: das war dein schwerster Traum!\n\nAber wie du von ihnen aufwachtest und zu dir kamst, also sollen sie selber von sich aufwachen—und zu dir kommen!“—\n\nSo sprach der jünger; und alle Anderen drängten sich nun um Zarathustra und ergriffen ihn bei den Händen und wollten ihn bereden, dass er vom Bette und von der Traurigkeit lasse und zu ihnen zurückkehre. Zarathustra aber sass aufgerichtet auf seinem Lager, und mit fremdem Blicke. Gleichwie Einer, der aus langer Fremde heimkehrt, sah er auf seine Jünger und prüfte ihre Gesichter; und noch erkannte er sie nicht. Als sie aber ihn hoben und auf die Füsse stellten, siehe, da verwandelte sich mit Einem Male sein Auge; er begriff Alles, was geschehen war, strich sich den Bart und sagte mit starker Stimme:\n\n„Wohlan! Diess nun hat seine Zeit; sorgt mir aber dafür, meine jünger, dass wir eine gute Mahlzeit machen, und in Kürze! Also gedenke ich Busse zu thun für schlimme Träume!\n\nDer Wahrsager aber soll an meiner Seite essen und trinken: und wahrlich, ich will ihm noch ein Meer zeigen, in dem er ertrinken kann!“\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra. Darauf aber blickte er dem jünger, welcher den Traumdeuter abgegeben hatte, lange in’s Gesicht und schüttelte dabei den Kopf. -","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0001","text":"„- und ich sahe eine grosse Traurigkeit über die Menschen kommen. Die Besten wurden ihrer Werke müde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0002","text":"Eine Lehre ergieng, ein Glauben lief neben ihr: „Alles ist leer, Alles ist gleich, Alles war!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0003","text":"Und von allen Hügeln klang es wieder: „Alles ist leer, Alles ist gleich, Alles war!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0004","text":"Wohl haben wir geerntet: aber warum wurden alle Früchte uns faul und braun? Was fiel vom bösen Monde bei der letzten Nacht hernieder?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0005","text":"Umsonst war alle Arbeit, Gift ist unser Wein geworden, böser Blick sengte unsre Felder und Herzen gelb.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0006","text":"Trocken wurden wir Alle; und fällt Feuer auf uns, so stäuben wir der Asche gleich:—ja das Feuer selber machten wir müde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0007","text":"Alle Brunnen versiegten uns, auch das Meer wich zurück. Aller Grund will reissen, aber die Tiefe will nicht schlingen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0008","text":"„Ach, wo ist noch ein Meer, in dem man ertrinken könnte“: so klingt unsre Klage - hinweg über flache Sümpfe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0009","text":"Wahrlich, zum Sterben wurden wir schon zu müde; nun wachen wir noch und leben fort—in Grabkammern!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0010","text":"Also hörte Zarathustra einen Wahrsager reden; und seine Weissagung gieng ihm zu Herzen und verwandelte ihn. Traurig gieng er umher und müde; und er wurde Denen gleich, von welchen der Wahrsager geredet hatte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0011","text":"Wahrlich, so sagte er zu seinen Jüngern, es ist um ein Kleines, so kommt diese lange Dämmerung. Ach, wie soll ich mein Licht hinüber retten!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0012","text":"Dass es mir nicht ersticke in dieser Traurigkeit! Ferneren Welten soll es ja Licht sein und noch fernsten Nächten!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0013","text":"Dergestalt im Herzen bekümmert gieng Zarathustra umher; und drei Tage lang nahm er nicht Trank und Speise zu sich, hatte keine Ruhe und verlor die Rede. Endlich geschah es, dass er in einen tiefen Schlaf verfiel. Seine jünger aber sassen um ihn in langen Nachtwachen und warteten mit Sorge, ob er wach werde und wieder rede und genesen sei von seiner Trübsal.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0014","text":"Diess aber ist die Rede, welche Zarathustra sprach, als er aufwachte; seine Stimme aber kam zu seinen Jüngern wie aus weiter Ferne.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0015","text":"Hört mir doch den Traum, den ich träumte, ihr Freunde, und helft mir seinen Sinn rathen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0016","text":"Ein Räthsel ist er mir noch, dieser Traum; sein Sinn ist verborgen in ihm und eingefangen und fliegt noch nicht über ihn hin mit freien Flügeln.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0017","text":"Allem Leben hatte ich abgesagt, so träumte mir. Zum Nacht- und Grabwächter war ich worden, dort auf der einsamen Berg-Burg des Todes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0018","text":"Droben hütete ich seine Särge: voll standen die dumpfen Gewölbe von solchen Siegeszeichen. Aus gläsernen Särgen blickte mich überwundenes Leben an.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0019","text":"Den Geruch verstaubter Ewigkeiten athmete ich: schwül und verstaubt lag meine Seele. Und wer hätte dort auch seine Seele lüften können!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0020","text":"Helle der Mitternacht war immer um mich, Einsamkeit kauerte neben ihr; und, zudritt, röchelnde Todesstille, die schlimmste meiner Freundinnen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0021","text":"Schlüssel führte ich, die rostigsten aller Schlüssel; und ich verstand es, damit das knarrendste aller Thore zu öffnen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0022","text":"Einem bitterbösen Gekrächze gleich lief der Ton durch die langen Gänge, wenn sich des Thores Flügel hoben: unhold schrie dieser Vogel, ungern wollte er geweckt sein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0023","text":"Aber furchtbarer noch und herzzuschnürender war es, wenn es wieder schwieg und rings stille ward, und ich allein sass in diesem tückischen Schweigen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0024","text":"So gieng mir und schlich die Zeit, wenn Zeit es noch gab: was weiss ich davon! Aber endlich geschah das, was mich weckte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0025","text":"Dreimal schlugen Schläge an’s Thor, gleich Donnern, es hallten und heulten die Gewölbe dreimal wieder: da gieng ich zum Thore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0026","text":"Alpa! rief ich, wer trägt seine Asche zu Berge? Alpa! Alpa! Wer trägt seine Asche zu Berge?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0027","text":"Und ich drückte den Schlüssel und hob am Thore und mühte mich. Aber noch keinen Fingerbreit stand es offen:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0028","text":"Da riss ein brausender Wind seine Flügel auseinander: pfeifend, schrillend und schneidend warf er mir einen schwarzen Sarg zu:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0029","text":"Und im Brausen und Pfeifen und Schrillen zerbarst der Sarg und spie tausendfältiges Gelächter aus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0030","text":"Und aus tausend Fratzen von Kindern, Engeln, Eulen, Narren und kindergrossen Schmetterlingen lachte und höhnte und brauste es wider mich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0031","text":"Grässlich erschrak ich darob: es warf mich nieder. Und ich schrie vor Grausen, wie nie ich schrie.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0032","text":"Aber der eigne Schrei weckte mich auf:—und ich kam zu mir.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0033","text":"Also erzählte Zarathustra seinen Traum und schwieg dann: denn er wusste noch nicht die Deutung seines Traumes. Aber der jünger, den er am meisten lieb hatte, erhob sich schnell, fasste die Hand Zarathustra’s und sprach:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0034","text":"„Dein Leben selber deutet uns diesen Traum, oh Zarathustra!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0035","text":"Bist du nicht selber der Wind mit schrillem Pfeifen, der den Burgen des Todes die Thore aufreisst?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0036","text":"Bist du nicht selber der Sarg voll bunter Bosheiten und Engelsfratzen des Lebens?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0037","text":"Wahrlich, gleich tausendfältigem Kindsgelächter kommt Zarathustra in alle Todtenkammern, lachend über diese Nacht- und Grabwächter, und wer sonst mit düstern Schlüsseln rasselt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0038","text":"Schrecken und umwerfen wirst du sie mit deinem Gelächter; Ohnmacht und Wachwerden wird deine Macht über sie beweisen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0039","text":"Und auch, wenn die lange Dämmerung kommt und die Todesmüdigkeit, wirst du an unserm Himmel, nicht untergehn, du Fürsprecher des Lebens!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0040","text":"Neue Sterne liessest du uns sehen und neue Nachtherrlichkeiten; wahrlich, das Lachen selber spanntest du wie ein buntes Gezelt über uns.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0041","text":"Nun wird immer Kindes-Lachen aus Särgen quellen; nun wird immer siegreich ein starker Wind kommen aller Todesmüdigkeit: dessen bist du uns selber Bürge und Wahrsager!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0042","text":"Wahrlich, sie selber träumtest du , deine Feinde: das war dein schwerster Traum!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0043","text":"Aber wie du von ihnen aufwachtest und zu dir kamst, also sollen sie selber von sich aufwachen—und zu dir kommen!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0044","text":"So sprach der jünger; und alle Anderen drängten sich nun um Zarathustra und ergriffen ihn bei den Händen und wollten ihn bereden, dass er vom Bette und von der Traurigkeit lasse und zu ihnen zurückkehre. Zarathustra aber sass aufgerichtet auf seinem Lager, und mit fremdem Blicke. Gleichwie Einer, der aus langer Fremde heimkehrt, sah er auf seine Jünger und prüfte ihre Gesichter; und noch erkannte er sie nicht. Als sie aber ihn hoben und auf die Füsse stellten, siehe, da verwandelte sich mit Einem Male sein Auge; er begriff Alles, was geschehen war, strich sich den Bart und sagte mit starker Stimme:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0045","text":"„Wohlan! Diess nun hat seine Zeit; sorgt mir aber dafür, meine jünger, dass wir eine gute Mahlzeit machen, und in Kürze! Also gedenke ich Busse zu thun für schlimme Träume!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0046","text":"Der Wahrsager aber soll an meiner Seite essen und trinken: und wahrlich, ich will ihm noch ein Meer zeigen, in dem er ertrinken kann!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0047","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra. Darauf aber blickte er dem jünger, welcher den Traumdeuter abgegeben hatte, lange in’s Gesicht und schüttelte dabei den Kopf. -","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"013636ef286f28c13f344bd88607f2d9ae1889ef2075729d73c879a182d5fc44","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182248175282Z_9bc33a3d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"The Soothsayer","source_sha256":"013636ef286f28c13f344bd88607f2d9ae1889ef2075729d73c879a182d5fc44","sha256":"4c759de4d38f2512a0b762ef43ac52f671465f5a2c9355861bc522f87fdd7500","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:22:48.175337+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0001","text":"“—and I saw a great sadness come over humankind. The best grew weary of their works.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0002","text":"A doctrine went forth, a belief ran alongside it: “All is empty, all is alike, all has been!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0003","text":"And from all hills it echoed back: “All is empty, all is alike, all has been!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0004","text":"Indeed, we have harvested: but why did all fruits become rotten and brown for us? What fell down from the evil moon on the last night?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0005","text":"All labour was in vain, our wine has become poison, an evil eye scorched our fields and hearts yellow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0006","text":"We all became dry; and if fire falls upon us, we scatter like ash:—indeed, we made fire itself weary.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0007","text":"All wells dried up for us, even the sea receded. All ground wants to tear open, but the depth does not want to swallow!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0008","text":"“Ah, where is there still a sea in which one could drown”: thus sounds our lament—away over shallow swamps.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0009","text":"Truly, we have already become too weary even to die; now we still keep watch and live on—in burial chambers!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0010","text":"Thus Zarathustra heard a soothsayer speak; and his prophecy went to his heart and transformed him. Sadly he went about, and weary; and he became like those of whom the soothsayer had spoken.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0011","text":"Truly, thus he said to his disciples, it will be but a little while before this long twilight comes. Ah, how shall I rescue my light across?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0012","text":"That it may not be stifled in this sadness! It is to be light for more distant worlds and for nights more distant still!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0013","text":"Thus troubled in his heart, Zarathustra went about; and for three days he took neither drink nor food, had no rest, and lost speech. At last it happened that he fell into a deep sleep. But his disciples sat around him in long night watches and waited anxiously to see whether he would awaken and speak again and be recovered from his melancholy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0014","text":"But this is the speech that Zarathustra spoke when he awoke; yet his voice came to his disciples as though from a great distance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0015","text":"Do listen to the dream I dreamed, my friends, and help me divine its meaning!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0016","text":"It is still a riddle to me, this dream; its meaning is concealed and captive within it, and does not yet fly above it on free wings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0017","text":"I had renounced all life, so I dreamed. I had become a night- and grave-warden there, upon the lonely mountain-castle of death.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0018","text":"Above, I guarded its coffins: the dim vaults stood full of such tokens of victory. From glass coffins conquered life looked out at me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0019","text":"I breathed the smell of dusty eternities: sultry and dusty my soul lay. And who there could have aired his soul!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0020","text":"The brightness of midnight was ever about me, solitude crouched beside it; and, making three, rattling deathly silence, the worst of my female friends.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0021","text":"I carried keys, the rustiest of all keys; and I knew how to open with them the creakiest of all gates.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0022","text":"Like a bitterly evil cawing, the sound ran through the long corridors when the gate’s leaves rose: this uncanny bird cried out, unwilling to be awakened.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0023","text":"But more terrible still and more constricting to the heart it was when it again fell silent and stillness came all around, and I sat alone in this treacherous silence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0024","text":"Thus time went and crept for me, if there still was time: what do I know of it! But at last that happened which awakened me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0025","text":"Three times blows struck against the gate, like thunder; three times the vaults echoed and howled back: then I went to the gate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0026","text":"Alpa! I cried, who bears his ashes up the mountain? Alpa! Alpa! Who bears his ashes up the mountain?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0027","text":"And I pressed the key and lifted at the gate and laboured. But it was not yet open even a finger’s breadth:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0028","text":"Then a roaring wind tore its wings apart: whistling, shrilling, and cutting, it flung a black coffin at me:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0029","text":"And amid the roaring and whistling and shrilling, the coffin burst apart and spewed forth manifold laughter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0030","text":"And from a thousand grimaces of children, angels, owls, fools, and butterflies the size of children, laughter and mockery and roaring came back at me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0031","text":"Terribly I was frightened thereby: it threw me down. And I screamed from horror, as I had never screamed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0032","text":"But my own scream woke me:—and I came to myself.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0033","text":"Thus Zarathustra told his dream and then fell silent: for he did not yet know the interpretation of his dream. But the disciple whom he loved most quickly rose, took Zarathustra’s hand, and spoke:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0034","text":"“Your life itself interprets this dream for us, O Zarathustra!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0035","text":"Are you not yourself the wind with shrill whistling that tears open the gates of the castles of death?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0036","text":"Are you not yourself the coffin full of the life’s colourful malices and angel-grimaces?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0037","text":"Verily, like manifold children’s laughter Zarathustra comes into all chambers of the dead, laughing at these guards of night and grave, and at whoever else rattles with gloomy keys.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0038","text":"You will terrify and overthrow them with your laughter; fainting and awakening will prove your power over them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0039","text":"And also, when the long twilight comes and weariness unto death, you will not go down from our heaven, you advocate of life!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0040","text":"You caused us to see new stars and new nocturnal glories; verily, you yourself stretched laughter like a colourful tent above us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0041","text":"Now children’s laughter will always well forth from coffins; now a strong wind will always come victorious over all weariness unto death: of this you yourself are our guarantor and soothsayer!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0042","text":"Verily, you yourself dreamed them, your enemies: that was your heaviest dream!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0043","text":"But as you awoke from them and came to yourself, so they themselves shall awaken from themselves—and come to you!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0044","text":"Thus spoke the disciple; and all the others now pressed around Zarathustra and took him by the hands and wished to persuade him to leave the bed and his sadness and return to them. But Zarathustra sat upright upon his couch, with a strange gaze. Like one returning home from a long foreignness, he looked at his disciples and scrutinised their faces; and he did not yet recognise them. But when they raised him and set him on his feet, behold, his eye suddenly changed; he understood all that had happened, stroked his beard, and said in a strong voice:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0045","text":"“Well then! This now has its time; but see to it for me, my disciples, that we make a good meal, and soon! Thus I intend to do penance for evil dreams!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0046","text":"But the soothsayer shall eat and drink at my side: and verily, I shall yet show him a sea in which he can drown!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERW:P0047","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra. But afterward he looked long into the face of the disciple who had acted as dream-interpreter, and shook his head as he did so. -","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERW:P0047"]}],"source_digest":"98a86acefd4f0e3e0aff60c5de28556c0b7527bb0e462baef23935c4bdf43ff3","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Der Wahrsager"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Der Wahrsager","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES","title":"On Redemption","order":57,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDES","text":"Als Zarathustra eines Tags über die grosse Brücke gieng, umringten ihn die Krüppel und Bettler, und ein Bucklichter redete also zu ihm:\n\n„Siehe, Zarathustra! Auch das Volk lernt von dir und gewinnt Glauben an deine Lehre: aber dass es ganz dir glauben soll, dazu bedarf es noch Eines—du musst erst noch uns Krüppel überreden! Hier hast du nun eine schöne Auswahl und wahrlich, eine Gelegenheit mit mehr als Einem Schopfe! Blinde kannst du heilen und Lahme laufen machen; und Dem, der zuviel hinter sich hat, könntest du wohl auch ein Wenig abnehmen:—das, meine ich, wäre die rechte Art, die Krüppel an Zarathustra glauben zu machen!“\n\nZarathustra aber erwiderte Dem, der da redete, also: „Wenn man dem Bucklichten seinen Buckel nimmt, so nimmt man ihm seinen Geist—also lehrt das Volk. Und wenn man dem Blinden seine Augen giebt, so sieht er zuviel schlimme Dinge auf Erden: also dass er Den verflucht, der ihn heilte. Der aber, welcher den Lahmen laufen macht, der thut ihm den grössten Schaden an: denn kaum kann er laufen, so gehn seine Laster mit ihm durch—also lehrt das Volk über Krüppel. Und warum sollte Zarathustra nicht auch vom Volke lernen, wenn das Volk von Zarathustra lernt?\n\nDas ist mir aber das Geringste, seit ich unter Menschen bin, dass ich sehe: „Diesem fehlt ein Auge und jenem ein Ohr und einem Dritten das Bein, und Andre giebt es, die verloren die Zunge oder die Nase oder den Kopf.“\n\nIch sehe und sah Schlimmeres und mancherlei so Abscheuliches, dass ich nicht von Jeglichem reden und von Einigem nicht einmal schweigen möchte: nämlich Menschen, denen es an Allem fehlt, ausser dass sie Eins zuviel haben—Menschen, welche Nichts weiter sind als ein grosses Auge, oder ein grosses Maul oder ein grosser Bauch oder irgend etwas Grosses,—umgekehrte Krüppel heisse ich Solche.\n\nUnd als ich aus meiner Einsamkeit kam und zum ersten Male über diese Brücke gieng: da traute ich meinen Augen nicht und sah hin, und wieder hin, und sagte endlich: „das ist ein Ohr! Ein Ohr, so gross wie ein Mensch!“ Ich sah noch besser hin: und wirklich, unter dem Ohre bewegte sich noch Etwas, das zum Erbarmen klein und ärmlich und schmächtig war. Und wahrhaftig, das ungeheure Ohr sass auf einem kleinen dünnen Stiele,—der Stiel aber war ein Mensch! Wer ein Glas vor das Auge nahm, konnte sogar noch ein kleines neidisches Gesichtchen erkennen; auch, dass ein gedunsenes Seelchen am Stiele baumelte. Das Volk sagte mir aber, das grosse Ohr sei nicht nur ein Mensch, sondern ein grosser Mensch, ein Genie. Aber ich glaubte dem Volke niemals, wenn es von grossen Menschen redete—und behielt meinen Glauben bei, dass es ein umgekehrter Krüppel sei, der an Allem zu wenig und an Einem zu viel habe.“\n\nAls Zarathustra so zu dem Bucklichten geredet hatte und zu Denen, welchen er Mundstück und Fürsprecher war, wandte er sich mit tiefem Unmuthe zu seinen Jüngern und sagte:\n\n„Wahrlich, meine Freunde, ich wandle unter den Menschen wie unter den Bruchstücken und Gliedmaassen von Menschen!\n\nDiess ist meinem Auge das Fürchterliche, dass ich den Menschen zertrümmert finde und zerstreuet wie über ein Schlacht- und Schlächterfeld hin.\n\nUnd flüchtet mein Auge vom Jetzt zum Ehemals: es findet immer das Gleiche: Bruchstücke und Gliedmaassen und grause Zufälle—aber keine Menschen!\n\nDas jetzt und das Ehemals auf Erden—ach! meine Freunde—das, ist mein Unerträglichstes; und ich wüsste nicht zu leben, wenn ich nicht noch ein Seher wäre, dessen, was kommen muss.\n\nEin Seher, ein Wollender, ein Schaffender, eine Zukunft selber und eine Brücke zur Zukunft—und ach, auch noch gleichsam ein Krüppel an dieser Brücke: das Alles ist Zarathustra.\n\nUnd auch ihr fragtet euch oft: „wer ist uns Zarathustra? Wie soll er uns heissen?“ Und gleich mir selber gabt ihr euch Fragen zur Antwort.\n\nIst er ein Versprechender? Oder ein Erfüller? Ein Erobernder? Oder ein Erbender? Ein Herbst? Oder eine Pflugschar? Ein Arzt? Oder ein Genesener?\n\nIst er ein Dichter? Oder ein Wahrhaftiger? Ein Befreier? Oder ein Bändiger? Ein Guter? Oder ein Böser?\n\nIch wandle unter Menschen als den Bruchstücken der Zukunft: jener Zukunft, die ich schaue.\n\nUnd das ist all mein Dichten und Trachten, dass ich in Eins dichte und zusammentragen was Bruchstück ist und Räthsel und grauser Zufall.\n\nUnd wie ertrüge ich es, Mensch zu sein, wenn der Mensch nicht auch Dichter und Räthselrather und der Erlöser des Zufalls wäre!\n\nDie Vergangnen zu erlösen und alles „Es war“ umzuschauen in ein „So wollte ich es!“—das hiesse mir erst Erlösung!\n\nWille—so heisst der Befreier und Freudebringer: also lehrte ich euch, meine Freunde! Und nun lernt diess hinzu: der Wille selber ist noch ein Gefangener.\n\nWollen befreit: aber wie heisst Das, was auch den Befreier noch in Ketten schlägt?\n\n„Es war“: also heisst des Willens Zähneknirschen und einsamste Trübsal. Ohnmächtig gegen Das, was gethan ist—ist er allem Vergangenen ein böser Zuschauer.\n\nNicht zurück kann der Wille wollen; dass er die Zeit nicht brechen kann und der Zeit Begierde,—das ist des Willens einsamste Trübsal.\n\nWollen befreit: was ersinnt sich das Wollen selber, dass es los seiner Trübsal werde und seines Kerkers spotte?\n\nAch, ein Narr wird jeder Gefangene! Närrisch erlöst sich auch der gefangene Wille.\n\nDass die Zeit nicht zurückläuft, das ist sein Ingrimm; „Das, was war“—so heisst der Stein, den er nicht wälzen kann.\n\nUnd so wälzt er Steine aus Ingrimm und Unmuth und übt Rache an dem, was nicht gleich ihm Grimm und Unmuth fühlt.\n\nAlso wurde der Wille, der Befreier, ein Wehethäter: und an Allem, was leiden kann, nimmt er Rache dafür, dass er nicht zurück kann.\n\nDiess, ja diess allein ist Rache selber: des Willens Widerwille gegen die Zeit und ihr „Es war.“\n\nWahrlich, eine grosse Narrheit wohnt in unserm Willen; und zum Fluche wurde es allem Menschlichen, dass diese Narrheit Geist lernte!\n\nDer Geist der Rache: meine Freunde, das war bisher der Menschen bestes Nachdenken; und wo Leid war, da sollte immer Strafe sein.\n\n„Strafe“ nämlich, so heisst sich die Rache selber: mit einem Lügenwort heuchelt sie sich ein gutes Gewissen.\n\nUnd weil im Wollenden selber Leid ist, darob dass es nicht zurück wollen kann, —also sollte Wollen selber und alles Leben—Strafe sein!\n\nUnd nun wälzte sich Wolke auf Wolke über den Geist: bis endlich der Wahnsinn predigte: „Alles vergeht, darum ist Alles werth zu vergehn!“\n\n„Und diess ist selber Gerechtigkeit, jenes Gesetz der Zeit, dass sie ihre Kinder fressen muss“: also predigte der Wahnsinn.\n\n„Sittlich sind die Dinge geordnet nach Recht und Strafe. Oh wo ist die Erlösung vom Fluss der Dinge und der Strafe Dasein“? Also predigte der Wahnsinn.\n\n„Kann es Erlösung geben, wenn es ein ewiges Recht giebt? Ach, unwälzbar ist der Stein „Es war“: ewig müssen auch alle Strafen sein!“ Also predigte der Wahnsinn.\n\n„Keine That kann vernichtet werden: wie könnte sie durch die Strafe ungethan werden! Diess, diess ist das Ewige an der Strafe „Dasein“, dass das Dasein auch ewig wieder That und Schuld sein muss!\n\nEs sei denn, dass der Wille endlich sich selber erlöste und Wollen zu Nicht-Wollen würde—“: doch ihr kennt, meine Brüder, diess Fabellied des Wahnsinns!\n\nWeg führte ich euch von diesen Fabelliedern, als ich euch lehrte: „der Wille ist ein Schaffender.“\n\nAlles „Es war“ ist ein Bruchstück, ein Räthsel, ein grauser Zufall—bis der schaffende Wille dazu sagt: „aber so wollte ich es!“\n\nBis der schaffende Wille dazu sagt: „Aber so will ich es! So werde ich’s wollen!“\n\nAber sprach er schon so? Und wann geschieht diess? Ist der Wille schon abgeschirrt von seiner eignen Thorheit?\n\nWurde der Wille sich selber schon Erlöser und Freudebringer? Verlernte er den Geist der Rache und alles Zähneknirschen?\n\nUnd wer lehrte ihn Versöhnung mit der Zeit, und Höheres als alle Versöhnung ist?\n\nHöheres als alle Versöhnung muss der Wille wollen, welcher der Wille zur Macht ist—: doch wie geschieht ihm das? Wer lehrte ihn auch noch das Zurückwollen?“\n\n—Aber an dieser Stelle seiner Rede geschah es, dass Zarathustra plötzlich innehielt und ganz einem Solchen gleich sah, der auf das Äusserste erschrickt. Mit erschrecktem Auge blickte er auf seine Jünger; sein Auge durchbohrte wie mit Pfeilen ihre Gedanken und Hintergedanken. Aber nach einer kleinen Weile lachte er schon wieder und sagte begütigt:\n\n„Es ist schwer, mit Menschen zu leben, weil Schweigen so schwer ist. Sonderlich für einen Geschwätzigen.“—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra. Der Bucklichte aber hatte dem Gespräche zugehört und sein Gesicht dabei bedeckt; als er aber Zarathustra lachen hörte, blickte er neugierig auf und sagte langsam:\n\n„Aber warum redet Zarathustra anders zu uns als zu seinen Jüngern?“\n\nZarathustra antwortete: „Was ist da zum Verwundern! Mit Bucklichten darf man schon bucklicht reden!“\n\n„Gut, sagte der Bucklichte; und mit Schülern darf man schon aus der Schule schwätzen.\n\nAber warum redet Zarathustra anders zu seinen Schülern—als zu sich selber?“—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0001","text":"Als Zarathustra eines Tags über die grosse Brücke gieng, umringten ihn die Krüppel und Bettler, und ein Bucklichter redete also zu ihm:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0002","text":"„Siehe, Zarathustra! Auch das Volk lernt von dir und gewinnt Glauben an deine Lehre: aber dass es ganz dir glauben soll, dazu bedarf es noch Eines—du musst erst noch uns Krüppel überreden! Hier hast du nun eine schöne Auswahl und wahrlich, eine Gelegenheit mit mehr als Einem Schopfe! Blinde kannst du heilen und Lahme laufen machen; und Dem, der zuviel hinter sich hat, könntest du wohl auch ein Wenig abnehmen:—das, meine ich, wäre die rechte Art, die Krüppel an Zarathustra glauben zu machen!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0003","text":"Zarathustra aber erwiderte Dem, der da redete, also: „Wenn man dem Bucklichten seinen Buckel nimmt, so nimmt man ihm seinen Geist—also lehrt das Volk. Und wenn man dem Blinden seine Augen giebt, so sieht er zuviel schlimme Dinge auf Erden: also dass er Den verflucht, der ihn heilte. Der aber, welcher den Lahmen laufen macht, der thut ihm den grössten Schaden an: denn kaum kann er laufen, so gehn seine Laster mit ihm durch—also lehrt das Volk über Krüppel. Und warum sollte Zarathustra nicht auch vom Volke lernen, wenn das Volk von Zarathustra lernt?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0004","text":"Das ist mir aber das Geringste, seit ich unter Menschen bin, dass ich sehe: „Diesem fehlt ein Auge und jenem ein Ohr und einem Dritten das Bein, und Andre giebt es, die verloren die Zunge oder die Nase oder den Kopf.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0005","text":"Ich sehe und sah Schlimmeres und mancherlei so Abscheuliches, dass ich nicht von Jeglichem reden und von Einigem nicht einmal schweigen möchte: nämlich Menschen, denen es an Allem fehlt, ausser dass sie Eins zuviel haben—Menschen, welche Nichts weiter sind als ein grosses Auge, oder ein grosses Maul oder ein grosser Bauch oder irgend etwas Grosses,—umgekehrte Krüppel heisse ich Solche.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0006","text":"Und als ich aus meiner Einsamkeit kam und zum ersten Male über diese Brücke gieng: da traute ich meinen Augen nicht und sah hin, und wieder hin, und sagte endlich: „das ist ein Ohr! Ein Ohr, so gross wie ein Mensch!“ Ich sah noch besser hin: und wirklich, unter dem Ohre bewegte sich noch Etwas, das zum Erbarmen klein und ärmlich und schmächtig war. Und wahrhaftig, das ungeheure Ohr sass auf einem kleinen dünnen Stiele,—der Stiel aber war ein Mensch! Wer ein Glas vor das Auge nahm, konnte sogar noch ein kleines neidisches Gesichtchen erkennen; auch, dass ein gedunsenes Seelchen am Stiele baumelte. Das Volk sagte mir aber, das grosse Ohr sei nicht nur ein Mensch, sondern ein grosser Mensch, ein Genie. Aber ich glaubte dem Volke niemals, wenn es von grossen Menschen redete—und behielt meinen Glauben bei, dass es ein umgekehrter Krüppel sei, der an Allem zu wenig und an Einem zu viel habe.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0007","text":"Als Zarathustra so zu dem Bucklichten geredet hatte und zu Denen, welchen er Mundstück und Fürsprecher war, wandte er sich mit tiefem Unmuthe zu seinen Jüngern und sagte:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0008","text":"„Wahrlich, meine Freunde, ich wandle unter den Menschen wie unter den Bruchstücken und Gliedmaassen von Menschen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0009","text":"Diess ist meinem Auge das Fürchterliche, dass ich den Menschen zertrümmert finde und zerstreuet wie über ein Schlacht- und Schlächterfeld hin.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0010","text":"Und flüchtet mein Auge vom Jetzt zum Ehemals: es findet immer das Gleiche: Bruchstücke und Gliedmaassen und grause Zufälle—aber keine Menschen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0011","text":"Das jetzt und das Ehemals auf Erden—ach! meine Freunde—das, ist mein Unerträglichstes; und ich wüsste nicht zu leben, wenn ich nicht noch ein Seher wäre, dessen, was kommen muss.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0012","text":"Ein Seher, ein Wollender, ein Schaffender, eine Zukunft selber und eine Brücke zur Zukunft—und ach, auch noch gleichsam ein Krüppel an dieser Brücke: das Alles ist Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0013","text":"Und auch ihr fragtet euch oft: „wer ist uns Zarathustra? Wie soll er uns heissen?“ Und gleich mir selber gabt ihr euch Fragen zur Antwort.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0014","text":"Ist er ein Versprechender? Oder ein Erfüller? Ein Erobernder? Oder ein Erbender? Ein Herbst? Oder eine Pflugschar? Ein Arzt? Oder ein Genesener?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0015","text":"Ist er ein Dichter? Oder ein Wahrhaftiger? Ein Befreier? Oder ein Bändiger? Ein Guter? Oder ein Böser?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0016","text":"Ich wandle unter Menschen als den Bruchstücken der Zukunft: jener Zukunft, die ich schaue.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0017","text":"Und das ist all mein Dichten und Trachten, dass ich in Eins dichte und zusammentragen was Bruchstück ist und Räthsel und grauser Zufall.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0018","text":"Und wie ertrüge ich es, Mensch zu sein, wenn der Mensch nicht auch Dichter und Räthselrather und der Erlöser des Zufalls wäre!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0019","text":"Die Vergangnen zu erlösen und alles „Es war“ umzuschauen in ein „So wollte ich es!“—das hiesse mir erst Erlösung!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0020","text":"Wille—so heisst der Befreier und Freudebringer: also lehrte ich euch, meine Freunde! Und nun lernt diess hinzu: der Wille selber ist noch ein Gefangener.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0021","text":"Wollen befreit: aber wie heisst Das, was auch den Befreier noch in Ketten schlägt?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0022","text":"„Es war“: also heisst des Willens Zähneknirschen und einsamste Trübsal. Ohnmächtig gegen Das, was gethan ist—ist er allem Vergangenen ein böser Zuschauer.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0023","text":"Nicht zurück kann der Wille wollen; dass er die Zeit nicht brechen kann und der Zeit Begierde,—das ist des Willens einsamste Trübsal.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0024","text":"Wollen befreit: was ersinnt sich das Wollen selber, dass es los seiner Trübsal werde und seines Kerkers spotte?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0025","text":"Ach, ein Narr wird jeder Gefangene! Närrisch erlöst sich auch der gefangene Wille.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0026","text":"Dass die Zeit nicht zurückläuft, das ist sein Ingrimm; „Das, was war“—so heisst der Stein, den er nicht wälzen kann.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0027","text":"Und so wälzt er Steine aus Ingrimm und Unmuth und übt Rache an dem, was nicht gleich ihm Grimm und Unmuth fühlt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0028","text":"Also wurde der Wille, der Befreier, ein Wehethäter: und an Allem, was leiden kann, nimmt er Rache dafür, dass er nicht zurück kann.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0029","text":"Diess, ja diess allein ist Rache selber: des Willens Widerwille gegen die Zeit und ihr „Es war.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0030","text":"Wahrlich, eine grosse Narrheit wohnt in unserm Willen; und zum Fluche wurde es allem Menschlichen, dass diese Narrheit Geist lernte!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0031","text":"Der Geist der Rache: meine Freunde, das war bisher der Menschen bestes Nachdenken; und wo Leid war, da sollte immer Strafe sein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0032","text":"„Strafe“ nämlich, so heisst sich die Rache selber: mit einem Lügenwort heuchelt sie sich ein gutes Gewissen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0033","text":"Und weil im Wollenden selber Leid ist, darob dass es nicht zurück wollen kann, —also sollte Wollen selber und alles Leben—Strafe sein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0034","text":"Und nun wälzte sich Wolke auf Wolke über den Geist: bis endlich der Wahnsinn predigte: „Alles vergeht, darum ist Alles werth zu vergehn!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0035","text":"„Und diess ist selber Gerechtigkeit, jenes Gesetz der Zeit, dass sie ihre Kinder fressen muss“: also predigte der Wahnsinn.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0036","text":"„Sittlich sind die Dinge geordnet nach Recht und Strafe. Oh wo ist die Erlösung vom Fluss der Dinge und der Strafe Dasein“? Also predigte der Wahnsinn.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0037","text":"„Kann es Erlösung geben, wenn es ein ewiges Recht giebt? Ach, unwälzbar ist der Stein „Es war“: ewig müssen auch alle Strafen sein!“ Also predigte der Wahnsinn.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0038","text":"„Keine That kann vernichtet werden: wie könnte sie durch die Strafe ungethan werden! Diess, diess ist das Ewige an der Strafe „Dasein“, dass das Dasein auch ewig wieder That und Schuld sein muss!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0039","text":"Es sei denn, dass der Wille endlich sich selber erlöste und Wollen zu Nicht-Wollen würde—“: doch ihr kennt, meine Brüder, diess Fabellied des Wahnsinns!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0040","text":"Weg führte ich euch von diesen Fabelliedern, als ich euch lehrte: „der Wille ist ein Schaffender.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0041","text":"Alles „Es war“ ist ein Bruchstück, ein Räthsel, ein grauser Zufall—bis der schaffende Wille dazu sagt: „aber so wollte ich es!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0042","text":"Bis der schaffende Wille dazu sagt: „Aber so will ich es! So werde ich’s wollen!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0043","text":"Aber sprach er schon so? Und wann geschieht diess? Ist der Wille schon abgeschirrt von seiner eignen Thorheit?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0044","text":"Wurde der Wille sich selber schon Erlöser und Freudebringer? Verlernte er den Geist der Rache und alles Zähneknirschen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0045","text":"Und wer lehrte ihn Versöhnung mit der Zeit, und Höheres als alle Versöhnung ist?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0046","text":"Höheres als alle Versöhnung muss der Wille wollen, welcher der Wille zur Macht ist—: doch wie geschieht ihm das? Wer lehrte ihn auch noch das Zurückwollen?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0047","text":"—Aber an dieser Stelle seiner Rede geschah es, dass Zarathustra plötzlich innehielt und ganz einem Solchen gleich sah, der auf das Äusserste erschrickt. Mit erschrecktem Auge blickte er auf seine Jünger; sein Auge durchbohrte wie mit Pfeilen ihre Gedanken und Hintergedanken. Aber nach einer kleinen Weile lachte er schon wieder und sagte begütigt:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0048","text":"„Es ist schwer, mit Menschen zu leben, weil Schweigen so schwer ist. Sonderlich für einen Geschwätzigen.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0049","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra. Der Bucklichte aber hatte dem Gespräche zugehört und sein Gesicht dabei bedeckt; als er aber Zarathustra lachen hörte, blickte er neugierig auf und sagte langsam:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0050","text":"„Aber warum redet Zarathustra anders zu uns als zu seinen Jüngern?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0051","text":"Zarathustra antwortete: „Was ist da zum Verwundern! Mit Bucklichten darf man schon bucklicht reden!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0052","text":"„Gut, sagte der Bucklichte; und mit Schülern darf man schon aus der Schule schwätzen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0053","text":"Aber warum redet Zarathustra anders zu seinen Schülern—als zu sich selber?“—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"3fb67c170e53ac436244eff2b8f5707aaef66a4ef770c5b22c9609b890ed089e","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182315999942Z_e04f58eb","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On Redemption","source_sha256":"3fb67c170e53ac436244eff2b8f5707aaef66a4ef770c5b22c9609b890ed089e","sha256":"630490c723af35e4851f0acde30f14815aa87a2fb0d9bc96379e81222560cb35","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:23:15.999986+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0001","text":"As Zarathustra was crossing the great bridge one day, the cripples and beggars surrounded him, and a hunchback spoke to him thus:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0002","text":"“Behold, Zarathustra! The people too are learning from you and gaining faith in your teaching: but for them to believe in you altogether, one thing more is needed—you must first persuade us cripples as well! Here now you have a fine selection, and truly, an opportunity with more than one handle! You can heal the blind and make the lame walk; and from him who has too much behind him, you might well take away a little:—that, I think, would be the right way to make the cripples believe in Zarathustra!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0003","text":"But Zarathustra answered the one who spoke thus: “When one takes the hunchback’s hump from him, one takes his spirit from him—thus the people teach. And when one gives the blind man his eyes, he sees too many evil things on earth: so that he curses the one who healed him. But he who makes the lame man walk does him the greatest harm: for scarcely can he walk than his vices run away with him—thus the people teach concerning cripples. And why should Zarathustra not also learn from the people, when the people learn from Zarathustra?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0004","text":"But this is the least of it for me, since I have been among human beings, that I see: “This one lacks an eye, that one an ear, and a third a leg; and there are others who have lost their tongue or their nose or their head.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0005","text":"I see and have seen worse things, and many things so abominable that I would not speak of every one of them, and of some I would not even wish to be silent: namely, human beings to whom everything is lacking, except that they have one thing too much—human beings who are nothing more than a great eye, or a great mouth, or a great belly, or something else great,—such people I call inverted cripples.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0006","text":"And when I came out of my solitude and crossed this bridge for the first time: I did not trust my eyes, and looked, and looked again, and finally said: “That is an ear! An ear as large as a human being!” I looked more closely: and indeed, beneath the ear something still moved, pitiably small and poor and feeble. And truly, the monstrous ear sat upon a little thin stalk,—but the stalk was a human being! Whoever put a glass before his eye could even make out a little envious face; also, that a bloated little soul dangled from the stalk. But the people told me that the great ear was not merely a human being, but a great human being, a genius. But I never believed the people when they spoke of great human beings—and I retained my belief that it was an inverted cripple, having too little of everything and too much of one thing.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0007","text":"When Zarathustra had thus spoken to the hunchback and to those for whom he was mouthpiece and advocate, he turned with deep indignation to his disciples and said:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0008","text":"“Truly, my friends, I walk among human beings as among fragments and limbs of human beings!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0009","text":"This is the terrible thing to my eye, that I find the human being shattered and scattered as over a battlefield and slaughterhouse.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0010","text":"And if my eye flees from the present to the past: it always finds the same thing: fragments and limbs and dreadful accidents—but no human beings!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0011","text":"The present and the past on earth—ah! my friends—that is what is most intolerable to me; and I would not know how to live if I were not also a seer of that which must come.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0012","text":"A seer, a willing one, a creator, a future itself and a bridge to the future—and ah, also, as it were, a cripple upon this bridge: all this is Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0013","text":"And you too have often asked yourselves: “Who is Zarathustra to us? What shall he be called by us?” And like myself, you gave yourselves questions as answers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0014","text":"Is he a promiser? Or a fulfiller? A conqueror? Or an heir? An autumn? Or a ploughshare? A physician? Or one recovered?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0015","text":"Is he a poet? Or a truthful one? A liberator? Or a tamer? A good one? Or an evil one?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0016","text":"I walk among human beings as among the fragments of the future: of that future which I behold.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0017","text":"And this is all my poetry and striving: that I compose into one and gather together what is fragment and riddle and dreadful accident.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0018","text":"And how could I endure being human, if the human being were not also poet and riddle-guesser and redeemer of accident!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0019","text":"To redeem those who have passed and to transform every “It was” into a “Thus I willed it!”—that alone would I call redemption!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0020","text":"Will—that is the name of the liberator and bringer of joy: thus I taught you, my friends! And now learn this in addition: the will itself is still a prisoner.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0021","text":"Willing liberates: but what is the name of that which still strikes even the liberator into chains?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0022","text":"“It was”: thus is called the will’s gnashing of teeth and loneliest melancholy. Powerless against that which has been done—it is an evil spectator of all that is past.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0023","text":"The will cannot will backwards; that it cannot break time and time’s desire,—that is the will’s loneliest melancholy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0024","text":"Willing liberates: what does willing itself devise, in order that it may become free of its melancholy and mock its prison?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0025","text":"Ah, every prisoner becomes a fool! The captive will, too, redeems itself foolishly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0026","text":"That time does not run backwards, that is its wrath; “That which was”—thus is called the stone that it cannot roll.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0027","text":"And so it rolls stones out of wrath and ill-humour and wreaks revenge upon that which does not feel wrath and ill-humour like itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0028","text":"Thus the will, the liberator, became a doer of woe: and upon all that can suffer it takes revenge for the fact that it cannot go back.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0029","text":"This, yes, this alone is revenge itself: the will’s aversion to time and its “It was.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0030","text":"Truly, a great folly dwells in our will; and it became a curse upon everything human that this folly learned spirit!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0031","text":"The spirit of revenge: my friends, that has hitherto been humanity’s best reflection; and where there was suffering, there was always supposed to be punishment.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0032","text":"“Punishment,” namely, is what revenge calls itself: with a lying word it feigns itself a good conscience.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0033","text":"And because there is suffering in the one who wills himself, because it cannot will backwards,—therefore willing itself and all life were supposed to be punishment!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0034","text":"And now cloud upon cloud rolled over the spirit: until at last madness preached: “Everything passes away; therefore everything is worthy to pass away!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0035","text":"“And this itself is justice, that law of time, that it must devour its children”: thus preached madness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0036","text":"“Things are morally ordered according to law and punishment. Oh, where is redemption from the flux of things and from the punishment ‘Existence’?” Thus preached madness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0037","text":"“Can there be redemption if there is an eternal law? Ah, unrollable is the stone ‘It was’: eternally must all punishments be as well!” Thus preached madness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0038","text":"“No deed can be destroyed: how could it be undone through punishment! This, this is the eternal thing about the punishment ‘Existence,’ that existence too must eternally again be deed and guilt!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0039","text":"Unless at last the will redeemed itself and willing became non-willing—”: but you know, my brothers, this fable-song of madness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0040","text":"I led you away from these fable-songs when I taught you: “the will is a creator.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0041","text":"Everything “It was” is a fragment, a riddle, a cruel accident—until the creating will says to it: “but thus I willed it!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0042","text":"Until the creating will says to it: “But thus I will it! Thus shall I will it!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0043","text":"But has it already spoken thus? And when does this happen? Has the will already been unharnessed from its own folly?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0044","text":"Has the will already become its own redeemer and bringer of joy? Has it unlearned the spirit of revenge and all gnashing of teeth?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0045","text":"And who taught it reconciliation with time, and that which is higher than all reconciliation?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0046","text":"Higher than all reconciliation must the will will, which is the will to power—: but how does this come to pass for it? Who taught it also to will backwards?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0047","text":"—But at this point in his speech it happened that Zarathustra suddenly stopped short and looked altogether like one who is frightened to the utmost. With frightened eyes he looked upon his disciples; his eye pierced, as with arrows, their thoughts and afterthoughts. But after a little while he laughed again and said soothingly:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0048","text":"“It is difficult to live with human beings because it is so difficult to be silent. Especially for a chatterbox.”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0049","text":"Thus Spoke Zarathustra. But the hunchback had listened to the conversation and covered his face as he did so; but when he heard Zarathustra laugh, he looked up curiously and said slowly:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0050","text":"“But why does Zarathustra speak differently to us than to his disciples?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0051","text":"Zarathustra replied: “What is there to wonder at! One may indeed speak hunchbackedly to hunchbacks!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0052","text":"“Good,” said the hunchback; “and with pupils one may indeed chatter out of school.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDES:P0053","text":"But why does Zarathustra speak differently to his pupils—than to himself?”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDES:P0053"]}],"source_digest":"849ec6fc0342999a740abf0422968db442e9504d19a95d543f2dbdbac49c6238","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von der Erlösung"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von der Erlösung","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK","title":"On Human Prudence","order":58,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDMK","text":"Nicht die Höhe: der Abhang ist das Furchtbare!\n\nDer Abhang, wo der Blick hinunter stürzt und die Hand hinauf greift. Da schwindelt dem Herzen vor seinem doppelten Willen.\n\nAch, Freunde, errathet ihr wohl auch meines Herzens doppelten Willen?\n\nDas, Das ist mein Abhang und meine Gefahr, dass mein Blick in die Höhe stürzt, und dass meine Hand sich halten und stützen möchte—an der Tiefe!\n\nAn den Menschen klammert sich mein Wille, mit Ketten binde ich mich an den Menschen, weil es mich hinauf reisst zum Obermenschen: denn dahin will mein andrer Wille.\n\nUnd dazu lebe ich blind unter den Menschen; gleich als ob ich sie nicht kennte: dass meine Hand ihren Glauben an Festes nicht ganz verliere.\n\nIch kenne euch Menschen nicht: diese Finsterniss und Tröstung ist oft um mich gebreitet.\n\nIch sitze am Thorwege für jeden Schelm und frage: wer will mich betrügen?\n\nDas ist meine erste Menschen-Klugheit, dass ich mich betrügen lasse, um nicht auf der Hut zu sein vor Betrügern.\n\nAch, wenn ich auf der Hut wäre vor dem Menschen: wie könnte meinem Balle der Mensch ein Anker sein! Zu leicht risse es mich hinauf und hinweg!\n\nDiese Vorsehung ist über meinem Schicksal, dass ich ohne Vorsicht sein muss.\n\nUnd wer unter Menschen nicht verschmachten will, muss lernen, aus allen Gläsern zu trinken; und wer unter Menschen rein bleiben will, muss verstehn, sich auch mit schmutzigem Wasser zu waschen.\n\nUnd also sprach ich oft mir zum Troste: „Wohlan! Wohlauf! Altes Herz! Ein Unglück missrieth dir: geniesse diess als dein—Glück!“\n\nDiess aber ist meine andre Menschen-Klugheit: ich schone die Eitlen mehr als die Stolzen.\n\nIst nicht verletzte Eitelkeit die Mutter aller Trauerspiele? Wo aber Stolz verletzt wird, da wächst wohl etwas Besseres noch, als Stolz ist.\n\nDamit das Leben gut anzuschaun sei, muss sein Spiel gut gespielt werden: dazu aber bedarf es guter Schauspieler.\n\nGute Schauspieler fand ich alle Eitlen: sie spielen und wollen, dass ihnen gern zugeschaut werde,—all ihr Geist ist bei diesem Willen.\n\nSie führen sich auf, sie erfinden sich; in ihrer Nähe liebe ich’s, dem Leben zuzuschaun,—es heilt von der Schwermuth.\n\nDarum schone ich die Eitlen, weil sie mir Arzte sind meiner Schwermuth und mich am Menschen fest halten als an einem Schauspiele.\n\nUnd dann: wer ermisst am Eitlen die ganze Tiefe seiner Bescheidenheit! Ich bin ihm gut und mitleidig ob seiner Bescheidenheit.\n\nVon euch will er seinen Glauben an sich lernen; er nährt sich an euren Blicken, er frisst das Lob aus euren Händen.\n\nEuren Lügen glaubt er noch, wenn ihr gut über ihn lügt: denn im Tiefsten seufzt sein Herz: „was bin ich !“\n\nUnd wenn das die rechte Tugend ist, die nicht um sich selber weiss: nun, der Eitle weiss nicht um seine Bescheidenheit!—\n\nDas ist aber meine dritte Menschen-Klugheit, dass ich mir den Anblick der Bösen nicht verleiden lasse durch eure Furchtsamkeit.\n\nIch bin selig, die Wunder zu sehn, welche heisse Sonne ausbrütet: Tiger und Palmen und Klapperschlangen.\n\nAuch unter Menschen giebt es schöne Brut heisser Sonne und viel Wunderwürdiges an den Bösen.\n\nZwar, wie eure Weisesten mir nicht gar so weise erschienen: so fand ich auch der Menschen Bosheit unter ihrem Rufe.\n\nUnd oft fragte ich mit Kopfschütteln: Warum noch klappern, ihr Klapperschlangen?\n\nWahrlich, es giebt auch für das Böse noch eine Zukunft! Und der heisseste Süden ist noch nicht entdeckt für den Menschen.\n\nWie Manches heisst jetzt schon ärgste Bosheit, was doch nur zwölf Schuhe breit und drei Monate lang ist! Einst aber werden grössere Drachen zur Welt kommen.\n\nDenn dass dem Übermenschen sein Drache nicht fehle, der Über-Drache, der seiner würdig ist: dazu muss viel heisse Sonne noch auf feuchten Urwald glühen!\n\nAus euren Wildkatzen müssen erst Tiger geworden sein und aus euren Giftkröten Krokodile: denn der gute Jäger soll eine gute Jagd haben!\n\nUnd wahrlich, ihr Guten und Gerechten! An euch ist Viel zum Lachen und zumal eure Furcht vor dem, was bisher „Teufel“ hiess!\n\nSo fremd seid ihr dem Grossen mit eurer Seele, dass euch der Übermensch furchtbar sein würde in seiner Güte!\n\nUnd ihr Weisen und Wissenden, ihr würdet vor dem Sonnenbrande der Weisheit flüchten, in dem der Übermensch mit Lust seine Nacktheit badet!\n\nIhr höchsten Menschen, denen mein Auge begegnete! das ist mein Zweifel an euch und mein heimliches Lachen: ich rathe, ihr würdet meinen Übermenschen—Teufel heissen!\n\nAch, ich ward dieser Höchsten und Besten müde: aus ihrer „Höhe“ verlangte mich hinauf, hinaus, hinweg zu dem Übermenschen!\n\nEin Grausen überfiel mich, als ich diese Besten nackend sah: da wuchsen mir die Flügel, fortzuschweben in ferne Zukünfte.\n\nIn fernere Zukünfte, in südlichere Süden, als je ein Bildner träumte: dorthin, wo Götter sich aller Kleider schämen!\n\nAber verkleidet will ich euch sehn, ihr Nächsten und Mitmenschen, und gut geputzt, und eitel, und würdig, als „die Guten und Gerechten,“—\n\nUnd verkleidet will ich selber unter euch sitzen,—dass ich euch und mich verkenne : das ist nämlich meine letzte Menschen-Klugheit.\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0001","text":"Nicht die Höhe: der Abhang ist das Furchtbare!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0002","text":"Der Abhang, wo der Blick hinunter stürzt und die Hand hinauf greift. Da schwindelt dem Herzen vor seinem doppelten Willen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0003","text":"Ach, Freunde, errathet ihr wohl auch meines Herzens doppelten Willen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0004","text":"Das, Das ist mein Abhang und meine Gefahr, dass mein Blick in die Höhe stürzt, und dass meine Hand sich halten und stützen möchte—an der Tiefe!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0005","text":"An den Menschen klammert sich mein Wille, mit Ketten binde ich mich an den Menschen, weil es mich hinauf reisst zum Obermenschen: denn dahin will mein andrer Wille.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0006","text":"Und dazu lebe ich blind unter den Menschen; gleich als ob ich sie nicht kennte: dass meine Hand ihren Glauben an Festes nicht ganz verliere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0007","text":"Ich kenne euch Menschen nicht: diese Finsterniss und Tröstung ist oft um mich gebreitet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0008","text":"Ich sitze am Thorwege für jeden Schelm und frage: wer will mich betrügen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0009","text":"Das ist meine erste Menschen-Klugheit, dass ich mich betrügen lasse, um nicht auf der Hut zu sein vor Betrügern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0010","text":"Ach, wenn ich auf der Hut wäre vor dem Menschen: wie könnte meinem Balle der Mensch ein Anker sein! Zu leicht risse es mich hinauf und hinweg!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0011","text":"Diese Vorsehung ist über meinem Schicksal, dass ich ohne Vorsicht sein muss.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0012","text":"Und wer unter Menschen nicht verschmachten will, muss lernen, aus allen Gläsern zu trinken; und wer unter Menschen rein bleiben will, muss verstehn, sich auch mit schmutzigem Wasser zu waschen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0013","text":"Und also sprach ich oft mir zum Troste: „Wohlan! Wohlauf! Altes Herz! Ein Unglück missrieth dir: geniesse diess als dein—Glück!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0014","text":"Diess aber ist meine andre Menschen-Klugheit: ich schone die Eitlen mehr als die Stolzen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0015","text":"Ist nicht verletzte Eitelkeit die Mutter aller Trauerspiele? Wo aber Stolz verletzt wird, da wächst wohl etwas Besseres noch, als Stolz ist.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0016","text":"Damit das Leben gut anzuschaun sei, muss sein Spiel gut gespielt werden: dazu aber bedarf es guter Schauspieler.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0017","text":"Gute Schauspieler fand ich alle Eitlen: sie spielen und wollen, dass ihnen gern zugeschaut werde,—all ihr Geist ist bei diesem Willen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0018","text":"Sie führen sich auf, sie erfinden sich; in ihrer Nähe liebe ich’s, dem Leben zuzuschaun,—es heilt von der Schwermuth.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0019","text":"Darum schone ich die Eitlen, weil sie mir Arzte sind meiner Schwermuth und mich am Menschen fest halten als an einem Schauspiele.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0020","text":"Und dann: wer ermisst am Eitlen die ganze Tiefe seiner Bescheidenheit! Ich bin ihm gut und mitleidig ob seiner Bescheidenheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0021","text":"Von euch will er seinen Glauben an sich lernen; er nährt sich an euren Blicken, er frisst das Lob aus euren Händen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0022","text":"Euren Lügen glaubt er noch, wenn ihr gut über ihn lügt: denn im Tiefsten seufzt sein Herz: „was bin ich !“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0023","text":"Und wenn das die rechte Tugend ist, die nicht um sich selber weiss: nun, der Eitle weiss nicht um seine Bescheidenheit!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0024","text":"Das ist aber meine dritte Menschen-Klugheit, dass ich mir den Anblick der Bösen nicht verleiden lasse durch eure Furchtsamkeit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0025","text":"Ich bin selig, die Wunder zu sehn, welche heisse Sonne ausbrütet: Tiger und Palmen und Klapperschlangen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0026","text":"Auch unter Menschen giebt es schöne Brut heisser Sonne und viel Wunderwürdiges an den Bösen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0027","text":"Zwar, wie eure Weisesten mir nicht gar so weise erschienen: so fand ich auch der Menschen Bosheit unter ihrem Rufe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0028","text":"Und oft fragte ich mit Kopfschütteln: Warum noch klappern, ihr Klapperschlangen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0029","text":"Wahrlich, es giebt auch für das Böse noch eine Zukunft! Und der heisseste Süden ist noch nicht entdeckt für den Menschen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0030","text":"Wie Manches heisst jetzt schon ärgste Bosheit, was doch nur zwölf Schuhe breit und drei Monate lang ist! Einst aber werden grössere Drachen zur Welt kommen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0031","text":"Denn dass dem Übermenschen sein Drache nicht fehle, der Über-Drache, der seiner würdig ist: dazu muss viel heisse Sonne noch auf feuchten Urwald glühen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0032","text":"Aus euren Wildkatzen müssen erst Tiger geworden sein und aus euren Giftkröten Krokodile: denn der gute Jäger soll eine gute Jagd haben!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0033","text":"Und wahrlich, ihr Guten und Gerechten! An euch ist Viel zum Lachen und zumal eure Furcht vor dem, was bisher „Teufel“ hiess!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0034","text":"So fremd seid ihr dem Grossen mit eurer Seele, dass euch der Übermensch furchtbar sein würde in seiner Güte!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0035","text":"Und ihr Weisen und Wissenden, ihr würdet vor dem Sonnenbrande der Weisheit flüchten, in dem der Übermensch mit Lust seine Nacktheit badet!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0036","text":"Ihr höchsten Menschen, denen mein Auge begegnete! das ist mein Zweifel an euch und mein heimliches Lachen: ich rathe, ihr würdet meinen Übermenschen—Teufel heissen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0037","text":"Ach, ich ward dieser Höchsten und Besten müde: aus ihrer „Höhe“ verlangte mich hinauf, hinaus, hinweg zu dem Übermenschen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0038","text":"Ein Grausen überfiel mich, als ich diese Besten nackend sah: da wuchsen mir die Flügel, fortzuschweben in ferne Zukünfte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0039","text":"In fernere Zukünfte, in südlichere Süden, als je ein Bildner träumte: dorthin, wo Götter sich aller Kleider schämen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0040","text":"Aber verkleidet will ich euch sehn, ihr Nächsten und Mitmenschen, und gut geputzt, und eitel, und würdig, als „die Guten und Gerechten,“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0041","text":"Und verkleidet will ich selber unter euch sitzen,—dass ich euch und mich verkenne : das ist nämlich meine letzte Menschen-Klugheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0042","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"3a86a4d0470223772a750c07348b6f53b2be4330b2505ec6f3c6c0b9f97e9a3c","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182332908437Z_e217f842","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On Human Prudence","source_sha256":"3a86a4d0470223772a750c07348b6f53b2be4330b2505ec6f3c6c0b9f97e9a3c","sha256":"a2b61b0b54c90f726c24cdfc344b4396cc0aa973748464d47c1de999f14215f2","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:23:32.908492+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0001","text":"Not the height: the slope is the terrible thing!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0002","text":"The slope, where the gaze plunges downward and the hand reaches upward. There the heart grows dizzy before its double will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0003","text":"Ah, friends, do you too perhaps guess my heart’s double will?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0004","text":"That, that is my slope and my danger, that my gaze plunges into the height, and that my hand would like to hold on and support itself—on the depth!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0005","text":"My will clings to man; with chains I bind myself to man, because it tears me upward toward the Overman: for thither my other will would go.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0006","text":"And for that purpose I live blind among men; as though I did not know them: so that my hand may not altogether lose its faith in something firm.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0007","text":"I do not know you men: this darkness and consolation are often spread around me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0008","text":"I sit at the gateway for every rogue and ask: who wishes to deceive me?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0009","text":"This is my first human wisdom, that I let myself be deceived, in order not to be on my guard against deceivers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0010","text":"Ah, if I were on my guard against man: how could man be an anchor for my ball! Too easily would it tear me upward and away!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0011","text":"This providence lies over my destiny, that I must be without caution.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0012","text":"And whoever does not wish to languish among men must learn to drink from all glasses; and whoever wishes to remain pure among men must understand how to wash himself even with dirty water.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0013","text":"And thus I often spoke to myself for consolation: “Well then! Up then! Old heart! A misfortune has miscarried for you: enjoy this as your—good fortune!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0014","text":"But this is my second human wisdom: I spare the vain more than the proud.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0015","text":"Is not wounded vanity the mother of all tragedies? But where pride is wounded, something perhaps still better than pride grows.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0016","text":"In order that life may be good to behold, its play must be well played: but for that good actors are needed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0017","text":"I found all the vain to be good actors: they act and wish to be gladly watched,—all their spirit is in this will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0018","text":"They perform themselves, they invent themselves; in their vicinity I love to watch life,—it cures one of melancholy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0019","text":"Therefore I spare the vain, because they are physicians of my melancholy and keep me fast to man as to a spectacle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0020","text":"And then: who can measure in the vain one the entire depth of his modesty! I am good and compassionate toward him on account of his modesty.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0021","text":"From you he wishes to learn his faith in himself; he nourishes himself on your glances, he devours praise from your hands.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0022","text":"He still believes your lies when you lie well about him: for in the deepest part his heart sighs: “what am I!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0023","text":"And if that is the proper virtue, which knows nothing of itself: well then, the vain one knows nothing of his modesty!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0024","text":"But this is my third human wisdom, that I do not let your timidity spoil for me the sight of the wicked.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0025","text":"I am blessed to see the wonders that hot sun hatches: tigers and palms and rattlesnakes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0026","text":"Among human beings, too, there is fair brood of hot sun and much wondrousness in the wicked.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0027","text":"Indeed, just as your wisest did not appear altogether so wise to me: so too I found the wickedness of men beneath its repute.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0028","text":"And often, shaking my head, I asked: Why still rattle, you rattlesnakes?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0029","text":"Verily, there is still a future even for evil! And the hottest south has not yet been discovered for man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0030","text":"How much is already called the most extreme wickedness now, which is after all only twelve shoes broad and three months long! But greater dragons will one day come into the world.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0031","text":"For so that the overman may not lack his dragon, the over-dragon worthy of him: much hot sun must yet glow upon damp primeval forest!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0032","text":"Your wildcats must first have become tigers and your poisonous toads crocodiles: for the good hunter should have good hunting!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0033","text":"And verily, you good and just! There is much in you to laugh at, and especially your fear of that which hitherto was called “devil”!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0034","text":"You are so alien to the great with your souls that the overman would be terrible to you in his goodness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0035","text":"And you wise and knowing ones, you would flee from the sunburn of wisdom, in which the overman gladly bathes his nakedness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0036","text":"You highest human beings whom my eye encountered! this is my doubt of you and my secret laughter: I surmise that you would call my overman—devil!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0037","text":"Ah, I grew weary of these highest and best: from their “height” I longed upward, outward, away to the overman!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0038","text":"A horror came over me when I saw these best ones naked: then wings grew for me, to soar away into distant futures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0039","text":"Into more distant futures, into more southerly souths than any sculptor ever dreamed: there, where gods are ashamed of all garments!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0040","text":"But I wish to see you disguised, you nearest ones and fellow human beings, and well adorned, and vain, and worthy, as “the Good and Just,”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0041","text":"And disguised I myself wish to sit among you,—so that I may mistake you and myself: this, namely, is my last human prudence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0042","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDMK:P0042"]}],"source_digest":"17dd03963ef175e87e0015365415d69adbfce9ee1945d1c80e5cc625b7f1b84b","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von der Menschen-Klugheit"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von der Menschen-Klugheit","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSS0","title":"The Stillest Hour","order":59,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_DSS0","text":"„Was geschah mir, meine Freunde? Ihr seht mich verstört, fortgetrieben, unwillig-folgsam, bereit zu gehen—ach, von euch fortzugehen!\n\nJa, noch Ein Mal muss Zarathustra in seine Einsamkeit: aber unlustig geht diessmal der Bär zurück in seine Höhle!\n\nWas geschah mir? Wer gebeut diess?—Ach, meine zornige Herrin will es so, sie sprach zu mir: nannte ich je euch schon ihren Namen?\n\nGestern gen Abend sprach zu mir meine stillste Stunde : das ist der Name meiner furchtbaren Herrin.\n\nUnd so geschah’s,—denn Alles muss ich euch sagen, dass euer Herz sich nicht verhärte gegen den plötzlich Scheidenden!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSS0:P0001","text":"„Was geschah mir, meine Freunde? Ihr seht mich verstört, fortgetrieben, unwillig-folgsam, bereit zu gehen—ach, von euch fortzugehen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSS0:P0002","text":"Ja, noch Ein Mal muss Zarathustra in seine Einsamkeit: aber unlustig geht diessmal der Bär zurück in seine Höhle!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSS0:P0003","text":"Was geschah mir? Wer gebeut diess?—Ach, meine zornige Herrin will es so, sie sprach zu mir: nannte ich je euch schon ihren Namen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSS0:P0004","text":"Gestern gen Abend sprach zu mir meine stillste Stunde : das ist der Name meiner furchtbaren Herrin.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSS0:P0005","text":"Und so geschah’s,—denn Alles muss ich euch sagen, dass euer Herz sich nicht verhärte gegen den plötzlich Scheidenden!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"27f97b647ea45b1fe9724b7899e8ce52c46e0a7cede6162448000e04e5ac1ec1","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182335601307Z_f49fab22","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"The Stillest Hour","source_sha256":"27f97b647ea45b1fe9724b7899e8ce52c46e0a7cede6162448000e04e5ac1ec1","sha256":"fe53eb369fa772bc82cb9abcdbb2ecbdd763cbeba483e6b395fa9d8888c2bbbf","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:23:35.601324+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSS0:P0001","text":"“What happened to me, my friends? You see me disturbed, driven away, unwillingly obedient, ready to go—ah, to go away from you!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSS0:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSS0:P0002","text":"Yes, once more Zarathustra must go into his solitude: but this time the bear goes back to his cave unwillingly!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSS0:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSS0:P0003","text":"What happened to me? Who commands this?—Ah, my angry mistress wills it so; she spoke to me: have I ever yet named her name to you?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSS0:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSS0:P0004","text":"Yesterday towards evening my stillest hour spoke to me: that is the name of my terrible mistress.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSS0:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSS0:P0005","text":"And so it happened,—for I must tell you everything, lest your heart grow hard against him who suddenly departs!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSS0:P0005"]}],"source_digest":"36a5382cc891fa9cf863db72bd858fb059118e51b69d137be6aa4c7e769b78ec","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Die stillste Stunde"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Die stillste Stunde","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_KIDS","title":"Do you know the terror of the sleeper?—","order":60,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_KIDS","text":"Kennt ihr den Schrecken des Einschlafenden?—\n\nBis in die Zehen hinein erschrickt er, darob, dass ihm der Boden weicht und der Traum beginnt.\n\nDieses sage ich euch zum Gleichniss. Gestern, zur stillsten Stunde, wich mir der Boden: der Traum begann.\n\nDer Zeiger rückte, die Uhr meines Lebens holte Athem—nie hörte ich solche Stille um mich: also dass mein Herz erschrak.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_KIDS:P0001","text":"Kennt ihr den Schrecken des Einschlafenden?—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_KIDS:P0002","text":"Bis in die Zehen hinein erschrickt er, darob, dass ihm der Boden weicht und der Traum beginnt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_KIDS:P0003","text":"Dieses sage ich euch zum Gleichniss. Gestern, zur stillsten Stunde, wich mir der Boden: der Traum begann.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_KIDS:P0004","text":"Der Zeiger rückte, die Uhr meines Lebens holte Athem—nie hörte ich solche Stille um mich: also dass mein Herz erschrak.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"552f45a18cfc82949408258bf09a440f71a0161de473fbde1804cb024d3c0c50","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182338215085Z_97debcf2","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Do you know the terror of the sleeper?—","source_sha256":"552f45a18cfc82949408258bf09a440f71a0161de473fbde1804cb024d3c0c50","sha256":"0ced3c69fe46cea7c1e0c417b2cc6483efcca7e3abe67af14605b1162577d2d8","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:23:38.215100+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_KIDS:P0001","text":"Do you know the terror of one falling asleep?—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_KIDS:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_KIDS:P0002","text":"Even into his toes he is startled, because the ground gives way beneath him and the dream begins.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_KIDS:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_KIDS:P0003","text":"This I say to you as a parable. Yesterday, at the stillest hour, the ground gave way beneath me: the dream began.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_KIDS:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_KIDS:P0004","text":"The hand moved on, the clock of my life drew breath—never had I heard such stillness around me: so that my heart was startled.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_KIDS:P0004"]}],"source_digest":"3142937be2238433d84054d1d37ab43925622156f43de6b3fd83a7d59f851970","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Kennt ihr den Schrecken des Einschlafenden?—"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Kennt ihr den Schrecken des Einschlafenden?—","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO","title":"Then it spoke to me without a voice: “You know it, Zarathustra?”—","order":61,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_DSEO","text":"Dann sprach es ohne Stimme zu mir: „Du weisst es, Zarathustra?“—\n\nUnd ich schrie vor Schrecken bei diesem Flüstern, und das Blut wich aus meinem Gesichte: aber ich schwieg.\n\nDa sprach es abermals ohne Stimme zu mir: „Du weisst es, Zarathustra, aber du redest es nicht!“—\n\nUnd ich antwortete endlich gleich einem Trotzigen: „Ja, ich weiss es, aber ich will es nicht reden!“\n\nDa sprach es wieder ohne Stimme zu mir: „Du willst nicht, Zarathustra? Ist diess auch wahr? Verstecke dich nicht in deinen Trotz!“—\n\nUnd ich weinte und zitterte wie ein Kind und sprach: „Ach, ich wollte schon, aber wie kann ich es! Erlass mir diess nur! Es ist über meine Kraft!“\n\nDa sprach es wieder ohne Stimme zu mir: „Was liegt an dir, Zarathustra! Sprich dein Wort und zerbrich!“—\n\nUnd ich antwortete: „Ach, ist es mein Wort? Wer bin ich? Ich warte des Würdigeren; ich bin nicht werth, an ihm auch nur zu zerbrechen.“\n\nDa sprach es wieder ohne Stimme zu mir: „Was liegt an dir? Du bist mir noch nicht demüthig genug. Die Demuth hat das härteste Fell.“—\n\nUnd ich antwortete: „Was trug nicht schon das Fell meiner Demuth! Am Fusse wohne ich meiner Höhe: wie hoch meine Gipfel sind? Niemand sagte es mir noch. Aber gut kenne ich meine Thäler.“\n\nDa sprach es wieder ohne Stimme zu mir: „Oh Zarathustra, wer Berge zu versetzen hat, der versetzt auch Thäler und Niederungen.“—\n\nUnd ich antwortete: „Noch versetzte mein Wort keine Berge, und was ich redete, erreichte die Menschen nicht. Ich gieng wohl zu den Menschen, aber noch langte ich nicht bei ihnen an.“\n\nDa sprach es wieder ohne Stimme zu mir: „Was weisst du davon ! Der Thau fällt auf das Gras, wenn die Nacht am verschwiegensten ist.“—\n\nUnd ich antwortete: „sie verspotteten mich, als ich meinen eigenen Weg fand und gieng; und in Wahrheit zitterten damals meine Füsse.“\n\nUnd so sprachen sie zu mir: „du verlerntest den Weg, nun verlernst du auch das Gehen!“\n\nDa sprach es wieder ohne Stimme zu mir: „Was liegt an ihrem Spotte! Du bist Einer, der das Gehorchen verlernt hat: nun sollst du befehlen!\n\nWeisst du nicht, wer Allen am nöthigsten thut? Der Grosses befiehlt.\n\nGrosses vollführen ist schwer: aber das Schwerere ist, Grosses befehlen.\n\nDas ist dein Unverzeihlichstes: du hast die Macht, und du willst nicht herrschen.“—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0001","text":"Dann sprach es ohne Stimme zu mir: „Du weisst es, Zarathustra?“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0002","text":"Und ich schrie vor Schrecken bei diesem Flüstern, und das Blut wich aus meinem Gesichte: aber ich schwieg.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0003","text":"Da sprach es abermals ohne Stimme zu mir: „Du weisst es, Zarathustra, aber du redest es nicht!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0004","text":"Und ich antwortete endlich gleich einem Trotzigen: „Ja, ich weiss es, aber ich will es nicht reden!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0005","text":"Da sprach es wieder ohne Stimme zu mir: „Du willst nicht, Zarathustra? Ist diess auch wahr? Verstecke dich nicht in deinen Trotz!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0006","text":"Und ich weinte und zitterte wie ein Kind und sprach: „Ach, ich wollte schon, aber wie kann ich es! Erlass mir diess nur! Es ist über meine Kraft!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0007","text":"Da sprach es wieder ohne Stimme zu mir: „Was liegt an dir, Zarathustra! Sprich dein Wort und zerbrich!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0008","text":"Und ich antwortete: „Ach, ist es mein Wort? Wer bin ich? Ich warte des Würdigeren; ich bin nicht werth, an ihm auch nur zu zerbrechen.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0009","text":"Da sprach es wieder ohne Stimme zu mir: „Was liegt an dir? Du bist mir noch nicht demüthig genug. Die Demuth hat das härteste Fell.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0010","text":"Und ich antwortete: „Was trug nicht schon das Fell meiner Demuth! Am Fusse wohne ich meiner Höhe: wie hoch meine Gipfel sind? Niemand sagte es mir noch. Aber gut kenne ich meine Thäler.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0011","text":"Da sprach es wieder ohne Stimme zu mir: „Oh Zarathustra, wer Berge zu versetzen hat, der versetzt auch Thäler und Niederungen.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0012","text":"Und ich antwortete: „Noch versetzte mein Wort keine Berge, und was ich redete, erreichte die Menschen nicht. Ich gieng wohl zu den Menschen, aber noch langte ich nicht bei ihnen an.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0013","text":"Da sprach es wieder ohne Stimme zu mir: „Was weisst du davon ! Der Thau fällt auf das Gras, wenn die Nacht am verschwiegensten ist.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0014","text":"Und ich antwortete: „sie verspotteten mich, als ich meinen eigenen Weg fand und gieng; und in Wahrheit zitterten damals meine Füsse.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0015","text":"Und so sprachen sie zu mir: „du verlerntest den Weg, nun verlernst du auch das Gehen!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0016","text":"Da sprach es wieder ohne Stimme zu mir: „Was liegt an ihrem Spotte! Du bist Einer, der das Gehorchen verlernt hat: nun sollst du befehlen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0017","text":"Weisst du nicht, wer Allen am nöthigsten thut? Der Grosses befiehlt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0018","text":"Grosses vollführen ist schwer: aber das Schwerere ist, Grosses befehlen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0019","text":"Das ist dein Unverzeihlichstes: du hast die Macht, und du willst nicht herrschen.“—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"455604ac1f9d18e79d824752741cb6d4b879697575e458eea280522e6dfbc56f","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182345836664Z_5870ef32","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Then it spoke to me without a voice: “You know it, Zarathustra?”—","source_sha256":"455604ac1f9d18e79d824752741cb6d4b879697575e458eea280522e6dfbc56f","sha256":"8d55103f2ae2d022eca5b29c5801625ddece7cf50b8ce9e6dc639bd4a89ec87f","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:23:45.836675+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0001","text":"Then it spoke to me without a voice: “You know it, Zarathustra?”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0002","text":"And I cried out in terror at this whisper, and the blood fled from my face: but I was silent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0003","text":"Then it spoke to me again without a voice: “You know it, Zarathustra, but you do not speak it!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0004","text":"And at last I answered like one defiant: “Yes, I know it, but I do not want to speak it!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0005","text":"Then it spoke to me again without a voice: “You do not want to, Zarathustra? Is that also true? Do not hide yourself in your defiance!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0006","text":"And I wept and trembled like a child and said: “Ah, I would indeed like to, but how can I! Spare me only this! It is beyond my strength!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0007","text":"Then it spoke to me again without a voice: “What does it matter about you, Zarathustra! Speak your word and break!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0008","text":"And I answered: “Ah, is it my word? Who am I? I await one more worthy; I am not worthy even to break upon it.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0009","text":"Then it spoke to me again without a voice: “What does it matter about you? You are not yet humble enough for me. Humility has the hardest hide.” —","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0010","text":"And I answered: “What has not already been borne by the hide of my humility! At the foot of my height I dwell: how high my peaks are? No one has yet told me. But I know my valleys well.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0011","text":"Then it spoke to me again without a voice: “O Zarathustra, he who has mountains to move also moves valleys and lowlands.” —","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0012","text":"And I answered: “My word has not yet moved any mountains, and what I spoke did not reach human beings. I did indeed go to human beings, but I have not yet arrived among them.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0013","text":"Then it spoke to me again without a voice: “What do you know of that! Dew falls upon the grass when the night is most silent.” —","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0014","text":"And I answered: “They mocked me when I found and went my own way; and in truth my feet trembled at that time.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0015","text":"And thus they spoke to me: “You unlearned the way; now you are also unlearning to walk!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0016","text":"Then it spoke to me again without a voice: “What does their mockery matter! You are one who has unlearned obedience: now you shall command!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0017","text":"Do you not know who is most necessary to all? He who commands great things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0018","text":"To accomplish great things is difficult: but the more difficult thing is to command great things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0019","text":"This is what is most unforgivable in you: you have power, and you do not wish to rule.”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DSEO:P0019"]}],"source_digest":"6d30fd80ac30d3f3b87b93c4cd5734b1885c5abbc77b5840f6b3eca81ec38869","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Dann sprach es ohne Stimme zu mir: „Du weisst es, Zarathustra?“—"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Dann sprach es ohne Stimme zu mir: „Du weisst es, Zarathustra?“—","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAM","title":"And I answered: “I lack the lion’s voice for all commanding.”","order":62,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_UIAM","text":"Und ich antwortete: „Mir fehlt des Löwen Stimme zu allem Befehlen.“\n\nDa sprach es wieder wie ein Flüstern zu mir: „Die stillsten Worte sind es, welche den Sturm bringen. Gedanken, die mit Taubenfüssen kommen, lenken die Welt.\n\nOh Zarathustra, du sollst gehen als ein Schatten dessen, was kommen muss: so wirst du befehlen und befehlend vorangehen.“—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAM:P0001","text":"Und ich antwortete: „Mir fehlt des Löwen Stimme zu allem Befehlen.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAM:P0002","text":"Da sprach es wieder wie ein Flüstern zu mir: „Die stillsten Worte sind es, welche den Sturm bringen. Gedanken, die mit Taubenfüssen kommen, lenken die Welt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAM:P0003","text":"Oh Zarathustra, du sollst gehen als ein Schatten dessen, was kommen muss: so wirst du befehlen und befehlend vorangehen.“—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"32e2e37078769c932c038102e290bfa8ddc492d3bfbec1f4200ffc815afa1878","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182348144565Z_ad948732","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"And I answered: “I lack the lion’s voice for all commanding.”","source_sha256":"32e2e37078769c932c038102e290bfa8ddc492d3bfbec1f4200ffc815afa1878","sha256":"f263907e550075503b223e9efa68fbf4ddce2414c651220ce477ff09d2b2ae97","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:23:48.144627+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAM:P0001","text":"And I answered: “I lack the lion’s voice for all commanding.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_UIAM:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAM:P0002","text":"Then it spoke to me again like a whisper: “It is the stillest words that bring the storm. Thoughts that come with doves’ feet guide the world.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_UIAM:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAM:P0003","text":"O Zarathustra, you shall go as a shadow of that which must come: thus you will command and, commanding, go before.”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_UIAM:P0003"]}],"source_digest":"38e7a55d9ab837ce99a7650a47de6452e2511c32285043bc010c058831bd4cc9","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Und ich antwortete: „Mir fehlt des Löwen Stimme zu allem Befehlen.“"}],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"Und ich antwortete: „Mir fehlt des Löwen Stimme zu allem Befehlen.“","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI","title":"And I answered: “I am ashamed.”","order":63,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_UIAI","text":"Und ich antwortete: „Ich schäme mich.“\n\nDa sprach es wieder ohne Stimme zu mir: „Du musst noch Kind werden und ohne Scham.\n\nDer Stolz der Jugend ist noch auf dir, spät bist du jung geworden: aber wer zum Kinde werden will, muss auch noch seine Jugend überwinden.“—\n\nUnd ich besann mich lange und zitterte. Endlich aber sagte ich, was ich zuerst sagte: „Ich will nicht.“\n\nDa geschah ein Lachen um mich. Wehe, wie diess Lachen mir die Eingeweide zerriss und das Herz aufschlitzte!\n\nUnd es sprach zum letzten Male zu mir: „Oh Zarathustra, deine Früchte sind reif, aber du bist nicht reif für deine Früchte!\n\nSo musst du wieder in die Einsamkeit: denn du sollst noch mürbe werden.“—\n\nUnd wieder lachte es und floh: dann wurde es stille um mich wie mit einer zwiefachen Stille. Ich aber lag am Boden, und der Schweiss floss mir von den Gliedern.\n\n—Nun hörtet ihr Alles, und warum ich in meine Einsamkeit zurück muss. Nichts verschwieg ich euch, meine Freunde.\n\nAber auch diess hörtet ihr von mir, wer immer noch aller Menschen Verschwiegenster ist—und es sein will!\n\nAch meine Freunde! Ich hätte euch noch Etwas zu sagen, ich hätte euch noch Etwas zu geben! Warum gebe ich es nicht? Bin ich denn geizig?“—\n\nAls Zarathustra aber diese Worte gesprochen hatte, überfiel ihn die Gewalt des Schmerzes und die Nähe des Abschieds von seinen Freunden, also dass er laut weinte; und Niemand wusste ihn zu trösten. Des Nachts aber gieng er allein fort und verliess seine Freunde.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0001","text":"Und ich antwortete: „Ich schäme mich.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0002","text":"Da sprach es wieder ohne Stimme zu mir: „Du musst noch Kind werden und ohne Scham.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0003","text":"Der Stolz der Jugend ist noch auf dir, spät bist du jung geworden: aber wer zum Kinde werden will, muss auch noch seine Jugend überwinden.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0004","text":"Und ich besann mich lange und zitterte. Endlich aber sagte ich, was ich zuerst sagte: „Ich will nicht.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0005","text":"Da geschah ein Lachen um mich. Wehe, wie diess Lachen mir die Eingeweide zerriss und das Herz aufschlitzte!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0006","text":"Und es sprach zum letzten Male zu mir: „Oh Zarathustra, deine Früchte sind reif, aber du bist nicht reif für deine Früchte!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0007","text":"So musst du wieder in die Einsamkeit: denn du sollst noch mürbe werden.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0008","text":"Und wieder lachte es und floh: dann wurde es stille um mich wie mit einer zwiefachen Stille. Ich aber lag am Boden, und der Schweiss floss mir von den Gliedern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0009","text":"—Nun hörtet ihr Alles, und warum ich in meine Einsamkeit zurück muss. Nichts verschwieg ich euch, meine Freunde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0010","text":"Aber auch diess hörtet ihr von mir, wer immer noch aller Menschen Verschwiegenster ist—und es sein will!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0011","text":"Ach meine Freunde! Ich hätte euch noch Etwas zu sagen, ich hätte euch noch Etwas zu geben! Warum gebe ich es nicht? Bin ich denn geizig?“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0012","text":"Als Zarathustra aber diese Worte gesprochen hatte, überfiel ihn die Gewalt des Schmerzes und die Nähe des Abschieds von seinen Freunden, also dass er laut weinte; und Niemand wusste ihn zu trösten. Des Nachts aber gieng er allein fort und verliess seine Freunde.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"124fe177e85369cae3f9764d382c12da6605c50bed6f0954d6e153b9935fd14c","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182353589362Z_801e1d99","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"And I answered: “I am ashamed.”","source_sha256":"124fe177e85369cae3f9764d382c12da6605c50bed6f0954d6e153b9935fd14c","sha256":"a802382b28cd854b541aacfe528dab9f2668b9a49586f0af77ba02e90499be91","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:23:53.589403+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0001","text":"And I answered: “I am ashamed.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0002","text":"Then it spoke to me again without a voice: “You must yet become a child and without shame.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0003","text":"The pride of youth is still upon you; late did you become young: but whoever wishes to become a child must also yet overcome his youth.”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0004","text":"And I reflected for a long time and trembled. But at last I said what I had said at first: “I will not.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0005","text":"Then laughter arose around me. Alas, how this laughter tore at my entrails and slit open my heart!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0006","text":"And it spoke to me for the last time: “O Zarathustra, your fruits are ripe, but you are not ripe for your fruits!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0007","text":"Thus you must return again into solitude: for you must yet become mellow.”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0008","text":"And again it laughed and fled: then it became still around me as with a twofold stillness. But I lay on the ground, and sweat flowed from my limbs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0009","text":"—Now you have heard everything, and why I must return into my solitude. I concealed nothing from you, my friends.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0010","text":"But you have also heard this from me, who is still the most secretive of all men—and wishes to be so!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0011","text":"Ah, my friends! I should still have something to say to you, I should still have something to give you! Why do I not give it? Am I then miserly?”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0012","text":"But when Zarathustra had spoken these words, he was overcome by the force of pain and by the nearness of parting from his friends, so that he wept aloud; and no one knew how to comfort him. But at night he went away alone and left his friends.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_UIAI:P0012"]}],"source_digest":"b9d3c742274b6249ec976e730b6d5003e1b077be99d6fa16bdbc9cc39db7238f","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Und ich antwortete: „Ich schäme mich.“"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Und ich antwortete: „Ich schäme mich.“","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DRIT","title":"Part Three","order":64,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_DRIT","text":"## Dritter Theil\n\n„Ihr seht nach Oben, wenn ihr nach Erhebung verlangt. Und ich sehe hinab, weil ich erhoben bin. Wer von euch kann zugleich lachen und erhoben sein? Wer auf den höchsten Bergen steigt, der lacht über alle Trauer-Spiele und Trauer-Ernste.“\n\nZarathustra, vom Lesen und Schreiben.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DRIT:P0001","text":"## Dritter Theil","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DRIT:P0002","text":"„Ihr seht nach Oben, wenn ihr nach Erhebung verlangt. Und ich sehe hinab, weil ich erhoben bin. Wer von euch kann zugleich lachen und erhoben sein? Wer auf den höchsten Bergen steigt, der lacht über alle Trauer-Spiele und Trauer-Ernste.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DRIT:P0003","text":"Zarathustra, vom Lesen und Schreiben.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"11077d82da731d065ba6b90028de851785e9cd4fbd91a5eb2ab9a0d1cf9a73d8","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182355611164Z_687953d5","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Three","source_sha256":"11077d82da731d065ba6b90028de851785e9cd4fbd91a5eb2ab9a0d1cf9a73d8","sha256":"3b175a4c469760d599ea861b1dcc8c67758e1178401d7dcb01d334a3ce4eee17","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:23:55.611213+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DRIT:P0001","text":"## Third Part","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DRIT:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DRIT:P0002","text":"“You look upwards when you desire elevation. And I look down, because I am elevated. Which of you can at once laugh and be elevated? Whoever climbs upon the highest mountains laughs at all tragic plays and tragic seriousness.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DRIT:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DRIT:P0003","text":"Zarathustra, On Reading and Writing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DRIT:P0003"]}],"source_digest":"f5203ea4bd3d04c8a2f8a1e19d08335d8845c857c20f6717190f96d710e2ba4f","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Dritter Theil"}],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"Dritter Theil","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2","title":"The Wanderer","order":65,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_DE_2","text":"## Der Wanderer\n\nUm Mitternacht war es, da nahm Zarathustra seinen Weg über den Rücken der Insel, dass er mit dem frühen Morgen an das andre Gestade käme: denn dort wollte er zu Schiff steigen. Es gab nämlich allda eine gute Rhede, an der auch fremde Schiffe gern vor Anker giengen; die nahmen Manchen mit sich, der von den glückseligen Inseln über das Meer wollte. Als nun Zarathustra so den Berg hinanstieg, gedachte er unterwegs des vielen einsamen Wanderns von Jugend an, und wie viele Berge und Rücken und Gipfel er schon gestiegen sei.\n\nIch bin ein Wanderer und ein Bergsteiger, sagte er zu seinem Herzen, ich liebe die Ebenen nicht und es scheint, ich kann nicht lange still sitzen.\n\nUnd was mir nun auch noch als Schicksal und Erlebniss komme,—ein Wandern wird darin sein und ein Bergsteigen: man erlebt endlich nur noch sich selber.\n\nDie Zeit ist abgeflossen, wo mir noch Zufälle begegnen durften; und was könnte jetzt noch zu mir fallen, was nicht schon mein Eigen wäre!\n\nEs kehrt nur zurück, es kommt mir endlich heim—mein eigen Selbst, und was von ihm lange in der Fremde war und zerstreut unter alle Dinge und Zufälle.\n\nUnd noch Eins weiss ich: ich stehe jetzt vor meinem letzten Gipfel und vor dem, was mir am längsten aufgespart war. Ach, meinen härtesten Weg muss ich hinan! Ach, ich begann meine einsamste Wanderung!\n\nWer aber meiner Art ist, der entgeht einer solchen Stunde nicht: der Stunde, die zu ihm redet: „Jetzo erst gehst du deinen Weg der Grösse! Gipfel und Abgrund—das ist jetzt in Eins beschlossen!\n\nDu gehst deinen Weg der Grösse: nun ist deine letzte Zuflucht worden, was bisher deine letzte Gefahr hiess!\n\nDu gehst deinen Weg der Grösse: das muss nun dein bester Muth sein, dass es hinter dir keinen Weg mehr giebt!\n\nDu gehst deinen Weg der Grösse; hier soll dir Keiner nachschleichen! Dein Fuss selber löschte hinter dir den Weg aus, und über ihm steht geschrieben: Unmöglichkeit.\n\nUnd wenn dir nunmehr alle Leitern fehlen, so musst du verstehen, noch auf deinen eigenen Kopf zu steigen: wie wolltest du anders aufwärts steigen?\n\nAuf deinen eigenen Kopf und hinweg über dein eigenes Herz! Jetzt muss das Mildeste an dir noch zum Härtesten werden.\n\nWer sich stets viel geschont hat, der kränkelt zuletzt an seiner vielen Schonung. Gelobt sei, was hart macht! Ich lobe das Land nicht, wo Butter und Honig—fliesst!\n\nVon sich absehn lernen ist nöthig, um Viel zu sehn:—diese Härte thut jedem Berge-Steigenden Noth.\n\nWer aber mit den Augen zudringlich ist als Erkennender, wie sollte der von allen Dingen mehr als ihre vorderen Gründe sehn!\n\nDu aber, oh Zarathustra, wolltest aller Dinge Grund schaun und Hintergrund: so musst du schon über dich selber steigen,—hinan, hinauf, bis du auch deine Sterne noch unter dir hast!\n\nJa! Hinab auf mich selber sehn und noch auf meine Sterne: das erst hiesse mir mein Gipfel , das blieb mir noch zurück als mein letzter Gipfel!—“\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra im Steigen zu sich, mit harten Sprüchlein sein Herz tröstend: denn er war wund am Herzen wie noch niemals zuvor. Und als er auf die Höhe des Bergrückens kam, siehe, da lag das andere Meer vor ihm ausgebreitet: und er stand still und schwieg lange. Die Nacht aber war kalt in dieser Höhe und klar und hellgestirnt.\n\nIch erkenne mein Loos, sagte er endlich mit Trauer. Wohlan! Ich bin bereit. Eben begann meine letzte Einsamkeit.\n\nAch, diese schwarze traurige See unter mir! Ach, diese schwangere nächtliche Verdrossenheit! Ach, Schicksal und See! Zu euch muss ich nun hinab steigen!\n\nVor meinem höchsten Berge stehe ich und vor meiner längsten Wanderung: darum muss ich erst tiefer hinab als ich jemals stieg:\n\n—tiefer hinab in den Schmerz als ich jemals stieg, bis hinein in seine schwärzeste Fluth! So will es mein Schicksal: Wohlan! Ich bin bereit.\n\nWoher kommen die höchsten Berge? so fragte ich einst. Da lernte ich, dass sie aus dem Meere kommen.\n\nDiess Zeugniss ist in ihr Gestein geschrieben und in die Wände ihrer Gipfel. Aus dem Tiefsten muss das Höchste zu seiner Höhe kommen.—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra auf der Spitze des Berges, wo es kalt war; als er aber in die Nähe des Meeres kam und zuletzt allein unter den Klippen stand, da war er unterwegs müde geworden und sehnsüchtiger als noch zuvor.\n\nEs schläft jetzt Alles noch, sprach er; auch das Meer schläft. Schlaftrunken und fremd blickt sein Auge nach mir.\n\nAber es athmet warm, das fühle ich. Und ich fühle auch, dass es träumt. Es windet sieh träumend auf harten Kissen.\n\nHorch! Horch! Wie es stöhnt von bösen Erinnerungen! Oder bösen Erwartungen?\n\nAch, ich bin traurig mit dir, du dunkles Ungeheuer, und mir selber noch gram um deinetwillen.\n\nAch, dass meine Hand nicht Stärke genug hat! Gerne, wahrlich, möchte ich dich von bösen Träumen erlösen!—\n\nUnd indem Zarathustra so sprach, lachte er mit Schwermuth und Bitterkeit über sich selber. „Wie! Zarathustra! sagte er, willst du noch dem Meere Trost singen?\n\nAch, du liebreicher Narr Zarathustra, du Vertrauens-Überseliger! Aber so warst du immer: immer kamst du vertraulich zu allem Furchtbaren.\n\nJedes Ungethüm wolltest du noch streicheln. Ein Hauch warmen Athems, ein Wenig weiches Gezottel an der Tatze—: und gleich warst du bereit, es zu lieben und zu locken.\n\nDie Liebe ist die Gefahr des Einsamsten, die Liebe zu Allem, wenn es nur lebt! Zum Lachen ist wahrlich meine Narrheit und meine Bescheidenheit in der Liebe!“—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra und lachte dabei zum andern Male: da aber gedachte er seiner verlassenen Freunde—, und wie als ob er sich mit seinen Gedanken an ihnen vergangen habe, zürnte er sich ob seiner Gedanken. Und alsbald geschah es, dass der Lachende weinte:—vor Zorn und Sehnsucht weinte Zarathustra bitterlich.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0001","text":"## Der Wanderer","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0002","text":"Um Mitternacht war es, da nahm Zarathustra seinen Weg über den Rücken der Insel, dass er mit dem frühen Morgen an das andre Gestade käme: denn dort wollte er zu Schiff steigen. Es gab nämlich allda eine gute Rhede, an der auch fremde Schiffe gern vor Anker giengen; die nahmen Manchen mit sich, der von den glückseligen Inseln über das Meer wollte. Als nun Zarathustra so den Berg hinanstieg, gedachte er unterwegs des vielen einsamen Wanderns von Jugend an, und wie viele Berge und Rücken und Gipfel er schon gestiegen sei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0003","text":"Ich bin ein Wanderer und ein Bergsteiger, sagte er zu seinem Herzen, ich liebe die Ebenen nicht und es scheint, ich kann nicht lange still sitzen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0004","text":"Und was mir nun auch noch als Schicksal und Erlebniss komme,—ein Wandern wird darin sein und ein Bergsteigen: man erlebt endlich nur noch sich selber.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0005","text":"Die Zeit ist abgeflossen, wo mir noch Zufälle begegnen durften; und was könnte jetzt noch zu mir fallen, was nicht schon mein Eigen wäre!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0006","text":"Es kehrt nur zurück, es kommt mir endlich heim—mein eigen Selbst, und was von ihm lange in der Fremde war und zerstreut unter alle Dinge und Zufälle.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0007","text":"Und noch Eins weiss ich: ich stehe jetzt vor meinem letzten Gipfel und vor dem, was mir am längsten aufgespart war. Ach, meinen härtesten Weg muss ich hinan! Ach, ich begann meine einsamste Wanderung!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0008","text":"Wer aber meiner Art ist, der entgeht einer solchen Stunde nicht: der Stunde, die zu ihm redet: „Jetzo erst gehst du deinen Weg der Grösse! Gipfel und Abgrund—das ist jetzt in Eins beschlossen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0009","text":"Du gehst deinen Weg der Grösse: nun ist deine letzte Zuflucht worden, was bisher deine letzte Gefahr hiess!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0010","text":"Du gehst deinen Weg der Grösse: das muss nun dein bester Muth sein, dass es hinter dir keinen Weg mehr giebt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0011","text":"Du gehst deinen Weg der Grösse; hier soll dir Keiner nachschleichen! Dein Fuss selber löschte hinter dir den Weg aus, und über ihm steht geschrieben: Unmöglichkeit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0012","text":"Und wenn dir nunmehr alle Leitern fehlen, so musst du verstehen, noch auf deinen eigenen Kopf zu steigen: wie wolltest du anders aufwärts steigen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0013","text":"Auf deinen eigenen Kopf und hinweg über dein eigenes Herz! Jetzt muss das Mildeste an dir noch zum Härtesten werden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0014","text":"Wer sich stets viel geschont hat, der kränkelt zuletzt an seiner vielen Schonung. Gelobt sei, was hart macht! Ich lobe das Land nicht, wo Butter und Honig—fliesst!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0015","text":"Von sich absehn lernen ist nöthig, um Viel zu sehn:—diese Härte thut jedem Berge-Steigenden Noth.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0016","text":"Wer aber mit den Augen zudringlich ist als Erkennender, wie sollte der von allen Dingen mehr als ihre vorderen Gründe sehn!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0017","text":"Du aber, oh Zarathustra, wolltest aller Dinge Grund schaun und Hintergrund: so musst du schon über dich selber steigen,—hinan, hinauf, bis du auch deine Sterne noch unter dir hast!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0018","text":"Ja! Hinab auf mich selber sehn und noch auf meine Sterne: das erst hiesse mir mein Gipfel , das blieb mir noch zurück als mein letzter Gipfel!—“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0019","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra im Steigen zu sich, mit harten Sprüchlein sein Herz tröstend: denn er war wund am Herzen wie noch niemals zuvor. Und als er auf die Höhe des Bergrückens kam, siehe, da lag das andere Meer vor ihm ausgebreitet: und er stand still und schwieg lange. Die Nacht aber war kalt in dieser Höhe und klar und hellgestirnt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0020","text":"Ich erkenne mein Loos, sagte er endlich mit Trauer. Wohlan! Ich bin bereit. Eben begann meine letzte Einsamkeit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0021","text":"Ach, diese schwarze traurige See unter mir! Ach, diese schwangere nächtliche Verdrossenheit! Ach, Schicksal und See! Zu euch muss ich nun hinab steigen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0022","text":"Vor meinem höchsten Berge stehe ich und vor meiner längsten Wanderung: darum muss ich erst tiefer hinab als ich jemals stieg:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0023","text":"—tiefer hinab in den Schmerz als ich jemals stieg, bis hinein in seine schwärzeste Fluth! So will es mein Schicksal: Wohlan! Ich bin bereit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0024","text":"Woher kommen die höchsten Berge? so fragte ich einst. Da lernte ich, dass sie aus dem Meere kommen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0025","text":"Diess Zeugniss ist in ihr Gestein geschrieben und in die Wände ihrer Gipfel. Aus dem Tiefsten muss das Höchste zu seiner Höhe kommen.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0026","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra auf der Spitze des Berges, wo es kalt war; als er aber in die Nähe des Meeres kam und zuletzt allein unter den Klippen stand, da war er unterwegs müde geworden und sehnsüchtiger als noch zuvor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0027","text":"Es schläft jetzt Alles noch, sprach er; auch das Meer schläft. Schlaftrunken und fremd blickt sein Auge nach mir.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0028","text":"Aber es athmet warm, das fühle ich. Und ich fühle auch, dass es träumt. Es windet sieh träumend auf harten Kissen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0029","text":"Horch! Horch! Wie es stöhnt von bösen Erinnerungen! Oder bösen Erwartungen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0030","text":"Ach, ich bin traurig mit dir, du dunkles Ungeheuer, und mir selber noch gram um deinetwillen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0031","text":"Ach, dass meine Hand nicht Stärke genug hat! Gerne, wahrlich, möchte ich dich von bösen Träumen erlösen!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0032","text":"Und indem Zarathustra so sprach, lachte er mit Schwermuth und Bitterkeit über sich selber. „Wie! Zarathustra! sagte er, willst du noch dem Meere Trost singen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0033","text":"Ach, du liebreicher Narr Zarathustra, du Vertrauens-Überseliger! Aber so warst du immer: immer kamst du vertraulich zu allem Furchtbaren.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0034","text":"Jedes Ungethüm wolltest du noch streicheln. Ein Hauch warmen Athems, ein Wenig weiches Gezottel an der Tatze—: und gleich warst du bereit, es zu lieben und zu locken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0035","text":"Die Liebe ist die Gefahr des Einsamsten, die Liebe zu Allem, wenn es nur lebt! Zum Lachen ist wahrlich meine Narrheit und meine Bescheidenheit in der Liebe!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0036","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra und lachte dabei zum andern Male: da aber gedachte er seiner verlassenen Freunde—, und wie als ob er sich mit seinen Gedanken an ihnen vergangen habe, zürnte er sich ob seiner Gedanken. Und alsbald geschah es, dass der Lachende weinte:—vor Zorn und Sehnsucht weinte Zarathustra bitterlich.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"7cf22b76df623adde03f2f1c00a235f1ba12876a247ca9e033ec727fb62749b2","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182415978153Z_3fbf2c73","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"The Wanderer","source_sha256":"7cf22b76df623adde03f2f1c00a235f1ba12876a247ca9e033ec727fb62749b2","sha256":"779bb7228a297a9cf18bff0483267ab1e06ccff91971cc7e0c61674c3c0b5c06","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:24:15.978168+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0001","text":"## The Wanderer","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0002","text":"It was midnight when Zarathustra set out across the ridge of the island, so that he might reach the other shore with the early morning: for there he wished to embark. For there was a good roadstead there, where foreign ships too gladly cast anchor; they took many a one with them who wished to cross the sea from the blessed isles. Now as Zarathustra thus climbed the mountain, he thought on the way of the many solitary wanderings since his youth, and of how many mountains and ridges and summits he had already climbed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0003","text":"I am a wanderer and a mountain-climber, he said to his heart; I do not love the plains, and it seems I cannot sit still for long.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0004","text":"And whatever may now yet come to me as fate and experience,—there will be wandering and mountain-climbing in it: in the end one experiences only oneself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0005","text":"The time has passed when accidents were still permitted to befall me; and what could now fall to me that were not already my own!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0006","text":"It only returns, it at last comes home to me—my own self, and that which of it was long abroad and scattered among all things and accidents.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0007","text":"And one thing more I know: I now stand before my final summit and before that which was reserved for me the longest. Ah, I must climb my hardest path! Ah, I began my loneliest wandering!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0008","text":"But whoever is of my kind does not escape such an hour: the hour that speaks to him: “Only now dost thou go thy path of greatness! Summit and abyss—that is now resolved into one!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0009","text":"Thou goest thy path of greatness: now that which hitherto was called thy final danger has become thy final refuge!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0010","text":"Thou goest thy path of greatness: this must now be thy best courage, that there is no longer any path behind thee!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0011","text":"Thou goest thy path of greatness; here none shall skulk after thee! Thy foot itself erased the path behind thee, and above it is written: Impossibility.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0012","text":"And if henceforth all ladders fail thee, then thou must understand how to climb even upon thine own head: how else wouldst thou climb upward?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0013","text":"Upon thine own head and beyond thine own heart! Now even that which is mildest in thee must become hardest.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0014","text":"He who has always spared himself much, in the end sickens from his much sparing. Praised be that which makes hard! I do not praise the land where butter and honey—flows!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0015","text":"Learning to look away from oneself is necessary in order to see much:—this hardness is needed by every mountain-climber.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0016","text":"But he who, as a knower, is importunate with his eyes, how should he see of all things more than their foregrounds!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0017","text":"But thou, O Zarathustra, wouldst behold the ground and background of all things: therefore thou must climb beyond thyself,—up, upward, until thou hast even thy stars beneath thee!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0018","text":"Yes! To look down upon myself and even upon my stars: that alone would be called my summit, that remained for me as my final summit!—“","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0019","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra to himself as he climbed, consoling his heart with hard little sayings: for he was wounded at heart as never before. And when he came to the height of the mountain ridge, behold, the other sea lay spread out before him: and he stood still and was silent for a long time. But the night was cold at this height, and clear and bright with stars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0020","text":"I recognize my lot, he said at last sorrowfully. Well then! I am ready. My final solitude has just begun.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0021","text":"Ah, this black mournful sea beneath me! Ah, this pregnant nocturnal despondency! Ah, fate and sea! Down to you must I now descend!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0022","text":"Before my highest mountain I stand and before my longest wandering: therefore I must first descend deeper than I ever climbed:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0023","text":"—deeper down into pain than I ever climbed, into its blackest flood! Thus my fate wills it: well then! I am ready.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0024","text":"Whence come the highest mountains? Thus I once asked. There I learned that they come from the sea.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0025","text":"This testimony is written in their rock and in the walls of their peaks. From the deepest must the highest come to its height.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0026","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra on the summit of the mountain, where it was cold; but when he came near the sea and at last stood alone among the cliffs, he had grown weary on the way and more longing than before.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0027","text":"Everything is still sleeping now, he said; the sea too sleeps. Its eye looks at me drowsy with sleep and strange.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0028","text":"But it breathes warmly, I feel that. And I also feel that it dreams. Dreaming, it writhes upon hard cushions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0029","text":"Listen! Listen! How it groans from evil memories! Or evil expectations?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0030","text":"Ah, I am sad with you, you dark monster, and angry with myself still on your account.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0031","text":"Ah, that my hand has not strength enough! Gladly, truly, would I deliver you from evil dreams!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0032","text":"And as Zarathustra spoke thus, he laughed at himself with melancholy and bitterness. “What! Zarathustra! he said, do you still wish to sing consolation to the sea?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0033","text":"Ah, you loving fool Zarathustra, you over-blessed one of trust! But thus you have always been: you always came familiarly to everything terrible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0034","text":"You still wanted to stroke every monster. A breath of warm breathing, a little soft shag on its paw—: and at once you were ready to love it and lure it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0035","text":"Love is the danger of the loneliest one, love for everything, provided only that it lives! Truly, my folly and my modesty in love are laughable!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0036","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra and laughed for the second time: but then he thought of his forsaken friends—and, as though he had sinned against them with his thoughts, he grew angry with himself on account of his thoughts. And immediately it came to pass that the one who had laughed wept:—from anger and longing Zarathustra wept bitterly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DE_2:P0036"]}],"source_digest":"320b056cf7133b4dc6a37524d7e787b129381e2d5286772c9030b9e6b0cb5c86","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Der Wanderer"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Der Wanderer","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3","title":"1.","order":66,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_1C_3","text":"## 1.\n\nAls es unter den Schiffsleuten ruchbar wurde, dass Zarathustra auf dem Schiffe sei,—denn es war ein Mann zugleich mit ihm an Bord gegangen, der von den glückseligen Inseln kam—da entstand eine grosse Neugierde und Erwartung. Aber Zarathustra schwieg zwei Tage und war kalt und taub vor Traurigkeit, also, dass er weder auf Blicke noch auf Fragen antwortete. Am Abende aber des zweiten Tages that er seine Ohren wieder auf, ob er gleich noch schwieg: denn es gab viel Seltsames und Gefährliches auf diesem Schiffe anzuhören, welches weither kam und noch weiterhin wollte. Zarathustra aber war ein Freund aller Solchen, die weite Reisen thun und nicht ohne Gefahr leben mögen. Und siehe! zuletzt wurde ihm im Zuhören die eigne Zunge gelöst, und das Eis seines Herzens brach: —da begann er also zu reden:\n\nEuch, den kühnen Suchern, Versuchern, und wer je sich mit listigen Segeln auf furchtbare Meere einschiffte,—\n\neuch, den Räthsel-Trunkenen, den Zwielicht-Frohen, deren Seele mit Flöten zu jedem Irr-Schlunde gelockt wird:\n\n—denn nicht wollt ihr mit feiger Hand einem Faden nachtasten; und, wo ihr errathen könnt, da hasst ihr es, zu erschliessen —\n\neuch allein erzähle ich das Räthsel, das ich sah ,—das Gesicht des Einsamsten.—\n\nDüster gierig ich jüngst durch leichenfarbne Dämmerung,—düster und hart, mit gepressten Lippen. Nicht nur Eine Sonne war mir untergegangen.\n\nEin Pfad, der trotzig durch Geröll stieg, ein boshafter, einsamer, dem nicht Kraut, nicht Strauch mehr zusprach: ein Bergpfad knirschte unter dem Trotz meines Fusses.\n\nStumm über höhnischem Geklirr von Kieseln schreitend, den Stein zertretend, der ihn gleiten liess: also zwang mein Fuss sich aufwärts.\n\nAufwärts:—dem Geiste zum Trotz, der ihn abwärts zog, abgrundwärts zog, dem Geiste der Schwere, meinem Teufel und Erzfeinde.\n\nAufwärts:—obwohl er auf mir sass, halb Zwerg, halb Maulwurf; lahm; lähmend; Blei durch mein Ohr, Bleitropfen-Gedanken in mein Hirn träufelnd.\n\n„Oh Zarathustra, raunte er höhnisch Silb’ um Silbe, du Stein der Weisheit! Du warfst dich hoch, aber jeder geworfene Stein muss—fallen!\n\nOh Zarathustra, du Stein der Weisheit, du Schleuderstein, du Stern-Zertrümmerer! Dich selber warfst du so hoch,—aber jeder geworfene Stein - muss fallen!\n\nVerurtheilt zu dir selber und zur eignen Steinigung: oh Zarathustra, weit warfst du ja den Stein,—aber auf dich wird er zurückfallen!“\n\nDrauf schwieg der Zwerg; und das währte lange. Sein Schweigen aber drückte mich; und solchermaassen zu Zwein ist man wahrlich einsamer als zu Einem!\n\nIch stieg, ich stieg, ich träumte, ich dachte,—aber Alles drückte mich. Einem Kranken glich ich, den seine schlimme Marter müde macht, und den wieder ein schlimmerer Traum aus dem Einschlafen weckt.—\n\nAber es giebt Etwas in mir, das ich Muth heisse: das schlug bisher mir jeden Unmuth todt. Dieser Muth hiess mich endlich stille stehn und sprechen: „Zwerg! Du! Oder ich!“—\n\nMuth nämlich ist der beste Todtschläger,—Muth, welcher angreift : denn in jedem Angriffe ist klingendes Spiel.\n\nDer Mensch aber ist das muthigste Thier: damit überwand er jedes Thier. Mit klingendem Spiele überwand er noch jeden Schmerz; Menschen-Schmerz aber ist der tiefste Schmerz.\n\nDer Muth schlägt auch den Schwindel todt an Abgründen: und wo stünde der Mensch nicht an Abgründen! Ist Sehen nicht selber—Abgründe sehen?\n\nMuth ist der beste Todtschläger: der Muth schlägt auch das Mitleiden todt. Mitleiden aber ist der tiefste Abgrund: so tief der Mensch in das Leben sieht, so tief sieht er auch in das Leiden.\n\nMuth aber ist der beste Todtschläger, Muth, der angreift: der schlägt noch den Tod todt, denn er spricht: „War das das Leben? Wohlan! Noch Ein Mal!“\n\nIn solchem Spruche aber ist viel klingendes Spiel. Wer Ohren hat, der höre.—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0002","text":"Als es unter den Schiffsleuten ruchbar wurde, dass Zarathustra auf dem Schiffe sei,—denn es war ein Mann zugleich mit ihm an Bord gegangen, der von den glückseligen Inseln kam—da entstand eine grosse Neugierde und Erwartung. Aber Zarathustra schwieg zwei Tage und war kalt und taub vor Traurigkeit, also, dass er weder auf Blicke noch auf Fragen antwortete. Am Abende aber des zweiten Tages that er seine Ohren wieder auf, ob er gleich noch schwieg: denn es gab viel Seltsames und Gefährliches auf diesem Schiffe anzuhören, welches weither kam und noch weiterhin wollte. Zarathustra aber war ein Freund aller Solchen, die weite Reisen thun und nicht ohne Gefahr leben mögen. Und siehe! zuletzt wurde ihm im Zuhören die eigne Zunge gelöst, und das Eis seines Herzens brach: —da begann er also zu reden:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0003","text":"Euch, den kühnen Suchern, Versuchern, und wer je sich mit listigen Segeln auf furchtbare Meere einschiffte,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0004","text":"euch, den Räthsel-Trunkenen, den Zwielicht-Frohen, deren Seele mit Flöten zu jedem Irr-Schlunde gelockt wird:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0005","text":"—denn nicht wollt ihr mit feiger Hand einem Faden nachtasten; und, wo ihr errathen könnt, da hasst ihr es, zu erschliessen —","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0006","text":"euch allein erzähle ich das Räthsel, das ich sah ,—das Gesicht des Einsamsten.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0007","text":"Düster gierig ich jüngst durch leichenfarbne Dämmerung,—düster und hart, mit gepressten Lippen. Nicht nur Eine Sonne war mir untergegangen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0008","text":"Ein Pfad, der trotzig durch Geröll stieg, ein boshafter, einsamer, dem nicht Kraut, nicht Strauch mehr zusprach: ein Bergpfad knirschte unter dem Trotz meines Fusses.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0009","text":"Stumm über höhnischem Geklirr von Kieseln schreitend, den Stein zertretend, der ihn gleiten liess: also zwang mein Fuss sich aufwärts.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0010","text":"Aufwärts:—dem Geiste zum Trotz, der ihn abwärts zog, abgrundwärts zog, dem Geiste der Schwere, meinem Teufel und Erzfeinde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0011","text":"Aufwärts:—obwohl er auf mir sass, halb Zwerg, halb Maulwurf; lahm; lähmend; Blei durch mein Ohr, Bleitropfen-Gedanken in mein Hirn träufelnd.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0012","text":"„Oh Zarathustra, raunte er höhnisch Silb’ um Silbe, du Stein der Weisheit! Du warfst dich hoch, aber jeder geworfene Stein muss—fallen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0013","text":"Oh Zarathustra, du Stein der Weisheit, du Schleuderstein, du Stern-Zertrümmerer! Dich selber warfst du so hoch,—aber jeder geworfene Stein - muss fallen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0014","text":"Verurtheilt zu dir selber und zur eignen Steinigung: oh Zarathustra, weit warfst du ja den Stein,—aber auf dich wird er zurückfallen!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0015","text":"Drauf schwieg der Zwerg; und das währte lange. Sein Schweigen aber drückte mich; und solchermaassen zu Zwein ist man wahrlich einsamer als zu Einem!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0016","text":"Ich stieg, ich stieg, ich träumte, ich dachte,—aber Alles drückte mich. Einem Kranken glich ich, den seine schlimme Marter müde macht, und den wieder ein schlimmerer Traum aus dem Einschlafen weckt.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0017","text":"Aber es giebt Etwas in mir, das ich Muth heisse: das schlug bisher mir jeden Unmuth todt. Dieser Muth hiess mich endlich stille stehn und sprechen: „Zwerg! Du! Oder ich!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0018","text":"Muth nämlich ist der beste Todtschläger,—Muth, welcher angreift : denn in jedem Angriffe ist klingendes Spiel.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0019","text":"Der Mensch aber ist das muthigste Thier: damit überwand er jedes Thier. Mit klingendem Spiele überwand er noch jeden Schmerz; Menschen-Schmerz aber ist der tiefste Schmerz.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0020","text":"Der Muth schlägt auch den Schwindel todt an Abgründen: und wo stünde der Mensch nicht an Abgründen! Ist Sehen nicht selber—Abgründe sehen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0021","text":"Muth ist der beste Todtschläger: der Muth schlägt auch das Mitleiden todt. Mitleiden aber ist der tiefste Abgrund: so tief der Mensch in das Leben sieht, so tief sieht er auch in das Leiden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0022","text":"Muth aber ist der beste Todtschläger, Muth, der angreift: der schlägt noch den Tod todt, denn er spricht: „War das das Leben? Wohlan! Noch Ein Mal!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0023","text":"In solchem Spruche aber ist viel klingendes Spiel. Wer Ohren hat, der höre.—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"96d19d91ced018d37cb7a783cb76c25adfdd3d9f470ab7544d20c1e09a993cc2","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182427434841Z_4aa81305","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1.","source_sha256":"96d19d91ced018d37cb7a783cb76c25adfdd3d9f470ab7544d20c1e09a993cc2","sha256":"863209381317057ab51c4c68ca094b06db087c2fb798774ac4a83d9ab1443aaf","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:24:27.434873+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0002","text":"When it became known among the seamen that Zarathustra was on the ship—for a man had gone aboard with him who came from the Blessed Isles—great curiosity and expectation arose. But Zarathustra was silent for two days and was cold and deaf from sorrow, so that he answered neither looks nor questions. In the evening of the second day, however, he opened his ears again, though he still remained silent: for there was much strange and dangerous matter to be heard on this ship, which had come from afar and intended to go still farther. But Zarathustra was a friend to all such as undertake long journeys and do not care to live without danger. And behold! at last, while listening, his own tongue was loosened, and the ice of his heart broke:—then he began to speak thus:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0003","text":"To you, bold seekers, tempters, and whoever has ever embarked with cunning sails upon dreadful seas,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0004","text":"to you, drunk with riddles, glad in twilight, whose soul is lured with flutes to every errant gulf:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0005","text":"—for you do not wish to feel your way along a thread with cowardly hand; and, where you can guess, there you hate to infer—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0006","text":"to you alone I tell the riddle that I saw,—the vision of the loneliest one.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0007","text":"Gloomily I went recently through corpse-coloured twilight,—gloomily and sternly, with lips pressed tight. More than one sun had set for me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0008","text":"A path that rose defiantly through rubble, a malicious, lonely one, to which neither herb nor shrub spoke any longer: a mountain path crunched beneath the defiance of my foot.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0009","text":"Walking silently above the mocking clatter of pebbles, crushing the stone that made it slip: thus my foot forced itself upward.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0010","text":"Upward:—in defiance of the spirit that drew it downward, drew it abyssward, the spirit of gravity, my devil and archenemy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0011","text":"Upward:—although he sat upon me, half dwarf, half mole; crippled; crippling; pouring lead through my ear, lead-drop thoughts into my brain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0012","text":"“O Zarathustra,” he whispered mockingly, syllable by syllable, “you stone of wisdom! You threw yourself high, but every stone thrown must—fall!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0013","text":"O Zarathustra, you stone of wisdom, you sling-stone, you star-shatterer! You threw yourself so high,—but every stone thrown—must fall!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0014","text":"Condemned to yourself and to your own stoning: O Zarathustra, far indeed did you throw the stone,—but back upon you it will fall!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0015","text":"Thereupon the dwarf fell silent; and that lasted long. But his silence oppressed me; and thus, being two, one is truly lonelier than being one!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0016","text":"I climbed, I climbed, I dreamed, I thought,—but everything oppressed me. I resembled a sick man, whom his grievous torment wearies, and whom an even worse dream awakens again from falling asleep.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0017","text":"But there is something in me that I call courage: hitherto it has slain for me every dejection. At last this courage commanded me to stand still and speak: “Dwarf! You! Or I!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0018","text":"For courage is the best slayer,—courage which attacks: for in every attack there is ringing play.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0019","text":"But man is the most courageous animal: thereby he overcame every animal. With ringing play he has also overcome every pain; but human pain is the deepest pain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0020","text":"Courage also slays dizziness at abysses: and where would man not stand at abysses! Is seeing not itself—seeing abysses?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0021","text":"Courage is the best slayer: courage also slays pity. But pity is the deepest abyss: as deeply as man looks into life, so deeply does he also look into suffering.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0022","text":"But courage is the best slayer, courage that attacks: it slays even death, for it says: “Was that life? Well then! Once more!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0023","text":"But in such a saying there is much ringing play. Whoever has ears, let him hear.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_3:P0023"]}],"source_digest":"15d6a6aa27c9b889652650197fb8ec410f39cc4116c4444a4b950e4a6b0139ac","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Vision and the Riddle","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On the Vision and the Riddle · 1","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On the Vision and the Riddle","1."],"original_title":"1."}],"source_title_context":"Vom Gesicht und Räthsel","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom Gesicht und Räthsel · 1","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Vom Gesicht und Räthsel","1."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"1.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3","title":"2.","order":67,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_2C_3","text":"## 2.\n\n„Halt! Zwerg! sprach ich. Ich! Oder du! Ich aber bin der Stärkere von uns Beiden—: du kennst meinen abgründlichen Gedanken nicht! Den —könntest du nicht tragen!“—\n\nDa geschah, was mich leichter machte: denn der Zwerg sprang mir von der Schulter, der Neugierige! Und er hockte sich auf einen Stein vor mich hin. Es war aber gerade da ein Thorweg, wo wir hielten.\n\n„Siehe diesen Thorweg! Zwerg! sprach ich weiter: der hat zwei Gesichter. Zwei Wege kommen hier zusammen: die gieng noch Niemand zu Ende.\n\nDiese lange Gasse zurück: die währt eine Ewigkeit. Und jene lange Gasse hinaus —das ist eine andre Ewigkeit.\n\nSie widersprechen sich, diese Wege; sie stossen sich gerade vor den Kopf:—und hier, an diesem Thorwege, ist es, wo sie zusammen kommen. Der Name des Thorwegs steht oben geschrieben: „Augenblick“.\n\nAber wer Einen von ihnen weiter gienge—und immer weiter und immer ferner: glaubst du, Zwerg, dass diese Wege sich ewig widersprechen?“—\n\n„Alles Gerade lügt, murmelte verächtlich der Zwerg. Alle Wahrheit ist krumm, die Zeit selber ist ein Kreis.“\n\n„Du Geist der Schwere! sprach ich zürnend, mache dir es nicht zu leicht! Oder ich lasse dich hocken, wo du hockst, Lahmfuss,—und ich trug dich hoch !\n\nSiehe, sprach ich weiter, diesen Augenblick! Von diesem Thorwege Augenblick läuft eine lange ewige Gasse rückwärts hinter uns liegt eine Ewigkeit.\n\nMuss nicht, was laufen kann von allen Dingen, schon einmal diese Gasse gelaufen sein? Muss nicht, was geschehn kann von allen Dingen, schon einmal geschehn, gethan, vorübergelaufen sein?\n\nUnd wenn Alles schon dagewesen ist: was hältst du Zwerg von diesem Augenblick? Muss auch dieser Thorweg nicht schon—dagewesen sein?\n\nUnd sind nicht solchermaassen fest alle Dinge verknotet, dass dieser Augenblick alle kommenden Dinge nach sich zieht? Also —- sich selber noch?\n\nDenn, was laufen kann von allen Dingen: auch in dieser langen Gasse hinaus — muss es einmal noch laufen!—\n\nUnd diese langsame Spinne, die im Mondscheine kriecht, und dieser Mondschein selber, und ich und du im Thorwege, zusammen flüsternd, von ewigen Dingen flüsternd—müssen wir nicht Alle schon dagewesen sein?\n\n—und wiederkommen und in jener anderen Gasse laufen, hinaus, vor uns, in dieser langen schaurigen Gasse—müssen wir nicht ewig wiederkommen?—“\n\nAlso redete ich, und immer leiser: denn ich fürchtete mich vor meinen eignen Gedanken und Hintergedanken. Da, plötzlich, hörte ich einen Hund nahe heulen .\n\nHörte ich jemals einen Hund so heulen? Mein Gedanke lief zurück. Ja! Als ich Kind war, in fernster Kindheit:\n\n—da hörte ich einen Hund so heulen. Und sah ihn auch, gesträubt, den Kopf nach Oben, zitternd, in stillster Mitternacht, wo auch Hunde an Gespenster glauben:\n\n—also dass es mich erbarmte. Eben nämlich gieng der volle Mond, todtschweigsam, über das Haus, eben stand er still, eine runde Gluth,—still auf flachem Dache, gleich als auf fremdem Eigenthume:—\n\ndarob entsetzte sich damals der Hund: denn Hunde glauben an Diebe und Gespenster. Und als ich wieder so heulen hörte, da erbarmte es mich abermals.\n\nWohin war jetzt Zwerg? und Thorweg? Und Spinne? Und alles Flüstern? Träumte ich denn? Wachte ich auf? Zwischen wilden Klippen stand ich mit Einem Male, allein, öde, im ödesten Mondscheine.\n\nAber da lag ein Mensch! Und da! Der Hund, springend, gesträubt, winselnd,—jetzt sah er mich kommen—da heulte er wieder, da schrie er:—hörte ich je einen Hund so Hülfe schrein?\n\nUnd, wahrlich, was ich sah, desgleichen sah ich nie. Einen jungen Hirten sah ich, sich windend, würgend, zuckend, verzerrten Antlitzes, dem eine schwarze schwere Schlange aus dem Munde hieng.\n\nSah ich je so viel Ekel und bleiches Grauen auf Einem Antlitze? Er hatte wohl geschlafen? Da kroch ihm die Schlange in den Schlund—da biss sie sich fest.\n\nMeine Hand riss die Schlange und riss:—umsonst! sie riss die Schlange nicht aus dem Schlunde. Da schrie es aus mir: „Beiss zu! Beiss zu!\n\nDen Kopf ab! Beiss zu!“—so schrie es aus mir, mein Grauen, mein Hass, mein Ekel, mein Erbarmen, all mein Gutes und Schlimmes schrie mit Einem Schrei aus mir.—\n\nIhr Kühnen um mich! Ihr Sucher, Versucher, und wer von euch mit listigen Segeln sich in unerforschte Meere einschiffte! Ihr Räthsel-Frohen!\n\nSo rathet mir doch das Räthsel, das ich damals schaute, so deutet mir doch das Gesicht des Einsamsten!\n\nDenn ein Gesicht war’s und ein Vorhersehn:— was sah ich damals im Gleichnisse? Und wer ist, der einst noch kommen muss?\n\nWer ist der Hirt, dem also die Schlange in den Schlund kroch? Wer ist der Mensch, dem also alles Schwerste, Schwärzeste in den Schlund kriechen wird?\n\n—Der Hirt aber biss, wie mein Schrei ihm rieth; er biss mit gutem Bisse! Weit weg spie er den Kopf der Schlange—: und sprang empor.—\n\nNicht mehr Hirt, nicht mehr Mensch,—ein Verwandelter, ein Umleuchteter, welcher lachte ! Niemals noch auf Erden lachte je ein Mensch, wie er lachte!\n\nOh meine Brüder, ich hörte ein Lachen, das keines Menschen Lachen war,—- und nun frisst ein Durst an mir, eine Sehnsucht, die nimmer stille wird.\n\nMeine Sehnsucht nach diesem Lachen frisst an mir: oh wie ertrage ich noch zu leben! Und wie ertrüge ich’s, jetzt zu sterben!—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0002","text":"„Halt! Zwerg! sprach ich. Ich! Oder du! Ich aber bin der Stärkere von uns Beiden—: du kennst meinen abgründlichen Gedanken nicht! Den —könntest du nicht tragen!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0003","text":"Da geschah, was mich leichter machte: denn der Zwerg sprang mir von der Schulter, der Neugierige! Und er hockte sich auf einen Stein vor mich hin. Es war aber gerade da ein Thorweg, wo wir hielten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0004","text":"„Siehe diesen Thorweg! Zwerg! sprach ich weiter: der hat zwei Gesichter. Zwei Wege kommen hier zusammen: die gieng noch Niemand zu Ende.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0005","text":"Diese lange Gasse zurück: die währt eine Ewigkeit. Und jene lange Gasse hinaus —das ist eine andre Ewigkeit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0006","text":"Sie widersprechen sich, diese Wege; sie stossen sich gerade vor den Kopf:—und hier, an diesem Thorwege, ist es, wo sie zusammen kommen. Der Name des Thorwegs steht oben geschrieben: „Augenblick“.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0007","text":"Aber wer Einen von ihnen weiter gienge—und immer weiter und immer ferner: glaubst du, Zwerg, dass diese Wege sich ewig widersprechen?“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0008","text":"„Alles Gerade lügt, murmelte verächtlich der Zwerg. Alle Wahrheit ist krumm, die Zeit selber ist ein Kreis.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0009","text":"„Du Geist der Schwere! sprach ich zürnend, mache dir es nicht zu leicht! Oder ich lasse dich hocken, wo du hockst, Lahmfuss,—und ich trug dich hoch !","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0010","text":"Siehe, sprach ich weiter, diesen Augenblick! Von diesem Thorwege Augenblick läuft eine lange ewige Gasse rückwärts hinter uns liegt eine Ewigkeit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0011","text":"Muss nicht, was laufen kann von allen Dingen, schon einmal diese Gasse gelaufen sein? Muss nicht, was geschehn kann von allen Dingen, schon einmal geschehn, gethan, vorübergelaufen sein?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0012","text":"Und wenn Alles schon dagewesen ist: was hältst du Zwerg von diesem Augenblick? Muss auch dieser Thorweg nicht schon—dagewesen sein?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0013","text":"Und sind nicht solchermaassen fest alle Dinge verknotet, dass dieser Augenblick alle kommenden Dinge nach sich zieht? Also —- sich selber noch?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0014","text":"Denn, was laufen kann von allen Dingen: auch in dieser langen Gasse hinaus — muss es einmal noch laufen!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0015","text":"Und diese langsame Spinne, die im Mondscheine kriecht, und dieser Mondschein selber, und ich und du im Thorwege, zusammen flüsternd, von ewigen Dingen flüsternd—müssen wir nicht Alle schon dagewesen sein?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0016","text":"—und wiederkommen und in jener anderen Gasse laufen, hinaus, vor uns, in dieser langen schaurigen Gasse—müssen wir nicht ewig wiederkommen?—“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0017","text":"Also redete ich, und immer leiser: denn ich fürchtete mich vor meinen eignen Gedanken und Hintergedanken. Da, plötzlich, hörte ich einen Hund nahe heulen .","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0018","text":"Hörte ich jemals einen Hund so heulen? Mein Gedanke lief zurück. Ja! Als ich Kind war, in fernster Kindheit:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0019","text":"—da hörte ich einen Hund so heulen. Und sah ihn auch, gesträubt, den Kopf nach Oben, zitternd, in stillster Mitternacht, wo auch Hunde an Gespenster glauben:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0020","text":"—also dass es mich erbarmte. Eben nämlich gieng der volle Mond, todtschweigsam, über das Haus, eben stand er still, eine runde Gluth,—still auf flachem Dache, gleich als auf fremdem Eigenthume:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0021","text":"darob entsetzte sich damals der Hund: denn Hunde glauben an Diebe und Gespenster. Und als ich wieder so heulen hörte, da erbarmte es mich abermals.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0022","text":"Wohin war jetzt Zwerg? und Thorweg? Und Spinne? Und alles Flüstern? Träumte ich denn? Wachte ich auf? Zwischen wilden Klippen stand ich mit Einem Male, allein, öde, im ödesten Mondscheine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0023","text":"Aber da lag ein Mensch! Und da! Der Hund, springend, gesträubt, winselnd,—jetzt sah er mich kommen—da heulte er wieder, da schrie er:—hörte ich je einen Hund so Hülfe schrein?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0024","text":"Und, wahrlich, was ich sah, desgleichen sah ich nie. Einen jungen Hirten sah ich, sich windend, würgend, zuckend, verzerrten Antlitzes, dem eine schwarze schwere Schlange aus dem Munde hieng.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0025","text":"Sah ich je so viel Ekel und bleiches Grauen auf Einem Antlitze? Er hatte wohl geschlafen? Da kroch ihm die Schlange in den Schlund—da biss sie sich fest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0026","text":"Meine Hand riss die Schlange und riss:—umsonst! sie riss die Schlange nicht aus dem Schlunde. Da schrie es aus mir: „Beiss zu! Beiss zu!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0027","text":"Den Kopf ab! Beiss zu!“—so schrie es aus mir, mein Grauen, mein Hass, mein Ekel, mein Erbarmen, all mein Gutes und Schlimmes schrie mit Einem Schrei aus mir.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0028","text":"Ihr Kühnen um mich! Ihr Sucher, Versucher, und wer von euch mit listigen Segeln sich in unerforschte Meere einschiffte! Ihr Räthsel-Frohen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0029","text":"So rathet mir doch das Räthsel, das ich damals schaute, so deutet mir doch das Gesicht des Einsamsten!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0030","text":"Denn ein Gesicht war’s und ein Vorhersehn:— was sah ich damals im Gleichnisse? Und wer ist, der einst noch kommen muss?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0031","text":"Wer ist der Hirt, dem also die Schlange in den Schlund kroch? Wer ist der Mensch, dem also alles Schwerste, Schwärzeste in den Schlund kriechen wird?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0032","text":"—Der Hirt aber biss, wie mein Schrei ihm rieth; er biss mit gutem Bisse! Weit weg spie er den Kopf der Schlange—: und sprang empor.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0033","text":"Nicht mehr Hirt, nicht mehr Mensch,—ein Verwandelter, ein Umleuchteter, welcher lachte ! Niemals noch auf Erden lachte je ein Mensch, wie er lachte!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0034","text":"Oh meine Brüder, ich hörte ein Lachen, das keines Menschen Lachen war,—- und nun frisst ein Durst an mir, eine Sehnsucht, die nimmer stille wird.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0035","text":"Meine Sehnsucht nach diesem Lachen frisst an mir: oh wie ertrage ich noch zu leben! Und wie ertrüge ich’s, jetzt zu sterben!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0036","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"23e33a355f5b2f57759c2f39d987279d83e10688016d4b05c3776adc6f5c6cc2","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182444212863Z_8b635bf3","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2.","source_sha256":"23e33a355f5b2f57759c2f39d987279d83e10688016d4b05c3776adc6f5c6cc2","sha256":"a11f4b086b6ac8eae3a48d4c0f2ec10fdda842882abf81d193586be7a3c75a80","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:24:44.212875+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0002","text":"“Stop! Dwarf! I said. I! Or you! But I am the stronger of us two—: you do not know my abysmal thought! That—you could not bear!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0003","text":"Then that happened which made me lighter: for the dwarf sprang from my shoulder, the curious one! And he squatted down upon a stone in front of me. But it was precisely there that there was a gateway where we halted.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0004","text":"“Behold this gateway! Dwarf! I went on: it has two faces. Two paths meet here: no one has yet gone them to their end.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0005","text":"This long lane backward: it lasts an eternity. And that long lane outward—that is another eternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0006","text":"They contradict one another, these paths; they collide head-on:—and here, at this gateway, is where they come together. The name of the gateway is written above: ‘Moment.’","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0007","text":"But if someone were to go farther along one of them—and ever farther and ever more distant: do you believe, Dwarf, that these paths eternally contradict one another?”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0008","text":"“Everything straight lies,” the dwarf muttered contemptuously. “All truth is crooked; time itself is a circle.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0009","text":"“You spirit of gravity!” I said angrily, “do not make it too easy for yourself! Or I shall leave you squatting where you squat, lamefoot,—and I carried you high!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0010","text":"Behold, I went on, this moment! From this gateway, Moment, a long eternal lane runs backward; behind us lies an eternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0011","text":"Must not whatever can run, of all things, have already run this lane once? Must not whatever can happen, of all things, have already happened, been done, passed by?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0012","text":"And if Everything has already been there: what do you think, Dwarf, of this moment? Must this gateway too not already—have been there?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0013","text":"And are not all things knotted together in such a manner that this moment draws all coming things after it? Thus—-itself too?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0014","text":"For whatever can run, of all things: even in this long lane outward—it must still run once!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0015","text":"And this slow spider which crawls in the moonlight, and this moonlight itself, and I and you in the gateway, whispering together, whispering of eternal things—must we not all have already been there?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0016","text":"—and return and run in that other lane, outward, before us, in this long terrible lane—must we not return eternally?—”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0017","text":"Thus I spoke, and ever more softly: for I was afraid of my own thoughts and afterthoughts. Then, suddenly, I heard a dog howling nearby.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0018","text":"Had I ever heard a dog howl thus? My thought ran back. Yes! When I was a child, in remotest childhood:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0019","text":"—then I heard a dog howl thus. And I saw it too, bristling, its head raised upward, trembling, in the stillest midnight, when even dogs believe in ghosts:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0020","text":"—so that I pitied it. For just then the full moon passed, deathly silent, over the house; just then it stood still, a round glow,—still upon the flat roof, as though upon чужой property:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0021","text":"at which the dog was then horrified: for dogs believe in thieves and ghosts. And when I heard such howling again, I pitied it once more.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0022","text":"Where now were dwarf? and gateway? And spider? And all whispering? Was I dreaming? Did I wake? Between wild cliffs I stood all at once, alone, desolate, in the most desolate moonlight.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0023","text":"But there lay a human being! And there! The dog, leaping, bristling, whimpering,—now it saw me coming—then it howled again, then it cried out:—had I ever heard a dog cry for help thus?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0024","text":"And, truly, what I saw, I had never seen the like of. I saw a young shepherd, writhing, choking, convulsing, with a distorted face, from whose mouth a black heavy snake hung.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0025","text":"Have I ever seen so much disgust and pale horror upon a single countenance? He must have been asleep? Then the snake crawled into his throat—then it bit fast.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0026","text":"My hand tore at the snake and tore:—in vain! it did not tear the snake out of his throat. Then it cried out of me: “Bite! Bite!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0027","text":"“Bite its head off! Bite!”—thus it cried out of me, my horror, my hatred, my disgust, my pity, all my good and evil cried out of me with a single cry.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0028","text":"You bold ones around me! You seekers, experimenters, and whoever among you embarked with cunning sails upon unexplored seas! You who rejoice in riddles!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0029","text":"Then solve for me the riddle that I beheld at that time, then interpret for me the vision of the most solitary one!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0030","text":"For it was a vision and a foreseeing:—what did I then see in parable? And who is he that must yet come one day?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0031","text":"Who is the shepherd into whose throat the snake thus crawled? Who is the human being into whose throat all that is heaviest, blackest will thus crawl?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0032","text":"—But the shepherd bit, as my cry advised him; he bit with a good bite! Far away he spat the head of the snake—: and sprang up.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0033","text":"No longer shepherd, no longer human being,—a transformed one, an illumined one, who laughed! Never yet on earth had any human being laughed as he laughed!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0034","text":"Oh my brothers, I heard a laughter that was no human laughter,—and now a thirst gnaws at me, a longing that will never be still.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0035","text":"My longing for this laughter gnaws at me: oh how can I still endure to live! And how could I endure it, to die now!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0036","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_3:P0036"]}],"source_digest":"e9a339fcd4942a44be1f87d9aa9ec29bdb25fabc0a063b2f52fa13d85a00d8b6","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Vision and the Riddle","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On the Vision and the Riddle · 2","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On the Vision and the Riddle","2."],"original_title":"2."}],"source_title_context":"Vom Gesicht und Räthsel","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom Gesicht und Räthsel · 2","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Vom Gesicht und Räthsel","2."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"2.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW","title":"On Involuntary Bliss","order":68,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDSW","text":"Mit solchen Räthseln und Bitternissen im Herzen fuhr Zarathustra über das Meer. Als er aber vier Tagereisen fern war von den glückseligen Inseln und von seinen Freunden, da hatte er allen seinen Schmerz überwunden—: siegreich und mit festen Füssen stand er wieder auf seinem Schicksal. Und damals redete Zarathustra also zu seinem frohlockenden Gewissen:\n\n„Allein bin ich wieder und will es sein, allein mit reinem Himmel und freiem Meere; und wieder ist Nachmittag um mich.\n\nDes Nachmittags fand ich zum ersten Male einst meine Freunde, des Nachmittags auch zum anderen Male:—zur Stunde, da alles Licht stiller wird.\n\nDenn was von Glück noch unterwegs ist zwischen Himmel und Erde, das sucht sich nun zur Herberge noch eine lichte Seele: vor Glück ist alles Licht jetzt stiller worden.\n\nOh Nachmittag meines Lebens! Einst stieg auch mein Glück zu Thale, dass es sich eine Herberge suche: da fand es diese offnen gastfreundlichen Seelen.\n\nOh Nachmittag meines Lebens! Was gab ich nicht hin, dass ich Eins hätte: diese lebendige Pflanzung meiner Gedanken und diess Morgenlicht meiner höchsten Hoffnung!\n\nGefährten suchte einst der Schaffende und Kinder seiner Hoffnung: und siehe, es fand sich, dass er sie nicht finden könne, es sei denn, er schaffe sie selber erst.\n\nAlso bin ich mitten in meinem Werke, zu meinen Kindern gehend und von ihnen kehrend: um seiner Kinder willen muss Zarathustra sich selbst vollenden.\n\nDenn von Grund aus liebt man nur sein Kind und Werk; und wo grosse Liebe zu sich selber ist, da ist sie der Schwangerschaft Wahrzeichen: so fand ich’s.\n\nNoch grünen mir meine Kinder in ihrem ersten Frühlinge, nahe bei einander stehend und gemeinsam von Winden geschüttelt, die Bäume meines Gartens und besten Erdreichs.\n\nUnd wahrlich! Wo solche Bäume bei einander stehn, da sind glückselige Inseln!\n\nAber einstmals will ich sie ausheben und einen jeden für sich allein stellen: dass er Einsamkeit lerne und Trotz und Vorsicht.\n\nKnorrig und gekrümmt und mit biegsamer Härte soll er mir dann am Meere dastehn, ein lebendiger Leuchtthurm unbesiegbaren Lebens.\n\nDort, wo die Stürme hinab in’s Meer stürzen, und des Gebirgs Rüssel Wasser trinkt, da soll ein jeder einmal seine Tag- und Nachtwachen haben, zu seiner Prüfung und Erkenntniss.\n\nErkannt und geprüft soll er werden, darauf, ob er meiner Art und Abkunft ist,—ob er eines langen Willens Herr sei, schweigsam, auch wenn er redet, und nachgebend also, dass er im Geben nimmt :—\n\n—dass er einst mein Gefährte werde und ein Mitschaffender und Mitfeiernder Zarathustra’s—: ein Solcher, der mir meinen Willen auf meine Tafeln schreibt: zu aller Dinge vollerer Vollendung.\n\nUnd um seinetwillen und seines Gleichen muss ich selber mich vollenden: darum weiche ich jetzt meinem Glücke aus und biete mich allem Unglücke an—zu meiner letzten Prüfung und Erkenntniss.\n\nUnd wahrlich, Zeit war’s, dass ich gierig; und des Wanderers Schatten und die längste Weile und die stillste Stunde—alle redeten mir zu: „es ist höchste Zeit!“\n\nDer Wind blies mir durch’s Schlüsselloch und sagte „Komm!“ Die Thür sprang mir listig auf und sagte „Geh!“\n\nAber ich lag angekettet an die Liebe zu meinen Kindern: das Begehren legte mir diese Schlinge, das Begehren nach Liebe, dass ich meiner Kinder Beute würde und mich an sie verlöre.\n\nBegehren—das heisst mir schon: mich verloren haben. Ich habe euch, meine Kinder! In diesem Haben soll Alles Sicherheit und Nichts Begehren sein.\n\nAber brütend lag die Sonne meiner Liebe auf mir, im eignen Safte kochte Zarathustra,—da flogen Schatten und Zweifel über mich weg.\n\nNach Frost und Winter gelüstete mich schon: „oh dass Frost und Winter mich wieder knacken und knirschen machten!“ seufzte ich:—da stiegen eisige Nebel aus mir auf.\n\nMeine Vergangenheit brach ihm Gräber, manch lebendig begrabner Schmerz wachte auf—: ausgeschlafen hatte er sich nur, versteckt in Leichen-Gewänder.\n\nAlso rief mir Alles in Zeichen zu: „es ist Zeit!“—Aber ich—hörte nicht: bis endlich mein Abgrund sich rührte und mein Gedanke mich biss.\n\nAch, abgründlicher Gedanke, der du mein Gedanke bist! Wann finde ich die Stärke, dich graben zu hören und nicht mehr zu zittern?\n\nBis zur Kehle hinauf klopft mir das Herz, wenn ich dich graben höre! Dein Schweigen noch will mich würgen, du abgründlich Schweigender!\n\nNoch wagte ich niemals, dich herauf zu rufen: genug schon, dass ich dich mit mir—trug! Noch war ich nicht stark genug zum letzten Löwen-Übermuthe und -Muthwillen.\n\nGenug des Furchtbaren war mir immer schon deine Schwere: aber einst soll ich noch die Stärke finden und die Löwen-Stimme, die dich herauf ruft!\n\nWenn ich mich dessen erst überwunden habe, dann will ich mich auch des Grösseren noch überwinden; und ein Sieg soll meiner Vollendung Siegel sein!—\n\nInzwischen treibe ich noch auf ungewissen Meeren; der Zufall schmeichelt mir, der glattzüngige; vorwärts und rückwärts schaue ich—, noch schaue ich kein Ende.\n\nNoch kam mir die Stunde meines letzten Kampfes nicht,—oder kommt sie wohl mir eben? Wahrlich, mit tückischer Schönheit schaut mich rings Meer und Leben an!\n\nOh Nachmittag meines Lebens! Oh Glück vor Abend! Oh Hafen auf hoher See! Oh Friede im Ungewissen! Wie misstraue ich euch Allen!\n\nWahrlich, misstrauisch bin ich gegen eure tückische Schönheit! Dem Liebenden gleiche ich, der allzusammtenem Lächeln misstraut.\n\nWie er die Geliebteste vor sich her stösst, zärtlich noch in seiner Härte, der Eifersüchtige—, also stosse ich diese selige Stunde vor mir her.\n\nHinweg mit dir, du selige Stunde! Mit dir kam mir eine Seligkeit wider Willen! Willig zu meinem tiefsten Schmerze stehe ich hier:—zur Unzeit kamst du!\n\nHinweg mit dir, du selige Stunde! Lieber nimm Herberge dort—bei meinen Kindern! Eile! und segne sie vor Abend noch mit meinem Glücke!\n\nDa naht schon der Abend: die Sonne sinkt. Dahin—mein Glück!—“\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra. Und er wartete auf sein Unglück die ganze Nacht: aber er wartete umsonst. Die Nacht blieb hell und still, und das Glück selber kam ihm immer näher und näher. Gegen Morgen aber lachte Zarathustra zu seinem Herzen und sagte spöttisch: „das Glück läuft mir nach. Das kommt davon, dass ich nicht den Weibern nachlaufe. Das Glück aber ist ein Weib.“","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0001","text":"Mit solchen Räthseln und Bitternissen im Herzen fuhr Zarathustra über das Meer. Als er aber vier Tagereisen fern war von den glückseligen Inseln und von seinen Freunden, da hatte er allen seinen Schmerz überwunden—: siegreich und mit festen Füssen stand er wieder auf seinem Schicksal. Und damals redete Zarathustra also zu seinem frohlockenden Gewissen:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0002","text":"„Allein bin ich wieder und will es sein, allein mit reinem Himmel und freiem Meere; und wieder ist Nachmittag um mich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0003","text":"Des Nachmittags fand ich zum ersten Male einst meine Freunde, des Nachmittags auch zum anderen Male:—zur Stunde, da alles Licht stiller wird.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0004","text":"Denn was von Glück noch unterwegs ist zwischen Himmel und Erde, das sucht sich nun zur Herberge noch eine lichte Seele: vor Glück ist alles Licht jetzt stiller worden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0005","text":"Oh Nachmittag meines Lebens! Einst stieg auch mein Glück zu Thale, dass es sich eine Herberge suche: da fand es diese offnen gastfreundlichen Seelen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0006","text":"Oh Nachmittag meines Lebens! Was gab ich nicht hin, dass ich Eins hätte: diese lebendige Pflanzung meiner Gedanken und diess Morgenlicht meiner höchsten Hoffnung!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0007","text":"Gefährten suchte einst der Schaffende und Kinder seiner Hoffnung: und siehe, es fand sich, dass er sie nicht finden könne, es sei denn, er schaffe sie selber erst.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0008","text":"Also bin ich mitten in meinem Werke, zu meinen Kindern gehend und von ihnen kehrend: um seiner Kinder willen muss Zarathustra sich selbst vollenden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0009","text":"Denn von Grund aus liebt man nur sein Kind und Werk; und wo grosse Liebe zu sich selber ist, da ist sie der Schwangerschaft Wahrzeichen: so fand ich’s.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0010","text":"Noch grünen mir meine Kinder in ihrem ersten Frühlinge, nahe bei einander stehend und gemeinsam von Winden geschüttelt, die Bäume meines Gartens und besten Erdreichs.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0011","text":"Und wahrlich! Wo solche Bäume bei einander stehn, da sind glückselige Inseln!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0012","text":"Aber einstmals will ich sie ausheben und einen jeden für sich allein stellen: dass er Einsamkeit lerne und Trotz und Vorsicht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0013","text":"Knorrig und gekrümmt und mit biegsamer Härte soll er mir dann am Meere dastehn, ein lebendiger Leuchtthurm unbesiegbaren Lebens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0014","text":"Dort, wo die Stürme hinab in’s Meer stürzen, und des Gebirgs Rüssel Wasser trinkt, da soll ein jeder einmal seine Tag- und Nachtwachen haben, zu seiner Prüfung und Erkenntniss.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0015","text":"Erkannt und geprüft soll er werden, darauf, ob er meiner Art und Abkunft ist,—ob er eines langen Willens Herr sei, schweigsam, auch wenn er redet, und nachgebend also, dass er im Geben nimmt :—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0016","text":"—dass er einst mein Gefährte werde und ein Mitschaffender und Mitfeiernder Zarathustra’s—: ein Solcher, der mir meinen Willen auf meine Tafeln schreibt: zu aller Dinge vollerer Vollendung.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0017","text":"Und um seinetwillen und seines Gleichen muss ich selber mich vollenden: darum weiche ich jetzt meinem Glücke aus und biete mich allem Unglücke an—zu meiner letzten Prüfung und Erkenntniss.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0018","text":"Und wahrlich, Zeit war’s, dass ich gierig; und des Wanderers Schatten und die längste Weile und die stillste Stunde—alle redeten mir zu: „es ist höchste Zeit!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0019","text":"Der Wind blies mir durch’s Schlüsselloch und sagte „Komm!“ Die Thür sprang mir listig auf und sagte „Geh!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0020","text":"Aber ich lag angekettet an die Liebe zu meinen Kindern: das Begehren legte mir diese Schlinge, das Begehren nach Liebe, dass ich meiner Kinder Beute würde und mich an sie verlöre.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0021","text":"Begehren—das heisst mir schon: mich verloren haben. Ich habe euch, meine Kinder! In diesem Haben soll Alles Sicherheit und Nichts Begehren sein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0022","text":"Aber brütend lag die Sonne meiner Liebe auf mir, im eignen Safte kochte Zarathustra,—da flogen Schatten und Zweifel über mich weg.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0023","text":"Nach Frost und Winter gelüstete mich schon: „oh dass Frost und Winter mich wieder knacken und knirschen machten!“ seufzte ich:—da stiegen eisige Nebel aus mir auf.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0024","text":"Meine Vergangenheit brach ihm Gräber, manch lebendig begrabner Schmerz wachte auf—: ausgeschlafen hatte er sich nur, versteckt in Leichen-Gewänder.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0025","text":"Also rief mir Alles in Zeichen zu: „es ist Zeit!“—Aber ich—hörte nicht: bis endlich mein Abgrund sich rührte und mein Gedanke mich biss.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0026","text":"Ach, abgründlicher Gedanke, der du mein Gedanke bist! Wann finde ich die Stärke, dich graben zu hören und nicht mehr zu zittern?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0027","text":"Bis zur Kehle hinauf klopft mir das Herz, wenn ich dich graben höre! Dein Schweigen noch will mich würgen, du abgründlich Schweigender!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0028","text":"Noch wagte ich niemals, dich herauf zu rufen: genug schon, dass ich dich mit mir—trug! Noch war ich nicht stark genug zum letzten Löwen-Übermuthe und -Muthwillen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0029","text":"Genug des Furchtbaren war mir immer schon deine Schwere: aber einst soll ich noch die Stärke finden und die Löwen-Stimme, die dich herauf ruft!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0030","text":"Wenn ich mich dessen erst überwunden habe, dann will ich mich auch des Grösseren noch überwinden; und ein Sieg soll meiner Vollendung Siegel sein!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0031","text":"Inzwischen treibe ich noch auf ungewissen Meeren; der Zufall schmeichelt mir, der glattzüngige; vorwärts und rückwärts schaue ich—, noch schaue ich kein Ende.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0032","text":"Noch kam mir die Stunde meines letzten Kampfes nicht,—oder kommt sie wohl mir eben? Wahrlich, mit tückischer Schönheit schaut mich rings Meer und Leben an!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0033","text":"Oh Nachmittag meines Lebens! Oh Glück vor Abend! Oh Hafen auf hoher See! Oh Friede im Ungewissen! Wie misstraue ich euch Allen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0034","text":"Wahrlich, misstrauisch bin ich gegen eure tückische Schönheit! Dem Liebenden gleiche ich, der allzusammtenem Lächeln misstraut.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0035","text":"Wie er die Geliebteste vor sich her stösst, zärtlich noch in seiner Härte, der Eifersüchtige—, also stosse ich diese selige Stunde vor mir her.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0036","text":"Hinweg mit dir, du selige Stunde! Mit dir kam mir eine Seligkeit wider Willen! Willig zu meinem tiefsten Schmerze stehe ich hier:—zur Unzeit kamst du!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0037","text":"Hinweg mit dir, du selige Stunde! Lieber nimm Herberge dort—bei meinen Kindern! Eile! und segne sie vor Abend noch mit meinem Glücke!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0038","text":"Da naht schon der Abend: die Sonne sinkt. Dahin—mein Glück!—“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0039","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra. Und er wartete auf sein Unglück die ganze Nacht: aber er wartete umsonst. Die Nacht blieb hell und still, und das Glück selber kam ihm immer näher und näher. Gegen Morgen aber lachte Zarathustra zu seinem Herzen und sagte spöttisch: „das Glück läuft mir nach. Das kommt davon, dass ich nicht den Weibern nachlaufe. Das Glück aber ist ein Weib.“","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"9ac06e9005438db3241051c0ad1769b4d82621a1111aa08e1dd24c86647dd78c","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182520018587Z_22bacccf","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On Involuntary Bliss","source_sha256":"9ac06e9005438db3241051c0ad1769b4d82621a1111aa08e1dd24c86647dd78c","sha256":"f1aba49f128fd99a7835177500a2e26bf73e587f5d340c48215688540e3a9cd6","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:25:20.018597+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0001","text":"With such riddles and bitterness in his heart Zarathustra sailed over the sea. But when he was four days’ journeys distant from the blessed isles and from his friends, he had overcome all his pain—: victorious, and with firm feet, he stood once more upon his destiny. And then Zarathustra spoke thus to his rejoicing conscience:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0002","text":"“Alone am I again, and I will be so, alone with pure sky and free sea; and once more it is afternoon around me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0003","text":"“In the afternoon I once found my friends for the first time, in the afternoon also for the second time:—at the hour when all light grows quieter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0004","text":"“For whatever happiness is still on its way between heaven and earth now seeks out a bright soul for its lodging: out of happiness all light has now grown quieter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0005","text":"“O afternoon of my life! Once too my happiness descended into the valley, seeking itself a lodging: there it found these open, hospitable souls.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0006","text":"“O afternoon of my life! What did I not give away that I might have One thing: this living planting of my thoughts and this morning-light of my highest hope!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0007","text":"“Once the creator sought companions and children of his hope: and behold, it turned out that he could not find them, unless he first created them himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0008","text":"“Thus I am in the midst of my work, going to my children and returning from them: for the sake of his children Zarathustra must perfect himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0009","text":"“For at bottom one loves only one’s child and work; and where there is great love for oneself, there it is the sign of pregnancy: thus I found it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0010","text":"“My children still grow green for me in their first spring, standing close together and shaken together by winds, the trees of my garden and best soil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0011","text":"“And verily! Where such trees stand together, there are blessed isles!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0012","text":"“But one day I will dig them up and set each one by itself: that it may learn solitude and defiance and caution.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0013","text":"“Gnarled and bent and with pliant hardness it shall then stand for me by the sea, a living beacon of invincible life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0014","text":"“There, where the storms plunge down into the sea, and the trunk of the mountain drinks water, there each one shall one day keep his watches by day and night, for his testing and knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0015","text":"“Known and tested he shall become, as to whether he is of my kind and lineage,—whether he be master of a long will, silent even when he speaks, and yielding in such a way that he takes in giving:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0016","text":"“—that he may one day become my companion and a fellow-creator and fellow-celebrant of Zarathustra—: one who writes my will for me upon my tablets: for the fuller perfection of all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0017","text":"“And for his sake and the sake of his like I must myself perfect myself: therefore I now evade my happiness and offer myself to every misfortune—for my last testing and knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0018","text":"“And verily, it was time that I should go; and the wanderer’s shadow and the longest tedium and the stillest hour—all spoke to me: ‘it is high time!’","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0019","text":"“The wind blew through my keyhole and said ‘Come!’ The door sprang open for me cunningly and said ‘Go!’","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0020","text":"“But I lay chained to love for my children: desire laid this noose upon me, the desire for love, that I might become my children’s prey and lose myself in them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0021","text":"“Desire—that already means to me: having lost myself. I have you, my children! In this having let everything be security and nothing desire.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0022","text":"“But the sun of my love lay brooding upon me, Zarathustra boiled in his own juice,—then shadows and doubts flew over me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0023","text":"“I already longed for frost and winter: ‘oh that frost and winter might again make me crack and crunch!’ I sighed:—then icy mists rose out of me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0024","text":"“My past broke open graves for it, many a pain buried alive awoke—: it had merely slept itself out, hidden in corpse-garments.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0025","text":"Thus everything called out to me in signs: “it is time!”—But I—I did not hear: until at last my abyss stirred and my thought bit me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0026","text":"Ah, abysmal thought, you who are my thought! When shall I find the strength to hear you digging and no longer tremble?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0027","text":"My heart pounds up to my throat when I hear you digging! Even your silence wishes to strangle me, you abysmally silent one!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0028","text":"Never yet have I dared to call you up: it was enough already that I—carried you with me! I was not yet strong enough for the final lion’s exuberance and wantonness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0029","text":"Your weight has always been terrible enough for me: but one day I shall yet find the strength and the lion’s voice that calls you up!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0030","text":"Once I have overcome myself in this, then I shall overcome myself in what is greater still; and a victory shall be the seal of my completion!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0031","text":"Meanwhile I still drift upon uncertain seas; chance, the smooth-tongued one, flatters me; I look forwards and backwards—, I still see no end.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0032","text":"The hour of my final battle has not yet come to me,—or is it perhaps coming to me now? Truly, with treacherous beauty sea and life look upon me all around!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0033","text":"O afternoon of my life! O happiness before evening! O harbour on the high seas! O peace amid uncertainty! How I mistrust you all!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0034","text":"Truly, I am mistrustful of your treacherous beauty! I resemble the lover who mistrusts every too-alluring smile.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0035","text":"As the jealous man pushes his most beloved before him, tender still in his harshness—, so I push this blissful hour before me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0036","text":"Away with you, blissful hour! With you a happiness against my will came to me! Willing for my deepest pain I stand here:—you came untimely!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0037","text":"Away with you, blissful hour! Rather take lodging there—with my children! Hasten! and bless them still before evening with my happiness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0038","text":"Now evening is already drawing near: the sun is sinking. Away—my happiness!—”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0039","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra. And he waited for his misfortune the whole night through: but he waited in vain. The night remained bright and still, and happiness itself came ever nearer and nearer to him. But toward morning Zarathustra laughed at his heart and said mockingly: “happiness is running after me. That comes from my not running after women. Happiness, however, is a woman.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDSW:P0039"]}],"source_digest":"345fa95e828a9be2e9f750f4159b151340bd27678da9167e6f7be0564cd9d12d","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von der Seligkeit wider Willen"}],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"Von der Seligkeit wider Willen","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0","title":"Before Sunrise","order":69,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VSA0","text":"Oh Himmel über mir, du Reiner! Tiefer! Du Licht-Abgrund! Dich schauend schaudere ich vor göttlichen Begierden.\n\nIn deine Höhe mich zu werfen—das ist meine Tiefe! In deine Reinheit mich zu bergen—das ist meine Unschuld!\n\nDen Gott verhüllt seine Schönheit: so verbirgst du deine Sterne. Du redest nicht: so kündest du mir deine Weisheit.\n\nStumm über brausendem Meere bist du heut mir aufgegangen, deine Liebe und deine Scham redet Offenbarung zu meiner brausenden Seele.\n\nDass du schön zu mir kamst, verhüllt in deine Schönheit, dass du stumm zu mir sprichst, offenbar in deiner Weisheit:\n\nOh wie erriethe ich nicht alles Schamhafte deiner Seele! Vor der Sonne kamst du zu mir, dem Einsamsten.\n\nWir sind Freunde von Anbeginn: uns ist Gram und Grauen und Grund gemeinsam; noch die Sonne ist uns gemeinsam.\n\nWir reden nicht zu einander, weil wir zu Vieles wissen—: wir schweigen uns an, wir lächeln uns unser Wissen zu.\n\nBist du nicht das Licht zu meinem Feuer? Hast du nicht die Schwester-Seele zu meiner Einsicht?\n\nZusammen lernten wir Alles; zusammen lernten wir über uns zu uns selber aufsteigen und wolkenlos lächeln:—\n\n—wolkenlos hinab lächeln aus lichten Augen und aus meilenweiter Ferne, wenn unter uns Zwang und Zweck und Schuld wie Regen dampfen.\n\nUnd wanderte ich allein: wes hungerte meine Seele in Nächten und Irr-Pfaden? Und stieg ich Berge, wen suchte ich je, wenn nicht dich, auf Bergen?\n\nUnd all mein Wandern und Bergsteigen: eine Noth war’s nur und ein Behelf des Unbeholfenen:— fliegen allein will mein ganzer Wille, in dich hinein fliegen!\n\nUnd wen hasste ich mehr, als ziehende Wolken und Alles, was dich befleckt? Und meinen eignen Hass hasste ich noch, weil er dich befleckte!\n\nDen ziehenden Wolken bin ich gram, diesen schleichenden Raub-Katzen: sie nehmen dir und mir, was uns gemein ist,—das ungeheure unbegrenzte Ja- und Amen-sagen.\n\nDiesen Mittlern und Mischern sind wir gram, den ziehenden Wolken: diesen Halb- und Halben, welche weder segnen lernten, noch von Grund aus fluchen.\n\nLieber will ich noch unter verschlossnem Himmel in der Tonne sitzen, lieber ohne Himmel im Abgrund sitzen, als dich, Licht-Himmel, mit Zieh-Wolken befleckt sehn!\n\nUnd oft gelüstete mich, sie mit zackichten Blitz-Golddrähten festzuheften, dass ich, gleich dem Donner, auf ihrem Kessel-Bauche die Pauke schlüge:—\n\n—ein zorniger Paukenschläger, weil sie mir dein Ja! und Amen! rauben, du Himmel über mir, du Reiner! Lichter! Du Licht-Abgrund!—weil sie dir mein Ja! und Amen! rauben.\n\nDenn lieber noch will ich Lärm und Donner und Wetter-Flüche, als diese bedächtige zweifelnde Katzen-Ruhe; und auch unter Menschen hasse ich am besten alle Leisetreter und Halb- und Halben und zweifelnde, zögernde Zieh-Wolken.\n\nUnd „wer nicht segnen kann, der soll fluchen lernen !“—diese helle Lehre fiel mir aus hellem Himmel, dieser Stern steht auch noch in schwarzen Nächten an meinem Himmel.\n\nIch aber bin ein Segnender und ein Ja-sager, wenn du nur um mich bist, du Reiner! Lichter! Du Licht-Abgrund!—in alle Abgründe trage ich da noch mein segnendes Ja-sagen.\n\nZum Segnenden bin ich worden und zum Ja-sagenden: und dazu rang ich lange und war ein Ringer, dass ich einst die Hände frei bekäme zum Segnen.\n\nDas aber ist mein Segnen: über jedwedem Ding als sein eigener Himmel stehn, als sein rundes Dach, seine azurne Glocke und ewige Sicherheit: und selig ist, wer also segnet!\n\nDenn alle Dinge sind getauft am Borne der Ewigkeit und jenseits von Gut und Böse; Gut und Böse selber aber sind nur Zwischenschatten und feuchte Trübsale und Zieh-Wolken.\n\nWahrlich, ein Segnen ist es und kein Lästern, wenn ich lehre: „über allen Dingen steht der Himmel Zufall, der Himmel Unschuld, der Himmel Ohngefähr, der Himmel Übermuth.“\n\n„Von Ohngefähr“—das ist der älteste Adel der Welt, den gab ich allen Dingen zurück, ich erlöste sie von der Knechtschaft unter dem Zwecke.\n\nDiese Freiheit und Himmels-Heiterkeit stellte ich gleich azurner Glocke über alle Dinge, als ich lehrte, dass über ihnen und durch sie kein „ewiger Wille“ —will.\n\nDiesen Übermuth und diese Narrheit stellte ich an die Stelle jenes Willens, als ich lehrte: „bei Allem ist Eins unmöglich—Vernünftigkeit!“\n\nEin Wenig Vernunft zwar, ein Same der Weisheit zerstreut von Stern zu Stern,—dieser Sauerteig ist allen Dingen eingemischt: um der Narrheit willen ist Weisheit allen Dingen eingemischt!\n\nEin Wenig Weisheit ist schon möglich; aber diese selige Sicherheit fand ich an allen Dingen: dass sie lieber noch auf den Füssen des Zufalls— tanzen .\n\nOh Himmel über mir, du Reiner! Hoher! Das ist mir nun deine Reinheit, dass es keine ewige Vernunft-Spinne und -Spinnennetze giebt:—\n\n—dass du mir ein Tanzboden bist für göttliche Zufälle, dass du mir ein Göttertisch bist für göttliche Würfel und Würfelspieler!—\n\nDoch du erröthest? Sprach ich Unaussprechbares? Lästerte ich, indem ich dich segnen wollte?\n\nOder ist es die Scham zu Zweien, welche dich erröthen machte?—Heissest du mich gehn und schweigen, weil nun—der Tag kommt?\n\nDie Welt ist tief—: und tiefer als je der Tag gedacht hat. Nicht Alles darf vor dem Tage Worte haben. Aber der Tag kommt: so scheiden wir nun!\n\nOh Himmel über mir, du Schamhafter! Glühender! Oh du mein Glück vor Sonnen-Aufgang! Der Tag kommt: so scheiden wir nun!—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0001","text":"Oh Himmel über mir, du Reiner! Tiefer! Du Licht-Abgrund! Dich schauend schaudere ich vor göttlichen Begierden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0002","text":"In deine Höhe mich zu werfen—das ist meine Tiefe! In deine Reinheit mich zu bergen—das ist meine Unschuld!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0003","text":"Den Gott verhüllt seine Schönheit: so verbirgst du deine Sterne. Du redest nicht: so kündest du mir deine Weisheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0004","text":"Stumm über brausendem Meere bist du heut mir aufgegangen, deine Liebe und deine Scham redet Offenbarung zu meiner brausenden Seele.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0005","text":"Dass du schön zu mir kamst, verhüllt in deine Schönheit, dass du stumm zu mir sprichst, offenbar in deiner Weisheit:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0006","text":"Oh wie erriethe ich nicht alles Schamhafte deiner Seele! Vor der Sonne kamst du zu mir, dem Einsamsten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0007","text":"Wir sind Freunde von Anbeginn: uns ist Gram und Grauen und Grund gemeinsam; noch die Sonne ist uns gemeinsam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0008","text":"Wir reden nicht zu einander, weil wir zu Vieles wissen—: wir schweigen uns an, wir lächeln uns unser Wissen zu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0009","text":"Bist du nicht das Licht zu meinem Feuer? Hast du nicht die Schwester-Seele zu meiner Einsicht?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0010","text":"Zusammen lernten wir Alles; zusammen lernten wir über uns zu uns selber aufsteigen und wolkenlos lächeln:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0011","text":"—wolkenlos hinab lächeln aus lichten Augen und aus meilenweiter Ferne, wenn unter uns Zwang und Zweck und Schuld wie Regen dampfen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0012","text":"Und wanderte ich allein: wes hungerte meine Seele in Nächten und Irr-Pfaden? Und stieg ich Berge, wen suchte ich je, wenn nicht dich, auf Bergen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0013","text":"Und all mein Wandern und Bergsteigen: eine Noth war’s nur und ein Behelf des Unbeholfenen:— fliegen allein will mein ganzer Wille, in dich hinein fliegen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0014","text":"Und wen hasste ich mehr, als ziehende Wolken und Alles, was dich befleckt? Und meinen eignen Hass hasste ich noch, weil er dich befleckte!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0015","text":"Den ziehenden Wolken bin ich gram, diesen schleichenden Raub-Katzen: sie nehmen dir und mir, was uns gemein ist,—das ungeheure unbegrenzte Ja- und Amen-sagen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0016","text":"Diesen Mittlern und Mischern sind wir gram, den ziehenden Wolken: diesen Halb- und Halben, welche weder segnen lernten, noch von Grund aus fluchen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0017","text":"Lieber will ich noch unter verschlossnem Himmel in der Tonne sitzen, lieber ohne Himmel im Abgrund sitzen, als dich, Licht-Himmel, mit Zieh-Wolken befleckt sehn!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0018","text":"Und oft gelüstete mich, sie mit zackichten Blitz-Golddrähten festzuheften, dass ich, gleich dem Donner, auf ihrem Kessel-Bauche die Pauke schlüge:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0019","text":"—ein zorniger Paukenschläger, weil sie mir dein Ja! und Amen! rauben, du Himmel über mir, du Reiner! Lichter! Du Licht-Abgrund!—weil sie dir mein Ja! und Amen! rauben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0020","text":"Denn lieber noch will ich Lärm und Donner und Wetter-Flüche, als diese bedächtige zweifelnde Katzen-Ruhe; und auch unter Menschen hasse ich am besten alle Leisetreter und Halb- und Halben und zweifelnde, zögernde Zieh-Wolken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0021","text":"Und „wer nicht segnen kann, der soll fluchen lernen !“—diese helle Lehre fiel mir aus hellem Himmel, dieser Stern steht auch noch in schwarzen Nächten an meinem Himmel.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0022","text":"Ich aber bin ein Segnender und ein Ja-sager, wenn du nur um mich bist, du Reiner! Lichter! Du Licht-Abgrund!—in alle Abgründe trage ich da noch mein segnendes Ja-sagen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0023","text":"Zum Segnenden bin ich worden und zum Ja-sagenden: und dazu rang ich lange und war ein Ringer, dass ich einst die Hände frei bekäme zum Segnen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0024","text":"Das aber ist mein Segnen: über jedwedem Ding als sein eigener Himmel stehn, als sein rundes Dach, seine azurne Glocke und ewige Sicherheit: und selig ist, wer also segnet!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0025","text":"Denn alle Dinge sind getauft am Borne der Ewigkeit und jenseits von Gut und Böse; Gut und Böse selber aber sind nur Zwischenschatten und feuchte Trübsale und Zieh-Wolken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0026","text":"Wahrlich, ein Segnen ist es und kein Lästern, wenn ich lehre: „über allen Dingen steht der Himmel Zufall, der Himmel Unschuld, der Himmel Ohngefähr, der Himmel Übermuth.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0027","text":"„Von Ohngefähr“—das ist der älteste Adel der Welt, den gab ich allen Dingen zurück, ich erlöste sie von der Knechtschaft unter dem Zwecke.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0028","text":"Diese Freiheit und Himmels-Heiterkeit stellte ich gleich azurner Glocke über alle Dinge, als ich lehrte, dass über ihnen und durch sie kein „ewiger Wille“ —will.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0029","text":"Diesen Übermuth und diese Narrheit stellte ich an die Stelle jenes Willens, als ich lehrte: „bei Allem ist Eins unmöglich—Vernünftigkeit!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0030","text":"Ein Wenig Vernunft zwar, ein Same der Weisheit zerstreut von Stern zu Stern,—dieser Sauerteig ist allen Dingen eingemischt: um der Narrheit willen ist Weisheit allen Dingen eingemischt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0031","text":"Ein Wenig Weisheit ist schon möglich; aber diese selige Sicherheit fand ich an allen Dingen: dass sie lieber noch auf den Füssen des Zufalls— tanzen .","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0032","text":"Oh Himmel über mir, du Reiner! Hoher! Das ist mir nun deine Reinheit, dass es keine ewige Vernunft-Spinne und -Spinnennetze giebt:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0033","text":"—dass du mir ein Tanzboden bist für göttliche Zufälle, dass du mir ein Göttertisch bist für göttliche Würfel und Würfelspieler!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0034","text":"Doch du erröthest? Sprach ich Unaussprechbares? Lästerte ich, indem ich dich segnen wollte?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0035","text":"Oder ist es die Scham zu Zweien, welche dich erröthen machte?—Heissest du mich gehn und schweigen, weil nun—der Tag kommt?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0036","text":"Die Welt ist tief—: und tiefer als je der Tag gedacht hat. Nicht Alles darf vor dem Tage Worte haben. Aber der Tag kommt: so scheiden wir nun!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0037","text":"Oh Himmel über mir, du Schamhafter! Glühender! Oh du mein Glück vor Sonnen-Aufgang! Der Tag kommt: so scheiden wir nun!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0038","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"fc7506f7342580c471715a20c09535d7463b7a7acb1bbe29d290481cfa92e505","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182540307011Z_b3fbbcc9","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Before Sunrise","source_sha256":"fc7506f7342580c471715a20c09535d7463b7a7acb1bbe29d290481cfa92e505","sha256":"790a65a2fbd1b670e70f2429e6378eaee27f8c7fddffff1f70eb9727d73162bc","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:25:40.307021+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0001","text":"O heaven above me, thou Pure One! Deep! Thou abyss of light! Beholding thee, I tremble with divine desires.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0002","text":"To cast myself into thy height—that is my depth! To conceal myself in thy purity—that is my innocence!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0003","text":"God is veiled by his beauty: thus thou concealest thy stars. Thou dost not speak: thus thou proclaimest thy wisdom to me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0004","text":"Mute above the roaring sea thou hast risen for me today; thy love and thy shame speak revelation to my roaring soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0005","text":"That thou camest to me beautifully, veiled in thy beauty, that thou speakest to me mutely, manifest in thy wisdom:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0006","text":"O how could I not have divined all that is shamefaced in thy soul! Thou camest to me before the sun, to me the loneliest one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0007","text":"We have been friends from the beginning: grief and dread and ground are common to us; even the sun is common to us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0008","text":"We do not speak to one another, because we know too much—: we are silent to one another, we smile our knowledge to one another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0009","text":"Art thou not the light for my fire? Hast thou not the sister-soul for my insight?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0010","text":"Together we learned Everything; together we learned to rise above ourselves to ourselves and smile cloudlessly:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0011","text":"—to smile cloudlessly downward out of bright eyes and from miles-wide distance, when beneath us compulsion and purpose and guilt steam like rain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0012","text":"And if I wandered alone: for what did my soul hunger in nights and paths of error? And if I climbed mountains, whom did I ever seek, if not thee, upon mountains?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0013","text":"And all my wandering and mountain-climbing: it was but a distress and a makeshift of the unhandy one:—my entire will wants only to fly, to fly into thee!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0014","text":"And whom did I hate more than passing clouds and all that defiles thee? And I hated even my own hatred, because it defiled thee!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0015","text":"I am wrathful toward the passing clouds, these creeping beasts of prey: they take from thee and me what is common to us,—the monstrous, boundless saying of Yes and Amen.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0016","text":"We are wrathful toward these mediators and mixers, the passing clouds: these half-and-halves, who have learned neither to bless nor to curse from the ground up.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0017","text":"Rather would I still sit in the tub beneath a closed heaven, rather sit in the abyss without heaven, than see thee, heaven of light, defiled with passing clouds!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0018","text":"And often I longed to pin them fast with jagged lightning-golden wires, so that, like thunder, I might beat the drum upon their cauldron-belly:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0019","text":"—an angry drummer, because they rob me of thy Yes! and Amen!, thou heaven above me, thou Pure One! Bright One! Thou abyss of light!—because they rob thee of my Yes! and Amen!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0020","text":"For I would rather still have noise and thunder and weather-curses than this deliberate, doubting feline stillness; and among men too I hate most of all every soft-treader and half-and-half and doubting, hesitant passing cloud.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0021","text":"And “he who cannot bless should learn to curse!”—this bright teaching fell to me from bright heaven; this star still stands in black nights in my heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0022","text":"But I am one who blesses and one who says Yes, if only thou art about me, thou Pure One! Bright One! Thou abyss of light!—then into all abysses I still carry my blessing Yes-saying.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0023","text":"I have become one who blesses and one who says Yes: and for this I struggled long and was a struggler, that I might one day get my hands free for blessing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0024","text":"But this is my blessing: to stand over every single thing as its own heaven, as its round roof, its azure bell and eternal security: and blessed is he who blesses thus!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0025","text":"For all things are baptized at the font of eternity and beyond good and evil; but good and evil themselves are merely intermediate shadows and damp afflictions and drifting clouds.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0026","text":"Verily, it is a blessing and not a blasphemy when I teach: “above all things stands the heaven Chance, the heaven Innocence, the heaven Haphazard, the heaven Overmirth.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0027","text":"“By haphazard”—that is the world's oldest nobility; I gave it back to all things, I redeemed them from servitude under purpose.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0028","text":"This freedom and heavenly serenity I set, like an azure bell, over all things, when I taught that no “eternal will”—wills over them and through them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0029","text":"This overmirth and this folly I set in the place of that will, when I taught: “with Everything one thing is impossible—Rationality!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0030","text":"A little reason indeed, a seed of wisdom scattered from star to star,—this leaven is mixed into all things: for the sake of folly wisdom is mixed into all things!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0031","text":"A little wisdom is already possible; but this blessed certainty I found in all things: that they would rather still—dance upon the feet of chance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0032","text":"O heaven above me, thou Pure One! Lofty One! This is now thy purity to me, that there is no eternal reason-spider and spider-webs:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0033","text":"—that thou art to me a dancing-floor for divine chances, that thou art to me a table of gods for divine dice and dice-players!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0034","text":"Yet thou blushest? Did I speak the unspeakable? Did I blaspheme when I meant to bless thee?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0035","text":"Or is it the shame of two that made thee blush?—Dost thou bid me go and be silent because now—the day comes?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0036","text":"The world is deep—: and deeper than day has ever thought. Not everything may have words before the day. But the day comes: thus we now part!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0037","text":"O heaven above me, thou Bashful One! Glowing One! O thou my happiness before sunrise! The day comes: thus we now part!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0038","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VSA0:P0038"]}],"source_digest":"40b71a4ab9d32863fde110895519f9942b4aa1c1832a3e479af39aac569bf502","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Vor Sonnen-Aufgang"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Vor Sonnen-Aufgang","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_4","title":"1.","order":70,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_1C_4","text":"## 1.\n\nAls Zarathustra wieder auf dem festen Lande war, gieng er nicht stracks auf sein Gebirge und seine Höhle los, sondern that viele Wege und Fragen und erkundete diess und das, also, dass er von sich selber im Scherze sagte: „siehe einen Fluss, der in vielen Windungen zurück zur Quelle fliesst!“ Denn er wollte in Erfahrung bringen, was sich inzwischen mit dem Menschen zugetragen habe: ob er grösser oder kleiner geworden sei. Und ein Mal sah er eine Reihe neuer Häuser; da wunderte er sich und sagte:\n\n„Was bedeuten diese Häuser? Wahrlich, keine grosse Seele stellte sie hin, sich zum Gleichnisse!\n\nNahm wohl ein blödes Kind sie aus seiner Spielschachtel? Dass doch ein anderes Kind sie wieder in seine Schachtel thäte!\n\nUnd diese Stuben und Kammern: können Männer da aus- und eingehen? Gemacht dünken sie mich für Seiden-Puppen; oder für Naschkatzen, die auch wohl an sich naschen lassen.“\n\nUnd Zarathustra blieb stehn und dachte nach. Endlich sagte er betrübt: „Es ist Alles kleiner geworden!\n\nÜberall sehe ich niedrigere Thore: wer meiner Art ist, geht da wohl noch hindurch, aber—er muss sich bücken!\n\nOh wann komme ich wieder in meine Heimat, wo ich mich nicht mehr bücken muss—nicht mehr bücken muss vor den Kleinen!“—Und Zarathustra seufzte und blickte in die Ferne.—\n\nDesselbigen Tages aber redete er seine Rede über die verkleinernde Tugend.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0002","text":"Als Zarathustra wieder auf dem festen Lande war, gieng er nicht stracks auf sein Gebirge und seine Höhle los, sondern that viele Wege und Fragen und erkundete diess und das, also, dass er von sich selber im Scherze sagte: „siehe einen Fluss, der in vielen Windungen zurück zur Quelle fliesst!“ Denn er wollte in Erfahrung bringen, was sich inzwischen mit dem Menschen zugetragen habe: ob er grösser oder kleiner geworden sei. Und ein Mal sah er eine Reihe neuer Häuser; da wunderte er sich und sagte:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0003","text":"„Was bedeuten diese Häuser? Wahrlich, keine grosse Seele stellte sie hin, sich zum Gleichnisse!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0004","text":"Nahm wohl ein blödes Kind sie aus seiner Spielschachtel? Dass doch ein anderes Kind sie wieder in seine Schachtel thäte!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0005","text":"Und diese Stuben und Kammern: können Männer da aus- und eingehen? Gemacht dünken sie mich für Seiden-Puppen; oder für Naschkatzen, die auch wohl an sich naschen lassen.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0006","text":"Und Zarathustra blieb stehn und dachte nach. Endlich sagte er betrübt: „Es ist Alles kleiner geworden!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0007","text":"Überall sehe ich niedrigere Thore: wer meiner Art ist, geht da wohl noch hindurch, aber—er muss sich bücken!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0008","text":"Oh wann komme ich wieder in meine Heimat, wo ich mich nicht mehr bücken muss—nicht mehr bücken muss vor den Kleinen!“—Und Zarathustra seufzte und blickte in die Ferne.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0009","text":"Desselbigen Tages aber redete er seine Rede über die verkleinernde Tugend.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"d8658f54292262d3e2908757eaf7340a11ec7323dc432b4e1d9654b54db000e7","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182545637307Z_be003f0b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1.","source_sha256":"d8658f54292262d3e2908757eaf7340a11ec7323dc432b4e1d9654b54db000e7","sha256":"56f7c4900e47bcc43a86b8645f2196c4d30c413f9684d5e06fd8e258e36a0b64","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:25:45.637335+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0002","text":"When Zarathustra was once again on firm land, he did not make straight for his mountains and his cave, but made many journeys and inquiries and explored this and that, so that he said of himself in jest: “behold a river that flows back to its source in many windings!” For he wished to learn what had meanwhile happened to man: whether he had become greater or smaller. And once he saw a row of new houses; then he marvelled and said:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0003","text":"“What do these houses mean? Verily, no great soul set them up as its likeness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0004","text":"Did perhaps a feeble-minded child take them out of its toy-box? If only another child would put them back into its box!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0005","text":"And these rooms and chambers: can men go out and in there? They seem to me made for silk dolls; or for sweet-tooths, who are also happy to let themselves be nibbled at.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0006","text":"And Zarathustra stood still and reflected. At last he said sorrowfully: “Everything has become smaller!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0007","text":"Everywhere I see lower gates: whoever is of my kind may still pass through there, but—he must stoop!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0008","text":"Oh, when shall I again come to my homeland, where I no longer have to stoop—no longer have to stoop before the small!”—And Zarathustra sighed and looked into the distance.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0009","text":"But on that same day he spoke his discourse on the virtue that makes small.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_4:P0009"]}],"source_digest":"d91f6c2b9ef5f9b1271a9e0d607d872c48b3e5979e13597b51a068242bd659c0","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Diminishing Virtue","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On the Diminishing Virtue · 1","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On the Diminishing Virtue","1."],"original_title":"1."}],"source_title_context":"Von der verkleinernden Tugend","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von der verkleinernden Tugend · 1","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von der verkleinernden Tugend","1."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"1.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4","title":"2.","order":71,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_2C_4","text":"## 2.\n\nIch gehe durch diess Volk und halte meine Augen offen: sie vergeben mir es nicht, dass ich auf ihre Tugenden nicht neidisch bin.\n\nSie beissen nach mir, weil ich zu ihnen sage: für kleine Leute sind kleine Tugenden nöthig—und weil es mir hart eingeht, dass kleine Leute nöthig sind!\n\nNoch gleiche ich dem Hahn hier auf fremdem Gehöfte, nach dem auch die Hennen beissen; doch darob bin ich diesen Hennen nicht ungut.\n\nIch bin höflich gegen sie wie gegen alles kleine Ärgerniss; gegen das Kleine stachlicht zu sein dünkt mich eine Weisheit für Igel.\n\nSie reden Alle von mir, wenn sie Abends um’s Feuer sitzen,—sie reden von mir, aber Niemand denkt—an mich!\n\nDiess ist die neue Stille, die ich lernte: ihr Lärm um mich breitet einen Mantel über meine Gedanken.\n\nSie lärmen unter einander: „was will uns diese düstere Wolke? sehen wir zu, dass sie uns nicht eine Seuche bringe!“\n\nUnd jüngst riss ein Weib sein Kind an sich, das zu mir wollte: „nehmt die Kinder weg! schrie es; solche Augen versengen Kinder-Seelen.“\n\nSie husten, wenn ich rede: sie meinen, Husten sei ein Einwand gegen starke Winde,—sie errathen Nichts vom Brausen meines Glückes!\n\n„Wir haben noch keine Zeit für Zarathustra“—so wenden sie ein; aber was liegt an einer Zeit, die für Zarathustra „keine Zeit hat“?\n\nUnd wenn sie gar mich rühmen: wie könnte ich wohl auf ihrem Ruhme einschlafen? Ein Stachel-Gürtel ist mir ihr Lob: es kratzt mich noch, wenn ich es von mir thue.\n\nUnd auch das lernte ich unter ihnen: der Lobende stellt sich, als gäbe er zurück, in Wahrheit aber will er mehr beschenkt sein!\n\nFragt meinen Fuss, ob ihm ihre Lob- und Lock-Weise gefällt! Wahrlich, nach solchem Takt und Tiktak mag er weder tanzen, noch stille stehn.\n\nZur kleinen Tugend möchten sie mich locken und loben; zum Tiktak des kleinen Glücks möchten sie meinen Fuss überreden.\n\nIch gehe durch diess Volk und halte die Augen offen: sie sind kleiner geworden und werden immer kleiner:—das aber macht ihre Lehre von Glück und Tugend.\n\nSie sind nämlich auch in der Tugend bescheiden—denn sie wollen Behagen. Mit Behagen aber verträgt sich nur die bescheidene Tugend.\n\nWohl lernen auch sie auf ihre Art Schreiten und Vorwärts-Schreiten: das heisse ich ihr Humpeln —. Damit werden sie jedem zum Anstosse, der Eile hat.\n\nUnd Mancher von ihnen geht vorwärts und blickt dabei zurück, mit versteiftem Nacken: dem renne ich gern wider den Leib.\n\nFuss und Augen sollen nicht lügen, noch sich einander Lügen strafen. Aber es ist viel Lügnerei bei den kleinen Leuten.\n\nEinige von ihnen wollen, aber die Meisten werden nur gewollt. Einige von ihnen sind ächt, aber die Meisten sind schlechte Schauspieler.\n\nEs giebt Schauspieler wider Wissen unter ihnen und Schauspieler wider Willen—, die Ächten sind immer selten, sonderlich die ächten Schauspieler.\n\nDes Mannes ist hier wenig: darum vermännlichen sich ihre Weiber. Denn nur wer Mannes genug ist, wird im Weibe das Weib —erlösen.\n\nUnd diese Heuchelei fand ich unter ihnen am schlimmsten: dass auch Die, welche befehlen, die Tugenden Derer heucheln, welche dienen.\n\n„Ich diene, du dienst, wir dienen“ —so betet hier auch die Heuchelei der Herrschenden,—und wehe, wenn der erste Herr nur der erste Diener ist!\n\nAch, auch in ihre Heucheleien verflog sich wohl meines Auges Neugier; und gut errieth ich all ihr Fliegen-Glück und ihr Summen um besonnte Fensterscheiben.\n\nSoviel Güte, soviel Schwäche sehe ich. Soviel Gerechtigkeit und Mitleiden, soviel Schwäche.\n\nRund, rechtlich und gütig sind sie mit einander, wie Sandkörnchen rund, rechtlich und gütig mit Sandkörnchen sind.\n\nBescheiden ein kleines Glück umarmen—das heissen sie „Ergebung“! und dabei schielen sie bescheiden schon nach einem neuen kleinen Glücke aus.\n\nSie wollen im Grunde einfältiglich Eins am meisten: dass ihnen Niemand wehe thue. So kommen sie jedermann zuvor und thun ihm wohl.\n\nDiess aber ist Feigheit : ob es schon „Tugend“ heisst.—\n\nUnd wenn sie einmal rauh reden, diese kleinen Leute: ich höre darin nur ihre Heiserkeit,—jeder Windzug nämlich macht sie heiser.\n\nKlug sind sie, ihre Tugenden haben kluge Finger. Aber ihnen fehlen die Fäuste, ihre Finger wissen nicht, sich hinter Fäuste zu verkriechen.\n\nTugend ist ihnen das, was bescheiden und zahm macht: damit machten sie den Wolf zum Hunde und den Menschen selber zu des Menschen bestem Hausthiere.\n\n„Wir setzten unsern Stuhl in die Mitte —das sagt mir ihr Schmunzeln—und ebenso weit weg von sterbenden Fechtern wie von vergnügten Säuen.“\n\nDiess aber ist— Mittelmässigkeit : ob es schon Mässigkeit heisst.—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0002","text":"Ich gehe durch diess Volk und halte meine Augen offen: sie vergeben mir es nicht, dass ich auf ihre Tugenden nicht neidisch bin.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0003","text":"Sie beissen nach mir, weil ich zu ihnen sage: für kleine Leute sind kleine Tugenden nöthig—und weil es mir hart eingeht, dass kleine Leute nöthig sind!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0004","text":"Noch gleiche ich dem Hahn hier auf fremdem Gehöfte, nach dem auch die Hennen beissen; doch darob bin ich diesen Hennen nicht ungut.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0005","text":"Ich bin höflich gegen sie wie gegen alles kleine Ärgerniss; gegen das Kleine stachlicht zu sein dünkt mich eine Weisheit für Igel.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0006","text":"Sie reden Alle von mir, wenn sie Abends um’s Feuer sitzen,—sie reden von mir, aber Niemand denkt—an mich!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0007","text":"Diess ist die neue Stille, die ich lernte: ihr Lärm um mich breitet einen Mantel über meine Gedanken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0008","text":"Sie lärmen unter einander: „was will uns diese düstere Wolke? sehen wir zu, dass sie uns nicht eine Seuche bringe!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0009","text":"Und jüngst riss ein Weib sein Kind an sich, das zu mir wollte: „nehmt die Kinder weg! schrie es; solche Augen versengen Kinder-Seelen.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0010","text":"Sie husten, wenn ich rede: sie meinen, Husten sei ein Einwand gegen starke Winde,—sie errathen Nichts vom Brausen meines Glückes!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0011","text":"„Wir haben noch keine Zeit für Zarathustra“—so wenden sie ein; aber was liegt an einer Zeit, die für Zarathustra „keine Zeit hat“?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0012","text":"Und wenn sie gar mich rühmen: wie könnte ich wohl auf ihrem Ruhme einschlafen? Ein Stachel-Gürtel ist mir ihr Lob: es kratzt mich noch, wenn ich es von mir thue.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0013","text":"Und auch das lernte ich unter ihnen: der Lobende stellt sich, als gäbe er zurück, in Wahrheit aber will er mehr beschenkt sein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0014","text":"Fragt meinen Fuss, ob ihm ihre Lob- und Lock-Weise gefällt! Wahrlich, nach solchem Takt und Tiktak mag er weder tanzen, noch stille stehn.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0015","text":"Zur kleinen Tugend möchten sie mich locken und loben; zum Tiktak des kleinen Glücks möchten sie meinen Fuss überreden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0016","text":"Ich gehe durch diess Volk und halte die Augen offen: sie sind kleiner geworden und werden immer kleiner:—das aber macht ihre Lehre von Glück und Tugend.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0017","text":"Sie sind nämlich auch in der Tugend bescheiden—denn sie wollen Behagen. Mit Behagen aber verträgt sich nur die bescheidene Tugend.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0018","text":"Wohl lernen auch sie auf ihre Art Schreiten und Vorwärts-Schreiten: das heisse ich ihr Humpeln —. Damit werden sie jedem zum Anstosse, der Eile hat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0019","text":"Und Mancher von ihnen geht vorwärts und blickt dabei zurück, mit versteiftem Nacken: dem renne ich gern wider den Leib.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0020","text":"Fuss und Augen sollen nicht lügen, noch sich einander Lügen strafen. Aber es ist viel Lügnerei bei den kleinen Leuten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0021","text":"Einige von ihnen wollen, aber die Meisten werden nur gewollt. Einige von ihnen sind ächt, aber die Meisten sind schlechte Schauspieler.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0022","text":"Es giebt Schauspieler wider Wissen unter ihnen und Schauspieler wider Willen—, die Ächten sind immer selten, sonderlich die ächten Schauspieler.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0023","text":"Des Mannes ist hier wenig: darum vermännlichen sich ihre Weiber. Denn nur wer Mannes genug ist, wird im Weibe das Weib —erlösen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0024","text":"Und diese Heuchelei fand ich unter ihnen am schlimmsten: dass auch Die, welche befehlen, die Tugenden Derer heucheln, welche dienen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0025","text":"„Ich diene, du dienst, wir dienen“ —so betet hier auch die Heuchelei der Herrschenden,—und wehe, wenn der erste Herr nur der erste Diener ist!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0026","text":"Ach, auch in ihre Heucheleien verflog sich wohl meines Auges Neugier; und gut errieth ich all ihr Fliegen-Glück und ihr Summen um besonnte Fensterscheiben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0027","text":"Soviel Güte, soviel Schwäche sehe ich. Soviel Gerechtigkeit und Mitleiden, soviel Schwäche.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0028","text":"Rund, rechtlich und gütig sind sie mit einander, wie Sandkörnchen rund, rechtlich und gütig mit Sandkörnchen sind.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0029","text":"Bescheiden ein kleines Glück umarmen—das heissen sie „Ergebung“! und dabei schielen sie bescheiden schon nach einem neuen kleinen Glücke aus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0030","text":"Sie wollen im Grunde einfältiglich Eins am meisten: dass ihnen Niemand wehe thue. So kommen sie jedermann zuvor und thun ihm wohl.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0031","text":"Diess aber ist Feigheit : ob es schon „Tugend“ heisst.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0032","text":"Und wenn sie einmal rauh reden, diese kleinen Leute: ich höre darin nur ihre Heiserkeit,—jeder Windzug nämlich macht sie heiser.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0033","text":"Klug sind sie, ihre Tugenden haben kluge Finger. Aber ihnen fehlen die Fäuste, ihre Finger wissen nicht, sich hinter Fäuste zu verkriechen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0034","text":"Tugend ist ihnen das, was bescheiden und zahm macht: damit machten sie den Wolf zum Hunde und den Menschen selber zu des Menschen bestem Hausthiere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0035","text":"„Wir setzten unsern Stuhl in die Mitte —das sagt mir ihr Schmunzeln—und ebenso weit weg von sterbenden Fechtern wie von vergnügten Säuen.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0036","text":"Diess aber ist— Mittelmässigkeit : ob es schon Mässigkeit heisst.—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"9589f34113630cd4e97669a6648b043a1786f4ce6eb5e2cedd399a05b9e78cc2","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182602469470Z_aa3f2e4b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2.","source_sha256":"9589f34113630cd4e97669a6648b043a1786f4ce6eb5e2cedd399a05b9e78cc2","sha256":"60f53658a42d2f15637fff5a484dd1d760b7a3affb56d471bf3e10db4eaccde2","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:26:02.469486+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0002","text":"I go through this people and keep my eyes open: they do not forgive me for not being envious of their virtues.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0003","text":"They bite at me because I tell them: small people need small virtues—and because it goes hard with me that small people are necessary!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0004","text":"I still resemble the cock here on a strange farmyard, at whom the hens too bite; yet for that reason I bear these hens no ill will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0005","text":"I am courteous towards them as towards every small annoyance; to be prickly towards what is small seems to me a wisdom for hedgehogs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0006","text":"They all speak of me when they sit round the fire in the evening,—they speak of me, but no one thinks—of me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0007","text":"This is the new silence that I learned: their noise about me spreads a cloak over my thoughts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0008","text":"They make a noise amongst themselves: “what does this gloomy cloud want of us? let us see to it that it does not bring us a pestilence!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0009","text":"And recently a woman snatched her child to herself, which wanted to come to me: “take the children away! she cried; such eyes scorch children’s souls.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0010","text":"They cough when I speak: they think coughing is an objection to strong winds,—they divine nothing of the roaring of my happiness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0011","text":"“We have no time yet for Zarathustra”—thus they object; but what does a time matter that “has no time” for Zarathustra?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0012","text":"And if they actually praise me: how could I possibly fall asleep upon their praise? Their praise is a belt of thorns to me: it still scratches me when I take it off.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0013","text":"And this too I learned among them: the praiser pretends as though he were giving back, but in truth he wants to be given more!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0014","text":"Ask my foot whether it likes their manner of praising and enticing! Verily, to such beat and ticktock it wishes neither to dance nor to stand still.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0015","text":"They would entice and praise me into small virtue; they would persuade my foot to the ticktock of small happiness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0016","text":"I go through this people and keep my eyes open: they have become smaller and become ever smaller:—but this is caused by their teaching of happiness and virtue.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0017","text":"For they are modest even in virtue—because they want comfort. But only modest virtue is compatible with comfort.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0018","text":"To be sure, in their own way they too learn to stride and stride forward: that I call their limping —. By this they become an obstacle to everyone who is in haste.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0019","text":"And many a one among them goes forward and looks backward at the same time, with stiffened neck: against him I like to run headlong.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0020","text":"Foot and eyes should not lie, nor convict one another of lying. But there is much lying among the small people.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0021","text":"Some of them will, but most are merely willed. Some of them are genuine, but most are bad actors.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0022","text":"There are actors among them against their knowledge and actors against their will—, the genuine ones are always rare, especially the genuine actors.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0023","text":"There is little of the man here: therefore their women become mannish. For only he who is enough of a man will—in woman—redeem the woman.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0024","text":"And this hypocrisy I found worst among them: that even those who command hypocritically assume the virtues of those who serve.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0025","text":"“I serve, you serve, we serve”—thus too does the hypocrisy of the rulers pray here,—and woe if the first lord is only the first servant!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0026","text":"Ah, into their hypocrisies too the curiosity of my eye perhaps strayed; and well did I guess all their fly-happiness and their buzzing around sunlit window-panes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0027","text":"So much goodness, so much weakness do I see. So much justice and pity, so much weakness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0028","text":"Round, lawful, and good are they with one another, as grains of sand are round, lawful, and good with grains of sand.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0029","text":"Modestly to embrace a little happiness—that they call “resignation”! and in doing so they already cast modest sidelong glances after a new little happiness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0030","text":"At bottom they most simply want one thing: that no one should hurt them. Thus they anticipate everyone and do him good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0031","text":"But this is cowardice: even though it be called “virtue.”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0032","text":"And when, once in a while, these little people speak roughly: in it I hear only their hoarseness,—for every draught of wind makes them hoarse.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0033","text":"They are clever; their virtues have clever fingers. But they lack fists; their fingers do not know how to hide behind fists.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0034","text":"Virtue is for them that which makes modest and tame: thereby they made the wolf into a dog and man himself into man’s best domestic animal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0035","text":"“We have set our chair in the middle—thus their smirking tells me—and equally far from dying fencers as from delighted sows.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0036","text":"But this is—mediocrity: even though it be called moderation.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_4:P0036"]}],"source_digest":"dc72a944ea184f0b05b26609d38d7ab31ae8cfd2fbf73cbbb299b226f8ce24a4","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Diminishing Virtue","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On the Diminishing Virtue · 2","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On the Diminishing Virtue","2."],"original_title":"2."}],"source_title_context":"Von der verkleinernden Tugend","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von der verkleinernden Tugend · 2","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von der verkleinernden Tugend","2."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"2.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3","title":"3.","order":72,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_3C_3","text":"## 3.\n\nIch gehe durch diess Volk und lasse manches Wort fallen: aber sie wissen weder zu nehmen noch zu behalten.\n\nSie wundern sich, dass ich nicht kam, auf Lüste und Laster zu lästern; und wahrlich, ich kam auch nicht, dass ich vor Taschendieben warnte!\n\nSie wundern sich, dass ich nicht bereit bin, ihre Klugheit noch zu witzigen und zu spitzigen: als ob sie noch nicht genug der Klüglinge hätten, deren Stimme mir gleich Schieferstiften kritzelt!\n\nUnd wenn ich rufe: „Flucht allen feigen Teufeln in euch, die gerne winseln und Hände falten und anbeten möchten“ : so rufen sie: „Zarathustra ist gottlos“.\n\nUnd sonderlich rufen es ihre Lehrer der Ergebung—; aber gerade ihnen liebe ich’s, in das Ohr zu schrein: Ja! Ich bin Zarathustra, der Gottlose!\n\nDiese Lehrer der Ergebung! Überall hin, wo es klein und krank und grindig ist, kriechen sie, gleich Läusen; und nur mein Ekel hindert mich, sie zu knacken.\n\nWohlan! Diess ist meine Predigt für ihre Ohren: ich bin Zarathustra, der Gottlose, der da spricht „wer ist gottloser denn ich, dass ich mich seiner Unterweisung freue?“\n\nIch bin Zarathustra, der Gottlose: wo finde ich Meines-Gleichen? Und alle Die sind Meines-Gleichen, die sich selber ihren Willen geben und alle Ergebung von sich abthun.\n\nIch bin Zarathustra, der Gottlose: ich koche mir noch jeden Zufall in meinem Topfe. Und erst, wenn er da gar gekocht ist, heisse ich ihn willkommen, als meine Speise.\n\nUnd wahrlich, mancher Zufall kam herrisch zu mir: aber herrischer noch sprach zu ihm mein Wille ,—da lag er schon bittend auf den Knieen—\n\n—bittend, dass er Herberge finde und Herz bei mir, und schmeichlerisch zuredend: „sieh doch; oh Zarathustra, wie nur Freund zu Freunde kommt!“—\n\nDoch was rede ich, wo Niemand meine Ohren hat! Und so will ich es hinaus in alle Winde rufen:\n\nIhr werdet immer kleiner, ihr kleinen Leute! Ihr bröckelt ab, ihr Behaglichen! Ihr geht mir noch zu Grunde—\n\n—an euren vielen kleinen Tugenden, an eurem vielen kleinen Unterlassen, an eurer vielen kleinen Ergebung!\n\nZu viel schonend, zu viel nachgebend: so ist euer Erdreich! Aber dass ein Baum gross werde, dazu will er um harte Felsen harte Wurzeln schlagen!\n\nAuch was ihr unterlasse, webt am Gewebe aller Menschen-Zukunft; auch euer Nichts ist ein Spinnennetz und eine Spinne, die von der Zukunft Blute lebt.\n\nUnd wenn ihr nehmt, so ist es wie stehlen, ihr kleinen Tugendhaften; aber noch unter Schelmen spricht die Ehre : „man soll nur stehlen, wo man nicht rauben kann.“\n\n„Es giebt sich“—das ist auch eine Lehre der Ergebung. Aber ich sage euch, ihr Behaglichen: es nimmt sich und wird immer mehr noch von euch nehmen!\n\nAch, dass ihr alles halbe Wollen von euch abthätet und entschlossen würdet zur Trägheit wie zur That!\n\nAch, dass ihr mein Wort verstündet: „thut immerhin, was ihr wollt,—aber seid erst Solche, die wollen können !“\n\n„Liebt immerhin euren Nächsten gleich euch,—aber seid mir erst solche, die sich selber lieben —\n\n—mit der grossen Liebe lieben, mit der grossen Verachtung lieben!“ Also spricht Zarathustra, der Gottlose.—\n\nDoch was rede ich, wo Niemand meine Ohren hat! Es ist hier noch eine Stunde zu früh für mich.\n\nMein eigner Vorläufer bin ich unter diesem Volke, mein eigner Hahnen-Ruf durch dunkle Gassen.\n\nAber ihre Stunde kommt! Und es kommt auch die meine! Stündlich werden sie kleiner, ärmer, unfruchtbarer,—armes Kraut! armes Erdreich!\n\nUnd bald sollen sie mir dastehn wie dürres Gras und Steppe, und wahrlich! ihrer selber müde—und mehr, als nach Wasser, nach Feuer lechzend!\n\nOh gesegnete Stunde des Blitzes! Oh Geheimniss vor Mittag!—Laufende Feuer will ich einst noch aus ihnen machen und Verkünder mit Flammen-Zungen:—\n\n—verkünden sollen sie einst noch mit Flammen-Zungen: Er kommt, er ist nahe, der grosse Mittag!\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0001","text":"## 3.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0002","text":"Ich gehe durch diess Volk und lasse manches Wort fallen: aber sie wissen weder zu nehmen noch zu behalten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0003","text":"Sie wundern sich, dass ich nicht kam, auf Lüste und Laster zu lästern; und wahrlich, ich kam auch nicht, dass ich vor Taschendieben warnte!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0004","text":"Sie wundern sich, dass ich nicht bereit bin, ihre Klugheit noch zu witzigen und zu spitzigen: als ob sie noch nicht genug der Klüglinge hätten, deren Stimme mir gleich Schieferstiften kritzelt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0005","text":"Und wenn ich rufe: „Flucht allen feigen Teufeln in euch, die gerne winseln und Hände falten und anbeten möchten“ : so rufen sie: „Zarathustra ist gottlos“.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0006","text":"Und sonderlich rufen es ihre Lehrer der Ergebung—; aber gerade ihnen liebe ich’s, in das Ohr zu schrein: Ja! Ich bin Zarathustra, der Gottlose!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0007","text":"Diese Lehrer der Ergebung! Überall hin, wo es klein und krank und grindig ist, kriechen sie, gleich Läusen; und nur mein Ekel hindert mich, sie zu knacken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0008","text":"Wohlan! Diess ist meine Predigt für ihre Ohren: ich bin Zarathustra, der Gottlose, der da spricht „wer ist gottloser denn ich, dass ich mich seiner Unterweisung freue?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0009","text":"Ich bin Zarathustra, der Gottlose: wo finde ich Meines-Gleichen? Und alle Die sind Meines-Gleichen, die sich selber ihren Willen geben und alle Ergebung von sich abthun.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0010","text":"Ich bin Zarathustra, der Gottlose: ich koche mir noch jeden Zufall in meinem Topfe. Und erst, wenn er da gar gekocht ist, heisse ich ihn willkommen, als meine Speise.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0011","text":"Und wahrlich, mancher Zufall kam herrisch zu mir: aber herrischer noch sprach zu ihm mein Wille ,—da lag er schon bittend auf den Knieen—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0012","text":"—bittend, dass er Herberge finde und Herz bei mir, und schmeichlerisch zuredend: „sieh doch; oh Zarathustra, wie nur Freund zu Freunde kommt!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0013","text":"Doch was rede ich, wo Niemand meine Ohren hat! Und so will ich es hinaus in alle Winde rufen:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0014","text":"Ihr werdet immer kleiner, ihr kleinen Leute! Ihr bröckelt ab, ihr Behaglichen! Ihr geht mir noch zu Grunde—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0015","text":"—an euren vielen kleinen Tugenden, an eurem vielen kleinen Unterlassen, an eurer vielen kleinen Ergebung!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0016","text":"Zu viel schonend, zu viel nachgebend: so ist euer Erdreich! Aber dass ein Baum gross werde, dazu will er um harte Felsen harte Wurzeln schlagen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0017","text":"Auch was ihr unterlasse, webt am Gewebe aller Menschen-Zukunft; auch euer Nichts ist ein Spinnennetz und eine Spinne, die von der Zukunft Blute lebt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0018","text":"Und wenn ihr nehmt, so ist es wie stehlen, ihr kleinen Tugendhaften; aber noch unter Schelmen spricht die Ehre : „man soll nur stehlen, wo man nicht rauben kann.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0019","text":"„Es giebt sich“—das ist auch eine Lehre der Ergebung. Aber ich sage euch, ihr Behaglichen: es nimmt sich und wird immer mehr noch von euch nehmen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0020","text":"Ach, dass ihr alles halbe Wollen von euch abthätet und entschlossen würdet zur Trägheit wie zur That!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0021","text":"Ach, dass ihr mein Wort verstündet: „thut immerhin, was ihr wollt,—aber seid erst Solche, die wollen können !“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0022","text":"„Liebt immerhin euren Nächsten gleich euch,—aber seid mir erst solche, die sich selber lieben —","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0023","text":"—mit der grossen Liebe lieben, mit der grossen Verachtung lieben!“ Also spricht Zarathustra, der Gottlose.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0024","text":"Doch was rede ich, wo Niemand meine Ohren hat! Es ist hier noch eine Stunde zu früh für mich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0025","text":"Mein eigner Vorläufer bin ich unter diesem Volke, mein eigner Hahnen-Ruf durch dunkle Gassen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0026","text":"Aber ihre Stunde kommt! Und es kommt auch die meine! Stündlich werden sie kleiner, ärmer, unfruchtbarer,—armes Kraut! armes Erdreich!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0027","text":"Und bald sollen sie mir dastehn wie dürres Gras und Steppe, und wahrlich! ihrer selber müde—und mehr, als nach Wasser, nach Feuer lechzend!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0028","text":"Oh gesegnete Stunde des Blitzes! Oh Geheimniss vor Mittag!—Laufende Feuer will ich einst noch aus ihnen machen und Verkünder mit Flammen-Zungen:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0029","text":"—verkünden sollen sie einst noch mit Flammen-Zungen: Er kommt, er ist nahe, der grosse Mittag!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0030","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"19fa4a2035cd810eebc7a261ebb001d68d7a94911f5bc73caca62534ffecaa8d","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182616839829Z_21842cf2","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"3.","source_sha256":"19fa4a2035cd810eebc7a261ebb001d68d7a94911f5bc73caca62534ffecaa8d","sha256":"97d6407e4fffa9a9dd8840295cb103ed76d94ac1eb286273c252b0ab787f1d73","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:26:16.839870+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0001","text":"## 3.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0002","text":"I go through this people and let many a word fall: but they know neither how to take nor how to keep.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0003","text":"They are astonished that I did not come to rail against pleasures and vices; and verily, neither did I come to warn against pickpockets!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0004","text":"They are astonished that I am not ready to make their cleverness yet more witty and pointed: as though they had not already enough of the cleverlings whose voices scratch upon me like slate pencils!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0005","text":"And when I cry: “A curse upon all the cowardly devils in you, who would like to whine and fold their hands and worship”: then they cry: “Zarathustra is godless.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0006","text":"And it is especially their teachers of resignation who cry it—but precisely to them I love to shout in the ear: Yes! I am Zarathustra, the godless one!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0007","text":"These teachers of resignation! Wherever there is anything small and sick and scabby, there they creep, like lice; and only my disgust prevents me from crushing them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0008","text":"Well then! This is my sermon for their ears: I am Zarathustra, the godless one, who says “who is more godless than I, that I should rejoice in his instruction?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0009","text":"I am Zarathustra, the godless one: where do I find my equal? And all those are my equals who give themselves their own will and cast off from themselves all resignation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0010","text":"I am Zarathustra, the godless one: I still cook every chance occurrence in my pot. And only when it has been thoroughly cooked there do I bid it welcome, as my food.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0011","text":"And verily, many a chance occurrence came masterfully to me: but still more masterfully did my will speak to it,—and there it already lay, pleading, upon its knees—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0012","text":"—pleading that it might find lodging and heart with me, and flatteringly persuading: “look now; O Zarathustra, how only friend comes to friend!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0013","text":"But what am I saying, where no one has my ears! And so I will cry it out into all the winds:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0014","text":"You are always becoming smaller, you little people! You are crumbling away, you comfortable ones! You will yet perish on me—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0015","text":"—through your many little virtues, through your many little abstentions, through your many little resignations!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0016","text":"Too yielding, too accommodating: such is your soil! But for a tree to grow great, it must strike hard roots around hard rocks!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0017","text":"Even what you refrain from doing weaves at the fabric of all human future; even your nothing is a spider's web and a spider that lives on the blood of the future.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0018","text":"And when you take, it is like stealing, you little virtuous ones; but even among knaves honour says: “one should steal only where one cannot rob.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0019","text":"“It gives itself”—that too is a teaching of resignation. But I tell you, you comfortable ones: it takes itself and will take ever more from you!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0020","text":"Ah, if only you would cast off from yourselves all half-willing and become as resolute for indolence as for action!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0021","text":"Ah, if only you understood my word: “do whatever you will,—but first be such as can will!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0022","text":"“Love your neighbour as yourselves, if you will,—but first be for me such as love themselves —","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0023","text":"—love with the great love, love with the great contempt!” Thus speaks Zarathustra, the godless one.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0024","text":"But what am I saying, where no one has my ears! It is still an hour too early for me here.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0025","text":"I am my own precursor among this people, my own cock-crow through dark alleys.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0026","text":"But their hour is coming! And mine too is coming! Hour by hour they become smaller, poorer, more barren,—poor herb! poor soil!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0027","text":"And soon they shall stand before me like withered grass and steppe, and truly! weary of themselves—and more than for water, thirsting for fire!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0028","text":"O blessed hour of lightning! O mystery before noon!—One day I shall yet make running fires of them and heralds with tongues of flame:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0029","text":"—one day they shall yet proclaim with tongues of flame: He is coming, he is near, the great noon!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0030","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_3:P0030"]}],"source_digest":"2dd86bc33283e77d9c467312e9c052343ef4f34433f84062f4cd8850f90ec573","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Diminishing Virtue","source_section_label":"3.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On the Diminishing Virtue · 3","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On the Diminishing Virtue","3."],"original_title":"3."}],"source_title_context":"Von der verkleinernden Tugend","source_section_label":"3.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von der verkleinernden Tugend · 3","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von der verkleinernden Tugend","3."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"3.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0","title":"On the Mount of Olives","order":73,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_ADL0","text":"Der Winter, ein schlimmer Gast, sitzt bei mir zu Hause; blau sind meine Hände von seiner Freundschaft Händedruck.\n\nIch ehre ihn, diesen schlimmen Gast, aber lasse gerne ihn allein sitzen. Gerne laufe ich ihm davon; und, läuft man gut , so entläuft man ihm!\n\nMit warmen Füssen und warmen Gedanken laufe ich dorthin, wo der Wind stille steht,—zum Sonnen-Winkel meines Ölbergs.\n\nDa lache ich meines gestrengen Gastes und bin ihm noch gut, dass er zu Hause mir die Fliegen wegfängt und vielen kleinen Lärm stille macht.\n\nEr leidet es nämlich nicht, wenn eine Mücke singen will, oder gar zwei; noch die Gasse macht er einsam, dass der Mondschein drin Nachts sich fürchtet.\n\nEin harter Gast ist er,—aber ich ehre ihn, und nicht bete ich, gleich den Zärtlingen, zum dickbäuchichten Feuer-Götzen.\n\nLieber noch ein Wenig zähneklappern als Götzen anbeten!—so will’s meine Art. Und sonderlich bin ich allen brünstigen dampfenden dumpfigen Feuer-Götzen gram.\n\nWen ich liebe, den liebe ich Winters besser als Sommers; besser spotte ich jetzt meiner Feinde und herzhafter, seit der Winter mir im Hause sitzt.\n\nHerzhaft wahrlich, selbst dann noch, wenn ich zu Bett krieche —: da lacht und muthwillt noch mein verkrochenes Glück; es lacht noch mein Lügen-Traum.\n\nIch—ein Kriecher? Niemals kroch ich im Leben vor Mächtigen; und log ich je, so log ich aus Liebe. Desshalb bin ich froh auch im Winter-Bette.\n\nEin geringes Bett wärmt mich mehr als ein reiches, denn ich bin eifersüchtig auf meine Armuth. Und im Winter ist sie mir am treuesten.\n\nMit einer Bosheit beginne ich jeden Tag, ich spotte des Winters mit einem kalten Bade: darob brummt mein gestrenger Hausfreund.\n\nAuch kitzle ich ihn gerne mit einem Wachskerzlein: dass er mir endlich den Himmel herauslasse aus aschgrauer Dämmerung.\n\nSonderlich boshaft bin ich nämlich des Morgens: zur frühen Stunde, da der Eimer am Brunnen klirrt und die Rosse warm durch graue Gassen wiehern:—\n\nUngeduldig warte ich da, dass mir endlich der lichte Himmel aufgehe, der schneebärtige Winter-Himmel, der Greis und Weisskopf,—\n\n—der Winter-Himmel, der schweigsame, der oft noch seine Sonne verschweigt!\n\nLernte ich wohl von ihm das lange lichte Schweigen? Oder lernte er’s von mir? Oder hat ein jeder von uns es selbst erfunden?\n\nAller guten Dinge Ursprung ist tausendfältig,—alle guten muthwilligen Dinge springen vor Lust in’s Dasein: wie sollten sie das immer nur—Ein Mal thun!\n\nEin gutes muthwilliges Ding ist auch das lange Schweigen und gleich dem Winter-Himmel blicken aus lichtem rundäugichten Antlitze:—\n\n—gleich ihm seine Sonne verschweigen und seinen unbeugsamen Sonnen-Willen: wahrlich, diese Kunst und diesen Winter-Muthwillen lernte ich gut !\n\nMeine liebste Bosheit und Kunst ist es, dass mein Schweigen lernte, sich nicht durch Schweigen zu verrathen.\n\nMit Worten und Würfeln klappernd überliste ich mir die feierlichen Warter: allen diesen gestrengen Aufpassern soll mein Wille und Zweck entschlüpfen.\n\nDass mir Niemand in meinen Grund und letzten Willen hinab sehe,—dazu erfand ich mir das lange lichte Schweigen.\n\nSo manchen Klugen fand ich: der verschleierte sein Antlitz und trübte sein Wasser, dass Niemand ihm hindurch und hinunter sehe.\n\nAber zu ihm gerade kamen die klügeren Misstrauer und Nussknacker: ihm gerade fischte man seinen verborgensten Fisch heraus!\n\nSondern die Hellen, die Wackern, die Durchsichtigen—das sind mir die klügsten Schweiger: denen so tief ihr Grund ist, dass auch das hellste Wasser ihn nicht—verräth.—\n\nDu schneebärtiger schweigender Winter-Himmel, du rundäugichter Weisskopf über mir! Oh du himmlisches Gleichniss meiner Seele und ihres Muthwillens!\n\nUnd muss ich mich nicht verbergen, gleich Einem, der Gold verschluckt hat,—dass man mir nicht die Seele aufschlitze?\n\nMuss ich nicht Stelzen tragen, dass sie meine langen Beine übersehen ,—alle diese Neidbolde und Leidholde, die um mich sind?\n\nDiese räucherigen, stubenwarmen, verbrauchten, vergrünten, vergrämelten Seelen —wie könnte ihr Neid mein Glück ertragen!\n\nSo zeige ich ihnen nur das Eis und den Winter auf meinen Gipfeln—und nicht , dass mein Berg noch alle Sonnengürtel um sich schlingt!\n\nSie hören nur meine Winter-Stürme pfeifen: und nicht , dass ich auch über warme Meere fahre, gleich sehnsüchtigen, schweren, heissen Südwinden.\n\nSie erbarmen sich noch meiner Unfälle und Zufälle:—aber mein Wort heisst: „lasst den Zufall zu mir kommen: unschuldig ist er, wie ein Kindlein!“\n\nWie könnten sie mein Glück ertragen, wenn ich nicht Unfälle und Winter-Nöthe und Eisbären-Mützen und Schneehimmel-Hüllen um mein Glück legte!\n\n—wenn ich mich nicht selbst ihres Mitleids erbarmte—des Mitleids dieser Neidbolde und Leidholde!\n\n—wenn ich nicht selber vor ihnen seufzte und frostklapperte und mich geduldsam in ihr Mitleid wickeln liesse !\n\nDiess ist der weise Muthwille und Wohlwille meiner Seele, dass sie ihren Winter und ihre Froststürme nicht verbirgt ; sie verbirgt auch ihre Frostbeulen nicht.\n\nDes Einen Einsamkeit ist die Flucht des Kranken; des Andern Einsamkeit die Flucht vor den Kranken.\n\nMögen sie mich klappern und seufzen hören vor Winterkälte, alle diese armen scheelen Schelme um mich! Mit solchem Geseufz und Geklapper flüchte ich noch vor ihren geheizten Stuben.\n\nMögen sie mich bemitleiden und bemitseufzen ob meiner Frostbeulen: „am Eis der Erkenntniss erfriert er uns noch!“—so klagen sie.\n\nInzwischen laufe ich mit warmen Füssen kreuz und quer auf meinem Ölberge: im Sonnen-Winkel meines Ölberges singe und spotte ich alles Mitleids.—\n\nAlso sang Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0001","text":"Der Winter, ein schlimmer Gast, sitzt bei mir zu Hause; blau sind meine Hände von seiner Freundschaft Händedruck.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0002","text":"Ich ehre ihn, diesen schlimmen Gast, aber lasse gerne ihn allein sitzen. Gerne laufe ich ihm davon; und, läuft man gut , so entläuft man ihm!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0003","text":"Mit warmen Füssen und warmen Gedanken laufe ich dorthin, wo der Wind stille steht,—zum Sonnen-Winkel meines Ölbergs.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0004","text":"Da lache ich meines gestrengen Gastes und bin ihm noch gut, dass er zu Hause mir die Fliegen wegfängt und vielen kleinen Lärm stille macht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0005","text":"Er leidet es nämlich nicht, wenn eine Mücke singen will, oder gar zwei; noch die Gasse macht er einsam, dass der Mondschein drin Nachts sich fürchtet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0006","text":"Ein harter Gast ist er,—aber ich ehre ihn, und nicht bete ich, gleich den Zärtlingen, zum dickbäuchichten Feuer-Götzen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0007","text":"Lieber noch ein Wenig zähneklappern als Götzen anbeten!—so will’s meine Art. Und sonderlich bin ich allen brünstigen dampfenden dumpfigen Feuer-Götzen gram.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0008","text":"Wen ich liebe, den liebe ich Winters besser als Sommers; besser spotte ich jetzt meiner Feinde und herzhafter, seit der Winter mir im Hause sitzt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0009","text":"Herzhaft wahrlich, selbst dann noch, wenn ich zu Bett krieche —: da lacht und muthwillt noch mein verkrochenes Glück; es lacht noch mein Lügen-Traum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0010","text":"Ich—ein Kriecher? Niemals kroch ich im Leben vor Mächtigen; und log ich je, so log ich aus Liebe. Desshalb bin ich froh auch im Winter-Bette.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0011","text":"Ein geringes Bett wärmt mich mehr als ein reiches, denn ich bin eifersüchtig auf meine Armuth. Und im Winter ist sie mir am treuesten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0012","text":"Mit einer Bosheit beginne ich jeden Tag, ich spotte des Winters mit einem kalten Bade: darob brummt mein gestrenger Hausfreund.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0013","text":"Auch kitzle ich ihn gerne mit einem Wachskerzlein: dass er mir endlich den Himmel herauslasse aus aschgrauer Dämmerung.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0014","text":"Sonderlich boshaft bin ich nämlich des Morgens: zur frühen Stunde, da der Eimer am Brunnen klirrt und die Rosse warm durch graue Gassen wiehern:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0015","text":"Ungeduldig warte ich da, dass mir endlich der lichte Himmel aufgehe, der schneebärtige Winter-Himmel, der Greis und Weisskopf,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0016","text":"—der Winter-Himmel, der schweigsame, der oft noch seine Sonne verschweigt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0017","text":"Lernte ich wohl von ihm das lange lichte Schweigen? Oder lernte er’s von mir? Oder hat ein jeder von uns es selbst erfunden?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0018","text":"Aller guten Dinge Ursprung ist tausendfältig,—alle guten muthwilligen Dinge springen vor Lust in’s Dasein: wie sollten sie das immer nur—Ein Mal thun!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0019","text":"Ein gutes muthwilliges Ding ist auch das lange Schweigen und gleich dem Winter-Himmel blicken aus lichtem rundäugichten Antlitze:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0020","text":"—gleich ihm seine Sonne verschweigen und seinen unbeugsamen Sonnen-Willen: wahrlich, diese Kunst und diesen Winter-Muthwillen lernte ich gut !","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0021","text":"Meine liebste Bosheit und Kunst ist es, dass mein Schweigen lernte, sich nicht durch Schweigen zu verrathen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0022","text":"Mit Worten und Würfeln klappernd überliste ich mir die feierlichen Warter: allen diesen gestrengen Aufpassern soll mein Wille und Zweck entschlüpfen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0023","text":"Dass mir Niemand in meinen Grund und letzten Willen hinab sehe,—dazu erfand ich mir das lange lichte Schweigen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0024","text":"So manchen Klugen fand ich: der verschleierte sein Antlitz und trübte sein Wasser, dass Niemand ihm hindurch und hinunter sehe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0025","text":"Aber zu ihm gerade kamen die klügeren Misstrauer und Nussknacker: ihm gerade fischte man seinen verborgensten Fisch heraus!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0026","text":"Sondern die Hellen, die Wackern, die Durchsichtigen—das sind mir die klügsten Schweiger: denen so tief ihr Grund ist, dass auch das hellste Wasser ihn nicht—verräth.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0027","text":"Du schneebärtiger schweigender Winter-Himmel, du rundäugichter Weisskopf über mir! Oh du himmlisches Gleichniss meiner Seele und ihres Muthwillens!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0028","text":"Und muss ich mich nicht verbergen, gleich Einem, der Gold verschluckt hat,—dass man mir nicht die Seele aufschlitze?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0029","text":"Muss ich nicht Stelzen tragen, dass sie meine langen Beine übersehen ,—alle diese Neidbolde und Leidholde, die um mich sind?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0030","text":"Diese räucherigen, stubenwarmen, verbrauchten, vergrünten, vergrämelten Seelen —wie könnte ihr Neid mein Glück ertragen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0031","text":"So zeige ich ihnen nur das Eis und den Winter auf meinen Gipfeln—und nicht , dass mein Berg noch alle Sonnengürtel um sich schlingt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0032","text":"Sie hören nur meine Winter-Stürme pfeifen: und nicht , dass ich auch über warme Meere fahre, gleich sehnsüchtigen, schweren, heissen Südwinden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0033","text":"Sie erbarmen sich noch meiner Unfälle und Zufälle:—aber mein Wort heisst: „lasst den Zufall zu mir kommen: unschuldig ist er, wie ein Kindlein!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0034","text":"Wie könnten sie mein Glück ertragen, wenn ich nicht Unfälle und Winter-Nöthe und Eisbären-Mützen und Schneehimmel-Hüllen um mein Glück legte!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0035","text":"—wenn ich mich nicht selbst ihres Mitleids erbarmte—des Mitleids dieser Neidbolde und Leidholde!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0036","text":"—wenn ich nicht selber vor ihnen seufzte und frostklapperte und mich geduldsam in ihr Mitleid wickeln liesse !","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0037","text":"Diess ist der weise Muthwille und Wohlwille meiner Seele, dass sie ihren Winter und ihre Froststürme nicht verbirgt ; sie verbirgt auch ihre Frostbeulen nicht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0038","text":"Des Einen Einsamkeit ist die Flucht des Kranken; des Andern Einsamkeit die Flucht vor den Kranken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0039","text":"Mögen sie mich klappern und seufzen hören vor Winterkälte, alle diese armen scheelen Schelme um mich! Mit solchem Geseufz und Geklapper flüchte ich noch vor ihren geheizten Stuben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0040","text":"Mögen sie mich bemitleiden und bemitseufzen ob meiner Frostbeulen: „am Eis der Erkenntniss erfriert er uns noch!“—so klagen sie.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0041","text":"Inzwischen laufe ich mit warmen Füssen kreuz und quer auf meinem Ölberge: im Sonnen-Winkel meines Ölberges singe und spotte ich alles Mitleids.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0042","text":"Also sang Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"cfbda5becd1881cbbdc58e473f30225d83a7db346425901c1485464e476da481","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182636715392Z_bf9c8d4c","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Mount of Olives","source_sha256":"cfbda5becd1881cbbdc58e473f30225d83a7db346425901c1485464e476da481","sha256":"2b602e91a6ade2b4c673d278a4f06bc5fc1d3669843120562e2c38979398a276","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:26:36.715432+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0001","text":"Winter, a grim guest, sits at home with me; my hands are blue from the handshake of his friendship.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0002","text":"I honour him, this grim guest, but gladly leave him sitting alone. Gladly I run away from him; and, if one runs well, one escapes him!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0003","text":"With warm feet and warm thoughts I run thither where the wind stands still,—to the sunny corner of my olive-mount.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0004","text":"There I laugh at my stern guest and am even fond of him for catching the flies for me at home and stilling much little noise.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0005","text":"For he does not tolerate it when a gnat wants to sing, or even two; he also makes the lane so desolate that the moonlight is afraid in it at night.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0006","text":"He is a hard guest,—but I honour him, and do not pray, like the tenderlings, to the pot-bellied fire-idol.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0007","text":"I would rather chatter my teeth a little longer than worship idols!—thus my nature wills it. And I am especially angry with all fervid, steaming, musty fire-idols.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0008","text":"Whom I love, I love better in winter than in summer; I mock my enemies better now, and more heartily, since winter sits in my house.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0009","text":"Heartily indeed, even when I creep into bed —: then my hidden-away happiness still laughs and is mischievous; my dream of lies still laughs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0010","text":"I—a crawler? Never in my life did I crawl before the mighty; and if ever I lied, I lied out of love. Therefore I am glad even in the winter-bed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0011","text":"A humble bed warms me more than a rich one, for I am jealous of my poverty. And in winter it is most faithful to me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0012","text":"I begin every day with a malice: I mock winter with a cold bath; at this my stern house-friend grumbles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0013","text":"I also like to tickle him with a little wax candle: so that at last he may let the sky out for me from the ash-grey twilight.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0014","text":"For I am especially malicious in the morning: in the early hour, when the bucket clatters at the well and the horses neigh warmly through grey lanes:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0015","text":"Impatiently I wait then for the bright sky finally to dawn upon me, the snow-bearded winter-sky, the old man and white-head,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0016","text":"—the winter-sky, the silent one, who often still conceals his sun!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0017","text":"Did I perhaps learn from him long bright silence? Or did he learn it from me? Or has each of us invented it himself?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0018","text":"The origin of all good things is manifold,—all good mischievous things leap into existence out of pleasure: how could they always do that only—Once!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0019","text":"Long silence too is a good mischievous thing, and, like the winter-sky, to look from a bright, round-eyed countenance:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0020","text":"—like him, to conceal one's sun and one's inflexible sun-will: verily, I learned this art and this winter-mischief well!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0021","text":"My dearest malice and art is that my silence has learned not to betray itself through silence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0022","text":"Clattering with words and dice, I outwit the solemn watchers: from all these stern overseers my will and purpose shall escape.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0023","text":"So that no one may look down into my ground and ultimate will,—for that I invented long bright silence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0024","text":"I found many a clever man: he veiled his countenance and muddied his water, so that no one might see through it and down into him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0025","text":"But it was precisely to him that the shrewder mistrusters and nutcrackers came: it was precisely from him that one fished out one’s most hidden fish!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0026","text":"But the bright ones, the valiant ones, the transparent ones—these are to me the wisest silent ones: their ground is so deep that even the brightest water does not—betray it.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0027","text":"You snow-bearded, silent winter-heaven, you round-eyed white-head above me! Oh you heavenly likeness of my soul and of its wilfulness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0028","text":"And must I not hide myself, like one who has swallowed gold,—lest they slit open my soul?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0029","text":"Must I not wear stilts, so that they overlook my long legs,—all these envy-bolts and suffering-bolts that are around me?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0030","text":"These smoky, room-warm, worn-out, greened-over, embittered souls—how could their envy bear my happiness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0031","text":"Thus I show them only the ice and winter upon my peaks—and not, that my mountain still winds all the girdles of the sun around itself!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0032","text":"They hear only my winter-storms whistling: and not, that I also voyage over warm seas, like yearning, heavy, hot south winds.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0033","text":"They still pity me for my mishaps and happenstances:—but my word is: “let chance come to me: innocent is it, like a little child!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0034","text":"How could they bear my happiness, if I did not place mishaps and winter-distresses and polar-bear caps and snow-heaven wrappings around my happiness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0035","text":"—if I did not myself take pity on their pity—on the pity of these envy-bolts and suffering-bolts!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0036","text":"—if I did not myself sigh and chatter with cold before them and patiently let myself be wrapped in their pity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0037","text":"This is the wise wilfulness and goodwill of my soul, that it does not conceal its winter and its frost-storms; neither does it conceal its chilblains.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0038","text":"One man’s solitude is the flight of the sick man; another’s solitude, the flight from the sick.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0039","text":"May they hear me chattering and sighing from winter-cold, all these poor squinting rogues around me! With such sighing and chattering I still flee from their heated rooms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0040","text":"May they pity me and sigh along with me over my chilblains: “on the ice of knowledge he will yet freeze to death on us!”—thus they lament.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0041","text":"Meanwhile I run to and fro with warm feet upon my Mount of Olives: in the sunny corner of my Mount of Olives I sing and mock at all pity.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0042","text":"Thus sang Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_ADL0:P0042"]}],"source_digest":"b45f751259d8f709efb675080230b325d01be50c224a5cae244285acb5a135f4","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Auf dem Ölberge"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Auf dem Ölberge","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0","title":"On Passing By","order":74,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VVB0","text":"Also, durch viel Volk und vielerlei Städte langsam hindurchschreitend, gierig Zarathustra auf Umwegen zurück zu seinem Gebirge und seiner Höhle. Und siehe, dabei kam er unversehens auch an das Stadtthor der grossen Stadt : hier aber sprang ein schäumender Narr mit ausgebreiteten Händen auf ihn zu und trat ihm in den Weg. Diess aber war der selbige Narr, welchen das Volk „den Affen Zarathustra’s“ hiess: denn er hatte ihm Etwas vom Satz und Fall der Rede abgemerkt und borgte wohl auch gerne vom Schatze seiner Weisheit. Der Narr aber redete also zu Zarathustra:\n\n„Oh Zarathustra, hier ist die grosse Stadt: hier hast du Nichts zu suchen und Alles zu verlieren.\n\nWarum wolltest du durch diesen Schlamm waten? Habe doch Mitleiden mit deinem Fusse! Speie lieber auf das Stadtthor und—kehre um!\n\nHier ist die Hölle für Einsiedler-Gedanken: hier werden grosse Gedanken lebendig gesotten und klein gekocht.\n\nHier verwesen alle grossen Gefühle: hier dürfen nur klapperdürre Gefühlchen klappern!\n\nRiechst du nicht schon die Schlachthäuser und Garküchen des Geistes? Dampft nicht diese Stadt vom Dunst geschlachteten Geistes?\n\nSiehst du nicht die Seelen hängen wie schlaffe schmutzige Lumpen?—Und sie machen noch Zeitungen aus diesen Lumpen!\n\nHörst du nicht, wie der Geist hier zum Wortspiel wurde? Widriges Wort-Spülicht bricht er heraus!—Und sie machen noch Zeitungen aus diesem Wort-Spülicht.\n\nSie hetzen einander und wissen nicht, wohin? Sie erhitzen einander und wissen nicht, warum? Sie klimpern mit ihrem Bleche, sie klingeln mit ihrem Golde.\n\nSie sind kalt und suchen sich Wärme bei gebrannten Wassern; sie sind erhitzt und suchen Kühle bei gefrorenen Geistern; sie sind Alle siech und süchtig an öffentlichen Meinungen.\n\nAlle Lüste und Laster sind hier zu Hause; aber es giebt hier auch Tugendhafte, es giebt viel anstellige angestellte Tugend:—\n\nViel anstellige Tugend mit Schreibfingern und hartem Sitz- und Warte-Fleische, gesegnet mit kleinen Bruststernen und ausgestopften steisslosen Töchtern.\n\nEs giebt hier auch viel Frömmigkeit und viel gläubige Speichel-Leckerei, Schmeichel-Bäckerei vor dem Gott der Heerschaaren.\n\n„Von Oben“ her träufelt ja der Stern und der gnädige Speichel; nach Oben hin sehnt sich jeder sternenlose Busen.\n\nDer Mond hat seinen Hof, und der Hof hat seine Mondkälber: zu Allem aber, was vom Hofe kommt, betet das Bettel-Volk und alle anstellige Bettel-Tugend.\n\n„Ich diene, du dienst, wir dienen“—so betet alle anstellige Tugend hinauf zum Fürsten: dass der verdiente Stern sich endlich an den schmalen Busen hefte!\n\nAber der Mond dreht sich noch um alles Irdische: so dreht sich auch der Fürst noch um das Aller-Irdischste—: das aber ist das Gold der Krämer.\n\nDer Gott der Heerschaaren ist kein Gott der Goldbarren; der Fürst denkt, aber der Krämer—lenkt!\n\nBei Allem, was licht und stark und gut in dir ist, oh Zarathustra! Speie auf diese Stadt der Krämer und kehre um!\n\nHier fliesst alles Blut faulicht und lauicht und schaumicht durch alle Adern: speie auf die grosse Stadt, welche der grosse Abraum ist, wo aller Abschaum zusammenschäumt!\n\nSpeie auf die Stadt der eingedrückten Seelen und schmalen Brüste, der spitzen Augen, der klebrigen Finger—\n\n—auf die Stadt der Aufdringlinge, der Unverschämten, der Schreib- und Schreihälse, der überheizten Ehrgeizigen:—\n\n—wo alles Anbrüchige, Anrüchige, Lüsterne, Düsterne, Übermürbe, Geschwürige, Verschwörerische zusammenschwärt:—\n\n—speie auf die grosse Stadt und kehre um!“—\n\nHier aber unterbrach Zarathustra den schäumenden Narren und hielt ihm den Mund zu.\n\n„Höre endlich auf! rief Zarathustra, mich ekelt lange schon deiner Rede und deiner Art!\n\nWarum wohntest du so lange am Sumpfe, dass du selber zum Frosch und zur Kröte werden musstest?\n\nFliesst dir nicht selber nun ein faulichtes schaumichtes Sumpf-Blut durch die Adern, dass du also quaken und lästern lerntest?\n\nWarum giengst du nicht in den Wald? Oder pflügtest die Erde? Ist das Meer nicht voll von grünen Eilanden?\n\nIch verachte dein Verachten; und wenn du mich warntest,—warum warntest du dich nicht selber?\n\nAus der Liebe allein soll mir mein Verachten und mein warnender Vogel auffliegen: aber nicht aus dem Sumpfe!—\n\nMan heisst dich meinen Affen, du schäumender Narr: aber ich heisse dich mein Grunze-Schwein,—durch Grunzen verdirbst du mir noch mein Lob der Narrheit.\n\nWas war es denn, was dich zuerst grunzen machte? Dass Niemand dir genug geschmeichelt hat:—darum setztest du dich hin zu diesem Unrathe, dass du Grund hättest viel zu grunzen,—\n\n—dass du Grund hättest zu vieler Rache ! Rache nämlich, du eitler Narr, ist all dein Schäumen, ich errieth dich wohl!\n\nAber dein Narren-Wort thut mir Schaden, selbst, wo du Recht hast! Und wenn Zarathustra’s Wort sogar hundert Mal Recht hätte : du würdest mit meinem Wort immer—Unrecht thun !“\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra; und er blickte die grosse Stadt an, seufzte und schwieg lange. Endlich redete er also:\n\nMich ekelt auch dieser grossen Stadt und nicht nur dieses Narren. Hier und dort ist Nichts zu bessern, Nichts zu bösern.\n\nWehe dieser grossen Stadt!—Und ich wollte, ich sähe schon die Feuersäule, in der sie verbrannt wird!\n\nDenn solche Feuersäulen müssen dem grossen Mittage vorangehn. Doch diess hat seine Zeit und sein eigenes Schicksal.—\n\nDiese Lehre aber gebe ich dir, du Narr, zum Abschiede: wo man nicht mehr lieben kann, da soll man— vorübergehn !—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra und gieng an dem Narren und der grossen Stadt vorüber.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0001","text":"Also, durch viel Volk und vielerlei Städte langsam hindurchschreitend, gierig Zarathustra auf Umwegen zurück zu seinem Gebirge und seiner Höhle. Und siehe, dabei kam er unversehens auch an das Stadtthor der grossen Stadt : hier aber sprang ein schäumender Narr mit ausgebreiteten Händen auf ihn zu und trat ihm in den Weg. Diess aber war der selbige Narr, welchen das Volk „den Affen Zarathustra’s“ hiess: denn er hatte ihm Etwas vom Satz und Fall der Rede abgemerkt und borgte wohl auch gerne vom Schatze seiner Weisheit. Der Narr aber redete also zu Zarathustra:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0002","text":"„Oh Zarathustra, hier ist die grosse Stadt: hier hast du Nichts zu suchen und Alles zu verlieren.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0003","text":"Warum wolltest du durch diesen Schlamm waten? Habe doch Mitleiden mit deinem Fusse! Speie lieber auf das Stadtthor und—kehre um!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0004","text":"Hier ist die Hölle für Einsiedler-Gedanken: hier werden grosse Gedanken lebendig gesotten und klein gekocht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0005","text":"Hier verwesen alle grossen Gefühle: hier dürfen nur klapperdürre Gefühlchen klappern!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0006","text":"Riechst du nicht schon die Schlachthäuser und Garküchen des Geistes? Dampft nicht diese Stadt vom Dunst geschlachteten Geistes?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0007","text":"Siehst du nicht die Seelen hängen wie schlaffe schmutzige Lumpen?—Und sie machen noch Zeitungen aus diesen Lumpen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0008","text":"Hörst du nicht, wie der Geist hier zum Wortspiel wurde? Widriges Wort-Spülicht bricht er heraus!—Und sie machen noch Zeitungen aus diesem Wort-Spülicht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0009","text":"Sie hetzen einander und wissen nicht, wohin? Sie erhitzen einander und wissen nicht, warum? Sie klimpern mit ihrem Bleche, sie klingeln mit ihrem Golde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0010","text":"Sie sind kalt und suchen sich Wärme bei gebrannten Wassern; sie sind erhitzt und suchen Kühle bei gefrorenen Geistern; sie sind Alle siech und süchtig an öffentlichen Meinungen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0011","text":"Alle Lüste und Laster sind hier zu Hause; aber es giebt hier auch Tugendhafte, es giebt viel anstellige angestellte Tugend:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0012","text":"Viel anstellige Tugend mit Schreibfingern und hartem Sitz- und Warte-Fleische, gesegnet mit kleinen Bruststernen und ausgestopften steisslosen Töchtern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0013","text":"Es giebt hier auch viel Frömmigkeit und viel gläubige Speichel-Leckerei, Schmeichel-Bäckerei vor dem Gott der Heerschaaren.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0014","text":"„Von Oben“ her träufelt ja der Stern und der gnädige Speichel; nach Oben hin sehnt sich jeder sternenlose Busen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0015","text":"Der Mond hat seinen Hof, und der Hof hat seine Mondkälber: zu Allem aber, was vom Hofe kommt, betet das Bettel-Volk und alle anstellige Bettel-Tugend.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0016","text":"„Ich diene, du dienst, wir dienen“—so betet alle anstellige Tugend hinauf zum Fürsten: dass der verdiente Stern sich endlich an den schmalen Busen hefte!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0017","text":"Aber der Mond dreht sich noch um alles Irdische: so dreht sich auch der Fürst noch um das Aller-Irdischste—: das aber ist das Gold der Krämer.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0018","text":"Der Gott der Heerschaaren ist kein Gott der Goldbarren; der Fürst denkt, aber der Krämer—lenkt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0019","text":"Bei Allem, was licht und stark und gut in dir ist, oh Zarathustra! Speie auf diese Stadt der Krämer und kehre um!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0020","text":"Hier fliesst alles Blut faulicht und lauicht und schaumicht durch alle Adern: speie auf die grosse Stadt, welche der grosse Abraum ist, wo aller Abschaum zusammenschäumt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0021","text":"Speie auf die Stadt der eingedrückten Seelen und schmalen Brüste, der spitzen Augen, der klebrigen Finger—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0022","text":"—auf die Stadt der Aufdringlinge, der Unverschämten, der Schreib- und Schreihälse, der überheizten Ehrgeizigen:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0023","text":"—wo alles Anbrüchige, Anrüchige, Lüsterne, Düsterne, Übermürbe, Geschwürige, Verschwörerische zusammenschwärt:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0024","text":"—speie auf die grosse Stadt und kehre um!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0025","text":"Hier aber unterbrach Zarathustra den schäumenden Narren und hielt ihm den Mund zu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0026","text":"„Höre endlich auf! rief Zarathustra, mich ekelt lange schon deiner Rede und deiner Art!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0027","text":"Warum wohntest du so lange am Sumpfe, dass du selber zum Frosch und zur Kröte werden musstest?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0028","text":"Fliesst dir nicht selber nun ein faulichtes schaumichtes Sumpf-Blut durch die Adern, dass du also quaken und lästern lerntest?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0029","text":"Warum giengst du nicht in den Wald? Oder pflügtest die Erde? Ist das Meer nicht voll von grünen Eilanden?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0030","text":"Ich verachte dein Verachten; und wenn du mich warntest,—warum warntest du dich nicht selber?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0031","text":"Aus der Liebe allein soll mir mein Verachten und mein warnender Vogel auffliegen: aber nicht aus dem Sumpfe!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0032","text":"Man heisst dich meinen Affen, du schäumender Narr: aber ich heisse dich mein Grunze-Schwein,—durch Grunzen verdirbst du mir noch mein Lob der Narrheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0033","text":"Was war es denn, was dich zuerst grunzen machte? Dass Niemand dir genug geschmeichelt hat:—darum setztest du dich hin zu diesem Unrathe, dass du Grund hättest viel zu grunzen,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0034","text":"—dass du Grund hättest zu vieler Rache ! Rache nämlich, du eitler Narr, ist all dein Schäumen, ich errieth dich wohl!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0035","text":"Aber dein Narren-Wort thut mir Schaden, selbst, wo du Recht hast! Und wenn Zarathustra’s Wort sogar hundert Mal Recht hätte : du würdest mit meinem Wort immer—Unrecht thun !“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0036","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra; und er blickte die grosse Stadt an, seufzte und schwieg lange. Endlich redete er also:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0037","text":"Mich ekelt auch dieser grossen Stadt und nicht nur dieses Narren. Hier und dort ist Nichts zu bessern, Nichts zu bösern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0038","text":"Wehe dieser grossen Stadt!—Und ich wollte, ich sähe schon die Feuersäule, in der sie verbrannt wird!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0039","text":"Denn solche Feuersäulen müssen dem grossen Mittage vorangehn. Doch diess hat seine Zeit und sein eigenes Schicksal.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0040","text":"Diese Lehre aber gebe ich dir, du Narr, zum Abschiede: wo man nicht mehr lieben kann, da soll man— vorübergehn !—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0041","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra und gieng an dem Narren und der grossen Stadt vorüber.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"ab73f856de9beb836154ef26ed98885e864ac50730cfb99d76b016e90235a28e","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182656414185Z_aeceb82b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On Passing By","source_sha256":"ab73f856de9beb836154ef26ed98885e864ac50730cfb99d76b016e90235a28e","sha256":"c2b02ece8ff3da7e371a6608cffa76c50e3edb19ea647181643e09d65c4bb6f2","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:26:56.414203+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0001","text":"Thus, slowly proceeding through much people and many kinds of cities, Zarathustra made his way eagerly, by detours, back to his mountains and his cave. And behold, in doing so he came unexpectedly also to the city gate of the great city: but here a foaming fool sprang towards him with outstretched hands and stepped into his path. This, however, was the very same fool whom the people called “Zarathustra’s ape”: for he had noticed something of his manner and turn of speech and was also fond of borrowing from the treasure of his wisdom. But the fool spoke thus to Zarathustra:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0002","text":"“O Zarathustra, here is the great city: here you have nothing to seek and everything to lose.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0003","text":"Why would you want to wade through this mud? Have pity on your foot! Rather spit upon the city gate and—turn back!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0004","text":"Here is hell for hermit-thoughts: here great thoughts are boiled alive and cooked small.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0005","text":"Here all great feelings decay: here only rattling-dry little feelings may rattle!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0006","text":"Do you not already smell the slaughterhouses and cookshops of the spirit? Does not this city steam with the vapour of slaughtered spirit?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0007","text":"Do you not see souls hanging like limp, dirty rags?—And they even make newspapers out of these rags!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0008","text":"Do you not hear how spirit here has become a play on words? He spews forth repulsive word-swill!—And they even make newspapers out of this word-swill.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0009","text":"They hound one another and do not know whither? They heat one another up and do not know why? They clink with their tin, they ring with their gold.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0010","text":"They are cold and seek warmth in fiery waters; they are heated and seek coolness in frozen spirits; they are all sickly and addicted to public opinions.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0011","text":"All lusts and vices are at home here; but there are also virtuous people here, there is much serviceable, employed virtue:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0012","text":"Much serviceable virtue with writing fingers and hard sitting- and waiting-flesh, blessed with little breast-stars and stuffed, buttockless daughters.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0013","text":"There is also much piety here and much believing spit-licking, flatter-baking before the God of Hosts.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0014","text":"‘From Above’ star and gracious spittle do indeed trickle down; every starless breast yearns upwards.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0015","text":"The moon has its court, and the court has its moon-calves: but to everything that comes from the court the beggar-people and all serviceable beggar-virtue pray.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0016","text":"‘I serve, you serve, we serve’—thus all serviceable virtue prays upwards to the prince: that the deserved star may at last fasten itself to the narrow breast!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0017","text":"But the moon still revolves around all that is earthly: so the prince too still revolves around the most earthly of all—: and that is the gold of shopkeepers.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0018","text":"The God of Hosts is no god of gold bars; the prince thinks, but the shopkeeper—steers!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0019","text":"By all that is bright and strong and good in you, O Zarathustra! Spit upon this city of shopkeepers and turn back!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0020","text":"Here all blood flows rottenly and lukewarmly and foamily through all veins: spit upon the great city, which is the great refuse-heap where all scum foams together!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0021","text":"Spit upon the city of indented souls and narrow breasts, of pointed eyes, of sticky fingers—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0022","text":"—upon the city of the importunate, the shameless, the writing- and shouting-necks, the overheated ambitious:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0023","text":"—where everything incipient, odorous, lustful, gloomy, overripe, ulcerous, conspiratorial swarms together:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0024","text":"—spit upon the great city and turn back!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0025","text":"But here Zarathustra interrupted the frothing fool and stopped his mouth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0026","text":"“At last, cease! cried Zarathustra, I have long been disgusted by your speech and your manner!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0027","text":"Why did you dwell so long by the swamp that you yourself had to become a frog and a toad?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0028","text":"Does not putrid, frothy swamp-blood now itself flow through your veins, so that you learned to croak and slander?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0029","text":"Why did you not go into the forest? Or plough the earth? Is the sea not full of green islands?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0030","text":"I despise your despising; and if you warned me,—why did you not warn yourself?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0031","text":"From love alone shall my despising and my warning bird fly forth for me: but not from the swamp!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0032","text":"They call you my ape, you frothing fool: but I call you my grunting pig,—by your grunting you are still spoiling my praise of folly for me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0033","text":"What, then, was it that first made you grunt? That no one flattered you enough:—therefore you sat down beside this filth, so that you might have reason to grunt much,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0034","text":"—that you might have reason for much revenge! For revenge, you vain fool, is all your frothing; I guessed you well!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0035","text":"But your fool’s word does me harm, even where you are right! And even if Zarathustra’s word were right a hundred times: you would always—do wrong with my word!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0036","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra; and he looked upon the great city, sighed, and was silent for a long time. At last he spoke thus:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0037","text":"I too am disgusted by this great city, and not only by this fool. Here and there there is nothing to improve, nothing to worsen.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0038","text":"Woe to this great city!—And I would that I already saw the pillar of fire in which it is burned!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0039","text":"For such pillars of fire must precede the great noon. But this has its time and its own destiny.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0040","text":"But I give you this teaching, you fool, as a farewell: where one can no longer love, there one should—pass by!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0041","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra and passed by the fool and the great city.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VVB0:P0041"]}],"source_digest":"7d516f9bf0958534bf57f600bb906321ac8a1ae288c7ce8df893c4faff9d5e1e","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Vom Vorübergehen"}],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"Vom Vorübergehen","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5","title":"1.","order":75,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_1C_5","text":"## 1.\n\nAch, liegt Alles schon welk und grau, was noch jüngst auf dieser Wiese grün und bunt stand? Und wie vielen Honig der Hoffnung trug ich von hier in meine Bienenkörbe!\n\nDiese jungen Herzen sind alle schon alt geworden,—und nicht alt einmal! nur müde, gemein, bequem:—sie heissen es „Wir sind wieder fromm geworden.“\n\nNoch jüngst sah ich sie in der Frühe auf tapferen Füssen hinauslaufen: aber ihre Füsse der Erkenntniss wurden müde, und nun verleumden sie auch noch ihre Morgen-Tapferkeit!\n\nWahrlich, Mancher von ihnen hob einst die Beine wie ein Tänzer, ihm winkte das Lachen in meiner Weisheit:—da besann er sich. Eben sah ich ihn krumm—zum Kreuze kriechen.\n\nUm Licht und Freiheit flatterten sie einst gleich Mücken und jungen Dichtern. Ein Wenig älter, ein Wenig kälter: und schon sind sie Dunkler und Munkler und Ofenhocker.\n\nVerzagte ihnen wohl das Herz darob, dass mich die Einsamkeit verschlang gleich einem Wallfische? Lauschte ihr Ohr wohl sehnsüchtig-lange umsonst nach mir und meinen Trompeten- und Herolds-Rufen?\n\n—Ach! Immer sind ihrer nur Wenige, deren Herz einen langen Muth und Übermuth hat; und solchen bleibt auch der Geist geduldsam. Der Rest aber ist feige .\n\nDer Rest: das sind immer die Allermeisten, der Alltag, der Überfluss, die Viel-zu-Vielen—diese alle sind feige!—\n\nWer meiner Art ist, dem werden auch die Erlebnisse meiner Art über den Weg laufen: also, dass seine ersten Gesellen Leichname und Possenreisser sein müssen.\n\nSeine zweiten Gesellen aber—die werden sich seine Gläubigen heissen: ein lebendiger Schwarm, viel Liebe, viel Thorheit, viel unbärtige Verehrung.\n\nAn diese Gläubigen soll Der nicht sein Herz binden, wer meiner Art unter Menschen ist; an diese Lenze und bunte Wiesen soll Der nicht glauben, wer die flüchtig-feige Menschenart kennt!\n\nKönnten sie anders, so würden sie auch anders wollen . Halb- und Halbe verderben alles Ganze. Dass Blätter welk werden,—was ist da zu klagen!\n\nLass sie fahren und fallen, oh Zarathustra, und klage nicht! Lieber noch blase mit raschelnden Winden unter sie,—\n\n—blase unter diese Blätter, oh Zarathustra: dass alles Welke schneller noch von dir davonlaufen!—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0002","text":"Ach, liegt Alles schon welk und grau, was noch jüngst auf dieser Wiese grün und bunt stand? Und wie vielen Honig der Hoffnung trug ich von hier in meine Bienenkörbe!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0003","text":"Diese jungen Herzen sind alle schon alt geworden,—und nicht alt einmal! nur müde, gemein, bequem:—sie heissen es „Wir sind wieder fromm geworden.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0004","text":"Noch jüngst sah ich sie in der Frühe auf tapferen Füssen hinauslaufen: aber ihre Füsse der Erkenntniss wurden müde, und nun verleumden sie auch noch ihre Morgen-Tapferkeit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0005","text":"Wahrlich, Mancher von ihnen hob einst die Beine wie ein Tänzer, ihm winkte das Lachen in meiner Weisheit:—da besann er sich. Eben sah ich ihn krumm—zum Kreuze kriechen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0006","text":"Um Licht und Freiheit flatterten sie einst gleich Mücken und jungen Dichtern. Ein Wenig älter, ein Wenig kälter: und schon sind sie Dunkler und Munkler und Ofenhocker.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0007","text":"Verzagte ihnen wohl das Herz darob, dass mich die Einsamkeit verschlang gleich einem Wallfische? Lauschte ihr Ohr wohl sehnsüchtig-lange umsonst nach mir und meinen Trompeten- und Herolds-Rufen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0008","text":"—Ach! Immer sind ihrer nur Wenige, deren Herz einen langen Muth und Übermuth hat; und solchen bleibt auch der Geist geduldsam. Der Rest aber ist feige .","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0009","text":"Der Rest: das sind immer die Allermeisten, der Alltag, der Überfluss, die Viel-zu-Vielen—diese alle sind feige!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0010","text":"Wer meiner Art ist, dem werden auch die Erlebnisse meiner Art über den Weg laufen: also, dass seine ersten Gesellen Leichname und Possenreisser sein müssen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0011","text":"Seine zweiten Gesellen aber—die werden sich seine Gläubigen heissen: ein lebendiger Schwarm, viel Liebe, viel Thorheit, viel unbärtige Verehrung.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0012","text":"An diese Gläubigen soll Der nicht sein Herz binden, wer meiner Art unter Menschen ist; an diese Lenze und bunte Wiesen soll Der nicht glauben, wer die flüchtig-feige Menschenart kennt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0013","text":"Könnten sie anders, so würden sie auch anders wollen . Halb- und Halbe verderben alles Ganze. Dass Blätter welk werden,—was ist da zu klagen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0014","text":"Lass sie fahren und fallen, oh Zarathustra, und klage nicht! Lieber noch blase mit raschelnden Winden unter sie,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0015","text":"—blase unter diese Blätter, oh Zarathustra: dass alles Welke schneller noch von dir davonlaufen!—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"3723276d107a321fe241b81b34292722849d56bfe641c1d71f6449f05cfca857","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182704193296Z_89b1e79a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1.","source_sha256":"3723276d107a321fe241b81b34292722849d56bfe641c1d71f6449f05cfca857","sha256":"0081e2cd1cee9976eb8fc28c3d087ca253bb21aaaadd7771dc01c3425c9dd1b4","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:27:04.193326+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0002","text":"Ah, does all that only lately stood green and colourful upon this meadow already lie withered and grey? And how much honey of hope I carried from here into my beehives!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0003","text":"These young hearts have all already grown old,—and not even old! only weary, vulgar, comfortable:—they call it “We have become pious again.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0004","text":"Only lately I saw them run out in the early morning upon valiant feet: but their feet of knowledge grew weary, and now they even slander their morning-valour!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0005","text":"Verily, many a one among them once lifted his legs like a dancer; the laughter in my wisdom beckoned to him:—then he bethought himself. Just now I saw him crooked—crawling to the cross.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0006","text":"Once they fluttered about light and freedom like gnats and young poets. A little older, a little colder: and already they are Darkeners and Mutterers and stove-sitters.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0007","text":"Did their hearts perhaps lose courage because solitude swallowed me like a whale? Did their ears perhaps listen yearningly-long in vain for me and my trumpet- and herald-calls?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0008","text":"—Ah! There are always only few among them whose hearts have a long courage and over-courage; and in such men the spirit too remains patient. But the rest are cowardly .","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0009","text":"The rest: these are always the vast majority, the everyday, the superfluity, the much-too-many—these all are cowardly!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0010","text":"He who is of my kind will also encounter experiences of my kind in his path: thus his first companions must be corpses and buffoons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0011","text":"But his second companions—they will call themselves his believers: a living swarm, much love, much folly, much unbearded veneration.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0012","text":"He who is of my kind among men should not bind his heart to these believers; he who knows the fleeting-cowardly human kind should not believe in these springs and colourful meadows!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0013","text":"Could they be otherwise, then they would also will otherwise . Halves and half-men spoil everything whole. That leaves wither,—what is there to lament in that!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0014","text":"Let them go and fall, O Zarathustra, and do not lament! Better still, blow rustling winds beneath them,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0015","text":"—blow beneath these leaves, O Zarathustra: that everything withered may run away from thee still faster!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_5:P0015"]}],"source_digest":"ecdf5b2d75a8526796f2c5ffa8841abcf7abe31d80db8668b2abcdd1942b21a7","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Apostates","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On the Apostates · 1","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On the Apostates","1."],"original_title":"1."}],"source_title_context":"Von den Abtrünnigen","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von den Abtrünnigen · 1","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von den Abtrünnigen","1."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"1.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5","title":"2.","order":76,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_2C_5","text":"## 2.\n\n„Wir sind wieder fromm geworden“ —so bekennen diese Abtrünnigen; und Manche von ihnen sind noch zu feige, also zu bekennen.\n\nDenen sehe ich in’s Auge,—denen sage ich es in’s Gesicht und in die Röthe ihrer Wangen: ihr seid Solche, welche wieder beten !\n\nEs ist aber eine Schmach, zu beten! Nicht für Alle, aber für dich und mich und wer auch im Kopfe sein Gewissen hat. Für dich ist es eine Schmach, zu beten!\n\nDu weisst es wohl: dein feiger Teufel in dir, der gerne Hände-falten und Hände-in-den-Schooss-legen und es bequemer haben möchte:—dieser feige Teufel redet dir zu „es giebt einen Gott!“\n\nDamit aber gehörst du zur lichtscheuen Art, denen Licht nimmer Ruhe lässt; nun musst du täglich deinen Kopf tiefer in Nacht und Dunst stecken!\n\nUnd wahrlich, du wähltest die Stunde gut: denn eben wieder fliegen die Nachtvögel aus. Die Stunde kam allem lichtscheuen Volke, die Abend- und Feierstunde, wo es nicht—„feiert.“\n\nIch höre und rieche es: es kam ihre Stunde für Jagd und Umzug, nicht zwar für eine wilde Jagd, sondern für eine zahme lahme schnüffelnde Leisetreter- und Leisebeter-Jagd,—\n\n—für eine Jagd auf seelenvolle Duckmäuser: alle Herzens- Mausefallen sind jetzt wieder aufgestellt! Und wo ich einen Vorhang aufhebe, da kommt ein Nachtfalterchen herausgestürzt.\n\nHockte es da wohl zusammen mit einem andern Nachtfalterchen? Denn überall rieche ich kleine verkrochne Gemeinden; und wo es Kämmerlein giebt, da giebt es neue Bet-Brüder drin und den Dunst von Bet-Brüdern.\n\nSie sitzen lange Abende bei einander und sprechen: lasset uns wieder werden wie die Kindlein und „lieber Gott“ sagen!—an Mund und Magen verdorben durch die frommen Zuckerbäcker.\n\nOder sie sehen lange Abende einer listigen lauernden Kreuzspinne zu, welche den Spinnen selber Klugheit predigt und also lehrt: „unter Kreuzen ist gut spinnen!“\n\nOder sie sitzen Tags über mit Angelruthen an Sümpfen und glauben sich tief damit; aber wer dort fischt, wo es keine Fische giebt, den heisse ich noch nicht einmal oberflächlich!\n\nOder sie lernen fromm-froh die Harfe schlagen bei einem Lieder-Dichter, der sich gern jungen Weibchen in’s Herz harfnen möchte:—denn er wurde der alten Weibchen müde und ihres Lobpreisens.\n\nOder sie lernen gruseln bei einem gelehrten Halb-Tollen, der in dunklen Zimmern wartet, dass ihm die Geister kommen—und der Geist ganz davonläuft!\n\nOder sie horchen einem alten umgetriebnen Schnurr- und Knurrpfeifer zu, der trüben Winden die Trübsal der Töne ablernte; nun pfeift er nach dem Winde und predigt in trüben Tönen Trübsal.\n\nUnd Einige von ihnen sind sogar Nachtwächter geworden: die verstehen jetzt in Hörner zu blasen und Nachts umherzugehn und alte Sachen aufzuwecken, die lange schon eingeschlafen sind.\n\nFünf Worte von alten Sachen hörte ich gestern Nachts an der Garten-Mauer: die kamen von solchen alten betrübten trocknen Nachtwächtern.\n\n„Für einen Vater sorgt er nicht genug um seine Kinder: Menschen-Väter thun diess besser!“—\n\n„Er ist zu alt! Er sorgt schon gar nicht mehr um seine Kinder“—also antwortete der andere Nachtwächter.\n\n„ Hat er denn Kinder? Niemand kann’s beweisen, wenn er’s selber nicht beweist! Ich wollte längst, er bewiese es einmal gründlich.“\n\n„Beweisen? Als ob Der je Etwas bewiesen hätte! Beweisen fällt ihm schwer; er hält grosse Stücke darauf, dass man ihm glaubt.“\n\n„Ja! Ja! Der Glaube macht ihn selig, der Glaube an ihn. Das ist so die Art alter Leute! So geht’s uns auch!“—\n\n—Also sprachen zu einander die zwei alten Nachtwächter und Lichtscheuchen, und tuteten darauf betrübt in ihre Hörner: so geschah’s gestern Nachts an der Garten-Mauer.\n\nMir aber wand sich das Herz vor Lachen und wollte brechen und wusste nicht, wohin? und sank in’s Zwerchfell.\n\nWahrlich, das wird noch mein Tod sein, dass ich vor Lachen ersticke, wenn ich Esel betrunken sehe und Nachtwächter also an Gott zweifeln höre.\n\nIst es denn nicht lange vorbei auch für alle solche Zweifel? Wer darf noch solche alte eingeschlafne lichtscheue Sachen aufwecken!\n\nMit den alten Göttern gieng es ja lange schon zu Ende:—und wahrlich, ein gutes fröhliches Götter-Ende hatten sie!\n\nSie „dämmerten“ sich nicht zu Tode,—das lügt man wohl! Vielmehr: sie haben sich selber einmal zu Tode— gelacht !\n\nDas geschah, als das gottloseste Wort von einem Gotte selber ausgieng,—das Wort: „Es ist Ein Gott! Du sollst keinen andern Gott haben neben mir!“—\n\n—ein alter Grimm-Bart von Gott, ein eifersüchtiger vergass sich also:\n\nUnd alle Götter lachten damals und wackelten auf ihren Stühlen und riefen: „Ist das nicht eben Göttlichkeit, dass es Götter, aber keinen Gott giebt?“\n\nWer Ohren hat, der höre.—\n\nAlso redete Zarathustra in der Stadt, die er liebte und welche zubenannt ist die bunte Kuh. Von hier nämlich hatte er nur noch zwei Tage zu gehen, dass er wieder in seine Höhle käme und zu seinen Thieren; seine Seele aber frohlockte beständig ob der Nähe seiner Heimkehr.—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0002","text":"„Wir sind wieder fromm geworden“ —so bekennen diese Abtrünnigen; und Manche von ihnen sind noch zu feige, also zu bekennen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0003","text":"Denen sehe ich in’s Auge,—denen sage ich es in’s Gesicht und in die Röthe ihrer Wangen: ihr seid Solche, welche wieder beten !","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0004","text":"Es ist aber eine Schmach, zu beten! Nicht für Alle, aber für dich und mich und wer auch im Kopfe sein Gewissen hat. Für dich ist es eine Schmach, zu beten!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0005","text":"Du weisst es wohl: dein feiger Teufel in dir, der gerne Hände-falten und Hände-in-den-Schooss-legen und es bequemer haben möchte:—dieser feige Teufel redet dir zu „es giebt einen Gott!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0006","text":"Damit aber gehörst du zur lichtscheuen Art, denen Licht nimmer Ruhe lässt; nun musst du täglich deinen Kopf tiefer in Nacht und Dunst stecken!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0007","text":"Und wahrlich, du wähltest die Stunde gut: denn eben wieder fliegen die Nachtvögel aus. Die Stunde kam allem lichtscheuen Volke, die Abend- und Feierstunde, wo es nicht—„feiert.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0008","text":"Ich höre und rieche es: es kam ihre Stunde für Jagd und Umzug, nicht zwar für eine wilde Jagd, sondern für eine zahme lahme schnüffelnde Leisetreter- und Leisebeter-Jagd,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0009","text":"—für eine Jagd auf seelenvolle Duckmäuser: alle Herzens- Mausefallen sind jetzt wieder aufgestellt! Und wo ich einen Vorhang aufhebe, da kommt ein Nachtfalterchen herausgestürzt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0010","text":"Hockte es da wohl zusammen mit einem andern Nachtfalterchen? Denn überall rieche ich kleine verkrochne Gemeinden; und wo es Kämmerlein giebt, da giebt es neue Bet-Brüder drin und den Dunst von Bet-Brüdern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0011","text":"Sie sitzen lange Abende bei einander und sprechen: lasset uns wieder werden wie die Kindlein und „lieber Gott“ sagen!—an Mund und Magen verdorben durch die frommen Zuckerbäcker.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0012","text":"Oder sie sehen lange Abende einer listigen lauernden Kreuzspinne zu, welche den Spinnen selber Klugheit predigt und also lehrt: „unter Kreuzen ist gut spinnen!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0013","text":"Oder sie sitzen Tags über mit Angelruthen an Sümpfen und glauben sich tief damit; aber wer dort fischt, wo es keine Fische giebt, den heisse ich noch nicht einmal oberflächlich!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0014","text":"Oder sie lernen fromm-froh die Harfe schlagen bei einem Lieder-Dichter, der sich gern jungen Weibchen in’s Herz harfnen möchte:—denn er wurde der alten Weibchen müde und ihres Lobpreisens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0015","text":"Oder sie lernen gruseln bei einem gelehrten Halb-Tollen, der in dunklen Zimmern wartet, dass ihm die Geister kommen—und der Geist ganz davonläuft!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0016","text":"Oder sie horchen einem alten umgetriebnen Schnurr- und Knurrpfeifer zu, der trüben Winden die Trübsal der Töne ablernte; nun pfeift er nach dem Winde und predigt in trüben Tönen Trübsal.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0017","text":"Und Einige von ihnen sind sogar Nachtwächter geworden: die verstehen jetzt in Hörner zu blasen und Nachts umherzugehn und alte Sachen aufzuwecken, die lange schon eingeschlafen sind.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0018","text":"Fünf Worte von alten Sachen hörte ich gestern Nachts an der Garten-Mauer: die kamen von solchen alten betrübten trocknen Nachtwächtern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0019","text":"„Für einen Vater sorgt er nicht genug um seine Kinder: Menschen-Väter thun diess besser!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0020","text":"„Er ist zu alt! Er sorgt schon gar nicht mehr um seine Kinder“—also antwortete der andere Nachtwächter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0021","text":"„ Hat er denn Kinder? Niemand kann’s beweisen, wenn er’s selber nicht beweist! Ich wollte längst, er bewiese es einmal gründlich.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0022","text":"„Beweisen? Als ob Der je Etwas bewiesen hätte! Beweisen fällt ihm schwer; er hält grosse Stücke darauf, dass man ihm glaubt.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0023","text":"„Ja! Ja! Der Glaube macht ihn selig, der Glaube an ihn. Das ist so die Art alter Leute! So geht’s uns auch!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0024","text":"—Also sprachen zu einander die zwei alten Nachtwächter und Lichtscheuchen, und tuteten darauf betrübt in ihre Hörner: so geschah’s gestern Nachts an der Garten-Mauer.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0025","text":"Mir aber wand sich das Herz vor Lachen und wollte brechen und wusste nicht, wohin? und sank in’s Zwerchfell.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0026","text":"Wahrlich, das wird noch mein Tod sein, dass ich vor Lachen ersticke, wenn ich Esel betrunken sehe und Nachtwächter also an Gott zweifeln höre.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0027","text":"Ist es denn nicht lange vorbei auch für alle solche Zweifel? Wer darf noch solche alte eingeschlafne lichtscheue Sachen aufwecken!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0028","text":"Mit den alten Göttern gieng es ja lange schon zu Ende:—und wahrlich, ein gutes fröhliches Götter-Ende hatten sie!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0029","text":"Sie „dämmerten“ sich nicht zu Tode,—das lügt man wohl! Vielmehr: sie haben sich selber einmal zu Tode— gelacht !","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0030","text":"Das geschah, als das gottloseste Wort von einem Gotte selber ausgieng,—das Wort: „Es ist Ein Gott! Du sollst keinen andern Gott haben neben mir!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0031","text":"—ein alter Grimm-Bart von Gott, ein eifersüchtiger vergass sich also:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0032","text":"Und alle Götter lachten damals und wackelten auf ihren Stühlen und riefen: „Ist das nicht eben Göttlichkeit, dass es Götter, aber keinen Gott giebt?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0033","text":"Wer Ohren hat, der höre.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0034","text":"Also redete Zarathustra in der Stadt, die er liebte und welche zubenannt ist die bunte Kuh. Von hier nämlich hatte er nur noch zwei Tage zu gehen, dass er wieder in seine Höhle käme und zu seinen Thieren; seine Seele aber frohlockte beständig ob der Nähe seiner Heimkehr.—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"bc30967d0e5d194c4466697c81826385af323c7acf5537328368c9df9c82a92a","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182727469530Z_51abf5fa","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2.","source_sha256":"bc30967d0e5d194c4466697c81826385af323c7acf5537328368c9df9c82a92a","sha256":"c6d7083958f584c56e064e06bd13d0987c3923ac45d45a0b4f2143393ce04d59","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:27:27.469580+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0002","text":"“We have become pious again”—thus confess these apostates; and some of them are still too cowardly to confess it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0003","text":"I look them in the eye,—I tell them to their faces and into the blush of their cheeks: you are such as pray again!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0004","text":"But it is a disgrace to pray! Not for everyone, but for you and me and whoever has his conscience in his head. For you it is a disgrace to pray!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0005","text":"You know it well: your cowardly devil within you, who would gladly fold hands and lay hands in the lap and have things more comfortable:—this cowardly devil tells you “there is a God!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0006","text":"But thereby you belong to the light-shunning kind, whom light never lets rest; now you must daily thrust your head deeper into night and vapour!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0007","text":"And verily, you chose the hour well: for just now the night-birds are flying out again. The hour came for every light-shunning people, the evening and festival hour, when it does not—“keep festival.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0008","text":"I hear and smell it: their hour for hunting and procession has come, not indeed for a wild hunt, but for a tame, lame, sniffing, soft-treading and soft-praying hunt,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0009","text":"—for a hunt after soulful cringers: all the heart-mousetraps are now set up again! And wherever I lift a curtain, a little moth comes darting out.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0010","text":"Was it perhaps huddling there together with another little moth? For everywhere I smell little hidden-away communities; and where there are little chambers, there are new praying-brethren within and the vapour of praying-brethren.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0011","text":"They sit together through long evenings and say: let us become again like little children and say “dear God”!—their mouths and stomachs corrupted by the pious confectioners.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0012","text":"Or through long evenings they watch a cunning, lurking cross-spider, which preaches cleverness to the spiders themselves and thus teaches: “under crosses it is good to spin!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0013","text":"Or they sit by day with fishing-rods at marshes and imagine themselves profound thereby; but whoever fishes where there are no fish, I do not call even superficial!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0014","text":"Or, piously merry, they learn to strike the harp with a song-poet who would gladly harp himself into the hearts of young women:—for he had grown weary of old women and their praises.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0015","text":"Or they learn to shudder with a learned half-madman, who waits in dark rooms for the spirits to come to him—and whose spirit runs clean away!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0016","text":"Or they listen to an old driven-about droning and growling whistler, who learned the sorrowfulness of tones from gloomy winds; now he whistles after the wind and preaches sorrowfulness in gloomy tones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0017","text":"And some of them have even become night-watchmen: they now understand how to blow horns and walk about at night and awaken old things which have long been asleep.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0018","text":"Five words about old things I heard last night at the garden-wall: they came from such old sorrowful dry night-watchmen.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0019","text":"“As a father, he does not care enough for his children: human fathers do this better!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0020","text":"“He is too old! He no longer cares at all for his children”—thus answered the other night-watchman.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0021","text":"“Has he then children? No one can prove it, unless he himself proves it! For a long time now I have wished that he would prove it thoroughly for once.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0022","text":"“Prove? As though He had ever proved anything! Proving is difficult for him; he sets great store by being believed.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0023","text":"“Yes! Yes! Belief makes him blessed, belief in him. That is the way of old people! That is how it is with us too!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0024","text":"—Thus spoke to one another the two old night-watchmen and light-scarecrows, and then sorrowfully tootled into their horns: thus it happened last night at the garden-wall.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0025","text":"But my heart twisted with laughter and wanted to burst and did not know whither? and sank into the diaphragm.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0026","text":"Verily, it will yet be my death, that I choke with laughter when I see asses drunk and hear night-watchmen thus doubt God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0027","text":"Has it not long since been over, then, for all such doubts as well? Who may still awaken such old, slumbering, light-shy things!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0028","text":"With the old gods it had indeed long been coming to an end:—and verily, they had a good, cheerful end of the gods!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0029","text":"They did not “grow dim” unto death,—that is surely a lie! Rather: they once laughed themselves—to death!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0030","text":"That happened when the most godless word issued from a god himself,—the word: “There is One God! Thou shalt have no other God beside me!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0031","text":"—an old grim-beard of a god, a jealous one, thus forgot himself:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0032","text":"And all the gods then laughed and rocked upon their chairs and cried: “Is it not divinity itself, that there are gods, but no God?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0033","text":"He who has ears, let him hear.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0034","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra in the city that he loved and which is surnamed the pied cow. For from here he had only two more days to walk before he would return to his cave and to his animals; but his soul rejoiced continually at the nearness of his homecoming.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_5:P0034"]}],"source_digest":"9c541e76929ac183c24ea3788d67d010b72a3466e568991f6ab76f35d2e6d514","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Apostates","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On the Apostates · 2","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On the Apostates","2."],"original_title":"2."}],"source_title_context":"Von den Abtrünnigen","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von den Abtrünnigen · 2","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von den Abtrünnigen","2."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"2.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH","title":"The Homecoming","order":77,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_DIEH","text":"Oh Einsamkeit! Du meine Heimat Einsamkeit! Zu lange lebte ich wild in wilder Fremde, als dass ich nicht mit Thränen zu dir heimkehrte!\n\nNun drohe mir nur mit dem Finger, wie Mütter drohn, nein lächle mir zu, wie Mütter lächeln, nun sprich nur: „Und wer war das, der wie ein Sturmwind einst von mir davonstürmte?—\n\n—der scheidend rief: zu lange sass ich bei der Einsamkeit, da verlernte ich das Schweigen! Das —lerntest du nun wohl?\n\nOh Zarathustra, Alles weiss ich: und dass du unter den Vielen verlassener warst, du Einer, als je bei mir!\n\nEin Anderes ist Verlassenheit, ein Anderes Einsamkeit: Das —lerntest du nun! Und dass du unter Menschen immer wild und fremd sein wirst:\n\n-Wild und fremd auch noch, wenn sie dich lieben: denn zuerst von Allem wollen sie geschont sein!\n\nHier aber bist du bei dir zu Heim und Hause; hier kannst du Alles hinausreden und alle Gründe ausschütten, Nichts schämt sich hier versteckter, verstockter Gefühle.\n\nHier kommen alle Dinge liebkosend zu deiner Rede und schmeicheln dir: denn sie wollen auf deinem Rücken reiten. Auf jedem Gleichniss reitest du hier zu jeder Wahrheit.\n\nAufrecht und aufrichtig darfst du hier zu allen Dingen reden: und wahrlich, wie Lob klingt es ihren Ohren, dass Einer mit allen Dingen—gerade redet!\n\nEin Anderes aber ist Verlassensein. Denn, weisst du noch, oh Zarathustra? Als damals dein Vogel über dir schrie, als du im Walde standest, unschlüssig, wohin? unkundig, einem Leichnam nahe:—\n\n—als du sprachst: mögen mich meine Thiere führen! Gefährlicher fand ich’s unter Menschen, als unter Thieren:— Das war Verlassenheit!\n\nUnd weisst du noch, oh Zarathustra? Als du auf deiner Insel sassest, unter leeren Eimern ein Brunnen Weins, gebend und ausgebend, unter Durstigen schenkend und ausschenkend:\n\n—bis du endlich durstig allein unter Trunkenen sassest und nächtlich klagtest „ist Nehmen nicht seliger als Geben? Und Stehlen noch seliger als Nehmen?“— Das war Verlassenheit!\n\nUnd weisst du noch, oh Zarathustra? Als deine stillste Stunde kam und dich von dir selber forttrieb, als sie mit bösem Flüstern sprach: „Sprich und zerbrich!“ -\n\n—als sie dir all dein Warten und Schweigen leid machte und deinen demüthigen Muth entmuthigte: Das war Verlassenheit!“—\n\nOh Einsamkeit! Du meine Heimat Einsamkeit! Wie selig und zärtlich redet deine Stimme zu mir!\n\nWir fragen einander nicht, wir klagen einander nicht, wir gehen offen mit einander durch offne Thüren.\n\nDenn offen ist es bei dir und hell; und auch die Stunden laufen hier auf leichteren Füssen. Im Dunklen nämlich trägt man schwerer an der Zeit, als im Lichte.\n\nHier springen mir alles Seins Worte und Wort-Schreine auf: alles Sein will hier Wort werden, alles Werden will hier von mir reden lernen.\n\nDa unten aber—da ist alles Reden umsonst! Da ist Vergessen und Vorübergehn die beste Weisheit: Das —lernte ich nun!\n\nWer Alles bei den Menschen begreifen wollte, der müsste Alles angreifen. Aber dazu habe ich zu reinliche Hände.\n\nIch mag schon ihren Athem nicht einathmen; ach, dass ich so lange unter ihrem Lärm und üblem Athem lebte!\n\nOh selige Stille um mich! Oh reine Gerüche um mich! Oh wie aus tiefer Brust diese Stille reinen Athem holt! Oh wie sie horcht, diese selige Stille!\n\nAber da unten—da redet Alles, da wird Alles überhört. Man mag seine Weisheit mit Glocken einläuten: die Krämer auf dem Markte werden sie mit Pfennigen überklingeln!\n\nAlles bei ihnen redet, Niemand weiss mehr zu verstehn. Alles fällt in’s Wasser, Nichts fällt mehr in tiefe Brunnen.\n\nAlles bei ihnen redet, Nichts geräth mehr und kommt zu Ende. Alles gackert, aber wer will noch still auf dem Neste sitzen und Eier brüten?\n\nAlles bei ihnen redet, Alles wird zerredet. Und was gestern noch zu hart war für die Zeit selber und ihren Zahn: heute hängt es zerschabt und zernagt aus den Mäulern der Heutigen.\n\nAlles bei ihnen redet, Alles wird verrathen. Und was einst Geheimniss hiess und Heimlichkeit tiefer Seelen, heute gehört es den Gassen-Trompetern und andern Schmetterlingen.\n\nOh Menschenwesen, du wunderliches! Du Lärm auf dunklen Gassen! Nun liegst du wieder hinter mir:—meine grösste Gefahr liegt hinter mir!\n\nIm Schonen und Mitleiden lag immer meine grösste Gefahr; und alles Menschenwesen will geschont und gelitten sein.\n\nMit verhaltenen Wahrheiten, mit Narrenhand und vernarrtem Herzen und reich an kleinen Lügen des Mitleidens:—also lebte ich immer unter Menschen.\n\nVerkleidet sass ich unter ihnen, bereit, mich zu verkennen, dass ich sie ertrüge, und gern mir zuredend „du Narr, du kennst die Menschen nicht!“\n\nMan verlernt die Menschen, wenn man unter Menschen lebt: zu viel Vordergrund ist an allen Menschen,—was sollen da weitsichtige, weit-süchtige Augen!\n\nUnd wenn sie mich verkannten: ich Narr schonte sie darob mehr, als mich: gewohnt zur Härte gegen mich und oft noch an mir selber mich rächend für diese Schonung.\n\nZerstochen von giftigen Fliegen und ausgehöhlt, dem Steine gleich, von vielen Tropfen Bosheit, so sass ich unter ihnen und redete mir noch zu: „unschuldig ist alles Kleine an seiner Kleinheit!“\n\nSonderlich Die, welche sich „die Guten“ heissen, fand ich als die giftigsten Fliegen: sie stechen in aller Unschuld, sie lügen in aller Unschuld; wie vermöchten sie, gegen mich—gerecht zu sein!\n\nWer unter den Guten lebt, den lehrt Mitleid lügen. Mitleid macht dumpfe Luft allen freien Seelen. Die Dummheit der Guten nämlich ist unergründlich.\n\nMich selber verbergen und meinen Reichthum— das lernte ich da unten: denn jeden fand ich noch arm am Geiste. Das war der Lug meines Mitleidens, dass ich bei jedem wusste,\n\n—dass ich jedem es ansah und anroch, was ihm Geistes genug und was ihm schon Geistes zuviel war!\n\nIhre steifen Weisen: ich hiess sie weise, nicht steif,—so lernte ich Worte verschlucken. Ihre Todtengräber: ich hiess sie Forscher und Prüfer,—so lernte ich Worte vertauschen.\n\nDie Todtengräber graben sich Krankheiten an. Unter altem Schutte ruhn schlimme Dünste. Man soll den Morast nicht aufrühren. Man soll auf Bergen leben.\n\nMit seligen Nüstern athme ich wieder Berges-Freiheit! Erlöst ist endlich meine Nase vom Geruch alles Menschenwesens!\n\nVon scharfen Lüften gekitzelt, wie von schäumenden Weinen, niest meine Seele,—niest und jubelt sich zu: Gesundheit!\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0001","text":"Oh Einsamkeit! Du meine Heimat Einsamkeit! Zu lange lebte ich wild in wilder Fremde, als dass ich nicht mit Thränen zu dir heimkehrte!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0002","text":"Nun drohe mir nur mit dem Finger, wie Mütter drohn, nein lächle mir zu, wie Mütter lächeln, nun sprich nur: „Und wer war das, der wie ein Sturmwind einst von mir davonstürmte?—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0003","text":"—der scheidend rief: zu lange sass ich bei der Einsamkeit, da verlernte ich das Schweigen! Das —lerntest du nun wohl?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0004","text":"Oh Zarathustra, Alles weiss ich: und dass du unter den Vielen verlassener warst, du Einer, als je bei mir!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0005","text":"Ein Anderes ist Verlassenheit, ein Anderes Einsamkeit: Das —lerntest du nun! Und dass du unter Menschen immer wild und fremd sein wirst:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0006","text":"-Wild und fremd auch noch, wenn sie dich lieben: denn zuerst von Allem wollen sie geschont sein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0007","text":"Hier aber bist du bei dir zu Heim und Hause; hier kannst du Alles hinausreden und alle Gründe ausschütten, Nichts schämt sich hier versteckter, verstockter Gefühle.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0008","text":"Hier kommen alle Dinge liebkosend zu deiner Rede und schmeicheln dir: denn sie wollen auf deinem Rücken reiten. Auf jedem Gleichniss reitest du hier zu jeder Wahrheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0009","text":"Aufrecht und aufrichtig darfst du hier zu allen Dingen reden: und wahrlich, wie Lob klingt es ihren Ohren, dass Einer mit allen Dingen—gerade redet!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0010","text":"Ein Anderes aber ist Verlassensein. Denn, weisst du noch, oh Zarathustra? Als damals dein Vogel über dir schrie, als du im Walde standest, unschlüssig, wohin? unkundig, einem Leichnam nahe:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0011","text":"—als du sprachst: mögen mich meine Thiere führen! Gefährlicher fand ich’s unter Menschen, als unter Thieren:— Das war Verlassenheit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0012","text":"Und weisst du noch, oh Zarathustra? Als du auf deiner Insel sassest, unter leeren Eimern ein Brunnen Weins, gebend und ausgebend, unter Durstigen schenkend und ausschenkend:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0013","text":"—bis du endlich durstig allein unter Trunkenen sassest und nächtlich klagtest „ist Nehmen nicht seliger als Geben? Und Stehlen noch seliger als Nehmen?“— Das war Verlassenheit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0014","text":"Und weisst du noch, oh Zarathustra? Als deine stillste Stunde kam und dich von dir selber forttrieb, als sie mit bösem Flüstern sprach: „Sprich und zerbrich!“ -","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0015","text":"—als sie dir all dein Warten und Schweigen leid machte und deinen demüthigen Muth entmuthigte: Das war Verlassenheit!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0016","text":"Oh Einsamkeit! Du meine Heimat Einsamkeit! Wie selig und zärtlich redet deine Stimme zu mir!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0017","text":"Wir fragen einander nicht, wir klagen einander nicht, wir gehen offen mit einander durch offne Thüren.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0018","text":"Denn offen ist es bei dir und hell; und auch die Stunden laufen hier auf leichteren Füssen. Im Dunklen nämlich trägt man schwerer an der Zeit, als im Lichte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0019","text":"Hier springen mir alles Seins Worte und Wort-Schreine auf: alles Sein will hier Wort werden, alles Werden will hier von mir reden lernen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0020","text":"Da unten aber—da ist alles Reden umsonst! Da ist Vergessen und Vorübergehn die beste Weisheit: Das —lernte ich nun!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0021","text":"Wer Alles bei den Menschen begreifen wollte, der müsste Alles angreifen. Aber dazu habe ich zu reinliche Hände.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0022","text":"Ich mag schon ihren Athem nicht einathmen; ach, dass ich so lange unter ihrem Lärm und üblem Athem lebte!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0023","text":"Oh selige Stille um mich! Oh reine Gerüche um mich! Oh wie aus tiefer Brust diese Stille reinen Athem holt! Oh wie sie horcht, diese selige Stille!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0024","text":"Aber da unten—da redet Alles, da wird Alles überhört. Man mag seine Weisheit mit Glocken einläuten: die Krämer auf dem Markte werden sie mit Pfennigen überklingeln!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0025","text":"Alles bei ihnen redet, Niemand weiss mehr zu verstehn. Alles fällt in’s Wasser, Nichts fällt mehr in tiefe Brunnen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0026","text":"Alles bei ihnen redet, Nichts geräth mehr und kommt zu Ende. Alles gackert, aber wer will noch still auf dem Neste sitzen und Eier brüten?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0027","text":"Alles bei ihnen redet, Alles wird zerredet. Und was gestern noch zu hart war für die Zeit selber und ihren Zahn: heute hängt es zerschabt und zernagt aus den Mäulern der Heutigen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0028","text":"Alles bei ihnen redet, Alles wird verrathen. Und was einst Geheimniss hiess und Heimlichkeit tiefer Seelen, heute gehört es den Gassen-Trompetern und andern Schmetterlingen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0029","text":"Oh Menschenwesen, du wunderliches! Du Lärm auf dunklen Gassen! Nun liegst du wieder hinter mir:—meine grösste Gefahr liegt hinter mir!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0030","text":"Im Schonen und Mitleiden lag immer meine grösste Gefahr; und alles Menschenwesen will geschont und gelitten sein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0031","text":"Mit verhaltenen Wahrheiten, mit Narrenhand und vernarrtem Herzen und reich an kleinen Lügen des Mitleidens:—also lebte ich immer unter Menschen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0032","text":"Verkleidet sass ich unter ihnen, bereit, mich zu verkennen, dass ich sie ertrüge, und gern mir zuredend „du Narr, du kennst die Menschen nicht!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0033","text":"Man verlernt die Menschen, wenn man unter Menschen lebt: zu viel Vordergrund ist an allen Menschen,—was sollen da weitsichtige, weit-süchtige Augen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0034","text":"Und wenn sie mich verkannten: ich Narr schonte sie darob mehr, als mich: gewohnt zur Härte gegen mich und oft noch an mir selber mich rächend für diese Schonung.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0035","text":"Zerstochen von giftigen Fliegen und ausgehöhlt, dem Steine gleich, von vielen Tropfen Bosheit, so sass ich unter ihnen und redete mir noch zu: „unschuldig ist alles Kleine an seiner Kleinheit!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0036","text":"Sonderlich Die, welche sich „die Guten“ heissen, fand ich als die giftigsten Fliegen: sie stechen in aller Unschuld, sie lügen in aller Unschuld; wie vermöchten sie, gegen mich—gerecht zu sein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0037","text":"Wer unter den Guten lebt, den lehrt Mitleid lügen. Mitleid macht dumpfe Luft allen freien Seelen. Die Dummheit der Guten nämlich ist unergründlich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0038","text":"Mich selber verbergen und meinen Reichthum— das lernte ich da unten: denn jeden fand ich noch arm am Geiste. Das war der Lug meines Mitleidens, dass ich bei jedem wusste,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0039","text":"—dass ich jedem es ansah und anroch, was ihm Geistes genug und was ihm schon Geistes zuviel war!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0040","text":"Ihre steifen Weisen: ich hiess sie weise, nicht steif,—so lernte ich Worte verschlucken. Ihre Todtengräber: ich hiess sie Forscher und Prüfer,—so lernte ich Worte vertauschen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0041","text":"Die Todtengräber graben sich Krankheiten an. Unter altem Schutte ruhn schlimme Dünste. Man soll den Morast nicht aufrühren. Man soll auf Bergen leben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0042","text":"Mit seligen Nüstern athme ich wieder Berges-Freiheit! Erlöst ist endlich meine Nase vom Geruch alles Menschenwesens!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0043","text":"Von scharfen Lüften gekitzelt, wie von schäumenden Weinen, niest meine Seele,—niest und jubelt sich zu: Gesundheit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0044","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"63a5afc718866101e4f410676167888668f9c44bd3c2aa2f7b8a6978df816d92","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182749807419Z_a930e1a5","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"The Homecoming","source_sha256":"63a5afc718866101e4f410676167888668f9c44bd3c2aa2f7b8a6978df816d92","sha256":"1fe3f3139de0868099871aca2178031266feb150419c6efa84a709a29905919a","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:27:49.807437+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0001","text":"O solitude! Thou my homeland solitude! Too long have I lived wild in wild foreignness, not to return home to thee with tears!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0002","text":"Now merely threaten me with thy finger, as mothers threaten, nay, smile upon me, as mothers smile, now merely say: “And who was it that once stormed away from me like a whirlwind?—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0003","text":"—who, departing, cried: too long have I sat with solitude, there I unlearned silence! That—thou hast now learned, perhaps?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0004","text":"O Zarathustra, I know everything: and that among the many thou wast more forsaken, thou one, than ever with me!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0005","text":"Forsakenness is one thing, solitude another: That—thou hast now learned! And that among men thou wilt always be wild and foreign:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0006","text":"—wild and foreign even when they love thee: for before all else they wish to be spared by thee!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0007","text":"But here thou art at home and in thy house with thyself; here thou canst speak out everything and pour out all grounds, here nothing is ashamed of concealed, obdurate feelings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0008","text":"Here all things come caressingly to thy speech and flatter thee: for they wish to ride upon thy back. Upon every similitude thou ridest here to every truth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0009","text":"Here thou mayest speak uprightly and sincerely to all things: and verily, it sounds like praise to their ears, that one speaks—straight—with all things!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0010","text":"But to be forsaken is another thing. For, dost thou still know, O Zarathustra? When at that time thy bird cried above thee, when thou stoodest in the forest, undecided, whither? unknowing, near to a corpse:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0011","text":"—when thou saidst: may my animals lead me! I found it more dangerous among men than among animals:— That was forsakenness!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0012","text":"And dost thou still know, O Zarathustra? When thou satst upon thy island, a well of wine among empty buckets, giving and dispensing, pouring out and pouring forth among the thirsty:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0013","text":"—until at last thou satst thirsty and alone among the drunken, and nightly didst lament: “is taking not more blessed than giving? And stealing yet more blessed than taking?”— That was forsakenness!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0014","text":"And dost thou still know, O Zarathustra? When thy stillest hour came and drove thee away from thyself, when it spoke with evil whispering: “Speak and break!” -","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0015","text":"—when it made thee weary of all thy waiting and silence and discouraged thy humble courage: That was forsakenness!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0016","text":"O solitude! Thou my homeland solitude! How blessedly and tenderly thy voice speaks to me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0017","text":"We do not question one another, we do not complain to one another, we go openly together through open doors.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0018","text":"For with thee it is open and bright; and the hours too run here on lighter feet. For in darkness one bears time more heavily than in the light.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0019","text":"Here all the words and word-shrines of being spring open for me: all being wishes here to become word, all becoming wishes here to learn from me how to speak.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0020","text":"But down there—there all speaking is in vain! There forgetting and passing-by are the best wisdom: That—I have now learned!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0021","text":"He who wished to comprehend everything among men would have to attack everything. But for that I have hands too clean.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0022","text":"I do not even like to breathe their breath; ah, that I lived so long amid their noise and foul breath!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0023","text":"O blessed silence around me! O pure scents around me! O how this silence draws pure breath from a deep breast! O how it listens, this blessed silence!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0024","text":"But down there—everything speaks, there everything is overheard. One may ring in one’s wisdom with bells: the shopkeepers in the market will out-ring it with pennies!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0025","text":"Everything among them talks; no one any longer knows how to understand. Everything falls into the water; nothing any longer falls into deep wells.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0026","text":"Everything among them talks; nothing any longer succeeds and comes to completion. Everything cackles, but who still wishes to sit quietly upon the nest and brood eggs?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0027","text":"Everything among them talks; everything is talked to pieces. And what yesterday was still too hard for time itself and its tooth: today it hangs chewed and gnawed from the mouths of those of today.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0028","text":"Everything among them talks; everything is betrayed. And what was once called secret and the secrecy of deep souls, today belongs to the street-trumpeters and other butterflies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0029","text":"O human being, thou strange one! Thou noise in dark streets! Now thou liest behind me once more:—my greatest danger lies behind me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0030","text":"In sparing and pitying always lay my greatest danger; and all human being wishes to be spared and suffered.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0031","text":"With restrained truths, with fool's hand and infatuated heart and rich in the little lies of pity:—thus I always lived among human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0032","text":"Disguised I sat among them, ready to misrecognise myself so that I might endure them, and gladly saying to myself, “thou fool, thou knowest not human beings!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0033","text":"One unlearns human beings when one lives among human beings: too much foreground is on all human beings,—what use are far-seeing, far-desiring eyes there!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0034","text":"And when they misrecognised me: I fool spared them for it more than myself: accustomed to hardness against myself and often still taking revenge upon myself for this sparing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0035","text":"Pricked by poisonous flies and hollowed out, like a stone, by many drops of malice, thus I sat among them and still said to myself: “everything small is innocent of its smallness!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0036","text":"Especially Those who call themselves “the Good,” I found to be the most poisonous flies: they sting in all innocence, they lie in all innocence; how could they—be just towards me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0037","text":"He who lives among the good, pity teaches to lie. Pity makes the air close for all free souls. For the stupidity of the good is unfathomable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0038","text":"To conceal myself and my wealth—this I learned down there: for I found everyone still poor in spirit. This was the lie of my pity, that I knew of everyone,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0039","text":"—that I saw and smelled upon everyone what was spirit enough for him and what was already too much spirit for him!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0040","text":"Their rigid wise men: I called them wise, not rigid,—thus I learned to swallow words. Their gravediggers: I called them researchers and examiners,—thus I learned to exchange words.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0041","text":"Gravediggers dig diseases into themselves. Under old rubble evil vapours rest. One ought not to stir up the mire. One ought to live upon mountains.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0042","text":"With blessed nostrils I breathe mountain-freedom again! At last my nose is delivered from the smell of all human being!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0043","text":"Tickled by sharp airs, as by foaming wines, my soul sneezes,—sneezes and jubilantly cries to itself: Health!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0044","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DIEH:P0044"]}],"source_digest":"faf5154dfddd54a3d18a0f8b0bbf352d754ee53b07a7099c31d3fce128351d63","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Die Heimkehr"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Die Heimkehr","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6","title":"1.","order":78,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_1C_6","text":"## 1.\n\nIm Traum, im letzten Morgentraume stand ich heut auf einem Vorgebirge,—jenseits der Welt, hielt eine Wage und wog die Welt.\n\nOh dass zu früh mir die Morgenröthe kam: die glühte mich wach, die Eifersüchtige! Eifersüchtig ist sie immer auf meine Morgentraum-Gluthen.\n\nMessbar für Den, der Zeit hat, wägbar für einen guten Wäger, erfliegbar für starke Fittige, errathbar für göttliche Nüsseknacker: also fand mein Traum die Welt:—\n\nMein Traum, ein kühner Segler, halb Schiff, halb Windsbraut, gleich Schmetterlingen schweigsam, ungeduldig gleich Edelfalken: wie hatte er doch zum Welt-Wägen heute Geduld und Weile!\n\nSprach ihm heimlich wohl meine Weisheit zu, meine lachende wache Tags-Weisheit, welche über alle „unendliche Welten“ spottet? Denn sie spricht: „wo Kraft ist, wird auch die Zahl Meisterin: die hat mehr Kraft.“\n\nWie sicher schaute mein Traum auf diese endliche Welt, nicht neugierig, nicht altgierig, nicht fürchtend, nicht bittend:—\n\n—als ob ein voller Apfel sich meiner Hand böte, ein reifer Goldapfel, mit kühl-sanfter sammtener Haut:—so bot sich mir die Welt:—\n\n—als ob ein Baum mir winke, ein breitästiger, starkwilliger, gekrümmt zur Lehne und noch zum Fussbrett für den Wegmüden: so stand die Welt auf meinem Vorgebirge:—\n\n—als ob zierliche Hände mir einen Schrein entgegentrügen,—einen Schrein offen für das Entzücken schamhafter verehrender Augen: also bot sich mir heute die Welt entgegen:—\n\n—nicht Räthsel genug, um Menschen-Liebe davon zu scheuchen, nicht Lösung genug, um Menschen-Weisheit einzuschläfern:—ein menschlich gutes Ding war mir heut die Welt, der man so Böses nachredet!\n\nWie danke ich es meinem Morgentraum, dass ich also in der Frühe heut die Welt wog! Als ein menschlich gutes Ding kam er zu mir, dieser Traum und Herzenströster!\n\nUnd dass ich’s ihm gleich thue am Tage und sein Bestes ihm nach- und ablerne: will ich jetzt die drei bösesten Dinge auf die Wage thun und menschlich gut abwägen.—\n\nWer da segnen lehrte, der lehrte auch fluchen: welches sind in der Welt die drei bestverfluchten Dinge? Diese will ich auf die Wage thun.\n\nWollust, Herrschsucht, Selbstsucht: diese Drei wurden bisher am besten verflucht und am schlimmsten beleu- und belügenmundet,—diese Drei will ich menschlich gut abwägen.\n\nWohlauf! Hier ist mein Vorgebirg und da das Meer: das wälzt sich zu mir heran, zottelig, schmeichlerisch, das getreue alte hundertköpfige Hunds-Ungethüm, das ich liebe.\n\nWohlauf! Hier will ich die Wage halten über gewälztem Meere: und auch einen Zeugen wähle ich, dass er zusehe,—dich, du Einsiedler-Baum, dich starkduftigen, breitgewölbten, den ich liebe!—\n\nAuf welcher Brücke geht zum Dereinst das Jetzt? Nach welchem Zwange zwingt das Hohe sich zum Niederen? Und was heisst auch das Höchste noch—hinaufwachsen?—\n\nNun steht die Wage gleich und still: drei schwere Fragen warf ich hinein, drei schwere Antworten trägt die andre Wagschale.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0002","text":"Im Traum, im letzten Morgentraume stand ich heut auf einem Vorgebirge,—jenseits der Welt, hielt eine Wage und wog die Welt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0003","text":"Oh dass zu früh mir die Morgenröthe kam: die glühte mich wach, die Eifersüchtige! Eifersüchtig ist sie immer auf meine Morgentraum-Gluthen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0004","text":"Messbar für Den, der Zeit hat, wägbar für einen guten Wäger, erfliegbar für starke Fittige, errathbar für göttliche Nüsseknacker: also fand mein Traum die Welt:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0005","text":"Mein Traum, ein kühner Segler, halb Schiff, halb Windsbraut, gleich Schmetterlingen schweigsam, ungeduldig gleich Edelfalken: wie hatte er doch zum Welt-Wägen heute Geduld und Weile!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0006","text":"Sprach ihm heimlich wohl meine Weisheit zu, meine lachende wache Tags-Weisheit, welche über alle „unendliche Welten“ spottet? Denn sie spricht: „wo Kraft ist, wird auch die Zahl Meisterin: die hat mehr Kraft.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0007","text":"Wie sicher schaute mein Traum auf diese endliche Welt, nicht neugierig, nicht altgierig, nicht fürchtend, nicht bittend:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0008","text":"—als ob ein voller Apfel sich meiner Hand böte, ein reifer Goldapfel, mit kühl-sanfter sammtener Haut:—so bot sich mir die Welt:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0009","text":"—als ob ein Baum mir winke, ein breitästiger, starkwilliger, gekrümmt zur Lehne und noch zum Fussbrett für den Wegmüden: so stand die Welt auf meinem Vorgebirge:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0010","text":"—als ob zierliche Hände mir einen Schrein entgegentrügen,—einen Schrein offen für das Entzücken schamhafter verehrender Augen: also bot sich mir heute die Welt entgegen:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0011","text":"—nicht Räthsel genug, um Menschen-Liebe davon zu scheuchen, nicht Lösung genug, um Menschen-Weisheit einzuschläfern:—ein menschlich gutes Ding war mir heut die Welt, der man so Böses nachredet!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0012","text":"Wie danke ich es meinem Morgentraum, dass ich also in der Frühe heut die Welt wog! Als ein menschlich gutes Ding kam er zu mir, dieser Traum und Herzenströster!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0013","text":"Und dass ich’s ihm gleich thue am Tage und sein Bestes ihm nach- und ablerne: will ich jetzt die drei bösesten Dinge auf die Wage thun und menschlich gut abwägen.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0014","text":"Wer da segnen lehrte, der lehrte auch fluchen: welches sind in der Welt die drei bestverfluchten Dinge? Diese will ich auf die Wage thun.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0015","text":"Wollust, Herrschsucht, Selbstsucht: diese Drei wurden bisher am besten verflucht und am schlimmsten beleu- und belügenmundet,—diese Drei will ich menschlich gut abwägen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0016","text":"Wohlauf! Hier ist mein Vorgebirg und da das Meer: das wälzt sich zu mir heran, zottelig, schmeichlerisch, das getreue alte hundertköpfige Hunds-Ungethüm, das ich liebe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0017","text":"Wohlauf! Hier will ich die Wage halten über gewälztem Meere: und auch einen Zeugen wähle ich, dass er zusehe,—dich, du Einsiedler-Baum, dich starkduftigen, breitgewölbten, den ich liebe!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0018","text":"Auf welcher Brücke geht zum Dereinst das Jetzt? Nach welchem Zwange zwingt das Hohe sich zum Niederen? Und was heisst auch das Höchste noch—hinaufwachsen?—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0019","text":"Nun steht die Wage gleich und still: drei schwere Fragen warf ich hinein, drei schwere Antworten trägt die andre Wagschale.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"deb50edb013f5e0113edb11738d19a65a9124f9f1d218ed4e3254ec7bde9da3f","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182803963464Z_fba9ace4","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1.","source_sha256":"deb50edb013f5e0113edb11738d19a65a9124f9f1d218ed4e3254ec7bde9da3f","sha256":"230575b3ac1f9bda3ce9796b8c4f7a3de1c187ac71a6a0b39e8ad9bfd3ab7dbb","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:28:03.963499+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0002","text":"In a dream, in the last morning dream, I stood today upon a promontory,—beyond the world, held a balance, and weighed the world.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0003","text":"Oh, that the dawn came to me too early: it glowed me awake, the jealous one! It is always jealous of the ardours of my morning dreams.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0004","text":"Measurable for him who has time, weighable for a good weigher, flyable-over for strong pinions, guessable for divine nutcrackers: thus my dream found the world:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0005","text":"My dream, a bold sailor, half ship, half wind-bride, silent like butterflies, impatient like noble falcons: how it nevertheless had patience and leisure today for weighing the world!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0006","text":"Did my wisdom perhaps secretly speak to it, my laughing, wakeful daytime-wisdom, which mocks at all “infinite worlds”? For it says: “where there is force, number too becomes mistress: it has more force.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0007","text":"How confidently my dream looked upon this finite world, not curious, not old-greedy, not fearful, not imploring:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0008","text":"—as though a full apple offered itself to my hand, a ripe golden apple, with cool-soft velvety skin:—thus the world offered itself to me:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0009","text":"—as though a tree beckoned to me, broad-branched, strong-willed, bent into a support and even into a footboard for the way-weary: thus the world stood upon my promontory:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0010","text":"—as though graceful hands bore a casket towards me,—a casket open for the delight of bashful, reverent eyes: thus today the world came towards me:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0011","text":"—not enough of a riddle to drive human love away from it, not enough of a solution to put human wisdom to sleep:—today the world was to me a humanly good thing, of which so much evil is spoken!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0012","text":"How I thank my morning dream that I thus weighed the world early today! As a humanly good thing it came to me, this dream and comforter of hearts!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0013","text":"And so that I may do the same as it by day and learn after and from it what is best in it: I now wish to put the three most evil things upon the balance and weigh them humanly well.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0014","text":"He who taught blessing also taught cursing: what are the three best-cursed things in the world? These I wish to put upon the balance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0015","text":"Voluptuousness, lust for power, selfishness: these three have hitherto been cursed best and most malignly spoken-of and lied-about,—these three I wish to weigh humanly well.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0016","text":"Come then! Here is my promontory and there the sea: it rolls itself towards me, shaggy, flattering, the faithful old hundred-headed dog-monster that I love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0017","text":"Come then! Here I wish to hold the balance above the rolling sea: and I also choose a witness that he may look on,—you, you hermit-tree, you strongly fragrant, broad-vaulted one, whom I love!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0018","text":"Upon what bridge does the Now pass over into the Some-day? By what compulsion does the High compel itself to the Low? And what does even the Highest still call—growing upward?—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0019","text":"Now the balance stands level and still: three heavy questions I threw into it, three heavy answers the other scale bears.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_6:P0019"]}],"source_digest":"f53dae43ee50d572e5cc83025efc922a0969ceee5b49d39000f678f012e7d7e4","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Three Evils","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On the Three Evils · 1","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On the Three Evils","1."],"original_title":"1."}],"source_title_context":"Von den drei Bösen","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von den drei Bösen · 1","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von den drei Bösen","1."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"1.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6","title":"2.","order":79,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_2C_6","text":"## 2.\n\nWollust: allen busshemdigen Leib-Verächtern ihr Stachel und Pfahl, und als „Welt“ verflucht bei allen Hinterweltlern: denn sie höhnt und narrt alle Wirr- und Irr-Lehrer.\n\nWollust: dem Gesindel das langsame Feuer, auf dem es verbrannt wird; allem wurmichten Holze, allen stinkenden Lumpen der bereite Brunst- und Brodel-Ofen.\n\nWollust: für die freien Herzen unschuldig und frei, das Garten-Glück der Erde, aller Zukunft Dankes-Überschwang an das Jetzt.\n\nWollust: nur dem Welken ein süsslich Gift, für die Löwen-Willigen aber die grosse Herzstärkung, und der ehrfürchtig geschonte Wein der Weine.\n\nWollust: das grosse Gleichniss-Glück für höheres Glück und höchste Hoffnung. Vielem nämlich ist Ehe verheissen und mehr als Ehe,—\n\n—Vielem, das fremder sich ist, als Mann und Weib:—und wer begriff es ganz, wie fremd sich Mann und Weib sind!\n\nWollust:—doch ich will Zäune um meine Gedanken haben und auch noch um meine Worte: dass mir nicht in meine Gärten die Schweine und Schwärmer brechen!—\n\nHerrschsucht: die Glüh-Geissel der härtesten Herzensharten; die grause Marter, die sich dem Grausamsten selber aufspart; die düstre Flamme lebendiger Scheiterhaufen.\n\nHerrschsucht: die boshafte Bremse, die den eitelsten Völkern aufgesetzt wird; die Verhöhnerin aller ungewissen Tugend; die auf jedem Rosse und jedem Stolze reitet.\n\nHerrschsucht: das Erdbeben, das alles Morsche und Höhlichte bricht und aufbricht; die rollende grollende strafende Zerbrecherin übertünchter Gräber; das blitzende Fragezeichen neben vorzeitigen Antworten.\n\nHerrschsucht: vor deren Blick der Mensch kriecht und duckt und fröhnt und niedriger wird als Schlange und Schwein:—bis endlich die grosse Verachtung aus ihm aufschreie—,\n\nHerrschsucht: die furchtbare Lehrerin der grossen Verachtung, welche Städten und Reichen in’s Antlitz predigt „hinweg mit dir!“—bis es aus ihnen selber aufschreie „hinweg mit mir !“\n\nHerrschsucht: die aber lockend auch zu Reinen und Einsamen und hinauf zu selbstgenugsamen Höhen steigt, glühend gleich einer Liebe, welche purpurne Seligkeiten lockend an Erdenhimmel malt.\n\nHerrschsucht: doch wer hiesse es Sucht , wenn das Hohe hinab nach Macht gelüstet! Wahrlich, nichts Sieches und Süchtiges ist an solchem Gelüsten und Niedersteigen!\n\nDass die einsame Höhe sich nicht ewig vereinsame und selbst begnüge; dass der Berg zu Thale komme und die Winde der Höhe zu den Niederungen:—\n\nOh wer fände den rechten Tauf- und Tugendnamen für solche Sehnsucht! „Schenkende Tugend“—so nannte das Unnennbare einst Zarathustra.\n\nUnd damals geschah es auch,—und wahrlich, es geschah zum ersten Male!—dass sein Wort die Selbstsucht selig pries, die heile, gesunde Selbstsucht, die aus mächtiger Seele quillt:—\n\n—aus mächtiger Seele, zu welcher der hohe Leib gehört, der schöne, sieghafte, erquickliche, um den herum jedwedes Ding Spiegel wird:\n\n—der geschmeidige überredende Leib, der Tänzer, dessen Gleichniss und Auszug die selbst-lustige Seele ist. Solcher Leiber und Seelen Selbst-Lust heisst sich selber: „Tugend.“\n\nMit ihren Worten von Gut und Schlecht schirmt sich solche Selbst-Lust wie mit heiligen Hainen; mit den Namen ihres Glücks bannt sie von sich alles Verächtliche.\n\nVon sich weg bannt sie alles Feige; sie spricht: Schlecht—das ist feige! Verächtlich dünkt ihr der immer Sorgende, Seufzende, Klägliche und wer auch die kleinsten Vortheile aufliest.\n\nSie verachtet auch alle wehselige Weisheit: denn, wahrlich, es giebt auch Weisheit, die im Dunklen blüht, eine Nachtschatten-Weisheit: als welche immer seufzt: „Alles ist eitel!“\n\nDas scheue Misstrauen gilt ihr gering, und Jeder, wer Schwüre statt Blicke und Hände will: auch alle allzu misstrauische Weisheit,—denn solche ist feiger Seelen Art.\n\nGeringer noch gilt ihr der Schnell-Gefällige, der Hündische, der gleich auf dem Rücken liegt, der Demüthige; und auch Weisheit giebt es, die demüthig und hündisch und fromm und schnellgefällig ist.\n\nVerhasst ist ihr gar und ein Ekel, wer nie sich wehren will, wer giftigen Speichel und böse Blicke hinunterschluckt, der All-zu-Geduldige, Alles-Dulder, Allgenügsame: das nämlich ist die knechtische Art.\n\nOb Einer vor Göttern und göttlichen Fusstritten knechtisch ist, ob vor Menschen und blöden Menschen-Meinungen: alle Knechts-Art speit sie an, diese selige Selbstsucht!\n\nSchlecht: so beisst sie Alles, was geknickt und knickerisch-knechtisch ist, unfreie Zwinker-Augen, gedruckte Herzen, und jene falsche nachgebende Art, welche mit breiten feigen Lippen küsst.\n\nUnd After-Weisheit: so heisst sie Alles, was Knechte und Greise und Müde witzeln; und sonderlich die ganze schlimme aberwitzige, überwitzige Priester-Narrheit!\n\nDie After-Weisen aber, alle die Priester, Weltmüden und wessen Seele von Weibs- und Knechtsart ist,—oh wie hat ihr Spiel von jeher der Selbstsucht übel mitgespielt!\n\nUnd Das gerade sollte Tugend sein und Tugend heissen, dass man der Selbstsucht übel mitspiele! Und „selbstlos“—so wünschten sich selber mit gutem Grunde alle diese weltmüden Feiglinge und Kreuzspinnen!\n\nAber denen Allen kommt nun der Tag, die Wandlung, das Richtschwert, der grosse Mittag : da soll Vieles offenbar werden!\n\nUnd wer das Ich heil und heilig spricht und die Selbstsucht selig, wahrlich, der spricht auch, was er weiss, ein Weissager: „Siehe, er kommt, er ist nahe, der grosse Mittag!“\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0002","text":"Wollust: allen busshemdigen Leib-Verächtern ihr Stachel und Pfahl, und als „Welt“ verflucht bei allen Hinterweltlern: denn sie höhnt und narrt alle Wirr- und Irr-Lehrer.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0003","text":"Wollust: dem Gesindel das langsame Feuer, auf dem es verbrannt wird; allem wurmichten Holze, allen stinkenden Lumpen der bereite Brunst- und Brodel-Ofen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0004","text":"Wollust: für die freien Herzen unschuldig und frei, das Garten-Glück der Erde, aller Zukunft Dankes-Überschwang an das Jetzt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0005","text":"Wollust: nur dem Welken ein süsslich Gift, für die Löwen-Willigen aber die grosse Herzstärkung, und der ehrfürchtig geschonte Wein der Weine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0006","text":"Wollust: das grosse Gleichniss-Glück für höheres Glück und höchste Hoffnung. Vielem nämlich ist Ehe verheissen und mehr als Ehe,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0007","text":"—Vielem, das fremder sich ist, als Mann und Weib:—und wer begriff es ganz, wie fremd sich Mann und Weib sind!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0008","text":"Wollust:—doch ich will Zäune um meine Gedanken haben und auch noch um meine Worte: dass mir nicht in meine Gärten die Schweine und Schwärmer brechen!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0009","text":"Herrschsucht: die Glüh-Geissel der härtesten Herzensharten; die grause Marter, die sich dem Grausamsten selber aufspart; die düstre Flamme lebendiger Scheiterhaufen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0010","text":"Herrschsucht: die boshafte Bremse, die den eitelsten Völkern aufgesetzt wird; die Verhöhnerin aller ungewissen Tugend; die auf jedem Rosse und jedem Stolze reitet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0011","text":"Herrschsucht: das Erdbeben, das alles Morsche und Höhlichte bricht und aufbricht; die rollende grollende strafende Zerbrecherin übertünchter Gräber; das blitzende Fragezeichen neben vorzeitigen Antworten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0012","text":"Herrschsucht: vor deren Blick der Mensch kriecht und duckt und fröhnt und niedriger wird als Schlange und Schwein:—bis endlich die grosse Verachtung aus ihm aufschreie—,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0013","text":"Herrschsucht: die furchtbare Lehrerin der grossen Verachtung, welche Städten und Reichen in’s Antlitz predigt „hinweg mit dir!“—bis es aus ihnen selber aufschreie „hinweg mit mir !“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0014","text":"Herrschsucht: die aber lockend auch zu Reinen und Einsamen und hinauf zu selbstgenugsamen Höhen steigt, glühend gleich einer Liebe, welche purpurne Seligkeiten lockend an Erdenhimmel malt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0015","text":"Herrschsucht: doch wer hiesse es Sucht , wenn das Hohe hinab nach Macht gelüstet! Wahrlich, nichts Sieches und Süchtiges ist an solchem Gelüsten und Niedersteigen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0016","text":"Dass die einsame Höhe sich nicht ewig vereinsame und selbst begnüge; dass der Berg zu Thale komme und die Winde der Höhe zu den Niederungen:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0017","text":"Oh wer fände den rechten Tauf- und Tugendnamen für solche Sehnsucht! „Schenkende Tugend“—so nannte das Unnennbare einst Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0018","text":"Und damals geschah es auch,—und wahrlich, es geschah zum ersten Male!—dass sein Wort die Selbstsucht selig pries, die heile, gesunde Selbstsucht, die aus mächtiger Seele quillt:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0019","text":"—aus mächtiger Seele, zu welcher der hohe Leib gehört, der schöne, sieghafte, erquickliche, um den herum jedwedes Ding Spiegel wird:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0020","text":"—der geschmeidige überredende Leib, der Tänzer, dessen Gleichniss und Auszug die selbst-lustige Seele ist. Solcher Leiber und Seelen Selbst-Lust heisst sich selber: „Tugend.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0021","text":"Mit ihren Worten von Gut und Schlecht schirmt sich solche Selbst-Lust wie mit heiligen Hainen; mit den Namen ihres Glücks bannt sie von sich alles Verächtliche.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0022","text":"Von sich weg bannt sie alles Feige; sie spricht: Schlecht—das ist feige! Verächtlich dünkt ihr der immer Sorgende, Seufzende, Klägliche und wer auch die kleinsten Vortheile aufliest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0023","text":"Sie verachtet auch alle wehselige Weisheit: denn, wahrlich, es giebt auch Weisheit, die im Dunklen blüht, eine Nachtschatten-Weisheit: als welche immer seufzt: „Alles ist eitel!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0024","text":"Das scheue Misstrauen gilt ihr gering, und Jeder, wer Schwüre statt Blicke und Hände will: auch alle allzu misstrauische Weisheit,—denn solche ist feiger Seelen Art.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0025","text":"Geringer noch gilt ihr der Schnell-Gefällige, der Hündische, der gleich auf dem Rücken liegt, der Demüthige; und auch Weisheit giebt es, die demüthig und hündisch und fromm und schnellgefällig ist.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0026","text":"Verhasst ist ihr gar und ein Ekel, wer nie sich wehren will, wer giftigen Speichel und böse Blicke hinunterschluckt, der All-zu-Geduldige, Alles-Dulder, Allgenügsame: das nämlich ist die knechtische Art.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0027","text":"Ob Einer vor Göttern und göttlichen Fusstritten knechtisch ist, ob vor Menschen und blöden Menschen-Meinungen: alle Knechts-Art speit sie an, diese selige Selbstsucht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0028","text":"Schlecht: so beisst sie Alles, was geknickt und knickerisch-knechtisch ist, unfreie Zwinker-Augen, gedruckte Herzen, und jene falsche nachgebende Art, welche mit breiten feigen Lippen küsst.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0029","text":"Und After-Weisheit: so heisst sie Alles, was Knechte und Greise und Müde witzeln; und sonderlich die ganze schlimme aberwitzige, überwitzige Priester-Narrheit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0030","text":"Die After-Weisen aber, alle die Priester, Weltmüden und wessen Seele von Weibs- und Knechtsart ist,—oh wie hat ihr Spiel von jeher der Selbstsucht übel mitgespielt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0031","text":"Und Das gerade sollte Tugend sein und Tugend heissen, dass man der Selbstsucht übel mitspiele! Und „selbstlos“—so wünschten sich selber mit gutem Grunde alle diese weltmüden Feiglinge und Kreuzspinnen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0032","text":"Aber denen Allen kommt nun der Tag, die Wandlung, das Richtschwert, der grosse Mittag : da soll Vieles offenbar werden!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0033","text":"Und wer das Ich heil und heilig spricht und die Selbstsucht selig, wahrlich, der spricht auch, was er weiss, ein Weissager: „Siehe, er kommt, er ist nahe, der grosse Mittag!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0034","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"483c0011cedbccd9f5e917e6b2f999e6ff2016b1609d38e4d74c5ca8478abd95","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182823463829Z_f7b44c8d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2.","source_sha256":"483c0011cedbccd9f5e917e6b2f999e6ff2016b1609d38e4d74c5ca8478abd95","sha256":"743f4ac00406310f6235180426aab3838000b89a1bb65cd81c05165600fdb71b","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:28:23.463856+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0002","text":"Voluptuousness: the sting and stake of all hair-shirted despisers of the body, and cursed as “world” among all otherworldlings: for it mocks and makes fools of all teachers of confusion and error.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0003","text":"Voluptuousness: the slow fire upon which the rabble is burned; the ready furnace of blaze and seething for all worm-eaten wood, all stinking rags.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0004","text":"Voluptuousness: innocent and free for free hearts, the garden-happiness of the earth, all future’s overflowing gratitude to the present.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0005","text":"Voluptuousness: a sweetish poison only to the withered, but to those willing as lions the great strengthening of the heart, and the reverently spared wine of wines.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0006","text":"Voluptuousness: the great parable-happiness for higher happiness and highest hope. For much is promised marriage and more than marriage,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0007","text":"—to much that is more alien to itself than man and woman are:—and who has wholly comprehended how alien man and woman are to one another!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0008","text":"Voluptuousness:—yet I will have fences around my thoughts and even around my words: that swine and enthusiasts may not break into my gardens!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0009","text":"Lust for dominion: the glowing whip of the hardest hard-hearted; the ghastly torment which reserves itself for the most cruel man himself; the sombre flame of living funeral pyres.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0010","text":"Lust for dominion: the malicious gadfly set upon the vainest peoples; the mocker of every uncertain virtue; that rides upon every horse and every pride.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0011","text":"Lust for dominion: the earthquake that breaks and bursts open all that is rotten and hollow; the rolling, growling, punishing shatterer of whitewashed graves; the flashing question-mark beside premature answers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0012","text":"Lust for dominion: before whose gaze man crawls and cringes and serves and becomes lower than snake and swine:—until at last the great contempt cries out of him—,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0013","text":"Lust for dominion: the terrible teacher of great contempt, which preaches to cities and empires, to their faces, “away with thee!”—until from themselves there cries out “away with me!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0014","text":"Lust for dominion: which, however, rises enticingly also to the pure and solitary, and up to self-sufficient heights, glowing like a love which enticingly paints purple bliss upon earthly heavens.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0015","text":"Lust for dominion: but who would call it lust, when the lofty yearns downward for power! Verily, there is nothing sickly or addicted in such yearning and descending!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0016","text":"That the solitary height may not eternally isolate and suffice unto itself; that the mountain may come into the valley and the winds of the height to the lowlands:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0017","text":"Oh, who would find the right baptismal and virtue-name for such longing! “Bestowing virtue”—thus Zarathustra once named the unnameable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0018","text":"And then it happened also,—and verily, it happened for the first time!—that his word praised selfishness as blessed, the wholesome, healthy selfishness that wells forth from a mighty soul:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0019","text":"—from a mighty soul, to which belongs the lofty body, the beautiful, victorious, refreshing body, around which every thing becomes a mirror:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0020","text":"—the supple, persuasive body, the dancer, whose parable and epitome is the self-delighting soul. The self-delight of such bodies and souls calls itself: “virtue.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0021","text":"With its words of good and bad such self-delight shields itself as with sacred groves; with the names of its happiness it banishes from itself all that is contemptible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0022","text":"Away from itself it banishes all that is cowardly; it says: Bad—that is cowardly! Contemptible to it seems the ever-worrying, sighing, wretched man, and whoever picks up even the smallest advantages.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0023","text":"It also despises all woeful wisdom: for, verily, there is also a wisdom that blooms in darkness, a nightshade-wisdom: which always sighs: “All is vanity!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0024","text":"Timid mistrust counts for little with it, and so does everyone who wants oaths instead of glances and hands: likewise all overly mistrustful wisdom,—for such is the manner of cowardly souls.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0025","text":"Still less does she value the quick-to-please man, the doggish man, who at once lies on his back, the humble man; and there is also a wisdom that is humble and doggish and pious and quick to please.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0026","text":"Utterly hateful and loathsome to her is whoever never wishes to defend himself, whoever swallows poisonous spittle and evil looks, the all-too-patient man, the sufferer of everything, the all-sufficient man: for that is the slavish kind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0027","text":"Whether one is slavish before gods and divine foot-treads, or before men and stupid human opinions: she spits upon every kind of slavishness, this blessed selfishness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0028","text":"Bad: thus she bites at everything that is broken and miserly-slavish, unfree winking eyes, oppressed hearts, and that false yielding kind which kisses with broad cowardly lips.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0029","text":"And pseudo-wisdom: thus she calls everything that slaves and old men and weary men make witty; and especially the whole wicked, absurd, over-clever priestly-foolishness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0030","text":"But the pseudo-wise, all those priests, world-weary men, and those whose soul is of womanly and slavish kind,—oh, how their game has from time immemorial played selfishness a wicked trick!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0031","text":"And precisely this was supposed to be virtue and to be called virtue, that one should play selfishness a wicked trick! And “selfless”—thus, with good reason, all these world-weary cowards and cross-spiders wished themselves!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0032","text":"But now there comes for them all the day, the transformation, the judging sword, the great noon: there much shall be made manifest!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0033","text":"And whoever pronounces the I whole and holy, and selfishness blessed, verily, he also speaks what he knows, as a prophet: “Behold, he comes, he is near, the great noon!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0034","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_6:P0034"]}],"source_digest":"cbbecc86176bba7cff4b4f73e3f21f4bf38e839b65c1c5b0a874ea05cd7e441b","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Three Evils","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On the Three Evils · 2","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On the Three Evils","2."],"original_title":"2."}],"source_title_context":"Von den drei Bösen","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von den drei Bösen · 2","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von den drei Bösen","2."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"2.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_7","title":"1.","order":80,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_1C_7","text":"## 1.\n\nMein Mundwerk—ist des Volks: zu grob und herzlich rede ich für die Seidenhasen. Und noch fremder klingt mein Wort allen Tinten-Fischen und Feder-Füchsen.\n\nMeine Hand—ist eine Narrenhand: wehe allen Tischen und Wänden, und was noch Platz hat für Narren-Zierath, Narren-Schmierath!\n\nMein Fuss—ist ein Pferdefuss; damit trapple und trabe ich über Stock und Stein, kreuz- und querfeld-ein und bin des Teufels vor Lust bei allem schnellen Laufen.\n\nMein Magen—ist wohl eines Adlers Magen? Denn er liebt am liebsten Lammfleisch. Gewisslich aber ist er eines Vogels Magen.\n\nVon unschuldigen Dingen genährt und von Wenigem, bereit und ungeduldig zu fliegen, davonzufliegen—das ist nun meine Art: wie sollte nicht Etwas daran von Vogel-Art sein!\n\nUnd zumal, dass ich dem Geist der Schwere feind bin, das ist Vogel-Art: und wahrlich, todfeind, erzfeind, urfeind! Oh wohin flog und verflog sich nicht schon meine Feindschaft!\n\nDavon könnte ich schon ein Lied singen—- und will es singen: ob ich gleich allein in leerem Hause bin und es meinen eignen Ohren singen muss.\n\nAndre Sänger giebt es freilich, denen macht das volle Haus erst ihre Kehle weide, ihre Hand gesprächig, ihr Auge ausdrücklich, ihr Herz wach:—Denen gleiche ich nicht.—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0002","text":"Mein Mundwerk—ist des Volks: zu grob und herzlich rede ich für die Seidenhasen. Und noch fremder klingt mein Wort allen Tinten-Fischen und Feder-Füchsen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0003","text":"Meine Hand—ist eine Narrenhand: wehe allen Tischen und Wänden, und was noch Platz hat für Narren-Zierath, Narren-Schmierath!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0004","text":"Mein Fuss—ist ein Pferdefuss; damit trapple und trabe ich über Stock und Stein, kreuz- und querfeld-ein und bin des Teufels vor Lust bei allem schnellen Laufen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0005","text":"Mein Magen—ist wohl eines Adlers Magen? Denn er liebt am liebsten Lammfleisch. Gewisslich aber ist er eines Vogels Magen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0006","text":"Von unschuldigen Dingen genährt und von Wenigem, bereit und ungeduldig zu fliegen, davonzufliegen—das ist nun meine Art: wie sollte nicht Etwas daran von Vogel-Art sein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0007","text":"Und zumal, dass ich dem Geist der Schwere feind bin, das ist Vogel-Art: und wahrlich, todfeind, erzfeind, urfeind! Oh wohin flog und verflog sich nicht schon meine Feindschaft!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0008","text":"Davon könnte ich schon ein Lied singen—- und will es singen: ob ich gleich allein in leerem Hause bin und es meinen eignen Ohren singen muss.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0009","text":"Andre Sänger giebt es freilich, denen macht das volle Haus erst ihre Kehle weide, ihre Hand gesprächig, ihr Auge ausdrücklich, ihr Herz wach:—Denen gleiche ich nicht.—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"38eda73839eb2f8333bf7c6e80234e9efa7ab16ca3360bec76837d801ea55bfb","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182831748846Z_8a5a4a49","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1.","source_sha256":"38eda73839eb2f8333bf7c6e80234e9efa7ab16ca3360bec76837d801ea55bfb","sha256":"0fcc27ca9469fdec554dfbecfbbbc30442e1665dc9a76ba395da60336706c825","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:28:31.748867+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0002","text":"My tongue—is of the people: I speak too coarsely and heartily for the silk-rabbits. And my word sounds stranger still to all ink-fishes and pen-foxes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0003","text":"My hand—is a fool’s hand: woe to all tables and walls, and whatever else still has room for fool’s ornamentation, fool’s smearing!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0004","text":"My foot—is a horse’s foot; with it I trample and trot over stock and stone, crosswise and across the fields, and am devilishly delighted with every swift running.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0005","text":"My stomach—is perhaps an eagle’s stomach? For it loves lamb’s flesh best. But certainly it is a bird’s stomach.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0006","text":"Nourished by innocent things and by little, ready and impatient to fly, to fly away—that is now my manner: how should there not be Something of bird-nature in it!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0007","text":"And above all, that I am hostile to the spirit of gravity, that is bird-nature: and verily, mortal enemy, arch-enemy, primordial enemy! Oh, whither has my hostility not already flown and flown away!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0008","text":"Of that I could already sing a song—and will sing it: even though I am alone in an empty house and must sing it to my own ears.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0009","text":"There are, to be sure, other singers: only a full house makes their throat glad, their hand talkative, their eye expressive, their heart awake:—I am not like them.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_7:P0009"]}],"source_digest":"b908ae0b12c6d32ad62dc998a7530dc782cfd776d64a6a401c0757611b1e5011","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Spirit of Gravity","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On the Spirit of Gravity · 1","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On the Spirit of Gravity","1."],"original_title":"1."}],"source_title_context":"Vom Geist der Schwere","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom Geist der Schwere · 1","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Vom Geist der Schwere","1."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"1.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7","title":"2.","order":81,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_2C_7","text":"## 2.\n\nWer die Menschen einst fliegen lehrt, der hat alle Grenzsteine verrückt; alle Grenzsteine selber werden ihm in die Luft fliegen, die Erde wird er neu taufen —als „die Leichte.“\n\nDer Vogel Strauss läuft schneller als das schnellste Pferd, aber auch er steckt noch den Kopf schwer in schwere Erde: also der Mensch, der noch nicht fliegen kann.\n\nSchwer heisst ihm Erde und Leben; und so will es der Geist der Schwere! Wer aber leicht werden will und ein Vogel, der muss sich selber lieben:—also lehre ich .\n\nNicht freilich mit der Liebe der Siechen und Süchtigen: denn bei denen stinkt auch die Eigenliebe!\n\nMan muss sich selber lieben lernen—also lehre ich—mit einer heilen und gesunden Liebe: dass man es bei sich selber aushalte und nicht umherschweife.\n\nSolches Umherschweifen tauft sich „Nächstenliebe“ : mit diesem Worte ist bisher am besten gelogen und geheuchelt worden, und sonderlich von Solchen, die aller Welt schwer fielen.\n\nUnd wahrlich, das ist kein Gebot für Heute und Morgen, sich lieben lernen . Vielmehr ist von allen Künsten diese die feinste, listigste, letzte und geduldsamste.\n\nFür seinen Eigener ist nämlich alles Eigene gut versteckt; und von allen Schatzgruben wird die eigne am spätesten ausgegraben,—also schafft es der Geist der Schwere.\n\nFast in der Wiege giebt man uns schon schwere Worte und Werthe mit: „gut“ und „böse“ —so heisst sich diese Mitgift. Um derentwillen vergiebt man uns, dass wir leben.\n\nUnd dazu lässt man die Kindlein zu sich kommen, dass man ihnen bei Zeiten wehre, sich selber zu lieben: also schafft es der Geist der Schwere.\n\nUnd wir—wir schleppen treulich, was man uns mitgiebt, auf harten Schultern und über rauhe Berge! Und schwitzen wir, so sagt man uns: „Ja, das Leben ist schwer zu tragen!“\n\nAber der Mensch nur ist sich schwer zu tragen! Das macht, er schleppt zu vieles Fremde auf seinen Schultern. Dem Kameele gleich kniet er nieder und lässt sich gut aufladen.\n\nSonderlich der starke, tragsame Mensch, dem Ehrfurcht innewohnt: zu viele fremde schwere Worte und Werthe lädt er auf sich,—nun dünkt das Leben ihm eine Wüste!\n\nUnd wahrlich! Auch manches Eigene ist schwer zu tragen! Und viel Inwendiges am Menschen ist der Auster gleich, nämlich ekel und schlüpfrig und schwer erfasslich—,\n\n—also dass eine edle Schale mit edler Zierath fürbitten muss. Aber auch diese Kunst muss man lernen: Schale haben und schönen Schein und kluge Blindheit!\n\nAbermals trügt über Manches am Menschen, dass manche Schale gering und traurig und zu sehr Schale ist. Viel verborgene Güte und Kraft wird nie errathen; die köstlichsten Leckerbissen finden keine Schmecker!\n\nDie Frauen wissen das, die köstlichsten: ein Wenig fetter, ein Wenig magerer—oh wie viel Schicksal liegt in so Wenigem!\n\nDer Mensch ist schwer zu entdecken und sich selber noch am schwersten; oft lügt der Geist über die Seele. Also schafft es der Geist der Schwere.\n\nDer aber hat sich selber entdeckt, welcher spricht: Das ist mein Gutes und Böses: damit hat er den Maulwurf und Zwerg stumm gemacht, welcher spricht „Allen gut, Allen bös.“\n\nWahrlich, ich mag auch Solche nicht, denen jegliches Ding gut und diese Welt gar die beste heisst. Solche nenne ich die Allgenügsamen.\n\nAllgenügsamkeit, die Alles zu schmecken weiss: das ist nicht der beste Geschmack! Ich ehre die widerspänstigen wählerischen Zungen und Mägen, welche „Ich“ und „Ja“ und „Nein“ sagen lernten.\n\nAlles aber kauen und verdauen—das ist eine rechte Schweine-Art! Immer I-a sagen—das lernte allein der Esel, und wer seines Geistes ist!—\n\nDas tiefe Gelb und das heisse Roth: so will es mein Geschmack,—der mischt Blut zu allen Farben. Wer aber sein Haus weiss tüncht, der verräth mir eine weissgetünchte Seele.\n\nIn Mumien verliebt die Einen, die Andern in Gespenster; und Beide gleich feind allem Fleisch und Blute—oh wie gehen Beide mir wider den Geschmack! Denn ich liebe Blut.\n\nUnd dort will ich nicht wohnen und weilen, wo Jedermann spuckt und speit: das ist nun mein Geschmack,—lieber noch lebte ich unter Dieben und Meineidigen. Niemand trägt Gold im Munde.\n\nWidriger aber sind mir noch alle Speichellecker; und das widrigste Thier von Mensch, das ich fand, das taufte ich Schmarotzer: das wollte nicht lieben und doch von Liebe leben.\n\nUnselig heisse ich Alle, die nur Eine Wahl haben: böse Thiere zu werden oder böse Thierbändiger: bei Solchen würde ich mir keine Hütten bauen.\n\nUnselig heisse ich auch Die, welche immer warten müssen,—die gehen mir wider den Geschmack: alle die Zöllner und Krämer und Könige und andren Länder- und Ladenhüter.\n\nWahrlich, ich lernte das Warten auch und von Grund aus,\n\n—aber nur das Warten auf mich . Und über Allem lernte ich stehn und gehn und laufen und springen und klettern und tanzen.\n\nDas ist aber meine Lehre: wer einst fliegen lernen will, der muss erst stehn und gehn und laufen und klettern und tanzen lernen:—man erfliegt das Fliegen nicht!\n\nMit Strickleitern lernte ich manches Fenster erklettern, mit hurtigen Beinen klomm ich auf hohe Masten: auf hohen Masten der Erkenntniss sitzen dünkte mich keine geringe Seligkeit,—\n\n—gleich kleinen Flammen flackern auf hohen Masten: ein kleines Licht zwar, aber doch ein grosser Trost für verschlagene Schiffer und Schiffbrüchige!—\n\nAuf vielerlei Weg und Weise kam ich zu meiner Wahrheit; nicht auf Einer Leiter stieg ich zur Höhe, wo mein Auge in meine Ferne schweift.\n\nUnd ungern nur fragte ich stets nach Wegen,—das gieng mir immer wider den Geschmack! Lieber fragte und versuchte ich die Wege selber.\n\nEin Versuchen und Fragen war all mein Gehen:—und wahrlich, auch antworten muss man lernen auf solches Fragen! Das aber—ist mein Geschmack:\n\n—kein guter, kein schlechter, aber mein Geschmack, dessen ich weder Scham noch Hehl mehr habe.\n\n„Das—ist nun mein Weg,—wo ist der eure?“ so antwortete ich Denen, welche mich „nach dem Wege“ fragten. Den Weg nämlich—den giebt es nicht!\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0002","text":"Wer die Menschen einst fliegen lehrt, der hat alle Grenzsteine verrückt; alle Grenzsteine selber werden ihm in die Luft fliegen, die Erde wird er neu taufen —als „die Leichte.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0003","text":"Der Vogel Strauss läuft schneller als das schnellste Pferd, aber auch er steckt noch den Kopf schwer in schwere Erde: also der Mensch, der noch nicht fliegen kann.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0004","text":"Schwer heisst ihm Erde und Leben; und so will es der Geist der Schwere! Wer aber leicht werden will und ein Vogel, der muss sich selber lieben:—also lehre ich .","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0005","text":"Nicht freilich mit der Liebe der Siechen und Süchtigen: denn bei denen stinkt auch die Eigenliebe!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0006","text":"Man muss sich selber lieben lernen—also lehre ich—mit einer heilen und gesunden Liebe: dass man es bei sich selber aushalte und nicht umherschweife.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0007","text":"Solches Umherschweifen tauft sich „Nächstenliebe“ : mit diesem Worte ist bisher am besten gelogen und geheuchelt worden, und sonderlich von Solchen, die aller Welt schwer fielen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0008","text":"Und wahrlich, das ist kein Gebot für Heute und Morgen, sich lieben lernen . Vielmehr ist von allen Künsten diese die feinste, listigste, letzte und geduldsamste.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0009","text":"Für seinen Eigener ist nämlich alles Eigene gut versteckt; und von allen Schatzgruben wird die eigne am spätesten ausgegraben,—also schafft es der Geist der Schwere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0010","text":"Fast in der Wiege giebt man uns schon schwere Worte und Werthe mit: „gut“ und „böse“ —so heisst sich diese Mitgift. Um derentwillen vergiebt man uns, dass wir leben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0011","text":"Und dazu lässt man die Kindlein zu sich kommen, dass man ihnen bei Zeiten wehre, sich selber zu lieben: also schafft es der Geist der Schwere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0012","text":"Und wir—wir schleppen treulich, was man uns mitgiebt, auf harten Schultern und über rauhe Berge! Und schwitzen wir, so sagt man uns: „Ja, das Leben ist schwer zu tragen!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0013","text":"Aber der Mensch nur ist sich schwer zu tragen! Das macht, er schleppt zu vieles Fremde auf seinen Schultern. Dem Kameele gleich kniet er nieder und lässt sich gut aufladen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0014","text":"Sonderlich der starke, tragsame Mensch, dem Ehrfurcht innewohnt: zu viele fremde schwere Worte und Werthe lädt er auf sich,—nun dünkt das Leben ihm eine Wüste!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0015","text":"Und wahrlich! Auch manches Eigene ist schwer zu tragen! Und viel Inwendiges am Menschen ist der Auster gleich, nämlich ekel und schlüpfrig und schwer erfasslich—,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0016","text":"—also dass eine edle Schale mit edler Zierath fürbitten muss. Aber auch diese Kunst muss man lernen: Schale haben und schönen Schein und kluge Blindheit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0017","text":"Abermals trügt über Manches am Menschen, dass manche Schale gering und traurig und zu sehr Schale ist. Viel verborgene Güte und Kraft wird nie errathen; die köstlichsten Leckerbissen finden keine Schmecker!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0018","text":"Die Frauen wissen das, die köstlichsten: ein Wenig fetter, ein Wenig magerer—oh wie viel Schicksal liegt in so Wenigem!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0019","text":"Der Mensch ist schwer zu entdecken und sich selber noch am schwersten; oft lügt der Geist über die Seele. Also schafft es der Geist der Schwere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0020","text":"Der aber hat sich selber entdeckt, welcher spricht: Das ist mein Gutes und Böses: damit hat er den Maulwurf und Zwerg stumm gemacht, welcher spricht „Allen gut, Allen bös.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0021","text":"Wahrlich, ich mag auch Solche nicht, denen jegliches Ding gut und diese Welt gar die beste heisst. Solche nenne ich die Allgenügsamen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0022","text":"Allgenügsamkeit, die Alles zu schmecken weiss: das ist nicht der beste Geschmack! Ich ehre die widerspänstigen wählerischen Zungen und Mägen, welche „Ich“ und „Ja“ und „Nein“ sagen lernten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0023","text":"Alles aber kauen und verdauen—das ist eine rechte Schweine-Art! Immer I-a sagen—das lernte allein der Esel, und wer seines Geistes ist!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0024","text":"Das tiefe Gelb und das heisse Roth: so will es mein Geschmack,—der mischt Blut zu allen Farben. Wer aber sein Haus weiss tüncht, der verräth mir eine weissgetünchte Seele.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0025","text":"In Mumien verliebt die Einen, die Andern in Gespenster; und Beide gleich feind allem Fleisch und Blute—oh wie gehen Beide mir wider den Geschmack! Denn ich liebe Blut.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0026","text":"Und dort will ich nicht wohnen und weilen, wo Jedermann spuckt und speit: das ist nun mein Geschmack,—lieber noch lebte ich unter Dieben und Meineidigen. Niemand trägt Gold im Munde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0027","text":"Widriger aber sind mir noch alle Speichellecker; und das widrigste Thier von Mensch, das ich fand, das taufte ich Schmarotzer: das wollte nicht lieben und doch von Liebe leben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0028","text":"Unselig heisse ich Alle, die nur Eine Wahl haben: böse Thiere zu werden oder böse Thierbändiger: bei Solchen würde ich mir keine Hütten bauen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0029","text":"Unselig heisse ich auch Die, welche immer warten müssen,—die gehen mir wider den Geschmack: alle die Zöllner und Krämer und Könige und andren Länder- und Ladenhüter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0030","text":"Wahrlich, ich lernte das Warten auch und von Grund aus,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0031","text":"—aber nur das Warten auf mich . Und über Allem lernte ich stehn und gehn und laufen und springen und klettern und tanzen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0032","text":"Das ist aber meine Lehre: wer einst fliegen lernen will, der muss erst stehn und gehn und laufen und klettern und tanzen lernen:—man erfliegt das Fliegen nicht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0033","text":"Mit Strickleitern lernte ich manches Fenster erklettern, mit hurtigen Beinen klomm ich auf hohe Masten: auf hohen Masten der Erkenntniss sitzen dünkte mich keine geringe Seligkeit,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0034","text":"—gleich kleinen Flammen flackern auf hohen Masten: ein kleines Licht zwar, aber doch ein grosser Trost für verschlagene Schiffer und Schiffbrüchige!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0035","text":"Auf vielerlei Weg und Weise kam ich zu meiner Wahrheit; nicht auf Einer Leiter stieg ich zur Höhe, wo mein Auge in meine Ferne schweift.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0036","text":"Und ungern nur fragte ich stets nach Wegen,—das gieng mir immer wider den Geschmack! Lieber fragte und versuchte ich die Wege selber.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0037","text":"Ein Versuchen und Fragen war all mein Gehen:—und wahrlich, auch antworten muss man lernen auf solches Fragen! Das aber—ist mein Geschmack:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0038","text":"—kein guter, kein schlechter, aber mein Geschmack, dessen ich weder Scham noch Hehl mehr habe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0039","text":"„Das—ist nun mein Weg,—wo ist der eure?“ so antwortete ich Denen, welche mich „nach dem Wege“ fragten. Den Weg nämlich—den giebt es nicht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0040","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"9529b76b829dcbac57ea1e00e14321bd9115555fd5b790ce61a947698ff5d6b2","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182856006605Z_81f28c17","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2.","source_sha256":"9529b76b829dcbac57ea1e00e14321bd9115555fd5b790ce61a947698ff5d6b2","sha256":"c843e1682caddee449459557509ffe1bd25addc58cc1d46fa8cd889b495cbeeb","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:28:56.006637+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0002","text":"He who once teaches human beings to fly has shifted all boundary-stones; all boundary-stones themselves will fly into the air for him, he will baptize the earth anew—as “the Light One.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0003","text":"The ostrich runs faster than the fastest horse, but it too still thrusts its head heavily into heavy earth: thus the human being who cannot yet fly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0004","text":"Earth and life are called heavy by him; and thus the spirit of gravity wills it! But he who would become light and a bird must love himself:—thus I teach .","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0005","text":"Not, to be sure, with the love of the sick and the addicted: for with them self-love too stinks!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0006","text":"One must learn to love oneself—thus I teach—with a wholesome and healthy love: so that one may endure being with oneself and not roam about.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0007","text":"Such roaming about baptizes itself “love of the neighbour”: with this word people have hitherto lied and dissembled best, and especially those who were burdensome to the whole world.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0008","text":"And truly, it is no commandment for Today and Tomorrow, to learn to love oneself . Rather, of all arts this is the finest, most cunning, latest, and most patient.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0009","text":"For to its owner all that is one's own is well concealed; and of all treasure-pits one's own is dug up last,—thus the spirit of gravity brings it about.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0010","text":"Almost in the cradle we are already given heavy words and values: “good” and “evil”—that is the name of this dowry. For its sake we are forgiven for living.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0011","text":"And to this end little children are allowed to come unto them, so that they may in good time prevent them from loving themselves: thus the spirit of gravity brings it about.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0012","text":"And we—we faithfully drag what is given us along, upon hard shoulders and over rough mountains! And if we sweat, we are told: “Yes, life is hard to bear!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0013","text":"But only the human being is hard to bear for himself! That is because he drags too much that is alien upon his shoulders. Like the camel, he kneels down and lets himself be well laden.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0014","text":"Especially the strong, load-bearing human being, in whom reverence dwells: he loads too many alien heavy words and values upon himself,—now life seems to him a desert!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0015","text":"And truly! Much that is one's own too is hard to bear! And much that is inward in the human being is like the oyster, namely disgusting and slippery and hard to grasp—,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0016","text":"—so that a noble shell with noble adornment must intercede. But this art too must be learned: to have shell and fair appearance and clever blindness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0017","text":"Again, much about the human being deceives, because many a shell is mean and sad and too much shell. Much hidden goodness and strength is never guessed; the most precious delicacies find no tasters!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0018","text":"Women know this, the most precious ones: a little fatter, a little leaner—oh, how much fate lies in so little!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0019","text":"The human being is hard to discover, and oneself hardest of all; often the spirit lies about the soul. Thus the spirit of gravity brings it about.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0020","text":"But he has discovered himself who says: This is my good and evil: thereby he has made dumb the mole and dwarf who says “Good to all, evil to all.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0021","text":"Truly, I do not like those either to whom every thing is called good and this world even the best. Such I call the all-satisfied.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0022","text":"All-satisfaction, which knows how to taste everything: that is not the best taste! I honour the recalcitrant, discriminating tongues and stomachs, which have learned to say “I” and “Yes” and “No.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0023","text":"But chewing and digesting everything—that is a veritable swine-like way! Always saying I-a—that the ass alone learned, and whoever is of its spirit!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0024","text":"The deep yellow and the hot red: thus my taste wills it,—it mixes blood into all colours. But he who whitewashes his house betrays to me a whitewashed soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0025","text":"Some in love with mummies, others with ghosts; and both alike hostile to all flesh and blood—oh, how both offend my taste! For I love blood.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0026","text":"And there I would not dwell and linger, where everyone spits and spews: that is now my taste,—I would rather live among thieves and perjurers. No one bears gold in his mouth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0027","text":"Yet more repugnant to me are all flatterers; and the most repugnant human animal I found, I christened parasite: it did not wish to love and yet wished to live from love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0028","text":"Unblessed I call all those who have but one choice: to become evil beasts or evil beast-tamers: among such I would build no huts for myself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0029","text":"Unblessed I call also those who must always wait,—they offend my taste: all those toll-collectors and shopkeepers and kings and other guardians of countries and shops.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0030","text":"Verily, I too learned waiting, and thoroughly,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0031","text":"—but only waiting for myself. And above all I learned to stand and walk and run and jump and climb and dance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0032","text":"But this is my teaching: whoever would one day learn to fly must first learn to stand and walk and run and climb and dance:—one does not fly one's way into flying!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0033","text":"With rope-ladders I learned to climb many a window, with nimble legs I clambered up high masts: to sit upon high masts of knowledge seemed to me no small blessedness,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0034","text":"—like little flames to flicker upon high masts: a small light indeed, yet a great consolation for storm-driven sailors and shipwrecked men!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0035","text":"By manifold paths and ways I came to my truth; not by one ladder did I climb to the height where my eye ranges over my distance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0036","text":"And only reluctantly did I always ask after paths,—that always offended my taste! I preferred to ask and try out the paths themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0037","text":"All my going was a trying and questioning:—and verily, one must also learn to answer such questioning! But that—is my taste:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0038","text":"—neither a good nor a bad one, but my taste, of which I no longer have either shame or concealment.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0039","text":"“This—is now my way,—where is yours?” thus I answered those who asked me “for the way.” For the way—that does not exist!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0040","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_7:P0040"]}],"source_digest":"d7fa4b3f440d4ad018fd368d7036c61541bd0c1951dbf73b48f26a602915f2c3","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Spirit of Gravity","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On the Spirit of Gravity · 2","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On the Spirit of Gravity","2."],"original_title":"2."}],"source_title_context":"Vom Geist der Schwere","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom Geist der Schwere · 2","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Vom Geist der Schwere","2."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"2.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_8","title":"1.","order":82,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_1C_8","text":"## 1.\n\nHier sitze ich und warte, alte zerbrochene Tafeln um mich und auch neue halb beschriebene Tafeln. Wann kommt meine Stunde?\n\n—die Stunde meines Niederganges, Unterganges: denn noch Ein Mal will ich zu den Menschen gehn.\n\nDess warte ich nun: denn erst müssen mir die Zeichen kommen, dass es meine Stunde sei,—nämlich der lachende Löwe mit dem Taubenschwarme.\n\nInzwischen rede ich als Einer, der Zeit hat, zu mir selber. Niemand erzählt mir Neues: so erzähle ich mir mich selber.—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_8:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_8:P0002","text":"Hier sitze ich und warte, alte zerbrochene Tafeln um mich und auch neue halb beschriebene Tafeln. Wann kommt meine Stunde?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_8:P0003","text":"—die Stunde meines Niederganges, Unterganges: denn noch Ein Mal will ich zu den Menschen gehn.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_8:P0004","text":"Dess warte ich nun: denn erst müssen mir die Zeichen kommen, dass es meine Stunde sei,—nämlich der lachende Löwe mit dem Taubenschwarme.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_8:P0005","text":"Inzwischen rede ich als Einer, der Zeit hat, zu mir selber. Niemand erzählt mir Neues: so erzähle ich mir mich selber.—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"7ad6c835e41e7f18435fde90448d98621acd29b835683ead9618c751a7645208","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182859536490Z_920f0f69","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1.","source_sha256":"7ad6c835e41e7f18435fde90448d98621acd29b835683ead9618c751a7645208","sha256":"2925def8c6d0e751b9e97350723d75fd12aebdfdcaf21a1d278edd4a24a25eff","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:28:59.536511+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_8:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_8:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_8:P0002","text":"Here I sit and wait, old broken tablets around me and also new tablets half inscribed. When will my hour come?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_8:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_8:P0003","text":"—the hour of my decline, my setting: for once more I wish to go among men.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_8:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_8:P0004","text":"For this I now wait: for first the signs must come to me that it is my hour,—namely the laughing lion with the flock of doves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_8:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_8:P0005","text":"Meanwhile I speak to myself as one who has time. No one tells me anything new: so I tell myself to myself.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_8:P0005"]}],"source_digest":"26bf661ebe87b0b1566c7e300733b07d35856120fe8612561d34fa713db9bf1e","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 1","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","1."],"original_title":"1."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 1","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","1."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"1.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8","title":"2.","order":83,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_2C_8","text":"## 2.\n\nAls ich zu den Menschen kam, da fand ich sie sitzen auf einem alten Dünkel: Alle dünkten sich lange schon zu wissen, was dem Menschen gut und böse sei.\n\nEine alte müde Sache dünkte ihnen alles Reden von Tugend; und wer gut schlafen wollte, der sprach vor Schlafengehen noch von „Gut“ und „Böse“ .\n\nDiese Schläferei störte ich auf, als ich lehrte: was gut und böse ist, das weiss noch Niemand :—es sei denn der Schaffende!\n\n—Das aber ist Der, welcher des Menschen Ziel schafft und der Erde ihren Sinn giebt und ihre Zukunft: Dieser erst schafft es, dass Etwas gut und böse ist.\n\nUnd ich hiess sie ihre alten Lehr-Stühle umwerfen, und wo nur jener alte Dünkel gesessen hatte; ich hiess sie lachen über ihre grossen Tugend-Meister und Heiligen und Dichter und Welt-Erlöser.\n\nÜber ihre düsteren Weisen hiess ich sie lachen, und wer je als schwarze Vogelscheuche warnend auf dem Baume des Lebens gesessen hatte.\n\nAn ihre grosse Gräberstrasse setzte ich mich und selber zu Aas und Geiern—und ich lachte über all ihr Einst und seine mürbe verfallende Herrlichkeit.\n\nWahrlich, gleich Busspredigern und Narrn schrie ich Zorn und Zeter über all ihr Grosses und Kleines—, dass ihr Bestes so gar klein ist! Dass ihr Bösestes so gar klein ist!—also lachte ich.\n\nMeine weise Sehnsucht schrie und lachte also aus mir, die auf Bergen geboren ist, eine wilde Weisheit wahrlich!—meine grosse flügelbrausende Sehnsucht.\n\nUnd oft riss sie mich fort und hinauf und hinweg und mitten im Lachen: da flog ich wohl schaudernd, ein Pfeil, durch sonnentrunkenes Entzücken:\n\n—hinaus in ferne Zukünfte, die kein Traum noch sah, in heissere Süden, als je sich Bildner träumten: dorthin, wo Götter tanzend sich aller Kleider schämen:—\n\n—dass ich nämlich in Gleichnissen rede und gleich Dichtern hinke und stammle: und wahrlich, ich schäme mich, dass ich noch Dichter sein muss!—\n\nWo alles Werden mich Götter-Tanz und Götter-Muthwillen dünkte, und die Welt los- und ausgelassen und zu sich selber zurückfliehend:—\n\n—als ein ewiges Sich-fliehn und -Wiedersuchen vieler Götter, als das selige Sich-Widersprechen, Sich-Wieder-hören, Sich-Wieder-Zugehören vieler Götter:—\n\nWo alle Zeit mich ein seliger Hohn auf Augenblicke dünkte, wo die Nothwendigkeit die Freiheit selber war, die selig mit dem Stachel der Freiheit spielte:—\n\nWo ich auch meinen alten Teufel und Erzfeind wiederfand, den Geist der Schwere und Alles, was er schuf: Zwang, Satzung, Noth und Folge und Zweck und Wille und Gut und Böse:—\n\nDenn muss nicht dasein, über das getanzt, hinweggetanzt werde? Müssen nicht um der Leichten, Leichtesten willen—Maulwürfe und schwere Zwerge dasein?—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0002","text":"Als ich zu den Menschen kam, da fand ich sie sitzen auf einem alten Dünkel: Alle dünkten sich lange schon zu wissen, was dem Menschen gut und böse sei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0003","text":"Eine alte müde Sache dünkte ihnen alles Reden von Tugend; und wer gut schlafen wollte, der sprach vor Schlafengehen noch von „Gut“ und „Böse“ .","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0004","text":"Diese Schläferei störte ich auf, als ich lehrte: was gut und böse ist, das weiss noch Niemand :—es sei denn der Schaffende!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0005","text":"—Das aber ist Der, welcher des Menschen Ziel schafft und der Erde ihren Sinn giebt und ihre Zukunft: Dieser erst schafft es, dass Etwas gut und böse ist.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0006","text":"Und ich hiess sie ihre alten Lehr-Stühle umwerfen, und wo nur jener alte Dünkel gesessen hatte; ich hiess sie lachen über ihre grossen Tugend-Meister und Heiligen und Dichter und Welt-Erlöser.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0007","text":"Über ihre düsteren Weisen hiess ich sie lachen, und wer je als schwarze Vogelscheuche warnend auf dem Baume des Lebens gesessen hatte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0008","text":"An ihre grosse Gräberstrasse setzte ich mich und selber zu Aas und Geiern—und ich lachte über all ihr Einst und seine mürbe verfallende Herrlichkeit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0009","text":"Wahrlich, gleich Busspredigern und Narrn schrie ich Zorn und Zeter über all ihr Grosses und Kleines—, dass ihr Bestes so gar klein ist! Dass ihr Bösestes so gar klein ist!—also lachte ich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0010","text":"Meine weise Sehnsucht schrie und lachte also aus mir, die auf Bergen geboren ist, eine wilde Weisheit wahrlich!—meine grosse flügelbrausende Sehnsucht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0011","text":"Und oft riss sie mich fort und hinauf und hinweg und mitten im Lachen: da flog ich wohl schaudernd, ein Pfeil, durch sonnentrunkenes Entzücken:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0012","text":"—hinaus in ferne Zukünfte, die kein Traum noch sah, in heissere Süden, als je sich Bildner träumten: dorthin, wo Götter tanzend sich aller Kleider schämen:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0013","text":"—dass ich nämlich in Gleichnissen rede und gleich Dichtern hinke und stammle: und wahrlich, ich schäme mich, dass ich noch Dichter sein muss!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0014","text":"Wo alles Werden mich Götter-Tanz und Götter-Muthwillen dünkte, und die Welt los- und ausgelassen und zu sich selber zurückfliehend:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0015","text":"—als ein ewiges Sich-fliehn und -Wiedersuchen vieler Götter, als das selige Sich-Widersprechen, Sich-Wieder-hören, Sich-Wieder-Zugehören vieler Götter:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0016","text":"Wo alle Zeit mich ein seliger Hohn auf Augenblicke dünkte, wo die Nothwendigkeit die Freiheit selber war, die selig mit dem Stachel der Freiheit spielte:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0017","text":"Wo ich auch meinen alten Teufel und Erzfeind wiederfand, den Geist der Schwere und Alles, was er schuf: Zwang, Satzung, Noth und Folge und Zweck und Wille und Gut und Böse:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0018","text":"Denn muss nicht dasein, über das getanzt, hinweggetanzt werde? Müssen nicht um der Leichten, Leichtesten willen—Maulwürfe und schwere Zwerge dasein?—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"7d380c6655b35a020cb7135488c572582a8d74a466d3923ff15e0de20d41d6ad","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182909181593Z_1c55c1b7","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2.","source_sha256":"7d380c6655b35a020cb7135488c572582a8d74a466d3923ff15e0de20d41d6ad","sha256":"4aeb297731a17697d42a505c2e453f593e3d695db56adb04ef08b0762ee7cf06","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:29:09.181620+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0002","text":"When I came among human beings, I found them sitting upon an old conceit: all had long thought themselves to know what was good and evil for the human being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0003","text":"All talk of virtue seemed to them an old, weary matter; and whoever wished to sleep well still spoke of “Good” and “Evil” before going to sleep.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0004","text":"I disturbed this slumber when I taught: what is good and evil, no one yet knows:—unless it be the creator!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0005","text":"—But that is he who creates the human being’s goal and gives the earth its meaning and its future: he alone first creates it that anything is good and evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0006","text":"And I bade them overthrow their old chairs of instruction, and wherever that old conceit alone had sat; I bade them laugh at their great masters of virtue and saints and poets and redeemers of the world.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0007","text":"I bade them laugh at their gloomy sages, and at whoever had ever sat warningly as a black scarecrow upon the tree of life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0008","text":"Upon their great road of graves I sat down, and myself beside carrion and vultures—and I laughed at all their Once and its crumbling, decaying splendour.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0009","text":"Verily, like preachers of repentance and fools I cried wrath and alarm over all their great and small things—, that their best is so utterly small! That their worst is so utterly small!—thus I laughed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0010","text":"Thus my wise longing cried and laughed out of me, it that was born upon mountains, a wild wisdom indeed!—my great, wing-rustling longing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0011","text":"And often it tore me away and upward and onward and into the midst of laughter: then, shuddering, I would fly like an arrow through sun-drunken rapture:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0012","text":"—out into distant futures that no dream yet saw, into hotter souths than sculptors ever dreamed: thither, where gods dancing are ashamed of all clothing:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0013","text":"—that is, I speak in parables and, like poets, limp and stammer: and verily, I am ashamed that I must still be a poet!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0014","text":"Where all becoming seemed to me a dance of gods and divine wantonness, and the world unbound and unleashed and fleeing back to itself:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0015","text":"—as an eternal fleeing-from-itself and seeking-itself-again of many gods, as the blissful contradicting-itself, hearing-itself-again, belonging-to-itself-again of many gods:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0016","text":"Where all time seemed to me a blissful mockery of moments, where necessity was freedom itself, which played blissfully with the goad of freedom:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0017","text":"Where I also found again my old devil and arch-enemy, the spirit of gravity and all that it created: compulsion, statute, necessity and consequence and purpose and will and good and evil:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0018","text":"For must there not be that over which one dances, dances away? Must there not, for the sake of the light, lightest ones—be moles and heavy dwarfs?—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_8:P0018"]}],"source_digest":"09ced5eb049707eb02eb3a0368c5a993d62e6ed0435e38dc989526b79f83bdc1","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 2","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","2."],"original_title":"2."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 2","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","2."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"2.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4","title":"3.","order":84,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_3C_4","text":"## 3.\n\nDort war’s auch, wo ich das Wort „Übermensch“ vom Wege auflas, und dass der Mensch Etwas sei, das überwunden werden müsse,\n\n—dass der Mensch eine Brücke sei und kein Zweck: sich selig preisend ob seines Mittags und Abends, als Weg zu neuen Morgenröthen:\n\n—das Zarathustra-Wort vom grossen Mittage, und was sonst ich über den Menschen aufhängte, gleich purpurnen zweiten Abendröthen.\n\nWahrlich, auch neue Sterne liess ich sie sehn sammt neuen Nächten; und über Wolken und Tag und Nacht spannte ich noch das Lachen aus wie ein buntes Gezelt.\n\nIch lehrte sie all mein Dichten und Trachten: in Eins zu dichten und zusammen zu tragen, was Bruchstück ist am Menschen und Räthsel und grauser Zufall,—\n\n—als Dichter, Räthselrather und Erlöser des Zufalls lehrte ich sie an der Zukunft schaffen, und Alles, das war —, schaffend zu erlösen.\n\nDas Vergangne am Menschen zu erlösen und alles „Es war“ umzuschauen, bis der Wille spricht: „Aber so wollte ich es! So werde ich’s wollen—“\n\n—Diess hiess ich ihnen Erlösung, Diess allein lehrte ich sie Erlösung heissen. -—\n\nNun warte ich meiner Erlösung—, dass ich zum letzten Male zu ihnen gehe.\n\nDenn noch Ein Mal will ich zu den Menschen: unter ihnen will ich untergehen, sterbend will ich ihnen meine reichste Gabe geben!\n\nDer Sonne lernte ich Das ab, wenn sie hinabgeht, die Überreiche: Gold schüttet sie da in’s Meer aus unerschöpflichem Reichthume,—\n\n—also, dass der ärmste Fischer noch mit goldenem Ruder rudert! Diess nämlich sah ich einst und wurde der Thränen nicht satt im Zuschauen.—\n\nDer Sonne gleich will auch Zarathustra untergehn: nun sitzt er hier und wartet, alte zerbrochne Tafeln um sich und auch neue Tafeln,—halbbeschriebene.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0001","text":"## 3.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0002","text":"Dort war’s auch, wo ich das Wort „Übermensch“ vom Wege auflas, und dass der Mensch Etwas sei, das überwunden werden müsse,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0003","text":"—dass der Mensch eine Brücke sei und kein Zweck: sich selig preisend ob seines Mittags und Abends, als Weg zu neuen Morgenröthen:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0004","text":"—das Zarathustra-Wort vom grossen Mittage, und was sonst ich über den Menschen aufhängte, gleich purpurnen zweiten Abendröthen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0005","text":"Wahrlich, auch neue Sterne liess ich sie sehn sammt neuen Nächten; und über Wolken und Tag und Nacht spannte ich noch das Lachen aus wie ein buntes Gezelt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0006","text":"Ich lehrte sie all mein Dichten und Trachten: in Eins zu dichten und zusammen zu tragen, was Bruchstück ist am Menschen und Räthsel und grauser Zufall,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0007","text":"—als Dichter, Räthselrather und Erlöser des Zufalls lehrte ich sie an der Zukunft schaffen, und Alles, das war —, schaffend zu erlösen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0008","text":"Das Vergangne am Menschen zu erlösen und alles „Es war“ umzuschauen, bis der Wille spricht: „Aber so wollte ich es! So werde ich’s wollen—“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0009","text":"—Diess hiess ich ihnen Erlösung, Diess allein lehrte ich sie Erlösung heissen. -—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0010","text":"Nun warte ich meiner Erlösung—, dass ich zum letzten Male zu ihnen gehe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0011","text":"Denn noch Ein Mal will ich zu den Menschen: unter ihnen will ich untergehen, sterbend will ich ihnen meine reichste Gabe geben!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0012","text":"Der Sonne lernte ich Das ab, wenn sie hinabgeht, die Überreiche: Gold schüttet sie da in’s Meer aus unerschöpflichem Reichthume,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0013","text":"—also, dass der ärmste Fischer noch mit goldenem Ruder rudert! Diess nämlich sah ich einst und wurde der Thränen nicht satt im Zuschauen.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0014","text":"Der Sonne gleich will auch Zarathustra untergehn: nun sitzt er hier und wartet, alte zerbrochne Tafeln um sich und auch neue Tafeln,—halbbeschriebene.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"128e61283eddec47741f73a10a17a0eb0fa26aef7688a3382cc124742d6c743d","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182917951716Z_bf912046","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"3.","source_sha256":"128e61283eddec47741f73a10a17a0eb0fa26aef7688a3382cc124742d6c743d","sha256":"adc00212e044852a040b6fc10a8297e0166ea1d57f59bedfd98aca932046a783","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:29:17.951739+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0001","text":"## 3.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0002","text":"It was there, too, that I picked up by the wayside the word “Overman,” and that man is something that must be overcome,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0003","text":"—that man is a bridge and not an end: calling himself blessed on account of his noon and evening, as a way to new dawns:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0004","text":"—the Zarathustra-word of the great noon, and whatever else I hung upon man, like purple second evening-reds.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0005","text":"Truly, I also made them see new stars together with new nights; and over clouds and day and night I stretched out laughter still further, like a motley tent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0006","text":"I taught them all my poetizing and striving: to poetize into one and to bring together what is fragment in man and riddle and grim chance,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0007","text":"—as poet, riddle-guesser, and redeemer of chance I taught them to create at the future, and, creating, to redeem all that was —.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0008","text":"To redeem the past in man and to reshape every “It was” until the will says: “But thus I willed it! Thus shall I will it—”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0009","text":"—This I called redemption for them; this alone I taught them to call redemption. -—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0010","text":"Now I await my redemption—that I may go to them for the last time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0011","text":"For once more I wish to go to men: among them I wish to go under, dying I wish to give them my richest gift!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0012","text":"This I learned from the sun, when it goes down, the over-rich one: then it pours gold into the sea out of inexhaustible wealth,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0013","text":"—so that even the poorest fisherman rows with a golden oar! For once I saw this and could not have enough of tears as I watched.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0014","text":"Like the sun, Zarathustra too wishes to go under: now he sits here and waits, old broken tablets around him and also new tablets,—half-written.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_4:P0014"]}],"source_digest":"8b4cc0299ab03b463e3a5bd6579a6aa7eaaff60586f76fc41a11c854c0b247e9","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"3.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 3","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","3."],"original_title":"3."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"3.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 3","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","3."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"3.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_2","title":"4.","order":85,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_4C_2","text":"## 4.\n\nSiehe, hier ist eine neue Tafel: aber wo sind meine Brüder, die sie mit mir zu Thale und in fleischerne Herzen tragen?—\n\nAlso heischt es meine grosse Liebe zu den Fernsten: schone deinen Nächsten nicht! Der Mensch ist Etwas, das überwunden werden muss.\n\nEs giebt vielerlei Weg und Weise der Überwindung.- da siehe du zu! Aber nur ein Possenreisser denkt: „der Mensch kann auch übersprungen werden.“\n\nÜberwinde dich selber noch in deinem Nächsten: und ein Recht, das du dir rauben kannst, sollst du dir nicht geben lassen!\n\nWas du thust, das kann dir Keiner wieder thun. Siehe, es giebt keine Vergeltung.\n\nWer sich nicht befehlen kann, der soll gehorchen. Und Mancher kann sich befehlen, aber da fehlt noch Viel, dass er sich auch gehorche!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_2:P0001","text":"## 4.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_2:P0002","text":"Siehe, hier ist eine neue Tafel: aber wo sind meine Brüder, die sie mit mir zu Thale und in fleischerne Herzen tragen?—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_2:P0003","text":"Also heischt es meine grosse Liebe zu den Fernsten: schone deinen Nächsten nicht! Der Mensch ist Etwas, das überwunden werden muss.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_2:P0004","text":"Es giebt vielerlei Weg und Weise der Überwindung.- da siehe du zu! Aber nur ein Possenreisser denkt: „der Mensch kann auch übersprungen werden.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_2:P0005","text":"Überwinde dich selber noch in deinem Nächsten: und ein Recht, das du dir rauben kannst, sollst du dir nicht geben lassen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_2:P0006","text":"Was du thust, das kann dir Keiner wieder thun. Siehe, es giebt keine Vergeltung.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_2:P0007","text":"Wer sich nicht befehlen kann, der soll gehorchen. Und Mancher kann sich befehlen, aber da fehlt noch Viel, dass er sich auch gehorche!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"b1d9487aa82c6e88a84dccd27fae62db583a5ad0e8efd62f426dd2889d8f3e54","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182921743248Z_74f24749","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"4.","source_sha256":"b1d9487aa82c6e88a84dccd27fae62db583a5ad0e8efd62f426dd2889d8f3e54","sha256":"2fe517322ec387e4d9579e2ed99c0da78ff3543f35365d5bba9e10da5c1efe50","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:29:21.743271+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_2:P0001","text":"## 4.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_2:P0002","text":"Behold, here is a new tablet: but where are my brothers who will carry it with me down into the valley and into fleshly hearts?—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_2:P0003","text":"Thus my great love for the most distant demands it: spare not your neighbour! Man is something that must be overcome.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_2:P0004","text":"There are many kinds of way and manner of overcoming.—there, see to yourself! But only a buffoon thinks: “man can also be jumped over.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_2:P0005","text":"Overcome yourself even in your neighbour: and a right that you can seize for yourself, you shall not let be given to you!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_2:P0006","text":"What you do, no one can do to you again. Behold, there is no requital.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_2:P0007","text":"He who cannot command himself should obey. And many a one can command himself, but much is still lacking for him also to obey himself!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_2:P0007"]}],"source_digest":"491ddc015c1bf84838bf5874e21761f0de25760711a7a689eb181da8c90220b1","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"4.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 4","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","4."],"original_title":"4."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"4.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 4","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","4."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"4.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_2","title":"5.","order":86,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_5C_2","text":"## 5.\n\nAlso will es die Art edler Seelen: sie wollen Nichts umsonst haben, am wenigsten das Leben.\n\nWer vom Pöbel ist, der will umsonst leben; wir Anderen aber, denen das Leben sich gab,—wir sinnen immer darüber, was wir am besten dagegen geben!\n\nUnd wahrlich, diess ist eine vornehme Rede, welche spricht: „was uns das Leben verspricht, das wollen wir —dem Leben halten!“\n\nMan soll nicht geniessen wollen, wo man nicht zu geniessen giebt. Und—man soll nicht geniessen wollen !\n\nGenuss und Unschuld nämlich sind die schamhaftesten Dinge: Beide wollen nicht gesucht sein. Man soll sie haben —, aber man soll eher noch nach Schuld und Schmerzen suchen !—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_2:P0001","text":"## 5.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_2:P0002","text":"Also will es die Art edler Seelen: sie wollen Nichts umsonst haben, am wenigsten das Leben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_2:P0003","text":"Wer vom Pöbel ist, der will umsonst leben; wir Anderen aber, denen das Leben sich gab,—wir sinnen immer darüber, was wir am besten dagegen geben!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_2:P0004","text":"Und wahrlich, diess ist eine vornehme Rede, welche spricht: „was uns das Leben verspricht, das wollen wir —dem Leben halten!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_2:P0005","text":"Man soll nicht geniessen wollen, wo man nicht zu geniessen giebt. Und—man soll nicht geniessen wollen !","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_2:P0006","text":"Genuss und Unschuld nämlich sind die schamhaftesten Dinge: Beide wollen nicht gesucht sein. Man soll sie haben —, aber man soll eher noch nach Schuld und Schmerzen suchen !—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"b8dd708263a3fd03fff055567a8337b6a54ba412eea130fcb948a8527c34bca3","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182925125898Z_3b30091d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"5.","source_sha256":"b8dd708263a3fd03fff055567a8337b6a54ba412eea130fcb948a8527c34bca3","sha256":"3093e60e34edaab0bfc01e10f6693a82ff6041c8ae84cea5fd5058640bf7f712","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:29:25.125921+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_2:P0001","text":"## 5.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_2:P0002","text":"Thus it is the way of noble souls: they wish to have nothing for nothing, least of all life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_2:P0003","text":"Whoever is of the rabble wishes to live for nothing; but we others, to whom life gave itself,—we are always pondering what we might best give in return for it!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_2:P0004","text":"And truly, this is a noble saying, which says: “what life promises us, that we wish to —keep faith with life!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_2:P0005","text":"One should not wish to enjoy where one does not give to enjoy. And—one should not wish to enjoy!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_2:P0006","text":"For enjoyment and innocence are the most shamefaced things: both do not wish to be sought. One should have them —, but one should rather still seek guilt and pains!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_2:P0006"]}],"source_digest":"74376202384ae4167fca552ef1e7bbec6ae64bd2e90b4c553361b7d6488691bb","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"5.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 5","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","5."],"original_title":"5."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"5.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 5","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","5."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"5.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_2","title":"6.","order":87,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_6C_2","text":"## 6.\n\nOh meine Brüder, wer ein Erstling ist, der wird immer geopfert. Nun aber sind wir Erstlinge.\n\nWir bluten Alle an geheimen Opfertischen, wir brennen und braten Alle zu Ehren alter Götzenbilder.\n\nUnser Bestes ist noch jung: das reizt alte Gaumen. Unser Fleisch ist zart, unser Fell ist nur ein Lamm-Fell:—wie sollten wir nicht alte Götzenpriester reizen!\n\nIn uns selber wohnt er noch, der alte Götzenpriester, der unser Bestes sich zum Schmause brät. Ach, meine Brüder, wie sollten Erstlinge nicht Opfer sein!\n\nAber so will es unsre Art; und ich liebe Die, welche sich nicht bewahren wollen. Die Untergehenden liebe ich mit meiner ganzen Liebe: denn sie gehn hinüber.—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_2:P0001","text":"## 6.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_2:P0002","text":"Oh meine Brüder, wer ein Erstling ist, der wird immer geopfert. Nun aber sind wir Erstlinge.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_2:P0003","text":"Wir bluten Alle an geheimen Opfertischen, wir brennen und braten Alle zu Ehren alter Götzenbilder.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_2:P0004","text":"Unser Bestes ist noch jung: das reizt alte Gaumen. Unser Fleisch ist zart, unser Fell ist nur ein Lamm-Fell:—wie sollten wir nicht alte Götzenpriester reizen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_2:P0005","text":"In uns selber wohnt er noch, der alte Götzenpriester, der unser Bestes sich zum Schmause brät. Ach, meine Brüder, wie sollten Erstlinge nicht Opfer sein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_2:P0006","text":"Aber so will es unsre Art; und ich liebe Die, welche sich nicht bewahren wollen. Die Untergehenden liebe ich mit meiner ganzen Liebe: denn sie gehn hinüber.—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"c5a4ccdab910a3218b9f1be6a1bfec0589014d5521bbc41cc4bf593f26957df7","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182928961570Z_7b472bcc","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"6.","source_sha256":"c5a4ccdab910a3218b9f1be6a1bfec0589014d5521bbc41cc4bf593f26957df7","sha256":"d47f927da6406827f3dae7685d2065b0f31c31e444b9fb99f5217d21c3b25188","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:29:28.961597+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_2:P0001","text":"## 6.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_2:P0002","text":"Oh my brothers, whoever is a firstling is always sacrificed. But now we are firstlings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_2:P0003","text":"We all bleed at secret sacrificial tables; we all burn and roast in honour of old idols.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_2:P0004","text":"What is best in us is still young: that excites old palates. Our flesh is tender, our hide is merely a lamb's hide:—how should we not excite old idol-priests!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_2:P0005","text":"Within ourselves he still dwells, the old idol-priest, who roasts what is best in us for his feast. Ah, my brothers, how should firstlings not be sacrifices!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_2:P0006","text":"But thus our kind wills it; and I love those who do not wish to preserve themselves. I love those who are going under with all my love: for they are going over.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_2:P0006"]}],"source_digest":"157fe252ffa1f714c67277426b6dee2fd7397f856302e0fad2af96fb0e160545","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"6.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 6","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","6."],"original_title":"6."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"6.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 6","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","6."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"6.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_2","title":"7.","order":88,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_7C_2","text":"## 7.\n\nWahr sein—das können Wenige! Und wer es kann, der will es noch nicht! Am wenigsten aber können es die Guten.\n\nOh diese Guten!—Gute Menschen reden nie die Wahrheit; für den Geist ist solchermaassen gut sein eine Krankheit.\n\nSie geben nach, diese Guten, sie ergeben sich, ihr Herz spricht nach, ihr Grund gehorcht; wer aber gehorcht, der hört sich selber nicht!\n\nAlles, was den Guten böse heisst, muss zusammen kommen, dass Eine Wahrheit geboren werde: oh meine Brüder, seid ihr auch böse genug zu dieser Wahrheit?\n\nDas verwegene Wagen, das lange Misstrauen, das grausame Nein, der Überdruss, das Schneiden in’s Lebendige—wie selten kommt das zusammen! Aus solchem Samen aber wird Wahrheit gezeugt!\n\nNeben dem bösen Gewissen wuchs bisher alles Wissen ! Zerbrecht, zerbrecht mir, ihr Erkennenden, die alten Tafeln!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_2:P0001","text":"## 7.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_2:P0002","text":"Wahr sein—das können Wenige! Und wer es kann, der will es noch nicht! Am wenigsten aber können es die Guten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_2:P0003","text":"Oh diese Guten!—Gute Menschen reden nie die Wahrheit; für den Geist ist solchermaassen gut sein eine Krankheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_2:P0004","text":"Sie geben nach, diese Guten, sie ergeben sich, ihr Herz spricht nach, ihr Grund gehorcht; wer aber gehorcht, der hört sich selber nicht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_2:P0005","text":"Alles, was den Guten böse heisst, muss zusammen kommen, dass Eine Wahrheit geboren werde: oh meine Brüder, seid ihr auch böse genug zu dieser Wahrheit?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_2:P0006","text":"Das verwegene Wagen, das lange Misstrauen, das grausame Nein, der Überdruss, das Schneiden in’s Lebendige—wie selten kommt das zusammen! Aus solchem Samen aber wird Wahrheit gezeugt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_2:P0007","text":"Neben dem bösen Gewissen wuchs bisher alles Wissen ! Zerbrecht, zerbrecht mir, ihr Erkennenden, die alten Tafeln!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"df80a7b7e3619c809abffa15630e7c966c2b7bc3c9b243cc83f473d0a3928a3f","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182932802396Z_8a595eb8","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"7.","source_sha256":"df80a7b7e3619c809abffa15630e7c966c2b7bc3c9b243cc83f473d0a3928a3f","sha256":"79d3356895a0e7684a4b8143579e382034e6cca61b559a1a80bc4bc510ed4356","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:29:32.802434+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_2:P0001","text":"## 7.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_2:P0002","text":"To be truthful—that is something few can do! And whoever can do it, does not yet will it! But least of all can the good do it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_2:P0003","text":"Oh these good ones!—Good people never speak the truth; for the spirit, being good in such a manner is a sickness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_2:P0004","text":"They yield, these good ones, they submit, their heart repeats after, their ground obeys; but whoever obeys does not hear himself!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_2:P0005","text":"Everything that is called evil by the good must come together, so that one truth may be born: oh my brothers, are you also evil enough for this truth?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_2:P0006","text":"The daring venture, the long mistrust, the cruel No, satiety, cutting into the living—how rarely does this come together! But from such seed truth is begotten!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_2:P0007","text":"Alongside the bad conscience all knowledge has hitherto grown! Break, break for me, you knowers, the old tables!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_2:P0007"]}],"source_digest":"c2ca148b7166940e9fcf6bdfc4846df3b0f34e73c0f92e7a333827a6ab5e4893","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"7.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 7","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","7."],"original_title":"7."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"7.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 7","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","7."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"7.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_3","title":"8.","order":89,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_8C_3","text":"## 8.\n\nWenn das Wasser Balken hat, wenn Stege und Geländer über den Fluss springen: wahrlich, da findet Keiner Glauben, der da spricht: „Alles ist im Fluss.“\n\nSondern selber die Tölpel widersprechen ihm. „Wie? sagen die Tölpel, Alles wäre im Flusse? Balken und Geländer sind doch über dem Flusse!“\n\n„ Über dem Flusse ist Alles fest, alle die Werthe der Dinge, die Brücken, Begriffe, alles „Gut“ und „Böse“: das ist Alles fest!“—\n\nKommt gar der harte Winter, der Fluss-Thierbändiger: dann lernen auch die Witzigsten Misstrauen; und, wahrlich, nicht nur die Tölpel sprechen dann: „Sollte nicht Alles— stille stehn ?“\n\n„Im Grunde steht Alles stille“ —, das ist eine rechte Winter-Lehre, ein gut Ding für unfruchtbare Zeit, ein guter Trost für Winterschläfer und Ofenhocker.\n\n„Im Grund steht Alles still“—: dagegen aber predigt der Thauwind!\n\nDer Thauwind, ein Stier, der kein pflügender Stier ist,—ein wüthender Stier, ein Zerstörer, der mit zornigen Hörnern Eis bricht! Eis aber— bricht Stege !\n\nOh meine Brüder, ist jetzt nicht Alles im Flusse ? Sind nicht alle Geländer und Stege in’s Wasser gefallen? Wer hielte sich noch an „Gut“ und „Böse“ ?\n\n„Wehe uns! Heil uns! Der Thauwind weht!“—Also predigt mir, oh meine Brüder, durch alle Gassen!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0001","text":"## 8.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0002","text":"Wenn das Wasser Balken hat, wenn Stege und Geländer über den Fluss springen: wahrlich, da findet Keiner Glauben, der da spricht: „Alles ist im Fluss.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0003","text":"Sondern selber die Tölpel widersprechen ihm. „Wie? sagen die Tölpel, Alles wäre im Flusse? Balken und Geländer sind doch über dem Flusse!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0004","text":"„ Über dem Flusse ist Alles fest, alle die Werthe der Dinge, die Brücken, Begriffe, alles „Gut“ und „Böse“: das ist Alles fest!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0005","text":"Kommt gar der harte Winter, der Fluss-Thierbändiger: dann lernen auch die Witzigsten Misstrauen; und, wahrlich, nicht nur die Tölpel sprechen dann: „Sollte nicht Alles— stille stehn ?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0006","text":"„Im Grunde steht Alles stille“ —, das ist eine rechte Winter-Lehre, ein gut Ding für unfruchtbare Zeit, ein guter Trost für Winterschläfer und Ofenhocker.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0007","text":"„Im Grund steht Alles still“—: dagegen aber predigt der Thauwind!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0008","text":"Der Thauwind, ein Stier, der kein pflügender Stier ist,—ein wüthender Stier, ein Zerstörer, der mit zornigen Hörnern Eis bricht! Eis aber— bricht Stege !","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0009","text":"Oh meine Brüder, ist jetzt nicht Alles im Flusse ? Sind nicht alle Geländer und Stege in’s Wasser gefallen? Wer hielte sich noch an „Gut“ und „Böse“ ?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0010","text":"„Wehe uns! Heil uns! Der Thauwind weht!“—Also predigt mir, oh meine Brüder, durch alle Gassen!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"d7ed38a0573fdcbcfc08702e648f09a7c46110b1f0949a8e4ee236f4088b2caa","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182938130459Z_d74385aa","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"8.","source_sha256":"d7ed38a0573fdcbcfc08702e648f09a7c46110b1f0949a8e4ee236f4088b2caa","sha256":"7b2c8235796a3dfff0c48edd5d8615f4e615be66cbecbe5c81de696b18a0a337","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:29:38.130482+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0001","text":"## 8.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0002","text":"When the water has beams, when footbridges and railings leap across the river: verily, no one finds belief there who says: “Everything is in flux.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0003","text":"Rather, the simpletons themselves contradict him. “What? say the simpletons, everything were in flux? Yet beams and railings are over the river!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0004","text":"“ Above the river everything is firm, all the values of things, the bridges, concepts, all ‘Good’ and ‘Evil’: all that is firm!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0005","text":"But when hard winter comes, the tamer of river-beasts: then even the wittiest learn distrust; and, verily, then not only the simpletons say: “Should not everything—stand still?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0006","text":"“At bottom everything stands still”—that is a proper winter doctrine, a good thing for barren times, a good consolation for hibernators and stove-sitters.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0007","text":"“At bottom everything stands still”—but against this the thaw-wind preaches!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0008","text":"The thaw-wind, a bull that is no ploughing bull,—a raging bull, a destroyer, which breaks ice with angry horns! But ice—breaks footbridges!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0009","text":"Oh my brothers, is not everything now in flux? Have not all railings and footbridges fallen into the water? Who would still hold to ‘Good’ and ‘Evil’?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0010","text":"“Woe unto us! Hail unto us! The thaw-wind blows!”—Thus preach to me, oh my brothers, through all the streets!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_3:P0010"]}],"source_digest":"d6bc3440d623119310ad0fa7ab63ee13fba14902d156f75a2f566a9280ad4cba","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"8.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 8","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","8."],"original_title":"8."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"8.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 8","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","8."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"8.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_4","title":"8.","order":90,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_8C_4","text":"## 8.\n\nEs giebt einen alten Wahn, der heisst Gut und Böse. Um Wahrsager und Sterndeuter drehte sich bisher das Rad dieses Wahns.\n\nEinst glaubte man an Wahrsager und Sterndeuter: und darum glaubte man „Alles ist Schicksal: du sollst, denn du musst!“\n\nDann wieder misstraute man allen Wahrsagern und Sterndeutern: und darum glaubte man „Alles ist Freiheit: du kannst, denn du willst!“\n\nOh meine Brüder, über Sterne und Zukunft ist bisher nur gewähnt, nicht gewusst worden: und darum ist über Gut und Böse bisher nur gewähnt, nicht gewusst worden!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_4:P0001","text":"## 8.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_4:P0002","text":"Es giebt einen alten Wahn, der heisst Gut und Böse. Um Wahrsager und Sterndeuter drehte sich bisher das Rad dieses Wahns.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_4:P0003","text":"Einst glaubte man an Wahrsager und Sterndeuter: und darum glaubte man „Alles ist Schicksal: du sollst, denn du musst!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_4:P0004","text":"Dann wieder misstraute man allen Wahrsagern und Sterndeutern: und darum glaubte man „Alles ist Freiheit: du kannst, denn du willst!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_4:P0005","text":"Oh meine Brüder, über Sterne und Zukunft ist bisher nur gewähnt, nicht gewusst worden: und darum ist über Gut und Böse bisher nur gewähnt, nicht gewusst worden!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"8070a6badf1d8206e85f22e674891e8190d54d90e2fa0e94a1a4f647b9bfc091","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182941176415Z_595021ab","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"8.","source_sha256":"8070a6badf1d8206e85f22e674891e8190d54d90e2fa0e94a1a4f647b9bfc091","sha256":"e00e0f09e64c1c59c38b5751e77f49a26fbab93abbb5554cf89324792872f3c4","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:29:41.176453+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_4:P0001","text":"## 8.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_4:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_4:P0002","text":"There is an old delusion called Good and Evil. Until now, the wheel of this delusion has revolved around soothsayers and astrologers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_4:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_4:P0003","text":"Once people believed in soothsayers and astrologers: and therefore they believed, “Everything is fate: thou shalt, for thou must!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_4:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_4:P0004","text":"Then again they mistrusted all soothsayers and astrologers: and therefore they believed, “Everything is freedom: thou canst, for thou wilt!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_4:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_4:P0005","text":"Oh my brothers, hitherto only conjecture, not knowledge, has existed concerning stars and the future: and therefore hitherto only conjecture, not knowledge, has existed concerning Good and Evil!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_4:P0005"]}],"source_digest":"84520d2f1e07d31f9fa0661722cee6a38dac75b0ab672ff30bc982cd3a89a819","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"8.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 8","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","8."],"original_title":"8."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"8.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 8","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","8."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"8.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_2","title":"10.","order":91,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_10_2","text":"## 10.\n\n„Du sollst nicht rauben! Du sollst nicht todtschlagen!“—solche Worte hiess man einst heilig; vor ihnen beugte man Knie und Köpfe und zog die Schuhe aus.\n\nAber ich frage euch: wo gab es je bessere Räuber und Todtschläger in der Welt, als es solche heilige Worte waren?\n\nIst in allem Leben selber nicht—Rauben und Todtschlagen? Und dass solche Worte heilig hiessen, wurde damit die Wahrheit selber nicht—todtgeschlagen?\n\nOder war es eine Predigt des Todes, dass heilig hiess, was allem Leben widersprach und widerrieth?—Oh meine Brüder, zerbrecht, zerbrecht mir die alten tafeln!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_2:P0001","text":"## 10.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_2:P0002","text":"„Du sollst nicht rauben! Du sollst nicht todtschlagen!“—solche Worte hiess man einst heilig; vor ihnen beugte man Knie und Köpfe und zog die Schuhe aus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_2:P0003","text":"Aber ich frage euch: wo gab es je bessere Räuber und Todtschläger in der Welt, als es solche heilige Worte waren?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_2:P0004","text":"Ist in allem Leben selber nicht—Rauben und Todtschlagen? Und dass solche Worte heilig hiessen, wurde damit die Wahrheit selber nicht—todtgeschlagen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_2:P0005","text":"Oder war es eine Predigt des Todes, dass heilig hiess, was allem Leben widersprach und widerrieth?—Oh meine Brüder, zerbrecht, zerbrecht mir die alten tafeln!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"6679f65c68de913d21bd8e1c24fea7801cf75812c0cb7773552df424bb8a76f9","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182944102785Z_040d2f3a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"10.","source_sha256":"6679f65c68de913d21bd8e1c24fea7801cf75812c0cb7773552df424bb8a76f9","sha256":"493ca3761bd62aad82e54892e76a5738f2d028c96b121001b95a7676ac4e7280","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:29:44.102828+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_2:P0001","text":"## 10.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_2:P0002","text":"“Thou shalt not rob! Thou shalt not kill!”—such words were once called holy; before them people bent knees and heads and took off their shoes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_2:P0003","text":"But I ask you: where in the world have there ever been better robbers and killers than such holy words were?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_2:P0004","text":"Is not—in all life itself—robbing and killing? And because such words were called holy, was truth itself not thereby—killed?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_2:P0005","text":"Or was it a sermon of death that what contradicted and counselled against all life was called holy?—Oh my brothers, break, break for me the old tables!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10_2:P0005"]}],"source_digest":"5ce3414454e5411db304ac8d987761035ef2c00dfa64945155b96e221845b2f7","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"10.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 10","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","10."],"original_title":"10."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"10.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 10","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","10."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"10.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11CH","title":"11.","order":92,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_11CH","text":"## 11.\n\nDiess ist mein Mitleid mit allem Vergangenen, dass ich sehe: es ist preisgegeben,—\n\n—der Gnade, dem Geiste, dem Wahnsinne jedes Geschlechtes preisgegeben, das kommt und Alles, was war, zu seiner Brücke umdeutet!\n\nEin grosser Gewalt-Herr könnte kommen, ein gewitzter Unhold, der mit seiner Gnade und Ungnade alles Vergangene zwänge und zwängte: bis es ihm Brücke würde und Vorzeichen und Herold und Hahnenschrei.\n\nDiess aber ist die andre Gefahr und mein andres Mitleiden:—wer vom Pöbel ist, dessen Gedenken geht zurück bis zum Grossvater,—mit dem Grossvater aber hört die Zeit auf.\n\nAlso ist alles Vergangene preisgegeben: denn es könnte einmal kommen, dass der Pöbel Herr würde und in seichten Gewässern alle Zeit ertränke.\n\nDarum, oh meine Brüder, bedarf es eines neuen Adels , der allem Pöbel und allem Gewalt-Herrischen Widersacher ist und auf neue Tafeln neu das Wort schreibt „edel“.\n\nVieler Edlen nämlich bedarf es und vielerlei Edlen, dass es Adel gebe! Oder, wie ich einst im Gleichniss sprach: „Das eben ist Göttlichkeit, dass es Götter, aber keinen Gott giebt!“","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_11CH:P0001","text":"## 11.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11CH:P0002","text":"Diess ist mein Mitleid mit allem Vergangenen, dass ich sehe: es ist preisgegeben,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11CH:P0003","text":"—der Gnade, dem Geiste, dem Wahnsinne jedes Geschlechtes preisgegeben, das kommt und Alles, was war, zu seiner Brücke umdeutet!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11CH:P0004","text":"Ein grosser Gewalt-Herr könnte kommen, ein gewitzter Unhold, der mit seiner Gnade und Ungnade alles Vergangene zwänge und zwängte: bis es ihm Brücke würde und Vorzeichen und Herold und Hahnenschrei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11CH:P0005","text":"Diess aber ist die andre Gefahr und mein andres Mitleiden:—wer vom Pöbel ist, dessen Gedenken geht zurück bis zum Grossvater,—mit dem Grossvater aber hört die Zeit auf.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11CH:P0006","text":"Also ist alles Vergangene preisgegeben: denn es könnte einmal kommen, dass der Pöbel Herr würde und in seichten Gewässern alle Zeit ertränke.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11CH:P0007","text":"Darum, oh meine Brüder, bedarf es eines neuen Adels , der allem Pöbel und allem Gewalt-Herrischen Widersacher ist und auf neue Tafeln neu das Wort schreibt „edel“.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11CH:P0008","text":"Vieler Edlen nämlich bedarf es und vielerlei Edlen, dass es Adel gebe! Oder, wie ich einst im Gleichniss sprach: „Das eben ist Göttlichkeit, dass es Götter, aber keinen Gott giebt!“","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"de49400c2d9588900ec911e3cf1a72daf4ef3d0b5aaed99d757faee06e16af6a","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182949344731Z_2f2b3284","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"11.","source_sha256":"de49400c2d9588900ec911e3cf1a72daf4ef3d0b5aaed99d757faee06e16af6a","sha256":"16300b33319551f3d6c7e773fab395cb419d09d5d4e71339169b3d1eaae89243","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:29:49.344755+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_11CH:P0001","text":"## 11.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11CH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11CH:P0002","text":"This is my pity for all that is past, that I see: it is surrendered,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11CH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11CH:P0003","text":"—surrendered to the grace, the spirit, the madness of every generation that comes and reinterprets everything that was as its bridge!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11CH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11CH:P0004","text":"A great tyrant might come, a cunning monster, who with his grace and disfavour would compel and distort all that is past: until it became for him bridge and portent and herald and cockcrow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11CH:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11CH:P0005","text":"But this is the other danger and my other pity:—he who is of the rabble, his remembrance reaches back to his grandfather,—but with the grandfather time comes to an end.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11CH:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11CH:P0006","text":"Thus all that is past is surrendered: for it might one day happen that the rabble became master and drowned all time in shallow waters.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11CH:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11CH:P0007","text":"Therefore, O my brothers, there is need of a new nobility, which is adversary to all rabble and all tyranny and writes anew upon new tablets the word “noble.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11CH:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11CH:P0008","text":"For many noble ones are needed, and noble ones of many kinds, if there is to be nobility! Or, as I once said in parable: “That precisely is divinity, that there are gods, but no God!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11CH:P0008"]}],"source_digest":"9a0677bc189b6760ac3990516d21282d64d5d6d932023e03bd8abf3204f99df1","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"11.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 11","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","11."],"original_title":"11."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"11.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 11","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","11."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"11.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH","title":"12.","order":93,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_12CH","text":"## 12.\n\nOh meine Brüder, ich weihe und weise euch zu einem neuen Adel: ihr sollt mir Zeuger und Züchter werden und Säemänner der Zukunft,—\n\n—wahrlich, nicht zu einem Adel, den ihr kaufen könntet gleich den Krämern und mit Krämer-Golde: denn wenig Werth hat Alles, was seinen Preis hat.\n\nNicht, woher ihr kommt, mache euch fürderhin eure Ehre, sondern wohin ihr geht! Euer Wille und euer Fuss, der über euch selber hinaus will,—das mache eure neue Ehre!\n\nWahrlich nicht, dass ihr einem Fürsten gedient habt—was liegt noch an Fürsten!—oder dem, was steht, zum Bollwerk wurdet, dass es fester stünde!\n\nNicht, dass euer Geschlecht an Höfen höfisch wurde, und ihr lerntet, bunt, einem Flamingo ähnlich, lange Stunden in flachen Teichen stehn.\n\n—Denn Stehen- können ist ein Verdienst bei Höflingen; und alle Höflinge glauben, zur Seligkeit nach dem Tode gehöre—Sitzen- dürfen !—\n\nNicht auch, dass ein Geist, den sie heilig nennen, eure Vorfahren in gelobte Länder führte, die ich nicht lobe: denn wo der schlimmste aller Bäume wuchs, das Kreuz,—an dem Lande ist Nichts zu loben!—\n\n—und wahrlich, wohin dieser „heilige Geist“ auch seine Ritter führte, immer liefen bei solchen Zügen—Ziegen und Gänse und Kreuz- und Querköpfe voran !—\n\nOh meine Brüder, nicht zurück soll euer Adel schauen, sondern hinaus ! Vertriebene sollt ihr sein aus allen Vater- und Urväterländern!\n\nEurer Kinder Land sollt ihr lieben: diese Liebe sei euer neuer Adel,—das unentdeckte, im feinsten Meere! Nach ihm heisse ich eure Segel suchen und suchen!\n\nAn euren Kindern sollt ihr gutmachen , dass ihr eurer Väter Kinder seid: alles Vergangene sollt ihr so erlösen! Diese neue Tafel stelle ich über euch!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0001","text":"## 12.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0002","text":"Oh meine Brüder, ich weihe und weise euch zu einem neuen Adel: ihr sollt mir Zeuger und Züchter werden und Säemänner der Zukunft,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0003","text":"—wahrlich, nicht zu einem Adel, den ihr kaufen könntet gleich den Krämern und mit Krämer-Golde: denn wenig Werth hat Alles, was seinen Preis hat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0004","text":"Nicht, woher ihr kommt, mache euch fürderhin eure Ehre, sondern wohin ihr geht! Euer Wille und euer Fuss, der über euch selber hinaus will,—das mache eure neue Ehre!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0005","text":"Wahrlich nicht, dass ihr einem Fürsten gedient habt—was liegt noch an Fürsten!—oder dem, was steht, zum Bollwerk wurdet, dass es fester stünde!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0006","text":"Nicht, dass euer Geschlecht an Höfen höfisch wurde, und ihr lerntet, bunt, einem Flamingo ähnlich, lange Stunden in flachen Teichen stehn.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0007","text":"—Denn Stehen- können ist ein Verdienst bei Höflingen; und alle Höflinge glauben, zur Seligkeit nach dem Tode gehöre—Sitzen- dürfen !—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0008","text":"Nicht auch, dass ein Geist, den sie heilig nennen, eure Vorfahren in gelobte Länder führte, die ich nicht lobe: denn wo der schlimmste aller Bäume wuchs, das Kreuz,—an dem Lande ist Nichts zu loben!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0009","text":"—und wahrlich, wohin dieser „heilige Geist“ auch seine Ritter führte, immer liefen bei solchen Zügen—Ziegen und Gänse und Kreuz- und Querköpfe voran !—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0010","text":"Oh meine Brüder, nicht zurück soll euer Adel schauen, sondern hinaus ! Vertriebene sollt ihr sein aus allen Vater- und Urväterländern!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0011","text":"Eurer Kinder Land sollt ihr lieben: diese Liebe sei euer neuer Adel,—das unentdeckte, im feinsten Meere! Nach ihm heisse ich eure Segel suchen und suchen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0012","text":"An euren Kindern sollt ihr gutmachen , dass ihr eurer Väter Kinder seid: alles Vergangene sollt ihr so erlösen! Diese neue Tafel stelle ich über euch!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"806733e33a0ec3a29d4dbea15b152f3adde5aef207c288f4ae16062bf744b358","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182955388820Z_f4d82cac","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"12.","source_sha256":"806733e33a0ec3a29d4dbea15b152f3adde5aef207c288f4ae16062bf744b358","sha256":"52e26fbc7a046c79ebc57c88fd3d61bf92ae1f2543d4c17c7dbfa37380dcf7a8","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:29:55.388902+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0001","text":"## 12.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_12CH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0002","text":"O my brothers, I consecrate and direct you to a new nobility: you shall become begetters and breeders for me, and sowers of the future,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_12CH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0003","text":"—truly, not to a nobility that you could buy like shopkeepers and with shopkeepers' gold: for little worth has all that has its price.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_12CH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0004","text":"Not whence you come shall henceforth make up your honour, but whither you go! Your will and your foot, which would go beyond yourselves,—that shall make up your new honour!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_12CH:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0005","text":"Truly not that you have served a prince—what still matters princes!—or that you became a bulwark for that which stands, so that it might stand more firmly!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_12CH:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0006","text":"Not that your race became courtly at courts, and you learned, variegated, like a flamingo, to stand for long hours in shallow pools.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_12CH:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0007","text":"—For being-able-to-stand is a merit among courtiers; and all courtiers believe that to blessedness after death belongs—being-allowed-to-sit!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_12CH:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0008","text":"Nor again that a spirit, which they call holy, led your forefathers into promised lands, which I do not praise: for where the worst of all trees grew, the cross,—there is nothing to praise in that land!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_12CH:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0009","text":"—and truly, wherever this “holy Spirit” led its knights, in such campaigns there always ran ahead—goats and geese and cross- and crooked-heads!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_12CH:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0010","text":"O my brothers, your nobility shall not look backward, but outward! You shall be exiles from all fatherlands and lands of your forefathers!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_12CH:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0011","text":"You shall love the land of your children: let this love be your new nobility,—the undiscovered, in the finest sea! After it I bid your sails seek and seek!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_12CH:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12CH:P0012","text":"In your children you shall make good the fact that you are children of your fathers: thus shall you redeem all that is past! This new tablet I set above you!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_12CH:P0012"]}],"source_digest":"dd9cc6b73befe628460060479874dec558d0fbcd6a7bcb52c8868b7afe0c5730","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"12.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 12","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","12."],"original_title":"12."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"12.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 12","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","12."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"12.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13CH","title":"13.","order":94,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_13CH","text":"## 13.\n\n„Wozu leben? Alles ist eitel! Leben—das ist Stroh dreschen; Leben—das ist sich verbrennen und doch nicht warm werden.“—\n\nSolch alterthümliches Geschwätz gilt immer noch als „Weisheit“; dass es aber alt ist und dumpfig riecht, darum wird es besser geehrt. Auch der Moder adelt.—\n\nKinder durften so reden: die scheuen das Feuer, weil es sie brannte! Es ist viel Kinderei in den alten Büchern der Weisheit.\n\nUnd wer immer „Stroh drischt“, wie sollte der auf das Dreschen lästern dürfen! Solchem Narren müsste man doch das Maul verbinden!\n\nSolche setzen sich zu Tisch und bringen Nichts mit, selbst den guten Hunger nicht:—und nun lästern sie „Alles ist eitel!“\n\nAber gut essen und trinken, oh meine Brüder, ist wahrlich keine eitle Kunst! Zerbrecht, zerbrecht mir die Tafeln der Nimmer-Frohen!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_13CH:P0001","text":"## 13.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13CH:P0002","text":"„Wozu leben? Alles ist eitel! Leben—das ist Stroh dreschen; Leben—das ist sich verbrennen und doch nicht warm werden.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13CH:P0003","text":"Solch alterthümliches Geschwätz gilt immer noch als „Weisheit“; dass es aber alt ist und dumpfig riecht, darum wird es besser geehrt. Auch der Moder adelt.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13CH:P0004","text":"Kinder durften so reden: die scheuen das Feuer, weil es sie brannte! Es ist viel Kinderei in den alten Büchern der Weisheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13CH:P0005","text":"Und wer immer „Stroh drischt“, wie sollte der auf das Dreschen lästern dürfen! Solchem Narren müsste man doch das Maul verbinden!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13CH:P0006","text":"Solche setzen sich zu Tisch und bringen Nichts mit, selbst den guten Hunger nicht:—und nun lästern sie „Alles ist eitel!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13CH:P0007","text":"Aber gut essen und trinken, oh meine Brüder, ist wahrlich keine eitle Kunst! Zerbrecht, zerbrecht mir die Tafeln der Nimmer-Frohen!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"40cff210e8ddf8f5e8722b90fea0d79c558b055cc8e884666ac7fa3a007013f5","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T182959351840Z_c14763ee","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"13.","source_sha256":"40cff210e8ddf8f5e8722b90fea0d79c558b055cc8e884666ac7fa3a007013f5","sha256":"07bccbec77f8ad3430842949230b25f1c1849b828e0298c1ef526611d05f42bb","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:29:59.351863+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_13CH:P0001","text":"## 13.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_13CH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13CH:P0002","text":"“What is the point of living? Everything is vain! Living—that is threshing straw; living—that is burning oneself and yet not getting warm.”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_13CH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13CH:P0003","text":"Such antiquated chatter still counts as “wisdom”; but because it is old and smells musty, it is held in better esteem. Even mould ennobles.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_13CH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13CH:P0004","text":"Children were permitted to speak thus: they shun the fire because it burned them! There is much childishness in the old books of wisdom.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_13CH:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13CH:P0005","text":"And whoever “threshes straw,” how should he be allowed to slander threshing! Such a fool ought surely to have his mouth bound!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_13CH:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13CH:P0006","text":"Such people sit down at table and bring nothing with them, not even a good appetite:—and now they slander, “Everything is vain!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_13CH:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13CH:P0007","text":"But eating and drinking well, oh my brothers, is truly no vain art! Break, break for me the tablets of those who are never joyful!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_13CH:P0007"]}],"source_digest":"a1e160a9c7accc21f0c505b487556ac47694273bee14d427751565ad16b4d7a8","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"13.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 13","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","13."],"original_title":"13."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"13.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 13","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","13."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"13.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14CH","title":"14.","order":95,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_14CH","text":"## 14.\n\n„Dem Reinen ist Alles rein“ —so spricht das Volk. Ich aber sage euch: den Schweinen wird Alles Schwein!\n\nDarum predigen die Schwärmer und Kopfhänger, denen auch das Herz niederhängt: „die Welt selber ist ein kothiges Ungeheuer.“\n\nDenn diese Alle sind unsäuberlichen Geistes; sonderlich aber Jene, welche nicht Ruhe, noch Rast haben, es sei denn, sie sehen die Welt von hinten ,—die Hinterweltler!\n\nDenen sage ich in’s Gesicht, ob es gleich nicht lieblich klingt: die Welt gleicht darin dem Menschen, dass sie einen Hintern hat,— so Viel ist wahr!\n\nEs giebt in der Welt viel Koth: so Viel ist wahr! Aber darum ist die Welt selber noch kein kothiges Ungeheuer!\n\nEs ist Weisheit darin, dass Vieles in der Welt übel riecht: der Ekel selber schafft Flügel und quellenahnende Kräfte!\n\nAn dem Besten ist noch Etwas zum Ekeln; und der Beste ist noch Etwas, das überwunden werden muss!—\n\nOh meine Brüder, es ist viel Weisheit darin, dass viel Koth in der Welt ist!—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_14CH:P0001","text":"## 14.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14CH:P0002","text":"„Dem Reinen ist Alles rein“ —so spricht das Volk. Ich aber sage euch: den Schweinen wird Alles Schwein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14CH:P0003","text":"Darum predigen die Schwärmer und Kopfhänger, denen auch das Herz niederhängt: „die Welt selber ist ein kothiges Ungeheuer.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14CH:P0004","text":"Denn diese Alle sind unsäuberlichen Geistes; sonderlich aber Jene, welche nicht Ruhe, noch Rast haben, es sei denn, sie sehen die Welt von hinten ,—die Hinterweltler!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14CH:P0005","text":"Denen sage ich in’s Gesicht, ob es gleich nicht lieblich klingt: die Welt gleicht darin dem Menschen, dass sie einen Hintern hat,— so Viel ist wahr!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14CH:P0006","text":"Es giebt in der Welt viel Koth: so Viel ist wahr! Aber darum ist die Welt selber noch kein kothiges Ungeheuer!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14CH:P0007","text":"Es ist Weisheit darin, dass Vieles in der Welt übel riecht: der Ekel selber schafft Flügel und quellenahnende Kräfte!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14CH:P0008","text":"An dem Besten ist noch Etwas zum Ekeln; und der Beste ist noch Etwas, das überwunden werden muss!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14CH:P0009","text":"Oh meine Brüder, es ist viel Weisheit darin, dass viel Koth in der Welt ist!—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"148e88bd37ab8edf8b498ee988518a8bf1e47cc778d55d0abf1e253bde563424","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183004550330Z_3664944d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"14.","source_sha256":"148e88bd37ab8edf8b498ee988518a8bf1e47cc778d55d0abf1e253bde563424","sha256":"a8e54729c0dc056b31fd6243e64e7bc11078f472b84cd1df3f99f101ee0b9f96","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:30:04.550354+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_14CH:P0001","text":"## 14.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_14CH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14CH:P0002","text":"“To the pure all things are pure”—thus speaks the people. But I say to you: to swine everything becomes swine!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_14CH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14CH:P0003","text":"Therefore the enthusiasts and hangdog men preach, whose hearts too hang down: “the world itself is a filthy monster.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_14CH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14CH:P0004","text":"For all these are of unclean spirit; but especially those who have neither rest nor respite, unless they see the world from behind,—the backworldsmen!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_14CH:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14CH:P0005","text":"To them I say to their faces, though it may not sound pleasant: the world resembles man in that it has an arse,—so much is true!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_14CH:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14CH:P0006","text":"There is much filth in the world: so much is true! But for that reason the world itself is not yet a filthy monster!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_14CH:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14CH:P0007","text":"There is wisdom in the fact that much in the world smells bad: disgust itself creates wings and powers that divine springs!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_14CH:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14CH:P0008","text":"Even the best still has something disgusting about it; and even the best is still something that must be overcome!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_14CH:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14CH:P0009","text":"Oh my brothers, there is much wisdom in there being much filth in the world!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_14CH:P0009"]}],"source_digest":"cc8482788d8c1d89160f7a8be257d733568dfa1890ec26701e5b323678f605a8","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"14.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 14","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","14."],"original_title":"14."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"14.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 14","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","14."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"14.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15CH","title":"15.","order":96,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_15CH","text":"## 15.\n\nSolche Sprüche hörte ich fromme Hinterweltler zu ihrem Gewissen reden; und wahrlich, ohne Arg und Falsch,—ob es Schon nichts Falscheres in der Welt giebt, noch Ärgeres.\n\n„Lass doch die Welt der Welt sein! Hebe dawider auch nicht Einen Finger auf!“\n\n„Lass, wer da wolle, die Leute würgen und stechen und schneiden und schaben: hebe dawider auch nicht Einen Finger auf! Darob lernen sie noch der Welt absagen.“\n\n„Und deine eigne Vernunft—die sollst du selber görgeln und würgen; denn es ist eine Vernunft von dieser Welt,—darob lernst du selber der Welt absagen.“ -\n\n—Zerbrecht, zerbrecht mir, oh meine Brüder, diese alten Tafeln der Frommen! Zersprecht mir die Sprüche der Welt-Verleumder!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_15CH:P0001","text":"## 15.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15CH:P0002","text":"Solche Sprüche hörte ich fromme Hinterweltler zu ihrem Gewissen reden; und wahrlich, ohne Arg und Falsch,—ob es Schon nichts Falscheres in der Welt giebt, noch Ärgeres.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15CH:P0003","text":"„Lass doch die Welt der Welt sein! Hebe dawider auch nicht Einen Finger auf!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15CH:P0004","text":"„Lass, wer da wolle, die Leute würgen und stechen und schneiden und schaben: hebe dawider auch nicht Einen Finger auf! Darob lernen sie noch der Welt absagen.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15CH:P0005","text":"„Und deine eigne Vernunft—die sollst du selber görgeln und würgen; denn es ist eine Vernunft von dieser Welt,—darob lernst du selber der Welt absagen.“ -","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15CH:P0006","text":"—Zerbrecht, zerbrecht mir, oh meine Brüder, diese alten Tafeln der Frommen! Zersprecht mir die Sprüche der Welt-Verleumder!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"7a32ced1cf0eee4a2e5d446ac54671e56cefab222eea6521281e29c4e6bbfad3","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183008102437Z_968b9f0e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"15.","source_sha256":"7a32ced1cf0eee4a2e5d446ac54671e56cefab222eea6521281e29c4e6bbfad3","sha256":"a160f8b045767466fe66ae94bbeced2cc9486e67d8d55427067ba3f5e4fd3b1e","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:30:08.102465+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_15CH:P0001","text":"## 15.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_15CH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15CH:P0002","text":"Such sayings I heard pious men of the world beyond speaking to their conscience; and verily, without malice and falsehood,—although there is nothing falser in the world, nor anything worse.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_15CH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15CH:P0003","text":"“Just let the world be the world! Do not lift even one finger against it!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_15CH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15CH:P0004","text":"“Let whoever wishes strangle and stab and cut and scrape people: do not lift even one finger against it! Thereby they may yet learn to renounce the world.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_15CH:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15CH:P0005","text":"“And your own reason—you yourself shall throttle and strangle it; for it is a reason of this world,—thereby you yourself learn to renounce the world.” -","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_15CH:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15CH:P0006","text":"—Break, break for me, O my brothers, these old tables of the pious! Break up for me the sayings of the slanderers of the world!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_15CH:P0006"]}],"source_digest":"bcd0757ce61f5e00f95f8893ef10776d6fb0d0d1bf8c02add90cdbe91327a911","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"15.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 15","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","15."],"original_title":"15."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"15.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 15","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","15."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"15.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH","title":"16.","order":97,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_16CH","text":"## 16.\n\n„Wer viel lernt, der verlernt alles heftige Begehren“ —das flüstert man heute sich zu auf allen dunklen Gassen.\n\n„Weisheit macht müde, es lohnt sich—Nichts; du sollst nicht begehren!“—diese neue Tafel fand ich hängen selbst auf offnen Märkten.\n\nZerbrecht mir, oh meine Brüder, zerbrecht mir auch diese neue Tafel! Die Welt-Müden hängten sie hin und die Prediger des Todes, und auch die Stockmeister: denn seht, es ist auch eine Predigt zur Knechtschaft!—\n\nDass sie schlecht lernten und das Beste nicht, und Alles zu früh und Alles zu geschwind: dass sie schlecht assen , daher kam ihnen jener verdorbene Magen,—\n\n—ein verdorbener Magen ist nämlich ihr Geist: der räth zum Tode! Denn wahrlich, meine Brüder, der Geist ist ein Magen!\n\nDas Leben ist ein Born der Lust: aber aus wem der verdorbene Magen redet, der Vater der Trübsal, dem sind alle Quellen vergiftet.\n\nErkennen: das ist Lust dem Löwen-willigen! Aber wer müde wurde, der wird selber nur „gewollt“, mit dem spielen alle Wellen.\n\nUnd so ist es immer schwacher Menschen Art: sie verlieren sich auf ihren Wegen. Und zuletzt fragt noch ihre Müdigkeit: „wozu giengen wir jemals Wege! Es ist Alles gleich!“\n\nDenen klingt es lieblich zu Ohren, dass gepredigt wird: „Es verlohnt sich Nichts! Ihr sollt nicht wollen!“ Diess aber ist eine Predigt zur Knechtschaft.\n\nOh meine Brüder, ein frischer Brause-Wind kommt Zarathustra allen Weg-Müden; viele Nasen wird er noch niesen machen!\n\nAuch durch Mauern bläst mein freier Athem, und hinein in Gefängnisse und eingefangne Geister!\n\nWollen befreit: denn Wollen ist Schaffen: so lehre ich. Und nur zum Schaffen sollt ihr lernen!\n\nUnd auch das Lernen sollt ihr erst von mir lernen , das Gut-Lernen!—Wer Ohren hat, der höre!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0001","text":"## 16.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0002","text":"„Wer viel lernt, der verlernt alles heftige Begehren“ —das flüstert man heute sich zu auf allen dunklen Gassen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0003","text":"„Weisheit macht müde, es lohnt sich—Nichts; du sollst nicht begehren!“—diese neue Tafel fand ich hängen selbst auf offnen Märkten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0004","text":"Zerbrecht mir, oh meine Brüder, zerbrecht mir auch diese neue Tafel! Die Welt-Müden hängten sie hin und die Prediger des Todes, und auch die Stockmeister: denn seht, es ist auch eine Predigt zur Knechtschaft!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0005","text":"Dass sie schlecht lernten und das Beste nicht, und Alles zu früh und Alles zu geschwind: dass sie schlecht assen , daher kam ihnen jener verdorbene Magen,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0006","text":"—ein verdorbener Magen ist nämlich ihr Geist: der räth zum Tode! Denn wahrlich, meine Brüder, der Geist ist ein Magen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0007","text":"Das Leben ist ein Born der Lust: aber aus wem der verdorbene Magen redet, der Vater der Trübsal, dem sind alle Quellen vergiftet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0008","text":"Erkennen: das ist Lust dem Löwen-willigen! Aber wer müde wurde, der wird selber nur „gewollt“, mit dem spielen alle Wellen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0009","text":"Und so ist es immer schwacher Menschen Art: sie verlieren sich auf ihren Wegen. Und zuletzt fragt noch ihre Müdigkeit: „wozu giengen wir jemals Wege! Es ist Alles gleich!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0010","text":"Denen klingt es lieblich zu Ohren, dass gepredigt wird: „Es verlohnt sich Nichts! Ihr sollt nicht wollen!“ Diess aber ist eine Predigt zur Knechtschaft.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0011","text":"Oh meine Brüder, ein frischer Brause-Wind kommt Zarathustra allen Weg-Müden; viele Nasen wird er noch niesen machen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0012","text":"Auch durch Mauern bläst mein freier Athem, und hinein in Gefängnisse und eingefangne Geister!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0013","text":"Wollen befreit: denn Wollen ist Schaffen: so lehre ich. Und nur zum Schaffen sollt ihr lernen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0014","text":"Und auch das Lernen sollt ihr erst von mir lernen , das Gut-Lernen!—Wer Ohren hat, der höre!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"af413f6d852b16b456fbf4907f1106445c340a63955f7bbf0e1a5a6424a3f640","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183015407759Z_6d8e1243","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"16.","source_sha256":"af413f6d852b16b456fbf4907f1106445c340a63955f7bbf0e1a5a6424a3f640","sha256":"0e1c87ad5bb6ed7cca00c288c189ed20ebc0c74de9a8d1dcbc3946a6831dc2ec","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:30:15.407804+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0001","text":"## 16.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_16CH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0002","text":"“He who learns much unlearns all vehement desire”—thus is whispered today in all dark alleyways.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_16CH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0003","text":"“Wisdom makes weary, it is worth—Nothing; thou shalt not desire!”—this new tablet I found hanging even in open marketplaces.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_16CH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0004","text":"Break for me, oh my brothers, break for me this new tablet too! The world-weary hung it there, and the preachers of death, and also the rod-masters: for behold, it too is a sermon unto servitude!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_16CH:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0005","text":"That they learned badly and not the best, and everything too early and everything too hastily: that they ate badly, from this came to them that spoiled stomach,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_16CH:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0006","text":"—for a spoiled stomach is their spirit: it counsels death! For verily, my brothers, the spirit is a stomach!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_16CH:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0007","text":"Life is a wellspring of delight: but for him out of whom the spoiled stomach speaks, the father of tribulation, all springs are poisoned.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_16CH:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0008","text":"Knowing: that is delight for the lion-willed! But he who has become weary is himself only “willed,” all waves play with him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_16CH:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0009","text":"And thus it is ever the way of weak human beings: they lose themselves on their paths. And at last even their weariness asks: “Why did we ever go on paths! Everything is the same!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_16CH:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0010","text":"To such ears it sounds sweet when it is preached: “Nothing is worthwhile! Ye shall not will!” But this is a sermon unto servitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_16CH:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0011","text":"Oh my brothers, a fresh blustering wind comes Zarathustra to all those weary of the way; he will yet make many noses sneeze!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_16CH:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0012","text":"My free breath blows even through walls, and into prisons and imprisoned spirits!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_16CH:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0013","text":"Willing liberates: for willing is creating: thus do I teach. And only for creating should ye learn!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_16CH:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16CH:P0014","text":"And learning too ye must first learn from me, good learning!—He who has ears, let him hear!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_16CH:P0014"]}],"source_digest":"ed22f664e34a41f86ce28578cfa58eef362e7b1314e4ba67b61fcee01a60657c","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"16.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 16","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","16."],"original_title":"16."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"16.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 16","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","16."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"16.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH","title":"17.","order":98,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_17CH","text":"## 17.\n\nDa steht der Nachen,—dort hinüber geht es vielleicht in’s grosse Nichts.—Aber wer will in diess „Vielleicht“ einsteigen?\n\nNiemand von euch will in den Todes-Nachen einsteigen! Wieso wollt ihr dann Welt-Müde sein!\n\nWeltmüde! Und noch nicht einmal Erd-Entrückte wurdet ihr! Lüstern fand ich euch immer noch nach Erde, verliebt noch in die eigne Erd-Müdigkeit!\n\nNicht umsonst hängt euch die Lippe herab:—ein kleiner Erden-Wunsch sitzt noch darauf! Und im Auge—schwimmt da nicht ein Wölkchen unvergessner Erden-Lust?\n\nEs giebt auf Erden viel gute Erfindungen, die einen nützlich, die andern angenehm: derentwegen ist die Erde zu lieben.\n\nUnd mancherlei so gut Erfundenes giebt es da, dass es ist wie des Weibes Busen: nützlich zugleich und angenehm.\n\nIhr Welt-Müden aber! Ihr Erden-Faulen! Euch soll man mit Ruthen streichen! Mit Ruthenstreichen soll man euch wieder muntre Beine machen.\n\nDenn: seid ihr nicht Kranke und verlebte Wichte, deren die Erde müde ist, so seid ihr schlaue Faulthiere oder naschhafte verkrochene Lust-Katzen. Und wollt ihr nicht wieder lustig laufen , so sollt ihr—dahinfahren!\n\nAn Unheilbaren soll man nicht Arzt sein wollen: also lehrt es Zarathustra:—so sollt ihr dahinfahren!\n\nAber es gehört mehr Muth dazu, ein Ende zu machen, als einen neuen Vers: das wissen alle Ärzte und Dichter.—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH:P0001","text":"## 17.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH:P0002","text":"Da steht der Nachen,—dort hinüber geht es vielleicht in’s grosse Nichts.—Aber wer will in diess „Vielleicht“ einsteigen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH:P0003","text":"Niemand von euch will in den Todes-Nachen einsteigen! Wieso wollt ihr dann Welt-Müde sein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH:P0004","text":"Weltmüde! Und noch nicht einmal Erd-Entrückte wurdet ihr! Lüstern fand ich euch immer noch nach Erde, verliebt noch in die eigne Erd-Müdigkeit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH:P0005","text":"Nicht umsonst hängt euch die Lippe herab:—ein kleiner Erden-Wunsch sitzt noch darauf! Und im Auge—schwimmt da nicht ein Wölkchen unvergessner Erden-Lust?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH:P0006","text":"Es giebt auf Erden viel gute Erfindungen, die einen nützlich, die andern angenehm: derentwegen ist die Erde zu lieben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH:P0007","text":"Und mancherlei so gut Erfundenes giebt es da, dass es ist wie des Weibes Busen: nützlich zugleich und angenehm.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH:P0008","text":"Ihr Welt-Müden aber! Ihr Erden-Faulen! Euch soll man mit Ruthen streichen! Mit Ruthenstreichen soll man euch wieder muntre Beine machen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH:P0009","text":"Denn: seid ihr nicht Kranke und verlebte Wichte, deren die Erde müde ist, so seid ihr schlaue Faulthiere oder naschhafte verkrochene Lust-Katzen. Und wollt ihr nicht wieder lustig laufen , so sollt ihr—dahinfahren!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH:P0010","text":"An Unheilbaren soll man nicht Arzt sein wollen: also lehrt es Zarathustra:—so sollt ihr dahinfahren!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH:P0011","text":"Aber es gehört mehr Muth dazu, ein Ende zu machen, als einen neuen Vers: das wissen alle Ärzte und Dichter.—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"8f154f5e37c6ce7cdf25597f3caf0abe6f6f3d41cc3da03541b7f8d616760927","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183021853134Z_91c9e64a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"17.","source_sha256":"8f154f5e37c6ce7cdf25597f3caf0abe6f6f3d41cc3da03541b7f8d616760927","sha256":"bd351be7899cc3afa68a83cc032b1a302d61fb8323b2a6a617689fe6e7033234","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:30:21.853160+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH:P0001","text":"## 17.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_17CH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH:P0002","text":"There stands the skiff,—perhaps it goes across there into the great nothingness.—But who would wish to embark upon this “perhaps”?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_17CH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH:P0003","text":"None of you wishes to embark upon the death-skiff! Why, then, do you wish to be weary of the world!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_17CH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH:P0004","text":"Weary of the world! And you have not even become estranged from the earth! I always found you still lusting after the earth, still enamoured of your own earth-weariness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_17CH:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH:P0005","text":"Not without reason does your lip hang down:—a small earthly wish still sits upon it! And in the eye—does there not float a little cloud of unforgotten earthly pleasure?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_17CH:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH:P0006","text":"There are many good inventions on earth, some useful, others pleasant: for their sake the earth is to be loved.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_17CH:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH:P0007","text":"And there are many things so well invented that they are like a woman's breast: useful and pleasant at once.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_17CH:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH:P0008","text":"But you weary-of-the-world! You earth-sluggards! You should be whipped with rods! With blows of rods one should make your legs lively again.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_17CH:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH:P0009","text":"For: if you are not sick and worn-out wretches, of whom the earth is weary, then you are cunning sloths or greedy, lurking pleasure-cats. And if you do not wish to run merrily again, then you should—pass away!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_17CH:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH:P0010","text":"One should not wish to be a physician to the incurable: thus Zarathustra teaches:—thus should you pass away!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_17CH:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17CH:P0011","text":"But it takes more courage to make an end than to make a new verse: all physicians and poets know that.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_17CH:P0011"]}],"source_digest":"247edfa99a4922fdfa35c7d8e886aa76869afa9732caea5a6d53266982755b68","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"17.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 17","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","17."],"original_title":"17."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"17.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 17","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","17."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"17.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18CH","title":"18.","order":99,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_18CH","text":"## 18.\n\nOh meine Brüder, es giebt Tafeln, welche die Ermüdung, und Tafeln, welche die Faulheit schuf, die faulige: ob sie schon gleich reden, so wollen sie doch ungleich gehört sein.—\n\nSeht hier diesen Verschmachtenden! Nur eine Spanne weit ist er noch von seinem Ziele, aber vor Müdigkeit hat er sich trotzig hier in den Staub gelegt: dieser Tapfere!\n\nVor Müdigkeit gähnt er Weg und Erde und Ziel und sich selber an: keinen Schritt will er noch weiter thun,—dieser Tapfere!\n\nNun glüht die Sonne auf ihn, und die Hunde lecken nach seinem Schweisse: aber er liegt da in seinem Trotze und will lieber verschmachten:—\n\n—eine Spanne weit von seinem Ziele verschmachten! Wahrlich, ihr werdet ihn noch an den Haaren in seinen Himmel ziehen müssen,—diesen Helden!\n\nBesser noch, ihr lasst ihn liegen, wohin er sich gelegt hat, dass der Schlaf ihm komme, der Tröster, mit kühlendem Rausche-Regen:\n\nLasst ihn liegen, bis er von selber wach wird, bis er von selber alle Müdigkeit widerruft und was Müdigkeit aus ihm lehrte!\n\nNur, meine Brüder, dass ihr die Hunde von ihm scheucht, die faulen Schleicher, und all das schwärmende Geschmeiss:—\n\n—all das schwärmende Geschmeiss der „Gebildeten“ , das sich am Schweisse jedes Helden—gütlich thut!—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_18CH:P0001","text":"## 18.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18CH:P0002","text":"Oh meine Brüder, es giebt Tafeln, welche die Ermüdung, und Tafeln, welche die Faulheit schuf, die faulige: ob sie schon gleich reden, so wollen sie doch ungleich gehört sein.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18CH:P0003","text":"Seht hier diesen Verschmachtenden! Nur eine Spanne weit ist er noch von seinem Ziele, aber vor Müdigkeit hat er sich trotzig hier in den Staub gelegt: dieser Tapfere!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18CH:P0004","text":"Vor Müdigkeit gähnt er Weg und Erde und Ziel und sich selber an: keinen Schritt will er noch weiter thun,—dieser Tapfere!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18CH:P0005","text":"Nun glüht die Sonne auf ihn, und die Hunde lecken nach seinem Schweisse: aber er liegt da in seinem Trotze und will lieber verschmachten:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18CH:P0006","text":"—eine Spanne weit von seinem Ziele verschmachten! Wahrlich, ihr werdet ihn noch an den Haaren in seinen Himmel ziehen müssen,—diesen Helden!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18CH:P0007","text":"Besser noch, ihr lasst ihn liegen, wohin er sich gelegt hat, dass der Schlaf ihm komme, der Tröster, mit kühlendem Rausche-Regen:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18CH:P0008","text":"Lasst ihn liegen, bis er von selber wach wird, bis er von selber alle Müdigkeit widerruft und was Müdigkeit aus ihm lehrte!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18CH:P0009","text":"Nur, meine Brüder, dass ihr die Hunde von ihm scheucht, die faulen Schleicher, und all das schwärmende Geschmeiss:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18CH:P0010","text":"—all das schwärmende Geschmeiss der „Gebildeten“ , das sich am Schweisse jedes Helden—gütlich thut!—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"9e7cd548beb3025cf085753b2cdbc61034f143601610c77709f714a6570738da","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183026443629Z_907796ee","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"18.","source_sha256":"9e7cd548beb3025cf085753b2cdbc61034f143601610c77709f714a6570738da","sha256":"c901d3216d9ebffa75e8658566a3eff60b3c2d9b0ce39271095a6213a217f543","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:30:26.443639+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_18CH:P0001","text":"## 18.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_18CH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18CH:P0002","text":"Oh my brothers, there are tablets that weariness created, and tablets that laziness created, the rotten laziness: though they speak alike, they nevertheless wish to be heard differently.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_18CH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18CH:P0003","text":"Behold this languishing man! Only a span remains between him and his goal, but out of weariness he has defiantly laid himself down here in the dust: this brave man!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_18CH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18CH:P0004","text":"Out of weariness he yawns at road and earth and goal and himself: he will take not one step further,—this brave man!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_18CH:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18CH:P0005","text":"Now the sun glows upon him, and the dogs lick after his sweat: but he lies there in his defiance and would rather languish:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_18CH:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18CH:P0006","text":"—languish a span away from his goal! Verily, you will yet have to pull him by the hair into his heaven,—this hero!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_18CH:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18CH:P0007","text":"Better still, you leave him lying where he has laid himself down, so that sleep may come to him, the comforter, with cooling rustling rain:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_18CH:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18CH:P0008","text":"Leave him lying until he wakes of his own accord, until of his own accord he revokes all weariness and whatever weariness taught out of him!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_18CH:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18CH:P0009","text":"Only, my brothers, drive away from him the dogs, the lazy creepers, and all that swarming vermin:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_18CH:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18CH:P0010","text":"—all that swarming vermin of the “educated,” which feasts—on the sweat of every hero!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_18CH:P0010"]}],"source_digest":"f9b9f04d8690e891416858987d59a8dc82aad9639b4c1738f1b390d210577b97","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"18.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 18","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","18."],"original_title":"18."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"18.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 18","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","18."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"18.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH","title":"19.","order":100,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_19CH","text":"## 19.\n\nIch schliesse Kreise um mich und heilige Grenzen; immer Wenigere steigen mit mir auf immer höhere Berge,—ich baue ein Gebirge aus immer heiligeren Bergen. -\n\nWohin ihr aber auch mit mir steigen mögt, oh meine Brüder: seht zu, dass nicht ein Schmarotzer mit euch steige!\n\nSchmarotzer: das ist ein Gewürm, ein kriechendes, geschmiegtes, das fett werden will an euren kranken wunden Winkeln.\n\nUnd das ist seine Kunst, dass er steigende Seelen erräth, wo sie müde sind: in euren Gram und Unmuth, in eure zarte Scham baut er sein ekles Nest.\n\nWo der Starke schwach, der Edle allzumild ist,—dahinein baut er sein ekles Nest: der Schmarotzer wohnt, wo der Grosse kleine wunde Winkel hat.\n\nWas ist die höchste Art alles Seienden und was die geringste? Der Schmarotzer ist die geringste Art; wer aber höchster Art ist, der ernährt die meisten Schmarotzer.\n\nDie Seele nämlich, welche die längste Leiter hat und am tiefsten hinunter kann: wie sollten nicht an der die meisten Schmarotzer sitzen?—\n\n—die umfänglichste Seele, welche am weitesten in sich laufen und irren und schweifen kann; die nothwendigste, welche sich aus Lust in den Zufall stürzt:—\n\n—die seiende Seele, welche in’s Werden taucht; die habende, welche in’s Wollen und Verlangen will :—\n\n—die sich selber fliehende, die sich selber im weitesten Kreise einholt; die weiseste Seele, welcher die Narrheit am süssesten zuredet:—\n\n—die sich selber liebendste, in der alle Dinge ihr Strömen und Wiederströmen und Ebbe und Fluth haben:—oh wie sollte die höchste Seele nicht die schlimmsten Schmarotzer haben?","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0001","text":"## 19.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0002","text":"Ich schliesse Kreise um mich und heilige Grenzen; immer Wenigere steigen mit mir auf immer höhere Berge,—ich baue ein Gebirge aus immer heiligeren Bergen. -","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0003","text":"Wohin ihr aber auch mit mir steigen mögt, oh meine Brüder: seht zu, dass nicht ein Schmarotzer mit euch steige!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0004","text":"Schmarotzer: das ist ein Gewürm, ein kriechendes, geschmiegtes, das fett werden will an euren kranken wunden Winkeln.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0005","text":"Und das ist seine Kunst, dass er steigende Seelen erräth, wo sie müde sind: in euren Gram und Unmuth, in eure zarte Scham baut er sein ekles Nest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0006","text":"Wo der Starke schwach, der Edle allzumild ist,—dahinein baut er sein ekles Nest: der Schmarotzer wohnt, wo der Grosse kleine wunde Winkel hat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0007","text":"Was ist die höchste Art alles Seienden und was die geringste? Der Schmarotzer ist die geringste Art; wer aber höchster Art ist, der ernährt die meisten Schmarotzer.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0008","text":"Die Seele nämlich, welche die längste Leiter hat und am tiefsten hinunter kann: wie sollten nicht an der die meisten Schmarotzer sitzen?—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0009","text":"—die umfänglichste Seele, welche am weitesten in sich laufen und irren und schweifen kann; die nothwendigste, welche sich aus Lust in den Zufall stürzt:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0010","text":"—die seiende Seele, welche in’s Werden taucht; die habende, welche in’s Wollen und Verlangen will :—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0011","text":"—die sich selber fliehende, die sich selber im weitesten Kreise einholt; die weiseste Seele, welcher die Narrheit am süssesten zuredet:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0012","text":"—die sich selber liebendste, in der alle Dinge ihr Strömen und Wiederströmen und Ebbe und Fluth haben:—oh wie sollte die höchste Seele nicht die schlimmsten Schmarotzer haben?","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"fe0d2dab8bac3a880f8051f599d840e072e45bcdb4f0293955e78335ad1fe7c2","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183032230140Z_0b823460","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"19.","source_sha256":"fe0d2dab8bac3a880f8051f599d840e072e45bcdb4f0293955e78335ad1fe7c2","sha256":"e36b76f01a829cf0608ad1fa433cd5a150426e9a61be06f03ab95b824684fb1f","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:30:32.230153+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0001","text":"## 19.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_19CH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0002","text":"I draw circles around myself and consecrate boundaries; ever fewer climb with me up ever higher mountains,—I build a mountain range out of ever more sacred mountains. -","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_19CH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0003","text":"But wherever you may climb with me, oh my brothers: take care that no parasite climbs with you!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_19CH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0004","text":"Parasite: that is a vermin, a creeping, ingratiating thing, that wants to grow fat on your sickly wounded corners.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_19CH:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0005","text":"And this is its art, that it divines ascending souls where they are weary: in your grief and ill-humour, in your tender shame it builds its disgusting nest.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_19CH:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0006","text":"Where the strong man is weak, the noble man all too mild,—there it builds its disgusting nest: the parasite dwells where the great man has small wounded corners.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_19CH:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0007","text":"What is the highest species of all that exists, and what the lowest? The parasite is the lowest species; but he who is of the highest species nourishes the most parasites.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_19CH:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0008","text":"Namely, the soul that has the longest ladder and can descend most deeply: how should not the most parasites sit upon it?—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_19CH:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0009","text":"—the most capacious soul, which can range and stray and wander furthest within itself; the most necessary, which out of pleasure casts itself into chance:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_19CH:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0010","text":"—the being soul, which plunges into becoming; the having soul, which wills into willing and desiring :—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_19CH:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0011","text":"—the soul fleeing itself, which catches up with itself in the widest circle; the wisest soul, to which folly speaks most sweetly:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_19CH:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19CH:P0012","text":"—the soul most loving of itself, in which all things have their flowing and reflowing and ebb and flood:—oh, how should the highest soul not have the worst parasites?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_19CH:P0012"]}],"source_digest":"1d4a7d4f5549e4dd36bc2f28602f217ee73d94fb4aebf4562dd35bf542f87f38","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"19.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 19","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","19."],"original_title":"19."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"19.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 19","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","19."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"19.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20CH","title":"20.","order":101,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_20CH","text":"## 20.\n\nOh meine Brüder, bin ich denn grausam? Aber ich sage: was fällt, das soll man auch noch stossen!\n\nDas Alles von Heute—das fällt, das verfällt: wer wollte es halten! Aber ich—ich will es noch stossen!\n\nKennt ihr die Wollust, die Steine in steile Tiefen rollt?—Diese Menschen von heute: seht sie doch, wie sie in meine Tiefen rollen!\n\nEin Vorspiel bin ich besserer Spieler, oh meine Brüder! Ein Beispiel! Thut nach meinem Beispiele!\n\nUnd wen ihr nicht fliegen lehrt, den lehrt mir—schneller fallen!—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_20CH:P0001","text":"## 20.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20CH:P0002","text":"Oh meine Brüder, bin ich denn grausam? Aber ich sage: was fällt, das soll man auch noch stossen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20CH:P0003","text":"Das Alles von Heute—das fällt, das verfällt: wer wollte es halten! Aber ich—ich will es noch stossen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20CH:P0004","text":"Kennt ihr die Wollust, die Steine in steile Tiefen rollt?—Diese Menschen von heute: seht sie doch, wie sie in meine Tiefen rollen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20CH:P0005","text":"Ein Vorspiel bin ich besserer Spieler, oh meine Brüder! Ein Beispiel! Thut nach meinem Beispiele!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20CH:P0006","text":"Und wen ihr nicht fliegen lehrt, den lehrt mir—schneller fallen!—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"e7560ae847cfd1aeea5ca356cd7cd1833ed5951da3ff95635213336e2ec5b1ef","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183035287917Z_561c95a1","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"20.","source_sha256":"e7560ae847cfd1aeea5ca356cd7cd1833ed5951da3ff95635213336e2ec5b1ef","sha256":"5022b6d5ce35d09e9e762d911df259a35618f2c7a3b1de1584d1312b1e879320","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:30:35.287942+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_20CH:P0001","text":"## 20.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_20CH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20CH:P0002","text":"Oh my brothers, then am I cruel? But I say: what falls, one should also give a push!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_20CH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20CH:P0003","text":"All this of Today—it falls, it decays: who would wish to hold it! But I—I wish to give it a push as well!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_20CH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20CH:P0004","text":"Do you know the voluptuousness of rolling stones into steep depths?—These human beings of today: just see how they roll into my depths!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_20CH:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20CH:P0005","text":"I am a prelude to better players, oh my brothers! An example! Do as I do!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_20CH:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20CH:P0006","text":"And whomever you do not teach to fly, teach him for me—to fall faster!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_20CH:P0006"]}],"source_digest":"f353b7f69bc3d9677466d56667e9eb6cf31c9aabac789fa9f6a6fb0def741b44","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"20.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 20","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","20."],"original_title":"20."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"20.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 20","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","20."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"20.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH","title":"21.","order":102,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_21CH","text":"## 21.\n\nIch liebe die Tapferen: aber es ist nicht genug, Hau-Degen sein,—man muss auch wissen Hau-schau- Wen !\n\nUnd oft ist mehr Tapferkeit darin, dass Einer an sich hält und vorübergeht: damit er sich dem würdigeren Feinde aufspare!\n\nIch sollt nur Feinde haben, die zu hassen sind, aber nicht Feinde zum Verachten: ihr müsst stolz auf euren Feind sein: also lehrte ich schon Ein Mal.\n\nDem würdigeren Feinde, oh meine Freunde, sollt ihr euch aufsparen: darum müsst ihr an Vielem vorübergehn,—\n\n—sonderlich an vielem Gesindel, das euch in die Ohren lärmt von Volk und Völkern.\n\nHaltet euer Auge rein von ihrem Für und Wider! Da giebt es viel Recht, viel Unrecht: wer da zusieht, wird zornig.\n\nDreinschaun, dreinhaun—das ist da Eins: darum geht weg in die Wälder und legt euer Schwert schlafen!\n\nGeht eure Wege! Und lasst Volk und Völker die ihren gehn!—dunkle Wege wahrlich, auf denen auch nicht Eine Hoffnung mehr wetterleuchtet!\n\nMag da der Krämer herrschen, wo Alles, was noch glänzt—Krämer-Gold ist! Es ist die Zeit der Könige nicht mehr: was sich heute Volk heisst, verdient keine Könige.\n\nSeht doch, wie diese Völker jetzt selber den Krämern gleich thun: sie lesen sich die kleinsten Vortheile noch aus jedem Kehricht!\n\nSie lauern einander auf, sie lauern einander Etwas ab,—das heissen sie „gute Nachbarschaft.“ Oh selige ferne Zeit, wo ein Volk sich sagte: „ich will über Völker— Herr sein!“\n\nDenn, meine Brüder: das Beste soll herrschen, das Beste will auch herrschen! Und wo die Lehre anders lautet, da— fehlt es am Besten.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0001","text":"## 21.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0002","text":"Ich liebe die Tapferen: aber es ist nicht genug, Hau-Degen sein,—man muss auch wissen Hau-schau- Wen !","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0003","text":"Und oft ist mehr Tapferkeit darin, dass Einer an sich hält und vorübergeht: damit er sich dem würdigeren Feinde aufspare!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0004","text":"Ich sollt nur Feinde haben, die zu hassen sind, aber nicht Feinde zum Verachten: ihr müsst stolz auf euren Feind sein: also lehrte ich schon Ein Mal.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0005","text":"Dem würdigeren Feinde, oh meine Freunde, sollt ihr euch aufsparen: darum müsst ihr an Vielem vorübergehn,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0006","text":"—sonderlich an vielem Gesindel, das euch in die Ohren lärmt von Volk und Völkern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0007","text":"Haltet euer Auge rein von ihrem Für und Wider! Da giebt es viel Recht, viel Unrecht: wer da zusieht, wird zornig.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0008","text":"Dreinschaun, dreinhaun—das ist da Eins: darum geht weg in die Wälder und legt euer Schwert schlafen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0009","text":"Geht eure Wege! Und lasst Volk und Völker die ihren gehn!—dunkle Wege wahrlich, auf denen auch nicht Eine Hoffnung mehr wetterleuchtet!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0010","text":"Mag da der Krämer herrschen, wo Alles, was noch glänzt—Krämer-Gold ist! Es ist die Zeit der Könige nicht mehr: was sich heute Volk heisst, verdient keine Könige.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0011","text":"Seht doch, wie diese Völker jetzt selber den Krämern gleich thun: sie lesen sich die kleinsten Vortheile noch aus jedem Kehricht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0012","text":"Sie lauern einander auf, sie lauern einander Etwas ab,—das heissen sie „gute Nachbarschaft.“ Oh selige ferne Zeit, wo ein Volk sich sagte: „ich will über Völker— Herr sein!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0013","text":"Denn, meine Brüder: das Beste soll herrschen, das Beste will auch herrschen! Und wo die Lehre anders lautet, da— fehlt es am Besten.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"1e7fa5668f598fac0a6f7fe46c8cae7d8739c24ea707c8c8cb9de59e3b651767","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183041344231Z_6187a2d1","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"21.","source_sha256":"1e7fa5668f598fac0a6f7fe46c8cae7d8739c24ea707c8c8cb9de59e3b651767","sha256":"ec14d4cefe758724b76ece8aa1d80f237a89880d58659ef5e16a8eacb45fb579","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:30:41.344257+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0001","text":"## 21.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_21CH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0002","text":"I love the courageous: but it is not enough to be a hewer of swords,—one must also know whom to hew!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_21CH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0003","text":"And often there is more courage in holding oneself back and passing by: so as to reserve oneself for the worthier enemy!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_21CH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0004","text":"I should have only enemies who are to be hated, but not enemies to be despised: you must be proud of your enemy: thus I taught once before.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_21CH:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0005","text":"For the worthier enemy, oh my friends, you should reserve yourselves: therefore you must pass by much,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_21CH:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0006","text":"—especially much rabble that clamours in your ears about people and peoples.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_21CH:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0007","text":"Keep your eye pure of their for and against! There is much right there, much wrong: whoever looks on becomes angry.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_21CH:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0008","text":"To look in, to strike in—that is one and the same there: therefore go away into the forests and put your sword to sleep!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_21CH:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0009","text":"Go your ways! And let people and peoples go theirs!—dark ways indeed, upon which not even a single hope flashes like lightning any longer!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_21CH:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0010","text":"Let the shopkeeper rule there, where all that still glitters—is shopkeeper's gold! The time of kings is no more: what today calls itself people deserves no kings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_21CH:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0011","text":"Just see how these peoples now themselves act like shopkeepers: they still pick out the smallest advantages from every bit of refuse!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_21CH:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0012","text":"They lie in wait for one another, they wheedle something from one another,—this they call “good neighbourliness.” Oh blessed distant time, when a people said to itself: “I will be lord—over peoples!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_21CH:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_21CH:P0013","text":"For, my brothers: the best shall rule, the best also wishes to rule! And where the teaching says otherwise, there—it lacks the best.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_21CH:P0013"]}],"source_digest":"f44b62fa145900391095a61b2098540f7727fe3b57942ee8c74257913bbd9cf9","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"21.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 21","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","21."],"original_title":"21."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"21.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 21","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","21."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"21.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_22CH","title":"22.","order":103,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_22CH","text":"## 22.\n\nWenn Die —Brod umsonst hätten, wehe! Wonach würden Die schrein! Ihr Unterhalt—das ist ihre rechte Unterhaltung; und sie sollen es schwer haben!\n\nRaubthiere sind es.- in ihrem „Arbeiten“ —da ist auch noch Rauben, in ihrem „Verdienen“ —da ist auch noch Überlisten! Darum sollen sie es schwer haben!\n\nBessere Raubthiere sollen sie also werden, feinere, klügere, menschen-ähnlichere : der Mensch nämlich ist das beste Raubthier.\n\nAllen Thieren hat der Mensch schon ihre Tugenden abgeraubt: das macht, von allen Thieren hat es der Mensch am schwersten gehabt.\n\nNur noch die Vögel sind über ihm. Und wenn der Mensch noch fliegen lernte, wehe! wohinauf —würde seine Raublust fliegen!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_22CH:P0001","text":"## 22.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_22CH:P0002","text":"Wenn Die —Brod umsonst hätten, wehe! Wonach würden Die schrein! Ihr Unterhalt—das ist ihre rechte Unterhaltung; und sie sollen es schwer haben!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_22CH:P0003","text":"Raubthiere sind es.- in ihrem „Arbeiten“ —da ist auch noch Rauben, in ihrem „Verdienen“ —da ist auch noch Überlisten! Darum sollen sie es schwer haben!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_22CH:P0004","text":"Bessere Raubthiere sollen sie also werden, feinere, klügere, menschen-ähnlichere : der Mensch nämlich ist das beste Raubthier.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_22CH:P0005","text":"Allen Thieren hat der Mensch schon ihre Tugenden abgeraubt: das macht, von allen Thieren hat es der Mensch am schwersten gehabt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_22CH:P0006","text":"Nur noch die Vögel sind über ihm. Und wenn der Mensch noch fliegen lernte, wehe! wohinauf —würde seine Raublust fliegen!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"7a77a10fd8968b06b400979ecd020a795a8873a07046e56c7f2f554d20888da8","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183044645441Z_d3262d4b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"22.","source_sha256":"7a77a10fd8968b06b400979ecd020a795a8873a07046e56c7f2f554d20888da8","sha256":"f8af8f7d92786f79c7ecad5a3161079184c030280c899399e027b73e33378699","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:30:44.645457+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_22CH:P0001","text":"## 22.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_22CH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_22CH:P0002","text":"If They had—bread for nothing, woe! What would They scream for! Their maintenance—that is their proper entertainment; and they should have it hard!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_22CH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_22CH:P0003","text":"They are beasts of prey.—in their “working” —there is still robbing, in their “earning” —there is still outwitting! Therefore they should have it hard!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_22CH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_22CH:P0004","text":"Thus they should become better beasts of prey, subtler, cleverer, more human-like: for man, namely, is the best beast of prey.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_22CH:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_22CH:P0005","text":"Man has already robbed all animals of their virtues: that is why, of all animals, man has had it hardest.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_22CH:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_22CH:P0006","text":"Only the birds are still above him. And if man were yet to learn to fly, woe! how high—would his lust for plunder fly!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_22CH:P0006"]}],"source_digest":"2dd34e460e2fa85b052064072c800d33f77174f3c3536989c6e0b12cb1871f0d","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"22.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 22","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","22."],"original_title":"22."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"22.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 22","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","22."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"22.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_23CH","title":"23.","order":104,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_23CH","text":"## 23.\n\nSo will ich Mann und Weib: kriegstüchtig den Einen, gebärtüchtig das Andre, beide aber tanztüchtig mit Kopf und Beinen.\n\nUnd verloren sei uns der Tag, wo nicht Ein Mal getanzt wurde! Und falsch heisse uns jede Wahrheit, bei der es nicht Ein Gelächter gab!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_23CH:P0001","text":"## 23.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_23CH:P0002","text":"So will ich Mann und Weib: kriegstüchtig den Einen, gebärtüchtig das Andre, beide aber tanztüchtig mit Kopf und Beinen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_23CH:P0003","text":"Und verloren sei uns der Tag, wo nicht Ein Mal getanzt wurde! Und falsch heisse uns jede Wahrheit, bei der es nicht Ein Gelächter gab!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"aaae6fc33e4fff0686fff1ce085e029aff79c04807c4f86701e7856138ea627e","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183046865212Z_760b8f1c","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"23.","source_sha256":"aaae6fc33e4fff0686fff1ce085e029aff79c04807c4f86701e7856138ea627e","sha256":"6b25e3050b3155c142fd5c74e9c44e03cf3ad22c2e3e7e4d9eeddbde39d348fc","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:30:46.865238+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_23CH:P0001","text":"## 23.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_23CH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_23CH:P0002","text":"Thus I will have man and woman: fit for war the one, fit for bearing children the other, but both fit for dancing with head and legs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_23CH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_23CH:P0003","text":"And lost be to us the day on which not once was danced! And false be called every truth with which there was not one laugh!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_23CH:P0003"]}],"source_digest":"0971c03e08a4f5041dc07168c3b9d4fbb5d3a09f41b810c88cd402300097aef2","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"23.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 23","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","23."],"original_title":"23."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"23.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 23","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","23."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"23.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_24CH","title":"24.","order":105,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_24CH","text":"## 24.\n\nEuer Eheschliessen: seht zu, dass es nicht ein schlechtes Schliessen sei! Ihr schlosset zu schnell: so folgt daraus—Ehebrechen!\n\nUnd besser noch Ehebrechen als Ehe-biegen, Ehelügen!—So sprach mir ein Weib: „wohl brach ich die Ehe, aber zuerst brach die Ehe—mich!“\n\nSchlimm-Gepaarte fand ich immer als die schlimmsten Rachsüchtigen: sie lassen es aller Welt entgelten, dass sie nicht mehr einzeln laufen.\n\nDesswillen will ich, dass Redliche zu einander reden: „wir lieben uns: lasst uns zusehn , dass wir uns lieb behalten! Oder soll unser Versprechen ein Versehen sein?“\n\n—„Gebt uns eine Frist und kleine Ehe, dass wir zusehn, ob wir zur grossen Ehe taugen! Es ist ein grosses Ding, immer zu Zwein sein!“\n\nAlso rathe ich allen Redlichen; und was wäre denn meine Liebe zum Übermenschen und zu Allem, was kommen soll, wenn ich anders riethe und redete!\n\nNicht nur fort euch zu pflanzen, sondern hinauf —dazu, oh meine Brüder, helfe euch der Garten der Ehe!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_24CH:P0001","text":"## 24.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_24CH:P0002","text":"Euer Eheschliessen: seht zu, dass es nicht ein schlechtes Schliessen sei! Ihr schlosset zu schnell: so folgt daraus—Ehebrechen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_24CH:P0003","text":"Und besser noch Ehebrechen als Ehe-biegen, Ehelügen!—So sprach mir ein Weib: „wohl brach ich die Ehe, aber zuerst brach die Ehe—mich!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_24CH:P0004","text":"Schlimm-Gepaarte fand ich immer als die schlimmsten Rachsüchtigen: sie lassen es aller Welt entgelten, dass sie nicht mehr einzeln laufen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_24CH:P0005","text":"Desswillen will ich, dass Redliche zu einander reden: „wir lieben uns: lasst uns zusehn , dass wir uns lieb behalten! Oder soll unser Versprechen ein Versehen sein?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_24CH:P0006","text":"—„Gebt uns eine Frist und kleine Ehe, dass wir zusehn, ob wir zur grossen Ehe taugen! Es ist ein grosses Ding, immer zu Zwein sein!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_24CH:P0007","text":"Also rathe ich allen Redlichen; und was wäre denn meine Liebe zum Übermenschen und zu Allem, was kommen soll, wenn ich anders riethe und redete!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_24CH:P0008","text":"Nicht nur fort euch zu pflanzen, sondern hinauf —dazu, oh meine Brüder, helfe euch der Garten der Ehe!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"81f9a31505bfc66e5f8814f339cbd1eb30d5810125ca3bb464e03e1f948e1421","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183051471531Z_f99652e3","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"24.","source_sha256":"81f9a31505bfc66e5f8814f339cbd1eb30d5810125ca3bb464e03e1f948e1421","sha256":"6aa712f61d98233b88dc12e21f787a7c3b944dcbbfe32eefd27c7e3a73be0463","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:30:51.471550+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_24CH:P0001","text":"## 24.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_24CH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_24CH:P0002","text":"Your contracting of marriage: see to it that it be not a bad closing! You closed too quickly: thus follows from it—breaking marriage!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_24CH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_24CH:P0003","text":"And still better breaking marriage than bending marriage, lying marriage!—Thus spoke to me a woman: “indeed I broke the marriage, but first the marriage—broke me!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_24CH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_24CH:P0004","text":"I have always found ill-paired people to be the worst vindictive ones: they make all the world pay for the fact that they no longer go about singly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_24CH:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_24CH:P0005","text":"For that reason I want upright people to say to one another: “we love each other: let us see to it that we keep loving each other! Or shall our promise be a mistake?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_24CH:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_24CH:P0006","text":"—“Give us a respite and a small marriage, so that we may see whether we are fit for the great marriage! It is a great thing, always to be two!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_24CH:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_24CH:P0007","text":"Thus I advise all upright people; and what would then be my love for the Overman and for all that is to come, if I advised and spoke otherwise!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_24CH:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_24CH:P0008","text":"Not merely to propagate yourselves, but upward—toward that, oh my brothers, may the garden of marriage help you!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_24CH:P0008"]}],"source_digest":"249560afe8d3b84f2e208b298b37e364ba43e4015aab87530c809b08d4c654bf","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"24.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 24","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","24."],"original_title":"24."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"24.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 24","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","24."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"24.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_25CH","title":"25.","order":106,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_25CH","text":"## 25.\n\nWer über alte Ursprünge weise wurde, siehe, der wird zuletzt nach Quellen der Zukunft suchen und nach neuen Ursprüngen.—\n\nOh meine Brüder, es ist nicht über lange, da werden neue Völker entspringen und neue Quellen hinab in neue Tiefen rauschen.\n\nDas Erdbeben nämlich—das verschüttet viel Brunnen, das schafft viel Verschmachten: das hebt auch innre Kräfte und Heimlichkeiten an’s Licht.\n\nDas Erdbeben macht neue Quellen offenbar. Im Erdbeben alter Völker brechen neue Quellen aus.\n\nUnd wer da ruft: „Siehe hier ein Brunnen für viele Durstige, Ein Herz für viele Sehnsüchtige, Ein Wille für viele Werkzeuge“ :—um den sammelt sich ein Volk , das ist: viel Versuchende.\n\nWer befehlen kann, wer gehorchen muss—Das wird da versucht! Ach, mit welch langem Suchen und Rathen und Missrathen und Lernen und Neu-Versuchen!\n\nDie Menschen-Gesellschaft: die ist ein Versuch, so lehre ich’s,—ein langes Suchen: sie sucht aber den Befehlenden!—\n\n—ein Versuch, oh meine Brüder! Und kein „Vertrag“! Zerbrecht, zerbrecht mir solch Wort der Weich-Herzen und Halb- und Halben!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_25CH:P0001","text":"## 25.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_25CH:P0002","text":"Wer über alte Ursprünge weise wurde, siehe, der wird zuletzt nach Quellen der Zukunft suchen und nach neuen Ursprüngen.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_25CH:P0003","text":"Oh meine Brüder, es ist nicht über lange, da werden neue Völker entspringen und neue Quellen hinab in neue Tiefen rauschen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_25CH:P0004","text":"Das Erdbeben nämlich—das verschüttet viel Brunnen, das schafft viel Verschmachten: das hebt auch innre Kräfte und Heimlichkeiten an’s Licht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_25CH:P0005","text":"Das Erdbeben macht neue Quellen offenbar. Im Erdbeben alter Völker brechen neue Quellen aus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_25CH:P0006","text":"Und wer da ruft: „Siehe hier ein Brunnen für viele Durstige, Ein Herz für viele Sehnsüchtige, Ein Wille für viele Werkzeuge“ :—um den sammelt sich ein Volk , das ist: viel Versuchende.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_25CH:P0007","text":"Wer befehlen kann, wer gehorchen muss—Das wird da versucht! Ach, mit welch langem Suchen und Rathen und Missrathen und Lernen und Neu-Versuchen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_25CH:P0008","text":"Die Menschen-Gesellschaft: die ist ein Versuch, so lehre ich’s,—ein langes Suchen: sie sucht aber den Befehlenden!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_25CH:P0009","text":"—ein Versuch, oh meine Brüder! Und kein „Vertrag“! Zerbrecht, zerbrecht mir solch Wort der Weich-Herzen und Halb- und Halben!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"6e30604d000c3692088347b865cb555912aa3dab4a3eaf8bf26ad2165ef41ac3","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183056272519Z_a3fa9903","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"25.","source_sha256":"6e30604d000c3692088347b865cb555912aa3dab4a3eaf8bf26ad2165ef41ac3","sha256":"26e2259635cae69fac98d3d41165d51edf4b5172d54a08445b997af82a14fd06","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:30:56.272528+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_25CH:P0001","text":"## 25.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_25CH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_25CH:P0002","text":"He who has become wise concerning ancient origins—behold, he will in the end seek after sources of the future and after new origins.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_25CH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_25CH:P0003","text":"Oh my brothers, it will not be long before new peoples will spring forth and new sources will rush down into new depths.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_25CH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_25CH:P0004","text":"For the earthquake—that buries many wells, that brings about much languishing: it also brings inner forces and secrets to light.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_25CH:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_25CH:P0005","text":"The earthquake makes new sources manifest. In the earthquake of old peoples new sources break forth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_25CH:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_25CH:P0006","text":"And whoever cries: “Behold here a well for many thirsty ones, A heart for many yearning ones, A will for many tools”—around him a people gathers, that is: many experimenters.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_25CH:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_25CH:P0007","text":"Who can command, who must obey—That is what is tried there! Ah, with what long seeking and counselling and miscarrying and learning and trying anew!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_25CH:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_25CH:P0008","text":"Human society: that is an experiment, thus I teach it,—a long seeking: but it seeks the commander!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_25CH:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_25CH:P0009","text":"—an experiment, oh my brothers! And no “contract”! Break, break for me such a word of soft hearts and half- and half-hearted ones!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_25CH:P0009"]}],"source_digest":"c07eca93e8acae81731cea03343617fab2a2be1424a18bd9effcc89010432d8d","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"25.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 25","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","25."],"original_title":"25."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"25.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 25","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","25."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"25.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH","title":"26.","order":107,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_26CH","text":"## 26.\n\nOh meine Brüder! Bei Welchen liegt doch die grösste Gefahr aller Menschen-Zukunft? Ist es nicht bei den Guten und Gerechten?—\n\n—als bei Denen, die sprechen und im Herzen fühlen: „wir wissen schon, was gut ist und gerecht, wir haben es auch; wehe Denen, die hier noch suchen!“—\n\nUnd was für Schaden auch die Bösen thun mögen: der Schaden der Guten ist der schädlichste Schaden!\n\nUnd was für Schaden auch die Welt-Verleumder thun mögen: der Schaden der Guten ist der schädlichste Schaden.\n\nOh meine Brüder, den Guten und Gerechten sah Einer einmal in’s Herz, der da sprach: „es sind die Pharisäer.“ Aber man verstand ihn nicht.\n\nDie Guten und Gerechten selber durften ihn nicht verstehen: ihr Geist ist eingefangen in ihr gutes Gewissen. Die Dummheit der Guten ist unergründlich klug.\n\nDas aber ist die Wahrheit: die Guten müssen Pharisäer sein,—sie haben keine Wahl!\n\nDie Guten müssen Den kreuzigen, der sich seine eigne Tugend erfindet! Das ist die Wahrheit!\n\nDer Zweite aber, der ihr Land entdeckte, Land, Herz und Erdreich der Guten und Gerechten: das war, der da fragte: „wen hassen sie am meisten?“\n\nDen Schaffenden hassen sie am meisten: den, der Tafeln bricht und alte Werthe, den Brecher—den heissen sie Verbrecher.\n\nDie Guten nämlich—die können nicht schaffen: die sind immer der Anfang vom Ende:-\n\n—sie kreuzigen Den, der neue Werthe auf neue Tafeln schreibt, sie opfern sich die Zukunft,—sie kreuzigen alle Menschen-Zukunft!\n\nDie Guten—die waren immer der Anfang vom Ende.—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0001","text":"## 26.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0002","text":"Oh meine Brüder! Bei Welchen liegt doch die grösste Gefahr aller Menschen-Zukunft? Ist es nicht bei den Guten und Gerechten?—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0003","text":"—als bei Denen, die sprechen und im Herzen fühlen: „wir wissen schon, was gut ist und gerecht, wir haben es auch; wehe Denen, die hier noch suchen!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0004","text":"Und was für Schaden auch die Bösen thun mögen: der Schaden der Guten ist der schädlichste Schaden!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0005","text":"Und was für Schaden auch die Welt-Verleumder thun mögen: der Schaden der Guten ist der schädlichste Schaden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0006","text":"Oh meine Brüder, den Guten und Gerechten sah Einer einmal in’s Herz, der da sprach: „es sind die Pharisäer.“ Aber man verstand ihn nicht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0007","text":"Die Guten und Gerechten selber durften ihn nicht verstehen: ihr Geist ist eingefangen in ihr gutes Gewissen. Die Dummheit der Guten ist unergründlich klug.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0008","text":"Das aber ist die Wahrheit: die Guten müssen Pharisäer sein,—sie haben keine Wahl!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0009","text":"Die Guten müssen Den kreuzigen, der sich seine eigne Tugend erfindet! Das ist die Wahrheit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0010","text":"Der Zweite aber, der ihr Land entdeckte, Land, Herz und Erdreich der Guten und Gerechten: das war, der da fragte: „wen hassen sie am meisten?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0011","text":"Den Schaffenden hassen sie am meisten: den, der Tafeln bricht und alte Werthe, den Brecher—den heissen sie Verbrecher.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0012","text":"Die Guten nämlich—die können nicht schaffen: die sind immer der Anfang vom Ende:-","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0013","text":"—sie kreuzigen Den, der neue Werthe auf neue Tafeln schreibt, sie opfern sich die Zukunft,—sie kreuzigen alle Menschen-Zukunft!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0014","text":"Die Guten—die waren immer der Anfang vom Ende.—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"70eed76d8ca9519d2db4d9df365d83bda2f0289002b758fe46534d2a4e5998c8","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183103825314Z_1131154f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"26.","source_sha256":"70eed76d8ca9519d2db4d9df365d83bda2f0289002b758fe46534d2a4e5998c8","sha256":"23fe0eb6bebbbd1106775ed4cf593aa7f7724e489738102b7b574c30df760c67","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:31:03.825327+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0001","text":"## 26.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_26CH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0002","text":"Oh my brothers! With whom, then, lies the greatest danger to all human future? Is it not with the good and the just?—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_26CH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0003","text":"—with those who speak and feel in their hearts: “we already know what is good and just, we have it too; woe to those who still seek here!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_26CH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0004","text":"And whatever harm the wicked may do: the harm done by the good is the most harmful harm!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_26CH:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0005","text":"And whatever harm the slanderers of the world may do: the harm done by the good is the most harmful harm.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_26CH:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0006","text":"Oh my brothers, once someone looked the good and the just in the heart, he who said: “they are the Pharisees.” But he was not understood.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_26CH:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0007","text":"The good and the just themselves were not permitted to understand him: their spirit is caught in their good conscience. The stupidity of the good is unfathomably clever.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_26CH:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0008","text":"But this is the truth: the good must be Pharisees,—they have no choice!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_26CH:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0009","text":"The good must crucify him who invents his own virtue! That is the truth!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_26CH:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0010","text":"But the second who discovered their land, the land, heart, and soil of the good and the just: that was he who asked: “whom do they hate most?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_26CH:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0011","text":"They hate the creator most: him who breaks tablets and old values, the breaker—they call him a criminal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_26CH:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0012","text":"For the good—cannot create: they are always the beginning of the end:-","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_26CH:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0013","text":"—they crucify him who writes new values on new tablets, they sacrifice the future unto themselves,—they crucify all human future!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_26CH:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_26CH:P0014","text":"The good—they have always been the beginning of the end.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_26CH:P0014"]}],"source_digest":"9ad206ed5266885ce2b179c04ff7849b6b29e45e8bf5e631bdcbe61602e9d20f","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"26.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 26","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","26."],"original_title":"26."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"26.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 26","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","26."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"26.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_27CH","title":"27.","order":108,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_27CH","text":"## 27.\n\nOh meine Brüder, verstandet ihr auch diess Wort? Und was ich einst sagte vom „letzten Menschen“ ?—\n\nBei Welchen liegt die grösste Gefahr aller Menschen-Zukunft? Ist es nicht bei den Guten und Gerechten?\n\nZerbrecht, zerbrecht mir die Guten und Gerechten!—Oh meine Brüder, verstandet ihr auch diess Wort?","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_27CH:P0001","text":"## 27.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_27CH:P0002","text":"Oh meine Brüder, verstandet ihr auch diess Wort? Und was ich einst sagte vom „letzten Menschen“ ?—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_27CH:P0003","text":"Bei Welchen liegt die grösste Gefahr aller Menschen-Zukunft? Ist es nicht bei den Guten und Gerechten?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_27CH:P0004","text":"Zerbrecht, zerbrecht mir die Guten und Gerechten!—Oh meine Brüder, verstandet ihr auch diess Wort?","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"00974796158d3c5202843753467395f7556097b0df587d1ec484eb038edfc62a","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183106089677Z_864d0e63","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"27.","source_sha256":"00974796158d3c5202843753467395f7556097b0df587d1ec484eb038edfc62a","sha256":"c8bcaeec43f0345e76d2185dd4b3826492c390a642aee0ea896c61a3d4bc38a3","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:31:06.089709+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_27CH:P0001","text":"## 27.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_27CH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_27CH:P0002","text":"Oh my brothers, did you also understand this word? And what I once said of the “last man”?—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_27CH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_27CH:P0003","text":"With whom does the greatest danger to all human futurity lie? Is it not with the good and the just?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_27CH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_27CH:P0004","text":"Break, break for me the good and the just!—Oh my brothers, did you also understand this word?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_27CH:P0004"]}],"source_digest":"e3882e58e8941b916c4ea1ba75fbc635b31fb3a3798b2f71d901da74df91a453","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"27.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 27","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","27."],"original_title":"27."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"27.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 27","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","27."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"27.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_28CH","title":"28.","order":109,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_28CH","text":"## 28.\n\nIhr flieht von mir? Ihr seid erschreckt? Ihr zittert vor diesem Worte?\n\nOh meine Brüder, als ich euch die Guten zerbrechen hiess und die Tafeln der Guten: da erst schiffte ich den Menschen ein auf seine hohe See.\n\nUnd nun erst kommt ihm der grosse Schrecken, das grosse Um-sich-sehn, die grosse Krankheit, der grosse Ekel, die grosse See-Krankheit.\n\nFalsche Küsten und falsche Sicherheiten lehrten euch die Guten; in Lügen der Guten wart ihr geboren und geborgen. Alles ist in den Grund hinein verlogen und verbogen durch die Guten.\n\nAber wer das Land „Mensch“ entdeckte, entdeckte auch das Land „Menschen-Zukunft“. Nun sollt ihr mir Seefahrer sein, wackere, geduldsame!\n\nAufrecht geht mir bei Zeiten, oh meine Brüder, lernt aufrecht gehn! Das Meer stürmt: Viele wollen an euch sich wieder aufrichten.\n\nDas Meer stürmt: Alles ist im Meere. Wohlan! Wohlauf! Ihr alten Seemanns-Herzen!\n\nWas Vaterland! Dorthin will unser Steuer, wo unser Kinder-Land ist! Dorthinaus, stürmischer als das Meer, stürmt unsre grosse Sehnsucht!—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_28CH:P0001","text":"## 28.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_28CH:P0002","text":"Ihr flieht von mir? Ihr seid erschreckt? Ihr zittert vor diesem Worte?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_28CH:P0003","text":"Oh meine Brüder, als ich euch die Guten zerbrechen hiess und die Tafeln der Guten: da erst schiffte ich den Menschen ein auf seine hohe See.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_28CH:P0004","text":"Und nun erst kommt ihm der grosse Schrecken, das grosse Um-sich-sehn, die grosse Krankheit, der grosse Ekel, die grosse See-Krankheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_28CH:P0005","text":"Falsche Küsten und falsche Sicherheiten lehrten euch die Guten; in Lügen der Guten wart ihr geboren und geborgen. Alles ist in den Grund hinein verlogen und verbogen durch die Guten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_28CH:P0006","text":"Aber wer das Land „Mensch“ entdeckte, entdeckte auch das Land „Menschen-Zukunft“. Nun sollt ihr mir Seefahrer sein, wackere, geduldsame!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_28CH:P0007","text":"Aufrecht geht mir bei Zeiten, oh meine Brüder, lernt aufrecht gehn! Das Meer stürmt: Viele wollen an euch sich wieder aufrichten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_28CH:P0008","text":"Das Meer stürmt: Alles ist im Meere. Wohlan! Wohlauf! Ihr alten Seemanns-Herzen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_28CH:P0009","text":"Was Vaterland! Dorthin will unser Steuer, wo unser Kinder-Land ist! Dorthinaus, stürmischer als das Meer, stürmt unsre grosse Sehnsucht!—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"d3d8c86f4ff03193d9001dbe49e260286605642c957cfe9cadfac3cd50e4e3ad","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183111216218Z_515dc7ae","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"28.","source_sha256":"d3d8c86f4ff03193d9001dbe49e260286605642c957cfe9cadfac3cd50e4e3ad","sha256":"fc7801a367fa53c62a9f7bd7661bcdbb4da96c2af6fc3acd8466ee47459b8fec","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:31:11.216300+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_28CH:P0001","text":"## 28.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_28CH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_28CH:P0002","text":"You flee from me? You are frightened? You tremble before this word?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_28CH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_28CH:P0003","text":"Oh my brothers, when I bade you break the good and the tablets of the good: only then did I embark man upon his high sea.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_28CH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_28CH:P0004","text":"And only now come upon him the great terror, the great looking-about-himself, the great sickness, the great disgust, the great sea-sickness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_28CH:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_28CH:P0005","text":"False coasts and false securities the good taught you; in the lies of the good were you born and sheltered. Everything has been lied and bent down into its ground by the good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_28CH:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_28CH:P0006","text":"But whoever discovered the land “man” also discovered the land “future-of-man.” Now you shall be seafarers for me, brave and patient ones!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_28CH:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_28CH:P0007","text":"Walk upright for me betimes, oh my brothers, learn to walk upright! The sea is storming: many want to set themselves upright again upon you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_28CH:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_28CH:P0008","text":"The sea is storming: everything is in the sea. Well then! Up then! You old seamen's hearts!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_28CH:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_28CH:P0009","text":"What fatherland! Thither our helm would go, where our children's land is! Out thither, more stormily than the sea, storms our great longing!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_28CH:P0009"]}],"source_digest":"ee76dda415ad58f7dcd77e1e422662829d9c9aa1641f2fffcee3d0782f85247b","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"28.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 28","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","28."],"original_title":"28."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"28.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 28","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","28."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"28.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_29CH","title":"29.","order":110,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_29CH","text":"## 29.\n\n„Warum so hart!—sprach zum Diamanten einst die Küchen-Kohle; sind wir denn nicht Nah-Verwandte?“—\n\nWarum so weich? Oh meine Brüder, also frage ich euch: seid ihr denn nicht—meine Brüder?\n\nWarum so weich, so weichend und nachgebend? Warum ist so viel Leugnung, Verleugnung in eurem Herzen? So wenig Schicksal in eurem Blicke?\n\nUnd wollt ihr nicht Schicksale sein und Unerbittliche: wie könntet ihr mit mir —siegen?\n\nUnd wenn eure Härte nicht blitzen und scheiden und zerschneiden will: wie könntet ihr einst mit mir—schaffen?\n\nDie Schaffenden nämlich sind hart. Und Seligkeit muss es euch dünken, eure Hand auf Jahrtausende zu drücken wie auf Wachs,—\n\n—Seligkeit, auf dem Willen von Jahrtausenden zu schreiben wie auf Erz,—härter als Erz, edler als Erz. Ganz hart ist allein das Edelste.\n\nDiese neue Tafel, oh meine Brüder, stelle ich über euch: werdet hart!—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_29CH:P0001","text":"## 29.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_29CH:P0002","text":"„Warum so hart!—sprach zum Diamanten einst die Küchen-Kohle; sind wir denn nicht Nah-Verwandte?“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_29CH:P0003","text":"Warum so weich? Oh meine Brüder, also frage ich euch: seid ihr denn nicht—meine Brüder?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_29CH:P0004","text":"Warum so weich, so weichend und nachgebend? Warum ist so viel Leugnung, Verleugnung in eurem Herzen? So wenig Schicksal in eurem Blicke?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_29CH:P0005","text":"Und wollt ihr nicht Schicksale sein und Unerbittliche: wie könntet ihr mit mir —siegen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_29CH:P0006","text":"Und wenn eure Härte nicht blitzen und scheiden und zerschneiden will: wie könntet ihr einst mit mir—schaffen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_29CH:P0007","text":"Die Schaffenden nämlich sind hart. Und Seligkeit muss es euch dünken, eure Hand auf Jahrtausende zu drücken wie auf Wachs,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_29CH:P0008","text":"—Seligkeit, auf dem Willen von Jahrtausenden zu schreiben wie auf Erz,—härter als Erz, edler als Erz. Ganz hart ist allein das Edelste.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_29CH:P0009","text":"Diese neue Tafel, oh meine Brüder, stelle ich über euch: werdet hart!—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"13c81db5f45737f7ce7ec958a06f5c0550d832c40e4d18f4f2b2d8ca442dbc29","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183115031864Z_47d52762","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"29.","source_sha256":"13c81db5f45737f7ce7ec958a06f5c0550d832c40e4d18f4f2b2d8ca442dbc29","sha256":"56f2ea72df6a0fa84a5dbd25044080a1cfbcb3826534ffb5bafcd15cfeec1966","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:31:15.031886+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_29CH:P0001","text":"## 29.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_29CH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_29CH:P0002","text":"“Why so hard!—the kitchen-coal once said to the diamond; are we not close relatives?”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_29CH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_29CH:P0003","text":"Why so soft? Oh my brothers, thus I ask you: are you not—my brothers?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_29CH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_29CH:P0004","text":"Why so soft, so yielding and accommodating? Why is there so much denial, self-denial in your heart? So little fate in your glance?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_29CH:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_29CH:P0005","text":"And if you do not wish to be fates and inexorable ones: how could you—with me—conquer?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_29CH:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_29CH:P0006","text":"And if your hardness will not flash and divide and cut asunder: how could you one day—with me—create?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_29CH:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_29CH:P0007","text":"For creators are hard. And it must seem bliss to you to press your hand upon millennia as upon wax,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_29CH:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_29CH:P0008","text":"—bliss, to write upon the will of millennia as upon bronze,—harder than bronze, nobler than bronze. Only that which is noblest is wholly hard.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_29CH:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_29CH:P0009","text":"This new tablet, oh my brothers, I set above you: become hard!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_29CH:P0009"]}],"source_digest":"7333c7beb7583d0a90fe21f0de2c0a756ce9ac2504a902930c65307523c8133c","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"29.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 29","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","29."],"original_title":"29."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"29.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 29","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","29."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"29.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH","title":"30.","order":111,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_30CH","text":"## 30.\n\nOh du mein Wille! Du Wende aller Noth du meine Nothwendigkeit! Bewahre mich vor allen kleinen Siegen!\n\nDu Schickung meiner Seele, die ich Schicksal heisse! Du-In-mir! Über-mir! Bewahre und spare mich auf zu Einem grossen Schicksale!\n\nUnd deine letzte Grösse, mein Wille, spare dir für dein Letztes auf,—dass du unerbittlich bist in deinem Siege! Ach, wer unterlag nicht seinem Siege!\n\nAch, wessen Auge dunkelte nicht in dieser trunkenen Dämmerung! Ach, wessen Fuss taumelte nicht und verlernte im Siege—stehen!—\n\n—Dass ich einst bereit und reif sei im grossen Mittage: bereit und reif gleich glühendem Erze, blitzschwangrer Wolke und schwellendem Milch-Euter:—\n\n—bereit zu mir selber und zu meinem verborgensten Willen: ein Bogen brünstig nach seinem Pfeile, ein Pfeil brünstig nach seinem Sterne:—\n\n—ein Stern bereit und reif in seinem Mittage, glühend, durchbohrt, selig vor vernichtenden Sonnen-Pfeilen:—\n\n—eine Sonne selber und ein unerbittlicher Sonnen-Wille, zum Vernichten bereit im Siegen!\n\nOh Wille, Wende aller Noth, du meine Nothwendigkeit! Spare mich auf zu Einem grossen Siege!—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH:P0001","text":"## 30.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH:P0002","text":"Oh du mein Wille! Du Wende aller Noth du meine Nothwendigkeit! Bewahre mich vor allen kleinen Siegen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH:P0003","text":"Du Schickung meiner Seele, die ich Schicksal heisse! Du-In-mir! Über-mir! Bewahre und spare mich auf zu Einem grossen Schicksale!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH:P0004","text":"Und deine letzte Grösse, mein Wille, spare dir für dein Letztes auf,—dass du unerbittlich bist in deinem Siege! Ach, wer unterlag nicht seinem Siege!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH:P0005","text":"Ach, wessen Auge dunkelte nicht in dieser trunkenen Dämmerung! Ach, wessen Fuss taumelte nicht und verlernte im Siege—stehen!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH:P0006","text":"—Dass ich einst bereit und reif sei im grossen Mittage: bereit und reif gleich glühendem Erze, blitzschwangrer Wolke und schwellendem Milch-Euter:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH:P0007","text":"—bereit zu mir selber und zu meinem verborgensten Willen: ein Bogen brünstig nach seinem Pfeile, ein Pfeil brünstig nach seinem Sterne:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH:P0008","text":"—ein Stern bereit und reif in seinem Mittage, glühend, durchbohrt, selig vor vernichtenden Sonnen-Pfeilen:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH:P0009","text":"—eine Sonne selber und ein unerbittlicher Sonnen-Wille, zum Vernichten bereit im Siegen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH:P0010","text":"Oh Wille, Wende aller Noth, du meine Nothwendigkeit! Spare mich auf zu Einem grossen Siege!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH:P0011","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"30b2c84a01deaffdc59c673d66009c5a2a2c603d3f04794188aa59e5a99a5880","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183119943318Z_401b2978","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"30.","source_sha256":"30b2c84a01deaffdc59c673d66009c5a2a2c603d3f04794188aa59e5a99a5880","sha256":"76308c18a19636779fe344af61e1cb4e9fb1b49942f533905a27c36c2f9f1ad8","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:31:19.943348+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH:P0001","text":"## 30.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_30CH:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH:P0002","text":"O thou my will! Thou turning of all need, thou my necessity! Preserve me from all small victories!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_30CH:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH:P0003","text":"Thou destiny of my soul, whom I call fate! Thou-in-me! Above-me! Preserve and spare me for one great destiny!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_30CH:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH:P0004","text":"And thy final greatness, my will, save for thy last thing,—that thou mayest be inexorable in thy victory! Ah, who did not succumb to his victory!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_30CH:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH:P0005","text":"Ah, whose eye did not grow dark in this drunken twilight! Ah, whose foot did not stagger and unlearn, in victory—to stand!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_30CH:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH:P0006","text":"—That I may one day be ready and ripe in the great noon: ready and ripe like glowing ore, lightning-pregnant cloud, and swelling milk-udder:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_30CH:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH:P0007","text":"—ready for myself and for my most hidden will: a bow ardent for its arrow, an arrow ardent for its star:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_30CH:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH:P0008","text":"—a star ready and ripe in its noon, glowing, pierced through, blissful before annihilating sun-arrows:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_30CH:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH:P0009","text":"—a sun itself and an inexorable sun-will, ready for annihilation in victory!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_30CH:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH:P0010","text":"O will, turning of all need, thou my necessity! Spare me for one great victory!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_30CH:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_30CH:P0011","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_30CH:P0011"]}],"source_digest":"d2d002f14630fa179eeffa6f11d20e291d99353d44f82a9fecba4db4eb58a0a3","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On Old and New Tablets","source_section_label":"30.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"On Old and New Tablets · 30","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","On Old and New Tablets","30."],"original_title":"30."}],"source_title_context":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln","source_section_label":"30.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Von alten und neuen Tafeln · 30","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Von alten und neuen Tafeln","30."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"30.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_9","title":"1.","order":112,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_1C_9","text":"## 1.\n\nEines Morgens, nicht lange nach seiner Rückkehr zur Höhle, sprang Zarathustra von seinem Lager auf wie ein Toller, schrie mit furchtbarer Stimme und gebärdete sich, als ob noch Einer auf dem Lager läge, der nicht davon aufstehn wolle; und also tönte Zarathustra’s Stimme, dass seine Thiere erschreckt hinzukamen, und dass aus allen Höhlen und Schlupfwinkeln, die Zarathustra’s Höhle benachbart waren, alles Gethier davon huschte,—fliegend, flatternd, kriechend, springend, wie ihm nur die Art von Fuss und Flügel gegeben war. Zarathustra aber redete diese Worte:\n\nHerauf, abgründlicher Gedanke, aus meiner Tiefe! Ich bin dein Hahn und Morgen-Grauen, verschlafener Wurm: auf! auf! Meine Stimme soll dich schon wach krähen!\n\nKnüpfe die Fessel deiner Ohren los: horche! Denn ich will dich hören! Auf! Auf! Hier ist Donners genug, dass auch Gräber horchen lernen!\n\nUnd wische den Schlaf und alles Blöde, Blinde aus deinen Augen! Höre mich auch mit deinen Augen: meine Stimme ist ein Heilmittel noch für Blindgeborne.\n\nUnd bist du erst wach, sollst du mir ewig wach bleiben. Nicht ist das meine Art, Urgrossmütter aus dem Schlafe wecken, dass ich sie heisse—weiterschlafen!\n\nDu regst dich, dehnst dich, röchelst? Auf! Auf! Nicht röcheln—reden sollst du mir! Zarathustra ruft dich, der Gottlose!\n\nIch, Zarathustra, der Fürsprecher des Lebens, der Fürsprecher des Leidens, der Fürsprecher des Kreises—dich rufe ich, meinen abgründlichsten Gedanken!\n\nHeil mir! Du kommst—ich höre dich! Mein Abgrund redet , meine letzte Tiefe habe ich an’s Licht gestülpt!\n\nHeil mir! Heran! Gieb die Hand—ha! lass! Haha!—Ekel, Ekel, Ekel—wehe mir!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0002","text":"Eines Morgens, nicht lange nach seiner Rückkehr zur Höhle, sprang Zarathustra von seinem Lager auf wie ein Toller, schrie mit furchtbarer Stimme und gebärdete sich, als ob noch Einer auf dem Lager läge, der nicht davon aufstehn wolle; und also tönte Zarathustra’s Stimme, dass seine Thiere erschreckt hinzukamen, und dass aus allen Höhlen und Schlupfwinkeln, die Zarathustra’s Höhle benachbart waren, alles Gethier davon huschte,—fliegend, flatternd, kriechend, springend, wie ihm nur die Art von Fuss und Flügel gegeben war. Zarathustra aber redete diese Worte:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0003","text":"Herauf, abgründlicher Gedanke, aus meiner Tiefe! Ich bin dein Hahn und Morgen-Grauen, verschlafener Wurm: auf! auf! Meine Stimme soll dich schon wach krähen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0004","text":"Knüpfe die Fessel deiner Ohren los: horche! Denn ich will dich hören! Auf! Auf! Hier ist Donners genug, dass auch Gräber horchen lernen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0005","text":"Und wische den Schlaf und alles Blöde, Blinde aus deinen Augen! Höre mich auch mit deinen Augen: meine Stimme ist ein Heilmittel noch für Blindgeborne.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0006","text":"Und bist du erst wach, sollst du mir ewig wach bleiben. Nicht ist das meine Art, Urgrossmütter aus dem Schlafe wecken, dass ich sie heisse—weiterschlafen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0007","text":"Du regst dich, dehnst dich, röchelst? Auf! Auf! Nicht röcheln—reden sollst du mir! Zarathustra ruft dich, der Gottlose!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0008","text":"Ich, Zarathustra, der Fürsprecher des Lebens, der Fürsprecher des Leidens, der Fürsprecher des Kreises—dich rufe ich, meinen abgründlichsten Gedanken!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0009","text":"Heil mir! Du kommst—ich höre dich! Mein Abgrund redet , meine letzte Tiefe habe ich an’s Licht gestülpt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0010","text":"Heil mir! Heran! Gieb die Hand—ha! lass! Haha!—Ekel, Ekel, Ekel—wehe mir!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"338574f58b5e7106fb85956e45bf0a1c673258b8183a669498d59ba3b18a0a9e","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183128606655Z_fa6e2c22","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1.","source_sha256":"338574f58b5e7106fb85956e45bf0a1c673258b8183a669498d59ba3b18a0a9e","sha256":"3b7796349d007947b11249cca24d47a7b3ce14b17c0b8d7e4295311027afe89c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:31:28.606682+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0002","text":"One morning, not long after his return to the cave, Zarathustra sprang up from his couch like one possessed, cried out in a dreadful voice, and gesticulated as though there were yet another lying on the couch who did not wish to rise from it; and thus Zarathustra’s voice resounded, so that his animals came up in alarm, and that from all the caves and hiding-places neighbouring Zarathustra’s cave every creature scurried away,—flying, fluttering, crawling, leaping, according as the kind of foot and wing had been given to it. But Zarathustra spoke these words:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0003","text":"Up, abysmal thought, out of my depth! I am thy cock and morning dawn, thou sleepy worm: up! up! My voice shall yet crow thee awake!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0004","text":"Untie the fetter of thine ears: listen! For I will hear thee! Up! Up! Here there is thunder enough for even graves to learn to listen!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0005","text":"And wipe the sleep and all dullness, blindness, out of thine eyes! Hear me also with thine eyes: my voice is still a remedy for those born blind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0006","text":"And once thou art awake, thou shalt remain awake for me eternally. It is not my way to awaken great-grandmothers from sleep, only to bid them—sleep on!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0007","text":"Thou stirrest, stretchest thyself, rattleth? Up! Up! Thou shalt not rattle—thou shalt speak to me! Zarathustra calleth thee, thou godless one!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0008","text":"I, Zarathustra, the advocate of life, the advocate of suffering, the advocate of the circle—I call thee, my most abysmal thought!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0009","text":"Hail to me! Thou comest—I hear thee! My abyss speaks, I have turned my final depth inside out into the light!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0010","text":"Hail to me! Come nearer! Give me thy hand—ha! let go! Haha!—disgust, disgust, disgust—woe unto me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1C_9:P0010"]}],"source_digest":"219b91d43b1aecb8488431a2dc801329012dab255f60def5f04a74f07358d43f","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Convalescent","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"The Convalescent · 1","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","The Convalescent","1."],"original_title":"1."}],"source_title_context":"Der Genesende","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Der Genesende · 1","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Der Genesende","1."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"1.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9","title":"2.","order":113,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_2C_9","text":"## 2.\n\nKaum aber hatte Zarathustra diese Worte gesprochen, da stürzte er nieder gleich einem Todten und blieb lange wie ein Todter. Als er aber wieder zu sich kam, da war er bleich und zitterte und blieb liegen und wollte lange nicht essen noch trinken. Solches Wesen dauerte an ihm sieben Tage; seine Thiere verliessen ihn aber nicht bei Tag und Nacht, es sei denn, dass der Adler ausflog, Speise zu holen. Und was er holte und zusammenraubte, das legte er auf Zarathustra’s Lager: also dass Zarathustra endlich unter gelben und rothen Beeren, Trauben, Rosenäpfeln, wohlriechendem Krautwerke und Pinien-Zapfen lag. Zu seinen Füssen aber waren zwei Lämmer gebreitet, welche der Adler mit Mühe ihren Hirten abgeraubt hatte.\n\nEndlich, nach sieben Tagen, richtete sich Zarathustra auf seinem Lager auf, nahm einen Rosenapfel in die Hand, roch daran und fand seinen Geruch lieblich. Da glaubten seine Thiere, die Zeit sei gekommen, mit ihm zu reden.\n\n„Oh Zarathustra, sagten sie, nun liegst du schon sieben Tage so, mit schweren Augen: willst du dich nicht endlich wieder auf deine Füsse stellen?\n\nTritt hinaus aus deiner Höhle: die Welt wartet dein wie ein Garten. Der Wind spielt mit schweren Wohlgerüchen, die zu dir wollen; und alle Bäche möchten dir nachlaufen.\n\nAlle Dinge sehnen sich nach dir, dieweil du sieben Tage allein bliebst,—tritt hinaus aus deiner Höhle! Alle Dinge wollen deine Ärzte sein!\n\nKam wohl eine neue Erkenntniss zu dir, eine saure, schwere? Gleich angesäuertem Teige lagst du, deine Seele gieng auf und schwoll über alle ihre Ränder.—„\n\n—Oh meine Thiere, antwortete Zarathustra, schwätzt also weiter und lasst mich zuhören! Es erquickt mich so, dass ihr schwätzt: wo geschwätzt wird, da liegt mir schon die Welt wie ein Garten.\n\nWie lieblich ist es, dass Worte und Töne da sind: sind nicht Worte und Töne Regenbogen und Schein-Brücken zwischen Ewig-Geschiedenem?\n\nZu jeder Seele gehört eine andre Welt; für jede Seele ist jede andre Seele eine Hinterwelt.\n\nZwischen dem Ähnlichsten gerade lügt der Schein am schönsten; denn die kleinste Kluft ist am schwersten zu überbrücken.\n\nFür mich—wie gäbe es ein Ausser-mir? Es giebt kein Aussen! Aber das vergessen wir bei allen Tönen; wie lieblich ist es, dass wir vergessen!\n\nSind nicht den Dingen Namen und Töne geschenkt, dass der Mensch sich an den Dingen erquicke? Es ist eine schöne Narrethei, das Sprechen: damit tanzt der Mensch über alle Dinge.\n\nWie lieblich ist alles Reden und alle Lüge der Töne! Mit Tönen tanzt unsre Liebe auf bunten Regenbögen.—\n\n—„Oh Zarathustra, sagten darauf die Thiere, Solchen, die denken wie wir, tanzen alle Dinge selber: das kommt und reicht sich die Hand und lacht und flieht—und kommt zurück.\n\nAlles geht, Alles kommt zurück; ewig rollt das Rad des Seins. Alles stirbt, Alles blüht wieder auf, ewig läuft das Jahr des Seins.\n\nAlles bricht, Alles wird neu gefügt; ewig baut sich das gleiche Haus des Seins. Alles scheidet, Alles grüsst sich wieder; ewig bleibt sich treu der Ring des Seins.\n\nIn jedem Nu beginnt das Sein; um jedes Hier rollt sich die Kugel Dort. Die Mitte ist überall. Krumm ist der Pfad der Ewigkeit.“—\n\n—Oh ihr Schalks-Narren und Drehorgeln! antwortete Zarathustra und lächelte wieder, wie gut wisst ihr, was sich in sieben Tagen erfüllen musste:—\n\n—und wie jenes Unthier mir in den Schlund kroch und mich würgte! Aber ich biss ihm den Kopf ab und spie ihn weg von mir.\n\nUnd ihr,—ihr machtet schon ein Leier-Lied daraus? Nun aber liege ich da, müde noch von diesem Beissen und Wegspein, krank noch von der eigenen Erlösung.\n\nUnd ihr schautet dem Allen zu? Oh meine Thiere, seid auch ihr grausam? Habt ihr meinem grossen Schmerze zuschaun wollen, wie Menschen thun? Der Mensch nämlich ist das grausamste Thier.\n\nBei Trauerspielen, Stierkämpfen und Kreuzigungen ist es ihm bisher am wohlsten geworden auf Erden; und als er sich die Hölle erfand, siehe, da war das sein Himmel auf Erden.\n\nWenn der grosse Mensch schreit—: flugs läuft der kleine hinzu; und die Zunge hängt ihm aus dem Halse vor Lüsternheit. Er aber heisst es sein „Mitleiden.“\n\nDer kleine Mensch, sonderlich der Dichter—wie eifrig klagt er das Leben in Worten an! Hört hin, aber überhört mir die Lust nicht, die in allem Anklagen ist!\n\nSolche Ankläger des Lebens: die überwindet das Leben mit einem Augenblinzeln. „Du liebst mich? sagt die Freche; warte noch ein Wenig, noch habe ich für dich nicht Zeit.“\n\nDer Mensch ist gegen sich selber das grausamste Thier; und bei Allem, was sich „Sünder“ und „Kreuzträger“ und „Büsser“ heisst, überhört mir die Wollust nicht, die in diesem Klagen und Anklagen ist!\n\nUnd ich selber—will ich damit des Menschen Ankläger sein? Ach, meine Thiere, Das allein lernte ich bisher, dass dem Menschen sein Bösestes nöthig ist zu seinem Besten,—\n\n—dass alles Böseste seine beste Kraft ist und der härteste Stein dem höchsten Schaffenden; und dass der Mensch besser und böser werden muss: -\n\nNicht an diess Marterholz war ich geheftet, dass ich weiss: der Mensch ist böse,—sondern ich schrie, wie noch Niemand geschrien hat:\n\n„Ach dass sein Bösestes so gar klein ist! Ach dass sein Bestes so gar klein ist!“\n\nDer grosse Überdruss am Menschen— der würgte mich und war mir in den Schlund gekrochen: und was der Wahrsager wahrsagte: „Alles ist gleich, es lohnt sich Nichts, Wissen würgt.“\n\nEine lange Dämmerung hinkte vor mir her, eine todesmüde, todestrunkene Traurigkeit, welche mit gähnendem Munde redete.\n\n„Ewig kehrt er wieder, der Mensch, dess du müde bist, der kleine Mensch“—so gähnte meine Traurigkeit und schleppte den Fuss und konnte nicht einschlafen.\n\nZur Höhle wandelte sich mir die Menschen-Erde, ihre Brust sank hinein, alles Lebendige ward mir Menschen-Moder und Knochen und morsche Vergangenheit.\n\nMein Seufzen sass auf allen Menschen-Gräbern und konnte nicht mehr aufstehn; mein Seufzen und Fragen unkte und würgte und nagte und klagte bei Tag und Nacht:\n\n—„ach, der Mensch kehrt ewig wieder! Der kleine Mensch kehrt ewig wieder!“—\n\nNackt hatte ich einst Beide gesehn, den grössten Menschen und den kleinsten Menschen: allzuähnlich einander,—allzumenschlich auch den Grössten noch!\n\nAllzuklein der Grösste!—Das war mein Überdruss am Menschen! Und ewige Wiederkunft auch des Kleinsten!—Das war mein Überdruss an allem Dasein!\n\nAch, Ekel! Ekel! Ekel!—- Also sprach Zarathustra und seufzte und schauderte; denn er erinnerte sich seiner Krankheit. Da liessen ihn aber seine Thiere nicht weiter reden.\n\n„Sprich nicht weiter, du Genesender!—so antworteten ihm seine Thiere, sondern geh hinaus, wo die Welt auf dich wartet gleich einem Garten.\n\nGeh hinaus zu den Rosen und Bienen und Taubenschwärmen! Sonderlich aber zu den Singe-Vögeln: dass du ihnen das Singen ablernst!\n\nSingen nämlich ist für Genesende; der Gesunde mag reden. Und wenn auch der Gesunde Lieder will, will er andre Lieder doch als der Genesende.“\n\n—„Oh ihr Schalks-Narren und Drehorgeln, so schweigt doch!—antwortete Zarathustra und lächelte über seine Thiere. Wie gut ihr wisst, welchen Trost ich mir selber in sieben Tagen erfand!\n\nDass ich wieder singen müsse,— den Trost erfand ich mir und diese Genesung: wollt ihr auch daraus gleich wieder ein Leier-Lied machen?“\n\n—„Sprich nicht weiter, antworteten ihm abermals seine Thiere; lieber noch, du Genesender, mache dir erst eine Leier zurecht, eine neue Leier!\n\nDenn siehe doch, oh Zarathustra! Zu deinen neuen Liedern bedarf es neuer Leiern.\n\nSinge und brause über, oh Zarathustra, heile mit neuen Liedern deine Seele: dass du dein grosses Schicksal tragest, das noch keines Menschen Schicksal war!\n\nDenn deine Thiere wissen es wohl, oh Zarathustra, wer du bist und werden musst: siehe, du bist der Lehrer der ewigen Wiederkunft—, das ist nun dein Schicksal!\n\nDass du als der Erste diese Lehre lehren musst,—wie sollte diess grosse Schicksal nicht auch deine grösste Gefahr und Krankheit sein!\n\nSiehe, wir wissen, was du lehrst: dass alle Dinge ewig wiederkehren und wir selber mit, und dass wir schon ewige Male dagewesen sind, und alle Dinge mit uns.\n\nDu lehrst, dass es ein grosses Jahr des Werdens giebt, ein Ungeheuer von grossem Jahre: das muss sich, einer Sanduhr gleich, immer wieder von Neuem umdrehn, damit es von Neuem ablaufe und auslaufe:—\n\n—so dass alle diese Jahre sich selber gleich sind, im Grössten und auch im Kleinsten,—so dass wir selber in jedem grossen Jahre uns selber gleich sind, im Grössten und auch im Kleinsten.\n\nUnd wenn du jetzt sterben wolltest, oh Zarathustra: siehe, wir wissen auch, wie du da zu dir sprechen würdest:—aber deine Thiere bitten dich, dass du noch nicht sterbest!\n\nDu würdest sprechen und ohne Zittern, vielmehr aufathmend vor Seligkeit: denn eine grosse Schwere und Schwüle wäre von dir genommen, du Geduldigster!—\n\n„Nun sterbe und schwinde ich, würdest du sprechen, und im Nu bin ich ein Nichts. Die Seelen sind so sterblich wie die Leiber.\n\nAber der Knoten von Ursachen kehrt wieder, in den ich verschlungen bin,—der wird mich wieder schaffen! Ich selber gehöre zu den Ursachen der ewigen Wiederkunft.\n\nIch komme wieder, mit dieser Sonne, mit dieser Erde, mit diesem Adler, mit dieser Schlange— nicht zu einem neuen Leben oder besseren Leben oder ähnlichen Leben:\n\n—ich komme ewig wieder zu diesem gleichen und selbigen Leben, im Grössten und auch im Kleinsten, dass ich wieder aller Dinge ewige Wiederkunft lehre,—\n\n—dass ich wieder das Wort spreche vom grossen Erden- und Menschen-Mittage, dass ich wieder den Menschen den Übermenschen künde.\n\nIch sprach mein Wort, ich zerbreche an meinem Wort: so will es mein ewiges Loos -, als Verkündiger gehe ich zu Grunde!\n\nDie Stunde kam nun, dass der Untergehende sich selber segnet. Also endet Zarathustra’s Untergang.““—\n\nAls die Thiere diese Worte gesprochen hatten, schwiegen sie und warteten, dass Zarathustra Etwas zu ihnen sagen werde: aber Zarathustra hörte nicht, dass sie schwiegen. Vielmehr lag er still, mit geschlossenen Augen, einem Schlafenden ähnlich, ob er schon nicht schlief: denn er unterredete sich eben mit seiner Seele. Die Schlange aber und der Adler, als sie ihn solchermaassen schweigsam fanden, ehrten die grosse Stille um ihn und machten sich behutsam davon.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0002","text":"Kaum aber hatte Zarathustra diese Worte gesprochen, da stürzte er nieder gleich einem Todten und blieb lange wie ein Todter. Als er aber wieder zu sich kam, da war er bleich und zitterte und blieb liegen und wollte lange nicht essen noch trinken. Solches Wesen dauerte an ihm sieben Tage; seine Thiere verliessen ihn aber nicht bei Tag und Nacht, es sei denn, dass der Adler ausflog, Speise zu holen. Und was er holte und zusammenraubte, das legte er auf Zarathustra’s Lager: also dass Zarathustra endlich unter gelben und rothen Beeren, Trauben, Rosenäpfeln, wohlriechendem Krautwerke und Pinien-Zapfen lag. Zu seinen Füssen aber waren zwei Lämmer gebreitet, welche der Adler mit Mühe ihren Hirten abgeraubt hatte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0003","text":"Endlich, nach sieben Tagen, richtete sich Zarathustra auf seinem Lager auf, nahm einen Rosenapfel in die Hand, roch daran und fand seinen Geruch lieblich. Da glaubten seine Thiere, die Zeit sei gekommen, mit ihm zu reden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0004","text":"„Oh Zarathustra, sagten sie, nun liegst du schon sieben Tage so, mit schweren Augen: willst du dich nicht endlich wieder auf deine Füsse stellen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0005","text":"Tritt hinaus aus deiner Höhle: die Welt wartet dein wie ein Garten. Der Wind spielt mit schweren Wohlgerüchen, die zu dir wollen; und alle Bäche möchten dir nachlaufen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0006","text":"Alle Dinge sehnen sich nach dir, dieweil du sieben Tage allein bliebst,—tritt hinaus aus deiner Höhle! Alle Dinge wollen deine Ärzte sein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0007","text":"Kam wohl eine neue Erkenntniss zu dir, eine saure, schwere? Gleich angesäuertem Teige lagst du, deine Seele gieng auf und schwoll über alle ihre Ränder.—„","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0008","text":"—Oh meine Thiere, antwortete Zarathustra, schwätzt also weiter und lasst mich zuhören! Es erquickt mich so, dass ihr schwätzt: wo geschwätzt wird, da liegt mir schon die Welt wie ein Garten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0009","text":"Wie lieblich ist es, dass Worte und Töne da sind: sind nicht Worte und Töne Regenbogen und Schein-Brücken zwischen Ewig-Geschiedenem?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0010","text":"Zu jeder Seele gehört eine andre Welt; für jede Seele ist jede andre Seele eine Hinterwelt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0011","text":"Zwischen dem Ähnlichsten gerade lügt der Schein am schönsten; denn die kleinste Kluft ist am schwersten zu überbrücken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0012","text":"Für mich—wie gäbe es ein Ausser-mir? Es giebt kein Aussen! Aber das vergessen wir bei allen Tönen; wie lieblich ist es, dass wir vergessen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0013","text":"Sind nicht den Dingen Namen und Töne geschenkt, dass der Mensch sich an den Dingen erquicke? Es ist eine schöne Narrethei, das Sprechen: damit tanzt der Mensch über alle Dinge.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0014","text":"Wie lieblich ist alles Reden und alle Lüge der Töne! Mit Tönen tanzt unsre Liebe auf bunten Regenbögen.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0015","text":"—„Oh Zarathustra, sagten darauf die Thiere, Solchen, die denken wie wir, tanzen alle Dinge selber: das kommt und reicht sich die Hand und lacht und flieht—und kommt zurück.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0016","text":"Alles geht, Alles kommt zurück; ewig rollt das Rad des Seins. Alles stirbt, Alles blüht wieder auf, ewig läuft das Jahr des Seins.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0017","text":"Alles bricht, Alles wird neu gefügt; ewig baut sich das gleiche Haus des Seins. Alles scheidet, Alles grüsst sich wieder; ewig bleibt sich treu der Ring des Seins.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0018","text":"In jedem Nu beginnt das Sein; um jedes Hier rollt sich die Kugel Dort. Die Mitte ist überall. Krumm ist der Pfad der Ewigkeit.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0019","text":"—Oh ihr Schalks-Narren und Drehorgeln! antwortete Zarathustra und lächelte wieder, wie gut wisst ihr, was sich in sieben Tagen erfüllen musste:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0020","text":"—und wie jenes Unthier mir in den Schlund kroch und mich würgte! Aber ich biss ihm den Kopf ab und spie ihn weg von mir.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0021","text":"Und ihr,—ihr machtet schon ein Leier-Lied daraus? Nun aber liege ich da, müde noch von diesem Beissen und Wegspein, krank noch von der eigenen Erlösung.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0022","text":"Und ihr schautet dem Allen zu? Oh meine Thiere, seid auch ihr grausam? Habt ihr meinem grossen Schmerze zuschaun wollen, wie Menschen thun? Der Mensch nämlich ist das grausamste Thier.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0023","text":"Bei Trauerspielen, Stierkämpfen und Kreuzigungen ist es ihm bisher am wohlsten geworden auf Erden; und als er sich die Hölle erfand, siehe, da war das sein Himmel auf Erden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0024","text":"Wenn der grosse Mensch schreit—: flugs läuft der kleine hinzu; und die Zunge hängt ihm aus dem Halse vor Lüsternheit. Er aber heisst es sein „Mitleiden.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0025","text":"Der kleine Mensch, sonderlich der Dichter—wie eifrig klagt er das Leben in Worten an! Hört hin, aber überhört mir die Lust nicht, die in allem Anklagen ist!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0026","text":"Solche Ankläger des Lebens: die überwindet das Leben mit einem Augenblinzeln. „Du liebst mich? sagt die Freche; warte noch ein Wenig, noch habe ich für dich nicht Zeit.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0027","text":"Der Mensch ist gegen sich selber das grausamste Thier; und bei Allem, was sich „Sünder“ und „Kreuzträger“ und „Büsser“ heisst, überhört mir die Wollust nicht, die in diesem Klagen und Anklagen ist!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0028","text":"Und ich selber—will ich damit des Menschen Ankläger sein? Ach, meine Thiere, Das allein lernte ich bisher, dass dem Menschen sein Bösestes nöthig ist zu seinem Besten,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0029","text":"—dass alles Böseste seine beste Kraft ist und der härteste Stein dem höchsten Schaffenden; und dass der Mensch besser und böser werden muss: -","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0030","text":"Nicht an diess Marterholz war ich geheftet, dass ich weiss: der Mensch ist böse,—sondern ich schrie, wie noch Niemand geschrien hat:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0031","text":"„Ach dass sein Bösestes so gar klein ist! Ach dass sein Bestes so gar klein ist!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0032","text":"Der grosse Überdruss am Menschen— der würgte mich und war mir in den Schlund gekrochen: und was der Wahrsager wahrsagte: „Alles ist gleich, es lohnt sich Nichts, Wissen würgt.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0033","text":"Eine lange Dämmerung hinkte vor mir her, eine todesmüde, todestrunkene Traurigkeit, welche mit gähnendem Munde redete.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0034","text":"„Ewig kehrt er wieder, der Mensch, dess du müde bist, der kleine Mensch“—so gähnte meine Traurigkeit und schleppte den Fuss und konnte nicht einschlafen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0035","text":"Zur Höhle wandelte sich mir die Menschen-Erde, ihre Brust sank hinein, alles Lebendige ward mir Menschen-Moder und Knochen und morsche Vergangenheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0036","text":"Mein Seufzen sass auf allen Menschen-Gräbern und konnte nicht mehr aufstehn; mein Seufzen und Fragen unkte und würgte und nagte und klagte bei Tag und Nacht:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0037","text":"—„ach, der Mensch kehrt ewig wieder! Der kleine Mensch kehrt ewig wieder!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0038","text":"Nackt hatte ich einst Beide gesehn, den grössten Menschen und den kleinsten Menschen: allzuähnlich einander,—allzumenschlich auch den Grössten noch!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0039","text":"Allzuklein der Grösste!—Das war mein Überdruss am Menschen! Und ewige Wiederkunft auch des Kleinsten!—Das war mein Überdruss an allem Dasein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0040","text":"Ach, Ekel! Ekel! Ekel!—- Also sprach Zarathustra und seufzte und schauderte; denn er erinnerte sich seiner Krankheit. Da liessen ihn aber seine Thiere nicht weiter reden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0041","text":"„Sprich nicht weiter, du Genesender!—so antworteten ihm seine Thiere, sondern geh hinaus, wo die Welt auf dich wartet gleich einem Garten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0042","text":"Geh hinaus zu den Rosen und Bienen und Taubenschwärmen! Sonderlich aber zu den Singe-Vögeln: dass du ihnen das Singen ablernst!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0043","text":"Singen nämlich ist für Genesende; der Gesunde mag reden. Und wenn auch der Gesunde Lieder will, will er andre Lieder doch als der Genesende.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0044","text":"—„Oh ihr Schalks-Narren und Drehorgeln, so schweigt doch!—antwortete Zarathustra und lächelte über seine Thiere. Wie gut ihr wisst, welchen Trost ich mir selber in sieben Tagen erfand!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0045","text":"Dass ich wieder singen müsse,— den Trost erfand ich mir und diese Genesung: wollt ihr auch daraus gleich wieder ein Leier-Lied machen?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0046","text":"—„Sprich nicht weiter, antworteten ihm abermals seine Thiere; lieber noch, du Genesender, mache dir erst eine Leier zurecht, eine neue Leier!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0047","text":"Denn siehe doch, oh Zarathustra! Zu deinen neuen Liedern bedarf es neuer Leiern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0048","text":"Singe und brause über, oh Zarathustra, heile mit neuen Liedern deine Seele: dass du dein grosses Schicksal tragest, das noch keines Menschen Schicksal war!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0049","text":"Denn deine Thiere wissen es wohl, oh Zarathustra, wer du bist und werden musst: siehe, du bist der Lehrer der ewigen Wiederkunft—, das ist nun dein Schicksal!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0050","text":"Dass du als der Erste diese Lehre lehren musst,—wie sollte diess grosse Schicksal nicht auch deine grösste Gefahr und Krankheit sein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0051","text":"Siehe, wir wissen, was du lehrst: dass alle Dinge ewig wiederkehren und wir selber mit, und dass wir schon ewige Male dagewesen sind, und alle Dinge mit uns.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0052","text":"Du lehrst, dass es ein grosses Jahr des Werdens giebt, ein Ungeheuer von grossem Jahre: das muss sich, einer Sanduhr gleich, immer wieder von Neuem umdrehn, damit es von Neuem ablaufe und auslaufe:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0053","text":"—so dass alle diese Jahre sich selber gleich sind, im Grössten und auch im Kleinsten,—so dass wir selber in jedem grossen Jahre uns selber gleich sind, im Grössten und auch im Kleinsten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0054","text":"Und wenn du jetzt sterben wolltest, oh Zarathustra: siehe, wir wissen auch, wie du da zu dir sprechen würdest:—aber deine Thiere bitten dich, dass du noch nicht sterbest!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0055","text":"Du würdest sprechen und ohne Zittern, vielmehr aufathmend vor Seligkeit: denn eine grosse Schwere und Schwüle wäre von dir genommen, du Geduldigster!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0056","text":"„Nun sterbe und schwinde ich, würdest du sprechen, und im Nu bin ich ein Nichts. Die Seelen sind so sterblich wie die Leiber.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0057","text":"Aber der Knoten von Ursachen kehrt wieder, in den ich verschlungen bin,—der wird mich wieder schaffen! Ich selber gehöre zu den Ursachen der ewigen Wiederkunft.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0058","text":"Ich komme wieder, mit dieser Sonne, mit dieser Erde, mit diesem Adler, mit dieser Schlange— nicht zu einem neuen Leben oder besseren Leben oder ähnlichen Leben:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0059","text":"—ich komme ewig wieder zu diesem gleichen und selbigen Leben, im Grössten und auch im Kleinsten, dass ich wieder aller Dinge ewige Wiederkunft lehre,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0060","text":"—dass ich wieder das Wort spreche vom grossen Erden- und Menschen-Mittage, dass ich wieder den Menschen den Übermenschen künde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0061","text":"Ich sprach mein Wort, ich zerbreche an meinem Wort: so will es mein ewiges Loos -, als Verkündiger gehe ich zu Grunde!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0062","text":"Die Stunde kam nun, dass der Untergehende sich selber segnet. Also endet Zarathustra’s Untergang.““—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0063","text":"Als die Thiere diese Worte gesprochen hatten, schwiegen sie und warteten, dass Zarathustra Etwas zu ihnen sagen werde: aber Zarathustra hörte nicht, dass sie schwiegen. Vielmehr lag er still, mit geschlossenen Augen, einem Schlafenden ähnlich, ob er schon nicht schlief: denn er unterredete sich eben mit seiner Seele. Die Schlange aber und der Adler, als sie ihn solchermaassen schweigsam fanden, ehrten die grosse Stille um ihn und machten sich behutsam davon.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"60340b7d9b04fdd5128e793ae4b73ea5e02ad33780688f655059258bd40a950b","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183206180564Z_7cf3de42","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2.","source_sha256":"60340b7d9b04fdd5128e793ae4b73ea5e02ad33780688f655059258bd40a950b","sha256":"29c81c4da84c10028985a942cd437a98da79e471f2b29d033259e96fbb8aa633","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:32:06.180574+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0002","text":"But scarcely had Zarathustra spoken these words when he collapsed like a dead man and remained for a long time like one dead. But when he came to himself again, he was pale and trembling and remained lying down and for a long time would neither eat nor drink. Such a condition lasted with him for seven days; but his animals did not leave him by day or by night, unless the eagle flew out to fetch food. And whatever he fetched and plundered together, he laid upon Zarathustra’s bed: so that Zarathustra at last lay beneath yellow and red berries, grapes, rose-apples, fragrant herbs, and pine-cones. But at his feet two lambs were spread out, which the eagle had with difficulty robbed from their shepherd.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0003","text":"At last, after seven days, Zarathustra raised himself up on his bed, took a rose-apple in his hand, smelled it, and found its scent delightful. Then his animals believed that the time had come to speak with him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0004","text":"“O Zarathustra, they said, now you have lain thus for seven days, with heavy eyes: do you not wish at last to stand again upon your feet?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0005","text":"Step out of your cave: the world awaits you like a garden. The wind plays with heavy fragrances that wish to come to you; and all brooks would like to run after you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0006","text":"All things long for you, because you remained alone for seven days,—step out of your cave! All things wish to be your physicians!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0007","text":"“Did some new knowledge come to you, a sour, heavy one? Like leavened dough you lay, your soul rose and swelled beyond all its borders.—”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0008","text":"—“O my animals, answered Zarathustra, chatter on thus and let me listen! It refreshes me so that you chatter: where there is chatter, there the world already lies before me like a garden.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0009","text":"How delightful it is that words and sounds exist: are not words and sounds rainbows and seeming bridges between things eternally sundered?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0010","text":"To every soul belongs another world; for every soul, every other soul is an afterworld.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0011","text":"Precisely between the most similar things appearance lies most beautifully; for the smallest gap is the hardest to bridge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0012","text":"For me—how could there be an outside-me? There is no outside! But we forget that with all sounds; how delightful it is that we forget!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0013","text":"Have names and sounds not been bestowed upon things, so that man may be refreshed by things? Speaking is a beautiful folly: by means of it man dances over all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0014","text":"How delightful is all speaking and all the lie of sounds! With sounds our love dances upon coloured rainbows.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0015","text":"—“O Zarathustra, the animals then said, for those who think as we do, all things themselves dance: they come and reach out their hand and laugh and flee—and come back.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0016","text":"Everything goes, everything comes back; eternally rolls the wheel of being. Everything dies, everything blossoms anew, eternally runs the year of being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0017","text":"Everything breaks, everything is newly joined together; eternally the same house of being builds itself. Everything parts, everything greets itself again; eternally the ring of being remains faithful to itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0018","text":"In every instant being begins; around every Here rolls the sphere There. The centre is everywhere. Crooked is the path of eternity.”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0019","text":"—O you mischief-fools and barrel-organs! answered Zarathustra, and smiled again, how well you know what had to be fulfilled in seven days:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0020","text":"—and how that monster crawled into my throat and choked me! But I bit its head off and spat it away from me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0021","text":"And you,—you have already made a barrel-organ tune out of it? But now I lie here, still weary from this biting and spitting away, still sick from my own deliverance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0022","text":"And you watched all this? O my animals, are you too cruel? Did you wish to watch my great pain, as men do? For man is the cruellest animal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0023","text":"At tragedies, bullfights, and crucifixions he has hitherto felt best on earth; and when he invented hell for himself, behold, that was his heaven on earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0024","text":"When the great man cries out—: swiftly the little one runs up; and his tongue hangs out of his throat with lustfulness. But he calls it his “compassion.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0025","text":"The little human being, especially the poet—how zealously he indicts life in words! Listen, but do not fail to hear for me the pleasure that is in all indictment!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0026","text":"Such accusers of life: life overcomes them with a blink of the eye. “You love me? says the brazen one; wait a little longer, I have no time for you yet.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0027","text":"Against himself the human being is the cruellest beast; and in all that calls itself “sinner” and “cross-bearer” and “penitent,” do not fail to hear for me the voluptuousness that is in this lamenting and accusing!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0028","text":"And I myself—would I thereby be the accuser of the human being? Ah, my animals, this alone have I learned hitherto, that the human being needs what is most evil in him for what is best in him,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0029","text":"—that all that is most evil is his best strength and the hardest stone for the highest creator; and that the human being must become better and more evil: -","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0030","text":"It was not to this wood of torment that I was fastened, that I know: the human being is evil,—but I cried out as no one has yet cried out:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0031","text":"“Ah, that what is most evil in him is so very small! Ah, that what is best in him is so very small!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0032","text":"The great weariness with the human being—that choked me and had crept down into my throat: and what the soothsayer had foretold: “Everything is the same, nothing is worth while, knowledge chokes.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0033","text":"A long twilight limped before me, a death-weary, death-intoxicated sadness, which spoke with gaping mouth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0034","text":"“Eternally he returns, the human being of whom you are weary, the little human being”—thus my sadness yawned and dragged its foot and could not fall asleep.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0035","text":"The human earth changed itself for me into a cave, its breast sank inwards, all living things became for me human mould and bones and mouldering past.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0036","text":"My sigh sat upon all human graves and could no longer rise; my sighing and questioning croaked ominously and choked and gnawed and lamented by day and night:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0037","text":"—“ah, the human being returns eternally! The little human being returns eternally!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0038","text":"Once I had seen them both naked, the greatest human being and the smallest human being: all too similar to one another,—all too human even the greatest still!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0039","text":"All too small the greatest!—That was my weariness with the human being! And eternal recurrence even of the smallest!—That was my weariness with all existence!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0040","text":"Ah, disgust! Disgust! Disgust!—- Thus spoke Zarathustra and sighed and shuddered; for he remembered his sickness. But then his animals did not let him speak further.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0041","text":"“Speak no further, you convalescent!—thus his animals answered him, but go out where the world awaits you like a garden.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0042","text":"Go out to the roses and bees and swarms of doves! But especially to the singing-birds: so that you may learn singing from them!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0043","text":"For singing is for convalescents; the healthy man may speak. And even if the healthy man wants songs, he wants songs other than those of the convalescent.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0044","text":"—“Oh you mischievous fools and barrel-organs, do be silent!—answered Zarathustra, and smiled at his animals. How well you know what consolation I invented for myself in seven days!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0045","text":"That I must sing again,—I invented for myself this consolation and this convalescence: do you also wish at once to make of it another lyre-song?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0046","text":"—“Speak no further, his animals answered him again; rather, you convalescent, first make yourself a lyre, a new lyre!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0047","text":"For behold, O Zarathustra! For your new songs new lyres are needed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0048","text":"Sing and surge over, O Zarathustra, heal your soul with new songs: so that you may bear your great destiny, which has yet been no human being’s destiny!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0049","text":"For your animals know well, O Zarathustra, who you are and must become: behold, you are the teacher of the eternal recurrence—, that is now your destiny!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0050","text":"That you, as the first, must teach this doctrine,—how should this great destiny not also be your greatest danger and sickness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0051","text":"Behold, we know what you teach: that all things eternally recur and we ourselves with them, and that we have already been here eternal times, and all things with us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0052","text":"You teach that there is a great year of becoming, a monster of a great year: this must, like an hourglass, turn itself over anew again and again, so that it may run and run out anew:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0053","text":"—so that all these years are alike to themselves, in the greatest as also in the smallest,—so that we ourselves in every great year are alike to ourselves, in the greatest as also in the smallest.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0054","text":"And if you now wished to die, O Zarathustra: behold, we also know how you would then speak to yourself:—but your animals entreat you not to die yet!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0055","text":"You would speak, and without trembling, rather breathing a sigh of relief from bliss: for a great heaviness and sultriness would have been taken from you, most patient one!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0056","text":"“Now I die and vanish, you would say, and in an instant I am a nothing. Souls are as mortal as bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0057","text":"But the knot of causes in which I am entwined recurs,—it will create me again! I myself belong to the causes of eternal recurrence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0058","text":"I return, with this sun, with this earth, with this eagle, with this serpent— not to a new life or a better life or a similar life:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0059","text":"—I return eternally to this same and selfsame life, in the greatest as also in the smallest, to teach again the eternal recurrence of all things,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0060","text":"—to speak again the word of the great noon of earth and man, to proclaim the Overman to men again.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0061","text":"I spoke my word, I break upon my word: thus my eternal lot wills it -, as a proclaimer I perish!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0062","text":"The hour has now come when the one who goes under blesses himself. Thus ends Zarathustra’s going-under.”“—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0063","text":"When the animals had spoken these words, they fell silent and waited for Zarathustra to say something to them: but Zarathustra did not hear that they were silent. Rather, he lay still, with closed eyes, like one asleep, although he did not sleep: for he was just conversing with his soul. But the serpent and the eagle, when they found him thus silent, honoured the great silence around him and cautiously went away.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2C_9:P0063"]}],"source_digest":"bff336fc077f632b95c26d923d45c2fd29e4477abfc75701e2fbbcd6c5734374","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Convalescent","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"The Convalescent · 2","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","The Convalescent","2."],"original_title":"2."}],"source_title_context":"Der Genesende","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Der Genesende · 2","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Der Genesende","2."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"2.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS","title":"On the Great Longing","order":114,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDGS","text":"Oh meine Seele, ich lehrte dich „Heute“ sagen wie „Einst“ und „Ehemals“ und über alles Hier und Da und Dort deinen Reigen hinweg tanzen.\n\nOh meine Seele, ich erlöste dich von allen Winkeln, ich kehrte Staub, Spinnen und Zwielicht von dir ab.\n\nOh meine Seele, ich wusch die kleine Scham und die Winkel-Tugend von dir ab und überredete dich, nackt vor den Augen der Sonne zu stehn.\n\nMit dem Sturme, welcher „Geist“ heisst, blies ich über deine wogende See; alle Wolken blies ich davon, ich erwürgte selbst die Würgerin, die „Sünde“ heisst.\n\nOh meine Seele, ich gab dir das Recht, Nein zu sagen wie der Sturm und Ja zu sagen wie offner Himmel Ja sagt: still wie Licht stehst du und gehst du nun durch verneinende Stürme.\n\nOh meine Seele, ich gab dir die Freiheit zurück über Erschaffnes und Unerschaffnes: und wer kennt, wie du sie kennst, die Wollust des Zukünftigen?\n\nOh meine Seele, ich lehrte dich das Verachten, das nicht wie ein Wurmfrass kommt, das grosse, das liebende Verachten, welches am meisten liebt, wo es am meisten verachtet.\n\nOh meine Seele, ich lehrte dich so überreden, dass du zu dir die Gründe selber überredest: der Sonne gleich, die das Meer noch zu seiner Höhe überredet.\n\nOh meine Seele, ich nahm von dir alles Gehorchen Kniebeugen und Herr-Sagen; ich gab dir selber den Namen „Wende der Noth“ und „Schicksal“.\n\nOh meine Seele, ich gab dir neue Namen und bunte Spielwerke, ich hiess dich „Schicksal“ und „Umfang der Umfänge“ und „Nabelschnur der Zeit“ und „azurne Glocke“ .\n\nOh meine Seele, deinem Erdreich gab ich alle Weisheit zu trinken, alle neuen Weine und auch alle unvordenklich alten starken Weine der Weisheit.\n\nOh meine Seele, jede Sonne goss ich auf dich und jede Nacht und jedes Schweigen und jede Sehnsucht:—da wuchsest du mir auf wie ein Weinstock.\n\nOh meine Seele, überreich und schwer stehst du nun da, ein Weinstock mit schwellenden Eutern und gedrängten braunen Gold-Weintrauben:—\n\n—gedrängt und gedrückt von deinem Glücke, wartend vor Überflusse und schamhaft noch ob deines Wartens.\n\nOh meine Seele, es giebt nun nirgends eine Seele, die liebender wäre und umfangender und umfänglicher! Wo wäre Zukunft und Vergangnes näher beisammen als bei dir?\n\nOh meine Seele, ich gab dir Alles, und alle meine Hände sind an dich leer geworden:—und nun! Nun sagst du mir lächelnd und voll Schwermuth: „Wer von uns hat zu danken?—\n\n—hat der Geber nicht zu danken, dass der Nehmende nahm? Ist Schenken nicht eine Nothdurft? Ist Nehmen nicht—Erbarmen?“—\n\nOh meine Seele, ich verstehe das Lächeln deiner Schwermuth: dein Über-Reichthum selber streckt nun sehnende Hände aus!\n\nDeine Fülle blickt über brausende Meere hin und sucht und wartet; die Sehnsucht der Über-Fülle blickt aus deinem lächelnden Augen-Himmel!\n\nUnd wahrlich, oh meine Seele! Wer sähe dein Lächeln und schmelze nicht vor Thränen? Die Engel selber schmelzen vor Thränen ob der Über-Güte deines Lächelns.\n\nDeine Güte und Über-Güte ist es, die nicht klagen und weinen will: und doch sehnt sich, oh meine Seele, dein Lächeln nach Thränen und dein zitternder Mund nach Schluchzen.\n\n„Ist alles Weinen nicht ein Klagen? Und alles Klagen nicht ein Anklagen?“ Also redest du zu dir selber, und darum willst du, oh meine Seele, lieber lächeln, als dein Leid ausschütten.\n\n—in stürzende Thränen ausschütten all dein Leid über deine Fülle und über all die Drängniss des Weinstocks nach Winzer und Winzermesser!\n\nAber willst du nicht weinen, nicht ausweinen deine purpurne Schwermuth, so wirst du singen müssen, oh meine Seele!—Siehe, ich lächle selber, der ich dir solches vorhersage:\n\n—singen, mit brausendem Gesange, bis alle Meere still werden, dass sie deiner Sehnsucht zuhorchen,—\n\n—bis über stille sehnsüchtige Meere der Nachen schwebt, das güldene Wunder, um dessen Gold alle guten schlimmen wunderlichen Dinge hüpfen:—\n\n—auch vieles grosse und kleine Gethier und Alles, was leichte wunderliche Füsse hat, dass es auf veilchenblauen Pfaden laufen kann,—\n\n—hin zu dem güldenen Wunder, dem freiwilligen Nachen und zu seinem Herrn: das aber ist der Winzer, der mit diamantenem Winzermesser wartet,—\n\n—dein grosser Löser, oh meine Seele, der Namenlose—- dem zukünftige Gesänge erst Namen finden! Und wahrlich, schon duftet dein Athem nach zukünftigen Gesängen,—\n\n—schon glühst du und träumst, schon trinkst du durstig an allen tiefen klingenden Trost-Brunnen, schon ruht deine Schwermuth in der Seligkeit zukünftiger Gesänge!—\n\nOh meine Seele, nun gab ich dir Alles und auch mein Letztes, und alle meine Hände sind an dich leer geworden:— dass ich dich singen hiess , siehe, das war mein Letztes!\n\nDass ich dich singen hiess, sprich nun, sprich: wer von uns hat jetzt—zu danken?—Besser aber noch: singe mir, singe, oh meine Seele! Und mich lass danken!—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0001","text":"Oh meine Seele, ich lehrte dich „Heute“ sagen wie „Einst“ und „Ehemals“ und über alles Hier und Da und Dort deinen Reigen hinweg tanzen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0002","text":"Oh meine Seele, ich erlöste dich von allen Winkeln, ich kehrte Staub, Spinnen und Zwielicht von dir ab.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0003","text":"Oh meine Seele, ich wusch die kleine Scham und die Winkel-Tugend von dir ab und überredete dich, nackt vor den Augen der Sonne zu stehn.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0004","text":"Mit dem Sturme, welcher „Geist“ heisst, blies ich über deine wogende See; alle Wolken blies ich davon, ich erwürgte selbst die Würgerin, die „Sünde“ heisst.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0005","text":"Oh meine Seele, ich gab dir das Recht, Nein zu sagen wie der Sturm und Ja zu sagen wie offner Himmel Ja sagt: still wie Licht stehst du und gehst du nun durch verneinende Stürme.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0006","text":"Oh meine Seele, ich gab dir die Freiheit zurück über Erschaffnes und Unerschaffnes: und wer kennt, wie du sie kennst, die Wollust des Zukünftigen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0007","text":"Oh meine Seele, ich lehrte dich das Verachten, das nicht wie ein Wurmfrass kommt, das grosse, das liebende Verachten, welches am meisten liebt, wo es am meisten verachtet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0008","text":"Oh meine Seele, ich lehrte dich so überreden, dass du zu dir die Gründe selber überredest: der Sonne gleich, die das Meer noch zu seiner Höhe überredet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0009","text":"Oh meine Seele, ich nahm von dir alles Gehorchen Kniebeugen und Herr-Sagen; ich gab dir selber den Namen „Wende der Noth“ und „Schicksal“.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0010","text":"Oh meine Seele, ich gab dir neue Namen und bunte Spielwerke, ich hiess dich „Schicksal“ und „Umfang der Umfänge“ und „Nabelschnur der Zeit“ und „azurne Glocke“ .","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0011","text":"Oh meine Seele, deinem Erdreich gab ich alle Weisheit zu trinken, alle neuen Weine und auch alle unvordenklich alten starken Weine der Weisheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0012","text":"Oh meine Seele, jede Sonne goss ich auf dich und jede Nacht und jedes Schweigen und jede Sehnsucht:—da wuchsest du mir auf wie ein Weinstock.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0013","text":"Oh meine Seele, überreich und schwer stehst du nun da, ein Weinstock mit schwellenden Eutern und gedrängten braunen Gold-Weintrauben:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0014","text":"—gedrängt und gedrückt von deinem Glücke, wartend vor Überflusse und schamhaft noch ob deines Wartens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0015","text":"Oh meine Seele, es giebt nun nirgends eine Seele, die liebender wäre und umfangender und umfänglicher! Wo wäre Zukunft und Vergangnes näher beisammen als bei dir?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0016","text":"Oh meine Seele, ich gab dir Alles, und alle meine Hände sind an dich leer geworden:—und nun! Nun sagst du mir lächelnd und voll Schwermuth: „Wer von uns hat zu danken?—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0017","text":"—hat der Geber nicht zu danken, dass der Nehmende nahm? Ist Schenken nicht eine Nothdurft? Ist Nehmen nicht—Erbarmen?“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0018","text":"Oh meine Seele, ich verstehe das Lächeln deiner Schwermuth: dein Über-Reichthum selber streckt nun sehnende Hände aus!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0019","text":"Deine Fülle blickt über brausende Meere hin und sucht und wartet; die Sehnsucht der Über-Fülle blickt aus deinem lächelnden Augen-Himmel!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0020","text":"Und wahrlich, oh meine Seele! Wer sähe dein Lächeln und schmelze nicht vor Thränen? Die Engel selber schmelzen vor Thränen ob der Über-Güte deines Lächelns.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0021","text":"Deine Güte und Über-Güte ist es, die nicht klagen und weinen will: und doch sehnt sich, oh meine Seele, dein Lächeln nach Thränen und dein zitternder Mund nach Schluchzen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0022","text":"„Ist alles Weinen nicht ein Klagen? Und alles Klagen nicht ein Anklagen?“ Also redest du zu dir selber, und darum willst du, oh meine Seele, lieber lächeln, als dein Leid ausschütten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0023","text":"—in stürzende Thränen ausschütten all dein Leid über deine Fülle und über all die Drängniss des Weinstocks nach Winzer und Winzermesser!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0024","text":"Aber willst du nicht weinen, nicht ausweinen deine purpurne Schwermuth, so wirst du singen müssen, oh meine Seele!—Siehe, ich lächle selber, der ich dir solches vorhersage:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0025","text":"—singen, mit brausendem Gesange, bis alle Meere still werden, dass sie deiner Sehnsucht zuhorchen,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0026","text":"—bis über stille sehnsüchtige Meere der Nachen schwebt, das güldene Wunder, um dessen Gold alle guten schlimmen wunderlichen Dinge hüpfen:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0027","text":"—auch vieles grosse und kleine Gethier und Alles, was leichte wunderliche Füsse hat, dass es auf veilchenblauen Pfaden laufen kann,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0028","text":"—hin zu dem güldenen Wunder, dem freiwilligen Nachen und zu seinem Herrn: das aber ist der Winzer, der mit diamantenem Winzermesser wartet,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0029","text":"—dein grosser Löser, oh meine Seele, der Namenlose—- dem zukünftige Gesänge erst Namen finden! Und wahrlich, schon duftet dein Athem nach zukünftigen Gesängen,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0030","text":"—schon glühst du und träumst, schon trinkst du durstig an allen tiefen klingenden Trost-Brunnen, schon ruht deine Schwermuth in der Seligkeit zukünftiger Gesänge!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0031","text":"Oh meine Seele, nun gab ich dir Alles und auch mein Letztes, und alle meine Hände sind an dich leer geworden:— dass ich dich singen hiess , siehe, das war mein Letztes!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0032","text":"Dass ich dich singen hiess, sprich nun, sprich: wer von uns hat jetzt—zu danken?—Besser aber noch: singe mir, singe, oh meine Seele! Und mich lass danken!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0033","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"4630e26370a64f6df4330f8154a126d6ad24e634e339ab2bf44569712aee8d64","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183227450929Z_180d0fa3","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On the Great Longing","source_sha256":"4630e26370a64f6df4330f8154a126d6ad24e634e339ab2bf44569712aee8d64","sha256":"df5c7ab568e7d033fc72269fd16cb441261256bc3d1fe98091e489c1ba64cdd2","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:32:27.450953+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0001","text":"O my soul, I taught you to say “Today” as “Once” and “Formerly” and to dance your round dance over all Here and There and Yonder.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0002","text":"O my soul, I redeemed you from all corners, I swept dust, spiders, and twilight away from you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0003","text":"O my soul, I washed the little shame and the corner-virtue away from you and persuaded you to stand naked before the eyes of the sun.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0004","text":"With the storm that is called “spirit,” I blew over your billowing sea; I blew away all clouds, I even strangled the strangler that is called “sin.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0005","text":"O my soul, I gave you the right to say No like the storm and to say Yes as open heaven says Yes: still as light, you now stand and go through negating storms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0006","text":"O my soul, I gave you back freedom over what is created and what is uncreated: and who knows, as you know it, the voluptuous delight of the future?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0007","text":"O my soul, I taught you the despising that does not come like a worm-eaten decay, the great, loving despising, which loves most where it despises most.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0008","text":"O my soul, I taught you to persuade in such a way that you persuade the reasons themselves over to yourself: like the sun, which persuades even the sea to rise to its height.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0009","text":"O my soul, I took from you all obeying, kneeling, and saying-Lord; I myself gave you the name “turning of distress” and “fate.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0010","text":"O my soul, I gave you new names and colourful playthings; I called you “fate” and “circumference of circumferences” and “umbilical cord of time” and “azure bell” .","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0011","text":"O my soul, I gave your soil all wisdom to drink, all new wines and also all immemorially old, strong wines of wisdom.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0012","text":"O my soul, I poured every sun upon you and every night and every silence and every longing:—then you grew up for me like a vine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0013","text":"O my soul, over-rich and heavy you now stand there, a vine with swelling udders and densely clustered brown golden grapes:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0014","text":"—crowded and pressed by your happiness, waiting before abundance and still ashamed of your waiting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0015","text":"O my soul, there is now nowhere a soul that would be more loving and more encompassing and more capacious! Where would future and past be nearer together than with you?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0016","text":"O my soul, I gave you Everything, and all my hands have become empty upon you:—and now! Now you say to me, smiling and full of melancholy: “Which of us has to give thanks?—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0017","text":"—does not the giver have to give thanks that the receiver received? Is giving not a necessity? Is taking not—compassion?”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0018","text":"O my soul, I understand the smile of your melancholy: your over-abundance itself now stretches forth longing hands!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0019","text":"Your fullness looks out over roaring seas and seeks and waits; the longing of over-fullness looks out from your smiling eye-heaven!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0020","text":"And verily, O my soul! Who would see your smile and not melt into tears? The angels themselves melt into tears at the over-goodness of your smile.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0021","text":"It is your goodness and over-goodness that does not wish to complain and weep: and yet, O my soul, your smile longs for tears and your trembling mouth for sobs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0022","text":"“Is not all weeping a complaining? And all complaining not an accusing?” Thus you speak to yourself, and therefore, O my soul, you would rather smile than pour out your suffering.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0023","text":"—pour out all your suffering in falling tears over your fullness and over all the vine’s urgency for vine-grower and vine-grower’s knife!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0024","text":"But if you will not weep, not weep out your purple melancholy, then you will have to sing, O my soul!—Behold, I myself smile, I who foretell such things to you:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0025","text":"—sing, with roaring song, until all seas grow still, so that they may hearken to your longing,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0026","text":"—until over still longing seas the boat glides, the golden wonder, around whose gold all good, wicked, wondrous things leap:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0027","text":"—also much great and small creaturehood and everything that has light wondrous feet, so that it can run upon violet-blue paths,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0028","text":"—toward the golden wonder, the voluntary boat and its lord: but that is the vintner, who waits with diamond vintner's knife,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0029","text":"—your great deliverer, O my soul, the nameless one— whom future songs shall first find names for! And verily, already your breath is fragrant with future songs,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0030","text":"—already you glow and dream, already you drink thirstily at all deep, sounding wells of consolation, already your melancholy rests in the bliss of future songs!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0031","text":"O my soul, now I gave you Everything and also my Last, and all my hands have become empty for you:— that I bade you sing, behold, that was my Last!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0032","text":"That I bade you sing, now speak, speak: which of us has now—to give thanks?—But better still: sing to me, sing, O my soul! And let me give thanks!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0033","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDGS:P0033"]}],"source_digest":"4bdb23d2aec60df2ba968bb108568f0247e864fb179d2065f82f5506558a2e5e","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von der grossen Sehnsucht"}],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"Von der grossen Sehnsucht","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10","title":"1.","order":115,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_1_10","text":"## 1.\n\n„In dein Auge schaute ich jüngst, oh Leben: Gold sah ich in deinem Nacht-Auge blinken,—mein Herz stand still vor dieser Wollust:\n\n—einen goldenen Kahn sah ich blinken auf mächtigen Gewässern, einen sinkenden, trinkenden, wieder winkenden goldenen Schaukel-Kahn!\n\nNach meinem Fusse, dem tanzwüthigen, warfst du einen Blick, einen lachenden fragenden schmelzenden Schaukel-Blick:\n\nZwei Mal nur regtest du deine Klapper mit kleinen Händen—da schaukelte schon mein Fuss vor Tanz-Wuth.—\n\nMeine Fersen bäumten sich, meine Zehen horchten, dich zu verstehen: trägt doch der Tänzer sein Ohr—in seinen Zehen!\n\nZu dir hin sprang ich: da flohst du zurück vor meinem Sprunge; und gegen mich züngelte deines fliehenden fliegenden Haars Zunge!\n\nVon dir weg sprang ich und von deinen Schlangen: da standst du schon, halbgewandt, das Auge voll Verlangen.\n\nMit krummen Blicken—lehrst du mich krumme Bahnen; auf krummen Bahnen lernt mein Fuss—Tücken!\n\nIch fürchte dich Nahe, ich liebe dich Ferne; deine Flucht lockt mich, dein Suchen stockt mich:—ich leide, aber was litt ich um dich nicht gerne!\n\nDeren Kälte zündet, deren Hass verführt, deren Flucht bindet, deren Spott—rührt:\n\n—wer hasste dich nicht, dich grosse Binderin, Umwinderin, Versucherin, Sucherin, Finderin! Wer liebte dich nicht, dich unschuldige, ungeduldige, windseilige, kindsäugige Sünderin!\n\nWohin ziehst du mich jetzt, du Ausbund und Unband? Und jetzt fliehst du mich wieder, du süsser Wildfang und Undank!\n\nIch tanze dir nach, ich folge dir auch auf geringer Spur. Wo bist du? Gieb mir die Hand! Oder einen Finger nur!\n\nHier sind Höhlen und Dickichte: wir werden uns verirren!—Halt! Steh still! Siehst du nicht Eulen und Fledermäuse schwirren?\n\nDu Eule! Du Fledermaus! Du willst mich äffen? Wo sind wir? Von den Hunden lerntest du diess Heulen und Kläffen.\n\nDu fletschest mich lieblich an mit weissen Zähnlein, deine bösen Augen springen gegen mich aus lockichtem Mähnlein!\n\nDas ist ein Tanz über Stock und Stein: ich bin der Jäger,—willst du mein Hund oder meine Gemse sein?\n\nJetzt neben mir! Und geschwind, du boshafte Springerin! Jetzt hinauf! Und hinüber!—Wehe! Da fiel ich selber im Springen hin!\n\nOh sieh mich liegen, du Übermuth, und um Gnade flehn! Gerne möchte ich mit dir —lieblichere Pfade gehn!\n\n—der Liebe Pfade durch stille bunte Büsche! Oder dort den See entlang: da schwimmen und tanzen Goldfische!\n\nDu bist jetzt müde? Da drüben sind Schafe und Abendröthen: ist es nicht schön, zu schlafen, wenn Schäfer flöten?\n\nDu bist so arg müde? Ich trage dich hin, lass nur die Arme sinken! Und hast du Durst,—ich hätte wohl Etwas, aber dein Mund will es nicht trinken!—\n\n—Oh diese verfluchte flinke gelenke Schlange und Schlupf-Hexe! Wo bist du hin? Aber im Gesicht fühle ich von deiner Hand zwei Tupfen und rothe Klexe!\n\nIch bin es wahrlich müde, immer dein schafichter Schäfer zu sein! Du Hexe, habe ich dir bisher gesungen, nun sollst du mir—schrein!\n\nNach dem Takt meiner Peitsche sollst du mir tanzen und schrein! Ich vergass doch die Peitsche nicht?—Nein!“—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0002","text":"„In dein Auge schaute ich jüngst, oh Leben: Gold sah ich in deinem Nacht-Auge blinken,—mein Herz stand still vor dieser Wollust:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0003","text":"—einen goldenen Kahn sah ich blinken auf mächtigen Gewässern, einen sinkenden, trinkenden, wieder winkenden goldenen Schaukel-Kahn!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0004","text":"Nach meinem Fusse, dem tanzwüthigen, warfst du einen Blick, einen lachenden fragenden schmelzenden Schaukel-Blick:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0005","text":"Zwei Mal nur regtest du deine Klapper mit kleinen Händen—da schaukelte schon mein Fuss vor Tanz-Wuth.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0006","text":"Meine Fersen bäumten sich, meine Zehen horchten, dich zu verstehen: trägt doch der Tänzer sein Ohr—in seinen Zehen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0007","text":"Zu dir hin sprang ich: da flohst du zurück vor meinem Sprunge; und gegen mich züngelte deines fliehenden fliegenden Haars Zunge!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0008","text":"Von dir weg sprang ich und von deinen Schlangen: da standst du schon, halbgewandt, das Auge voll Verlangen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0009","text":"Mit krummen Blicken—lehrst du mich krumme Bahnen; auf krummen Bahnen lernt mein Fuss—Tücken!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0010","text":"Ich fürchte dich Nahe, ich liebe dich Ferne; deine Flucht lockt mich, dein Suchen stockt mich:—ich leide, aber was litt ich um dich nicht gerne!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0011","text":"Deren Kälte zündet, deren Hass verführt, deren Flucht bindet, deren Spott—rührt:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0012","text":"—wer hasste dich nicht, dich grosse Binderin, Umwinderin, Versucherin, Sucherin, Finderin! Wer liebte dich nicht, dich unschuldige, ungeduldige, windseilige, kindsäugige Sünderin!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0013","text":"Wohin ziehst du mich jetzt, du Ausbund und Unband? Und jetzt fliehst du mich wieder, du süsser Wildfang und Undank!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0014","text":"Ich tanze dir nach, ich folge dir auch auf geringer Spur. Wo bist du? Gieb mir die Hand! Oder einen Finger nur!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0015","text":"Hier sind Höhlen und Dickichte: wir werden uns verirren!—Halt! Steh still! Siehst du nicht Eulen und Fledermäuse schwirren?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0016","text":"Du Eule! Du Fledermaus! Du willst mich äffen? Wo sind wir? Von den Hunden lerntest du diess Heulen und Kläffen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0017","text":"Du fletschest mich lieblich an mit weissen Zähnlein, deine bösen Augen springen gegen mich aus lockichtem Mähnlein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0018","text":"Das ist ein Tanz über Stock und Stein: ich bin der Jäger,—willst du mein Hund oder meine Gemse sein?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0019","text":"Jetzt neben mir! Und geschwind, du boshafte Springerin! Jetzt hinauf! Und hinüber!—Wehe! Da fiel ich selber im Springen hin!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0020","text":"Oh sieh mich liegen, du Übermuth, und um Gnade flehn! Gerne möchte ich mit dir —lieblichere Pfade gehn!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0021","text":"—der Liebe Pfade durch stille bunte Büsche! Oder dort den See entlang: da schwimmen und tanzen Goldfische!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0022","text":"Du bist jetzt müde? Da drüben sind Schafe und Abendröthen: ist es nicht schön, zu schlafen, wenn Schäfer flöten?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0023","text":"Du bist so arg müde? Ich trage dich hin, lass nur die Arme sinken! Und hast du Durst,—ich hätte wohl Etwas, aber dein Mund will es nicht trinken!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0024","text":"—Oh diese verfluchte flinke gelenke Schlange und Schlupf-Hexe! Wo bist du hin? Aber im Gesicht fühle ich von deiner Hand zwei Tupfen und rothe Klexe!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0025","text":"Ich bin es wahrlich müde, immer dein schafichter Schäfer zu sein! Du Hexe, habe ich dir bisher gesungen, nun sollst du mir—schrein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0026","text":"Nach dem Takt meiner Peitsche sollst du mir tanzen und schrein! Ich vergass doch die Peitsche nicht?—Nein!“—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"6b04a56021a0aa8757e8373ca8967408588d0fe424349c81f7db4d47aa63a4ef","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183239809207Z_457ff686","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1.","source_sha256":"6b04a56021a0aa8757e8373ca8967408588d0fe424349c81f7db4d47aa63a4ef","sha256":"224b8fdee206da6c1aa07651f0acc49be3f85b625d68d455b13c0fafa942ada0","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:32:39.809233+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0002","text":"“Into your eye I looked lately, O Life: I saw gold gleam in your night-eye,—my heart stood still before this delight:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0003","text":"—I saw a golden boat gleam upon mighty waters, a sinking, drinking, again beckoning golden rocking-boat!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0004","text":"At my foot, frenzied for dancing, you cast a glance, a laughing, questioning, melting rocking-glance:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0005","text":"Only twice did you stir your rattle with little hands—then already my foot rocked with dance-fury.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0006","text":"My heels reared, my toes listened, to understand you: for the dancer bears his ear—in his toes!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0007","text":"Toward you I sprang: then you fled back from my leap; and against me flickered the tongue of your fleeing, flying hair!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0008","text":"Away from you I sprang and from your serpents: then you were already standing there, half-turned, your eye full of longing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0009","text":"With crooked glances—you teach me crooked paths; on crooked paths my foot learns—cunning!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0010","text":"I fear you near, I love you far; your flight lures me, your seeking arrests me:—I suffer, but what would I not gladly suffer for your sake!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0011","text":"Whose cold kindles, whose hatred seduces, whose flight binds, whose mockery—moves:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0012","text":"—who did not hate you, you great binder, enwraptress, temptress, seeker, finder! Who did not love you, you innocent, impatient, wind-swift, child-eyed sinner!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0013","text":"Where are you drawing me now, you paragon and unbridled one? And now you flee me again, you sweet wild imp and ingrate!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0014","text":"I dance after you, I follow you even on the faintest track. Where are you? Give me your hand! Or only one finger!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0015","text":"Here are caves and thickets: we shall lose our way!—Halt! Stand still! Do you not see owls and bats whirring?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0016","text":"You owl! You bat! You want to make a fool of me? Where are we? You learned this howling and barking from the dogs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0017","text":"You bare your little white teeth lovingly at me, your wicked eyes leap out at me from your curly little mane!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0018","text":"This is a dance over stick and stone: I am the hunter,—will you be my dog or my chamois?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0019","text":"Now beside me! And quickly, you malicious leaper! Now up! And over!—Alas! There I myself fell down while leaping!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0020","text":"Oh, see me lying here, you overweening one, and begging for mercy! Gladly would I go with you —along more lovely paths!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0021","text":"—the paths of love through quiet, variegated bushes! Or there along the lake: golden fish swim and dance there!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0022","text":"Are you weary now? Over there are sheep and evening glows: is it not lovely to sleep when shepherds play their flutes?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0023","text":"Are you so terribly weary? I shall carry you there, only let your arms sink! And if you are thirsty,—I might well have something, but your mouth will not drink it!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0024","text":"—Oh, this accursed quick, supple serpent and slinking witch! Where have you gone? But upon my face I feel from your hand two dots and red smears!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0025","text":"Truly, I am weary of always being your sheepish shepherd! You witch, I have sung to you until now; now you shall—scream for me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_10:P0026","text":"To the beat of my whip you shall dance and scream for me! Surely I did not forget the whip?—No!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_10:P0026"]}],"source_digest":"8634e65b5431186838a3147bf37ee9a4a3d37a2714668d60252c79ca9158e7b6","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Other Dance Song","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"The Other Dance Song · 1","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","The Other Dance Song","1."],"original_title":"1."}],"source_title_context":"Das andere Tanzlied","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Das andere Tanzlied · 1","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Das andere Tanzlied","1."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"1.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10","title":"2.","order":116,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_2_10","text":"## 2.\n\nDa antwortete mir das Leben also und hielt sich dabei die zierlichen Ohren zu:\n\n„Oh Zarathustra! Klatsche doch nicht so fürchterlich mit deiner Peitsche! Du weisst es ja: Lärm mordet Gedanken,—und eben kommen mir so zärtliche Gedanken.\n\nWir sind Beide zwei rechte Thunichtgute und Thunichtböse. Jenseits von Gut und Böse fanden wir unser Eiland und unsre grüne Wiese—wir Zwei allein! Darum müssen wir schon einander gut sein!\n\nUnd lieben wir uns auch nicht von Grund aus—, muss man sich denn gram sein, wenn man sich nicht von Grund aus liebt?\n\nUnd dass ich dir gut bin und oft zu gut, Das weisst du: und der Grund ist, dass ich auf deine Weisheit eifersüchtig bin. Ah, diese tolle alte Närrin von Weisheit!\n\nWenn dir deine Weisheit einmal davonliefe, ach! da liefe dir schnell auch meine Liebe noch davon.“—\n\nDarauf blickte das Leben nachdenklich hinter sich und um sich und sagte leise: „Oh Zarathustra, du bist mir nicht treu genug!\n\nDu liebst mich lange nicht so sehr wie du redest; ich weiss, du denkst daran, dass du mich bald verlassen willst.\n\nEs giebt eine alte schwere schwere Brumm-Glocke: die brummt Nachts bis zu deiner Höhle hinauf:—\n\n—hörst du diese Glocke Mitternachts die Stunde schlagen, so denkst du zwischen Eins und Zwölf daran—\n\n—du denkst daran, oh Zarathustra, ich weiss es, dass du mich bald verlassen willst!“—\n\n„Ja, antwortete ich zögernd, aber du weisst es auch—“ Und ich sagte ihr Etwas in’s Ohr, mitten hinein zwischen ihre verwirrten gelben thörichten Haar-Zotteln.\n\nDu weisst Das, oh Zarathustra? Das weiss Niemand.—\n\nUnd wir sahen uns an und blickten auf die grüne Wiese, über welche eben der kühle Abend lief, und weinten mit einander.—Damals aber war mir das Leben lieber, als je alle meine Weisheit.—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0002","text":"Da antwortete mir das Leben also und hielt sich dabei die zierlichen Ohren zu:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0003","text":"„Oh Zarathustra! Klatsche doch nicht so fürchterlich mit deiner Peitsche! Du weisst es ja: Lärm mordet Gedanken,—und eben kommen mir so zärtliche Gedanken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0004","text":"Wir sind Beide zwei rechte Thunichtgute und Thunichtböse. Jenseits von Gut und Böse fanden wir unser Eiland und unsre grüne Wiese—wir Zwei allein! Darum müssen wir schon einander gut sein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0005","text":"Und lieben wir uns auch nicht von Grund aus—, muss man sich denn gram sein, wenn man sich nicht von Grund aus liebt?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0006","text":"Und dass ich dir gut bin und oft zu gut, Das weisst du: und der Grund ist, dass ich auf deine Weisheit eifersüchtig bin. Ah, diese tolle alte Närrin von Weisheit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0007","text":"Wenn dir deine Weisheit einmal davonliefe, ach! da liefe dir schnell auch meine Liebe noch davon.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0008","text":"Darauf blickte das Leben nachdenklich hinter sich und um sich und sagte leise: „Oh Zarathustra, du bist mir nicht treu genug!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0009","text":"Du liebst mich lange nicht so sehr wie du redest; ich weiss, du denkst daran, dass du mich bald verlassen willst.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0010","text":"Es giebt eine alte schwere schwere Brumm-Glocke: die brummt Nachts bis zu deiner Höhle hinauf:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0011","text":"—hörst du diese Glocke Mitternachts die Stunde schlagen, so denkst du zwischen Eins und Zwölf daran—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0012","text":"—du denkst daran, oh Zarathustra, ich weiss es, dass du mich bald verlassen willst!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0013","text":"„Ja, antwortete ich zögernd, aber du weisst es auch—“ Und ich sagte ihr Etwas in’s Ohr, mitten hinein zwischen ihre verwirrten gelben thörichten Haar-Zotteln.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0014","text":"Du weisst Das, oh Zarathustra? Das weiss Niemand.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0015","text":"Und wir sahen uns an und blickten auf die grüne Wiese, über welche eben der kühle Abend lief, und weinten mit einander.—Damals aber war mir das Leben lieber, als je alle meine Weisheit.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0016","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"ab3104bdf2846c9f9ec9baaadfb3ec4e8aa97bc566fead6d2e7adaa5cd02a51f","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183247755489Z_918ad88d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2.","source_sha256":"ab3104bdf2846c9f9ec9baaadfb3ec4e8aa97bc566fead6d2e7adaa5cd02a51f","sha256":"7fca335fac2af5e8547acc8831f7c9a5a937bd52c9e2c257af66e2659cf9bbaf","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:32:47.755499+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_10:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0002","text":"Then Life answered me thus, and in doing so held its dainty ears shut:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_10:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0003","text":"“O Zarathustra! Do not crack your whip so terribly! You know it well: noise murders thoughts,—and just now such tender thoughts are coming to me.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_10:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0004","text":"“We are both two true good-for-nothings and do-no-evils. Beyond good and evil we found our island and our green meadow—we two alone! Therefore we must be good to one another!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_10:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0005","text":"“And even if we do not love one another from the ground up—must one then be angry when one does not love from the ground up?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_10:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0006","text":"“And that I am good to you, and often too good, you know: and the reason is that I am jealous of your wisdom. Ah, that mad old fool of wisdom!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_10:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0007","text":"“If your wisdom should ever run away from you, alas! then my love too would quickly run away from you.”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_10:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0008","text":"Thereupon Life looked pensively behind itself and about itself and said softly: “O Zarathustra, you are not faithful enough to me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_10:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0009","text":"“You do not love me nearly as much as you speak of doing; I know that you are thinking of soon leaving me.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_10:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0010","text":"“There is an old, heavy, heavy humming bell: it hums up to your cave at night:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_10:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0011","text":"—when you hear this bell strike the hour of midnight, you think between One and Twelve of this—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_10:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0012","text":"—you think of this, O Zarathustra, I know it, that you will soon leave me!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_10:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0013","text":"“Yes,” I answered hesitantly, “but you know it too—” And I said Something into her ear, right amidst her tangled yellow foolish locks of hair.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_10:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0014","text":"“You know That, O Zarathustra? Nobody knows that.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_10:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0015","text":"And we looked at one another and gazed upon the green meadow, over which the cool evening was just passing, and wept together.—But then Life was dearer to me than all my wisdom had ever been.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_10:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_10:P0016","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_10:P0016"]}],"source_digest":"76c24283175d3d227ffce272c06ea082c820e89412e6b6fd38bed70f4c0d8ffe","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Other Dance Song","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"The Other Dance Song · 2","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","The Other Dance Song","2."],"original_title":"2."}],"source_title_context":"Das andere Tanzlied","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Das andere Tanzlied · 2","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Das andere Tanzlied","2."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"2.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_5","title":"3.","order":117,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_3C_5","text":"## 3.\n\nEins! Oh Mensch! Gieb Acht! Zwei! Was spricht die tiefe Mitternacht? Drei! „Ich schlief, ich schlief—,“ Vier! „Auf tiefen Traum bin ich erwacht:—“ Fünf! „Die Welt ist tief,“ Sechs! „Und tiefer als der Tag gedacht.“ Sieben! „Tief ist ihr Weh—,“ Acht! „Lust—tiefer noch als Herzeleid:“ Neun! „Weh spricht: Vergeh!“ Zehn! „Doch alle Lust will Ewigkeit—,“ Elf! „—will tiefe, tiefe Ewigkeit!“ Zwölf!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_5:P0001","text":"## 3.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_5:P0002","text":"Eins! Oh Mensch! Gieb Acht! Zwei! Was spricht die tiefe Mitternacht? Drei! „Ich schlief, ich schlief—,“ Vier! „Auf tiefen Traum bin ich erwacht:—“ Fünf! „Die Welt ist tief,“ Sechs! „Und tiefer als der Tag gedacht.“ Sieben! „Tief ist ihr Weh—,“ Acht! „Lust—tiefer noch als Herzeleid:“ Neun! „Weh spricht: Vergeh!“ Zehn! „Doch alle Lust will Ewigkeit—,“ Elf! „—will tiefe, tiefe Ewigkeit!“ Zwölf!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"9f55570c6c5cb52726de39aee1420718b5fe83a30dad5a335a040c377888c474","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183250445110Z_5be307d5","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"3.","source_sha256":"9f55570c6c5cb52726de39aee1420718b5fe83a30dad5a335a040c377888c474","sha256":"b446cbfad50a6f1b04fe5912b1c3a7a33ec2e9b763cdf386e5df206a727ccf43","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:32:50.445120+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_5:P0001","text":"## 3.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_5:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_5:P0002","text":"One! O man! Give heed! Two! What does deep midnight speak? Three! “I slept, I slept—,” Four! “From a deep dream I awoke:—” Five! “The world is deep,” Six! “And deeper than day has thought.” Seven! “Deep is its woe—,” Eight! “Joy—deeper still than heartache:” Nine! “Woe speaks: Pass away!” Ten! “Yet all joy wills eternity—,” Eleven! “—wills deep, deep eternity!” Twelve!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_5:P0002"]}],"source_digest":"2cd1f751b3d04024c969a9b4a989cbf5e215f0a796d00cdb037ac090af5a874f","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Other Dance Song","source_section_label":"3.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"The Other Dance Song · 3","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","The Other Dance Song","3."],"original_title":"3."}],"source_title_context":"Das andere Tanzlied","source_section_label":"3.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Das andere Tanzlied · 3","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Das andere Tanzlied","3."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"3.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_11","title":"1.","order":118,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_1_11","text":"## (Oder: das Ja- und Amen-Lied)\n\n## 1.\n\nWenn ich ein Wahrsager bin und voll jenes wahrsagerischen Geistes, der auf hohem Joche zwischen zwei Meeren wandelt,—\n\nzwischen Vergangenem und Zukünftigem als schwere Wolke wandelt,—schwülen Niederungen feind und Allem, was müde ist und nicht sterben, noch leben kann.-\n\nzum Blitze bereit im dunklen Busen und zum erlösenden Lichtstrahle, schwanger von Blitzen, die Ja! sagen, Ja! lachen, zu wahrsagerischen Blitzstrahlen:—\n\n—selig aber ist der also Schwangere! Und wahrlich, lange muss als schweres Wetter am Berge hängen, wer einst das Licht der Zukunft zünden soll!—\n\nOh wie sollte ich nicht nach der Ewigkeit brünstig sein und nach dem hochzeitlichen Ring der Ringe,—dem Ring de Wiederkunft!\n\nNie noch fand ich das Weib, von dem ich Kinder mochte, sei denn dieses Weib, das ich lieb: denn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!\n\nDenn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_11:P0001","text":"## (Oder: das Ja- und Amen-Lied)","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_11:P0002","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_11:P0003","text":"Wenn ich ein Wahrsager bin und voll jenes wahrsagerischen Geistes, der auf hohem Joche zwischen zwei Meeren wandelt,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_11:P0004","text":"zwischen Vergangenem und Zukünftigem als schwere Wolke wandelt,—schwülen Niederungen feind und Allem, was müde ist und nicht sterben, noch leben kann.-","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_11:P0005","text":"zum Blitze bereit im dunklen Busen und zum erlösenden Lichtstrahle, schwanger von Blitzen, die Ja! sagen, Ja! lachen, zu wahrsagerischen Blitzstrahlen:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_11:P0006","text":"—selig aber ist der also Schwangere! Und wahrlich, lange muss als schweres Wetter am Berge hängen, wer einst das Licht der Zukunft zünden soll!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_11:P0007","text":"Oh wie sollte ich nicht nach der Ewigkeit brünstig sein und nach dem hochzeitlichen Ring der Ringe,—dem Ring de Wiederkunft!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_11:P0008","text":"Nie noch fand ich das Weib, von dem ich Kinder mochte, sei denn dieses Weib, das ich lieb: denn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_11:P0009","text":"Denn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"25f7f6581f71b225d4a5d2666270203e42a41c35cea986e2ba6158a29ef6962f","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183254626528Z_f4bf0e46","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1.","source_sha256":"25f7f6581f71b225d4a5d2666270203e42a41c35cea986e2ba6158a29ef6962f","sha256":"7f944267978241728f67db4662052ca0bbba4dfad2303df22005c9d386d3987c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:32:54.626552+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_11:P0001","text":"## (Or: the Yes- and Amen-Song)","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_11:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_11:P0002","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_11:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_11:P0003","text":"If I am a soothsayer and full of that soothsaying spirit which walks on a high yoke between two seas,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_11:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_11:P0004","text":"walks as a heavy cloud between what is past and what is to come,—hostile to sultry lowlands and to all that is weary and can neither die nor yet live.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_11:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_11:P0005","text":"ready for lightning in its dark breast and for the redeeming ray of light, pregnant with lightnings that say Yes! that laugh Yes, with soothsaying flashes of lightning:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_11:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_11:P0006","text":"—but blessed is one thus pregnant! And verily, long must he hang upon the mountain as heavy weather who is someday to kindle the light of the future!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_11:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_11:P0007","text":"Oh, how should I not be ardent for eternity and for the nuptial ring of rings,—the ring of return!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_11:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_11:P0008","text":"Never yet have I found the woman from whom I desired children, unless it be this woman whom I love: for I love thee, O eternity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_11:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_11:P0009","text":"For I love thee, O eternity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_11:P0009"]}],"source_digest":"af4bde31cce961768f1d815ef866457df86cf7f28892185b025a4c045c4471a7","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Seven Seals","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"The Seven Seals · 1","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","The Seven Seals","1."],"original_title":"1."}],"source_title_context":"Die sieben Siegel","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Die sieben Siegel · 1","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Die sieben Siegel","1."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"1.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_11","title":"2.","order":119,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_2_11","text":"## 2.\n\nWenn mein Zorn je Gräber brach, Grenzsteine rückte und alte Tafeln zerbrochen in steile Tiefen rollte:\n\nWenn mein Hohn je vermoderte Worte zerblies, und ich wie ein Besen kam den Kreuzspinnen und als Fegewind alten verdumpften Grabkammern:\n\nWenn ich je frohlockend sass, wo alte Götter begraben liegen, weltsegnend, weltliebend neben den Denkmalen alter Welt-Verleumder:—\n\n—denn selbst Kirchen und Gottes-Gräber liebe ich, wenn der Himmel erst reinen Auges durch ihre zerbrochenen Decken blickt; gern sitze ich gleich Gras und rothem Mohne auf zerbrochnen Kirchen—\n\nOh wie sollte ich nicht nach der Ewigkeit brünstig sein und nach dem hochzeitlichen Ring der Ringe,—dem Ring de Wiederkunft!\n\nNie noch fand ich das Weib, von dem ich Kinder mochte, sei denn dieses Weib, das ich lieb: denn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!\n\nDenn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_11:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_11:P0002","text":"Wenn mein Zorn je Gräber brach, Grenzsteine rückte und alte Tafeln zerbrochen in steile Tiefen rollte:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_11:P0003","text":"Wenn mein Hohn je vermoderte Worte zerblies, und ich wie ein Besen kam den Kreuzspinnen und als Fegewind alten verdumpften Grabkammern:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_11:P0004","text":"Wenn ich je frohlockend sass, wo alte Götter begraben liegen, weltsegnend, weltliebend neben den Denkmalen alter Welt-Verleumder:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_11:P0005","text":"—denn selbst Kirchen und Gottes-Gräber liebe ich, wenn der Himmel erst reinen Auges durch ihre zerbrochenen Decken blickt; gern sitze ich gleich Gras und rothem Mohne auf zerbrochnen Kirchen—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_11:P0006","text":"Oh wie sollte ich nicht nach der Ewigkeit brünstig sein und nach dem hochzeitlichen Ring der Ringe,—dem Ring de Wiederkunft!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_11:P0007","text":"Nie noch fand ich das Weib, von dem ich Kinder mochte, sei denn dieses Weib, das ich lieb: denn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_11:P0008","text":"Denn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"4a50d546a76c139a4cbb1a03923f3bac2e9bd8d772d579eef9ae1ab4a82199a1","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183258732285Z_474542d2","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2.","source_sha256":"4a50d546a76c139a4cbb1a03923f3bac2e9bd8d772d579eef9ae1ab4a82199a1","sha256":"ef0c9bca0e67f12b43fdd105b46d45887e0de4fe2b3e37afdbc82dd98bf9f107","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:32:58.732306+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_11:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_11:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_11:P0002","text":"If ever my wrath broke graves, shifted boundary stones, and rolled old tablets, shattered, into steep depths:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_11:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_11:P0003","text":"If ever my scorn blew away mouldering words, and I came like a broom to the cross-spiders and as a sweeping wind to old musty burial chambers:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_11:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_11:P0004","text":"If ever I sat exultantly where old gods lie buried, world-blessing, world-loving beside the monuments of old world-slanderers:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_11:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_11:P0005","text":"—for I love even churches and God’s graves, when only heaven gazes with pure eye through their broken ceilings; gladly I sit, like grass and red poppy, upon broken churches—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_11:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_11:P0006","text":"Oh, how should I not ardently desire eternity and the nuptial ring of rings,—the ring of recurrence!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_11:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_11:P0007","text":"Never yet have I found the woman by whom I might wish to have children, save this woman whom I love: for I love you, oh eternity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_11:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_11:P0008","text":"For I love you, oh eternity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_11:P0008"]}],"source_digest":"b4295f8171579e08ca45a0e938a2b372fbf0223bdcc5c4eec3f3bd44ee36ae8c","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Seven Seals","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"The Seven Seals · 2","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","The Seven Seals","2."],"original_title":"2."}],"source_title_context":"Die sieben Siegel","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Die sieben Siegel · 2","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Die sieben Siegel","2."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"2.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_6","title":"3.","order":120,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_3C_6","text":"## 3.\n\nWenn je ein Hauch zu mir kam vom schöpferischen Hauche und von jener himmlischen Noth, die noch Zufälle zwingt, Sternen-Reigen zu tanzen:\n\nWenn ich je mit dem Lachen des schöpferischen Blitzes lachte, dem der lange Donner der That grollend, aber gehorsam nachfolgt:\n\nWenn ich je am Göttertisch der Erde mit Göttern Würfel spielte, dass die Erde bebte und brach und Feuerflüsse heraufschnob:—\n\n—denn ein Göttertisch ist die Erde, und zitternd von schöpferischen neuen Worten und Götter-Würfen:—\n\nOh wie sollte ich nicht nach der Ewigkeit brünstig sein und nach dem hochzeitlichen Ring der Ringe,—dem Ring de Wiederkunft!\n\nNie noch fand ich das Weib, von dem ich Kinder mochte, sei denn dieses Weib, das ich lieb: denn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!\n\nDenn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0001","text":"## 3.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0002","text":"Wenn je ein Hauch zu mir kam vom schöpferischen Hauche und von jener himmlischen Noth, die noch Zufälle zwingt, Sternen-Reigen zu tanzen:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0003","text":"Wenn ich je mit dem Lachen des schöpferischen Blitzes lachte, dem der lange Donner der That grollend, aber gehorsam nachfolgt:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0004","text":"Wenn ich je am Göttertisch der Erde mit Göttern Würfel spielte, dass die Erde bebte und brach und Feuerflüsse heraufschnob:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0005","text":"—denn ein Göttertisch ist die Erde, und zitternd von schöpferischen neuen Worten und Götter-Würfen:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0006","text":"Oh wie sollte ich nicht nach der Ewigkeit brünstig sein und nach dem hochzeitlichen Ring der Ringe,—dem Ring de Wiederkunft!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0007","text":"Nie noch fand ich das Weib, von dem ich Kinder mochte, sei denn dieses Weib, das ich lieb: denn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0008","text":"Denn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"0a4e082a14589e9db0ba817b2f43723581f96f7e37f2817b5e184a02a9b246d4","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183302703212Z_236b0c7b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"3.","source_sha256":"0a4e082a14589e9db0ba817b2f43723581f96f7e37f2817b5e184a02a9b246d4","sha256":"c2b4cac667107aaf6d806e9923dc5c02f75b76218557a1ff6eff434ed751a6b2","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:33:02.703222+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0001","text":"## 3.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0002","text":"If ever a breath came to me of the creative breath and of that heavenly necessity which compels even accidents to dance star-dances:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0003","text":"If ever I laughed with the laughter of creative lightning, which the long thunder of the deed follows, grumbling but obedient:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0004","text":"If ever at the gods’ table of the earth I played dice with gods, so that the earth trembled and broke and snorted up streams of fire:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0005","text":"—for the earth is a table of the gods, and trembling with creative new words and throws of the gods’ dice:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0006","text":"Oh, how should I not be ardent for eternity and for the nuptial ring of rings,—the ring of recurrence!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0007","text":"Never yet have I found the woman by whom I desired children, save this woman whom I love: for I love you, oh eternity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0008","text":"For I love you, oh eternity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_6:P0008"]}],"source_digest":"14331d4aa61b23b7aba1892835359713f5b288a8a59394a644ae8b3d3a4e8cf5","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Seven Seals","source_section_label":"3.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"The Seven Seals · 3","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","The Seven Seals","3."],"original_title":"3."}],"source_title_context":"Die sieben Siegel","source_section_label":"3.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Die sieben Siegel · 3","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Die sieben Siegel","3."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"3.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_3","title":"4.","order":121,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_4C_3","text":"## 4.\n\nWenn ich je vollen Zuges trank aus jenem schäumenden Würz- und Mischkruge, in dem alle Dinge gut gemischt sind:\n\nWenn meine Hand je Fernstes zum Nächsten goss und Feuer zu Geist und Lust zu Leid und Schlimmstes zum Gütigsten:\n\nWenn ich selber ein Korn bin von jenem erlösenden Salze, welches macht, dass alle Dinge im Mischkruge gut sich mischen:—\n\n—denn es giebt ein Salz, das Gutes mit Bösem bindet; und auch das Böseste ist zum Würzen würdig und zum letzten Überschäumen:—\n\nOh wie sollte ich nicht nach der Ewigkeit brünstig sein und nach dem hochzeitlichen Ring der Ringe,—dem Ring de Wiederkunft!\n\nNie noch fand ich das Weib, von dem ich Kinder mochte, sei denn dieses Weib, das ich lieb: denn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!\n\nDenn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0001","text":"## 4.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0002","text":"Wenn ich je vollen Zuges trank aus jenem schäumenden Würz- und Mischkruge, in dem alle Dinge gut gemischt sind:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0003","text":"Wenn meine Hand je Fernstes zum Nächsten goss und Feuer zu Geist und Lust zu Leid und Schlimmstes zum Gütigsten:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0004","text":"Wenn ich selber ein Korn bin von jenem erlösenden Salze, welches macht, dass alle Dinge im Mischkruge gut sich mischen:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0005","text":"—denn es giebt ein Salz, das Gutes mit Bösem bindet; und auch das Böseste ist zum Würzen würdig und zum letzten Überschäumen:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0006","text":"Oh wie sollte ich nicht nach der Ewigkeit brünstig sein und nach dem hochzeitlichen Ring der Ringe,—dem Ring de Wiederkunft!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0007","text":"Nie noch fand ich das Weib, von dem ich Kinder mochte, sei denn dieses Weib, das ich lieb: denn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0008","text":"Denn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"d6fefd566b30eaf9c8ec874bb9e2b10a5616f03b3171a8aee8a69e382eebb774","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183306898764Z_09011f1f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"4.","source_sha256":"d6fefd566b30eaf9c8ec874bb9e2b10a5616f03b3171a8aee8a69e382eebb774","sha256":"3684727d56498845df40e2deb4f687aa77e8fc2adc4b11848568039d6b6c2ada","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:33:06.898784+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0001","text":"## 4.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0002","text":"If ever I drank in full draughts from that foaming spice- and mixing-bowl in which all things are well mixed:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0003","text":"If ever my hand poured what was furthest into what was nearest, and fire into spirit and pleasure into pain and what was worst into what was kindest:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0004","text":"If I myself am a grain of that redeeming salt which causes all things to mix well in the mixing-bowl:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0005","text":"—for there is a salt that binds good with evil; and even the most evil is worthy of seasoning and of the final overflowing:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0006","text":"Oh, how should I not be ardent for eternity and for the nuptial ring of rings,—the ring of recurrence!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0007","text":"Never yet did I find the woman by whom I might have children, unless it be this woman whom I love: for I love thee, O eternity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0008","text":"For I love thee, O eternity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_3:P0008"]}],"source_digest":"70d5b2a7eb9bde77b006d25033a8c6b5d1528ad3547586205548e4398bc679ed","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Seven Seals","source_section_label":"4.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"The Seven Seals · 4","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","The Seven Seals","4."],"original_title":"4."}],"source_title_context":"Die sieben Siegel","source_section_label":"4.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Die sieben Siegel · 4","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Die sieben Siegel","4."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"4.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_3","title":"5.","order":122,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_5C_3","text":"## 5.\n\nWenn ich dem Meere hold bin und Allem, was Meeres-Art ist, und am holdesten noch, wenn es mir zornig widerspricht:\n\nWenn jene suchende Lust in mir ist, die nach Unentdecktem die Segel treibt, wenn eine Seefahrer-Lust in meiner Lust ist:\n\nWenn je mein Frohlocken rief: „die Küste schwand,—nun fiel mir die letzte Kette ab—\n\n—das Grenzenlose braust um mich, weit hinaus glänzt mir Raum und Zeit, wohlan! wohlauf! altes Herz!“—\n\nOh wie sollte ich nicht nach der Ewigkeit brünstig sein und nach dem hochzeitlichen Ring der Ringe,—dem Ring de Wiederkunft!\n\nNie noch fand ich das Weib, von dem ich Kinder mochte, sei denn dieses Weib, das ich lieb: denn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!\n\nDenn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0001","text":"## 5.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0002","text":"Wenn ich dem Meere hold bin und Allem, was Meeres-Art ist, und am holdesten noch, wenn es mir zornig widerspricht:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0003","text":"Wenn jene suchende Lust in mir ist, die nach Unentdecktem die Segel treibt, wenn eine Seefahrer-Lust in meiner Lust ist:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0004","text":"Wenn je mein Frohlocken rief: „die Küste schwand,—nun fiel mir die letzte Kette ab—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0005","text":"—das Grenzenlose braust um mich, weit hinaus glänzt mir Raum und Zeit, wohlan! wohlauf! altes Herz!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0006","text":"Oh wie sollte ich nicht nach der Ewigkeit brünstig sein und nach dem hochzeitlichen Ring der Ringe,—dem Ring de Wiederkunft!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0007","text":"Nie noch fand ich das Weib, von dem ich Kinder mochte, sei denn dieses Weib, das ich lieb: denn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0008","text":"Denn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"72b9b2f798f2086919a3711fc2d80bb0bb34f6977309fd5b27dae6ce4c1556f6","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183310922350Z_6d146397","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"5.","source_sha256":"72b9b2f798f2086919a3711fc2d80bb0bb34f6977309fd5b27dae6ce4c1556f6","sha256":"adf942629c6c2125893f87708706a3bdfbf1a106de5e1d4ce07ecb1e4e6a9f34","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:33:10.922372+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0001","text":"## 5.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0002","text":"If I am fond of the sea and of all that is sea-kind, and fondest of all when it angrily contradicts me:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0003","text":"If that seeking desire is in me which drives the sails toward the undiscovered, if a seafarer’s desire is in my desire:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0004","text":"If ever my jubilation cried: “the coast vanished,—now the last chain fell from me—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0005","text":"—the boundless roars around me, space and time gleam far out before me, well then! up and away! old heart!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0006","text":"Oh, how should I not be ardent for eternity and for the nuptial ring of rings,—the ring of recurrence!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0007","text":"Never yet have I found the woman by whom I might have children, unless it be this woman whom I love: for I love you, oh eternity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0008","text":"For I love you, oh eternity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_3:P0008"]}],"source_digest":"7804ce6eef0b8a1c3f1b11db19c37b630aa6212a83916ac0e12c5299e1fc8f61","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Seven Seals","source_section_label":"5.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"The Seven Seals · 5","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","The Seven Seals","5."],"original_title":"5."}],"source_title_context":"Die sieben Siegel","source_section_label":"5.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Die sieben Siegel · 5","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Die sieben Siegel","5."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"5.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_3","title":"6.","order":123,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_6C_3","text":"## 6.\n\nWenn meine Tugend eines Tänzers Tugend ist, und ich oft mit beiden Füssen in gold-smaragdenes Entzücken sprang:\n\nWenn meine Bosheit eine lachende Bosheit ist, heimisch unter Rosenhängen und Lilien-Hecken:\n\n—im Lachen nämlich ist alles Böse bei einander, aber heilig- und losgesprochen durch seine eigne Seligkeit:—\n\nUnd wenn Das mein A und O ist, dass alles Schwere leicht, aller Leib Tänzer, aller Geist Vogel werde: und wahrlich, Das ist mein A und O!—\n\nOh wie sollte ich nicht nach der Ewigkeit brünstig sein und nach dem hochzeitlichen Ring der Ringe,—dem Ring de Wiederkunft!\n\nNie noch fand ich das Weib, von dem ich Kinder mochte, sei denn dieses Weib, das ich lieb: denn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!\n\nDenn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0001","text":"## 6.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0002","text":"Wenn meine Tugend eines Tänzers Tugend ist, und ich oft mit beiden Füssen in gold-smaragdenes Entzücken sprang:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0003","text":"Wenn meine Bosheit eine lachende Bosheit ist, heimisch unter Rosenhängen und Lilien-Hecken:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0004","text":"—im Lachen nämlich ist alles Böse bei einander, aber heilig- und losgesprochen durch seine eigne Seligkeit:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0005","text":"Und wenn Das mein A und O ist, dass alles Schwere leicht, aller Leib Tänzer, aller Geist Vogel werde: und wahrlich, Das ist mein A und O!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0006","text":"Oh wie sollte ich nicht nach der Ewigkeit brünstig sein und nach dem hochzeitlichen Ring der Ringe,—dem Ring de Wiederkunft!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0007","text":"Nie noch fand ich das Weib, von dem ich Kinder mochte, sei denn dieses Weib, das ich lieb: denn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0008","text":"Denn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"56a2a72d9722978a5997efe831cfc0b3498b4c26a48b454e4fe3d553598419a1","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183314603472Z_1b51b054","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"6.","source_sha256":"56a2a72d9722978a5997efe831cfc0b3498b4c26a48b454e4fe3d553598419a1","sha256":"6b8271a09c3c4e38bae1abcf4c6e7931fa36ef6b095219565f4466f07e277a95","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:33:14.603484+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0001","text":"## 6.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0002","text":"If my virtue is a dancer’s virtue, and I have often leapt with both feet into golden-emerald delight:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0003","text":"If my malice is a laughing malice, at home beneath slopes of roses and hedges of lilies:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0004","text":"—for in laughter all evil is together, but sanctified and absolved by its own blessedness:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0005","text":"And if this is my A and O, that all heaviness become light, every body a dancer, every spirit a bird: and verily, this is my A and O!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0006","text":"Oh, how should I not be ardent for eternity and for the nuptial ring of rings,—the ring of recurrence!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0007","text":"Never yet have I found the woman by whom I might have children, unless it be this woman whom I love: for I love you, oh eternity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0008","text":"For I love you, oh eternity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_3:P0008"]}],"source_digest":"483ac4b62af2086d539647adece067a367eb4cc57b5835a2bd88a8148b298dba","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Seven Seals","source_section_label":"6.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"The Seven Seals · 6","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","The Seven Seals","6."],"original_title":"6."}],"source_title_context":"Die sieben Siegel","source_section_label":"6.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Die sieben Siegel · 6","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Die sieben Siegel","6."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"6.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_3","title":"7.","order":124,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_7C_3","text":"## 7.\n\nWenn ich je stille Himmel über mir ausspannte und mit eignen Flügeln in eigne Himmel flog:\n\nWenn ich spielend in tiefen Licht-Fernen schwamm, und meiner Freiheit Vogel-Weisheit kam:—\n\n—so aber spricht Vogel-Weisheit: „Siehe, es giebt kein Oben, kein Unten! Wirf dich umher, hinaus, zurück, du Leichter! Singe! sprich nicht mehr!\n\n—sind alle Worte nicht für die Schweren gemacht? Lügen dem Leichten nicht alle Worte! Singe! sprich nicht mehr!“—\n\nOh wie sollte ich nicht nach der Ewigkeit brünstig sein und nach dem hochzeitlichen Ring der Ringe,—dem Ring de Wiederkunft!\n\nNie noch fand ich das Weib, von dem ich Kinder mochte, sei denn dieses Weib, das ich lieb: denn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!\n\nDenn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0001","text":"## 7.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0002","text":"Wenn ich je stille Himmel über mir ausspannte und mit eignen Flügeln in eigne Himmel flog:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0003","text":"Wenn ich spielend in tiefen Licht-Fernen schwamm, und meiner Freiheit Vogel-Weisheit kam:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0004","text":"—so aber spricht Vogel-Weisheit: „Siehe, es giebt kein Oben, kein Unten! Wirf dich umher, hinaus, zurück, du Leichter! Singe! sprich nicht mehr!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0005","text":"—sind alle Worte nicht für die Schweren gemacht? Lügen dem Leichten nicht alle Worte! Singe! sprich nicht mehr!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0006","text":"Oh wie sollte ich nicht nach der Ewigkeit brünstig sein und nach dem hochzeitlichen Ring der Ringe,—dem Ring de Wiederkunft!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0007","text":"Nie noch fand ich das Weib, von dem ich Kinder mochte, sei denn dieses Weib, das ich lieb: denn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0008","text":"Denn ich liebe dich, oh Ewigkeit!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"9586bb95d895aedc0998a2d725fda4c0ccea0957818f1bd93cd13209362cba15","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183318308818Z_23e1c2d4","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"7.","source_sha256":"9586bb95d895aedc0998a2d725fda4c0ccea0957818f1bd93cd13209362cba15","sha256":"6b978419e67f2f122f2875a8cd9ac5de863a075c149a37c30563807de4024a73","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:33:18.308828+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0001","text":"## 7.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0002","text":"Whenever I spread tranquil heavens above myself and flew with my own wings into my own heavens:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0003","text":"Whenever I swam playfully in deep distances of light, and the bird-wisdom of my freedom came:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0004","text":"—thus, however, bird-wisdom speaks: “Behold, there is no Above, no Below! Throw yourself about, out, back, you light one! Sing! speak no more!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0005","text":"—are not all words made for the heavy? Do not all words lie to the light one! Sing! speak no more!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0006","text":"Oh, how should I not be ardent for eternity and for the nuptial ring of rings,—the ring of recurrence!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0007","text":"Never yet have I found the woman by whom I wished for children, unless it be this woman whom I love: for I love you, oh eternity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0008","text":"For I love you, oh eternity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_3:P0008"]}],"source_digest":"3d7676cd9191a4680235b629bb4dea66fb79ddfe65e50847a1224a88f7a4ef7c","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Seven Seals","source_section_label":"7.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Three"],"reading_title":"The Seven Seals · 7","reading_title_context":"Part Three","reading_heading_titles":["Part Three","The Seven Seals","7."],"original_title":"7."}],"source_title_context":"Die sieben Siegel","source_section_label":"7.","source_title_hierarchy":["Dritter Theil"],"reading_title":"Die sieben Siegel · 7","reading_title_context":"Dritter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Dritter Theil","Die sieben Siegel","7."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"7.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VULT","title":"Part Four and Last","order":125,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VULT","text":"## Vierter und letzter Theil\n\nAch, wo in der Welt geschahen grössere Thorheiten, als bei den Mitleidigen? Und was in der Weit stiftete mehr Leid, als die Thorheiten der Mitleidigen? Wehe allen Liebenden, die nicht noch eine Höhe haben, welche über ihrem Mitleiden ist! Also sprach der Teufel einst zu mir: „auch Gott hat seine Hölle: das ist seine Liebe zu den Menschen.“ Und jüngst hörte ich ihn diess Wort sagen: „Gott ist todt; an seinem Mitleiden mit den Menschen ist Gott gestorben.“\n\nZarathustra, Von den Mitleidigen","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VULT:P0001","text":"## Vierter und letzter Theil","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VULT:P0002","text":"Ach, wo in der Welt geschahen grössere Thorheiten, als bei den Mitleidigen? Und was in der Weit stiftete mehr Leid, als die Thorheiten der Mitleidigen? Wehe allen Liebenden, die nicht noch eine Höhe haben, welche über ihrem Mitleiden ist! Also sprach der Teufel einst zu mir: „auch Gott hat seine Hölle: das ist seine Liebe zu den Menschen.“ Und jüngst hörte ich ihn diess Wort sagen: „Gott ist todt; an seinem Mitleiden mit den Menschen ist Gott gestorben.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VULT:P0003","text":"Zarathustra, Von den Mitleidigen","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"f01d2d549f44f192399adaeef3c2300d013eeeb71fd392e7f936c1dc84d2eca2","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183320848259Z_94ae49d9","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Four and Last","source_sha256":"f01d2d549f44f192399adaeef3c2300d013eeeb71fd392e7f936c1dc84d2eca2","sha256":"b28258a8ef8879b619751fd807334c816c4440f977d732e728317becbc0cb2aa","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:33:20.848283+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VULT:P0001","text":"## Fourth and final part","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VULT:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VULT:P0002","text":"Ah, where in the world were greater follies committed than among the compassionate? And what in the world caused more suffering than the follies of the compassionate? Woe to all lovers who do not yet have a height that is above their compassion! Thus the devil once spoke to me: “God too has his hell: that is his love for human beings.” And recently I heard him speak this word: “God is dead; God died of his compassion for human beings.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VULT:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VULT:P0003","text":"Zarathustra, On the Compassionate","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VULT:P0003"]}],"source_digest":"47de06171a1f7a8ba2c7f8f48c8b0647c8e65f450b79a4a9b7433915652eb93c","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Vierter und letzter Theil"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Vierter und letzter Theil","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0","title":"The Honey Sacrifice","order":126,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_DHO0","text":"## Das Honig-Opfer\n\n—Und wieder liefen Monde und Jahre über Zarathustra’s Seele, und er achtete dessen nicht; sein Haar aber wurde weiss. Eines Tages, als er auf einem Steine vor seiner Höhle sass und still hinausschaute,—man schaut aber dort auf das Meer hinaus, und hinweg über gewundene Abgründe—da giengen seine Thiere nachdenklich um ihn herum und stellten sich endlich vor ihn hin.\n\n„Oh Zarathustra, sagten sie, schaust du wohl aus nach deinem Glücke?“—„Was liegt am Glücke! antwortete er, ich trachte lange nicht mehr nach Glücke, ich trachte nach meinem Werke.“—„Oh Zarathustra, redeten die Thiere abermals, Das sagst du als Einer, der des Guten übergenug hat. Liegst du nicht in einem himmelblauen See von Glück?“—„Ihr Schalks-Narren, antwortete Zarathustra und lächelte, wie gut wähltet ihr das Gleichniss! Aber ihr wisst auch, dass mein Glück schwer ist und nicht wie eine flüssige Wasserwelle: es drängt mich und will nicht von mir und thut gleich geschmolzenem Peche.“—\n\nDa giengen die Thiere wieder nachdenklich um ihn herum und stellten sich dann abermals vor ihn hin. „Oh Zarathustra, sagten sie, daher also kommt es, dass du selber immer gelber und dunkler wirst, obschon dein Haar weiss und flächsern aussehen will? Siehe doch, du sitzest in deinem Peche!“—„Was sagt ihr da, meine Thiere, sagte Zarathustra und lachte dazu, wahrlich, ich lästerte als ich von Peche sprach. Wie mir geschieht, so geht es allen Früchten, die reif werden. Es ist der Honig in meinen Adern, der mein Blut dicker und auch meine Seele stiller macht.“—„So wird es sein, oh Zarathustra, antworteten die Thiere und drängten sich an ihn; willst du aber nicht heute auf einen hohen Berg steigen? Die Luft ist rein, und man sieht heute mehr von der Welt als jemals.“—„Ja, meine Thiere, antwortete er, ihr rathet trefflich und mir nach dem Herzen: ich will heute auf einen hohen Berg steigen! Aber sorgt, dass dort Honig mir zur Hand sei, gelber, weisser, guter, eisfrischer Waben-Goldhonig. Denn wisset, ich will droben das Honig-Opfer bringen.“—\n\nAls Zarathustra aber oben auf der Höhe war, sandte er die Thiere heim, die ihn geleitet hatten, und fand, dass er nunmehr allein sei:—da lachte er aus ganzem Herzen, sah sich um und sprach also:\n\nDass ich von Opfern sprach und Honig-Opfern, eine List war’s nur meiner Rede und, wahrlich, eine nützliche Thorheit! Hier oben darf ich schon freier reden, als vor Einsiedler-Höhlen und Einsiedler-Hausthieren.\n\nWas opfern! Ich verschwende, was mir geschenkt wird, ich Verschwender mit tausend Händen: wie dürfte ich Das noch—Opfern heissen!\n\nUnd als ich nach Honig begehrte, begehrte ich nur nach Köder und süssem Seime und Schleime, nach dem auch Brummbären und wunderliche mürrische böse Vögel die Zunge lecken:\n\n—nach dem besten Köder, wie er Jägern und Fischfängern noththut. Denn wenn die Welt wie ein dunkler Thierwald ist und aller wilden Jäger Lustgarten, so dünkt sie mich noch mehr und lieber ein abgründliches reiches Meer,\n\n—ein Meer voll bunter Fische und Krebse, nach dem es auch Götter gelüsten möchte, dass sie an ihm zu Fischern würden und zu Netz-Auswerfern: so reich ist die Welt an Wunderlichem, grossem und kleinem!\n\nSonderlich die Menschen-Welt, das Menschen-Meer:—nach dem werfe ich nun meine goldene Angelruthe aus und spreche: thue dich auf, du Menschen-Abgrund!\n\nThue dich auf und wirf mir deine Fische und Glitzer-Krebse zu! Mit meinem besten Köder ködere ich mir heute die wunderlichsten Menschen-Fische!\n\n—mein Glück selber werfe ich hinaus in alle Weiten und Fernen, zwischen Aufgang, Mittag und Niedergang, ob nicht an meinem Glücke viele Menschen-Fische zerrn und zappeln lernen.\n\nBis sie, anbeissend an meine spitzen verborgenen Haken, hinauf müssen in meine Höhe, die buntesten Abgrund-Gründlinge zu dem boshaftigsten aller Menschen- Fischfänger.\n\nDer nämlich bin ich von Grund und Anbeginn, ziehend, heranziehend, hinaufziehend, aufziehend, ein Zieher, Züchter und Zuchtmeister, der sich nicht umsonst einstmals zusprach: „Werde, der du bist!“\n\nAlso mögen nunmehr die Menschen zu mir hinauf kommen: denn noch warte ich der Zeichen, dass es Zeit sei zu meinem Niedergange, noch gehe ich selber nicht unter, wie ich muss, unter Menschen.\n\nDazu warte ich hier, listig und spöttisch auf hohen Bergen, kein Ungeduldiger, kein Geduldiger, vielmehr Einer, der auch die Geduld verlernt hat,—weil er nicht mehr „duldet.“\n\nMein Schicksal nämlich lässt mir Zeit: es vergass mich wohl? Oder sitzt es hinter einem grossen Steine im Schatten und fängt Fliegen?\n\nUnd wahrlich, ich bin ihm gut darob, meinem ewigen Schicksale, dass es mich nicht hetzt und drängt und mir Zeit zu Possen lässt und Bosheiten: also dass ich heute zu einem Fischfange auf diesen hohen Berg stieg.\n\nFieng wohl je ein Mensch auf hohen Bergen Fische? Und wenn es auch eine Thorheit ist, was ich hier oben will und treibe: besser noch Diess, als dass ich da unten feierlich würde vor Warten und grün und gelb—\n\n—ein gespreitzter Zornschnauber vor Warten, ein heiliger Heule-Sturm aus Bergen, ein Ungeduldiger, der in die Thäler hinabruft: „Hört, oder ich peitsche euch mit der Geissel Gottes!“\n\nNicht dass ich solchen Zürnern darob gram würde: zum Lachen sind sie mir gut genung! Ungeduldig müssen sie schon sein, diese grossen Lärmtrommeln, welche heute oder niemals zu Worte kommen!\n\nIch aber und mein Schicksal—wir reden nicht zum Heute, wir reden auch nicht zum Niemals: wir haben zum Reden schon Geduld und Zeit und Überzeit. Denn einst muss er doch kommen und darf nicht vorübergehn.\n\nWer muss einst kommen und darf nicht vorübergehn? Unser grosser Hazar, das ist unser grosses fernes Menschen-Reich, das Zarathustra-Reich von tausend Jahren—\n\nWie ferne mag solches „Ferne“ sein? was geht’s mich an! Aber darum steht es mir doch nicht minder fest—, mit beiden Füssen stehe ich sicher auf diesem Grunde,\n\n—auf einem ewigen Grunde, auf hartem Urgesteine, auf diesem höchsten härtesten Urgebirge, zu dem alle Winde kommen als zur Wetterscheide, fragend nach Wo? und Woher? und Wohinaus?\n\nHier lache, lache meine helle heile Bosheit! Von hohen Bergen wirf hinab dein glitzerndes Spott-Gelächter! Ködere mit deinem Glitzern mir die schönsten Menschen-Fische!\n\nUnd was in allen Meeren mir zugehört, mein An-und-für-mich in allen Dingen— Das fische mir heraus, Das führe zu mir herauf: dess warte ich, der boshaftigste aller Fischfänger.\n\nHinaus, hinaus, meine Angel! Hinein, hinab, Köder meines Glücks! Träufle deinen süssesten Thau, mein Herzens-Honig! Beisse, meine Angel, in den Bauch aller schwarzen Trübsal!\n\nHinaus, hinaus, mein Auge! Oh welche vielen Meere rings um mich, welch dämmernde Menschen-Zukünfte! Und über mir—welch rosenrothe Stille! Welch entwölktes Schweigen!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0001","text":"## Das Honig-Opfer","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0002","text":"—Und wieder liefen Monde und Jahre über Zarathustra’s Seele, und er achtete dessen nicht; sein Haar aber wurde weiss. Eines Tages, als er auf einem Steine vor seiner Höhle sass und still hinausschaute,—man schaut aber dort auf das Meer hinaus, und hinweg über gewundene Abgründe—da giengen seine Thiere nachdenklich um ihn herum und stellten sich endlich vor ihn hin.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0003","text":"„Oh Zarathustra, sagten sie, schaust du wohl aus nach deinem Glücke?“—„Was liegt am Glücke! antwortete er, ich trachte lange nicht mehr nach Glücke, ich trachte nach meinem Werke.“—„Oh Zarathustra, redeten die Thiere abermals, Das sagst du als Einer, der des Guten übergenug hat. Liegst du nicht in einem himmelblauen See von Glück?“—„Ihr Schalks-Narren, antwortete Zarathustra und lächelte, wie gut wähltet ihr das Gleichniss! Aber ihr wisst auch, dass mein Glück schwer ist und nicht wie eine flüssige Wasserwelle: es drängt mich und will nicht von mir und thut gleich geschmolzenem Peche.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0004","text":"Da giengen die Thiere wieder nachdenklich um ihn herum und stellten sich dann abermals vor ihn hin. „Oh Zarathustra, sagten sie, daher also kommt es, dass du selber immer gelber und dunkler wirst, obschon dein Haar weiss und flächsern aussehen will? Siehe doch, du sitzest in deinem Peche!“—„Was sagt ihr da, meine Thiere, sagte Zarathustra und lachte dazu, wahrlich, ich lästerte als ich von Peche sprach. Wie mir geschieht, so geht es allen Früchten, die reif werden. Es ist der Honig in meinen Adern, der mein Blut dicker und auch meine Seele stiller macht.“—„So wird es sein, oh Zarathustra, antworteten die Thiere und drängten sich an ihn; willst du aber nicht heute auf einen hohen Berg steigen? Die Luft ist rein, und man sieht heute mehr von der Welt als jemals.“—„Ja, meine Thiere, antwortete er, ihr rathet trefflich und mir nach dem Herzen: ich will heute auf einen hohen Berg steigen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0005","text":"Aber sorgt, dass dort Honig mir zur Hand sei, gelber, weisser, guter, eisfrischer Waben-Goldhonig. Denn wisset, ich will droben das Honig-Opfer bringen.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0006","text":"Als Zarathustra aber oben auf der Höhe war, sandte er die Thiere heim, die ihn geleitet hatten, und fand, dass er nunmehr allein sei:—da lachte er aus ganzem Herzen, sah sich um und sprach also:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0007","text":"Dass ich von Opfern sprach und Honig-Opfern, eine List war’s nur meiner Rede und, wahrlich, eine nützliche Thorheit! Hier oben darf ich schon freier reden, als vor Einsiedler-Höhlen und Einsiedler-Hausthieren.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0008","text":"Was opfern! Ich verschwende, was mir geschenkt wird, ich Verschwender mit tausend Händen: wie dürfte ich Das noch—Opfern heissen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0009","text":"Und als ich nach Honig begehrte, begehrte ich nur nach Köder und süssem Seime und Schleime, nach dem auch Brummbären und wunderliche mürrische böse Vögel die Zunge lecken:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0010","text":"—nach dem besten Köder, wie er Jägern und Fischfängern noththut. Denn wenn die Welt wie ein dunkler Thierwald ist und aller wilden Jäger Lustgarten, so dünkt sie mich noch mehr und lieber ein abgründliches reiches Meer,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0011","text":"—ein Meer voll bunter Fische und Krebse, nach dem es auch Götter gelüsten möchte, dass sie an ihm zu Fischern würden und zu Netz-Auswerfern: so reich ist die Welt an Wunderlichem, grossem und kleinem!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0012","text":"Sonderlich die Menschen-Welt, das Menschen-Meer:—nach dem werfe ich nun meine goldene Angelruthe aus und spreche: thue dich auf, du Menschen-Abgrund!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0013","text":"Thue dich auf und wirf mir deine Fische und Glitzer-Krebse zu! Mit meinem besten Köder ködere ich mir heute die wunderlichsten Menschen-Fische!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0014","text":"—mein Glück selber werfe ich hinaus in alle Weiten und Fernen, zwischen Aufgang, Mittag und Niedergang, ob nicht an meinem Glücke viele Menschen-Fische zerrn und zappeln lernen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0015","text":"Bis sie, anbeissend an meine spitzen verborgenen Haken, hinauf müssen in meine Höhe, die buntesten Abgrund-Gründlinge zu dem boshaftigsten aller Menschen- Fischfänger.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0016","text":"Der nämlich bin ich von Grund und Anbeginn, ziehend, heranziehend, hinaufziehend, aufziehend, ein Zieher, Züchter und Zuchtmeister, der sich nicht umsonst einstmals zusprach: „Werde, der du bist!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0017","text":"Also mögen nunmehr die Menschen zu mir hinauf kommen: denn noch warte ich der Zeichen, dass es Zeit sei zu meinem Niedergange, noch gehe ich selber nicht unter, wie ich muss, unter Menschen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0018","text":"Dazu warte ich hier, listig und spöttisch auf hohen Bergen, kein Ungeduldiger, kein Geduldiger, vielmehr Einer, der auch die Geduld verlernt hat,—weil er nicht mehr „duldet.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0019","text":"Mein Schicksal nämlich lässt mir Zeit: es vergass mich wohl? Oder sitzt es hinter einem grossen Steine im Schatten und fängt Fliegen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0020","text":"Und wahrlich, ich bin ihm gut darob, meinem ewigen Schicksale, dass es mich nicht hetzt und drängt und mir Zeit zu Possen lässt und Bosheiten: also dass ich heute zu einem Fischfange auf diesen hohen Berg stieg.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0021","text":"Fieng wohl je ein Mensch auf hohen Bergen Fische? Und wenn es auch eine Thorheit ist, was ich hier oben will und treibe: besser noch Diess, als dass ich da unten feierlich würde vor Warten und grün und gelb—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0022","text":"—ein gespreitzter Zornschnauber vor Warten, ein heiliger Heule-Sturm aus Bergen, ein Ungeduldiger, der in die Thäler hinabruft: „Hört, oder ich peitsche euch mit der Geissel Gottes!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0023","text":"Nicht dass ich solchen Zürnern darob gram würde: zum Lachen sind sie mir gut genung! Ungeduldig müssen sie schon sein, diese grossen Lärmtrommeln, welche heute oder niemals zu Worte kommen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0024","text":"Ich aber und mein Schicksal—wir reden nicht zum Heute, wir reden auch nicht zum Niemals: wir haben zum Reden schon Geduld und Zeit und Überzeit. Denn einst muss er doch kommen und darf nicht vorübergehn.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0025","text":"Wer muss einst kommen und darf nicht vorübergehn? Unser grosser Hazar, das ist unser grosses fernes Menschen-Reich, das Zarathustra-Reich von tausend Jahren—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0026","text":"Wie ferne mag solches „Ferne“ sein? was geht’s mich an! Aber darum steht es mir doch nicht minder fest—, mit beiden Füssen stehe ich sicher auf diesem Grunde,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0027","text":"—auf einem ewigen Grunde, auf hartem Urgesteine, auf diesem höchsten härtesten Urgebirge, zu dem alle Winde kommen als zur Wetterscheide, fragend nach Wo? und Woher? und Wohinaus?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0028","text":"Hier lache, lache meine helle heile Bosheit! Von hohen Bergen wirf hinab dein glitzerndes Spott-Gelächter! Ködere mit deinem Glitzern mir die schönsten Menschen-Fische!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0029","text":"Und was in allen Meeren mir zugehört, mein An-und-für-mich in allen Dingen— Das fische mir heraus, Das führe zu mir herauf: dess warte ich, der boshaftigste aller Fischfänger.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0030","text":"Hinaus, hinaus, meine Angel! Hinein, hinab, Köder meines Glücks! Träufle deinen süssesten Thau, mein Herzens-Honig! Beisse, meine Angel, in den Bauch aller schwarzen Trübsal!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0031","text":"Hinaus, hinaus, mein Auge! Oh welche vielen Meere rings um mich, welch dämmernde Menschen-Zukünfte! Und über mir—welch rosenrothe Stille! Welch entwölktes Schweigen!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"34292be98bbe7a9f8fe38d81f7d8f632adf700da7aa030332c179f55822f3b17","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183345277149Z_fe50cbc7","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"The Honey Sacrifice","source_sha256":"34292be98bbe7a9f8fe38d81f7d8f632adf700da7aa030332c179f55822f3b17","sha256":"8b8b083b21af58f8695dca10a986f8aebad8351bf26974f15f09e9fe39a95901","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:33:45.277172+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0001","text":"## The Honey Sacrifice","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0002","text":"—And again moons and years passed over Zarathustra’s soul, and he paid no heed to it; but his hair grew white. One day, when he sat upon a stone before his cave and gazed silently outward—yet there one looks out upon the sea, and away beyond winding abysses—his animals went thoughtfully about him and at last stood before him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0003","text":"“O Zarathustra,” they said, “art thou perhaps looking out for thy happiness?”—“What matters happiness!” he answered, “I have long ceased to strive after happiness, I strive after my work.”—“O Zarathustra,” the animals spoke again, “thou sayest that as one who has more than enough of the good. Dost thou not lie in a sky-blue lake of happiness?”—“Ye rogue-fools,” Zarathustra answered and smiled, “how well ye chose the simile! But ye know also that my happiness is heavy and not like a fluid wave of water: it presses upon me and will not leave me and acts like molten pitch.”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0004","text":"Then the animals again went thoughtfully about him and then once more stood before him. “O Zarathustra,” they said, “is that then why thou thyself art always becoming yellower and darker, although thy hair would look white and flaxen? Behold, thou sittest in thy pitch!”—“What are ye saying there, my animals,” said Zarathustra, laughing as he did so, “verily, I blasphemed when I spoke of pitch. As it fares with me, so it fares with all fruits that ripen. It is the honey in my veins that makes my blood thicker and also my soul stiller.”—“So it will be, O Zarathustra,” answered the animals and pressed close to him; “but wilt thou not today climb a high mountain? The air is pure, and today one sees more of the world than ever.”—“Yes, my animals,” he answered, “ye counsel excellently and according to my heart: today I will climb a high mountain!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0005","text":"But see to it that I have honey at hand there, yellow, white, good, ice-fresh comb-gold honey. For know ye, above I will offer the honey sacrifice.”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0006","text":"But when Zarathustra was up on the height, he sent home the animals that had guided him, and found that he was now alone:—then he laughed with all his heart, looked about him, and spoke thus:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0007","text":"That I spoke of sacrifices and honey sacrifices was merely a ruse of my speech and, verily, a useful folly! Up here I may already speak more freely than before hermit-caves and hermit-house-animals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0008","text":"What sacrifice! I squander what is given to me, I, a squanderer with a thousand hands: how could I still call That—a sacrifice!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0009","text":"And when I desired honey, I desired only bait and sweet sap and slime, for which even grizzly bears and strange sullen wicked birds lick their tongues:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0010","text":"—for the best bait, such as hunters and fish-catchers need. For if the world is like a dark animal-forest and the pleasure-garden of all wild hunters, it seems to me still more and more dearly like an abysmal, rich sea,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0011","text":"—a sea full of colourful fishes and crabs, such that even gods might desire to become fishers and net-casters upon it: so rich is the world in the strange, great and small!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0012","text":"Especially the human world, the human sea:—into that I now cast my golden fishing-rod and say: open thyself, thou human abyss!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0013","text":"Open thyself and cast up to me thy fishes and glittering crabs! With my best bait I bait for myself today the strangest human-fishes!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0014","text":"—my happiness itself I cast out into all breadths and distances, between rising, noon, and setting, to see whether many human-fishes may learn to tug and wriggle at my happiness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0015","text":"Until, biting at my sharp hidden hooks, they must come up to my height, the most colourful abyss-bottom-dwellers, to the most malicious of all human-fish-catchers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0016","text":"For that indeed am I from ground and beginning, drawing, drawing toward me, drawing upward, rearing up, a drawer, breeder, and disciplinarian, who did not in vain once say to himself: “Become what thou art!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0017","text":"Thus may human beings now come up to me: for I still await the signs that it is time for my descent; I do not yet myself go under, as I must, among human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0018","text":"For that I wait here, cunning and mocking upon high mountains, no impatient man, no patient man, rather One who has also unlearned patience,—because he no longer “suffers.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0019","text":"For my destiny gives me time: has it perhaps forgotten me? Or does it sit behind a great stone in the shade and catch flies?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0020","text":"And verily, I am grateful to it for that, to my eternal destiny, that it does not hound and press me and leaves me time for pranks and wickedness: so that today I climbed this high mountain for a fishing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0021","text":"Did any human being ever catch fishes on high mountains? And even if what I will and do up here is a folly: it is still better than that I should become solemn down there from waiting, and green and yellow—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0022","text":"—a puffed-out snorter of wrath from waiting, a holy howling storm from the mountains, an impatient man who calls down into the valleys: “Hear, or I shall scourge you with the whip of God!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0023","text":"Not that I should be angry with such wrathful men on that account: they are good enough for laughter to me! Impatient they must indeed be, these great drums of noise, which today or never come to speech!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0024","text":"But I and my destiny—we do not speak to Today, neither do we speak to Never: we have patience and time and over-time enough for speaking. For one day he must yet come and may not pass by.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0025","text":"Who must one day come and may not pass by? Our great Hazar, that is our great distant human realm, the Zarathustra realm of a thousand years—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0026","text":"How distant may such “distance” be? what is it to me! Yet it stands no less firm for me on that account—, with both feet I stand securely upon this ground,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0027","text":"—upon an eternal ground, upon hard primordial rock, upon this highest, hardest primordial mountain range, to which all winds come as to the weather divide, asking Whither? and Whence? and Out where?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0028","text":"Here laugh, laugh, my bright, wholesome malice! From high mountains cast down your glittering laughter of mockery! With your glittering, lure me the most beautiful human-fish!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0029","text":"And whatever belongs to me in all seas, my in-and-for-itself in all things— Fish that out for me, bring that up to me: for this I wait, I, the most malicious of all fishers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0030","text":"Out, out, my fishing-rod! In, down, bait of my happiness! Drip your sweetest dew, honey of my heart! Bite, my fishing-rod, into the belly of all black melancholy!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0031","text":"Out, out, my eye! Oh, what many seas around me, what dawning human futures! And above me—what rosy silence! What cloudless stillness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHO0:P0031"]}],"source_digest":"77f3fa7b61daa21e017fa22bb2e80bac9d86bac5b3d08d950719a8c9bee33aa3","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Das Honig-Opfer"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Das Honig-Opfer","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN","title":"The Cry of Distress","order":127,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_DERN","text":"Des nächsten Tages sass Zarathustra wieder auf seinem Steine vor der Höhle, während die Thiere draussen in der Welt herumschweiften, dass sie neue Nahrung heimbrächten,—auch neuen Honig: denn Zarathustra hatte den alten Honig bis auf das letzte Korn verthan und verschwendet. Als er aber dermaassen dasass, mit einem Stecken in der Hand, und den Schatten seiner Gestalt auf der Erde abzeichnete, nachdenkend und, wahrlich! nicht über sich und seinen Schatten—da erschrak er mit Einem Male und fuhr zusammen: denn er sahe neben seinem Schatten noch einen andern Schatten. Und wie er schnell um sich blickte und aufstand, siehe, da stand der Wahrsager neben ihm, der selbe, den er einstmals an seinem Tische gespeist und getränkt hatte, der Verkündiger der grossen Müdigkeit, welcher lehrte: „Alles ist gleich, es lohnt sich Nichts, Welt ist ohne Sinn, Wissen würgt.“ Aber sein Antlitz hatte sich inzwischen verwandelt; und als ihm Zarathustra in die Augen blickte, wurde sein Herz abermals erschreckt: so viel schlimme Verkündigungen und aschgraue Blitze liefen über diess Gesicht.\n\nDer Wahrsager, der es wahrgenommen, was sich in Zarathustra’s Seele zutrug, wischte mit der Hand über sein Antlitz hin, wie als ob er dasselbe wegwischen wollte; desgleichen that auch Zarathustra. Und als Beide dergestalt sich schweigend gefasst und gekräftigt hatten, gaben sie sich die Hände, zum Zeichen, dass sie sich wiedererkennen wollten.\n\n„Sei mir willkommen, sagte Zarathustra, du Wahrsager der grossen Müdigkeit, du sollst nicht umsonst einstmals mein Tisch- und Gastfreund gewesen sein. Iss und trink auch heute bei mir und vergieb es, dass ein vergnügter alter Mann mit dir zu Tische sitzt!“—„Ein vergnügter alter Mann? antwortete der Wahrsager, den Kopf schüttelnd: wer du aber auch bist oder sein willst, oh Zarathustra, du bist es zum Längsten hier Oben gewesen,—dein Nachen soll über Kurzem nicht mehr im Trocknen sitzen!“—„Sitze ich denn im Trocknen?“ fragte Zarathustra lachend.—„Die Wellen um deinen Berg, antwortete der Wahrsager, steigen und steigen, die Wellen grosser Noth und Trübsal: die werden bald auch deinen Nachen heben und dich davontragen.“—Zarathustra schwieg hierauf und wunderte sich.—„Hörst du noch Nichts? fuhr der Wahrsager fort: rauscht und braust es nicht herauf aus der Tiefe?“—Zarathustra schwieg abermals und horchte: da hörte er einen langen, langen Schrei, welchen die Abgründe sich zuwarfen und weitergaben, denn keiner wollte ihn behalten: so böse klang er.\n\n„Du schlimmer Verkündiger, sprach endlich Zarathustra, das ist ein Nothschrei und der Schrei eines Menschen, der mag wohl aus einem schwarzen Meere kommen. Aber was geht mich Menschen-Noth an! Meine letzte Sünde, die mir aufgespart blieb,—weisst du wohl, wie sie heisst?“\n\n—„Mitleiden! antwortete der Wahrsager aus einem überströmenden Herzen und hob beide Hände empor—oh Zarathustra, ich komme, dass ich dich zu deiner letzten Sünde verführe!“—\n\nUnd kaum waren diese Worte gesprochen, da erscholl der Schrei abermals, und länger und ängstlicher als vorher, auch schon viel näher. „Hörst du? Hörst du, oh Zarathustra? rief der Wahrsager, dir gilt der Schrei, dich ruft er: komm, komm, komm, es ist Zeit, es ist höchste Zeit!“—\n\nZarathustra schwieg hierauf, verwirrt und erschüttert; endlich fragte er, wie Einer, der bei sich selber zögert: „Und wer ist das, der dort mich ruft?“\n\n„Aber du weisst es ja, antwortete der Wahrsager heftig, was verbirgst du dich? Der höhere Mensch ist es, der nach dir schreit!“\n\n„Der höhere Mensch? schrie Zarathustra von Grausen erfasst: was will der ? Was will der ? Der höhere Mensch! Was will der hier?“—und seine Haut bedeckte sich mit Schweiss.\n\nDer Wahrsager aber antwortete nicht auf die Angst Zarathustra’s, sondern horchte und horchte nach der Tiefe zu. Als es jedoch lange Zeit dort stille blieb, wandte er seinen Blick zurück und sahe Zarathustra stehn und zittern.\n\n„Oh Zarathustra, hob er mit trauriger Stimme an, du stehst nicht da wie Einer, den sein Glück drehend macht: du wirst tanzen müssen, dass du mir nicht umfällst!\n\nAber wenn du auch vor mir tanzen wolltest und alle deine Seitensprünge springen: Niemand soll mir doch sagen dürfen: „Siehe, hier tanzt der letzte frohe Mensch!“\n\nUmsonst käme Einer auf diese Höhe, der den hier suchte: Höhlen fände er wohl und Hinter-Höhlen, Verstecke für Versteckte, aber nicht Glücks-Schachte und Schatzkammern und neue Glücks-Goldadern.\n\nGlück—wie fände man wohl das Glück bei solchen Vergrabenen und Einsiedlern! Muss ich das letzte Glück noch auf glückseligen Inseln suchen und ferne zwischen vergessenen Meeren?\n\nAber Alles ist gleich, es lohnt sich Nichts, es hilft kein Suchen, es giebt auch keine glückseligen Inseln mehr!“—\n\nAlso seufzte der Wahrsager; bei seinem letzten Seufzer aber wurde Zarathustra wieder hell und sicher, gleich Einem, der aus einem tiefen Schlunde an’s Licht kommt. „Nein! Nein! Drei Mal Nein! rief er mit starker Stimme und strich sich den Bart— Das weiss ich besser! Es giebt noch glückselige Inseln! Stille davon , du seufzender Trauersack!\n\nHöre davon auf zu plätschern, du Regenwolke am Vormittag! Stehe ich denn nicht schon da, nass von deiner Trübsal und begossen wie ein Hund?\n\nNun schüttle ich mich und laufe dir davon, dass ich wieder trocken werde: dess darfst du nicht Wunder haben! Dünke ich dir unhöflich? Aber hier ist mein Hof.\n\nWas aber deinen höheren Menschen angeht: wohlan! ich suche ihn flugs in jenen Wäldern: daher kam sein Schrei. Vielleicht bedrängt ihn da ein böses Thier.\n\nEr ist in meinem Bereiche: darin soll er mir nicht zu Schaden kommen! Und wahrlich, es giebt viele böse Thiere bei mir.“—\n\nMit diesen Worten wandte sich Zarathustra zum Gehen. Da sprach der Wahrsager: „Oh Zarathustra, du bist ein Schelm!\n\nIch weiss es schon: du willst mich los sein! Lieber noch läufst du in die Wälder und stellst bösen Thieren nach!\n\nAber was hilft es dir? Des Abends wirst du doch mich wiederhaben, in deiner eignen Höhle werde ich dasitzen, geduldig und schwer wie ein Klotz—und auf dich warten!“\n\n„So sei’s! rief Zarathustra zurück im Fortgehn: und was mein ist in meiner Höhle, gehört auch dir, meinem Gastfreunde!\n\nSolltest du aber drin noch Honig finden, wohlan! so lecke ihn nur auf, du Brummbär, und versüsse deine Seele! Am Abende nämlich wollen wir Beide guter Dinge sein,\n\n—guter Dinge und froh darob, dass dieser Tag zu Ende gieng! Und du selber sollst zu meinen Liedern als mein Tanzbär tanzen.\n\nDu glaubst nicht daran? Du schüttelst den Kopf? Wohlan! Wohlauf! Alter Bär! Aber auch ich—bin ein Wahrsager.“\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0001","text":"Des nächsten Tages sass Zarathustra wieder auf seinem Steine vor der Höhle, während die Thiere draussen in der Welt herumschweiften, dass sie neue Nahrung heimbrächten,—auch neuen Honig: denn Zarathustra hatte den alten Honig bis auf das letzte Korn verthan und verschwendet. Als er aber dermaassen dasass, mit einem Stecken in der Hand, und den Schatten seiner Gestalt auf der Erde abzeichnete, nachdenkend und, wahrlich! nicht über sich und seinen Schatten—da erschrak er mit Einem Male und fuhr zusammen: denn er sahe neben seinem Schatten noch einen andern Schatten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0002","text":"Und wie er schnell um sich blickte und aufstand, siehe, da stand der Wahrsager neben ihm, der selbe, den er einstmals an seinem Tische gespeist und getränkt hatte, der Verkündiger der grossen Müdigkeit, welcher lehrte: „Alles ist gleich, es lohnt sich Nichts, Welt ist ohne Sinn, Wissen würgt.“ Aber sein Antlitz hatte sich inzwischen verwandelt; und als ihm Zarathustra in die Augen blickte, wurde sein Herz abermals erschreckt: so viel schlimme Verkündigungen und aschgraue Blitze liefen über diess Gesicht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0003","text":"Der Wahrsager, der es wahrgenommen, was sich in Zarathustra’s Seele zutrug, wischte mit der Hand über sein Antlitz hin, wie als ob er dasselbe wegwischen wollte; desgleichen that auch Zarathustra. Und als Beide dergestalt sich schweigend gefasst und gekräftigt hatten, gaben sie sich die Hände, zum Zeichen, dass sie sich wiedererkennen wollten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0004","text":"„Sei mir willkommen, sagte Zarathustra, du Wahrsager der grossen Müdigkeit, du sollst nicht umsonst einstmals mein Tisch- und Gastfreund gewesen sein. Iss und trink auch heute bei mir und vergieb es, dass ein vergnügter alter Mann mit dir zu Tische sitzt!“—„Ein vergnügter alter Mann? antwortete der Wahrsager, den Kopf schüttelnd: wer du aber auch bist oder sein willst, oh Zarathustra, du bist es zum Längsten hier Oben gewesen,—dein Nachen soll über Kurzem nicht mehr im Trocknen sitzen!“—„Sitze ich denn im Trocknen?“ fragte Zarathustra lachend.—„Die Wellen um deinen Berg, antwortete der Wahrsager, steigen und steigen, die Wellen grosser Noth und Trübsal: die werden bald auch deinen Nachen heben und dich davontragen.“—Zarathustra schwieg hierauf und wunderte sich.—„Hörst du noch Nichts?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0005","text":"fuhr der Wahrsager fort: rauscht und braust es nicht herauf aus der Tiefe?“—Zarathustra schwieg abermals und horchte: da hörte er einen langen, langen Schrei, welchen die Abgründe sich zuwarfen und weitergaben, denn keiner wollte ihn behalten: so böse klang er.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0006","text":"„Du schlimmer Verkündiger, sprach endlich Zarathustra, das ist ein Nothschrei und der Schrei eines Menschen, der mag wohl aus einem schwarzen Meere kommen. Aber was geht mich Menschen-Noth an! Meine letzte Sünde, die mir aufgespart blieb,—weisst du wohl, wie sie heisst?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0007","text":"—„Mitleiden! antwortete der Wahrsager aus einem überströmenden Herzen und hob beide Hände empor—oh Zarathustra, ich komme, dass ich dich zu deiner letzten Sünde verführe!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0008","text":"Und kaum waren diese Worte gesprochen, da erscholl der Schrei abermals, und länger und ängstlicher als vorher, auch schon viel näher. „Hörst du? Hörst du, oh Zarathustra? rief der Wahrsager, dir gilt der Schrei, dich ruft er: komm, komm, komm, es ist Zeit, es ist höchste Zeit!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0009","text":"Zarathustra schwieg hierauf, verwirrt und erschüttert; endlich fragte er, wie Einer, der bei sich selber zögert: „Und wer ist das, der dort mich ruft?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0010","text":"„Aber du weisst es ja, antwortete der Wahrsager heftig, was verbirgst du dich? Der höhere Mensch ist es, der nach dir schreit!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0011","text":"„Der höhere Mensch? schrie Zarathustra von Grausen erfasst: was will der ? Was will der ? Der höhere Mensch! Was will der hier?“—und seine Haut bedeckte sich mit Schweiss.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0012","text":"Der Wahrsager aber antwortete nicht auf die Angst Zarathustra’s, sondern horchte und horchte nach der Tiefe zu. Als es jedoch lange Zeit dort stille blieb, wandte er seinen Blick zurück und sahe Zarathustra stehn und zittern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0013","text":"„Oh Zarathustra, hob er mit trauriger Stimme an, du stehst nicht da wie Einer, den sein Glück drehend macht: du wirst tanzen müssen, dass du mir nicht umfällst!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0014","text":"Aber wenn du auch vor mir tanzen wolltest und alle deine Seitensprünge springen: Niemand soll mir doch sagen dürfen: „Siehe, hier tanzt der letzte frohe Mensch!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0015","text":"Umsonst käme Einer auf diese Höhe, der den hier suchte: Höhlen fände er wohl und Hinter-Höhlen, Verstecke für Versteckte, aber nicht Glücks-Schachte und Schatzkammern und neue Glücks-Goldadern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0016","text":"Glück—wie fände man wohl das Glück bei solchen Vergrabenen und Einsiedlern! Muss ich das letzte Glück noch auf glückseligen Inseln suchen und ferne zwischen vergessenen Meeren?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0017","text":"Aber Alles ist gleich, es lohnt sich Nichts, es hilft kein Suchen, es giebt auch keine glückseligen Inseln mehr!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0018","text":"Also seufzte der Wahrsager; bei seinem letzten Seufzer aber wurde Zarathustra wieder hell und sicher, gleich Einem, der aus einem tiefen Schlunde an’s Licht kommt. „Nein! Nein! Drei Mal Nein! rief er mit starker Stimme und strich sich den Bart— Das weiss ich besser! Es giebt noch glückselige Inseln! Stille davon , du seufzender Trauersack!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0019","text":"Höre davon auf zu plätschern, du Regenwolke am Vormittag! Stehe ich denn nicht schon da, nass von deiner Trübsal und begossen wie ein Hund?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0020","text":"Nun schüttle ich mich und laufe dir davon, dass ich wieder trocken werde: dess darfst du nicht Wunder haben! Dünke ich dir unhöflich? Aber hier ist mein Hof.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0021","text":"Was aber deinen höheren Menschen angeht: wohlan! ich suche ihn flugs in jenen Wäldern: daher kam sein Schrei. Vielleicht bedrängt ihn da ein böses Thier.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0022","text":"Er ist in meinem Bereiche: darin soll er mir nicht zu Schaden kommen! Und wahrlich, es giebt viele böse Thiere bei mir.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0023","text":"Mit diesen Worten wandte sich Zarathustra zum Gehen. Da sprach der Wahrsager: „Oh Zarathustra, du bist ein Schelm!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0024","text":"Ich weiss es schon: du willst mich los sein! Lieber noch läufst du in die Wälder und stellst bösen Thieren nach!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0025","text":"Aber was hilft es dir? Des Abends wirst du doch mich wiederhaben, in deiner eignen Höhle werde ich dasitzen, geduldig und schwer wie ein Klotz—und auf dich warten!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0026","text":"„So sei’s! rief Zarathustra zurück im Fortgehn: und was mein ist in meiner Höhle, gehört auch dir, meinem Gastfreunde!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0027","text":"Solltest du aber drin noch Honig finden, wohlan! so lecke ihn nur auf, du Brummbär, und versüsse deine Seele! Am Abende nämlich wollen wir Beide guter Dinge sein,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0028","text":"—guter Dinge und froh darob, dass dieser Tag zu Ende gieng! Und du selber sollst zu meinen Liedern als mein Tanzbär tanzen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0029","text":"Du glaubst nicht daran? Du schüttelst den Kopf? Wohlan! Wohlauf! Alter Bär! Aber auch ich—bin ein Wahrsager.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0030","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"e24a16f5e908c574d1684a922bcb57adb240fb466684c0cfe5b98cb59df2f5b2","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183406872014Z_c8a08791","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"The Cry of Distress","source_sha256":"e24a16f5e908c574d1684a922bcb57adb240fb466684c0cfe5b98cb59df2f5b2","sha256":"d926d5acfdfa3c7881ef313f1a5cff7666fad5de43e2548ec67722e4e83f7cd9","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:34:06.872042+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0001","text":"The next day Zarathustra again sat upon his stone before the cave, while the animals roamed about outside in the world, that they might bring home new food,—and new honey as well: for Zarathustra had used up and squandered the old honey down to the last grain. But as he sat there thus, with a staff in his hand, tracing the shadow of his figure upon the earth, reflecting and, truly! not upon himself and his shadow—he suddenly started in alarm and recoiled: for beside his shadow he saw yet another shadow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0002","text":"And when he swiftly looked about him and rose, behold, there stood beside him the soothsayer, the very one whom he had once fed and given drink at his table, the proclaimer of the great weariness, who taught: “All is the same, nothing is worth while, the world is without meaning, knowledge chokes.” But his countenance had meanwhile changed; and when Zarathustra looked him in the eyes, his heart was again alarmed: so many evil proclamations and ash-grey flashes passed across this face.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0003","text":"The soothsayer, who had perceived what was taking place in Zarathustra’s soul, passed his hand over his countenance, as though he wished to wipe it away; Zarathustra did likewise. And when both had thus silently collected and strengthened themselves, they gave each other their hands, as a sign that they wished to recognize one another again.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0004","text":"“Welcome to me,” said Zarathustra, “you soothsayer of the great weariness; you shall not have once been my table-companion and guest in vain. Eat and drink with me today too, and forgive it that a cheerful old man sits at table with you!”—“A cheerful old man?” answered the soothsayer, shaking his head: “but whoever you may be or wish to be, O Zarathustra, you have been up here for far too long,—your boat shall soon no longer sit upon dry ground!”—“Then do I sit upon dry ground?” asked Zarathustra, laughing.—“The waves around your mountain,” answered the soothsayer, “rise and rise, the waves of great distress and tribulation: they will soon lift your boat too and carry you away.”—Zarathustra thereupon fell silent and wondered.—“Do you hear nothing yet?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0005","text":"continued the soothsayer: “does it not rush and roar upward out of the depths?”—Zarathustra was silent again and listened: then he heard a long, long cry, which the abysses tossed to one another and passed on, for none wished to keep it: so evil did it sound.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0006","text":"“You evil proclaimer,” Zarathustra finally said, “that is a cry of distress and the cry of a human being; it may well come from a black sea. But what is human distress to me! My last sin, which was reserved for me,—do you know what it is called?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0007","text":"—“Compassion!” answered the soothsayer from an overflowing heart, and raised both hands aloft—“O Zarathustra, I come to seduce you to your last sin!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0008","text":"And scarcely had these words been spoken when the cry sounded again, longer and more anxious than before, and already much nearer. “Do you hear? Do you hear, O Zarathustra?” cried the soothsayer, “the cry is meant for you, it calls you: come, come, come, it is time, it is high time!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0009","text":"Zarathustra thereupon fell silent, confused and shaken; at last he asked, like one who hesitates within himself: “And who is it that calls me there?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0010","text":"“But you know it already,” answered the soothsayer vehemently, “why do you hide yourself? It is the higher man who cries out for you!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0011","text":"“The higher man?” cried Zarathustra, seized by horror: “what does he want? What does he want? The higher man! What does he want here?”—and his skin was covered with sweat.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0012","text":"But the soothsayer did not answer Zarathustra’s fear, but listened and listened toward the depths. Yet when for a long time all there remained still, he turned his gaze back and saw Zarathustra standing and trembling.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0013","text":"“O Zarathustra,” he began in a sorrowful voice, “you do not stand there like one whom his happiness makes whirl: you will have to dance, lest you fall over before me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0014","text":"But even if you wished to dance before me and leap all your side-leaps: no one must be permitted to say to me: ‘Behold, here dances the last joyful man!’","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0015","text":"In vain would one come to this height who sought him here: caves he would indeed find, and back-caves, hiding places for those concealed, but no shafts of happiness and treasure chambers and new golden veins of happiness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0016","text":"Happiness—how could one find happiness among such buried ones and hermits! Must I seek the last happiness still upon blessed islands and far away amid forgotten seas?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0017","text":"But all is the same, nothing is worth while, no seeking helps, there are no more blessed islands either!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0018","text":"Thus sighed the soothsayer; but at his last sigh Zarathustra became bright and sure again, like one who comes out into the light from a deep gorge. “No! No! Three times No!” he cried in a strong voice, and stroked his beard—“I know better than that! There are still blessed islands! Be silent about it, you sighing sack of sorrow!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0019","text":"Stop splashing on about it, you rain-cloud in the morning! Do I not already stand here, wet from your melancholy and drenched like a dog?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0020","text":"Now I shall shake myself and run away from you, so that I may become dry again: at this you must not wonder! Do I seem uncivil to you? But here is my court.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0021","text":"But as for your higher man: well then! I shall quickly seek him in those forests: his cry came from there. Perhaps some evil beast is pressing him there.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0022","text":"He is in my domain: there he shall come to no harm through me! And truly, there are many evil beasts with me.”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0023","text":"With these words Zarathustra turned to depart. Then the soothsayer said: “O Zarathustra, you are a rogue!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0024","text":"I know it already: you wish to be rid of me! You would rather run into the forests and pursue evil beasts!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0025","text":"But what good does it do you? In the evening you will have me back again, after all; in your own cave I shall be sitting, patient and heavy as a log—and waiting for you!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0026","text":"“So be it!” Zarathustra called back as he went on: “and what is mine in my cave belongs to you too, my guest-friend!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0027","text":"Should you still find honey in it, well then! just lick it up, you grumbling bear, and sweeten your soul! For in the evening we shall both be in good spirits,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0028","text":"—in good spirits and glad that this day came to an end! And you yourself shall dance to my songs as my dancing bear.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0029","text":"You do not believe it? You shake your head? Well then! Up now! Old bear! But I too—am a soothsayer.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERN:P0030","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERN:P0030"]}],"source_digest":"df1634ae3f393edcefca9f64fd2c9849e00ea71c1aa1550a8b818e225cdbd10e","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Der Nothschrei"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Der Nothschrei","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12","title":"1.","order":128,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_1_12","text":"## 1.\n\nZarathustra war noch keine Stunde in seinen Bergen und Wäldern unterwegs, da sahe er mit Einem Male einen seltsamen Aufzug. Gerade auf dem Wege, den er hinabwollte, kamen zwei Könige gegangen, mit Kronen und Purpurgürteln geschmückt und bunt wie Flamingo-Vögel: die trieben einen beladenen Esel vor sich her. „Was wollen diese Könige in meinem Reiche?“ sprach Zarathustra erstaunt zu seinem Herzen und versteckte Sich geschwind hinter einem Busche. Als aber die Könige bis zu ihm herankamen, sagte er, halblaut, wie Einer, der zu sich allein redet: „Seltsam! Seltsam! Wie reimt sich Das zusammen? Zwei Könige sehe ich—und nur Einen Esel!“\n\nDa machten die beiden Könige Halt, lächelten, sahen nach der Stelle hin, woher die Stimme kam, und sahen sich nachher selber in’s Gesicht. „Solcherlei denkt man wohl auch unter uns, sagte der König zur Rechten, aber man spricht es nicht aus.“\n\nDer König zur Linken aber zuckte mit den Achseln und antwortete: „Das mag wohl ein Ziegenhirt sein. Oder ein Einsiedler, der zu lange unter Felsen und Bäumen lebte. Gar keine Gesellschaft nämlich verdirbt auch die guten Sitten.“\n\n„Die guten Sitten? entgegnete unwillig und bitter der andre König: wem laufen wir denn aus dem Wege? Ist es nicht den „guten Sitten“? Unsrer „guten Gesellschaft“?\n\nLieber, wahrlich, unter Einsiedlern und Ziegenhirten als mit unserm vergoldeten falschen überschminkten Pöbel leben,—ob er sich schon „gute Gesellschaft“ heisst,\n\n—ob er sich schon „Adel“ heisst. Aber da ist Alles falsch und faul, voran das Blut, Dank alten schlechten Krankheiten und schlechteren Heil-Künstlern.\n\nDer Beste und Liebste ist mir heute noch ein gesunder Bauer, grob, listig, hartnäckig, langhaltig: das ist heute die vornehmste Art.\n\nDer Bauer ist heute der Beste; und Bauern-Art sollte Herr sein! Aber es ist das Reich des Pöbels,—ich lasse mir Nichts mehr vormachen. Pöbel aber, das heisst: Mischmasch.\n\nPöbel-Mischmasch: darin ist Alles in Allem durcheinander, Heiliger und Hallunke und Junker und Jude und jeglich Vieh aus der Arche Noäh.\n\nGute Sitten! Alles ist bei uns falsch und faul. Niemand weiss mehr zu verehren: dem gerade laufen wir davon. Es sind süssliche zudringliche Hunde, sie vergolden Palmenblätter.\n\nDieser Ekel würgt mich, dass wir Könige selber falsch wurden, überhängt und verkleidet durch alten vergilbten Grossväter-Prunk, Schaumünzen für die Dümmsten und die Schlauesten, und wer heute Alles mit der Macht Schacher treibt!\n\nWir sind nicht die Ersten—und müssen es doch bedeuten : dieser Betrügerei sind wir endlich satt und ekel geworden.\n\nDem Gesindel giengen wir aus dem Wege, allen diesen Schreihälsen und Schreib-Schmeissfliegen, dem Krämer-Gestank, dem Ehrgeiz-Gezappel, dem üblen Athem—: pfui, unter dem Gesindel leben,\n\n—pfui, unter dem Gesindel die Ersten zu bedeuten! Ach, Ekel! Ekel! Ekel! Was liegt noch an uns Königen!“—\n\n„Deine alte Krankheit fällt dich an, sagte hier der König zur Linken, der Ekel fällt dich an, mein armer Bruder. Aber du weisst es doch, es hört uns Einer zu.“\n\nSofort erhob sich Zarathustra, der zu diesen Reden Ohren und Augen aufgesperrt hatte, aus seinem Schlupfwinkel, trat auf die Könige zu und begann:\n\n„Der Euch zuhört, der Euch gerne zuhört, ihr Könige, der heisst Zarathustra.\n\nIch bin Zarathustra, der einst sprach: „Was liegt noch an Königen!“ Vergebt mir, ich freute mich, als Ihr zu einander sagtet: „Was liegt an uns Königen!“\n\nHier aber ist mein Reich und meine Herrschaft: was mögt Ihr wohl in meinem Reiche suchen? Vielleicht aber fandet Ihr unterwegs, was ich suche: nämlich den höheren Menschen.“\n\nAls Diess die Könige hörten, schlugen sie sich an die Brust und sprachen mit Einem Munde: „Wir sind erkannt!\n\nMit dem Schwerte dieses Wortes zerhaust du unsres Herzens dickste Finsterniss. Du entdecktest unsre Noth, denn siehe! Wir sind unterwegs, dass wir den höheren Menschen fänden—\n\n—den Menschen, der höher ist als wir: ob wir gleich Könige sind. Ihm führen wir diesen Esel zu. Der höchste Mensch nämlich soll auf Erden auch der höchste Herr sein.\n\nEs giebt kein härteres Unglück in allem Menschen-Schicksale, als wenn die Mächtigen der Erde nicht auch die ersten Menschen sind. Da wird Alles falsch und schief und ungeheuer.\n\nUnd wenn sie gar die letzten sind und mehr Vieh als Mensch: da steigt und steigt der Pöbel im Preise, und endlich spricht gar die Pöbel-Tugend: „siehe, ich allein bin Tugend!“—\n\nWas hörte ich eben? antwortete Zarathustra; welche Weisheit bei Königen! Ich bin entzückt, und, wahrlich, schon gelüstet’s mich, einen Reim darauf zu machen:—\n\n—mag es auch ein Reim werden, der nicht für Jedermanns Ohren taugt. Ich verlernte seit langem schon die Rücksicht auf lange Ohren. Wohlan! Wohlauf!\n\n(Hier aber geschah es, dass auch der Esel zu Worte kam: er sagte aber deutlich und mit bösem Willen I-A.)\n\nEinstmals—ich glaub’, im Jahr des Heiles Eins— Sprach die Sibylle, trunken sonder Weins: „Weh, nun geht’s schief! Verfall! Verfall! Nie sank die Welt so tief! Rom sank zur Hure und zur Huren-Bude, Rom’s Caesar sank zum Vieh, Gott selbst—ward Jude!“","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0002","text":"Zarathustra war noch keine Stunde in seinen Bergen und Wäldern unterwegs, da sahe er mit Einem Male einen seltsamen Aufzug. Gerade auf dem Wege, den er hinabwollte, kamen zwei Könige gegangen, mit Kronen und Purpurgürteln geschmückt und bunt wie Flamingo-Vögel: die trieben einen beladenen Esel vor sich her. „Was wollen diese Könige in meinem Reiche?“ sprach Zarathustra erstaunt zu seinem Herzen und versteckte Sich geschwind hinter einem Busche. Als aber die Könige bis zu ihm herankamen, sagte er, halblaut, wie Einer, der zu sich allein redet: „Seltsam! Seltsam! Wie reimt sich Das zusammen? Zwei Könige sehe ich—und nur Einen Esel!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0003","text":"Da machten die beiden Könige Halt, lächelten, sahen nach der Stelle hin, woher die Stimme kam, und sahen sich nachher selber in’s Gesicht. „Solcherlei denkt man wohl auch unter uns, sagte der König zur Rechten, aber man spricht es nicht aus.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0004","text":"Der König zur Linken aber zuckte mit den Achseln und antwortete: „Das mag wohl ein Ziegenhirt sein. Oder ein Einsiedler, der zu lange unter Felsen und Bäumen lebte. Gar keine Gesellschaft nämlich verdirbt auch die guten Sitten.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0005","text":"„Die guten Sitten? entgegnete unwillig und bitter der andre König: wem laufen wir denn aus dem Wege? Ist es nicht den „guten Sitten“? Unsrer „guten Gesellschaft“?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0006","text":"Lieber, wahrlich, unter Einsiedlern und Ziegenhirten als mit unserm vergoldeten falschen überschminkten Pöbel leben,—ob er sich schon „gute Gesellschaft“ heisst,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0007","text":"—ob er sich schon „Adel“ heisst. Aber da ist Alles falsch und faul, voran das Blut, Dank alten schlechten Krankheiten und schlechteren Heil-Künstlern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0008","text":"Der Beste und Liebste ist mir heute noch ein gesunder Bauer, grob, listig, hartnäckig, langhaltig: das ist heute die vornehmste Art.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0009","text":"Der Bauer ist heute der Beste; und Bauern-Art sollte Herr sein! Aber es ist das Reich des Pöbels,—ich lasse mir Nichts mehr vormachen. Pöbel aber, das heisst: Mischmasch.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0010","text":"Pöbel-Mischmasch: darin ist Alles in Allem durcheinander, Heiliger und Hallunke und Junker und Jude und jeglich Vieh aus der Arche Noäh.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0011","text":"Gute Sitten! Alles ist bei uns falsch und faul. Niemand weiss mehr zu verehren: dem gerade laufen wir davon. Es sind süssliche zudringliche Hunde, sie vergolden Palmenblätter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0012","text":"Dieser Ekel würgt mich, dass wir Könige selber falsch wurden, überhängt und verkleidet durch alten vergilbten Grossväter-Prunk, Schaumünzen für die Dümmsten und die Schlauesten, und wer heute Alles mit der Macht Schacher treibt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0013","text":"Wir sind nicht die Ersten—und müssen es doch bedeuten : dieser Betrügerei sind wir endlich satt und ekel geworden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0014","text":"Dem Gesindel giengen wir aus dem Wege, allen diesen Schreihälsen und Schreib-Schmeissfliegen, dem Krämer-Gestank, dem Ehrgeiz-Gezappel, dem üblen Athem—: pfui, unter dem Gesindel leben,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0015","text":"—pfui, unter dem Gesindel die Ersten zu bedeuten! Ach, Ekel! Ekel! Ekel! Was liegt noch an uns Königen!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0016","text":"„Deine alte Krankheit fällt dich an, sagte hier der König zur Linken, der Ekel fällt dich an, mein armer Bruder. Aber du weisst es doch, es hört uns Einer zu.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0017","text":"Sofort erhob sich Zarathustra, der zu diesen Reden Ohren und Augen aufgesperrt hatte, aus seinem Schlupfwinkel, trat auf die Könige zu und begann:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0018","text":"„Der Euch zuhört, der Euch gerne zuhört, ihr Könige, der heisst Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0019","text":"Ich bin Zarathustra, der einst sprach: „Was liegt noch an Königen!“ Vergebt mir, ich freute mich, als Ihr zu einander sagtet: „Was liegt an uns Königen!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0020","text":"Hier aber ist mein Reich und meine Herrschaft: was mögt Ihr wohl in meinem Reiche suchen? Vielleicht aber fandet Ihr unterwegs, was ich suche: nämlich den höheren Menschen.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0021","text":"Als Diess die Könige hörten, schlugen sie sich an die Brust und sprachen mit Einem Munde: „Wir sind erkannt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0022","text":"Mit dem Schwerte dieses Wortes zerhaust du unsres Herzens dickste Finsterniss. Du entdecktest unsre Noth, denn siehe! Wir sind unterwegs, dass wir den höheren Menschen fänden—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0023","text":"—den Menschen, der höher ist als wir: ob wir gleich Könige sind. Ihm führen wir diesen Esel zu. Der höchste Mensch nämlich soll auf Erden auch der höchste Herr sein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0024","text":"Es giebt kein härteres Unglück in allem Menschen-Schicksale, als wenn die Mächtigen der Erde nicht auch die ersten Menschen sind. Da wird Alles falsch und schief und ungeheuer.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0025","text":"Und wenn sie gar die letzten sind und mehr Vieh als Mensch: da steigt und steigt der Pöbel im Preise, und endlich spricht gar die Pöbel-Tugend: „siehe, ich allein bin Tugend!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0026","text":"Was hörte ich eben? antwortete Zarathustra; welche Weisheit bei Königen! Ich bin entzückt, und, wahrlich, schon gelüstet’s mich, einen Reim darauf zu machen:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0027","text":"—mag es auch ein Reim werden, der nicht für Jedermanns Ohren taugt. Ich verlernte seit langem schon die Rücksicht auf lange Ohren. Wohlan! Wohlauf!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0028","text":"(Hier aber geschah es, dass auch der Esel zu Worte kam: er sagte aber deutlich und mit bösem Willen I-A.)","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0029","text":"Einstmals—ich glaub’, im Jahr des Heiles Eins— Sprach die Sibylle, trunken sonder Weins: „Weh, nun geht’s schief! Verfall! Verfall! Nie sank die Welt so tief! Rom sank zur Hure und zur Huren-Bude, Rom’s Caesar sank zum Vieh, Gott selbst—ward Jude!“","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"0f8030d7e91e11c98adf5ddf4da7d8abab0924239db1c691d7de86f68425a84b","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183425125953Z_305cc605","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1.","source_sha256":"0f8030d7e91e11c98adf5ddf4da7d8abab0924239db1c691d7de86f68425a84b","sha256":"4b68abe828321d393e5c213b1095c9aa47b23dcbae433ffb4dc4dabb2b1e099c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:34:25.126145+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0002","text":"Zarathustra had not yet been on his way through his mountains and forests for an hour when all at once he saw a strange procession. Precisely on the path down which he wished to go, two kings came walking, adorned with crowns and purple girdles and colourful as flamingo birds: they drove a laden ass before them. “What do these kings want in my realm?” Zarathustra said in astonishment to his heart and quickly hid himself behind a bush. But when the kings came near him, he said half aloud, like one speaking only to himself: “Strange! Strange! How does that rhyme together? I see two kings—and only one ass!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0003","text":"Then the two kings halted, smiled, looked towards the place from which the voice came, and afterwards looked each other in the face. “One probably thinks such things among ourselves too,” said the king on the right, “but one does not speak them aloud.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0004","text":"But the king on the left shrugged his shoulders and answered: “That may well be a goatherd. Or a hermit who has lived too long among rocks and trees. For no company at all also corrupts good manners.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0005","text":"“Good manners?” retorted the other king, vexed and bitterly: “Whom, then, are we avoiding? Is it not ‘good manners’? Our ‘good society’?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0006","text":"Truly, I would rather live among hermits and goatherds than with our gilded, false, painted-over rabble,—though it calls itself ‘good society,’","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0007","text":"—though it calls itself ‘nobility.’ But there everything is false and rotten, above all the blood, thanks to old bad diseases and still worse healers.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0008","text":"The best and dearest to me today is still a healthy peasant, coarse, cunning, stubborn, enduring: that is today the noblest kind.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0009","text":"The peasant is today the best; and peasant-kind ought to be master! But it is the realm of the rabble,—I shall no longer let anything be put over on me. Rabble, however, means: hodgepodge.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0010","text":"Rabble-hodgepodge: in it everything is mixed up with everything else, saint and scoundrel and squire and Jew and every beast from Noah’s Ark.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0011","text":"Good manners! With us all is false and rotten. No one knows any longer how to revere: that is precisely what we are fleeing from. They are saccharine, importunate dogs; they gild palm leaves.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0012","text":"This disgust chokes me: that we kings ourselves have become false, hung about and disguised by old yellowed grandfatherly splendour, display coins for the stupidest and the cleverest, and for whoever today hucksters with power!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0013","text":"We are not the first—and yet we must signify it: at last we have become weary and disgusted with this deception.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0014","text":"We avoided the riffraff, all these bawlers and scribbling blowflies, the shopkeeper’s stench, the twitching of ambition, foul breath—: fie, to live among the riffraff,”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0015","text":"—fie, to signify the first among the riffraff! Ah, disgust! Disgust! Disgust! What matters anything further about us kings!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0016","text":"“Your old sickness is coming upon you,” said here the king on the left, “disgust is coming upon you, my poor brother. But you know, someone is listening to us.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0017","text":"At once Zarathustra, who had opened wide his ears and eyes to these speeches, rose from his hiding place, stepped up to the kings, and began:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0018","text":"“He who listens to you, who gladly listens to you, you kings, is called Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0019","text":"I am Zarathustra, who once said: ‘What matters anything further about kings!’ Forgive me, I rejoiced when you said to one another: ‘What matters about us kings!’","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0020","text":"But here is my realm and my dominion: what might you be seeking in my realm? Yet perhaps on the way you found what I seek: namely, the higher human being.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0021","text":"When the kings heard this, they struck their breasts and spoke with one voice: “We are recognised!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0022","text":"With the sword of this word you hew asunder the thickest darkness of our hearts. You discovered our need, for behold! we are on our way to find the higher human being—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0023","text":"—the human being who is higher than we are: though we are kings. We are leading this ass to him. For the highest human being must also be the highest lord on earth.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0024","text":"There is no harsher misfortune in all human destiny than when the mighty of the earth are not also the foremost human beings. Then everything becomes false and crooked and monstrous.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0025","text":"And if they are actually the last and more beast than human: then the rabble rises and rises in price, and at last indeed the rabble-virtue speaks: “behold, I alone am virtue!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0026","text":"What did I hear just now? answered Zarathustra; what wisdom among kings! I am delighted, and, truly, I already feel the desire to make a rhyme on it:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0027","text":"—though it may become a rhyme that is not fit for everyone’s ears. Long since I unlearned consideration for long ears. Well then! Up!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0028","text":"(But here it happened that the ass too had its say: yet it said clearly and with evil intent I-A.)","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_12:P0029","text":"Once—I think, in the year of salvation One—\nThe Sibyl spoke, drunk without wine:\n“Woe, now it is going wrong! Decay! Decay!\nNever did the world sink so low!\nRome sank into a whore and a whorehouse,\nRome’s Caesar sank into a beast, God himself—became a Jew!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_12:P0029"]}],"source_digest":"f66e4b276cb7b0d399a6b9c7f33d7eca16859ee980d02e5be615cbe56bf5cb4c","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"Conversation with the Kings","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"Conversation with the Kings · 1","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","Conversation with the Kings","1."],"original_title":"1."}],"source_title_context":"Gespräch mit den Königen","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Gespräch mit den Königen · 1","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Gespräch mit den Königen","1."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"1.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12","title":"2.","order":129,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_2_12","text":"## 2.\n\nAn diesen Reimen Zarathustra’s weideten sich die Könige; der König zur Rechten aber sprach: „oh Zarathustra, wie gut thaten wir, dass wir auszogen, dich zu sehn!\n\nDeine Feinde nämlich zeigten uns dein Bild in ihrem Spiegel: da blicktest du mit der Fratze eines Teufels und hohnlachend: also dass wir uns vor dir fürchteten.\n\nAber was half’s! Immer wieder stachst du uns in Ohr und Herz mit deinen Sprüchen. Da sprachen wir endlich: was liegt daran, wie er aussieht!\n\nWir müssen ihn hören , ihn, der lehrt „ihr sollt den Frieden lieben als Mittel zu neuen Kriegen, und den kurzen Frieden mehr als den langen!“\n\nNiemand sprach je so kriegerische Worte: „Was ist gut? Tapfer sein ist gut. Der gute Krieg ist’s, der jede Sache heiligt.“\n\nOh Zarathustra, unsrer Väter Blut rührte sich bei solchen Worten in unserm Leibe: das war wie die Rede des Frühlings zu alten Weinfässern.\n\nWenn die Schwerter durcheinander liefen gleich rothgefleckten Schlangen, da wurden unsre Väter dem Leben gut; alles Friedens Sonne dünkte sie flau und lau, der lange Frieden aber machte Scham.\n\nWie sie seufzten, unsre Väter, wenn sie an der Wand blitzblanke ausgedorrte Schwerter sahen! Denen gleich dürsteten sie nach Krieg. Ein Schwert nämlich will Blut trinken und funkelt vor Begierde.“—\n\n—Als die Könige dergestalt mit Eifer von dem Glück ihrer Väter redeten und schwätzten, überkam Zarathustra keine kleine Lust, ihres Eifers zu spotten: denn ersichtlich waren es sehr friedfertige Könige, welche er vor sich sah, solche mit alten und feinen Gesichtern. Aber er bezwang sich. „Wohlan! sprach er, dorthin führt der Weg, da liegt die Höhle Zarathustra’s; und dieser Tag soll einen langen Abend haben! Jetzt aber ruft mich eilig ein Nothschrei fort von Euch.\n\nEs ehrt meine Höhle, wenn Könige in ihr sitzen und warten wollen: aber, freilich, Ihr werdet lange warten müssen!\n\nJe nun! Was thut’s! Wo lernt man heute besser warten als an Höfen? Und der Könige ganze Tugend, die ihnen übrig blieb,—heisst sie heute nicht: Warten- können ?“\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0002","text":"An diesen Reimen Zarathustra’s weideten sich die Könige; der König zur Rechten aber sprach: „oh Zarathustra, wie gut thaten wir, dass wir auszogen, dich zu sehn!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0003","text":"Deine Feinde nämlich zeigten uns dein Bild in ihrem Spiegel: da blicktest du mit der Fratze eines Teufels und hohnlachend: also dass wir uns vor dir fürchteten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0004","text":"Aber was half’s! Immer wieder stachst du uns in Ohr und Herz mit deinen Sprüchen. Da sprachen wir endlich: was liegt daran, wie er aussieht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0005","text":"Wir müssen ihn hören , ihn, der lehrt „ihr sollt den Frieden lieben als Mittel zu neuen Kriegen, und den kurzen Frieden mehr als den langen!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0006","text":"Niemand sprach je so kriegerische Worte: „Was ist gut? Tapfer sein ist gut. Der gute Krieg ist’s, der jede Sache heiligt.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0007","text":"Oh Zarathustra, unsrer Väter Blut rührte sich bei solchen Worten in unserm Leibe: das war wie die Rede des Frühlings zu alten Weinfässern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0008","text":"Wenn die Schwerter durcheinander liefen gleich rothgefleckten Schlangen, da wurden unsre Väter dem Leben gut; alles Friedens Sonne dünkte sie flau und lau, der lange Frieden aber machte Scham.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0009","text":"Wie sie seufzten, unsre Väter, wenn sie an der Wand blitzblanke ausgedorrte Schwerter sahen! Denen gleich dürsteten sie nach Krieg. Ein Schwert nämlich will Blut trinken und funkelt vor Begierde.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0010","text":"—Als die Könige dergestalt mit Eifer von dem Glück ihrer Väter redeten und schwätzten, überkam Zarathustra keine kleine Lust, ihres Eifers zu spotten: denn ersichtlich waren es sehr friedfertige Könige, welche er vor sich sah, solche mit alten und feinen Gesichtern. Aber er bezwang sich. „Wohlan! sprach er, dorthin führt der Weg, da liegt die Höhle Zarathustra’s; und dieser Tag soll einen langen Abend haben! Jetzt aber ruft mich eilig ein Nothschrei fort von Euch.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0011","text":"Es ehrt meine Höhle, wenn Könige in ihr sitzen und warten wollen: aber, freilich, Ihr werdet lange warten müssen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0012","text":"Je nun! Was thut’s! Wo lernt man heute besser warten als an Höfen? Und der Könige ganze Tugend, die ihnen übrig blieb,—heisst sie heute nicht: Warten- können ?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0013","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"34a624ffb78824f96fe20bf3a43376d69f8f61904dab3634b98f29360c56a946","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183432990220Z_b56d2fba","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2.","source_sha256":"34a624ffb78824f96fe20bf3a43376d69f8f61904dab3634b98f29360c56a946","sha256":"af1235c0f58fada0e6a56bfddc46df2bd8640e448e1192e098dd2aa7ad093ed6","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:34:32.990268+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_12:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0002","text":"The kings delighted in these rhymes of Zarathustra's; but the king on the right said: “Oh Zarathustra, how well we did to set out to see you!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_12:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0003","text":"For your enemies showed us your image in their mirror: there you looked upon us with the grimace of a devil and mockingly laughing: so that we were afraid of you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_12:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0004","text":"But what did it avail! Again and again you pierced our ear and heart with your sayings. Then at last we said: what does it matter how he looks!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_12:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0005","text":"We must hear him, him who teaches ‘you shall love peace as a means to new wars, and the short peace more than the long!’","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_12:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0006","text":"Never did anyone speak such warlike words: ‘What is good? To be brave is good. It is good war that hallows every cause.’","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_12:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0007","text":"Oh Zarathustra, the blood of our fathers stirred in our bodies at such words: it was like the speech of spring to old wine-casks.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_12:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0008","text":"When swords ran against one another like red-spotted serpents, then our fathers grew fond of life; all the sun of peace seemed to them insipid and lukewarm, but long peace made them ashamed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_12:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0009","text":"How they sighed, our fathers, when they saw glitteringly bright, withered-up swords on the wall! Like them, they thirsted for war. For a sword wants to drink blood and sparkles with desire.’”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_12:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0010","text":"—As the kings thus spoke and chattered zealously of the happiness of their fathers, Zarathustra was seized by no small desire to mock their zeal: for evidently they were very peaceable kings whom he saw before him, such as had old and delicate faces. But he mastered himself. “Well then!” he said, “the path leads thither, there lies Zarathustra’s cave; and this day is to have a long evening! But now a cry of distress urgently calls me away from you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_12:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0011","text":"It honours my cave if kings wish to sit and wait in it: but, certainly, you will have to wait a long time!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_12:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0012","text":"Well now! What does it matter! Where does one today learn to wait better than at courts? And the whole virtue of kings that has remained to them—is it not called today: being-able-to-wait?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_12:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_12:P0013","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_12:P0013"]}],"source_digest":"134ef63a3fcf750b0ded5eefeb9db1f3d6134f585c8e7cbcd4fab91f37e1b9b3","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"Conversation with the Kings","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"Conversation with the Kings · 2","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","Conversation with the Kings","2."],"original_title":"2."}],"source_title_context":"Gespräch mit den Königen","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Gespräch mit den Königen · 2","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Gespräch mit den Königen","2."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"2.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB","title":"The Leech","order":130,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_DERB","text":"Und Zarathustra gieng nachdenklich weiter und tiefer, durch Wälder und vorbei an moorigen Gründen; wie es aber Jedem ergeht, der über schwere Dinge nachdenkt, so trat er unversehens dabei auf einen Menschen. Und siehe, da sprützten ihm mit Einem Male ein Weheschrei und zwei Flüche und zwanzig schlimme Schimpfworte in’s Gesicht: also dass er in seinem Schrecken den Stock erhob und auch auf den Getretenen noch zuschlug. Gleich darauf aber kam ihm die Besinnung; und sein Herz lachte über die Thorheit, die er eben gethan hatte.\n\n„Vergieb, sagte er zu dem Getretenen, der sich grimmig erhoben und gesetzt hatte, vergieb und vernimm vor Allem erst ein Gleichniss.\n\nWie ein Wanderer, der von fernen Dingen träumt, unversehens auf einsamer Strasse einen schlafenden Hund anstösst, einen Hund, der in der Sonne liegt:\n\n—wie da Beide auffahren, sich anfahren, Todfeinden gleich, diese zwei zu Tod Erschrockenen: also ergieng es uns.\n\nUnd doch! Und doch—wie wenig hat gefehlt, dass sie einander liebkosten, dieser Hund und dieser Einsame! Sind sie doch Beide—Einsame!“\n\n—„Wer du auch sein magst, sagte immer noch grimmig der Getretene, du trittst mir auch mit deinem Gleichniss zu nahe, und nicht nur mit deinem Fusse!\n\nSiehe doch, bin ich denn ein Hund?“—und dabei erhob sich der Sitzende und zog seinen nackten Arm aus dem Sumpfe. Zuerst nämlich hatte er ausgestreckt am Boden gelegen, verborgen und unkenntlich gleich Solchen, die einem Sumpf-Wilde auflauern.\n\n„Aber was treibst du doch!“ rief Zarathustra erschreckt, denn er sahe, dass über den nackten Arm weg viel Blut floss,—was ist dir zugestossen? Biss dich, du Unseliger, ein schlimmes Thier?\n\nDer Blutende lachte, immer noch erzürnt. „Was geht’s dich an! sagte er und wollte weitergehn. Hier bin ich heim und in meinem Bereiche. Mag mich fragen, wer da will: einem Tölpel aber werde ich schwerlich antworten.“\n\n„Du irrst, sagte Zarathustra mitleidig und hielt ihn fest, du irrst: hier bist du nicht bei dir, sondern in meinem Reiche, und darin soll mir Keiner zu Schaden kommen.\n\nNenne mich aber immerhin, wie du willst,—ich bin, der ich sein muss. Ich selber heisse mich Zarathustra.\n\nWohlan! Dort hinauf geht der Weg zu Zarathustra’s Höhle: die ist nicht fern,—willst du nicht bei mir deiner Wunden warten?\n\nEs gieng dir schlimm, du Unseliger, in diesem Leben: erst biss dich das Thier, und dann—trat dich der Mensch!“—\n\nAls aber der Getretene den Namen Zarathustra’s hörte, verwandelte er sich. „Was geschieht mir doch! rief er aus, wer kümmert mich denn noch in diesem Leben, als dieser Eine Mensch, nämlich Zarathustra, und jenes Eine Thier, das vom Blute lebt, der Blutegel?\n\nDes Blutegels halber lag ich hier an diesem Sumpfe wie ein Fischer, und schon war mein ausgehängter Arm zehn Mal angebissen, da beisst noch ein schönerer Igel nach meinem Blute, Zarathustra selber!\n\nOh Glück! Oh Wunder! Gelobt sei dieser Tag, der mich in diesen Sumpf lockte! Gelobt sei der beste lebendigste Schröpfkopf, der heut lebt, gelobt sei der grosse Gewissens-Blutegel Zarathustra!“—\n\nAlso sprach der Getretene; und Zarathustra freute sich über seine Worte und ihre feine ehrfürchtige Art. „Wer bist du? fragte er und reichte ihm die Hand, zwischen uns bleibt Viel aufzuklären und aufzuheitern: aber schon, dünkt mich, wird es reiner heller Tag.“\n\n„Ich bin der Gewissenhafte des Geistes , antwortete der Gefragte, und in Dingen des Geistes nimmt es nicht leicht Einer strenger, enger und härter als ich, ausgenommen der, von dem ich’s lernte, Zarathustra selber.\n\nLieber Nichts wissen, als Vieles halb wissen! Lieber ein Narr sein auf eigne Faust, als ein Weiser nach fremdem Gutdünken! Ich—gehe auf den Grund:\n\n—was liegt daran, ob er gross oder klein ist? Ob er Sumpf oder Himmel heisst? Eine Hand breit Grund ist mir genung: wenn er nur wirklich Grund und Boden ist!\n\n—eine Hand breit Grund: darauf kann man stehn. In der rechten Wissen-Gewissenschaft giebt es nichts Grosses und nichts Kleines.“\n\n„So bist du vielleicht der Erkenner des Blutegels? fragte Zarathustra; und du gehst dem Blutegel nach bis auf die letzten Gründe, du Gewissenhafter?“\n\n„Oh Zarathustra, antwortete der Getretene, das wäre ein Ungeheures, wie dürfte ich mich dessen unterfangen!\n\nWess ich aber Meister und Kenner bin, das ist des Blutegels Hirn :—das ist meine Welt!\n\nUnd es ist auch eine Welt! Vergieb aber, dass hier mein Stolz zu Worte kommt, denn ich habe hier nicht meines Gleichen. Darum sprach ich „hier bin ich heim.“\n\nWie lange gehe ich schon diesem Einen nach, dem Hirn des Blutegels, dass die schlüpfrige Wahrheit mir hier nicht mehr entschlüpfe! Hier ist mein Reich!\n\n—darob warf ich alles Andere fort, darob wurde mir alles. Andre gleich; und dicht neben meinem Wissen lagert mein schwarzes Unwissen.\n\nMein Gewissen des Geistes will es so von mir, dass ich Eins weiss und sonst Alles nicht weiss: es ekelt mich aller Halben des Geistes, aller Dunstigen, Schwebenden, Schwärmerischen.\n\nWo meine Redlichkeit aufhört, bin ich blind und will auch blind sein. Wo ich aber wissen will, will ich auch redlich sein, nämlich hart, streng, eng, grausam, unerbittlich.\n\nDass du einst sprachst, oh Zarathustra: „Geist ist das Leben, das selber in’s Leben schneidet,“ das führte und verführte mich zu deiner Lehre. Und, wahrlich, mit eignem Blute mehrte ich mir das eigne Wissen!“\n\n—„Wie der Augenschein lehrt,“ fiel Zarathustra ein; denn immer noch floss das Blut an dem nackten Arme des Gewissenhaften herab. Es hatten nämlich zehn Blutegel sich in denselben eingebissen.\n\n„Oh du wunderlicher Gesell, wie Viel lehrt mich dieser Augenschein da, nämlich du selber! Und nicht Alles dürfte ich vielleicht in deine strengen Ohren giessen!\n\nWohlan! So scheiden wir hier! Doch möchte ich gerne dich wiederfinden. Dort hinauf führt der Weg zu meiner Höhle: heute Nacht sollst du dort mein lieber Gast sein!\n\nGerne möchte ich’s auch an deinem Leibe wieder gut machen, dass Zarathustra dich mit Füssen trat: darüber denke ich nach. Jetzt aber ruft mich ein Nothschrei eilig fort von dir.“\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0001","text":"Und Zarathustra gieng nachdenklich weiter und tiefer, durch Wälder und vorbei an moorigen Gründen; wie es aber Jedem ergeht, der über schwere Dinge nachdenkt, so trat er unversehens dabei auf einen Menschen. Und siehe, da sprützten ihm mit Einem Male ein Weheschrei und zwei Flüche und zwanzig schlimme Schimpfworte in’s Gesicht: also dass er in seinem Schrecken den Stock erhob und auch auf den Getretenen noch zuschlug. Gleich darauf aber kam ihm die Besinnung; und sein Herz lachte über die Thorheit, die er eben gethan hatte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0002","text":"„Vergieb, sagte er zu dem Getretenen, der sich grimmig erhoben und gesetzt hatte, vergieb und vernimm vor Allem erst ein Gleichniss.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0003","text":"Wie ein Wanderer, der von fernen Dingen träumt, unversehens auf einsamer Strasse einen schlafenden Hund anstösst, einen Hund, der in der Sonne liegt:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0004","text":"—wie da Beide auffahren, sich anfahren, Todfeinden gleich, diese zwei zu Tod Erschrockenen: also ergieng es uns.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0005","text":"Und doch! Und doch—wie wenig hat gefehlt, dass sie einander liebkosten, dieser Hund und dieser Einsame! Sind sie doch Beide—Einsame!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0006","text":"—„Wer du auch sein magst, sagte immer noch grimmig der Getretene, du trittst mir auch mit deinem Gleichniss zu nahe, und nicht nur mit deinem Fusse!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0007","text":"Siehe doch, bin ich denn ein Hund?“—und dabei erhob sich der Sitzende und zog seinen nackten Arm aus dem Sumpfe. Zuerst nämlich hatte er ausgestreckt am Boden gelegen, verborgen und unkenntlich gleich Solchen, die einem Sumpf-Wilde auflauern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0008","text":"„Aber was treibst du doch!“ rief Zarathustra erschreckt, denn er sahe, dass über den nackten Arm weg viel Blut floss,—was ist dir zugestossen? Biss dich, du Unseliger, ein schlimmes Thier?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0009","text":"Der Blutende lachte, immer noch erzürnt. „Was geht’s dich an! sagte er und wollte weitergehn. Hier bin ich heim und in meinem Bereiche. Mag mich fragen, wer da will: einem Tölpel aber werde ich schwerlich antworten.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0010","text":"„Du irrst, sagte Zarathustra mitleidig und hielt ihn fest, du irrst: hier bist du nicht bei dir, sondern in meinem Reiche, und darin soll mir Keiner zu Schaden kommen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0011","text":"Nenne mich aber immerhin, wie du willst,—ich bin, der ich sein muss. Ich selber heisse mich Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0012","text":"Wohlan! Dort hinauf geht der Weg zu Zarathustra’s Höhle: die ist nicht fern,—willst du nicht bei mir deiner Wunden warten?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0013","text":"Es gieng dir schlimm, du Unseliger, in diesem Leben: erst biss dich das Thier, und dann—trat dich der Mensch!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0014","text":"Als aber der Getretene den Namen Zarathustra’s hörte, verwandelte er sich. „Was geschieht mir doch! rief er aus, wer kümmert mich denn noch in diesem Leben, als dieser Eine Mensch, nämlich Zarathustra, und jenes Eine Thier, das vom Blute lebt, der Blutegel?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0015","text":"Des Blutegels halber lag ich hier an diesem Sumpfe wie ein Fischer, und schon war mein ausgehängter Arm zehn Mal angebissen, da beisst noch ein schönerer Igel nach meinem Blute, Zarathustra selber!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0016","text":"Oh Glück! Oh Wunder! Gelobt sei dieser Tag, der mich in diesen Sumpf lockte! Gelobt sei der beste lebendigste Schröpfkopf, der heut lebt, gelobt sei der grosse Gewissens-Blutegel Zarathustra!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0017","text":"Also sprach der Getretene; und Zarathustra freute sich über seine Worte und ihre feine ehrfürchtige Art. „Wer bist du? fragte er und reichte ihm die Hand, zwischen uns bleibt Viel aufzuklären und aufzuheitern: aber schon, dünkt mich, wird es reiner heller Tag.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0018","text":"„Ich bin der Gewissenhafte des Geistes , antwortete der Gefragte, und in Dingen des Geistes nimmt es nicht leicht Einer strenger, enger und härter als ich, ausgenommen der, von dem ich’s lernte, Zarathustra selber.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0019","text":"Lieber Nichts wissen, als Vieles halb wissen! Lieber ein Narr sein auf eigne Faust, als ein Weiser nach fremdem Gutdünken! Ich—gehe auf den Grund:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0020","text":"—was liegt daran, ob er gross oder klein ist? Ob er Sumpf oder Himmel heisst? Eine Hand breit Grund ist mir genung: wenn er nur wirklich Grund und Boden ist!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0021","text":"—eine Hand breit Grund: darauf kann man stehn. In der rechten Wissen-Gewissenschaft giebt es nichts Grosses und nichts Kleines.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0022","text":"„So bist du vielleicht der Erkenner des Blutegels? fragte Zarathustra; und du gehst dem Blutegel nach bis auf die letzten Gründe, du Gewissenhafter?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0023","text":"„Oh Zarathustra, antwortete der Getretene, das wäre ein Ungeheures, wie dürfte ich mich dessen unterfangen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0024","text":"Wess ich aber Meister und Kenner bin, das ist des Blutegels Hirn :—das ist meine Welt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0025","text":"Und es ist auch eine Welt! Vergieb aber, dass hier mein Stolz zu Worte kommt, denn ich habe hier nicht meines Gleichen. Darum sprach ich „hier bin ich heim.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0026","text":"Wie lange gehe ich schon diesem Einen nach, dem Hirn des Blutegels, dass die schlüpfrige Wahrheit mir hier nicht mehr entschlüpfe! Hier ist mein Reich!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0027","text":"—darob warf ich alles Andere fort, darob wurde mir alles. Andre gleich; und dicht neben meinem Wissen lagert mein schwarzes Unwissen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0028","text":"Mein Gewissen des Geistes will es so von mir, dass ich Eins weiss und sonst Alles nicht weiss: es ekelt mich aller Halben des Geistes, aller Dunstigen, Schwebenden, Schwärmerischen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0029","text":"Wo meine Redlichkeit aufhört, bin ich blind und will auch blind sein. Wo ich aber wissen will, will ich auch redlich sein, nämlich hart, streng, eng, grausam, unerbittlich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0030","text":"Dass du einst sprachst, oh Zarathustra: „Geist ist das Leben, das selber in’s Leben schneidet,“ das führte und verführte mich zu deiner Lehre. Und, wahrlich, mit eignem Blute mehrte ich mir das eigne Wissen!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0031","text":"—„Wie der Augenschein lehrt,“ fiel Zarathustra ein; denn immer noch floss das Blut an dem nackten Arme des Gewissenhaften herab. Es hatten nämlich zehn Blutegel sich in denselben eingebissen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0032","text":"„Oh du wunderlicher Gesell, wie Viel lehrt mich dieser Augenschein da, nämlich du selber! Und nicht Alles dürfte ich vielleicht in deine strengen Ohren giessen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0033","text":"Wohlan! So scheiden wir hier! Doch möchte ich gerne dich wiederfinden. Dort hinauf führt der Weg zu meiner Höhle: heute Nacht sollst du dort mein lieber Gast sein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0034","text":"Gerne möchte ich’s auch an deinem Leibe wieder gut machen, dass Zarathustra dich mit Füssen trat: darüber denke ich nach. Jetzt aber ruft mich ein Nothschrei eilig fort von dir.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0035","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"7ca778ef30b2095cc1b9976b76c8348a042580954b157cc30c7d59476ada19b8","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183453434670Z_1873cb5e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"The Leech","source_sha256":"7ca778ef30b2095cc1b9976b76c8348a042580954b157cc30c7d59476ada19b8","sha256":"729353a6723b9ad54ed11e064d309b9fc3f081e4a722e4457ecae6f84412a2f3","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:34:53.434693+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0001","text":"And Zarathustra went on thoughtfully and ever deeper, through forests and past marshy ground; but as happens to everyone who reflects on grave matters, he unexpectedly stepped upon a human being. And behold, all at once a cry of pain and two curses and twenty vile insults spurted into his face: so that in his fright he raised his stick and struck the one he had stepped on as well. Immediately thereafter, however, he regained his senses; and his heart laughed at the folly he had just committed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0002","text":"“Forgive me,” he said to the one stepped on, who had risen and sat down grimly, “forgive me and first of all hear a parable.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0003","text":"Like a wanderer who dreams of distant things and unexpectedly bumps into a sleeping dog on a lonely road, a dog lying in the sun:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0004","text":"—as then both start up, rush at one another, like mortal enemies, these two frightened to death: so it happened to us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0005","text":"And yet! And yet—how little was lacking for them to caress one another, this dog and this solitary one! After all, they are both—solitary ones!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0006","text":"—“Whoever you may be,” said the one stepped on, still grimly, “you also tread too close to me with your parable, and not only with your foot!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0007","text":"Just look, am I then a dog?”—and with that the one sitting rose and drew his naked arm out of the swamp. For at first he had lain stretched out upon the ground, hidden and unrecognizable like those who lie in wait for swamp game.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0008","text":"“But what are you doing!” Zarathustra cried in alarm, for he saw much blood flowing over the naked arm,—“what has happened to you? Did some evil beast bite you, you unfortunate man?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0009","text":"The bleeding man laughed, still enraged. “What is it to you!” he said, and wanted to go on. “Here I am at home and in my domain. Let whoever wishes question me: but I shall hardly answer a fool.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0010","text":"“You are mistaken,” said Zarathustra compassionately and held him fast, “you are mistaken: here you are not at home, but in my realm, and in it no one shall come to harm through me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0011","text":"Yet call me whatever you will,—I am he who I must be. I myself call myself Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0012","text":"Well then! Up there goes the path to Zarathustra’s cave: it is not far,—will you not tend to your wounds with me?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0013","text":"It went badly for you, you unfortunate man, in this life: first the beast bit you, and then—the human being stepped on you!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0014","text":"But when the one stepped on heard Zarathustra’s name, he was transformed. “What is happening to me!” he cried, “who still concerns me in this life but this one human being, namely Zarathustra, and that one beast which lives on blood, the leech?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0015","text":"For the sake of the leech I lay here by this swamp like a fisherman, and already my outstretched arm had been bitten ten times, when an even fairer leech bites after my blood, Zarathustra himself!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0016","text":"Oh happiness! Oh miracle! Blessed be this day that lured me into this swamp! Blessed be the best, most living cupping-glass that lives today; blessed be Zarathustra, the great leech of conscience!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0017","text":"Thus spoke the one stepped on; and Zarathustra rejoiced at his words and at their fine, reverent manner. “Who are you?” he asked and offered him his hand, “much remains between us to be clarified and brightened: but already, it seems to me, the day is becoming purer and brighter.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0018","text":"“I am the conscientious one of the spirit,” answered the man questioned, “and in matters of the spirit no one proceeds more strictly, narrowly, and harshly than I, except him from whom I learned it, Zarathustra himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0019","text":"Better to know nothing than to know much only halfway! Better to be a fool on one’s own authority than a wise man according to another’s good pleasure! I—go to the bottom:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0020","text":"—what does it matter whether it is great or small? Whether it is called swamp or heaven? A handbreadth of ground is enough for me: provided only that it is truly ground and soil!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0021","text":"—a handbreadth of ground: one can stand upon that. In genuine science of knowledge there is nothing great and nothing small.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0022","text":"“So perhaps you are the knower of the leech?” Zarathustra asked; “and you pursue the leech down to the ultimate grounds, you conscientious one?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0023","text":"“Oh Zarathustra,” answered the one stepped on, “that would be monstrous; how could I undertake such a thing!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0024","text":"But that of which I am master and connoisseur is the leech’s brain:—that is my world!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0025","text":"And it is a world too! Forgive me, however, that my pride here finds words, for here I have no equal. Therefore I said, “here I am at home.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0026","text":"How long have I already been pursuing this one thing, the brain of the leech, so that slippery truth may no longer slip away from me here! Here is my realm!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0027","text":"—for that reason I cast all else away, for that reason all else became indifferent to me; and close beside my knowledge lies encamped my black ignorance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0028","text":"My conscience of the spirit requires this of me, that I know one thing and otherwise know nothing at all: I am disgusted by all half-measures of the spirit, by everything misty, floating, enthusiastic.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0029","text":"Where my honesty ends, I am blind and also wish to be blind. But where I wish to know, there I also wish to be honest, namely hard, strict, narrow, cruel, inexorable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0030","text":"That you once said, O Zarathustra: “Spirit is the life that itself cuts into life,”—that led and seduced me to your teaching. And, verily, with my own blood I increased my own knowledge!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0031","text":"—“As the evidence of the eyes teaches,” Zarathustra interposed; for the blood was still flowing down the bare arm of the conscientious one. For ten leeches had bitten into it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0032","text":"“O strange fellow, how much this evidence of the eyes teaches me, namely you yourself! And perhaps I should not pour everything into your strict ears!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0033","text":"Well then! Here we part! Yet I should gladly like to find you again. Up there the path leads to my cave: tonight you shall be my dear guest there!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0034","text":"I should also gladly make amends to you in your body for Zarathustra having trodden on you: about that I am thinking. But now a cry of distress urgently calls me away from you.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERB:P0035","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERB:P0035"]}],"source_digest":"fcd67eb4c74d4e0dcd7c649d95d53aa8895f95932dd734eccdbb0806b70d3a04","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Der Blutegel"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Der Blutegel","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13","title":"1.","order":131,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_1_13","text":"## 1.\n\nAls aber Zarathustra um einen Felsen herumbog, da sahe er, nicht weit unter sich, auf dem gleichen Wege, einen Menschen, der die Glieder warf wie ein Tobsüchtiger und endlich bäuchlings zur Erde niederstürzte. „Halt! sprach da Zarathustra zu seinem Herzen, Der dort muss wohl der höhere Mensch sein, von ihm kam jener schlimme Nothschrei,—ich will sehn, ob da zu helfen ist.“ Als er aber hinzulief, an die Stelle, wo der Mensch auf dem Boden lag, fand er einen zitternden alten Mann mit stieren Augen; und wie sehr sich Zarathustra mühte, dass er ihn aufrichte und wieder auf seine Beine stelle, es war umsonst. Auch schien der Unglückliche nicht zu merken, dass jemand um ihn sei; vielmehr sah er sich immer mit rührenden Gebärden um, wie ein von aller Welt Verlassener und Vereinsamter. Zuletzt aber, nach vielem Zittern, Zucken und Sich-zusammen-Krümmen, begann er also zu jammern:\n\nWer wärmt mich, wer liebt mich noch? Gebt heisse Hände! Gebt Herzens-Kohlenbecken! Hingestreckt, schaudernd, Halbtodtem gleich, dem man die Füsse wärmt— Geschüttelt, ach! von unbekannten Fiebern, Zitternd vor spitzen eisigen Frost-Pfeilen, Von dir gejagt, Gedanke! Unnennbarer! Verhüllter! Entsetzlicher! Du Jäger hinter Wolken! Darniedergeblitzt von dir, Du höhnisch Auge, das mich aus Dunklem anblickt: —so liege ich, Biege mich, winde mich, gequält Von allen ewigen Martern, Getroffen Von Dir, grausamster Jäger, Du unbekannter—Gott!\n\nTriff tiefer, Triff Ein Mal noch! Zerstich, zerbrich diess Herz! Was soll diess Martern Mit zähnestumpfen Pfeilen? Was blickst du wieder, Der Menschen-Qual nicht müde, Mit schadenfrohen Götter-Blitz-Augen? Nicht tödten willst du, Nur martern, martern? Wozu— mich martern, Du schadenfroher unbekannter Gott?—\n\nHaha! Du schleichst heran? Bei solcher Mitternacht Was willst du? Sprich! Du drängst mich, drückst mich— Ha! schon viel zu nahe! Weg! Weg! Du hörst mich athmen, Du behorchst mein Herz, Du Eifersüchtiger— Worauf doch eifersüchtig? Weg! Weg! Wozu die Leiter? Willst du hinein , In’s Herz, Einsteigen, in meine heimlichsten Gedanken einsteigen? Schamloser! Unbekannter—Dieb! Was willst du dir erstehlen, Was willst du dir erhorchen, Was willst du dir erfoltern, Du Folterer! Du—Henker-Gott! Oder soll ich, dem Hunde gleich, Vor dir mich wälzen? Hingebend, begeistert-ausser-mir, Dir—Liebe zuwedeln?\n\nUmsonst! Stich weiter, Grausamster Stachel! Nein, Kein Hund—dein Wild nur bin ich, Grausamster Jäger! Dein stolzester Gefangner, Du Räuber hinter Wolken... Sprich endlich, Was willst du, Wegelagerer, von mir ? Du Blitz-Verhüllter! Unbekannter! Sprich, Was willst du, unbekannter Gott?—-\n\nWie? Lösegeld? Was willst du Lösegelds? Verlange Viel—das räth mein Stolz! Und rede kurz—das räth mein andrer Stolz!\n\nHaha! Mich—willst du? Mich? Mich—ganz?\n\nHaha! Und marterst mich, Narr, der du bist, Zermarterst meinen Stolz? Gieb Liebe mir—wer wärmt mich noch? Wer liebt mich noch?—gieb heisse Hände, Gieb Herzens-Kohlenbecken, Gieb mir, dem Einsamsten, Den Eis, ach! siebenfaches Eis Nach Feinden selber, Nach Feinden schmachten lehrt, Gieb, ja ergieb, Grausamster Feind, Mir— dich !...\n\nDavon! Da floh er selber, Mein letzter einziger Genoss, Mein grosser Feind, Mein Unbekannter, Mein Henker-Gott!...\n\n—Nein! Komm zurück, Mit allen deinen Martern! Zum Letzten aller Einsamen Oh komm zurück! All meine Thränen-Bäche laufen Zu dir den Lauf!\n\nUnd meine letzte Herzens-Flamme— Dir glüht sie auf! Oh komm zurück, Mein unbekannter Gott! Mein Schmerz! Mein letztes Glück!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0002","text":"Als aber Zarathustra um einen Felsen herumbog, da sahe er, nicht weit unter sich, auf dem gleichen Wege, einen Menschen, der die Glieder warf wie ein Tobsüchtiger und endlich bäuchlings zur Erde niederstürzte. „Halt! sprach da Zarathustra zu seinem Herzen, Der dort muss wohl der höhere Mensch sein, von ihm kam jener schlimme Nothschrei,—ich will sehn, ob da zu helfen ist.“ Als er aber hinzulief, an die Stelle, wo der Mensch auf dem Boden lag, fand er einen zitternden alten Mann mit stieren Augen; und wie sehr sich Zarathustra mühte, dass er ihn aufrichte und wieder auf seine Beine stelle, es war umsonst. Auch schien der Unglückliche nicht zu merken, dass jemand um ihn sei; vielmehr sah er sich immer mit rührenden Gebärden um, wie ein von aller Welt Verlassener und Vereinsamter. Zuletzt aber, nach vielem Zittern, Zucken und Sich-zusammen-Krümmen, begann er also zu jammern:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0003","text":"Wer wärmt mich, wer liebt mich noch? Gebt heisse Hände! Gebt Herzens-Kohlenbecken! Hingestreckt, schaudernd, Halbtodtem gleich, dem man die Füsse wärmt— Geschüttelt, ach! von unbekannten Fiebern, Zitternd vor spitzen eisigen Frost-Pfeilen, Von dir gejagt, Gedanke! Unnennbarer! Verhüllter! Entsetzlicher! Du Jäger hinter Wolken! Darniedergeblitzt von dir, Du höhnisch Auge, das mich aus Dunklem anblickt: —so liege ich, Biege mich, winde mich, gequält Von allen ewigen Martern, Getroffen Von Dir, grausamster Jäger, Du unbekannter—Gott!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0004","text":"Triff tiefer, Triff Ein Mal noch! Zerstich, zerbrich diess Herz! Was soll diess Martern Mit zähnestumpfen Pfeilen? Was blickst du wieder, Der Menschen-Qual nicht müde, Mit schadenfrohen Götter-Blitz-Augen? Nicht tödten willst du, Nur martern, martern? Wozu— mich martern, Du schadenfroher unbekannter Gott?—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0005","text":"Haha! Du schleichst heran? Bei solcher Mitternacht Was willst du? Sprich! Du drängst mich, drückst mich— Ha! schon viel zu nahe! Weg! Weg! Du hörst mich athmen, Du behorchst mein Herz, Du Eifersüchtiger— Worauf doch eifersüchtig? Weg! Weg! Wozu die Leiter? Willst du hinein , In’s Herz, Einsteigen, in meine heimlichsten Gedanken einsteigen? Schamloser! Unbekannter—Dieb! Was willst du dir erstehlen, Was willst du dir erhorchen, Was willst du dir erfoltern, Du Folterer! Du—Henker-Gott! Oder soll ich, dem Hunde gleich, Vor dir mich wälzen? Hingebend, begeistert-ausser-mir, Dir—Liebe zuwedeln?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0006","text":"Umsonst! Stich weiter, Grausamster Stachel! Nein, Kein Hund—dein Wild nur bin ich, Grausamster Jäger! Dein stolzester Gefangner, Du Räuber hinter Wolken... Sprich endlich, Was willst du, Wegelagerer, von mir ? Du Blitz-Verhüllter! Unbekannter! Sprich, Was willst du, unbekannter Gott?—-","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0007","text":"Wie? Lösegeld? Was willst du Lösegelds? Verlange Viel—das räth mein Stolz! Und rede kurz—das räth mein andrer Stolz!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0008","text":"Haha! Mich—willst du? Mich? Mich—ganz?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0009","text":"Haha! Und marterst mich, Narr, der du bist, Zermarterst meinen Stolz? Gieb Liebe mir—wer wärmt mich noch? Wer liebt mich noch?—gieb heisse Hände, Gieb Herzens-Kohlenbecken, Gieb mir, dem Einsamsten, Den Eis, ach! siebenfaches Eis Nach Feinden selber, Nach Feinden schmachten lehrt, Gieb, ja ergieb, Grausamster Feind, Mir— dich !...","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0010","text":"Davon! Da floh er selber, Mein letzter einziger Genoss, Mein grosser Feind, Mein Unbekannter, Mein Henker-Gott!...","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0011","text":"—Nein! Komm zurück, Mit allen deinen Martern! Zum Letzten aller Einsamen Oh komm zurück! All meine Thränen-Bäche laufen Zu dir den Lauf!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0012","text":"Und meine letzte Herzens-Flamme— Dir glüht sie auf! Oh komm zurück, Mein unbekannter Gott! Mein Schmerz! Mein letztes Glück!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"f4aea4bf633c51cbb4c424e3da87e43bbb847a794b7417c3bdad53fc9bcab859","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183506835213Z_b64df5b7","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1.","source_sha256":"f4aea4bf633c51cbb4c424e3da87e43bbb847a794b7417c3bdad53fc9bcab859","sha256":"b8a03d95eb0f8ef8b28dd85cb061c903952e6cd67e74a10086ca20e716b407d0","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:35:06.835228+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_13:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0002","text":"But when Zarathustra rounded a rock, he saw, not far below him, on the same path, a man who flung his limbs about like a madman and finally fell face down to the ground. “Stop!” Zarathustra then said to his heart, “That one must surely be the higher man; from him came that evil cry of distress,—I will see whether help may be given there.” But when he ran to the place where the man lay upon the ground, he found a trembling old man with staring eyes; and however Zarathustra strove to raise him up and set him again upon his feet, it was in vain. Nor did the unfortunate man seem to notice that anyone was about him; rather, he kept looking about him with moving gestures, like one abandoned and made solitary by all the world. But at last, after much trembling, twitching, and curling himself together, he began to lament thus:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_13:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0003","text":"Who warms me, who still loves me? Give hot hands! Give heart-braziers! Stretched out, shuddering, like one half-dead, whose feet are being warmed— Shaken, alas! by unknown fevers, Trembling before sharp icy frost-arrows, Hunted by thee, Thought! Unnameable! Veiled! Terrible! Thou hunter behind clouds! Struck down by lightning from thee, Thou mocking eye, that looks upon me out of darkness: —thus I lie, Bend myself, writhe myself, tormented By all eternal tortures, Struck By Thee, cruellest hunter, Thou unknown—God!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_13:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0004","text":"Strike deeper, Strike once more! Pierce, break this heart! What is this tormenting With blunt-toothed arrows? Why dost thou look again, Not weary of human anguish, With maliciously joyful god-lightning-eyes? Thou wilt not kill, Only torment, torment? Wherefore— torment me, Thou maliciously joyful unknown God?—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_13:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0005","text":"Haha! Thou stealest near? At such midnight What wilt thou? Speak! Thou pressest, crushest me— Ha! already far too near! Away! Away! Thou hearest me breathe, Thou listenest at my heart, Thou Jealous One— Yet jealous of what? Away! Away! What need of the ladder? Wilt thou climb in, Into the heart, Climb into my most secret thoughts? Shameless! Unknown—thief! What wilt thou steal for thyself, What wilt thou overhear for thyself, What wilt thou torture out for thyself, Thou torturer! Thou—executioner-God! Or shall I, like a dog, Wallow before thee? Devoted, inspired-beside-myself, Wag love at thee?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_13:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0006","text":"In vain! Pierce further, Cruellest sting! No, No dog—only thy game am I, Cruellest hunter! Thy proudest prisoner, Thou robber behind clouds... Speak at last, What dost thou want, highwayman, of me? Thou lightning-veiled one! Unknown one! Speak, What dost thou want, unknown God?—-","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_13:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0007","text":"What? Ransom? What ransom dost thou want? Demand Much—that is what my pride counsels! And speak briefly—that is what my other pride counsels!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_13:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0008","text":"Haha! Me—dost thou want? Me? Me—entirely?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_13:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0009","text":"Haha! And thou tormentest me, fool that thou art, Torturest my pride to pieces? Give me love—who still warms me? Who still loves me?—give hot hands, Give heart-braziers, Give to me, the loneliest one, Whom ice, ah! sevenfold ice Even after enemies, After enemies, teaches to languish, Give, yea yield, cruellest enemy, To me—thyself!...","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_13:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0010","text":"Away! Then he himself fled, My last sole companion, My great enemy, My Unknown One, My executioner-God!...","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_13:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0011","text":"—No! Come back, With all thy torments! To the last of all lonely ones Oh come back! All my streams of tears run Their course toward thee!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_13:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_13:P0012","text":"And my last flame of the heart— For thee it glows up! Oh come back, My unknown God! My pain! My last happiness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_13:P0012"]}],"source_digest":"063ec7032cbce8b2696a2012e0802d41dfa06982de26915cf4f5119a4a19b89e","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Magician","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"The Magician · 1","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","The Magician","1."],"original_title":"1."}],"source_title_context":"Der Zauberer","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Der Zauberer · 1","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Der Zauberer","1."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"1.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13","title":"2.","order":132,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_2_13","text":"## 2.\n\n—Hier aber konnte sich Zarathustra nicht länger halten, nahm seinen Stock und schlug mit allen Kräften auf den jammernden los. „Halt ein! schrie er ihm zu, mit ingrimmigem Lachen, halt ein, du Schauspieler! Du Falschmünzer! Du Lügner aus dem Grunde! Ich erkenne dich wohl!\n\nIch will dir schon warme Beine machen, du schlimmer Zauberer, ich verstehe mich gut darauf, Solchen wie du bist—einzuheizen!“\n\n—„Lass ab, sagte der alte Mann und sprang vom Boden auf, schlage nicht mehr, oh Zarathustra! Ich trieb’s also nur zum Spiele!\n\nSolcherlei gehört zu meiner Kunst; dich selber wollte ich auf die Probe stellen, als ich dir diese Probe gab! Und, wahrlich, du hast mich gut durchschaut!\n\nAber auch du—gabst mir von dir keine kleine Probe: du bist hart , du weiser Zarathustra! Hart schlägst du zu mit deinen „Wahrheiten“, dein Knüttel erzwingt von mir— diese Wahrheit!“\n\n—„Schmeichle nicht, antwortete Zarathustra, immer noch erregt und finsterblickend, du Schauspieler aus dem Grunde! Du bist falsch: was redest du —von Wahrheit!\n\nDu Pfau der Pfauen, du Meer der Eitelkeit, was spieltest du vor mir, du schlimmer Zauberer, an wen sollte ich glauben, als du in solcher Gestalt jammertest?“\n\n„Den Büsser des Geistes, sagte der alte Mann, den —spielte ich: du selber erfandest einst diess Wort—\n\n—den Dichter und Zauberer, der gegen sich selber endlich seinen Geist wendet, den Verwandelten, der an seinem bösen Wissen und Gewissen erfriert.\n\nUnd gesteh es nur ein: es währte lange, oh Zarathustra, bis du hinter meine Kunst und Lüge kamst! Du glaubtest an meine Noth, als du mir den Kopf mit beiden Händen hieltest,—\n\n—ich hörte dich jammern „man hat ihn zu wenig geliebt, zu wenig geliebt!“ Dass ich dich soweit betrog, darüber frohlockte inwendig meine Bosheit.“\n\n„Du magst Feinere betrogen haben als mich, sagte Zarathustra hart. Ich bin nicht auf der Hut vor Betrügern, ich muss ohne Vorsicht sein: so will es mein Loos.\n\nDu aber— musst betrügen: so weit kenne ich dich! Du musst immer zwei- drei- vier- und fünfdeutig sein! Auch was du jetzt bekanntest, war mir lange nicht wahr und nicht falsch genung!\n\nDu schlimmer Falschmünzer, wie könntest du anders! Deine Krankheit würdest du noch schminken, wenn du dich deinem Arzte nackt zeigtest.\n\nSo schminktest du eben vor mir deine Lüge, als du sprachst: „ich trieb’s also nur zum Spiele!“ Es war auch Ernst darin, du bist Etwas von einem Büsser des Geistes!\n\nIch errathe dich wohl: du wurdest der Bezauberer Aller, aber gegen dich hast du keine Lüge und List mehr übrig,—du selber bist dir entzaubert!\n\nDu erntetest den Ekel ein, als deine Eine Wahrheit. Kein Wort ist mehr an dir ächt, aber dein Mund: nämlich der Ekel, der an deinem Munde klebt.“—\n\n—„Wer bist du doch! schrie hier der alte Zauberer mit einer trotzigen Stimme, wer darf also zu mir reden, dem Grössten, der heute lebt?“—und ein grüner Blitz schoss aus seinem Auge nach Zarathustra. Aber gleich darauf verwandelte er sich und sagte traurig:\n\n„Oh Zarathustra, ich bin’s müde, es ekelt mich meiner Künste, ich bin nicht gross , was verstelle ich mich! Aber, du weisst es wohl—ich suchte nach Grösse!\n\nEinen grossen Menschen wollte ich vorstellen und überredete Viele: aber diese Lüge gieng über meine Kraft. An ihr zerbreche ich.\n\nOh Zarathustra, Alles ist Lüge an mir; aber dass ich zerbreche—diess mein Zerbrechen ist ächt !“—\n\n„Es ehrt dich, sprach Zarathustra düster und zur Seite niederblickend, es ehrt dich, dass du nach Grösse suchtest, aber es verräth dich auch. Du bist nicht gross.\n\nDu schlimmer alter Zauberer, das ist dein Bestes und Redlichstes, was ich an dir ehre, dass du deiner müde wurdest und es aussprachst: „ich bin nicht gross.“\n\nDarin ehre ich dich als einen Büsser des Geistes: und wenn auch nur für einen Hauch und Husch, diesen Einen Augenblick warst du—ächt.\n\nAber sprich, was suchst du hier in meinen Wäldern und Felsen? Und wenn du mir dich in den Weg legtest, welche Probe wolltest du von mir?—\n\n—wess versuchtest du mich ?“—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra, und seine Augen funkelten. Der alte Zauberer schwieg eine Weile, dann sagte er: „Versuchte ich dich? Ich—suche nur.\n\nOh Zarathustra, ich suche einen Ächten, Rechten, Einfachen, Eindeutigen, einen Menschen aller Redlichkeit, ein Gefäss der Weisheit, einen Heiligen der Erkenntniss, einen grossen Menschen!\n\nWeisst du es denn nicht, oh Zarathustra? Ich suche Zarathustra.“\n\n—Und hier entstand ein langes Stillschweigen zwischen Beiden; Zarathustra aber versank tief hinein in sich selber, also dass er die Augen schloss. Dann aber, zu seinem Unterredner zurückkehrend, ergriff er die Hand des Zauberers und sprach, voller Artigkeit und Arglist:\n\n„Wohlan! Dort hinauf führt der Weg, da liegt die Höhle Zarathustra’s. In ihr darfst du suchen, wen du finden möchtest.\n\nUnd frage meine Thiere um Rath, meinen Adler und meine Schlange: die sollen dir suchen helfen. Meine Höhle aber ist gross.\n\nIch selber freilich—ich sah noch keinen grossen Menschen. Was gross ist, dafür ist das Auge der Feinsten heute grob. Es ist das Reich des Pöbels.\n\nSo Manchen fand ich schon, der streckte und blähte sich, und das Volk schrie: „Seht da, einen grossen Menschen!“ Aber was helfen alle Blasebälge! Zuletzt fährt der Wind heraus.\n\nZuletzt platzt ein Frosch, der sich zu lange aufblies: da fährt der Wind heraus. Einem Geschwollnen in den Bauch stechen, das heisse ich eine brave Kurzweil. Hört das, ihr Knaben!\n\nDiess Heute ist des Pöbels: wer weiss da noch, was gross, was klein ist! Wer suchte da mit Glück nach Grösse! Ein Narr allein: den Narren glückt’s.\n\nDu suchst nach grossen Menschen, du wunderlicher Narr? Wer lehrte’s dich? Ist heute dazu die Zeit? Oh du schlimmer Sucher, was—versuchst du mich?“—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra, getrösteten Herzens, und gierig lachend seines Wegs fürbass.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0002","text":"—Hier aber konnte sich Zarathustra nicht länger halten, nahm seinen Stock und schlug mit allen Kräften auf den jammernden los. „Halt ein! schrie er ihm zu, mit ingrimmigem Lachen, halt ein, du Schauspieler! Du Falschmünzer! Du Lügner aus dem Grunde! Ich erkenne dich wohl!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0003","text":"Ich will dir schon warme Beine machen, du schlimmer Zauberer, ich verstehe mich gut darauf, Solchen wie du bist—einzuheizen!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0004","text":"—„Lass ab, sagte der alte Mann und sprang vom Boden auf, schlage nicht mehr, oh Zarathustra! Ich trieb’s also nur zum Spiele!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0005","text":"Solcherlei gehört zu meiner Kunst; dich selber wollte ich auf die Probe stellen, als ich dir diese Probe gab! Und, wahrlich, du hast mich gut durchschaut!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0006","text":"Aber auch du—gabst mir von dir keine kleine Probe: du bist hart , du weiser Zarathustra! Hart schlägst du zu mit deinen „Wahrheiten“, dein Knüttel erzwingt von mir— diese Wahrheit!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0007","text":"—„Schmeichle nicht, antwortete Zarathustra, immer noch erregt und finsterblickend, du Schauspieler aus dem Grunde! Du bist falsch: was redest du —von Wahrheit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0008","text":"Du Pfau der Pfauen, du Meer der Eitelkeit, was spieltest du vor mir, du schlimmer Zauberer, an wen sollte ich glauben, als du in solcher Gestalt jammertest?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0009","text":"„Den Büsser des Geistes, sagte der alte Mann, den —spielte ich: du selber erfandest einst diess Wort—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0010","text":"—den Dichter und Zauberer, der gegen sich selber endlich seinen Geist wendet, den Verwandelten, der an seinem bösen Wissen und Gewissen erfriert.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0011","text":"Und gesteh es nur ein: es währte lange, oh Zarathustra, bis du hinter meine Kunst und Lüge kamst! Du glaubtest an meine Noth, als du mir den Kopf mit beiden Händen hieltest,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0012","text":"—ich hörte dich jammern „man hat ihn zu wenig geliebt, zu wenig geliebt!“ Dass ich dich soweit betrog, darüber frohlockte inwendig meine Bosheit.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0013","text":"„Du magst Feinere betrogen haben als mich, sagte Zarathustra hart. Ich bin nicht auf der Hut vor Betrügern, ich muss ohne Vorsicht sein: so will es mein Loos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0014","text":"Du aber— musst betrügen: so weit kenne ich dich! Du musst immer zwei- drei- vier- und fünfdeutig sein! Auch was du jetzt bekanntest, war mir lange nicht wahr und nicht falsch genung!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0015","text":"Du schlimmer Falschmünzer, wie könntest du anders! Deine Krankheit würdest du noch schminken, wenn du dich deinem Arzte nackt zeigtest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0016","text":"So schminktest du eben vor mir deine Lüge, als du sprachst: „ich trieb’s also nur zum Spiele!“ Es war auch Ernst darin, du bist Etwas von einem Büsser des Geistes!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0017","text":"Ich errathe dich wohl: du wurdest der Bezauberer Aller, aber gegen dich hast du keine Lüge und List mehr übrig,—du selber bist dir entzaubert!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0018","text":"Du erntetest den Ekel ein, als deine Eine Wahrheit. Kein Wort ist mehr an dir ächt, aber dein Mund: nämlich der Ekel, der an deinem Munde klebt.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0019","text":"—„Wer bist du doch! schrie hier der alte Zauberer mit einer trotzigen Stimme, wer darf also zu mir reden, dem Grössten, der heute lebt?“—und ein grüner Blitz schoss aus seinem Auge nach Zarathustra. Aber gleich darauf verwandelte er sich und sagte traurig:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0020","text":"„Oh Zarathustra, ich bin’s müde, es ekelt mich meiner Künste, ich bin nicht gross , was verstelle ich mich! Aber, du weisst es wohl—ich suchte nach Grösse!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0021","text":"Einen grossen Menschen wollte ich vorstellen und überredete Viele: aber diese Lüge gieng über meine Kraft. An ihr zerbreche ich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0022","text":"Oh Zarathustra, Alles ist Lüge an mir; aber dass ich zerbreche—diess mein Zerbrechen ist ächt !“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0023","text":"„Es ehrt dich, sprach Zarathustra düster und zur Seite niederblickend, es ehrt dich, dass du nach Grösse suchtest, aber es verräth dich auch. Du bist nicht gross.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0024","text":"Du schlimmer alter Zauberer, das ist dein Bestes und Redlichstes, was ich an dir ehre, dass du deiner müde wurdest und es aussprachst: „ich bin nicht gross.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0025","text":"Darin ehre ich dich als einen Büsser des Geistes: und wenn auch nur für einen Hauch und Husch, diesen Einen Augenblick warst du—ächt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0026","text":"Aber sprich, was suchst du hier in meinen Wäldern und Felsen? Und wenn du mir dich in den Weg legtest, welche Probe wolltest du von mir?—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0027","text":"—wess versuchtest du mich ?“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0028","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra, und seine Augen funkelten. Der alte Zauberer schwieg eine Weile, dann sagte er: „Versuchte ich dich? Ich—suche nur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0029","text":"Oh Zarathustra, ich suche einen Ächten, Rechten, Einfachen, Eindeutigen, einen Menschen aller Redlichkeit, ein Gefäss der Weisheit, einen Heiligen der Erkenntniss, einen grossen Menschen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0030","text":"Weisst du es denn nicht, oh Zarathustra? Ich suche Zarathustra.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0031","text":"—Und hier entstand ein langes Stillschweigen zwischen Beiden; Zarathustra aber versank tief hinein in sich selber, also dass er die Augen schloss. Dann aber, zu seinem Unterredner zurückkehrend, ergriff er die Hand des Zauberers und sprach, voller Artigkeit und Arglist:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0032","text":"„Wohlan! Dort hinauf führt der Weg, da liegt die Höhle Zarathustra’s. In ihr darfst du suchen, wen du finden möchtest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0033","text":"Und frage meine Thiere um Rath, meinen Adler und meine Schlange: die sollen dir suchen helfen. Meine Höhle aber ist gross.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0034","text":"Ich selber freilich—ich sah noch keinen grossen Menschen. Was gross ist, dafür ist das Auge der Feinsten heute grob. Es ist das Reich des Pöbels.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0035","text":"So Manchen fand ich schon, der streckte und blähte sich, und das Volk schrie: „Seht da, einen grossen Menschen!“ Aber was helfen alle Blasebälge! Zuletzt fährt der Wind heraus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0036","text":"Zuletzt platzt ein Frosch, der sich zu lange aufblies: da fährt der Wind heraus. Einem Geschwollnen in den Bauch stechen, das heisse ich eine brave Kurzweil. Hört das, ihr Knaben!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0037","text":"Diess Heute ist des Pöbels: wer weiss da noch, was gross, was klein ist! Wer suchte da mit Glück nach Grösse! Ein Narr allein: den Narren glückt’s.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0038","text":"Du suchst nach grossen Menschen, du wunderlicher Narr? Wer lehrte’s dich? Ist heute dazu die Zeit? Oh du schlimmer Sucher, was—versuchst du mich?“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0039","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra, getrösteten Herzens, und gierig lachend seines Wegs fürbass.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"6d43a55d26941f60e2338da1ad6e53eac0dfe9213f94b3d3d614ff362a0334ad","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183533583475Z_b48ae191","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2.","source_sha256":"6d43a55d26941f60e2338da1ad6e53eac0dfe9213f94b3d3d614ff362a0334ad","sha256":"72902624fc0b68f5372f8a1f592ea786953bc5e9eeea1434400fef6fb5d3e129","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:35:33.583495+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0002","text":"—But here Zarathustra could no longer contain himself, took his staff and struck the lamenting man with all his strength. “Stop! he cried at him, with wrathful laughter, stop, you actor! You counterfeiter! You liar from the ground up! I know you well enough!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0003","text":"I shall make your legs warm, you wicked magician; I know well how to—give such as you a warming!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0004","text":"—“Desist, said the old man and sprang up from the ground, strike no more, oh Zarathustra! I did it merely in play!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0005","text":"Such things belong to my art; I wished to put you yourself to the test, when I gave you this test! And, verily, you saw through me well!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0006","text":"But you too—gave me no slight proof of yourself: you are hard, you wise Zarathustra! You strike hard with your “truths”; your cudgel compels from me—this truth!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0007","text":"—“Do not flatter, answered Zarathustra, still excited and darkly glaring, you actor from the ground up! You are false: what are you saying—of truth!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0008","text":"You peacock of peacocks, you sea of vanity, what did you enact before me, you wicked magician; whom was I to believe, when you lamented in such a guise?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0009","text":"“The penitent of the spirit, said the old man, that is whom—I played: you yourself once coined this word—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0010","text":"—the poet and magician who at last turns his spirit against himself, the transformed one, who freezes in his evil knowledge and conscience.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0011","text":"And only admit it: it took a long time, oh Zarathustra, before you came behind my art and lie! You believed in my distress, when you held my head with both hands,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0012","text":"—I heard you lament, “he has been loved too little, loved too little!” That I deceived you so far, my malice rejoiced inwardly over that.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0013","text":"“You may have deceived subtler men than me, said Zarathustra harshly. I am not on my guard against deceivers; I must be without caution: thus my lot wills it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0014","text":"But you—must deceive: so much I know of you! You must always be two-, three-, four-, and five-meaning! Even what you now confessed was for me not true and not false enough!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0015","text":"You wicked counterfeiter, how could you do otherwise! You would still paint up your sickness, if you showed yourself naked to your physician.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0016","text":"Thus you just now painted up your lie before me, when you said: “I did it merely in play!” There was earnest in it too; you are something of a penitent of the spirit!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0017","text":"I guess you well: you became the enchanter of all, but against yourself you have no lie and cunning left,—you yourself are disenchanted!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0018","text":"You reaped disgust as your one truth. No word is any longer genuine in you, but your mouth: namely, the disgust that clings to your mouth.”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0019","text":"—“Who are you then! cried the old magician here in a defiant voice, who may speak thus to me, the greatest man living today?”—and a green flash shot from his eye towards Zarathustra. But immediately thereafter he changed and said sadly:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0020","text":"“Oh Zarathustra, I am weary of it; my arts disgust me, I am not great, why do I pretend? But, you know well enough—I sought greatness!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0021","text":"I wished to portray a great man and persuaded many: but this lie exceeded my strength. It breaks me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0022","text":"Oh Zarathustra, everything in me is lie; but that I break—this breaking of mine is genuine!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0023","text":"“It honours you, spoke Zarathustra gloomily and looking down to one side, it honours you that you sought greatness, but it also betrays you. You are not great.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0024","text":"You wicked old magician, this is your best and most honest quality, which I honour in you: that you grew weary of yourself and spoke it out: ‘I am not great.’","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0025","text":"In this I honour you as a penitent of the spirit: and even if only for a breath and a flash, for this one moment you were—genuine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0026","text":"But speak, what do you seek here in my forests and rocks? And if you laid yourself in my path, what test did you wish of me?—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0027","text":"—of what did you tempt me?”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0028","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra, and his eyes flashed. The old magician was silent for a while, then he said: “Did I tempt you? I—only seek.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0029","text":"Oh Zarathustra, I seek a Genuine One, a Right One, a Simple One, an Unambiguous One, a man of all probity, a vessel of wisdom, a saint of knowledge, a great man!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0030","text":"Do you not know it, O Zarathustra? I seek Zarathustra.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0031","text":"—And here a long silence arose between the two; but Zarathustra sank deep into himself, so that he closed his eyes. But then, returning to his interlocutor, he took the magician’s hand and spoke, full of courtesy and guile:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0032","text":"“Well then! The path leads up there, there lies Zarathustra’s cave. In it you may seek whom you wish to find.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0033","text":"And ask my animals for counsel, my eagle and my serpent: they are to help you seek. But my cave is large.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0034","text":"I myself, to be sure—I have not yet seen any great man. For what is great, the eye of the finest is coarse today. It is the realm of the rabble.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0035","text":"Many a one have I already found who stretched and puffed himself up, and the people cried: “Look there, a great man!” But what good are all bellows! In the end the wind escapes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0036","text":"In the end a frog bursts that has puffed itself up too long: then the wind escapes. To stab a swollen one in the belly, that I call a fine pastime. Hear this, you boys!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0037","text":"This Today belongs to the rabble: who still knows there what is great, what is small! Who would seek greatness there with success! A fool alone: fools succeed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0038","text":"You seek great men, you strange fool? Who taught it you? Is this the time for it today? O you evil seeker, what—do you tempt in me?”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_13:P0039","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra, comforted at heart, and went laughing greedily on his way.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_13:P0039"]}],"source_digest":"8e952597f8703eb6a2c6db845dc645e50697da65ca4bf39d32193d97bbe8418d","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Magician","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"The Magician · 2","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","The Magician","2."],"original_title":"2."}],"source_title_context":"Der Zauberer","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Der Zauberer · 2","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Der Zauberer","2."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"2.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD","title":"Out of Service","order":133,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_AUSD","text":"Nicht lange aber, nachdem Zarathustra sich von dem Zauberer losgemacht hatte, sahe er wiederum Jemanden am Wege sitzen, den er gierig, nämlich einen schwarzen langen Mann mit einem hageren Bleichgesicht: der verdross ihn gewaltig. „Wehe, sprach er zu seinem Herzen, da, sitzt vermummte Trübsal, das dünkt mich von der Art der Priester: was wollen die in meinem Reiche?\n\nWie! Kaum bin ich jenem Zauberer entronnen: muss mir da wieder ein anderer Schwarzkünstler über den Weg laufen,—\n\n—irgend ein Hexenmeister mit Handauflegen, ein dunkler Wunderthäter von Gottes Gnaden, ein gesalbter Welt-Verleumder, den der Teufel holen möge!\n\nAber der Teufel ist nie am Platze, wo er am Platze wäre: immer kommt er zu spät, dieser vermaledeite Zwerg und Klumpfuss!“—\n\nAlso fluchte Zarathustra ungeduldig in seinem Herzen und gedachte, wie er abgewandten Blicks an dem schwarzen Manne vorüberschlüpfe: aber siehe, es kam anders. Im gleichen Augenblicke nämlich hatte ihn schon der Sitzende erblickt; und nicht unähnlich einem Solchen, dem ein unvermuthetes Glück zustösst, sprang er auf und gieng auf Zarathustra los.\n\n„Wer du auch bist, du Wandersmann, sprach er, hilf einem Verirrten, einem Suchenden, einem alten Manne, der hier leicht zu Schaden kommt!\n\nDiese Welt hier ist mir fremd und fern, auch hörte ich wilde Thiere heulen; und Der, welcher mir hätte Schutz bieten können, der ist selber nicht mehr.\n\nIch suchte den letzten frommen Menschen, einen Heiligen und Einsiedler, der allein in seinem Walde noch Nichts davon gehört hatte, was alle Welt heute weiss.“\n\n„ Was weiss heute alle Welt? fragte Zarathustra. Etwa diess, dass der alte Gott nicht mehr lebt, an den alle Welt einst geglaubt hat?“\n\n„Du sagst es, antwortete der alte Mann betrübt. Und ich diente diesem alten Gotte bis zu seiner letzten Stunde.\n\nNun aber bin ich ausser Dienst, ohne Herrn, und doch nicht frei, auch keine Stunde mehr lustig, es sei denn in Erinnerungen.\n\nDazu stieg ich in diese Berge, dass ich endlich wieder ein Fest mir machte, wie es einem alten Papste und Kirchen-Vater zukommt: denn wisse, ich bin der letzte Papst!—ein Fest frommer Erinnerungen und Gottesdienste.\n\nNun aber ist er selber todt, der frömmste Mensch, jener Heilige im Walde, der seinen Gott beständig mit Singen und Brummen lobte.\n\nIhn selber fand ich nicht mehr, als ich seine Hütte fand,—wohl aber zwei Wölfe darin, welche um seinen Tod heulten—denn alle Thiere liebten ihn. Da lief ich davon.\n\nKam ich also umsonst in diese Wälder und Berge? Da entschloss sich mein Herz, dass ich einen Anderen suchte, den Frömmsten aller Derer, die nicht an Gott glauben—, dass ich Zarathustra suchte!“\n\nAlso sprach der Greis und blickte scharfen Auges Den an, welcher vor ihm stand; Zarathustra aber ergriff die Hand des alten Papstes und betrachtete sie lange mit Bewunderung.\n\n„Siehe da, du Ehrwürdiger, sagte er dann, welche schöne und lange Hand! Das ist die Hand eines Solchen, der immer Segen ausgetheilt hat. Nun aber hält sie Den fest, welchen du suchst, mich, Zarathustra.\n\nIch bin’s, der gottlose Zarathustra, der da spricht: wer ist gottloser als ich, dass ich mich seiner Unterweisung freue?“—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra und durchbohrte mit seinen Blicken die Gedanken und Hintergedanken des alten Papstes. Endlich begann dieser:\n\n„Wer ihn am meisten liebte und besass, der hat ihn nun am meisten auch verloren -:\n\n—siehe, ich selber bin wohl von uns Beiden jetzt der Gottlosere? Aber wer könnte daran sich freuen!“—\n\n„Du dientest ihm bis zuletzt, fragte Zarathustra nachdenklich, nach einem tiefen Schweigen, du weisst, wie er starb? Ist es wahr, was man spricht, dass ihn das Mitleiden erwürgte,\n\n—dass er es sah, wie der Mensch am Kreuze hieng, und es nicht ertrug, dass die Liebe zum Menschen seine Hölle und zuletzt sein Tod wurde?“—\n\nDer alte Papst aber antwortete nicht, sondern blickte scheu und mit einem schmerzlichen und düsteren Ausdrucke zur Seite.\n\n„Lass ihn fahren, sagte Zarathustra nach einem langen Nachdenken, indem er immer noch dem alten Manne gerade in’s Auge blickte.\n\nLass ihn fahren, er ist dahin. Und ob es dich auch ehrt, dass du diesem Todten nur Gutes nachredest, so weisst du so gut als ich, wer er war; und dass er wunderliche Wege gieng.“\n\n„Unter drei Augen gesprochen, sagte erheitert der alte Papst (denn er war auf Einem Auge blind), in Dingen Gottes bin ich aufgeklärter als Zarathustra selber —und darf es sein.\n\nMeine Liebe diente ihm lange Jahre, mein Wille gierig allem seinen Willen nach. Ein guter Diener aber weiss Alles, und Mancherlei auch, was sein Herr sich selbst verbirgt.\n\nEs war ein verborgener Gott, voller Heimlichkeit. Wahrlich zu einem Sohne sogar kam er nicht anders als auf Schleichwegen. An der Thür seines Glaubens steht der Ehebruch.\n\nWer ihn als einen Gott der Liebe preist, denkt nicht hoch genug von der Liebe selber. Wollte dieser Gott nicht auch Richter sein? Aber der Liebende liebt jenseits von Lohn und Vergeltung.\n\nAls er jung war, dieser Gott aus dem Morgenlande, da war er hart und rachsüchtig und erbaute sich eine Hölle zum Ergötzen seiner Lieblinge.\n\nEndlich aber wurde er alt und weich und mürbe und mitleidig, einem Grossvater ähnlicher als einem Vater, am ähnlichsten aber einer wackeligen alten Grossmutter.\n\nDa sass er, welk, in seinem Ofenwinkel, härmte sich ob seiner schwachen Beine, weltmüde, willensmüde, und erstickte eines Tags an seinem allzugrossen Mitleiden.“—\n\n„Du alter Papst, sagte hier Zarathustra dazwischen, hast du Das mit Augen angesehn? Es könnte wohl so abgegangen sein: so, und auch anders. Wenn Götter sterben, sterben sie immer viele Arten Todes.\n\nAber wohlan! So oder so, so und so—er ist dahin! Er gieng meinen Ohren und Augen wider den Geschmack, Schlimmeres möchte ich ihm nicht nachsagen.\n\nIch liebe Alles, was hell blickt und redlich redet. Aber er—du weisst es ja, du alter Priester, es war Etwas von deiner Art an ihm, von Priester-Art—er war vieldeutig.\n\nEr war auch undeutlich. Was hat er uns darob gezürnt, dieser Zornschnauber, dass wir ihn schlecht verstanden Aber warum sprach er nicht reinlicher?\n\nUnd lag es an unsern Ohren, warum gab er uns Ohren, die ihn schlecht hörten? War Schlamm in unsern Ohren, wohlan! wer legte ihn hinein?\n\nZu Vieles missrieth ihm, diesem Töpfer, der nicht ausgelernt hatte! Dass er aber Rache an seinen Töpfen und Geschöpfen nahm, dafür dass sie ihm schlecht geriethen,—das war eine Sünde wider den guten Geschmack .\n\nEs giebt auch in der Frömmigkeit guten Geschmack: der sprach endlich „Fort mit einem solchen Gotte! Lieber keinen Gott, lieber auf eigne Faust Schicksal machen, lieber Narr sein, lieber selber Gott sein!““\n\n—„Was höre ich! sprach hier der alte Papst mit gespitzten Ohren; oh Zarathustra, du bist frömmer als du glaubst, mit einem solchen Unglauben! Irgend ein Gott in dir bekehrte dich zu deiner Gottlosigkeit.\n\nIst es nicht deine Frömmigkeit selber, die dich nicht mehr an einen Gott glauben lässt? Und deine übergrosse Redlichkeit wird dich auch noch jenseits von Gut und Böse wegfuhren!\n\nSiehe, doch, was blieb dir aufgespart? Du hast Augen und Hand und Mund, die sind zum Segnen vorher bestimmt seit Ewigkeit. Man segnet nicht mit der Hand allein.\n\nIn deiner Nähe, ob du schon der Gottloseste sein willst, wittere ich einen heimlichen Weih- und Wohlgeruch von langen Segnungen: mir wird wohl und wehe dabei.\n\nLass mich deinen Gast sein, oh Zarathustra, für eine einzige Nacht! Nirgends auf Erden wird es mir jetzt wohler als bei dir!“—\n\n„Amen! So soll es sein! sprach Zarathustra mit grosser Verwunderung, dort hinauf führt der Weg, da liegt die Höhle Zarathustra’s.\n\nGerne, fürwahr, würde ich dich selber dahin geleiten, du Ehrwürdiger, denn ich liebe alle frommen Menschen. Aber jetzt ruft mich eilig ein Nothschrei weg von dir.\n\nIn meinem Bereiche soll mir Niemand zu Schaden kommen; meine Höhle ist ein guter Hafen. Und am liebsten möchte ich jedweden Traurigen wieder auf festes Land und feste Beine stellen.\n\nWer aber nähme dir deine Schwermuth von der Schulter? Dazu bin ich zu schwach. Lange, wahrlich, möchten wir warten, bis dir Einer deinen Gott wieder aufweckt.\n\nDieser alte Gott nämlich lebt nicht mehr: der ist gründlich todt.“—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0001","text":"Nicht lange aber, nachdem Zarathustra sich von dem Zauberer losgemacht hatte, sahe er wiederum Jemanden am Wege sitzen, den er gierig, nämlich einen schwarzen langen Mann mit einem hageren Bleichgesicht: der verdross ihn gewaltig. „Wehe, sprach er zu seinem Herzen, da, sitzt vermummte Trübsal, das dünkt mich von der Art der Priester: was wollen die in meinem Reiche?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0002","text":"Wie! Kaum bin ich jenem Zauberer entronnen: muss mir da wieder ein anderer Schwarzkünstler über den Weg laufen,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0003","text":"—irgend ein Hexenmeister mit Handauflegen, ein dunkler Wunderthäter von Gottes Gnaden, ein gesalbter Welt-Verleumder, den der Teufel holen möge!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0004","text":"Aber der Teufel ist nie am Platze, wo er am Platze wäre: immer kommt er zu spät, dieser vermaledeite Zwerg und Klumpfuss!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0005","text":"Also fluchte Zarathustra ungeduldig in seinem Herzen und gedachte, wie er abgewandten Blicks an dem schwarzen Manne vorüberschlüpfe: aber siehe, es kam anders. Im gleichen Augenblicke nämlich hatte ihn schon der Sitzende erblickt; und nicht unähnlich einem Solchen, dem ein unvermuthetes Glück zustösst, sprang er auf und gieng auf Zarathustra los.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0006","text":"„Wer du auch bist, du Wandersmann, sprach er, hilf einem Verirrten, einem Suchenden, einem alten Manne, der hier leicht zu Schaden kommt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0007","text":"Diese Welt hier ist mir fremd und fern, auch hörte ich wilde Thiere heulen; und Der, welcher mir hätte Schutz bieten können, der ist selber nicht mehr.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0008","text":"Ich suchte den letzten frommen Menschen, einen Heiligen und Einsiedler, der allein in seinem Walde noch Nichts davon gehört hatte, was alle Welt heute weiss.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0009","text":"„ Was weiss heute alle Welt? fragte Zarathustra. Etwa diess, dass der alte Gott nicht mehr lebt, an den alle Welt einst geglaubt hat?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0010","text":"„Du sagst es, antwortete der alte Mann betrübt. Und ich diente diesem alten Gotte bis zu seiner letzten Stunde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0011","text":"Nun aber bin ich ausser Dienst, ohne Herrn, und doch nicht frei, auch keine Stunde mehr lustig, es sei denn in Erinnerungen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0012","text":"Dazu stieg ich in diese Berge, dass ich endlich wieder ein Fest mir machte, wie es einem alten Papste und Kirchen-Vater zukommt: denn wisse, ich bin der letzte Papst!—ein Fest frommer Erinnerungen und Gottesdienste.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0013","text":"Nun aber ist er selber todt, der frömmste Mensch, jener Heilige im Walde, der seinen Gott beständig mit Singen und Brummen lobte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0014","text":"Ihn selber fand ich nicht mehr, als ich seine Hütte fand,—wohl aber zwei Wölfe darin, welche um seinen Tod heulten—denn alle Thiere liebten ihn. Da lief ich davon.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0015","text":"Kam ich also umsonst in diese Wälder und Berge? Da entschloss sich mein Herz, dass ich einen Anderen suchte, den Frömmsten aller Derer, die nicht an Gott glauben—, dass ich Zarathustra suchte!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0016","text":"Also sprach der Greis und blickte scharfen Auges Den an, welcher vor ihm stand; Zarathustra aber ergriff die Hand des alten Papstes und betrachtete sie lange mit Bewunderung.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0017","text":"„Siehe da, du Ehrwürdiger, sagte er dann, welche schöne und lange Hand! Das ist die Hand eines Solchen, der immer Segen ausgetheilt hat. Nun aber hält sie Den fest, welchen du suchst, mich, Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0018","text":"Ich bin’s, der gottlose Zarathustra, der da spricht: wer ist gottloser als ich, dass ich mich seiner Unterweisung freue?“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0019","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra und durchbohrte mit seinen Blicken die Gedanken und Hintergedanken des alten Papstes. Endlich begann dieser:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0020","text":"„Wer ihn am meisten liebte und besass, der hat ihn nun am meisten auch verloren -:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0021","text":"—siehe, ich selber bin wohl von uns Beiden jetzt der Gottlosere? Aber wer könnte daran sich freuen!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0022","text":"„Du dientest ihm bis zuletzt, fragte Zarathustra nachdenklich, nach einem tiefen Schweigen, du weisst, wie er starb? Ist es wahr, was man spricht, dass ihn das Mitleiden erwürgte,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0023","text":"—dass er es sah, wie der Mensch am Kreuze hieng, und es nicht ertrug, dass die Liebe zum Menschen seine Hölle und zuletzt sein Tod wurde?“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0024","text":"Der alte Papst aber antwortete nicht, sondern blickte scheu und mit einem schmerzlichen und düsteren Ausdrucke zur Seite.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0025","text":"„Lass ihn fahren, sagte Zarathustra nach einem langen Nachdenken, indem er immer noch dem alten Manne gerade in’s Auge blickte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0026","text":"Lass ihn fahren, er ist dahin. Und ob es dich auch ehrt, dass du diesem Todten nur Gutes nachredest, so weisst du so gut als ich, wer er war; und dass er wunderliche Wege gieng.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0027","text":"„Unter drei Augen gesprochen, sagte erheitert der alte Papst (denn er war auf Einem Auge blind), in Dingen Gottes bin ich aufgeklärter als Zarathustra selber —und darf es sein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0028","text":"Meine Liebe diente ihm lange Jahre, mein Wille gierig allem seinen Willen nach. Ein guter Diener aber weiss Alles, und Mancherlei auch, was sein Herr sich selbst verbirgt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0029","text":"Es war ein verborgener Gott, voller Heimlichkeit. Wahrlich zu einem Sohne sogar kam er nicht anders als auf Schleichwegen. An der Thür seines Glaubens steht der Ehebruch.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0030","text":"Wer ihn als einen Gott der Liebe preist, denkt nicht hoch genug von der Liebe selber. Wollte dieser Gott nicht auch Richter sein? Aber der Liebende liebt jenseits von Lohn und Vergeltung.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0031","text":"Als er jung war, dieser Gott aus dem Morgenlande, da war er hart und rachsüchtig und erbaute sich eine Hölle zum Ergötzen seiner Lieblinge.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0032","text":"Endlich aber wurde er alt und weich und mürbe und mitleidig, einem Grossvater ähnlicher als einem Vater, am ähnlichsten aber einer wackeligen alten Grossmutter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0033","text":"Da sass er, welk, in seinem Ofenwinkel, härmte sich ob seiner schwachen Beine, weltmüde, willensmüde, und erstickte eines Tags an seinem allzugrossen Mitleiden.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0034","text":"„Du alter Papst, sagte hier Zarathustra dazwischen, hast du Das mit Augen angesehn? Es könnte wohl so abgegangen sein: so, und auch anders. Wenn Götter sterben, sterben sie immer viele Arten Todes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0035","text":"Aber wohlan! So oder so, so und so—er ist dahin! Er gieng meinen Ohren und Augen wider den Geschmack, Schlimmeres möchte ich ihm nicht nachsagen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0036","text":"Ich liebe Alles, was hell blickt und redlich redet. Aber er—du weisst es ja, du alter Priester, es war Etwas von deiner Art an ihm, von Priester-Art—er war vieldeutig.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0037","text":"Er war auch undeutlich. Was hat er uns darob gezürnt, dieser Zornschnauber, dass wir ihn schlecht verstanden Aber warum sprach er nicht reinlicher?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0038","text":"Und lag es an unsern Ohren, warum gab er uns Ohren, die ihn schlecht hörten? War Schlamm in unsern Ohren, wohlan! wer legte ihn hinein?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0039","text":"Zu Vieles missrieth ihm, diesem Töpfer, der nicht ausgelernt hatte! Dass er aber Rache an seinen Töpfen und Geschöpfen nahm, dafür dass sie ihm schlecht geriethen,—das war eine Sünde wider den guten Geschmack .","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0040","text":"Es giebt auch in der Frömmigkeit guten Geschmack: der sprach endlich „Fort mit einem solchen Gotte! Lieber keinen Gott, lieber auf eigne Faust Schicksal machen, lieber Narr sein, lieber selber Gott sein!““","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0041","text":"—„Was höre ich! sprach hier der alte Papst mit gespitzten Ohren; oh Zarathustra, du bist frömmer als du glaubst, mit einem solchen Unglauben! Irgend ein Gott in dir bekehrte dich zu deiner Gottlosigkeit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0042","text":"Ist es nicht deine Frömmigkeit selber, die dich nicht mehr an einen Gott glauben lässt? Und deine übergrosse Redlichkeit wird dich auch noch jenseits von Gut und Böse wegfuhren!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0043","text":"Siehe, doch, was blieb dir aufgespart? Du hast Augen und Hand und Mund, die sind zum Segnen vorher bestimmt seit Ewigkeit. Man segnet nicht mit der Hand allein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0044","text":"In deiner Nähe, ob du schon der Gottloseste sein willst, wittere ich einen heimlichen Weih- und Wohlgeruch von langen Segnungen: mir wird wohl und wehe dabei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0045","text":"Lass mich deinen Gast sein, oh Zarathustra, für eine einzige Nacht! Nirgends auf Erden wird es mir jetzt wohler als bei dir!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0046","text":"„Amen! So soll es sein! sprach Zarathustra mit grosser Verwunderung, dort hinauf führt der Weg, da liegt die Höhle Zarathustra’s.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0047","text":"Gerne, fürwahr, würde ich dich selber dahin geleiten, du Ehrwürdiger, denn ich liebe alle frommen Menschen. Aber jetzt ruft mich eilig ein Nothschrei weg von dir.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0048","text":"In meinem Bereiche soll mir Niemand zu Schaden kommen; meine Höhle ist ein guter Hafen. Und am liebsten möchte ich jedweden Traurigen wieder auf festes Land und feste Beine stellen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0049","text":"Wer aber nähme dir deine Schwermuth von der Schulter? Dazu bin ich zu schwach. Lange, wahrlich, möchten wir warten, bis dir Einer deinen Gott wieder aufweckt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0050","text":"Dieser alte Gott nämlich lebt nicht mehr: der ist gründlich todt.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0051","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"d8c786970c24be5946f22b56b38edcba3320535f6996a9e45895fd6ea1ddf1b0","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183600540605Z_1eeaae87","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Out of Service","source_sha256":"d8c786970c24be5946f22b56b38edcba3320535f6996a9e45895fd6ea1ddf1b0","sha256":"9dbd3d7637799dc2f4d0e2b897e5692a4902365358ed135b1acac9547c611534","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:36:00.540635+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0001","text":"But not long after Zarathustra had freed himself from the magician, he again saw someone sitting by the way, someone eager-looking, namely a black, tall man with a gaunt pale face: he greatly vexed him. “Alas,” he said to his heart, “there sits shrouded melancholy; he seems to me of the priestly kind: what do they want in my realm?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0002","text":"“What! Hardly have I escaped that magician: must another black artist cross my path again,—”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0003","text":"—some sorcerer who lays on hands, a dark worker of miracles by God’s grace, an anointed slanderer of the world, whom the devil may take!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0004","text":"But the devil is never where he would be in place: he always comes too late, this accursed dwarf and clubfoot!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0005","text":"Thus Zarathustra cursed impatiently in his heart, and thought how he might slip past the black man with averted gaze: but behold, it turned out otherwise. For at that very moment the seated man had already caught sight of him; and, not unlike one upon whom an unexpected piece of good fortune befalls, he leapt up and rushed at Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0006","text":"“Whoever you may be, you wayfarer,” he said, “help one who has gone astray, one who is seeking, an old man who can easily come to harm here!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0007","text":"“This world here is strange and remote to me; I also heard wild beasts howling; and he who might have offered me protection is himself no more.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0008","text":"“I sought the last pious man, a saint and hermit, who alone in his forest had still heard nothing of what all the world knows today.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0009","text":"“What does all the world know today?” asked Zarathustra. “Perhaps this, that the old God no longer lives, in whom all the world once believed?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0010","text":"“You say it,” the old man replied sadly. “And I served this old God until his last hour.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0011","text":"“But now I am out of service, without a master, and yet not free; nor merry for even an hour any more, save in recollections.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0012","text":"“For this reason I climbed into these mountains, so that at last I might again make myself a feast, as befits an old Pope and Father of the Church: for know that I am the last Pope!—a feast of pious recollections and divine services.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0013","text":"“But now he himself is dead, the most pious man, that saint in the forest who constantly praised his God with singing and humming.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0014","text":"“I found him himself no longer when I found his hut,—but two wolves in it, which howled over his death—for all the animals loved him. Then I ran away.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0015","text":"“Had I then come into these forests and mountains in vain? Then my heart resolved that I should seek another, the most pious of all those who do not believe in God—that I should seek Zarathustra!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0016","text":"Thus spoke the old man and looked with keen eyes at him who stood before him; but Zarathustra took the old Pope’s hand and gazed at it long with admiration.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0017","text":"“Behold, venerable one,” he then said, “what a beautiful and long hand! This is the hand of one who has always bestowed blessings. But now it holds fast the one whom you seek, me, Zarathustra.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0018","text":"“It is I, the godless Zarathustra, who speaks thus: who is more godless than I, that I might rejoice in his instruction?”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0019","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra, and with his glances pierced the thoughts and afterthoughts of the old Pope. At last the latter began:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0020","text":"“He who loved and possessed him most has now also lost him most—:”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0021","text":"—behold, I myself am perhaps now the more godless of us two? But who could rejoice in that!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0022","text":"“You served him to the end,” Zarathustra asked thoughtfully, after a deep silence, “you know how he died? Is what is said true, that compassion strangled him,”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0023","text":"—that he saw how man hung upon the cross, and could not bear it, that love for man became his hell and finally his death?”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0024","text":"But the old Pope did not answer, but looked timidly to one side, with a painful and gloomy expression.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0025","text":"“Let him go,” said Zarathustra after long reflection, while still looking the old man straight in the eye.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0026","text":"Let him go, he is gone. And though it does you honour that you speak only good of this dead man, you know as well as I who he was; and that he went strange ways.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0027","text":"“Between ourselves,” said the old Pope cheerfully (for he was blind in one eye), “in matters of God I am more enlightened than Zarathustra himself—and may be so.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0028","text":"My love served him for many years, my will eagerly followed all his will. But a good servant knows everything, and many a thing, too, that his master conceals from himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0029","text":"He was a hidden God, full of secrecy. Truly, even to a son he came only by stealthy ways. At the door of his faith stands adultery.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0030","text":"Whoever praises him as a God of love does not think highly enough of love itself. Did this God not also wish to be a judge? But the lover loves beyond reward and retribution.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0031","text":"When he was young, this God from the Orient, he was hard and vengeful and built himself a hell for the delight of his favourites.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0032","text":"But at last he became old and soft and frail and compassionate, more like a grandfather than a father, yet most like a tottering old grandmother.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0033","text":"There he sat, withered, in his stove-corner, grieving over his weak legs, weary of the world, weary of will, and one day choked on his all-too-great compassion.”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0034","text":"“You old Pope,” Zarathustra here interposed, “did you see that with your own eyes? It may well have happened thus: thus, and otherwise too. When gods die, they always die many kinds of death.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0035","text":"But come! Thus or thus, thus and thus—he is gone! He offended my ears and eyes; I would not speak worse of him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0036","text":"I love all that looks brightly and speaks honestly. But he—you know it, old priest—there was something of your kind about him, something priestly—he was ambiguous.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0037","text":"He was also indistinct. How he raged at us, this snorter of wrath, because we understood him poorly! But why did he not speak more plainly?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0038","text":"And if it lay in our ears, why did he give us ears that heard him poorly? Was there mud in our ears—well then! who put it in?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0039","text":"Too much went wrong for him, this potter who had not finished learning! But that he took revenge on his pots and creatures because they turned out badly for him—that was a sin against good taste.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0040","text":"There is good taste even in piety: at last it said, “Away with such a God! Better no God, better to make fate by one’s own hand, better to be a fool, better oneself to be God!””","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0041","text":"—“What do I hear!” said the old Pope here, with pricked ears; “oh Zarathustra, you are more pious than you believe, with such unbelief! Some God in you converted you to your godlessness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0042","text":"Is it not your piety itself that no longer lets you believe in a God? And your excessive honesty will yet lead you beyond good and evil!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0043","text":"See, then, what has been reserved for you? You have eyes and hand and mouth, which have been destined for blessing from eternity. One does not bless with the hand alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0044","text":"In your vicinity, though you would be the most godless of men, I scent a secret consecratory and salutary fragrance of long blessings: it makes me feel well and ill.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0045","text":"Let me be your guest, oh Zarathustra, for a single night! Nowhere on earth shall I feel better than with you!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0046","text":"“Amen! So be it!” said Zarathustra in great astonishment; “the path leads up there, there lies Zarathustra’s cave.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0047","text":"Gladly, truly, I would myself escort you thither, you venerable one, for I love all pious people. But now a cry of distress urgently calls me away from you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0048","text":"Within my domain no one shall come to harm; my cave is a good harbour. And above all I should like to set every sad person again upon firm land and firm legs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0049","text":"But who would take your melancholy from your shoulder? For that I am too weak. Long indeed might we wait until someone awakens your God again for you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0050","text":"For this old God no longer lives: he is thoroughly dead.” —","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0051","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_AUSD:P0051"]}],"source_digest":"608b27638355c9b91be87563aeba740d07891c4a46ef00665fe78627e19f9c65","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Ausser Dienst"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Ausser Dienst","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM","title":"The Ugliest Man","order":134,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_DHSM","text":"—Und wieder liefen Zarathustra’s Füsse durch Berge und Wälder, und seine Augen suchten und suchten, aber nirgends war Der zu sehen, welchen sie sehn wollten, der grosse Nothleidende und Nothschreiende. Auf dem ganzen Wege aber frohlockte er in seinem Herzen und war dankbar. „Welche guten Dinge, sprach er, schenkte mir doch dieser Tag, zum Entgelt, dass er schlimm begann! Welche seltsamen Unterredner fand ich!\n\nAn deren Worten will ich lange nun kauen gleich als an guten Körnern; klein soll mein Zahn sie mahlen und malmen, bis sie mir wie Milch in die Seele fliessen!“—\n\nAls aber der Weg wieder um einen Felsen bog, veränderte sich mit Einem Male die Landschaft, und Zarathustra trat in ein Reich des Todes. Hier starrten schwarze und rothe Klippen empor: kein Gras, kein Baum, keine Vogelstimme. Es war nämlich ein Thal, welches alle Thiere mieden, auch die Raubthiere-, nur dass eine Art hässlicher, dicker, grüner Schlangen, wenn sie alt wurden, hierher kamen, um zu sterben. Darum nannten diess Thal die Hirten: Schlangen-Tod.\n\nZarathustra aber versank in eine schwarze Erinnerung, denn ihm war, als habe er schon ein Mal in diesem Thal gestanden. Und vieles Schwere legte sich ihm über den Sinn: also, dass er langsam gieng und immer langsamer und endlich still stand. Da aber sahe er, als er die Augen aufthat, Etwas, das am Wege sass, gestaltet wie ein Mensch und kaum wie ein Mensch, etwas Unaussprechliches. Und mit Einem Schlage überfiel Zarathustra die grosse Scham darob, dass er so Etwas mit den Augen angesehn habe: erröthend bis hinauf an sein weisses Haar, wandte er den Blick ab und hob den Fuss, dass er diese schlimme Stelle verlasse. Da aber wurde die todte Öde laut: vom Boden auf nämlich quoll es gurgelnd und röchelnd, wie Wasser Nachts durch verstopfte Wasser-Röhren gurgelt und röchelt; und zuletzt wurde daraus eine Menschen-Stimme und Menschen-Rede:—die lautete also.\n\n„Zarathustra! Zarathustra! Rathe mein Räthsel! Sprich, sprich! Was ist die Rache am Zeugen ?\n\nIch locke dich zurück, hier ist glattes Eis! Sieh zu, sieh zu, ob dein Stolz sich hier nicht die Beine bricht!\n\nDu dünkst dich weise, du stolzer Zarathustra! So rathe doch das Räthsel, du harter Nüsseknacker,—das Räthsel, das ich bin! So sprich doch—wer bin ich !“\n\n—Als aber Zarathustra diese Worte gehört hatte,—was glaubt ihr wohl, dass sich da mit seiner Seele zutrug? Das Mitleiden fiel ihn an; und er sank mit Einem Male nieder, wie ein Eichbaum, der lange vielen Holzschlägern widerstanden hat,—schwer, plötzlich, zum Schrecken selber für Die, welche ihn fällen wollten. Aber schon stand er wieder vom Boden auf, und sein Antlitz wurde hart.\n\n„Ich erkenne dich wohl, sprach er mit einer erzenen Stimme: du bist der Mörder Gottes! Lass mich gehn.\n\nDu ertrugst Den nicht, der dich sah,—der dich immer und durch und durch sah, du hässlichster Mensch! Du nahmst Rache an diesem Zeugen!“\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra und wollte davon; aber der Unaussprechliche fasste nach einem Zipfel seines Gewandes und begann von Neuem zu gurgeln und nach Worten zu suchen. „Bleib!“ sagte er endlich—\n\n—„bleib! Geh nicht vorüber! Ich errieth, welche Axt dich zu Boden schlug: Heil dir, oh Zarathustra, dass du wieder stehst!\n\nDu erriethest, ich weiss es gut, wie Dem zu Muthe ist, der ihn tödtete,—dem Mörder Gottes. Bleib! Setze dich her zu mir, es ist nicht umsonst.\n\nZu wem wollte ich, wenn nicht zu dir? Bleib, setze dich! Blicke mich aber nicht an! Ehre also—meine Hässlichkeit!\n\nSie verfolgen mich: nun bist du meine letzte Zuflucht. Nicht mit ihrem Hasse, nicht mit ihren Häschern:—oh solcher Verfolgung würde ich spotten und stolz und froh sein!\n\nWar nicht aller Erfolg bisher bei den Gut-Verfolgten? Und wer gut verfolgt, lernt leicht folgen :—ist er doch einmal—hinterher! Aber ihr Mitleid ist’s—\n\n—ihr Mitleid ist’s, vor dem ich flüchte und dir zuflüchte. Oh Zarathustra, schütze mich, du meine letzte Zuflucht, du Einziger, der mich errieth:\n\n—du erriethest, wie Dem zu Muthe ist, welcher ihn tödtete. Bleib! Und willst du gehn, du Ungeduldiger: geh nicht den Weg, den ich kam. Der Weg ist schlecht.\n\nZürnst du mir, dass ich zu lange schon rede-rade-breche? Dass ich schon dir rathe? Aber wisse, ich bin’s, der hässlichste Mensch,\n\n—der auch die grössten schwersten Füsse hat. Wo ich gieng, ist der Weg schlecht. Ich trete alle Wege todt und zu Schanden.\n\nDass du aber an mir vorübergiengst, schweigend; dass du erröthetest, ich sah es wohl: daran erkannte ich dich als Zarathustra.\n\nJedweder Andere hätte mir sein Almosen zugeworfen, sein Mitleiden, mit Blick und Rede. Aber dazu—bin ich nicht Bettler genug, das erriethest du—\n\n—dazu bin ich zu reich , reich an Grossem, an Furchtbarem, am Hässlichsten, am Unaussprechlichsten! Deine Scham, oh Zarathustra, ehrte mich!\n\nMit Noth kam ich heraus aus dem Gedräng der Mitleidigen,—dass ich den Einzigen fände, der heute lehrt „Mitleiden ist zudringlich“—dich, oh Zarathustra!\n\n—sei es eines Gottes, sei es der Menschen Mitleiden: Mitleiden geht gegen die Scham. Und nicht-helfen-wollen kann vornehmer sein als jene Tugend, die zuspringt.\n\nDas aber heisst heute Tugend selber bei allen kleinen Leuten, das Mitleiden:—die haben keine Ehrfurcht vor grossem Unglück, vor grosser Hässlichkeit, vor grossem Missrathen.\n\nÜber diese Alle blicke ich hinweg, wie ein Hund über die Rücken wimmelnder Schafheerden wegblickt. Es sind kleine wohlwollige wohlwillige graue Leute.\n\nWie ein Reiher verachtend über flache Teiche wegblickt, mit zurückgelegtem Kopfe: so blicke ich über das Gewimmel grauer kleiner Wellen und Willen und Seelen weg.\n\nZu lange hat man ihnen Recht gegeben, diesen kleinen Leuten: so gab man ihnen endlich auch die Macht—nun lehren sie: „gut ist nur, was kleine Leute gut heissen.“\n\nUnd „Wahrheit“ heisst heute, was der Prediger sprach, der selber aus ihnen herkam, jener wunderliche Heilige und Fürsprecher der kleinen Leute, welcher von sich zeugte „ich—bin die Wahrheit.“\n\nDieser Unbescheidne macht nun lange schon den kleinen Leuten den Kamm hoch schwellen—er, der keinen kleinen Irrthum lehrte, als er lehrte „ich—bin die Wahrheit.“\n\nWard einem Unbescheidnen jemals höflicher geantwortet?—Du aber, oh Zarathustra, giengst an ihm vorüber und sprachst: „Nein! Nein! Drei Mal Nein!“\n\nDu warntest vor seinem Irrthum, du warntest als der Erste vor dem Mitleiden—nicht Alle, nicht Keinen, sondern dich und deine Art.\n\nDu schämst dich an der Scham des grossen Leidenden; und wahrlich, wenn du sprichst „von dem Mitleiden her kommt eine grosse Wolke, habt Acht, ihr Menschen!“\n\n—wenn du lehrst „alle Schaffenden sind hart, alle grosse Liebe ist über ihrem Mitleiden“: oh Zarathustra, wie gut dünkst du mich eingelernt auf Wetter-Zeichen!\n\nDu selber aber—warne dich selber auch vor deinem Mitleiden! Denn Viele sind zu dir unterwegs, viele Leidende, Zweifelnde, Verzweifelnde, Ertrinkende, Frierende—\n\nIch warne dich auch vor mir. Du erriethest mein bestes, schlimmstes Räthsel, mich selber und was ich that. Ich kenne die Axt, die dich fällt.\n\nAber er— musste sterben: er sah mit Augen, welche Alles sahn,—er sah des Menschen Tiefen und Gründe, alle seine verhehlte Schmach und Hässlichkeit.\n\nSein Mitleiden kannte keine Scham: er kroch in meine schmutzigsten Winkel. Dieser Neugierigste, Über-Zudringliche, Über-Mitleidige musste sterben.\n\nEr sah immer mich : an einem solchen Zeugen wollte ich Rache haben—oder selber nicht leben.\n\nDer Gott, der Alles sah, auch den Menschen dieser Gott musste sterben! Der Mensch erträgt es nicht, dass solch ein Zeuge lebt.“\n\nAlso, sprach der hässlichste Mensch. Zarathustra aber erhob sich und schickte sich an fortzugehn: denn ihn fröstelte bis in seine Eingeweide.\n\n„Du Unaussprechlicher, sagte er, du warntest mich vor deinem Wege. Zum Danke dafür lobe ich dir den meinen. Siehe, dort hinauf liegt die Höhle Zarathustra’s.\n\nMeine Höhle ist gross und tief und hat viele Winkel; da findet der Versteckteste sein Versteck. Und dicht bei ihr sind hundert Schlüpfe und Schliche für kriechendes, flatterndes und springendes Gethier.\n\nDu Ausgestossener, der du dich selber ausstiessest, du willst nicht unter Menschen und Menschen-Mitleid wohnen? Wohlan, so thu’s mir gleich! So lernst du auch von mir; nur der Thäter lernt.\n\nUnd rede zuerst und -nächst mit meinen Thieren! Das stolzeste Thier und das klügste Thier—die möchten uns Beiden wohl die rechten Rathgeber sein!“—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra und gieng seiner Wege, nachdenklicher und langsamer noch als zuvor: denn er fragte sich Vieles und wusste sich nicht leicht zu antworten.\n\n„Wie arm ist doch der Mensch! dachte er in seinem Herzen, wie hässlich, wie röchelnd, wie voll verborgener Scham!\n\nMan sagt mir, dass der Mensch sich selber liebe: ach, wie gross muss diese Selber-Liebe sein! Wie viel Verachtung hat sie wider sich!\n\nAuch dieser da liebte sich, wie er sich verachtete,—ein grosser Liebender ist er mir und ein grosser Verächter.\n\nKeinen fand ich noch, der sich tiefer verachtet hätte: auch Das ist Höhe. Wehe, war Der vielleicht der höhere Mensch, dessen Schrei ich hörte?\n\nIch liebe die grossen Verachtenden. Der Mensch aber ist Etwas, das überwunden werden muss.“—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0001","text":"—Und wieder liefen Zarathustra’s Füsse durch Berge und Wälder, und seine Augen suchten und suchten, aber nirgends war Der zu sehen, welchen sie sehn wollten, der grosse Nothleidende und Nothschreiende. Auf dem ganzen Wege aber frohlockte er in seinem Herzen und war dankbar. „Welche guten Dinge, sprach er, schenkte mir doch dieser Tag, zum Entgelt, dass er schlimm begann! Welche seltsamen Unterredner fand ich!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0002","text":"An deren Worten will ich lange nun kauen gleich als an guten Körnern; klein soll mein Zahn sie mahlen und malmen, bis sie mir wie Milch in die Seele fliessen!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0003","text":"Als aber der Weg wieder um einen Felsen bog, veränderte sich mit Einem Male die Landschaft, und Zarathustra trat in ein Reich des Todes. Hier starrten schwarze und rothe Klippen empor: kein Gras, kein Baum, keine Vogelstimme. Es war nämlich ein Thal, welches alle Thiere mieden, auch die Raubthiere-, nur dass eine Art hässlicher, dicker, grüner Schlangen, wenn sie alt wurden, hierher kamen, um zu sterben. Darum nannten diess Thal die Hirten: Schlangen-Tod.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0004","text":"Zarathustra aber versank in eine schwarze Erinnerung, denn ihm war, als habe er schon ein Mal in diesem Thal gestanden. Und vieles Schwere legte sich ihm über den Sinn: also, dass er langsam gieng und immer langsamer und endlich still stand. Da aber sahe er, als er die Augen aufthat, Etwas, das am Wege sass, gestaltet wie ein Mensch und kaum wie ein Mensch, etwas Unaussprechliches. Und mit Einem Schlage überfiel Zarathustra die grosse Scham darob, dass er so Etwas mit den Augen angesehn habe: erröthend bis hinauf an sein weisses Haar, wandte er den Blick ab und hob den Fuss, dass er diese schlimme Stelle verlasse. Da aber wurde die todte Öde laut: vom Boden auf nämlich quoll es gurgelnd und röchelnd, wie Wasser Nachts durch verstopfte Wasser-Röhren gurgelt und röchelt; und zuletzt wurde daraus eine Menschen-Stimme und Menschen-Rede:—die lautete also.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0005","text":"„Zarathustra! Zarathustra! Rathe mein Räthsel! Sprich, sprich! Was ist die Rache am Zeugen ?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0006","text":"Ich locke dich zurück, hier ist glattes Eis! Sieh zu, sieh zu, ob dein Stolz sich hier nicht die Beine bricht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0007","text":"Du dünkst dich weise, du stolzer Zarathustra! So rathe doch das Räthsel, du harter Nüsseknacker,—das Räthsel, das ich bin! So sprich doch—wer bin ich !“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0008","text":"—Als aber Zarathustra diese Worte gehört hatte,—was glaubt ihr wohl, dass sich da mit seiner Seele zutrug? Das Mitleiden fiel ihn an; und er sank mit Einem Male nieder, wie ein Eichbaum, der lange vielen Holzschlägern widerstanden hat,—schwer, plötzlich, zum Schrecken selber für Die, welche ihn fällen wollten. Aber schon stand er wieder vom Boden auf, und sein Antlitz wurde hart.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0009","text":"„Ich erkenne dich wohl, sprach er mit einer erzenen Stimme: du bist der Mörder Gottes! Lass mich gehn.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0010","text":"Du ertrugst Den nicht, der dich sah,—der dich immer und durch und durch sah, du hässlichster Mensch! Du nahmst Rache an diesem Zeugen!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0011","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra und wollte davon; aber der Unaussprechliche fasste nach einem Zipfel seines Gewandes und begann von Neuem zu gurgeln und nach Worten zu suchen. „Bleib!“ sagte er endlich—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0012","text":"—„bleib! Geh nicht vorüber! Ich errieth, welche Axt dich zu Boden schlug: Heil dir, oh Zarathustra, dass du wieder stehst!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0013","text":"Du erriethest, ich weiss es gut, wie Dem zu Muthe ist, der ihn tödtete,—dem Mörder Gottes. Bleib! Setze dich her zu mir, es ist nicht umsonst.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0014","text":"Zu wem wollte ich, wenn nicht zu dir? Bleib, setze dich! Blicke mich aber nicht an! Ehre also—meine Hässlichkeit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0015","text":"Sie verfolgen mich: nun bist du meine letzte Zuflucht. Nicht mit ihrem Hasse, nicht mit ihren Häschern:—oh solcher Verfolgung würde ich spotten und stolz und froh sein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0016","text":"War nicht aller Erfolg bisher bei den Gut-Verfolgten? Und wer gut verfolgt, lernt leicht folgen :—ist er doch einmal—hinterher! Aber ihr Mitleid ist’s—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0017","text":"—ihr Mitleid ist’s, vor dem ich flüchte und dir zuflüchte. Oh Zarathustra, schütze mich, du meine letzte Zuflucht, du Einziger, der mich errieth:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0018","text":"—du erriethest, wie Dem zu Muthe ist, welcher ihn tödtete. Bleib! Und willst du gehn, du Ungeduldiger: geh nicht den Weg, den ich kam. Der Weg ist schlecht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0019","text":"Zürnst du mir, dass ich zu lange schon rede-rade-breche? Dass ich schon dir rathe? Aber wisse, ich bin’s, der hässlichste Mensch,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0020","text":"—der auch die grössten schwersten Füsse hat. Wo ich gieng, ist der Weg schlecht. Ich trete alle Wege todt und zu Schanden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0021","text":"Dass du aber an mir vorübergiengst, schweigend; dass du erröthetest, ich sah es wohl: daran erkannte ich dich als Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0022","text":"Jedweder Andere hätte mir sein Almosen zugeworfen, sein Mitleiden, mit Blick und Rede. Aber dazu—bin ich nicht Bettler genug, das erriethest du—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0023","text":"—dazu bin ich zu reich , reich an Grossem, an Furchtbarem, am Hässlichsten, am Unaussprechlichsten! Deine Scham, oh Zarathustra, ehrte mich!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0024","text":"Mit Noth kam ich heraus aus dem Gedräng der Mitleidigen,—dass ich den Einzigen fände, der heute lehrt „Mitleiden ist zudringlich“—dich, oh Zarathustra!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0025","text":"—sei es eines Gottes, sei es der Menschen Mitleiden: Mitleiden geht gegen die Scham. Und nicht-helfen-wollen kann vornehmer sein als jene Tugend, die zuspringt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0026","text":"Das aber heisst heute Tugend selber bei allen kleinen Leuten, das Mitleiden:—die haben keine Ehrfurcht vor grossem Unglück, vor grosser Hässlichkeit, vor grossem Missrathen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0027","text":"Über diese Alle blicke ich hinweg, wie ein Hund über die Rücken wimmelnder Schafheerden wegblickt. Es sind kleine wohlwollige wohlwillige graue Leute.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0028","text":"Wie ein Reiher verachtend über flache Teiche wegblickt, mit zurückgelegtem Kopfe: so blicke ich über das Gewimmel grauer kleiner Wellen und Willen und Seelen weg.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0029","text":"Zu lange hat man ihnen Recht gegeben, diesen kleinen Leuten: so gab man ihnen endlich auch die Macht—nun lehren sie: „gut ist nur, was kleine Leute gut heissen.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0030","text":"Und „Wahrheit“ heisst heute, was der Prediger sprach, der selber aus ihnen herkam, jener wunderliche Heilige und Fürsprecher der kleinen Leute, welcher von sich zeugte „ich—bin die Wahrheit.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0031","text":"Dieser Unbescheidne macht nun lange schon den kleinen Leuten den Kamm hoch schwellen—er, der keinen kleinen Irrthum lehrte, als er lehrte „ich—bin die Wahrheit.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0032","text":"Ward einem Unbescheidnen jemals höflicher geantwortet?—Du aber, oh Zarathustra, giengst an ihm vorüber und sprachst: „Nein! Nein! Drei Mal Nein!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0033","text":"Du warntest vor seinem Irrthum, du warntest als der Erste vor dem Mitleiden—nicht Alle, nicht Keinen, sondern dich und deine Art.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0034","text":"Du schämst dich an der Scham des grossen Leidenden; und wahrlich, wenn du sprichst „von dem Mitleiden her kommt eine grosse Wolke, habt Acht, ihr Menschen!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0035","text":"—wenn du lehrst „alle Schaffenden sind hart, alle grosse Liebe ist über ihrem Mitleiden“: oh Zarathustra, wie gut dünkst du mich eingelernt auf Wetter-Zeichen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0036","text":"Du selber aber—warne dich selber auch vor deinem Mitleiden! Denn Viele sind zu dir unterwegs, viele Leidende, Zweifelnde, Verzweifelnde, Ertrinkende, Frierende—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0037","text":"Ich warne dich auch vor mir. Du erriethest mein bestes, schlimmstes Räthsel, mich selber und was ich that. Ich kenne die Axt, die dich fällt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0038","text":"Aber er— musste sterben: er sah mit Augen, welche Alles sahn,—er sah des Menschen Tiefen und Gründe, alle seine verhehlte Schmach und Hässlichkeit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0039","text":"Sein Mitleiden kannte keine Scham: er kroch in meine schmutzigsten Winkel. Dieser Neugierigste, Über-Zudringliche, Über-Mitleidige musste sterben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0040","text":"Er sah immer mich : an einem solchen Zeugen wollte ich Rache haben—oder selber nicht leben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0041","text":"Der Gott, der Alles sah, auch den Menschen dieser Gott musste sterben! Der Mensch erträgt es nicht, dass solch ein Zeuge lebt.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0042","text":"Also, sprach der hässlichste Mensch. Zarathustra aber erhob sich und schickte sich an fortzugehn: denn ihn fröstelte bis in seine Eingeweide.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0043","text":"„Du Unaussprechlicher, sagte er, du warntest mich vor deinem Wege. Zum Danke dafür lobe ich dir den meinen. Siehe, dort hinauf liegt die Höhle Zarathustra’s.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0044","text":"Meine Höhle ist gross und tief und hat viele Winkel; da findet der Versteckteste sein Versteck. Und dicht bei ihr sind hundert Schlüpfe und Schliche für kriechendes, flatterndes und springendes Gethier.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0045","text":"Du Ausgestossener, der du dich selber ausstiessest, du willst nicht unter Menschen und Menschen-Mitleid wohnen? Wohlan, so thu’s mir gleich! So lernst du auch von mir; nur der Thäter lernt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0046","text":"Und rede zuerst und -nächst mit meinen Thieren! Das stolzeste Thier und das klügste Thier—die möchten uns Beiden wohl die rechten Rathgeber sein!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0047","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra und gieng seiner Wege, nachdenklicher und langsamer noch als zuvor: denn er fragte sich Vieles und wusste sich nicht leicht zu antworten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0048","text":"„Wie arm ist doch der Mensch! dachte er in seinem Herzen, wie hässlich, wie röchelnd, wie voll verborgener Scham!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0049","text":"Man sagt mir, dass der Mensch sich selber liebe: ach, wie gross muss diese Selber-Liebe sein! Wie viel Verachtung hat sie wider sich!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0050","text":"Auch dieser da liebte sich, wie er sich verachtete,—ein grosser Liebender ist er mir und ein grosser Verächter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0051","text":"Keinen fand ich noch, der sich tiefer verachtet hätte: auch Das ist Höhe. Wehe, war Der vielleicht der höhere Mensch, dessen Schrei ich hörte?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0052","text":"Ich liebe die grossen Verachtenden. Der Mensch aber ist Etwas, das überwunden werden muss.“—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"d83e4cc1a54651e608c957e8a77ab47ebf98148b56571a17403f4b56d700a1a0","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183633391404Z_eed25fc3","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"The Ugliest Man","source_sha256":"d83e4cc1a54651e608c957e8a77ab47ebf98148b56571a17403f4b56d700a1a0","sha256":"451dc2ccbe108e9f6d37de3b2d18e65c4b2a9bf71bb62b7e19d77967d90d32fe","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:36:33.391432+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0001","text":"—And once again Zarathustra’s feet ran through mountains and forests, and his eyes sought and sought, but nowhere was he to be seen whom they wished to see, the great sufferer and crier of distress. But all along the way he rejoiced in his heart and was grateful. “What good things,” he said, “this day has bestowed upon me, in recompense for its having begun badly! What strange interlocutors I found!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0002","text":"I shall now chew long upon their words as upon good grains; my teeth shall grind and crush them small, until they flow into my soul like milk!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0003","text":"But when the path bent once more around a rock, the landscape suddenly changed, and Zarathustra entered a realm of death. Here black and red cliffs stared upwards: no grass, no tree, no bird’s call. For it was a valley which all animals avoided, even beasts of prey—except that a kind of ugly, fat, green snakes, when they grew old, came here to die. Therefore the shepherds called this valley: Serpent-Death.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0004","text":"But Zarathustra sank into a black recollection, for it seemed to him that he had once before stood in this valley. And much heaviness settled upon his mind, so that he walked slowly and more and more slowly and finally stood still. But then, when he opened his eyes, he saw Something sitting by the path, shaped like a human being and scarcely like a human being, something inexpressible. And in a single stroke great shame assailed Zarathustra for having beheld such a thing with his eyes: blushing up to his white hair, he turned his gaze away and raised his foot in order to leave this evil place. But then the dead desolation became vocal: for from the ground there welled gurgling and rattling, as water gurgles and rattles at night through blocked water-pipes; and at last it became a human voice and human speech:—it sounded thus.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0005","text":"“Zarathustra! Zarathustra! Guess my riddle! Speak, speak! What is revenge upon the witness?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0006","text":"I lure you back; here is smooth ice! Take care, take care that your pride does not break its legs here!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0007","text":"You think yourself wise, you proud Zarathustra! Then guess the riddle, you hard nutcracker,—the riddle that I am! Then speak—who am I!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0008","text":"—But when Zarathustra had heard these words,—what do you suppose then happened to his soul? Pity assailed him; and he sank down at once, like an oak which has long withstood many woodcutters’ blows,—heavily, suddenly, terrifying even those who had wished to fell it. But already he rose again from the ground, and his countenance became hard.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0009","text":"“I know you well,” he said in a brazen voice: “you are the murderer of God! Let me go.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0010","text":"You could not bear the One who saw you,—who always saw you through and through, you ugliest human being! You took revenge upon this witness!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0011","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra and wished to depart; but the inexpressible one seized a corner of his garment and began anew to gurgle and search for words. “Stay!” he said at last—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0012","text":"—“stay! Do not pass by! I guessed which axe struck you to the ground: hail to you, O Zarathustra, that you stand again!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0013","text":"You guessed, I know well, how he feels who killed him,—the murderer of God. Stay! Sit down here with me; it is not for nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0014","text":"To whom should I go, if not to you? Stay, sit down! But do not look at me! Thus honour—my ugliness!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0015","text":"They pursue me: now you are my last refuge. Not with their hatred, not with their pursuers:—oh, at such persecution I should mock and be proud and glad!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0016","text":"Has not all success hitherto been with those well persecuted? And whoever is well persecuted easily learns to follow:—after all, he is—behind! But it is their pity—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0017","text":"—it is their pity from which I flee and to which I flee to you. O Zarathustra, protect me, you my last refuge, you only one who guessed me:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0018","text":"—you guessed how he feels who killed him. Stay! And if you wish to go, you impatient one: do not take the path by which I came. The path is bad.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0019","text":"Are you angry with me because I have long been speaking in broken, stumbling speech? Because I already advise you? But know, it is I, the ugliest human being,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0020","text":"—who also has the largest, heaviest feet. Where I walked, the path is bad. I tread all paths dead and to ruin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0021","text":"But that you passed me by, silently; that you blushed, I saw it well: by that I recognized you as Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0022","text":"Every other would have thrown me his alms, his pity, with look and speech. But for that—I am not beggar enough, you guessed that—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0023","text":"—for that I am too rich, rich in what is great, in what is terrible, in what is ugliest, in what is inexpressible! Your shame, O Zarathustra, honoured me!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0024","text":"With difficulty I escaped from the throng of the pitying,—so that I might find the only one who today teaches ‘Pity is intrusive’—you, O Zarathustra!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0025","text":"—be it that of a god, be it the pity of men: pity goes against shame. And not-wanting-to-help can be nobler than that virtue which springs to aid.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0026","text":"But today, among all little people, pity itself is called virtue:—they have no reverence for great misfortune, for great ugliness, for great failure.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0027","text":"Over all these I look away, as a dog looks away over the backs of swarming flocks of sheep. They are little well-disposed, well-willing grey people.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0028","text":"As a heron looks contemptuously away over shallow ponds, its head drawn back: thus I look away over the swarming of grey little waves and wills and souls.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0029","text":"For too long one has granted them right, these little people: thus one finally gave them power too—now they teach: “good is only what little people call good.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0030","text":"And “truth” today is called what the preacher spoke, who himself came forth from among them, that strange saint and advocate of the little people, who testified of himself “I—am the truth.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0031","text":"For a long time now this immodest man has made the little people swell their crests high—he who taught no small error when he taught “I—am the truth.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0032","text":"Was an immodest man ever answered more courteously?—But you, O Zarathustra, passed him by and spoke: “No! No! Three times No!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0033","text":"You warned against his error; you were the first to warn against pity—not all, not none, but yourself and your kind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0034","text":"You are ashamed at the shame of the great sufferer; and verily, when you say “from pity comes a great cloud, take heed, you human beings!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0035","text":"—when you teach “all creators are hard, all great love is above its pity”: O Zarathustra, how well instructed in weather-signs you seem to me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0036","text":"But you yourself—warn yourself too against your pity! For many are on their way to you, many sufferers, doubters, despairers, drowning men, freezing men—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0037","text":"I warn you also against myself. You guessed my best, worst riddle, myself and what I did. I know the axe that fells you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0038","text":"But he—had to die: he saw with eyes that saw Everything,—he saw the depths and grounds of man, all his concealed disgrace and ugliness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0039","text":"His pity knew no shame: he crept into my filthiest corners. This most curious, over-intrusive, over-pitiful one had to die.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0040","text":"He always saw me: upon such a witness I wished to have revenge—or not to live myself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0041","text":"The God who saw Everything, even man—this God had to die! Man cannot bear it that such a witness lives.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0042","text":"Thus spoke the ugliest man. But Zarathustra rose and made ready to depart: for he shivered to his very entrails.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0043","text":"“You unutterable one,” he said, “you warned me against your way. In thanks for that, I commend mine to you. Behold, up there lies Zarathustra’s cave.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0044","text":"My cave is large and deep and has many corners; there the most hidden finds his hiding-place. And close by it are a hundred shelters and contrivances for creeping, fluttering, and leaping creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0045","text":"You outcast, who cast yourself out, you do not wish to dwell among men and human pity? Well then, do as I do! Thus you too will learn from me; only the doer learns.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0046","text":"And speak first and -next with my animals! The proudest animal and the cleverest animal—they might well be the right counsellors for us both!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0047","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra and went on his way, still more thoughtful and slower than before: for he asked himself many things and did not readily know how to answer himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0048","text":"“How poor man is! he thought in his heart, how ugly, how rattling, how full of hidden shame!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0049","text":"They tell me that man loves himself: ah, how great must this self-love be! How much contempt it has against itself!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0050","text":"This one too loved himself, as he despised himself,—to me he is a great lover and a great despiser.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0051","text":"As yet I have found none who would have despised himself more deeply: that too is height. Alas, was that perhaps the higher man whose cry I heard?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0052","text":"I love the great despisers. But man is something that must be overcome.”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DHSM:P0052"]}],"source_digest":"88915a5f5a9058f3ec8c2f3b7cd7196634f3d2f207cc2f1cd7f7f6f85e2cd27a","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Der hässlichste Mensch"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Der hässlichste Mensch","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0","title":"The Voluntary Beggar","order":135,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_DFB0","text":"Als Zarathustra den hässlichsten Menschen verlassen hatte, fror ihn, und er fühlte sich einsam: es gieng ihm nämlich vieles Kalte und Einsame durch die Sinne, also, dass darob auch seine Glieder kälter wurden. Indem er aber weiter und weiter stieg, hinauf, hinab, bald an grünen Weiden vorbei, aber auch über wilde steinichte Lager, wo ehedem wohl ein ungeduldiger Bach sich zu Bett gelegt hatte.- da wurde ihm mit Einem Male wieder wärmer und herzlicher zu Sinne.\n\n„Was geschah mir doch? fragte er sich, etwas Warmes und Lebendiges erquickt mich, das muss in meiner Nähe sein.\n\nSchon bin ich weniger allein; unbewusste Gefährten und Brüder schweifen um mich, ihr warmer Athem rührt an meine Seele.“\n\nAls er aber um sich spähete und nach den Tröstern seiner Einsamkeit suchte: siehe, da waren es Kühe, welche auf einer Anhöhe bei einander standen; deren Nähe und Geruch hatten sein Herz erwärmt. Diese Kühe aber schienen mit Eifer einem Redenden zuzuhören und gaben nicht auf Den Acht, der herankam. Wie aber Zarathustra ganz in ihrer Nähe war, hörte er deutlich, dass eine Menschen-Stimme aus der Mitte der Kühe heraus redete; und ersichtlich hatten sie allesammt ihre Köpfe dem Redenden zugedreht.\n\nDa sprang Zarathustra mit Eifer hinauf und drängte die Thiere auseinander, denn er fürchtete, dass hier jemandem ein Leids geschehn sei, welchem schwerlich das Mitleid von Kühen abhelfen mochte. Aber darin hatte er sich getäuscht; denn siehe, da sass ein Mensch auf der Erde und schien den Thieren zuzureden, dass sie keine Scheu vor ihm haben sollten, ein friedfertiger Mensch und Berg-Prediger, aus dessen Augen die Güte selber predigte. „Was suchst du hier?“ rief Zarathustra mit Befremden.\n\n„Was ich hier suche? antwortete er: das Selbe, was du suchst, du Störenfried! nämlich das Glück auf Erden.\n\nDazu aber möchte ich von diesen Kühen lernen. Denn, weisst du wohl, einen halben Morgen schon rede ich ihnen zu, und eben wollten sie mir Bescheid geben. Warum doch störst du sie?\n\nSo wir nicht umkehren und werden wie die Kühe, so kommen wir nicht in das Himmelreich. Wir sollten ihnen nämlich Eins ablernen: das Wiederkäuen.\n\nUnd wahrlich, wenn der Mensch auch die ganze Welt gewönne und lernte das Eine nicht, das Wiederkäuen: was hülfe es! Er würde nicht seine Trübsal los\n\n—seine grosse Trübsal: die aber heisst heute Ekel . Wer hat heute von Ekel nicht Herz, Mund und Augen voll? Auch du! Auch du! Aber siehe doch diese Kühe an!“—\n\nAlso sprach der Berg-Prediger und wandte dann seinen eignen Blick Zarathustra zu,—denn bisher hieng er mit Liebe an den Kühen—: da aber verwandelte er sich. „Wer ist das, mit dem ich rede? rief er erschreckt und sprang vom Boden empor.\n\nDiess ist der Mensch ohne Ekel, diess ist Zarathustra selber, der Überwinder des grossen Ekels, diess ist das Auge, diess ist der Mund, diess ist das Herz Zarathustra’s selber.“\n\nUnd indem er also sprach, küsste er Dem, zu welchem er redete, die Hände, mit überströmenden Augen, und gebärdete sich ganz als Einer, dem ein kostbares Geschenk und Kleinod unversehens vom Himmel fällt. Die Kühe aber schauten dem Allen zu und wunderten sich.\n\n„Sprich nicht von mir, du Wunderlicher! Lieblicher! sagte Zarathustra und wehrte seiner Zärtlichkeit, sprich mir erst von dir! Bist du nicht der freiwillige Bettler, der einst einen grossen Reichthum von sich warf,—\n\n—der sich seines Reichthums schämte und der Reichen, und zu den Ärmsten floh, dass er ihnen seine Fülle und sein Herz schenke? Aber sie nahmen ihn nicht an.“\n\n„Aber sie nahmen mich nicht an, sagte der freiwillige Bettler, du weisst es ja. So gieng ich endlich zu den Thieren und zu diesen Kühen.“\n\n„Da lerntest du, unterbrach Zarathustra den Redenden, wie es schwerer ist, recht geben als recht nehmen, und dass gut schenken eine Kunst ist und die letzte listigste Meister-Kunst der Güte.“\n\n„Sonderlich heutzutage, antwortete der freiwillige Bettler: heute nämlich, wo alles Niedrige aufständisch ward und scheu und auf seine Art hoffährtig: nämlich auf Pöbel-Art.\n\nDenn es kam die Stunde, du weisst es ja, für den grossen schlimmen langen langsamen Pöbel- und Sklaven-Aufstand: der wächst und wächst!\n\nNun empört die Niedrigen alles Wohlthun und kleine Weggeben; und die Überreichen mögen auf der Hut sein!\n\nWer heute gleich bauchichten Flaschen tröpfelt aus allzuschmalen Hälsen:—solchen Flaschen bricht man heute gern den Hals.\n\nLüsterne Gier, gallichter Neid, vergrämte Rachsucht, Pöbel-Stolz: das sprang mir Alles in’s Gesicht. Es ist nicht mehr wahr, dass die Armen selig sind. Das Himmelreich aber ist bei den Kühen.“\n\nUnd warum ist es nicht bei den Reichen? fragte Zarathustra versuchend, während er den Kühen wehrte, die den Friedfertigen zutraulich anschnauften.\n\n„Was versuchst du mich? antwortete dieser. Du weisst es selber besser noch als ich. Was trieb mich doch zu den Ärmsten, oh Zarathustra? War es nicht der Ekel vor unsern Reichsten?\n\n—vor den Sträflingen des Reichthums, welche sich ihren Vortheil aus jedem Kehricht auflesen, mit kalten Augen, geilen Gedanken, vor diesem Gesindel, das gen Himmel stinkt,\n\n—vor diesem vergüldeten verfälschten Pöbel, dessen Väter Langfinger oder Aasvögel oder Lumpensammler waren, mit Weibern willfährig, lüstern, vergesslich:—sie haben’s nämlich alle nicht weit zur Hure—\n\nPöbel oben, Pöbel unten! Was ist heute noch „Arm“ und „Reich“! Diesen Unterschied verlernte ich,—da floh ich davon, weiter, immer weiter, bis ich zu diesen Kühen kam.“\n\nAlso sprach der Friedfertige und schnaufte selber und schwitzte bei seinen Worten: also dass die Kühe sich von Neuem wunderten. Zarathustra aber sah ihm immer mit Lächeln in’s Gesicht, als er so hart redete, und schüttelte dazu schweigend den Kopf.\n\n„Du thust dir Gewalt an, du Berg-Prediger, wenn du solche harte Worte brauchst. Für solche Härte wuchs dir nicht der Mund, nicht das Auge.\n\nAuch, wie mich dünkt, dein Magen selber nicht: dem widersteht all solches Zürnen und Hassen und Überschäumen. Dein Magen will sanftere Dinge: du bist kein Fleischer.\n\nVielmehr dünkst du mich ein Pflanzler und Wurzelmann. Vielleicht malmst du Körner. Sicherlich aber bist du fleischlichen Freuden abhold und liebst den Honig.“\n\n„Du erriethst mich gut, antwortete der freiwillige Bettler, mit erleichtertem Herzen. Ich liebe den Honig, ich malme auch Körner, denn ich suchte, was lieblich mundet und reinen Athem macht:\n\n—auch was lange Zeit braucht, ein Tag- und Maul-Werk für sanfte Müssiggänger und Tagediebe.\n\nAm weitesten freilich brachten es diese Kühe: die erfanden sich das Wiederkäuen und In-der-Sonne-Liegen. Auch enthalten sie sich aller schweren Gedanken, welche das Herz blähn.“\n\n„- Wohlan! sagte Zarathustra: du solltest auch meine Thiere sehn, meinen Adler und meine Schlange,—ihres Gleichen giebt es heute nicht auf Erden.\n\nSiehe, dorthin führt der Weg zu meiner Höhle: sei diese Nacht ihr Gast. Und rede mit meinen Thieren vom Glück der Thiere,—\n\n—bis ich selber heimkomme. Denn jetzt ruft ein Nothschrei Mich eilig weg von dir. Auch findest du neuen Honig bei mir, eisfrischen Waben-Goldhonig: den iss!\n\nJetzt aber nimm flugs Abschied von deinen Kühen, du Wunderlicher! Lieblicher! ob es dir schon schwer werden mag. Denn es sind deine wärmsten Freunde und Lehrmeister!“—\n\n„- Einen ausgenommen, den ich noch lieber habe, antwortete der freiwillige Bettler. Du selber bist gut und besser noch als eine Kuh, oh Zarathustra!“\n\n„Fort, fort mit dir! du arger Schmeichler! schrie Zarathustra mit Bosheit, was verdirbst du mich mit solchem Lob und Schmeichel-Honig?“\n\n„Fort, fort von mir!“ schrie er noch Ein Mal und schwang seinen Stock nach dem zärtlichen Bettler: der aber lief hurtig davon.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0001","text":"Als Zarathustra den hässlichsten Menschen verlassen hatte, fror ihn, und er fühlte sich einsam: es gieng ihm nämlich vieles Kalte und Einsame durch die Sinne, also, dass darob auch seine Glieder kälter wurden. Indem er aber weiter und weiter stieg, hinauf, hinab, bald an grünen Weiden vorbei, aber auch über wilde steinichte Lager, wo ehedem wohl ein ungeduldiger Bach sich zu Bett gelegt hatte.- da wurde ihm mit Einem Male wieder wärmer und herzlicher zu Sinne.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0002","text":"„Was geschah mir doch? fragte er sich, etwas Warmes und Lebendiges erquickt mich, das muss in meiner Nähe sein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0003","text":"Schon bin ich weniger allein; unbewusste Gefährten und Brüder schweifen um mich, ihr warmer Athem rührt an meine Seele.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0004","text":"Als er aber um sich spähete und nach den Tröstern seiner Einsamkeit suchte: siehe, da waren es Kühe, welche auf einer Anhöhe bei einander standen; deren Nähe und Geruch hatten sein Herz erwärmt. Diese Kühe aber schienen mit Eifer einem Redenden zuzuhören und gaben nicht auf Den Acht, der herankam. Wie aber Zarathustra ganz in ihrer Nähe war, hörte er deutlich, dass eine Menschen-Stimme aus der Mitte der Kühe heraus redete; und ersichtlich hatten sie allesammt ihre Köpfe dem Redenden zugedreht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0005","text":"Da sprang Zarathustra mit Eifer hinauf und drängte die Thiere auseinander, denn er fürchtete, dass hier jemandem ein Leids geschehn sei, welchem schwerlich das Mitleid von Kühen abhelfen mochte. Aber darin hatte er sich getäuscht; denn siehe, da sass ein Mensch auf der Erde und schien den Thieren zuzureden, dass sie keine Scheu vor ihm haben sollten, ein friedfertiger Mensch und Berg-Prediger, aus dessen Augen die Güte selber predigte. „Was suchst du hier?“ rief Zarathustra mit Befremden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0006","text":"„Was ich hier suche? antwortete er: das Selbe, was du suchst, du Störenfried! nämlich das Glück auf Erden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0007","text":"Dazu aber möchte ich von diesen Kühen lernen. Denn, weisst du wohl, einen halben Morgen schon rede ich ihnen zu, und eben wollten sie mir Bescheid geben. Warum doch störst du sie?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0008","text":"So wir nicht umkehren und werden wie die Kühe, so kommen wir nicht in das Himmelreich. Wir sollten ihnen nämlich Eins ablernen: das Wiederkäuen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0009","text":"Und wahrlich, wenn der Mensch auch die ganze Welt gewönne und lernte das Eine nicht, das Wiederkäuen: was hülfe es! Er würde nicht seine Trübsal los","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0010","text":"—seine grosse Trübsal: die aber heisst heute Ekel . Wer hat heute von Ekel nicht Herz, Mund und Augen voll? Auch du! Auch du! Aber siehe doch diese Kühe an!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0011","text":"Also sprach der Berg-Prediger und wandte dann seinen eignen Blick Zarathustra zu,—denn bisher hieng er mit Liebe an den Kühen—: da aber verwandelte er sich. „Wer ist das, mit dem ich rede? rief er erschreckt und sprang vom Boden empor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0012","text":"Diess ist der Mensch ohne Ekel, diess ist Zarathustra selber, der Überwinder des grossen Ekels, diess ist das Auge, diess ist der Mund, diess ist das Herz Zarathustra’s selber.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0013","text":"Und indem er also sprach, küsste er Dem, zu welchem er redete, die Hände, mit überströmenden Augen, und gebärdete sich ganz als Einer, dem ein kostbares Geschenk und Kleinod unversehens vom Himmel fällt. Die Kühe aber schauten dem Allen zu und wunderten sich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0014","text":"„Sprich nicht von mir, du Wunderlicher! Lieblicher! sagte Zarathustra und wehrte seiner Zärtlichkeit, sprich mir erst von dir! Bist du nicht der freiwillige Bettler, der einst einen grossen Reichthum von sich warf,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0015","text":"—der sich seines Reichthums schämte und der Reichen, und zu den Ärmsten floh, dass er ihnen seine Fülle und sein Herz schenke? Aber sie nahmen ihn nicht an.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0016","text":"„Aber sie nahmen mich nicht an, sagte der freiwillige Bettler, du weisst es ja. So gieng ich endlich zu den Thieren und zu diesen Kühen.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0017","text":"„Da lerntest du, unterbrach Zarathustra den Redenden, wie es schwerer ist, recht geben als recht nehmen, und dass gut schenken eine Kunst ist und die letzte listigste Meister-Kunst der Güte.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0018","text":"„Sonderlich heutzutage, antwortete der freiwillige Bettler: heute nämlich, wo alles Niedrige aufständisch ward und scheu und auf seine Art hoffährtig: nämlich auf Pöbel-Art.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0019","text":"Denn es kam die Stunde, du weisst es ja, für den grossen schlimmen langen langsamen Pöbel- und Sklaven-Aufstand: der wächst und wächst!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0020","text":"Nun empört die Niedrigen alles Wohlthun und kleine Weggeben; und die Überreichen mögen auf der Hut sein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0021","text":"Wer heute gleich bauchichten Flaschen tröpfelt aus allzuschmalen Hälsen:—solchen Flaschen bricht man heute gern den Hals.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0022","text":"Lüsterne Gier, gallichter Neid, vergrämte Rachsucht, Pöbel-Stolz: das sprang mir Alles in’s Gesicht. Es ist nicht mehr wahr, dass die Armen selig sind. Das Himmelreich aber ist bei den Kühen.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0023","text":"Und warum ist es nicht bei den Reichen? fragte Zarathustra versuchend, während er den Kühen wehrte, die den Friedfertigen zutraulich anschnauften.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0024","text":"„Was versuchst du mich? antwortete dieser. Du weisst es selber besser noch als ich. Was trieb mich doch zu den Ärmsten, oh Zarathustra? War es nicht der Ekel vor unsern Reichsten?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0025","text":"—vor den Sträflingen des Reichthums, welche sich ihren Vortheil aus jedem Kehricht auflesen, mit kalten Augen, geilen Gedanken, vor diesem Gesindel, das gen Himmel stinkt,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0026","text":"—vor diesem vergüldeten verfälschten Pöbel, dessen Väter Langfinger oder Aasvögel oder Lumpensammler waren, mit Weibern willfährig, lüstern, vergesslich:—sie haben’s nämlich alle nicht weit zur Hure—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0027","text":"Pöbel oben, Pöbel unten! Was ist heute noch „Arm“ und „Reich“! Diesen Unterschied verlernte ich,—da floh ich davon, weiter, immer weiter, bis ich zu diesen Kühen kam.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0028","text":"Also sprach der Friedfertige und schnaufte selber und schwitzte bei seinen Worten: also dass die Kühe sich von Neuem wunderten. Zarathustra aber sah ihm immer mit Lächeln in’s Gesicht, als er so hart redete, und schüttelte dazu schweigend den Kopf.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0029","text":"„Du thust dir Gewalt an, du Berg-Prediger, wenn du solche harte Worte brauchst. Für solche Härte wuchs dir nicht der Mund, nicht das Auge.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0030","text":"Auch, wie mich dünkt, dein Magen selber nicht: dem widersteht all solches Zürnen und Hassen und Überschäumen. Dein Magen will sanftere Dinge: du bist kein Fleischer.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0031","text":"Vielmehr dünkst du mich ein Pflanzler und Wurzelmann. Vielleicht malmst du Körner. Sicherlich aber bist du fleischlichen Freuden abhold und liebst den Honig.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0032","text":"„Du erriethst mich gut, antwortete der freiwillige Bettler, mit erleichtertem Herzen. Ich liebe den Honig, ich malme auch Körner, denn ich suchte, was lieblich mundet und reinen Athem macht:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0033","text":"—auch was lange Zeit braucht, ein Tag- und Maul-Werk für sanfte Müssiggänger und Tagediebe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0034","text":"Am weitesten freilich brachten es diese Kühe: die erfanden sich das Wiederkäuen und In-der-Sonne-Liegen. Auch enthalten sie sich aller schweren Gedanken, welche das Herz blähn.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0035","text":"„- Wohlan! sagte Zarathustra: du solltest auch meine Thiere sehn, meinen Adler und meine Schlange,—ihres Gleichen giebt es heute nicht auf Erden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0036","text":"Siehe, dorthin führt der Weg zu meiner Höhle: sei diese Nacht ihr Gast. Und rede mit meinen Thieren vom Glück der Thiere,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0037","text":"—bis ich selber heimkomme. Denn jetzt ruft ein Nothschrei Mich eilig weg von dir. Auch findest du neuen Honig bei mir, eisfrischen Waben-Goldhonig: den iss!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0038","text":"Jetzt aber nimm flugs Abschied von deinen Kühen, du Wunderlicher! Lieblicher! ob es dir schon schwer werden mag. Denn es sind deine wärmsten Freunde und Lehrmeister!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0039","text":"„- Einen ausgenommen, den ich noch lieber habe, antwortete der freiwillige Bettler. Du selber bist gut und besser noch als eine Kuh, oh Zarathustra!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0040","text":"„Fort, fort mit dir! du arger Schmeichler! schrie Zarathustra mit Bosheit, was verdirbst du mich mit solchem Lob und Schmeichel-Honig?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0041","text":"„Fort, fort von mir!“ schrie er noch Ein Mal und schwang seinen Stock nach dem zärtlichen Bettler: der aber lief hurtig davon.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"a2924b4e9b6ef5373d1672e47d1b8a3f6bcb75b149397d0eb951a86d29195f71","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183657559374Z_abca7b10","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"The Voluntary Beggar","source_sha256":"a2924b4e9b6ef5373d1672e47d1b8a3f6bcb75b149397d0eb951a86d29195f71","sha256":"b5108c82f12944991b31d2c6ed9ddf245fe7046f909770716d92ffc3bdbd99d0","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:36:57.559398+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0001","text":"When Zarathustra had left the ugliest man, he grew cold, and he felt lonely: for much that was cold and lonely passed through his senses, so that his limbs too grew colder thereby. But as he climbed on and on, up and down, now past green meadows, but also over wild stony beds, where once perhaps an impatient brook had laid itself down to rest—then, all at once, his feelings grew warmer and more heartfelt again.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0002","text":"“What has happened to me? he asked himself, something warm and living refreshes me; it must be near me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0003","text":"Already I am less alone; unconscious companions and brothers roam about me, their warm breath touches my soul.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0004","text":"But when he looked about him and sought the comforters of his solitude: behold, they were cows, which stood together upon a height; their proximity and smell had warmed his heart. But these cows seemed eagerly to be listening to a speaker and paid no heed to the one who approached. Yet when Zarathustra was quite near them, he heard distinctly that a human voice was speaking from the midst of the cows; and evidently they had all turned their heads toward the speaker.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0005","text":"Then Zarathustra sprang up eagerly and pressed the animals apart, for he feared that some harm had befallen someone here, which the compassion of cows could scarcely remedy. But in this he had been mistaken; for behold, there sat a man on the ground and seemed to be telling the animals that they should not be shy of him, a peaceable man and mountain-preacher, from whose eyes goodness itself preached. “What are you seeking here?” cried Zarathustra in astonishment.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0006","text":"“What am I seeking here? he answered: the same thing that you seek, you disturber of the peace! namely happiness on earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0007","text":"But for that I wish to learn from these cows. For, do you know, for half a morning already I have been speaking to them, and just now they were about to give me an answer. Why do you disturb them?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0008","text":"Unless we turn back and become like cows, we shall not enter the kingdom of heaven. For we ought to learn one thing from them: rumination.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0009","text":"And truly, even if man won the whole world and did not learn the one thing, rumination: what good would it do him! He would not get rid of his melancholy","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0010","text":"—his great melancholy: which today is called disgust. Who today does not have heart, mouth, and eyes full of disgust? You too! You too! But just look at these cows!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0011","text":"Thus spoke the mountain-preacher and then turned his own gaze upon Zarathustra,—for until then he had hung lovingly upon the cows—: but then he changed. “Who is this with whom I speak? he cried in fright and sprang up from the ground.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0012","text":"This is the man without disgust, this is Zarathustra himself, the conqueror of the great disgust, this is the eye, this is the mouth, this is Zarathustra’s heart itself.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0013","text":"And as he spoke thus, he kissed the hands of the one to whom he spoke, with overflowing eyes, and behaved altogether like one upon whom a precious gift and jewel unexpectedly falls from heaven. But the cows looked on at all this and marvelled.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0014","text":"“Do not speak of me, you strange one! Dear one! said Zarathustra, warding off his tenderness, first speak to me of yourself! Are you not the voluntary beggar who once cast a great wealth away from himself,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0015","text":"—who was ashamed of his wealth and of the rich, and fled to the poorest, in order to give them his abundance and his heart? But they did not accept him.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0016","text":"“But they did not accept me, said the voluntary beggar, you know it well. Thus I finally went to the animals and to these cows.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0017","text":"“There you learned, Zarathustra interrupted the speaker, how it is harder to give rightly than to take rightly, and that giving well is an art and the last, most cunning master-art of goodness.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0018","text":"“Especially nowadays, answered the voluntary beggar: nowadays, namely, when everything low became rebellious and shy and, in its own way, arrogant: namely, in the manner of the mob.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0019","text":"For the hour came, you know it well, for the great, evil, long, slow uprising of the mob and slaves: it grows and grows!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0020","text":"Now every benefaction and small bestowal provokes the lowly; and let the over-rich be on their guard!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0021","text":"Whoever today drips like pot-bellied bottles from all-too-narrow necks:—such bottles today people gladly break the necks of.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0022","text":"Lustful greed, bilious envy, embittered vengefulness, mob-pride: all that leapt into my face. It is no longer true that the poor are blessed. But the kingdom of heaven is with the cows.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0023","text":"And why is it not with the rich? Zarathustra asked temptingly, while he held back the cows, which were trustfully snuffling at the peaceable man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0024","text":"“Why do you tempt me? he answered. You yourself know it even better than I. What drove me to the poorest, O Zarathustra? Was it not disgust with our richest?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0025","text":"—before the convicts of wealth, who pick their advantage out of every rubbish heap, with cold eyes, lecherous thoughts, before this rabble that stinks to heaven,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0026","text":"—before this gilded, adulterated rabble, whose fathers were pickpockets or carrion-birds or rag-and-bone men, compliant, lustful, forgetful with women:—for they are all not far from being whores—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0027","text":"rabble above, rabble below! What is still ‘Poor’ and ‘Rich’ today! I unlearned that distinction,—then I fled from it, farther, ever farther, until I came to these cows.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0028","text":"Thus spoke the Peaceable One, and himself snorted and sweated at his words: so that the cows wondered anew. But Zarathustra kept looking into his face with a smile as he spoke so harshly, and silently shook his head the while.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0029","text":"“You do violence to yourself, you mountain-preacher, when you need such harsh words. For such harshness neither your mouth nor your eye grew.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0030","text":"Also, it seems to me, not even your stomach: it resists all such raging and hating and overflowing. Your stomach wants gentler things: you are no butcher.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0031","text":"Rather, you seem to me a planter and root-man. Perhaps you grind grain. But certainly you are averse to fleshly pleasures and love honey.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0032","text":"“You have guessed me well,” answered the voluntary beggar, with a relieved heart. “I love honey, and I also grind grain, for I sought what tastes sweet and makes the breath pure:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0033","text":"—also what takes a long time, a day- and mouth-work for gentle idlers and time-thieves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0034","text":"These cows, to be sure, have carried it farthest: they invented rumination and lying in the sun. They also abstain from all heavy thoughts that bloat the heart.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0035","text":"“- Well then!” said Zarathustra, “you should also see my animals, my eagle and my serpent,—their like does not exist on earth today.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0036","text":"Behold, the path there leads to my cave: be its guest this night. And speak with my animals about the happiness of animals,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0037","text":"—until I myself come home. For now a cry of distress calls me hastily away from you. You will also find new honey with me, ice-fresh comb-golden honey: eat it!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0038","text":"But now swiftly take leave of your cows, you strange one! Sweet one! though it may be hard for you. For they are your warmest friends and teachers!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0039","text":"“- Except for one whom I love still more,” answered the voluntary beggar. “You yourself are good, and better even than a cow, O Zarathustra!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0040","text":"“Away, away with you! you wicked flatterer!” Zarathustra cried maliciously, “why do you corrupt me with such praise and flattery-honey?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0041","text":"“Away, away from me!” he cried once more and swung his stick at the tender beggar: but the latter ran quickly away.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DFB0:P0041"]}],"source_digest":"45f35d13ae232ba73a86022fd2d341b3b7bfa23c5e077da18d1853ad3b033253","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Der freiwillige Bettler"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Der freiwillige Bettler","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS","title":"The Shadow","order":136,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_DERS","text":"Kaum aber war der freiwillige Bettler davongelaufen und Zarathustra wieder mit sich allein, da hörte er hinter sich eine neue Stimme: die rief „Halt! Zarathustra! So warte doch! Ich bin’s ja, oh Zarathustra, ich, dein Schatten!“ Aber Zarathustra wartete nicht, denn ein plötzlicher Verdruss überkam ihn ob des vielen Zudrangs und Gedrängs in seinen Bergen. „Wo ist meine Einsamkeit hin? sprach er.\n\nEs wird mir wahrlich zu viel; diess Gebirge wimmelt, mein Reich ist nicht mehr von dieser Welt, ich brauche neue Berge.\n\nMein Schatten ruft mich? Was liegt an meinem Schatten! Mag er mir nachlaufen! ich—laufe ihm davon.“—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra zu seinem Herzen und lief davon. Aber Der, welcher hinter ihm war, folgte ihm nach: so dass alsbald drei Laufende hinter einander her waren, nämlich voran der freiwillige Bettler, dann Zarathustra und zudritt und -hinterst sein Schatten. Nicht lange liefen sie so, da kam Zarathustra zur Besinnung über seine Thorheit und schüttelte mit Einem Rucke allen Verdruss und Überdruss von sich.\n\n„Wie! sprach er, geschahen nicht von je die lächerlichsten Dinge bei uns alten Einsiedlern und Heiligen?\n\nWahrlich, meine Thorheit wuchs hoch in den Bergen! Nun höre ich sechs alte Narren-Beine hinter einander her klappern!\n\nDarf aber Zarathustra sich wohl vor einem Schatten fürchten? Auch dünkt mich zu guterletzt, dass er längere Beine hat als ich.“\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra, lachend mit Augen und Eingeweiden, blieb stehen und drehte sich schnell herum—und siehe, fast warf er dabei seinen Nachfolger und Schatten zu Boden: so dicht schon folgte ihm derselbe auf den Fersen, und so schwach war er auch. Als er ihn nämlich mit Augen prüfte, erschrak er wie vor einem plötzlichen Gespenste: so dünn, schwärzlich, hohl und überlebt sah dieser Nachfolger aus.\n\n„Wer bist du? fragte Zarathustra heftig, was treibst du hier? Und wesshalb heissest du dich meinen Schatten? Du gefällst mir nicht.“\n\n„Vergieb mir, antwortete der Schatten, dass ich’s bin; und wenn ich dir nicht gefalle, wohlan, oh Zarathustra! darin lobe ich dich und deinen guten Geschmack.\n\nEin Wanderer bin ich, der viel schon hinter deinen Fersen her gieng: immer unterwegs, aber ohne Ziel, auch ohne Heim: also dass mir wahrlich wenig zum ewigen Juden fehlt, es sei denn, dass ich nicht ewig, und auch nicht Jude bin.\n\nWie? Muss ich immerdar unterwegs sein? Von jedem Winde gewirbelt, unstät, fortgetrieben? Oh Erde, du wardst mir zu rund!\n\nAuf jeder Oberfläche sass ich schon, gleich müdem Staube schlief ich ein auf Spiegeln und Fensterscheiben: Alles nimmt von mir, Nichts giebt, ich werde dünn,—fast gleiche ich einem Schatten.\n\nDir aber, oh Zarathustra, flog und zog ich am längsten nach, und, verbarg ich mich schon vor dir, so war ich doch dein bester Schatten: wo du nur gesessen hast, sass ich auch.\n\nMit dir bin ich in fernsten, kältesten Welten umgegangen, einem Gespenste gleich, das freiwillig über Winterdächer und Schnee läuft.\n\nMit dir strebte ich in jedes Verbotene, Schlimmste, Fernste: und wenn irgend Etwas an mir Tugend ist, so ist es, dass ich vor keinem Verbote Furcht hatte.\n\nMit dir zerbrach ich, was je mein Herz verehrte, alle Grenzsteine und Bilder warf ich um, den gefährlichsten Wünschen lief ich nach,—wahrlich, über jedwedes Verbrechen lief ich einmal hinweg.\n\nMit dir verlernte ich den Glauben an Worte und Werthe und grosse Namen. Wenn der Teufel sich häutet, fällt da nicht auch sein Name ab? der ist nämlich auch Haut. Der Teufel selber ist vielleicht—Haut.\n\n„Nichts ist wahr, Alles ist erlaubt“: so sprach ich mir zu. In die kältesten Wasser stürzte ich mich, mit Kopf und Herzen. Ach, wie oft stand ich darob nackt als rother Krebs da!\n\nAch, wohin kam mir alles Gute und alle Scham und aller Glaube an die Guten! Ach, wohin ist jene verlogne Unschuld, die ich einst besass, die Unschuld der Guten und ihrer edlen Lügen!\n\nZu oft, wahrlich, folgte ich der Wahrheit dicht auf dem Fusse: da trat sie mir vor den Kopf. Manchmal meinte ich zu lügen, und siehe! da erst traf ich—die Wahrheit.\n\nZu Viel klärte sich mir auf: nun geht es mich Nichts mehr an. Nichts lebt mehr, das ich liebe,—wie sollte ich noch mich selber lieben?\n\n„Leben, wie ich Lust habe, oder gar nicht leben“: so will ich’s, so will’s auch der Heiligste. Aber, wehe! wie habe ich noch—Lust?\n\nHabe ich —noch ein Ziel? Einen Hafen, nach dem mein Segel läuft?\n\nEinen guten Wind? Ach, nur wer weiss, wohin er fährt, weiss auch, welcher Wind gut und sein Fahrwind ist.\n\nWas blieb mir noch zurück? Ein Herz müde und frech; ein unstäter Wille; Flatter-Flügel; ein zerbrochnes Rückgrat.\n\nDiess Suchen nach meinem Heim: oh Zarathustra, weisst du wohl, diess Suchen war meine Heimsuchung, es frisst mich auf.\n\n„Wo ist— mein Heim?“ Darnach frage und suche und suchte ich, das fand ich nicht. Oh ewiges Überall, oh ewiges Nirgendwo, oh ewiges—Umsonst!“\n\nAlso sprach der Schatten, und Zarathustra’s Gesicht verlängerte sich bei seinen Worten. „Du bist mein Schatten! sagte er endlich, mit Traurigkeit.\n\nDeine Gefahr ist keine kleine, du freier Geist und Wanderer! Du hast einen schlimmen Tag gehabt: sieh zu, dass dir nicht noch ein schlimmerer Abend kommt!\n\nSolchen Unstäten, wie du, dünkt zuletzt auch ein Gefängniss selig. Sahst du je, wie eingefangne Verbrecher schlafen? Sie schlafen ruhig, sie gemessen ihre neue Sicherheit.\n\nHüte dich, dass dich nicht am Ende noch ein enger Glaube einfängt, ein harter, strenger Wahn! Dich nämlich verführt und versucht nunmehr Jegliches, das eng und fest ist.\n\nDu hast das Ziel verloren: wehe, wie wirst du diesen Verlust verscherzen und verschmerzen? Damit—hast du auch den Weg verloren!\n\nDu armer Schweifender, Schwärmender, du müder Schmetterling! willst du diesen Abend eine Rast und Heimstätte haben? So gehe hinauf zu meiner Höhle!\n\nDorthin führt der Weg zu meiner Höhle. Und jetzo will ich Schnell wieder von dir davonlaufen. Schon liegt es wie ein Schatten auf mir.\n\nIch will allein laufen, dass es wieder hell um mich werde. Dazu muss ich noch lange lustig auf den Beinen sein. Des Abends aber wird bei mir—getanzt!“—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0001","text":"Kaum aber war der freiwillige Bettler davongelaufen und Zarathustra wieder mit sich allein, da hörte er hinter sich eine neue Stimme: die rief „Halt! Zarathustra! So warte doch! Ich bin’s ja, oh Zarathustra, ich, dein Schatten!“ Aber Zarathustra wartete nicht, denn ein plötzlicher Verdruss überkam ihn ob des vielen Zudrangs und Gedrängs in seinen Bergen. „Wo ist meine Einsamkeit hin? sprach er.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0002","text":"Es wird mir wahrlich zu viel; diess Gebirge wimmelt, mein Reich ist nicht mehr von dieser Welt, ich brauche neue Berge.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0003","text":"Mein Schatten ruft mich? Was liegt an meinem Schatten! Mag er mir nachlaufen! ich—laufe ihm davon.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0004","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra zu seinem Herzen und lief davon. Aber Der, welcher hinter ihm war, folgte ihm nach: so dass alsbald drei Laufende hinter einander her waren, nämlich voran der freiwillige Bettler, dann Zarathustra und zudritt und -hinterst sein Schatten. Nicht lange liefen sie so, da kam Zarathustra zur Besinnung über seine Thorheit und schüttelte mit Einem Rucke allen Verdruss und Überdruss von sich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0005","text":"„Wie! sprach er, geschahen nicht von je die lächerlichsten Dinge bei uns alten Einsiedlern und Heiligen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0006","text":"Wahrlich, meine Thorheit wuchs hoch in den Bergen! Nun höre ich sechs alte Narren-Beine hinter einander her klappern!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0007","text":"Darf aber Zarathustra sich wohl vor einem Schatten fürchten? Auch dünkt mich zu guterletzt, dass er längere Beine hat als ich.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0008","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra, lachend mit Augen und Eingeweiden, blieb stehen und drehte sich schnell herum—und siehe, fast warf er dabei seinen Nachfolger und Schatten zu Boden: so dicht schon folgte ihm derselbe auf den Fersen, und so schwach war er auch. Als er ihn nämlich mit Augen prüfte, erschrak er wie vor einem plötzlichen Gespenste: so dünn, schwärzlich, hohl und überlebt sah dieser Nachfolger aus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0009","text":"„Wer bist du? fragte Zarathustra heftig, was treibst du hier? Und wesshalb heissest du dich meinen Schatten? Du gefällst mir nicht.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0010","text":"„Vergieb mir, antwortete der Schatten, dass ich’s bin; und wenn ich dir nicht gefalle, wohlan, oh Zarathustra! darin lobe ich dich und deinen guten Geschmack.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0011","text":"Ein Wanderer bin ich, der viel schon hinter deinen Fersen her gieng: immer unterwegs, aber ohne Ziel, auch ohne Heim: also dass mir wahrlich wenig zum ewigen Juden fehlt, es sei denn, dass ich nicht ewig, und auch nicht Jude bin.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0012","text":"Wie? Muss ich immerdar unterwegs sein? Von jedem Winde gewirbelt, unstät, fortgetrieben? Oh Erde, du wardst mir zu rund!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0013","text":"Auf jeder Oberfläche sass ich schon, gleich müdem Staube schlief ich ein auf Spiegeln und Fensterscheiben: Alles nimmt von mir, Nichts giebt, ich werde dünn,—fast gleiche ich einem Schatten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0014","text":"Dir aber, oh Zarathustra, flog und zog ich am längsten nach, und, verbarg ich mich schon vor dir, so war ich doch dein bester Schatten: wo du nur gesessen hast, sass ich auch.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0015","text":"Mit dir bin ich in fernsten, kältesten Welten umgegangen, einem Gespenste gleich, das freiwillig über Winterdächer und Schnee läuft.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0016","text":"Mit dir strebte ich in jedes Verbotene, Schlimmste, Fernste: und wenn irgend Etwas an mir Tugend ist, so ist es, dass ich vor keinem Verbote Furcht hatte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0017","text":"Mit dir zerbrach ich, was je mein Herz verehrte, alle Grenzsteine und Bilder warf ich um, den gefährlichsten Wünschen lief ich nach,—wahrlich, über jedwedes Verbrechen lief ich einmal hinweg.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0018","text":"Mit dir verlernte ich den Glauben an Worte und Werthe und grosse Namen. Wenn der Teufel sich häutet, fällt da nicht auch sein Name ab? der ist nämlich auch Haut. Der Teufel selber ist vielleicht—Haut.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0019","text":"„Nichts ist wahr, Alles ist erlaubt“: so sprach ich mir zu. In die kältesten Wasser stürzte ich mich, mit Kopf und Herzen. Ach, wie oft stand ich darob nackt als rother Krebs da!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0020","text":"Ach, wohin kam mir alles Gute und alle Scham und aller Glaube an die Guten! Ach, wohin ist jene verlogne Unschuld, die ich einst besass, die Unschuld der Guten und ihrer edlen Lügen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0021","text":"Zu oft, wahrlich, folgte ich der Wahrheit dicht auf dem Fusse: da trat sie mir vor den Kopf. Manchmal meinte ich zu lügen, und siehe! da erst traf ich—die Wahrheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0022","text":"Zu Viel klärte sich mir auf: nun geht es mich Nichts mehr an. Nichts lebt mehr, das ich liebe,—wie sollte ich noch mich selber lieben?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0023","text":"„Leben, wie ich Lust habe, oder gar nicht leben“: so will ich’s, so will’s auch der Heiligste. Aber, wehe! wie habe ich noch—Lust?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0024","text":"Habe ich —noch ein Ziel? Einen Hafen, nach dem mein Segel läuft?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0025","text":"Einen guten Wind? Ach, nur wer weiss, wohin er fährt, weiss auch, welcher Wind gut und sein Fahrwind ist.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0026","text":"Was blieb mir noch zurück? Ein Herz müde und frech; ein unstäter Wille; Flatter-Flügel; ein zerbrochnes Rückgrat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0027","text":"Diess Suchen nach meinem Heim: oh Zarathustra, weisst du wohl, diess Suchen war meine Heimsuchung, es frisst mich auf.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0028","text":"„Wo ist— mein Heim?“ Darnach frage und suche und suchte ich, das fand ich nicht. Oh ewiges Überall, oh ewiges Nirgendwo, oh ewiges—Umsonst!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0029","text":"Also sprach der Schatten, und Zarathustra’s Gesicht verlängerte sich bei seinen Worten. „Du bist mein Schatten! sagte er endlich, mit Traurigkeit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0030","text":"Deine Gefahr ist keine kleine, du freier Geist und Wanderer! Du hast einen schlimmen Tag gehabt: sieh zu, dass dir nicht noch ein schlimmerer Abend kommt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0031","text":"Solchen Unstäten, wie du, dünkt zuletzt auch ein Gefängniss selig. Sahst du je, wie eingefangne Verbrecher schlafen? Sie schlafen ruhig, sie gemessen ihre neue Sicherheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0032","text":"Hüte dich, dass dich nicht am Ende noch ein enger Glaube einfängt, ein harter, strenger Wahn! Dich nämlich verführt und versucht nunmehr Jegliches, das eng und fest ist.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0033","text":"Du hast das Ziel verloren: wehe, wie wirst du diesen Verlust verscherzen und verschmerzen? Damit—hast du auch den Weg verloren!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0034","text":"Du armer Schweifender, Schwärmender, du müder Schmetterling! willst du diesen Abend eine Rast und Heimstätte haben? So gehe hinauf zu meiner Höhle!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0035","text":"Dorthin führt der Weg zu meiner Höhle. Und jetzo will ich Schnell wieder von dir davonlaufen. Schon liegt es wie ein Schatten auf mir.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0036","text":"Ich will allein laufen, dass es wieder hell um mich werde. Dazu muss ich noch lange lustig auf den Beinen sein. Des Abends aber wird bei mir—getanzt!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0037","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"b4d564bcb7ec4f5deb558b24c53df2c4769d33872e9f280cb5e1ddd15f8cf70e","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183718561037Z_8c12a313","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"The Shadow","source_sha256":"b4d564bcb7ec4f5deb558b24c53df2c4769d33872e9f280cb5e1ddd15f8cf70e","sha256":"1b0e630b104233451f2097655c052a38d42bf577a84492a7f64b1ba05ff65bf4","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:37:18.561055+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0001","text":"But scarcely had the voluntary beggar run away and Zarathustra again been alone with himself, when he heard a new voice behind him: it cried, “Stop! Zarathustra! Just wait! It is I, O Zarathustra, I, your shadow!” But Zarathustra did not wait, for a sudden vexation came over him at the great thronging and crowding in his mountains. “Where has my solitude gone? he said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0002","text":"Truly, it is becoming too much for me; these mountains swarm, my kingdom is no longer of this world, I need new mountains.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0003","text":"My shadow calls me? What does my shadow matter to me! Let it run after me! I—I run away from it.”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0004","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra to his heart and ran away. But he who was behind him followed after him: so that soon three runners were running one after another, namely, first the voluntary beggar, then Zarathustra, and third and last his shadow. They had not run thus for long when Zarathustra came to his senses concerning his folly and with one jerk shook all vexation and surfeit off himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0005","text":"“What! he said, have not the most ridiculous things always happened among us old hermits and saints?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0006","text":"Truly, my folly grew high in the mountains! Now I hear six old fool-legs clattering one after another behind me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0007","text":"But may Zarathustra well be afraid of a shadow? Besides, it seems to me in the end that it has longer legs than I.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0008","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra, laughing with eyes and entrails, stopped, and quickly turned round—and behold, he nearly threw his pursuer and shadow to the ground: so closely was the latter already following at his heels, and so weak was he as well. For when he examined him with his eyes, he was startled as before a sudden apparition: so thin, blackish, hollow, and over-lived did this pursuer look.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0009","text":"“Who are you? Zarathustra asked vehemently, what are you doing here? And why do you call yourself my shadow? I do not like you.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0010","text":"“Forgive me, answered the shadow, that it is I; and if I do not please you, well then, O Zarathustra! in this I praise you and your good taste.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0011","text":"I am a wanderer who has long walked after your heels: always on the way, but without a goal, and also without a home: so that truly little is lacking for me to be the eternal Jew, except that I am not eternal, nor am I a Jew.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0012","text":"What? Must I be forever on the way? Whirled by every wind, unsettled, driven onward? O earth, you have become too round for me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0013","text":"I have already sat upon every surface; like weary dust I fell asleep on mirrors and windowpanes: Everything takes from me, Nothing gives, I grow thin,—I almost resemble a shadow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0014","text":"But after you, O Zarathustra, I flew and wandered longest, and though I hid myself from you, I was nonetheless your best shadow: wherever you sat, I sat too.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0015","text":"With you I have gone about in the most distant, coldest worlds, like a ghost that voluntarily walks over winter roofs and snow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0016","text":"With you I strove toward every forbidden, worst, most distant thing: and if anything in me is virtue, it is that I had no fear of any prohibition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0017","text":"With you I broke what my heart had ever revered, I cast down all boundary stones and images, I ran after the most dangerous desires,—truly, I once ran over every crime.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0018","text":"With you I unlearned belief in words and values and great names. When the devil sheds his skin, does not his name fall off as well? for that too is skin. The devil himself is perhaps—skin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0019","text":"“Nothing is true, everything is permitted”: thus I spoke to myself. I plunged into the coldest waters, with head and heart. Ah, how often I stood there naked as a red crab on that account!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0020","text":"Ah, where did all goodness and all shame and all faith in the good go for me! Ah, where has that lying innocence gone which I once possessed, the innocence of the good and of their noble lies!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0021","text":"Too often, truly, I followed truth close at its heels: then it stepped in front of my head. Sometimes I thought to lie, and behold! only then did I encounter—truth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0022","text":"Too much became clear to me: now nothing concerns me any longer. Nothing lives any longer that I love,—how should I still love myself?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0023","text":"“To live as I please, or not to live at all”: thus I will it, thus the holiest wills it too. But, alas! how can I still—please?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0024","text":"Have I—still a goal? A harbour toward which my sail runs?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0025","text":"A good wind? Ah, only he who knows where he is sailing knows, too, which wind is good and is his fair wind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0026","text":"What was left to me? A heart weary and impudent; an inconstant will; fluttering wings; a broken backbone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0027","text":"This seeking for my home: oh Zarathustra, you surely know, this seeking was my visitation, it devours me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0028","text":"“Where is—my home?” After that I ask and seek and sought; that I did not find. Oh eternal everywhere, oh eternal nowhere, oh eternal—in vain!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0029","text":"Thus spoke the shadow, and Zarathustra’s face grew longer at his words. “You are my shadow!” he said at last, sadly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0030","text":"Your danger is no small one, you free spirit and wanderer! You have had an evil day: see to it that an even worse evening does not yet come upon you!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0031","text":"To such unsettled ones as you, in the end even a prison seems blessed. Have you ever seen how captured criminals sleep? They sleep peacefully, they enjoy their new security.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0032","text":"Beware lest in the end some narrow faith yet catch you, some hard, stern delusion! For everything that is narrow and firm now seduces and tempts you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0033","text":"You have lost the goal: alas, how will you squander and overcome this loss? Thereby—you have lost the way as well!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0034","text":"You poor roamer, dreamer, you weary butterfly! Do you wish to have a resting-place and home this evening? Then go up to my cave!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0035","text":"The path to my cave leads there. And now I want quickly to run away from you again. Already it lies upon me like a shadow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0036","text":"I want to run alone, so that it may grow bright around me again. For that I must still be merrily on my legs for a long while. But in the evening there shall be—dancing at my place!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DERS:P0037","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DERS:P0037"]}],"source_digest":"9da5a0bee4ff423d837dd668252e07798b80a8905a45d7af08a9d5c023dc4f30","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Der Schatten"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Der Schatten","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT","title":"At Noon","order":137,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_MITT","text":"—Und Zarathustra lief und lief und fand Niemanden mehr und war allein und fand immer wieder sich und genoss und schlürfte seine Einsamkeit und dachte an gute Dinge,—stundenlang. Um die Stunde des Mittags aber, als die Sonne gerade über Zarathustra’s Haupte stand, kam er an einem alten krummen und knorrichten Baume vorbei, der von der reichen Liebe eines Weinstocks rings umarmt und vor sich selber verborgen war: von dem hiengen gelbe Trauben in Fülle dem Wandernden entgegen. Da gelüstete ihn, einen kleinen Durst zu löschen und sich eine Traube abzubrechen; als er aber schon den Arm dazu ausstreckte, da gelüstete ihn etwas Anderes noch mehr: nämlich sich neben den Baum niederzulegen, um die Stunde des vollkommnen Mittags, und zu schlafen.\n\nDiess that Zarathustra; und sobald er auf dem Boden lag, in der Stille und Heimlichkeit des bunten Grases, hatte er auch schon seinen kleinen Durst vergessen und schlief ein. Denn, wie das Sprichwort Zarathustra’s sagt: Eins ist nothwendiger als das Andre. Nur dass seine Augen offen blieben:—sie wurden nämlich nicht satt, den Baum und die Liebe des Weinstocks zu sehn und zu preisen. Im Einschlafen aber sprach Zarathustra also zu seinem Herzen:\n\nStill! Still! Ward die Welt nicht eben vollkommen? Was geschieht mir doch?\n\nWie ein zierlicher Wind, ungesehn, auf getäfeltem Meere tanzt, leicht, federleicht: so—tanzt der Schlaf auf mir,\n\nKein Auge drückt er mir zu, die Seele lässt er mir wach. Leicht ist er, wahrlich! federleicht.\n\nEr überredet mich, ich weiss nicht wie?, er betupft mich innewendig mit schmeichelnder Hand, er zwingt mich. Ja, er zwingt mich, dass meine Seele sich ausstreckt:—\n\n—wie sie mir lang und müde wird, meine wunderliche Seele! Kam ihr eines siebenten Tages Abend gerade am Mittage? Wandelte sie zu lange schon selig zwischen guten und reifen Dingen?\n\nSie streckt sich lang aus, lang,—länger! sie liegt stille, meine wunderliche Seele. Zu viel Gutes hat sie schon geschmeckt, diese. goldene Traurigkeit drückt sie, sie verzieht den Mund.\n\n—Wie ein Schiff, das in seine stillste Bucht einlief:—nun lehnt es sich an die Erde, der langen Reisen müde und der ungewissen Meere. Ist die Erde nicht treuer?\n\nWie solch ein Schiff sich dem Lande anlegt, anschmiegt:—da genügt’s, dass eine Spinne vom Lande her zu ihm ihren Faden spinnt. Keiner stärkeren Taue bedarf es da.\n\nWie solch ein müdes Schiff in der stillsten Bucht: so ruhe auch ich nun der Erde nahe, treu, zutrauend, wartend, mit den leisesten Fäden ihr angebunden.\n\nOh Glück! Oh Glück! Willst du wohl singen, oh meine Seele? Du liegst im Grase. Aber das ist die heimliche feierliche Stunde, wo kein Hirt seine Flöte bläst.\n\nScheue dich! Heisser Mittag schläft auf den Fluren. Singe. nicht! Still! Die Welt ist vollkommen.\n\nSinge nicht, du Gras-Geflügel, oh meine Seele! Flüstere nicht einmal! Sieh doch —still! der alte Mittag schläft, er bewegt den Mund: trinkt er nicht eben einen Tropfen Glücks—\n\n—einen alten braunen Tropfen goldenen Glücks, goldenen Weins? Es huscht über ihn hin, sein Glück lacht. So—lacht ein Gott. Still!—\n\n—„Zum Glück, wie wenig genügt schon zum Glücke!“ So sprach ich einst, und dünkte mich klug. Aber es war eine Lästerung: das lernte ich nun. Kluge Narrn reden besser.\n\nDas Wenigste gerade, das Leiseste, Leichteste, einer Eidechse Rascheln, ein Hauch, ein Husch, ein Augen-Blidk— Wenig macht die Art des besten Glücks. Still!\n\n—Was geschah mir: Horch! Flog die Zeit wohl davon? Falle ich nicht? Fiel ich nicht—horch! in den Brunnen der Ewigkeit?\n\n—Was geschieht mir? Still! Es sticht mich—wehe—in’s Herz? In’s Herz! Oh zerbrich, zerbrich, Herz, nach solchem Glücke, nach solchem Stiche!\n\n—Wie? Ward die Welt nicht eben vollkommen? Rund und reif? Oh des goldenen runden Reifs—wohin fliegt er wohl? Laufe ich ihm nach! Husch!\n\nStill—- (und hier dehnte sich Zarathustra und fühlte, dass er schlafe.)—\n\nAuf! sprach er zu sich selber, du Schläfer! Du Mittagsschläfer! Wohlan, wohlauf, ihr alten Beine! Zeit ist’s und Überzeit, manch gut Stück Wegs blieb euch noch zurück—\n\nNun schlieft ihr euch aus, wie lange doch? Eine halbe Ewigkeit! Wohlan, wohlauf nun, mein altes Herz! Wie lange erst darfst du nach solchem Schlaf—dich auswachen?\n\n(Aber da schlief er schon von Neuem ein, und seine Seele sprach gegen ihn und wehrte sich und legte sich wieder hin)—„Lass mich doch! Still! Ward nicht die Welt eben vollkommen? Oh des goldnen runden Balls!“—\n\n„Steh auf, sprach Zarathustra, du kleine Diebin, du Tagediebin! Wie? Immer noch sich strecken, gähnen, seufzen, hinunterfallen in tiefe Brunnen?\n\nWer bist du doch! Oh meine Seele!“ (und hier erschrak er, denn ein Sonnenstrahl fiel vom Himmel herunter auf sein Gesicht)\n\n„Oh Himmel über mir, sprach er seufzend und setzte sich aufrecht, du schaust mir zu? Du horchst meiner wunderlichen Seele zu?\n\nWann trinkst du diesen Tropfen Thau’s, der auf alle Erden-Dinge niederfiel,—wann trinkst du diese wunderliche Seele—\n\n—wann, Brunnen der Ewigkeit! du heiterer schauerlicher Mittags-Abgrund! wann trinkst du meine Seele in dich zurück?“\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra und erhob sich von seinem Lager am Baume wie aus einer fremden Trunkenheit: und siehe, da stand die Sonne immer noch gerade über seinem Haupte. Es möchte aber Einer daraus mit Recht abnehmen, dass Zarathustra damals nicht lange geschlafen habe.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0001","text":"—Und Zarathustra lief und lief und fand Niemanden mehr und war allein und fand immer wieder sich und genoss und schlürfte seine Einsamkeit und dachte an gute Dinge,—stundenlang. Um die Stunde des Mittags aber, als die Sonne gerade über Zarathustra’s Haupte stand, kam er an einem alten krummen und knorrichten Baume vorbei, der von der reichen Liebe eines Weinstocks rings umarmt und vor sich selber verborgen war: von dem hiengen gelbe Trauben in Fülle dem Wandernden entgegen. Da gelüstete ihn, einen kleinen Durst zu löschen und sich eine Traube abzubrechen; als er aber schon den Arm dazu ausstreckte, da gelüstete ihn etwas Anderes noch mehr: nämlich sich neben den Baum niederzulegen, um die Stunde des vollkommnen Mittags, und zu schlafen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0002","text":"Diess that Zarathustra; und sobald er auf dem Boden lag, in der Stille und Heimlichkeit des bunten Grases, hatte er auch schon seinen kleinen Durst vergessen und schlief ein. Denn, wie das Sprichwort Zarathustra’s sagt: Eins ist nothwendiger als das Andre. Nur dass seine Augen offen blieben:—sie wurden nämlich nicht satt, den Baum und die Liebe des Weinstocks zu sehn und zu preisen. Im Einschlafen aber sprach Zarathustra also zu seinem Herzen:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0003","text":"Still! Still! Ward die Welt nicht eben vollkommen? Was geschieht mir doch?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0004","text":"Wie ein zierlicher Wind, ungesehn, auf getäfeltem Meere tanzt, leicht, federleicht: so—tanzt der Schlaf auf mir,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0005","text":"Kein Auge drückt er mir zu, die Seele lässt er mir wach. Leicht ist er, wahrlich! federleicht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0006","text":"Er überredet mich, ich weiss nicht wie?, er betupft mich innewendig mit schmeichelnder Hand, er zwingt mich. Ja, er zwingt mich, dass meine Seele sich ausstreckt:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0007","text":"—wie sie mir lang und müde wird, meine wunderliche Seele! Kam ihr eines siebenten Tages Abend gerade am Mittage? Wandelte sie zu lange schon selig zwischen guten und reifen Dingen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0008","text":"Sie streckt sich lang aus, lang,—länger! sie liegt stille, meine wunderliche Seele. Zu viel Gutes hat sie schon geschmeckt, diese. goldene Traurigkeit drückt sie, sie verzieht den Mund.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0009","text":"—Wie ein Schiff, das in seine stillste Bucht einlief:—nun lehnt es sich an die Erde, der langen Reisen müde und der ungewissen Meere. Ist die Erde nicht treuer?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0010","text":"Wie solch ein Schiff sich dem Lande anlegt, anschmiegt:—da genügt’s, dass eine Spinne vom Lande her zu ihm ihren Faden spinnt. Keiner stärkeren Taue bedarf es da.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0011","text":"Wie solch ein müdes Schiff in der stillsten Bucht: so ruhe auch ich nun der Erde nahe, treu, zutrauend, wartend, mit den leisesten Fäden ihr angebunden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0012","text":"Oh Glück! Oh Glück! Willst du wohl singen, oh meine Seele? Du liegst im Grase. Aber das ist die heimliche feierliche Stunde, wo kein Hirt seine Flöte bläst.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0013","text":"Scheue dich! Heisser Mittag schläft auf den Fluren. Singe. nicht! Still! Die Welt ist vollkommen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0014","text":"Singe nicht, du Gras-Geflügel, oh meine Seele! Flüstere nicht einmal! Sieh doch —still! der alte Mittag schläft, er bewegt den Mund: trinkt er nicht eben einen Tropfen Glücks—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0015","text":"—einen alten braunen Tropfen goldenen Glücks, goldenen Weins? Es huscht über ihn hin, sein Glück lacht. So—lacht ein Gott. Still!—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0016","text":"—„Zum Glück, wie wenig genügt schon zum Glücke!“ So sprach ich einst, und dünkte mich klug. Aber es war eine Lästerung: das lernte ich nun. Kluge Narrn reden besser.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0017","text":"Das Wenigste gerade, das Leiseste, Leichteste, einer Eidechse Rascheln, ein Hauch, ein Husch, ein Augen-Blidk— Wenig macht die Art des besten Glücks. Still!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0018","text":"—Was geschah mir: Horch! Flog die Zeit wohl davon? Falle ich nicht? Fiel ich nicht—horch! in den Brunnen der Ewigkeit?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0019","text":"—Was geschieht mir? Still! Es sticht mich—wehe—in’s Herz? In’s Herz! Oh zerbrich, zerbrich, Herz, nach solchem Glücke, nach solchem Stiche!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0020","text":"—Wie? Ward die Welt nicht eben vollkommen? Rund und reif? Oh des goldenen runden Reifs—wohin fliegt er wohl? Laufe ich ihm nach! Husch!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0021","text":"Still—- (und hier dehnte sich Zarathustra und fühlte, dass er schlafe.)—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0022","text":"Auf! sprach er zu sich selber, du Schläfer! Du Mittagsschläfer! Wohlan, wohlauf, ihr alten Beine! Zeit ist’s und Überzeit, manch gut Stück Wegs blieb euch noch zurück—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0023","text":"Nun schlieft ihr euch aus, wie lange doch? Eine halbe Ewigkeit! Wohlan, wohlauf nun, mein altes Herz! Wie lange erst darfst du nach solchem Schlaf—dich auswachen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0024","text":"(Aber da schlief er schon von Neuem ein, und seine Seele sprach gegen ihn und wehrte sich und legte sich wieder hin)—„Lass mich doch! Still! Ward nicht die Welt eben vollkommen? Oh des goldnen runden Balls!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0025","text":"„Steh auf, sprach Zarathustra, du kleine Diebin, du Tagediebin! Wie? Immer noch sich strecken, gähnen, seufzen, hinunterfallen in tiefe Brunnen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0026","text":"Wer bist du doch! Oh meine Seele!“ (und hier erschrak er, denn ein Sonnenstrahl fiel vom Himmel herunter auf sein Gesicht)","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0027","text":"„Oh Himmel über mir, sprach er seufzend und setzte sich aufrecht, du schaust mir zu? Du horchst meiner wunderlichen Seele zu?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0028","text":"Wann trinkst du diesen Tropfen Thau’s, der auf alle Erden-Dinge niederfiel,—wann trinkst du diese wunderliche Seele—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0029","text":"—wann, Brunnen der Ewigkeit! du heiterer schauerlicher Mittags-Abgrund! wann trinkst du meine Seele in dich zurück?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0030","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra und erhob sich von seinem Lager am Baume wie aus einer fremden Trunkenheit: und siehe, da stand die Sonne immer noch gerade über seinem Haupte. Es möchte aber Einer daraus mit Recht abnehmen, dass Zarathustra damals nicht lange geschlafen habe.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"4e81f6f6168ad4a43c51d1c1f6cbde18a74d3468811df5590a0c7478aeb6a4e2","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183736877555Z_34710d16","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"At Noon","source_sha256":"4e81f6f6168ad4a43c51d1c1f6cbde18a74d3468811df5590a0c7478aeb6a4e2","sha256":"f67afa0ef897c34833b1af5036518a138ee69fb554581e91e90ef833b7235d43","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:37:36.877576+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0001","text":"—And Zarathustra ran and ran and found no one any more and was alone and again and again found himself and enjoyed and sipped at his solitude and thought of good things,—for hours. But about the hour of noon, when the sun stood directly above Zarathustra’s head, he passed by an old crooked and gnarled tree, which was embraced all round by the rich love of a vine and hidden from itself: from it hung yellow grapes in abundance towards the wanderer. Then he felt the desire to quench a small thirst and to pluck himself a grape; but when he had already stretched out his arm to do so, he felt the desire for something else still more: namely, to lie down beside the tree, at the hour of perfect noon, and to sleep.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0002","text":"This Zarathustra did; and as soon as he lay upon the ground, in the stillness and secrecy of the many-coloured grass, he had already forgotten his small thirst and fell asleep. For, as Zarathustra’s proverb says: One thing is more necessary than another. Only his eyes remained open:—for they did not tire of seeing and praising the tree and the love of the vine. But as he fell asleep Zarathustra spoke thus to his heart:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0003","text":"Hush! Hush! Has not the world just now become perfect? What is happening to me?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0004","text":"Like a delicate wind, unseen, dancing upon a tiled sea, light, feather-light: thus—sleep dances upon me,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0005","text":"It closes no eye for me, it leaves my soul awake. Light is it, verily! feather-light.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0006","text":"It persuades me, I know not how?, it touches me inwardly with flattering hand, it compels me. Yes, it compels me, so that my soul stretches itself out:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0007","text":"—as it becomes long and weary to me, my strange soul! Did an evening of a seventh day come upon it precisely at noon? Has it wandered blissfully too long already among good and ripe things?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0008","text":"It stretches itself out long, long,—longer! it lies still, my strange soul. It has already tasted too much good, this. golden sadness presses upon it, it twists its mouth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0009","text":"—Like a ship that has entered its stillest bay:—now it leans against the earth, weary of long voyages and uncertain seas. Is not the earth more faithful?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0010","text":"When such a ship draws up to the land, nestles against it:—then it suffices for a spider to spin its thread to it from the land. No stronger ropes are needed there.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0011","text":"Like such a weary ship in the stillest bay: so I too now rest near the earth, faithful, trusting, waiting, bound to it with the finest threads.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0012","text":"Oh happiness! Oh happiness! Would you like to sing, oh my soul? You lie in the grass. But this is the secret solemn hour when no shepherd plays his flute.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0013","text":"Be shy! Hot noon sleeps upon the fields. Do not sing! Hush! The world is perfect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0014","text":"Do not sing, you grass-fowl, oh my soul! Do not even whisper! Just see—hush! old noon sleeps, it moves its mouth: is it not just drinking a drop of happiness—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0015","text":"—an old brown drop of golden happiness, of golden wine? It flits over him, his happiness laughs. Thus—a god laughs. Hush!—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0016","text":"—“For happiness, how little already suffices for happiness!” Thus I once spoke, and thought myself wise. But it was a blasphemy: that I have now learned. Wise fools speak better.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0017","text":"Precisely the least thing, the quietest, lightest, a lizard’s rustling, a breath, a flitting, a blink of an eye—Little makes up the manner of the best happiness. Hush!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0018","text":"—What happened to me: Hark! Has time perhaps flown away? Am I not falling? Did I not fall—hark! into the well of eternity?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0019","text":"—What is happening to me? Hush! It pricks me—alas—in the heart? In the heart! Oh break, break, heart, after such happiness, after such a prick!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0020","text":"—What? Has not the world just now become perfect? Round and ripe? Oh, the golden round ring—whither does it fly, I wonder? Do I run after it! Hush!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0021","text":"Hush—- (and here Zarathustra stretched himself and felt that he was asleep.)—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0022","text":"Up! he said to himself, you sleeper! You noon-sleeper! Come then, up, you old legs! It is time and over-time, many a good stretch of road still remains for you—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0023","text":"Now you have slept yourselves out, how long indeed? Half an eternity! Come then, up now, my old heart! How long only may you, after such a sleep—wake yourself out?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0024","text":"(But there he had already fallen asleep anew, and his soul spoke against him and resisted and lay down again)—“Only leave me! Hush! Has not the world just now become perfect? Oh, the golden round ball!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0025","text":"“Rise, said Zarathustra, you little thief, you thief of the day! What? Still stretching, yawning, sighing, falling down into deep wells?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0026","text":"“Who are you, after all! Oh my soul!” (and here he was startled, for a ray of sunlight fell down from heaven upon his face)","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0027","text":"“Oh heaven above me, he said sighing and sat upright, are you watching me? Are you listening to my strange soul?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0028","text":"When will you drink this drop of dew that fell down upon all earthly things,—when will you drink this strange soul—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0029","text":"—when, well of eternity! you serene, dreadful abyss of noon! when will you drink my soul back into yourself?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_MITT:P0030","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra and rose from his bed beneath the tree as though from a strange intoxication: and behold, the sun still stood directly above his head. From this one might rightly infer that Zarathustra had not slept long at that time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_MITT:P0030"]}],"source_digest":"d949729225b2b1eacb3b0b4c56870c6bcead609b7f9482b6ed4ea9a1df05b9d7","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Mittags"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Mittags","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0","title":"The Greeting","order":138,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_DBS0","text":"Am späten Nachmittage war es erst, dass Zarathustra, nach langem umsonstigen Suchen und Umherstreifen, wieder zu seiner Höhle heimkam. Als er aber derselben gegenüberstand, nicht zwanzig Schritt mehr von ihr ferne, da geschah das, was er jetzt am wenigsten erwartete: von Neuem hörte er den grossen Nothschrei . Und, erstaunlich! diess Mal kam derselbige aus seiner eignen Höhle. Es war aber ein langer vielfältiger seltsamer Schrei, und Zarathustra unterschied deutlich, dass er sich aus vielen Stimmen zusammensetze: mochte er schon, aus der Ferne gehört, gleich dem Schrei aus einem einzigen Munde klingen.\n\nDa sprang Zarathustra auf seine Höhle zu, und siehe! welches Schauspiel erwartete ihn erst nach diesem Hörspiele! Denn da sassen sie allesammt bei einander, an denen er des Tags vorübergegangen war: der König zur Rechten und der König zur Linken, der alte Zauberer, der Papst, der freiwillige Bettler, der Schatten, der Gewissenhafte des Geistes, der traurige Wahrsager und der Esel; der hässlichste Mensch aber hatte sich eine Krone aufgesetzt und zwei Purpurgürtel umgeschlungen,—denn er liebte es, gleich allen Hässlichen, sich zu verkleiden und schön zu thun. Inmitten aber dieser betrübten Gesellschaft stand der Adler Zarathustra’s, gesträubt und unruhig, denn er sollte auf zu Vieles antworten, wofür sein Stolz keine Antwort hatte; die kluge Schlange aber hieng um seinen Hals.\n\nDiess Alles schaute Zarathustra mit grosser Verwunderung; dann prüfte er jeden Einzelnen seiner Gäste mit leutseliger Neugierde, las ihre Seelen ab und wunderte sich von Neuem. Inzwischen hatten sich die Versammelten von ihren Sitzen erhoben und warteten mit Ehrfurcht, dass Zarathustra reden werde. Zarathustra aber sprach also:\n\n„Ihr Verzweifelnden! Ihr Wunderlichen! Ich hörte also euren Nothschrei? Und nun weiss ich auch, wo Der zu suchen ist, den ich umsonst heute suchte: der höhere Mensch—:\n\n—in meiner eignen Höhle sitzt er, der höhere Mensch! Aber was wundere ich mich! Habe ich ihn nicht selber zu mir gelockt durch Honig-Opfer und listige Lockrufe meines Glücks?\n\nDoch dünkt mir, ihr taugt euch schlecht zur Gesellschaft, ihr macht einander das Herz unwirsch, ihr Nothschreienden, wenn ihr hier beisammen sitzt? Es muss erst Einer kommen,\n\n—Einer, der euch wieder lachen macht, ein guter fröhlicher Hanswurst, ein Tänzer und Wind und Wildfang, irgend ein alter Narr:—was dünket euch?\n\nVergebt mir doch, ihr Verzweifelnden, dass ich vor euch mit solch kleinen Worten rede, unwürdig, wahrlich!, solcher Gäste! Aber ihr errathet nicht, was mein Herz muthwillig macht:—\n\n—ihr selber thut es und euer Anblick, vergebt es mir! Jeder nämlich wird muthig, der einem Verzweifelnden zuschaut. Einem Verzweifelnden zuzusprechen—dazu dünkt sich jeder stark genug.\n\nMir selber gabt ihr diese Kraft,—eine gute Gabe, meine hohen Gäste! Ein rechtschaffnes Gastgeschenk! Wohlan, so zürnt nun nicht, dass ich euch auch vom Meinigen anbiete.\n\nDiess hier ist mein Reich und meine Herrschaft: was aber mein ist, für diesen Abend und diese Nacht soll es euer sein. Meine Thiere sollen euch dienen: meine Höhle sei eure Ruhestatt!\n\nBei mir zu Heim-und-Hause soll Keiner verzweifeln, in meinem Reviere schütze ich jeden vor seinen wilden Thieren. Und das ist das Erste, was ich euch anbiete: Sicherheit!\n\nDas Zweite aber ist: mein kleiner Finger. Und habt ihr den erst, so nehmt nur noch die ganze Hand, wohlan! und das Herz dazu! Willkommen hier, willkommen, meine Gastfreunde!“\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra und lachte vor Liebe und Bosheit. Nach dieser Begrüssung verneigten sich seine Gäste abermals und schwiegen ehrfürchtig; der König zur Rechten aber antwortete ihm in ihrem Namen.\n\n„Daran, oh Zarathustra, wie du uns Hand und Gruss botest, erkennen wir dich als Zarathustra. Du erniedrigtest dich vor uns; fast thatest du unserer Ehrfurcht wehe—:\n\n—wer aber vermochte gleich dir sich mit solchem Stolze zu erniedrigen? Das richtet uns selber auf, ein Labsal ist es unsern Augen und Herzen.\n\nDiess allein nur zu schaun, stiegen gern wir auf höhere Berge, als dieser Berg ist. Als Schaulustige nämlich kamen wir, wir wollten sehn, was trübe Augen hell macht.\n\nUnd siehe, schon ist es vorbei mit allem unsern Nothschrein. Schon steht Sinn und Herz uns offen und ist entzückt. Wenig fehlt: und unser Muth wird muthwillig.\n\nNichts, oh Zarathustra, wächst Erfreulicheres auf Erden, als ein hoher starker Wille: der ist ihr schönstes Gewächs. Eine ganze Landschaft erquickt sich an Einem solchen Baume.\n\nDer Pinie vergleiche ich, wer gleich dir, oh Zarathustra, aufwächst: lang, schweigend, hart, allein, besten biegsamsten Holzes, herrlich,—\n\n—zuletzt aber hinausgreifend mit starken grünen Ästen nach seiner Herrschaft, starke Fragen fragend vor Winden und Wettern und was immer auf Höhen heimisch ist,\n\n—stärker antwortend, ein Befehlender, ein Siegreicher: oh wer sollte nicht, solche Gewächse zu schaun, auf hohe Berge steigen?\n\nDeines Baumes hier, oh Zarathustra, erlabt sich auch der Düstere, der Missrathene, an deinem Anblicke wird auch der Unstäte sicher und heilt sein Herz.\n\nUnd wahrlich, zu deinem Berge und Baume richten sich heute viele Augen; eine grosse Sehnsucht hat sich aufgemacht, und Manche lernten fragen: wer ist Zarathustra?\n\nUnd wem du jemals dein Lied und deinen Honig in’s Ohr geträufelt: alle die Versteckten, die Einsiedler, die Zweisiedler sprachen mit Einem Male zu ihrem Herzen:\n\n„Lebt Zarathustra noch? Es lohnt sich nicht mehr zu leben, Alles ist gleich, Alles ist umsonst: oder—wir müssen mit Zarathustra leben!“\n\n„Warum kommt er nicht, der sich so lange ankündigte? also fragen Viele; verschlang ihn die Einsamkeit? Oder sollen wir wohl zu ihm kommen?“\n\nNun geschieht’s, dass die Einsamkeit selber mürbe wird und zerbricht, einem Grabe gleich, das zerbricht und seine Todten nicht mehr halten kann. Überall sieht man Auferstandene.\n\nNun steigen und steigen die Wellen um deinen Berg, oh Zarathustra. Und wie hoch auch deine Höhe ist, Viele müssen zu dir hinauf; dein Nachen soll nicht lange mehr im Trocknen sitzen.\n\nUnd dass wir Verzweifelnde jetzt in deine Höhle kamen und schon nicht mehr verzweifeln: ein Wahr- und Vorzeichen ist es nur, davon, dass Bessere zu dir unterwegs sind,—\n\n—denn er selber ist zu dir unterwegs, der letzte Rest Gottes unter Menschen, das ist: alle die Menschen der grossen Sehnsucht, des grossen Ekels, des grossen Überdrusses,\n\n—Alle, die nicht leben wollen, oder sie lernen wieder hoffen —oder sie lernen von dir, oh Zarathustra, die grosse Hoffnung!“\n\nAlso sprach der König zur Rechten und ergriff die Hand Zarathustra’s, um sie zu küssen; aber Zarathustra wehrte seiner Verehrung und trat erschreckt zurück, schweigend und plötzlich wie in weite Fernen entfliehend. Nach einer kleinen Weile aber war er schon wieder bei seinen Gästen, blickte sie mit hellen, prüfenden Augen an und sprach:\n\nMeine Gäste, ihr höheren Menschen, ich will deutsch und deutlich mit euch reden. Nicht auf euch wartete ich hier in diesen Bergen.\n\n(„Deutsch und deutlich? Dass Gott erbarm! sagte hier der König zur Linken, bei Seite; man merkt, er kennt die lieben Deutschen nicht, dieser Weise aus dem Morgenlande!\n\nAber er meint „deutsch und derb“—wohlan! Das ist heutzutage noch nicht der schlimmste Geschmack!“)\n\n„Ihr mögt wahrlich insgesammt höhere Menschen sein, fuhr Zarathustra fort: aber für mich—seid ihr nicht hoch und stark genug.\n\nFür mich, das heisst: für das Unerbittliche, das in mir schweigt, aber nicht immer schweigen wird. Und gehört ihr zu mir, so doch nicht als mein rechter Arm.\n\nWer nämlich selber auf kranken und zarten Beinen steht, gleich euch, der will vor Allem, ob er’s weiss oder sich verbirgt: dass er geschont werde.\n\nMeine Arme und meine Beine aber schone ich nicht, ich schone meine Krieger nicht: wieso könntet ihr zu meinem Kriege taugen?\n\nMit euch verdürbe ich mir jeden Sieg noch. Und Mancher von euch fiele schon um, wenn er nur den lauten Schall meiner Trommeln hörte.\n\nAuch seid ihr mir nicht schön genug und wohlgeboren. Ich brauche reine glatte Spiegel für meine Lehren; auf eurer Oberfläche verzerrt sich noch mein eignes Bildniss.\n\nEure Schultern drückt manche Last, manche Erinnerung; manch schlimmer Zwerg hockt in euren Winkeln. Es giebt verborgenen Pöbel auch in euch.\n\nUnd seid ihr auch hoch und höherer Art: Vieles an euch ist krumm und missgestalt. Da ist kein Schmied in der Welt, der euch mir zurecht und gerade schlüge.\n\nIhr seid nur Brücken: mögen Höhere auf euch hinüber schreiten! Ihr bedeutet Stufen: so zürnt Dem nicht, der über euch hinweg in seine Höhe steigt!\n\nAus eurem Samen mag auch mir einst ein ächter Sohn und vollkommener Erbe wachsen: aber das ist ferne. Ihr selber seid Die nicht, welchen mein Erbgut und Name zugehört.\n\nNicht auf euch warte ich hier in diesen Bergen, nicht mit euch darf ich zum letzten Male niedersteigen. Als Vorzeichen kamt ihr mir nur, dass schon Höhere zu mir unterwegs sind,—\n\n— nicht die Menschen der grossen Sehnsucht, des grossen Ekels, des grossen Überdrusses und Das, was ihr den Überrest Gottes nanntet.\n\n—Nein! Nein! Drei Mal Nein! Auf Andere warte ich hier in diesen Bergen und will meinen Fuss nicht ohne sie von dannen heben,\n\n—auf Höhere, Stärkere, Sieghaftere, Wohlgemuthere, Solche, die rechtwinklig gebaut sind an Leib und Seele: lachende Löwen müssen kommen!\n\nOh, meine Gastfreunde, ihr Wunderlichen,—hörtet ihr noch Nichts von meinen Kindern? Und dass sie zu mir unterwegs sind?\n\nSprecht mir doch von meinen Gärten, von meinen glückseligen Inseln, von meiner neuen schönen Art,—warum sprecht ihr mir nicht davon?\n\nDiess Gastgeschenk erbitte ich mir von eurer Liebe, dass ihr mir von meinen Kindern sprecht. Hierzu bin ich reich, hierzu ward ich arm: was gab ich nicht hin,\n\n—was gäbe ich nicht hin, dass ich Eins hätte: diese Kinder, diese lebendige Pflanzung, diese Lebensbäume meines Willens und meiner höchsten Hoffnung!“\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra und hielt plötzlich inne in seiner Rede: denn ihn überfiel seine Sehnsucht, und er schloss Augen und Mund vor der Bewegung seines Herzens. Und auch alle seine Gäste schwiegen und standen still und bestürzt: nur dass der alte Wahrsager mit Händen und Gebärden Zeichen gab.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0001","text":"Am späten Nachmittage war es erst, dass Zarathustra, nach langem umsonstigen Suchen und Umherstreifen, wieder zu seiner Höhle heimkam. Als er aber derselben gegenüberstand, nicht zwanzig Schritt mehr von ihr ferne, da geschah das, was er jetzt am wenigsten erwartete: von Neuem hörte er den grossen Nothschrei . Und, erstaunlich! diess Mal kam derselbige aus seiner eignen Höhle. Es war aber ein langer vielfältiger seltsamer Schrei, und Zarathustra unterschied deutlich, dass er sich aus vielen Stimmen zusammensetze: mochte er schon, aus der Ferne gehört, gleich dem Schrei aus einem einzigen Munde klingen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0002","text":"Da sprang Zarathustra auf seine Höhle zu, und siehe! welches Schauspiel erwartete ihn erst nach diesem Hörspiele! Denn da sassen sie allesammt bei einander, an denen er des Tags vorübergegangen war: der König zur Rechten und der König zur Linken, der alte Zauberer, der Papst, der freiwillige Bettler, der Schatten, der Gewissenhafte des Geistes, der traurige Wahrsager und der Esel; der hässlichste Mensch aber hatte sich eine Krone aufgesetzt und zwei Purpurgürtel umgeschlungen,—denn er liebte es, gleich allen Hässlichen, sich zu verkleiden und schön zu thun. Inmitten aber dieser betrübten Gesellschaft stand der Adler Zarathustra’s, gesträubt und unruhig, denn er sollte auf zu Vieles antworten, wofür sein Stolz keine Antwort hatte; die kluge Schlange aber hieng um seinen Hals.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0003","text":"Diess Alles schaute Zarathustra mit grosser Verwunderung; dann prüfte er jeden Einzelnen seiner Gäste mit leutseliger Neugierde, las ihre Seelen ab und wunderte sich von Neuem. Inzwischen hatten sich die Versammelten von ihren Sitzen erhoben und warteten mit Ehrfurcht, dass Zarathustra reden werde. Zarathustra aber sprach also:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0004","text":"„Ihr Verzweifelnden! Ihr Wunderlichen! Ich hörte also euren Nothschrei? Und nun weiss ich auch, wo Der zu suchen ist, den ich umsonst heute suchte: der höhere Mensch—:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0005","text":"—in meiner eignen Höhle sitzt er, der höhere Mensch! Aber was wundere ich mich! Habe ich ihn nicht selber zu mir gelockt durch Honig-Opfer und listige Lockrufe meines Glücks?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0006","text":"Doch dünkt mir, ihr taugt euch schlecht zur Gesellschaft, ihr macht einander das Herz unwirsch, ihr Nothschreienden, wenn ihr hier beisammen sitzt? Es muss erst Einer kommen,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0007","text":"—Einer, der euch wieder lachen macht, ein guter fröhlicher Hanswurst, ein Tänzer und Wind und Wildfang, irgend ein alter Narr:—was dünket euch?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0008","text":"Vergebt mir doch, ihr Verzweifelnden, dass ich vor euch mit solch kleinen Worten rede, unwürdig, wahrlich!, solcher Gäste! Aber ihr errathet nicht, was mein Herz muthwillig macht:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0009","text":"—ihr selber thut es und euer Anblick, vergebt es mir! Jeder nämlich wird muthig, der einem Verzweifelnden zuschaut. Einem Verzweifelnden zuzusprechen—dazu dünkt sich jeder stark genug.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0010","text":"Mir selber gabt ihr diese Kraft,—eine gute Gabe, meine hohen Gäste! Ein rechtschaffnes Gastgeschenk! Wohlan, so zürnt nun nicht, dass ich euch auch vom Meinigen anbiete.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0011","text":"Diess hier ist mein Reich und meine Herrschaft: was aber mein ist, für diesen Abend und diese Nacht soll es euer sein. Meine Thiere sollen euch dienen: meine Höhle sei eure Ruhestatt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0012","text":"Bei mir zu Heim-und-Hause soll Keiner verzweifeln, in meinem Reviere schütze ich jeden vor seinen wilden Thieren. Und das ist das Erste, was ich euch anbiete: Sicherheit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0013","text":"Das Zweite aber ist: mein kleiner Finger. Und habt ihr den erst, so nehmt nur noch die ganze Hand, wohlan! und das Herz dazu! Willkommen hier, willkommen, meine Gastfreunde!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0014","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra und lachte vor Liebe und Bosheit. Nach dieser Begrüssung verneigten sich seine Gäste abermals und schwiegen ehrfürchtig; der König zur Rechten aber antwortete ihm in ihrem Namen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0015","text":"„Daran, oh Zarathustra, wie du uns Hand und Gruss botest, erkennen wir dich als Zarathustra. Du erniedrigtest dich vor uns; fast thatest du unserer Ehrfurcht wehe—:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0016","text":"—wer aber vermochte gleich dir sich mit solchem Stolze zu erniedrigen? Das richtet uns selber auf, ein Labsal ist es unsern Augen und Herzen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0017","text":"Diess allein nur zu schaun, stiegen gern wir auf höhere Berge, als dieser Berg ist. Als Schaulustige nämlich kamen wir, wir wollten sehn, was trübe Augen hell macht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0018","text":"Und siehe, schon ist es vorbei mit allem unsern Nothschrein. Schon steht Sinn und Herz uns offen und ist entzückt. Wenig fehlt: und unser Muth wird muthwillig.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0019","text":"Nichts, oh Zarathustra, wächst Erfreulicheres auf Erden, als ein hoher starker Wille: der ist ihr schönstes Gewächs. Eine ganze Landschaft erquickt sich an Einem solchen Baume.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0020","text":"Der Pinie vergleiche ich, wer gleich dir, oh Zarathustra, aufwächst: lang, schweigend, hart, allein, besten biegsamsten Holzes, herrlich,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0021","text":"—zuletzt aber hinausgreifend mit starken grünen Ästen nach seiner Herrschaft, starke Fragen fragend vor Winden und Wettern und was immer auf Höhen heimisch ist,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0022","text":"—stärker antwortend, ein Befehlender, ein Siegreicher: oh wer sollte nicht, solche Gewächse zu schaun, auf hohe Berge steigen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0023","text":"Deines Baumes hier, oh Zarathustra, erlabt sich auch der Düstere, der Missrathene, an deinem Anblicke wird auch der Unstäte sicher und heilt sein Herz.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0024","text":"Und wahrlich, zu deinem Berge und Baume richten sich heute viele Augen; eine grosse Sehnsucht hat sich aufgemacht, und Manche lernten fragen: wer ist Zarathustra?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0025","text":"Und wem du jemals dein Lied und deinen Honig in’s Ohr geträufelt: alle die Versteckten, die Einsiedler, die Zweisiedler sprachen mit Einem Male zu ihrem Herzen:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0026","text":"„Lebt Zarathustra noch? Es lohnt sich nicht mehr zu leben, Alles ist gleich, Alles ist umsonst: oder—wir müssen mit Zarathustra leben!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0027","text":"„Warum kommt er nicht, der sich so lange ankündigte? also fragen Viele; verschlang ihn die Einsamkeit? Oder sollen wir wohl zu ihm kommen?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0028","text":"Nun geschieht’s, dass die Einsamkeit selber mürbe wird und zerbricht, einem Grabe gleich, das zerbricht und seine Todten nicht mehr halten kann. Überall sieht man Auferstandene.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0029","text":"Nun steigen und steigen die Wellen um deinen Berg, oh Zarathustra. Und wie hoch auch deine Höhe ist, Viele müssen zu dir hinauf; dein Nachen soll nicht lange mehr im Trocknen sitzen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0030","text":"Und dass wir Verzweifelnde jetzt in deine Höhle kamen und schon nicht mehr verzweifeln: ein Wahr- und Vorzeichen ist es nur, davon, dass Bessere zu dir unterwegs sind,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0031","text":"—denn er selber ist zu dir unterwegs, der letzte Rest Gottes unter Menschen, das ist: alle die Menschen der grossen Sehnsucht, des grossen Ekels, des grossen Überdrusses,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0032","text":"—Alle, die nicht leben wollen, oder sie lernen wieder hoffen —oder sie lernen von dir, oh Zarathustra, die grosse Hoffnung!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0033","text":"Also sprach der König zur Rechten und ergriff die Hand Zarathustra’s, um sie zu küssen; aber Zarathustra wehrte seiner Verehrung und trat erschreckt zurück, schweigend und plötzlich wie in weite Fernen entfliehend. Nach einer kleinen Weile aber war er schon wieder bei seinen Gästen, blickte sie mit hellen, prüfenden Augen an und sprach:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0034","text":"Meine Gäste, ihr höheren Menschen, ich will deutsch und deutlich mit euch reden. Nicht auf euch wartete ich hier in diesen Bergen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0035","text":"(„Deutsch und deutlich? Dass Gott erbarm! sagte hier der König zur Linken, bei Seite; man merkt, er kennt die lieben Deutschen nicht, dieser Weise aus dem Morgenlande!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0036","text":"Aber er meint „deutsch und derb“—wohlan! Das ist heutzutage noch nicht der schlimmste Geschmack!“)","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0037","text":"„Ihr mögt wahrlich insgesammt höhere Menschen sein, fuhr Zarathustra fort: aber für mich—seid ihr nicht hoch und stark genug.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0038","text":"Für mich, das heisst: für das Unerbittliche, das in mir schweigt, aber nicht immer schweigen wird. Und gehört ihr zu mir, so doch nicht als mein rechter Arm.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0039","text":"Wer nämlich selber auf kranken und zarten Beinen steht, gleich euch, der will vor Allem, ob er’s weiss oder sich verbirgt: dass er geschont werde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0040","text":"Meine Arme und meine Beine aber schone ich nicht, ich schone meine Krieger nicht: wieso könntet ihr zu meinem Kriege taugen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0041","text":"Mit euch verdürbe ich mir jeden Sieg noch. Und Mancher von euch fiele schon um, wenn er nur den lauten Schall meiner Trommeln hörte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0042","text":"Auch seid ihr mir nicht schön genug und wohlgeboren. Ich brauche reine glatte Spiegel für meine Lehren; auf eurer Oberfläche verzerrt sich noch mein eignes Bildniss.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0043","text":"Eure Schultern drückt manche Last, manche Erinnerung; manch schlimmer Zwerg hockt in euren Winkeln. Es giebt verborgenen Pöbel auch in euch.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0044","text":"Und seid ihr auch hoch und höherer Art: Vieles an euch ist krumm und missgestalt. Da ist kein Schmied in der Welt, der euch mir zurecht und gerade schlüge.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0045","text":"Ihr seid nur Brücken: mögen Höhere auf euch hinüber schreiten! Ihr bedeutet Stufen: so zürnt Dem nicht, der über euch hinweg in seine Höhe steigt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0046","text":"Aus eurem Samen mag auch mir einst ein ächter Sohn und vollkommener Erbe wachsen: aber das ist ferne. Ihr selber seid Die nicht, welchen mein Erbgut und Name zugehört.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0047","text":"Nicht auf euch warte ich hier in diesen Bergen, nicht mit euch darf ich zum letzten Male niedersteigen. Als Vorzeichen kamt ihr mir nur, dass schon Höhere zu mir unterwegs sind,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0048","text":"— nicht die Menschen der grossen Sehnsucht, des grossen Ekels, des grossen Überdrusses und Das, was ihr den Überrest Gottes nanntet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0049","text":"—Nein! Nein! Drei Mal Nein! Auf Andere warte ich hier in diesen Bergen und will meinen Fuss nicht ohne sie von dannen heben,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0050","text":"—auf Höhere, Stärkere, Sieghaftere, Wohlgemuthere, Solche, die rechtwinklig gebaut sind an Leib und Seele: lachende Löwen müssen kommen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0051","text":"Oh, meine Gastfreunde, ihr Wunderlichen,—hörtet ihr noch Nichts von meinen Kindern? Und dass sie zu mir unterwegs sind?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0052","text":"Sprecht mir doch von meinen Gärten, von meinen glückseligen Inseln, von meiner neuen schönen Art,—warum sprecht ihr mir nicht davon?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0053","text":"Diess Gastgeschenk erbitte ich mir von eurer Liebe, dass ihr mir von meinen Kindern sprecht. Hierzu bin ich reich, hierzu ward ich arm: was gab ich nicht hin,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0054","text":"—was gäbe ich nicht hin, dass ich Eins hätte: diese Kinder, diese lebendige Pflanzung, diese Lebensbäume meines Willens und meiner höchsten Hoffnung!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0055","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra und hielt plötzlich inne in seiner Rede: denn ihn überfiel seine Sehnsucht, und er schloss Augen und Mund vor der Bewegung seines Herzens. Und auch alle seine Gäste schwiegen und standen still und bestürzt: nur dass der alte Wahrsager mit Händen und Gebärden Zeichen gab.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"e784a8625fe962307d3323647cf149415a4c789fd5326f9003f8681ad55dbd65","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183808724730Z_99d27f39","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"The Greeting","source_sha256":"e784a8625fe962307d3323647cf149415a4c789fd5326f9003f8681ad55dbd65","sha256":"c7eaf259bf8f8ea718b58c7155ee35188d67d8f6ac9deb0538403c42987557da","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:38:08.724745+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0001","text":"It was only in the late afternoon that Zarathustra, after long fruitless searching and wandering, came home again to his cave. But when he stood opposite it, not twenty paces distant from it, there occurred what he now least expected: once more he heard the great cry of distress. And, to his astonishment! this time it came from his own cave. It was, however, a long, manifold, strange cry, and Zarathustra clearly distinguished that it was composed of many voices: though, heard from afar, it might sound like the cry from a single mouth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0002","text":"Then Zarathustra sprang towards his cave, and behold! what a spectacle awaited him after this auditory spectacle! For there they all sat together, those whom he had passed by during the day: the king on the right and the king on the left, the old magician, the pope, the voluntary beggar, the shadow, the conscientious one of the spirit, the sorrowful soothsayer, and the ass; but the ugliest man had put on a crown and wound two purple girdles around himself,—for he loved, like all ugly people, to disguise himself and to act handsome. But in the midst of this afflicted company stood Zarathustra's eagle, bristling and uneasy, for it was expected to answer too much to which its pride had no answer; while the clever serpent hung about its neck.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0003","text":"Zarathustra looked upon all this with great astonishment; then he examined each individual one of his guests with kindly curiosity, read their souls, and marvelled anew. Meanwhile those assembled had risen from their seats and waited reverently for Zarathustra to speak. But Zarathustra spoke thus:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0004","text":"“You despairing ones! You strange ones! So I heard your cry of distress? And now I also know where he is to be sought whom I sought in vain today: the higher man—:”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0005","text":"—he sits in my own cave, the higher man! But why am I surprised? Have I not myself lured him to me through honey-sacrifices and crafty alluring calls of my happiness?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0006","text":"Yet it seems to me that you are ill-suited to company, you make one another's hearts ill-tempered, you criers of distress, when you sit together here? There must first come One,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0007","text":"—One who makes you laugh again, a good merry Hanswurst, a dancer and wind and wild young scamp, some old fool:—what do you think?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0008","text":"Forgive me, you despairing ones, for speaking before you with such small words, unworthy, truly!, of such guests! But you do not guess what makes my heart mischievous:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0009","text":"—you yourselves do it, and your appearance; forgive me! For everyone becomes courageous who looks upon one in despair. To address one in despair—everyone thinks himself strong enough for that.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0010","text":"You yourselves gave me this strength,—a good gift, my lofty guests! A proper guest-gift! Well then, do not now be angry that I too offer you something of my own.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0011","text":"This here is my kingdom and my dominion: but what is mine shall be yours for this evening and this night. My animals shall serve you: my cave shall be your resting-place!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0012","text":"With me, at home, no one shall despair; in my domain I protect everyone from his wild beasts. And that is the first thing I offer you: security!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0013","text":"But the second is: my little finger. And once you have that, then take the whole hand as well, come now! and the heart besides! Welcome here, welcome, my guest-friends!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0014","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra and laughed from love and malice. After this greeting his guests bowed once more and were reverently silent; but the king on the right answered him in their name.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0015","text":"“From this, O Zarathustra, from the way you offered us hand and greeting, we recognize you as Zarathustra. You humbled yourself before us; you almost wounded our reverence—:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0016","text":"—but who, like you, could humble himself with such pride? That raises us up ourselves; it is a refreshment to our eyes and hearts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0017","text":"To see this alone, we gladly climbed higher mountains than this mountain is. For we came as spectators; we wished to see what makes dim eyes bright.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0018","text":"And behold, already all our cries of distress are over. Already mind and heart stand open within us and are enraptured. Little is lacking: and our courage becomes mischievous.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0019","text":"Nothing, O Zarathustra, grows more delightful on earth than a lofty, strong will: it is her fairest growth. An entire landscape is refreshed by one such tree.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0020","text":"I compare to the pine whoever grows up like you, O Zarathustra: tall, silent, hard, alone, of the finest, most pliant wood, splendid,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0021","text":"—but at last reaching out with strong green branches after its dominion, asking strong questions of winds and weather and whatever else is at home on the heights,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0022","text":"—answering more strongly, a commander, a victor: O who would not climb high mountains to behold such growths?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0023","text":"At your tree here, O Zarathustra, the gloomy one too, the ill-formed one, refreshes himself; at the sight of you even the unsettled one becomes secure and heals his heart.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0024","text":"And truly, many eyes today turn toward your mountain and tree; a great longing has arisen, and some have learned to ask: who is Zarathustra?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0025","text":"And all those into whose ear you have ever dripped your song and your honey: all the hidden ones, the hermits, the two-hermits spoke all at once to their hearts:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0026","text":"“Is Zarathustra still alive? It is no longer worth living, everything is alike, everything is in vain: or—we must live with Zarathustra!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0027","text":"“Why does he not come, he who announced himself so long ago? thus many ask; has solitude swallowed him up? Or should we perhaps go to him?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0028","text":"Now it happens that solitude itself grows soft and breaks apart, like a grave that breaks apart and can no longer hold its dead. Everywhere one sees those who have risen again.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0029","text":"Now the waves rise and rise around your mountain, O Zarathustra. And however high your height may be, many must climb up to you; your boat shall not remain dry for much longer.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0030","text":"And that we despairing ones have now come into your cave and already despair no longer: it is only a true sign and portent that better ones are on their way to you,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0031","text":"—for he himself is on his way to you, the last remnant of God among human beings, that is: all those human beings of great longing, great disgust, great weariness,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0032","text":"—all who do not wish to live, or who learn to hope again—or who learn from you, O Zarathustra, the great hope!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0033","text":"Thus spoke the king on the right and took Zarathustra’s hand in order to kiss it; but Zarathustra resisted his veneration and stepped back in fright, silent and suddenly, as though fleeing into distant reaches. But after a little while he was already again with his guests, looked at them with bright, scrutinizing eyes, and spoke:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0034","text":"My guests, you higher human beings, I wish to speak German and plainly with you. It was not for you that I waited here in these mountains.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0035","text":"(“‘German and plainly’? God have mercy! said the king on the left, aside; one can see that he does not know the dear Germans, this sage from the Orient!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0036","text":"But he means ‘German and blunt’—well then! Nowadays that is still not the worst taste!”)","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0037","text":"“You may indeed altogether be higher human beings, Zarathustra continued: but for me—you are not high and strong enough.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0038","text":"For me, that is: for the inexorable that is silent within me, but will not always remain silent. And if you belong to me, you do not belong as my right arm.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0039","text":"For whoever himself stands on sickly and tender legs, like you, wants above all, whether he knows it or conceals it: to be spared.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0040","text":"But I do not spare my arms and my legs, I do not spare my warriors: how could you be fit for my war?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0041","text":"With you I should spoil every victory for myself. And many a one among you would already fall over if he merely heard the loud sound of my drums.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0042","text":"Nor are you beautiful and well-born enough for me. I need pure, smooth mirrors for my teachings; upon your surface even my own image is still distorted.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0043","text":"Your shoulders are pressed by many a burden, many a memory; many an evil dwarf squats in your corners. There is hidden rabble in you too.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0044","text":"And even if you are lofty and of a loftier kind: much in you is crooked and misshapen. There is no smith in the world who could hammer you straight and right for me.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0045","text":"You are only bridges: may higher ones cross over upon you! You signify steps: so do not be angry with him who climbs over you into his height!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0046","text":"From your seed there may one day grow for me a genuine son and perfect heir: but that is far off. You yourselves are not those to whom my inheritance and name belong.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0047","text":"It is not for you that I wait here in these mountains; nor may I descend for the last time with you. You came to me only as a portent that higher ones are already on their way to me,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0048","text":"—not the human beings of great longing, great disgust, great weariness, and that which you called the remnant of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0049","text":"—No! No! Three times no! It is for Others that I wait here in these mountains, and without them I will not lift my foot to depart hence,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0050","text":"—for Higher, Stronger, More Victorious, More Cheerful ones, such as are built at right angles in body and soul: laughing lions must come!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0051","text":"Oh, my guest-friends, you strange ones,—have you heard nothing yet of my children? And that they are on their way to me?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0052","text":"Do speak to me of my gardens, of my blessed isles, of my new beautiful kind,—why do you not speak to me of them?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0053","text":"This guest-gift I ask of your love, that you speak to me of my children. For this I am rich, for this I became poor: what have I not given away,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0054","text":"—what would I not give away, that I might have One thing: these children, this living plantation, these trees of life of my will and of my highest hope!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0055","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra and suddenly paused in his speech: for longing overcame him, and he closed eyes and mouth because of the stirring of his heart. And all his guests too were silent and stood still and dismayed: except that the old soothsayer made signs with hands and gestures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DBS0:P0055"]}],"source_digest":"62f91378a2ee4cd092e8edb6c468dfb7c55d32b4927ab1d851c7a5bd4d93f20f","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Die Begrüssung"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Die Begrüssung","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA","title":"The Last Supper","order":139,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_DASA","text":"An dieser Stelle nämlich unterbrach der Wahrsager die Begrüssung Zarathustra’s und seiner Gäste: er drängte sich vor, wie Einer, der keine Zeit zu verlieren hat, fasste die Hand Zarathustra’s und rief: „Aber Zarathustra!\n\nEins ist nothwendiger als das Andre, so redest du selber: wohlan, Eins ist mir jetzt nothwendiger als alles Andere.\n\nEin Wort zur rechten Zeit: hast du mich nicht zum Mahle eingeladen? Und hier sind viele, die lange Wege machten. Du willst uns doch nicht mit Reden abspeisen?\n\nAuch gedachtet ihr Alle mir schon zu viel des Erfrierens, Ertrinkens, Erstickens und andrer Leibes-Nothstände: Keiner aber gedachte meines Nothstandes, nämlich des Verhungerns—„\n\n(Also sprach der Wahrsager; wie die Thiere Zarathustra’s aber diese Worte hörten, liefen sie vor Schrecken davon. Denn sie sahen, dass was sie auch am Tage heimgebracht hatten, nicht genug sein werde, den Einen Wahrsager zu stopfen.)\n\n„Eingerechnet das Verdursten, fuhr der Wahrsager fort. Und ob ich schon Wasser hier plätschern höre, gleich Reden der Weisheit, nämlich reichlich und unermüdlich: ich—will Wein !\n\nNicht jeder ist gleich Zarathustra ein geborner Wassertrinker. Wasser taugt auch nicht für Müde und Verwelkte: uns gebührt Wein,— der erst giebt plötzliches Genesen und stegreife Gesundheit!“\n\nBei dieser Gelegenheit, da der Wahrsager nach Wein begehrte, geschah es, dass auch der König zur Linken, der Schweigsame, einmal zu Worte kam. „Für Wein, sprach er, trugen wir Sorge, ich sammt meinem Bruder, dem Könige zur Rechten: wir haben Weins genug,—einen ganzen Esel voll. So fehlt Nichts als Brod.“\n\n„Brod? entgegnete Zarathustra und lachte dazu. Nur gerade Brod haben Einsiedler nicht. Aber der Mensch lebt nicht vom Brod allein, sondern auch vom Fleische guter Lämmer, deren ich zwei habe:\n\n— Die soll man geschwinde schlachten und würzig, mit Salbei, zubereiten: so liebe ich’s. Und auch an Wurzeln und Früchten fehlt es nicht, gut genug selbst für Lecker- und Schmeckerlinge; noch an Nüssen und andern Räthseln zum Knacken.\n\nAlso wollen wir in Kürze eine gute Mahlzeit machen. Wer aber mit essen will, muss auch mit Hand anlegen, auch die Könige. Bei Zarathustra nämlich darf auch ein König Koch sein.“\n\nMit diesem Vorschlage war Allen nach dem Herzen geredet: nur dass der freiwillige Bettler sich gegen Fleisch und Wein und Würzen sträubte.\n\n„Nun hört mir doch diesen Schlemmer Zarathustra! sagte er scherzhaft: geht man dazu in Höhlen und Hoch-Gebirge, dass man solche Mahlzeiten macht?\n\nNun freilich verstehe ich, was er einst uns lehrte: „Gelobt sei die kleine Armuth!“ Und warum er die Bettler abschaffen will.“\n\n„Sei guter Dinge, antwortete ihm Zarathustra, wie ich es bin. Bleibe bei deiner Sitte, du Trefflicher, malme deine Körner, trink dein Wasser, lobe deine Küche: wenn sie dich nur fröhlich macht!\n\nIch bin ein Gesetz nur für die Meinen, ich bin kein Gesetz für Alle. Wer aber zu mir gehört, der muss von starken Knochen sein, auch von leichten Füssen,—\n\n—lustig zu Kriegen und Festen, kein Düsterling, kein Traum-Hans, bereit zum Schwersten wie zu seinem Feste, gesund und heil.\n\nDas Beste gehört den Meinen und mir; und giebt man’s uns nicht, so nehmen wir’s:—die beste Nahrung, den reinsten Himmel, die stärksten Gedanken, die schönsten Fraun!“—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra; der König zur Rechten aber entgegnete: „Seltsam! Vernahm man je solche kluge Dinge aus dem Munde eines Weisen?\n\nUnd wahrlich, das ist das Seltsamste an einem Weisen, wenn er zu alledem auch noch klug und kein Esel ist.“\n\nAlso sprach der König zur Rechten und wunderte sich; der Esel aber sagte zu seiner Rede mit bösem Willen I-A. Diess aber war der Anfang von jener langen Mahlzeit, welche „das Abendmahl“ in den Historien-Büchern genannt wird. Bei derselben aber wurde von nichts Anderem geredet als vom höheren Menschen .","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0001","text":"An dieser Stelle nämlich unterbrach der Wahrsager die Begrüssung Zarathustra’s und seiner Gäste: er drängte sich vor, wie Einer, der keine Zeit zu verlieren hat, fasste die Hand Zarathustra’s und rief: „Aber Zarathustra!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0002","text":"Eins ist nothwendiger als das Andre, so redest du selber: wohlan, Eins ist mir jetzt nothwendiger als alles Andere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0003","text":"Ein Wort zur rechten Zeit: hast du mich nicht zum Mahle eingeladen? Und hier sind viele, die lange Wege machten. Du willst uns doch nicht mit Reden abspeisen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0004","text":"Auch gedachtet ihr Alle mir schon zu viel des Erfrierens, Ertrinkens, Erstickens und andrer Leibes-Nothstände: Keiner aber gedachte meines Nothstandes, nämlich des Verhungerns—„","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0005","text":"(Also sprach der Wahrsager; wie die Thiere Zarathustra’s aber diese Worte hörten, liefen sie vor Schrecken davon. Denn sie sahen, dass was sie auch am Tage heimgebracht hatten, nicht genug sein werde, den Einen Wahrsager zu stopfen.)","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0006","text":"„Eingerechnet das Verdursten, fuhr der Wahrsager fort. Und ob ich schon Wasser hier plätschern höre, gleich Reden der Weisheit, nämlich reichlich und unermüdlich: ich—will Wein !","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0007","text":"Nicht jeder ist gleich Zarathustra ein geborner Wassertrinker. Wasser taugt auch nicht für Müde und Verwelkte: uns gebührt Wein,— der erst giebt plötzliches Genesen und stegreife Gesundheit!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0008","text":"Bei dieser Gelegenheit, da der Wahrsager nach Wein begehrte, geschah es, dass auch der König zur Linken, der Schweigsame, einmal zu Worte kam. „Für Wein, sprach er, trugen wir Sorge, ich sammt meinem Bruder, dem Könige zur Rechten: wir haben Weins genug,—einen ganzen Esel voll. So fehlt Nichts als Brod.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0009","text":"„Brod? entgegnete Zarathustra und lachte dazu. Nur gerade Brod haben Einsiedler nicht. Aber der Mensch lebt nicht vom Brod allein, sondern auch vom Fleische guter Lämmer, deren ich zwei habe:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0010","text":"— Die soll man geschwinde schlachten und würzig, mit Salbei, zubereiten: so liebe ich’s. Und auch an Wurzeln und Früchten fehlt es nicht, gut genug selbst für Lecker- und Schmeckerlinge; noch an Nüssen und andern Räthseln zum Knacken.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0011","text":"Also wollen wir in Kürze eine gute Mahlzeit machen. Wer aber mit essen will, muss auch mit Hand anlegen, auch die Könige. Bei Zarathustra nämlich darf auch ein König Koch sein.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0012","text":"Mit diesem Vorschlage war Allen nach dem Herzen geredet: nur dass der freiwillige Bettler sich gegen Fleisch und Wein und Würzen sträubte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0013","text":"„Nun hört mir doch diesen Schlemmer Zarathustra! sagte er scherzhaft: geht man dazu in Höhlen und Hoch-Gebirge, dass man solche Mahlzeiten macht?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0014","text":"Nun freilich verstehe ich, was er einst uns lehrte: „Gelobt sei die kleine Armuth!“ Und warum er die Bettler abschaffen will.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0015","text":"„Sei guter Dinge, antwortete ihm Zarathustra, wie ich es bin. Bleibe bei deiner Sitte, du Trefflicher, malme deine Körner, trink dein Wasser, lobe deine Küche: wenn sie dich nur fröhlich macht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0016","text":"Ich bin ein Gesetz nur für die Meinen, ich bin kein Gesetz für Alle. Wer aber zu mir gehört, der muss von starken Knochen sein, auch von leichten Füssen,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0017","text":"—lustig zu Kriegen und Festen, kein Düsterling, kein Traum-Hans, bereit zum Schwersten wie zu seinem Feste, gesund und heil.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0018","text":"Das Beste gehört den Meinen und mir; und giebt man’s uns nicht, so nehmen wir’s:—die beste Nahrung, den reinsten Himmel, die stärksten Gedanken, die schönsten Fraun!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0019","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra; der König zur Rechten aber entgegnete: „Seltsam! Vernahm man je solche kluge Dinge aus dem Munde eines Weisen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0020","text":"Und wahrlich, das ist das Seltsamste an einem Weisen, wenn er zu alledem auch noch klug und kein Esel ist.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0021","text":"Also sprach der König zur Rechten und wunderte sich; der Esel aber sagte zu seiner Rede mit bösem Willen I-A. Diess aber war der Anfang von jener langen Mahlzeit, welche „das Abendmahl“ in den Historien-Büchern genannt wird. Bei derselben aber wurde von nichts Anderem geredet als vom höheren Menschen .","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"ffa5aea136c45e4ecfc1e1d7191f6c29ba86c6dd85df28e8f671e0100a0b17a7","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183821056277Z_5ca7d8a2","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"The Last Supper","source_sha256":"ffa5aea136c45e4ecfc1e1d7191f6c29ba86c6dd85df28e8f671e0100a0b17a7","sha256":"562c91e8376535274758feff5dbc1378cacd1755446858542bbb25bc16110df9","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:38:21.056306+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0001","text":"For at this point the soothsayer interrupted Zarathustra’s greeting of himself and his guests: he pushed forward, like one who has no time to lose, seized Zarathustra’s hand, and cried: “But Zarathustra!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASA:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0002","text":"“One thing is more necessary than another, thus you yourself speak: well then, one thing is now more necessary to me than anything else.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASA:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0003","text":"“A word at the right time: did you not invite me to a meal? And here are many who have travelled long distances. Surely you do not mean to fob us off with speeches?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASA:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0004","text":"“You have all already thought too much for me of freezing, drowning, suffocating, and other bodily distresses: but no one thought of my distress, namely that of starving—”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASA:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0005","text":"(Thus spoke the soothsayer; but when Zarathustra’s animals heard these words, they ran away in fright. For they saw that whatever they had brought home during the day would not be enough to stuff the one soothsayer.)","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASA:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0006","text":"“Including thirst,” the soothsayer continued. “And although I hear water splashing here, like speeches of wisdom, namely plentifully and untiringly: I—want wine!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASA:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0007","text":"“Not everyone is, like Zarathustra, a born water-drinker. Water is also no good for the weary and withered: wine befits us,—for it alone suddenly gives recovery and health ready to hand!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASA:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0008","text":"On this occasion, when the soothsayer demanded wine, it happened that the king on the left, the silent one, also spoke up once. “For wine,” he said, “we have made provision, I together with my brother, the king on the right: we have wine enough,—a whole ass-load. Thus nothing is lacking but bread.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASA:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0009","text":"“Bread?” Zarathustra replied, laughing as he did so. “It is precisely bread that hermits do not have. But man does not live by bread alone, but also by the flesh of good lambs, of which I have two:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASA:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0010","text":"— They should be slaughtered quickly and prepared savourily, with sage: that is how I like it. Nor is there any lack of roots and fruits, good enough even for dainty eaters and gourmets; nor of nuts and other riddles to crack.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASA:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0011","text":"Thus we shall soon make a good meal. But whoever wishes to eat with us must also lend a hand, kings too. For with Zarathustra a king may also be a cook.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASA:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0012","text":"With this proposal, all were spoken to according to their hearts: except that the voluntary beggar recoiled from meat and wine and spices.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASA:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0013","text":"“Now just listen to this gourmand Zarathustra!” he said jokingly. “Does one go into caves and high mountains in order to make such meals?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASA:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0014","text":"“Now indeed I understand what he once taught us: ‘Praised be little poverty!’ And why he wishes to abolish the beggars.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASA:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0015","text":"“Be of good cheer,” Zarathustra answered him, “as I am. Keep to your custom, you excellent man, grind your grains, drink your water, praise your kitchen: provided only that it makes you cheerful!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASA:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0016","text":"“I am a law only for my own, I am no law for all. But whoever belongs to me must be of strong bones, and also of light feet,—”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASA:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0017","text":"— merry for wars and festivals, no gloomy one, no dream-Hans, ready for the most difficult thing as for his festival, healthy and whole.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASA:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0018","text":"“The best belongs to my own and to me; and if it is not given to us, then we take it:—the best nourishment, the purest sky, the strongest thoughts, the most beautiful women!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASA:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0019","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra; but the king on the right replied: “Strange! Has one ever heard such clever things from the mouth of a sage?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASA:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0020","text":"“And truly, this is the strangest thing about a sage, when, in addition to all that, he is also clever and not an ass.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASA:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASA:P0021","text":"Thus spoke the king on the right and marvelled; but the ass responded to his speech with ill will: I-A. But this was the beginning of that long meal which is called “the Last Supper” in the history books. At it, however, nothing else was spoken of than the higher man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASA:P0021"]}],"source_digest":"f57e7d5f07bb297d1ca1d466fc94d0a6fa666f5999cd752a42cbd46239a93d60","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Das Abendmahl"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Das Abendmahl","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_14","title":"1.","order":140,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_1_14","text":"## 1.\n\nAls ich zum ersten Male zu den Menschen kam, da that ich die Einsiedler-Thorheit, die grosse Thorheit: ich stellte mich auf den Markt.\n\nUnd als ich zu Allen redete, redete ich zu Keinem. Des Abends aber waren Seiltänzer meine Genossen, und Leichname; und ich selber fast ein Leichnam.\n\nMit dem neuen Morgen aber kam mir eine neue Wahrheit: da lernte ich sprechen „Was geht mich Markt und Pöbel und Pöbel-Lärm und lange Pöbel-Ohren an!“\n\nIhr höheren Menschen, Diess lernt von mir: auf dem Markt glaubt Niemand an höhere Menschen. Und wollt ihr dort reden, wohlan! Der Pöbel aber blinzelt „wir sind Alle gleich.“\n\n„Ihr höheren Menschen,—so blinzelt der Pöbel—es giebt keine höheren Menschen, wir sind Alle gleich, Mensch ist Mensch, vor Gott—sind wir Alle gleich!“\n\nVor Gott!—Nun aber starb dieser Gott. Vor dem Pöbel aber wollen wir nicht gleich sein. Ihr höheren Menschen, geht weg vom Markt!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_14:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_14:P0002","text":"Als ich zum ersten Male zu den Menschen kam, da that ich die Einsiedler-Thorheit, die grosse Thorheit: ich stellte mich auf den Markt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_14:P0003","text":"Und als ich zu Allen redete, redete ich zu Keinem. Des Abends aber waren Seiltänzer meine Genossen, und Leichname; und ich selber fast ein Leichnam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_14:P0004","text":"Mit dem neuen Morgen aber kam mir eine neue Wahrheit: da lernte ich sprechen „Was geht mich Markt und Pöbel und Pöbel-Lärm und lange Pöbel-Ohren an!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_14:P0005","text":"Ihr höheren Menschen, Diess lernt von mir: auf dem Markt glaubt Niemand an höhere Menschen. Und wollt ihr dort reden, wohlan! Der Pöbel aber blinzelt „wir sind Alle gleich.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_14:P0006","text":"„Ihr höheren Menschen,—so blinzelt der Pöbel—es giebt keine höheren Menschen, wir sind Alle gleich, Mensch ist Mensch, vor Gott—sind wir Alle gleich!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_14:P0007","text":"Vor Gott!—Nun aber starb dieser Gott. Vor dem Pöbel aber wollen wir nicht gleich sein. Ihr höheren Menschen, geht weg vom Markt!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"794639974ec92e905990c50e1c533006af56a256209d7941237e73cede1c6c1e","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183837599078Z_b3f77777","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1.","source_sha256":"794639974ec92e905990c50e1c533006af56a256209d7941237e73cede1c6c1e","sha256":"171cb9cd113dae5e5d722dd36a797faf1b49bfafb0a23f7ca20962926bc8b124","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:38:37.599102+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_14:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_14:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_14:P0002","text":"When I first came among men, I committed the hermit’s folly, the great folly: I placed myself in the marketplace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_14:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_14:P0003","text":"And when I spoke to All, I spoke to None. But in the evening tightrope-walkers were my companions, and corpses; and I myself almost a corpse.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_14:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_14:P0004","text":"But with the new morning a new truth came to me: then I learned to say, “What are marketplace and rabble and rabble-noise and long rabble-ears to me!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_14:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_14:P0005","text":"You higher men, learn this from me: in the marketplace no one believes in higher men. And if you wish to speak there, very well! But the rabble winks: “we are all equal.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_14:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_14:P0006","text":"“You higher men,—thus the rabble winks—there are no higher men, we are all equal, man is man, before God—we are all equal!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_14:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_14:P0007","text":"Before God!—But now this God has died. Before the rabble, however, we do not wish to be equal. You higher men, go away from the marketplace!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_14:P0007"]}],"source_digest":"2a50ca427726699265666b9e058d7a5823bbff422bd29aacac447136b740c6df","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Higher Man","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"On the Higher Man · 1","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","On the Higher Man","1."],"original_title":"1."}],"source_title_context":"Vom höheren Menschen","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom höheren Menschen · 1","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Vom höheren Menschen","1."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"1.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_14","title":"2.","order":141,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_2_14","text":"## 2.\n\nVor Gott!—Nun aber starb dieser Gott! Ihr höheren Menschen, dieser Gott war eure grösste Gefahr.\n\nSeit er im Grabe liegt, seid ihr erst wieder auferstanden. Nun erst kommt der grosse Mittag, nun erst wird der höhere Mensch—Herr!\n\nVerstandet ihr diess Wort, oh meine Brüder? Ihr seid erschreckt: wird euren Herzen schwindlig? Klafft euch hier der Abgrund? Kläfft euch hier der Höllenhund?\n\nWohlan! Wohlauf! Ihr höheren Menschen! Nun erst kreisst der Berg der Menschen-Zukunft. Gott starb: nun wollen wir ,—dass der Übermensch lebe.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_14:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_14:P0002","text":"Vor Gott!—Nun aber starb dieser Gott! Ihr höheren Menschen, dieser Gott war eure grösste Gefahr.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_14:P0003","text":"Seit er im Grabe liegt, seid ihr erst wieder auferstanden. Nun erst kommt der grosse Mittag, nun erst wird der höhere Mensch—Herr!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_14:P0004","text":"Verstandet ihr diess Wort, oh meine Brüder? Ihr seid erschreckt: wird euren Herzen schwindlig? Klafft euch hier der Abgrund? Kläfft euch hier der Höllenhund?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_14:P0005","text":"Wohlan! Wohlauf! Ihr höheren Menschen! Nun erst kreisst der Berg der Menschen-Zukunft. Gott starb: nun wollen wir ,—dass der Übermensch lebe.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"0555d05b4200fc1f7172305e0f7b5c5fd7bb0a674c4a05d6ea7d98eb4554fa79","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183840483244Z_1d261971","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2.","source_sha256":"0555d05b4200fc1f7172305e0f7b5c5fd7bb0a674c4a05d6ea7d98eb4554fa79","sha256":"17b06069689fc4951ed169eb4c871d9e40f893cf0ad3bccabd5af888410ed54a","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:38:40.483267+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_14:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_14:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_14:P0002","text":"Before God!—But now this God died! You higher men, this God was your greatest danger.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_14:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_14:P0003","text":"Only since he lies in the grave have you risen again. Only now does the great noon come, only now does the higher man become—master!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_14:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_14:P0004","text":"Did you understand this word, oh my brothers? You are alarmed: do your hearts grow dizzy? Does the abyss gape before you here? Does the hell-hound bark at you here?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_14:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_14:P0005","text":"Well then! Up! You higher men! Only now does the mountain of the human future go into labour. God died: now we will,—that the Overman shall live.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_14:P0005"]}],"source_digest":"3a7ed9e821f9cd74f1ce6ba180b01ea653960053fdba3c76829a43be9b433a99","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Higher Man","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"On the Higher Man · 2","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","On the Higher Man","2."],"original_title":"2."}],"source_title_context":"Vom höheren Menschen","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom höheren Menschen · 2","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Vom höheren Menschen","2."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"2.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7","title":"3.","order":142,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_3C_7","text":"## 3.\n\nDie Sorglichsten fragen heute: „wie bleibt der Mensch erhalten?“ Zarathustra aber fragt als der Einzige und Erste: „wie wird der Mensch überwunden ?“\n\nDer Übermensch liegt mir am Herzen, der ist mein Erstes und Einziges,—und nicht der Mensch: nicht der Nächste, nicht der Ärmste, nicht der Leidendste, nicht der Beste—\n\nOh meine Brüder, was ich lieben kann am Menschen, das ist, dass er ein Übergang ist und ein Untergang. Und auch an euch ist vieles, das mich lieben und hoffen macht.\n\nDass ihr verachtetet, ihr höheren Menschen, das macht mich hoffen. Die grossen Verachtenden nämlich sind die grossen Verehrenden.\n\nDass ihr verzweifeltet, daran ist Viel zu ehren. Denn ihr lerntet nicht, wie ihr euch ergäbet, ihr lerntet die kleinen Klugheiten nicht.\n\nHeute nämlich wurden die kleinen Leute Herr: die predigen Alle Ergebung und Bescheidung und Klugheit und Fleiss und Rücksicht und das lange Und-so-weiter der kleinen Tugenden.\n\nWas von Weibsart ist, was von Knechtsart stammt und sonderlich der Pöbel-Mischmasch: Das will nun Herr werden alles Menschen-Schicksals—oh Ekel! Ekel! Ekel!\n\nDas frägt und frägt und wird nicht müde: „Wie erhält sich der Mensch, am besten, am längsten, am angenehmsten?“ Damit—sind sie die Herrn von Heute.\n\nDiese Herrn von Heute überwindet mir, oh meine Brüder,—diese kleinen Leute: die sind des Übermenschen grösste Gefahr!\n\nÜberwindet mir, ihr höheren Menschen, die kleinen Tugenden, die kleinen Klugheiten, die Sandkorn-Rücksichten, den Ameisen-Kribbelkram, das erbärmliche Behagen, das „Glück der Meisten“ —!\n\nUnd lieber verzweifelt, als dass ihr euch ergebt. Und, wahrlich, ich liebe euch dafür, dass ihr heute nicht zu leben wisst, ihr höheren Menschen! So nämlich lebt ihr —am Besten!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0001","text":"## 3.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0002","text":"Die Sorglichsten fragen heute: „wie bleibt der Mensch erhalten?“ Zarathustra aber fragt als der Einzige und Erste: „wie wird der Mensch überwunden ?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0003","text":"Der Übermensch liegt mir am Herzen, der ist mein Erstes und Einziges,—und nicht der Mensch: nicht der Nächste, nicht der Ärmste, nicht der Leidendste, nicht der Beste—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0004","text":"Oh meine Brüder, was ich lieben kann am Menschen, das ist, dass er ein Übergang ist und ein Untergang. Und auch an euch ist vieles, das mich lieben und hoffen macht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0005","text":"Dass ihr verachtetet, ihr höheren Menschen, das macht mich hoffen. Die grossen Verachtenden nämlich sind die grossen Verehrenden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0006","text":"Dass ihr verzweifeltet, daran ist Viel zu ehren. Denn ihr lerntet nicht, wie ihr euch ergäbet, ihr lerntet die kleinen Klugheiten nicht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0007","text":"Heute nämlich wurden die kleinen Leute Herr: die predigen Alle Ergebung und Bescheidung und Klugheit und Fleiss und Rücksicht und das lange Und-so-weiter der kleinen Tugenden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0008","text":"Was von Weibsart ist, was von Knechtsart stammt und sonderlich der Pöbel-Mischmasch: Das will nun Herr werden alles Menschen-Schicksals—oh Ekel! Ekel! Ekel!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0009","text":"Das frägt und frägt und wird nicht müde: „Wie erhält sich der Mensch, am besten, am längsten, am angenehmsten?“ Damit—sind sie die Herrn von Heute.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0010","text":"Diese Herrn von Heute überwindet mir, oh meine Brüder,—diese kleinen Leute: die sind des Übermenschen grösste Gefahr!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0011","text":"Überwindet mir, ihr höheren Menschen, die kleinen Tugenden, die kleinen Klugheiten, die Sandkorn-Rücksichten, den Ameisen-Kribbelkram, das erbärmliche Behagen, das „Glück der Meisten“ —!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0012","text":"Und lieber verzweifelt, als dass ihr euch ergebt. Und, wahrlich, ich liebe euch dafür, dass ihr heute nicht zu leben wisst, ihr höheren Menschen! So nämlich lebt ihr —am Besten!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"46a6a6d1a04303c8f5b59531655e9b2e30d24227cf46519ee012c3c7a1b7411d","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183846743283Z_b1ef4325","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"3.","source_sha256":"46a6a6d1a04303c8f5b59531655e9b2e30d24227cf46519ee012c3c7a1b7411d","sha256":"f072af2c2b179ab4f1e78d67132a7e5786372261b7c449d1a1c0f2140b277e44","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:38:46.743312+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0001","text":"## 3.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0002","text":"The most solicitous today ask: “how is man preserved?” But Zarathustra, as the only one and the first, asks: “how is man overcome?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0003","text":"The overman lies close to my heart; he is my first and only concern,—and not man: not the neighbour, not the poorest, not the most suffering, not the best—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0004","text":"Oh my brothers, what I can love in man is that he is a transition and a downfall. And in you too there is much that makes me love and hope.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0005","text":"That you despised, you higher men, makes me hope. For the great despisers are the great venerators.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0006","text":"That you despaired, therein there is much to honour. For you did not learn how to submit yourselves; you did not learn the little prudences.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0007","text":"For today the little people have become masters: they all preach submission and modesty and prudence and diligence and consideration and the long et-cetera of the little virtues.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0008","text":"Whatever is of woman-kind, whatever stems from slave-kind, and especially the rabble-mishmash: all that now wants to become master of all human destiny—oh disgust! disgust! disgust!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0009","text":"It asks and asks and does not grow weary: “How does man preserve himself, best, longest, most pleasantly?” Thereby—they are the masters of today.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0010","text":"Overcome for me these masters of today, oh my brothers,—these little people: they are the overman’s greatest danger!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0011","text":"Overcome for me, you higher men, the little virtues, the little prudences, the grain-of-sand considerations, the ant-like trifling, the miserable comfort, the “happiness of the greatest number” —!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0012","text":"And rather despair than submit yourselves. And, truly, I love you for it, that today you do not know how to live, you higher men! For thus you live —best!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_7:P0012"]}],"source_digest":"facabe945ca95796a01191ec6693a685e2836435d26e59186b8a0361e3813e11","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Higher Man","source_section_label":"3.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"On the Higher Man · 3","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","On the Higher Man","3."],"original_title":"3."}],"source_title_context":"Vom höheren Menschen","source_section_label":"3.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom höheren Menschen · 3","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Vom höheren Menschen","3."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"3.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_4","title":"4.","order":143,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_4C_4","text":"## 4.\n\nHabt ihr Muth, oh meine Brüder? Seid ihr herzhaft? Nicht Muth vor Zeugen, sondern Einsiedler- und Adler-Muth, dem auch kein Gott mehr zusieht?\n\nKalte Seelen, Maulthiere, Blinde, Trunkene heissen mir nicht herzhaft. Herz hat, wer Furcht kennt, aber Furcht zwingt , er den Abgrund sieht, aber mit Stolz .\n\nWer den Abgrund sieht, aber mit Adlers-Augen, wer mit Adlers-Krallen den Abgrund fasst : Der hat Muth.—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_4:P0001","text":"## 4.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_4:P0002","text":"Habt ihr Muth, oh meine Brüder? Seid ihr herzhaft? Nicht Muth vor Zeugen, sondern Einsiedler- und Adler-Muth, dem auch kein Gott mehr zusieht?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_4:P0003","text":"Kalte Seelen, Maulthiere, Blinde, Trunkene heissen mir nicht herzhaft. Herz hat, wer Furcht kennt, aber Furcht zwingt , er den Abgrund sieht, aber mit Stolz .","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_4:P0004","text":"Wer den Abgrund sieht, aber mit Adlers-Augen, wer mit Adlers-Krallen den Abgrund fasst : Der hat Muth.—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"51fff284788b2b409fa055e10c0f47d8375572a9f559e3c16e9855daa136e4ea","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183849990987Z_61074a07","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"4.","source_sha256":"51fff284788b2b409fa055e10c0f47d8375572a9f559e3c16e9855daa136e4ea","sha256":"23051b4d84a777edf87d20e9d9a0b90b7da0e970338ac9af79ae9e9ae77ce1f2","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:38:49.991010+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_4:P0001","text":"## 4.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_4:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_4:P0002","text":"Have you courage, oh my brothers? Are you stout-hearted? Not courage before witnesses, but hermit- and eagle-courage, upon which no god any longer looks?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_4:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_4:P0003","text":"Cold souls, mules, blind men, drunkards do not count as stout-hearted with me. He has heart who knows fear, but compels fear, who sees the abyss, but with pride .","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_4:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_4:P0004","text":"He who sees the abyss, but with eagle-eyes, who grasps the abyss with eagle-talons: He has courage.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_4:P0004"]}],"source_digest":"e1ec146fd178a78251421e57fb861965b69b1fb5472a2ae7498125e1cf11a2ee","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Higher Man","source_section_label":"4.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"On the Higher Man · 4","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","On the Higher Man","4."],"original_title":"4."}],"source_title_context":"Vom höheren Menschen","source_section_label":"4.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom höheren Menschen · 4","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Vom höheren Menschen","4."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"4.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_4","title":"5.","order":144,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_5C_4","text":"## 5.\n\n„Der Mensch ist böse“ —so sprachen mir zum Troste alle Weisesten. Ach, wenn es heute nur noch wahr ist! Denn das Böse ist des Menschen beste Kraft.\n\n„Der Mensch muss besser und böser werden“ —so lehre ich . Das Böseste ist nöthig zu des Übermenschen Bestem.\n\nDas mochte gut sein für jenen Prediger der kleinen Leute, dass er litt und trug an des Menschen Sünde. Ich aber erfreue mich der grossen Sünde als meines grossen Trostes .—\n\nSolches ist aber nicht für lange Ohren gesagt. Jedwedes Wort gehört auch nicht in jedes Maul. Das sind feine ferne Dinge: nach denen sollen nicht Schafs-Klauen greifen!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_4:P0001","text":"## 5.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_4:P0002","text":"„Der Mensch ist böse“ —so sprachen mir zum Troste alle Weisesten. Ach, wenn es heute nur noch wahr ist! Denn das Böse ist des Menschen beste Kraft.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_4:P0003","text":"„Der Mensch muss besser und böser werden“ —so lehre ich . Das Böseste ist nöthig zu des Übermenschen Bestem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_4:P0004","text":"Das mochte gut sein für jenen Prediger der kleinen Leute, dass er litt und trug an des Menschen Sünde. Ich aber erfreue mich der grossen Sünde als meines grossen Trostes .—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_4:P0005","text":"Solches ist aber nicht für lange Ohren gesagt. Jedwedes Wort gehört auch nicht in jedes Maul. Das sind feine ferne Dinge: nach denen sollen nicht Schafs-Klauen greifen!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"9e3a016e9501e6b5da1c5d4180eeb06d61bd52c3105be442b6fdf1f2fdca32c9","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183852922288Z_b6385e48","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"5.","source_sha256":"9e3a016e9501e6b5da1c5d4180eeb06d61bd52c3105be442b6fdf1f2fdca32c9","sha256":"df44e4428e989330a93dd2a5b81ffb8b74c8a8476fa92af28dcb32b5e89f6eed","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:38:52.922330+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_4:P0001","text":"## 5.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_4:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_4:P0002","text":"“Man is evil”—thus all the wisest spoke to me for consolation. Ah, if only it is still true today! For evil is man's best strength.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_4:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_4:P0003","text":"“Man must become better and more evil”—thus I teach. The most evil is necessary for the best of the overman.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_4:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_4:P0004","text":"It may have been good for that preacher of the little people that he suffered and bore man's sin. But I rejoice in great sin as in my great consolation.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_4:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_4:P0005","text":"But such things are not said for long ears. Nor does every word belong in every mouth. These are fine, distant things: sheep's claws should not reach after them!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_4:P0005"]}],"source_digest":"499e3d7659a114e8157c82ba72286fa5abadcc11297172692606d08dee4a5446","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Higher Man","source_section_label":"5.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"On the Higher Man · 5","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","On the Higher Man","5."],"original_title":"5."}],"source_title_context":"Vom höheren Menschen","source_section_label":"5.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom höheren Menschen · 5","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Vom höheren Menschen","5."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"5.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_4","title":"6.","order":145,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_6C_4","text":"## 6.\n\nIhr höheren Menschen, meint ihr, ich sei da, gut zu machen, was ihr schlecht machtet?\n\nOder ich wollte fürderhin euch Leidende bequemer betten? Oder euch Unstäten, Verirrten, Verkletterten neue leichtere Fusssteige zeigen?\n\nNein! Nein! Drei Mal Nein! Immer Mehr, immer Bessere eurer Art sollen zu Grunde gehn,—denn ihr sollt es immer schlimmer und härter haben. So allein—\n\n—so allein wächst der Mensch in die Höhe, wo der Blitz ihn trifft und zerbricht: hoch genug für den Blitz!\n\nAuf Weniges, auf Langes, auf Fernes geht mein Sinn und meine Sehnsucht: was gienge mich euer kleines, vieles, kurzes Elend an!\n\nIhr leidet mir noch nicht genug! Denn ihr leidet an euch, ihr littet noch nicht am Menschen . Ihr würdet lügen, wenn ihr’s anders sagtet! Ihr leidet Alle nicht, woran ich litt.—","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_4:P0001","text":"## 6.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_4:P0002","text":"Ihr höheren Menschen, meint ihr, ich sei da, gut zu machen, was ihr schlecht machtet?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_4:P0003","text":"Oder ich wollte fürderhin euch Leidende bequemer betten? Oder euch Unstäten, Verirrten, Verkletterten neue leichtere Fusssteige zeigen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_4:P0004","text":"Nein! Nein! Drei Mal Nein! Immer Mehr, immer Bessere eurer Art sollen zu Grunde gehn,—denn ihr sollt es immer schlimmer und härter haben. So allein—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_4:P0005","text":"—so allein wächst der Mensch in die Höhe, wo der Blitz ihn trifft und zerbricht: hoch genug für den Blitz!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_4:P0006","text":"Auf Weniges, auf Langes, auf Fernes geht mein Sinn und meine Sehnsucht: was gienge mich euer kleines, vieles, kurzes Elend an!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_4:P0007","text":"Ihr leidet mir noch nicht genug! Denn ihr leidet an euch, ihr littet noch nicht am Menschen . Ihr würdet lügen, wenn ihr’s anders sagtet! Ihr leidet Alle nicht, woran ich litt.—","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"6966c953bf65000311fead7798df3deb458fbb0329fe9477ecc30076c2439a4e","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183857240774Z_dcc47061","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"6.","source_sha256":"6966c953bf65000311fead7798df3deb458fbb0329fe9477ecc30076c2439a4e","sha256":"084c1f2093487097d387f4dc2feefdd4f18cb0af14a647697930371a3ef56481","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:38:57.240798+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_4:P0001","text":"## 6.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_4:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_4:P0002","text":"You higher human beings, do you think I am here to make good what you made bad?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_4:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_4:P0003","text":"Or that henceforth I wished to make a more comfortable bed for you sufferers? Or to show you restless, straying, over-clambering ones new, easier footpaths?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_4:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_4:P0004","text":"No! No! Three times No! Ever more, ever better ones of your kind shall perish,—for things shall become ever worse and harder for you. Thus alone—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_4:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_4:P0005","text":"—thus alone does the human being grow upward, where lightning strikes and shatters him: high enough for the lightning!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_4:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_4:P0006","text":"My mind and my longing are directed toward little, toward what is long, toward what is distant: what concern to me is your small, manifold, brief misery!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_4:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_4:P0007","text":"You do not yet suffer enough for me! For you suffer from yourselves; you have not yet suffered from the human being. You would be lying if you said otherwise! None of you suffers what I suffered.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_4:P0007"]}],"source_digest":"bb46d2a31818ab17229a0de9174ecbc3e989d6e099e228b5cb7e70e9e424ce31","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Higher Man","source_section_label":"6.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"On the Higher Man · 6","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","On the Higher Man","6."],"original_title":"6."}],"source_title_context":"Vom höheren Menschen","source_section_label":"6.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom höheren Menschen · 6","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Vom höheren Menschen","6."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"6.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_4","title":"7.","order":146,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_7C_4","text":"## 7.\n\nEs ist mir nicht genug, dass der Blitz nicht mehr schadet. Nicht ableiten will ich ihn: er soll lernen für mich —arbeiten.—\n\nMeine Weisheit sammlet sich lange schon gleich einer Wolke, sie wird stiller und dunkler. So thut jede Weisheit, welche einst Blitze gebären soll.—\n\nDiesen Menschen von Heute will ich nicht Licht sein, nicht Licht heissen. Die —will ich blenden: Blitz meiner Weisheit! Stich ihnen die Augen aus!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_4:P0001","text":"## 7.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_4:P0002","text":"Es ist mir nicht genug, dass der Blitz nicht mehr schadet. Nicht ableiten will ich ihn: er soll lernen für mich —arbeiten.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_4:P0003","text":"Meine Weisheit sammlet sich lange schon gleich einer Wolke, sie wird stiller und dunkler. So thut jede Weisheit, welche einst Blitze gebären soll.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_4:P0004","text":"Diesen Menschen von Heute will ich nicht Licht sein, nicht Licht heissen. Die —will ich blenden: Blitz meiner Weisheit! Stich ihnen die Augen aus!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"90ea1a015b58b069f8d7b07ea62e0328bffbefa2038878f9d28e941f8be08746","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183859849177Z_c0e466c7","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"7.","source_sha256":"90ea1a015b58b069f8d7b07ea62e0328bffbefa2038878f9d28e941f8be08746","sha256":"67ef052e645cc865ce1288dd054f9f9f5cb28d377ce1692adba6849e61c0372b","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:38:59.849206+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_4:P0001","text":"## 7.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_4:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_4:P0002","text":"It is not enough for me that lightning no longer does harm. I do not wish to divert it: it shall learn to —work for me.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_4:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_4:P0003","text":"My wisdom has long been gathering like a cloud; it grows quieter and darker. Thus does every wisdom that is one day to give birth to lightning.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_4:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_4:P0004","text":"To these people of Today I do not wish to be light, not to be called light. —I wish to blind them: lightning of my wisdom! Put out their eyes!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_4:P0004"]}],"source_digest":"c5bb4c4c04be0483e5d928191fe7c5f7037c6df50f6bbb0f504c0c1541ca7ad8","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Higher Man","source_section_label":"7.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"On the Higher Man · 7","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","On the Higher Man","7."],"original_title":"7."}],"source_title_context":"Vom höheren Menschen","source_section_label":"7.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom höheren Menschen · 7","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Vom höheren Menschen","7."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"7.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_5","title":"8.","order":147,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_8C_5","text":"## 8.\n\nWollt Nichts über euer Vermögen: es giebt eine schlimme Falschheit bei Solchen, die über ihr Vermögen wollen.\n\nSonderlich, wenn sie grosse Dinge wollen! Denn sie wecken Misstrauen gegen grosse Dinge, diese feinen Falschmünzer und Schauspieler:—\n\n—bis sie endlich falsch vor sich selber sind, schieläugig, übertünchter Wurmfrass, bemäntelt durch starke Worte, durch Aushänge-Tugenden, durch glänzende falsche Werke.\n\nHabt da eine gute Vorsicht, ihr höheren Menschen! Nichts nämlich gilt mir heute kostbarer und seltner als Redlichkeit.\n\nIst diess Heute nicht des Pöbels? Pöbel aber weiss nicht, was gross, was klein, was gerade und redlich ist: der ist unschuldig krumm, der lügt immer.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_5:P0001","text":"## 8.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_5:P0002","text":"Wollt Nichts über euer Vermögen: es giebt eine schlimme Falschheit bei Solchen, die über ihr Vermögen wollen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_5:P0003","text":"Sonderlich, wenn sie grosse Dinge wollen! Denn sie wecken Misstrauen gegen grosse Dinge, diese feinen Falschmünzer und Schauspieler:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_5:P0004","text":"—bis sie endlich falsch vor sich selber sind, schieläugig, übertünchter Wurmfrass, bemäntelt durch starke Worte, durch Aushänge-Tugenden, durch glänzende falsche Werke.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_5:P0005","text":"Habt da eine gute Vorsicht, ihr höheren Menschen! Nichts nämlich gilt mir heute kostbarer und seltner als Redlichkeit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_5:P0006","text":"Ist diess Heute nicht des Pöbels? Pöbel aber weiss nicht, was gross, was klein, was gerade und redlich ist: der ist unschuldig krumm, der lügt immer.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"494a5cfffecfe9238513eed5bdf272d9e8435117f30064601ce89965f1906ef6","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183903237901Z_68b2cbbf","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"8.","source_sha256":"494a5cfffecfe9238513eed5bdf272d9e8435117f30064601ce89965f1906ef6","sha256":"1bd38ea82b9d304ef105c9d06c2843dc34578489ad87317776b1e6f30893af72","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:39:03.237924+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_5:P0001","text":"## 8.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_5:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_5:P0002","text":"Will nothing beyond your capacity: there is a bad falseness in those who will beyond their capacity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_5:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_5:P0003","text":"Especially when they will great things! For these subtle counterfeiters and actors awaken mistrust of great things:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_5:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_5:P0004","text":"—until at last they are false before themselves, squint-eyed, whitewashed worm-eaten decay, cloaked by strong words, by placard-virtues, by glittering false works.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_5:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_5:P0005","text":"Take good precaution there, you higher men! For nothing is more precious and rarer to me today than probity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_5:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_5:P0006","text":"Is not this Today that of the rabble? But the rabble does not know what is great, what is small, what is straight and upright: it is innocently crooked, it always lies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_5:P0006"]}],"source_digest":"83a82080e117a91d6b325d43356ad3403f0307188ef410084a66e74ccf4bae8f","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Higher Man","source_section_label":"8.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"On the Higher Man · 8","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","On the Higher Man","8."],"original_title":"8."}],"source_title_context":"Vom höheren Menschen","source_section_label":"8.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom höheren Menschen · 8","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Vom höheren Menschen","8."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"8.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_6","title":"8.","order":148,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_8C_6","text":"## 8.\n\nHabt heute ein gutes Misstrauen, ihr höheren Menschen, ihr Beherzten! Ihr Offenherzigen! Und haltet eure Gründe geheim! Diess Heute nämlich ist des Pöbels.\n\nWas der Pöbel ohne Gründe einst glauben lernte, wer könnte ihm durch Gründe Das —umwerfen?\n\nUnd auf dem Markte überzeugt man mit Gebärden. Aber Gründe machen den Pöbel misstrauisch.\n\nUnd wenn da einmal Wahrheit zum Siege kam, so fragt euch Mit gutem Misstrauen: „welch starker Irrthum hat für sie gekämpft?“\n\nHütet euch auch vor den Gelehrten! Die hassen euch: denn sie sind unfruchtbar! Sie haben kalte vertrocknete Augen, vor ihnen liegt jeder Vogel entfedert.\n\nSolche brüsten sich damit, dass sie nicht lügen: aber Ohnmacht zur Lüge ist lange noch nicht Liebe zur Wahrheit. Hütet euch!\n\nFreiheit von Fieber ist lange noch nicht Erkenntniss! Ausgekälteten Geistern glaube ich nicht. Wer nicht lügen kann, weiss nicht, was Wahrheit ist.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0001","text":"## 8.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0002","text":"Habt heute ein gutes Misstrauen, ihr höheren Menschen, ihr Beherzten! Ihr Offenherzigen! Und haltet eure Gründe geheim! Diess Heute nämlich ist des Pöbels.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0003","text":"Was der Pöbel ohne Gründe einst glauben lernte, wer könnte ihm durch Gründe Das —umwerfen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0004","text":"Und auf dem Markte überzeugt man mit Gebärden. Aber Gründe machen den Pöbel misstrauisch.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0005","text":"Und wenn da einmal Wahrheit zum Siege kam, so fragt euch Mit gutem Misstrauen: „welch starker Irrthum hat für sie gekämpft?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0006","text":"Hütet euch auch vor den Gelehrten! Die hassen euch: denn sie sind unfruchtbar! Sie haben kalte vertrocknete Augen, vor ihnen liegt jeder Vogel entfedert.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0007","text":"Solche brüsten sich damit, dass sie nicht lügen: aber Ohnmacht zur Lüge ist lange noch nicht Liebe zur Wahrheit. Hütet euch!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0008","text":"Freiheit von Fieber ist lange noch nicht Erkenntniss! Ausgekälteten Geistern glaube ich nicht. Wer nicht lügen kann, weiss nicht, was Wahrheit ist.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"074d0d80ec3ac07b39556d92ab5f06fa691cc761a82617b839073c655909c1c5","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183907808946Z_8a256ed9","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"8.","source_sha256":"074d0d80ec3ac07b39556d92ab5f06fa691cc761a82617b839073c655909c1c5","sha256":"727a1e5cbd1bb5a07c108dcbccc122ab2c3f47a74b2fdb91a2a3b73975f57c7b","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:39:07.808970+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0001","text":"## 8.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0002","text":"Have a good mistrust today, you higher men, you courageous ones! You open-hearted ones! And keep your reasons secret! For this Today is that of the rabble.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0003","text":"What the rabble once learned to believe without reasons, who could overthrow for it through reasons — that?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0004","text":"And in the marketplace one convinces with gestures. But reasons make the rabble mistrustful.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0005","text":"And if truth ever came there to victory, then ask yourselves with good mistrust: “what strong error has fought for it?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0006","text":"Beware also of the scholars! They hate you: for they are barren! They have cold, dried-up eyes; before them every bird lies plucked of its feathers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0007","text":"Such men boast that they do not lie: but impotence for lying is still far from being love of truth. Beware!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0008","text":"Freedom from fever is still far from being knowledge! I do not believe in spirits chilled out. He who cannot lie does not know what truth is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_6:P0008"]}],"source_digest":"6adcba5ba823ea6918395f393ca1135a802f93ffe4379e1f4ed929e6215327f1","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Higher Man","source_section_label":"8.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"On the Higher Man · 8","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","On the Higher Man","8."],"original_title":"8."}],"source_title_context":"Vom höheren Menschen","source_section_label":"8.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom höheren Menschen · 8","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Vom höheren Menschen","8."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"8.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_3","title":"10.","order":149,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_10_3","text":"## 10.\n\nWollt ihr hoch hinaus, so braucht die eignen Beine! Lasst euch nicht empor tragen , setzt euch nicht auf fremde Rücken und Köpfe!\n\nDu aber stiegst zu Pferde? Du reitest nun hurtig hinauf zu deinem Ziele? Wohlan, mein Freund! Aber dein lahmer Fuss sitzt auch mit zu Pferde!\n\nWenn du an deinem Ziele bist, wenn du von deinem Pferde springst: auf deiner Höhe gerade, du höherer Mensch—wirst du stolpern!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_3:P0001","text":"## 10.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_3:P0002","text":"Wollt ihr hoch hinaus, so braucht die eignen Beine! Lasst euch nicht empor tragen , setzt euch nicht auf fremde Rücken und Köpfe!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_3:P0003","text":"Du aber stiegst zu Pferde? Du reitest nun hurtig hinauf zu deinem Ziele? Wohlan, mein Freund! Aber dein lahmer Fuss sitzt auch mit zu Pferde!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_3:P0004","text":"Wenn du an deinem Ziele bist, wenn du von deinem Pferde springst: auf deiner Höhe gerade, du höherer Mensch—wirst du stolpern!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"17819609da7ac55142886c8d6bda9b8800dae999b799cb21617dc1846dd2367f","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183910499287Z_e65b588a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"10.","source_sha256":"17819609da7ac55142886c8d6bda9b8800dae999b799cb21617dc1846dd2367f","sha256":"b6f4dac3825305b9e1372bf93c2487354b5f81953940e3c0dac898b34c66d98a","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:39:10.499304+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_3:P0001","text":"## 10.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10_3:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_3:P0002","text":"If you want to climb high, then you need your own legs! Do not let yourselves be carried upward; do not seat yourselves on others’ backs and heads!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10_3:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_3:P0003","text":"But did you mount a horse? Are you now riding briskly upward toward your goal? Well then, my friend! But your lame foot too is seated on horseback!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10_3:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_3:P0004","text":"When you are at your goal, when you leap from your horse: precisely at your height, you higher human being—you will stumble!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10_3:P0004"]}],"source_digest":"996c4e93bb880c3a3bf2818e732e0dbf4d672bdcdca266ca47c911b8633aeea1","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Higher Man","source_section_label":"10.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"On the Higher Man · 10","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","On the Higher Man","10."],"original_title":"10."}],"source_title_context":"Vom höheren Menschen","source_section_label":"10.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom höheren Menschen · 10","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Vom höheren Menschen","10."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"10.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_2","title":"11.","order":150,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_11_2","text":"## 11.\n\nIhr Schaffenden, ihr höheren Menschen! Man ist nur für das eigne Kind schwanger.\n\nLasst euch Nichts vorreden, einreden! Wer ist denn euer Nächster? Und handelt ihr auch „für den Nächsten“ ,—ihr schafft doch nicht für ihn!\n\nVerlernt mir doch diess „Für“, ihr Schaffenden: eure Tugend gerade will es, dass ihr kein Ding mit „für“ und „um“ und „weil“ thut. Gegen diese falschen kleinen Worte sollt ihr euer Ohr zukleben.\n\nDas „für den Nächsten“ ist die Tugend nur der kleinen Leute: da heisst es „gleich und gleich“ und „Hand wäscht Hand“:—sie haben nicht Recht noch Kraft zu eurem Eigennutz!\n\nIn eurem Eigennutz, ihr Schaffenden, ist der Schwangeren Vorsicht und Vorsehung! Was Niemand noch mit Augen sah, die Frucht: die schirmt und schont und nährt eure ganze Liebe.\n\nWo eure ganze Liebe ist, bei eurem Kinde, da ist auch eure ganze Tugend! Euer Werk, euer Wille ist euer „Nächster“: lasst euch keine falschen Werthe einreden!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_2:P0001","text":"## 11.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_2:P0002","text":"Ihr Schaffenden, ihr höheren Menschen! Man ist nur für das eigne Kind schwanger.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_2:P0003","text":"Lasst euch Nichts vorreden, einreden! Wer ist denn euer Nächster? Und handelt ihr auch „für den Nächsten“ ,—ihr schafft doch nicht für ihn!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_2:P0004","text":"Verlernt mir doch diess „Für“, ihr Schaffenden: eure Tugend gerade will es, dass ihr kein Ding mit „für“ und „um“ und „weil“ thut. Gegen diese falschen kleinen Worte sollt ihr euer Ohr zukleben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_2:P0005","text":"Das „für den Nächsten“ ist die Tugend nur der kleinen Leute: da heisst es „gleich und gleich“ und „Hand wäscht Hand“:—sie haben nicht Recht noch Kraft zu eurem Eigennutz!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_2:P0006","text":"In eurem Eigennutz, ihr Schaffenden, ist der Schwangeren Vorsicht und Vorsehung! Was Niemand noch mit Augen sah, die Frucht: die schirmt und schont und nährt eure ganze Liebe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_2:P0007","text":"Wo eure ganze Liebe ist, bei eurem Kinde, da ist auch eure ganze Tugend! Euer Werk, euer Wille ist euer „Nächster“: lasst euch keine falschen Werthe einreden!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"78fae486bbbf9c029f00df58e9bc80672eac6a23e833e10d6bd24817f4d38e09","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183915637759Z_bf9b57d0","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"11.","source_sha256":"78fae486bbbf9c029f00df58e9bc80672eac6a23e833e10d6bd24817f4d38e09","sha256":"788c6880d27f427ccf97d8064889b64eb616916e073a149757099903ac90c5af","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:39:15.637769+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_2:P0001","text":"## 11.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_2:P0002","text":"You creators, you higher human beings! One is pregnant only with one's own child.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_2:P0003","text":"Do not let anything be talked into you, instilled into you! For who is your neighbour? And even if you act “for your neighbour”—you do not create for him!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_2:P0004","text":"But unlearn this “for,” you creators: your virtue itself demands that you do nothing with “for” and “in order to” and “because.” You should stop up your ears against these false little words.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_2:P0005","text":"“For one’s neighbour” is the virtue only of little people: there they say “like and like” and “one hand washes the other”:—they have neither right nor strength to your self-interest!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_2:P0006","text":"In your self-interest, you creators, there is the caution and foresight of the pregnant woman! What no one has yet seen with eyes, the fruit: it shields and spares and nourishes your entire love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11_2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_2:P0007","text":"Where your entire love is, with your child, there too is your entire virtue! Your work, your will is your “neighbour”: do not let false values be instilled into you!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11_2:P0007"]}],"source_digest":"cccd8095cc0551b3218b5aade1a212010d7d10299a5f12e9486e498b08aac27d","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Higher Man","source_section_label":"11.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"On the Higher Man · 11","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","On the Higher Man","11."],"original_title":"11."}],"source_title_context":"Vom höheren Menschen","source_section_label":"11.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom höheren Menschen · 11","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Vom höheren Menschen","11."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"11.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12_2","title":"12.","order":151,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_12_2","text":"## 12.\n\nIhr Schaffenden, ihr höheren Menschen! Wer gebären muss, der ist krank; wer aber geboren hat, ist unrein.\n\nFragt die Weiber: man gebiert nicht, weil es Vergnügen macht. Der Schmerz macht Hühner und Dichter gackern.\n\nIhr Schaffenden, an euch ist viel Unreines. Das macht, ihr musstet Mütter sein.\n\nEin neues Kind: oh wie viel neuer Schmutz kam auch zur Welt! Geht bei Seite! Und wer geboren hat, soll seine Seele rein waschen!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_12_2:P0001","text":"## 12.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12_2:P0002","text":"Ihr Schaffenden, ihr höheren Menschen! Wer gebären muss, der ist krank; wer aber geboren hat, ist unrein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12_2:P0003","text":"Fragt die Weiber: man gebiert nicht, weil es Vergnügen macht. Der Schmerz macht Hühner und Dichter gackern.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12_2:P0004","text":"Ihr Schaffenden, an euch ist viel Unreines. Das macht, ihr musstet Mütter sein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12_2:P0005","text":"Ein neues Kind: oh wie viel neuer Schmutz kam auch zur Welt! Geht bei Seite! Und wer geboren hat, soll seine Seele rein waschen!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"947f009382c7e488b97cf3e4dc15fbc9dc17c2fdbe7a979eeff98f365f648303","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183919875406Z_1dc4e969","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"12.","source_sha256":"947f009382c7e488b97cf3e4dc15fbc9dc17c2fdbe7a979eeff98f365f648303","sha256":"e52f9433d7068dc28b8047898254462c74d858aeade856041201389fa42c1919","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:39:19.875434+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_12_2:P0001","text":"## 12.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_12_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12_2:P0002","text":"You creators, you higher human beings! Whoever must give birth is ill; but whoever has given birth is impure.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_12_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12_2:P0003","text":"Ask the women: one does not give birth because it is pleasurable. Pain makes hens and poets cackle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_12_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12_2:P0004","text":"You creators, there is much that is impure about you. That is because you had to be mothers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_12_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12_2:P0005","text":"A new child: oh, how much new filth also came into the world! Step aside! And whoever has given birth shall wash their soul clean!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_12_2:P0005"]}],"source_digest":"08c5299920f8403f03910b7c04fcf57b548840b48e1c51d2a0ff018928b241a9","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Higher Man","source_section_label":"12.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"On the Higher Man · 12","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","On the Higher Man","12."],"original_title":"12."}],"source_title_context":"Vom höheren Menschen","source_section_label":"12.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom höheren Menschen · 12","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Vom höheren Menschen","12."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"12.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13_2","title":"13.","order":152,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_13_2","text":"## 13.\n\nSeid nicht tugendhaft über eure Kräfte! Und wollt Nichts von euch wider die Wahrscheinlichkeit!\n\nGeht in den Fusstapfen, wo schon eurer Väter Tugend gierig! Wie wolltet ihr hoch steigen, wenn nicht eurer Väter Wille mit euch steigt?\n\nWer aber Erstling sein will, sehe zu, dass er nicht auch Letztling werde! Und wo die Laster eurer Väter sind, darin sollt ihr nicht Heilige bedeuten wollen!\n\nWessen Väter es mit Weibern hielten und mit starken Weinen und Wildschweinen: was wäre es, wenn Der von sich Keuschheit wollte?\n\nEine Narrheit wäre es! Viel, wahrlich, dünkt es mich für einen Solchen, wenn er Eines oder zweier oder dreier Weiber Mann ist.\n\nUnd stiftete er Klöster und schriebe über die Thür: „der Weg zum Heiligen,“—ich spräche doch: wozu! es ist eine neue Narrheit!\n\nEr stiftete sich selber ein Zucht- und Fluchthaus: wohl bekomm’s! Aber ich glaube nicht daran.\n\nIn der Einsamkeit wächst, was Einer in sie bringt, auch das innere Vieh. Solchergestalt widerräth sich Vielen die Einsamkeit.\n\nGab es Schmutzigeres bisher auf Erden als Wüsten-Heilige? Um die herum war nicht nur der Teufel los,—sondern auch das Schwein.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_13_2:P0001","text":"## 13.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13_2:P0002","text":"Seid nicht tugendhaft über eure Kräfte! Und wollt Nichts von euch wider die Wahrscheinlichkeit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13_2:P0003","text":"Geht in den Fusstapfen, wo schon eurer Väter Tugend gierig! Wie wolltet ihr hoch steigen, wenn nicht eurer Väter Wille mit euch steigt?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13_2:P0004","text":"Wer aber Erstling sein will, sehe zu, dass er nicht auch Letztling werde! Und wo die Laster eurer Väter sind, darin sollt ihr nicht Heilige bedeuten wollen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13_2:P0005","text":"Wessen Väter es mit Weibern hielten und mit starken Weinen und Wildschweinen: was wäre es, wenn Der von sich Keuschheit wollte?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13_2:P0006","text":"Eine Narrheit wäre es! Viel, wahrlich, dünkt es mich für einen Solchen, wenn er Eines oder zweier oder dreier Weiber Mann ist.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13_2:P0007","text":"Und stiftete er Klöster und schriebe über die Thür: „der Weg zum Heiligen,“—ich spräche doch: wozu! es ist eine neue Narrheit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13_2:P0008","text":"Er stiftete sich selber ein Zucht- und Fluchthaus: wohl bekomm’s! Aber ich glaube nicht daran.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13_2:P0009","text":"In der Einsamkeit wächst, was Einer in sie bringt, auch das innere Vieh. Solchergestalt widerräth sich Vielen die Einsamkeit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13_2:P0010","text":"Gab es Schmutzigeres bisher auf Erden als Wüsten-Heilige? Um die herum war nicht nur der Teufel los,—sondern auch das Schwein.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"212f938737be0887630ee45929181bb87c3843c523ec1a1d2256260ab0424a04","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183924908775Z_e2eb0b65","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"13.","source_sha256":"212f938737be0887630ee45929181bb87c3843c523ec1a1d2256260ab0424a04","sha256":"8dde4d461e0abbedc515e425ec41a9d98bd87ab40225ce8cc744c8990132d8de","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:39:24.908796+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_13_2:P0001","text":"## 13.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_13_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13_2:P0002","text":"Do not be virtuous beyond your powers! And do not will anything of yourselves contrary to probability!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_13_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13_2:P0003","text":"Go in the footsteps where your fathers’ virtue already walked greedily! How would you rise high if your fathers’ will did not rise with you?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_13_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13_2:P0004","text":"But whoever wishes to be firstling, let him see to it that he does not also become lastling! And where your fathers’ vices are, there you should not wish to signify saints!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_13_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13_2:P0005","text":"He whose fathers kept company with women and with strong wines and wild boars: what would it be if he wanted chastity of himself?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_13_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13_2:P0006","text":"It would be a folly! Much indeed, it seems to me, for such a one, if he is the husband of one or two or three women.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_13_2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13_2:P0007","text":"And if he founded monasteries and wrote over the door: “the way to the saint,”—I would still say: what for! it is a new folly!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_13_2:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13_2:P0008","text":"He founded for himself a house of discipline and refuge: may it agree with him! But I do not believe in it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_13_2:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13_2:P0009","text":"In solitude there grows what one brings into it, including the inner beast. Thus solitude is ill-advised for many.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_13_2:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_13_2:P0010","text":"Has there hitherto been anything filthier on earth than desert saints? Around them not only was the devil let loose,—but also the pig.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_13_2:P0010"]}],"source_digest":"6e73dbe728f651362be6e721e7bfdafdaed821cd3685c302f0f2e094b9a86cac","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Higher Man","source_section_label":"13.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"On the Higher Man · 13","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","On the Higher Man","13."],"original_title":"13."}],"source_title_context":"Vom höheren Menschen","source_section_label":"13.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom höheren Menschen · 13","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Vom höheren Menschen","13."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"13.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14_2","title":"14.","order":153,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_14_2","text":"## 14.\n\nScheu, beschämt, ungeschickt, einem Tiger gleich, dem der Sprung missrieth: also, ihr höheren Menschen, sah ich oft euch bei Seite schleichen. Ein Wurf missrieth euch.\n\nAber, ihr Würfelspieler, was liegt daran! Ihr lerntet nicht spielen und spotten, wie man spielen und spotten muss! Sitzen wir nicht immer an einem grossen Spott- und Spieltische?\n\nUnd wenn euch Grosses missrieth, seid ihr selber darum—missrathen? Und missriethet ihr selber, missrieth darum—der Mensch? Missrieth aber der Mensch: wohlan! wohlauf!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_14_2:P0001","text":"## 14.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14_2:P0002","text":"Scheu, beschämt, ungeschickt, einem Tiger gleich, dem der Sprung missrieth: also, ihr höheren Menschen, sah ich oft euch bei Seite schleichen. Ein Wurf missrieth euch.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14_2:P0003","text":"Aber, ihr Würfelspieler, was liegt daran! Ihr lerntet nicht spielen und spotten, wie man spielen und spotten muss! Sitzen wir nicht immer an einem grossen Spott- und Spieltische?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14_2:P0004","text":"Und wenn euch Grosses missrieth, seid ihr selber darum—missrathen? Und missriethet ihr selber, missrieth darum—der Mensch? Missrieth aber der Mensch: wohlan! wohlauf!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"cc916cc40ede937bfdd61a5bfa31cf0cb7d3b303e775947242d839a9321cf158","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183928000466Z_357098ef","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"14.","source_sha256":"cc916cc40ede937bfdd61a5bfa31cf0cb7d3b303e775947242d839a9321cf158","sha256":"e56c20ae3745e0a7487413cbdb16f016de4d6be7026abf1ddcc77251cb43c9dc","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:39:28.000514+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_14_2:P0001","text":"## 14.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_14_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14_2:P0002","text":"Shy, ashamed, awkward, like a tiger whose leap has miscarried: thus, ye higher men, have I often seen you slink aside. A throw miscarried for you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_14_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14_2:P0003","text":"But, ye dice-players, what does it matter! You have not learned to play and mock, as one must play and mock! Do we not always sit at a great table of mockery and play?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_14_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_14_2:P0004","text":"And if something great miscarried for you, have you yourselves thereby—miscarried? And if you yourselves miscarried, did therefore—man miscarry? But if man miscarried: well then! up!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_14_2:P0004"]}],"source_digest":"c01db408b4a48328cbd5a536dfa81c28162acdd396e3c145868bcf6a2f629164","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Higher Man","source_section_label":"14.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"On the Higher Man · 14","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","On the Higher Man","14."],"original_title":"14."}],"source_title_context":"Vom höheren Menschen","source_section_label":"14.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom höheren Menschen · 14","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Vom höheren Menschen","14."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"14.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15_2","title":"15.","order":154,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_15_2","text":"## 15.\n\nJe höher von Art, je seltener geräth ein Ding. Ihr höheren Menschen hier, seid ihr nicht alle—missgerathen?\n\nSeid guten Muths, was liegt daran! Wie Vieles ist noch möglich! Lernt über euch selber lachen, wie man lachen muss!\n\nWas Wunders auch, dass ihr missriethet und halb geriethet, ihr Halb-Zerbrochenen! Drängt und stösst sich nicht in euch—des Menschen Zukunft ?\n\nDes Menschen Fernstes, Tiefstes, Sternen-Höchstes, seine ungeheure Kraft: schäumt Das nicht alles gegen einander in eurem Topfe?\n\nWas Wunders, dass mancher Topf zerbricht! Lernt über euch lachen, wie man lachen muss! Ihr höheren Menschen, oh wie Vieles ist noch möglich!\n\nUnd wahrlich, wie Viel gerieth schon! Wie reich ist diese Erde an kleinen guten vollkommenen Dingen, an Wohlgerathenem!\n\nStellt kleine gute vollkommne Dinge um euch, ihr höheren Menschen! Deren goldene Reife heilt das Herz. Vollkommnes lehrt hoffen.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_15_2:P0001","text":"## 15.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15_2:P0002","text":"Je höher von Art, je seltener geräth ein Ding. Ihr höheren Menschen hier, seid ihr nicht alle—missgerathen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15_2:P0003","text":"Seid guten Muths, was liegt daran! Wie Vieles ist noch möglich! Lernt über euch selber lachen, wie man lachen muss!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15_2:P0004","text":"Was Wunders auch, dass ihr missriethet und halb geriethet, ihr Halb-Zerbrochenen! Drängt und stösst sich nicht in euch—des Menschen Zukunft ?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15_2:P0005","text":"Des Menschen Fernstes, Tiefstes, Sternen-Höchstes, seine ungeheure Kraft: schäumt Das nicht alles gegen einander in eurem Topfe?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15_2:P0006","text":"Was Wunders, dass mancher Topf zerbricht! Lernt über euch lachen, wie man lachen muss! Ihr höheren Menschen, oh wie Vieles ist noch möglich!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15_2:P0007","text":"Und wahrlich, wie Viel gerieth schon! Wie reich ist diese Erde an kleinen guten vollkommenen Dingen, an Wohlgerathenem!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15_2:P0008","text":"Stellt kleine gute vollkommne Dinge um euch, ihr höheren Menschen! Deren goldene Reife heilt das Herz. Vollkommnes lehrt hoffen.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"bcbc53c0ebdf9485b64b0f576b1c3418b9261ec520297eab71e7359cac34a5fc","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183931973807Z_6841d4e6","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"15.","source_sha256":"bcbc53c0ebdf9485b64b0f576b1c3418b9261ec520297eab71e7359cac34a5fc","sha256":"46700af5f89af60def7a3bbc3a3f9b8684b3bcb8cec5123984823c791ba6d10a","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:39:31.973835+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_15_2:P0001","text":"## 15.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_15_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15_2:P0002","text":"The higher in kind a thing is, the more rarely does it succeed. You higher human beings here, are you not all—failures?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_15_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15_2:P0003","text":"Take heart, what does it matter! How much is still possible! Learn to laugh at yourselves as one must laugh!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_15_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15_2:P0004","text":"What wonder, too, that you miscarried and half-succeeded, you Half-Broken ones! Does not the future of man—press and thrust within you?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_15_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15_2:P0005","text":"Man’s most distant, deepest, star-highest, his immense force: does not all that foam against one another in your pot?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_15_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15_2:P0006","text":"What wonder that many a pot breaks! Learn to laugh at yourselves as one must laugh! You higher human beings, oh how much is still possible!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_15_2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15_2:P0007","text":"And truly, how much has already succeeded! How rich this earth is in small good perfect things, in things well-turned-out!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_15_2:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_15_2:P0008","text":"Set small good perfect things around yourselves, you higher human beings! Their golden ripeness heals the heart. What is perfect teaches hope.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_15_2:P0008"]}],"source_digest":"fe0a7456c8909c7d87a481163b26fc939e794a3c675f4be07ce2770ec7eabf7c","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Higher Man","source_section_label":"15.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"On the Higher Man · 15","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","On the Higher Man","15."],"original_title":"15."}],"source_title_context":"Vom höheren Menschen","source_section_label":"15.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom höheren Menschen · 15","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Vom höheren Menschen","15."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"15.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16_2","title":"16.","order":155,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_16_2","text":"## 16.\n\nWelches war hier auf Erden bisher die grösste Sünde? War es nicht das Wort Dessen, der sprach: „Wehe Denen, die hier lachen!“\n\nFand er zum Lachen auf der Erde selber keine Gründe? So suchte er nur schlecht. Ein Kind findet hier noch Gründe.\n\nDer—liebte nicht genug: sonst hätte er auch uns geliebt, die Lachenden! Aber er hasste und höhnte uns, Heulen und Zähneklappern verhiess er uns.\n\nMuss man denn gleich fluchen, wo man nicht liebt? Das—dünkt mich ein schlechter Geschmack. Aber so that er, dieser Unbedingte. Er kam vom Pöbel.\n\nUnd er selber liebte nur nicht genug: sonst hätte er weniger gezürnt, dass man ihn nicht liebe. Alle grosse Liebe will nicht Liebe:—die will mehr.\n\nGeht aus dem Wege allen solchen Unbedingten! Das ist eine arme kranke Art, eine Pöbel-Art: sie sehn schlimm diesem Leben zu, sie haben den bösen Blick für diese Erde.\n\nGeht aus dem Wege allen solchen Unbedingten! Sie haben Schwere Füsse und schwüle Herzen:—sie wissen nicht zu tanzen. Wie möchte Solchen wohl die Erde leicht sein!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_16_2:P0001","text":"## 16.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16_2:P0002","text":"Welches war hier auf Erden bisher die grösste Sünde? War es nicht das Wort Dessen, der sprach: „Wehe Denen, die hier lachen!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16_2:P0003","text":"Fand er zum Lachen auf der Erde selber keine Gründe? So suchte er nur schlecht. Ein Kind findet hier noch Gründe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16_2:P0004","text":"Der—liebte nicht genug: sonst hätte er auch uns geliebt, die Lachenden! Aber er hasste und höhnte uns, Heulen und Zähneklappern verhiess er uns.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16_2:P0005","text":"Muss man denn gleich fluchen, wo man nicht liebt? Das—dünkt mich ein schlechter Geschmack. Aber so that er, dieser Unbedingte. Er kam vom Pöbel.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16_2:P0006","text":"Und er selber liebte nur nicht genug: sonst hätte er weniger gezürnt, dass man ihn nicht liebe. Alle grosse Liebe will nicht Liebe:—die will mehr.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16_2:P0007","text":"Geht aus dem Wege allen solchen Unbedingten! Das ist eine arme kranke Art, eine Pöbel-Art: sie sehn schlimm diesem Leben zu, sie haben den bösen Blick für diese Erde.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16_2:P0008","text":"Geht aus dem Wege allen solchen Unbedingten! Sie haben Schwere Füsse und schwüle Herzen:—sie wissen nicht zu tanzen. Wie möchte Solchen wohl die Erde leicht sein!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"fddf2a44b84cf4aca137ca76b205fcb6c92d3d43b1eaa3b6782fdd2b5ef3056b","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183936233826Z_efca1aa1","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"16.","source_sha256":"fddf2a44b84cf4aca137ca76b205fcb6c92d3d43b1eaa3b6782fdd2b5ef3056b","sha256":"15d07a05588ea9bcbcee5af36d78d9e04b17cdf1c26942e4c598963c75760978","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:39:36.233838+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_16_2:P0001","text":"## 16.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_16_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16_2:P0002","text":"What has hitherto been the greatest sin here on earth? Was it not the word of Him who said: “Woe unto those who laugh here!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_16_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16_2:P0003","text":"Did he find no grounds for laughter on earth itself? Then he sought poorly. A child still finds grounds here.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_16_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16_2:P0004","text":"He—did not love enough: otherwise he would have loved us too, we who laugh! But he hated and mocked us; he promised us weeping and gnashing of teeth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_16_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16_2:P0005","text":"Must one immediately curse where one does not love? That—seems to me in poor taste. But that is what he did, this unconditional one. He came from the rabble.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_16_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16_2:P0006","text":"And he himself merely did not love enough: otherwise he would have been less angered that one did not love him. All great love does not want love:—it wants more.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_16_2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16_2:P0007","text":"Get out of the way of all such unconditional ones! That is a poor, sick kind, a rabble kind: they look grimly upon this life; they have the evil eye for this earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_16_2:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_16_2:P0008","text":"Get out of the way of all such unconditional ones! They have heavy feet and sultry hearts:—they do not know how to dance. How could the earth possibly be light for such as they!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_16_2:P0008"]}],"source_digest":"684fdded3fbd4cf90ee651b4594780a96d71ea5c6975876fa478c45f161b5d68","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Higher Man","source_section_label":"16.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"On the Higher Man · 16","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","On the Higher Man","16."],"original_title":"16."}],"source_title_context":"Vom höheren Menschen","source_section_label":"16.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom höheren Menschen · 16","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Vom höheren Menschen","16."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"16.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17_2","title":"17.","order":156,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_17_2","text":"## 17.\n\nKrumm kommen alle guten Dinge ihrem Ziele nahe. Gleich Katzen machen sie Buckel, sie schnurren innewendig vor ihrem nahen Glücke,—alle guten Dinge lachen.\n\nDer Schritt verräth, ob Einer schon auf seiner Bahn schreitet: so seht mich gehn! Wer aber seinem Ziel nahe kommt, der tanzt.\n\nUnd, wahrlich, zum Standbild ward ich nicht, noch stehe ich nicht da, starr, stumpf, steinern, eine Säule; ich liebe geschwindes Laufen.\n\nUnd wenn es auf Erden auch Moor und dicke Trübsal giebt: wer leichte Füsse hat, läuft über Schlamm noch hinweg und tanzt wie auf gefegtem Eise.\n\nErhebt eure Herzen, meine Brüder, hoch! höher! Und vergesst mir auch die Beine nicht! Erhebt auch eure Beine, ihr guten Tänzer, und besser noch: ihr steht auch auf dem Kopf!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_17_2:P0001","text":"## 17.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17_2:P0002","text":"Krumm kommen alle guten Dinge ihrem Ziele nahe. Gleich Katzen machen sie Buckel, sie schnurren innewendig vor ihrem nahen Glücke,—alle guten Dinge lachen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17_2:P0003","text":"Der Schritt verräth, ob Einer schon auf seiner Bahn schreitet: so seht mich gehn! Wer aber seinem Ziel nahe kommt, der tanzt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17_2:P0004","text":"Und, wahrlich, zum Standbild ward ich nicht, noch stehe ich nicht da, starr, stumpf, steinern, eine Säule; ich liebe geschwindes Laufen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17_2:P0005","text":"Und wenn es auf Erden auch Moor und dicke Trübsal giebt: wer leichte Füsse hat, läuft über Schlamm noch hinweg und tanzt wie auf gefegtem Eise.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17_2:P0006","text":"Erhebt eure Herzen, meine Brüder, hoch! höher! Und vergesst mir auch die Beine nicht! Erhebt auch eure Beine, ihr guten Tänzer, und besser noch: ihr steht auch auf dem Kopf!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"93c53c6bb1e8044d76aaa32790fe211c9ba35e98829ce5fbcd6f4a1b1a9e7748","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183939780101Z_a2144050","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"17.","source_sha256":"93c53c6bb1e8044d76aaa32790fe211c9ba35e98829ce5fbcd6f4a1b1a9e7748","sha256":"52e1fcbea1bee5fba8ce7306ff228aa9c8b53b13f7769a3df6eee91089c1df4d","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:39:39.780127+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_17_2:P0001","text":"## 17.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_17_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17_2:P0002","text":"All good things approach their goal crookedly. Like cats they arch their backs, they purr inwardly over their approaching happiness,—all good things laugh.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_17_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17_2:P0003","text":"The step betrays whether someone is already walking upon his path: thus see me go! But whoever comes near to his goal, dances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_17_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17_2:P0004","text":"And, verily, I did not become a statue, nor do I stand there, rigid, dull, stony, a column; I love swift running.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_17_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17_2:P0005","text":"And even if there is mire and thick tribulation on earth: whoever has light feet still runs over mud and dances as though on swept ice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_17_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_17_2:P0006","text":"Lift up your hearts, my brothers, high! higher! And do not forget your legs either! Lift up your legs too, you good dancers, and better still: stand on your heads as well!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_17_2:P0006"]}],"source_digest":"7277d5f11e5def5d53c2259790abc4bbe2a22f55b38581ca71016897111b6f41","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Higher Man","source_section_label":"17.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"On the Higher Man · 17","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","On the Higher Man","17."],"original_title":"17."}],"source_title_context":"Vom höheren Menschen","source_section_label":"17.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom höheren Menschen · 17","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Vom höheren Menschen","17."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"17.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18_2","title":"18.","order":157,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_18_2","text":"## 18.\n\nDiese Krone des Lachenden, diese Rosenkranz-Krone: ich selber setzte mir diese Krone auf, ich selber sprach heilig mein Gelächter. Keinen Anderen fand ich heute stark genug dazu.\n\nZarathustra der Tänzer, Zarathustra der Leichte, der mit den Flügeln winkt, ein Flugbereiter, allen Vögeln zuwinkend, bereit und fertig, ein Selig-Leichtfertiger:—\n\nZarathustra der Wahrsager, Zarathustra der Wahrlacher, kein Ungeduldiger, kein Unbedingter, Einer, der Sprünge und Seitensprünge liebt; ich selber setzte mir diese Krone auf!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_18_2:P0001","text":"## 18.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18_2:P0002","text":"Diese Krone des Lachenden, diese Rosenkranz-Krone: ich selber setzte mir diese Krone auf, ich selber sprach heilig mein Gelächter. Keinen Anderen fand ich heute stark genug dazu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18_2:P0003","text":"Zarathustra der Tänzer, Zarathustra der Leichte, der mit den Flügeln winkt, ein Flugbereiter, allen Vögeln zuwinkend, bereit und fertig, ein Selig-Leichtfertiger:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18_2:P0004","text":"Zarathustra der Wahrsager, Zarathustra der Wahrlacher, kein Ungeduldiger, kein Unbedingter, Einer, der Sprünge und Seitensprünge liebt; ich selber setzte mir diese Krone auf!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"c7c9d35505dda316aa8bc0ead6550c42945933f9e14c75a4adf3fd394b635f11","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183942905780Z_26764a78","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"18.","source_sha256":"c7c9d35505dda316aa8bc0ead6550c42945933f9e14c75a4adf3fd394b635f11","sha256":"9b4bac69fd001ebec273b5102edf4aad25f8728eb3286130aacd5197df41b331","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:39:42.905801+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_18_2:P0001","text":"## 18.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_18_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18_2:P0002","text":"This crown of the laughing one, this rosary-crown: I myself placed this crown upon myself, I myself declared my laughter holy. Today I found no one else strong enough for it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_18_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18_2:P0003","text":"Zarathustra the dancer, Zarathustra the light one, who beckons with his wings, ready for flight, beckoning to all birds, ready and prepared, a blissfully light-hearted one:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_18_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_18_2:P0004","text":"Zarathustra the soothsayer, Zarathustra the truth-laugher, no impatient one, no unconditional one, one who loves leaps and side-leaps; I myself placed this crown upon myself!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_18_2:P0004"]}],"source_digest":"e158715cc767146b3dee76967c5a775287f82dfe0e900083751046cfd90a5d81","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Higher Man","source_section_label":"18.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"On the Higher Man · 18","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","On the Higher Man","18."],"original_title":"18."}],"source_title_context":"Vom höheren Menschen","source_section_label":"18.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom höheren Menschen · 18","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Vom höheren Menschen","18."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"18.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19_2","title":"19.","order":158,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_19_2","text":"## 19.\n\nErhebt eure Herzen, meine Brüder, hoch! höher! Und vergesst mir auch die Beine nicht! Erhebt auch eure Beine, ihr guten Tänzer, und besser noch: ihr steht auch auf dem Kopf!\n\nEs giebt auch im Glück schweres Gethier, es giebt Plumpfüssler von Anbeginn. Wunderlich müht sie sich ab, einem Elephanten gleich, der sich müht auf dem Kopf zu stehn.\n\nBesser aber noch närrisch sein vor Glücke als närrisch vor Unglücke, besser plump tanzen als lahm gehn. So lernt mir doch meine Weisheit ab: auch das schlimmste Ding hat zwei gute Kehrseiten,—\n\n—auch das schlimmste Ding hat gute Tanzbeine: so lernt mir doch euch selbst, ihr höheren Menschen, auf eure rechten Beine stellen!\n\nSo verlernt mir doch Trübsal-Blasen und alle Pöbel-Traurigkeit! Oh wie traurig dünken mich heute des Pöbels Hanswürste noch! Diess Heute aber ist des Pöbels.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_19_2:P0001","text":"## 19.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19_2:P0002","text":"Erhebt eure Herzen, meine Brüder, hoch! höher! Und vergesst mir auch die Beine nicht! Erhebt auch eure Beine, ihr guten Tänzer, und besser noch: ihr steht auch auf dem Kopf!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19_2:P0003","text":"Es giebt auch im Glück schweres Gethier, es giebt Plumpfüssler von Anbeginn. Wunderlich müht sie sich ab, einem Elephanten gleich, der sich müht auf dem Kopf zu stehn.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19_2:P0004","text":"Besser aber noch närrisch sein vor Glücke als närrisch vor Unglücke, besser plump tanzen als lahm gehn. So lernt mir doch meine Weisheit ab: auch das schlimmste Ding hat zwei gute Kehrseiten,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19_2:P0005","text":"—auch das schlimmste Ding hat gute Tanzbeine: so lernt mir doch euch selbst, ihr höheren Menschen, auf eure rechten Beine stellen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19_2:P0006","text":"So verlernt mir doch Trübsal-Blasen und alle Pöbel-Traurigkeit! Oh wie traurig dünken mich heute des Pöbels Hanswürste noch! Diess Heute aber ist des Pöbels.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"55eb15f71e44050c0e40a1621044fd531868e293fc5b6eb7f5561c22bf015787","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183946509124Z_dac081e7","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"19.","source_sha256":"55eb15f71e44050c0e40a1621044fd531868e293fc5b6eb7f5561c22bf015787","sha256":"f0ee949c05d93f2010587770b72c77c4fb2afd073f7528ac8e8cb0de3dae7ccf","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:39:46.509149+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_19_2:P0001","text":"## 19.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_19_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19_2:P0002","text":"Raise your hearts high, my brothers! higher! And do not forget your legs either! Raise your legs too, you good dancers, and better still: stand on your heads as well!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_19_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19_2:P0003","text":"There are also heavy creatures in happiness; there are clumsy-footed ones from the beginning. Strangely they toil, like an elephant toiling to stand on its head.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_19_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19_2:P0004","text":"But it is still better to be foolish from happiness than foolish from misfortune, better to dance clumsily than to walk lame. So learn my wisdom from me: even the worst thing has two good reverse sides,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_19_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19_2:P0005","text":"—even the worst thing has good dancing legs: so learn from me to set yourselves, you higher human beings, upon your proper legs!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_19_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_19_2:P0006","text":"So unlearn from me the blowing of melancholy and all rabble-sadness! Oh, how sad the rabble's buffoons seem to me today! But this Today is the rabble's.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_19_2:P0006"]}],"source_digest":"f6b2bf3366b28edf1f729905afb486780e9603d7551da7877e47aefbbb84d36d","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Higher Man","source_section_label":"19.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"On the Higher Man · 19","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","On the Higher Man","19."],"original_title":"19."}],"source_title_context":"Vom höheren Menschen","source_section_label":"19.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom höheren Menschen · 19","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Vom höheren Menschen","19."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"19.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20_2","title":"20.","order":159,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_20_2","text":"## 20.\n\nDem Winde thut mir gleich, wenn er aus seinen Berghöhlen stürzt: nach seiner eignen Pfeife will er tanzen, die Meere zittern und hüpfen unter seinen Fusstapfen.\n\nDer den Eseln Flügel giebt, der Löwinnen melkt, gelobt sei dieser gute unbändige Geist, der allem Heute und allem Pöbel wie ein Sturmwind kommt,—\n\n—der Distel- und Tiftelköpfen feind ist und allen welken Blättern und Unkräutern: gelobt sei dieser wilde gute freie Sturmgeist, welcher auf Mooren und Trübsalen wie auf Wiesen tanzt!\n\nDer die Pöbel-Schwindhunde hasst und alles missrathene düstere Gezücht: gelobt sei dieser Geist aller freien Geister, der lachende Sturm, welcher allen Schwarzsichtigen, Schwärsüchtigen Staub in die Augen bläst!\n\nIhr höheren Menschen, euer Schlimmstes ist: ihr lerntet alle nicht tanzen, wie man tanzen muss—über euch hinweg tanzen! Was liegt daran, dass ihr missriethet!\n\nWie Vieles ist noch möglich! So lernt doch über euch hinweg lachen! Erhebt eure Herzen, ihr guten Tänzer, hoch! höher! Und vergesst mir auch das gute Lachen nicht!\n\nDiese Krone des Lachenden, diese Rosenkranz-Krone: euch, meinen Brüdern, werfe ich diese Krone zu! Das Lachen sprach ich heilig; ihr höheren Menschen, lernt mir—lachen!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_20_2:P0001","text":"## 20.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20_2:P0002","text":"Dem Winde thut mir gleich, wenn er aus seinen Berghöhlen stürzt: nach seiner eignen Pfeife will er tanzen, die Meere zittern und hüpfen unter seinen Fusstapfen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20_2:P0003","text":"Der den Eseln Flügel giebt, der Löwinnen melkt, gelobt sei dieser gute unbändige Geist, der allem Heute und allem Pöbel wie ein Sturmwind kommt,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20_2:P0004","text":"—der Distel- und Tiftelköpfen feind ist und allen welken Blättern und Unkräutern: gelobt sei dieser wilde gute freie Sturmgeist, welcher auf Mooren und Trübsalen wie auf Wiesen tanzt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20_2:P0005","text":"Der die Pöbel-Schwindhunde hasst und alles missrathene düstere Gezücht: gelobt sei dieser Geist aller freien Geister, der lachende Sturm, welcher allen Schwarzsichtigen, Schwärsüchtigen Staub in die Augen bläst!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20_2:P0006","text":"Ihr höheren Menschen, euer Schlimmstes ist: ihr lerntet alle nicht tanzen, wie man tanzen muss—über euch hinweg tanzen! Was liegt daran, dass ihr missriethet!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20_2:P0007","text":"Wie Vieles ist noch möglich! So lernt doch über euch hinweg lachen! Erhebt eure Herzen, ihr guten Tänzer, hoch! höher! Und vergesst mir auch das gute Lachen nicht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20_2:P0008","text":"Diese Krone des Lachenden, diese Rosenkranz-Krone: euch, meinen Brüdern, werfe ich diese Krone zu! Das Lachen sprach ich heilig; ihr höheren Menschen, lernt mir—lachen!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"d61a60f0a70fb6353b1679ba20e4a1b9b155d435a93b400d5f99cfb758deefae","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183951927846Z_94b0d00f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"20.","source_sha256":"d61a60f0a70fb6353b1679ba20e4a1b9b155d435a93b400d5f99cfb758deefae","sha256":"86f2bcabbcb970c8440c62281fae51894cda7626f4946908b14d985f0b891df2","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:39:51.927874+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_20_2:P0001","text":"## 20.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_20_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20_2:P0002","text":"I am akin to the wind when it rushes down from its mountain caves: it wants to dance to its own pipe, the seas tremble and leap beneath its footfalls.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_20_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20_2:P0003","text":"He who gives wings to asses, who milks lionesses, blessed be this good untamed spirit, who comes like a storm-wind to every Today and every rabble,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_20_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20_2:P0004","text":"—who is hostile to thistle-heads and quibbling heads and to all withered leaves and weeds: blessed be this wild good free storm-spirit, who dances over moors and afflictions as over meadows!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_20_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20_2:P0005","text":"He who hates the rabble's whippet-dogs and all ill-begotten gloomy brood: blessed be this spirit of all free spirits, the laughing storm, which blows dust into the eyes of all the dark-sighted and gloom-addicted!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_20_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20_2:P0006","text":"You higher men, your worst fault is this: you have all failed to learn to dance as one must dance—dance beyond yourselves! What does it matter that you have miscarried!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_20_2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20_2:P0007","text":"How much is still possible! Then learn to laugh beyond yourselves! Lift up your hearts, you good dancers, high! higher! And do not forget for me the good laughter either!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_20_2:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_20_2:P0008","text":"This crown of the laughing one, this rose-wreath crown: to you, my brothers, I throw this crown! I pronounced laughter holy; you higher men, learn from me—to laugh!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_20_2:P0008"]}],"source_digest":"679ae65c1c4985b56ec5cf97124ad9f5f2edc449332adabfd71cbc03ba727f69","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"On the Higher Man","source_section_label":"20.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"On the Higher Man · 20","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","On the Higher Man","20."],"original_title":"20."}],"source_title_context":"Vom höheren Menschen","source_section_label":"20.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Vom höheren Menschen · 20","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Vom höheren Menschen","20."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"20.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_15","title":"1.","order":160,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_1_15","text":"## 1.\n\nAls Zarathustra diese Reden sprach, stand er nahe dem Eingange seiner Höhle; mit den letzten Worten aber entschlüpfte er seinen Gästen und floh für eine kurze Weile in’s Freie.\n\n„Oh reine Gerüche um mich, rief er aus, oh selige Stille um mich! Aber wo sind meine Thiere? Heran, heran, mein Adler und meine Schlange!\n\nSagt mir doch, meine Thiere: diese höheren Menschen insgesammt— riechen sie vielleicht nicht gut? Oh reine Gerüche um mich! Jetzo weiss und fühle ich erst, wie ich euch, meine Thiere, liebe.“\n\n—Und Zarathustra sprach nochmals: „ich liebe euch, meine Thiere!“ Der Adler aber und die Schlange drängten sich an ihn, als er diese Worte sprach, und sahen zu ihm hinauf. Solchergestalt waren sie zu drei still beisammen und schnüffelten und schlürften mit einander die gute Luft. Denn die Luft war hier draussen besser als bei den höheren Menschen.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_15:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_15:P0002","text":"Als Zarathustra diese Reden sprach, stand er nahe dem Eingange seiner Höhle; mit den letzten Worten aber entschlüpfte er seinen Gästen und floh für eine kurze Weile in’s Freie.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_15:P0003","text":"„Oh reine Gerüche um mich, rief er aus, oh selige Stille um mich! Aber wo sind meine Thiere? Heran, heran, mein Adler und meine Schlange!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_15:P0004","text":"Sagt mir doch, meine Thiere: diese höheren Menschen insgesammt— riechen sie vielleicht nicht gut? Oh reine Gerüche um mich! Jetzo weiss und fühle ich erst, wie ich euch, meine Thiere, liebe.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_15:P0005","text":"—Und Zarathustra sprach nochmals: „ich liebe euch, meine Thiere!“ Der Adler aber und die Schlange drängten sich an ihn, als er diese Worte sprach, und sahen zu ihm hinauf. Solchergestalt waren sie zu drei still beisammen und schnüffelten und schlürften mit einander die gute Luft. Denn die Luft war hier draussen besser als bei den höheren Menschen.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"2bfc2e0906f89695886c292680767d62f18c4ebadddfe5cde3de491e5eaca979","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T183955471758Z_1ffba965","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1.","source_sha256":"2bfc2e0906f89695886c292680767d62f18c4ebadddfe5cde3de491e5eaca979","sha256":"3176169373f9fde5c2fbd8c119b80de99207caf3cd588997c5bc7a669c996548","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:39:55.471780+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_15:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_15:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_15:P0002","text":"As Zarathustra spoke these discourses, he stood near the entrance of his cave; but with the last words he slipped away from his guests and fled out into the open for a short while.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_15:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_15:P0003","text":"“Oh pure scents around me,” he cried, “oh blessed silence around me! But where are my animals? Come hither, come hither, my eagle and my serpent!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_15:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_15:P0004","text":"“Tell me then, my animals: these higher men altogether—perhaps they do not smell good? Oh pure scents around me! Only now do I know and feel how I love you, my animals.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_15:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_15:P0005","text":"—And Zarathustra spoke once more: “I love you, my animals!” But the eagle and the serpent pressed close to him as he spoke these words, and looked up at him. Thus the three of them were quietly together and sniffed and drank in the good air with one another. For the air out here was better than among the higher men.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_15:P0005"]}],"source_digest":"6c5f21efa5ac311486420dfb9c84d943fa1bae504c546da968392d1c0cd8d814","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Song of Melancholy","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"The Song of Melancholy · 1","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","The Song of Melancholy","1."],"original_title":"1."}],"source_title_context":"Das Lied der Schwermuth","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Das Lied der Schwermuth · 1","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Das Lied der Schwermuth","1."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"1.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15","title":"2.","order":161,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_2_15","text":"## 2.\n\nKaum aber hatte Zarathustra seine Höhle verlassen, da erhob sich der alte Zauberer, sah listig umher und sprach: „Er ist hinaus!\n\nUnd schon, ihr höheren Menschen—dass ich euch mit diesem Lob- und Schmeichel-Namen kitzle, gleich ihm selber—schon fällt mich mein schlimmer Trug- und Zaubergeist an, mein schwermüthiger Teufel,\n\n—welcher diesem Zarathustra ein Widersacher ist aus dem Grunde: vergebt es ihm! Nun will er vor euch zaubern, er hat gerade seine Stunde; umsonst ringe ich mit diesem bösen Geiste.\n\nEuch Allen, welche Ehren ihr euch mit Worten geben mögt, ob ihr euch „die freien Geister“ nennt oder „die Wahrhaftigen“ oder „die Büsser des Geistes“ oder „die Entfesselten“ oder „die grossen Sehnsüchtigen“—\n\n—euch Allen, die ihr am grossen Ekel leidet gleich mir, denen der alte Gott starb und noch kein neuer Gott in Wiegen und Windeln liegt,—euch Allen ist mein böser Geist und Zauber-Teufel hold.\n\nIch kenne euch, ihr höheren Menschen, ich kenne ihn,—ich kenne auch diesen Unhold, den ich wider Willen liebe, diesen Zarathustra: er selber dünkt mich öfter gleich einer schönen Heiligen-Larve,\n\n—gleich einem neuen wunderlichen Mummenschanze, in dem sich mein böser Geist, der schwermüthige Teufel, gefällt:—ich liebe Zarathustra, so dünkt mich oft, um meines bösen Geistes Willen.—\n\nAber schon fällt der mich an und zwingt mich, dieser Geist der Schwermuth, dieser Abend-Dämmerungs-Teufel: und, wahrlich, ihr höheren Menschen, es gelüstet ihn—\n\n—macht nur die Augen auf!—es gelüstet ihn, nackt zu kommen, ob männlich, ob weiblich, noch weiss ich’s nicht: aber er kommt, er zwingt mich, wehe! macht eure Sinne auf!\n\nDer Tag klingt ab, allen Dingen kommt nun der Abend, auch den besten Dingen; hört nun und seht, ihr höheren Menschen, welcher Teufel, ob Mann, ob Weib, dieser Geist der Abend-Schwermuth ist!“\n\nAlso sprach der alte Zauberer, sah listig umher und griff dann zu seiner Harfe.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0002","text":"Kaum aber hatte Zarathustra seine Höhle verlassen, da erhob sich der alte Zauberer, sah listig umher und sprach: „Er ist hinaus!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0003","text":"Und schon, ihr höheren Menschen—dass ich euch mit diesem Lob- und Schmeichel-Namen kitzle, gleich ihm selber—schon fällt mich mein schlimmer Trug- und Zaubergeist an, mein schwermüthiger Teufel,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0004","text":"—welcher diesem Zarathustra ein Widersacher ist aus dem Grunde: vergebt es ihm! Nun will er vor euch zaubern, er hat gerade seine Stunde; umsonst ringe ich mit diesem bösen Geiste.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0005","text":"Euch Allen, welche Ehren ihr euch mit Worten geben mögt, ob ihr euch „die freien Geister“ nennt oder „die Wahrhaftigen“ oder „die Büsser des Geistes“ oder „die Entfesselten“ oder „die grossen Sehnsüchtigen“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0006","text":"—euch Allen, die ihr am grossen Ekel leidet gleich mir, denen der alte Gott starb und noch kein neuer Gott in Wiegen und Windeln liegt,—euch Allen ist mein böser Geist und Zauber-Teufel hold.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0007","text":"Ich kenne euch, ihr höheren Menschen, ich kenne ihn,—ich kenne auch diesen Unhold, den ich wider Willen liebe, diesen Zarathustra: er selber dünkt mich öfter gleich einer schönen Heiligen-Larve,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0008","text":"—gleich einem neuen wunderlichen Mummenschanze, in dem sich mein böser Geist, der schwermüthige Teufel, gefällt:—ich liebe Zarathustra, so dünkt mich oft, um meines bösen Geistes Willen.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0009","text":"Aber schon fällt der mich an und zwingt mich, dieser Geist der Schwermuth, dieser Abend-Dämmerungs-Teufel: und, wahrlich, ihr höheren Menschen, es gelüstet ihn—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0010","text":"—macht nur die Augen auf!—es gelüstet ihn, nackt zu kommen, ob männlich, ob weiblich, noch weiss ich’s nicht: aber er kommt, er zwingt mich, wehe! macht eure Sinne auf!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0011","text":"Der Tag klingt ab, allen Dingen kommt nun der Abend, auch den besten Dingen; hört nun und seht, ihr höheren Menschen, welcher Teufel, ob Mann, ob Weib, dieser Geist der Abend-Schwermuth ist!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0012","text":"Also sprach der alte Zauberer, sah listig umher und griff dann zu seiner Harfe.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"a0e6ff06b6b0190ffe13808aed4deb3c3913f2fbe8a29bb4d4dea47cc5e8dc06","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184004013570Z_81791aa5","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2.","source_sha256":"a0e6ff06b6b0190ffe13808aed4deb3c3913f2fbe8a29bb4d4dea47cc5e8dc06","sha256":"c2b244cf7b18af13f68f6fee48db4ddcbe9aaef9943e52add26622b1bc2217d7","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:40:04.013593+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_15:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0002","text":"But scarcely had Zarathustra left his cave when the old magician rose up, looked cunningly about, and said: “He has gone out!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_15:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0003","text":"And already, you higher men—that I tickle you with this name of praise and flattery, like him himself—already my evil spirit of deception and magic assails me, my melancholy devil,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_15:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0004","text":"—who is an adversary of this Zarathustra from the ground up: forgive him for it! Now he wishes to perform magic before you; he has just his hour; in vain do I struggle with this evil spirit.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_15:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0005","text":"To all of you who may confer honours upon yourselves with words, whether you call yourselves “the free spirits” or “the truthful ones” or “the penitents of the spirit” or “the unshackled” or “the great yearners”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_15:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0006","text":"—to all of you who, like me, suffer from the great nausea, whose old God died and for whom no new God yet lies in cradle and swaddling-clothes,—to all of you my evil spirit and magic-devil is well disposed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_15:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0007","text":"I know you, you higher men, I know him,—I know also this monster whom I love against my will, this Zarathustra: he himself often seems to me like a beautiful saintly mask,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_15:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0008","text":"—like a new strange masquerade in which my evil spirit, the melancholy devil, delights:—I love Zarathustra, so it often seems to me, for the sake of my evil spirit.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_15:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0009","text":"But already he assails me and compels me, this spirit of melancholy, this twilight devil: and, verily, you higher men, he desires—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_15:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0010","text":"—only open your eyes!—he desires to come naked, whether male, whether female, I do not yet know: but he comes, he compels me, alas! open your senses!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_15:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0011","text":"The day dies away, evening now comes to all things, even to the best things; now hear and see, you higher men, what devil, whether man, whether woman, this spirit of evening-melancholy is!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_15:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_15:P0012","text":"Thus spoke the old magician, looked cunningly about, and then reached for his harp.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_15:P0012"]}],"source_digest":"77f109ec6bce09dc5cd843617ca286fad83ba7b7fd4b128731fd50afd8f5f406","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Song of Melancholy","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"The Song of Melancholy · 2","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","The Song of Melancholy","2."],"original_title":"2."}],"source_title_context":"Das Lied der Schwermuth","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Das Lied der Schwermuth · 2","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Das Lied der Schwermuth","2."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"2.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_8","title":"3.","order":162,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_3C_8","text":"## 3.\n\nBei abgehellter Luft, Wenn schon des Thau’s Tröstung Zur Erde niederquillt, Unsichtbar, auch ungehört: —Denn zartes Schuhwerk trägt Der Tröster Thau gleich allen Trost-Milden—: Gedenkst du da, gedenkst du, heisses Herz, Wie einst du durstetest, Nach himmlischen Thränen und Thau-Geträufel Versengt und müde durstetest, Dieweil auf gelben Gras-Pfaden Boshaft abendliche Sonnenblicke Durch schwarze Bäume um dich liefen, Blendende Sonnen-Gluthblicke, schadenfrohe.\n\n„Der Wahrheit Freier? Du?—so höhnten sie— Nein! Nur ein Dichter! Ein Thier, ein listiges, raubendes, schleichendes, Das lügen muss, Das wissentlich, willentlich lügen muss: Nach Beute lüstern, Bunt verlarvt, Sich selber Larve, Sich selbst zur Beute— Das —der Wahrheit Freier? Nein! Nur Narr! Nur Dichter! Nur Buntes redend, Aus Narren-Larven bunt herausschreiend, Herumsteigend auf lügnerischen Wort-Brücken, Auf bunten Regenbogen, Zwischen falschen Himmeln Und falschen Erden, Herumschweifend, herumschwebend,— Nur Narr! Nur Dichter!...\n\nDas —der Wahrheit Freier? Nicht still, starr, glatt, kalt, Zum Bilde worden, Zur Gottes-Säule, Nicht aufgestellt vor Tempeln, Eines Gottes Thürwart: Nein! Feindselig solchen Wahrheits-Standbildern, In jeder Wildniss heimischer als vor Tempeln, Voll Katzen-Muthwillens, Durch jedes Fenster springend Husch! in jeden Zufall, Jedem Urwalde zuschnüffelnd, Süchtig-sehnsüchtig zuschnüffelnd, Dass du in Urwäldern Unter buntgefleckten Raubthieren Sündlich-gesund und bunt und schön liefest, Mit lüsternen Lefzen, Selig-höhnisch, selig-höllisch, selig-blutgierig, Raubend, schleichend, lügend liefest:...\n\nOder, dem Adler gleich, der lange, Lange starr in Abgründe blickt, In seine Abgründe:... -- Oh wie sie sich hier hinab, Hinunter, hinein, In immer tiefere Tiefen ringeln!— Dann, Plötzlich, geraden Zugs, Gezückten Flugs, Auf Lämmer stossen, Jach hinab, heisshungrig, Nach Lämmern lüstern, Gram allen Lamms-Seelen, Grimmig-gram Allem, was blickt Schafmässig, lammäugig, krauswollig, Grau, mit Lamms-Schafs-Wohlwollen!\n\nAlso Adlerhaft, pantherhaft Sind des Dichters Sehnsüchte, Sind deine Sehnsüchte unter tausend Larven, Du Narr! Du Dichter!\n\nDer du den Menschen schautest So Gott als Schaf—: Den Gott zerreissen im Menschen Wie das Schaf im Menschen, Und zerreisend lachen —\n\nDas , Das ist deine Seligkeit! Eines Panthers und Adlers Seligkeit! Eines Dichters und Narren Seligkeit!“—\n\nBei abgehellter Luft, Wenn schon des Monds Sichel Grün zwischen Purpurröthen Und neidisch hinschleicht: —dem Tage feind, Mit jedem Schritte heimlich An Rosen-Hängematten Hinsichelnd, bis sie sinken, Nacht-abwärts blass hinabsinken:\n\nSo sank ich selber einstmals Aus meinem Wahrheits-Wahnsinne, Aus meinen Tages-Sehnsüchten, Des Tages müde, krank vom Lichte, —sank abwärts, abendwärts, schattenwärts: Von Einer Wahrheit Verbrannt und durstig: —gedenkst du noch, gedenkst du, heisses Herz, Wie da du durstetest?— Dass ich verbannt sei Von aller Wahrheit, Nur Narr! Nur Dichter!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0001","text":"## 3.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0002","text":"Bei abgehellter Luft, Wenn schon des Thau’s Tröstung Zur Erde niederquillt, Unsichtbar, auch ungehört: —Denn zartes Schuhwerk trägt Der Tröster Thau gleich allen Trost-Milden—: Gedenkst du da, gedenkst du, heisses Herz, Wie einst du durstetest, Nach himmlischen Thränen und Thau-Geträufel Versengt und müde durstetest, Dieweil auf gelben Gras-Pfaden Boshaft abendliche Sonnenblicke Durch schwarze Bäume um dich liefen, Blendende Sonnen-Gluthblicke, schadenfrohe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0003","text":"„Der Wahrheit Freier? Du?—so höhnten sie— Nein! Nur ein Dichter! Ein Thier, ein listiges, raubendes, schleichendes, Das lügen muss, Das wissentlich, willentlich lügen muss: Nach Beute lüstern, Bunt verlarvt, Sich selber Larve, Sich selbst zur Beute— Das —der Wahrheit Freier? Nein! Nur Narr! Nur Dichter! Nur Buntes redend, Aus Narren-Larven bunt herausschreiend, Herumsteigend auf lügnerischen Wort-Brücken, Auf bunten Regenbogen, Zwischen falschen Himmeln Und falschen Erden, Herumschweifend, herumschwebend,— Nur Narr! Nur Dichter!...","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0004","text":"Das —der Wahrheit Freier? Nicht still, starr, glatt, kalt, Zum Bilde worden, Zur Gottes-Säule, Nicht aufgestellt vor Tempeln, Eines Gottes Thürwart: Nein! Feindselig solchen Wahrheits-Standbildern, In jeder Wildniss heimischer als vor Tempeln, Voll Katzen-Muthwillens, Durch jedes Fenster springend Husch! in jeden Zufall, Jedem Urwalde zuschnüffelnd, Süchtig-sehnsüchtig zuschnüffelnd, Dass du in Urwäldern Unter buntgefleckten Raubthieren Sündlich-gesund und bunt und schön liefest, Mit lüsternen Lefzen, Selig-höhnisch, selig-höllisch, selig-blutgierig, Raubend, schleichend, lügend liefest:...","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0005","text":"Oder, dem Adler gleich, der lange, Lange starr in Abgründe blickt, In seine Abgründe:... -- Oh wie sie sich hier hinab, Hinunter, hinein, In immer tiefere Tiefen ringeln!— Dann, Plötzlich, geraden Zugs, Gezückten Flugs, Auf Lämmer stossen, Jach hinab, heisshungrig, Nach Lämmern lüstern, Gram allen Lamms-Seelen, Grimmig-gram Allem, was blickt Schafmässig, lammäugig, krauswollig, Grau, mit Lamms-Schafs-Wohlwollen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0006","text":"Also Adlerhaft, pantherhaft Sind des Dichters Sehnsüchte, Sind deine Sehnsüchte unter tausend Larven, Du Narr! Du Dichter!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0007","text":"Der du den Menschen schautest So Gott als Schaf—: Den Gott zerreissen im Menschen Wie das Schaf im Menschen, Und zerreisend lachen —","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0008","text":"Das , Das ist deine Seligkeit! Eines Panthers und Adlers Seligkeit! Eines Dichters und Narren Seligkeit!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0009","text":"Bei abgehellter Luft, Wenn schon des Monds Sichel Grün zwischen Purpurröthen Und neidisch hinschleicht: —dem Tage feind, Mit jedem Schritte heimlich An Rosen-Hängematten Hinsichelnd, bis sie sinken, Nacht-abwärts blass hinabsinken:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0010","text":"So sank ich selber einstmals Aus meinem Wahrheits-Wahnsinne, Aus meinen Tages-Sehnsüchten, Des Tages müde, krank vom Lichte, —sank abwärts, abendwärts, schattenwärts: Von Einer Wahrheit Verbrannt und durstig: —gedenkst du noch, gedenkst du, heisses Herz, Wie da du durstetest?— Dass ich verbannt sei Von aller Wahrheit, Nur Narr! Nur Dichter!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"c56b6328cd0e75b1b55251a87e284d22b2898847cc3245a769339baa9d093b44","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184017195302Z_fa64562f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"3.","source_sha256":"c56b6328cd0e75b1b55251a87e284d22b2898847cc3245a769339baa9d093b44","sha256":"f8262db295211773be11e8cf3ba1061d233269021b4abd4c82d1a69912451caf","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:40:17.195335+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0001","text":"## 3.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0002","text":"In lightened air, When already the dew’s consolation Pours down to earth, Invisible, also unheard: —For delicate footwear wears The consoler Dew, like every solace-mild one—: Dost thou then remember, dost thou remember, hot heart, How once thou thirstedst, After heavenly tears and dew-drops Scorched and weary thou thirstedst, Whilst upon yellow grass-paths Malicious evening sun-glances Ran about thee through black trees, Dazzling sun-glow-glances, maliciously gleeful.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0003","text":"“A suitor of truth? Thou?—thus they mocked— No! Only a poet! A beast, cunning, predatory, stealthy, That must lie, That knowingly, willingly must lie: Lusting after prey, Colourfully masked, Itself a mask, Itself its own prey— That—a suitor of truth? No! Only fool! Only poet! Only speaking motley, Crying out motley from fools’ masks, Clambering about upon lying word-bridges, Upon coloured rainbows, Between false heavens And false earths, Roaming about, hovering about,— Only fool! Only poet!...","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0004","text":"That—a suitor of truth? Not still, rigid, smooth, cold, Become an image, A god’s pillar, Not set up before temples, A god’s doorkeeper: No! Hostile to such truth-statues, More at home in every wilderness than before temples, Full of cat-like wantonness, Leaping through every window Whisk! into every chance, Sniffing at every primeval forest, Addictedly-longingly sniffing, That thou in primeval forests Among colour-spotted beasts of prey Didst walk sinfully-healthy and motley and beautiful, With lusting lips, Blissfully-mockingly, blissfully-hellishly, blissfully-bloodthirstily, Predating, stealing, lying thou didst walk:...","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0005","text":"Or, like the eagle, which long, Long stares into abysses, Into its abysses:... -- Oh how they coil themselves down here, Downward, inward, Into ever deeper depths!— Then, Suddenly, straight in flight, Unsheathed flight, Pounce upon lambs, Headlong down, hot-hungry, Lusting after lambs, Hating all lamb-souls, Grimly hating everything that looks Sheepish, lamb-eyed, curly-woolled, Grey, with lamb-sheep-goodwill!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0006","text":"Thus eagle-like, panther-like Are the poet’s longings, Are thy longings beneath a thousand masks, thou fool! Thou poet!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0007","text":"Thou who didst behold man As god and sheep—: To tear the god in man As the sheep in man, And, tearing, to laugh —","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0008","text":"That, That is thy bliss! A panther’s and eagle’s bliss! A poet’s and fool’s bliss!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0009","text":"In lightened air, When already the moon’s sickle Green amid purple reddenings And enviously steals along: —hostile to the day, With every step secretly Sickle-toward rose-hammocks, until they sink, Nightward pale sink down:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0010","text":"Thus once I myself sank From my truth-madness, From my day-longings, Weary of the day, sick from the light, —sank downward, eveningward, shadowward: Burned and thirsty from One truth: —dost thou still remember, dost thou remember, hot heart, How then thou thirstedst?— May I be banished From all truth, Only fool! Only poet!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_8:P0010"]}],"source_digest":"6ce665126346055ac85983612a6c1eb8db51d92242e63e8ac1685420882b7db1","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Song of Melancholy","source_section_label":"3.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"The Song of Melancholy · 3","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","The Song of Melancholy","3."],"original_title":"3."}],"source_title_context":"Das Lied der Schwermuth","source_section_label":"3.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Das Lied der Schwermuth · 3","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Das Lied der Schwermuth","3."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"3.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0","title":"On Science","order":163,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_VDW0","text":"Also sang der Zauberer; und Alle, die beisammen waren, giengen gleich Vögeln unvermerkt in das Netz seiner listigen und schwermüthigen Wollust. Nur der Gewissenhafte des Geistes war nicht eingefangen: er nahm flugs dem Zauberer die Harfe weg und rief „Luft! Lasst gute Luft herein! Lass Zarathustra herein! Du machst diese Höhle schwül und giftig, du schlimmer alter Zauberer!\n\nDu verfährst, du Falscher, Feiner, zu unbekannten Begierden und Wildnissen. Und wehe, wenn Solche, wie du, von der Wahrheit Redens und Wesens machen!\n\nWehe allen freien Geistern, welche nicht vor solchen Zauberern auf der Hut sind! Dahin ist es mit ihrer Freiheit: du lehrst und lockst zurück in Gefängnisse,—\n\n—du alter schwermüthiger Teufel, aus deiner Klage klingt eine Lockpfeife, du gleichst Solchen, welche mit ihrem Lobe der Keuschheit heimlich zu Wollüsten laden!“\n\nAlso sprach der Gewissenhafte; der alte Zauberer aber blickte um sich, genoss seines Sieges und verschluckte darüber den Verdruss, welchen ihm der Gewissenhafte machte. „Sei still! sagte er mit bescheidener Stimme, gute Lieder wollen gut wiederhallen; nach guten Liedern soll man lange schweigen.\n\nSo thun es diese Alle, die höheren Menschen. Du aber hast wohl Wenig von meinem Lied verstanden? In dir ist Wenig von einem Zaubergeiste.“\n\n„Du lobst mich, entgegnete der Gewissenhafte, indem du mich von dir abtrennst, wohlan! Aber ihr Anderen, was sehe ich? Ihr sitzt alle noch mit lüsternen Augen da—:\n\nIhr freien Seelen, wohin ist eure Freiheit! Fast, dünkt mich’s, gleicht ihr Solchen, die lange schlimmen tanzenden nackten Mädchen zusahn: eure Seelen tanzen selber!\n\nIn euch, ihr höheren Menschen, muss Mehr von Dem sein, was der Zauberer seinen bösen Zauber- und Truggeist nennt:—wir müssen wohl verschieden sein.\n\nUnd wahrlich, wir sprachen und dachten genug mitsammen, ehe Zarathustra heimkam zu seiner Höhle, als dass ich nicht wüsste: wir sind verschieden.\n\nWir suchen Verschiednes auch hier oben, ihr und ich. Ich nämlich suche mehr Sicherheit , desshalb kam ich zu Zarathustra. Der nämlich ist noch der festeste Thurm und Wille—\n\n—heute, wo Alles wackelt, wo alle Erde bebt. Ihr aber, wenn ich eure Augen sehe, die ihr macht, fast dünkt mich’s, ihr sucht mehr Unsicherheit ,\n\n—mehr Schauder, mehr Gefahr, mehr Erdbeben. Euch gelüstet, fast dünkt mich’s so, vergebt meinem Dünkel, ihr höheren Menschen—\n\n—euch gelüstet nach dem schlimmsten gefährlichsten Leben, das mir am meisten Furcht macht, nach dem Leben wilder Thiere, nach Wäldern, Höhlen, steilen Bergen und Irr- Schlünden.\n\nUnd nicht die Führer aus der Gefahr gefallen euch am besten, sondern die euch von allen Wegen abführen, die Verführer. Aber, wenn solch Gelüsten an euch wirklich ist, so dünkt es mich trotzdem unmöglich .\n\nFurcht nämlich—das ist des Menschen Erb- und Grundgefühl; aus der Furcht erklärt sich jegliches, Erbsünde und Erbtugend. Aus der Furcht wuchs auch meine Tugend, die heisst: Wissenschaft.\n\nDie Furcht nämlich vor wildem Gethier—die wurde dem Menschen am längsten angezüchtet, einschliesslich das Thier, das er in sich selber birgt und fürchtet:—Zarathustra heisst es „das innere Vieh“.\n\nSolche lange alte Furcht, endlich fein geworden, geistlich, geistig—heute, dünkt mich, heisst sie: Wissenschaft.“—\n\nAlso sprach der Gewissenhafte; aber Zarathustra, der eben in seine Höhle zurückkam und die letzte Rede gehört und errathen hatte, warf dem Gewissenhaften eine Hand voll Rosen zu und lachte ob seiner „Wahrheiten“ . „Wie! rief er, was hörte ich da eben? Wahrlich, mich dünkt, du bist ein Narr oder ich selber bin’s: und deine „Wahrheit“ stelle ich rucks und flugs auf den Kopf.\n\nFurcht nämlich—ist unsre Ausnahme. Muth aber und Abenteuer und Lust am Ungewissen, am Ungewagten,— Muth dünkt mich des Menschen ganze Vorgeschichte.\n\nDen wildesten muthigsten Thieren hat er alle ihre Tugenden abgeneidet und abgeraubt: so erst wurde er—zum Menschen.\n\nDieser Muth, endlich fein geworden, geistlich, geistig, dieser Menschen-Muth mit Adler-Flügeln und Schlangen-Klugheit: der , dünkt mich, heisst heute—„\n\n„Zarathustra“! schrien Alle, die beisammen sassen, wie aus Einem Munde und machten dazu ein grosses Gelächter; es hob sich aber von ihnen wie eine schwere Wolke. Auch der Zauberer lachte und sprach mit Klugheit: „Wohlan! Er ist davon, mein böser Geist!\n\nUnd habe ich euch nicht selber vor ihm gewarnt, als ich sagte, dass er ein Betrüger sei, ein Lug- und Truggeist?\n\nSonderlich nämlich, wenn er sich nackend zeigt. Aber was kann ich für seine Tücken! Habe ich ihn und die Welt geschaffen?\n\nWohlan! Seien wir wieder gut und guter Dinge! Und ob schon Zarathustra böse blickt—seht ihn doch! er ist mir gram—:\n\n—bevor die Nacht kommt, lernt er wieder, mich lieben und loben, er kann nicht lange leben, ohne solche Thorheiten zu thun.\n\nDer —liebt seine Feinde: diese Kunst versteht er am besten von Allen, die ich sah. Aber er nimmt Rache dafür—an seinen Freunden!“\n\nAlso sprach der alte Zauberer, und die höheren Menschen zollten ihm Beifall: so dass Zarathustra herumgieng und mit Bosheit und Liebe seinen Freunden die Hände schüttelte,—gleichsam als Einer, der an Allen Etwas gutzumachen und abzubitten hat. Als er aber dabei an die Thür seiner Höhle kam, siehe, da gelüstete ihn schon wieder nach der guten Luft da draussen und nach seinen Thieren,—und er wollte hinaus schlüpfen.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0001","text":"Also sang der Zauberer; und Alle, die beisammen waren, giengen gleich Vögeln unvermerkt in das Netz seiner listigen und schwermüthigen Wollust. Nur der Gewissenhafte des Geistes war nicht eingefangen: er nahm flugs dem Zauberer die Harfe weg und rief „Luft! Lasst gute Luft herein! Lass Zarathustra herein! Du machst diese Höhle schwül und giftig, du schlimmer alter Zauberer!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0002","text":"Du verfährst, du Falscher, Feiner, zu unbekannten Begierden und Wildnissen. Und wehe, wenn Solche, wie du, von der Wahrheit Redens und Wesens machen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0003","text":"Wehe allen freien Geistern, welche nicht vor solchen Zauberern auf der Hut sind! Dahin ist es mit ihrer Freiheit: du lehrst und lockst zurück in Gefängnisse,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0004","text":"—du alter schwermüthiger Teufel, aus deiner Klage klingt eine Lockpfeife, du gleichst Solchen, welche mit ihrem Lobe der Keuschheit heimlich zu Wollüsten laden!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0005","text":"Also sprach der Gewissenhafte; der alte Zauberer aber blickte um sich, genoss seines Sieges und verschluckte darüber den Verdruss, welchen ihm der Gewissenhafte machte. „Sei still! sagte er mit bescheidener Stimme, gute Lieder wollen gut wiederhallen; nach guten Liedern soll man lange schweigen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0006","text":"So thun es diese Alle, die höheren Menschen. Du aber hast wohl Wenig von meinem Lied verstanden? In dir ist Wenig von einem Zaubergeiste.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0007","text":"„Du lobst mich, entgegnete der Gewissenhafte, indem du mich von dir abtrennst, wohlan! Aber ihr Anderen, was sehe ich? Ihr sitzt alle noch mit lüsternen Augen da—:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0008","text":"Ihr freien Seelen, wohin ist eure Freiheit! Fast, dünkt mich’s, gleicht ihr Solchen, die lange schlimmen tanzenden nackten Mädchen zusahn: eure Seelen tanzen selber!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0009","text":"In euch, ihr höheren Menschen, muss Mehr von Dem sein, was der Zauberer seinen bösen Zauber- und Truggeist nennt:—wir müssen wohl verschieden sein.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0010","text":"Und wahrlich, wir sprachen und dachten genug mitsammen, ehe Zarathustra heimkam zu seiner Höhle, als dass ich nicht wüsste: wir sind verschieden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0011","text":"Wir suchen Verschiednes auch hier oben, ihr und ich. Ich nämlich suche mehr Sicherheit , desshalb kam ich zu Zarathustra. Der nämlich ist noch der festeste Thurm und Wille—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0012","text":"—heute, wo Alles wackelt, wo alle Erde bebt. Ihr aber, wenn ich eure Augen sehe, die ihr macht, fast dünkt mich’s, ihr sucht mehr Unsicherheit ,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0013","text":"—mehr Schauder, mehr Gefahr, mehr Erdbeben. Euch gelüstet, fast dünkt mich’s so, vergebt meinem Dünkel, ihr höheren Menschen—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0014","text":"—euch gelüstet nach dem schlimmsten gefährlichsten Leben, das mir am meisten Furcht macht, nach dem Leben wilder Thiere, nach Wäldern, Höhlen, steilen Bergen und Irr- Schlünden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0015","text":"Und nicht die Führer aus der Gefahr gefallen euch am besten, sondern die euch von allen Wegen abführen, die Verführer. Aber, wenn solch Gelüsten an euch wirklich ist, so dünkt es mich trotzdem unmöglich .","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0016","text":"Furcht nämlich—das ist des Menschen Erb- und Grundgefühl; aus der Furcht erklärt sich jegliches, Erbsünde und Erbtugend. Aus der Furcht wuchs auch meine Tugend, die heisst: Wissenschaft.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0017","text":"Die Furcht nämlich vor wildem Gethier—die wurde dem Menschen am längsten angezüchtet, einschliesslich das Thier, das er in sich selber birgt und fürchtet:—Zarathustra heisst es „das innere Vieh“.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0018","text":"Solche lange alte Furcht, endlich fein geworden, geistlich, geistig—heute, dünkt mich, heisst sie: Wissenschaft.“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0019","text":"Also sprach der Gewissenhafte; aber Zarathustra, der eben in seine Höhle zurückkam und die letzte Rede gehört und errathen hatte, warf dem Gewissenhaften eine Hand voll Rosen zu und lachte ob seiner „Wahrheiten“ . „Wie! rief er, was hörte ich da eben? Wahrlich, mich dünkt, du bist ein Narr oder ich selber bin’s: und deine „Wahrheit“ stelle ich rucks und flugs auf den Kopf.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0020","text":"Furcht nämlich—ist unsre Ausnahme. Muth aber und Abenteuer und Lust am Ungewissen, am Ungewagten,— Muth dünkt mich des Menschen ganze Vorgeschichte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0021","text":"Den wildesten muthigsten Thieren hat er alle ihre Tugenden abgeneidet und abgeraubt: so erst wurde er—zum Menschen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0022","text":"Dieser Muth, endlich fein geworden, geistlich, geistig, dieser Menschen-Muth mit Adler-Flügeln und Schlangen-Klugheit: der , dünkt mich, heisst heute—„","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0023","text":"„Zarathustra“! schrien Alle, die beisammen sassen, wie aus Einem Munde und machten dazu ein grosses Gelächter; es hob sich aber von ihnen wie eine schwere Wolke. Auch der Zauberer lachte und sprach mit Klugheit: „Wohlan! Er ist davon, mein böser Geist!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0024","text":"Und habe ich euch nicht selber vor ihm gewarnt, als ich sagte, dass er ein Betrüger sei, ein Lug- und Truggeist?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0025","text":"Sonderlich nämlich, wenn er sich nackend zeigt. Aber was kann ich für seine Tücken! Habe ich ihn und die Welt geschaffen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0026","text":"Wohlan! Seien wir wieder gut und guter Dinge! Und ob schon Zarathustra böse blickt—seht ihn doch! er ist mir gram—:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0027","text":"—bevor die Nacht kommt, lernt er wieder, mich lieben und loben, er kann nicht lange leben, ohne solche Thorheiten zu thun.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0028","text":"Der —liebt seine Feinde: diese Kunst versteht er am besten von Allen, die ich sah. Aber er nimmt Rache dafür—an seinen Freunden!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0029","text":"Also sprach der alte Zauberer, und die höheren Menschen zollten ihm Beifall: so dass Zarathustra herumgieng und mit Bosheit und Liebe seinen Freunden die Hände schüttelte,—gleichsam als Einer, der an Allen Etwas gutzumachen und abzubitten hat. Als er aber dabei an die Thür seiner Höhle kam, siehe, da gelüstete ihn schon wieder nach der guten Luft da draussen und nach seinen Thieren,—und er wollte hinaus schlüpfen.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"5ac18ae4131f457b41c5d230a3f4f52733dfbf9e5b6307ea0313b505cf543c56","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184036338696Z_c44f21f3","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"On Science","source_sha256":"5ac18ae4131f457b41c5d230a3f4f52733dfbf9e5b6307ea0313b505cf543c56","sha256":"4af41a9946a28deed09f05eb8918ce1a8ddf5c91aecbbeeff879e4047280cfdd","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:40:36.338710+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0001","text":"Thus sang the magician; and all who were gathered together went, like birds, unawares into the net of his cunning and melancholy voluptuousness. Only the Conscientious One of the Spirit was not caught: swiftly he took the harp from the magician and cried, “Air! Let good air in! Let Zarathustra in! You make this cave sultry and poisonous, you wicked old magician!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0002","text":"“You seduce, you false, subtle one, toward unknown desires and wildernesses. And woe when such as you make talk and business out of truth!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0003","text":"“Woe to all free spirits who are not on their guard against such magicians! Their freedom is gone: you teach and lure them back into prisons,—”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0004","text":"“—you old melancholy devil, from your lament there sounds a lure-pipe; you resemble those who, with their praise of chastity, secretly invite to voluptuousness!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0005","text":"Thus spoke the Conscientious One; but the old magician looked about him, enjoyed his victory, and thereupon swallowed the annoyance which the Conscientious One caused him. “Be still!” he said in a modest voice, “good songs wish to resound well; after good songs one should remain silent for a long time.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0006","text":"“Thus do all these, the higher men. But you have perhaps understood little of my song? There is little of a magician-spirit in you.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0007","text":"“You praise me,” replied the Conscientious One, “by separating me from yourself; well then! But you others, what do I see? You are all still sitting there with lustful eyes—:”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0008","text":"“You free souls, where has your freedom gone! You almost seem to me like those who for a long time watched wicked dancing naked girls: your souls themselves are dancing!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0009","text":"“In you, you higher men, there must be more of that which the magician calls his evil magic- and deceiving spirit:—we must indeed be different.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0010","text":"“And truly, we spoke and thought enough together before Zarathustra came home to his cave that I should not know: we are different.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0011","text":"“We seek different things up here too, you and I. For I seek more security; that is why I came to Zarathustra. For he is still the firmest tower and will—”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0012","text":"“—today, when everything wavers, when all the earth trembles. But you, when I see the eyes you make, you almost seem to me to seek more insecurity,”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0013","text":"“—more shuddering, more danger, more earthquakes. You desire, it almost seems to me so—forgive my conceit, you higher men—”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0014","text":"“—you desire the worst, most dangerous life, which frightens me most, the life of wild beasts, forests, caves, steep mountains, and erring abysses.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0015","text":"“And it is not the guides out of danger who please you best, but those who lead you away from all paths, the seducers. But if such desire is really in you, then it nevertheless seems impossible to me.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0016","text":"“For fear—that is man’s inherited and fundamental feeling; from fear everything is explained, original sin and inherited virtue. From fear too grew my virtue, which is called: science.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0017","text":"“For fear of wild beasts—that was bred into man the longest, including the beast that he harbours and fears within himself:—Zarathustra calls it ‘the inner beast.’”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0018","text":"“Such long-standing old fear, finally become subtle, spiritual, intellectual—today, it seems to me, is called: science.”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0019","text":"Thus spoke the Conscientious One; but Zarathustra, who had just returned to his cave and had heard and guessed the last speech, threw a handful of roses at the Conscientious One and laughed at his “truths.” “What! he cried, what did I just hear? Truly, it seems to me that you are a fool, or I myself am one: and I shall turn your ‘truth’ upside down in no time.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0020","text":"“For fear—is our exception. But courage and adventure and pleasure in the uncertain, in the untried,—courage seems to me to be the whole prehistory of man.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0021","text":"“From the wildest, most courageous animals he envied and robbed all their virtues: only thus did he become—man.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0022","text":"“This courage, finally become subtle, spiritual, intellectual, this human courage with eagle-wings and serpent-wisdom: this, it seems to me, is called today—”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0023","text":"“‘Zarathustra’!” cried all who sat gathered together, as though from one mouth, and added a great laughter; but it rose from them like a heavy cloud. The magician too laughed and said shrewdly: “Well then! He is gone, my evil spirit!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0024","text":"“And did I not myself warn you against him, when I said that he was a deceiver, a lying and deceiving spirit?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0025","text":"Especially, that is, when he shows himself naked. But what can I do about his tricks! Did I create him and the world?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0026","text":"Come now! Let us be good again and of good cheer! And though Zarathustra looks angry—just look at him! he is angry with me—:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0027","text":"—before night comes, he will learn again to love and praise me; he cannot live long without doing such follies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0028","text":"He—loves his enemies: this art he understands best of all those I have seen. But he takes revenge for it—on his friends!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0029","text":"Thus spoke the old magician, and the higher men applauded him: so that Zarathustra went about and shook hands with his friends in malice and love,—just like one who has something to make good and to beg forgiveness for from everyone. But when in doing so he came to the door of his cave, behold, he already longed again for the good air outside and for his animals,—and wanted to slip out.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_VDW0:P0029"]}],"source_digest":"5303ed5ab2865410119334eb4b10ac61788fdd045d15cb1db8dff2d5dde2e2d7","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Von der Wissenschaft"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Von der Wissenschaft","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16","title":"1.","order":164,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_1_16","text":"## 1.\n\n„Gehe nicht davon! sagte da der Wanderer, welcher sich den Schatten Zarathustra’s nannte, bleibe bei uns, es möchte uns sonst die alte dumpfe Trübsal wieder anfallen.\n\nSchon gab uns jener alte Zauberer von seinem Schlimmsten zum Besten, und siehe doch, der gute fromme Papst da hat Thränen in den Augen und hat sich ganz wieder auf’s Meer der Schwermuth eingeschifft.\n\nDiese Könige mögen wohl vor uns noch gute Miene machen: das lernten Die nämlich von uns Allen heute am Besten! Hätten sie aber keine Zeugen, ich wette, auch bei ihnen fienge das böse Spiel wieder an—\n\n—das böse Spiel der ziehenden Wolken, der feuchten Schwermuth, der verhängten Himmel, der gestohlenen Sonnen, der heulenden Herbst-Winde,\n\n—das böse Spiel unsres Heulens und Nothschreiens: bleibe bei uns, oh Zarathustra! Hier ist viel verborgenes Elend, das reden will, viel Abend, viel Wolke, viel dumpfe Luft!\n\nDu nährtest uns mit starker Manns-Kost und kräftigen Sprüchen: lass es nicht zu, dass uns zum Nachtisch die weichlichen weiblichen Geister wieder anfallen!\n\nDu allein machst die Luft um dich herum stark und klar! Fand ich je auf Erden so gute Luft als bei dir in deiner Höhle?\n\nViele Länder sah ich doch, meine Nase lernte vielerlei Luft prüfen und abschätzen: aber bei dir schmecken meine Nüstern ihre grösste Lust!\n\nEs sei denn,—es sei denn—, oh vergieb eine alte Erinnerung! Vergieb mir ein altes Nachtisch-Lied, das ich einst unter Töchtern der Wüste dichtete:—\n\n—bei denen nämlich gab es gleich gute helle morgenländische Luft; dort war ich am fernsten vom wolkigen feuchten schwermüthigen Alt-Europa!\n\nDamals liebte ich solcherlei Morgenland-Mädchen und andres blaues Himmelreich, über dem keine Wolken und keine Gedanken hängen.\n\nIhr glaubt es nicht, wie artig sie dasassen, wenn sie nicht tanzten, tief, aber ohne Gedanken, wie kleine Geheimnisse, wie bebänderte Räthsel, wie Nachtisch-Nüsse—\n\nbunt und fremd fürwahr! aber ohne Wolken: Räthsel, die sich rathen lassen: solchen Mädchen zu Liebe erdachte ich damals einen Nachtisch-Psalm.“\n\nAlso sprach der Wanderer und Schatten; und ehe Jemand ihm antwortete, hatte er schon die Harfe des alten Zauberers ergriffen, die Beine gekreuzt und blickte gelassen und weise um sich:—mit den Nüstern aber zog er langsam und fragend die Luft ein, wie Einer, der in neuen Ländern neue fremde Luft kostet. Darauf hob er mit einer Art Gebrüll zu singen an.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0002","text":"„Gehe nicht davon! sagte da der Wanderer, welcher sich den Schatten Zarathustra’s nannte, bleibe bei uns, es möchte uns sonst die alte dumpfe Trübsal wieder anfallen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0003","text":"Schon gab uns jener alte Zauberer von seinem Schlimmsten zum Besten, und siehe doch, der gute fromme Papst da hat Thränen in den Augen und hat sich ganz wieder auf’s Meer der Schwermuth eingeschifft.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0004","text":"Diese Könige mögen wohl vor uns noch gute Miene machen: das lernten Die nämlich von uns Allen heute am Besten! Hätten sie aber keine Zeugen, ich wette, auch bei ihnen fienge das böse Spiel wieder an—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0005","text":"—das böse Spiel der ziehenden Wolken, der feuchten Schwermuth, der verhängten Himmel, der gestohlenen Sonnen, der heulenden Herbst-Winde,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0006","text":"—das böse Spiel unsres Heulens und Nothschreiens: bleibe bei uns, oh Zarathustra! Hier ist viel verborgenes Elend, das reden will, viel Abend, viel Wolke, viel dumpfe Luft!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0007","text":"Du nährtest uns mit starker Manns-Kost und kräftigen Sprüchen: lass es nicht zu, dass uns zum Nachtisch die weichlichen weiblichen Geister wieder anfallen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0008","text":"Du allein machst die Luft um dich herum stark und klar! Fand ich je auf Erden so gute Luft als bei dir in deiner Höhle?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0009","text":"Viele Länder sah ich doch, meine Nase lernte vielerlei Luft prüfen und abschätzen: aber bei dir schmecken meine Nüstern ihre grösste Lust!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0010","text":"Es sei denn,—es sei denn—, oh vergieb eine alte Erinnerung! Vergieb mir ein altes Nachtisch-Lied, das ich einst unter Töchtern der Wüste dichtete:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0011","text":"—bei denen nämlich gab es gleich gute helle morgenländische Luft; dort war ich am fernsten vom wolkigen feuchten schwermüthigen Alt-Europa!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0012","text":"Damals liebte ich solcherlei Morgenland-Mädchen und andres blaues Himmelreich, über dem keine Wolken und keine Gedanken hängen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0013","text":"Ihr glaubt es nicht, wie artig sie dasassen, wenn sie nicht tanzten, tief, aber ohne Gedanken, wie kleine Geheimnisse, wie bebänderte Räthsel, wie Nachtisch-Nüsse—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0014","text":"bunt und fremd fürwahr! aber ohne Wolken: Räthsel, die sich rathen lassen: solchen Mädchen zu Liebe erdachte ich damals einen Nachtisch-Psalm.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0015","text":"Also sprach der Wanderer und Schatten; und ehe Jemand ihm antwortete, hatte er schon die Harfe des alten Zauberers ergriffen, die Beine gekreuzt und blickte gelassen und weise um sich:—mit den Nüstern aber zog er langsam und fragend die Luft ein, wie Einer, der in neuen Ländern neue fremde Luft kostet. Darauf hob er mit einer Art Gebrüll zu singen an.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"ddd24144a6db89d977753c2904cab61e859a72f8cf1c380bdad98cbe243bd9cf","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184046280187Z_b1dffd66","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1.","source_sha256":"ddd24144a6db89d977753c2904cab61e859a72f8cf1c380bdad98cbe243bd9cf","sha256":"faf10020fea57b7a1dda9d6bdf09f720dbaa976d3fb8f786497ebc08652ef6af","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:40:46.280197+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_16:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0002","text":"“Do not go away! said the Wanderer, who called himself Zarathustra’s shadow, stay with us, else the old dull melancholy might again befall us.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_16:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0003","text":"“That old magician has just treated us to the worst of his wares, and behold, the good pious Pope there has tears in his eyes and has quite embarked again upon the sea of melancholy.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_16:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0004","text":"“These kings may well still put on a good face before us: for that, indeed, they learned best of all from us today! But if they had no witnesses, I wager the wicked game would begin again with them too—”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_16:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0005","text":"“—the wicked game of drifting clouds, damp melancholy, shrouded heavens, stolen suns, howling autumn winds,”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_16:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0006","text":"“—the wicked game of our howling and cries of distress: stay with us, O Zarathustra! Here is much hidden misery that wishes to speak, much evening, much cloud, much stifling air!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_16:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0007","text":"“You nourished us with strong man’s fare and vigorous sayings: do not permit the effeminate feminine spirits to assail us again for dessert!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_16:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0008","text":"“You alone make the air around you strong and clear! Did I ever find such good air on earth as with you in your cave?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_16:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0009","text":"“I have indeed seen many lands; my nose learned to test and appraise many kinds of air: but with you my nostrils taste their greatest delight!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_16:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0010","text":"“Unless,—unless—, oh forgive an old recollection! Forgive me an old dessert-song, which I once composed among Daughters of the Desert:—”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_16:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0011","text":"“—for among them, that is, there was equally good, bright Oriental air; there I was farthest from cloudy, damp, melancholy Old Europe!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_16:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0012","text":"“At that time I loved such Oriental girls and another blue kingdom of heaven, over which no clouds and no thoughts hang.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_16:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0013","text":"“You would not believe how prettily they sat there, when they were not dancing, profound but without thoughts, like little secrets, like ribboned riddles, like dessert-nuts—”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_16:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0014","text":"“variegated and strange indeed! but without clouds: riddles that allow themselves to be guessed: for the love of such girls I then devised a dessert-psalm.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_16:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_16:P0015","text":"Thus spoke the Wanderer and Shadow; and before anyone answered him, he had already seized the old magician’s harp, crossed his legs, and looked calmly and wisely about him:—but with his nostrils he slowly and inquisitively drew in the air, like one who in new lands tastes new strange air. Thereupon he began to sing with a kind of roar.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_16:P0015"]}],"source_digest":"2d92f77ba9d7058bfbff77c7dac1cc408126ad1dc3301afe7f32c8ee9c1bfb36","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"Among Daughters of the Desert","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"Among Daughters of the Desert · 1","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","Among Daughters of the Desert","1."],"original_title":"1."}],"source_title_context":"Unter Töchtern der Wüste","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Unter Töchtern der Wüste · 1","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Unter Töchtern der Wüste","1."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"1.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16","title":"2.","order":165,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_2_16","text":"## 2.\n\nDie Wüste wächst: weh Dem, der Wüsten birgt!\n\n—Ha! Feierlich! In der That feierlich! Ein würdiger Anfang! Afrikanisch feierlich! Eines Löwen würdig, Oder eines moralischen Brüllaffen— —aber Nichts für euch, Ihr allerliebsten Freundinnen, Zu deren Füssen mir Zum ersten Male, Einem Europäer, unter Palmen Zu sitzen vergönnt ist. Sela.\n\nWunderbar wahrlich! Da sitze ich nun, Der Wüste nahe und bereits So fern wieder der Wüste, Auch in Nichts noch verwüstet: Nämlich hinabgeschluckt Von dieser kleinsten Oasis—: —sie sperrte gerade gähnend Ihr liebliches Maul auf. Das wohlriechendste aller Mäulchen: Da fiel ich hinein, Hinab, hindurch—unter euch, Ihr allerliebsten Freundinnen! Sela.\n\nHeil, Heil jenem Wallfische, Wenn er also es seinem Gaste Wohl sein liess!—ihr versteht Meine gelehrte Anspielung? Heil seinem Bauche, Wenn er also Ein so lieblicher Oasis-Bauch war Gleich diesem: was ich aber in Zweifel ziehe, —dafür komme ich aus Europa, Das zweifelsüchtiger ist als alle Ältlichen Eheweibchen. Möge Gott es bessern! Amen!\n\nDa sitze ich nun, In dieser kleinsten Oasis, Einer Dattel gleich, Braun, durchsüsst, goldschwürig, lüstern Nach einem runden Mädchenmunde, Mehr noch aber nach mädchenhaften Eiskalten schneeweissen schneidigen Beisszähnen: nach denen nämlich Lechzt das Herz allen heissen Datteln. Sela.\n\nDen genannten Südfrüchten Ähnlich, allzuähnlich Liege ich hier, von kleinen Flügelkäfern Umtänzelt und umspielt, Insgleichen von noch kleineren Thörichteren boshafteren Wünschen und Einfällen, Umlagert von euch, Ihr stummen, ihr ahnungsvollen Mädchen-Katzen, Dudu und Suleika, — umsphinxt , dass ich in Ein Wort Viel Gefühle stopfe: (Vergebe mir Gott Diese Sprach-Sünde!) —sitze hier, die beste Luft schnüffelnd, Paradieses-Luft wahrlich, Lichte leichte Luft, goldgestreifte, So gute Luft nur je Vom Monde herabfiel— Sei es aus Zufall, Oder geschah es aus Übermuthe? Wie die alten Dichter erzählen. Ich Zweifler aber ziehe es In Zweifel, dafür aber komme ich Aus Europa, Das zweifelsüchtiger ist als alle Ältlichen Eheweibchen. Möge Gott es bessern! Amen!\n\nDiese schönste Luft trinkend, Mit Nüstern geschwellt gleich Bechern, Ohne Zukunft, ohne Erinnerungen, So sitze ich hier, ihr Allerliebsten Freundinnen, Und sehe der Palme zu, Wie sie, einer Tänzerin gleich, Sich biegt und schmiegt und in der Hüfte wiegt, —man thut es mit, sieht man lange zu! Einer Tänzerin gleich, die, wie mir scheinen will, Zu lange schon, gefährlich lange Immer, immer nur auf Einem Beine stand? —da vergass sie darob, wie mir scheinen will, Das andre Beinchen? Vergebens wenigstens Suchte ich das vermisste Zwillings-Kleinod —nämlich das andre Bein— In der heiligen Nähe Ihres allerliebsten, allerzierlichsten Fächer- und Flatter- und Flitterröckchens. ja, wenn ihr mir, ihr schönen Freundinnen, Ganz glauben wollt: Sie hat es verloren! Es ist dahin! Auf ewig dahin! Das andre Bein! Oh schade um dieses liebliche andre Bein! Wo—mag es wohl weilen und verlassen trauern? Das einsame Bein? In Furcht vielleicht vor einem Grimmen gelben blondgelockten Löwen-Unthiere? Oder gar schon Abgenagt, abgeknabbert— Erbärmlich, wehe! wehe! abgeknabbert! Sela.\n\nOh weint mir nicht, Weiche Herzen! Weint mir nicht, ihr Dattel-Herzen! Milch-Busen! Ihr Süssholz-Herz- Beutelchen! Weine nicht mehr, Bleiche Dudu! Sei ein Mann, Suleika! Muth! Muth! —Oder sollte vielleicht Etwas Stärkendes, Herz-Stärkendes, Hier am Platze sein? Ein gesalbter Spruch? Ein feierlicher Zuspruch?—\n\nHa! Herauf, Würde! Tugend-Würde! Europäer-Würde! Blase, blase wieder, Blasebalg der Tugend! Ha! Noch Ein Mal brüllen, Moralisch brüllen! Als moralischer Löwe Vor den Töchtern der Wüste brüllen! —Denn Tugend-Geheul, Ihr allerliebsten Mädchen, Ist mehr als Alles Europäer-Inbrunst, Europäer-Heisshunger! Und da stehe ich schon, Als Europäer, Ich kann nicht anders, Gott helfe mir! Amen!\n\nDie Wüste wächst: weh Dem, der Wüsten birgt!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0002","text":"Die Wüste wächst: weh Dem, der Wüsten birgt!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0003","text":"—Ha! Feierlich! In der That feierlich! Ein würdiger Anfang! Afrikanisch feierlich! Eines Löwen würdig, Oder eines moralischen Brüllaffen— —aber Nichts für euch, Ihr allerliebsten Freundinnen, Zu deren Füssen mir Zum ersten Male, Einem Europäer, unter Palmen Zu sitzen vergönnt ist. Sela.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0004","text":"Wunderbar wahrlich! Da sitze ich nun, Der Wüste nahe und bereits So fern wieder der Wüste, Auch in Nichts noch verwüstet: Nämlich hinabgeschluckt Von dieser kleinsten Oasis—: —sie sperrte gerade gähnend Ihr liebliches Maul auf. Das wohlriechendste aller Mäulchen: Da fiel ich hinein, Hinab, hindurch—unter euch, Ihr allerliebsten Freundinnen! Sela.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0005","text":"Heil, Heil jenem Wallfische, Wenn er also es seinem Gaste Wohl sein liess!—ihr versteht Meine gelehrte Anspielung? Heil seinem Bauche, Wenn er also Ein so lieblicher Oasis-Bauch war Gleich diesem: was ich aber in Zweifel ziehe, —dafür komme ich aus Europa, Das zweifelsüchtiger ist als alle Ältlichen Eheweibchen. Möge Gott es bessern! Amen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0006","text":"Da sitze ich nun, In dieser kleinsten Oasis, Einer Dattel gleich, Braun, durchsüsst, goldschwürig, lüstern Nach einem runden Mädchenmunde, Mehr noch aber nach mädchenhaften Eiskalten schneeweissen schneidigen Beisszähnen: nach denen nämlich Lechzt das Herz allen heissen Datteln. Sela.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0007","text":"Den genannten Südfrüchten Ähnlich, allzuähnlich Liege ich hier, von kleinen Flügelkäfern Umtänzelt und umspielt, Insgleichen von noch kleineren Thörichteren boshafteren Wünschen und Einfällen, Umlagert von euch, Ihr stummen, ihr ahnungsvollen Mädchen-Katzen, Dudu und Suleika, — umsphinxt , dass ich in Ein Wort Viel Gefühle stopfe: (Vergebe mir Gott Diese Sprach-Sünde!) —sitze hier, die beste Luft schnüffelnd, Paradieses-Luft wahrlich, Lichte leichte Luft, goldgestreifte, So gute Luft nur je Vom Monde herabfiel— Sei es aus Zufall, Oder geschah es aus Übermuthe? Wie die alten Dichter erzählen. Ich Zweifler aber ziehe es In Zweifel, dafür aber komme ich Aus Europa, Das zweifelsüchtiger ist als alle Ältlichen Eheweibchen. Möge Gott es bessern! Amen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0008","text":"Diese schönste Luft trinkend, Mit Nüstern geschwellt gleich Bechern, Ohne Zukunft, ohne Erinnerungen, So sitze ich hier, ihr Allerliebsten Freundinnen, Und sehe der Palme zu, Wie sie, einer Tänzerin gleich, Sich biegt und schmiegt und in der Hüfte wiegt, —man thut es mit, sieht man lange zu! Einer Tänzerin gleich, die, wie mir scheinen will, Zu lange schon, gefährlich lange Immer, immer nur auf Einem Beine stand? —da vergass sie darob, wie mir scheinen will, Das andre Beinchen? Vergebens wenigstens Suchte ich das vermisste Zwillings-Kleinod —nämlich das andre Bein— In der heiligen Nähe Ihres allerliebsten, allerzierlichsten Fächer- und Flatter- und Flitterröckchens. ja, wenn ihr mir, ihr schönen Freundinnen, Ganz glauben wollt: Sie hat es verloren! Es ist dahin! Auf ewig dahin! Das andre Bein! Oh schade um dieses liebliche andre Bein! Wo—mag es wohl weilen und verlassen trauern?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0009","text":"Das einsame Bein? In Furcht vielleicht vor einem Grimmen gelben blondgelockten Löwen-Unthiere? Oder gar schon Abgenagt, abgeknabbert— Erbärmlich, wehe! wehe! abgeknabbert! Sela.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0010","text":"Oh weint mir nicht, Weiche Herzen! Weint mir nicht, ihr Dattel-Herzen! Milch-Busen! Ihr Süssholz-Herz- Beutelchen! Weine nicht mehr, Bleiche Dudu! Sei ein Mann, Suleika! Muth! Muth! —Oder sollte vielleicht Etwas Stärkendes, Herz-Stärkendes, Hier am Platze sein? Ein gesalbter Spruch? Ein feierlicher Zuspruch?—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0011","text":"Ha! Herauf, Würde! Tugend-Würde! Europäer-Würde! Blase, blase wieder, Blasebalg der Tugend! Ha! Noch Ein Mal brüllen, Moralisch brüllen! Als moralischer Löwe Vor den Töchtern der Wüste brüllen! —Denn Tugend-Geheul, Ihr allerliebsten Mädchen, Ist mehr als Alles Europäer-Inbrunst, Europäer-Heisshunger! Und da stehe ich schon, Als Europäer, Ich kann nicht anders, Gott helfe mir! Amen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0012","text":"Die Wüste wächst: weh Dem, der Wüsten birgt!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"0d8c405c95fd8413dee7ff8317addc49112e2e1a6bff7eca854de94211da483c","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184102428613Z_b2a3d875","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2.","source_sha256":"0d8c405c95fd8413dee7ff8317addc49112e2e1a6bff7eca854de94211da483c","sha256":"f69a4d324b2c962272cda72776ff5ce1c27e9f7d7fdf1733f17b12632757e86b","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:41:02.428623+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_16:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0002","text":"The desert grows: woe unto him who harbours deserts!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_16:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0003","text":"—Ha! Solemn! Truly solemn! A worthy beginning! Africanly solemn! Worthy of a lion, Or of a moral howler monkey— —but nothing for you, You dearest friends, At whose feet I am granted, For the first time, as a European, To sit beneath palms. Selah.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_16:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0004","text":"Wonderful indeed! Here I now sit, Near the desert and already So far again from the desert, Not yet devastated in anything: Namely swallowed down By this tiniest oasis—: —it had just opened wide its lovely mouth in a yawn. The most fragrant of all little mouths: Into it I fell, Down, through—among you, You dearest friends! Selah.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_16:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0005","text":"Hail, hail to that whale, If he thus made it well for his guest!—you understand My learned allusion? Hail to his belly, If it was thus Such a lovely oasis-belly As this one: which, however, I doubt, —for I come from Europe, Which is more addicted to doubt than all elderly old wives. May God improve it! Amen!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_16:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0006","text":"Here I now sit, In this tiniest oasis, Like a date, Brown, sugar-sweet, gold-blistered, lusting After a round maiden's mouth, More still, however, after maidenly Ice-cold, snow-white, cutting biting teeth: for those indeed The heart of all hot dates Thirsts. Selah.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_16:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0007","text":"Like those named southern fruits, All too similar, I lie here, Danced and played around by little winged beetles, Likewise besieged by still smaller, More foolish, more malicious wishes and notions, Beset by you, You silent, you prescient girl-cats, Dudu and Suleika, —sphinxed about, so that I stuff Many feelings into one word: (May God forgive me This linguistic sin!) —I sit here, sniffing the best air, Paradise-air indeed, Bright, light air, gold-striped, Air so good only ever Fell down from the moon— Be it by chance, Or did it happen from high spirits? As the ancient poets relate. But I, the doubter, draw it Into doubt; for I come From Europe, Which is more addicted to doubt than all Elderly old wives. May God improve it! Amen!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_16:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0008","text":"Drinking this most beautiful air, With nostrils swollen like Goblets, Without future, without memories, Thus I sit here, you dearest friends, And watch the palm, How, like a dancer, It bends and nestles and sways at the hip, —one does it along with her, if one watches long enough! Like a dancer who, as it seems to me, Has for too long already, dangerously long, Always, always stood only upon one leg? —did she thereby forget, as it seems to me, The other little leg? At least in vain I sought the missing twin-treasure —namely the other leg— In the sacred proximity Of its dearest, most dainty Little fan-and-flutter-and-flitter-skirt. Yes, if you, you beautiful friends, Will wholly believe me: She has lost it! It is gone! Gone for ever! The other leg! Oh, what a pity for this lovely other leg! Where—may it be lingering and grieving in abandonment?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_16:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0009","text":"The lonely leg? Perhaps in fear of a grim Yellow, blond-curled lion-monster? Or perhaps already Gnawed off, nibbled away— Pitiably, alas! alas! nibbled away! Selah.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_16:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0010","text":"Oh, do not weep for me, Soft hearts! Do not weep for me, you date-hearts! Milk-breasts! You liquorice-heart Little pouches! Weep no more, Pale Dudu! Be a man, Suleika! Courage! Courage! —Or should perhaps Something strengthening, heart-strengthening, Be in place here? An anointed saying? A solemn exhortation?—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_16:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0011","text":"Ha! Up, dignity! Virtue-dignity! European-dignity! Blow, blow again, Bellows of virtue! Ha! Roar once more, Roar morally! As a moral lion Roar before the daughters of the desert! —For virtue-howling, You dearest girls, Is more than everything: European fervour, European hot hunger! And already I stand here, As a European, I can do no other, God help me! Amen!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_16:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_16:P0012","text":"The desert grows: woe unto him who harbours deserts!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_16:P0012"]}],"source_digest":"45deb5f12eba645ebf5cd5fa77c9dba55460e3ec6b6d6ad5d96cdd7b17908330","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"Among Daughters of the Desert","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"Among Daughters of the Desert · 2","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","Among Daughters of the Desert","2."],"original_title":"2."}],"source_title_context":"Unter Töchtern der Wüste","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Unter Töchtern der Wüste · 2","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Unter Töchtern der Wüste","2."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"2.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17","title":"1.","order":166,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_1_17","text":"## 1.\n\nNach dem Liede des Wanderers und Schattens wurde die Höhle mit Einem Male voll Lärmens und Lachens; und da die versammelten Gäste alle zugleich redeten, und auch der Esel, bei einer solchen Ermuthigung, nicht mehr still blieb, überkam Zarathustra ein kleiner Widerwille und Spott gegen seinen Besuch: ob er sich gleich ihrer Fröhlichkeit erfreute. Denn sie dünkte ihm ein Zeichen der Genesung. So schlüpfte er hinaus in’s Freie und sprach zu seinen Thieren.\n\n„Wo ist nun ihre Noth hin? sprach er, und schon athmete er selber von seinem kleinen Überdrusse auf,—bei mir verlernten sie, wie mich dünkt, das Nothschrein!\n\n—wenn auch, leider, noch nicht das Schrein.“ Und Zarathustra hielt sich die Ohren zu, denn eben mischte sich das I-A des Esels wunderlich mit dem Jubel-Lärm dieser höheren Menschen.\n\n„Sie sind lustig, begann er wieder, und wer weiss? vielleicht auf ihres Wirthes Unkosten; und lernten sie von mir lachen, so ist es doch nicht mein Lachen, das sie lernten.\n\nAber was liegt daran! Es sind alte Leute: sie genesen auf ihre Art, sie lachen auf ihre Art; meine Ohren haben schon Schlimmeres erduldet und wurden nicht unwirsch.\n\nDieser Tag ist ein Sieg: er weicht schon, er flieht, der Geist der Schwere , mein alter Erzfeind! Wie gut will dieser Tag enden, der so schlimm und schwer begann!\n\nUnd enden will er. Schon kommt der Abend: über das Meer her reitet er, der gute Reiter! Wie er sich wiegt, der Selige, Heimkehrende, in seinen purpurnen Sätteln!\n\nDer Himmel blickt klar dazu, die Welt liegt tief: oh all ihr Wunderlichen, die ihr zu mir kamt, es lohnt sich schon, bei mir zu leben!“\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra. Und wieder kam da das Geschrei und Gelächter der höheren Menschen aus der Höhle: da begann er von Neuem.\n\n„Sie beissen an, mein Köder wirkt, es weicht auch ihnen ihr Feind, der Geist der Schwere. Schon lernen sie über sich selber lachen: höre ich recht?\n\nMeine Manns-Kost wirkt, mein Saft- und Kraft-Spruch: und wahrlich, ich nährte sie nicht mit Bläh-Gemüsen! Sondern mit Krieger-Kost, mit Eroberer-Kost: neue Begierden weckte ich.\n\nNeue Hoffnungen sind in ihren Armen und Beinen, ihr Herz streckt sich aus. Sie finden neue Worte, bald wird ihr Geist Muthwillen athmen.\n\nSolche Kost mag freilich nicht für Kinder sein, noch auch für sehnsüchtige alte und junge Weibchen. Denen überredet man anders die Eingeweide; deren Arzt und Lehrer bin ich nicht.\n\nDer Ekel weicht diesen höheren Menschen: wohlan! das ist mein Sieg. In meinem Reiche werden sie sicher, alle dumme Scham läuft davon, sie schütten sich aus.\n\nSie schütten ihr Herz aus, gute Stunden kehren ihnen zurück, sie feiern und käuen wieder,—sie werden dankbar .\n\nDas nehme ich als das beste Zeichen: sie werden dankbar. Nicht lange noch, und sie denken sich Feste aus und stellen Denksteine ihren alten Freuden auf.\n\nEs sind Genesende !“ Also sprach Zarathustra fröhlich zu seinem Herzen und schaute hinaus; seine Thiere aber drängten sich an ihn und ehrten sein Glück und sein Stillschweigen.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0002","text":"Nach dem Liede des Wanderers und Schattens wurde die Höhle mit Einem Male voll Lärmens und Lachens; und da die versammelten Gäste alle zugleich redeten, und auch der Esel, bei einer solchen Ermuthigung, nicht mehr still blieb, überkam Zarathustra ein kleiner Widerwille und Spott gegen seinen Besuch: ob er sich gleich ihrer Fröhlichkeit erfreute. Denn sie dünkte ihm ein Zeichen der Genesung. So schlüpfte er hinaus in’s Freie und sprach zu seinen Thieren.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0003","text":"„Wo ist nun ihre Noth hin? sprach er, und schon athmete er selber von seinem kleinen Überdrusse auf,—bei mir verlernten sie, wie mich dünkt, das Nothschrein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0004","text":"—wenn auch, leider, noch nicht das Schrein.“ Und Zarathustra hielt sich die Ohren zu, denn eben mischte sich das I-A des Esels wunderlich mit dem Jubel-Lärm dieser höheren Menschen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0005","text":"„Sie sind lustig, begann er wieder, und wer weiss? vielleicht auf ihres Wirthes Unkosten; und lernten sie von mir lachen, so ist es doch nicht mein Lachen, das sie lernten.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0006","text":"Aber was liegt daran! Es sind alte Leute: sie genesen auf ihre Art, sie lachen auf ihre Art; meine Ohren haben schon Schlimmeres erduldet und wurden nicht unwirsch.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0007","text":"Dieser Tag ist ein Sieg: er weicht schon, er flieht, der Geist der Schwere , mein alter Erzfeind! Wie gut will dieser Tag enden, der so schlimm und schwer begann!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0008","text":"Und enden will er. Schon kommt der Abend: über das Meer her reitet er, der gute Reiter! Wie er sich wiegt, der Selige, Heimkehrende, in seinen purpurnen Sätteln!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0009","text":"Der Himmel blickt klar dazu, die Welt liegt tief: oh all ihr Wunderlichen, die ihr zu mir kamt, es lohnt sich schon, bei mir zu leben!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0010","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra. Und wieder kam da das Geschrei und Gelächter der höheren Menschen aus der Höhle: da begann er von Neuem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0011","text":"„Sie beissen an, mein Köder wirkt, es weicht auch ihnen ihr Feind, der Geist der Schwere. Schon lernen sie über sich selber lachen: höre ich recht?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0012","text":"Meine Manns-Kost wirkt, mein Saft- und Kraft-Spruch: und wahrlich, ich nährte sie nicht mit Bläh-Gemüsen! Sondern mit Krieger-Kost, mit Eroberer-Kost: neue Begierden weckte ich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0013","text":"Neue Hoffnungen sind in ihren Armen und Beinen, ihr Herz streckt sich aus. Sie finden neue Worte, bald wird ihr Geist Muthwillen athmen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0014","text":"Solche Kost mag freilich nicht für Kinder sein, noch auch für sehnsüchtige alte und junge Weibchen. Denen überredet man anders die Eingeweide; deren Arzt und Lehrer bin ich nicht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0015","text":"Der Ekel weicht diesen höheren Menschen: wohlan! das ist mein Sieg. In meinem Reiche werden sie sicher, alle dumme Scham läuft davon, sie schütten sich aus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0016","text":"Sie schütten ihr Herz aus, gute Stunden kehren ihnen zurück, sie feiern und käuen wieder,—sie werden dankbar .","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0017","text":"Das nehme ich als das beste Zeichen: sie werden dankbar. Nicht lange noch, und sie denken sich Feste aus und stellen Denksteine ihren alten Freuden auf.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0018","text":"Es sind Genesende !“ Also sprach Zarathustra fröhlich zu seinem Herzen und schaute hinaus; seine Thiere aber drängten sich an ihn und ehrten sein Glück und sein Stillschweigen.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"ec5b2d8b03be79ab6536cca7c8f1b9d47829738a67624b9e1a77c18aacb7955f","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184114323375Z_31e33896","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1.","source_sha256":"ec5b2d8b03be79ab6536cca7c8f1b9d47829738a67624b9e1a77c18aacb7955f","sha256":"c0f8432c45b3431b1e6855b6ac28068e53ba8d6e159f39538b3f123a61029285","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:41:14.323384+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_17:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0002","text":"After the song of the Wanderer and Shadow, the cave suddenly became full of noise and laughter; and since all the assembled guests were speaking at once, and even the ass, with such encouragement, no longer remained silent, Zarathustra was overcome by a slight aversion and mockery toward his visitors: although he rejoiced in their merriment. For it seemed to him a sign of recovery. Thus he slipped out into the open and spoke to his animals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_17:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0003","text":"“Where, now, has their distress gone?” he said, and he himself already breathed a sigh of relief from his slight surfeit,—“with me they have unlearned, it seems to me, crying out in distress!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_17:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0004","text":"—though, alas, not yet crying out.” And Zarathustra held his ears, for just then the I-A of the ass mingled strangely with the jubilant clamour of these higher men.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_17:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0005","text":"“They are merry,” he began again, “and who knows? perhaps at their host’s expense; and if they learned laughter from me, it is nevertheless not my laughter that they learned.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_17:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0006","text":"But what does it matter! They are old people: they recover in their own way, they laugh in their own way; my ears have endured worse already and did not grow peevish.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_17:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0007","text":"This day is a victory: already he gives way, he flees, the spirit of gravity, my old arch-enemy! How well this day will end, which began so badly and heavily!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_17:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0008","text":"And it will end. Already evening comes: over the sea he rides, the good rider! How he sways, the blessed one, returning home, in his purple saddles!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_17:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0009","text":"The sky looks clear upon it, the world lies deep: oh all you strange ones, who came to me, it is already worth while to live with me!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_17:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0010","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra. And again there came the shouting and laughter of the higher men from the cave: then he began anew.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_17:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0011","text":"“They are biting, my bait is working; their enemy too, the spirit of gravity, is giving way before them. Already they are learning to laugh at themselves: am I hearing rightly?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_17:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0012","text":"My man’s fare is working, my saying of sap and strength: and verily, I did not feed them on flatulent vegetables! Rather on warrior’s fare, conqueror’s fare: I awakened new desires.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_17:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0013","text":"New hopes are in their arms and legs, their heart stretches itself out. They find new words; soon their spirit will breathe high-spiritedness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_17:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0014","text":"Such fare may indeed not be for children, nor for yearning old and young little women either. Their entrails are persuaded otherwise; I am not their physician and teacher.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_17:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0015","text":"Disgust is departing from these higher men: well then! that is my victory. In my realm they become secure, all foolish shame runs away, they pour themselves out.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_17:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0016","text":"They pour out their hearts, good hours return to them, they celebrate and chew the cud again,—they become grateful.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_17:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0017","text":"I take that as the best sign: they become grateful. Not long now, and they will devise festivals and set up memorial stones to their old joys.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_17:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_17:P0018","text":"They are convalescents!” Thus spoke Zarathustra joyfully to his heart and looked outward; but his animals pressed close to him and honoured his happiness and his silence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_17:P0018"]}],"source_digest":"f6e21d512cd44170ca96f79095c0fbd7611d963b1865d13fa9b96b6018d7ab43","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Awakening","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"The Awakening · 1","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","The Awakening","1."],"original_title":"1."}],"source_title_context":"Die Erweckung","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Die Erweckung · 1","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Die Erweckung","1."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"1.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17","title":"2.","order":167,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_2_17","text":"## 2.\n\nPlötzlich aber erschrak das Ohr Zarathustra’s: die Höhle nämlich, welche bisher voller Lärmens und Gelächters war, wurde mit Einem Male todtenstill;—seine Nase aber roch einen wohlriechenden Qualm und Weihrauch, wie von brennenden Pinien-Zapfen.\n\n„Was geschieht? Was treiben sie?“ fragte er sich und schlich zum Eingange heran, dass er seinen Gästen, unvermerkt, zusehn könne. Aber, Wunder über Wunder! was musste er da mit seinen eignen Augen sehn!\n\n„Sie sind Alle wieder fromm geworden, sie beten , sie sind toll!“ —sprach er und verwundene sich über die Maassen. Und, fürwahr!, alle diese höheren Menschen, die zwei Könige, der Papst ausser Dienst, der schlimme Zauberer, der freiwillige Bettler, der Wanderer und Schatten, der alte Wahrsager, der Gewissenhafte des Geistes und der hässlichste Mensch: sie lagen Alle gleich Kindern und gläubigen alten Weibchen auf den Knien und beteten den Esel an. Und eben begann der hässlichste Mensch zu gurgeln und zu schnauben, wie als ob etwas Unaussprechliches aus ihm heraus wolle; als er es aber wirklich bis zu Worten gebracht hatte, siehe, da war es eine fromme seltsame Litanei zur Lobpreisung des angebeteten und angeräucherten Esels. Diese Litanei aber klang also:\n\nAmen! Und Lob und Ehre und Weisheit und Dank und Preis und Stärke sei unserm Gott, von Ewigkeit zu Ewigkeit!\n\n—Der Esel aber schrie dazu I-A.\n\nEr trägt unsre Last, er nahm Knechtsgestalt an, er ist geduldsam von Herzen und redet niemals Nein; und wer seinen Gott liebt, der züchtigt ihn.\n\n—Der Esel aber schrie dazu I-A.\n\nEr redet nicht: es sei denn, dass er zur Welt, die er Schuf, immer Ja sagt: also preist er seine Welt. Seine Schlauheit ist es, die nicht redet: so bekommt er selten Unrecht.\n\n—Der Esel aber schrie dazu I-A.\n\nUnscheinbar geht er durch die Welt. Grau ist die Leib-Farbe, in welche er seine Tugend hüllt. Hat er Geist, so verbirgt er ihn; Jedermann aber glaubt an seine langen Ohren.\n\n—Der Esel aber schrie dazu I-A.\n\nWelche verborgene Weisheit ist das, dass er lange Ohren trägt und allein ja und nimmer Nein sagt! Hat er nicht die Welt erschaffen nach seinem Bilde, nämlich so dumm als möglich?\n\n—Der Esel aber schrie dazu I-A.\n\nDu gehst gerade und krumme Wege; es kümmert dich wenig, was uns Menschen gerade oder krumm dünkt. Jenseits von Gut und Böse ist dein Reich. Es ist deine Unschuld, nicht zu wissen, was Unschuld ist.\n\n—Der Esel aber schrie dazu I-A.\n\nSiehe doch, wie du Niemanden von dir stössest, die Bettler nicht, noch die Könige. Die Kindlein lässest du zu dir kommen, und wenn dich die bösen Buben locken, so sprichst du einfältiglich I-A.\n\n—Der Esel aber schrie dazu I-A.\n\nDu liebst Eselinnen und frische Feigen, du bist kein Kostverächter. Eine Distel kitzelt dir das Herz, wenn du gerade Hunger hast. Darin liegt eines Gottes Weisheit.\n\n—Der Esel aber schrie dazu I-A.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0002","text":"Plötzlich aber erschrak das Ohr Zarathustra’s: die Höhle nämlich, welche bisher voller Lärmens und Gelächters war, wurde mit Einem Male todtenstill;—seine Nase aber roch einen wohlriechenden Qualm und Weihrauch, wie von brennenden Pinien-Zapfen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0003","text":"„Was geschieht? Was treiben sie?“ fragte er sich und schlich zum Eingange heran, dass er seinen Gästen, unvermerkt, zusehn könne. Aber, Wunder über Wunder! was musste er da mit seinen eignen Augen sehn!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0004","text":"„Sie sind Alle wieder fromm geworden, sie beten , sie sind toll!“ —sprach er und verwundene sich über die Maassen. Und, fürwahr!, alle diese höheren Menschen, die zwei Könige, der Papst ausser Dienst, der schlimme Zauberer, der freiwillige Bettler, der Wanderer und Schatten, der alte Wahrsager, der Gewissenhafte des Geistes und der hässlichste Mensch: sie lagen Alle gleich Kindern und gläubigen alten Weibchen auf den Knien und beteten den Esel an. Und eben begann der hässlichste Mensch zu gurgeln und zu schnauben, wie als ob etwas Unaussprechliches aus ihm heraus wolle; als er es aber wirklich bis zu Worten gebracht hatte, siehe, da war es eine fromme seltsame Litanei zur Lobpreisung des angebeteten und angeräucherten Esels. Diese Litanei aber klang also:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0005","text":"Amen! Und Lob und Ehre und Weisheit und Dank und Preis und Stärke sei unserm Gott, von Ewigkeit zu Ewigkeit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0006","text":"—Der Esel aber schrie dazu I-A.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0007","text":"Er trägt unsre Last, er nahm Knechtsgestalt an, er ist geduldsam von Herzen und redet niemals Nein; und wer seinen Gott liebt, der züchtigt ihn.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0008","text":"—Der Esel aber schrie dazu I-A.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0009","text":"Er redet nicht: es sei denn, dass er zur Welt, die er Schuf, immer Ja sagt: also preist er seine Welt. Seine Schlauheit ist es, die nicht redet: so bekommt er selten Unrecht.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0010","text":"—Der Esel aber schrie dazu I-A.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0011","text":"Unscheinbar geht er durch die Welt. Grau ist die Leib-Farbe, in welche er seine Tugend hüllt. Hat er Geist, so verbirgt er ihn; Jedermann aber glaubt an seine langen Ohren.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0012","text":"—Der Esel aber schrie dazu I-A.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0013","text":"Welche verborgene Weisheit ist das, dass er lange Ohren trägt und allein ja und nimmer Nein sagt! Hat er nicht die Welt erschaffen nach seinem Bilde, nämlich so dumm als möglich?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0014","text":"—Der Esel aber schrie dazu I-A.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0015","text":"Du gehst gerade und krumme Wege; es kümmert dich wenig, was uns Menschen gerade oder krumm dünkt. Jenseits von Gut und Böse ist dein Reich. Es ist deine Unschuld, nicht zu wissen, was Unschuld ist.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0016","text":"—Der Esel aber schrie dazu I-A.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0017","text":"Siehe doch, wie du Niemanden von dir stössest, die Bettler nicht, noch die Könige. Die Kindlein lässest du zu dir kommen, und wenn dich die bösen Buben locken, so sprichst du einfältiglich I-A.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0018","text":"—Der Esel aber schrie dazu I-A.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0019","text":"Du liebst Eselinnen und frische Feigen, du bist kein Kostverächter. Eine Distel kitzelt dir das Herz, wenn du gerade Hunger hast. Darin liegt eines Gottes Weisheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0020","text":"—Der Esel aber schrie dazu I-A.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"a937aff7f364e936b93265430bdf206959b7e39fa0525074fc084a18c6754dca","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184124195829Z_3f410fc9","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2.","source_sha256":"a937aff7f364e936b93265430bdf206959b7e39fa0525074fc084a18c6754dca","sha256":"13bfc0404fe573c66ac72e67010ac0a0f63c57ebd8e9e814dd5919f634289471","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:41:24.195839+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_17:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0002","text":"But suddenly Zarathustra’s ear was startled: for the cave, which until then had been full of noise and laughter, became deathly still all at once;—but his nose smelled a fragrant smoke and incense, as from burning pine-cones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_17:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0003","text":"“What is happening? What are they doing?” he asked himself and stole up to the entrance, so that he might watch his guests unobserved. But, wonder upon wonder! what did he have to see there with his own eyes!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_17:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0004","text":"“They have all become pious again, they are praying, they are mad!”—he said and marvelled beyond measure. And, indeed!, all these higher men, the two kings, the pope out of service, the wicked magician, the voluntary beggar, the wanderer and shadow, the old soothsayer, the conscientious one of the spirit, and the ugliest man: they all lay on their knees like children and believing old women and worshipped the ass. And just then the ugliest man began to gurgle and snort, as though something unutterable wished to come out of him; but when he had actually managed to bring it into words, behold, it was a pious, strange litany in praise of the worshipped and incense-bedecked ass. This litany, however, sounded thus:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_17:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0005","text":"Amen! And praise and honour and wisdom and thanks and glory and strength be unto our God, from eternity to eternity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_17:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0006","text":"—But the ass cried thereto I-A.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_17:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0007","text":"He bears our burden, he took the form of a servant, he is patient at heart and never says No; and whoever loves his God chastises him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_17:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0008","text":"—But the ass cried thereto I-A.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_17:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0009","text":"He does not speak: unless it be that he always says Yes to the world which he Created: thus he praises his world. It is his cunning that he does not speak: thus he is seldom in the wrong.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_17:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0010","text":"—But the ass cried thereto I-A.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_17:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0011","text":"Unassuming, he goes through the world. Grey is the colour of the body in which he wraps his virtue. If he has spirit, he conceals it; but everyone believes in his long ears.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_17:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0012","text":"—But the ass cried thereto I-A.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_17:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0013","text":"What hidden wisdom is this, that he wears long ears and says only yes and never no! Has he not created the world in his own image, namely as stupid as possible?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_17:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0014","text":"—But the ass cried thereto I-A.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_17:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0015","text":"You go straight and crooked paths; it concerns you little what seems straight or crooked to us human beings. Beyond good and evil is your kingdom. It is your innocence not to know what innocence is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_17:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0016","text":"—But the ass cried thereto I-A.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_17:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0017","text":"See, how you thrust no one away from you, neither beggars nor kings. You let the little children come unto you, and when the wicked boys entice you, you simply say I-A.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_17:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0018","text":"—But the ass cried thereto I-A.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_17:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0019","text":"You love she-asses and fresh figs, you are not a despiser of food. A thistle tickles your heart when you are just hungry. Therein lies a god’s wisdom.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_17:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_17:P0020","text":"—But the ass cried thereto I-A.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_17:P0020"]}],"source_digest":"c76c05307fcad9b7d34728f210d6fc62313231086d02b1c5ad94e8687c3a3bdb","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Awakening","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"The Awakening · 2","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","The Awakening","2."],"original_title":"2."}],"source_title_context":"Die Erweckung","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Die Erweckung · 2","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Die Erweckung","2."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"2.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18","title":"1.","order":168,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_1_18","text":"## 1.\n\nAn dieser Stelle der Litanei aber konnte Zarathustra sich nicht länger bemeistern, schrie selber I-A, lauter noch als der Esel, und sprang mitten unter seine tollgewordenen Gäste.\n\n„Aber was treibt ihr da, ihr Menschenkinder? rief er, indem er die Betenden vom Boden empor riss. Wehe, wenn euch Jemand Anderes zusähe als Zarathustra:\n\nJeder würde urtheilen, ihr wäret mit eurem neuen Glauben die ärgsten Gotteslästerer oder die thörichtsten aller alten Weiblein!\n\nUnd du selber, du alter Papst, wie stimmt Das mit dir selber zusammen, dass du solchergestalt einen Esel hier als Gott anbetest?“—\n\n„Oh Zarathustra, antwortete der Papst, vergieb mir, aber in Dingen Gottes bin ich aufgeklärter noch als du. Und so ist’s billig.\n\nLieber Gott also anbeten, in dieser Gestalt, als in gar keiner Gestalt! Denke über diesen Spruch nach, mein hoher Freund: du erräthst geschwind, in solchem Spruch steckt Weisheit.\n\nDer, welcher sprach „Gott ist ein Geist“—der machte bisher auf Erden den grössten Schritt und Sprung zum Unglauben: solch Wort ist auf Erden nicht leicht wieder gut zu machen!\n\nMein altes Herz springt und hüpft darob, dass es auf Erden noch Etwas anzubeten giebt. Vergieb das, oh Zarathustra, einem alten frommen Papst-Herzen!—„\n\n—„Und du, sagte Zarathustra zu dem Wanderer und Schatten, du nennst und wähnst dich einen freien Geist? Und treibst hier solchen Götzen- und Pfaffendienst?\n\nSchlimmer, wahrlich, treibst du’s hier noch als bei deinen schlimmen braunen Mädchen, du schlimmer neuer Gläubiger!“\n\n„Schlimm genug, antwortete der Wanderer und Schatten, du hast Recht: aber was kann ich dafür! Der alte Gott lebt wieder, Oh Zarathustra, du magst reden, was du willst.\n\nDer hässlichste Mensch ist an Allem schuld: der hat ihn wieder auferweckt. Und wenn er sagt, dass er ihn einst getödtet habe: Tod ist bei Göttern immer nur ein Vorurtheil.“\n\n—Und du, sprach Zarathustra, du schlimmer alter Zauberer, was thatest du! Wer soll, in dieser freien Zeit, fürderhin an dich glauben, wenn du an solche Götter-Eseleien glaubst?\n\nEs war eine Dummheit, was du thatest; wie konntest du, du Kluger, eine solche Dummheit thun!\n\n„Oh Zarathustra, antwortete der kluge Zauberer, du hast Recht, es war eine Dummheit,—es ist mir auch schwer genug geworden.“\n\n—„Und du gar, sagte Zarathustra, zu dem Gewissenhaften des Geistes, erwäge doch und lege den Finger an deine Nase! Geht hier denn Nichts wider dein Gewissen? Ist dein Geist nicht zu reinlich für diess Beten und den Dunst dieser Betbrüder?“\n\n„Es ist Etwas daran, antwortete der Gewissenhafte und legte den Finger an die Nase, es ist Etwas an diesem Schauspiele, das meinem Gewissen sogar wohlthut.\n\nVielleicht, dass ich an Gott nicht glauben darf: gewiss aber ist, dass Gott mir in dieser Gestalt noch am glaubwürdigsten dünkt.\n\nGott soll ewig sein, nach dem Zeugnisse der Frömmsten: wer so viel Zeit hat, lässt sich Zeit. So langsam und so dumm als möglich: damit kann ein Solcher es doch sehr weit bringen.\n\nUnd wer des Geistes zu viel hat, der möchte sich wohl in die Dumm- und Narrheit selber vernarren. Denke über dich selber nach, oh Zarathustra!\n\nDu selber—wahrlich! auch du könntest wohl aus Überfluss und Weisheit zu einem Esel werden.\n\nGeht nicht ein vollkommner Weiser gern auf den krümmsten Wegen? Der Augenschein lehrt es, oh Zarathustra,— dein Augenschein!“\n\n—„Und du selber zuletzt, sprach Zarathustra und wandte sich gegen den hässlichsten Menschen, der immer noch auf dem Boden lag, den Arm zu dem Esel emporhebend (er gab ihm nämlich Wein zu trinken). Sprich, du Unaussprechlicher, was hast du da gemacht!\n\nDu dünkst mich verwandelt, dein Auge glüht, der Mantel des Erhabenen liegt um deine Hässlichkeit: was thatest du?\n\nIst es denn wahr, was jene sagen, dass du ihn wieder auferwecktest? Und wozu? War er nicht mit Grund abgetödtet und abgethan?\n\nDu selber dünkst mich aufgeweckt: was thatest du? was kehrtest du um? Was bekehrtest du dich? Sprich, du Unaussprechlicher?“\n\n„Oh Zarathustra, antwortete der hässlichste Mensch, du bist ein Schelm!\n\nOb Der noch lebt oder wieder lebt oder gründlich todt ist,—wer von uns Beiden weiss Das am Besten? Ich frage dich.\n\nEins aber weiss ich,—von dir selber lernte ich’s einst, oh Zarathustra: wer am gründlichsten tödten will, der lacht .\n\n„Nicht durch Zorn, sondern durch Lachen tödtet man“—so sprachst du einst. Oh Zarathustra, du Verborgener, du Vernichter ohne Zorn, du gefährlicher Heiliger, - du bist ein Schelm!“","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0002","text":"An dieser Stelle der Litanei aber konnte Zarathustra sich nicht länger bemeistern, schrie selber I-A, lauter noch als der Esel, und sprang mitten unter seine tollgewordenen Gäste.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0003","text":"„Aber was treibt ihr da, ihr Menschenkinder? rief er, indem er die Betenden vom Boden empor riss. Wehe, wenn euch Jemand Anderes zusähe als Zarathustra:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0004","text":"Jeder würde urtheilen, ihr wäret mit eurem neuen Glauben die ärgsten Gotteslästerer oder die thörichtsten aller alten Weiblein!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0005","text":"Und du selber, du alter Papst, wie stimmt Das mit dir selber zusammen, dass du solchergestalt einen Esel hier als Gott anbetest?“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0006","text":"„Oh Zarathustra, antwortete der Papst, vergieb mir, aber in Dingen Gottes bin ich aufgeklärter noch als du. Und so ist’s billig.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0007","text":"Lieber Gott also anbeten, in dieser Gestalt, als in gar keiner Gestalt! Denke über diesen Spruch nach, mein hoher Freund: du erräthst geschwind, in solchem Spruch steckt Weisheit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0008","text":"Der, welcher sprach „Gott ist ein Geist“—der machte bisher auf Erden den grössten Schritt und Sprung zum Unglauben: solch Wort ist auf Erden nicht leicht wieder gut zu machen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0009","text":"Mein altes Herz springt und hüpft darob, dass es auf Erden noch Etwas anzubeten giebt. Vergieb das, oh Zarathustra, einem alten frommen Papst-Herzen!—„","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0010","text":"—„Und du, sagte Zarathustra zu dem Wanderer und Schatten, du nennst und wähnst dich einen freien Geist? Und treibst hier solchen Götzen- und Pfaffendienst?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0011","text":"Schlimmer, wahrlich, treibst du’s hier noch als bei deinen schlimmen braunen Mädchen, du schlimmer neuer Gläubiger!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0012","text":"„Schlimm genug, antwortete der Wanderer und Schatten, du hast Recht: aber was kann ich dafür! Der alte Gott lebt wieder, Oh Zarathustra, du magst reden, was du willst.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0013","text":"Der hässlichste Mensch ist an Allem schuld: der hat ihn wieder auferweckt. Und wenn er sagt, dass er ihn einst getödtet habe: Tod ist bei Göttern immer nur ein Vorurtheil.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0014","text":"—Und du, sprach Zarathustra, du schlimmer alter Zauberer, was thatest du! Wer soll, in dieser freien Zeit, fürderhin an dich glauben, wenn du an solche Götter-Eseleien glaubst?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0015","text":"Es war eine Dummheit, was du thatest; wie konntest du, du Kluger, eine solche Dummheit thun!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0016","text":"„Oh Zarathustra, antwortete der kluge Zauberer, du hast Recht, es war eine Dummheit,—es ist mir auch schwer genug geworden.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0017","text":"—„Und du gar, sagte Zarathustra, zu dem Gewissenhaften des Geistes, erwäge doch und lege den Finger an deine Nase! Geht hier denn Nichts wider dein Gewissen? Ist dein Geist nicht zu reinlich für diess Beten und den Dunst dieser Betbrüder?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0018","text":"„Es ist Etwas daran, antwortete der Gewissenhafte und legte den Finger an die Nase, es ist Etwas an diesem Schauspiele, das meinem Gewissen sogar wohlthut.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0019","text":"Vielleicht, dass ich an Gott nicht glauben darf: gewiss aber ist, dass Gott mir in dieser Gestalt noch am glaubwürdigsten dünkt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0020","text":"Gott soll ewig sein, nach dem Zeugnisse der Frömmsten: wer so viel Zeit hat, lässt sich Zeit. So langsam und so dumm als möglich: damit kann ein Solcher es doch sehr weit bringen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0021","text":"Und wer des Geistes zu viel hat, der möchte sich wohl in die Dumm- und Narrheit selber vernarren. Denke über dich selber nach, oh Zarathustra!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0022","text":"Du selber—wahrlich! auch du könntest wohl aus Überfluss und Weisheit zu einem Esel werden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0023","text":"Geht nicht ein vollkommner Weiser gern auf den krümmsten Wegen? Der Augenschein lehrt es, oh Zarathustra,— dein Augenschein!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0024","text":"—„Und du selber zuletzt, sprach Zarathustra und wandte sich gegen den hässlichsten Menschen, der immer noch auf dem Boden lag, den Arm zu dem Esel emporhebend (er gab ihm nämlich Wein zu trinken). Sprich, du Unaussprechlicher, was hast du da gemacht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0025","text":"Du dünkst mich verwandelt, dein Auge glüht, der Mantel des Erhabenen liegt um deine Hässlichkeit: was thatest du?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0026","text":"Ist es denn wahr, was jene sagen, dass du ihn wieder auferwecktest? Und wozu? War er nicht mit Grund abgetödtet und abgethan?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0027","text":"Du selber dünkst mich aufgeweckt: was thatest du? was kehrtest du um? Was bekehrtest du dich? Sprich, du Unaussprechlicher?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0028","text":"„Oh Zarathustra, antwortete der hässlichste Mensch, du bist ein Schelm!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0029","text":"Ob Der noch lebt oder wieder lebt oder gründlich todt ist,—wer von uns Beiden weiss Das am Besten? Ich frage dich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0030","text":"Eins aber weiss ich,—von dir selber lernte ich’s einst, oh Zarathustra: wer am gründlichsten tödten will, der lacht .","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0031","text":"„Nicht durch Zorn, sondern durch Lachen tödtet man“—so sprachst du einst. Oh Zarathustra, du Verborgener, du Vernichter ohne Zorn, du gefährlicher Heiliger, - du bist ein Schelm!“","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"6b12768b6a7670ebb74e45f504f21bf66e88236d797cc412b0edc951e4d95255","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184141719896Z_98ff1d33","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1.","source_sha256":"6b12768b6a7670ebb74e45f504f21bf66e88236d797cc412b0edc951e4d95255","sha256":"11ae2ebfa587f651dcc43127fb4b75b790c40a5a6a5023acb4d9b083a0c3736c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:41:41.719927+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0002","text":"But at this point in the litany Zarathustra could no longer restrain himself, himself cried I-A, louder still than the ass, and sprang into the midst of his guests gone mad.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0003","text":"“But what are you doing there, you children of men? he cried, as he tore the praying men up from the ground. Woe, if anyone other than Zarathustra were to see you:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0004","text":"Everyone would judge that, with your new faith, you were the worst blasphemers of God or the most foolish of all old women!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0005","text":"“And you yourself, you old Pope, how does it accord with yourself that you worship an ass here as God in such a manner?”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0006","text":"“Oh Zarathustra, the Pope answered, forgive me, but in matters of God I am more enlightened even than you. And that is proper.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0007","text":"It is better to worship God thus, in this form, than in no form at all! Reflect upon this saying, my lofty friend: you will quickly guess that wisdom lies in such a saying.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0008","text":"He who said ‘God is a spirit’—he has hitherto made the greatest step and leap toward unbelief on earth: such a word is not easily made good again on earth!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0009","text":"My old heart leaps and skips for joy that there is still Something to worship on earth. Forgive this, oh Zarathustra, to an old pious Pope-heart!—“","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0010","text":"—“And you, said Zarathustra to the Wanderer and Shadow, you call and imagine yourself a free spirit? And here you practise such idolatry and priestcraft?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0011","text":"Truly, here you behave even worse than with your bad brown girls, you bad new believer!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0012","text":"“Bad enough, answered the Wanderer and Shadow, you are right: but what can I do about it! The old God lives again, O Zarathustra, say what you will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0013","text":"The ugliest man is to blame for everything: he has resurrected him again. And if he says that he once killed him: death is always only a prejudice among gods.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0014","text":"—And you, said Zarathustra, you bad old magician, what did you do! Who, in this free age, is henceforth to believe in you if you believe in such god-ass foolishness?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0015","text":"It was a stupidity, what you did; how could you, you clever one, do such a stupidity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0016","text":"“Oh Zarathustra, answered the clever magician, you are right, it was a stupidity,—it has also cost me dearly enough.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0017","text":"—“And you of all people, said Zarathustra to the Conscientious One of Spirit, consider it and put your finger to your nose! Does nothing here go against your conscience? Is your spirit not too cleanly for this praying and the vapour of these praying brethren?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0018","text":"“There is something in it, answered the Conscientious One and put his finger to his nose, there is something in this spectacle that even does my conscience good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0019","text":"Perhaps I may not believe in God: but it is certain that God seems to me most credible in this form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0020","text":"God is supposed to be eternal, according to the testimony of the most pious: whoever has so much time takes his time. As slowly and as stupidly as possible: thereby such a one can nevertheless get very far.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0021","text":"And whoever has too much spirit might well fool himself into stupidity and folly. Reflect upon yourself, oh Zarathustra!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0022","text":"You yourself—truly! you too could well become an ass out of abundance and wisdom.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0023","text":"Does not a perfect wise man gladly go along the most crooked paths? Appearances teach it, oh Zarathustra,—your appearance!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0024","text":"—“And you yourself lastly, said Zarathustra, turning against the ugliest man, who was still lying on the ground, raising his arm toward the ass (for he was giving it wine to drink). Speak, you Unspeakable One, what have you done there!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0025","text":"You seem transformed to me; your eye glows, the mantle of the sublime lies about your ugliness: what did you do?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0026","text":"Is it then true what those people say, that you awakened him again? And to what end? Was he not, with good reason, killed and done away with?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0027","text":"You yourself seem awakened to me: what did you do? what did you turn around? To what did you convert yourself? Speak, you Unspeakable One!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0028","text":"“O Zarathustra,” replied the ugliest man, “you are a rogue!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0029","text":"Whether That One still lives or lives again or is thoroughly dead,—which of the two of us knows That best? I ask you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0030","text":"But one thing I know,—I myself once learned it from you, O Zarathustra: he who wishes to kill most thoroughly, laughs .","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_18:P0031","text":"“One kills not through anger, but through laughter”—thus you once spoke. O Zarathustra, you Hidden One, you destroyer without anger, you dangerous saint, - you are a rogue!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_18:P0031"]}],"source_digest":"2a4e9cc26a55b49e9d0c99384a551a63470b13908fff44b1ba5b7288695cbd74","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Ass Festival","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"The Ass Festival · 1","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","The Ass Festival","1."],"original_title":"1."}],"source_title_context":"Das Eselsfest","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Das Eselsfest · 1","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Das Eselsfest","1."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"1.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_18","title":"2.","order":169,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_2_18","text":"## 2.\n\nDa aber geschah es, dass Zarathustra, verwundert über lauter solche Schelmen-Antworten, zur Thür seiner Höhle zurück sprang und, gegen alle seine Gäste gewendet, mit starker Stimme schrie:\n\n„Oh ihr Schalks-Narren allesammt, ihr Possenreisser! Was verstellt und versteckt ihr euch vor mir!\n\nWie doch einem jeden von euch das Herz zappelte vor Lust und Bosheit, darob, dass ihr endlich einmal wieder wurdet wie die Kindlein, nämlich fromm,—\n\n—dass ihr endlich wieder thatet wie Kinder thun, nämlich betetet, hände-faltetet und „lieber Gott“ sagtet!\n\nAber nun lasst mir diese Kinderstube, meine eigne Höhle, wo heute alle Kinderei zu Hause ist. Kühlt hier draussen euren heissen Kinder-Übermuth und Herzenslärm ab!\n\nFreilich: so ihr nicht werdet wie die Kindlein, so kommt ihr nicht in das Himmelreich. (Und Zarathustra zeigte mit den Händen nach Oben.)\n\nAber wir wollen auch gar nicht in’s Himmelreich: Männer sind wir worden,—so wollen wir das Erdenreich.“","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_18:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_18:P0002","text":"Da aber geschah es, dass Zarathustra, verwundert über lauter solche Schelmen-Antworten, zur Thür seiner Höhle zurück sprang und, gegen alle seine Gäste gewendet, mit starker Stimme schrie:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_18:P0003","text":"„Oh ihr Schalks-Narren allesammt, ihr Possenreisser! Was verstellt und versteckt ihr euch vor mir!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_18:P0004","text":"Wie doch einem jeden von euch das Herz zappelte vor Lust und Bosheit, darob, dass ihr endlich einmal wieder wurdet wie die Kindlein, nämlich fromm,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_18:P0005","text":"—dass ihr endlich wieder thatet wie Kinder thun, nämlich betetet, hände-faltetet und „lieber Gott“ sagtet!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_18:P0006","text":"Aber nun lasst mir diese Kinderstube, meine eigne Höhle, wo heute alle Kinderei zu Hause ist. Kühlt hier draussen euren heissen Kinder-Übermuth und Herzenslärm ab!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_18:P0007","text":"Freilich: so ihr nicht werdet wie die Kindlein, so kommt ihr nicht in das Himmelreich. (Und Zarathustra zeigte mit den Händen nach Oben.)","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_18:P0008","text":"Aber wir wollen auch gar nicht in’s Himmelreich: Männer sind wir worden,—so wollen wir das Erdenreich.“","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"64318db6d915e8efd963cf6d61fb474aed1f7457f70c542df20091dc9c767375","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184146222707Z_9b0298f2","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2.","source_sha256":"64318db6d915e8efd963cf6d61fb474aed1f7457f70c542df20091dc9c767375","sha256":"af91a12357a2a68bca9b224067656073ffe9c90eb956d19f09419ad8197f8085","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:41:46.222719+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_18:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_18:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_18:P0002","text":"But then it happened that Zarathustra, astonished at nothing but such roguish answers, sprang back to the door of his cave and, turning towards all his guests, cried out in a strong voice:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_18:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_18:P0003","text":"“Oh, all you prankster-fools, you buffoons! Why do you disguise and hide yourselves from me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_18:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_18:P0004","text":"How every one of your hearts fluttered with pleasure and malice at the thought that at last you had once again become like little children, namely pious,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_18:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_18:P0005","text":"—that you at last again did as children do, namely prayed, folded your hands, and said ‘dear God’!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_18:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_18:P0006","text":"But now leave me this nursery, my own cave, where today all childishness is at home. Outside here, cool your hot childish exuberance and clamour of hearts!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_18:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_18:P0007","text":"To be sure: unless you become like little children, you shall not enter the kingdom of heaven. (And Zarathustra pointed upwards with his hands.)","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_18:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_18:P0008","text":"But we do not want to enter the kingdom of heaven at all: we have become men,—so we want the kingdom of earth.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_18:P0008"]}],"source_digest":"b39f8d0efa3b86aa767f73b260efe1d81c24f07318eb121bc634580e23adc4be","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Ass Festival","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"The Ass Festival · 2","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","The Ass Festival","2."],"original_title":"2."}],"source_title_context":"Das Eselsfest","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Das Eselsfest · 2","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Das Eselsfest","2."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"2.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_9","title":"3.","order":170,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_3C_9","text":"## 3.\n\nUnd noch einmal hob Zarathustra an zu reden. „Oh meine neuen Freunde, sprach er,—ihr Wunderlichen, ihr höheren Menschen, wie gut gefallt ihr mir nun,—\n\n—seit ihr wieder fröhlich wurdet! Ihr seid wahrlich Alle aufgeblüht: mich dünkt, solchen Blumen, wie ihr seid, thun neue Feste noth,\n\n—ein kleiner tapferer Unsinn, irgend ein Gottesdienst und Eselsfest, irgend ein alter fröhlicher Zarathustra-Narr, ein Brausewind, der euch die Seelen hell bläst.\n\nVergesst die Nacht und diess Eselsfest nicht, ihr höheren Menschen! Das erfandet ihr bei mir, Das nehme ich als gutes Wahrzeichen,—Solcherlei erfinden nur Genesende!\n\nUnd feiert ihr es abermals, dieses Eselsfest, thut’s euch zu Liebe, thut’s auch mir zu Liebe! Und zu meinem Gedächtniss!“\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_9:P0001","text":"## 3.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_9:P0002","text":"Und noch einmal hob Zarathustra an zu reden. „Oh meine neuen Freunde, sprach er,—ihr Wunderlichen, ihr höheren Menschen, wie gut gefallt ihr mir nun,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_9:P0003","text":"—seit ihr wieder fröhlich wurdet! Ihr seid wahrlich Alle aufgeblüht: mich dünkt, solchen Blumen, wie ihr seid, thun neue Feste noth,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_9:P0004","text":"—ein kleiner tapferer Unsinn, irgend ein Gottesdienst und Eselsfest, irgend ein alter fröhlicher Zarathustra-Narr, ein Brausewind, der euch die Seelen hell bläst.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_9:P0005","text":"Vergesst die Nacht und diess Eselsfest nicht, ihr höheren Menschen! Das erfandet ihr bei mir, Das nehme ich als gutes Wahrzeichen,—Solcherlei erfinden nur Genesende!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_9:P0006","text":"Und feiert ihr es abermals, dieses Eselsfest, thut’s euch zu Liebe, thut’s auch mir zu Liebe! Und zu meinem Gedächtniss!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_9:P0007","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"766468b64b8ab51b8b37328d9b4c281c46c189432d139641e171cdc08b1a2d9a","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184150062821Z_cf38768e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"3.","source_sha256":"766468b64b8ab51b8b37328d9b4c281c46c189432d139641e171cdc08b1a2d9a","sha256":"23bd6b2578d4100764c62244fdc78b863c441b6d315056ccb052f0c982eb5c4e","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:41:50.062841+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_9:P0001","text":"## 3.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_9:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_9:P0002","text":"And once more Zarathustra began to speak. “O my new friends, he said,—you wonderful ones, you higher men, how well you please me now,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_9:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_9:P0003","text":"—since you have become cheerful again! Verily, you have all blossomed: it seems to me that such flowers as you are need new festivals,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_9:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_9:P0004","text":"—a little brave nonsense, some divine service and ass-festival, some old cheerful Zarathustra-fool, a blustering wind that blows your souls bright.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_9:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_9:P0005","text":"Do not forget the night and this ass-festival, you higher men! You invented that here with me; I take it as a good omen,—only convalescents invent such things!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_9:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_9:P0006","text":"And if you celebrate it again, this ass-festival, do it for your own sake, do it also for my sake! And in my memory!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_9:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3C_9:P0007","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3C_9:P0007"]}],"source_digest":"8256b6dae83909c049a1874b7a188ae7696e63b4ee8b2f92f90aca734c4d5a44","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Ass Festival","source_section_label":"3.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"The Ass Festival · 3","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","The Ass Festival","3."],"original_title":"3."}],"source_title_context":"Das Eselsfest","source_section_label":"3.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Das Eselsfest · 3","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Das Eselsfest","3."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"3.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_19","title":"1.","order":171,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_1_19","text":"## 1.\n\nInzwischen aber war Einer nach dem Andern hinaus getreten, in’s Freie und in die kühle nachdenkliche Nacht; Zarathustra selber aber führte den hässlichsten Menschen an der Hand, dass er ihm seine Nacht-Welt und den grossen runden Mond und die silbernen Wasserstürze bei seiner Höhle zeige. Da standen sie endlich still bei einander, lauter alte Leute, aber mit einem getrösteten tapferen Herzen und verwundert bei sich, dass es ihnen auf Erden so wohl war; die Heimlichkeit der Nacht aber kam ihnen näher und näher an’s Herz. Und von Neuem dachte Zarathustra bei sich: „oh wie gut sie mir nun gefallen, diese höheren Menschen!“—aber er sprach es nicht aus, denn er ehrte ihr Glück und ihr Stillschweigen.—\n\nDa aber geschah Das, was an jenem erstaunlichen langen Tage das Erstaunlichste war: der hässlichste Mensch begann noch ein Mal und zum letzten Mal zu gurgeln und zu schnauben, und als er es bis zu Worten gebracht hatte, siehe, da sprang eine Frage rund und reinlich aus seinem Munde, eine gute tiefe klare Frage, welche Allen, die ihm zuhörten, das Herz im Leibe bewegte.\n\n„Meine Freunde insgesammt, sprach der hässlichste Mensch, was dünket euch? Um dieses Tags Willen— ich bin’s zum ersten Male zufrieden, dass ich das ganze Leben lebte.\n\nUnd dass ich so viel bezeuge, ist mir noch nicht genug. Es lohnt sich auf der Erde zu leben: Ein Tag, Ein Fest mit Zarathustra lehrte mich die Erde lieben.\n\n„War Das —das Leben?“ will ich zum Tode sprechen. „Wohlan! Noch Ein Mal!“\n\nMeine Freunde, was dünket euch? Wollt ihr nicht gleich mir zum Tode sprechen: War Das—das Leben? Um Zarathustra’s Willen, wohlan! Noch Ein Mal!“—\n\nAlso sprach der hässlichste Mensch; es war aber nicht lange vor Mitternacht. Und was glaubt ihr wohl, dass damals sich zutrug? Sobald die höheren Menschen seine Frage hörten, wurden sie sich mit Einem Male ihrer Verwandlung und Genesung bewusst, und wer ihnen dieselbe gegeben habe: da sprangen sie auf Zarathustra zu, dankend, verehrend, liebkosend, ihm die Hände küssend, so wie es der Art eines Jeden eigen war: also dass Einige lachten, Einige weinten. Der alte Wahrsager aber tanzte vor Vergnügen; und wenn er auch, wie manche Erzähler meinen, damals voll süssen Weines war, so war er gewisslich noch voller des süssen Lebens und hatte aller Müdigkeit abgesagt. Es giebt sogar Solche, die erzählen, dass damals der Esel getanzt habe: nicht umsonst nämlich habe ihm der hässlichste Mensch vorher Wein zu trinken gegeben. Diess mag sich nun so verhalten oder auch anders; und wenn in Wahrheit an jenem Abende der Esel nicht getanzt hat, so geschahen doch damals grössere und seltsamere Wunderdinge als es das Tanzen eines Esels wäre. Kurz, wie das Sprichwort Zarathustra’s lautet: „was liegt daran!“","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_19:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_19:P0002","text":"Inzwischen aber war Einer nach dem Andern hinaus getreten, in’s Freie und in die kühle nachdenkliche Nacht; Zarathustra selber aber führte den hässlichsten Menschen an der Hand, dass er ihm seine Nacht-Welt und den grossen runden Mond und die silbernen Wasserstürze bei seiner Höhle zeige. Da standen sie endlich still bei einander, lauter alte Leute, aber mit einem getrösteten tapferen Herzen und verwundert bei sich, dass es ihnen auf Erden so wohl war; die Heimlichkeit der Nacht aber kam ihnen näher und näher an’s Herz. Und von Neuem dachte Zarathustra bei sich: „oh wie gut sie mir nun gefallen, diese höheren Menschen!“—aber er sprach es nicht aus, denn er ehrte ihr Glück und ihr Stillschweigen.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_19:P0003","text":"Da aber geschah Das, was an jenem erstaunlichen langen Tage das Erstaunlichste war: der hässlichste Mensch begann noch ein Mal und zum letzten Mal zu gurgeln und zu schnauben, und als er es bis zu Worten gebracht hatte, siehe, da sprang eine Frage rund und reinlich aus seinem Munde, eine gute tiefe klare Frage, welche Allen, die ihm zuhörten, das Herz im Leibe bewegte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_19:P0004","text":"„Meine Freunde insgesammt, sprach der hässlichste Mensch, was dünket euch? Um dieses Tags Willen— ich bin’s zum ersten Male zufrieden, dass ich das ganze Leben lebte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_19:P0005","text":"Und dass ich so viel bezeuge, ist mir noch nicht genug. Es lohnt sich auf der Erde zu leben: Ein Tag, Ein Fest mit Zarathustra lehrte mich die Erde lieben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_19:P0006","text":"„War Das —das Leben?“ will ich zum Tode sprechen. „Wohlan! Noch Ein Mal!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_19:P0007","text":"Meine Freunde, was dünket euch? Wollt ihr nicht gleich mir zum Tode sprechen: War Das—das Leben? Um Zarathustra’s Willen, wohlan! Noch Ein Mal!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_19:P0008","text":"Also sprach der hässlichste Mensch; es war aber nicht lange vor Mitternacht. Und was glaubt ihr wohl, dass damals sich zutrug? Sobald die höheren Menschen seine Frage hörten, wurden sie sich mit Einem Male ihrer Verwandlung und Genesung bewusst, und wer ihnen dieselbe gegeben habe: da sprangen sie auf Zarathustra zu, dankend, verehrend, liebkosend, ihm die Hände küssend, so wie es der Art eines Jeden eigen war: also dass Einige lachten, Einige weinten. Der alte Wahrsager aber tanzte vor Vergnügen; und wenn er auch, wie manche Erzähler meinen, damals voll süssen Weines war, so war er gewisslich noch voller des süssen Lebens und hatte aller Müdigkeit abgesagt. Es giebt sogar Solche, die erzählen, dass damals der Esel getanzt habe: nicht umsonst nämlich habe ihm der hässlichste Mensch vorher Wein zu trinken gegeben.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_19:P0009","text":"Diess mag sich nun so verhalten oder auch anders; und wenn in Wahrheit an jenem Abende der Esel nicht getanzt hat, so geschahen doch damals grössere und seltsamere Wunderdinge als es das Tanzen eines Esels wäre. Kurz, wie das Sprichwort Zarathustra’s lautet: „was liegt daran!“","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"7ece23cad6186c35acc10831aedb2807a9ae826fd80767b1c817945618fdef50","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184157978086Z_eecfa515","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1.","source_sha256":"7ece23cad6186c35acc10831aedb2807a9ae826fd80767b1c817945618fdef50","sha256":"417e62b97273a19063c45a49226eb035bba9920415c9622bcd8d6e8d0892f7ca","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:41:57.978133+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_19:P0001","text":"## 1.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_19:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_19:P0002","text":"Meanwhile, however, one after another had stepped outside, into the open and into the cool, pensive night; Zarathustra himself, however, led the ugliest man by the hand, to show him his night-world and the great round moon and the silver waterfalls near his cave. At last they stood still together, all old men, but with comforted, courageous hearts, and astonished within themselves that they felt so well upon the earth; and the secrecy of the night came ever closer to their hearts. And Zarathustra thought to himself anew: “oh, how well they please me now, these higher men!”—but he did not say it aloud, for he honoured their happiness and their silence.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_19:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_19:P0003","text":"But then there occurred what was most astonishing on that astonishing long day: the ugliest man began once more, and for the last time, to gurgle and snort, and when he had managed to form words, behold, a question sprang round and clean from his mouth, a good, deep, clear question, which stirred the hearts in the bodies of all who listened to him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_19:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_19:P0004","text":"“My friends altogether,” said the ugliest man, “what think you? For the sake of this day—I am for the first time content that I lived my whole life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_19:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_19:P0005","text":"And that I bear witness to so much is still not enough for me. It is worth living upon the earth: one day, one feast with Zarathustra taught me to love the earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_19:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_19:P0006","text":"‘Was that—life?’ I shall say to death. ‘Well then! Once more!’","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_19:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_19:P0007","text":"My friends, what think you? Will you not, like me, say to death: Was that—life? For Zarathustra’s sake, well then! Once more!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_19:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_19:P0008","text":"Thus spoke the ugliest man; but it was not long before midnight. And what do you suppose happened then? As soon as the higher men heard his question, they became aware at once of their transformation and recovery, and of who had bestowed them upon them: then they sprang toward Zarathustra, giving thanks, revering him, caressing him, kissing his hands, each according to his own manner: so that some laughed, some wept. But the old soothsayer danced for joy; and even if, as some narrators maintain, he was then full of sweet wine, he was assuredly still fuller of sweet life and had renounced all weariness. There are even some who tell that the ass danced then: for not without reason had the ugliest man previously given him wine to drink.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_19:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1_19:P0009","text":"Now this may have been so, or otherwise; and if in truth the ass did not dance that evening, then greater and stranger miracles occurred at that time than the dancing of an ass would be. In short, as Zarathustra’s proverb goes: “what does it matter!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_1_19:P0009"]}],"source_digest":"01d39a02ca8451a863e888601e22b088c19b1dc0a00324a0b6ec5f4f7f0464af","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song · 1","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","The Night-Wanderer’s Song","1."],"original_title":"1."}],"source_title_context":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied","source_section_label":"1.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied · 1","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Das Nachtwandler-Lied","1."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"1.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_19","title":"2.","order":172,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_2_19","text":"## 2.\n\nZarathustra aber, als sich diess mit dem hässlichsten Menschen zutrug, stand da, wie ein Trunkener: sein Blick erlosch, seine Zunge lallte, seine Füsse schwankten. Und wer möchte auch errathen, welche Gedanken dabei über Zarathustra’s Seele liefen? Ersichtlich aber wich sein Geist zurück und floh voraus und war in weiten Fernen und gleichsam „auf hohem Joche, wie geschrieben steht, zwischen zwei Meeren,\n\n—zwischen Vergangenem und Zukünftigem als schwere Wolke wandelnd.“ Allgemach aber, während ihn die höheren Menschen in den Armen hielten, kam er ein Wenig zu sich selber zurück und wehrte mit den Händen dem Gedränge der Verehrenden und Besorgten; doch sprach er nicht. Mit Einem Male aber wandte er schnell den Kopf, denn er schien Etwas zu hören: da legte er den Finger an den Mund und sprach: „Kommt!“\n\nUnd alsbald wurde es rings still und heimlich; aus der Tiefe aber kam langsam der Klang einer Glocke herauf. Zarathustra horchte darnach, gleich den höheren Menschen; dann aber legte er zum andern Male den Finger an den Mund und sprach wiederum: „Kommt! Kommt! Es geht gen Mitternacht!“—und seine Stimme hatte sich verwandelt. Aber immer noch rührte er sich nicht von der Stelle: da wurde es noch stiller und heimlicher, und Alles horchte, auch der Esel, und Zarathustra’s Ehrenthiere, der Adler und die Schlange, insgleichen die Höhle Zarathustra’s und der grosse kühle Mond und die Nacht selber. Zarathustra aber legte zum dritten Male die Hand an den Mund und sprach:\n\nKommt! Kommt! Kommt! Lasst uns jetzo wandeln! Es ist die Stunde: lasst uns in die Nacht wandeln!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_19:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_19:P0002","text":"Zarathustra aber, als sich diess mit dem hässlichsten Menschen zutrug, stand da, wie ein Trunkener: sein Blick erlosch, seine Zunge lallte, seine Füsse schwankten. Und wer möchte auch errathen, welche Gedanken dabei über Zarathustra’s Seele liefen? Ersichtlich aber wich sein Geist zurück und floh voraus und war in weiten Fernen und gleichsam „auf hohem Joche, wie geschrieben steht, zwischen zwei Meeren,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_19:P0003","text":"—zwischen Vergangenem und Zukünftigem als schwere Wolke wandelnd.“ Allgemach aber, während ihn die höheren Menschen in den Armen hielten, kam er ein Wenig zu sich selber zurück und wehrte mit den Händen dem Gedränge der Verehrenden und Besorgten; doch sprach er nicht. Mit Einem Male aber wandte er schnell den Kopf, denn er schien Etwas zu hören: da legte er den Finger an den Mund und sprach: „Kommt!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_19:P0004","text":"Und alsbald wurde es rings still und heimlich; aus der Tiefe aber kam langsam der Klang einer Glocke herauf. Zarathustra horchte darnach, gleich den höheren Menschen; dann aber legte er zum andern Male den Finger an den Mund und sprach wiederum: „Kommt! Kommt! Es geht gen Mitternacht!“—und seine Stimme hatte sich verwandelt. Aber immer noch rührte er sich nicht von der Stelle: da wurde es noch stiller und heimlicher, und Alles horchte, auch der Esel, und Zarathustra’s Ehrenthiere, der Adler und die Schlange, insgleichen die Höhle Zarathustra’s und der grosse kühle Mond und die Nacht selber. Zarathustra aber legte zum dritten Male die Hand an den Mund und sprach:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_19:P0005","text":"Kommt! Kommt! Kommt! Lasst uns jetzo wandeln! Es ist die Stunde: lasst uns in die Nacht wandeln!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"a7569ad4355134e9d10a4b775a95d6c7a5c21aa2b57aea017a44e7a68be3d394","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184204032551Z_96aaeef4","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2.","source_sha256":"a7569ad4355134e9d10a4b775a95d6c7a5c21aa2b57aea017a44e7a68be3d394","sha256":"19ff8c50b823d41f52ea6ff6e6f7776d9d070f93fd185e7ff1bfa502b439569f","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:42:04.032575+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_19:P0001","text":"## 2.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_19:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_19:P0002","text":"But Zarathustra, when this happened with the ugliest man, stood there like a drunkard: his gaze was extinguished, his tongue stammered, his feet staggered. And who could guess what thoughts were passing through Zarathustra’s soul? Evidently, however, his spirit recoiled and fled ahead and was in far distances and, as it were, “upon a high ridge, as it is written, between two seas,”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_19:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_19:P0003","text":"—“wandering between what was past and what was to come as a heavy cloud.” Gradually, however, while the higher men held him in their arms, he came a little back to himself and fended off with his hands the throng of the reverent and anxious; but he did not speak. All at once, however, he quickly turned his head, for he seemed to hear something: then he put his finger to his mouth and said: “Come!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_19:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_19:P0004","text":"And immediately it became still and secretive all around; but from the depths the sound of a bell slowly rose up. Zarathustra listened to it, like the higher men; but then for the second time he put his finger to his mouth and said again: “Come! Come! It is nearing midnight!”—and his voice had changed. But still he did not move from the spot: then it became stiller and more secretive, and everything listened, including the ass, and Zarathustra’s animals of honour, the eagle and the serpent, likewise Zarathustra’s cave and the great cool moon and the night itself. But for the third time Zarathustra put his hand to his mouth and said:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_19:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_2_19:P0005","text":"Come! Come! Come! Let us walk now! It is the hour: let us walk into the night!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_2_19:P0005"]}],"source_digest":"65c838a087f83c649a6c761d80c25a08b362d8c0e224a323fbba2f6bfdacf232","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song · 2","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","The Night-Wanderer’s Song","2."],"original_title":"2."}],"source_title_context":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied","source_section_label":"2.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied · 2","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Das Nachtwandler-Lied","2."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"2.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3_10","title":"3.","order":173,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_3_10","text":"## 3.\n\nIhr höheren Menschen, es geht gen Mitternacht: da will ich euch Etwas in die Ohren sagen, wie jene alte Glocke es mir in’s Ohr sagt,—\n\n—so heimlich, so schrecklich, so herzlich, wie jene Mitternachts-Glocke zu mir es redet, die mehr erlebt hat als Ein Mensch:\n\n—welche schon eurer Väter Herzens-Schmerzens-Schläge abzählte—ach! ach! wie sie seufzt! wie sie im Traume lacht! die alte tiefe tiefe Mitternacht!\n\nStill! Still! Da hört sich Manches, das am Tage nicht laut werden darf; nun aber, bei kühler Luft, da auch aller Lärm eurer Herzen stille ward,—\n\n—nun redet es, nun hört es sich, nun schleicht es sich in nächtliche überwache Seelen: ach! ach! wie sie seufzt! wie sie im Traume lacht!\n\n—hörst du’s nicht, wie sie heimlich, schrecklich, herzlich zu dir redet, die alte tiefe tiefe Mitternacht? Oh Mensch, gieb Acht!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_3_10:P0001","text":"## 3.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3_10:P0002","text":"Ihr höheren Menschen, es geht gen Mitternacht: da will ich euch Etwas in die Ohren sagen, wie jene alte Glocke es mir in’s Ohr sagt,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3_10:P0003","text":"—so heimlich, so schrecklich, so herzlich, wie jene Mitternachts-Glocke zu mir es redet, die mehr erlebt hat als Ein Mensch:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3_10:P0004","text":"—welche schon eurer Väter Herzens-Schmerzens-Schläge abzählte—ach! ach! wie sie seufzt! wie sie im Traume lacht! die alte tiefe tiefe Mitternacht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3_10:P0005","text":"Still! Still! Da hört sich Manches, das am Tage nicht laut werden darf; nun aber, bei kühler Luft, da auch aller Lärm eurer Herzen stille ward,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3_10:P0006","text":"—nun redet es, nun hört es sich, nun schleicht es sich in nächtliche überwache Seelen: ach! ach! wie sie seufzt! wie sie im Traume lacht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3_10:P0007","text":"—hörst du’s nicht, wie sie heimlich, schrecklich, herzlich zu dir redet, die alte tiefe tiefe Mitternacht? Oh Mensch, gieb Acht!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"aaf0cfe1ad740ffc8583844504203c318435b4932fae6e3497f8d46422bf64a4","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184208273533Z_9697803a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"3.","source_sha256":"aaf0cfe1ad740ffc8583844504203c318435b4932fae6e3497f8d46422bf64a4","sha256":"c0f9831a5e6f146d3e2da0fc5e904aa716ed5465f3834bdb524321ab0fb0f376","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:42:08.273546+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_3_10:P0001","text":"## 3.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3_10:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3_10:P0002","text":"You higher human beings, it is nearing midnight: then I want to say something into your ears, as that old bell says it into my ear,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3_10:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3_10:P0003","text":"—so secretly, so terribly, so heartfully, as that midnight-bell speaks to me, which has experienced more than one human being:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3_10:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3_10:P0004","text":"—which has already counted off the heart-pain-beats of your fathers—ah! ah! how it sighs! how it laughs in its dream! the old deep, deep midnight!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3_10:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3_10:P0005","text":"Hush! Hush! Here one hears many things that may not become loud by day; but now, in the cool air, when all the tumult of your hearts too has become still,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3_10:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3_10:P0006","text":"—now it speaks, now it is heard, now it creeps into nocturnal wakeful souls: ah! ah! how it sighs! how it laughs in its dream!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3_10:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_3_10:P0007","text":"—do you not hear it, how it speaks secretly, terribly, heartfully to you, the old deep, deep midnight? O human being, take heed!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_3_10:P0007"]}],"source_digest":"0efe7eaa2a48e3fa582de12bbfc5f31a9156968fd9ed2a3d5c45b3e814be2553","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song","source_section_label":"3.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song · 3","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","The Night-Wanderer’s Song","3."],"original_title":"3."}],"source_title_context":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied","source_section_label":"3.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied · 3","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Das Nachtwandler-Lied","3."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"3.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_5","title":"4.","order":174,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_4C_5","text":"## 4.\n\nWehe mir! Wo ist die Zeit hin? Sank ich nicht in tiefe Brunnen? Die Welt schläft—\n\nAch! Ach! Der Hund heult, der Mond scheint. Lieber will ich sterben, sterben, als euch sagen, was mein Mitternachts-Herz eben denkt.\n\nNun starb ich schon. Es ist dahin. Spinne, was spinnst du um mich? Willst du Blut? Ach! Ach! der Thau fällt, die Stunde kommt—\n\n—die Stunde, wo mich fröstelt und friert, die fragt und fragt und fragt: „wer hat Herz genug dazu?\n\n—wer soll der Erde Herr sein? Wer will sagen: so sollt ihr laufen, ihr grossen und kleinen Ströme!“\n\n—die Stunde naht: oh Mensch, du höherer Mensch, gieb Acht! diese Rede ist für feine Ohren, für deine Ohren was spricht die tiefe Mitternacht?","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_5:P0001","text":"## 4.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_5:P0002","text":"Wehe mir! Wo ist die Zeit hin? Sank ich nicht in tiefe Brunnen? Die Welt schläft—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_5:P0003","text":"Ach! Ach! Der Hund heult, der Mond scheint. Lieber will ich sterben, sterben, als euch sagen, was mein Mitternachts-Herz eben denkt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_5:P0004","text":"Nun starb ich schon. Es ist dahin. Spinne, was spinnst du um mich? Willst du Blut? Ach! Ach! der Thau fällt, die Stunde kommt—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_5:P0005","text":"—die Stunde, wo mich fröstelt und friert, die fragt und fragt und fragt: „wer hat Herz genug dazu?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_5:P0006","text":"—wer soll der Erde Herr sein? Wer will sagen: so sollt ihr laufen, ihr grossen und kleinen Ströme!“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_5:P0007","text":"—die Stunde naht: oh Mensch, du höherer Mensch, gieb Acht! diese Rede ist für feine Ohren, für deine Ohren was spricht die tiefe Mitternacht?","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"85dd5711cf10ffff304b93fd82bffe2d843ccfa4692b1bcfb3da22c890ed98ca","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184211515236Z_6c3a537f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"4.","source_sha256":"85dd5711cf10ffff304b93fd82bffe2d843ccfa4692b1bcfb3da22c890ed98ca","sha256":"721299972e9d13d00bd61000938d2079dd3457add6ce2bb5caebff094e991e91","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:42:11.515258+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_5:P0001","text":"## 4.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_5:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_5:P0002","text":"Woe is me! Where has time gone? Did I not sink into deep wells? The world sleeps—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_5:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_5:P0003","text":"Ah! Ah! The dog howls, the moon shines. I would rather die, die, than tell you what my midnight-heart is thinking just now.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_5:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_5:P0004","text":"Now I have already died. It is gone. Spider, what are you spinning around me? Do you want blood? Ah! Ah! the dew falls, the hour comes—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_5:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_5:P0005","text":"—the hour when I shiver and freeze, which asks and asks and asks: “who has heart enough for it?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_5:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_5:P0006","text":"—who shall be lord of the earth? Who wishes to say: thus shall you run, you great and little streams!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_5:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_4C_5:P0007","text":"—the hour approaches: O man, you higher man, take heed! this speech is for fine ears, for your ears what does deep midnight speak?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_4C_5:P0007"]}],"source_digest":"87a8e573c4a9e62aa5f17768395695e5b8456a5f4552536ce8cb2575d5bd0f0f","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song","source_section_label":"4.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song · 4","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","The Night-Wanderer’s Song","4."],"original_title":"4."}],"source_title_context":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied","source_section_label":"4.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied · 4","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Das Nachtwandler-Lied","4."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"4.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_5","title":"5.","order":175,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_5C_5","text":"## 5.\n\nEs trägt mich dahin, meine Seele tanzt. Tagewerk! Tagewerk! Wer soll der Erde Herr sein?\n\nDer Mond ist kühl, der Wind schweigt. Ach! Ach! Flogt ihr schon hoch genug? Ihr tanztet: aber ein Bein ist doch kein Flügel.\n\nIhr guten Tänzer, nun ist alle Lust vorbei, Wein ward Hefe, jeder Becher ward mürbe, die Gräber stammeln.\n\nIhr flogt nicht hoch genug: nun stammeln die Gräber „erlöst doch die Todten! Warum ist so lange Nacht? Macht uns nicht der Mond trunken?“\n\nIhr höheren Menschen, erlöst doch die Gräber, weckt die Leichname auf! Ach, was gräbt noch der Wurm? Es naht, es naht die Stunde,—\n\n—es brummt die Glocke, es schnarrt noch das Herz, es gräbt noch der Holzwurm, der Herzenswurm. Ach! Ach! Die Welt ist tief!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_5:P0001","text":"## 5.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_5:P0002","text":"Es trägt mich dahin, meine Seele tanzt. Tagewerk! Tagewerk! Wer soll der Erde Herr sein?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_5:P0003","text":"Der Mond ist kühl, der Wind schweigt. Ach! Ach! Flogt ihr schon hoch genug? Ihr tanztet: aber ein Bein ist doch kein Flügel.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_5:P0004","text":"Ihr guten Tänzer, nun ist alle Lust vorbei, Wein ward Hefe, jeder Becher ward mürbe, die Gräber stammeln.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_5:P0005","text":"Ihr flogt nicht hoch genug: nun stammeln die Gräber „erlöst doch die Todten! Warum ist so lange Nacht? Macht uns nicht der Mond trunken?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_5:P0006","text":"Ihr höheren Menschen, erlöst doch die Gräber, weckt die Leichname auf! Ach, was gräbt noch der Wurm? Es naht, es naht die Stunde,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_5:P0007","text":"—es brummt die Glocke, es schnarrt noch das Herz, es gräbt noch der Holzwurm, der Herzenswurm. Ach! Ach! Die Welt ist tief!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"77e8ba67b3589f756a6f75f0ff5071544bfdfb94aee295ca23ad4441a6431f2a","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184215439927Z_829169a0","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"5.","source_sha256":"77e8ba67b3589f756a6f75f0ff5071544bfdfb94aee295ca23ad4441a6431f2a","sha256":"f238156a6b256227aa5e9eb992aef07a1cd690e8e60f4461b05f258aa15ea1ba","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:42:15.439953+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_5:P0001","text":"## 5.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_5:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_5:P0002","text":"It bears me thither, my soul dances. Day's work! Day's work! Who is to be lord of the earth?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_5:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_5:P0003","text":"The moon is cool, the wind is silent. Ah! Ah! Have you flown high enough already? You danced: but one leg is still not a wing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_5:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_5:P0004","text":"You good dancers, now all pleasure is over, wine has become dregs, every cup has become brittle, the graves stammer.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_5:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_5:P0005","text":"You did not fly high enough: now the graves stammer, “redeem the dead, then! Why is the night so long? Does not the moon make us drunk?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_5:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_5:P0006","text":"You higher men, redeem the graves, awaken the corpses! Ah, what is the worm still burrowing for? The hour approaches, approaches,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_5:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_5C_5:P0007","text":"—the bell hums, the heart still rasps, the woodworm, the heartworm, still burrows. Ah! Ah! The world is deep!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_5C_5:P0007"]}],"source_digest":"c62ee1c7565d8b9a6d61963fb79bb6b1d53e26df1f53008fa77ef32e3bfdb15c","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song","source_section_label":"5.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song · 5","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","The Night-Wanderer’s Song","5."],"original_title":"5."}],"source_title_context":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied","source_section_label":"5.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied · 5","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Das Nachtwandler-Lied","5."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"5.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_5","title":"6.","order":176,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_6C_5","text":"## 6.\n\nSüsse Leier! Süsse Leier! Ich liebe deinen Ton, deinen trunkenen Unken-Ton!—wie lang her, wie fern her kommt mir dein Ton, weit her, von den Teichen der Liebe!\n\nDu alte Glocke, du süsse Leier! Jeder Schmerz riss dir in’s Herz, Vaterschmerz, Väterschmerz, Urväterschmerz, deine Rede wurde reif,-\n\n—reif gleich goldenem Herbste und Nachmittage, gleich meinem Einsiedlerherzen - nun redest du: die Welt selber ward reif, die Traube bräunt,\n\n—nun will sie sterben, vor Glück sterben. Ihr höheren Menschen, riecht ihr’s nicht? Es quillt heimlich ein Geruch herauf,\n\n—ein Duft und Geruch der Ewigkeit, ein rosenseliger, brauner Gold-Wein-Geruch von altem Glücke,\n\nvon trunkenem Mitternachts-Sterbeglücke, welches singt: die Welt ist tief und tiefer als der Tag gedacht!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_5:P0001","text":"## 6.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_5:P0002","text":"Süsse Leier! Süsse Leier! Ich liebe deinen Ton, deinen trunkenen Unken-Ton!—wie lang her, wie fern her kommt mir dein Ton, weit her, von den Teichen der Liebe!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_5:P0003","text":"Du alte Glocke, du süsse Leier! Jeder Schmerz riss dir in’s Herz, Vaterschmerz, Väterschmerz, Urväterschmerz, deine Rede wurde reif,-","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_5:P0004","text":"—reif gleich goldenem Herbste und Nachmittage, gleich meinem Einsiedlerherzen - nun redest du: die Welt selber ward reif, die Traube bräunt,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_5:P0005","text":"—nun will sie sterben, vor Glück sterben. Ihr höheren Menschen, riecht ihr’s nicht? Es quillt heimlich ein Geruch herauf,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_5:P0006","text":"—ein Duft und Geruch der Ewigkeit, ein rosenseliger, brauner Gold-Wein-Geruch von altem Glücke,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_5:P0007","text":"von trunkenem Mitternachts-Sterbeglücke, welches singt: die Welt ist tief und tiefer als der Tag gedacht!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"447b994a6a4e8636b2e88c0ad43fcc28ac13f88f4bdb325a7401365fb8817eb1","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184218784175Z_c9b8438f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"6.","source_sha256":"447b994a6a4e8636b2e88c0ad43fcc28ac13f88f4bdb325a7401365fb8817eb1","sha256":"509798da5a8239ab0285297f199341f431b5bee207f18cb5c2648334aa4c167e","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:42:18.784210+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_5:P0001","text":"## 6.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_5:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_5:P0002","text":"Sweet lyre! Sweet lyre! I love your tone, your drunken toad-tone!—how long ago, how far away does your tone come to me, from far away, from the ponds of love!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_5:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_5:P0003","text":"You old bell, you sweet lyre! Every pain tore into your heart, father-pain, fathers-pain, forefathers-pain, your speech became ripe,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_5:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_5:P0004","text":"—ripe like golden autumn and afternoon, like my hermit's heart—now you speak: the world itself has become ripe, the grape is browning,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_5:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_5:P0005","text":"—now it wants to die, to die of happiness. You higher men, do you not smell it? A scent wells up secretly,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_5:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_5:P0006","text":"—a fragrance and scent of eternity, a rose-blessed, brown gold-wine scent of old happiness,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_5:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_6C_5:P0007","text":"of drunken midnight death-happiness, which sings: the world is deep and deeper than day has thought!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_6C_5:P0007"]}],"source_digest":"fbb77903e41ad7d4e9dc17892886b44e4f027b9e63aa5bcd1aa38239acbd9824","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song","source_section_label":"6.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song · 6","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","The Night-Wanderer’s Song","6."],"original_title":"6."}],"source_title_context":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied","source_section_label":"6.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied · 6","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Das Nachtwandler-Lied","6."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"6.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_5","title":"7.","order":177,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_7C_5","text":"## 7.\n\nLass mich! Lass mich! Ich bin zu rein für dich. Rühre mich nicht an! Ward meine Welt nicht eben vollkommen?\n\nMeine Haut ist zu rein für deine Hände. Lass mich, du dummer tölpischer dumpfer Tag! Ist die Mitternacht nicht heller?\n\nDie Reinsten sollen der Erde Herrn sein, die Unerkanntesten, Stärksten, die Mitternachts-Seelen, die heller und tiefer sind als jeder Tag.\n\nOh Tag, du tappst nach mir? Du tastest nach meinem Glücke? Ich bin dir reich, einsam, eine Schatzgrube, eine Goldkammer?\n\nOh Welt, du willst mich ? Bin ich dir weltlich? Bin ich dir geistlich? Bin ich dir göttlich? Aber Tag und Welt, ihr seid zu plump,—\n\n—habt klügere Hände, greift nach tieferem Glücke, nach tieferem Unglücke, greift nach irgend einem Gotte, greift nicht nach mir:\n\n—mein Unglück, mein Glück ist tief, du wunderlicher Tag, aber doch bin ich kein Gott, keine Gottes-Hölle: tief ist ihr Weh.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0001","text":"## 7.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0002","text":"Lass mich! Lass mich! Ich bin zu rein für dich. Rühre mich nicht an! Ward meine Welt nicht eben vollkommen?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0003","text":"Meine Haut ist zu rein für deine Hände. Lass mich, du dummer tölpischer dumpfer Tag! Ist die Mitternacht nicht heller?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0004","text":"Die Reinsten sollen der Erde Herrn sein, die Unerkanntesten, Stärksten, die Mitternachts-Seelen, die heller und tiefer sind als jeder Tag.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0005","text":"Oh Tag, du tappst nach mir? Du tastest nach meinem Glücke? Ich bin dir reich, einsam, eine Schatzgrube, eine Goldkammer?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0006","text":"Oh Welt, du willst mich ? Bin ich dir weltlich? Bin ich dir geistlich? Bin ich dir göttlich? Aber Tag und Welt, ihr seid zu plump,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0007","text":"—habt klügere Hände, greift nach tieferem Glücke, nach tieferem Unglücke, greift nach irgend einem Gotte, greift nicht nach mir:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0008","text":"—mein Unglück, mein Glück ist tief, du wunderlicher Tag, aber doch bin ich kein Gott, keine Gottes-Hölle: tief ist ihr Weh.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"f4b7388b71d57fd7550ad8980acd761e450100b147101cfaf2e7ae01d4b57646","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184222546677Z_5bd1205e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"7.","source_sha256":"f4b7388b71d57fd7550ad8980acd761e450100b147101cfaf2e7ae01d4b57646","sha256":"2e0b72f4effc3939aa1b5ef1e4be37d6cfcfd5a1aa70823abdbf539c78f0dc26","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:42:22.546703+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0001","text":"## 7.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0002","text":"Leave me! Leave me! I am too pure for you. Do not touch me! Was my world not just perfect?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0003","text":"My skin is too pure for your hands. Leave me, you stupid, clumsy, dull day! Is midnight not brighter?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0004","text":"The purest are to be lords of the earth, the most unrecognized, strongest ones, the midnight-souls, who are brighter and deeper than every day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0005","text":"Oh day, are you groping after me? Are you feeling after my happiness? Am I to you rich, solitary, a treasure-pit, a gold-chamber?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0006","text":"Oh world, do you want me? Am I worldly to you? Am I spiritual to you? Am I divine to you? But day and world, you are too clumsy,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0007","text":"—have cleverer hands, reach for deeper happiness, for deeper misfortune, reach for some god, do not reach for me:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0008","text":"—my misfortune, my happiness is deep, you strange day, but still I am no god, no god's-hell: deep is their woe.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_7C_5:P0008"]}],"source_digest":"30eeb0852d09286d99721e2260f99829b796e9dbd002c98ae9b9e15d6bb1f130","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song","source_section_label":"7.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song · 7","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","The Night-Wanderer’s Song","7."],"original_title":"7."}],"source_title_context":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied","source_section_label":"7.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied · 7","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Das Nachtwandler-Lied","7."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"7.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_7","title":"8.","order":178,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_8C_7","text":"## 8.\n\nGottes Weh ist tiefer, du wunderliche Welt! Greife nach Gottes Weh, nicht nach mir! Was bin ich! Eine trunkene süsse Leier,—\n\neine Mitternachts-Leier, eine Glocken-Unke, die Niemand versteht, aber welche reden muss , vor Tauben, ihr höheren Menschen! Denn ihr versteht mich nicht!\n\nDahin! Dahin! Oh Jugend! Oh Mittag! Oh Nachmittag! Nun kam Abend und Nacht und Mitternacht,—der Hund heult, der Wind:\n\n—ist der Wind nicht ein Hund? Er winselt, er kläfft, er heult. Ach! Ach! wie sie seufzt! wie sie lacht, wie sie röchelt und keucht, die Mitternacht!\n\nWie sie eben nüchtern spricht, diese trunkene Dichterin! sie übertrat wohl ihre Trunkenheit? sie wurde überwach? sie käut zurück?\n\n—ihr Weh käut sie zurück, im Traume, die alte tiefe Mitternacht, und mehr noch ihre Lust. Lust nämlich, wenn schon Weh tief ist: Lust ist tiefer noch als Herzeleid.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_7:P0001","text":"## 8.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_7:P0002","text":"Gottes Weh ist tiefer, du wunderliche Welt! Greife nach Gottes Weh, nicht nach mir! Was bin ich! Eine trunkene süsse Leier,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_7:P0003","text":"eine Mitternachts-Leier, eine Glocken-Unke, die Niemand versteht, aber welche reden muss , vor Tauben, ihr höheren Menschen! Denn ihr versteht mich nicht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_7:P0004","text":"Dahin! Dahin! Oh Jugend! Oh Mittag! Oh Nachmittag! Nun kam Abend und Nacht und Mitternacht,—der Hund heult, der Wind:","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_7:P0005","text":"—ist der Wind nicht ein Hund? Er winselt, er kläfft, er heult. Ach! Ach! wie sie seufzt! wie sie lacht, wie sie röchelt und keucht, die Mitternacht!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_7:P0006","text":"Wie sie eben nüchtern spricht, diese trunkene Dichterin! sie übertrat wohl ihre Trunkenheit? sie wurde überwach? sie käut zurück?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_7:P0007","text":"—ihr Weh käut sie zurück, im Traume, die alte tiefe Mitternacht, und mehr noch ihre Lust. Lust nämlich, wenn schon Weh tief ist: Lust ist tiefer noch als Herzeleid.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"123ffcdb07fc7e3557fe27685952545e3aca85c3383f48557c4c5c1573349e81","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184226410737Z_ea946861","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"8.","source_sha256":"123ffcdb07fc7e3557fe27685952545e3aca85c3383f48557c4c5c1573349e81","sha256":"2ab58842d7525c2df40484ba6fb70febeef8f117748ee019d0d90e76e0a70630","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:42:26.410746+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_7:P0001","text":"## 8.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_7:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_7:P0002","text":"God's woe is deeper, you wondrous world! Reach for God's woe, not for me! What am I! A drunken sweet lyre,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_7:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_7:P0003","text":"a midnight-lyre, a bell-toad, which no one understands, but which must speak, before deaf people, you higher men! For you do not understand me!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_7:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_7:P0004","text":"Away! Away! Oh youth! Oh noon! Oh afternoon! Now came evening and night and midnight,—the dog howls, the wind:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_7:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_7:P0005","text":"—is not the wind a dog? It whimpers, it yelps, it howls. Ah! Ah! how it sighs! how it laughs, how it rattles and pants, midnight!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_7:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_7:P0006","text":"How soberly it speaks just now, this drunken poetess! Has it perhaps overcome its drunkenness? has it become over-awake? does it chew the cud?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_7:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_7:P0007","text":"—it chews the cud of its woe, in a dream, old deep midnight, and still more of its joy. For joy, even if woe is deep: joy is deeper still than heartbreak.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_7:P0007"]}],"source_digest":"09176aafe08d0c27b0764eeecd3c4eefb4bba1c3e1b57eb2488003a3e48940b3","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song","source_section_label":"8.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song · 8","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","The Night-Wanderer’s Song","8."],"original_title":"8."}],"source_title_context":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied","source_section_label":"8.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied · 8","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Das Nachtwandler-Lied","8."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"8.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_8","title":"8.","order":179,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_8C_8","text":"## 8.\n\nDu Weinstock! Was preisest du mich? Ich schnitt dich doch! Ich bin grausam, du blutest—: was will dein Lob meiner trunkenen Grausamkeit?\n\n„Was vollkommen ward, alles Reife—will sterben!“ so redest du. Gesegnet, gesegnet sei das Winzermesser! Aber alles Unreife will leben: wehe!\n\nWeh spricht: „Vergeh! Weg, du Wehe!“ Aber Alles, was leidet, will leben, dass es reif werde und lustig und sehnsüchtig,\n\n—sehnsüchtig nach Fernerem, Höherem, Hellerem. „Ich will Erben, so spricht Alles, was leidet, ich will Kinder, ich will nicht mich ,“—\n\nLust aber will nicht Erben, nicht Kinder,—Lust will sich selber, will Ewigkeit, will Wiederkunft, will Alles-sich-ewig-gleich.\n\nWeh spricht: „Brich, blute, Herz! Wandle, Bein! Flügel, flieg! Hinan! Hinauf! Schmerz!“ Wohlan! Wohlauf! Oh mein altes Herz: Weh spricht: „vergeh!“","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_8:P0001","text":"## 8.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_8:P0002","text":"Du Weinstock! Was preisest du mich? Ich schnitt dich doch! Ich bin grausam, du blutest—: was will dein Lob meiner trunkenen Grausamkeit?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_8:P0003","text":"„Was vollkommen ward, alles Reife—will sterben!“ so redest du. Gesegnet, gesegnet sei das Winzermesser! Aber alles Unreife will leben: wehe!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_8:P0004","text":"Weh spricht: „Vergeh! Weg, du Wehe!“ Aber Alles, was leidet, will leben, dass es reif werde und lustig und sehnsüchtig,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_8:P0005","text":"—sehnsüchtig nach Fernerem, Höherem, Hellerem. „Ich will Erben, so spricht Alles, was leidet, ich will Kinder, ich will nicht mich ,“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_8:P0006","text":"Lust aber will nicht Erben, nicht Kinder,—Lust will sich selber, will Ewigkeit, will Wiederkunft, will Alles-sich-ewig-gleich.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_8:P0007","text":"Weh spricht: „Brich, blute, Herz! Wandle, Bein! Flügel, flieg! Hinan! Hinauf! Schmerz!“ Wohlan! Wohlauf! Oh mein altes Herz: Weh spricht: „vergeh!“","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"98b4a949e0444f3a829109f8b9561cd05755500e94624748a248adfaeb9c329f","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184230960512Z_0752131a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"8.","source_sha256":"98b4a949e0444f3a829109f8b9561cd05755500e94624748a248adfaeb9c329f","sha256":"8e8ec5a56ee8ab1ba363fca14fd3e093da3662a69203e343bb9f6d2dab24c9fc","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:42:30.960551+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_8:P0001","text":"## 8.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_8:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_8:P0002","text":"You vine! Why do you praise me? After all, I cut you! I am cruel, you bleed—: what does your praise of my drunken cruelty want?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_8:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_8:P0003","text":"“Whatever became perfect, all ripeness—wants to die!” thus you speak. Blessed, blessed be the vintner’s knife! But everything unripe wants to live: woe!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_8:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_8:P0004","text":"Woe speaks: “Pass away! Away, you woe!” But Everything that suffers wants to live, so that it may become ripe and merry and longing,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_8:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_8:P0005","text":"—longing for what is more distant, higher, brighter. “I want heirs, thus speaks Everything that suffers, I want children, I do not want myself,”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_8:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_8:P0006","text":"But joy does not want heirs, not children,—joy wants itself, wants eternity, wants recurrence, wants everything-eternally-the-same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_8:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_8C_8:P0007","text":"Woe speaks: “Break, bleed, heart! Walk, leg! Fly, wing! Up! Upward! Pain!” Come then! Up then! Oh my old heart: woe speaks: “pass away!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_8C_8:P0007"]}],"source_digest":"81746eaaf8b1d788745eccdaa209fb0e5ac18e2fd8727d657a18c4527de636b7","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song","source_section_label":"8.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song · 8","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","The Night-Wanderer’s Song","8."],"original_title":"8."}],"source_title_context":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied","source_section_label":"8.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied · 8","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Das Nachtwandler-Lied","8."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"8.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_4","title":"10.","order":180,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_10_4","text":"## 10.\n\nIhr höheren Menschen, was dünket euch? Bin ich ein Wahrsager? Ein Träumender? Trunkener? Ein Traumdeuter? Eine Mitternachts-Glocke?\n\nEin Tropfen Thau’s? Ein Dunst und Duft der Ewigkeit? Hört ihr’s nicht? Riecht ihr’s nicht? Eben ward meine Welt vollkommen, Mitternacht ist auch Mittag,—\n\nSchmerz ist auch eine Lust, Fluch ist auch ein Segen, Nacht ist auch eine Sonne,—geht davon oder ihr lernt: ein Weiser ist auch ein Narr.\n\nSagtet ihr jemals ja zu Einer Lust? Oh, meine Freunde, so sagtet ihr Ja auch zu allem Wehe. Alle Dinge sind verkettet, verfädelt, verliebt,—\n\n—wolltet ihr jemals Ein Mal Zwei Mal, spracht ihr jemals „du gefällst mir, Glück! Husch! Augenblick!“ so wolltet ihr Alles zurück!\n\n—Alles von neuem, Alles ewig, Alles verkettet, verfädelt, verliebt, oh so liebtet ihr die Welt,—\n\n—ihr Ewigen, liebt sie ewig und allezeit: und auch zum Weh sprecht ihr: vergeh, aber komm zurück! Denn alle Lust will—Ewigkeit!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_4:P0001","text":"## 10.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_4:P0002","text":"Ihr höheren Menschen, was dünket euch? Bin ich ein Wahrsager? Ein Träumender? Trunkener? Ein Traumdeuter? Eine Mitternachts-Glocke?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_4:P0003","text":"Ein Tropfen Thau’s? Ein Dunst und Duft der Ewigkeit? Hört ihr’s nicht? Riecht ihr’s nicht? Eben ward meine Welt vollkommen, Mitternacht ist auch Mittag,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_4:P0004","text":"Schmerz ist auch eine Lust, Fluch ist auch ein Segen, Nacht ist auch eine Sonne,—geht davon oder ihr lernt: ein Weiser ist auch ein Narr.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_4:P0005","text":"Sagtet ihr jemals ja zu Einer Lust? Oh, meine Freunde, so sagtet ihr Ja auch zu allem Wehe. Alle Dinge sind verkettet, verfädelt, verliebt,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_4:P0006","text":"—wolltet ihr jemals Ein Mal Zwei Mal, spracht ihr jemals „du gefällst mir, Glück! Husch! Augenblick!“ so wolltet ihr Alles zurück!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_4:P0007","text":"—Alles von neuem, Alles ewig, Alles verkettet, verfädelt, verliebt, oh so liebtet ihr die Welt,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_4:P0008","text":"—ihr Ewigen, liebt sie ewig und allezeit: und auch zum Weh sprecht ihr: vergeh, aber komm zurück! Denn alle Lust will—Ewigkeit!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"7b111368b0e4c20e061c598a24cb8ee899ced3a2aa4625823229bd8290f54aea","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184239853923Z_34a2a09f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"10.","source_sha256":"7b111368b0e4c20e061c598a24cb8ee899ced3a2aa4625823229bd8290f54aea","sha256":"d99ce05365e098d14f1dfcbfcb0ae881dc6e23958a91e2b0c1a08af98ff028c3","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:42:39.853953+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_4:P0001","text":"## 10.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10_4:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_4:P0002","text":"You higher human beings, what think you? Am I a soothsayer? A dreamer? A drunkard? A dream-interpreter? A midnight bell?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10_4:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_4:P0003","text":"A drop of dew? A vapour and fragrance of eternity? Do you not hear it? Do you not smell it? Just now my world became perfect, midnight is also midday,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10_4:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_4:P0004","text":"Pain is also a pleasure, curse is also a blessing, night is also a sun,—go away, or you will learn: a wise man is also a fool.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10_4:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_4:P0005","text":"Did you ever say yes to a single pleasure? Oh, my friends, then you also said Yes to all woe. All things are entwined, entangled, enamoured,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10_4:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_4:P0006","text":"—did you ever will Once Twice, did you ever say, “you please me, happiness! Hush! Moment!” then you willed Everything back!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10_4:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_4:P0007","text":"—Everything anew, Everything eternal, Everything entwined, entangled, enamoured, oh then you loved the world,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10_4:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_10_4:P0008","text":"—you eternal ones, love it eternally and for all time: and to woe, too, you say: pass away, but return! For all pleasure wills—eternity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_10_4:P0008"]}],"source_digest":"b48ab781324c558c27b58d1192b572a2078f9bbb4c925b3f44ca63fb6916abf5","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song","source_section_label":"10.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song · 10","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","The Night-Wanderer’s Song","10."],"original_title":"10."}],"source_title_context":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied","source_section_label":"10.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied · 10","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Das Nachtwandler-Lied","10."],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"10.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_3","title":"11.","order":181,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_11_3","text":"## 11.\n\nAlle Lust will aller Dinge Ewigkeit, will Honig, will Hefe, will trunkene Mitternacht, will Gräber, will Gräber-Thränen-Trost, will vergüldetes Abendroth -\n\n— was will nicht Lust! sie ist durstiger, herzlicher, hungriger, schrecklicher, heimlicher als alles Weh, sie will sich , sie beisst in sich , des Ringes Wille ringt in ihr,—\n\n—sie will Liebe, sie will Hass, sie ist überreich, schenkt, wirft weg, bettelt, dass Einer sie nimmt, dankt dem Nehmenden, sie möchte gern gehasst sein,—\n\n—so reich ist Lust, dass sie nach Wehe durstet, nach Hölle, nach Hass, nach Schmach, nach dem Krüppel, nach Welt ,—denn diese Welt, oh ihr kennt sie ja!\n\nIhr höheren Menschen, nach euch sehnt sie sich, die Lust, die unbändige, selige,—nach eurem Weh, ihr Missrathenen! Nach Missrathenem sehnt sich alle ewige Lust.\n\nDenn alle Lust will sich selber, drum will sie auch Herzeleid! Oh Glück, oh Schmerz! Oh brich, Herz! Ihr höheren Menschen, lernt es doch, Lust will Ewigkeit,\n\n—Lust will aller Dinge Ewigkeit, will tiefe, tiefe Ewigkeit!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_3:P0001","text":"## 11.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_3:P0002","text":"Alle Lust will aller Dinge Ewigkeit, will Honig, will Hefe, will trunkene Mitternacht, will Gräber, will Gräber-Thränen-Trost, will vergüldetes Abendroth -","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_3:P0003","text":"— was will nicht Lust! sie ist durstiger, herzlicher, hungriger, schrecklicher, heimlicher als alles Weh, sie will sich , sie beisst in sich , des Ringes Wille ringt in ihr,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_3:P0004","text":"—sie will Liebe, sie will Hass, sie ist überreich, schenkt, wirft weg, bettelt, dass Einer sie nimmt, dankt dem Nehmenden, sie möchte gern gehasst sein,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_3:P0005","text":"—so reich ist Lust, dass sie nach Wehe durstet, nach Hölle, nach Hass, nach Schmach, nach dem Krüppel, nach Welt ,—denn diese Welt, oh ihr kennt sie ja!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_3:P0006","text":"Ihr höheren Menschen, nach euch sehnt sie sich, die Lust, die unbändige, selige,—nach eurem Weh, ihr Missrathenen! Nach Missrathenem sehnt sich alle ewige Lust.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_3:P0007","text":"Denn alle Lust will sich selber, drum will sie auch Herzeleid! Oh Glück, oh Schmerz! Oh brich, Herz! Ihr höheren Menschen, lernt es doch, Lust will Ewigkeit,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_3:P0008","text":"—Lust will aller Dinge Ewigkeit, will tiefe, tiefe Ewigkeit!","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"f5e515d136c2656773b82594c74b06b3d0c4c215db6237907f4537e7e269999f","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184243946228Z_adc16e82","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"11.","source_sha256":"f5e515d136c2656773b82594c74b06b3d0c4c215db6237907f4537e7e269999f","sha256":"8d0d50075f4fbea4b5ecb5325dfe4f899f9ab5734ddec7fa94eec95b5fe54fb3","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:42:43.946252+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_3:P0001","text":"## 11.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11_3:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_3:P0002","text":"All joy wants the eternity of all things, wants honey, wants leaven, wants drunken midnight, wants graves, wants the consolation of grave-tears, wants gilded evening-red -","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11_3:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_3:P0003","text":"— what does joy not want! it is thirstier, heartier, hungrier, more terrible, more secret than all woe; it wants itself, it bites into itself, the will of the ring wrestles within it,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11_3:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_3:P0004","text":"—it wants love, it wants hate, it is over-rich, gives, throws away, begs that someone take it, thanks the taker, it would gladly be hated,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11_3:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_3:P0005","text":"—so rich is joy that it thirsts for woe, for hell, for hate, for disgrace, for the cripple, for the world,—for this world, oh you know it well enough!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11_3:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_3:P0006","text":"You higher human beings, it longs for you, joy, the unbridled, blissful,—for your woe, you failures! All eternal joy longs for what has failed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11_3:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_3:P0007","text":"For all joy wants itself, therefore it also wants heartache! Oh happiness, oh pain! Oh break, heart! You higher human beings, learn it then, joy wants eternity,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11_3:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_11_3:P0008","text":"—joy wants the eternity of all things, wants deep, deep eternity!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_11_3:P0008"]}],"source_digest":"c2dd954cdcb95faa702d8f0d190c8b304deef5657b599804cf81e0870434a256","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song","source_section_label":"11.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song · 11","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","The Night-Wanderer’s Song","11."],"original_title":"11."}],"source_title_context":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied","source_section_label":"11.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied · 11","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Das Nachtwandler-Lied","11."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"11.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12_3","title":"12.","order":182,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_12_3","text":"## 12.\n\nLerntet ihr nun mein Lied? Erriethet ihr, was es will? Wohlan! Wohlauf! Ihr höheren Menschen, so singt mir nun meinen Rundgesang!\n\nSingt mir nun selber das Lied, dess Name ist „Noch ein Mal“, dess Sinn ist „in alle Ewigkeit!“, singt, ihr höheren Menschen, Zarathustra’s Rundgesang!\n\nOh Mensch! Gieb Acht! Was spricht die tiefe Mitternacht? „Ich schlief, ich schlief—, Aus tiefem Traum bin ich erwacht:— Die Welt ist tief, Und tiefer als der Tag gedacht. Tief ist ihr Weh—, Lust—tiefer noch als Herzeleid: Weh spricht: Vergeh! Doch alle Lust will Ewigkeit will tiefe, tiefe Ewigkeit!“","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_12_3:P0001","text":"## 12.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12_3:P0002","text":"Lerntet ihr nun mein Lied? Erriethet ihr, was es will? Wohlan! Wohlauf! Ihr höheren Menschen, so singt mir nun meinen Rundgesang!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12_3:P0003","text":"Singt mir nun selber das Lied, dess Name ist „Noch ein Mal“, dess Sinn ist „in alle Ewigkeit!“, singt, ihr höheren Menschen, Zarathustra’s Rundgesang!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12_3:P0004","text":"Oh Mensch! Gieb Acht! Was spricht die tiefe Mitternacht? „Ich schlief, ich schlief—, Aus tiefem Traum bin ich erwacht:— Die Welt ist tief, Und tiefer als der Tag gedacht. Tief ist ihr Weh—, Lust—tiefer noch als Herzeleid: Weh spricht: Vergeh! Doch alle Lust will Ewigkeit will tiefe, tiefe Ewigkeit!“","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"dcd2c91bf16c36b9c7e7e1e446fda90880f6793d871a1fb38ff8aaf275bf3255","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184247473218Z_0900466f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"12.","source_sha256":"dcd2c91bf16c36b9c7e7e1e446fda90880f6793d871a1fb38ff8aaf275bf3255","sha256":"4f02c7c53c3e2296fe9befef5d2de9b1b6b81ef07f6c15b7d499a43d8c272f5c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:42:47.473262+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_12_3:P0001","text":"## 12.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_12_3:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12_3:P0002","text":"Have you now learned my song? Have you guessed what it wants? Well then! Up now! You higher human beings, sing me now my roundelay!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_12_3:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12_3:P0003","text":"Now sing me yourselves the song whose name is “Once More,” whose meaning is “unto all eternity!”—sing, you higher human beings, Zarathustra’s roundelay!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_12_3:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_12_3:P0004","text":"O human being! Take heed! What does deep midnight speak? “I slept, I slept—, From a deep dream I awoke:— The world is deep, And deeper than day has thought. Deep is its woe—, Joy—deeper still than heartache: Woe speaks: Pass away! But all joy wants eternity—wants deep, deep eternity!”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_12_3:P0004"]}],"source_digest":"024a7687d43927290c02151c506d4bf3ab37bb6893080466bb6974080ee91232","source_language_code":"de","source_title_context":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song","source_section_label":"12.","source_title_hierarchy":["Part Four and Last"],"reading_title":"The Night-Wanderer’s Song · 12","reading_title_context":"Part Four and Last","reading_heading_titles":["Part Four and Last","The Night-Wanderer’s Song","12."],"original_title":"12."}],"source_title_context":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied","source_section_label":"12.","source_title_hierarchy":["Vierter und letzter Theil"],"reading_title":"Das Nachtwandler-Lied · 12","reading_title_context":"Vierter und letzter Theil","reading_heading_titles":["Vierter und letzter Theil","Das Nachtwandler-Lied","12."],"passage_basis":"repaired-grounding","original_title":"12.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ","title":"The Sign","order":183,"chapter_id":"CH_97E9_DASZ","text":"Des Morgens aber nach dieser Nacht sprang Zarathustra von seinem Lager auf, gürtete sich die Lenden und kam heraus aus seiner Höhle, glühend und stark, wie eine Morgensonne, die aus dunklen Bergen kommt.\n\n„Du grosses Gestirn, sprach er, wie er einstmal gesprochen hatte, du tiefes Glücks-Auge, was wäre all dein Glück, wenn du nicht Die hättest, welchen du leuchtest!\n\nUnd wenn sie in ihren Kammern blieben, während du schon wach bist und kommst und schenkst und austheilst: wie würde darob deine stolze Scham zürnen!\n\nWohlan! sie schlafen noch, diese höheren Menschen, während ich wach bin: das sind nicht meine rechten Gefährten! Nicht auf sie warte ich hier in meinen Bergen.\n\nZu meinem Werke will ich, zu meinem Tage: aber sie verstehen nicht, was die Zeichen meines Morgens sind, mein Schritt—ist für sie kein Weckruf.\n\nSie schlafen noch in meiner Höhle, ihr Traum käut noch an meinen Mitternächten. Das Ohr, das nach mir horcht,—das gehorchende Ohr fehlt in ihren Gliedern.“\n\n—Diess hatte Zarathustra zu seinem Herzen gesprochen, als die Sonne aufgieng: da blickte er fragend in die Höhe, denn er hörte über sich den scharfen Ruf seines Adlers. „Wohlan! rief er hinauf, so gefällt und gebührt es mir. Meine Thiere sind wach, denn ich bin wach.\n\nMein Adler ist wach und ehrt gleich mir die Sonne. Mit Adlers-Klauen greift er nach dem neuen Lichte. Ihr seid meine rechten Thiere; ich liebe euch.\n\nAber noch fehlen mir meine rechten Menschen!“—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra; da aber geschah es, dass er sich plötzlich wie von unzähligen Vögeln umschwärmt und umflattert hörte,—das Geschwirr so vieler Flügel aber und das Gedräng um sein Haupt war so gross, dass er die Augen schloss. Und wahrlich, einer Wolke gleich fiel es über ihn her, einer Wolke von Pfeilen gleich, welche sich über einen neuen Feind ausschüttet. Aber siehe, hier war es eine Wolke der Liebe, und über einen neuen Freund.\n\n„Was geschieht mir?“ dachte Zarathustra in seinem erstaunten Herzen und liess sich langsam auf dem grossen Steine nieder, der neben dem Ausgange seiner Höhle lag. Aber, indem er mit den Händen um sich und über sich und unter sich griff, und den zärtlichen Vögeln wehrte, siehe, da geschah ihm etwas noch Seltsameres: er griff nämlich dabei unvermerkt in ein dichtes warmes Haar-Gezottel hinein; zugleich aber erscholl vor ihm ein Gebrüll,—ein sanftes langes Löwen-Brüllen.\n\n„Das Zeichen kommt,“ sprach Zarathustra und sein Herz verwandelte sich. Und in Wahrheit, als es helle vor ihm wurde, da lag ihm ein gelbes mächtiges Gethier zu Füssen und schmiegte das Haupt an seine Knie und wollte nicht von ihm lassen vor Liebe und that einem Hunde gleich, welcher seinen alten Herrn wiederfindet. Die Tauben aber waren mit ihrer Liebe nicht minder eifrig als der Löwe; und jedes Mal, wenn eine Taube über die Nase des Löwen huschte, schüttelte der Löwe das Haupt und wunderte sich und lachte dazu.\n\nZu dem Allen sprach Zarathustra nur Ein Wort: „meine Kinder sind nahe, meine Kinder“—, dann wurde er ganz stumm. Sein Herz aber war gelöst, und aus seinen Augen tropften Thränen herab und fielen auf seine Hände. Und er achtete keines Dings mehr und sass da, unbeweglich und ohne dass er sich noch gegen die Thiere wehrte. Da flogen die Tauben ab und zu und setzten sich ihm auf die Schulter und liebkosten sein weisses Haar und wurden nicht müde mit Zärtlichkeit und Frohlocken. Der starke Löwe aber leckte immer die Thränen, welche auf die Hände Zarathustra’s herabfielen und brüllte und brummte schüchtern dazu. Also trieben es diese Thiere.—\n\nDiess Alles dauerte eine lange Zeit, oder eine kurze Zeit: denn, recht gesprochen, giebt es für dergleichen Dinge auf Erden keine Zeit—. Inzwischen aber waren die höheren Menschen in der Höhle Zarathustra’s wach geworden und ordneten sich mit einander zu einem Zuge an, dass sie Zarathustra entgegen giengen und ihm den Morgengruss böten: denn sie hatten gefunden, als sie erwachten, dass er schon nicht mehr unter ihnen weilte. Als sie aber zur Thür der Höhle gelangten, und das Geräusch ihrer Schritte ihnen voranlief, da stutzte der Löwe gewaltig, kehrte sich mit Einem Male von Zarathustra ab und sprang, wild brüllend, auf die Höhle los; die höheren Menschen aber, als sie ihn brüllen hörten, schrien alle auf, wie mit Einem Munde, und flohen zurück und waren im Nu verschwunden.\n\nZarathustra selber aber, betäubt und fremd, erhob sich von seinem Sitze, sah um sich, stand staunend da, fragte sein Herz, besann sich und war allein. „Was hörte ich doch? sprach er endlich langsam, was geschah mir eben?“\n\nUnd schon kam ihm die Erinnerung, und er begriff mit Einem Blicke Alles, was zwischen Gestern und Heute sich begeben hatte. „Hier ist ja der Stein, sprach er und strich sich den Bart, auf dem sass ich gestern am Morgen; und hier trat der Wahrsager zu mir, und hier hörte ich zuerst den Schrei, den ich eben hörte, den grossen Nothschrei.\n\nOh ihr höheren Menschen, von eurer Noth war’s ja, dass gestern am Morgen jener alte Wahrsager mir wahrsagte,—\n\n—zu eurer Noth wollte er mich verfuhren und versuchen: oh Zarathustra, sprach er zu mir, ich komme, dass ich dich zu deiner letzten Sünde verführe.\n\nZu meiner letzten Sünde? rief Zarathustra und lachte zornig über sein eigenes Wort: was blieb mir doch aufgespart als meine letzte Sünde?“\n\n—Und noch ein Mal versank Zarathustra in sich und setzte sich wieder auf den grossen Stein nieder und sann nach. Plötzlich sprang er empor,—\n\n„Mitleiden! Das Mitleiden mit dem höheren Menschen! schrie er auf, und sein Antlitz verwandelte sich in Erz. Wohlan! Das —hatte seine Zeit!\n\nMein Leid und mein Mitleiden—was liegt daran! Trachte ich denn nach Glücke ? Ich trachte nach meinem Werke !\n\nWohlan! Der Löwe kam, meine Kinder sind nahe, Zarathustra ward reif, meine Stunde kam:—\n\nDies ist mein Morgen, mein Tag hebt an: herauf nun, herauf, du grosser Mittag!“—\n\nAlso sprach Zarathustra und verliess seine Höhle, glühend und stark, wie eine Morgensonne, die aus dunklen Bergen kommt.\n\n*** END OF THE PROJECT GUTENBERG EBOOK ALSO SPRACH ZARATHUSTRA: EIN BUCH FÜR ALLE UND KEINEN ***\n\nUpdated editions will replace the previous one—the old editions will be renamed.\n\nCreating the works from print editions not protected by U.S. copyright law means that no one owns a United States copyright in these works, so the Foundation (and you!) can copy and distribute it in the United States without permission and without paying copyright royalties. Special rules, set forth in the General Terms of Use part of this license, apply to copying and distributing Project Gutenberg™ electronic works to protect the PROJECT GUTENBERG™ concept and trademark. Project Gutenberg is a registered trademark, and may not be used if you charge for an eBook, except by following the terms of the trademark license, including paying royalties for use of the Project Gutenberg trademark. If you do not charge anything for copies of this eBook, complying with the trademark license is very easy. You may use this eBook for nearly any purpose such as creation of derivative works, reports, performances and research. Project Gutenberg eBooks may be modified and printed and given away—you may do practically ANYTHING in the United States with eBooks not protected by U.S. copyright law. Redistribution is subject to the trademark license, especially commercial redistribution.\n\nSTART: FULL LICENSE","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0001","text":"Des Morgens aber nach dieser Nacht sprang Zarathustra von seinem Lager auf, gürtete sich die Lenden und kam heraus aus seiner Höhle, glühend und stark, wie eine Morgensonne, die aus dunklen Bergen kommt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0002","text":"„Du grosses Gestirn, sprach er, wie er einstmal gesprochen hatte, du tiefes Glücks-Auge, was wäre all dein Glück, wenn du nicht Die hättest, welchen du leuchtest!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0003","text":"Und wenn sie in ihren Kammern blieben, während du schon wach bist und kommst und schenkst und austheilst: wie würde darob deine stolze Scham zürnen!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0004","text":"Wohlan! sie schlafen noch, diese höheren Menschen, während ich wach bin: das sind nicht meine rechten Gefährten! Nicht auf sie warte ich hier in meinen Bergen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0005","text":"Zu meinem Werke will ich, zu meinem Tage: aber sie verstehen nicht, was die Zeichen meines Morgens sind, mein Schritt—ist für sie kein Weckruf.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0006","text":"Sie schlafen noch in meiner Höhle, ihr Traum käut noch an meinen Mitternächten. Das Ohr, das nach mir horcht,—das gehorchende Ohr fehlt in ihren Gliedern.“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0007","text":"—Diess hatte Zarathustra zu seinem Herzen gesprochen, als die Sonne aufgieng: da blickte er fragend in die Höhe, denn er hörte über sich den scharfen Ruf seines Adlers. „Wohlan! rief er hinauf, so gefällt und gebührt es mir. Meine Thiere sind wach, denn ich bin wach.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0008","text":"Mein Adler ist wach und ehrt gleich mir die Sonne. Mit Adlers-Klauen greift er nach dem neuen Lichte. Ihr seid meine rechten Thiere; ich liebe euch.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0009","text":"Aber noch fehlen mir meine rechten Menschen!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0010","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra; da aber geschah es, dass er sich plötzlich wie von unzähligen Vögeln umschwärmt und umflattert hörte,—das Geschwirr so vieler Flügel aber und das Gedräng um sein Haupt war so gross, dass er die Augen schloss. Und wahrlich, einer Wolke gleich fiel es über ihn her, einer Wolke von Pfeilen gleich, welche sich über einen neuen Feind ausschüttet. Aber siehe, hier war es eine Wolke der Liebe, und über einen neuen Freund.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0011","text":"„Was geschieht mir?“ dachte Zarathustra in seinem erstaunten Herzen und liess sich langsam auf dem grossen Steine nieder, der neben dem Ausgange seiner Höhle lag. Aber, indem er mit den Händen um sich und über sich und unter sich griff, und den zärtlichen Vögeln wehrte, siehe, da geschah ihm etwas noch Seltsameres: er griff nämlich dabei unvermerkt in ein dichtes warmes Haar-Gezottel hinein; zugleich aber erscholl vor ihm ein Gebrüll,—ein sanftes langes Löwen-Brüllen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0012","text":"„Das Zeichen kommt,“ sprach Zarathustra und sein Herz verwandelte sich. Und in Wahrheit, als es helle vor ihm wurde, da lag ihm ein gelbes mächtiges Gethier zu Füssen und schmiegte das Haupt an seine Knie und wollte nicht von ihm lassen vor Liebe und that einem Hunde gleich, welcher seinen alten Herrn wiederfindet. Die Tauben aber waren mit ihrer Liebe nicht minder eifrig als der Löwe; und jedes Mal, wenn eine Taube über die Nase des Löwen huschte, schüttelte der Löwe das Haupt und wunderte sich und lachte dazu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0013","text":"Zu dem Allen sprach Zarathustra nur Ein Wort: „meine Kinder sind nahe, meine Kinder“—, dann wurde er ganz stumm. Sein Herz aber war gelöst, und aus seinen Augen tropften Thränen herab und fielen auf seine Hände. Und er achtete keines Dings mehr und sass da, unbeweglich und ohne dass er sich noch gegen die Thiere wehrte. Da flogen die Tauben ab und zu und setzten sich ihm auf die Schulter und liebkosten sein weisses Haar und wurden nicht müde mit Zärtlichkeit und Frohlocken. Der starke Löwe aber leckte immer die Thränen, welche auf die Hände Zarathustra’s herabfielen und brüllte und brummte schüchtern dazu. Also trieben es diese Thiere.—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0014","text":"Diess Alles dauerte eine lange Zeit, oder eine kurze Zeit: denn, recht gesprochen, giebt es für dergleichen Dinge auf Erden keine Zeit—. Inzwischen aber waren die höheren Menschen in der Höhle Zarathustra’s wach geworden und ordneten sich mit einander zu einem Zuge an, dass sie Zarathustra entgegen giengen und ihm den Morgengruss böten: denn sie hatten gefunden, als sie erwachten, dass er schon nicht mehr unter ihnen weilte. Als sie aber zur Thür der Höhle gelangten, und das Geräusch ihrer Schritte ihnen voranlief, da stutzte der Löwe gewaltig, kehrte sich mit Einem Male von Zarathustra ab und sprang, wild brüllend, auf die Höhle los; die höheren Menschen aber, als sie ihn brüllen hörten, schrien alle auf, wie mit Einem Munde, und flohen zurück und waren im Nu verschwunden.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0015","text":"Zarathustra selber aber, betäubt und fremd, erhob sich von seinem Sitze, sah um sich, stand staunend da, fragte sein Herz, besann sich und war allein. „Was hörte ich doch? sprach er endlich langsam, was geschah mir eben?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0016","text":"Und schon kam ihm die Erinnerung, und er begriff mit Einem Blicke Alles, was zwischen Gestern und Heute sich begeben hatte. „Hier ist ja der Stein, sprach er und strich sich den Bart, auf dem sass ich gestern am Morgen; und hier trat der Wahrsager zu mir, und hier hörte ich zuerst den Schrei, den ich eben hörte, den grossen Nothschrei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0017","text":"Oh ihr höheren Menschen, von eurer Noth war’s ja, dass gestern am Morgen jener alte Wahrsager mir wahrsagte,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0018","text":"—zu eurer Noth wollte er mich verfuhren und versuchen: oh Zarathustra, sprach er zu mir, ich komme, dass ich dich zu deiner letzten Sünde verführe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0019","text":"Zu meiner letzten Sünde? rief Zarathustra und lachte zornig über sein eigenes Wort: was blieb mir doch aufgespart als meine letzte Sünde?“","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0020","text":"—Und noch ein Mal versank Zarathustra in sich und setzte sich wieder auf den grossen Stein nieder und sann nach. Plötzlich sprang er empor,—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0021","text":"„Mitleiden! Das Mitleiden mit dem höheren Menschen! schrie er auf, und sein Antlitz verwandelte sich in Erz. Wohlan! Das —hatte seine Zeit!","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0022","text":"Mein Leid und mein Mitleiden—was liegt daran! Trachte ich denn nach Glücke ? Ich trachte nach meinem Werke !","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0023","text":"Wohlan! Der Löwe kam, meine Kinder sind nahe, Zarathustra ward reif, meine Stunde kam:—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0024","text":"Dies ist mein Morgen, mein Tag hebt an: herauf nun, herauf, du grosser Mittag!“—","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0025","text":"Also sprach Zarathustra und verliess seine Höhle, glühend und stark, wie eine Morgensonne, die aus dunklen Bergen kommt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0026","text":"*** END OF THE PROJECT GUTENBERG EBOOK ALSO SPRACH ZARATHUSTRA: EIN BUCH FÜR ALLE UND KEINEN ***","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0027","text":"Updated editions will replace the previous one—the old editions will be renamed.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0028","text":"Creating the works from print editions not protected by U.S. copyright law means that no one owns a United States copyright in these works, so the Foundation (and you!) can copy and distribute it in the United States without permission and without paying copyright royalties. Special rules, set forth in the General Terms of Use part of this license, apply to copying and distributing Project Gutenberg™ electronic works to protect the PROJECT GUTENBERG™ concept and trademark. Project Gutenberg is a registered trademark, and may not be used if you charge for an eBook, except by following the terms of the trademark license, including paying royalties for use of the Project Gutenberg trademark. If you do not charge anything for copies of this eBook, complying with the trademark license is very easy.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0029","text":"You may use this eBook for nearly any purpose such as creation of derivative works, reports, performances and research. Project Gutenberg eBooks may be modified and printed and given away—you may do practically ANYTHING in the United States with eBooks not protected by U.S. copyright law. Redistribution is subject to the trademark license, especially commercial redistribution.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0030","text":"START: FULL LICENSE","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"650f15f5231bcd5c54acbe42457eba833d505bc56cce70e04105377ccf128402","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T184307032185Z_70a877c8","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"The Sign","source_sha256":"650f15f5231bcd5c54acbe42457eba833d505bc56cce70e04105377ccf128402","sha256":"2c78fc722f9d6ce2fce9f2d64fb6f3c69e8e61a502739510537dd32d51c89def","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:43:07.032222+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0001","text":"But in the morning after this night, Zarathustra sprang up from his bed, girded his loins, and came out of his cave, glowing and strong, like a morning sun that emerges from dark mountains.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0002","text":"“Thou great star,” he said, as he had once said, “thou deep eye of happiness, what would all thy happiness be if thou hadst not those for whom thou shinest!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0003","text":"And if they remained in their chambers while thou art already awake and comest and givest and distributest: how would thy proud shame be angered thereby!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0004","text":"Well then! they still sleep, these higher men, while I am awake: they are not my true companions! It is not for them that I wait here in my mountains.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0005","text":"To my work I will go, to my day: but they do not understand what the signs of my morning are; my step—is no call to awaken for them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0006","text":"They still sleep in my cave; their dream still chews upon my midnights. The ear that hearkens after me,—the obedient ear is lacking in their limbs.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0007","text":"—Thus Zarathustra had spoken to his heart when the sun rose: then he looked questioningly upward, for he heard above him the piercing cry of his eagle. “Well then!” he cried upward, “thus it pleases and behooves me. My animals are awake, for I am awake.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0008","text":"My eagle is awake and, like me, honours the sun. With eagle-claws it reaches for the new light. Ye are my true animals; I love you.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0009","text":"But my true men are still lacking to me!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0010","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra; but then it happened that he suddenly heard himself as though swarmed and fluttered about by countless birds,—yet the whirring of so many wings and the thronging about his head were so great that he closed his eyes. And verily, it fell upon him like a cloud, like a cloud of arrows pouring itself out over a new enemy. But behold, here it was a cloud of love, and over a new friend.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0011","text":"“What is happening to me?” thought Zarathustra in his astonished heart, and slowly seated himself upon the great stone that lay beside the entrance to his cave. But as he felt about him and above him and beneath him with his hands, and warded off the tender birds, behold, something still stranger happened to him: for in doing so he inadvertently grasped a thick warm tangle of hair; at the same time, a roaring resounded before him,—a gentle, long lion’s roar.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0012","text":"“The sign is coming,” said Zarathustra, and his heart was transformed. And in truth, when it grew bright before him, a yellow mighty beast lay at his feet and pressed its head against his knees and would not leave him for love, behaving like a dog that finds its old master again. But the doves were no less eager in their love than the lion; and every time a dove flitted over the lion’s nose, the lion shook its head and marvelled and laughed besides.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0013","text":"To all this Zarathustra spoke only one word: “my children are near, my children”—, then he became altogether silent. But his heart was unloosed, and tears dripped down from his eyes and fell upon his hands. And he heeded nothing more and sat there, motionless and no longer warding himself against the animals. Then the doves flew to and fro and settled upon his shoulder and caressed his white hair and did not tire of tenderness and rejoicing. But the strong lion kept licking the tears that fell upon Zarathustra’s hands and roared and growled shyly the while. Thus these animals behaved.—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0014","text":"All this lasted a long time, or a short time: for, properly speaking, there is no time on earth for such things—. Meanwhile the higher men in Zarathustra’s cave had awakened and arranged themselves together in a procession, so that they might go to meet Zarathustra and offer him the morning greeting: for when they awoke, they had found that he was no longer among them. But when they came to the door of the cave, and the sound of their steps ran ahead of them, the lion started violently, turned away from Zarathustra all at once, and sprang toward the cave with a wild roar; but the higher men, when they heard him roar, all cried out as with one mouth, and fled back and vanished in an instant.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0015","text":"Zarathustra himself, however, stunned and estranged, rose from his seat, looked about him, stood there in amazement, questioned his heart, recollected himself, and was alone. “What did I hear?” he finally said slowly, “what has just happened to me?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0016","text":"And already remembrance came to him, and with a single glance he understood everything that had occurred between yesterday and today. “Here indeed is the stone,” he said, stroking his beard, “upon which I sat yesterday morning; and here the soothsayer came to me, and here I first heard the cry that I have just heard, the great cry of distress.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0017","text":"Oh ye higher men, it was of your distress that that old soothsayer prophesied to me yesterday morning,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0018","text":"—into your distress he wished to seduce and tempt me: oh Zarathustra, he said to me, I come to seduce thee to thy last sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0019","text":"To my last sin? cried Zarathustra, and laughed angrily at his own word: what then remained reserved for me but my last sin?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0020","text":"—And once more Zarathustra sank into himself and sat down again upon the great stone and pondered. Suddenly he sprang up,—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0021","text":"“Compassion! Compassion with the higher man!” he cried out, and his countenance changed into bronze. “Well then! That—had its time!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0022","text":"My suffering and my compassion—what matter are they! Do I then strive after happiness? I strive after my work!","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0023","text":"Well then! The lion came, my children are near, Zarathustra has ripened, my hour has come:—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0024","text":"This is my morning, my day begins: arise now, arise, thou great noon!”—","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0025","text":"Thus spoke Zarathustra and left his cave, glowing and strong, like a morning sun that comes from dark mountains.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0026","text":"*** END OF THE PROJECT GUTENBERG EBOOK THUS SPOKE ZARATHUSTRA: A BOOK FOR ALL AND NONE ***","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0027","text":"Updated editions will replace the previous one—the old editions will be renamed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0028","text":"Creating the works from print editions not protected by U.S. copyright law means that no one owns a United States copyright in these works, so the Foundation (and you!) can copy and distribute it in the United States without permission and without paying copyright royalties. Special rules, set forth in the General Terms of Use part of this license, apply to copying and distributing Project Gutenberg™ electronic works to protect the PROJECT GUTENBERG™ concept and trademark. Project Gutenberg is a registered trademark, and may not be used if you charge for an eBook, except by following the terms of the trademark license, including paying royalties for use of the Project Gutenberg trademark. If you do not charge anything for copies of this eBook, complying with the trademark license is very easy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0029","text":"You may use this eBook for nearly any purpose such as creation of derivative works, reports, performances and research. Project Gutenberg eBooks may be modified and printed and given away—you may do practically ANYTHING in the United States with eBooks not protected by U.S. copyright law. Redistribution is subject to the trademark license, especially commercial redistribution.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0030","text":"START: FULL LICENSE","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_97E9_DASZ:P0030"]}],"source_digest":"814bed5cd0356f55dcfeb24e733d4bc1a001d5e8ca8b919266113867bd75a243","source_language_code":"de","original_title":"Das Zeichen"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Das Zeichen","title_language_code":"en"}],"units":[{"id":"CH_97E9_1100","layer":"CH","title":"Zarathustra’s Prologue · 1","order":1,"parents":["RAW_97E9_1100"],"revision":"CH_97E9_1100@R001","sha256":"cf883b5755bbf0bb1cd1c1bf31fff351fcfdbe731a296609f271f424f9ee8a55","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:AS1","text":"Eine über zehn Jahre angesammelte Weisheit wird als Überfülle und nicht mehr als erfüllender Besitz problematisch.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:AS2","text":"Weisheit erhält ihre Wirksamkeit erst durch Empfänger, ausgestreckte Hände und Austeilung.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:AS3","text":"Die Sonne dient als Modell eines Glücks, das aus seinem Überfluss an andere übergeht.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:AS4","text":"Zarathustra bestimmt seine notwendige Bewegung als Abstieg aus der Einsamkeit in die menschliche Welt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:AS5","text":"Der Abstieg ist zugleich Selbstentäußerung, Wiederwerdung zum Menschen und Beginn eines Untergangs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:A1","text":"Glück ist nicht unabhängig von denjenigen, denen es zugutekommt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:A2","text":"Lang andauernde Einsamkeit ermöglicht die Ansammlung geistiger Überfülle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:A3","text":"Angesammelte Weisheit kann in Überdruss und Bedürfnis nach Abgabe umschlagen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:A4","text":"Überfluss verlangt nach Empfängern, an denen er sich verteilt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:A5","text":"Austeilung verwandelt Besitz in eine Wirkung, die über den Besitzer hinausreicht.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:A6","text":"Der Sonnenuntergang modelliert den Übergang vom Leuchten über anderen zum Leuchten für andere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:A7","text":"Der Zugang zu den Menschen erfordert den Abstieg aus der isolierten Höhe.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:A8","text":"Zarathustras Abstieg bedeutet zugleich Verlust einer Sonderstellung und Rückkehr zur menschlichen Existenz.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:NX1","text":"Weisheit ist nicht als abgeschlossener Eigenbesitz hinreichend.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:NX2","text":"Glück ist nicht als neidlose Selbstgenügsamkeit ohne Empfänger vollständig.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:NX3","text":"Der Weise darf seine Überfülle nicht dauerhaft zurückhalten.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:NX4","text":"Zarathustras Bewegung führt nicht weiter in die Einsamkeit, sondern aus ihr heraus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:NX5","text":"Der Abstieg ist nicht bloß räumliche Veränderung, sondern die Aufhebung einer übermenschlichen Distanz.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:NX6","text":"Das Ende der Absonderung erscheint nicht als bloße Niederlage, sondern als notwendige Erfüllung der Überfülle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:D1","text":"Weil angesammelte Weisheit in Überdruss umschlägt und Empfänger benötigt, muss Zarathustra seine Weisheit verschenken.","refs":["CH_97E9_1100:A3","CH_97E9_1100:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:D2","text":"Weil Austeilung den Besitzer überschreitet, wird die Rückkehr zu den Menschen zur Bedingung der Wirksamkeit von Zarathustras Weisheit.","refs":["CH_97E9_1100:A5","CH_97E9_1100:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:D3","text":"Weil die Sonne ihren Überfluss durch tägliches Auf- und Untergehen vermittelt, wird ihr Gang zum formalen Vorbild für Zarathustras Bewegung.","refs":["CH_97E9_1100:A6","CH_97E9_1100:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:D4","text":"Zarathustras Untergang bezeichnet den Übergang von gespeicherter Weisheit zu mitgeteiltem Wissen.","refs":["CH_97E9_1100:A3","CH_97E9_1100:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:D5","text":"Die Wiederwerdung zum Menschen ist die soziale Form der Selbstentäußerung.","refs":["CH_97E9_1100:A7","CH_97E9_1100:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:SD1","text":"Das System ersetzt das Ideal der gesteigerten Selbstbewahrung durch ein Ideal der wirksamen Verausgabung.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:SD2","text":"Die höchste geistige Position wird nicht durch Distanz, sondern durch kontrollierten Abstieg bestimmt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:SD3","text":"Überfluss wird erst dann positiv bestimmt, wenn er sich in fremde Bereicherung und eigene Entleerung übersetzt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:SD4","text":"Der Untergang fungiert als produktive Transformation: Die Auflösung der Sonderstellung erzeugt die Möglichkeit menschlicher Wirkung.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:DNX1","text":"Eine Weisheit, die bei ihrem Besitzer verbleibt, gilt strukturell als unvollendet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:DNX2","text":"Ein Glück ohne Bedürftige, Empfänger oder Teilnehmende wird als unzureichendes Glück ausgeschlossen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:DNX3","text":"Geistige Höhe ohne anschließende Rückkehr in die menschliche Sphäre kann keine generative Funktion erfüllen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:DNX4","text":"Besitzlogik und Mitteilungslogik werden nicht als gleichwertige Formen geistiger Existenz behandelt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:DNX5","text":"Die Identität des Weisen als dauerhaft vom Menschen Getrennten wird als nicht aufrechterhaltbar vorausgesetzt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_97E9_1100:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"repaired-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_97E9_1100:SNX1","tex